        hemo:6000335,
        hemo:6000336,
        hemo:6000337 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000329,
        hemo:6000330,
        hemo:6000331,
        hemo:6000332,
        hemo:6000333,
        hemo:6000334,
        hemo:6000335,
        hemo:6000336 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000337 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the evidence held at an interim look of an ongoing trial, the size of the remaining sample, the population whose treatment the result will inform and the cost of continuing to the expected gain from continuing the trial over stopping it now. Vervaart and colleagues define the value of extending an ongoing study as the expectation, over the further data given the data already collected, of the largest posterior expected net benefit, less the largest expected net benefit given the data so far, so the baseline is the decision that would be made on the interim evidence (their equation 2). The general EVSI is HE-FN-EVSI-001, and for normal incremental net benefit the live formulas apply with interim inputs, the interim posterior mean in place of the prior mean in the closed form of HE-FM-EVSI-003 and the interim effective sample size in place of n0 in the preposterior variance of HE-FM-EVSI-002, with population scaling as in HE-FM-EVSI-004. The interim normal update is HE-FM-BAD-001, whose precision form gives the effective sample size form of Chick and colleagues when the prior variance is the sampling variance per pair divided by n0 and the data variance is that variance divided by the pairs observed. ENBS and its cost terms are HE-FN-ENBS-001 and HE-FM-ENBS-001 to HE-FM-ENBS-003, and the interim EVPI ceiling is HE-FM-EVPI-004 at the posterior standard deviation. This package adds the one-stage comparison of continuing with stopping in the model of Chick, Forster and Pertile with a switching cost and online learning, and its computational function runs the decision for one interim look. The net gain of continuing with the participants' opportunity loss is HE-FM-ENBS-001 for the remaining pairs, with the cost of HE-FM-ENBS-002 at n equal to 2m, a variable cost of c/2 per participant, a share of one half on the arm expected to be inferior and the cost of the further analysis in place of the committed fixed cost, so it is computed in the computational function and not restated. Group sequential stopping on clinical evidence by alpha spending (HE-FN-ASP-001) is a different rule. Notation follows the Adaptive Value of Information article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Interim value of information function for the stop or continue decision in an ongoing trial" ;
    hemo:9000014 "g(mu_t, n_t, m, sigma_X, P, c, I, d_on) = Delta" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-AVOI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "interim-value-of-information-function-for-the-stop-or-continue-decision-in-an-ongoing-trial" .

hemo:5000157 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Population mean outcome of a cohort model run by stratum against one cohort at averaged inputs"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-COHM-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000351,
        hemo:6000352,
        hemo:6000353,
        hemo:6000354,
        hemo:6000355,
        hemo:6000356 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000351,
        hemo:6000352,
        hemo:6000353 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000354,
        hemo:6000355,
        hemo:6000356 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the shares of a starting population in two strata and the constant per-cycle death probability of each stratum to the population mean life expectancy found by running each stratum as its own cohort and weighting the results, to the life expectancy of one cohort run at the share-weighted mean probability, and to the gap between the two. HE-CF-COHM-001 carries the same comparison through a treatment that cuts each probability in proportion, to the QALY gains and ICERs of the article's worked example. TSD 15 states that a cohort run on average characteristics gives a biased estimate of the population mean where outcomes are non-linear in those characteristics, and the ISPOR-SMDM report states that outputs for a population-based starting cohort need one run for each stratum, aggregated across strata. Existing records are cited and not restated. The article's constant-risk life expectancy, 1/q minus one half, is the one-state case of HE-FM-ABS-001, whose 1/q cycles equal the mean geometric stay L_j of HE-FM-MKCH-002 and D_mean of HE-FM-MKA-004 with cycles of one year, less the half cycle that HE-ER-ABS-002 and HE-CF-MKCH-001 subtract for a cohort counted at the start of each cycle. The life years one stratum gains from a treatment are the difference of two values of that life expectancy, the ICER of the resulting QALY gain is HE-FM-ICER-001, and a proportional cut e gives the intervention risk of HE-FM-ARR-002 with a relative risk of 1 minus e, so none of these is restated. The share-weighted population effect is HE-FM-ATE-001, the value of deciding by subgroup is HE-FM-ATE-004, and HE-ER-CHN-002 shows why subgroup ICERs are not averaged. The cohort update and trace totals are HE-FM-MM-001, HE-FN-CSIM-001, HE-FM-CSIM-001 and HE-CF-CSIM-001, the second-order approximation to the mean of a curved output is HE-FM-EV-002, the background hazard of a mixed-sex cohort is HE-FM-BGM-003 with the error of reading a hazard at the mean age in HE-ER-BGM-007, and continuous-time life expectancy at constant rates is HE-FM-CONT-002. Notation follows the Cohort Model article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Population mean outcome of a cohort model run by stratum against one cohort at averaged inputs" ;
    hemo:9000014 "k(w, q_1, q_2) = (L_strat, L_avg, gap_L)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-COHM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "population-mean-outcome-of-a-cohort-model-run-by-stratum-against-one-cohort-at-averaged-inputs" .

hemo:5000162 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Decision uncertainty function giving the probability that the expected-value choice is wrong"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-DCU-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the uncertain net benefit of each option at a stated cost-effectiveness threshold and returns P_error, the probability that the option chosen on expected net benefit is not the option with the highest net benefit, the decision uncertainty that PMG36 section 4.7.2 asks a model to quantify. From a table of probabilistic draws P_error is one minus the acceptability of the chosen option, live as HE-FM-CEAC-003 (function HE-FN-CEAC-001), where the share N_d*/N of the article is the multi-option CEAC of HE-FM-CEAC-002 or the two-option CEAC of HE-FM-CEAC-001, and the draws procedure for three options is HE-CF-EVPI-001. With two options and a normal incremental net benefit the acceptability of the chosen option is the standard normal cumulative probability at the absolute mean divided by its standard deviation, the probability of HE-FM-AN-004 read for the chosen option, so P_error is the upper normal tail at that ratio, the same tail that enters the loss term of HE-FM-EVPI-004. That combination of live records is cited, not restated. The split of EVPI into P_error times the mean loss when wrong is HE-FM-EVPI-003, EVPI itself is HE-FM-VOI-001 and HE-FM-EVPI-001, population EVPI is HE-FM-VOI-002, the incremental net monetary benefit of the article's worked example is HE-FM-NMB-002 and the maximum net benefit rule is HE-FM-NMB-003. The Monte Carlo standard error of an error probability estimated from N draws is the binomial standard error of HE-FM-BINOM-004 with N in place of n, and the number of runs belongs to the PSA package, so neither is restated. This package adds the error probability read from the p-value of a test of incremental net benefit. Notation follows the Decision Uncertainty article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Decision uncertainty function giving the probability that the expected-value choice is wrong" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d(NB_d(theta), lambda) = P_error" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-DCU-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "decision-uncertainty-function-giving-the-probability-that-the-expected-value-choice-is-wrong" .

hemo:5000166 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Life years gained as the area between two survival curves and the share observed within a trial"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-LYG-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000407,
        hemo:6000408,
        hemo:6000409,
        hemo:6000410,
        hemo:6000411,
        hemo:6000412,
        hemo:6000413,
        hemo:6000414 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000407,
        hemo:6000408,
        hemo:6000409,
        hemo:6000410 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000411,
        hemo:6000412,
        hemo:6000413,
        hemo:6000414 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the survival curves of an intervention and its comparator, a trial follow-up time tau and a model horizon T to the life years gained within follow-up, the life years gained over the horizon, the share of that gain lying within follow-up and the gain lying after it. The gain over a horizon is the area between the two curves, the integral of S_1(t) minus S_0(t), which Royston and Parmar give as the difference in restricted mean survival time between two arms. Existing records are cited and not restated. The area under one curve is the restricted mean survival time of HE-FM-ADMC-003 for a Kaplan-Meier step function, the interval area A_j of HE-FM-BTH-001 under a constant hazard and the trapezoid life years of HE-CF-PSM-001, so the undiscounted gain is the difference of two such areas. The discounted gain is the present value of a stream, HE-FM-DR-002, applied to the yearly gains; the article's step 3, with year 1 undiscounted, places the gain of year k at time k minus 1 (t starting at 0 in HE-FM-DR-002, the start-of-year timing of HE-FM-DISC-002), whereas HE-CF-PSM-001 discounts each interval at its midpoint. The share of a gain that discounting removes, 1 minus the discounted total over the undiscounted total, equals rise divided by (1 plus rise) for the level stream of HE-CF-DISC-001 (derived here), so it is not drafted either. The cost per life year gained is HE-FM-ICER-001 with life years as the effect, and the QALY gain when both arms live at one constant utility is that utility times the life years gained, the constant-utility case of HE-FM-QALY-001 and of HE-FM-PSM-003 with equal state utilities. Mean survival with a cure fraction is HE-FN-CRM-001, the expected cycles alive of a constant-risk cohort follow from HE-FM-ABS-001, and the share of one arm's mean survival that comes from an exponential tail is P_tail of HE-FM-ADMC-004. Discounted quality-adjusted life expectancy belongs to the QALY Shortfall concept. Notation follows the Life Years Gained article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Life years gained as the area between two survival curves and the share observed within a trial" ;
    hemo:9000014 "G(S_1, S_0, tau, T) = (dRMST, dLY_life, phi, dLY_beyond)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-LYG-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "life-years-gained-as-the-area-between-two-survival-curves-and-the-share-observed-within-a-trial" .

hemo:5000169 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Landmark survival comparison by a status reached during follow-up and the immortal-time bias of grouping from the start"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-LMA-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000437,
        hemo:6000438,
        hemo:6000439,
        hemo:6000440,
        hemo:6000441,
        hemo:6000442,
        hemo:6000443,
        hemo:6000444 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000437,
        hemo:6000438,
        hemo:6000439,
        hemo:6000440,
        hemo:6000441 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000442,
        hemo:6000443,
        hemo:6000444 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps follow-up data for patients grouped by a status reached after follow-up begins, such as tumour response, to a comparison of the groups that does not credit the time before the status to one group, made either by restarting the clock at a landmark time s for the patients still event free at s or by splitting person-time at the change of status, and to the bias of the naive grouping from the start. HE-FM-LMA-002 gives that bias as a factor F in a constant-hazard rate ratio of responders when the person-time responders spent before responding is counted as responder time, each rate ratio having the form of HE-FM-HR-005. The article's conditional survival S(t | T > s) = S(t) / S(s) is the live HE-FM-LTR-001, S_cond = S_t / S_l with the entry time l in the place of the landmark s, and is not restated. The article's whole-cohort value 0.495 / 0.85, about 0.582, is that formula applied, and so are its landmark proportions, since in the naive non-responder group of 130 patients survival to the landmark is 100/130 and 0.385 divided by 100/130 is 0.50. The fall in the naive survival gap is HE-FM-LTR-001 again, because the responder proportion 49/70 is the same in both groupings and the gap falls by S_cond minus S_t of the naive non-responder group, 0.50 minus 0.385, the 11.5 points between the article's gaps of 31.5 and 20 points; each gap has the form of HE-FM-ARR-001. The same ratio is the residual stay R_ab of HE-FM-MKA-002 and S_res of HE-FM-MKA-003 for geometric and exponential stays, and one minus it over a single cycle is the cycle probability of HE-FN-CUMH-001 and HE-FM-CUMH-002, since 1 minus exp(minus (H(t) minus H(t minus Delta))) equals 1 minus S(t) / S(t minus Delta). Relative survival HE-FM-BGM-001 divides by expected background survival at the same time, not by the cohort's own survival at an earlier time, so it is a different ratio. Landmark survival from patient records is the left-truncated Kaplan-Meier curve of HE-CF-LTR-001 run in each status group with every entry time set to s, built on the product-limit step of HE-FM-ADMC-002. The landmark Cox model h(t | x(s), T > s) = h_0s(t) exp(beta_s x(s)) is an ordinary Cox model fitted to the patients at risk at s, with its hazard ratio from the coefficient as in HE-FM-HR-002; it has no closed-form calculator line and is given for reading only. Notation follows the Landmark Analysis article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Landmark survival comparison by a status reached during follow-up and the immortal-time bias of grouping from the start" ;
    hemo:9000014 "k(d_R, PT_R, I, d_N, PT_N) = (RR_naive, RR_split, F)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-LMA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "landmark-survival-comparison-by-a-status-reached-during-follow-up-and-the-immortal-time-bias-of-grouping-from-the-start" .

hemo:5000172 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Delta-adjusted analysis of QALYs lost to follow-up with its tipping-point shift"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-LTFU-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000462,
        hemo:6000463,
        hemo:6000464,
        hemo:6000465,
        hemo:6000466,
        hemo:6000467,
        hemo:6000468,
        hemo:6000469,
        hemo:6000470,
        hemo:6000471,
        hemo:6000472 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000462,
        hemo:6000463,
        hemo:6000464,
        hemo:6000465,
        hemo:6000466,
        hemo:6000467,
        hemo:6000468,
        hemo:6000469 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000470,
        hemo:6000471,
        hemo:6000472 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes each arm's mean QALYs among participants still followed, the proportion of each arm lost to follow-up, an assumed shift delta in the mean QALYs of those lost from the new treatment arm, a ratio r that sets the shift for those lost from usual care at r times delta, the incremental cost and a cost-effectiveness threshold, and returns the delta-adjusted QALY gain and ICER under that shift and the tipping-point shift at which the conclusion changes. The arm mean over everyone randomised, E_bar, is one minus p times E_obs plus p times (E_obs plus delta), which equals E_obs plus p times delta. That is the two-part mixture of HE-FM-LOCF-001 with tau read as p, mu_end as E_obs and mu_last as E_obs plus delta, so its bias_mu, tau times (mu_last minus mu_end), becomes p times delta. The QALY gain under shifts in both arms is Delta_tilde of HE-FM-LOCF-002 with the same reading in each arm, the observed gain plus p_T times delta_T minus p_C times delta_C, here with delta_T equal to delta and delta_C equal to r times delta. The ICER under a shift is HE-FM-ICER-001 and the gain needed at the threshold, Delta_C / lambda, is H_d of HE-FM-CET-002. These live records are cited, not restated, and HE-CF-LTFU-001 runs them in sequence for the article's scenarios. The inverse probability of censoring weights of HE-FN-DCEN-001 and HE-FM-DCEN-001 to HE-FM-DCEN-004 assume that censoring is unrelated to the unseen outcome once measured covariates are allowed for, and the administrative censoring time of HE-FM-ADMC-001 is set by design, so neither represents a departure from missing at random. This package adds the shift at which the delta-adjusted QALY gain falls to Delta_C / lambda. Notation follows the Loss to Follow-Up article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Delta-adjusted analysis of QALYs lost to follow-up with its tipping-point shift" ;
    hemo:9000014 "m(E_obs_T, E_obs_C, p_T, p_C, delta, r, Delta_C, lambda) = (Delta_E, ICER, delta_star)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-LTFU-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "delta-adjusted-analysis-of-qalys-lost-to-follow-up-with-its-tipping-point-shift" .

hemo:5000173 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Case-mix adjusted provider cost comparison with a ranking reversal rule"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-CMAD-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000473,
        hemo:6000474,
        hemo:6000475,
        hemo:6000476,
        hemo:6000477,
        hemo:6000478,
        hemo:6000479,
        hemo:6000480,
        hemo:6000481,
        hemo:6000482 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000473,
        hemo:6000474,
        hemo:6000475,
        hemo:6000476,
        hemo:6000477,
        hemo:6000478 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000479,
        hemo:6000480,
        hemo:6000481,
        hemo:6000482 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the actual costs, case numbers and expected costs at national mean unit costs of two providers to the ratio of their crude costs per case, the ratio of their expected costs per case, the ratio of their indirectly standardised cost indices and a flag for a crude ranking that the adjustment reverses. The article's three methods are live and are cited, not restated. Cost per weighted case, C_h divided by the sum of the case weights, is C_h / CM_h with CM_h = sum n_hg w_g of HE-FM-CWT-003, equal to c_bar times R_h, and the case mix index CMI_h = CM_h / n_h is an output of HE-CF-CWT-001, so the article's (C_h / n_h) / CMI_h is the same quantity. With the weights w_g = c_bar_g / c_bar of HE-FM-CWT-001 the expected cost E_h = sum n_hg c_bar_g equals CM_h times c_bar, so the cost index I_h = 100 A_h / E_h is 100 times R_h of HE-FM-CWT-003, and HE-FM-ALOS-004 applies the same indirect standardisation to bed-days. The PROMs adjusted score Adj_j is the mean over a provider's patients of the need-standardised value of HE-FM-HEQ-002, y_i minus yX_i plus y_bar, with the case-mix prediction in place of yX_i. Direct standardisation on a common mix is the weighted sum of HE-FM-INEQ-001, with case groups in place of age groups, unit costs in place of rates and the pooled case shares as the standard. HE-ER-CWT-004 and HE-ER-ALOS-006 show in numbers a crude ranking that adjustment reverses, and this package adds the condition under which the reversal happens. HE-AS-CWT-006 states that up-coding raises CM_h and lowers R_h with no change in efficiency. The article's upcoding step is HE-FM-CWT-003 recomputed on the recoded counts, an expected cost of E_h plus the number of cases moved times the gap between the two groups' national mean unit costs, so it is cited, not restated. The case mix index and the observed-to-expected benchmark comparison have their own pages. Notation follows the Case-Mix Adjustment article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Case-mix adjusted provider cost comparison with a ranking reversal rule" ;
    hemo:9000014 "v(A_1, n_1, E_1, A_2, n_2, E_2) = (rho, mu, RI, rev)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-CMAD-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "case-mix-adjusted-provider-cost-comparison-with-a-ranking-reversal-rule" .

hemo:5000174 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Function for the negative likelihood ratio of a negative result on two tests with conditional dependence"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-NLR-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000483,
        hemo:6000484,
        hemo:6000485,
        hemo:6000486,
        hemo:6000487,
        hemo:6000488,
        hemo:6000489 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000483,
        hemo:6000484,
        hemo:6000485,
        hemo:6000486,
        hemo:6000487,
        hemo:6000488 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000489 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the sensitivities Se_1 and Se_2 and specificities Sp_1 and Sp_2 of two tests and their conditional covariances, C_pos among people with the condition and C_neg among people without it, and returns the negative likelihood ratio LR_neg_12 of a negative result on both, through the combined sensitivity and specificity of the rule that calls a person positive when either test is positive. LR_neg = (1 minus Se) / Sp is live as HE-FM-DXA-002, and the post-test probability after a negative result, one minus the negative predictive value of HE-FM-DXA-003, is the odds update that HE-VT-DXA-006 checks. The highest pre-test probability at which a negative result brings the risk down to p*, p* / (p* + (1 minus p*) LR_neg), and the largest ratio that does so at a pre-test probability p, p* (1 minus p) / ((1 minus p*) p), are that live update with one minus the negative predictive value set to p* and solved for p or for the ratio, so they are cited here and not drafted (derived). In the article's example the first is about 21.9%, and it is also the pre-test probability above which confirming everyone beats testing first when the triage test is free (derived). The article's threshold p* = C_conf / L_miss is the decision-curve threshold HE-FM-DCA-004 with G = L_miss minus C_conf and L = C_conf (derived). The pathway's net monetary benefit against confirming everyone and its break-even pre-test probability are HE-FM-DXA-005 and HE-CF-DXA-001 with confirming everyone entered as a test of sensitivity 1, specificity 0 and no cost (derived), which returns the article's 19.0%. Deeks and Altman note that likelihood ratios can be treated like risk ratios for confidence intervals, so the interval for LR_neg is HE-FM-PWMA-001 with a = FN, n1 = TP + FN, c = TN and n2 = TN + FP, exponentiated as in HE-FM-HR-006. Notation follows the Negative Likelihood Ratio article, with the two-test symbols added here." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Function for the negative likelihood ratio of a negative result on two tests with conditional dependence" ;
    hemo:9000014 "f(Se_1, Se_2, Sp_1, Sp_2, C_pos, C_neg) = LR_neg_12" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-NLR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "function-for-the-negative-likelihood-ratio-of-a-negative-result-on-two-tests-with-conditional-dependence" .

hemo:5000176 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-alternative incremental cost and effect"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EE-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000002 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000015 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000494,
        hemo:6000495,
        hemo:6000496,
        hemo:6000497,
        hemo:6000498,
        hemo:6000499 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000494,
        hemo:6000495,
        hemo:6000496,
        hemo:6000497 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000498,
        hemo:6000499 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000012,
        hemo:7000013,
        hemo:7000014 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000016,
        hemo:7000017,
        hemo:7000018,
        hemo:7000019 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Calculates the differences in cost and effect when a new intervention is compared with a relevant alternative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-alternative incremental cost and effect" ;
    hemo:9000010 "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 2022; last updated 31 March 2026. Chapter 4 Economic evaluation, table 4.1 (summary of the reference case) and section 4.2.16 (ICERs as expected additional total cost over expected additional QALYs). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Using average instead of incremental results. Comparing separate average costs or outcomes does not show the change produced by choosing one alternative instead of another." ;
    hemo:9000014 "Delta_C = C_new - C_comparator; Delta_E = E_new - E_comparator" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EE-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-alternative-incremental-cost-and-effect" .

hemo:5000177 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Sequential incremental cost-effectiveness ratio"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CEA-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000003 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000025 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000500,
        hemo:6000501,
        hemo:6000502,
        hemo:6000503,
        hemo:6000504 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000500,
