    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Values time alive by closeness to death instead of by progression status, using only the OS curve. Over a lifetime horizon each person's time in the final window of length a before death is the smaller of a and their survival time, and its mean W is the area under the OS curve from 0 to a. For an exponential OS curve W is (1 minus exp(minus lambda_OS a)) divided by lambda_OS, and the remaining 1 divided by lambda_OS minus W years are valued at the utility for time further from death." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Partitioned survival QALYs with utilities by time to death" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Hatswell AJ, Pennington B, Pericleous L, Rowen D, Lebmeier M, Lee D. Patient-reported utilities in advanced or metastatic melanoma, including analysis of utilities by time to death. Health and Quality of Life Outcomes. 2014;12:140. Abstract and Results: in patient-level data from the ipilimumab MDX010-20 trial, utility stayed high until about 180 days before death and fell in the final months; time to death gave similar or better predictions of patient utility than progression status, and including both improved model fit. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12955-014-0140-1",
        "Woods B, Sideris E, Palmer S, Latimer N, Soares M. NICE DSU Technical Support Document 19: partitioned survival analysis for decision modelling in health care: a critical review. Sheffield: Decision Support Unit, ScHARR, University of Sheffield; 2017. Section 2.2: the area under the extrapolated OS curve provides an estimate of mean life expectancy, the quantity split here into time near to and further from death. https://doi.org/10.15131/shef.data.33848653" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Adding a progression decrement to time-to-death utilities from separate curves. Combining progression and time-to-death utilities needs the joint timing of progression and death, for example how much progressed time falls inside the final window. Separate PFS and OS curves do not provide it, so a combined structure needs patient-level data or extra assumptions.",
        "Applying the lifetime window formula over a truncated horizon. Over a five-year horizon in the standard-care example, about 8.2% of the cohort is still alive at the end and has not reached its final window within the model. Taking the area under OS to five years with the lifetime W gives about 1.2245 QALYs, against about 1.2325 when only the part of each final window inside the horizon is counted. The difference comes only from people still alive at the horizon." ;
    hemo:9000014 "W = (1 - exp(-lambda_OS * a)) / lambda_OS; QALY_TTD = u_far * (1 / lambda_OS - W) + u_near * W" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-PSM-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "partitioned-survival-qalys-with-utilities-by-time-to-death" .

hemo:5000595 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Non-mixture cure model all-cause survival in the relative survival form"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CRM-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000124 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003205 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002503,
        hemo:6002504,
        hemo:6002505,
        hemo:6002506 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002504,
        hemo:6002505,
        hemo:6002506 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002503 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003203,
        hemo:7003204 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003206,
        hemo:7003207,
        hemo:7003208,
        hemo:7003209 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Bounds the excess cumulative hazard instead of splitting the cohort: relative survival is the cure fraction raised to the power F_z(t), a cumulative distribution function that is 0 at the start and approaches 1 as time tends to infinity, so relative survival falls from 1 towards pi. Multiplying by expected survival gives all-cause survival. TSD 21 gives pi to the power F_z(t) as equation 12 and notes that the model extends to the relative survival setting in the same way as the mixture model." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Non-mixture cure model all-cause survival in the relative survival form" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Latimer NR, Rutherford MJ. Mixture and non-mixture cure models for health technology assessment. PharmacoEconomics. 2024;42(10):1073-1090. Section on non-mixture cure models (no split into cured and uncured groups, no survival model specific to uncured patients) and discussion of choosing boundary knots (a 5-year boundary knot gave hazards that fell steeply at around 2 years). https://doi.org/10.1007/s40273-024-01406-7",
        "Rutherford MJ, Lambert PC, Sweeting MJ, Pennington B, Crowther MJ, Abrams KR, Latimer NR. NICE DSU Technical Support Document 21: Flexible methods for survival analysis. Sheffield: Decision Support Unit, ScHARR, University of Sheffield; 2020 (updated March 2022). Section 3.6.2, equation 12, the non-mixture model S(t) = pi to the power F_z(t) with F_z a cumulative distribution function, its extension to the relative survival setting by incorporating expected survival, and the cure time fixed through the final boundary knot of a flexible parametric cure model. https://doi.org/10.15131/shef.data.33849118" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Putting a survival function in the exponent of the non-mixture cure model. F_z(t) must be a cumulative distribution function. Its complement, a survival function, makes relative survival start at pi and rise to 1: with pi = 0.25 the model would give relative survival of 0.25 at time zero instead of 1.",
        "Setting the non-mixture cure time earlier than the data support. Fixing the final boundary knot early forces the excess hazard down too soon. Latimer and Rutherford report that a 5-year boundary knot gave hazards that fell steeply at around 2 years, so the knot position needs clinical justification and a check of the fitted hazard." ;
    hemo:9000014 "S = S_star * pi^F" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CRM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "non-mixture-cure-model-all-cause-survival-in-the-relative-survival-form" .

hemo:5000598 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected value of a product of two correlated quantities"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EV-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000125 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003230 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002518,
        hemo:6002519,
        hemo:6002520,
        hemo:6002521 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002519,
        hemo:6002520,
        hemo:6002521 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002518 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003228,
        hemo:7003229 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003231,
        hemo:7003232,
        hemo:7003233,
        hemo:7003234 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives the expected value of a product, such as the number of admissions times the cost per admission, from the two means and their covariance. The identity is exact for any joint distribution with finite variances. The product of the means equals the expected product only when the covariance is zero, for example when the two quantities are independent; a positive covariance raises the expected product above the product of the means and a negative one lowers it." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected value of a product of two correlated quantities" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Casella G, Berger RL. Statistical Inference. 2nd ed. Pacific Grove, CA: Duxbury; 2002 (reprinted Boca Raton: Chapman and Hall/CRC; 2024). Section 4.5, covariance and correlation: the covariance of X and Y equals E[XY] minus E[X]E[Y], so the expected product is the product of the means plus the covariance; independent variables have zero covariance. Textbook result. https://doi.org/10.1201/9781003456285" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Combining admission counts and unit costs from separate sources as if independent. When counts and unit costs come from different studies, the covariance cannot be estimated and is set to zero without being stated. The resulting expected cost rests on an independence assumption that patient-level data recording both quantities could check.",
        "Multiplying mean admissions by mean cost per admission. Multiplying the two means drops the covariance: £4,500 in place of £4,800 per patient in the article's example, an understatement of 6.25 per cent of the expected cost that carries into every budget built on it." ;
    hemo:9000014 "E_NU = E_N * E_U + Cov_NU" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EV-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-value-of-a-product-of-two-correlated-quantities" .

hemo:5000603 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Potential years of life lost before a cut-off age for deaths in one age group"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-YLL-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000126 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003280 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002538,
        hemo:6002539,
        hemo:6002540,
        hemo:6002541 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002539,
        hemo:6002540,
        hemo:6002541 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002538 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003278,
        hemo:7003279 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003281,
        hemo:7003282,
        hemo:7003283,
        hemo:7003284,
        hemo:7003285 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Counts, for each death before a cut-off age, the years between the midpoint of its age group and the cut-off, and nothing for deaths at or above the cut-off, following the method described by the US Centers for Disease Control (CDC). Unlike HE-FM-YLL-001 it uses no life table, so deaths in old age drop out of the total. The function max returns the larger of its arguments." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Potential years of life lost before a cut-off age for deaths in one age group" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Centers for Disease Control. Years of potential life lost before ages 65 and 85: United States, 1989-1990. Morbidity and Mortality Weekly Report. 1992;41(18):313-315 (full text read). The measure reflects deaths before a conventional cut-off, usually 65 years, and is calculated by multiplying the deaths in an age category by the difference between the cut-off age and the mid-year of the age category, then adding the products. https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/00016649.htm",
        "World Health Organization. WHO methods and data sources for global burden of disease estimates 2000-2021. Global Health Estimates Technical Paper WHO/DDI/DNA/GHE/2024.3. Geneva: WHO; 2024 (full text read). Section 2: the DALY extends the Potential Years of Life Lost measure to include lost good health, and the GBD 1990 study chose not to use an arbitrary age cut-off such as 70 years for the loss function used for YLLs. https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/gho-documents/global-health-estimates/ghe2021_daly_methods.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Comparing potential years of life lost computed with different cut-off ages. The same 100 deaths at ages 75 to 79 count nothing with a cut-off of 70 and 750 years with 85, so totals and rankings change with the cut-off. WHO records that GBD 1990 chose not to use an arbitrary cut-off such as 70 years.",
        "Reading potential years of life lost as years of life lost. In the article's example potential years of life lost before 70 total 2,715 against 5,169.1 YLLs under GBD 2010, because the cut-off drops the older deaths and no life table is used. The two measures answer different questions, and only YLLs enter the DALY.",
        "Using the lower bound of the age group in place of its midpoint. Placing the 20 deaths at ages 1 to 4 at age 1 in place of 3 gives 1,380 potential years before 70 in place of 1,340 (computed here for illustration), and the bias grows with wider age groups." ;
    hemo:9000014 "PYLL_a = N_a * max(C - m_a, 0)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-YLL-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "potential-years-of-life-lost-before-a-cut-off-age-for-deaths-in-one-age-group" .

hemo:5000610 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Prevalence odds ratio from a prevalence ratio and the unexposed prevalence"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-PR-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000130 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003347 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002574,
        hemo:6002575,
        hemo:6002576,
        hemo:6002577 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002575,
        hemo:6002576 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002574 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003345,
        hemo:7003346 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003348,
        hemo:7003349,
        hemo:7003350,
        hemo:7003351,
        hemo:7003352 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Converts a prevalence ratio into the prevalence odds ratio that logistic regression reports for the same cross-sectional data, given the prevalence among the unexposed. The cross-product a d / (b c) of the two-by-two table gives the same value. The factor (1 minus P_0) / (1 minus P_1) exceeds 1 whenever P_1 exceeds P_0 and falls below 1 whenever P_1 is lower, so the POR lies further from 1 than the PR whenever the two prevalences differ." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Prevalence odds ratio from a prevalence ratio and the unexposed prevalence" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Barros AJD, Hirakata VN. Alternatives for logistic regression in cross-sectional studies: an empirical comparison of models that directly estimate the prevalence ratio. BMC Medical Research Methodology. 2003;3:21. Background (the odds ratio can strongly overestimate the PR for frequent outcomes; controlling for confounding is not equivalent) and Methods (log-binomial convergence, Poisson regression with robust variance). https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2288-3-21",
        "Tamhane AR, Westfall AO, Burkholder GA, Cutter GR. Prevalence odds ratio versus prevalence ratio: choice comes with consequences. Statistics in Medicine. 2016;35(30):5730-5735. Table 3: POR = ad/bc and POR = [1 minus c/(c+d)] / [1 minus a/(a+b)] x PR; Discussion: the two are closer when the outcome is rare. https://doi.org/10.1002/sim.7059",
        "Zocchetti C, Consonni D, Bertazzi PA. Relationship between prevalence rate ratios and odds ratios in cross-sectional studies. International Journal of Epidemiology. 1997;26(1):220-223. Abstract: the POR is always further from the null value than the prevalence rate ratio, and the discrepancy depends much more on the prevalence of the condition than on that of the exposure. https://doi.org/10.1093/ije/26.1.220" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Falling back to logistic regression when a prevalence ratio model fails. Log-binomial models that estimate an adjusted PR may fail to converge, most often with a continuous covariate, several categorical covariates or a high prevalence. Switching silently to logistic regression changes the reported measure from a PR to a POR. Poisson regression with a robust sandwich variance estimates the PR directly.",
        "Reporting a prevalence odds ratio as a prevalence ratio. With the survey prevalences of 0.30 and 0.15, the POR of 2.43 is about 21% above the PR of 2.00. Read as a relative prevalence, or as a relative risk beside true relative risks when priorities are set, it exaggerates the association, as Barros and Hirakata warn for frequent outcomes in cross-sectional studies." ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_1 = PR * P_0; POR = PR * (1 - P_0) / (1 - P_1)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-PR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "prevalence-odds-ratio-from-a-prevalence-ratio-and-the-unexposed-prevalence" .

hemo:5000612 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Subgroup prevalences from an overall prevalence, an exposed share and a prevalence ratio"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-PR-004" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000130 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003363 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002581,
        hemo:6002582,
        hemo:6002583,
        hemo:6002584,
        hemo:6002585,
        hemo:6002586,
        hemo:6002587 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002584,
        hemo:6002585,
        hemo:6002586,
        hemo:6002587 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002583 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003361,
        hemo:7003362 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003364,
        hemo:7003365,
        hemo:7003366,
        hemo:7003367,
        hemo:7003368 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Recovers the prevalence among the unexposed and among the exposed when local records give only the overall prevalence and the exposure mix, and a published PR gives the relative prevalence. It rearranges P = e P_1 + (1 minus e) P_0 with P_1 = PR x P_0, and multiplies the exposed prevalence by the exposed population to size an eligible population for a budget impact analysis." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Subgroup prevalences from an overall prevalence, an exposed share and a prevalence ratio" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Sullivan SD, Mauskopf JA, Augustovski F, Jaime Caro J, Lee KM, Minchin M, et al. Budget impact analysis-principles of good practice: report of the ISPOR 2012 Budget Impact Analysis Good Practice II Task Force. Value in Health. 2014;17(1):5-14. Abstract: estimating the size of the eligible population is a key element, using data that reflect the decision maker's population. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2013.08.2291" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Multiplying the overall prevalence by the prevalence ratio. Treating the overall prevalence as the unexposed baseline overstates the exposed prevalence. In the article's neighbouring area it gives 0.435 instead of 0.30 and an illustrative 39,150 eligible exposed cases instead of 27,000." ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_0 = P / (1 + e * (PR - 1)); P_1 = PR * P_0; n_1 = N * e * P_1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-PR-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "subgroup-prevalences-from-an-overall-prevalence-an-exposed-share-and-a-prevalence-ratio" .

hemo:5000614 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Economically justifiable price range between a lower and an upper threshold"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EJP-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000131 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003386 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002594,
        hemo:6002595,
        hemo:6002596,
        hemo:6002597,
        hemo:6002598,
        hemo:6002599,
        hemo:6002600 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002596,
        hemo:6002597,
        hemo:6002598,
        hemo:6002599,
        hemo:6002600 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002594,
        hemo:6002595 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003384,
        hemo:7003385 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003387,
        hemo:7003388,
        hemo:7003389,
        hemo:7003390,
        hemo:7003391,
        hemo:7003392 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Applies HE-FM-EJP-001 at two thresholds to give the range of justifiable prices per unit that an EJP analysis reports, for example at the ends of NICE's GBP 25,000 to GBP 35,000 per QALY range (PMG36 sections 6.3.4 to 6.3.8). The range is the gap between the thresholds times Delta_E divided by U wide, so it widens with the QALY gain, and each extra 0.1 QALY raises each end by its threshold times 0.1 divided by U." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Economically justifiable price range between a lower and an upper threshold" ;
    hemo:9000010 "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). London: NICE; published 31 January 2022, last updated 31 March 2026. Sections 6.3.4 to 6.3.8: below a most plausible ICER of GBP 25,000 per QALY gained (GBP 100,000 for highly specialised technologies) the decision is normally based on the cost-effectiveness estimate; across GBP 25,000 to GBP 35,000 the committee refers explicitly to uncertainty, uncaptured benefits and health inequalities; above GBP 35,000 it needs an increasingly stronger case. https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2",
        "Zeevat F, Luttjeboer J, Paulissen JHJ, van der Schans J, Beutels P, Boersma C, Postma MJ. Exploratory analysis of the economically justifiable price of a hypothetical RSV vaccine for older adults in the Netherlands and the United Kingdom. Journal of Infectious Diseases. 2022;226(Suppl 1):S102-S109. Methods (threshold analysis to calculate the EJP at each willingness-to-pay threshold) and results (EJPs at EUR 20,000 and EUR 50,000 per QALY in the Netherlands and GBP 20,000 and GBP 30,000 per QALY in the United Kingdom). https://doi.org/10.1093/infdis/jiab118" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Reporting one EJP as the justifiable price. A single figure hides the spread: across the two thresholds and three scenarios in the article, Drug X's EJP runs from GBP 540 to GBP 1,340 per pack. Reporting only GBP 740 or only GBP 1,060 overstates how precisely the evidence fixes the price.",
        "Taking the EJP range from thresholds of an earlier analysis. Zeevat and colleagues used GBP 20,000 and GBP 30,000 per QALY for the United Kingdom in 2022. Those figures would give Drug X GBP 580 and GBP 900 per pack (computed here for illustration) instead of GBP 740 and GBP 1,060 at the thresholds in NICE's manual as updated in March 2026." ;
    hemo:9000014 "EJP_L = (lambda_L * Delta_E - Delta_C_other) / U; EJP_U = (lambda_U * Delta_E - Delta_C_other) / U" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EJP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "economically-justifiable-price-range-between-a-lower-and-an-upper-threshold" .

hemo:5000615 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Severity-weighted economically justifiable price under NICE QALY weights"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EJP-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000131 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003394 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002601,
        hemo:6002602,
        hemo:6002603,
        hemo:6002604,
        hemo:6002605,
        hemo:6002606 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002602,
        hemo:6002603,
        hemo:6002604,
        hemo:6002605,
        hemo:6002606 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002601 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003393 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003395,
        hemo:7003396,
        hemo:7003397,
        hemo:7003398,
        hemo:7003399,
        hemo:7003400 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "NICE's committee may apply a greater weight to QALYs for conditions with a high degree of severity, 1.2 or 1.7 depending on the absolute and proportional QALY shortfall (PMG36 sections 6.2.16 to 6.2.18). Multiplying Delta_E by the weight w in HE-FM-EJP-001 raises the EJP by w minus 1, times lambda times Delta_E, divided by U. With w equal to 1 the formula returns the unweighted EJP." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Severity-weighted economically justifiable price under NICE QALY weights" ;
    hemo:9000010 "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). London: NICE; published 31 January 2022, last updated 31 March 2026. Sections 6.2.16 to 6.2.18 and table 6.1: the committee may apply a greater weight to QALYs for conditions with a high degree of severity; weights of 1.2 and 1.7 are set by absolute and proportional QALY shortfall, whichever implies the greater severity. https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Applying the severity weight to the unweighted EJP. Multiplying Drug X's unweighted EJP of GBP 740 by 1.2 gives GBP 888 per pack instead of GBP 900, because it also scales Delta_C_other, which the weight does not touch (computed here for illustration). The weight belongs on the QALY gain." ;
    hemo:9000014 "EJP_w = (w * lambda * Delta_E - Delta_C_other) / U" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EJP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "severity-weighted-economically-justifiable-price-under-nice-qaly-weights" .

hemo:5000624 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Ratio of the annualised capital charge to straight-line depreciation"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ANLS-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000134 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003506 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002650,
        hemo:6002651,
        hemo:6002652,
        hemo:6002653 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002651,
        hemo:6002652 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002650 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003505 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003507,
        hemo:7003508,
        hemo:7003509,
        hemo:7003510,
        hemo:7003511 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "With no resale value, the annualised charge, K divided by A, and the straight-line charge, K divided by n, differ by the ratio n divided by A, which depends only on the useful life and the rate. Because A is smaller than n at any positive rate, the ratio exceeds 1 and rises with the useful life, and it tends to 1 as r approaches zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ratio of the annualised capital charge to straight-line depreciation" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Global Health Cost Consortium. Reference Case for Estimating the Costs of Global Health Services and Interventions. 2017. Principle 12, which states that financial cost estimates use straight-line depreciation and economic costs use an annualisation factor that adjusts the years of life for opportunity cost by means of a discount rate. https://ghcosting.org/pages/standards/principles/pricing_and_valuation.php",
        "Turner HC, Sandmann FG, Downey LE, Orangi S, Teerawattananon Y, Vassall A, Jit M. What are economic costs and when should they be used in health economic studies? Cost Effectiveness and Resource Allocation. 2023, volume 21, article 31. Figure 1, which notes that the annualisation factor is smaller than the expected lifespan, 4.58 against 5 in its example, so the annualised economic cost exceeds the annualised financial cost, even from a provider or payer perspective. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12962-023-00436-w" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Treating the annualisation gap as small for buildings. The 11% gap for a five-year device at 3.5% does not carry over to long-lived assets. For a 60-year building the economic charge is about 2.4 times the straight-line charge, so straight-line depreciation understates building costs far more than equipment costs." ;
    hemo:9000014 "A = (1 - (1+r)^(-n)) / r; R_SL = n / A" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ANLS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ratio-of-the-annualised-capital-charge-to-straight-line-depreciation" .

hemo:5000625 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Random-effects meta-regression model with one study-level covariate"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-MREG-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000135 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003515 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002654,
        hemo:6002655,
        hemo:6002656,
        hemo:6002657,
        hemo:6002658,
        hemo:6002659,
        hemo:6002660,
        hemo:6002661 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002655,
        hemo:6002656,
        hemo:6002657,
        hemo:6002658,
        hemo:6002659,
        hemo:6002660,
        hemo:6002661 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002654 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003512,
        hemo:7003513,
        hemo:7003514 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003516,
        hemo:7003517,
        hemo:7003518,
        hemo:7003519 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Writes the effect estimate of study i as a point on a regression line in the covariate x_i plus two error terms, the study's deviation u_i from the line, with u_i ~ N(0, tau2), and its sampling error e_i, with e_i ~ N(0, v_i) and v_i treated as known. The slope beta_1 is the change in the expected effect per unit increase in x_i, and tau2 is the residual between-study variance not accounted for by the covariate. A fixed-effect meta-regression omits u_i, so sampling error is the only random variation. Further covariates enter as further terms, with a categorical characteristic of J groups coded as J minus 1 dummy variables." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Random-effects meta-regression model with one study-level covariate" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Deeks JJ, Higgins JPT, Altman DG, McKenzie JE, Veroniki AA (editors). Chapter 10, Analysing data and undertaking meta-analyses, in Cochrane Handbook for Systematic Reviews of Interventions version 6.5. Cochrane; 2024. Section 10.11.3.1 (random-effects model preferred for comparing subgroups), section 10.11.4 (effect estimate as outcome, precision weighting, residual heterogeneity, log scale for ratio measures, dummy variables, fewer than ten studies), sections 10.11.5.1 to 10.11.5.5 (pre-specification and aggregation bias), 10.11.6 (observational nature) and 10.11.7 (comparator group risk and regression to the mean). https://www.cochrane.org/authors/handbooks-and-manuals/handbook/current/chapter-10",
        "Dias S, Sutton AJ, Welton NJ, Ades AE. NICE DSU Technical Support Document 3, Heterogeneity, subgroups, meta-regression, bias and bias-adjustment. Decision Support Unit; 2011, last updated April 2012. Section 1.1 (aim of meta-regression and spurious significance with few precise trials), section 4.3.1 (BCG vaccine example in which the fixed-effect model fitted very poorly) and section 4.4 (regression on the true baseline rather than the observed baseline risk). https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD3-Heterogeneity.final-report.08.05.12.pdf",
        "Geissbühler M, Hincapié CA, Aghlmandi S, Zwahlen M, Jüni P, da Costa BR. Most published meta-regression analyses based on aggregate data suffer from methodological pitfalls, a meta-epidemiological study. BMC Medical Research Methodology. 2021;21, article 123. Abstract and results, 57 of 81 aggregate-data meta-regressions with at least one pitfall, 53 susceptible to ecological fallacy. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12874-021-01310-0",
        "Viechtbauer W. Conducting meta-analyses in R with the metafor package. Journal of Statistical Software. 2010;36(3). Section 2.2 (mixed-effects model with u_i ~ N(0, tau2) and residual heterogeneity not accounted for by the moderators), section 2.4 (two-step fitting with weighted least squares) and section 3.3 (Knapp and Hartung adjustment with a t distribution on k minus p degrees of freedom). https://doi.org/10.18637/jss.v036.i03" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Fitting a fixed-effect meta-regression by default. Omitting u_i treats all variation around the line as sampling error. The Cochrane Handbook generally prefers a random-effects model for comparing groups of studies because a fixed-effect comparison carries a high risk of false-positive results, and in the TSD 3 example on BCG vaccine trials and latitude the fixed-effect model fitted very poorly, with a posterior mean residual deviance of 40 against 26 data points.",
        "Reading a trial-level slope as a patient-level relationship. When a patient characteristic such as age enters only as a study mean, a strong relationship within each trial can be invisible across trials with similar mean ages, and the direction across studies can even be the opposite of the direction within them. Geissbühler and colleagues found 53 of 81 aggregate-data meta-regressions (65%) susceptible to this ecological fallacy.",
        "Regressing effects on the observed comparator group risk. The observed risk in each study's comparator group is part of its effect estimate, so a risk that is high by chance tends to come with a larger effect. The Cochrane Handbook calls the result a false correlation from regression to the mean, and TSD 3 instead uses the true baseline, estimated with its uncertainty in a Bayesian model, as the covariate." ;
    hemo:9000014 "y_i = beta_0 + beta_1 * x_i + u_i + e_i" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-MREG-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "random-effects-meta-regression-model-with-one-study-level-covariate" .

hemo:5000626 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Random-effects precision weight of a study in a meta-regression"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-MREG-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000135 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003521 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002662,
        hemo:6002663,
        hemo:6002664 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002663,
        hemo:6002664 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002662 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003520 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003522,
        hemo:7003523,
        hemo:7003524,
        hemo:7003525,
        hemo:7003526 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Weight given to study i when the regression coefficients are estimated by weighted least squares. The weight is the inverse of the study's within-study variance plus the residual between-study variance, so larger studies have more influence on the slope, but less than in a fixed-effect meta-regression, where tau2 is zero and the weight is 1/v_i." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Random-effects precision weight of a study in a meta-regression" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Deeks JJ, Higgins JPT, Altman DG, McKenzie JE, Veroniki AA (editors). Chapter 10, Analysing data and undertaking meta-analyses, in Cochrane Handbook for Systematic Reviews of Interventions version 6.5. Cochrane; 2024. Section 10.11.4, which states that larger studies have more influence because studies are weighted by the precision of their effect estimates, and that residual heterogeneity should be allowed for. https://www.cochrane.org/authors/handbooks-and-manuals/handbook/current/chapter-10",
        "Viechtbauer W. Conducting meta-analyses in R with the metafor package. Journal of Statistical Software. 2010;36(3). Section 2.3 (fixed-effects weights typically 1/v_i) and section 2.4 (coefficients estimated by weighted least squares with weights 1/(v_i + tau2) once tau2 has been estimated). https://doi.org/10.18637/jss.v036.i03" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Adding tau instead of tau2 to a meta-regression weight. Adding the between-study standard deviation to a variance mixes scales. With a variance of 0.01 and a residual tau2 of 0.03, tau is about 0.173 and the weight falls to about 5.46 instead of 25.",
        "Weighting a meta-regression with the tau2 from the meta-analysis without covariates. Using the between-study variance before the covariate is added, 0.09 in the article's example, instead of the residual 0.03 gives the larger trial a weight of 10 instead of 25 and the smaller one about 7.69 instead of 14.29. The weights become too equal and the analysis ignores the heterogeneity the covariate has explained." ;
    hemo:9000014 "w_i = 1 / (v_i + tau2)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-MREG-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "random-effects-precision-weight-of-a-study-in-a-meta-regression" .

hemo:5000627 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Proportion of between-study variance accounted for by meta-regression covariates"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-MREG-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000135 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003528 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002665,
        hemo:6002666,
        hemo:6002667 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002666,
        hemo:6002667 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002665 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003527 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003529,
        hemo:7003530,
        hemo:7003531,
        hemo:7003532 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compares the between-study variance of a random-effects meta-analysis without covariates with the residual between-study variance of the meta-regression, and expresses the fall as a share of the first. It describes heterogeneity among true effects only, not the sampling error in the observed estimates." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Proportion of between-study variance accounted for by meta-regression covariates" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Viechtbauer W. Conducting meta-analyses in R with the metafor package. Journal of Statistical Software. 2010;36(3). Section 3.4 example, in which the residual tau2 of 0.1108 against 0.3132 means 65% of the heterogeneity is accounted for by the two moderators, and the likelihood ratio test example giving the share as 100% times the difference between the reduced-model and full-model tau2, divided by the reduced-model tau2. https://doi.org/10.18637/jss.v036.i03" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Comparing tau2 from different sets of studies. When studies without a value for the covariate drop out of the meta-regression, comparing its residual tau2 with the tau2 from the full meta-analysis mixes the effect of the covariate with the effect of removing studies. The reference model is refitted to the studies used in the meta-regression.",
        "Reading the explained share as a share of all variation in the estimates. The ratio describes the between-study variance only. A covariate that accounts for 67% of tau2 has not explained 67% of the spread in the observed estimates, part of which is sampling error. Both tau2 values are also uncertain with few studies, as the standard error of 0.0845 for the residual estimate of 0.1108 in the BCG example shows." ;
    hemo:9000014 "R2_het = (tau2_RE - tau2_MR) / tau2_RE" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-MREG-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "proportion-of-between-study-variance-accounted-for-by-meta-regression-covariates" .

hemo:5000628 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Relative change in a ratio measure for a change in a meta-regression covariate"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-MREG-004" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000135 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003534 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002668,
        hemo:6002669,
        hemo:6002670 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002669,
        hemo:6002670 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002668 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003533 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003535,
        hemo:7003536,
        hemo:7003537,
        hemo:7003538 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "When a ratio measure is regressed on the log scale, the exponential of the slope is the factor by which the ratio measure changes per unit increase in the covariate. For a change of Delta_x units the factor is the exponential of beta_1 times Delta_x." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Relative change in a ratio measure for a change in a meta-regression covariate" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Deeks JJ, Higgins JPT, Altman DG, McKenzie JE, Veroniki AA (editors). Chapter 10, Analysing data and undertaking meta-analyses, in Cochrane Handbook for Systematic Reviews of Interventions version 6.5. Cochrane; 2024. Section 10.11.4, which states that the log-transformed value of a ratio measure should always be used and that the exponential of the regression coefficient estimates the relative change in the effect per unit increase in the explanatory variable. https://www.cochrane.org/authors/handbooks-and-manuals/handbook/current/chapter-10" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Reading the exponentiated slope as the treatment odds ratio. A factor of 1.35 does not mean that treatment raises the odds of the event by 35%. It means the odds ratio is 1.35 times larger for each unit of the covariate, so an odds ratio of 0.670 at the mean would be about 0.905 one unit higher.",
        "Regressing odds ratios or risk ratios on the ratio scale. A slope fitted to untransformed ratio measures cannot be exponentiated into a relative change and treats a halving and a doubling asymmetrically. The Cochrane Handbook advises the log-transformed value in every case." ;
    hemo:9000014 "RC = exp(beta_1 * Delta_x)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-MREG-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "relative-change-in-a-ratio-measure-for-a-change-in-a-meta-regression-covariate" .

hemo:5000631 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Per-period discount rate implied by the simple hyperbola at a delay of t periods"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-HYPD-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000136 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003572 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002679,
        hemo:6002680,
        hemo:6002681 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002680,
        hemo:6002681 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002679 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003570,
        hemo:7003571 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003573,
        hemo:7003574,
        hemo:7003575,
        hemo:7003576,
        hemo:7003577 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "The per-period rate between delays t and t + 1 is the ratio of the two weights minus 1, D_H(t)/D_H(t + 1) minus 1, which simplifies to alpha/(1 + alpha t). It equals alpha for the first period and falls steadily as the delay grows, whereas the same calculation on the constant-rate factor of HE-FM-DR-001 returns r at every delay. A declining per-period rate is what Frederick and colleagues mean by hyperbolic discounting." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Per-period discount rate implied by the simple hyperbola at a delay of t periods" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Frederick S, Loewenstein G, O'Donoghue T. Time discounting and time preference: a critical review. Journal of Economic Literature. 2002;40(2) 351-401. Section 4.1, page 360, which adopts hyperbolic discounting to mean that a person has a declining rate of time preference, with footnote 13 listing the hyperbolic forms. https://doi.org/10.1257/002205102320161311",
        "Frederick S, Loewenstein G, O'Donoghue T. Time discounting and time preference: a critical review. Journal of Economic Literature. 2002;40(2) 351-401. Section 5.1, page 366, which gives the per-period discount rate between now and the next period and between two future periods for the quasi-hyperbolic form, the same ratio-of-weights definition applied here to the simple hyperbola. https://doi.org/10.1257/002205102320161311" ;
    hemo:9000011 "First-period hyperbolic rate quoted as the rate at every delay. Treating alpha = 0.4 a month as a constant rate puts a weight of about 0.0126 on an outcome 13 months ahead, 1.4 to the power minus 13, against about 0.161 under the simple hyperbola (computed here for illustration). Alpha is the rate for the first period only." ;
    hemo:9000014 "rho_t = alpha / (1 + alpha * t)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-HYPD-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "per-period-discount-rate-implied-by-the-simple-hyperbola-at-a-delay-of-t-periods" .

hemo:5000632 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Generalised hyperbolic discount factor of Loewenstein and Prelec"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-HYPD-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000136 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003580 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002682,
        hemo:6002683,
        hemo:6002684,
        hemo:6002685 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002683,
        hemo:6002684,
        hemo:6002685 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002682 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003578,
        hemo:7003579 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003581,
        hemo:7003582,
        hemo:7003583,
        hemo:7003584 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Raises 1 + alpha t to the power minus beta divided by alpha, the form that Frederick and colleagues attribute to Loewenstein and Prelec. Beta sets the overall level of discounting and alpha how far the curve departs from a constant rate. Setting beta equal to alpha returns the simple hyperbola of HE-FM-HYPD-001, and as alpha approaches zero the weight approaches exp(minus beta t), the continuous constant-rate factor (HE-FM-CONT-003 gives the continuous rate for an annual rate). The beta here is not the present-bias factor of HE-FM-HYPD-004." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Generalised hyperbolic discount factor of Loewenstein and Prelec" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Frederick S, Loewenstein G, O'Donoghue T. Time discounting and time preference: a critical review. Journal of Economic Literature. 2002;40(2) 351-401. Page 360, footnote 13, which states that Loewenstein and Prelec (1992) suggested D(t) = 1/(1 + alpha t) to the power beta over alpha. https://doi.org/10.1257/002205102320161311" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Division by alpha dropped from the generalised hyperbola exponent. Writing (1 + alpha t) to the power minus beta gives about 0.889 two periods ahead with alpha = 0.4 and beta = 0.2, instead of 0.745 (computed here for illustration), and loses the constant-rate limit.",
        "Generalised hyperbola beta confused with the quasi-hyperbolic present-bias factor. The article warns that the two betas differ. Entering a present-bias estimate of 0.7 as the generalised beta with alpha = 0.4 gives about 0.555 one period ahead, against 0.693 for the quasi-hyperbolic form with beta = 0.7 and delta = 0.99 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "D_G = (1 + alpha * t)^(-beta / alpha)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-HYPD-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "generalised-hyperbolic-discount-factor-of-loewenstein-and-prelec" .

hemo:5000633 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Quasi-hyperbolic beta-delta discount weight with its short-run and long-run per-period rates"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-HYPD-004" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000136 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003587 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002686,
        hemo:6002687,
        hemo:6002688,
        hemo:6002689,
        hemo:6002690,
        hemo:6002691 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002687,
        hemo:6002688,
        hemo:6002689 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002686,
        hemo:6002690,
        hemo:6002691 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003585,
        hemo:7003586 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003588,
        hemo:7003589,
        hemo:7003590,
        hemo:7003591,
        hemo:7003592 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "For a delay of one period or more the weight is beta times delta to the power t, while an outcome now keeps a weight of 1, so beta is a one-off discount on everything that is not immediate. The per-period rate is r_0 between now and the next period and r_L between any two later periods, as Frederick and colleagues give them. Setting beta = 1 returns exponential discounting with the factor delta, the HE-FM-DR-001 factor with r equal to (1 minus delta)/delta, and Wang and Sloan read beta below 1 as present bias." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Quasi-hyperbolic beta-delta discount weight with its short-run and long-run per-period rates" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Frederick S, Loewenstein G, O'Donoghue T. Time discounting and time preference: a critical review. Journal of Economic Literature. 2002;40(2) 351-401. Section 5.1, page 366, which gives D(k) = 1 for k = 0 and beta delta to the power k for k above 0, first introduced by Phelps and Pollak and used from Laibson onwards, with per-period rates (1 minus beta delta)/beta delta between now and the next period and (1 minus delta)/delta between future periods. https://doi.org/10.1257/002205102320161311",
        "Gruber J, Köszegi B. Is addiction \"rational\"? Theory and evidence. NBER Working Paper 7507. Cambridge, MA, National Bureau of Economic Research, January 2000. Page 35, which describes the quasi-hyperbolic model as a theoretical device that limits intrapersonal conflict to exactly one period and asks that the period length be chosen with that limitation in mind. https://doi.org/10.3386/w7507",
        "Wang Y, Sloan FA. Present bias and health. Journal of Risk and Uncertainty. 2018;57(2) 177-198. Section 2, where a payoff t periods away is discounted by beta delta to the power t and the time-consistent model is the special case beta = 1, and section 5.3 with Table 4, which reports estimates of delta and beta of 0.88 and 0.38. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11166-018-9289-z" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Present-bias factor applied to the immediate outcome. Weighting the present period by beta as well removes the bias. In the article's example smoking today would be worth 7 against 9.009 for quitting, so the smoker would quit at once instead of postponing (computed here for illustration).",
        "Present-bias factor compounded with delay. Writing (beta delta)^t applies beta once per period instead of once in total. With beta = 0.7 and delta = 0.99 the weight two periods ahead becomes about 0.480 instead of 0.686 (computed here for illustration), which is steep exponential discounting at the factor 0.693 and gives no preference reversal." ;
    hemo:9000014 "D_Q = beta * delta^t; r_0 = (1 - beta * delta) / (beta * delta); r_L = (1 - delta) / delta" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-HYPD-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "quasi-hyperbolic-beta-delta-discount-weight-with-its-short-run-and-long-run-per-period-rates" .

hemo:5000635 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "EVSI of a study with a positive or negative result for two options"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EVSI-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000137 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003605 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002699,
        hemo:6002700,
        hemo:6002701,
        hemo:6002702,
        hemo:6002703,
        hemo:6002704,
        hemo:6002705,
        hemo:6002706,
        hemo:6002707,
        hemo:6002708,
        hemo:6002709,
        hemo:6002710,
        hemo:6002711 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002700,
        hemo:6002701,
        hemo:6002702,
        hemo:6002703,
        hemo:6002704 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002699 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003601,
        hemo:7003602,
        hemo:7003603,
        hemo:7003604 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003606,
        hemo:7003607,
        hemo:7003608,
        hemo:7003609,
        hemo:7003610,
        hemo:7003611 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Evaluates the EVSI expectation exactly when the proposed study can give only two results and the choice is between standard care A and a new option B whose net benefit depends on whether B works (state F) or not (state U). Bayes theorem turns the prior probability p of state F and the chance q that the result points the right way into the probability of each result and the posterior probability of F after it. The better option is chosen after each result, and the expected value of choosing after the study, V_S, is compared with the value of choosing now, V_0. A result adds value only when it changes the choice, so EVSI is zero when every result leaves the current choice in place and equals EVPI when q is 1." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EVSI of a study with a positive or negative result for two options" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Ades AE, Lu G, Claxton K. Expected value of sample information calculations in medical decision modeling. Medical Decision Making. 2004;24(2):207-227. The abstract states that the article derives simple and nested Monte Carlo methods that extend EVSI calculations to medical decision applications with multiple sources of uncertainty, with approximate methods for relative measures such as risk differences, odds ratios, risk ratios and hazard ratios. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X04263162",
        "Kunst N, Wilson ECF, Glynn D, Alarid-Escudero F, Baio G, Brennan A, Fairley M, Goldhaber-Fiebert JD, Jackson C, Jalal H, Menzies NA, Strong M, Thom H, Heath A. Computing the expected value of sample information efficiently. Practical guidance and recommendations for four model-based methods. Value in Health. 2020;23(6):734-742. Equation 2 writes EVSI as the expectation over the data X of the maximum over interventions of posterior expected net benefit, less the maximum of expected net benefit on current evidence, and the section on the decision-analytic model states that, assuming risk neutrality, the intervention with the highest expected net benefit is implemented. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2020.02.010",
        "Rothery C, Strong M, Koffijberg HE, Basu A, Ghabri S, Knies S, Murray JF, Sanders Schmidler GD, Steuten L, Fenwick E. Value of information analytical methods. Report 2 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(3):277-286. Equation 10 defines EVSI as the expectation over the new data X of the maximum over options of the posterior expected utility, less the maximum over options of the expected utility on current information. Algorithms 5 and 6 set out the double-loop and single-loop Monte Carlo schemes, which sample parameters from the prior, generate a plausible dataset, update to the posterior and average the value of the best option across datasets. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2020.01.004",
        "Strong M, Oakley JE, Brennan A, Breeze P. Estimating the expected value of sample information using the probabilistic sensitivity analysis sample. A fast, nonparametric regression-based method. Medical Decision Making. 2015;35(5):570-583. The introduction lists the costs of delaying or reversing adoption, full implementation of the adoption decision and studies that extend across jurisdictions as factors that affect EVSI, and states that the article computes per-person EVSI within a single jurisdiction under the assumption of costless reversibility with perfect implementation and no delay in either the study or the adoption. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X15575286" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Keeping the current choice after an unfavourable study result. Valuing S1 as if B were kept after every result gives 0.48 times GBP 1,256.25 plus 0.52 times about GBP 859.62, which is the GBP 1,050 of the current decision, so EVSI comes out as zero for any study. The value of S1 lies entirely in switching to A after a negative result, 0.52 times about GBP 140.38, or GBP 73 per patient.",
        "Reading EVSI as a forecast that the study will favour the new option. A high EVSI does not predict a positive result. In the example the positive result of S1 leaves the decision unchanged and adds nothing, and all GBP 73 per patient comes from the negative result that reverses adoption." ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_pos = p * q + (1 - p) * (1 - q); pF_pos = p * q / P_pos; pF_neg = p * (1 - q) / (1 - P_pos); B_pos = pF_pos * NB_BF + (1 - pF_pos) * NB_BU; B_neg = pF_neg * NB_BF + (1 - pF_neg) * NB_BU; V_S = P_pos * max(NB_A, B_pos) + (1 - P_pos) * max(NB_A, B_neg); V_0 = max(NB_A, p * NB_BF + (1 - p) * NB_BU); EVSI = V_S - V_0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EVSI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "evsi-of-a-study-with-a-positive-or-negative-result-for-two-options" .

hemo:5000640 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Equity-weighted ICER for one group and the equivalent threshold"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EQWA-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000138 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003700 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002738,
        hemo:6002739,
        hemo:6002740,
        hemo:6002741,
        hemo:6002742,
        hemo:6002743 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002739,
        hemo:6002740,
        hemo:6002741,
        hemo:6002743 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002738,
        hemo:6002742 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003697,
        hemo:7003698,
        hemo:7003699 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003701,
        hemo:7003702,
        hemo:7003703,
        hemo:7003704,
        hemo:7003705 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Divides the incremental cost by the weighted QALY gain of a group that carries a single weight w. Because the weight is the same for everyone in the group, the weighted ICER equals the unweighted ICER divided by w, and judging it against a threshold lambda gives the same decision as judging the unweighted ICER against w times lambda. The second part of the formula returns that equivalent threshold, the route the Norwegian committee proposed in 2014 through differential threshold ranges by severity." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equity-weighted ICER for one group and the equivalent threshold" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Cookson R, Mirelman AJ, Griffin S, Asaria M, Dawkins B, Norheim OF, Verguet S, Culyer AJ. Using cost-effectiveness analysis to address health equity concerns. Value in Health. 2017;20(2):206-212. Discussion section, which reports that in 2014 the third Norwegian Committee on Priority Setting in the Health Sector proposed three categories of disease severity with differential cost-effectiveness threshold ranges. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2016.11.027",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 13 October 2026. Chapter 6 Committee recommendations, section 6.3.7 (ICERs in the range of GBP 25,000 to GBP 35,000 per QALY gained), section 6.2.11 (modifiers applied through QALY weighting) and section 6.3.2 (net health benefit with and without the QALY weighting). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations",
        "Wailoo A, Bell Gorrod H, Dunning L, Kenny J, Leckenby E, Shah K. The NICE experience of designing and utilising severity weights. Health Policy OPEN. 2026;10:100156. Section 1 Background, which states that the severity weights are applied to the health benefits, the incremental QALYs, estimated in the cost-effectiveness assessment. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.hpopen.2025.100156" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Applying the equity weight to the ICER and to the threshold together. Comparing the weighted ICER of GBP 29,412 with the raised threshold of GBP 59,500 applies the weight twice. It is the same as a weight of 2.89 on the unweighted ICER (1.7 × 1.7 = 2.89), well above the largest NICE severity weight. Either the ICER or the threshold carries the weight, not both.",
        "Reading the equity-weighted ICER as the cost per QALY gained. The weight moves subgroup A from GBP 50,000 to about GBP 29,412 per weighted QALY, but each QALY gained still costs GBP 50,000 and displaces the same care. Reporting GBP 29,412 as the cost per QALY hides the health given up to act on the severity judgement." ;
    hemo:9000014 "ICER_w = Delta_C / (w * Delta_E); lambda_w = w * lambda" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EQWA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equity-weighted-icer-for-one-group-and-the-equivalent-threshold" .

hemo:5000642 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Equity switching weight at which a programme breaks even"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EQWA-004" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000138 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003718 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002751,
        hemo:6002752,
        hemo:6002753,
        hemo:6002754 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002752,
        hemo:6002753,
        hemo:6002754 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002751 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003715,
        hemo:7003716,
        hemo:7003717 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003719,
        hemo:7003720,
        hemo:7003721,
        hemo:7003722,
        hemo:7003723 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Sets the weighted net health benefit to zero and solves for the weight, giving the incremental cost divided by lambda times the QALY gain, which is the unweighted ICER divided by lambda. A weight at or above w_star makes the programme acceptable at lambda, so the value states how much concern for the group is needed to justify a programme that is not cost-effective without weights. A value below 1 means the programme is cost-effective unweighted." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equity switching weight at which a programme breaks even" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Cookson R, Mirelman AJ, Griffin S, Asaria M, Dawkins B, Norheim OF, Verguet S, Culyer AJ. Using cost-effectiveness analysis to address health equity concerns. Value in Health. 2017;20(2):206-212. Section on equity-weighting analysis, which uses equity weights to quantify how much concern for equity is required to choose cost-ineffective options that improve equity, and the Discussion, which recommends equity-weighting analysis as an aid to deliberation through sensitivity analysis of different equity parameters. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2016.11.027",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 13 October 2026. Chapter 6 Committee recommendations, table 6.1 (QALY weights of 1, 1.2 and 1.7) and section 6.3.2 (net health benefit at GBP 25,000 and GBP 35,000 per QALY with and without the QALY weighting). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Break-even weight for a programme that loses health. With an illustrative extra cost of GBP 30,000 and a QALY loss of 0.1 at GBP 35,000 per QALY, the formula returns about minus 8.57, which has no meaning as a weight. A weight above 1 enlarges a loss, so the break-even weight is computed only for a QALY gain.",
        "Judging the break-even weight at one value of a QALY only. Subgroup A's weight of 1.7 clears the break-even weight of about 1.43 at GBP 35,000 per QALY but not the 2.0 needed at GBP 25,000. Reporting only the upper value suggests the severity weight is enough across the whole NICE range." ;
    hemo:9000014 "w_star = Delta_C / (lambda * Delta_E)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EQWA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equity-switching-weight-at-which-a-programme-breaks-even" .

hemo:5000647 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "DALY rate per 100,000 population for a cause"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-DALY-005" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000139 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003774 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002779,
        hemo:6002780,
        hemo:6002781 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002780,
        hemo:6002781 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002779 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003773 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003775,
        hemo:7003776,
        hemo:7003777 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Divides the DALYs from a cause by the population in which they arise and scales the result to 100,000 people, the form in which GBD 2021 reports all-age and age-specific rates. A rate removes the effect of population size but not of age structure; age-standardised rates weight age-specific rates by a standard population, as in HE-FM-INEQ-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "DALY rate per 100,000 population for a cause" ;
    hemo:9000010 "GBD 2021 Diseases and Injuries Collaborators. Global incidence, prevalence, years lived with disability (YLDs), disability-adjusted life-years (DALYs), and healthy life expectancy (HALE) for 371 diseases and injuries in 204 countries and territories and 811 subnational locations, 1990-2021, a systematic analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study 2021. The Lancet. 2024;403(10440) 2133-2161 (full text read). Methods, where metrics are estimated as counts, all-age and age-specific rates per 100 000 population and age-standardised rates per 100 000 using the GBD standard population. Findings, with global DALYs of 2.63 billion in 2010 and 2.88 billion in 2021 and a 14.2% fall in the global age-standardised all-cause DALY rate between 2010 and 2019, reversed during the first 2 years of the COVID-19 pandemic. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(24)00757-8" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Reading a rise in DALY counts as worsening population health. GBD 2021 found that global DALYs rose to 2.88 billion in 2021 against 2.63 billion in 2010, while the age-standardised DALY rate fell by 14.2% between 2010 and 2019, so much of the rise came from population growth and ageing. The age-standardised rate then rose again in 2020 and 2021 during the COVID-19 pandemic. Counts and all-age rates are read alongside the age-standardised rate." ;
    hemo:9000014 "DALY_rate = DALY / Pop * 100000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-DALY-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "daly-rate-per-100-000-population-for-a-cause" .

hemo:5000652 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-option EVPI in QALYs from incremental net health benefit draws"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EVPI-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000141 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003888 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002813,
        hemo:6002814,
        hemo:6002815,
        hemo:6002816,
        hemo:6002817,
        hemo:6002818,
        hemo:6002819 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002814,
        hemo:6002815,
        hemo:6002816,
        hemo:6002819 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002818 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003885,
        hemo:7003886,
        hemo:7003887 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003889,
        hemo:7003890,
        hemo:7003891,
        hemo:7003892,
        hemo:7003893,
        hemo:7003894 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "With one new option and one comparator, the comparator's net benefit can be set to zero in every draw and the new option's to its incremental net health benefit INHB_s, its incremental QALYs less its incremental cost divided by the threshold. EVPI in QALYs is then the mean of the positive part of INHB_s less the positive part of its mean. Multiplying by the threshold gives the same EVPI in money, because net monetary benefit is the threshold times net health benefit in every draw." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-option EVPI in QALYs from incremental net health benefit draws" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Fenwick E, Steuten L, Knies S, Ghabri S, Basu A, Murray JF, Koffijberg HE, Strong M, Sanders Schmidler GD, Rothery C. Value of information analysis for research decisions, an introduction. Report 1 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(2):139-150. Box 4, which states its EVPI in units of net health benefit (NHB) and takes the current decision as the option with the highest expected NHB, the choice of a risk-neutral decision maker. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2020.01.001",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance, the manual (PMG36). London, NICE, 2022, last updated 13 October 2026. Section 4.10.8, which asks for expected net health benefits to be presented when appropriate, using values placed on a QALY gain of GBP 25,000 and GBP 35,000. https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation",
        "Rothery C, Strong M, Koffijberg HE, Basu A, Ghabri S, Knies S, Murray JF, Sanders Schmidler GD, Steuten L, Fenwick E. Value of information analytical methods. Report 2 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(3):277-286. Section on reporting, which states that VOI is expressed in net health benefit or net monetary benefit when a cost-effectiveness model is used, and that because both depend on the threshold, VOI is reported for explicit thresholds or as a function of the threshold. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2020.01.004" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Converting a two-option EVPI to money at a different threshold. Multiplying the EVPI of 0.06 QALYs found at GBP 25,000 by GBP 35,000 gives GBP 2,100 per patient. The EVPI at GBP 35,000 is about 0.0171 QALYs, or GBP 600, because the threshold also changes which draws favour the drug.",
        "Reporting a single two-option EVPI as if it did not depend on the threshold. In the article's example EVPI is 0.06 QALYs per patient at GBP 25,000 per QALY and about 0.017 at GBP 35,000, more than two thirds lower. A single figure without its threshold cannot be compared with the cost of research." ;
    hemo:9000014 "INHB_s = dQ_s - dC_s / lambda; EVPI_H = sum_(s=1)^S [max(INHB_s, 0)] / S - max(sum_(s=1)^S [INHB_s] / S, 0); EVPI_M = lambda * EVPI_H" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EVPI-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-option-evpi-in-qalys-from-incremental-net-health-benefit-draws" .

hemo:5000653 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "EVPI as the probability of a wrong current decision times the mean loss when wrong"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EVPI-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000141 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003897 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002820,
        hemo:6002821,
        hemo:6002822 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002821,
        hemo:6002822 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002820 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003895,
        hemo:7003896 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003898,
        hemo:7003899,
        hemo:7003900,
        hemo:7003901,
        hemo:7003902 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Splits the mean opportunity loss into how often the option chosen on current evidence is not the best option, P_err, and the mean loss in the draws where it is not, L_wrong. The product equals EVPI because the draws where the chosen option is best add no loss. P_err is one minus the acceptability of the chosen option (HE-FM-CEAC-003), so the formula links the acceptability curve to EVPI and shows why a small chance of a costly error can give a larger EVPI than a large chance of a small one." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EVPI as the probability of a wrong current decision times the mean loss when wrong" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Fenwick E, Steuten L, Knies S, Ghabri S, Basu A, Murray JF, Koffijberg HE, Strong M, Sanders Schmidler GD, Rothery C. Value of information analysis for research decisions, an introduction. Report 1 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(2):139-150. Box 4, which notes that iterations where uncertainty has not led to an incorrect decision carry no opportunity loss, that the incorrect iterations carry some loss, and that EVPI is the average of the loss over all iterations. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2020.01.001",
        "Steuten L, Thavorn K, Cipriano LE, Guertin JR, McQueen B, Schmidler G, Sullivan S, Wailoo A, Sculpher M. Methods to analyze and present uncertainty in economic evaluation to support HTA decisions. HEMA Report 2. Health Economics Methods Advisory, 2026. Glossary entry on the cost of uncertainty, the health expected to be lost by deciding on current evidence, combining how likely a wrong choice is with how much would be lost if it were made, and section 6.4, which asks reports to combine outputs on the probability of an incorrect decision with its consequences. https://hemamethods.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/HEMA-Report-2_For-Publication_100726.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Reading the error probability alone as the cost of uncertainty. Between GBP 25,000 and GBP 35,000 per QALY the error probability in the article's example halves, 40% at the lower value and 20% at the higher, while EVPI falls by more than two thirds, 0.06 QALYs at the lower value and about 0.017 at the higher. The probability of a wrong decision says nothing about the size of the loss.",
        "Reporting the mean loss when wrong as EVPI. Dividing the total loss of 0.30 QALYs in the article's example by the 2 draws with a loss gives 0.15 QALYs, the loss when wrong, not the EVPI of 0.06. The loss is averaged over all draws, or the loss when wrong is multiplied by P_err." ;
    hemo:9000014 "EVPI = P_err * L_wrong" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EVPI-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "evpi-as-the-probability-of-a-wrong-current-decision-times-the-mean-loss-when-wrong" .

hemo:5000654 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-option EVPI for a normally distributed incremental net benefit"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EVPI-004" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000141 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003906 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002823,
        hemo:6002824,
        hemo:6002825,
        hemo:6002826,
        hemo:6002827,
        hemo:6002828 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002824,
        hemo:6002825,
        hemo:6002827 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002828 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003903,
        hemo:7003904,
        hemo:7003905 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003907,
        hemo:7003908,
        hemo:7003909,
        hemo:7003910,
        hemo:7003911,
        hemo:7003912 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "When the incremental net benefit of the new option over its comparator is normal with mean mu_INB and standard deviation sigma_INB, EVPI has a closed form, sigma_INB times the unit normal loss integral at z, the absolute mean divided by the standard deviation. pdf_z is the standard normal density at z, computed in the formula, and cdf_z is the standard normal cumulative probability at z, which the site calculator cannot compute and so takes as an input from NORM.S.DIST or a table. The form needs no draws and has no Monte Carlo error. For a given sigma_INB the loss term is largest at z equal to zero, where EVPI is sigma_INB divided by the square root of 2 pi, about 0.3989 sigma_INB." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-option EVPI for a normally distributed incremental net benefit" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Lee TY, Gustafson P, Sadatsafavi M. Closed-form solution of the unit normal loss integral in 2 dimensions, with application in value-of-information analysis. Medical Decision Making. 2023;43(5):621-626. Introduction, which defines the unit normal loss integral for a normal Y with mean mu and variance sigma squared as E(max(Y, 0)) = mu[1-Phi(-mu/sigma)] + sigma phi(-mu/sigma), attributes it to Raiffa and Schlaifer, and states that for two strategies with a normal incremental net benefit EVPI has a closed form using this integral, applicable to model-based and data-driven evaluations. The loss term with the absolute mean follows by subtracting the positive part of mu. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X231171166",
        "Rothery C, Strong M, Koffijberg HE, Basu A, Ghabri S, Knies S, Murray JF, Sanders Schmidler GD, Steuten L, Fenwick E. Value of information analytical methods. Report 2 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(3):277-286. Section on the expected value of perfect information, which defines EVPI as the expected maximum utility over options less the maximum of the expected utilities. With the comparator at zero, the second term is the positive part of mu_INB. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2020.01.004" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Applying the normal EVPI to three or more options. Running the closed form on each pairwise comparison and adding or taking the largest result does not give the EVPI of a choice among three options, because the best option in each state depends on all of them together.",
        "Using the Monte Carlo standard error as the spread in normal EVPI. Dividing the standard deviation of the five illustrative draws, about 0.2168 QALYs, by the square root of 5 gives the standard error of their mean, about 0.0970, and an EVPI of about 0.0133 QALYs instead of 0.0560. sigma_INB measures the uncertainty about incremental net benefit, not the precision of the simulation.",
        "Using the signed mean in the normal EVPI loss term. For a mean of minus GBP 1,000 and a standard deviation of GBP 2,000, using z equal to minus 0.5 in the loss term gives about GBP 1,395.59 instead of GBP 395.59, overstating EVPI by exactly the absolute mean. The loss term needs the absolute mean." ;
    hemo:9000014 "z = abs(mu_INB) / sigma_INB; pdf_z = exp(-z^2 / 2) / 2.5066283; EVPI_N = sigma_INB * (pdf_z - z * (1 - cdf_z))" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EVPI-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-option-evpi-for-a-normally-distributed-incremental-net-benefit" .

hemo:5000656 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Single-loop EVPPI shortcut for a model linear in the remaining parameters"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EVPP-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000142 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003952 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002838,
        hemo:6002839,
        hemo:6002840,
        hemo:6002841,
        hemo:6002842,
        hemo:6002843 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002840,
        hemo:6002841,
        hemo:6002842,
        hemo:6002843 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002838 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003950,
        hemo:7003951 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003953,
        hemo:7003954,
        hemo:7003955,
        hemo:7003956,
        hemo:7003957 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Replaces the inner loop by one model run for each draw of theta_i with the remaining parameters theta_c set at their mean. The second ISPOR report allows this when the model is linear or multilinear in theta_c, with no correlation between complementary parameters that are multiplied together, and prefers single-loop methods where they apply because they reduce Monte Carlo error. Here the incremental net benefit at draw k is alpha_k plus beta_k times theta_c, with alpha_k and beta_k depending on theta_i only, so its expectation over theta_c is that line evaluated at the mean mu_c. Brennan and colleagues show that the shortcut can be substantially inaccurate when even relatively small correlation or nonlinearity is present." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Single-loop EVPPI shortcut for a model linear in the remaining parameters" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Brennan A, Kharroubi S, O'Hagan A, Chilcott J. Calculating partial expected value of perfect information via Monte Carlo sampling algorithms. Medical Decision Making. 2007;27(4) 448-470. Abstract, which reports that alternative computation methods and shortcut algorithms are discussed with the mathematical conditions for their use, and that if relatively small correlation or nonlinearity is present the shortcut algorithm can be substantially inaccurate. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X07302555",
        "Rothery C, Strong M, Koffijberg HE, Basu A, Ghabri S, Knies S, Murray JF, Sanders Schmidler GD, Steuten L, Fenwick E. Value of information analytical methods. Report 2 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(3) 277-286. Section on EVPPI computation, which states that when the model is linear or multilinear in the complementary parameters, with no correlation between those multiplied together, the double loop can be replaced by a single loop using their mean values, Algorithm 3, and the preference for single-loop methods where applicable to reduce Monte Carlo error. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2020.01.004",
        "Strong M, Oakley JE, Brennan A. Estimating multiparameter partial expected value of perfect information from a probabilistic sensitivity analysis sample, a nonparametric regression approach. Medical Decision Making. 2014;34(3) 311-326. Methods section after Equation 4, which notes that in the linear case the inner expectation has an analytic solution and in the multilinear case it does if inputs are independent, while the outer expectation rarely has one because of the maximisation step. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X13505910" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Marginal mean used for a remaining parameter correlated with theta_i. When theta_c is correlated with theta_i, the mean that applies at draw k is the mean of theta_c given theta_i at that draw, and a single marginal mean for every draw misstates each conditional expected net benefit. Brennan and colleagues report that even relatively small correlation can make the shortcut substantially inaccurate.",
        "Single-loop shortcut applied to a model curved in the remaining parameter. Suppose, for illustration, that a remaining parameter takes the values 0 or 2 with equal probability, so its mean is 1, and enters squared, with incremental net benefit GBP 1,000 plus GBP 250 times its square given high A and GBP -1,600 plus GBP 250 times its square given low A. The true conditional means are GBP 1,500 and GBP -1,100 and the EVPPI for A is GBP 550. Plugging in the mean gives GBP 1,250 and GBP -1,350, a current decision that wrongly rejects the new treatment and an EVPPI of GBP 625 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "ibar_k = alpha_k + beta_k * mu_c; EVPPI_1L = sum_(k=1)^K [max(ibar_k, 0)] / K - max(sum_(k=1)^K [ibar_k] / K, 0)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EVPP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "single-loop-evppi-shortcut-for-a-model-linear-in-the-remaining-parameters" .

hemo:5000661 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Ceiling on the ENBS of a study design from population EVPI"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ENBS-004" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000143 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004003 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002862,
        hemo:6002863,
        hemo:6002864 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002863,
        hemo:6002864 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002862 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004001,
        hemo:7004002 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004004,
        hemo:7004005,
        hemo:7004006 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "No finite study delivers perfect information, so the population EVSI of any design is at most population EVPI (HE-FM-VOI-002) and the ENBS of a design costing C_n cannot exceed population EVPI less that cost. When the ceiling is zero or below for every feasible design, research is not worthwhile and the value of information process stops before any EVSI is computed, the first screen in the sequence set out by the ISPOR task force. A positive ceiling is necessary but not sufficient, so ENBS itself is still required." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ceiling on the ENBS of a study design from population EVPI" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Fenwick E, Steuten L, Knies S, et al. Value of information analysis for research decisions, an introduction. Report 1 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(2) 139-150. Section on establishing whether more research is potentially worthwhile and good practice recommendation 5, where research is not worthwhile when its expected cost exceeds population EVPI, and population EVPI above the cost of research is a necessary, but not sufficient, condition because perfect information is not achievable with a finite sample. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2020.01.001",
        "Rothery C, Strong M, Koffijberg HE, et al. Value of information analytical methods. Report 2 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(3) 277-286. Section on key concepts, definitions and notation, after equation 7, where a population EV(P)PI below the estimated costs of any research study is a sufficient condition for research not being of value, and one above the cost is necessary but not sufficient. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2020.01.004",
        "Steuten L, Thavorn K, Cipriano LE, et al. Methods to Analyze and Present Uncertainty in Economic Evaluation to Support HTA Decisions (HEMA Report 2). Health Economics Methods Advisory; 2026. Table 5.2, page 41, where research costing less than EVPI is a first hurdle, with ENBS above zero still to be shown. https://hemamethods.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/HEMA-Report-2_For-Publication_100726.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Population EVPI above the study cost read as proof that the study is worthwhile. With an illustrative discounted population of 4,000, population EVPI is GBP 1,600,000, above the GBP 1,460,000 cost of the article's 600-patient trial, yet its ENBS is minus GBP 260,000 (computed here for illustration). Population EVPI is a ceiling, and only ENBS for a feasible design shows that a study repays its cost." ;
    hemo:9000014 "ENBS_ub = EVPI_pop - C_n" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ENBS-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ceiling-on-the-enbs-of-a-study-design-from-population-evpi" .

hemo:5000664 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Net-benefit regression coefficient on a treatment indicator"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-NBA-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000144 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004028 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002890,
        hemo:6002891,
        hemo:6002892,
        hemo:6002893,
        hemo:6002894,
        hemo:6002895,
        hemo:6002896,
        hemo:6002897,
        hemo:6002898 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002892,
        hemo:6002893,
        hemo:6002894,
        hemo:6002895,
        hemo:6002896 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002897,
        hemo:6002898 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004025,
        hemo:7004026,
        hemo:7004027 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004029,
        hemo:7004030,
        hemo:7004031,
        hemo:7004032,
        hemo:7004033,
        hemo:7004034 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each patient's net benefit at threshold lambda, lambda times the patient's effect minus the patient's cost, is regressed on a treatment indicator T_i, 1 for the new option and 0 for the comparator, in the model NB_i = alpha + beta T_i + epsilon_i. With no other regressors the fitted intercept is the comparator arm's mean net benefit and the coefficient is the difference in arm means, which equals incremental net monetary benefit (HE-FM-NMB-002), so the confidence interval for beta is the interval for incremental net benefit. The calculator works from the arm means. Covariates added to the regression give an adjusted estimate of beta, which Hoch, Briggs and Willan proposed for imperfect randomisation and for subgroups." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net-benefit regression coefficient on a treatment indicator" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Hoch JS, Briggs AH, Willan AR. Something old, something new, something borrowed, something blue: a framework for the marriage of health econometrics and cost-effectiveness analysis. Health Economics. 2002;11(5):415-430. Abstract (full text not checked), which places the net-benefit framework in a standard regression framework and lists the use of established econometric techniques, adjustment for imperfect randomisation and the identification of subgroups as practical advantages. https://doi.org/10.1002/hec.678",
        "Hoch JS, Hay A, Isaranuwatchai W, Thavorn K, Leighl NB, Tu D, Trenaman L, Dewa CS, O'Callaghan C, Pater J, Jonker D, Chen BE, Mittmann N. Advantages of the net benefit regression framework for trial-based economic evaluations of cancer treatments: an example from the Canadian Cancer Trials Group CO.17 trial. BMC Cancer. 2019;19:552. Methods section on incremental net benefit regression, where each patient's net benefit is willingness to pay times the patient's effect minus the patient's cost, regressed on a treatment indicator with or without covariates, the coefficient b_TX equals incremental net benefit, and the checks that a willingness to pay equal to the ICER gives b_TX of zero and a value of zero gives minus Delta_C. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12885-019-5779-x" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Intercept read as incremental net benefit. The intercept is the comparator's mean net benefit, GBP 9,500 in the illustrative trial at GBP 20,000 per QALY, a level that depends on which costs and effects are counted. The incremental value is the treatment coefficient, GBP 500.",
        "Treatment indicator coded in reverse in a net-benefit regression. Coding 1 for the comparator returns a coefficient of minus GBP 500 for the illustrative trial at GBP 20,000 per QALY instead of GBP 500, and the intercept becomes the new arm's mean of GBP 10,000 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "NB_1 = lambda * E_1 - C_1; NB_0 = lambda * E_0 - C_0; alpha = NB_0; beta = NB_1 - NB_0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-NBA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-benefit-regression-coefficient-on-a-treatment-indicator" .

hemo:5000667 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Gradient of an IQWiG efficiency frontier segment and the net cost at which a new intervention reaches the extended last segment"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CEF-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000145 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004058 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002917,
        hemo:6002918,
        hemo:6002919,
        hemo:6002920,
        hemo:6002921,
        hemo:6002922,
        hemo:6002923,
        hemo:6002924 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002920,
        hemo:6002921,
        hemo:6002922,
        hemo:6002923,
        hemo:6002924 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002918,
        hemo:6002919 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004055,
        hemo:7004056,
        hemo:7004057 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004059,
        hemo:7004060,
        hemo:7004061,
        hemo:7004062 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the IQWiG efficiency frontier benefit B is on the vertical axis and total net cost per patient K on the horizontal axis, the reverse of the usual cost-effectiveness plane, so the gradient g of a segment is the incremental benefit per incremental cost and its reciprocal lambda_g is the sequential ICER of HE-FM-CEA-001. General Methods version 6.1 extended the last segment to judge a new intervention with more benefit and more cost than the comparators, and took the reciprocal of its gradient as the threshold for the net health benefit used to derive a price. K_max is the net cost per patient at which a new intervention with benefit B_new lies on that extension, where its net health benefit is zero. Version 8.0 (section 4.15) states that extending the last segment is no longer necessary, so K_max is a version 6.1 calculation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Gradient of an IQWiG efficiency frontier segment and the net cost at which a new intervention reaches the extended last segment" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG). General Methods, version 6.1 of 24 January 2022. Cologne, IQWiG, 2022. Section 4.6.2, which states that the gradient of the line segment between 2 interventions gives the incremental benefit per incremental costs, section 4.6.3, which plots benefit on the vertical axis and total net costs per patient on the horizontal axis and extends the last segment for new interventions with more benefit and more costs, section 4.1.9 on the extrapolated last segment as the reciprocal of the current willingness to pay, and section 4.9.2, where the threshold for the net health benefit is the reciprocal of the gradient of the last segment and the price is the maximum cost that keeps the net health benefit at least zero. https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-6-1.pdf",
        "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG). General Methods, version 8.0 of 19 December 2025. Cologne, IQWiG, 2025. Section 4.15 on health economic evaluations outside §35b SGB V, which plots cost-effectiveness ratios for more than 2 treatments as one or more efficiency frontiers formed with absolute and extended dominance and states that it is not necessary to extend the last segment, previously intended for deriving a price recommendation. https://doi.org/10.60584/General-Methods_V8.0" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Extending an earlier segment of the efficiency frontier. If an earlier segment joins EUR 5,000 and 25 points to EUR 8,000 and 40 points, its gradient is 0.005 points per euro. Extending it beyond EUR 8,000 and 40 points gives EUR 12,200 for the new intervention with 61 points, EUR 4,200 below the EUR 16,400 from the last segment (computed here for illustration). Only the last segment applies.",
        "Reading an efficiency frontier gradient as a cost per unit of benefit. A gradient of 0.0025 points per euro is benefit per cost, the reciprocal of EUR 400 per point. A steeper segment means greater efficiency, the opposite direction to an ICER, where a lower ratio is better." ;
    hemo:9000014 "g = (B_j - B_i) / (K_j - K_i); lambda_g = 1 / g; K_max = K_j + (B_new - B_j) / g" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CEF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "gradient-of-an-iqwig-efficiency-frontier-segment-and-the-net-cost-at-which-a-new-intervention-reaches-the-extended-last-segment" .

hemo:5000669 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Implied inequality aversion from the switching weight by inverting the Atkinson relative weight"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EET-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000146 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004075 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002935,
        hemo:6002936,
        hemo:6002937,
        hemo:6002938 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002936,
        hemo:6002937,
        hemo:6002938 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002935 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004072,
        hemo:7004073,
        hemo:7004074 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004076,
        hemo:7004077,
        hemo:7004078,
        hemo:7004079,
        hemo:7004080 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Inverts the live Atkinson relative weight HE-FM-ATK-004, in which a small gain to the worse-off group counts (h_better / h_worse) to the power epsilon times a gain to the better-off group. Setting that weight equal to the switching weight w_star, the w_s of HE-FM-WE-004 for the two options, and taking natural logarithms gives the approximate inequality aversion at which the two options tie. Cookson and colleagues note that an equity parameter implies a set of weights once combined with the existing distribution of health, and this formula reads the relation backwards. It is an approximation because the weights are taken at baseline health, while the exact switch solves the comparison of equally distributed equivalent health. The function log is the natural logarithm." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Implied inequality aversion from the switching weight by inverting the Atkinson relative weight" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Asaria M, Griffin S, Cookson R. Distributional cost-effectiveness analysis: a tutorial. Medical Decision Making. 2016;36(1):8-19. Section Analyzing tradeoffs between total health and health inequality using social welfare indices, with Figure 4, which show the comparison of EDE health between two strategies across levels of inequality aversion and state that the targeted strategy would be preferred above an Atkinson parameter of 8. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X15583266",
        "Cookson R, Mirelman AJ, Griffin S, Asaria M, Dawkins B, Norheim OF, Verguet S, Culyer AJ. Using cost-effectiveness analysis to address health equity concerns. Value in Health. 2017;20(2):206-212. Section Equity-Weighting Analysis (Valuing the Importance of Equity), which states that an equity parameter indirectly implies a specific set of equity weights when combined with the existing distribution of health, and that these implied weights change with that distribution. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2016.11.027",
        "Robson M, Asaria M, Cookson R, Tsuchiya A, Ali S. Eliciting the level of health inequality aversion in England. Health Economics. 2017;26(10):1328-1334. Methods, which give the initial distribution of quality-adjusted life expectancy as 62 and 74 years, and Table 1 with footnote b, which reports the median Atkinson parameter of 10.95 and the implied weight of 6.95 on a marginal gain to the poorest fifth relative to the richest fifth at initial health. https://doi.org/10.1002/hec.3430" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Reading the approximate aversion as the exact switching value. The formula gives about 2.38 for the article's example, but the EDE comparison switches at about 2.35. At an aversion of 2.36 the EDE of F already exceeds that of E by about 0.0002 years, although 2.36 lies below the approximate value.",
        "Reporting a negative aversion when no trade-off exists. A switching weight below 1 means the fairer option has at least as much total health. An illustrative weight of 0.8 with 74 and 66 years returns about minus 1.95, which is not a valid Atkinson parameter, and the case is reported as having no trade-off.",
        "Switching weight taken from per-person gains without population shares. Entering the per-person group gains in HE-FM-WE-004, whose group QALYs are totals, gives a switching weight of 0.35 divided by 0.40, or 0.875, in place of 0.21 divided by 0.16, or 1.3125. F then appears to be preferred even with equal weights, and the implied aversion comes out at about minus 1.17 instead of 2.38.",
        "Using mean health in place of the better-off group's health. Replacing 74 years with the population mean of 70.8 in the ratio gives about 3.87 instead of 2.38, because the weight in HE-FM-ATK-004 compares the two groups, not one group with the mean." ;
    hemo:9000014 "epsilon_star = log(w_star) / log(h_better / h_worse)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EET-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "implied-inequality-aversion-from-the-switching-weight-by-inverting-the-atkinson-relative-weight" .

hemo:5000670 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Geometric distribution of cycles spent in a Markov state on one visit with a constant stay probability"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-MKA-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000147 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004084 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002939,
        hemo:6002940,
        hemo:6002941,
        hemo:6002942,
        hemo:6002943,
        hemo:6002944 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002940,
        hemo:6002941 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002939,
        hemo:6002942,
        hemo:6002943,
        hemo:6002944 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004081,
        hemo:7004082,
        hemo:7004083 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004085,
        hemo:7004086,
        hemo:7004087,
        hemo:7004088,
        hemo:7004089 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives the probability that one visit to state i lasts exactly d cycles, at least d cycles or at most d cycles, counting the cycle of entry, when the probability of staying for another cycle is the same p_ii in every cycle of the stay. Each cycle acts as a trial in which the person leaves with probability 1 minus p_ii, so the stay ends at the first exit and its length has the geometric distribution of Grinstead and Snell. Its mean, 1 divided by 1 minus p_ii, is the mean episode length L_j of HE-FM-MKCH-002 and is not restated. The median is the smallest whole number of cycles at or above d_star, the first d at which F_d reaches one half; when d_star is itself a whole number F_d equals exactly one half there and that number is the median." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Geometric distribution of cycles spent in a Markov state on one visit with a constant stay probability" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Grinstead CM, Snell JL. Grinstead and Snell's Introduction to Probability. The CHANCE Project version of 4 July 2006, based on the 2nd edition published by the American Mathematical Society. Chapter 5, section 5.1, Geometric Distribution, pages 184 to 186, which defines T as the number of trials up to and including the first success with P(T = j) = q^(j-1) p for j = 1, 2, 3 and onwards, gives P(T > k) = q^k in Example 5.1, and simulates T as the least integer at or above log(1 minus rnd) divided by log q, the rule applied here to the median with rnd at one half. Taking the exit from the state as the success gives p = 1 minus p_ii. https://math.dartmouth.edu/~prob/prob/prob.pdf",
        "Sonnenberg FA, Beck JR. Markov models in medical decision making: a practical guide. Medical Decision Making. 1993;13(4) 322-338. Page 326, The Markov Property, which states that the behaviour of the process after any cycle depends only on its description in that cycle, so that the process has no memory for earlier cycles and all patients in a state have the same prognosis whatever their previous histories, and page 325, Types of Markov Processes, which calls the process with transition probabilities constant over time a Markov chain. The geometric length of a stay follows from these statements and is not itself stated in the paper. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X9301300409" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Mean stay of a memoryless Markov state read as its typical stay. A geometric stay is skewed to the right. With p_ii = 0.90613 the mean is about 10.65 cycles but the median is 8, and 62.7% of stays have ended within 10 cycles (computed here for illustration), so quoting the mean as the typical stay overstates it.",
        "One-cycle shift between counting conventions for a Markov state stay. The mean stay counting the cycle of entry is 1 divided by 1 minus p_ii, 8.333 cycles for the event-free state at 0.88, while a trace counted at the end of each cycle from model start credits p_ii/(1-p_ii), 7.333. Comparing a mean taken under one convention with a model run under the other misstates the stay by a full cycle, about 12% here." ;
    hemo:9000014 "f_d = p_ii^(d - 1) * (1 - p_ii); G_d = p_ii^(d - 1); F_d = 1 - p_ii^d; d_star = log(0.5) / log(p_ii)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-MKA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "geometric-distribution-of-cycles-spent-in-a-markov-state-on-one-visit-with-a-constant-stay-probability" .

hemo:5000672 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Exponential residual time and median sojourn in a Markov state with a constant exit rate"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-MKA-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000147 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004099 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002951,
        hemo:6002952,
        hemo:6002953,
        hemo:6002954,
        hemo:6002955 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002952,
        hemo:6002953,
        hemo:6002954 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002951,
        hemo:6002955 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004097,
        hemo:7004098 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004100,
        hemo:7004101,
        hemo:7004102,
        hemo:7004103 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "The continuous-time counterpart of HE-FM-MKA-002. With a constant total exit rate lambda the time spent in the state is exponential, with survival exp(minus lambda t) and mean 1 divided by lambda as in HE-FM-BTH-003 and HE-FM-CONT-002, which are not restated. S_res, the conditional survival ratio of HE-FM-LTR-001 applied to exponential survival, is the probability of staying a further time s after a time tau already spent, which equals exp(minus lambda s) whatever tau is, and t_med is the time by which half of all stays have ended. Grinstead and Snell state that the exponential is the only continuous density with the memoryless property, so in continuous time a constant exit rate and a stay that ignores time already spent go together. In a cycle model the stay probability is exp(minus lambda Delta), 1 minus the exit probability of HE-FM-TP-001, and t_med divided by Delta equals d_star of HE-FM-MKA-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Exponential residual time and median sojourn in a Markov state with a constant exit rate" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Grinstead CM, Snell JL. Grinstead and Snell's Introduction to Probability. The CHANCE Project version of 4 July 2006, based on the 2nd edition published by the American Mathematical Society. Chapter 5, section 5.2 exercises, Exercise 22, page 221, which defines the median of X as the value m with F(m) = 1/2 and asks for it for the exponential, giving log 2 divided by lambda from F above; and Chapter 6, section 6.3, Example 6.26, page 273, which shows that E(X) = 1/lambda. https://math.dartmouth.edu/~prob/prob/prob.pdf",
        "Grinstead CM, Snell JL. Grinstead and Snell's Introduction to Probability. The CHANCE Project version of 4 July 2006, based on the 2nd edition published by the American Mathematical Society. Chapter 5, section 5.2, Exponential and Gamma Densities, pages 205 to 207, which defines the density lambda e^(-lambda x), gives the distribution function F(x) = 1 minus e^(-lambda x) and shows that P(T > r + s | T > r) = P(T > s) = e^(-lambda s); and Chapter 2, Example 2.17, page 68, which states that the only continuous density with the memoryless property is the exponential. https://math.dartmouth.edu/~prob/prob/prob.pdf",
        "Sonnenberg FA, Beck JR. Markov models in medical decision making: a practical guide. Medical Decision Making. 1993;13(4) 322-338. Page 323, which states that if the rate of haemorrhage is a constant 0.05 per person per year the average time before a haemorrhage is 1/0.05 or 20 years. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X9301300409" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Mean sojourn taken from one exit rate when a Markov state has competing exits. With an illustrative death rate of 0.05 and progression rate of 0.10 a year, the mean stay is 1 divided by 0.15, about 6.67 years, not the 20 years implied by the death rate alone, because every exit ends the stay (HE-FM-TP-004 and HE-FM-CONT-002).",
        "Median exponential sojourn taken as half the mean. At 0.05 a year half the mean is 10 years, but the median is log 2 divided by 0.05, about 13.86 years, so the shortcut understates it by about 28% (computed here for illustration). The median is about 0.693 of the mean at any constant rate." ;
    hemo:9000014 "S_res = exp(-lambda * (tau + s)) / exp(-lambda * tau); t_med = log(2) / lambda" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-MKA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "exponential-residual-time-and-median-sojourn-in-a-markov-state-with-a-constant-exit-rate" .

hemo:5000673 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Mean stay of a discrete-cycle Markov state against the exponential mean at the same constant exit rate"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-MKA-004" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000147 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004106 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002956,
        hemo:6002957,
        hemo:6002958,
        hemo:6002959,
        hemo:6002960,
        hemo:6002961 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002957,
        hemo:6002958 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002961 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004104,
        hemo:7004105 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004107,
        hemo:7004108,
        hemo:7004109,
        hemo:7004110,
        hemo:7004111 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compares the mean stay in a cycle model whose stay probability comes from a constant exit rate with the mean time in the state of the continuous process it represents. Here p_ii is 1 minus the exit probability of HE-FM-TP-001, D_mean turns the mean cycles of HE-FM-MKCH-002 into time by multiplying by the cycle length, and T_mean is the exponential mean of HE-FM-CONT-002. The geometric count credits a whole cycle for the cycle of entry and for every cycle begun in the state, so D_mean exceeds T_mean, and the excess gap_c, in cycles, is above one half and below one, and approaches one half as lambda times Delta shrinks. This is the overestimate from counting membership at the start of each cycle that Sonnenberg and Beck describe, a matter of counting that a half-cycle correction addresses, not of memory." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Mean stay of a discrete-cycle Markov state against the exponential mean at the same constant exit rate" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Grinstead CM, Snell JL. Grinstead and Snell's Introduction to Probability. The CHANCE Project version of 4 July 2006, based on the 2nd edition published by the American Mathematical Society. Chapter 6, section 6.1, Example 6.4, page 228, which shows that the time to the first success with P(T = j) = q^(j-1) p has E(T) = 1/p, and section 6.3, Example 6.26, page 273, which shows that an exponential variable with parameter lambda has E(X) = 1/lambda. https://math.dartmouth.edu/~prob/prob/prob.pdf",
        "Sonnenberg FA, Beck JR. Markov models in medical decision making: a practical guide. Medical Decision Making. 1993;13(4) 322-338. Pages 329 to 330, The Half-Cycle Correction, which states that counting at the end of each cycle consistently underestimates the area under the survival curve and counting at the beginning consistently overestimates it, and that the correction assumes transitions occur on average halfway through each cycle. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X9301300409" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Counting gap read as a structural effect of the Markov assumption. The 0.508-year difference between the article's 10.653 discrete post-event years and the continuous 10.145 comes from whole-cycle counting at one constant rate, whereas the difference between 16 years with the tunnel and 10.653 without it comes from the missing time since entry. A half-cycle correction changes the first and leaves the second untouched, as the article notes." ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_ii = exp(-lambda * Delta); D_mean = Delta / (1 - p_ii); T_mean = 1 / lambda; gap_c = (D_mean - T_mean) / Delta" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-MKA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "mean-stay-of-a-discrete-cycle-markov-state-against-the-exponential-mean-at-the-same-constant-exit-rate" .

hemo:5000676 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Equity-efficiency impact plane quadrant of a programme against its comparator"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-DCEA-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000148 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004135 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002986,
        hemo:6002987,
        hemo:6002988,
        hemo:6002989,
        hemo:6002990,
        hemo:6002991 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002990,
        hemo:6002991 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002986,
        hemo:6002987,
        hemo:6002988,
        hemo:6002989 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004131,
        hemo:7004132,
        hemo:7004133,
        hemo:7004134 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004136,
        hemo:7004137,
        hemo:7004138,
        hemo:7004139,
        hemo:7004140,
        hemo:7004141 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Places a programme on the equity-efficiency impact plane from the sign of its change in total health, net of the health opportunity cost, and the sign of its change in an inequality index, with the comparator at the origin. A win-win programme raises total health and lowers inequality and a lose-lose programme does the reverse, while win-lose and lose-win programmes need a trade-off analysis such as the change in EDE health of HE-FM-DCEA-002. The sources name the four quadrants without classifying a point on an axis, so the formula applies the tie rule of the live dominance record HE-FM-DOM-001, with less inequality taking the place of lower cost. No change on one axis with an improvement on the other counts as win-win, the mirror case counts as lose-lose, and no change on either axis gives no quadrant." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equity-efficiency impact plane quadrant of a programme against its comparator" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Cookson R, Griffin S, Norheim OF, Culyer AJ, Chalkidou K. Distributional cost-effectiveness analysis comes of age. Value in Health. 2021;24(1):118-120. Section The Equity-Efficiency Impact Plane and Figure 1, which place the comparator at the origin, set out the four logical possibilities as the win-win, lose-lose, win-lose and lose-win quadrants, and allow equity impact in units of interest to the decision maker, including the gap between the best and worst off. Section Equity-Efficiency Trade-Offs, which requires trade-off analysis in the win-lose and lose-win cases. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2020.10.001",
        "Cookson R, Mirelman AJ, Griffin S, Asaria M, Dawkins B, Norheim OF, Verguet S, Culyer AJ. Using cost-effectiveness analysis to address health equity concerns. Value in Health. 2017;20(2):206-212. Section Health Equity Trade-offs and Figure 1, the health equity impact plane, whose vertical axis is the net total health impact, total health benefits less the forgone health benefits of spending the same money on other programmes, and whose horizontal axis is the net health equity impact after allowing for programme opportunity costs, with quadrants I to IV named win-win, win-lose, lose-lose and lose-win. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2016.11.027" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Reversing the orientation of the inequality index on the impact plane. Measuring the gap as the more deprived group's health less the other group's turns the article's widening of 0.020 years into minus 0.020 and moves the programme out of the win-lose quadrant into the win-win one, reporting a fall in inequality that did not happen.",
        "Treating a win-lose programme as one to reject. The quadrant shows that a trade-off exists and leaves open which objective prevails. The article's programme is win-lose, yet at the cited public aversion of 10.95 it still raises EDE health by 31.8 EDE QALYs under equal sharing, so the decision rests on HE-FM-DCEA-002 or on an explicit judgement about the trade-off." ;
    hemo:9000014 "WW = (d_H >= 0) and (d_I <= 0) and (d_H > 0 or d_I < 0); LL = (d_H <= 0) and (d_I >= 0) and (d_H < 0 or d_I > 0); WL = (d_H > 0) and (d_I > 0); LW = (d_H < 0) and (d_I < 0)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-DCEA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equity-efficiency-impact-plane-quadrant-of-a-programme-against-its-comparator" .

hemo:5000677 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Green Book social time preference rate from its Ramsey components with the health discount rate"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-TPR-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000149 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004146 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002992,
        hemo:6002993,
        hemo:6002994,
        hemo:6002995,
        hemo:6002996,
        hemo:6002997,
        hemo:6002998,
        hemo:6002999 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002992,
        hemo:6002993,
        hemo:6002994,
        hemo:6002995 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002999 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004142,
        hemo:7004143,
        hemo:7004144,
        hemo:7004145 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004147,
        hemo:7004148,
        hemo:7004149,
        hemo:7004150,
        hemo:7004151,
        hemo:7004152 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Adds pure time preference delta and catastrophic risk L to give time preference rho, and adds the wealth effect, the elasticity of marginal utility mu times the growth rate g of real per capita consumption, to give the social time preference rate, as in paragraphs 2.2 to 2.8 of the Treasury's supplementary guidance on discounting (February 2026). With the Table 2.A values for years 1 to 30 the two parts are 1.5% and 2.0% and the rate is 3.5%. The health discount rate r_HD leaves out the wealth effect (paragraph 2.16), so it equals rho, 1.5%, which is broader than pure time preference because it keeps catastrophic risk. Freeman, Groom and Spackman, in their report for HM Treasury, and Attema and colleagues write the same sum as the Ramsey rule." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Green Book social time preference rate from its Ramsey components with the health discount rate" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Attema AE, Brouwer WBF, Claxton K. Discounting in economic evaluations. PharmacoEconomics. 2018;36(7) 745-758. Box 1, discounting in equations, which gives the Ramsey equation D = delta + epsilon g and its extension with a factor gamma for catastrophic risk, and the section on the normative or positive approach, which states that the Ramsey route assumes all components, pure time preference included, are normatively relevant. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40273-018-0672-z",
        "Freeman M, Groom B, Spackman M. Social discount rates for cost-benefit analysis, a report for HM Treasury. London, HM Treasury, February 2018. Part 1, page 3, section on the derivation of a social rate of time preference, which writes the Ramsey rule as SDR = STP = rho + eta g, with rho made up of pure time preference delta and, in HM Treasury practice, a component L for certain types of risk. https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6983375013622473b51ca9ca/Social_Discount_Rates_for_Cost-Benefit_Analysis_A_Report_for_HM_Treasury.pdf",
        "HM Treasury. Discounting, Green Book supplementary guidance. London, HM Treasury, February 2026 (ISBN 978-1-918417-18-0), page last updated 5 February 2026. Chapter 2, paragraphs 2.1 to 2.8 and Table 2.A, which give STPR = rho + mu g with rho = delta + L, the values 0.5%, 1.0%, 1.0 and 2.0% for years 1 to 30 and the sums 1.5%, 2.0% and 3.5%; paragraphs 2.10 and 2.12 for the ranges of pure time preference and the elasticity; paragraphs 2.16 and 2.17, which set the health discount rate at 1.5% because the wealth effect is excluded and apply it in every year of the appraisal; paragraph 2.19 on real terms. https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/698f30a4492ea446ea7f43d3/Green_Book_supplementary_guidance_-_discounting.pdf",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance, the manual (PMG36). London, NICE, 2022, last updated 13 October 2026. Chapter 4, economic evaluation, section 4.5.1, which states that for the reference case costs and health effects should be discounted at the same rate of 3.5% per year, and section 4.5.3, which allows 1.5% a year for both when all of its criteria are met. https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Green Book health rate taken as pure time preference alone. Discounting health at 0.5% drops catastrophic risk from the time preference term. A QALY in year 20 then keeps a weight of 0.9051 against 0.7425 at the 1.5% health rate (computed here for illustration), as in step 3 of the article's worked example.",
        "Green Book health rate used in a NICE reference case. Discounting QALYs at 1.5% in place of the 3.5% of PMG36 section 4.5.1 puts a weight of 0.7425 on a QALY in year 20 against 0.5026, about 48% more discounted health (computed here for illustration), which lowers the cost per QALY of programmes with distant gains.",
        "Treasury 3.5% read as the public's impatience. Only 0.5 of the 3.5 points is pure time preference. Setting it to zero lowers the rate to 3.0% (Table 3.B), so a value in year 20 keeps a weight of 0.5537 instead of 0.5026 (Annex A Tables A.3 and A.1), far from the full weight of 1 that a rate of zero would give." ;
    hemo:9000014 "rho = delta + L; W_e = mu * g; STPR = rho + W_e; r_HD = STPR - W_e" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-TPR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "green-book-social-time-preference-rate-from-its-ramsey-components-with-the-health-discount-rate" .

hemo:5000678 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Implied annual discount rate for health from a TEMPUS open-ended stated choice"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-TPR-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000149 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004156 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003000,
        hemo:6003001,
        hemo:6003002 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003001,
        hemo:6003002 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003000 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004153,
        hemo:7004154,
        hemo:7004155 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004157,
        hemo:7004158,
        hemo:7004159,
        hemo:7004160,
        hemo:7004161,
        hemo:7004162,
        hemo:7004163,
        hemo:7004164 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Converts one answer to a TEMPUS open-ended question into the constant annual rate it implies. The respondent states the largest number of days y of ill health in year s at which a one-off minor treatment, delaying 20 days of ill health expected in 2 years' time, would still be worthwhile. With b = 1/(1 + r) the answer implies 20 b^2 = y b^s, which gives equation (1) of Cairns and van der Pol (2000). An answer above 20 days gives a positive rate, exactly 20 days a zero rate and fewer than 20 a negative rate. The rate is the r of the factor in HE-FM-DR-001, and rates that fall as the delay grows point to the non-constant forms of HE-FN-HYPD-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Implied annual discount rate for health from a TEMPUS open-ended stated choice" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Attema AE, Brouwer WBF, Claxton K. Discounting in economic evaluations. PharmacoEconomics. 2018;36(7) 745-758. Section on the height of discount rates, which reports that studies measuring health-related time preferences found average discount rates up to 45%, with much heterogeneity across methods and samples. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40273-018-0672-z",
        "Cairns JA, van der Pol MM. The estimation of marginal time preference in a UK-wide sample (TEMPUS) project. Health Technology Assessment. 2000;4(1) i-iv, 1-83. Chapter 5, open-ended method, section on estimating implied discount rates, page 20, equation (1), r = (y/20)^(1/(s minus 2)) minus 1 with b = 1/(1 + r), for y days of ill health s years ahead judged equivalent to 20 days 2 years ahead; the same chapter classifies responses into positive, negative and zero rates. The executive summary reports that stationarity was violated, that hyperbolic models fitted better and that the rates should not be over-emphasised because the sample was unrepresentative. https://doi.org/10.3310/hta4010",
        "van der Pol MM, Cairns JA. Negative and zero time preference for health. Health Economics. 2000;9(2) 171-175. Abstract, which reports that the majority of respondents have positive discount rates but a substantial number have negative or zero rates. https://doi.org/10.1002/(SICI)1099-1050(200003)9:2<171::AID-HEC492>3.0.CO;2-Z" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Individual implied rate used as the social discount rate. Applying the respondent's long-delay rate of 5.49% would leave a weight of 0.343 on a QALY in year 20 against 0.503 at the 3.5% of the NICE reference case, as in step 3 of the article's worked example, so prevention with distant gains would be valued about a third lower.",
        "TEMPUS delay counted from now in place of from year 2. Using s in the exponent instead of s minus 2 spreads the trade-off over years that are not part of the delay. For 24 days in year 4 the rate becomes 4.66% instead of 9.54% (computed here for illustration), about half the true value.",
        "TEMPUS ratio inverted to 20 over y. Writing (20/y) in place of (y/20) turns positive time preference into negative. For 24 days in year 4 the rate becomes minus 8.71% instead of plus 9.54% (computed here for illustration), so the respondent would be misclassified as preferring ill health sooner." ;
    hemo:9000014 "r = (y / 20)^(1 / (s - 2)) - 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-TPR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "implied-annual-discount-rate-for-health-from-a-tempus-open-ended-stated-choice" .

hemo:5000683 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Wagstaff two-group CES health social welfare with a group weight and curvature"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-SWF-004" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000150 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004226 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003021,
        hemo:6003022,
        hemo:6003023,
        hemo:6003024,
        hemo:6003025 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003022,
        hemo:6003023,
        hemo:6003024,
        hemo:6003025 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003021 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004224,
        hemo:7004225 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004227,
        hemo:7004228,
        hemo:7004229,
        hemo:7004230,
        hemo:7004231 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Combines the health of two groups through a constant elasticity of substitution (CES) function, the iso-elastic form of Wagstaff 1991 that Asaria, Griffin and Cookson describe as the most commonly used in the health inequalities literature. The weight alpha pivots the welfare contours around the line of equal health and r sets their curvature. At r = -1 W is the weighted sum alpha h_a + (1-alpha) h_b, the inequality-neutral utilitarian case, and as r approaches infinity W approaches the smaller of the two health values, the maximin of HE-FM-SWF-003. With alpha = 0.5, W is the EDE health of HE-FM-ATK-002 for two groups with epsilon = 1 + r, so the isoelastic and CES families rank distributions identically. Unlike the weighted two-group social welfare of HE-FM-WE-004, whose weight on a group's QALYs is fixed, the weight here changes with the health gap (HE-FM-SWF-005)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Wagstaff two-group CES health social welfare with a group weight and curvature" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Asaria M, Griffin S, Cookson R. Measuring health inequality in the context of cost-effectiveness analysis. In Health and Inequality. Research on Economic Inequality, volume 21. Emerald Group Publishing, 2013, pages 491-507. Author manuscript, section on social welfare functions used in the health inequalities literature, which gives W = [alpha h_a^(-r) + (1-alpha) h_b^(-r)]^(-1/r) as the iso-elastic form proposed by Wagstaff (1991) and the most commonly used in that literature, with alpha the relative weight that pivots the function around the line of equal health and r setting its curvature, and a later passage stating that the Dolan and Tsuchiya parameters are difficult to identify separately. https://doi.org/10.1108/S1049-2585(2013)0000021024",
        "Dolan P, Tsuchiya A. The social welfare function and individual responsibility: some theoretical issues and empirical evidence from health. HEDS Discussion Paper 03/01. Sheffield, University of Sheffield, 2003, revised version in Journal of Health Economics. 2009;28(1):210-220. Section I, equation [1], the two-group CES function with alpha at least 0 and at most 1, r of minus 1 or above and r not 0, for groups of equal size that are homogeneous, and the text after it, in which r = -1 gives the inequality-neutral utilitarian function and r approaching infinity the maximin Rawlsian function. https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/11035/" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Entering the Atkinson epsilon as the CES curvature r. The CES curvature and the Atkinson parameter differ by 1, epsilon = 1 + r, so the English median epsilon of 10.95 (HE-SR-ATK-007) corresponds to r of 9.95. Entering r = 10.95 for groups at 60 and 72 years with equal weights gives W of about 63.1817 instead of 63.3600, a more inequality-averse function than intended.",
        "Outer exponent of the CES welfare written as 1 over r. Writing the outer power as 1/r in place of minus 1/r returns about 0.0153 instead of 65.4545 years for 60 and 72 years with alpha = 0.5 and r = 1, a value in reciprocal years that ranks distributions in reverse." ;
    hemo:9000014 "W = (alpha * h_a^(-r) + (1 - alpha) * h_b^(-r))^(-1 / r)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-SWF-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "wagstaff-two-group-ces-health-social-welfare-with-a-group-weight-and-curvature" .

hemo:5000684 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Marginal rate of substitution and contour slope of the two-group CES health welfare"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-SWF-005" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000150 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004234 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003026,
        hemo:6003027,
        hemo:6003028,
        hemo:6003029,
        hemo:6003030,
        hemo:6003031 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003028,
        hemo:6003029,
        hemo:6003030,
        hemo:6003031 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003027 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004232,
        hemo:7004233 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004235,
        hemo:7004236,
        hemo:7004237,
        hemo:7004238,
        hemo:7004239 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Divides the marginal welfare of health in group a by that in group b. The result is the health of group b that could be given up for one more unit to group a with W unchanged, and the social indifference curve through the point, drawn with h_a on the horizontal axis, has slope minus MRS. Dolan and Tsuchiya read the MRS as the equity weight on a marginal gain to group a relative to group b. At equal health it reduces to alpha/(1-alpha) for any r, which is how they identify alpha from survey choices, and with alpha = 0.5 it equals the Atkinson relative weight of HE-FM-ATK-004 with epsilon = 1 + r when group a is the less healthy, so the factor alpha/(1-alpha) is what this formula adds. Williams' fair innings equity weights come from these slopes at current health on the contour whose EDE is 70 QALYs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Marginal rate of substitution and contour slope of the two-group CES health welfare" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Asaria M, Griffin S, Cookson R. Measuring health inequality in the context of cost-effectiveness analysis. In Health and Inequality. Research on Economic Inequality, volume 21. Emerald Group Publishing, 2013, pages 491-507. Author manuscript, section on social welfare functions used in the health inequalities literature, which reports that Williams (1997) set alpha at 0.5, used a range of r values and an EDE of 70 QALYs as the fair innings, and took the slopes of the tangents at current health as equity weights for cost-effectiveness analysis, and a later passage stating that these weights need constant updating as the distribution of health changes. https://doi.org/10.1108/S1049-2585(2013)0000021024",
        "Dolan P, Tsuchiya A. The social welfare function and individual responsibility: some theoretical issues and empirical evidence from health. HEDS Discussion Paper 03/01. Sheffield, University of Sheffield, 2003, revised version in Journal of Health Economics. 2009;28(1):210-220. Section III, equation [4], which gives the MRS of the CES function as alpha/(1-alpha) times (U_B/U_A)^(1+r) and calls it the equity weight, and states that along the 45 degree line the MRS is independent of r and a function of alpha alone, and section IV, in which the median respondent is indifferent between saving 100 lives in group A and 45 in group B, giving alpha of 0.31 and a weight of about half (0.45). https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/11035/" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Exponent r in place of 1 plus r in the CES marginal rate. The exponent on the health ratio is 1 + r, which is the Atkinson epsilon. Using r gives 1.2 instead of 1.44 for 60 and 72 years at r = 1, and at the English median epsilon of 10.95 (r of 9.95) it gives about 6.14 instead of the 7.36 of HE-ER-ATK-007 between the same groups.",
        "Treating a CES equity weight as fixed while health changes. The weight depends on the current gap. With r = 1 and equal weights it is 1.44 at 60 and 72 years but about 1.19 once group a reaches 66 years, so a weight computed once and applied to large gains overstates the priority on the later gains." ;
    hemo:9000014 "MRS = (alpha / (1 - alpha)) * (h_b / h_a)^(1 + r); slope = -MRS" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-SWF-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "marginal-rate-of-substitution-and-contour-slope-of-the-two-group-ces-health-welfare" .

hemo:5000691 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "One-step infection update of a susceptible agent from its own uniform random number"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ABM-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000153 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004298 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003077,
        hemo:6003078,
        hemo:6003079,
        hemo:6003080,
        hemo:6003081 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003078,
        hemo:6003079,
        hemo:6003080 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003081 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004295,
        hemo:7004296,
        hemo:7004297 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004299,
        hemo:7004300,
        hemo:7004301,
        hemo:7004302,
        hemo:7004303 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Turns the per-step infection probability of HE-FM-ABM-001 into the agent's next state by comparing it with a uniform random number U drawn for that agent. The agent becomes infected, X_next equal to 1, when U is below P_inf, and otherwise stays susceptible with X_next equal to 0, so over many draws the share infected approaches P_inf. Drawing U from a stream reserved for the agent, the per-agent seed that Chhatwal and He describe for common random numbers, gives the same agent the same U under each strategy, so a strategy that lowers P_inf can avert that agent's infection and cannot cause it. A single draw against P_inf has the same distribution as one draw per contact against p, which the computational function under HE-FM-ABM-001 uses as an independent check." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One-step infection update of a susceptible agent from its own uniform random number" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Chhatwal J, He T. Economic evaluations with agent-based modelling, an introduction. PharmacoEconomics. 2015;33(5):423-433. Section 2.6, which states that specific events such as transmission of disease can occur within each discrete time step, that the sequence of events within each cycle is important and that all agents are asked to update their characteristics in a certain order, and section 3.1, which implements common random numbers in agent-based models by assigning a unique random seed to each agent so that each agent's natural history or behaviour does not vary across strategies unless that agent accepts or rejects the intervention. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40273-015-0254-2",
        "Krijkamp EM, Alarid-Escudero F, Enns EA, Jalal HJ, Hunink MGM, Pechlivanoglou P. Microsimulation modeling for health decision sciences using R, a tutorial. Medical Decision Making. 2018;38(3):400-422. Section on stochastic variation and random sampling in microsimulation models, which explains that simulated individuals should be as similar as possible across comparators and that this can be achieved by explicitly setting a seed number per individual, and the section on the vectorised implementation, in which the samplev function draws each individual's state at t + 1 from vectors of random numbers generated from a U(0,1) distribution and the individual's transition probabilities. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X18754513" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Fresh random numbers for each strategy in the agent update. If each strategy draws new uniform numbers, the per-agent difference in infection between three and two contacts has a variance of 0.271 × 0.729 + 0.19 × 0.81, about 0.351, against 0.081 × 0.919, about 0.074, when each agent keeps its draw. About 4.7 times as many runs are then needed for the same standard error of the infections averted.",
        "Updating agents in sequence within a step without stating the order. If agent B, a contact of an infectious agent A, is updated before agent C, whose only contact is B, then C can be infected in the same step with probability 0.10 × 0.10, or 0.01, for a per-contact probability of 0.10, while with start-of-step states that probability is zero. The order changes the speed of spread and is part of the model specification." ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_inf = 1 - (1 - p)^k; X_next = (U < P_inf)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ABM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-step-infection-update-of-a-susceptible-agent-from-its-own-uniform-random-number" .

hemo:5000693 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Monte Carlo runs needed for a target standard error of a share of agent-based model runs"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ABM-004" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000153 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004315 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003085,
        hemo:6003086,
        hemo:6003087 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003086,
        hemo:6003087 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003085 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004313,
        hemo:7004314 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004316,
        hemo:7004317,
        hemo:7004318,
        hemo:7004319,
        hemo:7004320 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "For an output that is a share of runs, such as the share in which an outbreak becomes a major one, each run gives a 0 or 1 result and the Monte Carlo standard error of the share is the binomial standard error, the square root of P_exp(1 minus P_exp) over the number of runs, the standard error of an estimated binomial probability in HE-FM-BINOM-004. Putting in the expected share and rearranging gives the number of runs R_prop for a target standard error e_share, equation 1 of Morris and colleagues. The product P_exp(1 minus P_exp) is largest at a share of 0.5, so 0.25 divided by the squared target is a worst-case count when the share is unknown." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Monte Carlo runs needed for a target standard error of a share of agent-based model runs" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Morris TP, White IR, Crowther MJ. Using simulation studies to evaluate statistical methods. Statistics in Medicine. 2019;38(11):2074-2102. Section 5.3, equation 1, which sets the number of repetitions as the expected coverage times one minus the expected coverage divided by the square of the required Monte Carlo standard error, gives 1,900 repetitions for a coverage of 95% with a standard error of 0.5%, and states that the worst case occurs at 50%, where 10,000 repetitions keep the standard error below 0.5%. https://doi.org/10.1002/sim.8086" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Reading the share of first-order runs as the probability of cost-effectiveness. The share of runs at fixed inputs in which a strategy comes out cost-effective measures chance between simulated histories, first-order uncertainty. The probability that a strategy is cost-effective for a decision comes from draws of uncertain inputs, second-order uncertainty, which Chhatwal and He treat separately under probabilistic sensitivity analysis.",
        "Share and target standard error in different units. Entering the target of 0.5% as 0.5 while the share is entered as 0.95 gives 0.19, rounded up to 1 run, instead of 1,900. The share and the target are entered in the same units, both as proportions or both as percentages." ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_prop = P_exp * (1 - P_exp) / e_share^2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ABM-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "monte-carlo-runs-needed-for-a-target-standard-error-of-a-share-of-agent-based-model-runs" .

hemo:5000694 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Relative survival as all-cause survival over expected background survival"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-BGM-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000154 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004323 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003088,
        hemo:6003089,
        hemo:6003090 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003089,
        hemo:6003090 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003088 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004321,
        hemo:7004322 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004324,
        hemo:7004325,
        hemo:7004326,
        hemo:7004327,
        hemo:7004328 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Divides the all-cause survival of the patient group at time t by the survival expected at the same time in a comparable group of the general population, matched on age, sex and calendar year. This is equation 14 of NICE DSU TSD 21, the survival-scale form of the additive hazard HE-FM-BTH-005. Because all-cause survival is the product of expected survival and survival from the excess hazard, relative survival equals exp of minus the cumulative excess hazard (HE-FN-CUMH-001), so it falls only while the excess hazard is above zero and levels off at the cure fraction in a mixture cure model (HE-FM-CRM-001). A value above 1 means that cumulative modelled mortality up to t is below that of the general population." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Relative survival as all-cause survival over expected background survival" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Rutherford MJ, Lambert PC, Sweeting MJ, Pennington B, Crowther MJ, Abrams KR, Latimer NR. NICE DSU Technical Support Document 21: Flexible methods for survival analysis. Sheffield, Decision Support Unit, ScHARR, University of Sheffield; 2020 (updated March 2022). Section 3.7.2, equation 14, which states that relative survival is the ratio of the all-cause survival and the expected survival in the background population, obtained by transferring equation 13, the additive split of the all-cause mortality rate, to the survival scale; the same section lists the assumption that life-table rates are fixed and known with no associated uncertainty, and section 3.7.3 asks for a population exchangeable in other-cause rates. https://doi.org/10.15131/shef.data.33849118",
        "Rutherford MJ, Lambert PC, Sweeting MJ, Pennington B, Crowther MJ, Abrams KR, Latimer NR. NICE DSU Technical Support Document 21: Flexible methods for survival analysis. Sheffield, Decision Support Unit, ScHARR, University of Sheffield; 2020 (updated March 2022). Section 6.2 (pages 75 and 76), which states that it is unlikely that the mortality rate in patients with the disease of interest will be lower than that in the general population and that in nearly all cases it would not be expected to be lower; section 4.3.5, which states that other cause mortality must be incorporated, otherwise a proportion of patients will be predicted never to die; and section 4.2 on general population rates that may be too low when comorbidity is common. https://doi.org/10.15131/shef.data.33849118" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Expected survival divided by all-cause survival. Inverting the ratio returns 2.2259 in place of 0.4493 at 10 years in the article's example (computed here for illustration). A relative survival above 1 for a group with excess mortality is the sign of the inversion.",
        "Relative survival used in place of all-cause survival. Leaving out expected survival treats relative survival as the survival of the patients, so cured patients never die, which TSD 21 section 4.3.5 warns against. In the article's illustrative cure model life years to 30 years rise from 11.93 to 14.40, an overstatement of 2.47 years, or 21%." ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_t = S_t / S_star_t" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-BGM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "relative-survival-as-all-cause-survival-over-expected-background-survival" .

hemo:5000696 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Marginal expected survival and background hazard of a cohort mixing two sexes"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-BGM-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000154 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004340 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003094,
        hemo:6003095,
        hemo:6003096,
        hemo:6003097,
        hemo:6003098,
        hemo:6003099,
        hemo:6003100 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003096,
        hemo:6003097,
        hemo:6003098,
        hemo:6003099,
        hemo:6003100 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003095 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004338,
        hemo:7004339 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004341,
        hemo:7004342,
        hemo:7004343,
        hemo:7004344,
        hemo:7004345 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Averages expected survival over the members of a cohort and weights each group's background hazard by its expected survival, the grouped form of equations 18 and 19 of NICE DSU TSD 21 for two groups such as women and men. Because the group with the higher hazard dies sooner, its weight in the marginal hazard shrinks over time, and the marginal background hazard falls below a fixed blend of the two hazards. The same form applies to age groups in a cohort with a spread of entry ages, where it avoids reading one hazard at the mean age. S_star is the expected survival of HE-FM-BGM-001 and h_star the background hazard of HE-FM-BTH-005 for the whole cohort." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Marginal expected survival and background hazard of a cohort mixing two sexes" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Office for National Statistics. National life tables QMI. Last revised 10 December 2025. Section 6, methods used to produce the national life tables data, which states that life tables are usually constructed separately for males and females because of their different mortality patterns, and defines the mortality rate between age x and x + 1. https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/birthsdeathsandmarriages/lifeexpectancies/methodologies/nationallifetablesqmi",
        "Rutherford MJ, Lambert PC, Sweeting MJ, Pennington B, Crowther MJ, Abrams KR, Latimer NR. NICE DSU Technical Support Document 21: Flexible methods for survival analysis. Sheffield, Decision Support Unit, ScHARR, University of Sheffield; 2020 (updated March 2022). Section 6.2, equations 18 and 19 (page 75), which give marginal expected survival as the average of the expected survival of the N subjects and the marginal expected hazard as a weighted average of the subjects' expected hazard rates, with expected survival as the weights, as a reference because the mortality rate in patients with the disease of interest is unlikely to be lower than in the general population. https://doi.org/10.15131/shef.data.33849118" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Background hazard read at the mean starting age. Mortality rises steeply with age, so the hazard at the mean age is below the average hazard of a cohort with a spread of ages. With illustrative hazards that double every 10 years, 0.010 at 60, 0.020 at 70 and 0.040 at 80, a cohort split equally between ages 60 and 80 has an average entry hazard of 0.025, while the hazard at the mean age of 70 is 0.020 (computed here for illustration).",
        "Fixed equal-weight blend of female and male background hazards. Averaging the two hazards with the starting shares at every time ignores that men die sooner. In the first worked example the blend gives 0.025 against 0.024667, and the gap widens as the cohort ages; TSD 21 section 6.2 weights each expected hazard by expected survival instead." ;
    hemo:9000014 "S_star = w_f * S_f + (1 - w_f) * S_m; h_star = (w_f * S_f * h_f + (1 - w_f) * S_m * h_m) / S_star" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-BGM-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "marginal-expected-survival-and-background-hazard-of-a-cohort-mixing-two-sexes" .

hemo:5000697 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Basic reproduction number of the SIR model as transmission rate times mean infectious period"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-BRN-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000155 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004348 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003101,
        hemo:6003102,
        hemo:6003103,
        hemo:6003104 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003102,
        hemo:6003103 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003104 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004346,
        hemo:7004347 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004349,
        hemo:7004350,
        hemo:7004351,
        hemo:7004352,
        hemo:7004353 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the simple susceptible, infectious and recovered (SIR) model, one infectious person in a fully susceptible population causes new infections at rate beta and stays infectious for a mean time D equal to 1/gamma, so R_0 is beta times D, or beta/gamma. Van den Driessche derives R_0 as the product of the transmission rate and the mean infectious time and shows that R_0 = 1 is a sharp threshold, with the number infectious falling steadily to zero below it and first rising above it. The WHO guide writes the same quantity for a homogeneously mixing population as p c D, the probability of transmission per contact times the contact rate times the duration of infectiousness, so beta here corresponds to p times c. Models with a latent stage, demography or several groups take R_0 from the next-generation matrix of HE-FM-BRN-005." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Basic reproduction number of the SIR model as transmission rate times mean infectious period" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Delamater PL, Street EJ, Leslie TF, Yang YT, Jacobsen KH. Complexity of the basic reproduction number (R0). Emerging Infectious Diseases. 2019;25(1):1-4. The abstract, which states that R_0 is not a biological constant for a pathogen, a rate over time or a measure of disease severity, and the section Various Names for R0, which states that R_0 does not indicate whether new cases occur within 24 hours of the initial case or months later and is most accurately described in terms of cases per case. https://doi.org/10.3201/eid2501.171901",
        "World Health Organization. WHO guide for standardization of economic evaluations of immunization programmes, 2nd edition (WHO/IVB/19.10). Geneva, WHO, 2019. Section 6.1.1, which writes R_0 = pcD for a homogeneously mixing population without immigration or emigration, with p the probability of transmission following exposure, c the average number of new contacts per unit of time and D the duration of infectiousness, and states that the reproduction number depends on infectious disease characteristics (p and D) and population characteristics (c and x). https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/329389",
        "van den Driessche P. Reproduction numbers of infectious disease models. Infectious Disease Modelling. 2017;2(3):288-303. Section 2.1, which defines R_0 = beta S_0/gamma for the SIR model as the product of the transmission rate, the mean infectious time 1/gamma and S_0, notes that R_0 is independent of the fraction dying from the disease, states that R_0 = 1 acts as a sharp threshold between the disease dying out and causing an epidemic, and gives R_0 = beta S_0/(d + gamma) once births and natural deaths are included. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.idm.2017.06.002" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Infectious period of 10 days entered as the recovery rate. Typing 10 days into the recovery rate cell gives R_0 = 0.3/10 = 0.03 instead of 3 for the article's infection, which would show an epidemic-prone infection as one that dies out at once.",
        "Same basic reproduction number read as the same speed of spread. R_0 carries no time unit. Two infections with R_0 of 3, one with a 10-day and one with a 3-day mean infectious period (beta of 0.30 and 1.0 per day), have early growth rates of 0.2 and about 0.667 per day by the SIR relation r = gamma(R_0 minus 1), and doubling times of about 3.47 and 1.04 days (computed here for illustration). Delamater and colleagues stress that R_0 does not show whether secondary cases appear within a day or months later." ;
    hemo:9000014 "D = 1 / gamma; R_0 = beta * D" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-BRN-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "basic-reproduction-number-of-the-sir-model-as-transmission-rate-times-mean-infectious-period" .

hemo:5000698 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Effective reproduction number as the basic reproduction number times the susceptible proportion"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-BRN-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000155 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004356 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003105,
        hemo:6003106,
        hemo:6003107 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003106,
        hemo:6003107 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003105 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004354,
        hemo:7004355 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004357,
        hemo:7004358,
        hemo:7004359,
        hemo:7004360,
        hemo:7004361 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Once part of the population is immune, each case meets fewer people who can be infected. The WHO guide gives R_t = R_0 x_t for a homogeneously mixing population, where x_t is the proportion of the population still susceptible at time t, and Pitman and colleagues define the effective reproduction number in the same way. Vaccination and past infection lower x_t and so R_t, while R_0 stays the same because its definition assumes a fully susceptible population. R_t above 1 means new infections per generation are rising and below 1 falling. With an all-or-nothing vaccine of effectiveness E given to a proportion V before any spread, x_t equals 1 minus V times E, which is the starting susceptible proportion s_0 of HE-CF-ATR-001 when V times E is entered there as the fully protected proportion." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Effective reproduction number as the basic reproduction number times the susceptible proportion" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Pitman R, Fisman D, Zaric GS, Postma M, Kretzschmar M, Edmunds J, Brisson M. Dynamic transmission modeling. A report of the ISPOR-SMDM Modeling Good Research Practices Task Force-5. Value in Health. 2012;15(6):828-834. Section on the basic reproduction number, which defines R_0 as the average number of secondary infections generated by a typical case in a fully susceptible population and the effective reproduction number as R_0 multiplied by the susceptible fraction of the population s(t), with a value of 1 giving a threshold for invasion. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2012.06.011",
        "World Health Organization. WHO guide for standardization of economic evaluations of immunization programmes, 2nd edition (WHO/IVB/19.10). Geneva, WHO, 2019. Section 6.1.1, which defines the effective reproduction number R_t as the number of secondary cases a single infectious individual causes on average at time t and gives R_t = R_0 x_t, assuming a negligible proportion of the population is infectious, and section 6.2, which states that vaccination reduces the proportion of susceptibles x_t while R_0 remains constant. https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/329389" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Current reproduction number quoted as the basic reproduction number. An estimate of R_t made after immunity has built up understates R_0. Reading the article's R_t of 1.65 as R_0 gives a herd immunity threshold of 1 minus 1/1.65, about 0.394, instead of 0.667 (computed here for illustration), so the coverage target would be set far too low." ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_t = R_0 * x_t" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-BRN-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "effective-reproduction-number-as-the-basic-reproduction-number-times-the-susceptible-proportion" .

hemo:5000699 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Herd immunity threshold and critical vaccination coverage for a vaccine of effectiveness E"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-BRN-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000155 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004364 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003108,
        hemo:6003109,
        hemo:6003110,
        hemo:6003111 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003109,
        hemo:6003111 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003110 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004362,
        hemo:7004363 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004365,
        hemo:7004366,
        hemo:7004367,
        hemo:7004368,
        hemo:7004369,
        hemo:7004370 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Sustained spread stops once the susceptible proportion is below 1/R_0, so the proportion that must be immune is H = 1 minus 1/R_0, the herd immunity threshold of the WHO guide, which calls the matching level the critical effective immunisation level. Van den Driessche gives the same condition for a perfect vaccine, and Pitman and colleagues state that in a homogeneously mixing population coverage has to be greater than this value for herd immunity to occur. An all-or-nothing vaccine that fully protects a proportion E of those vaccinated immunises V times E of the population, so the critical coverage is V_c = H/E, the form Masterson and colleagues use, and when E is below H, V_c exceeds 1 and that vaccine alone cannot stop sustained spread. Pitman and colleagues and van den Driessche state the condition as coverage greater than the threshold, while the WHO guide writes p_c as at least 1 minus 1/R_0. At coverage exactly V_c the effective reproduction number of HE-FM-BRN-002 equals 1, the boundary that HE-CF-ATR-001 classifies as no major epidemic, since it returns a final attack rate of 0 whenever R_0 s_0 is 1 or less." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Herd immunity threshold and critical vaccination coverage for a vaccine of effectiveness E" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Masterson SG, Lobel L, Carroll MW, Wass MN, Michaelis M. Herd immunity to Ebolaviruses is not a realistic target for current vaccination strategies. Frontiers in Immunology. 2018;9:1025. Materials and methods, equations 1 and 2, which compute the herd immunity threshold I_c = 1 minus 1/R_0 and the critical vaccination coverage V_c = I_c/E with E the vaccine effectiveness, citing Anderson 1992 and Fine, Eames and Heymann 2011 among others, and the abstract, which reports a threshold of 75% at R_0 of 4 and more than 80% coverage needed at an effectiveness of 90%. https://doi.org/10.3389/fimmu.2018.01025",
        "Pitman R, Fisman D, Zaric GS, Postma M, Kretzschmar M, Edmunds J, Brisson M. Dynamic transmission modeling. A report of the ISPOR-SMDM Modeling Good Research Practices Task Force-5. Value in Health. 2012;15(6):828-834. Section on the basic reproduction number, which states that in a homogeneously mixing population, for herd immunity to occur, vaccine coverage V_c has to be greater than 1 minus 1/R_0. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2012.06.011",
        "World Health Organization. WHO guide for standardization of economic evaluations of immunization programmes, 2nd edition (WHO/IVB/19.10). Geneva, WHO, 2019. Section 6.2, which gives the threshold susceptible proportion x* = 1/R_0, the herd immunity threshold H = 1 minus x* = 1 minus 1/R_0 and the critical effective immunisation level p_c of at least 1 minus 1/R_0, states that actual coverage should be greater because the protective efficacy of a vaccine is usually imperfect and it is not always given at birth, gives measles R_0 of 9.3 to 15.6 with a critical proportion of 89 to 94% in Table 7, and reports that crude estimates are overestimates or underestimates under age-dependent transmission. https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/329389",
        "van den Driessche P. Reproduction numbers of infectious disease models. Infectious Disease Modelling. 2017;2(3):288-303. Section 2.1, which states that if a perfect vaccine is given to a fraction p of the population the disease will not spread if (1 minus p) R_0 is below 1, that is if p exceeds 1 minus 1/R_0, giving herd immunity. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.idm.2017.06.002" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Herd immunity threshold read as the share that will be infected. The threshold marks where incidence starts to fall, not where the epidemic ends. With no programme an SIR epidemic with R_0 of 3 infects about 94% of the population (the article's final size, computed as in HE-CF-ATR-001), well beyond the threshold of 66.7%, because infections continue while the epidemic declines.",
        "Herd immunity threshold used as the coverage target for an imperfect vaccine. Vaccinating 66.7% of the population with a 90% effective vaccine immunises 60%, leaving R_t of 3 times 0.4, or 1.2, so spread continues; the article's critical coverage is 74.1%." ;
    hemo:9000014 "H = 1 - 1 / R_0; V_c = H / E" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-BRN-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "herd-immunity-threshold-and-critical-vaccination-coverage-for-a-vaccine-of-effectiveness-e" .

hemo:5000700 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Reproduction number from the early exponential growth rate under three generation interval shapes"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-BRN-004" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000155 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004373 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003112,
        hemo:6003113,
        hemo:6003114,
        hemo:6003115,
        hemo:6003116,
        hemo:6003117,
        hemo:6003118 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003113,
        hemo:6003114,
        hemo:6003115 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003116,
        hemo:6003117,
        hemo:6003118 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004371,
        hemo:7004372 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004374,
        hemo:7004375,
        hemo:7004376,
        hemo:7004377,
        hemo:7004378 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the early phase of an outbreak new infections grow as exp(r t), and Wallinga and Lipsitch show that the reproduction number implied by a growth rate r depends only on the generation interval distribution, through R = 1/M(minus r) with M its moment generating function. An exponential generation interval with mean T_c gives R = 1 plus r T_c; a latent stage and an infectious stage, each exponentially distributed with means T_E and T_I, give (1 plus r T_E)(1 plus r T_I); and a fixed interval gives exp(r T_c), the upper bound for a given growth rate and mean. In the SIR model T_c is the mean infectious period 1/gamma, so the exponential form is van den Driessche's R_0 = 1 plus r/gamma, the inverse of r = gamma(R_0 minus 1). A growth rate taken from an epidemic doubling time T_d, as used in HE-FM-CFR-003, is log(2)/T_d." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Reproduction number from the early exponential growth rate under three generation interval shapes" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Diekmann O, Heesterbeek JA, Roberts MG. The construction of next-generation matrices for compartmental epidemic models. Journal of the Royal Society Interface. 2010;7(47):873-885. Abstract and appendix A, which prove for compartmental systems that R_0, defined as the dominant eigenvalue of the next-generation matrix, and the real-time growth rate r of the early phase are connected so that R_0 is above 1 if and only if r is above 0, and R_0 = 1 if and only if r = 0. https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2009.0386",
        "Wallinga J, Lipsitch M. How generation intervals shape the relationship between growth rates and reproductive numbers. Proceedings of the Royal Society B. 2007;274(1609):599-604. Section 2(b), equation 2.9, R = 1/M(minus r) with M the moment generating function of the generation interval distribution; section 3(a), equation 3.1, R = 1 plus r/b for an exponential distribution with 1/b the mean generation interval, and equation 3.2, R = (1 plus r/b_1)(1 plus r/b_2) for exponential latent and infectious periods with mean T_c = 1/b_1 plus 1/b_2; section 3(c), equation 3.5, R = exp(r T_c) for a delta distribution; and the abstract, which states that the equations differ only in the assumed shape of the generation interval distribution and that equal generation intervals give an upper bound. https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2006.3754",
        "van den Driessche P. Reproduction numbers of infectious disease models. Infectious Disease Modelling. 2017;2(3):288-303. Section 2.1, which gives the early growth I(t) approximately equal to I(0) exp[gamma(R_0 minus 1)t], and section 9.6, which states that if the exponential growth rate r of the initial phase is observed then R_0 = 1 plus r/gamma, a formula based on the simple SIR model with limited applicability. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.idm.2017.06.002" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Infectious period used as the generation interval when there is a latent stage. Ignoring the 4-day latent stage of the illustrative example and using R = 1 plus r T_I with the 6-day infectious period gives 2.2 instead of 3.96 at a growth rate of 0.2 per day, which would put the herd immunity threshold at about 0.545 instead of 0.747 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "T_c = T_E + T_I; R_exp = 1 + r * T_c; R_seir = (1 + r * T_E) * (1 + r * T_I); R_delta = exp(r * T_c)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-BRN-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "reproduction-number-from-the-early-exponential-growth-rate-under-three-generation-interval-shapes" .

hemo:5000706 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Japanese foreign average price adjustment of a calculated new drug price"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-BOC-005" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000156 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004441 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003146,
        hemo:6003147,
        hemo:6003148 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003147,
        hemo:6003148 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003146 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004439,
        hemo:7004440 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004442,
        hemo:7004443,
        hemo:7004444,
        hemo:7004445,
        hemo:7004446,
        hemo:7004447 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Moves a price calculated by cost accounting, or by similar efficacy comparison for a product without a similar pharmacological action, towards the foreign average price when it is far from it, as the 2021 MHLW slide sets out. Above 1.25 times the foreign average the price becomes P_calc/3 plus 5 P_fap/6, below 0.75 times it becomes P_calc/3 plus P_fap/2 up to twice P_calc, and between the two it is unchanged. The single line writes the slide's branches with min and max (derived here); the branches meet the unchanged price at exactly 1.25 and 0.75 times P_fap, so a tie at either threshold gives the same price whichever branch is applied. P_fap is the mean of the converted prices of the four-country basket after the slide's trimming of an outlying highest price, a mean that HE-CF-BOC-002 computes for any number of countries, and the threshold test is HE-FM-BOC-004." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Japanese foreign average price adjustment of a calculated new drug price" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare (Abe Y). Japan's NHI Drug Price System. Presentation, 1 April 2021, hosted by the Pharmaceuticals and Medical Devices Agency. Slide headed Foreign Average Price Adjustment, part 1 (page 17 of the PDF), which gives the adjusted price above 1.25 fold as (1/3 times the calculated value over the foreign average price plus 5/6) times the foreign average price and below 0.75 times as (1/3 times the same ratio plus 1/2) times the foreign average price, up to twice the calculated value, with the two trimming rules for the foreign average price. https://www.pmda.go.jp/files/000248690.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Price cut to exactly 1.25 times the foreign average. Setting the price to the threshold gives 131.25 in place of about 134.17 for a calculated price of 140 against 105. The slide's formula keeps one third of the excess above 1.25 times the foreign average, 2.92 here (derived here).",
        "Raise applied without the cap at twice the calculated price. Without the cap a calculated price of 20 against 105 rises to about 59.17, almost three times the calculated value, against the capped 40 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_adj = min(max(P_calc, min(P_calc / 3 + P_fap / 2, 2 * P_calc)), P_calc / 3 + 5 * P_fap / 6)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-BOC-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "japanese-foreign-average-price-adjustment-of-a-calculated-new-drug-price" .

hemo:5000709 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Force of infection and new infection flow under frequency-dependent transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CMPM-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000158 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004467 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003163,
        hemo:6003164,
        hemo:6003165,
        hemo:6003166,
        hemo:6003167,
        hemo:6003168 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003164,
        hemo:6003165,
        hemo:6003166,
        hemo:6003167 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003168 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004465,
        hemo:7004466 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004468,
        hemo:7004469,
        hemo:7004470,
        hemo:7004471 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "The force of infection is the rate at which each susceptible person is infected. Under frequency-dependent, or standard, transmission it is the transmission rate beta times the infectious proportion I/N, and new infections per unit time are lambda times S, the flow from the susceptible to the infectious compartment. The WHO guide writes the incidence as lambda X = p c (Y/N) X, so beta here corresponds to p times c, the probability of transmission per contact times the contact rate. Because lambda rises and falls with I, a model that recalculates it at every step is dynamic and one that holds it fixed, or takes it from a run without the programme, is static (HE-FM-CMPM-005). Hethcote contrasts this standard incidence with the mass action form eta I S, for which beta equals eta N, the form van den Driessche writes as beta S I with force of infection beta I. The per-person probability of infection within a step under a constant lambda follows HE-FM-TP-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Force of infection and new infection flow under frequency-dependent transmission" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Hethcote HW. The mathematics of infectious diseases. SIAM Review. 2000;42(4):599-653. Section 2.1, Formulating Epidemiology Models (pages 602 and 603), which defines the standard incidence (beta I/N) S as the number of new cases per unit time, with beta the average number of adequate contacts of a person per unit time, compares it with the simple mass action law eta I S, for which beta = eta N, and concludes that the standard incidence is more realistic for human diseases. https://doi.org/10.1137/S0036144500371907",
        "World Health Organization. WHO guide for standardization of economic evaluations of immunization programmes, 2nd edition (WHO/IVB/19.10). Geneva, WHO, 2019. Section 6.1.2, Incidence and the force of infection, which defines the force of infection lambda as the probability per unit of time that a susceptible person becomes infected, the per-susceptible rate of infection, and gives the incidence of new cases as lambda X = p c (Y/N) X, with X the number of susceptible people, Y the number of infectious people, N the total population, p the probability of transmission given contact and c the number of contacts. https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/329389",
        "van den Driessche P. Reproduction numbers of infectious disease models. Infectious Disease Modelling. 2017;2(3):288-303. Section 2.1, SIR epidemic model, which models transmission by mass action with the term beta S I, beta being the transmission rate constant and beta I the force of infection, in the equations dS/dt = minus beta S I, dI/dt = beta S I minus gamma I and dR/dt = f gamma I. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.idm.2017.06.002" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Frequency-dependent transmission rate entered into the mass action form. Entering the article's beta of 0.3 per day into beta S I without dividing by N gives 297,000 new infections a day in a population of 10,000 instead of 29.7. Hethcote shows that the mass action coefficient eta equals beta/N, here 0.00003 per person per day, so a coefficient taken from one form is rescaled before use in the other.",
        "Susceptible count used in place of the whole population in the force of infection. Dividing by S instead of N gives 0.3 times 100/9,900, about 0.00303, and 30.0 infections on the article's day 1, 1% too many, and the error grows as susceptibles are used up. With 5,000 susceptibles left and 100 infectious the wrong force is 0.006, twice the correct 0.003 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = beta * I / N; F_SI = lambda * S" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CMPM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "force-of-infection-and-new-infection-flow-under-frequency-dependent-transmission" .

hemo:5000710 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Force of infection on one group from infectious people in two mixing groups"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CMPM-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000158 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004473 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003169,
        hemo:6003170,
        hemo:6003171,
        hemo:6003172,
        hemo:6003173,
        hemo:6003174,
        hemo:6003175 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003170,
        hemo:6003171,
        hemo:6003172,
        hemo:6003173,
        hemo:6003174,
        hemo:6003175 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003169 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004472 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004474,
        hemo:7004475,
        hemo:7004476 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "When the population is split into groups, such as age or risk groups, each compartment is stratified by group and the force of infection on susceptible people in group a sums the transmission from the infectious people of every group, each weighted by a transmission rate beta_ab and the infectious proportion I_b/N_b of group b. Written out for two groups the formula gives lambda_1, and lambda_2 follows by exchanging the indices 1 and 2. Hethcote's SEIR model with age groups writes the force of infection on group i as a contact-weighted sum over the infectious fractions of all groups, with separable contact rates, which is the case beta_ab = b_a b~_b N_b/N of the matrix form here (derived); a full matrix of beta_ab, the article's notation, allows any pattern of mixing. When beta_1b equals beta times N_b/N for both groups, mixing is random and lambda_1 reduces to beta I/N of HE-FM-CMPM-002 (derived). The reproduction number of a stratified model is the dominant eigenvalue of its next-generation matrix, HE-FM-BRN-005 and HE-CF-BRN-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Force of infection on one group from infectious people in two mixing groups" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Hethcote HW. The mathematics of infectious diseases. SIAM Review. 2000;42(4):599-653. Section 6.1, Formulation of the SEIR Model with Age Groups (pages 634 and 635), which assumes contact rates that are constant on each age interval and writes, in equation 6.2, the force of infection on age group i as lambda_i = b_i times the sum over groups j of b~_j i_j, with i_j the infectious people of group j as a fraction of the whole population. The section does not print the full-matrix form lambda_a = sum over b of beta_ab I_b/N_b; the row derives Hethcote's separable form as a special case of it. https://doi.org/10.1137/S0036144500371907",
        "Pitman R, Fisman D, Zaric GS, Postma M, Kretzschmar M, Edmunds J, Brisson M. Dynamic transmission modeling. A report of the ISPOR-SMDM Modeling Good Research Practices Task Force-5. Value in Health. 2012;15(6):828-834. Best practice V-6, which asks models that use differential equations to provide them, to tabulate all initial values and parameters, including the mixing matrix, and to supply details of the type of mixing considered. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2012.06.011" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Group infectious counts divided by the whole population instead of the group size. Dividing I_b by N rather than N_b understates the force on the group whose own prevalence is high. In the illustrative children and adults example it gives 0.0032 instead of 0.0115 per day, about 72% too low, and in the random-mixing example 0.00168 instead of 0.003 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda_1 = beta_11 * I_1 / N_1 + beta_12 * I_2 / N_2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CMPM-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "force-of-infection-on-one-group-from-infectious-people-in-two-mixing-groups" .

hemo:5000714 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Jevons elementary aggregate of three matched consumer prices"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CPI-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000159 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004500 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003208,
        hemo:6003209,
        hemo:6003210,
        hemo:6003211,
        hemo:6003212,
        hemo:6003213,
        hemo:6003214 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003208,
        hemo:6003209,
        hemo:6003210,
        hemo:6003211,
        hemo:6003212,
        hemo:6003213 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003214 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004498,
        hemo:7004499 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004501,
        hemo:7004502,
        hemo:7004503,
        hemo:7004504 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Combines the prices of matched products within one elementary aggregate, where no expenditure weights exist, as the geometric mean of their price relatives, the formula the ONS technical manual names as the one primarily used at this level in the UK CPI and CPIH (section 3.4). Each price carries the same weight, the reciprocal of the number of prices. The manual gives an algebraically equivalent form, the ratio of the geometric means of current and base prices. J is a ratio, and 100 times it gives the elementary index on the scale of HE-FM-CPI-001; item and higher-level indices are weighted averages of these elementary indices (section 3.5). The formula is written out for three prices so that the calculator runs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Jevons elementary aggregate of three matched consumer prices" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Office for National Statistics. Consumer Prices Indices Technical Manual, 2019. Newport, Office for National Statistics, 2019, page last revised 26 March 2025. Section 3.4, elementary aggregates, which states that unweighted formulae are used at the lowest level, treating all products as equally important with a weight equal to the reciprocal of the number of prices, defines the Jevons index as the geometric mean of price relatives, the Dutot index as the ratio of average prices and the Carli index as the arithmetic mean of price relatives, names the Jevons index as the elementary aggregate formula primarily used in UK consumer price indices, gives it as the n-th root of the product of the relatives p_i^t / p_i^0 and as the ratio of geometric means of prices, requires prices for matching products, and records that Eurostat regulations forbid the Carli index and that it can introduce chain drift. The page carries a notice dated 25 March 2026 that the manual has been replaced by the ONS technical guidance, whose article Traditional data aggregates in consumer prices (last revised 25 March 2026, section 12) states that the CPI and CPIH use the Jevons index for local collection items and a mixture of the Dutot and Jevons formulations for central items, and gives the same two forms of the Jevons index. https://www.ons.gov.uk/economy/inflationandpriceindices/methodologies/consumerpricesindicestechnicalmanual2019" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Carli arithmetic mean of relatives in place of the Jevons index. The arithmetic mean of the worked example relatives is 1, no change, against a Jevons fall of about 0.33%, and an arithmetic mean is never below the geometric mean of the same relatives (computed here for illustration). The manual records that Eurostat regulations forbid the Carli index and that in certain circumstances, combined with chain-linking of the within-year indices, it introduces an upward bias known as chain drift (section 3.4).",
        "Unmatched replacement product entered as a Jevons price change. Replacing the third product, priced 4.00 in the base month, with a different product at 3.00 and treating it as the same item gives a Jevons index of 0.937889, a spurious fall of about 6.2% (illustrative, computed here for illustration). The manual excludes such a price or makes a quality adjustment (sections 3.4 and 9)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "J = (p1_t / p1_0 * p2_t / p2_0 * p3_t / p3_0)^(1 / 3)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CPI-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "jevons-elementary-aggregate-of-three-matched-consumer-prices" .

hemo:5000720 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Budget implied by a fixed cost-effectiveness ratio across four independent programmes"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CELT-004" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000160 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004548 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003251,
        hemo:6003252,
        hemo:6003253,
        hemo:6003254,
        hemo:6003255,
        hemo:6003256,
        hemo:6003257,
        hemo:6003258,
        hemo:6003259,
        hemo:6003260,
        hemo:6003261,
        hemo:6003262,
        hemo:6003263,
        hemo:6003264 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003256,
        hemo:6003257,
        hemo:6003258,
        hemo:6003259,
        hemo:6003260,
        hemo:6003261,
        hemo:6003262,
        hemo:6003263,
        hemo:6003264 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003255 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004546,
        hemo:7004547 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004549,
        hemo:7004550,
        hemo:7004551,
        hemo:7004552,
        hemo:7004553 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Under the fixed ratio rule a programme is adopted when its ICER does not exceed a threshold lambda, tested here as Delta_C_i at most lambda times Delta_E_i, which is an incremental net monetary benefit of HE-FM-NMB-002 of zero or above. Each a_i is 1 for an adopted programme and 0 otherwise, and B_lambda adds up the incremental costs of the adopted programmes, the budget the threshold implies. Sacristán and colleagues state that the fixed ratio rule and the fixed budget rule coincide if this implied budget is the same as the budget constraint of the fixed budget rule. A B_lambda above the budget means the threshold adopts more than the budget pays for, and one below it leaves money that could buy more health." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Budget implied by a fixed cost-effectiveness ratio across four independent programmes" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Marseille E, Larson B, Kazi DS, Kahn JG, Rosen S. Thresholds for the cost-effectiveness of interventions: alternative approaches. Bulletin of the World Health Organization. 2015;93(2):118-124. Section League tables, which describes the league table as an approach that side-steps the threshold question and focuses instead on getting the largest health impact for the budget. https://doi.org/10.2471/BLT.14.138206",
        "Sacristán JA, Abellán-Perpiñán JM, Dilla T, Soto J, Oliva J. Some reflections on the use of inappropriate comparators in CEA. Cost Effectiveness and Resource Allocation. 2020;18:29. Introduction, which states that under the fixed ratio rule, or threshold approach, a new intervention is adopted if its ICER does not exceed a cost per QALY threshold, and that both decision rules coincide if the budget implicitly determined by the fixed ratio rule is the same as the budget constraint assumed in the fixed budget rule. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12962-020-00226-8" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Adopting the marginal programme in full at the critical ratio. Setting the threshold at the critical ratio of GBP 20,000 per QALY and adopting every programme at or below it funds all of D and spends GBP 1,150,000, GBP 250,000 more than the budget. Under a fixed budget the tie at the critical ratio is settled by the share of HE-FM-CELT-001, not by the threshold.",
        "Setting the threshold below the critical ratio under a fixed budget. A threshold of GBP 15,000 per QALY adopts A, B and C and leaves GBP 150,000 of the GBP 900,000 unspent, which at D's GBP 20,000 per QALY would have bought 7.5 QALYs (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "a_1 = (Delta_C_1 <= lambda * Delta_E_1); a_2 = (Delta_C_2 <= lambda * Delta_E_2); a_3 = (Delta_C_3 <= lambda * Delta_E_3); a_4 = (Delta_C_4 <= lambda * Delta_E_4); B_lambda = a_1 * Delta_C_1 + a_2 * Delta_C_2 + a_3 * Delta_C_3 + a_4 * Delta_C_4" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CELT-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "budget-implied-by-a-fixed-cost-effectiveness-ratio-across-four-independent-programmes" .

hemo:5000722 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Relative change in a QALY gain and in the ICER when an analysis is rescored with another value set"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CSVS-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000161 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004564 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003286,
        hemo:6003287,
        hemo:6003288,
        hemo:6003289 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003286,
        hemo:6003287 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003288,
        hemo:6003289 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004562,
        hemo:7004563 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004565,
        hemo:7004566,
        hemo:7004567,
        hemo:7004568,
        hemo:7004569 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compares the QALY gain of the same treatment scored with two value sets, each the HE-FM-EQ3L-003 gain or any other QALY gain, and expresses the second as a share of the first. r_Q is the share of the QALY gain that changes with the value set, and with the incremental cost unchanged the ICER of HE-FM-CUA-001 changes by r_I, the reciprocal ratio less 1 (derived). The two changes are not mirror images, since (1 + r_Q) times (1 + r_I) equals 1, so a fall of 13% in the QALY gain raises the ICER by more than 13%. The gap in incremental net health benefit (HE-FM-CET-002) between the two sets is dQ_A minus dQ_B, since the cost term is the same under both (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Relative change in a QALY gain and in the ICER when an analysis is rescored with another value set" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Gerlinger C, Bamber L, Leverkus F, Schwenke C, Haberland C, Schmidt G, Endrikat J. Comparing the EQ-5D-5L utility index based on value sets of different countries. Impact on the interpretation of clinical study results. BMC Research Notes. 2019;12:18. Main text, Results, which states that in many cases the direction of a change between two health states differed between countries as well as its size, with the change of 44444 into 55511 valued as an improvement of 0.679 units in the Netherlands and a worsening of 0.169 units in Uruguay, and that 44444 was valued at minus 0.289 in the Netherlands and plus 0.353 in Uruguay. Open access. https://doi.org/10.1186/s13104-019-4067-9",
        "Navega Biz A, Hernández Alava M, Wailoo A. Switching from EQ-5D-3L to EQ-5D-5L in England. The impact in NICE technology appraisals. Value in Health. 2026;29(5):794-801. Methods, which state that the mapped 5L values were applied in the cost-effectiveness models and all remaining model parameters remained unchanged, and Abstract and Results, which report 39 appraisal decisions with utility values mapped to the EQ-5D-5L using the eq5dmap model with a new UK value set, a mean increase of 12.5% in absolute incremental QALYs and a mean decrease of 11.1% in ICERs for 17 oncology decisions, and a decrease of 37.5% in incremental QALYs with an increase of 61.4% in ICERs for 11 non-oncology decisions without a survival gain, given as mean absolute changes over the decisions in each group. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2026.03.007" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Computing an ICER change after the value set reverses the gain. Gerlinger and colleagues report that a change of EQ-5D-5L state 44444 into 55511 is an improvement of 0.679 under the Dutch set and a worsening of 0.169 under the Uruguayan one. Held for one year these are QALY changes of 0.679 and minus 0.169, so r_Q is minus 1.2489 and r_I is minus 5.0178, a number with no meaning as a change in cost per QALY gained (computed here). An r_Q below minus 1 signals the reversal, and the result is then read by dominance or net health benefit.",
        "Reading a mean ICER change from a mean QALY change. The reciprocal identity holds for one decision, not for averages across decisions. Navega Biz and colleagues report a mean fall of 37.5% in incremental QALYs and a mean rise of 61.4% in ICERs for 11 non-oncology decisions without a survival gain, while the identity applied to a single 37.5% fall gives a rise of 60.0% (derived). Each decision's ICER change has to be computed from its own pair of gains before averaging.",
        "Taking the ICER change as minus the QALY change. A fall in the QALY gain raises the ICER by more than the fall. In the article's example the QALY gain falls 13.3% and the ICER rises 15.4%, not 13.3%, and for the UK and Zimbabwe comparison a fall of 46.3% raises the ICER by 86.3%, not 46.3% (computed here). The gap matters most near a threshold, where the article's two sets put the same treatment on opposite sides of GBP 25,000 per QALY." ;
    hemo:9000014 "r_Q = dQ_B / dQ_A - 1; r_I = dQ_A / dQ_B - 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CSVS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "relative-change-in-a-qaly-gain-and-in-the-icer-when-an-analysis-is-rescored-with-another-value-set" .

hemo:5000723 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Count-based D1 value set form with interaction terms in place of a constant"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CSVS-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000161 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004573 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003290,
        hemo:6003291,
        hemo:6003292,
        hemo:6003293,
        hemo:6003294,
        hemo:6003295,
        hemo:6003296,
        hemo:6003297,
        hemo:6003298,
        hemo:6003299,
        hemo:6003300,
        hemo:6003301,
        hemo:6003302,
        hemo:6003303,
        hemo:6003304 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003291,
        hemo:6003292,
        hemo:6003293,
        hemo:6003294,
        hemo:6003295,
        hemo:6003296,
        hemo:6003297,
        hemo:6003301,
        hemo:6003302,
        hemo:6003303,
        hemo:6003304 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003290 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004570,
        hemo:7004571,
        hemo:7004572 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004574,
        hemo:7004575,
        hemo:7004576,
        hemo:7004577,
        hemo:7004578,
        hemo:7004579 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Scores an EQ-5D-3L profile with the D1 form that Shaw, Johnson and Coons used for the US time trade-off set. There is no constant and no N3 term. The level decrements are subtracted as in HE-FM-EQ3L-001, and the interaction effects of problems on several dimensions enter through counts beyond the first, D1 for dimensions with any problem, I2 for dimensions at level 2 (squared) and I3 for dimensions at level 3 (linear and squared). HE-AS-EQ3L-003 records that this form cannot be scored with the constant and N3 formula, and Rand-Hendriksen and colleagues show that it equals a constant model with every level decrement shifted by the D1 coefficient. The coefficients in the examples are illustrative, not the published US values." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Count-based D1 value set form with interaction terms in place of a constant" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Janssen B, Szende A, Ramos-Goñi JM. Data and methods. In Szende A, Janssen B, Cabases J, editors. Self-reported population health. An international perspective based on EQ-5D. Dordrecht, Springer, 2014, chapter 2. Notes to Table 2.2, which define D1 as the additional number of dimensions at either level 2 or level 3, I2 as the number of dimensions at level 2 beyond the first and I3 as the number of dimensions at level 3 beyond the first, terms used by the United States set of Shaw 2005 in the table. Open access. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-007-7596-1_2",
        "Rand-Hendriksen K, Augestad LA, Dahl FA. A critical re-evaluation of the regression model specification in the US D1 EQ-5D value function. Population Health Metrics. 2012;10(1):2. Methods, subsection The D1 variable, model, and tariff values, which defines the ten level dummies, I2 and I3 as the numbers of dimensions at levels 2 and 3 beyond the first, their squares, and D1 as the number of dimensions beyond the first not at level 1, states that I2 was excluded from the final model and that the model was fitted on disutilities, with Table 1 listing the published US D1 tariff; Results and Table 2, which state that the constant of the equivalent model is the negative of the D1 coefficient, that using D1 adds the constant to each of the ten dummy coefficients, and that the two forms give identical values for all 242 predicted states. Open access. https://doi.org/10.1186/1478-7954-10-2",
        "Shaw JW, Johnson JA, Coons SJ. US valuation of the EQ-5D health states. Development and testing of the D1 valuation model. Medical Care. 2005;43(3):203-220. Abstract, which reports that respondents from a US probability sample valued 13 of 243 states by time trade-off, that methods were investigated to account for interaction effects caused by problems on several dimensions, and that the D1 model included ordinal terms for departures from perfect health as well as interaction effects, and that the D1 model, in a random effects specification, gave a good fit to the observed time trade-off values. Only the abstract was checked; the coefficient table was not seen. https://doi.org/10.1097/00005650-200503000-00003" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Counting every dimension with a problem in D1. D1 counts dimensions beyond the first. Counting all of them gives a single-problem state a D1 credit it should not have, so with the illustrative coefficients 21111 scores 0.97 in place of 0.85 and 22222 scores 0.69 in place of 0.57 (computed here).",
        "Scoring a D1 value set with the constant and N3 formula. Entering a D1 set's level decrements into HE-FM-EQ3L-001 with a zero constant and N3 drops all the count terms. With the illustrative coefficients 22222 then scores 0.25 in place of 0.57 and 33333 minus 1.50 in place of minus 0.30 (computed here), so a D1 set needs its own scoring rule, as HE-AS-EQ3L-003 states." ;
    hemo:9000014 "D_1 = max(n_2 + n_3 - 1, 0); I_2 = max(n_2 - 1, 0); I_3 = max(n_3 - 1, 0); U = 1 - (beta_MO + beta_SC + beta_UA + beta_PD + beta_AD) - a_D1 * D_1 - a_I22 * I_2^2 - a_I3 * I_3 - a_I32 * I_3^2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CSVS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "count-based-d1-value-set-form-with-interaction-terms-in-place-of-a-constant" .

hemo:5000724 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-option error probability as half the two-sided p-value of incremental net benefit"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-DCU-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000162 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004582 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003305,
        hemo:6003306 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003305 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003306 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004580,
        hemo:7004581 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004583,
        hemo:7004584,
        hemo:7004585,
        hemo:7004586 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "In a two-option comparison analysed by a test of incremental net benefit at a stated threshold, such as net benefit regression, the expected-value choice follows the sign of the estimate. Hoch and colleagues state that the probability that the new treatment is cost-effective is the one-sided p-value when the estimate is negative and one minus the one-sided p-value when it is positive, so in both cases the probability that the choice is wrong is the one-sided p-value, half the two-sided p-value that statistical software usually reports (derived from those statements). The result needs no normal distribution function. For a z test it equals the upper normal tail at the absolute statistic, which is the two-option normal error probability given by HE-FM-AN-004 read through HE-FM-CEAC-003 (derived). HE-ER-AN-007 covers the related slip of taking one minus the two-sided p-value as the probability that net benefit is positive." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-option error probability as half the two-sided p-value of incremental net benefit" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Hoch JS, Rockx MA, Krahn AD. Using the net benefit regression framework to construct cost-effectiveness acceptability curves, an example using data from a trial of external loop recorders versus Holter monitoring for ambulatory monitoring of community acquired syncope. BMC Health Services Research. 2006;6:68. Methods section, which states that regression programs report a two-sided p-value while a one-sided probability is needed, that it is converted by dividing by two, and that the probability that the new treatment is cost-effective is the one-sided p-value when the coefficient is negative and one minus it when the coefficient is positive. The same section states that the p-value of a parametric analysis is derived from a distributional assumption and that non-parametric methods like bootstrapping may offer better alternatives, and the Discussion states that a CEAC indicates a 50% chance of cost-effectiveness when the threshold equals the sample estimate of the ICER. That the error probability is the one-sided p-value in both cases, and 0.5 at the ICER, is derived from these statements. https://doi.org/10.1186/1472-6963-6-68",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 13 October 2026. Chapter 4 Economic evaluation, section 4.7.2 (the model should quantify the decision uncertainty, the probability that a different decision would be reached if the true cost effectiveness of each technology could be ascertained before making the decision) and section 4.7.15 (present the probability that the treatment is cost effective at maximum acceptable ICERs of GBP 25,000 to GBP 35,000 per QALY gained and the error probability, that the treatment is not cost effective, particularly if there are more than 2 alternatives). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2",
        "Steuten L, Thavorn K, Cipriano LE, Guertin JR, McQueen B, Schmidler G, Sullivan S, Wailoo A, Sculpher M. Methods to analyze and present uncertainty in economic evaluation to support HTA decisions. HEMA Report 2. Health Economics Methods Advisory, 2026. Table 5.2, footnote to the row on the probability of a wrong decision given current evidence (printed page 43), which defines it as the probability that the option with the highest expected net health benefit is in fact incorrect, notes that for a choice between two options it typically reaches around 50% where the ICER is close to the threshold, and that it can be higher or lower than 50% where the distribution of incremental net health benefit is markedly skewed or more than two options are compared. https://hemamethods.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/HEMA-Report-2_For-Publication_100726.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "One-sample p-value of probabilistic draws read as the decision uncertainty error probability. Halving the p-value of a one-sample test of the mean of 1,000 probabilistic draws, with an illustrative mean incremental net benefit of GBP 1,000 and standard deviation of GBP 2,000, uses the standard error of the mean, about GBP 63.25, so z is about 15.81 and the error probability comes out at about 0. The spread of the draws gives about 0.30854, the upper normal tail at 1,000 divided by 2,000 (HE-FM-AN-004 read through HE-FM-CEAC-003, computed here for illustration). The p-value measures the Monte Carlo precision of the mean and falls as more draws are run, while the decision uncertainty stays the same. The halved p-value applies to a test whose standard error describes the uncertainty about incremental net benefit itself, such as a trial-based net benefit regression.",
        "Sign of the estimate ignored when reading the p-value. Taking one minus the one-sided p-value as the error probability gives about 0.772 at GBP 30,000 per QALY, the probability that adopting is right. When the estimate is negative the new option is rejected and the error probability is still the one-sided p-value, the probability that the new option is cost-effective.",
        "Two-sided p-value reported as the error probability. Reporting the two-sided p-value of about 0.456 at GBP 30,000 per QALY as the error probability doubles it, since the choice is wrong only when net benefit lies on the other side of zero, a probability of about 0.228." ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_error_p = p_two / 2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-DCU-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-option-error-probability-as-half-the-two-sided-p-value-of-incremental-net-benefit" .

hemo:5000728 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Rise in the ICER when incremental patient time cost is added to health service costs"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-PTC-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000164 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004618 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003334,
        hemo:6003335,
        hemo:6003336,
        hemo:6003337,
        hemo:6003338,
        hemo:6003339,
        hemo:6003340 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003335,
        hemo:6003336,
        hemo:6003337,
        hemo:6003338 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003340 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004616,
        hemo:7004617 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004619,
        hemo:7004620,
        hemo:7004621,
        hemo:7004622,
        hemo:7004623 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the incremental cost-effectiveness ratio from the health service view, computed with HE-FM-ICER-001, and adds the incremental patient time cost divided by the same QALY gain, which gives the ratio from a limited societal perspective that counts patient time. Because the QALY gain is unchanged, the rise in the ICER is the incremental time cost over the QALY gain, and the rise as a share of the health service ICER equals the incremental time cost over the incremental health service cost (both derived here from HE-FM-ICER-001). The time cost of each option comes from HE-FM-PTC-001 or HE-CF-PTC-001. In net benefit terms the same step lowers the incremental net monetary benefit of HE-FM-NMB-002 by the incremental time cost, which also covers a QALY gain of zero or below." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Rise in the ICER when incremental patient time cost is added to health service costs" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Breslau RM, Cohen JT, Diaz J, Malcolm B, Neumann PJ. A review of HTA guidelines on societal and novel value elements. International Journal of Technology Assessment in Health Care. 2023;39(1):e31. Methods, subsection on identification of societal and novel value elements, which states that the Second Panel recommended an impact inventory that enumerates societal elements in the informal healthcare sector, namely patient-time costs, unpaid caregiver-time costs and transportation costs. https://doi.org/10.1017/S026646232300017X",
        "Jonas DE, Russell LB, Sandler RS, Chou J, Pignone M. Value of patient time invested in the colonoscopy screening process: time requirements for colonoscopy study. Medical Decision Making. 2008;28(1):56-65. Abstract, which states that including patient time costs would raise the cost per life-year saved with screening colonoscopy by 68%, to USD 22,000 against USD 13,100, and that the rise could lie anywhere in a range of 17% to 224% depending on the time interval valued; the 68% is the same proportional rise as g_ICER here, on cost per life-year saved rather than per QALY (22,000 over 13,100, less 1, is 0.679, derived). https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X07309643",
        "Kim DD, Silver MC, Kunst N, Cohen JT, Ollendorf DA, Neumann PJ. Perspective and costing in cost-effectiveness analysis, 1974-2018. PharmacoEconomics. 2020;38(10):1135-1145. Methods, Working Definitions subsection, which defines the limited societal perspective as accounting for cost components beyond those of the healthcare sector perspective, including patient time, patient transportation, unpaid caregiver time and productivity loss; the Discussion names the open question whether lost productivity from morbidity is already captured in measuring QALYs. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40273-020-00942-2",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 13 October 2026. Sections 4.2.7 and 4.2.9, which state that the perspective adopted on costs should be that of the NHS and PSS and that productivity costs should not be included, and section 4.4.23, which states that costs paid by patients may be included when the NHS or PSS reimburses them and that costs paid by patients and not reimbursed may be presented separately. The chapter does not mention patients' own time. https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2",
        "Sanders GD, Neumann PJ, Basu A, Brock DW, Feeny D, Krahn M, et al. Recommendations for conduct, methodological practices, and reporting of cost-effectiveness analyses: Second Panel on Cost-Effectiveness in Health and Medicine. JAMA. 2016;316(10):1093-1103. Abstract, which recommends that all cost-effectiveness analyses report two reference case analyses, one from a health care sector perspective and one from a societal perspective, and an impact inventory, a structured table of consequences inside and outside the formal health care sector. https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2016.12195" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Patient time ICER reported as the NICE reference-case result. Reporting ICER_S, GBP 28,950 per QALY in the article's example, as the NICE reference-case result mixes perspectives, since PMG36 section 4.2.9 sets the reference-case perspective on costs as the NHS and PSS, which gives GBP 15,000. PMG36 section 4.4.23 allows costs paid by patients and not reimbursed by the NHS and PSS to be presented separately; showing patient time separately in the same way is an inference, since the manual's chapter on economic evaluation does not mention patients' time.",
        "Total patient time cost of the new option added to the incremental cost. Adding the new option's whole time cost rather than the difference from the comparator counts the comparator's patient time as if it were extra. In the article's example the ICER becomes GBP 32,550 per QALY in place of GBP 28,950, GBP 3,600 or 12% too high (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "dC_T = C_T_new - C_T_comp; ICER_S = ICER_H + dC_T / dE; g_ICER = (ICER_S - ICER_H) / ICER_H" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-PTC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "rise-in-the-icer-when-incremental-patient-time-cost-is-added-to-health-service-costs" .

hemo:5000729 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Patient time cost with time valued by employment status"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-PTC-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000164 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004626 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003341,
        hemo:6003342,
        hemo:6003343,
        hemo:6003344,
        hemo:6003345,
        hemo:6003346 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003342,
        hemo:6003344,
        hemo:6003345,
        hemo:6003346 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003341 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004624,
        hemo:7004625 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004627,
        hemo:7004628,
        hemo:7004629,
        hemo:7004630,
        hemo:7004631 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Values a person's hours in care at the person's own hourly wage when employed for wages and at an hourly value of home production otherwise, the approach Yabroff and colleagues used in a sensitivity analysis, and multiplies by the person's hours from HE-FM-PTC-001 or HE-CF-PTC-001. The base case of the same study used one median wage for everyone, the w of HE-FM-PTC-001, so that each person's time counted equally. In that study the choice cut annual time costs for cancer survivors aged 65 and over to USD 359, against USD 913 in the base case. The value differs from the human capital value of HE-FM-ABSN-002, which values absence from paid work as lost output, usually at gross earnings including employer-paid costs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Patient time cost with time valued by employment status" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Russell LB. Completing costs: patients' time. Medical Care. 2009;47(7 Suppl 1):S89-S93. Abstract, which states that the concept of opportunity cost, often approximated by a market price, is the basis for valuing all resources, that the wage is a reasonable proxy for the value patients place on their own time, and that wage data are well measured and readily available. https://doi.org/10.1097/MLR.0b013e31819bc077",
        "Tranmer JE, Guerriere DN, Ungar WJ, Coyte PC. Valuing patient and caregiver time: a review of the literature. PharmacoEconomics. 2005;23(5):449-459. Abstract, which presents and critiques the human capital method, the friction cost method and the Washington Panel approach, finds the methods of valuation inconsistent and frequently controversial, and notes a lack of research on time lost from unpaid labour and leisure. https://doi.org/10.2165/00019053-200523050-00005",
        "Yabroff KR, Guy GP Jr, Ekwueme DU, McNeel T, Rozjabek HM, Dowling E, Li C, Virgo KS. Annual patient time costs associated with medical care among cancer survivors in the United States. Medical Care. 2014;52(7):594-601. Methods, Measures subsection, which values patient time at the median U.S. wage of USD 16.57 per hour in 2011 in the base case and, in sensitivity analyses, at actual hourly wages for participants employed for wages and at USD 5.32 per hour, the value of home production adjusted to 2011 dollars, for those not employed; Table 4, which reports annual time costs of USD 912.96 and USD 358.80 for survivors aged 65 and over under the two approaches; and the Discussion, which explains the choice of a single median wage valuing each person's time equally and warns of inequities for low-income and other vulnerable populations. https://doi.org/10.1097/MLR.0000000000000151" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Group means of hours and value of time multiplied in place of person-level costs. Multiplying mean hours by a mean value of time ignores that hours and employment go together. In an illustrative group where 30% are employed, with 20 hours a year at USD 15, and 70% are not, with 50 hours at USD 5.32, the person-level mean is USD 276.20, while 41 mean hours times a mean value of USD 8.224 gives USD 337.18, 22% too high (computed here for illustration).",
        "Valuation by employment status treated as neutral between groups. The same 55.1 hours are worth USD 293.13 at the home production value and USD 913.01 at the median wage, 68% less for a person not employed (computed here for illustration). Yabroff and colleagues warn that methods which value time differently for different populations may lead to inequities, particularly for low-income and other vulnerable groups, and kept a single median wage for their base case." ;
    hemo:9000014 "v_i = E_i * w_i + (1 - E_i) * w_hp; C_T_i = h_i * v_i" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-PTC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "patient-time-cost-with-time-valued-by-employment-status" .

hemo:5000733 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Share of the life years gained over the model horizon observed within trial follow-up"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-LYG-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000166 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004666 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003365,
        hemo:6003366,
        hemo:6003367,
        hemo:6003368,
        hemo:6003369,
        hemo:6003370,
        hemo:6003371,
        hemo:6003372 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003365,
        hemo:6003366,
        hemo:6003367,
        hemo:6003368 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003371,
        hemo:6003372 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004663,
        hemo:7004664,
        hemo:7004665 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004667,
        hemo:7004668,
        hemo:7004669,
        hemo:7004670,
        hemo:7004671 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the restricted mean survival times of the two arms at a follow-up time tau and their mean survival over the model horizon, and returns the within-trial gain, the gain over the horizon, the share of that gain lying within follow-up and the gain lying after it. The restricted means come from HE-FM-ADMC-003 for a Kaplan-Meier curve or from the modelled curves, as in the article's step 6, and the horizon means from an extrapolation such as HE-FM-BTH-002 or HE-FN-CRM-001. The difference of two restricted means is the measure of Royston and Parmar and is not new here; the share phi and the gain after follow-up are derived here. HE-FM-ADMC-004 gives the share of one arm's mean survival that comes from its tail, whereas 1 minus phi is the difference between the arms in the area after tau divided by the gain over the horizon, which depends on both arms' means as well as their tail shares (derived here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Share of the life years gained over the model horizon observed within trial follow-up" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Latimer N. NICE DSU Technical Support Document 14: survival analysis for economic evaluations alongside clinical trials, extrapolation with patient-level data. Sheffield, Decision Support Unit, ScHARR, University of Sheffield; June 2011, last updated March 2013. Executive summary (page 4), which states that without extrapolation estimates of the survival benefit are restricted to that observed in the trial and are likely to underestimate the true survival gain, and section 2 (page 12), which states that the base case should use extrapolation of the fitted probability distribution, with results based only on the observed data giving information on the importance of the extrapolated period in the mean. https://sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD14-Survival-analysis.updated-March-2013.v2.pdf",
        "Latimer NR, Adler AI. Extrapolation beyond the end of trials to estimate long term survival and cost effectiveness. BMJ Medicine. 2022;1(1):e000094. Key messages, which state that extrapolation beyond the trial is usually needed to estimate long term effects and that survival models can give widely varying results, and the section on the rationale for survival modelling with figure 1, which describes the area between the curves within the trial as a restricted mean benefit and the area between the extrapolated curves as the mean lifetime survival benefit relevant for economic evaluation. https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjmed-2021-000094",
        "Royston P, Parmar MK. Restricted mean survival time: an alternative to the hazard ratio for the design and analysis of randomized trials with a time-to-event outcome. BMC Medical Research Methodology. 2013;13:152. Background, which recalls the authors' earlier proposal to express the treatment effect as the difference in restricted mean survival time between the randomised arms at a suitable follow-up time t*, and Methods, section Definition of RMST, which reads the restricted mean of equation 1 as the t*-year life expectancy and gives the difference between arms as the area between the survival curves, in the unnumbered expression that follows equation 1. https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2288-13-152" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Restricted means of the two arms taken at different follow-up times. Setting the new treatment's restricted mean at two years, 1.55, against the comparator's at three years, 1.45, gives a within-trial gain of 0.10 and a share of 15.4% instead of 0.30 and 46.2% for the article's curves (computed here). Both restricted means are taken at the same tau.",
        "Within-trial life years gained used as the effect in a cost per life year. Holding the article's GBP 12,000 fixed for illustration, the within-trial gain of 0.30 gives GBP 40,000 per life year against GBP 18,462 on the undiscounted gain of 0.65 over the horizon (computed here), more than double. TSD 14 warns that a survival benefit restricted to what the trial observed is likely to underestimate the true survival gain.",
        "Within-trial share of life years gained read from one arm's tail share. Taking the new treatment's restricted mean as a share of its own mean, 1.55 over 2.15, suggests that 72.1% of the benefit is observed in the trial, while the share of the gain is 46.2% for the article's figures (computed here). The tail share of HE-FM-ADMC-004 describes one arm's survival; the share of a gain needs both arms." ;
    hemo:9000014 "dRMST = RMST_1 - RMST_0; dLY_life = LY_1 - LY_0; phi = dRMST / dLY_life; dLY_beyond = dLY_life - dRMST" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-LYG-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "share-of-the-life-years-gained-over-the-model-horizon-observed-within-trial-follow-up" .

hemo:5000734 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Cobb-Douglas gross investment in health from medical care, own time and schooling"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-HPF-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000167 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004675 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003373,
        hemo:6003374,
        hemo:6003375,
        hemo:6003376,
        hemo:6003377,
        hemo:6003378 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003374,
        hemo:6003375,
        hemo:6003376,
        hemo:6003377,
        hemo:6003378 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003373 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004672,
        hemo:7004673,
        hemo:7004674 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004676,
        hemo:7004677,
        hemo:7004678,
        hemo:7004679,
        hemo:7004680 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gross investment is the efficiency factor exp(rho_H E) times medical care raised to the power 1-K times own time raised to the power K, the exponential form of Grossman's estimating equation ln I = rho_H E + (1-K) ln M + K ln TH. The exponents sum to one, so doubling both inputs doubles I, and 1-K is the elasticity of gross investment with respect to medical care. Schooling enters as a factor-neutral efficiency shift, so one more year multiplies I by exp(rho_H) for the same inputs, a rise of about 3.05% when rho_H is 0.03. The result I is the gross investment of HE-FM-DFH-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cobb-Douglas gross investment in health from medical care, own time and schooling" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Grossman M. The Demand for Health: A Theoretical and Empirical Investigation. National Bureau of Economic Research, New York; 1972. Chapter 1, A Stock Approach to the Demand for Health, pages 1 to 10. Page 2, equation (1-3), the household production function of gross investment in medical care, own time and the stock of human capital, the statement that a shift in human capital changes the efficiency of production in the nonmarket sector as technology does in the market sector, and the assumption that all production functions are homogeneous of degree one in the goods and time inputs. https://www.nber.org/system/files/chapters/c3485/c3485.pdf",
        "Grossman M. The human capital model of the demand for health. NBER Working Paper 7078. National Bureau of Economic Research, Cambridge, MA; 1999. Published as The human capital model, in Culyer AJ, Newhouse JP, editors. Handbook of Health Economics. Vol 1A. Elsevier, Amsterdam; 2000. p. 347-408. Section 2.1, equation (2-3), the gross investment function It = It(Mt, THt; E) with E exogenous or predetermined, the efficiency effect of knowledge capital following Michael and Becker, linear homogeneity in the market goods and own time inputs and the ruling out of joint production; section 3.2, the factor-neutrality assumption that a one unit increase in E raises the marginal products of M and TH by the same percentage rho_H, the percentage increase in gross investment per unit of E; section 5.1, equation (5-3), the Cobb-Douglas form rho_H E + (1-K) ln Mt + K ln THt with intercepts suppressed, an elasticity of substitution of one, 1-K as the share of medical care in total cost and the elasticity of gross investment with respect to medical care, and the downward bias of ordinary least squares estimates of the production function with two-stage least squares as the consistent alternative. https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w7078/w7078.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Cross-sectional regression of health on medical care read as the production function. Fitting the production function by ordinary least squares across people gives a coefficient on medical care that Grossman shows is biased downward, because people with high unobserved depreciation demand more care and hold less health, and unmeasured differences in efficiency bias it the same way. Grossman notes that two-stage least squares with wealth, wage rates and the price of care as instruments can give consistent estimates, provided the instruments are valid and explain enough of the variation in care. A negative association between care and health across people does not by itself show that the elasticity 1-K is zero or negative.",
        "Gross investment in health read as the health stock. I is the flow added to the stock in the period, not the stock. In the article's example 20 units of gross investment take a stock of 100 with depreciation of 0.15 to 105 through HE-FM-DFH-001, so the 3.05% rise in I from one more year of schooling raises next period's stock by 0.61 units, about 0.6% (computed here for illustration).",
        "Total years of schooling used as E with a suppressed intercept. Because the intercept that Grossman's estimating form leaves unstated is absorbed into the reference level, rho_H E is a shift from that level. Entering 12 years of schooling in place of 0 for the article's reference person raises I to 28.67 against 20, 43% too high (computed here for illustration), while the comparison the article draws, one more year against the reference, gives 20.61." ;
    hemo:9000014 "I = exp(rho_H * E) * M^(1 - K) * TH^K" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-HPF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cobb-douglas-gross-investment-in-health-from-medical-care-own-time-and-schooling" .

hemo:5000738 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Proportional rise in expected reimbursement of an uncapped specific excess layer under uniform claims inflation"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ATTP-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000168 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004715 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003412,
        hemo:6003413,
        hemo:6003414,
        hemo:6003415,
        hemo:6003416,
        hemo:6003417,
        hemo:6003418,
        hemo:6003419,
        hemo:6003420,
        hemo:6003421,
        hemo:6003422,
        hemo:6003423 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003413,
        hemo:6003414,
        hemo:6003415,
        hemo:6003416,
        hemo:6003417,
        hemo:6003418,
        hemo:6003419,
        hemo:6003421 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003423 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004712,
        hemo:7004713,
        hemo:7004714 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004716,
        hemo:7004717,
        hemo:7004718,
        hemo:7004719,
        hemo:7004720 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives the expected reimbursement per member of an uncapped excess layer with full reimbursement before and after every claim level rises by the same inflation rate g while the attachment point stays fixed in money, the proportional rise rise_E in that expected reimbursement, and its ratio mult_E to g. Lee 1988 states that with the retention fixed the expected excess loss payment rises proportionally much more than the general rate of inflation. Miccolis 1977 shows that under a uniform inflation factor the expected loss limited at k equals the factor times the earlier expected loss limited at k divided by the factor (equation 17), from which E_g equals (1 + g) times the expected excess of current claims over A / (1 + g) (derived), so inflating claims has the same effect as lowering the attachment point in real terms, and without a cap mult_E is at least 1 (derived, checked over a grid of inputs). A coinsurance rate multiplies E_0 and E_g alike and cancels from rise_E; with a cap the top of the layer can already be used up and the rise can fall below g. The general price-year adjustment of a single cost is HE-FM-COST-002." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Proportional rise in expected reimbursement of an uncapped specific excess layer under uniform claims inflation" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Lee YS. The mathematics of excess of loss coverages and retrospective rating, a graphical approach. Proceedings of the Casualty Actuarial Society. 1988;75:49-77. Section 3, Trend (pages 61 to 63), which treats inflation as a uniform multiplicative factor on every loss, states that an excess of loss coverage is more seriously affected by inflation and that if the retention remains fixed the expected excess loss payment is increased proportionally much more than indicated by the general rate of inflation (Figure 11), and gives in equation 3.5 the inflated expected basic limits loss as the factor times the earlier expected loss limited at the limit divided by the factor. https://www.casact.org/sites/default/files/database/proceed_proceed88_88049.pdf",
        "Miccolis RS. On the theory of increased limits and excess of loss pricing. Proceedings of the Casualty Actuarial Society. 1977;64:27-59. Section Trend (pages 35 to 37), which notes that limits and retentions remain fixed while loss severity is shifting, models trend as a transformation of loss size with each loss increased by the same multiplicative factor above one, and derives in equation 17 that the expected loss limited at k after trend equals the factor times the earlier expected loss limited at k divided by the factor, with the excess of loss form in equation 19. The uncapped excess form used here follows from equation 17 and the scaling of the mean (derived). https://www.casact.org/sites/default/files/database/proceed_proceed77_77027.pdf",
        "National Association of Insurance Commissioners. Stop Loss Insurance Model Act (Model 92). Adopted 1995, amended 1999. Section 3A(1)(a), which bars an insurer from issuing a stop-loss policy with an annual attachment point for claims incurred per individual lower than USD 20,000, section 3A(3), which lets the commissioner amend the dollar amounts upon consideration of the medical components of the Consumer Price Index and publish any change at least six months before it takes effect, and the drafting note that a state may wish to adjust the dollar amounts to reflect medical costs in the particular state. https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/model-law-092.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Holding a money attachment point or regulatory floor constant across years of claims inflation. A multi-year model in constant prices that keeps a money threshold, such as the USD 20,000 minimum annual attachment point per individual of the NAIC Stop Loss Insurance Model Act, at its face value treats it as if it rose in step with claims. With illustrative claims inflation of 5% a year and the threshold unchanged in money, after five years USD 20,000 corresponds to about USD 15,671 of current claims (derived), and the uncapped layer above it grows faster than claims each year (derived). The model act lets the commissioner amend its dollar amounts upon consideration of the medical components of the Consumer Price Index, and its drafting note says a state may wish to adjust them to reflect medical costs in that state.",
        "Projecting a fixed specific attachment point layer at the general claims inflation rate. Projecting the USD 550 expected reimbursement at a USD 100,000 attachment point forward at the 10% claims inflation rate gives USD 605 per member, USD 40 below the USD 645 that the inflated distribution gives, about USD 40,000 too little for 1,000 members in one year. The gap widens with each further year at a fixed attachment point, to about USD 84 per member after two years of 10% inflation (derived, USD 749.50 against USD 665.50)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "E_0 = p_1 * max(C_1 - A, 0) + p_2 * max(C_2 - A, 0) + p_3 * max(C_3 - A, 0); E_g = p_1 * max((1 + g) * C_1 - A, 0) + p_2 * max((1 + g) * C_2 - A, 0) + p_3 * max((1 + g) * C_3 - A, 0); rise_E = E_g / E_0 - 1; mult_E = rise_E / g" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ATTP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "proportional-rise-in-expected-reimbursement-of-an-uncapped-specific-excess-layer-under-uniform-claims-inflation" .

hemo:5000739 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Immortal-time bias factor in a responder rate ratio from pre-response person-time counted as responder time"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-LMA-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000169 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004724 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003424,
        hemo:6003425,
        hemo:6003426,
        hemo:6003427,
        hemo:6003428,
        hemo:6003429,
        hemo:6003430,
        hemo:6003431 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003424,
        hemo:6003425,
        hemo:6003426,
        hemo:6003427,
        hemo:6003428 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003431 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004721,
        hemo:7004722,
        hemo:7004723 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004725,
        hemo:7004726,
        hemo:7004727,
        hemo:7004728,
        hemo:7004729,
        hemo:7004730 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compares two constant-hazard rate ratios of responders against patients without a response, each of the form of HE-FM-HR-005. The naive ratio RR_naive adds the immortal time I, the person-time responders spent before responding, to the responders' person-time, while RR_split moves it to the side without a response up to the time status changes, as the extended Cox model with a time-varying covariate does in Giobbie-Hurder, Gelber and Regan. No death can fall in I, so the event counts are the same in both ratios and F, their ratio, equals PT_R PT_N divided by (PT_R + I)(PT_N + I) (derived here), which depends on person-time alone. F is 1 when I is zero and falls further below 1 as I grows (checked over a grid in Python), so the naive ratio always flatters responders, and dividing a naive rate ratio by F recovers the time-split ratio." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Immortal-time bias factor in a responder rate ratio from pre-response person-time counted as responder time" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Anderson JR, Cain KC, Gelber RD. Analysis of survival by tumor response. Journal of Clinical Oncology. 1983;1(11):710-719. doi:10.1200/JCO.1983.1.11.710. Abstract, which states that the usual method of comparing responders and nonresponders is biased in favour of responders, that two valid methods are discussed, and that a comparison by response category should not be used for inference about treatment effectiveness. The person-time form of the bias in HE-FM-LMA-002 is derived here and is not taken from the paper. https://doi.org/10.1200/JCO.1983.1.11.710",
        "Giobbie-Hurder A, Gelber RD, Regan MM. Challenges of guarantee-time bias. Journal of Clinical Oncology. 2013;31(23):2963-2969. doi:10.1200/JCO.2013.49.5283 (full text read in PubMed Central, PMC3732313). Analytic Methods, Extended Cox Model With Time-Varying Covariates, which states that all patient data are used, that patients who develop the classifying event are switched into its group at the time it occurs and remain there, and that the resulting hazard ratio compares patients after the event with those who had not yet or never had it. The person-time split and the closed form of F are derived here for constant hazards. https://doi.org/10.1200/JCO.2013.49.5283",
        "Giobbie-Hurder A, Gelber RD, Regan MM. Challenges of guarantee-time bias. Journal of Clinical Oncology. 2013;31(23):2963-2969. doi:10.1200/JCO.2013.49.5283 (full text read in PubMed Central, PMC3732313). Background and Figure 1, which state that patients in the transplantation group were guaranteed to survive at least until a donor was available, that their survival reflects the total time before and after transplantation, and that the extra waiting time is guarantee time. https://doi.org/10.1200/JCO.2013.49.5283",
        "Suissa S. Immortal time bias in pharmaco-epidemiology. American Journal of Epidemiology. 2008;167(4):492-499. doi:10.1093/aje/kwm324. Abstract, which defines immortal time as a span of cohort follow-up during which, because of exposure definition, the outcome could not occur, and states that the bias in the rate ratio from misclassified or excluded immortal time increases with the duration of immortal time. The closed form of F is derived here and is not taken from the paper. https://doi.org/10.1093/aje/kwm324" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Immortal time dropped instead of moved to the side without a response. Removing I from the responders without adding it to the person-time without a response gives a rate ratio of 0.458 in the illustration, still below the time-split 0.535 (computed here). Suissa names excluded as well as misclassified immortal time as a source of bias in the rate ratio.",
        "Pre-response time counted as responder time in a rate ratio. In the article-based illustration the naive rate ratio of 0.354 suggests a death rate 65% lower for responders, against 47% lower with person-time split at the response (computed here for illustration). Suissa reports that the bias in the rate ratio from misclassified immortal time increases with the duration of immortal time." ;
    hemo:9000014 "RR_naive = (d_R / (PT_R + I)) / (d_N / PT_N); RR_split = (d_R / PT_R) / (d_N / (PT_N + I)); F = RR_naive / RR_split" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-LMA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "immortal-time-bias-factor-in-a-responder-rate-ratio-from-pre-response-person-time-counted-as-responder-time" .

hemo:5000742 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Review Manager standard error of Hedges' adjusted g"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-HEDG-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000170 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004751 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003445,
        hemo:6003446,
        hemo:6003447,
        hemo:6003448,
        hemo:6003449 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003446,
        hemo:6003447,
        hemo:6003449 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003448 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004749,
        hemo:7004750 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004752,
        hemo:7004753,
        hemo:7004754,
        hemo:7004755,
        hemo:7004756 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Review Manager, the software Cochrane reviews use, gives the standard error of Hedges' adjusted g from the total sample size N, the two group sizes and the corrected estimate g, with N less 3.94 in the denominator of the effect term. It differs from SE_g of HE-FM-HEDG-002 because J is not applied as a multiplier and the effect term is g squared over twice (N less 3.94) in place of d squared over twice N, and it is slightly larger in small trials. Reproducing the weights of a Cochrane forest plot needs this form, while the article's interval uses HE-FM-HEDG-002. g comes from HE-FM-HEDG-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Review Manager standard error of Hedges' adjusted g" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Deeks JJ, Higgins JPT, on behalf of the Cochrane Statistical Methods Group. Statistical algorithms in Review Manager. Cochrane; May 2022. Section Standardized difference in means (standardized mean difference) (page 3), which gives the standard error of the SMD as the square root of N over n_1 n_2 plus SMD squared over 2(N minus 3.94), and the section on inverse-variance methods, which weights each study by the reciprocal of the square of the standard error from the individual study section. https://documentation.cochrane.org/revman-kb/files/210600101/210600103/1/1654774257333/Statistical_methods_in_revman.pdf",
        "Higgins JPT, Li T, Deeks JJ (editors). Chapter 6: Choosing effect measures and computing estimates of effect. In: Higgins JPT, Thomas J, Chandler J, Cumpston M, Li T, Page MJ, Welch VA (editors). Cochrane Handbook for Systematic Reviews of Interventions version 6.5. Cochrane; 2024. Section 6.5.1.2, The standardized mean difference, which states that the SMD used in Cochrane reviews is the effect size known in social science as Hedges' (adjusted) g, with a pooled standard deviation in the denominator that assumes the standard deviations in the two groups are similar. https://www.cochrane.org/authors/handbooks-and-manuals/handbook/current/chapter-06" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Correction factor applied again to the Review Manager standard error. Multiplying SE_RM by J, as the What Works Clearinghouse form does for its own expression, mixes two conventions and shrinks the Review Manager value. For the article's trial it gives about 0.4316 instead of 0.4486, computed here for illustration.",
        "Two standard error formulas for Hedges' g mixed in one meta-analysis. Taking SE_RM for some studies and SE_g of HE-FM-HEDG-002 for others weights like studies unequally. For a trial the size of the article's, the Review Manager form gives a weight about 8% lower (0.4294 squared over 0.4486 squared, about 0.916), computed here for illustration." ;
    hemo:9000014 "N = n_1 + n_2; SE_RM = sqrt(N / (n_1 * n_2) + g^2 / (2 * (N - 3.94)))" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-HEDG-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "review-manager-standard-error-of-hedges-adjusted-g" .

hemo:5000743 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected overstatement of a population standardised effect by Cohen's d in small samples"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-HEDG-004" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000170 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004759 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003450,
        hemo:6003451,
        hemo:6003452,
        hemo:6003453,
        hemo:6003454 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003451,
        hemo:6003453 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003454 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004757,
        hemo:7004758 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004760,
        hemo:7004761,
        hemo:7004762,
        hemo:7004763,
        hemo:7004764 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives the mean of the uncorrected estimate d over repeated samples when the population standardised difference is delta, using Hedges' result that this mean is delta divided by the exact factor, here replaced by the approximate J of HE-FM-HEDG-001. Bias_d is the amount by which d overstates delta on average. The relative overstatement Bias_d over delta depends only on m, and with the approximate J it is about 25% at 4 degrees of freedom, about 4% at 20 and under 0.4% at 198 (computed here). It describes the estimator before any correction and does not shrink when more small studies are averaged." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected overstatement of a population standardised effect by Cohen's d in small samples" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Hedges LV. Distribution theory for Glass's estimator of effect size and related estimators. Journal of Educational Statistics. 1981;6(2):107-128. Theorem 1 (page 111), equation 6a, which gives the expectation of the uncorrected estimate as delta over c(m); the Remark on the consistency of G (page 112), which states that with five subjects per group and 4 degrees of freedom the estimator overestimates delta by approximately 25 percent, that as the number of experiments increases the bias of the averaged estimator is unchanged while its variance tends to zero, and section 4 with Figure 1 (pages 112 and 113), which plots the ratio of the expectation of the uncorrected estimate to delta against m. https://doi.org/10.3102/10769986006002107" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Bias of Cohen's d taken as delta times one minus J. The reduction from d to g is d times one minus J, but the expected bias of d is delta times (1/J minus 1), which is larger. At 4 degrees of freedom the first reading gives 20% instead of 25%, and at 20 degrees of freedom about 3.8% instead of about 3.9%, computed here for illustration.",
        "Expecting pooling of many small studies to remove the bias of Cohen's d. Hedges shows that the average of uncorrected estimates from many small studies is not a consistent estimator of delta, because each estimate carries the same bias while the variance of the average falls, so the average estimates the wrong quantity more precisely. Ten studies with 4 degrees of freedom each and a delta of 0.5 have an expected average d of about 0.627 whatever their number, computed here for illustration with the exact factor." ;
    hemo:9000014 "J = 1 - 3 / (4 * m - 1); E_d = delta / J; Bias_d = E_d - delta" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-HEDG-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-overstatement-of-a-population-standardised-effect-by-cohen-s-d-in-small-samples" .

hemo:5000744 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Pigouvian subsidy bill, transfer to inframarginal users and bill per induced unit"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-PIGS-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000171 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004767 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003455,
        hemo:6003456,
        hemo:6003457,
        hemo:6003458,
        hemo:6003459,
        hemo:6003460,
        hemo:6003461 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003459,
        hemo:6003460,
        hemo:6003461 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003456,
        hemo:6003457,
        hemo:6003458 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004765,
        hemo:7004766 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004768,
        hemo:7004769,
        hemo:7004770,
        hemo:7004771,
        hemo:7004772 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Multiplies the per-unit subsidy by the uptake it produces to give the bill to the public budget, and by the uptake there would have been without it to give the transfer to inframarginal users, people paid for doing what they would have done anyway. The rest of the bill is paid on the induced units, and dividing the whole bill by the induced units gives the public money paid out for each extra unit the subsidy brings about. The bill is a transfer and not a cost to society in itself; the social cost of raising it is the excess burden of HE-FM-DWL-004 with the bill as G, which HE-FM-PIGS-002 carries into the net welfare change. With linear demand the uptake under the subsidy is the private quantity of HE-FM-EXT-002 plus s / b." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pigouvian subsidy bill, transfer to inframarginal users and bill per induced unit" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Goodkin-Gold M, Kremer M, Snyder CM, Williams HL. Optimal vaccine subsidies for endemic and epidemic diseases. NBER Working Paper 28085. Cambridge, MA: National Bureau of Economic Research; 2020. Section 4.2, Targeting Subsidies, which states that a uniform subsidy to induce more high-cost consumers to vaccinate could require paying a high subsidy to inframarginal consumers, amplifying programme expense, and supposes USD 10 worth of lentils per child immunised with nine inframarginal children receiving the subsidy for every marginal child, so that USD 100 of subsidy must be paid out for each new immunisation induced. https://www.nber.org/papers/w28085",
        "Office of Management and Budget. Circular No. A-94 (Transmittal Memo No. 64). Guidelines and discount rates for benefit-cost analysis of federal programs. Washington, DC: OMB; 1992. Section 6.a(1), Incremental Benefits and Costs, which asks analyses to identify the extent to which a policy such as a subsidy programme promotes substitutes for activities of a similar nature that would occur without it, and to record either the displaced activities as costs or only incremental gains as benefits, and section 6.a(4), Transfers, which states that there are no economic gains from a pure transfer payment because the benefits to those who receive it are matched by the costs borne by those who pay for it, so that transfers are excluded from net present value and their distributional effects discussed. https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/a094.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Pigouvian subsidy bill counted as a cost to society. Subtracting the whole bill from the welfare gain treats a transfer as a resource cost. In the article's example the GBP 864,000 bill against the GBP 90,000 gain gives a net loss of GBP 774,000, when the social cost of raising the bill at the illustrative 20p excess burden is GBP 172,800 and the net loss GBP 82,800 (computed here for illustration). OMB Circular A-94 excludes pure transfers from net present value and asks for their distributional effects to be discussed.",
        "Subsidy rate read as the budget cost of each extra unit. The rate understates what the budget pays for each unit the subsidy brings about whenever most users are inframarginal. In the article's example GBP 12 a course buys 12 thousand extra courses for GBP 864,000, which is GBP 72 per extra course, six times the rate, because 60 of every 72 subsidised courses would have been taken up anyway (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "B = s * Q_u; T = s * Q_p; B_n = s * (Q_u - Q_p); c_n = B / (Q_u - Q_p)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-PIGS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pigouvian-subsidy-bill-transfer-to-inframarginal-users-and-bill-per-induced-unit" .

hemo:5000745 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Net welfare change of a uniform or targeted Pigouvian subsidy with costly public funds"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-PIGS-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000171 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004776 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003462,
        hemo:6003463,
        hemo:6003464,
        hemo:6003465,
        hemo:6003466,
        hemo:6003467,
        hemo:6003468,
        hemo:6003469,
        hemo:6003470,
        hemo:6003471,
        hemo:6003472,
        hemo:6003473,
        hemo:6003474 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003468,
        hemo:6003469,
        hemo:6003470,
        hemo:6003471,
        hemo:6003472,
        hemo:6003473,
        hemo:6003474 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003462,
        hemo:6003463 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004773,
        hemo:7004774,
        hemo:7004775 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004777,
        hemo:7004778,
        hemo:7004779,
        hemo:7004780,
        hemo:7004781,
        hemo:7004782 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Measures the welfare gained as a per-unit subsidy s raises uptake from the private quantity Q_p of HE-FM-EXT-002 by s / b, the area between marginal social benefit and marginal cost over the induced units, and subtracts the excess burden of raising the bill, lambda times the bill of HE-FM-PIGS-001. W charges the excess burden on the whole bill of a uniform subsidy and W_T on a subsidy confined to the induced units. With the article's curves W reduces to the article's 15s minus 0.625s squared minus 0.2s(60 + s). At any rate S_g equals the deadweight loss of HE-FM-EXT-002 less the triangle of HE-FM-DWL-001 left between Q_p plus dQ and the efficient quantity, so at the Pigouvian rate it equals that deadweight loss and at a rate of d_0 it falls short by the overshoot loss (derived here and checked on a grid of curves). The general form in a, b, c, m and g is derived here from the article's example." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net welfare change of a uniform or targeted Pigouvian subsidy with costly public funds" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Goodkin-Gold M, Kremer M, Snyder CM, Williams HL. Optimal vaccine subsidies for endemic and epidemic diseases. NBER Working Paper 28085. Cambridge, MA: National Bureau of Economic Research; 2020. Section 3.4, Normative Measures, which writes welfare as W(Q) = SB(Q) minus cQ with marginal social benefit the derivative of SB and the first best solving MSB(Q**) = c, and section 3.7, Government Subsidies, which states that competitive firms pass the subsidy through to consumers and that GS** = MEX(Q**), the familiar result that setting the subsidy equal to the marginal externality is optimal. Section 2.4, also titled Normative Measures, gives in equation (22) a steady-state marginal externality that rises with coverage below Q0 and is zero once enough vaccine is available to eradicate the disease. https://www.nber.org/papers/w28085",
        "Office of Management and Budget. Circular No. A-94 (Transmittal Memo No. 64). Guidelines and discount rates for benefit-cost analysis of federal programs. Washington, DC: OMB; 1992. Section 11, Special Guidance for Public Investment, which states that taxes impose a burden in excess of the revenues they raise, that a reasonable estimate of the marginal excess burden is 25 cents per dollar of revenue, and that costs in the form of public expenditures are multiplied by 1.25 in a supplementary analysis; the section is not required for cost-effectiveness analyses. https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/a094.pdf",
        "Pigou AC. The Economics of Welfare. 4th ed. London: Macmillan; 1932. First published 1920. Part II, Chapter XI, section 11, which states that where the value of the marginal social net product exceeds that of the marginal private net product, certain rates of bounty increase the national dividend provided that the funds for the bounty can be raised by a mere transfer that does not inflict any indirect injury on production. https://oll.libertyfund.org/titles/pigou-the-economics-of-welfare" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Excess burden charged only on the induced units of a uniform subsidy. A uniform subsidy is paid to inframarginal users too, so its excess burden falls on the whole bill. Charging it only on the induced courses in the article's example reports a net gain of GBP 61,200 when the uniform subsidy of GBP 12 loses GBP 82,800, a gap of GBP 144,000 equal to the excess burden on the GBP 720,000 transfer (computed here for illustration).",
        "Uptake response to a Pigouvian subsidy taken as the rate itself. Uptake rises by s / b, not by s. In the steeper-demand example, with b of 2, writing dQ as 12 in place of 6 gives a welfare gain of zero in place of GBP 45,000 and a net change of minus GBP 100,800 in place of minus GBP 41,400 (computed here for illustration). The two coincide only when b is 1, as in the article's curves." ;
    hemo:9000014 "Q_p = (a - c) / b; d_0 = m - g * Q_p; dQ = s / b; S_g = d_0 * dQ - 0.5 * (b + g) * dQ^2; W = S_g - lambda * s * (Q_p + dQ); W_T = S_g - lambda * s * dQ" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-PIGS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-welfare-change-of-a-uniform-or-targeted-pigouvian-subsidy-with-costly-public-funds" .

hemo:5000746 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Welfare-maximising uniform Pigouvian subsidy when public funds are costly"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-PIGS-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000171 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004785 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003475,
        hemo:6003476,
        hemo:6003477,
        hemo:6003478,
        hemo:6003479,
        hemo:6003480,
        hemo:6003481,
        hemo:6003482,
        hemo:6003483,
        hemo:6003484 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003479,
        hemo:6003480,
        hemo:6003481,
        hemo:6003482,
        hemo:6003483,
        hemo:6003484 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003475,
        hemo:6003476 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004783,
        hemo:7004784 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004786,
        hemo:7004787,
        hemo:7004788,
        hemo:7004789,
        hemo:7004790,
        hemo:7004791 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Finds the uniform per-unit subsidy that maximises the net welfare W of HE-FM-PIGS-002 and the net welfare it gives. Setting the derivative of W to zero gives s_opt, which falls below the Pigouvian rate of HE-FM-EXT-002 whenever lambda is above zero and d_0 is positive, because each extra pound of rate is paid on every inframarginal unit as well as on the induced ones. With lambda of zero and a positive d_0, s_opt equals the Pigouvian rate and W_opt the deadweight loss of HE-FM-EXT-002. A subsidy adds net welfare only when the external benefit at the private quantity exceeds lambda times b times Q_p; otherwise the formula returns zero. The closed form is derived here from W and checked against the article's figures." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Welfare-maximising uniform Pigouvian subsidy when public funds are costly" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Goodkin-Gold M, Kremer M, Snyder CM, Williams HL. Optimal vaccine subsidies for endemic and epidemic diseases. NBER Working Paper 28085. Cambridge, MA: National Bureau of Economic Research; 2020. Section 3.7, Government Subsidies, which constrains the subsidy to be nonnegative; section 4.2, Targeting Subsidies, which states that authorities may face frictions in raising or dispersing public funds that make programme expenses a relevant consideration; and section 6, Conclusion, which states that with a monopoly supplier the per-unit subsidy needed for the first best can be prohibitively expensive and that, with even a small deadweight loss of taxation, such a subsidy would fail a cost-benefit test. https://www.nber.org/papers/w28085",
        "Office of Management and Budget. Circular No. A-94 (Transmittal Memo No. 64). Guidelines and discount rates for benefit-cost analysis of federal programs. Washington, DC: OMB; 1992. Section 11.b, Exceptions, which states that a different figure may be used where specific information clearly suggests that the excess burden is lower or higher than 25 percent, with an explanation, and that an investment funded by user charges that function like market prices has an excess burden of zero. https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/a094.pdf",
        "Pigou AC. The Economics of Welfare. 4th ed. London: Macmillan; 1932. First published 1920. Part II, Chapter XI, section 11, which states that there will be one rate of bounty the granting of which would have the optimum effect, provided that the funds for the bounty can be raised by a mere transfer that does not inflict any indirect injury on production. https://oll.libertyfund.org/titles/pigou-the-economics-of-welfare" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Excess burden charged only on the transfer to inframarginal users. Counting the excess burden on lambda s Q_p alone leaves out the part paid on the induced units, so the derivative loses its 2 lambda b term. For the article's curves the rate becomes 3 / 1.25, GBP 2.40, with a net gain of GBP 2,448 against GBP 2,727 at GBP 1.82 (computed here for illustration).",
        "Pigouvian rate kept when public funds are costly. Setting the uniform subsidy at the external benefit at the efficient quantity, GBP 12 in the article's example, ignores Pigou's proviso. At an excess burden of 20p a pound it turns a net gain of about GBP 2,700 at GBP 1.82 into a net loss of GBP 82,800, as the article shows." ;
    hemo:9000014 "Q_p = (a - c) / b; d_0 = m - g * Q_p; s_opt = max(0, b * (d_0 - lambda * b * Q_p) / (b + g + 2 * lambda * b)); W_opt = max(0, d_0 - lambda * b * Q_p)^2 / (2 * (b + g + 2 * lambda * b))" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-PIGS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "welfare-maximising-uniform-pigouvian-subsidy-when-public-funds-are-costly" .

hemo:5000893 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "GDP-adjusted reference price of a medicine in one reference country"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000114 obo:IAO_0000125 ;
    obo:IAO_0000115 "Gives a reference country's net price of a medicine adjusted by the ratio of the home country's GDP per capita at purchasing power parity to the reference country's."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "A HEMO-native record, for a formula the Functions and Formulae module does not yet hold. It is listed in module-backlog.md for the module curator."@en,
        "The adjusted price is defined as RHEO competency query cq39 computes it, and Turner et al. 2019 support only the use of purchasing power parity. The benchmark is then the price of the country ranked at the rule's stated position, a rule the 0.1 grammar does not hold."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000117 "Michelle Ashley (drafted with Claude)" ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "RHEO 0.4.0 competency query cq39-reference-price-benchmark; Turner et al. 2019" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HEMO-FM-IRP-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000156 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003701,
        hemo:6003702,
        hemo:6003703,
        hemo:6003704 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003702,
        hemo:6003703,
        hemo:6003704 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003701 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005969 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006093 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8001319 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8001311 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000003 "rheo 0.3.0 cq39-reference-price-benchmark.rq" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = p * G_h / G_r" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>a</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><ci>p</ci><ci>G_h</ci></apply><ci>G_r</ci></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "a = \\frac{p \\, G_{h}}{G_{r}}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HEMO-FM-IRP-001" .

hemo:5000897 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Net days in accounts receivable function for a provider paid claim by claim"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-DAR-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003756,
        hemo:6003757,
        hemo:6003758,
        hemo:6003759 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003756,
        hemo:6003757,
        hemo:6003758 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003759 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a provider's net patient receivables, its net patient service revenue and the calendar days of the revenue period to net days in accounts receivable, the receivables balance expressed as days of average daily net revenue. The measure belongs to providers paid claim by claim, under fee-for-service or case-based payment such as the case-weighted payment per case of HE-FM-CWT-002, where every episode of care becomes a receivable until it is collected. HE-FM-DAR-001 gives the measure and HE-CF-DAR-001 builds its denominator from monthly revenue and actual calendar days as HFMA MAP Key FM-1 requires. Split by payer group, the overall figure is the revenue-weighted mean of the groups' own figures, the weighted mean of HE-FM-CRAT-001 and of the first part of HE-FM-CWT-001 with daily revenue in place of member or case numbers, so it is not drafted again. Since the balance equals r_bar times DAR, the cash released when collection shortens is r_bar times the fall in days, and the annual cost of carrying the balance at a cost of capital i is i times the receivables balance, the same rate-times-balance product as the return R_t = r * B_t on the balance still tied up in HE-CF-ANLS-002. The article's other use of released cash, held as cash and divided by daily cash operating expenses to give extra days cash on hand, is an alternative to the saving, not an addition to it. DAR equals the mean time a unit of revenue waits for cash, the W of Little's law in HE-FM-DES-003, only in a steady state with constant daily revenue, where the closing balance equals the time-average balance (derived); away from that state the two differ. Notation follows the Days in Accounts Receivable article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net days in accounts receivable function for a provider paid claim by claim" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d(AR, R, D) = DAR" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-DAR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-days-in-accounts-receivable-function-for-a-provider-paid-claim-by-claim" .

hemo:5000898 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Heterotrait-monotrait ratio of indicator correlations for discriminant validity"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-DISV-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003760,
        hemo:6003761,
