    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the correlation matrix of the indicators of two or more multi-item constructs, with each indicator assigned to one construct, to the heterotrait-monotrait ratio HTMT_ij for each pair of constructs, the mean correlation between the indicators of constructs i and j divided by the geometric mean of the mean correlations among the indicators within each construct, for comparison with a cut-off of 0.85 or 0.90. HE-FM-DISV-001 writes the ratio for two indicators per construct, the case of the article's worked example, and HE-CF-DISV-001 computes it for any numbers of indicators and every pair of constructs. The article's known-groups step is the live HE-FM-CD-001 applied to the mild and moderate groups (pooled standard deviations of 0.2102 and 17.03, standardised differences of 0.24 and 0.94), its QALY gains of 0.10 and 0.24 are HE-FM-QALY-001, the sum of utility times duration, for two constant-utility states over 2 years differenced, and its ratios of £30,000 and £12,500 per QALY are HE-FM-ICER-001, so none of them is restated. The factor of 2.4 by which the ratio would be overstated is two HE-FM-ICER-001 ratios with the same incremental cost divided, which equals the inverse ratio of the two QALY gains. The multitrait-multimethod checks in the article's steps 1 to 3 are differences of correlations set against the analyst's prespecified cut-offs and have no formula record. Correcting prevalence and cost for misclassification against a gold standard is HE-FN-CRV-001, a criterion-validity function and a different property. Notation follows the Discriminant Validity article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Heterotrait-monotrait ratio of indicator correlations for discriminant validity" ;
    hemo:9000014 "htmt(R, K_i, K_j) = HTMT_ij" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-DISV-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "heterotrait-monotrait-ratio-of-indicator-correlations-for-discriminant-validity" .

hemo:5000899 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Healthcare production function mapping staff, capital, consumables and technology to provider activity"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-HCPF-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003764,
        hemo:6003765,
        hemo:6003766,
        hemo:6003767,
        hemo:6003768 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003764,
        hemo:6003765,
        hemo:6003766,
        hemo:6003767 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003768 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a provider's labour L, capital K, drugs and consumables M and technology and organisation T to the maximum activity Q it can produce in a period, such as consultations or case-mix-adjusted episodes. It is the provider counterpart of the household function HE-FN-HPF-001, in which health itself is the output. The quantities read off the function are held in live records and are cited here, not restated. The Cobb-Douglas form with exponents summing to one is HE-FM-HPF-001 with A = exp(rho_H E), alpha = 1-K and beta = K, K there being the own time share, and the marginal products, each exponent times output over the input, and their ratio, the marginal rate of technical substitution, take the form of those in HE-FM-HPF-002, which computes them only for exponents summing to one; the same form holds for any exponents (derived). Marginal cost as the price of an input over its marginal product, MC = w / MP_L when labour is the only variable input, is Grossman's condition held in HE-SR-HPF-003 and tested in HE-VT-HPF-006, and average variable cost w / AP_L is the unit labour cost w / a_h of HE-FM-BAUM-001. A discrete marginal product from a production schedule is a change in output over a change in input, the same difference-quotient form as HE-FM-ICER-001, and an average product is a partial productivity ratio such as the bed turnover rate of HE-FM-BTO-001. Distance from an estimated frontier is measured by HE-FM-EFF-001 to HE-FM-EFF-003. Average cost, cost functions and the marginal cost of a change in scale belong to the Average Cost, Cost Function and Marginal Analysis pages. This package adds the degree of returns to scale of a Cobb-Douglas function whose output elasticities are estimated freely (HE-FM-HCPF-001). Notation follows the Healthcare Production Function article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Healthcare production function mapping staff, capital, consumables and technology to provider activity" ;
    hemo:9000014 "f(L, K, M, T) = Q" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-HCPF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "healthcare-production-function-mapping-staff-capital-consumables-and-technology-to-provider-activity" .

hemo:5000900 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Health state utility from a time trade-off answer"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-UTIL-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003769,
        hemo:6003770,
        hemo:6003771 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003769,
        hemo:6003770 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003771 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps one respondent's answer to a time trade-off question, the years T_y in full health judged equal to T_x years in a health state, to the utility of that state on the scale with full health at 1 and immediate death at 0. The standard gamble needs no separate formula, since the expected value of a gamble giving full health, valued at 1, with probability p and immediate death, valued at 0, otherwise is HE-FM-CHN-001 with two branches (the general form is HE-FN-EV-001) and equals p, so the indifference probability is itself the utility. The time trade-off rule HE-FM-UTIL-001 is the equality of the QALYs of the two sides of the question, each valued with HE-FM-QALY-001, solved for the utility. Lipman, Brouwer and Attema print it, and no live record states it. The utilities enter QALYs through HE-FM-QALY-001 and cost per QALY through HE-FM-ICER-001 and HE-FM-CUA-001 (HE-CF-ICUR-001 from arm-level costs and QALYs), and the article's worked example, including its toxicity period valued at minus 0.20, is a re-application of HE-FM-QALY-001, HE-FM-DISU-001 and HE-FM-ICER-001 and is not restated. Utilities of EQ-5D profiles come from value sets, HE-FN-EQ3L-001 and the UK time trade-off tariff HE-FM-EQ3L-002, and utility decrements from HE-FN-DISU-001 and HE-CF-DISU-001. Notation follows the Utility article, with the time trade-off durations of Lipman, Brouwer and Attema." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health state utility from a time trade-off answer" ;
    hemo:9000014 "v(T_x, T_y) = U_TTO" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-UTIL-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-state-utility-from-a-time-trade-off-answer" .

hemo:5000903 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Grossman demand for the health stock as a ratio between two states with the implied gross investment"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-DDHC-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000896 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006180 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003791,
        hemo:6003792,
        hemo:6003793,
        hemo:6003794,
        hemo:6003795,
        hemo:6003796,
        hemo:6003797,
        hemo:6003798,
        hemo:6003799 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003791,
        hemo:6003792,
        hemo:6003793,
        hemo:6003794,
        hemo:6003795,
        hemo:6003796,
        hemo:6003797 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003799 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006177,
        hemo:7006178,
        hemo:7006179 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006181,
        hemo:7006182,
        hemo:7006183,
        hemo:7006184,
        hemo:7006185,
        hemo:7006186,
        hemo:7006187,
        hemo:7006188 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Grossman's demand function for the health stock, ln H = eps ln W minus eps ln pi minus eps ln delta with the intercept suppressed, is written as the ratio of the stock demanded after a change to the stock demanded before it, so that the intercept cancels. With pi held fixed a 1% rise in the wage raises the stock demanded by eps percent, while a 1% rise in the marginal cost of gross investment or in the depreciation rate lowers it by eps percent. The wage also raises pi through the own time input, so with pi taken from HE-FM-HPF-003 the net wage elasticity of the stock is (1-K) eps and schooling raises it by rho_H eps per year, Grossman's reduced form for health. The second step applies HE-FM-DFH-001 with net investment small, so gross investment is about delta H and its ratio is the depreciation ratio times H_ratio (derived here), which for a change in delta alone is (delta_1 / delta_0) to the power 1-eps and rises with depreciation when eps is below 1. The function follows from the pure investment condition HE-FM-DFH-002 once the marginal efficiency schedule has the constant elasticity eps." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Grossman demand for the health stock as a ratio between two states with the implied gross investment" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Grossman M. The Demand for Health: A Theoretical and Empirical Investigation. NBER Occasional Paper 119. National Bureau of Economic Research, New York; 1972. Chapter IV, An Empirical Formulation of the Model, section 1, Structure and reduced form, pages 39 and 40, which derives from the production function of healthy days, equation (4-1), the constant-elasticity MEC schedule with eps = 1/(1 + C) and gives the stock of health demand function, equation (4-2), with the cost of capital r minus pi_tilde plus delta inside the logarithm, and its form with the real-own rate of interest equal to zero, equation (4-3), ln H = B' plus eps ln W minus eps ln pi minus eps ln delta. https://www.nber.org/system/files/chapters/c3488/c3488.pdf",
        "Grossman M. The human capital model. In Culyer AJ, Newhouse JP, editors. Handbook of Health Economics. Vol 1A. Elsevier, Amsterdam; 2000. p. 347-408. Section 3.1, Depreciation rate effects, equation (29), the healthy time function h = 8,760 minus B H^(-C) with B and C positive constants, equation (30), the corresponding MEC schedule ln gamma = ln BC minus (C + 1) ln H plus ln W minus ln pi, and the statement that its elasticity is eps = 1/(1 + C), below one since C is positive, with the conclusion that if eps is below one gross investment and the depreciation rate are positively correlated over the life cycle while gross investment and the stock of health are negatively correlated. Section 3.2, Market and nonmarket efficiency, equation (31), the wage elasticity of health (1-K) eps, and equation (35), the schooling effect rho_H eps. Section 5.1, Structure and reduced form, equation (42), the demand function for the stock of health, obtained by solving equation (30) for ln H, with intercepts suppressed and the real-own rate of interest equal to zero; equation (44), the identity that gross investment equals net investment plus depreciation; equation (45), the reduced form for health. The ratio form and the gross investment ratio are derived here. https://doi.org/10.1016/S1574-0064(00)80166-3" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Fall in the health stock demanded read as a fall in gross investment and care. In the article's example the stock demanded falls by about 29% when depreciation doubles, yet gross investment, and with it care, rises by about 41%. Grossman shows that when eps is below one gross investment and the depreciation rate are positively correlated over the life cycle while gross investment and the stock of health are negatively correlated, so less health demanded does not mean less care bought.",
        "Positive real-own rate of interest left out of the cost of health capital. The formula assumes a zero real-own rate. If r minus pi_tilde were 0.03, doubling depreciation to 0.04 from 0.02 would raise the cost of capital to 0.07 from 0.05, a factor of 1.4, and with eps of 0.5 the stock demanded would fall to 0.8452 of its level, a fall of 15.5% against the 29.3% that the zero-rate formula gives (computed here for illustration with the cost of capital of HE-FM-DFH-002).",
        "Price of medical care used in place of the marginal cost of gross investment. pi is the unit cost of HE-FM-HPF-003, not the price of care. With K of 0.5 a 10% rise in the price of care raises pi by 4.88%, so with eps of 0.5 the stock demanded falls by 2.35%, while entering the price ratio as the cost ratio gives a fall of 4.65%, about twice as large (computed here for illustration). HE-ER-DFH-004 makes the same point for the pure investment condition." ;
    hemo:9000014 "H_ratio = (W_1 / W_0)^eps * (pi_1 / pi_0)^(-eps) * (delta_1 / delta_0)^(-eps); I_ratio = (delta_1 / delta_0) * H_ratio" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-DDHC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "grossman-demand-for-the-health-stock-as-a-ratio-between-two-states-with-the-implied-gross-investment" .

hemo:5000904 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Grossman reduced-form demand for medical care with its wage, price, schooling and depreciation elasticities"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-DDHC-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000896 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006193 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003800,
        hemo:6003801,
        hemo:6003802,
        hemo:6003803,
        hemo:6003804,
        hemo:6003805,
