        hemo:6003810 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003817 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006189,
        hemo:7006190,
        hemo:7006191,
        hemo:7006192 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006194,
        hemo:7006195,
        hemo:7006196,
        hemo:7006197,
        hemo:7006198,
        hemo:7006199,
        hemo:7006200,
        hemo:7006201,
        hemo:7006202 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Grossman's reduced-form demand function for medical care, ln M = [(1-K) eps + K] ln W minus [(1-K) eps + K] ln P plus rho_H (eps minus 1) E plus (1-eps) ln delta, with intercepts suppressed and the small net disinvestment term left out, is written as the ratio of care demanded after and before a change. It follows from the health stock demand of HE-FM-DDHC-001, gross investment of about delta H from HE-FM-DFH-001, the unit cost pi of HE-FM-HPF-003 and the least-cost care M = (1-K) C / P of HE-FM-HPF-002, and every coefficient contains eps, the parameter of the demand for health. The price elasticity splits into a scale part, minus (1-K) eps, through the smaller health stock demanded when care is dearer, and a substitution part, minus K, towards own time. Schooling changes care by rho_H (eps minus 1) per year and depreciation by the elasticity 1-eps, so with eps below 1 the more educated use less care and people whose health depreciates faster use more. For a change in the price of care alone the ratio is HE-FM-XPE-003 with the own-price elasticity e_MP." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Grossman reduced-form demand for medical care with its wage, price, schooling and depreciation elasticities" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Grossman M. The Demand for Health: A Theoretical and Empirical Investigation. NBER Occasional Paper 119. National Bureau of Economic Research, New York; 1972. Chapter IV, An Empirical Formulation of the Model, section 1, Structure and reduced form, pages 40 and 41, equation (4-5), the Cobb-Douglas gross investment function, and equations (4-6) and (4-7), the reduced form demand curves for health and medical care, with the statements that the last term in (4-7) could be ignored if net disinvestment were small relative to the rate of depreciation and that the equations contain no intercepts because all variables are expressed as deviations from their means. https://www.nber.org/system/files/chapters/c3488/c3488.pdf",
        "Grossman M. The human capital model. In Culyer AJ, Newhouse JP, editors. Handbook of Health Economics. Vol 1A. Elsevier, Amsterdam; 2000. p. 347-408. Section 5.1, Structure and reduced form, which states that equations (42) to (44) and the least-cost condition that the ratio of the marginal products of care and time equals the ratio of the price of care to the wage generate the reduced form demand curves, equation (46) for medical care with coefficients [(1-K) eps + K] on ln W, minus [(1-K) eps + K] on ln P, rho_H (eps minus 1) on E and 1-eps on the depreciation terms; that the last term could be ignored if net disinvestment were small relative to the rate of depreciation; that the investment model predicts a positive wage and a negative price coefficient for care and, if eps is below one, a negative schooling and a positive age coefficient; and that with an elasticity of substitution other than one the term K in the wage and price elasticities would be multiplied by it. Section 3.2, Market and nonmarket efficiency, equation (32), the wage elasticity of care K sigma_p plus (1-K) eps, equation (33) for care rationed by time with a third input, and equation (36), the schooling effect rho_H (eps minus 1). The ratio form and the split of the price elasticity into scale and substitution parts are derived here from equation (46). https://doi.org/10.1016/S1574-0064(00)80166-3",
        "Grossman M. The human capital model. In Culyer AJ, Newhouse JP, editors. Handbook of Health Economics. Vol 1A. Elsevier, Amsterdam; 2000. p. 347-408. Section 6.1, Empirical extensions with cross-sectional data, which states that if the conditional demand function is estimated with health treated as exogenous, the depreciation rate in the initial period is positively correlated with medical care and negatively correlated with health, so that the coefficient of health is biased downward and could well be negative. https://doi.org/10.1016/S1574-0064(00)80166-3" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Coefficient on log depreciation read as the percentage change for a doubling. The coefficient 1-eps applies to ln delta, so with eps of 0.5 a doubling of depreciation raises care by exp(0.5 times ln 2), 41.4%, not by 50%, which overstates the rise by about a fifth (computed here for illustration). The article takes the same route, 0.347 and its exponential.",
        "Negative health coefficient in a conditional demand for care read as evidence against derived demand. Grossman states that when the conditional demand for care is estimated with health treated as exogenous, the initial depreciation rate in the disturbance is positively correlated with care and negatively with health, so the coefficient of health is biased downward and could well be negative. In HE-EX-DDHC-005 the same doubling of depreciation gives 41% more care with 29% less health (HE-EX-DDHC-001).",
        "Price elasticity of care taken as the substitution part or the scale part alone. With eps of 0.5 and K of 0.4 the price elasticity of care is -0.7. Using the substitution part alone, -0.4, understates the response by 43%, and using the scale part alone, -0.3, by 57% (computed here for illustration). The scale part is the response of the health stock demanded to the price of care, so a care elasticity estimated with health held fixed leaves it out (derived here)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "e_MW = (1 - K) * eps + K; e_MP_scale = -(1 - K) * eps; e_MP_subst = -K; e_MP = e_MP_scale + e_MP_subst; s_ME = rho_H * (eps - 1); e_Mdelta = 1 - eps; M_ratio = exp(e_MW * log(W_1 / W_0) + e_MP * log(P_1 / P_0) + s_ME * (E_1 - E_0) + e_Mdelta * log(delta_1 / delta_0))" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-DDHC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "grossman-reduced-form-demand-for-medical-care-with-its-wage-price-schooling-and-depreciation-elasticities" .

hemo:5000907 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Returns to scale of a Cobb-Douglas hospital production function with free output elasticities"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-HCPF-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000899 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006227 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003831,
        hemo:6003832,
        hemo:6003833,
        hemo:6003834,
        hemo:6003835,
        hemo:6003836,
        hemo:6003837,
        hemo:6003838,
        hemo:6003839,
        hemo:6003840 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003832,
        hemo:6003833,
        hemo:6003834,
        hemo:6003835,
        hemo:6003836,
        hemo:6003838 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003837,
        hemo:6003840 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006224,
        hemo:7006225,
        hemo:7006226 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006228,
        hemo:7006229,
        hemo:7006230,
        hemo:7006231,
        hemo:7006232,
        hemo:7006233 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives a provider's activity from labour and capital in the Cobb-Douglas form that hospital studies estimate, with the output elasticities alpha and beta left free, and returns the degree of returns to scale RTS, their sum, with the output Q_s and the ratio g_s when every input is multiplied by s. Sielska and Nojszewska define economies of scale as the sum of the output elasticities, the percentage change in output when all inputs rise by 1%, and g_s equals s to the power RTS for any s (derived), so returns to scale are decreasing when RTS is below 1 and increasing when it is above 1. With alpha + beta = 1 the function is the constant-returns HE-FM-HPF-001, under which g_s always equals s. The marginal products alpha Q / L and beta Q / K have the form of those in HE-FM-HPF-002, each exponent times output over the input, a form that holds at any RTS (derived), although HE-FM-HPF-002 computes them only for exponents summing to one. RTS is a property of the estimated function and is the same at every size, unlike scale efficiency HE-FM-EFF-003, a ratio of frontier scores for one unit." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Returns to scale of a Cobb-Douglas hospital production function with free output elasticities" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Cylus J, Papanicolas I, Smith PC, editors. Health system efficiency: how to make measurement matter for policy and management. Copenhagen: European Observatory on Health Systems and Policies; 2016. The Glossary, page xvi, defines decreasing returns to scale as a proportionate increase in all inputs leading to a lower proportionate increase in outputs, increasing returns to scale as one leading to a greater proportionate increase in outputs, and constant returns to scale as a given increase in inputs always giving the same increase in outputs, regardless of the scale of operations. https://iris.who.int/items/5e2a6617-4313-4c47-bb14-1e3ddcc3890f",
        "Sielska A, Nojszewska E. Production function for modeling hospital activities. The case of Polish county hospitals. PLoS One. 2022;17(5):e0268350. The Methods section defines economies of scale as the sum of the elasticities of production with respect to each factor, showing by how many percent production changes when the employment of all factors rises by 1%, in an unnumbered expression before equation (5), which gives this elasticity of scale as a limit. Equations (8) to (10) give the Cobb-Douglas function as alpha_0 times the product of each factor raised to its power alpha_i, its logarithmic form and the elasticity of production of each factor, equal to alpha_i and not dependent on the employment of factors, and the section states that the functions were estimated by least squares in R. The Discussion reports, for the two-factor Cobb-Douglas function with electricity and nurses, that a 1% rise in electricity costs raises patient-days by 0.48% and a 1% rise in nurse FTEs by 0.2%, and that economies of scale do not exceed 1, so a 1% rise in both inputs gives less than 1% more patient service capacity. The text after equation (11) describes Cobb-Douglas economies of scale as the sum of the elasticities but prints the expression with a product sign; the records follow the sum. The sum 0.68 and the ratio s to the power RTS are computed here and are not printed in the paper. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0268350" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Diminishing marginal returns to staff read as decreasing returns to scale. Diminishing returns concern one input rising with the others fixed, returns to scale all inputs rising together. With elasticities of 0.5 each, 40 units of capital and A of 1, raising labour from 10 to 20 lowers its marginal product alpha Q / L (HE-FM-HPF-002) from 1 to 0.7071, yet doubling both inputs doubles output because RTS is 1 (computed here for illustration). A falling marginal product of nurses in a fixed building says nothing on its own about the returns to expanding the whole hospital.",
        "Linear reading of returns to scale for a large change in hospital inputs. RTS is the percentage response to a small proportional change. For a large change the exact ratio is s to the power RTS. Doubling both inputs with RTS of 0.68 raises output by 60.2%, while multiplying the 100% rise by 0.68 gives 68% (computed here for illustration).",
        "Output elasticities multiplied instead of added for hospital returns to scale. The degree of returns to scale is the sum of the output elasticities. Multiplying Sielska and Nojszewska's 0.2 and 0.48 gives 0.096 and implies that a 1% rise in nurses and electricity raises patient-days by about 0.096%, about a seventh of the 0.68% that the sum gives (computed here for illustration).",
        "Returns to scale of two included inputs read as the returns of the whole hospital. If an illustrative hospital's full function also had doctors with an elasticity of 0.32, its returns to scale would be 1, while the elasticities of nurses and electricity in that function, 0.2 and 0.48, sum to 0.68. A 1% rise in nurses and electricity alone, with doctors fixed, then raises output by 0.68%, and a 1% rise in all three by 1% (derived, computed here for illustration). A sum below 1 from a function that leaves out inputs does not by itself show decreasing returns to scale for the hospital." ;
    hemo:9000014 "Q = A * L^alpha * K^beta; RTS = alpha + beta; Q_s = A * (s * L)^alpha * (s * K)^beta; g_s = Q_s / Q" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-HCPF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "returns-to-scale-of-a-cobb-douglas-hospital-production-function-with-free-output-elasticities" .

hemo:5000908 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Time trade-off utility of a chronic state under the linear QALY model"@en ;
