    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006234,
        hemo:7006235,
        hemo:7006236 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006238,
        hemo:7006239,
        hemo:7006240,
        hemo:7006241,
        hemo:7006242 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "The time trade-off utility is the ratio of the years in full health T_y to the years in the state T_x that the respondent judges equal. The rule follows from the indifference once full health is valued at 1, immediate death at 0 and utility of life duration is linear in years, as Lipman, Brouwer and Attema set out, and it gives one respondent's value for one state judged at least as good as immediate death. The standard gamble asked about the same state gives the indifference probability itself as the utility (HE-FN-UTIL-001), and the two methods tend to give different values (HE-ER-UTIL-001)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Time trade-off utility of a chronic state under the linear QALY model" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Arnold D, Girling A, Stevens A, Lilford R. Comparison of direct and indirect methods of estimating health state utilities for resource allocation: review and empirical analysis. BMJ. 2009;339:b2688. doi:10.1136/bmj.b2688 (full text read in PubMed Central, PMC2714630). Abstract, Results, which reports 32 studies yielding 83 instances and mean utility values of 0.81 for the standard gamble and 0.77 for the time trade off. https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.b2688",
        "Lipman SA, Brouwer WBF, Attema AE. QALYs without bias? Nonparametric correction of time trade-off and standard gamble weights based on prospect theory. Health Economics. 2019;28(7):843-854. doi:10.1002/hec.3895 (full text read in PubMed Central, PMC6618285). Section 2.1, SG and TTO correction for PT, which states that TTO and SG weights are often derived under expected utility and the linear QALY model, with L(T) = T, U(FH) = 1 and V(D) = 0, and that under these assumptions the indifferences between (beta_x, T_x) and (FH, T_y), and between (beta_x, T_x) and the prospect giving (FH, T_x) with probability p and D otherwise, give the TTO weight U(beta_x) = T_y / T_x and the SG weight U(beta_x) = p (unnumbered expressions). The abstract names the corrections for loss aversion, probability weighting and nonlinear utility of life duration. https://doi.org/10.1002/hec.3895",
        "Morimoto T, Fukui T. Utilities measured by rating scale, time trade-off, and standard gamble: review and reference for health care professionals. Journal of Epidemiology. 2002;12(2):160-178. doi:10.2188/jea.12.160 (full text read in PubMed Central, PMC10468341). Introduction, which states that the SG was developed by von Neumann and Morgenstern, that one strategy gives perfect health with probability p and death with probability 1-p and the other a certain intermediate health status, that p is varied until the respondent registers indifference, at which point the utility value is p, and that the TTO assesses how long a period in perfect health is equivalent to a given period of ill health. The abstract reports a strong tendency for the rating scale to yield the lowest and the SG the highest values. https://doi.org/10.2188/jea.12.160",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 13 October 2026. Chapter 4, section 4.3.2 (valuing health-related quality of life according to preferences for a health state relative to other states, usually perfect health and death), section 4.3.4 (valuation of public preferences from a representative sample of the UK population using a choice-based method) and section 4.3.5 (different methods used to measure health-related quality of life produce different utility values, and results from different methods or instruments cannot always be compared). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2",
        "Torrance GW. Measurement of health state utilities for economic appraisal. Journal of Health Economics. 1986;5(1):1-30. doi:10.1016/0167-6296(86)90020-2. Abstract, which states that the rating scale, standard gamble and time trade-off are described in detail and that their use is described for both chronic and temporary health states and for both health states considered better than death and those considered worse than death. https://doi.org/10.1016/0167-6296(86)90020-2" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Applying the time trade-off ratio to a state judged worse than dead. A respondent who prefers immediate death to T_x years in the state has no T_y of zero or more that makes the two sides equal, so T_y over T_x cannot fall below 0 and the ratio stops at 0 (derived). Values below 0, which the live HE-VR-QALY-001 allows for states worse than dead, need a task framed for states worse than death, whose use Torrance describes, and no formula for that case is given here.",
        "Mixing standard gamble and time trade-off utilities in one comparison. Morimoto and Fukui report a strong tendency for the standard gamble to give the highest values of the three methods they review. In the review of 32 studies by Arnold and colleagues the mean values were 0.81 across the cases valued by standard gamble and 0.77 across those valued by time trade-off, and PMG36 section 4.3.5 states that results from different methods or instruments cannot always be compared. Valuing the same state at 0.81 for one option and 0.77 for its comparator over 5 years creates a difference of 0.20 QALYs that no treatment produced (illustrative, derived).",
        "Recording the share of time given up as the time trade-off utility. The share of life given up, T_x minus T_y over T_x, is one minus U_TTO. In the first worked example it is 0.25, so recording it as the utility values a state the respondent rated at 0.75 at a third of that value (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "U_TTO = T_y / T_x" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-UTIL-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "time-trade-off-utility-of-a-chronic-state-under-the-linear-qaly-model" .

hemo:5000913 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "0-1 integer programming function for funding indivisible health programmes within a fixed budget"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-IPH-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004125,
        hemo:6004126,
        hemo:6004127,
        hemo:6004128,
        hemo:6004129 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004125,
        hemo:6004126,
        hemo:6004127 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004128,
        hemo:6004129 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the QALY gain q_i and the budget cost c_i of each of n candidate programmes or medicines, each funded in full or not at all, and a fixed budget B, and returns the package of funding decisions x_i, each 0 or 1, that gives the most QALYs with the cost of the package at most B, together with the QALYs H_star it buys. The league-table rule for whole programmes, HE-FM-CELT-003, funds in ascending order of ICER and passes over a programme that no longer fits, so it returns a package that fits the budget but not always the best one, and the divisible fill of HE-FM-CELT-001, filled for a whole table by HE-CF-CELT-001, part-funds the marginal programme and so gives health that whole programmes may not be able to deliver. Neither record searches the packages, and HE-FM-CELT-003 leaves the best choice of whole programmes to this article. HE-FM-IPH-001 gives H_star for four programmes by checking every package, with its gap to the health of a funding rule and its distance below the divisible fill, and HE-CF-IPH-001 enumerates the packages for any number of programmes and adds programmes that a rule requires to be funded. The critical ratio of a league table is HE-FM-CELT-002 and the budget implied by a threshold is HE-FM-CELT-004; shadow prices and Lagrangian conditions belong to the Constrained Optimisation article. The article's set-up cost form, a fixed cost switched on by a binary set-up variable plus a cost per person covered, is a model-building device for mixed-integer programmes and is not drafted here. Notation follows the Integer Programming article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "0-1 integer programming function for funding indivisible health programmes within a fixed budget" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p(c_i, q_i, B) = (x_i, H_star)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-IPH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "0-1-integer-programming-function-for-funding-indivisible-health-programmes-within-a-fixed-budget" .

hemo:5000914 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Capacity utilisation of a health service with plant capacity measured on a frontier"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-CAPU-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004130,
        hemo:6004131,
        hemo:6004132 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004130,
        hemo:6004131 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004132 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a health service's use of its capacity to a capacity utilisation figure. The simple measure in the article, capacity used over capacity available in the same units and period times 100, is a plain proportion of the form of the first part of HE-FM-PR-001, P_1 = a / (a + b), with a the capacity used and b the spare capacity, and of HE-FM-PDC-001, so it is not restated. Its bed form, occupied over available bed-days, is the occupancy o defined in HE-VR-ALOS-006 and used in HE-FM-ALOS-002, HE-FM-BTO-003 and HE-FM-BTO-004, and the empty bed-days of a period are the numerator of HE-FM-BTO-002. The fixed cost of a bed spread over the patients it served is HE-FM-BTO-005, the break-even volume of a service with fixed costs is HE-FM-BEA-002, annualised capital cost is HE-FM-COST-003 and HE-CF-ANLS-001, and the utilisation of a single-server queue is rho in HE-FM-ARRP-005. The average cost form AC = F / Q + v of the article belongs to the average-cost concept. HE-FM-CAPU-001 is plant capacity utilisation in frontier analysis, which divides the output efficiency of a unit when only its fixed inputs constrain production by its output efficiency when all inputs do, so that technical inefficiency (HE-FM-EFF-001) is set aside before unused plant capacity is measured. It is built like scale efficiency HE-FM-EFF-003 but compares a different pair of frontiers. Notation follows the Capacity Utilisation article, which describes the plant capacity measure in words, with the symbols of Valdmanis and colleagues." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Capacity utilisation of a health service with plant capacity measured on a frontier" ;
    hemo:9000014 "g(E_F, E_V) = PI" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-CAPU-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "capacity-utilisation-of-a-health-service-with-plant-capacity-measured-on-a-frontier" .

hemo:5000915 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Health spending as a share of GDP function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-HGDP-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004133,
        hemo:6004134,
        hemo:6004135 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004133,
        hemo:6004134 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004135 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the current health expenditure and the gross domestic product of one country in one year, both in the same national currency at current prices, to the share of GDP spent on health, reported as a percentage. The change in the share between two years is HE-FM-HGDP-001 evaluated in each year and divided, which regroups as the growth factor of spending divided by the growth factor of GDP (derived identity, not a separate formula). Spending per person in international dollars is a cost converted at purchasing power parity with HE-FM-BOC-002 and a target factor of 1, as in HE-EX-BOC-005, then divided by the population. The administrative share of current health expenditure is HE-FM-ACR-001, and the Baumol records give a relative unit cost and a model share of working hours (HE-FN-BAUM-001) and a component of real health spending growth (HE-FM-BAUM-004), which HE-ER-BAUM-008 separates from a change in the GDP share. If spending is proportional to GDP raised to an income elasticity eta, the share is proportional to GDP raised to eta minus 1 and rises with GDP only when eta exceeds 1 (derived); the only live income elasticity is the Variable HE-VR-XPE-021. Notation follows the Health Spending as GDP Share article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health spending as a share of GDP function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "s(CHE, GDP) = S" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-HGDP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-spending-as-a-share-of-gdp-function" .

hemo:5000917 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Erlang loss sizing function for hospital beds with refused admissions"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-CAPP-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004145,
        hemo:6004146,
        hemo:6004147,
        hemo:6004148,
        hemo:6004149,
        hemo:6004150,
        hemo:6004151 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004145,
        hemo:6004146,
        hemo:6004147 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004148,
        hemo:6004149,
        hemo:6004150,
        hemo:6004151 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a ward's daily arrival rate, mean length of stay and number of staffed beds to the offered load, the share of arriving patients who find every bed full and are refused, the refusals a year and the occupancy the beds then achieve, and uses these to find the beds needed for a refusal target or at the lowest total cost of beds and refusals. The first estimate of beds, expected bed-days divided by the bed-days one bed supplies at a target occupancy, is the live throughput identity HE-FM-ALOS-002 solved for the beds with admissions in place of discharges, and the average number of occupied beds is Little's law, HE-FM-DES-003; neither is drafted again. The loss probability is not the Poisson overflow of HE-CF-ARRP-001, which gives the chance that more patients arrive in one hour than the service can absorb, a count of arrivals rather than of occupied beds, and not the single-server wait of HE-FM-ARRP-005, in which patients queue. HE-FM-CAPP-001 writes the loss model term by term for a six-bed unit, and HE-CF-CAPP-001 runs it for any number of beds by the Erlang B recursion and finds the beds for a refusal target and the number beyond which one more bed costs more than the refusals it avoids. Notation follows the Capacity Planning article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Erlang loss sizing function for hospital beds with refused admissions" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c(lambda, L_bar, N) = (a, P_block, R, o_ach)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-CAPP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "erlang-loss-sizing-function-for-hospital-beds-with-refused-admissions" .

hemo:5000920 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Phelps second-order expected welfare loss at a coinsurance rate"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-MHHI-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000912 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006293 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004168,
        hemo:6004169,
        hemo:6004170,
        hemo:6004171,
        hemo:6004172,
        hemo:6004173,
        hemo:6004174,
        hemo:6004175 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004171,
        hemo:6004172,
        hemo:6004173,
        hemo:6004174,
        hemo:6004175 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004168 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006289,
        hemo:7006290,
        hemo:7006291,
        hemo:7006292 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006294,
        hemo:7006295,
        hemo:7006296,
        hemo:7006297,
        hemo:7006298,
        hemo:7006299 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Adds two losses that move in opposite directions as cover changes. The first is the risk premium of the spending the patient still bears, one half of r times the variance of that spending, which is sigma squared times C squared when insurance pays the share 1 minus C of every bill. The second is the welfare-loss triangle of insurance-induced care, one half of (1 minus C) squared times eps times mu, which is HE-FM-MH-002 with the demand slope written as eps mu over the square of the full price (derived, for a straight-line demand curve with elasticity eps at the full price). Lowering C cuts the first term and raises the second, both quadratically, so the expected loss has a single minimum at the rate given by HE-FM-MHHI-002. Phelps prints the sum as his equation (4), with the elasticity entered as a negative number and subtracted." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Phelps second-order expected welfare loss at a coinsurance rate" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Phelps CE. Medical insurance: risk spreading vs. moral hazard revisited. Working paper. Rochester, NY: University of Rochester; April 2002. Section II, The Model, equation (2c), whose variance term minus r sigma squared over 2 Phelps says replicates Pratt's conclusion, with the statement that insurance paying 1 minus C of any loss reduces the risk to C squared sigma squared; equation (3), the expected moral hazard loss one half of (1 minus C) squared times E(pm eta) with the elasticity evaluated at the full-price demand; and equation (4), the sum of the two losses, with the statement that smaller C decreases the risk losses quadratically and increases the moral hazard losses quadratically. The appendix sizes the ignored income-price term. https://assets.csom.umn.edu/assets/147138.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Entering the price elasticity with its negative sign in the Phelps loss. Phelps writes the elasticity as a negative number and subtracts its term, while the formula here takes the absolute value. Typing minus 0.1 for the illustrative hospital care turns the full-insurance loss of 50 into minus 50, a gain from induced use that the model cannot produce.",
        "Reading the second-order risk premium as exact for hospital spending. The risk term ignores skewness. Phelps finds that for hospital care in the RAND data the third and fourth order terms are of the same order of magnitude as the variance term, so the second-order risk premium for heavily skewed hospital spending is an approximation to report with that caveat, even though the optimal rate he computes changes little between the second and fourth order.",
        "Using the standard deviation of spending in place of its variance. The risk term needs sigma squared. With the illustrative hospital standard deviation of 5,000 used unsquared, the no-insurance risk premium is 0.25 instead of 1,250, and the loss at the optimal rate is 46.23 instead of 48.08, so the value of cover almost disappears." ;
    hemo:9000014 "RP = 0.5 * r * sigma^2 * C^2; MH = 0.5 * (1 - C)^2 * eps * mu; WL = RP + MH" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-MHHI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "phelps-second-order-expected-welfare-loss-at-a-coinsurance-rate" .

hemo:5000921 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Phelps optimal coinsurance rate from risk aversion, spending variance and price elasticity"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-MHHI-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000912 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006303 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004176,
        hemo:6004177,
        hemo:6004178,
        hemo:6004179,
        hemo:6004180 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004177,
        hemo:6004178,
        hemo:6004179,
        hemo:6004180 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004176 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006300,
        hemo:7006301,
        hemo:7006302 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006304,
        hemo:7006305,
        hemo:7006306,
        hemo:7006307,
        hemo:7006308,
        hemo:7006309,
        hemo:7006310 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives the coinsurance rate that minimises the expected welfare loss of HE-FM-MHHI-001, found by setting its derivative with respect to C to zero. The rate is eps mu over eps mu plus r sigma squared, which Phelps puts in words as the moral hazard loss over the sum of the moral hazard loss and the risk premium. It rises with the price elasticity and falls with risk aversion and with the variance of spending, so a large, barely price-sensitive risk such as hospital care calls for near-complete cover and small, predictable, price-sensitive spending for a high rate. Phelps also gives it in relative terms with the coefficient of variation and the share of income at risk (his equation (7)), the same rule divided through by mu (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Phelps optimal coinsurance rate from risk aversion, spending variance and price elasticity" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Phelps CE. Medical insurance: risk spreading vs. moral hazard revisited. Working paper. Rochester, NY: University of Rochester; April 2002. Section II, The Model, equation (5a), the first-order condition, equation (5b), C* = minus E(pm eta) over r sigma squared minus E(pm eta), with the statement that r above 0 and minus eta above 0 make C* lie above 0 and below 1, equation (6), C* as the moral hazard loss over the moral hazard loss plus the risk premium, footnote 4 on the second-order condition, and equation (7), the relative form with the coefficient of variation. Section III, Measuring Welfare Losses and C*, which takes r of about 0.0001, Table 1 (base-case mu, eta, sigma squared and C*, including 0.07 for hospital and 0.49 for physician care) and Table 2 (C* for r of 0.00005, 0.0001, 0.0002 and 0.0005, including 0.32 for physician care at 0.0002). https://assets.csom.umn.edu/assets/147138.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Applying one coinsurance rate to all types of care. The optimal rate is service-specific. Charging the outpatient optimum of 150 / 175 = 0.857 on the illustrative hospital care raises its expected loss in HE-FM-MHHI-001 to 919.39 against 48.08 at its own optimum, and charging the hospital optimum of 100 / 2,600 = 0.038 on the outpatient visits raises theirs to 69.36 against 10.71.",
        "Entering the elasticity as a negative number in the optimal rate. Phelps writes the elasticity as negative and the formula here takes its absolute value. Typing minus 0.1 for the illustrative hospital care gives minus 0.042, and minus 0.3 for the outpatient visits gives 1.2, both outside the range above 0 and below 1 that Phelps shows the optimum must lie in.",
        "Risk aversion and spending in different currency units. r = 0.0001 is per dollar. Entering the illustrative hospital spending in thousands of dollars (mu = 1, sigma = 5) with the same r gives 0.1 / 0.1025 = 0.976 instead of 0.038.",
        "Using the standard deviation of spending in place of its variance in the optimal rate. For the illustrative hospital care, r times sigma is 0.5 where r times sigma squared is 2,500, so the rate comes out at 0.995 instead of 0.038 and recommends almost no cover for the risk that most needs it." ;
    hemo:9000014 "C_star = eps * mu / (eps * mu + r * sigma^2)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-MHHI-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "phelps-optimal-coinsurance-rate-from-risk-aversion-spending-variance-and-price-elasticity" .

hemo:5000923 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Plant capacity utilisation from output efficiency with fixed inputs and with all inputs"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CAPU-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000914 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006323 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004195,
        hemo:6004196,
        hemo:6004197 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004196,
        hemo:6004197 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004195 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006320,
        hemo:7006321,
        hemo:7006322 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006324,
        hemo:7006325,
        hemo:7006326,
        hemo:7006327,
        hemo:7006328 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Divides the output efficiency score of a hospital or other unit measured with only its fixed inputs, such as beds and rostered staff, constraining output and its variable inputs unrestricted, by its output efficiency score with all inputs constraining output, both from one data envelopment analysis under constant returns to scale. The all-input score E_V carries the technical inefficiency of the unit, so dividing by it leaves the part of the shortfall from capacity output that is due to unused plant, the Johansen definition of plant capacity that Valdmanis and colleagues adopt. A value of 1 means that the unit, once on the frontier, could not raise its outputs in proportion without more fixed inputs, and a value below 1 shows unused plant capacity that could treat proportionately more patients. In 68 Thai public hospitals in 1999 the mean was 0.954. Unlike a bed occupancy ratio, the measure sets outputs against what the fixed inputs could produce." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Plant capacity utilisation from output efficiency with fixed inputs and with all inputs" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Valdmanis V, Kumanarayake L, Lertiendumrong J. Capacity in Thai public hospitals and the production of care for poor and nonpoor patients. Health Services Research. 2004;39(6 Pt 2):2117-2134. doi:10.1111/j.1475-6773.2004.00335.x (full text read in PubMed Central, PMC1361115). The Model subsection on capacity to provide increased services, the plant capacity approach, adopts the Johansen definition of plant capacity, states that the plant capacity measure PI is the ratio of the output efficiency where some inputs are variable and others fixed (E_F) to the output efficiency where all inputs are treated as variable (E_V), that a value below 1 indicates unused or underutilised plant capacity, that inefficiencies are factored out first and that the analysis is restricted to constant returns to scale, citing the methods of Färe, Grosskopf and Kokkelenberg (1989) and others. The Appendix gives the two output-based programmes, the second with the fixed inputs restricted and the variable inputs unrestricted. The Data section classes beds, doctors, nurses and other staff as fixed inputs and the three expenditure inputs as variable, and Table 4 reports a sample mean of 0.954 for 1999. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1475-6773.2004.00335.x" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Inverting the plant capacity utilisation ratio. Dividing E_V by E_F gives the factor by which output on the frontier could rise if the unused plant were brought into use, not the share of capacity in use. In the first worked example it returns 1.0526, which read as utilisation would claim that the hospital works above its capacity output (derived).",
        "Reading the fixed-input score alone as capacity utilisation. E_F on its own is observed output over capacity output and equals PI times E_V (derived), so it mixes technical inefficiency with unused plant. Valdmanis and colleagues factor out inefficiency first because they measure plant capacity and not inefficient production. In the first worked example E_F of 0.76 would suggest that 24% of capacity output is unused, when 5% is due to idle plant and the rest of the gap to technical inefficiency (derived).",
        "Taking the all-input score from a variable returns frontier. Both scores must come from constant returns frontiers. If E_F is from the constant returns programme but E_V is taken from a variable returns frontier, the ratio also carries scale efficiency (HE-FM-EFF-003). With E_F 0.76 and constant and variable returns scores for E_V of 0.80 and 0.90, PI falls from 0.95 to 0.844, and the scale effect is reported as idle plant (illustrative, derived).",
        "Treating a bed occupancy ratio as plant capacity utilisation. An occupancy ratio such as HE-VR-ALOS-006 sets one used input against its capacity and says nothing about what was produced, as the article notes. A hospital can show high bed occupancy while its fixed inputs could support more output with the same variable spending, and a ratio of used to available beds cannot reveal that. PI compares outputs with what the fixed inputs could produce on the frontier." ;
    hemo:9000014 "PI = E_F / E_V" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CAPU-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "plant-capacity-utilisation-from-output-efficiency-with-fixed-inputs-and-with-all-inputs" .

hemo:5000924 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Current health expenditure as a percentage of gross domestic product"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-HGDP-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000915 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006332 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004198,
        hemo:6004199,
        hemo:6004200 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004199,
        hemo:6004200 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004198 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006329,
        hemo:7006330,
        hemo:7006331 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006333,
        hemo:7006334,
        hemo:7006335,
        hemo:7006336,
        hemo:7006337,
        hemo:7006338 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Divides current health expenditure by gross domestic product for the same country and year and multiplies by 100. The WHO Global Health Expenditure Database defines its indicator che_gdp in this way, with both parts held in million current national currency units, so no exchange rate or purchasing power parity enters the share. The OECD uses the same ratio in Health at a Glance and keeps GDP as the denominator for every country. The share moves whenever either part moves, so a value read on its own says nothing about the level of spending." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Current health expenditure as a percentage of gross domestic product" ;
    hemo:9000010 "OECD. Health at a Glance 2025: OECD Indicators. Paris: OECD Publishing; 2025. doi:10.1787/8f9e3f98-en. Chapter 7, Health expenditure in relation to GDP, p. 154, which states that the share varies over time because of differences in both the growth of health spending and overall economic growth, and its Definition and comparability box, which defines expenditure on health as the final consumption of health goods and services (current health expenditure) under the System of Health Accounts 2011, covering all financing arrangements, medical services and goods, population health and prevention programmes and administration, and states that GNI may be more meaningful than GDP in Ireland and Luxembourg but that GDP is kept as the denominator for all countries for consistency. https://doi.org/10.1787/8f9e3f98-en",
        "World Health Organization. Global Health Expenditure Database, GHED_data.xlsx, last updated 12 December 2025. Geneva: WHO. The Codebook sheet defines che_gdp, Current Health Expenditure (CHE) as % Gross Domestic Product (GDP), as CHE divided by GDP; che as the sum of financing schemes HF.1, HF.2, HF.3, HF.4 and HF.nec in million current national currency units; gdp in million current national currency units; and che_ppp_pc as current health expenditure in current international dollars at PPP per capita. The Data sheet gives the United Kingdom and Norway values used in the worked examples and tests, and the 2023 shares and per person values for Ireland and the United Kingdom. The database home page notes that WHO adjusts and estimates national figures and that country data may still differ in definitions, data collection methods, population coverage and estimation methods. https://apps.who.int/nha/database/Home/IndicatorsDownload/en",
        "World Health Organization. Global spending on health: emerging from the pandemic. Geneva: WHO; 2024. ISBN 978-92-4-010449-5. Chapter 1, footnote 1 to the section Health spending during the COVID-19 pandemic, which states that the terms health spending and total health spending in the report are used synonymously with current health expenditure and that capital expenditure on health is not included. https://iris.who.int/items/412c1749-123a-4520-927d-6f076abf7f88" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Health spending share of GDP formatted as a percentage after multiplying by 100. HE-IM-HGDP-001 already multiplies by 100, so a result cell set to the Excel Percentage format shows the United Kingdom 2019 share as 997.95%, or 998% with no decimals, rather than 9.98. Either keep the factor of 100 with a General or Number format, or drop it and use the Percentage format, but not both.",
        "Reading a low GDP share as low spending per person. In the 2023 GHED figures Ireland spent 6.6% of GDP on health against 11.0% in the United Kingdom, yet its current health expenditure per person was 8,269 international dollars at purchasing power parity against 6,365, about 30% higher. The OECD notes that in Ireland and Luxembourg a significant part of GDP is repatriated profits not available for national consumption, and that gross national income may be a more meaningful denominator there. Levels per person are compared through HE-FM-BOC-002, not through S.",
        "Reading a rise or fall in the GDP share as a change in health spending. Between 2021 and 2022 Norway's share fell to 7.91% from 9.81% while its health spending in kroner rose by a factor of 1.0684, because GDP rose by a factor of 1.3259, and the ratio of the two shares, 0.8058, equals the ratio of the two growth factors (derived). The United Kingdom's share rose by a factor of 1.2129 in 2020 with spending up by a factor of 1.1421 and GDP down by a factor of 0.9416. A change in S has to be read with the change in CHE and in GDP beside it.",
        "Spending and GDP taken from different years in the GDP share. Dividing the United Kingdom's 2020 health spending of 254,603 million pounds by the 2019 GDP of 2,233,921 million pounds gives 11.40% instead of the 12.10% of HE-EX-HGDP-002, because the 2020 fall in GDP is left out (derived). The same mistake arises when a GDP series is revised after the spending figures were taken, so both parts should come from one release." ;
    hemo:9000014 "S = CHE / GDP * 100" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-HGDP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "current-health-expenditure-as-a-percentage-of-gross-domestic-product" .

hemo:5000925 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Lifetime infection risk of an unvaccinated child and infections per birth in an endemic infection with vaccination at birth"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-VEXT-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000916 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006341 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004201,
        hemo:6004202,
        hemo:6004203,
        hemo:6004204 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004202,
        hemo:6004203 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004204 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006339,
        hemo:7006340 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006342,
        hemo:7006343,
        hemo:7006344,
        hemo:7006345,
        hemo:7006346,
        hemo:7006347 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "In an endemic infection with lifelong immunity, births and deaths at rate mu and a share v of each birth cohort given a fully protective vaccine at birth, the susceptible share settles at 1/R_0 while the infection persists, so the force of infection lambda settles where the unvaccinated births mu(1 minus v) balance the outflow (lambda + mu)/R_0 from the susceptible pool. An unvaccinated child then leaves the susceptible state by infection or by death, and its lifetime chance of infection lambda/(lambda + mu), the long-horizon limit of HE-FM-TP-004 (derived), becomes pi = 1 minus 1/(R_0(1 minus v)), Eq. 15 of Bauch and Earn and equation (15) of Goodkin-Gold and colleagues, which falls as coverage rises and is zero at or above the threshold H of HE-FM-BRN-003. The same expression is 1 minus 1/R_t with R_t = R_0(1 minus v) from HE-FM-BRN-002 (derived), but HE-FM-BRN-003 is defined for the basic reproduction number and returns an immune share, not a probability of infection. Infections per birth n are the unvaccinated share times that risk, the pathway probability of HE-FM-CHN-002 with factors 1 minus v and pi, which equals (1 minus v) minus 1/R_0 below H (derived from the endemic infected share in Eq. 14 of Bauch and Earn), so each extra vaccination below H removes exactly one infection in total; the share infected in a closed epidemic without births is the final size of HE-CF-ATR-001, a different quantity." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Lifetime infection risk of an unvaccinated child and infections per birth in an endemic infection with vaccination at birth" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Bauch CT, Earn DJ. Vaccination and the theory of games. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America. 2004;101(36):13391-13394. Section Incorporation of an Epidemic Model, which uses the SIR model with births and deaths at rate mu and vaccination of a proportion p of newborns, assuming that individuals are never infected before being vaccinated, gives the critical coverage p_crit = 1 minus 1/R_0 for R_0 above 1 (Eq. 12), the endemic state S-hat = 1 minus p_crit (Eq. 13) and I-hat = f/(1 + f)(p_crit minus p) (Eq. 14), and expresses the probability that an unvaccinated individual eventually becomes infected as the proportion of susceptible individuals becoming infected versus dying in any unit time, pi_p = 1 minus 1/(R_0(1 minus p)) (Eq. 15), noting that the parameter f does not appear in it; and the Appendix, which states that pi_p decreases with p until p reaches the eradication threshold and that pi_p = 0 for p at or above p_crit. https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0403823101",
        "Goodkin-Gold M, Kremer M, Snyder CM, Williams HL. Optimal vaccine subsidies for endemic and epidemic diseases. NBER Working Paper 28085. Cambridge, MA: National Bureau of Economic Research; 2020. Section 2.1, Epidemiological Model, which adds births and deaths at rate mu and immunisation of newborns to the SIR model and gives the steady-state susceptible share as the smaller of 1 minus theta Q/mu and 1/R_0 (equation 7) and the infected share mu/(alpha + mu) times (1 minus 1/R_0 minus theta Q/mu), floored at 0 (equation 8); and section 2.2, Consumer Demand, which treats infection and natural death as competing Poisson risks and gives the probability that a susceptible consumer is infected over the lifetime as beta I/(beta I + mu) (equation 14), equal to 1 minus 1/((1 minus theta Q/mu) R_0) below the eradication quantity Q_0 and 0 at or above it (equation 15). https://www.nber.org/papers/w28085" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Epidemic final size used as the lifetime risk in an endemic infection. The final size of a closed epidemic without births, HE-CF-ATR-001, answers a different question. Applied to the article's R_0 of 5 with 0.4 of the population susceptible it returns a final attack rate of about 0.319 of the whole population, or about 0.797 of the unvaccinated, against the endemic lifetime risk of 0.5 at 60% coverage, so read as the risk of an unvaccinated child it overstates the private benefit of vaccination by about 59% and understates the external share.",
        "Unvaccinated child's risk applied to every birth in the endemic infection count. pi is the risk of a child who is not vaccinated. Multiplying it by all births in place of the unvaccinated births gives 0.5 × 1,000 = 500 infections per 1,000 births at the article's 60% coverage in place of 200, two and a half times the true count, and inflates the loss under voluntary uptake from GBP 100,000 to GBP 250,000." ;
    hemo:9000014 "pi = max(0, 1 - 1 / (R_0 * (1 - v))); n = (1 - v) * pi" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-VEXT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "lifetime-infection-risk-of-an-unvaccinated-child-and-infections-per-birth-in-an-endemic-infection-with-vaccination-at-birth" .

hemo:5000926 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Private and external benefit of one vaccination at a given coverage in an endemic infection"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-VEXT-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000916 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006350 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004205,
        hemo:6004206,
        hemo:6004207,
        hemo:6004208,
        hemo:6004209,
        hemo:6004210 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004206,
        hemo:6004207,
        hemo:6004208 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004209,
        hemo:6004210 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006348,
        hemo:7006349 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006351,
        hemo:7006352,
        hemo:7006353,
        hemo:7006354,
        hemo:7006355 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Splits the value of one more vaccination at coverage v, with a loss L per infection valued the same by the family and by society, into the marginal private benefit to the vaccinated child, L times the lifetime risk pi it avoids (the expected value of HE-FM-CHN-001 with branch values L and 0), and the marginal external benefit to unvaccinated children, L/(R_0(1 minus v)), which is L times 1 minus pi below the threshold H and zero at or above it. Below H the two add to L, because each extra vaccination there removes exactly one infection in total (HE-FM-VEXT-001), so the marginal social benefit of HE-FM-EXT-001 stays at L while its split moves towards the external side as coverage rises (derived). Goodkin-Gold and colleagues derive the same split, the marginal private benefit theta H Phi_I, the marginal social benefit theta H and the marginal externality theta H/((1 minus theta Q/mu) R_0) below the eradication quantity and zero above it, with theta of 1 and Q/mu equal to v for a fully protective vaccine. The external share is 0.5 at the article's 60% coverage and 0.8 at 75%, so a subsidy set at the marginal external benefit, as in HE-FM-EXT-002 with linear curves, depends on the coverage at which that benefit is read." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Private and external benefit of one vaccination at a given coverage in an endemic infection" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Boulier BL, Datta TS, Goldfarb RS. Vaccination externalities. The B.E. Journal of Economic Analysis & Policy. 2007;7(1). Abstract, which states that a standard epidemiological model is used to analyse how the magnitude of the externality varies with the number of vaccinations, vaccine efficacy and disease infectiousness, that the size of the externality is not necessarily monotonic in the number vaccinated, vaccine efficacy or disease infectiousness, and that the marginal externality of a vaccination can be greater than one case of illness prevented among the nonvaccinated. https://doi.org/10.2202/1935-1682.1487",
        "Goodkin-Gold M, Kremer M, Snyder CM, Williams HL. Optimal vaccine subsidies for endemic and epidemic diseases. NBER Working Paper 28085. Cambridge, MA: National Bureau of Economic Research; 2020. Section 2.2, Consumer Demand, which gives the marginal private benefit of a risk-neutral consumer as theta H Phi_I(Q), with H the expected harm of an infection and Phi_I the lifetime infection probability, and zero at or above Q_0 (equation 16); and section 2.4, Normative Measures, which gives the marginal social benefit as theta H below Q_0 and zero at or above it (equation 21), defines the marginal externality as marginal social benefit less marginal private benefit, theta H/((1 minus theta Q/mu) R_0) below Q_0 and zero above it (equation 22), and states that for Q below Q_0 the marginal social benefit is a constant that does not vary with R_0. https://www.nber.org/papers/w28085" ;
    hemo:9000011 "External benefit counted as L times 1 minus pi at or above the threshold. Above the threshold pi is 0, so L times 1 minus pi returns the whole GBP 500 as external benefit, when one more vaccination prevents no infection and the marginal externality is zero, as Goodkin-Gold and colleagues state. Applied to the 200 vaccinations above the threshold in the article's universal requirement it credits GBP 100,000 of external benefit that does not exist.",
        "External benefit of a vaccination treated as a fixed amount per dose. Carrying the GBP 250 external benefit found at the article's 60% coverage to the move at 75% coverage understates it by GBP 150, since it is GBP 400 there, and understates the subsidy that the marginal external benefit would justify. The external benefit per vaccination depends on the coverage at which it is read, as the article's misreadings list says." ;
    hemo:9000014 "pi = max(0, 1 - 1 / (R_0 * (1 - v))); MPB = L * pi; MEB = (v < 1 - 1 / R_0) * L / (R_0 * (1 - v))" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-VEXT-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "private-and-external-benefit-of-one-vaccination-at-a-given-coverage-in-an-endemic-infection" .

hemo:5000927 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Coverage reached by voluntary vaccination when families weigh private cost against expected loss from infection"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-VEXT-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000916 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006358 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004211,
        hemo:6004212,
        hemo:6004213,
        hemo:6004214,
        hemo:6004215 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004211,
        hemo:6004212,
        hemo:6004214 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004215 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006356,
        hemo:7006357 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006359,
        hemo:7006360,
        hemo:7006361,
        hemo:7006362,
        hemo:7006363,
        hemo:7006364 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "A family that bears a private cost c per vaccination, counting any price and its own time and travel, and expects a loss L if the child is infected vaccinates while L times pi exceeds c, so in a homogeneous population uptake settles where the unvaccinated child's lifetime risk equals r = c/L. Solving pi(v) = r with HE-FM-VEXT-001 gives v_vol = 1 minus 1/(R_0(1 minus r)), the convergently stable Nash equilibrium P* of Bauch and Earn's vaccination game with r read as their relative risk r_v/r_i, and, with P/(theta H) equal to r, theta times d(P)/mu, the share immunised under the demand d(P) of Goodkin-Gold and colleagues, which for a fully protective vaccine is the share vaccinated. When r is at least the risk with no vaccination, 1 minus 1/R_0, nobody vaccinates, and writing max(1 minus r, 1/R_0) in the denominator returns that 0 and avoids a division by zero for r of 1 or more (derived; it equals the piecewise result on a grid of R_0 and r). For any r above zero v_vol is below the threshold H of HE-FM-BRN-003, Bauch and Earn's result that voluntary vaccination cannot eradicate the disease, and free provision lowers c and raises v_vol without reaching H while any private cost remains." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Coverage reached by voluntary vaccination when families weigh private cost against expected loss from infection" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Bauch CT, Earn DJ. Vaccination and the theory of games. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America. 2004;101(36):13391-13394. Section The Vaccination Game, subsections Description of Game and Characterization of Nash Equilibria, which give the payoff E(P, p) = minus r P minus pi_p(1 minus P) with relative risk r = r_v/r_i (Eq. 2) and state that if r is at least pi_0 the convergently stable Nash equilibrium is never vaccinate and otherwise vaccinate with a probability P* above 0 and below 1; section Incorporation of an Epidemic Model, which gives the mixed equilibrium obtained by solving r = pi_P* as P* = 1 minus 1/(R_0(1 minus r)) (Eq. 17); section Results and Discussion, which states that for any perceived relative risk r above 0 the expected uptake is below the eradication threshold and that parents can be expected to play a pure nonvaccinator strategy if r > pi_0, that is if r > 1 minus 1/R_0 (Eq. 18); and the Appendix, which shows that the Nash equilibrium is the unique solution of pi_P* = r when r < pi_0. https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0403823101",
        "Goodkin-Gold M, Kremer M, Snyder CM, Williams HL. Optimal vaccine subsidies for endemic and epidemic diseases. NBER Working Paper 28085. Cambridge, MA: National Bureau of Economic Research; 2020. Section 2.2, Consumer Demand, which states that consumers buy the vaccine when its marginal private benefit exceeds the price, that when some but not all buy they must be indifferent so that the price equals the marginal private benefit, and gives the demand d(P) = (mu/theta)(1 minus 1/((1 minus P/(theta H)) R_0)) (equation 17) with its boundary cases (equation 18); and section 2.5, Perfectly Competitive Equilibrium, which sets the price at the cost c, bounds case LR2 by R_0 at most 1/(1 minus c~), with c~ = c/(theta H), a case in which the marginal private benefit is below c even if no other consumer buys and the vaccine quantity stays 0, and states that some but not all newborns buy in the next range of R_0, case LR3. https://www.nber.org/papers/w28085" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Bauch and Earn's interior equilibrium used when nobody vaccinates. Eq. 17 holds only when r is below 1 minus 1/R_0. With R_0 of 5, a cost ratio of 0.9 gives 1 minus 1/(5 × 0.1), or minus 1, and a cost ratio of 1.2 gives 2, neither a coverage; the equilibrium in both cases is 0, which the max term in the formula returns.",
        "Resource cost used in place of the family's private cost in voluntary uptake. Free provision changes the cost the family sees, not the cost to society. Entering the full GBP 250 resource cost when the family bears only GBP 100 predicts coverage of 0.6 in place of 0.75, a shortfall from the threshold of 0.2 in place of 0.05, four times too large." ;
    hemo:9000014 "r = c / L; v_vol = 1 - 1 / (R_0 * max(1 - r, 1 / R_0))" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-VEXT-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "coverage-reached-by-voluntary-vaccination-when-families-weigh-private-cost-against-expected-loss-from-infection" .

hemo:5000931 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Incentive and participation slacks of a two-type procurement menu"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-MECH-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000919 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006399 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004236,
        hemo:6004237,
        hemo:6004238,
        hemo:6004239,
        hemo:6004240,
        hemo:6004241,
        hemo:6004242,
        hemo:6004243,
        hemo:6004244,
        hemo:6004245,
        hemo:6004246 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004236,
        hemo:6004237,
        hemo:6004238,
        hemo:6004239,
        hemo:6004240,
        hemo:6004241 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004246 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006395,
        hemo:7006396,
        hemo:7006397,
        hemo:7006398 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006400,
        hemo:7006401,
        hemo:7006402,
        hemo:7006403,
        hemo:7006404 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "A purchaser offers two contracts, (t_L, q_L) intended for a low-cost supplier and (t_H, q_H) for a high-cost one, and cannot observe which type it faces. Each type's information rent is its transfer less its cost on its own contract, and each incentive slack is that rent less what the type would earn by taking the other contract at its own cost. The menu is incentive feasible when both incentive slacks and both rents are at least zero, inequalities (2.9) to (2.12) of Laffont and Martimort. These are the article's incentive compatibility and participation constraints for one participant with two types and an outside option of zero. A low-cost type that takes the high-cost contract earns the high-cost type's rent plus (theta_H minus theta_L) q_H, which is why a menu that leaves both types no rent fails. Each rent takes the form of the margin in HE-FM-CTH-001 with no reimbursed share (derived), there computed per case of hospital care; the incentive slacks, which value one type's cost on the other type's contract, have no live counterpart." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Incentive and participation slacks of a two-type procurement menu" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Laffont JJ, Martimort D. The Theory of Incentives I. The Principal-Agent Model. Authors' manuscript dated 6 February 2001, deposited in Toulouse Capitole Publications, record 14941 (full text read). Published as The Theory of Incentives. The Principal-Agent Model. Princeton University Press; 2002. doi:10.1515/9781400829453 (not seen). The printed book numbers the chapter 2 sections one lower than the manuscript (its section 2.3 is Incentive Feasible Menu of Contracts) and its equation numbers were not checked, so the section and equation numbers below are the manuscript's. Chapter 2, section 2.4.1 (Incentive Compatibility and Participation), Definition 2.1 and inequalities (2.9) and (2.10), the incentive constraints t minus theta q at least t-bar minus theta q-bar for the efficient type and the reverse for the inefficient type; inequalities (2.11) and (2.12), the participation constraints with a zero status quo; Definition 2.2, a menu is incentive feasible when (2.9) to (2.12) hold; footnote 8 on weak preference. Section 2.5 (Information Rents), (2.18), the efficient type's payoff from mimicking equals the inefficient type's rent plus Delta theta times the inefficient type's output. Sections 2.2.1 and 2.2.2 set the linear cost, the two types and the verifiable quantity and transfer. https://publications.ut-capitole.fr/id/eprint/14941/1/Laffont_14941.pdf",
        "Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences. Mechanism design theory. Scientific background on the Sveriges Riksbank Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel 2007. 2007 (full text read). Section 2.1 (Incentive compatibility and the revelation principle) states Hurwicz's definition of an incentive-compatible direct mechanism, adds a participation constraint that no agent be made worse off by participating, and states that by the revelation principle much of the literature seeks a direct mechanism that maximises the goal function subject to the incentive-compatibility constraint and, where appropriate, the participation constraint, the problem the article displays. https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/advanced-economicsciences2007.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Checking participation only in a screening menu. A menu that pays each type its cost, such as 120 procedures for GBP 480,000 and 100 for GBP 500,000, passes both participation constraints, yet the low-cost type takes the second contract and earns GBP 100,000 (computed here). The purchaser then buys 100 procedures for GBP 500,000 from a supplier whose cost is GBP 400,000, so a menu is checked against all four constraints.",
        "Mimicking payoff evaluated at the wrong type's cost. The incentive constraint of the low-cost type values the high-cost contract at the low cost, t_H minus theta_L q_H. Using theta_H there turns IC_L into U_L minus U_H, which is 0 for the first-best menu of HE-EX-MECH-002, so a menu that fails by 100 (GBP 100,000) is passed as feasible (computed here).",
        "Strict inequality used for a binding screening constraint. The purchaser's optimal menu leaves the low-cost type exactly indifferent, IC_L = 0 in HE-EX-MECH-001. Testing IC_L > 0 in place of IC_L >= 0 rejects the second-best menu, and with costs typed as 4.0 and 4.6 a binding slack can carry a floating-point residue (about 6E-14 in a Python check of the second-best menu for those costs), which the ROUND in HE-IM-MECH-001 removes." ;
    hemo:9000014 "U_L = t_L - theta_L * q_L; U_H = t_H - theta_H * q_H; IC_L = U_L - (t_H - theta_L * q_H); IC_H = U_H - (t_L - theta_H * q_L); feasible = (IC_L >= 0) and (IC_H >= 0) and (U_L >= 0) and (U_H >= 0)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-MECH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "incentive-and-participation-slacks-of-a-two-type-procurement-menu" .

hemo:5000937 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Incentive bonus and limited liability rent for unobserved effort paid on an imperfect indicator"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-PAP-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005228,
        hemo:6005229,
        hemo:6005230,
        hemo:6005231,
        hemo:6005232,
        hemo:6005233,
        hemo:6005234 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005228,
        hemo:6005229,
        hemo:6005230,
        hemo:6005231 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005232,
        hemo:6005233,
        hemo:6005234 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the extra cost to an agent of a high effort level, the probabilities that a verifiable indicator is met with and without that effort, and the most the agent can be made to pay the principal, to the smallest bonus per indicator met that makes the agent choose the high effort, the principal's expected payment and the agent's expected rent. This is the hidden action, or moral hazard, form of the principal-agent problem with a risk-neutral agent protected by limited liability, as in chapter 4 of Laffont and Martimort. The mechanism-design records HE-FN-MECH-001, HE-FM-MECH-001, HE-FM-MECH-002 and HE-CF-MECH-001 treat hidden information instead, a supplier's cost type known only to itself and screened with a menu of contracts, and the Ellis and McGuire records HE-FN-CTH-001 to HE-CF-CTH-001 set a mixed payment on the case and its recorded cost, both verifiable, with no payment on an indicator of unobserved effort. Demand-side moral hazard in insurance is HE-FM-MH-001 and HE-FM-MHHI-002. The expected payment is a two-branch expected value of the form of HE-FM-CHN-001, and the rent takes the form of the margin of HE-FM-CTH-001 with no reimbursed share. Whether the principal should buy the effort at all compares the gain in its expected benefit with the expected payment, condition (4.23) of Laffont and Martimort when ell = 0, a single comparison that needs no separate formula (HE-ER-PAP-002). Multitasking and gaming are described in the article without an equation. Notation follows the Principal-Agent Problem article, with the liability limit ell taken from Laffont and Martimort." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Incentive bonus and limited liability rent for unobserved effort paid on an imperfect indicator" ;
    hemo:9000014 "h(c, p_H, p_L, ell) = (b_star, E_pay, R)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-PAP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "incentive-bonus-and-limited-liability-rent-for-unobserved-effort-paid-on-an-imperfect-indicator" .

hemo:5000943 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Non-inferiority and superiority decision function for an active control trial"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-ACTR-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005288,
        hemo:6005289,
        hemo:6005290,
        hemo:6005291,
        hemo:6005292,
        hemo:6005293,
        hemo:6005294,
        hemo:6005295,
        hemo:6005296,
        hemo:6005297 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005288,
        hemo:6005289,
        hemo:6005290,
        hemo:6005291,
        hemo:6005292,
        hemo:6005293 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005294,
        hemo:6005295,
        hemo:6005296,
        hemo:6005297 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the proportions of patients with a favourable outcome in the test and active control arms of a two-arm randomised trial, the arm sizes, a normal critical value and a margin fixed in the protocol to the two-sided interval for the risk difference and to the readings for non-inferiority and superiority. The steps before the decision are live records. The risk difference d is the test proportion minus the control proportion, the difference of HE-FM-ARR-001 with its sign reversed, because HE-FM-ARR-001 subtracts the intervention risk of an adverse event from the comparator risk and HE-AS-ARR-002 reverses the subtraction for a beneficial outcome. Each arm's standard error is HE-FM-BINOM-004, the variance of a difference of two independent arm estimates is the sum of their variances as in HE-VR-AN-002 and HE-FM-NMA-001, and the interval limits are the Wald limits of HE-FM-AN-001. HE-FM-ACTR-001 adds the comparison of the lower limit with minus the margin and with zero. The article's economic check is a citation of live records, the incremental net health benefit being HE-FM-CET-002, its break-even shortfall in cures being HE-FM-CET-002 solved for the shortfall under the article's illustrative linear cost and QALY model (compare the break-even price of HE-FM-BEA-001), and the probability of a positive net health benefit following the z statistic of HE-FM-AN-004. Bioequivalence on the ratio scale is HE-FM-ANDA-001, and the least-cost choice once outcomes are shown to be equivalent is HE-FM-CMA-001. Notation follows the Active Control Trial article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Non-inferiority and superiority decision function for an active control trial" ;
    hemo:9000014 "ni(p_T, p_C, n_T, n_C, z, M) = (L_d, U_d, NI, SUP)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-ACTR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "non-inferiority-and-superiority-decision-function-for-an-active-control-trial" .

hemo:5000944 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Sullivan active life expectancy from a period life table and dependency prevalence"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-ALE-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005298,
        hemo:6005299,
        hemo:6005300,
        hemo:6005301,
        hemo:6005302,
        hemo:6005303,
        hemo:6005304 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005298,
        hemo:6005299,
        hemo:6005300 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005301,
        hemo:6005302,
        hemo:6005303,
        hemo:6005304 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the survivors of a period life table at a starting age, the person-years it assigns to each later age band and the proportion of each band who are dependent in activities of daily living to active life expectancy, life expectancy and the expected years lived dependent at that age. Sullivan's method splits the person-years of each band by the dependency prevalence (HE-FM-ALE-001), and the Computational Function HE-CF-ALE-001 adds the standard binomial variance and a 95% interval for any number of bands. The sum takes the form of HE-FM-ASF-002 with r of 0 and of HE-FM-QALY-001, but both records are defined for utility values, while the weight here is the share of survivors free of dependency, with dependent years counted as zero. HALE weights non-healthy time by severity and draws on years lived with disability (HE-FM-DWT-001), and the years of life lost and DALY records (HE-FM-YLL-001, HE-FN-DALY-001) measure health gaps, not health expectancies. Expected care costs per person are HE-FM-COST-001 with the expected dependent years as the quantity and a cost per dependent year as the unit cost. A multistate life table follows transitions between independent, dependent and dead states; with constant rates and no recovery the expected time in each living state is HE-FM-CONT-002, and HE-FM-MKCH-002 splits expected time in a state that can be re-entered into episodes, so those records are cited, not restated. Notation follows the Active Life Expectancy article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Sullivan active life expectancy from a period life table and dependency prevalence" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a(l_x, L_i, pi_i) = (ALE_x, e_x, DLE_x, s_A)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-ALE-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "sullivan-active-life-expectancy-from-a-period-life-table-and-dependency-prevalence" .

hemo:5000945 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Smallest effort bonus, expected payment and limited liability rent for a binary effort choice"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-PAP-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000937 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006454 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005305,
        hemo:6005306,
        hemo:6005307,
        hemo:6005308,
        hemo:6005309,
        hemo:6005310,
        hemo:6005311,
        hemo:6005312 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005306,
        hemo:6005307,
        hemo:6005308,
        hemo:6005309 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005312 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006450,
        hemo:7006451,
        hemo:7006452,
        hemo:7006453 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006455,
        hemo:7006456,
        hemo:7006457,
        hemo:7006458,
        hemo:7006459,
        hemo:7006460,
        hemo:7006461,
        hemo:7006462,
        hemo:7006463 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "A risk-neutral agent, such as a general practice, chooses a high effort that costs it c per patient or a low effort that costs nothing, and is paid a bonus for each patient whose recorded indicator is met, which happens with probability p_H after high effort and p_L otherwise. The smallest bonus that satisfies the incentive constraint, p_H b minus c at least p_L b, is b_star = c / (p_H minus p_L), the gap between the transfers (4.20) and (4.19) of Laffont and Martimort, and the principal also charges a fixed fee F per patient, the smaller of the liability limit ell and p_L b_star, which combines the two cases of their Proposition 4.2 (derived). The expected payment, the two-branch expected value of HE-FM-CHN-001 with branches b_star minus F and minus F, is the second-best cost of inducing effort, and the rent R is that payment less c, the form of the HE-FM-CTH-001 margin with no reimbursed share. With ell = 0 the rent is c p_L / (p_H minus p_L), which equals what the agent would earn without the effort and, for p_L above 0, c / (p_H / p_L minus 1) (derived), the limited liability rent (4.21) that the authors describe as the extra payment caused by moral hazard combined with limited liability. At ell equal to p_L b_star the rent (4.21) of the second case of their Proposition 4.2 falls to zero, and for a larger ell the first case applies, with no rent." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Smallest effort bonus, expected payment and limited liability rent for a binary effort choice" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Laffont JJ, Martimort D. The Theory of Incentives I. The Principal-Agent Model. Authors' manuscript dated 6 February 2001, deposited in Toulouse Capitole Publications, record 14941 (full text read). Published as The Theory of Incentives. The Principal-Agent Model. Princeton University Press; 2002. doi:10.1515/9781400829453 (not seen), so the section, equation and page numbers below are the manuscript's. Chapter 4, on moral hazard. Section 4.2.2 (Incentive Feasible Contracts), pages 151 and 152, writes the incentive constraint (4.3) as a weak inequality with the participation constraint (4.4) and Definition 4.1. Section 4.3 (Risk Neutrality and First-Best Implementation), pages 154 and 155, gives the transfers (4.12) and (4.13) with an expected payment equal to the disutility of effort psi, and Remark 2 notes that they make the incentive constraint exactly binding. Section 4.4 (The Trade-Off between Limited Liability Rent Extraction and Efficiency), pages 156 to 159, adds the limited liability constraints (4.17) and (4.18), and Proposition 4.2 gives, for a liability limit l at most pi_0 psi / Delta pi, the transfers (4.19) t SB = minus l and (4.20) t-bar SB = minus l plus psi / Delta pi and the expected limited liability rent (4.21) EU SB = minus l plus pi_0 psi / Delta pi, with no rent for a larger l. The text calls this rent the additional payment the principal incurs because of moral hazard combined with limited liability, and with l = 0 gives (4.22), the condition (4.23) for inducing effort, the second-best cost C SB = pi_1 psi / Delta pi, Figure 4.3 and Proposition 4.3. The records map c to psi, p_H to pi_1, p_L to pi_0 and ell to l. https://publications.ut-capitole.fr/id/eprint/14941/1/Laffont_14941.pdf",
        "Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, Committee for the Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel. Oliver Hart and Bengt Holmström: contract theory. Scientific background on the Sveriges Riksbank Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel 2016. 10 October 2016 (full text read). Section 2.1 (Optimal Incentive Contracts), page 5, notes that payments between principal and agent are constrained by their financial resources, a constraint first set aside. On page 6, in the paragraph on hidden action and the classic moral-hazard model, a risk-neutral agent with sufficient financial resources is given a franchise contract, paying a fixed fee and receiving all of the realised benefit, so that it chooses the first-best action, and footnote 11 adds that managerial wealth constraints may be binding. The records follow this in letting a fee up to ell remove the rent (HE-EX-PAP-003). https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/advanced-economicsciences2016.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Bonus set so that the expected payment just covers the cost. Setting the bonus to c / p_H, so that a practice making the review is paid its cost on average, ignores that the indicator is also met without the review. With the weak indicator of HE-EX-PAP-001 the bonus is 42.86 pounds, the practice earns nothing above cost from the thorough review and 19.29 pounds per patient without it, so it skips the review and the purchaser pays 19.29 pounds per patient for indicators met by chance (derived).",
        "Cutting the bonus below the smallest bonus to save the rent. The rent cannot be reduced by paying less than b_star when no fee can be charged. With the weak indicator a bonus of 100 pounds raises the practice's expected bonus by 25 pounds for a review costing 30, so it skips the review and the purchaser pays 45 pounds per patient for indicators met by chance and buys no reviews (derived). Only a fee or an indicator that separates the two efforts better lowers the rent.",
        "Judging whether to buy the effort against its cost alone. For an agent that cannot be charged a fee, condition (4.23) of Laffont and Martimort induces effort only when the gain in expected benefit is at least the second-best cost, E_pay here, which exceeds the first-best cost c by the rent, so with limited liability some effort worth more than its cost is not worth buying (their Figure 4.3 and Proposition 4.3). With the weak indicator of HE-EX-PAP-001 a review worth at least 30 and less than 84 pounds per patient to the purchaser would be bought if effort were observed but not through the bonus; with the sharper indicator of HE-EX-PAP-002 that band narrows to at least 30 and less than about 38.57 pounds (derived).",
        "Probabilities typed as percentages. Entering p_H and p_L as 70 and 45 makes the difference 25 and the bonus 1.20 pounds. The practice's real chances are still 0.70 and 0.45, so the review raises its expected bonus by 0.30 pounds against a cost of 30 pounds, it skips the review, and the purchaser pays 0.54 pounds per patient for indicators met by chance (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "b_star = c / (p_H - p_L); F = min(ell, p_L * b_star); E_pay = p_H * b_star - F; R = E_pay - c" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-PAP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "smallest-effort-bonus-expected-payment-and-limited-liability-rent-for-a-binary-effort-choice" .

hemo:5000948 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Symmetric Nash equilibrium use of a shared resource by N users and the share of its rent dissipated"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-TOTC-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000939 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006489 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005344,
        hemo:6005345,
        hemo:6005346,
        hemo:6005347,
        hemo:6005348,
        hemo:6005349,
        hemo:6005350,
        hemo:6005351,
        hemo:6005352,
        hemo:6005353,
        hemo:6005354 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005345,
        hemo:6005346,
        hemo:6005347,
        hemo:6005348 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005354 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006486,
        hemo:7006487,
        hemo:7006488 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006490,
        hemo:7006491,
        hemo:7006492,
        hemo:7006493,
        hemo:7006494,
        hemo:7006495,
        hemo:7006496,
        hemo:7006497 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each of N identical users chooses q_i to maximise q_i (a less c less k Q), where Q is the use of all users together. Setting the derivative to zero gives a less c less k Q less k q_i equal to zero, and with identical users the unique equilibrium gives each user (a less c) / (k (N + 1)), the result Falk, Fehr and Fischbacher state as Proposition 1. The chain then gives total use, the net value of each unit at that use and the rent V_E, the net value to the group. Q_s is the joint optimum, the efficient quantity of HE-FM-PIGT-001 with b and g equal to k and m equal to zero (also the equilibrium at N = 1), V_s the rent it yields, (a less c) squared / (4k), which equals the open-access deadweight loss of HE-FM-PIGT-002 with the same b, g and m (derived), and d_E the share of V_s lost at the equilibrium, which equals ((N less 1) / (N + 1)) squared (derived, checked on a grid). Use rises and the rent falls as users are added, and the rent tends to zero as N grows, the open-access outcome." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Symmetric Nash equilibrium use of a shared resource by N users and the share of its rent dissipated" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Delaney J, Jacobson S. Payments or persuasion: common pool resource management with price and non-price measures. Working paper, March 2015 (read in full); published as Environmental and Resource Economics. 2016;65(4):747-772. doi:10.1007/s10640-015-9923-z (published version not seen). Section 2.1, A common pool resource appropriation game, sets each agent's payoff as alpha times unused tokens plus (beta less gamma times total extraction) times own extraction, states that it is mathematically identical to the payoff of Ostrom and colleagues (1994), and gives the symmetric Nash equilibrium (beta less alpha) / ((n + 1) gamma) and social optimum (beta less alpha) / (2 n gamma) per agent, equal only for n = 1 or beta at or below alpha. The Reviewers' Appendix derives both from the first-order conditions. https://web.williams.edu/Economics/wp/DelaneyJacobson_PaymentsOrPersuasion.pdf",
        "Falk A, Fehr E, Fischbacher U. Appropriating the Commons: A Theoretical Explanation. Chapter 5 in National Research Council. The Drama of the Commons. Washington, DC, The National Academies Press; 2002. doi:10.17226/10287 (chapter read in the publisher's online edition). The section The Standard Common-Pool Resource Game (pages 164 and 165) states Proposition 1 (Selfish Nash Equilibrium), that with purely selfish preferences the unique Nash equilibrium is symmetric with individual appropriation a / (b (n + 1)), for payoffs written e plus a x_i less b x_i times total appropriation after the unit cost is netted out, and gives 8 per player, a total of 64 and a social optimum of 36 for eight players with the Walker and colleagues revenue 23 times total tokens less 0.25 times its square and a cost of 5. The Appendix to Chapter 5 (pages 185 and 186) derives the best reply, the total n a / (b (n + 1)) and the social optimum a / (2b). The records write their a as a less c. https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/10287/chapter/7",
        "Walker JM, Ostrom E, Gardner R. Rent-dissipation in common-pool resource environments: experimental evidence. Workshop in Political Theory and Policy Analysis working paper W88-7, Indiana University, September 1988 (read in full in the Digital Library of the Commons). Its introduction traces the rent dissipation hypothesis to Gordon (1954). Section III, Theoretical Predictions, contrasts group optimality (maximum rents, where marginal revenue product equals marginal cost), the Nash equilibrium and complete rent dissipation (where average revenue product equals marginal cost), and prints the payoff of eight players with a revenue of 25 times total tokens less 0.3 times its square and an outside return of 5. Its pure-strategy Nash equilibria total 59 tokens and earn approximately 41% of the rents possible, against 33 tokens for maximum rents and 67 for zero rents. The continuous equilibrium of HE-FM-TOTC-001 gives 59.3 tokens and keeps 39.5% (derived). https://dlc.dlib.indiana.edu/dlc/items/a2bc4d30-01b6-4646-b673-4c405465dbac" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Dropping each user's own share of the loss from the first-order condition. A user who takes the value of a course as fixed sets a less c less k Q to zero, the average-return behaviour of the rent dissipation literature, and the total becomes (a less c) / k for every N, the open-access limit Q_0 of HE-FM-PIGT-001 with b equal to k. In the article's example it gives 8,000 courses and a rent of zero for four prescribers instead of 6,400 courses and 102,400 pounds (derived). The full open-access loss holds only as N grows.",
        "Measuring dissipation on gross yield rather than net value. Delaney and Jacobson define gross efficiency on the yield of the resource, which leaves out the opportunity cost of use, and note that it equals net efficiency in their experiment because the outside return is set to zero. With the article's values the gross yield is 240,000 pounds at the optimum and 230,400 pounds at the equilibrium, a loss of 4%, while the net rent falls by 36% (derived). The share d_E uses net values, as the article does.",
        "Reading every rise in antibiotic use as a loss to society. The formula counts only the loss of effectiveness from use and no benefit to anyone but the patient. Where treatment stops transmission, use protects others, and Porco and colleagues found that more antibiotic use benefits society in some circumstances, so the predicted overuse and dissipated share hold only when the external effect of use is a cost, as assumed in HE-AS-TOTC-003." ;
    hemo:9000014 "q_E = (a - c) / (k * (N + 1)); Q_E = N * q_E; v_E = a - c - k * Q_E; V_E = Q_E * v_E; Q_s = (a - c) / (2 * k); V_s = Q_s * (a - c - k * Q_s); d_E = 1 - V_E / V_s" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-TOTC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "symmetric-nash-equilibrium-use-of-a-shared-resource-by-n-users-and-the-share-of-its-rent-dissipated" .

hemo:5000949 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Per-unit fee that brings N users of a shared resource to the joint optimum, with use and revenue under the fee"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-TOTC-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000939 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006501 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005355,
        hemo:6005356,
        hemo:6005357,
        hemo:6005358,
        hemo:6005359,
        hemo:6005360,
        hemo:6005361,
        hemo:6005362 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005356,
        hemo:6005357,
        hemo:6005358,
        hemo:6005359 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005362 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006498,
        hemo:7006499,
        hemo:7006500 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006502,
        hemo:7006503,
        hemo:7006504,
        hemo:7006505,
        hemo:7006506,
        hemo:7006507,
        hemo:7006508 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "A fee t_s on each unit raises every user's cost to c plus t_s, so use under the fee is the equilibrium of HE-FM-TOTC-001 with that cost. Setting the resulting total equal to the joint optimum (a less c) / (2k) gives t_s, the optimal Pigouvian amount Delaney and Jacobson state for their common pool resource game. It equals the loss one more unit imposes on the other N less 1 users at the optimum, k (N less 1) Q_s / N, the marginal external cost of HE-FM-EXT-001 valued at the efficient quantity as in HE-FN-PIGT-001 (derived). The user already bears their own share, so the fee is below the whole marginal external cost k Q_s, the t_star of HE-FM-PIGT-001 with b and g equal to k and m equal to zero, and tends to it, (a less c) / 2, as N grows (derived). Revenue R_t is paid by the users to the authority as a transfer and does not reduce the group's net value. A quota of Q_s / N units per user reaches the same use." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Per-unit fee that brings N users of a shared resource to the joint optimum, with use and revenue under the fee" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Delaney J, Jacobson S. Payments or persuasion: common pool resource management with price and non-price measures. Working paper, March 2015 (read in full); published as Environmental and Resource Economics. 2016;65(4):747-772. doi:10.1007/s10640-015-9923-z (published version not seen). Section 2.1 states the optimal (Pigouvian) subsidy delta = (beta less alpha)(n less 1) / (2n), and section 2.2 adds delta per token to the return of the outside option and cites Pigou (1920) for the optimal subsidy as the difference between marginal private and marginal social cost at the optimal quantity. The Reviewers' Appendix gives the equilibrium with the subsidy, (beta less alpha less delta) / ((n + 1) gamma), and finds delta by setting it equal to the social optimum. Section 3 sets beta of 0.36, alpha of zero, gamma of 0.01 and n of 3, and section 3.1 sets delta at 0.12 dollars, with a symmetric Nash equilibrium of 6 tokens that yields the social optimum, and footnote 8 treats the subsidy as a transfer. https://web.williams.edu/Economics/wp/DelaneyJacobson_PaymentsOrPersuasion.pdf",
        "Falk A, Fehr E, Fischbacher U. Appropriating the Commons: A Theoretical Explanation. Chapter 5 in National Research Council. The Drama of the Commons. Washington, DC, The National Academies Press; 2002. doi:10.17226/10287 (chapter read in the publisher's online edition). The Appendix to Chapter 5 (page 186) maximises the sum of the players' payoffs and gives the social optimum of total appropriation a / (2b), the target the fee reaches, and the section The Standard Common-Pool Resource Game (pages 164 and 165) gives 36 for the Walker and colleagues parameters and states that selfish subjects ignore the negative externality they impose on the other players. https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/10287/chapter/7" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Charging the whole marginal external cost when users are few. Setting the fee at k Q_s, the t_star of HE-FM-PIGT-001 with b and g equal to k and m equal to zero, ignores the share of the loss each user already bears. In the article's example a fee of 40 pounds for four prescribers cuts use to 800 courses each, 3,200 in all, below the optimum, and the rent to 153,600 pounds instead of 160,000 (derived). The whole external cost is the right fee only as N grows.",
        "Counting the fee revenue as a loss of value. The 120,000 pounds raised in the article's example is paid by the prescribers to the authority, and the group's net value at the optimum is still 160,000 pounds. Subtracting the revenue would show 40,000 pounds and make the fee look worse than leaving the four prescribers alone at 102,400 pounds. Delaney and Jacobson likewise treat their subsidy as a transfer with no efficiency cost in itself, apart from the cost of raising the funds.",
        "Setting the fee at the external cost observed before the fee. Valuing the loss on the other users at the untaxed equilibrium, k (N less 1) q_E, gives 48 pounds per course in the article's example, the external loss of its step 3. Each prescriber then chooses 640 courses, 2,560 in all, and the rent is 139,264 pounds, below the 160,000 pounds of the optimum (derived). The fee is the external cost at the optimum, as HE-FN-PIGT-001 sets it and as HE-FM-PIGT-003 shows for a tax set at the pre-tax external cost." ;
    hemo:9000014 "t_s = (a - c) * (N - 1) / (2 * N); q_t = (a - c - t_s) / (k * (N + 1)); Q_t = N * q_t; R_t = t_s * Q_t" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-TOTC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "per-unit-fee-that-brings-n-users-of-a-shared-resource-to-the-joint-optimum-with-use-and-revenue-under-the-fee" .

hemo:5000952 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Shared savings payment of an accountable care organisation with the minimum savings rate gate and payment limit"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ACO-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000941 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006533 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005376,
        hemo:6005377,
        hemo:6005378,
        hemo:6005379,
        hemo:6005380,
        hemo:6005381,
        hemo:6005382,
        hemo:6005383,
        hemo:6005384,
        hemo:6005385,
        hemo:6005386 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005377,
        hemo:6005378,
        hemo:6005379,
        hemo:6005381,
        hemo:6005382,
        hemo:6005383,
        hemo:6005385 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005386 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006530,
        hemo:7006531,
        hemo:7006532 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006534,
        hemo:7006535,
        hemo:7006536,
        hemo:7006537,
        hemo:7006538,
        hemo:7006539,
        hemo:7006540 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Computes the payment CMS makes to an accountable care organisation for a performance year in which its assigned beneficiaries' average per capita spending E falls below the updated benchmark B. The savings ratio g_S must reach the minimum savings rate MSR, and once it does the final sharing rate, s times the quality factor q, applies to all the savings on a first dollar basis, as 42 CFR 425.605(d) and 425.610(e) state. The payment may not exceed the performance payment limit PPL, a share c_P of total benchmark expenditure, 10% in the BASIC track and 20% in ENHANCED; that last step is the cap form of HE-FM-ASL-001 with no attachment point. Because the gate is a test and not a deductible, the payment jumps from zero to s q MSR N B at the MSR when the cap does not bind (derived), USD 2.4 million for the article's Level E ACO. A low revenue BASIC ACO that beats its benchmark but not its MSR is paid under 425.605(h) at half the sharing rate, entered as s halved with MSR set to 0." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Shared savings payment of an accountable care organisation with the minimum savings rate gate and payment limit" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Code of Federal Regulations, Title 42, section 425.512, Determining the ACO quality performance standard for performance years beginning on or after January 1, 2021. eCFR text up to date as of 15 October 2026. Paragraph (a)(5) sets for performance years from 2024 the standard of a quality score at or above the 40th percentile of MIPS Quality performance category scores and, in (a)(5)(ii), an alternative standard of a score at or above the 10th percentile of the performance benchmark on at least one outcome measure of the APP Plus set from 2025. Paragraph (b)(3) states that for 2025 the quality score with bonus points may not exceed 100 percent. https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/section-425.512",
        "Code of Federal Regulations, Title 42, section 425.605, Calculation of shared savings and losses under the BASIC track. eCFR text up to date as of 15 October 2026, last amended 5 November 2025 (90 FR 50018). Paragraph (a) compares per capita Parts A and B spending with the updated benchmark and requires it to be below by at least the minimum savings rate. Paragraph (b) sets the MSR options, a sliding scale for one-sided levels and a zero, 0.5 to 2.0% or size-based MSR/MLR for two-sided levels. Paragraph (d)(1)(i) to (v), sub-paragraph (A)(4), gives sharing rates of 40% for Levels A and B and 50% for Levels C to E from 2024, multiplied by the quality score for the alternative standard; sub-paragraph (B) applies the rate on a first dollar basis once the MSR is met and limits shared savings to 10% of the updated benchmark. Paragraph (h) allows a low revenue ACO below its benchmark to be paid at one half of the applicable rate, and (i)(1) computes the limit on total benchmark expenditure as per capita benchmark times assigned beneficiary person years. Section 425.20 (same eCFR date) defines a low revenue ACO as one whose participants' Parts A and B fee-for-service revenue is less than 35 percent of its assigned beneficiaries' Parts A and B fee-for-service expenditure. https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/section-425.605",
        "Code of Federal Regulations, Title 42, section 425.610, Calculation of shared savings and losses under the ENHANCED track. eCFR text up to date as of 15 October 2026. Paragraph (b)(2) requires spending below the benchmark by at least the MSR; (d)(4) sets the sharing rate at 75% for performance years from 2024, multiplied by the quality score for an ACO meeting the alternative quality performance standard; (e)(1) applies it on a first dollar basis and (e)(2) caps shared savings at 20% of the updated benchmark; (l)(2) computes the limit from per capita benchmark times assigned beneficiary person years. https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/section-425.610",
        "Social Security Act, section 1899 (42 U.S.C. 1395jjj), Shared savings program, as amended, text read on the Cornell Legal Information Institute page on 18 October 2026. Subsection (d)(1)(B)(i) requires spending to be at least a percent specified by the Secretary below the benchmark, set to account for normal variation in expenditures and based on the number of assigned beneficiaries. Subsection (d)(2) allows a percent of the difference between spending and the benchmark to be paid to the ACO as shared savings, subject to the quality performance standards, leaves the remainder with Medicare and requires limits on the total amount of shared savings. https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/1395jjj" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Applying the performance payment limit to savings before sharing. The limit caps the amount of shared savings the ACO receives, not the savings that are shared (42 CFR 425.610(e)(2), 425.605(d)(1)(v)(B)(2)). In the ENHANCED example, capping savings at 20% of the benchmark and then taking three quarters gives USD 36 million instead of the USD 48 million limit that binds on the payment.",
        "Multiplying by the quality score when the quality standard is met. The quality score scales the sharing rate only for an ACO that meets the alternative quality performance standard; an ACO that meets the standard itself is paid the full rate. Applying a 70% score in the first worked example would pay USD 2.52 million instead of USD 3.6 million.",
        "Treating the minimum savings rate as a deductible. Once the MSR is met the sharing rate applies to all savings on a first dollar basis (42 CFR 425.605(d)(1)(v)(B)(1)). Sharing only the savings above the MSR pays 0.5 times 120 per beneficiary in the first worked example, USD 1.2 million instead of USD 3.6 million, two thirds too little, and removes the jump at the MSR." ;
    hemo:9000014 "g_S = (B - E) / B; S_raw = s * q * N * (B - E); PPL = c_P * N * B; P = (g_S >= MSR) * min(S_raw, PPL)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ACO-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "shared-savings-payment-of-an-accountable-care-organisation-with-the-minimum-savings-rate-gate-and-payment-limit" .

hemo:5000953 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Shared losses of a two-sided BASIC accountable care organisation with the minimum loss rate and loss recoupment limit"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ACO-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000941 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006543 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005387,
        hemo:6005388,
        hemo:6005389,
        hemo:6005390,
        hemo:6005391,
        hemo:6005392,
        hemo:6005393,
        hemo:6005394,
        hemo:6005395,
        hemo:6005396,
        hemo:6005397,
        hemo:6005398 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005388,
        hemo:6005389,
        hemo:6005390,
        hemo:6005392,
        hemo:6005393,
        hemo:6005395,
        hemo:6005396,
        hemo:6005397 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005398 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006541,
        hemo:7006542 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006544,
        hemo:7006545,
        hemo:7006546,
        hemo:7006547,
        hemo:7006548,
        hemo:7006549,
        hemo:7006550 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Computes what an ACO in BASIC Level C, D or E repays CMS when its per capita spending E exceeds the updated benchmark B. The loss ratio g_L must reach the minimum loss rate MLR, which mirrors the MSR, and the fixed 30% shared loss rate r_L then applies to the whole excess, the loss counterpart of HE-FM-ACO-001. The amount may not exceed the loss recoupment limit LRL, a share rho_R of the ACO participants' Medicare fee-for-service revenue, never more than a share rho_B of total benchmark expenditure, as 42 CFR 425.605(d)(1)(iii) to (v) and (i)(2) set out. The final min step is the cap form of HE-FM-ASL-001 with no attachment point. With the article's case 3 figures a 3% overspend gives USD 2.16 million, the article's result, whenever the limit is at least that amount." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Shared losses of a two-sided BASIC accountable care organisation with the minimum loss rate and loss recoupment limit" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. Medicare and Medicaid Programs; CY 2023 Payment Policies Under the Physician Fee Schedule and Other Changes to Part B Payment and Coverage Policies; Medicare Shared Savings Program Requirements; Implementing Requirements for Manufacturers of Certain Single-dose Container or Single-use Package Drugs To Provide Refunds With Respect to Discarded Amounts; and COVID-19 Interim Final Rules. Final rule, CMS-1770-F. Federal Register 2022;87(222):69404-70700, 18 November 2022. The preamble section Generally Applicable Nominal Amount Standard, under Advanced APM Criteria (87 FR 70117), finalises as proposed the removal of the 2024 end date from 414.1415(c)(3)(i)(A), so that the 8 percent revenue-based nominal amount standard applies to all QP performance periods beginning on or after 1 January 2023; the amendment to 414.1415 in the same rule gives the paragraph its current wording, \"For QP Performance Periods beginning in 2023, 8 percent\". https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2022-11-18/pdf/2022-23873.pdf",
        "Code of Federal Regulations, Title 42, section 414.1415, Advanced APM criteria. eCFR text up to date as of 15 October 2026. Paragraph (c)(3)(i)(B) sets the generally applicable nominal amount standard at 3 percent of the expected expenditures for which an APM entity is responsible, so the Level E benchmark-based loss recoupment limit of 425.605(d)(1)(v)(D)(2) is 4% of the updated benchmark. Paragraph (c)(3)(i)(A) gives the revenue-based standard as 8 percent of revenue for QP performance periods beginning in 2023, the Level E revenue-based limit of 425.605(d)(1)(v)(D)(1); HE-SR-ACO-008 records that this applies to every QP performance period from 2023 on. https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/section-414.1415",
        "Code of Federal Regulations, Title 42, section 425.605, Calculation of shared savings and losses under the BASIC track. eCFR text up to date as of 15 October 2026. Paragraph (b)(2) has a two-sided ACO choose a zero, symmetrical 0.5 to 2.0% or size-based MSR/MLR, and (b)(4) makes it share losses when spending is above the benchmark by at least the MLR. Paragraphs (d)(1)(iii), (iv) and (v), sub-paragraph (C), fix the shared loss rate at 30% for Levels C, D and E. Sub-paragraph (D) limits losses to 2% of ACO participant revenue but not above 1% of the benchmark in Level C, 4% of revenue but not above 2% of the benchmark in Level D, and in Level E the percentage of 414.1415(c)(3)(i)(A) of revenue but not above one point more than the 414.1415(c)(3)(i)(B) percentage of the benchmark. Paragraph (f) reduces losses for extreme and uncontrollable circumstances after the rate and limit, and (i)(2) defines the revenue-based and benchmark-based limits. https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/section-425.605" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Treating the minimum loss rate as a deductible on losses. The 30% loss rate applies to the whole excess once the MLR is reached. Applying it only to the excess above the MLR gives 0.3 times 120 per beneficiary in the first Level D example, USD 720,000 instead of USD 2.16 million, a third of the amount owed.",
        "Using the revenue-based loss recoupment limit without its benchmark ceiling. The revenue-based limit applies only while it is below the benchmark-based limit (42 CFR 425.605(d)(1)(iii)(D)(2)). In the Level C example the revenue-based figure alone would let CMS recoup USD 4 million when the limit is USD 2.4 million, 1% of the benchmark." ;
    hemo:9000014 "g_L = (E - B) / B; L_raw = r_L * N * (E - B); LRL = min(rho_R * Rev, rho_B * N * B); SL = (g_L >= MLR) * min(L_raw, LRL)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ACO-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "shared-losses-of-a-two-sided-basic-accountable-care-organisation-with-the-minimum-loss-rate-and-loss-recoupment-limit" .

hemo:5000954 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Shared losses of an ENHANCED track accountable care organisation with the quality-based loss rate"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ACO-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000941 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006553 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005399,
        hemo:6005400,
        hemo:6005401,
        hemo:6005402,
        hemo:6005403,
        hemo:6005404,
        hemo:6005405,
        hemo:6005406 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005400,
        hemo:6005402,
        hemo:6005403,
        hemo:6005404,
        hemo:6005405 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005406 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006551,
        hemo:7006552 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006554,
        hemo:7006555,
        hemo:7006556,
        hemo:7006557,
        hemo:7006558,
        hemo:7006559 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Computes what an ACO in the ENHANCED track repays CMS when per capita spending E exceeds the benchmark B by at least the MLR. The shared loss rate r_E is 1 minus 75% of the quality score Q, held to at least 40% and at most 75%, and is 75% when the ACO meets neither quality standard, as 42 CFR 425.610(f)(4) sets for performance years from 2024. Entering Q = 0 for an ACO that meets neither standard returns the same 75%, since the ceiling binds for every score up to one third, and the floor of 40% binds for every score of 0.8 or more (derived, checked on a grid of 1,001 scores). Losses apply to the whole excess and may not exceed 15% of total benchmark expenditure under 425.610(g), the cap form of HE-FM-ASL-001 with no attachment point; there is no revenue-based limit in this track." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Shared losses of an ENHANCED track accountable care organisation with the quality-based loss rate" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Code of Federal Regulations, Title 42, section 425.610, Calculation of shared savings and losses under the ENHANCED track. eCFR text up to date as of 15 October 2026, last amended 5 November 2025 (90 FR 50019). Paragraph (b)(1) has the ACO choose a zero, symmetrical 0.5 to 2.0% or size-based MSR/MLR, and (b)(3) makes it share losses when spending is above the benchmark by at least the MLR. Paragraph (f)(4) sets, for performance years from 2024, a shared loss rate of 1 minus the product of 75 percent and the quality score, not above 75 percent and not below 40 percent, for an ACO meeting either quality standard, and 75 percent for one meeting neither. Paragraph (g) limits losses to 15 percent of the updated benchmark and (l)(2) computes the limit from per capita benchmark times assigned beneficiary person years; (i) reduces losses for extreme and uncontrollable circumstances after the rate and the limit. https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/section-425.610" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Leaving out the 40% floor on the ENHANCED loss rate. Without the floor a quality score of 90% gives a rate of 0.325 and losses of USD 2.34 million in the article's population, against the USD 2.88 million the 40% floor requires. The floor binds for every score of 0.8 or more (derived).",
        "Taking the ENHANCED loss rate as 1 minus the 75% sharing rate. The rule multiplies 75% by the quality score before subtracting from 1. Using 1 minus 0.75, or 0.25, for every ACO ignores both the quality score and the 40% floor; with a 70% score it would collect USD 1.8 million instead of USD 3.42 million." ;
    hemo:9000014 "r_E = min(max(1 - 0.75 * Q, 0.4), 0.75); g_L = (E - B) / B; SL_E = (g_L >= MLR) * min(r_E * N * (E - B), 0.15 * N * B)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ACO-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "shared-losses-of-an-enhanced-track-accountable-care-organisation-with-the-quality-based-loss-rate" .

hemo:5000957 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Non-inferiority and superiority readings of a risk difference against a margin"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ACTR-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000943 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006582 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005441,
        hemo:6005442,
        hemo:6005443,
        hemo:6005444,
        hemo:6005445,
        hemo:6005446,
        hemo:6005447,
        hemo:6005448,
        hemo:6005449,
        hemo:6005450,
        hemo:6005451,
        hemo:6005452 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005442,
        hemo:6005443,
        hemo:6005444,
        hemo:6005445,
        hemo:6005447,
        hemo:6005450 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005449,
        hemo:6005451,
        hemo:6005452 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006578,
        hemo:7006579,
        hemo:7006580,
        hemo:7006581 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006583,
        hemo:7006584,
        hemo:7006585,
        hemo:7006586,
        hemo:7006587,
        hemo:7006588,
        hemo:7006589 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Forms the difference in the proportion of patients with a favourable outcome, test minus active control, and its two-sided Wald interval, then reads the lower limit against minus the non-inferiority margin M and against zero. The chained steps are live records, d as HE-FM-ARR-001 with the subtraction reversed for a favourable outcome, as HE-AS-ARR-002 states, SE_d as the sum of the two arm variances of HE-FM-BINOM-004 (the rule of HE-VR-AN-002 and HE-FM-NMA-001), and L_d and U_d as HE-FM-AN-001. The new step is the decision, which ICH E9 section 3.3.2 and CPMP/EWP/482/99 section II.3 state as the interval lying wholly above minus the margin, and which the FDA guidance writes as the upper bound for control minus test lying below M. NI and SUP equal 1 when the reading is shown and 0 otherwise, so a trial can show non-inferiority without superiority, and when both are shown the superiority claim is allowed by CPMP/EWP/482/99 section IV.1." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Non-inferiority and superiority readings of a risk difference against a margin" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Committee for Medicinal Products for Human Use. Guideline on the choice of the non-inferiority margin (EMEA/CPMP/EWP/2158/99). London, EMEA; adopted July 2005, in effect January 2006. Section 2 (General Considerations) asks for the choice of margin to be justified in the study protocol and states that defining the margin as a proportion of the difference between active comparator and placebo is not appropriate. Section 3 (Demonstrating Efficacy) asks for a conservative approach to the margin when constancy of effect cannot be assured. https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/guideline-choice-non-inferiority-margin_en.pdf",
        "Committee for Proprietary Medicinal Products. Points to consider on switching between superiority and non-inferiority (CPMP/EWP/482/99). London, EMEA; 27 July 2000. Section II.1 treats a two-sided 95% interval for the difference that excludes zero as usually equivalent to a significant difference at the 5% level and takes values to the right of zero as better on the new treatment. Section II.3 states that for non-inferiority the two-sided 95% interval should lie entirely to the right of minus the margin. Section II.4 assumes two-sided 95% intervals for all clinical trials, one-sided intervals at 97.5% and 90% intervals for bioequivalence. Section III requires the margin to be set down in the protocol, and section IV.1 accepts superiority when the interval also lies above zero. https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/points-consider-switching-between-superiority-and-non-inferiority_en.pdf",
        "International Conference on Harmonisation. ICH E10 Choice of control group and related issues in clinical trials. Step 4 guideline, 20 July 2000. Section 1.5.1.1 (Historical Evidence of Sensitivity to Drugs Effects and Choosing the Non-inferiority Margin) states that the margin cannot be greater than the smallest effect size the active drug would reliably be expected to have compared with placebo in the setting of the planned trial. Section 1.5 (Assay Sensitivity) states that a successful non-inferiority trial generally does not contain direct evidence of assay sensitivity. https://database.ich.org/sites/default/files/E10_Guideline.pdf",
        "International Conference on Harmonisation. ICH E9 Statistical principles for clinical trials. Step 4 guideline, 5 February 1998. Section 3.3.2 (Trials to Show Equivalence or Non-inferiority) states that only the lower margin is needed for the active control non-inferiority trial, that a one-sided interval should be used, that the interval approach has a one-sided test counterpart for the treatment difference (investigational product minus control) against the lower margin, and that concluding non-inferiority from a non-significant test of no difference is inappropriate. Section 5.5 (Estimation, Confidence Intervals and Hypothesis Testing) prefers one-sided type I errors at half the conventional two-sided level in regulatory settings. https://database.ich.org/sites/default/files/E9_Guideline.pdf",
        "Newcombe RG. Interval estimation for the difference between independent proportions: comparison of eleven methods. Statistics in Medicine. 1998;17(8):873-890. The study evaluates unconditional interval methods for the difference between binomial proportions, finds computationally simpler methods prone to aberrations and poor coverage, and reports that the methods of Mee and of Miettinen and Nurminen and a method combining Wilson score intervals for the two proportions perform well. https://doi.org/10.1002/(SICI)1097-0258(19980430)17:8%3C873::AID-SIM779%3E3.0.CO;2-I",
        "Schumi J, Wittes JT. Through the looking glass: understanding non-inferiority. Trials. 2011;12:106. The section Superiority, equivalence, and non-inferiority states that non-inferiority is judged on the lower bound of the interval for the difference against minus the margin, and that a narrow interval can show a new treatment non-inferior and inferior at once. The section Choosing the margin, conceptually describes the 95-95 method, with M1 the lower limit of the interval for the control effect against placebo and M2 a smaller margin that preserves a set fraction of it. https://doi.org/10.1186/1745-6215-12-106",
        "US Food and Drug Administration. Non-inferiority clinical trials to establish effectiveness. Guidance for industry. Silver Spring, MD, FDA; November 2016. Section III.A (The Non-Inferiority Hypothesis) states the null hypothesis C minus T at least M and establishes non-inferiority when the upper bound of the two-sided interval for C minus T is less than M; its Figure 2, result 6, shows a study that meets M1 while T is inferior to C. Section III.C states that the 95% two-sided interval corresponds to a one-sided test size of 0.025. Section IV.C.1 (The Fixed Margin Approach) defines the 95%-95% method, with M1 the lower bound of the historical interval and the trial interval used to rule out C minus T above M2, and notes 10 to 15% margins on the risk difference scale for antibiotics. https://www.fda.gov/media/78504/download" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Concluding non-inferiority from a non-significant difference. ICH E9 section 3.3.2 calls it inappropriate to conclude non-inferiority from a non-significant test of no difference. In the second worked example the interval of minus 0.0642 to plus 0.0242 contains zero, so the difference is not significant, yet with a 5-point margin the lower limit lies 1.42 points beyond minus 0.05 and non-inferiority is not shown.",
        "Reading an adverse outcome in the favourable outcome orientation. If the outcome is failure, with failure in 16% on T and 14% on C from 500 per arm, and d is entered as test minus control (plus 0.02), the lower limit is minus 0.0242 and a 5-point margin appears to be met. Entered in the orientation of HE-AS-ACTR-002, control minus test (minus 0.02), the lower limit is minus 0.0642 and non-inferiority is not shown (illustrative, derived). The reversed sign turns a failed non-inferiority result into a pass whenever M is above 0.024246 and at most 0.064246, the two unrounded lower limits (derived).",
        "Reading non-inferior as no worse than the control. NI equal to 1 rules out a loss larger than M and nothing more. In the third worked example the 10-point margin is met while the whole interval lies below zero, so T is statistically worse than C by between 0.6 and 3.4 points, a result the FDA guidance section III.A (Figure 2, result 6) and Schumi and Wittes (2011) both describe as non-inferior and inferior at once. A model should carry d and SE_d, not a zero difference.",
        "Using a 90% interval for a clinical non-inferiority trial. Two-sided 90% intervals are the norm for bioequivalence (CPMP/EWP/482/99 section II.4; HE-FM-ANDA-001), not for clinical non-inferiority, where the two-sided interval is 95%. With z of 1.645 the first worked example gives a lower limit of minus 0.0571 instead of minus 0.0642, so an illustrative 6-point margin appears to be met when the 95% interval does not meet it (derived). The 90% interval corresponds to a one-sided level of 0.05, twice the 0.025 the FDA guidance ties to the 95% interval." ;
    hemo:9000014 "d = p_T - p_C; SE_d = sqrt(p_T * (1 - p_T) / n_T + p_C * (1 - p_C) / n_C); L_d = d - z * SE_d; U_d = d + z * SE_d; NI = (L_d > -M); SUP = (L_d > 0)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ACTR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "non-inferiority-and-superiority-readings-of-a-risk-difference-against-a-margin" .

hemo:5000964 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Strict dominance with tie tolerances"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000114 obo:IAO_0000125 ;
    obo:IAO_0000115 "Gives 1 when an option costs less and is more effective than its comparator, each by more than its tie tolerance, and 0 otherwise."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "A HEMO-native record, for a formula the Functions and Formulae module does not yet hold. It is listed in module-backlog.md for the module curator."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000117 "Michelle Ashley (drafted with Claude)" ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "Drummond et al. 2015; Briggs et al. 2006" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HEMO-FM-DOMS-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005559,
        hemo:6005560,
        hemo:6005561,
        hemo:6005562,
        hemo:6005563 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005560,
        hemo:6005561,
        hemo:6005562,
        hemo:6005563 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005559 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7007325 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007365,
        hemo:7007371 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8001618 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8001607 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:1000058 hemo:5000198 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000014 "DOM_s = (Delta_C < -tau_C) and (Delta_E > tau_E)" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>DOM_s</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"logic1\">and</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">lt</csymbol><ci>Delta_C</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">unary_minus</csymbol><ci>tau_C</ci></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">gt</csymbol><ci>Delta_E</ci><ci>tau_E</ci></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{DOM}_{s} = \\Delta_{C} < -\\tau_{C} \\land \\Delta_{E} > \\tau_{E}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HEMO-FM-DOMS-001" .

