hemo:7000650 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "C_FC in the test Dutch costing manual absence shorter than the friction period"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6000791 ;
    rheo:2000001 7656.960000000000036 .

hemo:7000651 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Utility linear in cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The cost attribute enters the utility function linearly, so the marginal utility of money is constant across the cost range. Estimates outside the range of costs shown to respondents are extrapolations." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Utility linear in cost" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-WTP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "utility-linear-in-cost" .

hemo:7000652 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Coefficients on the same scale and model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "beta_k and beta_c come from the same estimated model. Scale differences between respondents cancel in the ratio, so WTP is comparable across groups when the coefficients are not." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Coefficients on the same scale and model" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-WTP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "coefficients-on-the-same-scale-and-model" .

hemo:7000653 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Marginal WTP from model coefficients"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000248 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the attribute coefficient in AttributeCoef and the cost coefficient in CostCoef, Excel returns marginal WTP, or #N/A when the cost coefficient is not negative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Marginal WTP from model coefficients" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-WTP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "marginal-wtp-from-model-coefficients" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(CostCoef<0,-AttributeCoef/CostCoef,NA())" .

hemo:7000654 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Cost coefficient has the expected sign"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Check that the estimated cost coefficient is negative. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE means respondents did not trade money against the attributes as expected, and ratios to it cannot be read as WTP." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cost coefficient has the expected sign" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-WTP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cost-coefficient-has-the-expected-sign" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CostCoef<0" .

hemo:7000655 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "WTP reproduces indifference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Add the attribute coefficient to the cost coefficient multiplied by the marginal WTP. Expected result: TRUE, because paying the WTP exactly offsets the attribute's utility." ;
    hemo:9000009 "WTP reproduces indifference" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-WTP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "wtp-reproduces-indifference" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(AttributeCoef+CostCoef*MWTP)<0.000001" .

hemo:7000656 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Individual rather than group consultation for type 2 diabetes"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In a discrete choice experiment on lifestyle programmes, individual consultation had a coefficient of 0.50 and groups of 10 patients minus 0.46, a difference of 0.96, and the cost coefficient was minus 0.75 per 100 euros, or minus 0.0075 per euro. Marginal WTP for individual consultation is 128 euros a year, as reported by Veldwijk and colleagues." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Individual rather than group consultation for type 2 diabetes" ;
    hemo:9000014 "beta_k = 0.96; beta_c = -0.0075; MWTP_k = 128" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-WTP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "individual-rather-than-group-consultation-for-type-2-diabetes" .

hemo:7000657 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Ten kilograms of anticipated weight loss"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The same study estimated a coefficient of 0.72 for an expected outcome of 10 kg weight loss. From the rounded coefficients, marginal WTP is 96 euros a year; the authors report 97 euros from unrounded estimates." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ten kilograms of anticipated weight loss" ;
    hemo:9000014 "beta_k = 0.72; beta_c = -0.0075; MWTP_k = 96" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-WTP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ten-kilograms-of-anticipated-weight-loss" .

hemo:7000658 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Representative simple random sample"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Scaling mean WTP by population size is valid when the sample is a simple random sample of the population with complete responses. Otherwise responses are weighted to the population before aggregation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Representative simple random sample" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-WTP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "representative-simple-random-sample" .

hemo:7000659 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Protest responses classified before averaging"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Follow-up questions separate protest zeros, which reject the scenario or payment vehicle, from genuine zeros. Excluding protesters assumes their values resemble those of other respondents; counting them as zeros understates mean WTP. Both results are commonly reported." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Protest responses classified before averaging" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-WTP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "protest-responses-classified-before-averaging" .

hemo:7000660 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One valuation per person and consistent units"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each w_i values the same change, paid through the same vehicle and period, such as a one-off payment through local taxation, and N_pop counts the same units as the sample, such as people or households." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One valuation per person and consistent units" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-WTP-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-valuation-per-person-and-consistent-units" .

hemo:7000661 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Mean WTP and aggregate benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000249 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With stated values of the included respondents in StatedValues and the population in Population, the first formula returns mean WTP and the second the aggregate benefit." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Mean WTP and aggregate benefit" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-WTP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "mean-wtp-and-aggregate-benefit" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AVERAGE(StatedValues); =AVERAGE(StatedValues)*Population" .

hemo:7000662 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Aggregate equals the scaled sum"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compare the aggregate benefit with the sum of stated values multiplied by the population and divided by the number of respondents. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows a median, or a count including excluded protesters, used in the aggregation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Aggregate equals the scaled sum" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-WTP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "aggregate-equals-the-scaled-sum" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(AggregateBenefit-SUM(StatedValues)*Population/COUNT(StatedValues))<0.01" .

hemo:7000663 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Payment card survey with protest zeros counted"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Ten respondents mark 0, 0, 0, 10, 20, 20, 50, 100, 100 and 200 pounds; two of the zeros are protests. Counting all ten gives mean WTP of 50 pounds and, for a population of 100,000, an aggregate benefit of 5,000,000 pounds, below the programme's present value cost of 5,500,000 pounds. The figures are illustrative, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Payment card survey with protest zeros counted" ;
    hemo:9000014 "w_i = [0,0,0,10,20,20,50,100,100,200]; n = 10; N_pop = 100000; WTP_mean = 50; B = 5000000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-WTP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "payment-card-survey-with-protest-zeros-counted" .

hemo:7000664 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Payment card survey with protest zeros excluded"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Excluding the two protest zeros leaves eight respondents, a mean of 62.5 pounds and an aggregate benefit of 6,250,000 pounds, above the cost. The sign of net benefit turns on the treatment of two respondents." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Payment card survey with protest zeros excluded" ;
    hemo:9000014 "w_i = [0,10,20,20,50,100,100,200]; n = 8; N_pop = 100000; WTP_mean = 62.5; B = 6250000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-WTP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "payment-card-survey-with-protest-zeros-excluded" .

hemo:7000665 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Frontier estimated from comparable units"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "y_max comes from a frontier estimated for units with comparable case mix, quality and technology. It is the best performance observed in the data under the model, not an absolute maximum." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Frontier estimated from comparable units" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EFF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "frontier-estimated-from-comparable-units" .

hemo:7000666 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Output measured consistently"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Outputs are defined and adjusted in the same way for every unit, so that a higher count reflects more of the same service and not easier patients or lower quality." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Output measured consistently" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EFF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "output-measured-consistently" .

hemo:7000667 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Technical efficiency score from frontier output"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000250 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With observed output in ObservedOutput and frontier output in FrontierOutput, Excel returns the score." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Technical efficiency score from frontier output" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-EFF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "technical-efficiency-score-from-frontier-output" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ObservedOutput/FrontierOutput" .

hemo:7000668 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Score no greater than 1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Check that observed output does not exceed frontier output. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows a frontier estimated without the unit, from a different output definition, or a data error." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Score no greater than 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EFF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "score-no-greater-than-1" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ObservedOutput>=0,ObservedOutput<=FrontierOutput)" .

hemo:7000669 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Outpatient clinic below its frontier"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A clinic delivers 10,000 consultations a year, and the frontier estimated from comparable clinics puts the maximum from its staff and budget at 12,500. Output-oriented technical efficiency is 0.8. The figures are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Outpatient clinic below its frontier" ;
    hemo:9000014 "y = 10000; y_max = 12500; TE_O = 0.8" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EFF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "outpatient-clinic-below-its-frontier" .

hemo:7000670 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Input prices known and common to the comparison"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Allocative and cost efficiency require input prices. Units are compared at their own prices, and differences in prices between units are not treated as inefficiency unless a price efficiency term is added." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Input prices known and common to the comparison" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EFF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "input-prices-known-and-common-to-the-comparison" .

hemo:7000671 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Input orientation and proportional reduction"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Technical efficiency here reduces all inputs in the same proportion at fixed output. The decomposition CE = TE_I multiplied by AE holds for this input-oriented measure; an output-oriented technical efficiency score is not combined with cost-based measures." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Input orientation and proportional reduction" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EFF-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "input-orientation-and-proportional-reduction" .

hemo:7000672 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Frontier-literature meaning of allocative efficiency"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "AE here is the least-cost choice of inputs at given prices. It differs from allocative efficiency across programmes, the distribution of resources among competing uses to best meet the stated objective." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Frontier-literature meaning of allocative efficiency" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EFF-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "frontier-literature-meaning-of-allocative-efficiency" .

hemo:7000673 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Three efficiency scores from three costs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000251 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With actual cost in ActualCost, technically efficient cost in TechEfficientCost and minimum cost in MinimumCost, the three formulas return cost, technical and allocative efficiency." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Three efficiency scores from three costs" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-EFF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "three-efficiency-scores-from-three-costs" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MinimumCost/ActualCost; =TechEfficientCost/ActualCost; =MinimumCost/TechEfficientCost" .

hemo:7000674 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Cost efficiency equals the product"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Multiply technical by allocative efficiency and compare with cost efficiency. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows costs valued at different prices or technical efficiency taken from an output-oriented model." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cost efficiency equals the product" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EFF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cost-efficiency-equals-the-product" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(CostEff-TechEff*AllocEff)<0.000001" .

hemo:7000675 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Costs in the required order"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Check that the minimum cost is no greater than the technically efficient cost, which is no greater than actual cost. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Costs in the required order" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EFF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "costs-in-the-required-order" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(MinimumCost<=TechEfficientCost,TechEfficientCost<=ActualCost)" .

hemo:7000676 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Hospital with technical and input-mix inefficiency"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A hospital spends 2,000,000 pounds a year. Reducing all its inputs in proportion to the frontier would cost 1,700,000 pounds, and the least-cost input mix for the same output would cost 1,530,000 pounds. Cost efficiency is 0.765, technical efficiency 0.85 and allocative efficiency 0.9, and 0.85 × 0.9 = 0.765. The figures are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Hospital with technical and input-mix inefficiency" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C_obs = 2000000; C_tec = 1700000; C_min = 1530000; CE = 0.765; TE_I = 0.85; AE = 0.9" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EFF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "hospital-with-technical-and-input-mix-inefficiency" .

hemo:7000677 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Same units, variables and orientation for both frontiers"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Both scores come from the same sample, inputs, outputs and orientation, differing only in the returns to scale assumption, so TE_CRS is no greater than TE_VRS." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same units, variables and orientation for both frontiers" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EFF-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-units-variables-and-orientation-for-both-frontiers" .

hemo:7000678 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Direction of scale inefficiency found separately"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "A scale efficiency below 1 does not show whether the unit is too small or too large. A further model under non-increasing returns to scale identifies which applies." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Direction of scale inefficiency found separately" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EFF-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "direction-of-scale-inefficiency-found-separately" .

hemo:7000679 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Scale efficiency from two DEA scores"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000252 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the constant returns score in ScoreCRS and the variable returns score in ScoreVRS, Excel returns scale efficiency." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Scale efficiency from two DEA scores" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-EFF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "scale-efficiency-from-two-dea-scores" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ScoreCRS/ScoreVRS" .

hemo:7000680 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant returns score no greater than variable returns"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Check that the constant returns score does not exceed the variable returns score. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows scores from different samples, variables or orientations." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant returns score no greater than variable returns" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EFF-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-returns-score-no-greater-than-variable-returns" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ScoreCRS<=ScoreVRS" .

hemo:7000681 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Small community hospital"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A community hospital scores 0.72 against a constant returns frontier and 0.80 against a variable returns frontier. Scale efficiency is 0.9, so most of its measured inefficiency is pure technical inefficiency. The figures are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Small community hospital" ;
    hemo:9000014 "TE_CRS = 0.72; TE_VRS = 0.80; SE = 0.9" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EFF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "small-community-hospital" .

hemo:7000682 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Marginal values at the current level of activity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each term is the effect of one more unit at the level of activity being assessed. External effects such as protection from herd immunity change as coverage rises, so the values hold only at that margin, and the comparison does not by itself identify the efficient level of activity." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Marginal values at the current level of activity" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EXT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "marginal-values-at-the-current-level-of-activity" .

hemo:7000683 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "No double counting within the stated perspective"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "An effect already counted as a private cost or benefit within the analysis is not added again as an external one. Which effects are external depends on the perspective of the decision being analysed." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No double counting within the stated perspective" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EXT-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-double-counting-within-the-stated-perspective" .

hemo:7000684 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Decision-maker bears the private terms in full"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The private comparison assumes that the person deciding pays the whole of MPC and receives the whole of MPB, and all four inputs are valued in the same currency and price year. Insurance or existing subsidies change the private terms and are entered first." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Decision-maker bears the private terms in full" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EXT-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "decision-maker-bears-the-private-terms-in-full" .

hemo:7000685 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Social values and net benefits from four inputs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000253 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the four marginal values in MargPrivCost, MargExtCost, MargPrivBen and MargExtBen, the formulas return marginal social cost, marginal social benefit, net social benefit and net private benefit; the fifth flags a margin at which the private choice goes against the social one." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Social values and net benefits from four inputs" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-EXT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "social-values-and-net-benefits-from-four-inputs" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MargPrivCost+MargExtCost; =MargPrivBen+MargExtBen; =MargPrivBen+MargExtBen-MargPrivCost-MargExtCost; =MargPrivBen-MargPrivCost; =SIGN(NetSocialBen)<>SIGN(NetPrivateBen)" .

hemo:7000686 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Gap between the two net benefits equals the net external effect"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Subtract net private from net social benefit and compare with MEB minus MEC. Expected result: TRUE; in the vaccination example the gap is 20. FALSE shows an external effect entered on the wrong side or counted twice." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Gap between the two net benefits equals the net external effect" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EXT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "gap-between-the-two-net-benefits-equals-the-net-external-effect" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((NetSocialBen-NetPrivateBen)-(MargExtBen-MargExtCost))<0.000001" .

hemo:7000687 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Individual declines a vaccination that society values"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Using the article's illustrative values, the marginal private benefit of vaccination is 55 units and the marginal external benefit from reduced transmission 20, so marginal social benefit is 75; with no external cost, marginal social cost equals the private cost of 60. Society gains 15 units from one more vaccination, but the individual faces a net loss of 5 and declines. A subsidy of more than 5 units at this margin would reverse the decision." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Individual declines a vaccination that society values" ;
    hemo:9000014 "MPC = 60; MEC = 0; MPB = 55; MEB = 20; MSC = 60; MSB = 75; NB_S = 15; NB_P = -5" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EXT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "individual-declines-a-vaccination-that-society-values" .

hemo:7000688 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Antibiotic course taken although society loses"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An illustrative antibiotic course has a marginal private cost of 10 units and a marginal private benefit of 12. Its contribution to resistance is an external cost of consumption of 4 units, entered as a negative marginal external benefit, so marginal social benefit is 8. The individual gains 2 units and takes the course, while society loses 2." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Antibiotic course taken although society loses" ;
    hemo:9000014 "MPC = 10; MEC = 0; MPB = 12; MEB = -4; MSC = 10; MSB = 8; NB_S = -2; NB_P = 2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EXT-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "antibiotic-course-taken-although-society-loses" .

hemo:7000689 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Linear curves over the relevant range"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Marginal private and external benefit fall linearly and marginal cost is constant between Q_p and Q_s. Transmission models usually give a non-linear external benefit, so the linear form is an approximation around the current coverage." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Linear curves over the relevant range" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EXT-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "linear-curves-over-the-relevant-range" .

hemo:7000690 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Interior solution below full coverage"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "a is greater than c, so some people vaccinate privately, and Q_s lies within the population that can be vaccinated. At very high coverage the marginal external benefit can reach zero and the formulas no longer apply." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Interior solution below full coverage" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EXT-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "interior-solution-below-full-coverage" .

hemo:7000691 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Subsidy passed through to the price faced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The subsidy lowers the price faced by each person by s, so private choice moves along the same demand curve to the point where marginal private benefit equals c minus s." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Subsidy passed through to the price faced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EXT-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "subsidy-passed-through-to-the-price-faced" .

hemo:7000692 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Linear Pigouvian subsidy and deadweight loss"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000254 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the curve parameters in named cells DemandIntercept, DemandSlope, MargCost, ExtIntercept and ExtSlope, the four formulas return the private quantity, the efficient quantity, the subsidy and the deadweight loss." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Linear Pigouvian subsidy and deadweight loss" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-EXT-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "linear-pigouvian-subsidy-and-deadweight-loss" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(DemandIntercept-MargCost)/DemandSlope; =(DemandIntercept+ExtIntercept-MargCost)/(DemandSlope+ExtSlope); =ExtIntercept-ExtSlope*EfficientQty; =0.5*(EfficientQty-PrivateQty)*(ExtIntercept-ExtSlope*PrivateQty)" .

hemo:7000693 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Subsidy moves private choice to the efficient quantity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compute the quantity people choose when the price they face falls by the subsidy and compare with the efficient quantity. Expected result: TRUE; in the first example both are 57.5." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Subsidy moves private choice to the efficient quantity" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EXT-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "subsidy-moves-private-choice-to-the-efficient-quantity" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((DemandIntercept-(MargCost-Subsidy))/DemandSlope-EfficientQty)<0.000001" .

hemo:7000694 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Efficient quantity above the private quantity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For a positive externality the efficient quantity exceeds the private quantity and the deadweight loss is positive. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows a sign error in the external benefit or a slope entered as negative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Efficient quantity above the private quantity" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EXT-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "efficient-quantity-above-the-private-quantity" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(EfficientQty>PrivateQty,DeadweightLoss>0)" .

hemo:7000695 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Vaccination subsidy consistent with the article's margin"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Marginal private benefit is 100 minus Q per vaccination, the marginal cost 60 and the marginal external benefit 29 minus 0.2 × Q, with Q in thousands; at 45 thousand these give the article's margin of 55, 20 and 60. People choose 40 thousand vaccinations, the efficient number is 57.5 thousand, the Pigouvian subsidy is 17.5 per vaccination and the deadweight loss is 183.75 thousand. The figures are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Vaccination subsidy consistent with the article's margin" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 100; b = 1; c = 60; m = 29; g = 0.2; Q_p = 40; Q_s = 57.5; s = 17.5; DWL = 183.75" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EXT-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "vaccination-subsidy-consistent-with-the-article-s-margin" .

hemo:7000696 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant external benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "If the external benefit stays at 20 per vaccination whatever the coverage, the subsidy equals that constant benefit, the efficient number rises to 60 thousand and the deadweight loss is 200 thousand." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant external benefit" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 100; b = 1; c = 60; m = 20; g = 0; Q_p = 40; Q_s = 60; s = 20; DWL = 200" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EXT-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-external-benefit" .

hemo:7000697 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No external benefit leaves take-up unchanged"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With no external benefit (m and g both zero), the private and efficient quantities coincide at 40 thousand, so no subsidy is needed and there is no deadweight loss. The case checks that the formula reduces to the private market when the externality disappears." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No external benefit leaves take-up unchanged" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 100; b = 1; c = 60; m = 0; g = 0; Q_p = 40; Q_s = 40; s = 0; DWL = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EXT-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-external-benefit-leaves-take-up-unchanged" .

hemo:7000698 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Demand curve measures marginal benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The patient's demand curve is read as the value of care to the patient. Nyman's access motive and the behavioural hazard argument of Baicker, Mullainathan and Schwartzstein both reject this reading for part of the extra use, and then the triangle overstates, or can even reverse, the true welfare effect." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Demand curve measures marginal benefit" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "demand-curve-measures-marginal-benefit" .

hemo:7000699 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Linear demand between the two prices"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Demand is linear between c × p and p, so the area between the cost line and the demand curve is a triangle. With a convex demand curve the triangle is an approximation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Linear demand between the two prices" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "linear-demand-between-the-two-prices" .

hemo:7000700 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Resource cost constant and fully passed to the uninsured price"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each unit costs p to provide whatever the quantity, and p is the price an uninsured patient would pay. Supplier responses and differences between price and resource cost are excluded." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Resource cost constant and fully passed to the uninsured price" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MH-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "resource-cost-constant-and-fully-passed-to-the-uninsured-price" .

hemo:7000701 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Welfare loss triangle from prices and quantities"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000255 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the unit cost in UnitCost, the coinsurance rate in CoinsRate and the quantities in QtyInsured and QtyFullPrice, Excel returns the welfare loss." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Welfare loss triangle from prices and quantities" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-MH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "welfare-loss-triangle-from-prices-and-quantities" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=0.5*(UnitCost-CoinsRate*UnitCost)*(QtyInsured-QtyFullPrice)" .

hemo:7000702 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Inputs in their valid ranges"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Check that the coinsurance rate is at least 0 and below 1 and that insured use is no lower than use at the full price. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows a coinsurance rate entered as a percentage or quantities swapped." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Inputs in their valid ranges" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "inputs-in-their-valid-ranges" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(CoinsRate>=0,CoinsRate<1,QtyInsured>=QtyFullPrice)" .

hemo:7000703 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Outpatient visits under 25 per cent coinsurance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Visits cost 100 pounds each. At the full price a person uses 4 visits a year; with 25% coinsurance the patient pays 25 pounds and uses 5.5. The welfare loss attributed to the extra 1.5 visits is 56.25 pounds per person a year. The figures are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Outpatient visits under 25 per cent coinsurance" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p = 100; c = 0.25; q_1 = 5.5; q_0 = 4; WL = 56.25" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "outpatient-visits-under-25-per-cent-coinsurance" .

hemo:7000704 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Same visits under full insurance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With no cost sharing the same linear demand curve gives 6 visits a year, and the welfare loss rises to 100 pounds per person a year." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same visits under full insurance" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p = 100; c = 0; q_1 = 6; q_0 = 4; WL = 100" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-visits-under-full-insurance" .

hemo:7000705 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant demand slope"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "D is the same at every price between c × p and p. Estimates of D from one range of cost sharing do not carry over to a very different range." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant demand slope" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MH-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-demand-slope" .

hemo:7000706 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Price reduction as the only change"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Insurance changes only the price paid at the point of use. Income effects of the premium and changes in supplier behaviour are ignored." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Price reduction as the only change" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MH-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "price-reduction-as-the-only-change" .

hemo:7000707 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Extra use and welfare loss from the demand slope"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000256 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the demand slope in DemandSlope, the unit cost in UnitCost and the coinsurance rate in CoinsRate, the formulas return extra use and the welfare loss." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Extra use and welfare loss from the demand slope" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-MH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "extra-use-and-welfare-loss-from-the-demand-slope" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=DemandSlope*(UnitCost-CoinsRate*UnitCost); =0.5*DemandSlope*(UnitCost-CoinsRate*UnitCost)^2" .

hemo:7000708 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Linear model agrees with the triangle"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compute the triangle from the extra use and compare with the squared form. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows a demand slope entered with the wrong sign or units." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Linear model agrees with the triangle" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "linear-model-agrees-with-the-triangle" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(0.5*(UnitCost-CoinsRate*UnitCost)*ExtraUse-WelfareLoss)<0.000001" .

hemo:7000709 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Demand slope consistent with the visits example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A slope of 0.02 visits per pound reproduces the visits example: 25% coinsurance lowers the price paid by 75 pounds, adding 1.5 visits and a welfare loss of 56.25 pounds. The figures are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Demand slope consistent with the visits example" ;
    hemo:9000014 "D = 0.02; p = 100; c = 0.25; Delta_q = 1.5; WL = 56.25" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MH-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "demand-slope-consistent-with-the-visits-example" .

hemo:7000710 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Fifty per cent coinsurance on the same demand curve"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At 50% coinsurance the price paid falls by 50 pounds, adding 1 visit and a welfare loss of 25 pounds. Halving the coinsurance rate to 25% more than doubles the loss." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fifty per cent coinsurance on the same demand curve" ;
    hemo:9000014 "D = 0.02; p = 100; c = 0.5; Delta_q = 1; WL = 25" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MH-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fifty-per-cent-coinsurance-on-the-same-demand-curve" .

hemo:7000711 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Two arrangements that differ in price only"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The two groups differ only in the price of care, as under random assignment to plans. A comparison of self-selected insured and uninsured people mixes the price effect with adverse selection." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two arrangements that differ in price only" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MH-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-arrangements-that-differ-in-price-only" .

hemo:7000712 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "A single price summarises each plan"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each plan is represented by one price, such as its coinsurance rate. Real plans have deductibles and out-of-pocket maximums, so the price faced changes during the year and the elasticity depends on which price is used." ;
    hemo:9000009 "A single price summarises each plan" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MH-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "a-single-price-summarises-each-plan" .

hemo:7000713 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Arc elasticity from two plans"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000257 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With spending in Spend1 and Spend2 and prices in Price1 and Price2, Excel returns the arc elasticity." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Arc elasticity from two plans" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-MH-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "arc-elasticity-from-two-plans" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=((Spend2-Spend1)/(Spend2+Spend1))/((Price2-Price1)/(Price2+Price1))" .

hemo:7000714 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Midpoint percentages reproduce the elasticity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Recompute with each change divided by the average of its two values. Expected result: TRUE, because the factors of 2 cancel. FALSE shows a change divided by the first value instead of the average." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Midpoint percentages reproduce the elasticity" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MH-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "midpoint-percentages-reproduce-the-elasticity" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(((Spend2-Spend1)/AVERAGE(Spend1,Spend2))/((Price2-Price1)/AVERAGE(Price1,Price2))-ArcElasticity)<0.000001" .

hemo:7000715 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "RAND free care plan against the 25 per cent coinsurance plan"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Aron-Dine and colleagues report mean total annual spending of 2,170 dollars (2011 prices) in the free care plan and 648 dollars less, 1,522 dollars, in the 25% coinsurance plan. The arc elasticity with respect to the coinsurance rate is about minus 0.18, the figure they report for this pair." ;
    hemo:9000009 "RAND free care plan against the 25 per cent coinsurance plan" ;
    hemo:9000014 "q_1 = 2170; q_2 = 1522; p_1 = 0; p_2 = 0.25; eta_arc = -0.18" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MH-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "rand-free-care-plan-against-the-25-per-cent-coinsurance-plan" .

hemo:7000716 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "RAND free care plan against the 95 per cent coinsurance plan"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Spending in the 95% coinsurance plan was 845 dollars below free care, 1,325 dollars. The simple arc elasticity from these rounded means is about minus 0.24; Aron-Dine and colleagues report minus 0.234 for this pair from their own estimates." ;
    hemo:9000009 "RAND free care plan against the 95 per cent coinsurance plan" ;
    hemo:9000014 "q_1 = 2170; q_2 = 1325; p_1 = 0; p_2 = 0.95; eta_arc = -0.24" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MH-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "rand-free-care-plan-against-the-95-per-cent-coinsurance-plan" .

hemo:7000717 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Depreciation rate exogenous but age-dependent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "In Grossman's model delta_t is given to the individual and varies with age; it is not chosen. Death occurs when the stock falls to a minimum level." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Depreciation rate exogenous but age-dependent" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DFH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "depreciation-rate-exogenous-but-age-dependent" .

hemo:7000718 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Health stock and investment in common units"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "H_t and I_t are measured in the same units, which are analytical rather than observable. The equation describes the transition of the stock, not whether the investment is optimal." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health stock and investment in common units" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DFH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-stock-and-investment-in-common-units" .

hemo:7000719 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Next-period health stock"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000258 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the current stock in HealthStock, the depreciation rate in DeprRate and gross investment in GrossInvestment, Excel returns the stock carried into the next period." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Next-period health stock" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-DFH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "next-period-health-stock" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=HealthStock-DeprRate*HealthStock+GrossInvestment" .

hemo:7000720 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Net investment equals gross investment minus depreciation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compare the change in the stock with gross investment minus depreciation, as in Grossman's equation (2-2). Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows depreciation applied to the new stock instead of the current one." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net investment equals gross investment minus depreciation" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DFH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-investment-equals-gross-investment-minus-depreciation" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((NextStock-HealthStock)-(GrossInvestment-DeprRate*HealthStock))<0.000001" .

hemo:7000721 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Depreciation rate and investment in their valid ranges"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Check that the depreciation rate is above 0 and below 1 and that gross investment is not negative, since health capital cannot be sold. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows a rate entered as a percentage or negative investment." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Depreciation rate and investment in their valid ranges" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DFH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "depreciation-rate-and-investment-in-their-valid-ranges" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(DeprRate>0,DeprRate<1,GrossInvestment>=0)" .

hemo:7000722 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Investment that raises the stock"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Using the article's illustrative values, a stock of 100 units with 8% depreciation and 12 units of gross investment rises to 104 units." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Investment that raises the stock" ;
    hemo:9000014 "H_t = 100; delta_t = 0.08; I_t = 12; H_(t+1) = 104" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DFH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "investment-that-raises-the-stock" .

hemo:7000723 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Investment below depreciation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With only 5 units of gross investment the stock falls to 97 units, because depreciation of 8 units exceeds investment." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Investment below depreciation" ;
    hemo:9000014 "H_t = 100; delta_t = 0.08; I_t = 5; H_(t+1) = 97" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DFH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "investment-below-depreciation" .

hemo:7000724 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Higher depreciation later in life"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "If the depreciation rate rises to 12%, the same 12 units of investment only maintain the stock at 100 units." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Higher depreciation later in life" ;
    hemo:9000014 "H_t = 100; delta_t = 0.12; I_t = 12; H_(t+1) = 100" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DFH-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "higher-depreciation-later-in-life" .

hemo:7000725 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Pure investment model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The marginal utility of healthy time is set to zero, so the psychic return drops out of Grossman's general condition. In the pure consumption version the monetary return is zero instead, and some predictions differ." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pure investment model" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DFH-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pure-investment-model" .

hemo:7000726 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant returns in producing gross investment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gross investment is produced with constant returns to scale and input prices that do not depend on the quantity produced, so the marginal cost pi_(t-1) equals the average cost. The condition also omits the small product of delta_t and pi_tilde_(t-1), which Grossman assumes is close to zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant returns in producing gross investment" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DFH-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-returns-in-producing-gross-investment" .

hemo:7000727 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Interior solution with positive gross investment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gross investment is positive in period t, so the first-order condition holds with equality. G_t falls as the stock rises, which makes the marginal efficiency schedule slope downward." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Interior solution with positive gross investment" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DFH-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "interior-solution-with-positive-gross-investment" .

hemo:7000728 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Marginal efficiency and opportunity cost of health capital"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000259 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs in Wage, MargProdHealth, MargCostInvest, InterestRate, MargCostGrowth and DeprRate, the first formula returns the marginal efficiency of health capital and the second its opportunity cost." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Marginal efficiency and opportunity cost of health capital" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-DFH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "marginal-efficiency-and-opportunity-cost-of-health-capital" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=Wage*MargProdHealth/MargCostInvest; =InterestRate-MargCostGrowth+DeprRate" .

hemo:7000729 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Optimality condition holds"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compare the marginal efficiency with the opportunity cost. Expected result: TRUE at the optimal stock. A marginal efficiency above the opportunity cost means the stock is below its optimum; below it, the stock is above its optimum." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Optimality condition holds" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DFH-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "optimality-condition-holds" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(MargEfficiency-OppCostCapital)<0.000001" .

hemo:7000730 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Optimal stock at a younger age"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A worker values a healthy day at 150 pounds, one more unit of health capital adds 0.4 healthy days a year and gross investment costs 750 pounds a unit, so gamma_t is 0.08. With interest of 3%, a 1% rise in the marginal cost of investment and depreciation of 6%, the opportunity cost is also 0.08, so the stock is at its optimum. The figures are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Optimal stock at a younger age" ;
    hemo:9000014 "W_t = 150; G_t = 0.4; pi_(t-1) = 750; r = 0.03; pi_tilde_(t-1) = 0.01; delta_t = 0.06; gamma_t = 0.08; UC_t = 0.08" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DFH-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "optimal-stock-at-a-younger-age" .

hemo:7000731 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Optimal stock after depreciation rises with age"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When depreciation rises to 9%, the opportunity cost rises to 0.11. The condition is restored only at a lower stock, where the marginal product of health capital has risen to 0.55 healthy days a year. This is the model's prediction that the optimal stock falls with age." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Optimal stock after depreciation rises with age" ;
    hemo:9000014 "W_t = 150; G_t = 0.55; pi_(t-1) = 750; r = 0.03; pi_tilde_(t-1) = 0.01; delta_t = 0.09; gamma_t = 0.11; UC_t = 0.11" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DFH-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "optimal-stock-after-depreciation-rises-with-age" .

hemo:7000732 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Income unchanged by the policy"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Only prices change, so e0_u0 and e1_u1 both equal income. For a change in the quantity of a non-market good, such as a person's own health, the same logic applies with the expenditure function defined over that quantity." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Income unchanged by the policy" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-WE-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "income-unchanged-by-the-policy" .

hemo:7000733 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Known preferences"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The expenditure function comes from a specified utility function or an estimated demand system. Stated-preference surveys estimate CV or EV directly as willingness to pay or to accept, as set out on the willingness-to-pay page." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Known preferences" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-WE-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "known-preferences" .

hemo:7000734 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "CV and EV from four expenditure values"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000260 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the four expenditure values in ExpOldPriceOldUtil, ExpNewPriceOldUtil, ExpOldPriceNewUtil and ExpNewPriceNewUtil, Excel returns CV and EV." ;
    hemo:9000009 "CV and EV from four expenditure values" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-WE-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cv-and-ev-from-four-expenditure-values" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ExpOldPriceOldUtil-ExpNewPriceOldUtil; =ExpOldPriceNewUtil-ExpNewPriceNewUtil" .

hemo:7000735 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Ordering of CV and EV for a normal good"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For a normal good EV is at least CV, whether the price falls or rises. Expected result: TRUE in both worked examples. FALSE shows expenditure values attached to the wrong utility level." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ordering of CV and EV for a normal good" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-WE-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ordering-of-cv-and-ev-for-a-normal-good" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=EquivVar>=CompVar" .

hemo:7000736 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Halving the price of a health service with Cobb-Douglas preferences"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A person with income 1,000 spends 20% of it on a health service whose price falls from 20 to 10. With Cobb-Douglas preferences the expenditure needed for the original utility falls to 870.55, and reaching the new utility at the old prices would cost 1,148.70. CV is 129.45 and EV 148.70; the consumer surplus from the demand curve, about 138.63, lies between them. The figures are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Halving the price of a health service with Cobb-Douglas preferences" ;
    hemo:9000014 "e0_u0 = 1000; e1_u0 = 870.55; e0_u1 = 1148.70; e1_u1 = 1000; CV = 129.45; EV = 148.70" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-WE-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "halving-the-price-of-a-health-service-with-cobb-douglas-preferences" .

hemo:7000737 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Doubling the price of the same service"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Reversing the change, a price rise from 10 to 20, gives CV of minus 148.70 and EV of minus 129.45: the compensating variation of a loss equals minus the equivalent variation of the matching gain." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Doubling the price of the same service" ;
    hemo:9000014 "e0_u0 = 1000; e1_u0 = 1148.70; e0_u1 = 870.55; e1_u1 = 1000; CV = -148.70; EV = -129.45" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-WE-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "doubling-the-price-of-the-same-service" .

hemo:7000738 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Straight-line demand between the old and new prices"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The demand curve is a straight line between the two observed points. For a curved demand function the trapezoid is an approximation; with the Cobb-Douglas demand of the CV and EV example it gives 150 against the exact area of 138.63." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Straight-line demand between the old and new prices" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-WE-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "straight-line-demand-between-the-old-and-new-prices" .

hemo:7000739 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Small income effects"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Consumer surplus approximates CV and EV well when spending on the good is a small share of income and income effects are modest." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Small income effects" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-WE-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "small-income-effects" .

hemo:7000740 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Consumer surplus change from two price and quantity points"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000261 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the old and new prices in PriceOld and PriceNew and the quantities in QtyOld and QtyNew, Excel returns the change in consumer surplus." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Consumer surplus change from two price and quantity points" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-WE-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "consumer-surplus-change-from-two-price-and-quantity-points" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(PriceOld-PriceNew)*(QtyOld+QtyNew)/2" .

hemo:7000741 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Rectangle plus triangle equals the trapezoid"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Split the area into the saving on units bought at both prices and the triangle on the extra units. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Rectangle plus triangle equals the trapezoid" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-WE-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "rectangle-plus-triangle-equals-the-trapezoid" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((PriceOld-PriceNew)*MIN(QtyOld,QtyNew)+0.5*(PriceOld-PriceNew)*ABS(QtyNew-QtyOld)-ConsSurplusChange)<0.000001" .

hemo:7000742 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Cut in an outpatient charge"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A charge falls from 20 to 10 pounds a visit and use rises from 4 to 5 visits a year. Consumer surplus rises by 45 pounds a person a year. The figures are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cut in an outpatient charge" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_0 = 20; P_1 = 10; Q_0 = 4; Q_1 = 5; Delta_CS = 45" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-WE-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cut-in-an-outpatient-charge" .

hemo:7000743 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Introducing a charge for a free service"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Introducing a charge of 10 pounds on a free service reduces use from 5 to 4 visits and consumer surplus by 45 pounds a person a year; the 40 pounds collected is a transfer, and the remaining 5 pounds is the deadweight loss triangle." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Introducing a charge for a free service" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_0 = 0; P_1 = 10; Q_0 = 5; Q_1 = 4; Delta_CS = -45" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-WE-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "introducing-a-charge-for-a-free-service" .

hemo:7000744 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal weight on every unit of money"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each pound counts the same whoever gains or loses it. Because willingness to pay is bounded by income, the sum gives more weight to the preferences of people with more money unless distributional weights are applied." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equal weight on every unit of money" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-WE-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equal-weight-on-every-unit-of-money" .

hemo:7000745 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Hypothetical compensation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Passing the test shows that compensation is possible, not that it is paid. Without payment the losers remain worse off." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Hypothetical compensation" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-WE-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "hypothetical-compensation" .

hemo:7000746 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Consistent ranking"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Scitovsky showed that a change and its reversal can both pass the Kaldor test when the change alters the goods available. The double criterion requires that the change passes and its reversal fails." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Consistent ranking" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-WE-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "consistent-ranking" .

hemo:7000747 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Aggregate net gain from group sizes and money measures"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000262 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With group sizes in GroupSizes and money measures per person, negative for losers, in GainPerPerson, Excel returns the aggregate net gain; the second formula applies the Kaldor test." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Aggregate net gain from group sizes and money measures" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-WE-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "aggregate-net-gain-from-group-sizes-and-money-measures" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(GroupSizes,GainPerPerson); =SUMPRODUCT(GroupSizes,GainPerPerson)>0" .

hemo:7000748 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Gains minus losses equal the net sum"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compute total gains and total losses separately and compare their difference with the net sum. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows a loss entered as a positive number." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Gains minus losses equal the net sum" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-WE-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "gains-minus-losses-equal-the-net-sum" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(SUMPRODUCT(GroupSizes,GainPerPerson*(GainPerPerson>0))+SUMPRODUCT(GroupSizes,GainPerPerson*(GainPerPerson<0))-AggregateNetGain)<0.01" .

hemo:7000749 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Concentrating a specialist service"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative example, 1,000 people in Group A would each pay up to 300 pounds for the change and 1,000 people in Group B would each need 200 pounds in compensation. The aggregate net gain is 100,000 pounds, so the change passes the Kaldor test although it is not a Pareto improvement." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Concentrating a specialist service" ;
    hemo:9000014 "N_i = [1000,1000]; CV_i = [300,-200]; n = 2; NG = 100000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-WE-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "concentrating-a-specialist-service" .

hemo:7000750 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Larger losing group"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "If the outlying group had 1,600 members, each needing 200 pounds, the aggregate net gain would be minus 20,000 pounds and the change would fail the test." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Larger losing group" ;
    hemo:9000014 "N_i = [1000,1600]; CV_i = [300,-200]; n = 2; NG = -20000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-WE-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "larger-losing-group" .

hemo:7000751 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Linear weighting within each group"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every QALY in a group receives the same weight, so the function is linear. An inequality-averse function that is concave in each person's health gives weights that change with the level of health." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Linear weighting within each group" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-WE-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "linear-weighting-within-each-group" .

hemo:7000752 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Weights are value judgements"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The weight expresses a distributional judgement, even when elicited from public preference surveys. Reporting the switching weight lets decision-makers see how strong that judgement must be to change the ranking." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Weights are value judgements" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-WE-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "weights-are-value-judgements" .

hemo:7000753 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Weighted welfare scores and switching weight"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000263 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the QALY totals in QAlyAX, QAlyBX, QAlyAY and QAlyBY and the weight in WeightB, the formulas return the two weighted scores and the switching weight." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Weighted welfare scores and switching weight" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-WE-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "weighted-welfare-scores-and-switching-weight" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=QAlyAX+WeightB*QAlyBX; =QAlyAY+WeightB*QAlyBY; =(QAlyAX-QAlyAY)/(QAlyBY-QAlyBX)" .

hemo:7000754 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Options tie at the switching weight"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Evaluate both weighted scores at the switching weight. Expected result: TRUE, because the scores are equal there." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Options tie at the switching weight" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-WE-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "options-tie-at-the-switching-weight" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((QAlyAX+SwitchWeight*QAlyBX)-(QAlyAY+SwitchWeight*QAlyBY))<0.000001" .

hemo:7000755 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Reconfiguration against outreach with a weight of 2"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's example, option X gives groups A and B 20,030 and 11,990 QALYs and option Y 20,000 and 12,015. With a weight of 2 on group B, X scores 44,010 and Y 44,030, so Y is preferred; the switching weight is 30 divided by 25, or 1.2." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Reconfiguration against outreach with a weight of 2" ;
    hemo:9000014 "QA_X = 20030; QB_X = 11990; QA_Y = 20000; QB_Y = 12015; w = 2; W_X = 44010; W_Y = 44030; w_s = 1.2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-WE-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "reconfiguration-against-outreach-with-a-weight-of-2" .

hemo:7000756 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Same options under the utilitarian weight"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a weight of 1 the function is the utilitarian sum: X scores 32,020 and Y 32,015, so X is preferred, as in the cost-benefit result." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same options under the utilitarian weight" ;
    hemo:9000014 "QA_X = 20030; QB_X = 11990; QA_Y = 20000; QB_Y = 12015; w = 1; W_X = 32020; W_Y = 32015; w_s = 1.2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-WE-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-options-under-the-utilitarian-weight" .

hemo:7000757 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Rules effective from 1 January 2019"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The ceiling price regulation was published on 5 January 2017 and took effect on 1 January 2019. For a new drug without an AMP, the ceiling price is estimated from the wholesale acquisition cost minus the rebate percentage until an AMP is available." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Rules effective from 1 January 2019" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-340B-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "rules-effective-from-1-january-2019" .

hemo:7000758 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Quarterly calculation by product"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The manufacturer calculates the ceiling price for each National Drug Code every quarter. AMP and URA refer to the same unit of measure and the preceding quarter." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Quarterly calculation by product" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-340B-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "quarterly-calculation-by-product" .

hemo:7000759 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Ceiling price with penny pricing"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000264 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the AMP in AvgMfrPrice and the unit rebate amount in UnitRebateAmt, Excel calculates to six decimal places and applies the 0.01 dollar floor; ROUND(,2) of the result gives the published value." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ceiling price with penny pricing" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-340B-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ceiling-price-with-penny-pricing" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MAX(ROUND(AvgMfrPrice-UnitRebateAmt,6),0.01)" .

hemo:7000760 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Ceiling price within its legal bounds"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Check that the ceiling price is at least 0.01 dollars and no higher than AMP. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows a negative calculated price published without the floor, or a rebate entered with the wrong sign." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ceiling price within its legal bounds" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-340B-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ceiling-price-within-its-legal-bounds" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(CeilingPrice>=0.01,CeilingPrice<=AvgMfrPrice)" .

hemo:7000761 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Branded drug with the minimum rebate only"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Using the article's illustrative figures, a branded single-source drug has an AMP of 100 dollars and a unit rebate amount of 23.10 dollars, the 23.1% minimum rebate. The ceiling price is 76.90 dollars per unit." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Branded drug with the minimum rebate only" ;
    hemo:9000014 "AMP = 100; URA = 23.10; P_340B = 76.90" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-340B-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "branded-drug-with-the-minimum-rebate-only" .

hemo:7000762 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Drug with a large inflation rebate at the penny floor"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An AMP of 100 dollars with a unit rebate amount of 101.10 dollars, possible for a drug whose price has risen much faster than inflation, gives a calculated price of minus 1.10 dollars. The ceiling price is set at 0.01 dollars. The figures are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Drug with a large inflation rebate at the penny floor" ;
    hemo:9000014 "AMP = 100; URA = 101.10; P_340B = 0.01" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-340B-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "drug-with-a-large-inflation-rebate-at-the-penny-floor" .

hemo:7000763 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Single-source or innovator multiple-source drug"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The 23.1% minimum applies to these drugs for rebate periods after 31 December 2009. Clotting factors and drugs approved exclusively for paediatric indications have a 17.1% minimum, generic drugs follow a different rebate under section 1927(c)(3), and line extensions of oral solid drugs have a further alternative calculation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Single-source or innovator multiple-source drug" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-340B-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "single-source-or-innovator-multiple-source-drug" .

hemo:7000764 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Rebate periods from 1 January 2024"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The statute capped the basic and additional rebates together at 100% of AMP only for rebate periods before 1 January 2024. For earlier quarters the total is capped at AMP." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Rebate periods from 1 January 2024" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-340B-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "rebate-periods-from-1-january-2024" .

hemo:7000765 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Unit rebate amount from AMP, best price and CPI-U"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000265 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs in AvgMfrPrice, BestPrice, BaseAMP, CPICurrent and CPIBase, Excel returns the basic plus additional rebate per unit." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Unit rebate amount from AMP, best price and CPI-U" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-340B-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "unit-rebate-amount-from-amp-best-price-and-cpi-u" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MAX(AvgMfrPrice-BestPrice,0.231*AvgMfrPrice)+MAX(AvgMfrPrice-BaseAMP*CPICurrent/CPIBase,0)" .

hemo:7000766 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Basic rebate at least the statutory minimum"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Check that the unit rebate amount is at least 23.1% of AMP. Expected result: TRUE for a single-source or innovator drug. FALSE shows the best-price term used alone." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Basic rebate at least the statutory minimum" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-340B-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "basic-rebate-at-least-the-statutory-minimum" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=UnitRebateAmt>=0.231*AvgMfrPrice-0.000001" .

hemo:7000767 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Branded drug with no inflation rebate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Using the article's figures, a drug has an AMP of 100 dollars and a best price of 85. Its base-period AMP of 95 dollars, uprated by a CPI-U rise from 300 to 330, is 104.50, above the current AMP, so there is no additional rebate. The URA is the minimum rebate of 23.10 dollars. The CPI values are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Branded drug with no inflation rebate" ;
    hemo:9000014 "AMP = 100; BP = 85; AMP_b = 95; CPI_t = 330; CPI_b = 300; URA = 23.10" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-340B-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "branded-drug-with-no-inflation-rebate" .

hemo:7000768 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Branded drug with an inflation rebate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The same drug launched at a base AMP of 60 dollars when the CPI-U stood at 250, with the index now at 320. The uprated base AMP is 76.80, so the additional rebate is 23.20 and the URA 46.30 dollars. The figures are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Branded drug with an inflation rebate" ;
    hemo:9000014 "AMP = 100; BP = 85; AMP_b = 60; CPI_t = 320; CPI_b = 250; URA = 46.30" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-340B-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "branded-drug-with-an-inflation-rebate" .

hemo:7000769 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Inflation rebate large enough to exceed AMP"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A drug launched at 10 dollars when the CPI-U stood at 150, now priced at 100 with the index at 330, has an uprated base AMP of 22 and an additional rebate of 78. The URA of 101.10 dollars exceeds AMP, and the ceiling price falls to the 0.01 dollar floor. The figures are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Inflation rebate large enough to exceed AMP" ;
    hemo:9000014 "AMP = 100; BP = 85; AMP_b = 10; CPI_t = 330; CPI_b = 150; URA = 101.10" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-340B-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "inflation-rebate-large-enough-to-exceed-amp" .

hemo:7000770 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Payment unaffected by the entity's acquisition cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The payer pays the same amount whatever the entity paid for the drug. Where a payer adjusts payment for 340B acquisition, R is the adjusted rate." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Payment unaffected by the entity's acquisition cost" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-340B-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "payment-unaffected-by-the-entity-s-acquisition-cost" .

hemo:7000771 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Eligible patient and no duplicate discount"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The unit is dispensed to a patient of the covered entity, and for Medicaid patients the unit is not also subject to a Medicaid rebate." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Eligible patient and no duplicate discount" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-340B-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "eligible-patient-and-no-duplicate-discount" .

hemo:7000772 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Spread and share for one unit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000266 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the reimbursement in Reimbursement and the 340B price in Price340B, the formulas return the spread and its share of the payment." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Spread and share for one unit" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-340B-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "spread-and-share-for-one-unit" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=Reimbursement-Price340B; =(Reimbursement-Price340B)/Reimbursement" .

hemo:7000773 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Spread and price add back to the payment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Add the spread to the 340B price and compare with the reimbursement. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Spread and price add back to the payment" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-340B-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "spread-and-price-add-back-to-the-payment" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(Spread+Price340B-Reimbursement)<0.005" .

hemo:7000774 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Unit reimbursed at 106 per cent of average sales price"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Following the article, a unit bought at the ceiling price of 76.90 dollars is reimbursed at 106% of an average sales price of 98 dollars, 103.88 dollars. The spread is 26.98 dollars, about 26% of the payment. The figures are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Unit reimbursed at 106 per cent of average sales price" ;
    hemo:9000014 "R = 103.88; P_340B = 76.90; S = 26.98; S_share = 0.2597" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-340B-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "unit-reimbursed-at-106-per-cent-of-average-sales-price" .

hemo:7000775 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Same unit from a payer with a lower rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "If the payer reimburses 85 dollars for the same unit, the spread falls to 8.10 dollars, about 10% of the payment." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same unit from a payer with a lower rate" ;
    hemo:9000014 "R = 85; P_340B = 76.90; S = 8.10; S_share = 0.0953" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-340B-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-unit-from-a-payer-with-a-lower-rate" .

hemo:7000776 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Log-normal pharmacokinetic measures"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "AUC and Cmax are analysed after log transformation, as the FDA guidance recommends; normality of the residuals is not tested and is not a reason to analyse on the original scale." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Log-normal pharmacokinetic measures" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ANDA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "log-normal-pharmacokinetic-measures" .

hemo:7000777 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal-tails interval and two one-sided tests"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each limit of the 90% interval is by itself a 95% one-sided bound, so the interval test is equivalent to two one-sided tests at the 5% level." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equal-tails interval and two one-sided tests" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ANDA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equal-tails-interval-and-two-one-sided-tests" .

hemo:7000778 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Standard limits for most drugs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The 80.00% to 125.00% limits apply for a broad range of drugs. Narrow therapeutic index drugs, highly variable drugs and some product-specific guidances use scaled or tighter criteria." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Standard limits for most drugs" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ANDA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "standard-limits-for-most-drugs" .

hemo:7000779 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "90 per cent interval for the ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000267 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the mean log difference in MeanLogDiff, its standard error in SELogDiff and the residual degrees of freedom in DF, the formulas return the ratio and its 90% limits." ;
    hemo:9000009 "90 per cent interval for the ratio" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ANDA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "90-per-cent-interval-for-the-ratio" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=EXP(MeanLogDiff); =EXP(MeanLogDiff-T.INV(0.95,DF)*SELogDiff); =EXP(MeanLogDiff+T.INV(0.95,DF)*SELogDiff)" .

hemo:7000780 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Rounded interval within the limits"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Round each limit in per cent to two decimal places and compare with 80.00 and 125.00. Expected result: TRUE for the first example and FALSE for the second." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Rounded interval within the limits" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ANDA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "rounded-interval-within-the-limits" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ROUND(LowerRatio*100,2)>=80,ROUND(UpperRatio*100,2)<=125)" .

hemo:7000781 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Interval symmetric on the log scale"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The point estimate is the geometric mean of the two limits. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows limits computed on the ratio scale by adding and subtracting a margin." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Interval symmetric on the log scale" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ANDA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "interval-symmetric-on-the-log-scale" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(SQRT(LowerRatio*UpperRatio)-RatioEstimate)<0.000001" .

hemo:7000782 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Generic that meets the limits"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A two-period crossover in 24 subjects gives a mean log difference of minus 0.051293, a ratio of 0.95, with a standard error of 0.06. With 22 degrees of freedom the t quantile is 1.717144, so the 90% interval is 85.70% to 105.31%, inside the limits. The figures are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Generic that meets the limits" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d = -0.051293; SE = 0.06; t_crit = 1.717144; GMR = 0.9500; GMR_L = 0.8570; GMR_U = 1.0531" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ANDA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "generic-that-meets-the-limits" .

hemo:7000783 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Generic that fails the lower limit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A ratio of 0.88, a mean log difference of minus 0.127833, with a standard error of 0.07 gives an interval of 78.03% to 99.24%. The point estimate is inside the limits but the lower confidence limit is not, so bioequivalence is not shown." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Generic that fails the lower limit" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d = -0.127833; SE = 0.07; t_crit = 1.717144; GMR = 0.8800; GMR_L = 0.7803; GMR_U = 0.9924" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ANDA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "generic-that-fails-the-lower-limit" .

hemo:7000784 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Balanced sequences and no carryover"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The two sequences have equal numbers of subjects and there is no carryover from the first period. With unequal sequences the factor 2 divided by n becomes one half of the sum of 1 over n_1 and 1 over n_2." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Balanced sequences and no carryover" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ANDA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "balanced-sequences-and-no-carryover" .

hemo:7000785 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Common within-subject variance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Test and reference share the same within-subject variance. Replicate designs estimate them separately, as used for highly variable and narrow therapeutic index drugs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Common within-subject variance" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ANDA-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "common-within-subject-variance" .

hemo:7000786 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Standard error from the residual mean square"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000268 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the residual mean square in ResidualMS and the number of subjects in Subjects, Excel returns the standard error; the second formula gives the within-subject coefficient of variation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Standard error from the residual mean square" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ANDA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "standard-error-from-the-residual-mean-square" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SQRT(2*ResidualMS/Subjects); =SQRT(EXP(ResidualMS)-1)" .

hemo:7000787 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Standard error falls with the square root of sample size"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Quadruple the number of subjects and compare the new standard error with half the old one. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Standard error falls with the square root of sample size" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ANDA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "standard-error-falls-with-the-square-root-of-sample-size" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(SQRT(2*ResidualMS/(4*Subjects))-0.5*SQRT(2*ResidualMS/Subjects))<0.000001" .

hemo:7000788 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Residual mean square of 0.0432 in 24 subjects"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A residual mean square of 0.0432 on the log scale, a within-subject coefficient of variation of about 21%, gives a standard error of 0.06 with 24 subjects, the value used in the passing example. The figures are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Residual mean square of 0.0432 in 24 subjects" ;
    hemo:9000014 "MSE = 0.0432; n = 24; SE = 0.06" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ANDA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "residual-mean-square-of-0-0432-in-24-subjects" .

hemo:7000789 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Residual mean square of 0.0588 in 24 subjects"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A residual mean square of 0.0588 gives a standard error of 0.07, the value used in the failing example." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Residual mean square of 0.0588 in 24 subjects" ;
    hemo:9000014 "MSE = 0.0588; n = 24; SE = 0.07" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ANDA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "residual-mean-square-of-0-0588-in-24-subjects" .

hemo:7000790 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Price level of the reduction matches the price used"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The FDA reductions measured with average manufacturer prices apply to manufacturer-level prices; the invoice-price reductions apply to pharmacy purchase prices. The reduction and the brand price come from the same level." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Price level of the reduction matches the price used" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ANDA-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "price-level-of-the-reduction-matches-the-price-used" .

hemo:7000791 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Immediate and complete switching"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every dose switches to the generic when it enters and the reduction applies from that date. In practice some use stays with the brand, entrants arrive over time and the timing depends on patent challenges, the 30-month stay and 180-day exclusivity." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Immediate and complete switching" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ANDA-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "immediate-and-complete-switching" .

hemo:7000792 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Post-entry price and annual cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000269 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the brand price in BrandPrice, the reduction in PriceReduction and the doses a year in DosesPerYear, the formulas return the generic price and the annual cost." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Post-entry price and annual cost" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ANDA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "post-entry-price-and-annual-cost" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=BrandPrice*(1-PriceReduction); =BrandPrice*(1-PriceReduction)*DosesPerYear" .

hemo:7000793 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Reduction within its valid range"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Check that the reduction is in the range 0 to 1 inclusive and that the annual cost does not exceed the pre-entry annual cost. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows a reduction entered as a percentage." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Reduction within its valid range" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ANDA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "reduction-within-its-valid-range" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(PriceReduction>=0,PriceReduction<=1,AnnualCost<=BrandPrice*DosesPerYear)" .

hemo:7000794 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Four generic competitors at manufacturer prices"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Using the article's example, a brand costing 10 dollars a daily dose meets four generic competitors, a median AMP reduction of 79% in the FDA analysis. The generic price is 2.10 dollars a dose and the annual cost 766.50 dollars, against 3,650 before entry." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Four generic competitors at manufacturer prices" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_b = 10; k = 0.79; N_d = 365; P_g = 2.10; C_ann = 766.50" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ANDA-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "four-generic-competitors-at-manufacturer-prices" .

hemo:7000795 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "One generic competitor at manufacturer prices"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a single generic producer the median AMP reduction was 39%, giving 6.10 dollars a dose and 2,226.50 dollars a year." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One generic competitor at manufacturer prices" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_b = 10; k = 0.39; N_d = 365; P_g = 6.10; C_ann = 2226.50" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ANDA-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-generic-competitor-at-manufacturer-prices" .

hemo:7000796 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal follow-up for every patient in the arm"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "All N patients are followed for the whole period. When arms are treated or followed for different lengths of time, for example because one drug is stopped early for toxicity or progression, the proportion understates the event frequency per unit of treatment time in the arm with less time at risk, and the person-time rate HE-FM-AER-002 is used instead." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equal follow-up for every patient in the arm" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AER-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equal-follow-up-for-every-patient-in-the-arm" .

hemo:7000797 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Same adverse event definition in every arm"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The event term, the severity cut-off (for example CTCAE grade 3 or higher) and the attribution rule are the same in every arm compared. All-cause counts in both arms keep the comparison randomised; counts judged related to the drug depend on investigator attribution." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same adverse event definition in every arm" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AER-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-adverse-event-definition-in-every-arm" .

hemo:7000798 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Adverse event proportion in one cell"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000270 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Excel divides the named cell holding the number of patients with an event by the named cell holding the number at risk." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Adverse event proportion in one cell" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AER-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "adverse-event-proportion-in-one-cell" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=PatientsWithEvent/PatientsAtRisk" .

hemo:7000799 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Adverse event numerator counts patients, not episodes"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The number of patients with an event cannot exceed the number at risk. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows that episodes were counted in the numerator or that the denominator comes from a different population." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Adverse event numerator counts patients, not episodes" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AER-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "adverse-event-numerator-counts-patients-not-episodes" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(PatientsWithEvent>=0,PatientsWithEvent<=PatientsAtRisk,PatientsAtRisk>0)" .

hemo:7000800 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Grade 3 or higher diarrhoea on the new drug over 24 weeks"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative trial, 36 of 200 patients on the new drug have grade 3 or higher diarrhoea within 24 weeks, a proportion of 0.18. The figure describes the whole 24 weeks, not each four-week cycle." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Grade 3 or higher diarrhoea on the new drug over 24 weeks" ;
    hemo:9000014 "n = 36; N = 200; P = 0.18" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AER-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "grade-3-or-higher-diarrhoea-on-the-new-drug-over-24-weeks" .

hemo:7000801 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Grade 3 or higher diarrhoea on the comparator over 24 weeks"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the same illustrative trial, 16 of 200 patients on the comparator have the event within 24 weeks, a proportion of 0.08." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Grade 3 or higher diarrhoea on the comparator over 24 weeks" ;
    hemo:9000014 "n = 16; N = 200; P = 0.08" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AER-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "grade-3-or-higher-diarrhoea-on-the-comparator-over-24-weeks" .

hemo:7000802 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant adverse event rate over follow-up"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The rate is an average over the follow-up period. If events concentrate in the first weeks of treatment, a single rate understates the early risk and overstates the later risk." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant adverse event rate over follow-up" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AER-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-adverse-event-rate-over-follow-up" .

hemo:7000803 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Event count and person-time defined together"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "For first events, each patient's time at risk stops at the first episode; for all episodes, it runs to the end of follow-up or treatment. Dividing all episodes by time to first event, or first events by total time, misstates the rate." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Event count and person-time defined together" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AER-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "event-count-and-person-time-defined-together" .

hemo:7000804 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Adverse event rate per person-time in one cell"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000271 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Excel divides the named cell holding the event count by the named cell holding the person-time at risk." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Adverse event rate per person-time in one cell" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AER-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "adverse-event-rate-per-person-time-in-one-cell" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=EventCount/PersonTime" .

hemo:7000805 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Adverse event rate inputs and time unit match the model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The count is not negative, person-time is positive, and the time unit of the rate matches the time unit of the model cycle, held as text in two named cells. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows an invalid input or a rate that would mis-scale every cycle probability." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Adverse event rate inputs and time unit match the model" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AER-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "adverse-event-rate-inputs-and-time-unit-match-the-model" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(EventCount>=0,PersonTime>0,RateTimeUnit=CycleTimeUnit)" .

hemo:7000806 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Adverse event rate on a drug stopped early"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative figures: 20 first episodes during 1,000 patient-weeks at risk give a rate of 0.02 per patient-week." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Adverse event rate on a drug stopped early" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d = 20; PT = 1000; r = 0.02" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AER-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "adverse-event-rate-on-a-drug-stopped-early" .

hemo:7000807 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Adverse event rate on a comparator with longer exposure"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative figures: the same 20 first episodes during 2,000 patient-weeks at risk give 0.01 per patient-week, half the rate of the arm stopped early although the proportions with an event are equal." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Adverse event rate on a comparator with longer exposure" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d = 20; PT = 2000; r = 0.01" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AER-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "adverse-event-rate-on-a-comparator-with-longer-exposure" .

hemo:7000808 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant adverse event rate across the trial period"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The risk of a first episode is the same in every week of follow-up, so each cycle on treatment receives the same probability. If events concentrate early, the uniform cycle probability understates the risk in the first cycles." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant adverse event rate across the trial period" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AER-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-adverse-event-rate-across-the-trial-period" .

hemo:7000809 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Event-free patients as the adverse event risk pool"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "p_t is the probability of a first episode among patients still event-free, so the model removes patients from the at-risk pool after a first episode. If the same probability is applied to the whole cohort in every cycle, recurrence is implied: in the article's example six cycles at about 0.0325 give about 0.195 episodes per patient rather than 0.18, and an adverse event cost of about £351 rather than £324 at £1,800 an episode." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Event-free patients as the adverse event risk pool" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AER-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "event-free-patients-as-the-adverse-event-risk-pool" .

hemo:7000810 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Adverse event as the single exit during the trial period"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The rescaling is exact only when the adverse event is the single exit from the state. When death, progression or stopping treatment compete with it, converting each transition separately gives incorrect numbers of patients moving to each state, so the rates are converted jointly (HE-FM-TP-004) or the model is restructured." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Adverse event as the single exit during the trial period" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AER-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "adverse-event-as-the-single-exit-during-the-trial-period" .

hemo:7000811 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Rescaled adverse event probability in one cell"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000272 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Excel raises the complement of the trial proportion to the ratio of the cycle length to the follow-up period, using named cells in the same time unit." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Rescaled adverse event probability in one cell" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AER-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "rescaled-adverse-event-probability-in-one-cell" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1-(1-TrialProportion)^(CycleLength/FollowUp)" .

hemo:7000812 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Adverse event cycle probability from a rate in one cell"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000272 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Excel returns the cycle probability from named cells holding the adverse event rate and the cycle length in the same time unit." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Adverse event cycle probability from a rate in one cell" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AER-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "adverse-event-cycle-probability-from-a-rate-in-one-cell" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1-EXP(-AERate*CycleLength)" .

hemo:7000813 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Compounded cycle probabilities reproduce the adverse event proportion"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compound the cycle probability over the number of cycles in the follow-up period. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows that the proportion was divided by the number of cycles or rescaled with mismatched time units." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Compounded cycle probabilities reproduce the adverse event proportion" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AER-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "compounded-cycle-probabilities-reproduce-the-adverse-event-proportion" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(1-(1-AECycleProb)^(FollowUp/CycleLength)-TrialProportion)<1E-9" .

hemo:7000814 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Adverse event cycle probability below rate times cycle length"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For a positive rate the cycle probability is above zero and below the rate multiplied by the cycle length. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows that the rate was used directly as a probability." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Adverse event cycle probability below rate times cycle length" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AER-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "adverse-event-cycle-probability-below-rate-times-cycle-length" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(AECycleProb>0,AECycleProb<AERate*CycleLength)" .

hemo:7000815 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Eighteen per cent over 24 weeks rescaled to four-week cycles"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The new drug's 24-week proportion of 0.18 implies a rate of about 0.00827 per patient-week and a four-week probability of about 0.0325. Compounded over six cycles, the probability reproduces the observed 0.18, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Eighteen per cent over 24 weeks rescaled to four-week cycles" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P = 0.18; T = 24; t = 4; r = 0.0082688; p_t = 0.032534" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AER-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "eighteen-per-cent-over-24-weeks-rescaled-to-four-week-cycles" .

hemo:7000816 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Eight per cent over 24 weeks rescaled to four-week cycles"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The comparator's 24-week proportion of 0.08 implies a rate of about 0.00347 per patient-week and a four-week probability of about 0.0138." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Eight per cent over 24 weeks rescaled to four-week cycles" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P = 0.08; T = 24; t = 4; r = 0.0034742; p_t = 0.013801" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AER-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "eight-per-cent-over-24-weeks-rescaled-to-four-week-cycles" .

hemo:7000817 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One cost per episode of each adverse event type"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every episode of event type k costs the same, whatever the arm and whenever it occurs. Where later episodes or episodes in one arm are managed differently, the event type is split." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One cost per episode of each adverse event type" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AER-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-cost-per-episode-of-each-adverse-event-type" .

hemo:7000818 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Stated rule for the adverse events costed"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The events included are stated with a reason. Restricting them to a severity grade, a frequency cut-off or events that differ between arms removes costs, and the restriction is a structural choice to test in scenario analysis." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Stated rule for the adverse events costed" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AER-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "stated-rule-for-the-adverse-events-costed" .

hemo:7000819 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected adverse event cost with SUMPRODUCT"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000273 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the event probabilities in a range named AEProbs and the episode costs in a range of the same size named AEEpisodeCosts, Excel returns the expected cost per patient." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected adverse event cost with SUMPRODUCT" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AER-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-adverse-event-cost-with-sumproduct" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(AEProbs,AEEpisodeCosts)" .

hemo:7000820 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Adverse event probabilities entered as proportions for costing"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every probability lies between 0 and 1. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows percentages entered as whole numbers, for example 18 rather than 0.18, which multiplies the expected cost by 100." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Adverse event probabilities entered as proportions for costing" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AER-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "adverse-event-probabilities-entered-as-proportions-for-costing" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(MIN(AEProbs)>=0,MAX(AEProbs)<=1)" .

hemo:7000821 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected diarrhoea cost on the new drug over 24 weeks"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative figures: with a probability of 0.18 and £1,800 to manage each episode, the expected adverse event cost is £324 per patient over 24 weeks." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected diarrhoea cost on the new drug over 24 weeks" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_k = [0.18]; c_k = [1800]; K = 1; C_AE = 324" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AER-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-diarrhoea-cost-on-the-new-drug-over-24-weeks" .

hemo:7000822 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected diarrhoea cost on the comparator over 24 weeks"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a probability of 0.08 and the same episode cost, the expected cost is £144 per patient, so the new drug adds £180 per patient in adverse event costs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected diarrhoea cost on the comparator over 24 weeks" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_k = [0.08]; c_k = [1800]; K = 1; C_AE = 144" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AER-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-diarrhoea-cost-on-the-comparator-over-24-weeks" .

hemo:7000823 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Short-lived adverse events as one-off decrements"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each episode lowers utility for a limited time and then resolves. Events that cause death, long admissions or lasting disability are modelled as separate health states rather than as one-off decrements." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Short-lived adverse events as one-off decrements" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AER-012" ;
    hemo:9000023 "short-lived-adverse-events-as-one-off-decrements" .

hemo:7000824 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Health state utilities free of adverse event effects"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The utilities of the health states in the model do not already reflect adverse effects. Craig and colleagues found that 53% of the HTA models that included utilities derived them from patients on treatment, so those values could already capture adverse effects." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health state utilities free of adverse event effects" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AER-013" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-state-utilities-free-of-adverse-event-effects" .

hemo:7000825 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected adverse event QALY loss with SUMPRODUCT"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000274 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the event probabilities in AEProbs, the disutilities in AEDisutilities and the episode durations in years in AEEpisodeYears, all ranges of the same size, Excel returns the expected QALY loss per patient." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected adverse event QALY loss with SUMPRODUCT" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AER-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-adverse-event-qaly-loss-with-sumproduct" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(AEProbs,AEDisutilities,AEEpisodeYears)" .

hemo:7000826 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Adverse event episode durations entered in years"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every episode duration is below one year. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows durations entered in days, or an event long enough to need its own health state." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Adverse event episode durations entered in years" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AER-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "adverse-event-episode-durations-entered-in-years" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MAX(AEEpisodeYears)<1" .

hemo:7000827 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected QALY loss from diarrhoea on the new drug"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative figures: a utility decrement of 0.10 for 14 days is a loss of about 0.00384 QALYs per episode. With a probability of 0.18 the expected loss is about 0.00069 QALYs per patient." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected QALY loss from diarrhoea on the new drug" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_k = [0.18]; u_k = [0.10]; tau_k = [0.038356]; K = 1; Q_AE = 0.00069" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AER-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-qaly-loss-from-diarrhoea-on-the-new-drug" .

hemo:7000828 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected QALY loss from diarrhoea on the comparator"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a probability of 0.08 the expected loss is about 0.00031 QALYs per patient. The difference of about 0.00038 QALYs is worth about £8 at an illustrative £20,000 per QALY, the figure used in the article (the NICE manual PMG36, as updated in March 2026, uses £25,000 to £35,000 per QALY). At either value it is small beside the £180 difference in adverse event costs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected QALY loss from diarrhoea on the comparator" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_k = [0.08]; u_k = [0.10]; tau_k = [0.038356]; K = 1; Q_AE = 0.00031" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AER-012" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-qaly-loss-from-diarrhoea-on-the-comparator" .

hemo:7000829 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Candidate models fitted to identical survival data"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every candidate is fitted by maximum likelihood to the same patients, the same endpoint and the same data cut. A model fitted to a different subset or a later data cut has a likelihood for different observations, and its AIC cannot be compared with the others." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Candidate models fitted to identical survival data" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AIC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "candidate-models-fitted-to-identical-survival-data" .

hemo:7000830 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Parameter count includes every estimated parameter"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "k counts all parameters estimated from the data. NICE DSU TSD 14 gives the exponential one parameter, the Weibull, Gompertz, log-logistic and log-normal two each, and the generalised gamma three. In spline-based models the count rises with the number of knots." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Parameter count includes every estimated parameter" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AIC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "parameter-count-includes-every-estimated-parameter" .

hemo:7000831 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Candidate set chosen before fitting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The set of candidate distributions is specified before the models are fitted, and it need not contain a true model. Burnham and Anderson treat a well-chosen set of models specified in advance as a precondition of the method." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Candidate set chosen before fitting" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AIC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "candidate-set-chosen-before-fitting" .

hemo:7000832 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "AIC in one cell from the log-likelihood and parameter count"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000275 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the maximised log-likelihood in a cell named LogLik and the number of estimated parameters in a cell named Params, the formula returns the AIC." ;
    hemo:9000009 "AIC in one cell from the log-likelihood and parameter count" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AIC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "aic-in-one-cell-from-the-log-likelihood-and-parameter-count" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=2*Params-2*LogLik" .

hemo:7000833 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Recomputed AIC matches the fitting software"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compare the AIC reported by the survival software, in a cell named ReportedAIC, with the value recomputed from the reported log-likelihood. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows that the parameter count entered differs from the one the software used, or that the log-likelihood was copied with the wrong sign." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Recomputed AIC matches the fitting software" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AIC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "recomputed-aic-matches-the-fitting-software" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ReportedAIC-(2*Params-2*LogLik))<1E-6" .

hemo:7000834 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Log-normal row of the six-curve example reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Two parameters and a maximised log-likelihood of -603.2 return an AIC of 1210.4. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Log-normal row of the six-curve example reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AIC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "log-normal-row-of-the-six-curve-example-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((2*2-2*(-603.2))-1210.4)<1E-9" .

hemo:7000835 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "AIC of the log-normal curve in a six-curve comparison"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative example, a two-parameter log-normal curve fitted to one trial arm of 300 patients has a maximised log-likelihood of -603.2. Its AIC is 4 plus 1206.4, or 1210.4, the lowest of the six candidates." ;
    hemo:9000009 "AIC of the log-normal curve in a six-curve comparison" ;
    hemo:9000014 "k = 2; lnL = -603.2; AIC = 1210.4" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AIC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "aic-of-the-log-normal-curve-in-a-six-curve-comparison" .

hemo:7000836 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "AIC of the generalised gamma curve in a six-curve comparison"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The three-parameter generalised gamma fits the same data better by 0.4 log-likelihood units, at -602.8, but pays 2 more in penalty. Its AIC of 1211.6 ranks it second, behind the log-normal." ;
    hemo:9000009 "AIC of the generalised gamma curve in a six-curve comparison" ;
    hemo:9000014 "k = 3; lnL = -602.8; AIC = 1211.6" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AIC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "aic-of-the-generalised-gamma-curve-in-a-six-curve-comparison" .

hemo:7000837 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "AIC of the exponential curve in a six-curve comparison"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The one-parameter exponential has the smallest penalty but the poorest fit, with a maximised log-likelihood of -612.4, giving an AIC of 1226.8." ;
    hemo:9000009 "AIC of the exponential curve in a six-curve comparison" ;
    hemo:9000014 "k = 1; lnL = -612.4; AIC = 1226.8" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AIC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "aic-of-the-exponential-curve-in-a-six-curve-comparison" .

hemo:7000838 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Differences read within one candidate set"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "All AIC values come from models fitted to the same observations, and AIC_min is taken over the whole candidate set. The rule-of-thumb bands are rough guides to the strength of evidence, not significance thresholds." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Differences read within one candidate set" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AIC-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "differences-read-within-one-candidate-set" .

hemo:7000839 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "AIC difference in a column of candidate models"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000276 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the AIC values of all candidates in a range named AICRange and the AIC of the current model in a cell named AICValue, the formula returns its difference from the best-scoring model." ;
    hemo:9000009 "AIC difference in a column of candidate models" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AIC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "aic-difference-in-a-column-of-candidate-models" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AICValue-MIN(AICRange)" .

hemo:7000840 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Best-scoring model has an AIC difference of zero"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The smallest difference in the column of AIC differences, named DeltaRange, is exactly 0 and none is negative. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows that the minimum was taken over a different range or that a reference model other than the best was used." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Best-scoring model has an AIC difference of zero" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AIC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "best-scoring-model-has-an-aic-difference-of-zero" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MIN(DeltaRange)=0" .

hemo:7000841 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Weibull curve 3.8 units behind the log-normal"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the six-curve example the Weibull has an AIC of 1214.2 and the log-normal the minimum of 1210.4. The Weibull is 3.8 units behind, with less support than the log-normal on the rule of thumb." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Weibull curve 3.8 units behind the log-normal" ;
    hemo:9000014 "AIC_i = 1214.2; AIC_min = 1210.4; delta = 3.8" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AIC-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "weibull-curve-3-8-units-behind-the-log-normal" .

hemo:7000842 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Generalised gamma curve 1.2 units behind the log-normal"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The generalised gamma, at 1211.6, is 1.2 units behind the log-normal and within the band of substantial support." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Generalised gamma curve 1.2 units behind the log-normal" ;
    hemo:9000014 "AIC_i = 1211.6; AIC_min = 1210.4; delta = 1.2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AIC-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "generalised-gamma-curve-1-2-units-behind-the-log-normal" .

hemo:7000843 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Exponential curve 16.4 units behind the log-normal"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The exponential, at 1226.8, is 16.4 units behind and has almost no support in the set." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Exponential curve 16.4 units behind the log-normal" ;
    hemo:9000014 "AIC_i = 1226.8; AIC_min = 1210.4; delta = 16.4" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AIC-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "exponential-curve-16-4-units-behind-the-log-normal" .

hemo:7000844 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Weights conditional on the full candidate set"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The weights depend on which models are in the set. The ratio of two weights does not, because it equals exp of half the difference between their AIC differences." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Weights conditional on the full candidate set" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AIC-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "weights-conditional-on-the-full-candidate-set" .

hemo:7000845 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Akaike weight from a column of AIC differences"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000277 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With all AIC differences in a range named DeltaRange and the current model's difference in a cell named Delta, SUMPRODUCT forms the sum S without an array entry." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Akaike weight from a column of AIC differences" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AIC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "akaike-weight-from-a-column-of-aic-differences" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=EXP(-Delta/2)/SUMPRODUCT(EXP(-DeltaRange/2))" .

hemo:7000846 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Akaike weights sum to one"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Add the weights of all candidates, held in a range named WeightRange. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows that a candidate was left out of the sum S or that the weights were computed from different differences." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Akaike weights sum to one" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AIC-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "akaike-weights-sum-to-one" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(SUM(WeightRange)-1)<1E-9" .

hemo:7000847 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Akaike weight of the log-normal curve"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The six exponentials of minus half the AIC differences sum to about 2.2561 in the article's example (2.25605 unrounded). The log-normal, with a difference of 0, has a weight of 1 divided by 2.25605, about 0.4433, shown as 0.44 in the article's table." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Akaike weight of the log-normal curve" ;
    hemo:9000014 "delta = 0; S = 2.25605; w = 0.4433" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AIC-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "akaike-weight-of-the-log-normal-curve" .

hemo:7000848 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Akaike weight of the Weibull curve"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The Weibull, 3.8 units behind, has a relative likelihood of about 0.1496 and a weight of about 0.066, shown as 0.07 in the article's table." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Akaike weight of the Weibull curve" ;
    hemo:9000014 "delta = 3.8; S = 2.25605; w = 0.0663" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AIC-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "akaike-weight-of-the-weibull-curve" .

hemo:7000849 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Akaike weight of the generalised gamma curve"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The generalised gamma, 1.2 units behind, has a relative likelihood of about 0.5488 and a weight of about 0.24." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Akaike weight of the generalised gamma curve" ;
    hemo:9000014 "delta = 1.2; S = 2.25605; w = 0.2433" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AIC-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "akaike-weight-of-the-generalised-gamma-curve" .

hemo:7000850 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Choice of n for censored survival data"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The sample size in the penalty is a choice when data are censored. Volinsky and Raftery proposed the number of uncensored events rather than the number of patients, which lowers the penalty when censoring is heavy. The same n is used for every candidate." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Choice of n for censored survival data" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AIC-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "choice-of-n-for-censored-survival-data" .

hemo:7000851 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal prior probabilities for posterior model probabilities"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "BIC differences convert into approximate posterior model probabilities only when every candidate model has an equal prior probability." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equal prior probabilities for posterior model probabilities" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AIC-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equal-prior-probabilities-for-posterior-model-probabilities" .

hemo:7000852 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "BIC in one cell for comparison with AIC"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000278 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Excel LN is the natural logarithm. With named cells LogLik, Params and SampleSize, the formula returns the BIC." ;
    hemo:9000009 "BIC in one cell for comparison with AIC" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AIC-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "bic-in-one-cell-for-comparison-with-aic" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=Params*LN(SampleSize)-2*LogLik" .

hemo:7000853 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "BIC minus AIC equals the extra penalty"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For the same model, with the two criteria in cells named BICValue and AICValue, BIC exceeds AIC by k times the natural logarithm of n minus 2. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows that LOG was used instead of LN or that the two criteria used different parameter counts." ;
    hemo:9000009 "BIC minus AIC equals the extra penalty" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AIC-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "bic-minus-aic-equals-the-extra-penalty" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((BICValue-AICValue)-Params*(LN(SampleSize)-2))<1E-9" .

hemo:7000854 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "BIC of the log-normal curve with 300 patients"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With n of 300, the two-parameter log-normal with a maximised log-likelihood of -603.2 has a BIC of about 1217.81, the lowest of the six curves." ;
    hemo:9000009 "BIC of the log-normal curve with 300 patients" ;
    hemo:9000014 "k = 2; n = 300; lnL = -603.2; BIC = 1217.81" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AIC-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "bic-of-the-log-normal-curve-with-300-patients" .

hemo:7000855 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "BIC of the generalised gamma curve with 300 patients"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The three-parameter generalised gamma has a BIC of about 1222.71. Its distance behind the log-normal grows from 1.2 units on AIC to 4.90 units on BIC, while the two-parameter models keep their AIC gaps." ;
    hemo:9000009 "BIC of the generalised gamma curve with 300 patients" ;
    hemo:9000014 "k = 3; n = 300; lnL = -602.8; BIC = 1222.71" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AIC-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "bic-of-the-generalised-gamma-curve-with-300-patients" .

hemo:7000856 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Sample size exceeds the parameter count plus one"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The correction is defined only when n is greater than k plus 1. All candidates are compared on AICc, not a mixture of AIC and AICc." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Sample size exceeds the parameter count plus one" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AIC-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "sample-size-exceeds-the-parameter-count-plus-one" .

hemo:7000857 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "AICc in one cell with the small-sample correction"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000279 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With named cells LogLik, Params and SampleSize, the formula returns AICc." ;
    hemo:9000009 "AICc in one cell with the small-sample correction" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AIC-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "aicc-in-one-cell-with-the-small-sample-correction" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=2*Params-2*LogLik+2*Params*(Params+1)/(SampleSize-Params-1)" .

hemo:7000858 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "AICc exceeds AIC by the correction term"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For the same model, with the two criteria in cells named AICcValue and AICValue, AICc is above AIC and the gap equals the correction term. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows that the correction used a different n or k from the AIC." ;
    hemo:9000009 "AICc exceeds AIC by the correction term" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AIC-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "aicc-exceeds-aic-by-the-correction-term" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(AICcValue>AICValue,ABS(AICcValue-AICValue-2*Params*(Params+1)/(SampleSize-Params-1))<1E-9)" .

hemo:7000859 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "AICc of the generalised gamma curve with 300 patients"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With 300 patients the ratio of sample size to parameters is 100 for the generalised gamma, and the correction adds only about 0.08 to its AIC of 1211.6. The ranking in the six-curve example is unchanged." ;
    hemo:9000009 "AICc of the generalised gamma curve with 300 patients" ;
    hemo:9000014 "k = 3; lnL = -602.8; n = 300; AICc = 1211.68" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AIC-012" ;
    hemo:9000023 "aicc-of-the-generalised-gamma-curve-with-300-patients" .

hemo:7000860 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "AICc of a three-parameter curve in a hypothetical arm of 30 patients"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In a hypothetical arm of 30 patients, a three-parameter model with a maximised log-likelihood of -100 has an AIC of 206 and an AICc of about 206.92, because the correction rises to about 0.92." ;
    hemo:9000009 "AICc of a three-parameter curve in a hypothetical arm of 30 patients" ;
    hemo:9000014 "k = 3; lnL = -100; n = 30; AICc = 206.92" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AIC-013" ;
    hemo:9000023 "aicc-of-a-three-parameter-curve-in-a-hypothetical-arm-of-30-patients" .

hemo:7000861 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Deaths measure information for a survival outcome"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "With equal allocation the information in the log-rank statistic is approximately one quarter of the number of deaths, so a ratio of deaths approximates the ratio of information. Treating events as information assumes proportional hazards between the arms." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Deaths measure information for a survival outcome" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ASP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "deaths-measure-information-for-a-survival-outcome" .

hemo:7000862 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Final number of deaths estimated while the trial runs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "D is a planning figure that has to be estimated while the trial is in progress. If the estimate of D is revised, the information fractions of later analyses, and so the error they may spend, change with it." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Final number of deaths estimated while the trial runs" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ASP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "final-number-of-deaths-estimated-while-the-trial-runs" .

hemo:7000863 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Information fraction from death counts in one cell"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000280 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Excel divides the deaths observed at the analysis by the planned number of deaths, held in named cells." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Information fraction from death counts in one cell" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ASP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "information-fraction-from-death-counts-in-one-cell" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=Deaths/PlannedDeaths" .

hemo:7000864 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Information fraction lies between 0 and 1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Check that the fraction is above 0 and no greater than 1. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows that the two counts were swapped, that patients were entered instead of deaths, or that observed deaths have passed the planned total so that D needs revisiting." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Information fraction lies between 0 and 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ASP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "information-fraction-lies-between-0-and-1" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(InfoFraction>0,InfoFraction<=1)" .

hemo:7000865 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "First interim look at 100 of 400 planned deaths"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A survival trial planned to end at 400 deaths holds its first interim analysis at 100 deaths, so the information fraction is 0.25." ;
    hemo:9000009 "First interim look at 100 of 400 planned deaths" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d_k = 100; D = 400; t_k = 0.25" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ASP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "first-interim-look-at-100-of-400-planned-deaths" .

hemo:7000866 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Third interim look at 300 of 400 planned deaths"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At the third analysis of the same trial 300 deaths have been observed, giving an information fraction of 0.75." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Third interim look at 300 of 400 planned deaths" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d_k = 300; D = 400; t_k = 0.75" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ASP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "third-interim-look-at-300-of-400-planned-deaths" .

hemo:7000867 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One-sided alpha halved inside the O'Brien-Fleming type quantile"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The function is written with alpha/2 inside the normal quantile, so with alpha set to the one-sided level it returns exactly alpha at t = 1. The trial tests for efficacy in one direction." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One-sided alpha halved inside the O'Brien-Fleming type quantile" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ASP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-sided-alpha-halved-inside-the-o-brien-fleming-type-quantile" .

hemo:7000868 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "O'Brien-Fleming type boundaries solved numerically"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The spend fixes the boundaries only through the joint normal distribution of the test statistics. At the first look the critical value is simply Phi^-1(1 minus alpha_OF(t_1)); from the second look onwards it is found by numerical integration in group sequential software." ;
    hemo:9000009 "O'Brien-Fleming type boundaries solved numerically" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ASP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "o-brien-fleming-type-boundaries-solved-numerically" .

hemo:7000869 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "O'Brien-Fleming type cumulative spend in one cell"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000281 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Excel uses NORM.S.INV for Phi^-1 and NORM.S.DIST with TRUE for Phi, on named cells holding the one-sided level and the information fraction." ;
    hemo:9000009 "O'Brien-Fleming type cumulative spend in one cell" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ASP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "o-brien-fleming-type-cumulative-spend-in-one-cell" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=2-2*NORM.S.DIST(NORM.S.INV(1-Alpha/2)/SQRT(InfoFraction),TRUE)" .

hemo:7000870 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "O'Brien-Fleming type function spends the full level at t of 1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Evaluate the function at the end of the trial. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows that the quantile was taken at 1 minus alpha rather than 1 minus alpha/2, or that a two-sided level was entered." ;
    hemo:9000009 "O'Brien-Fleming type function spends the full level at t of 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ASP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "o-brien-fleming-type-function-spends-the-full-level-at-t-of-1" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(2-2*NORM.S.DIST(NORM.S.INV(1-Alpha/2)/SQRT(1),TRUE)-Alpha)<1E-9" .

hemo:7000871 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "First O'Brien-Fleming type boundary from the spend"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Convert the spend at a quarter of the information into the first critical value. Expected result: TRUE, matching the 4.333 in the article's table." ;
    hemo:9000009 "First O'Brien-Fleming type boundary from the spend" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ASP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "first-o-brien-fleming-type-boundary-from-the-spend" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND(NORM.S.INV(1-(2-2*NORM.S.DIST(NORM.S.INV(1-0.025/2)/SQRT(0.25),TRUE))),3)=4.333" .

hemo:7000872 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "O'Brien-Fleming type spend at a quarter of the information"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a one-sided level of 0.025, Phi^-1(0.9875) is 2.2414. Divided by the square root of 0.25, which is 0.5, it gives 4.4828, and 2 minus 2 Phi(4.4828) is about 0.0000074. The first boundary is then 4.333." ;
    hemo:9000009 "O'Brien-Fleming type spend at a quarter of the information" ;
    hemo:9000014 "alpha = 0.025; t = 0.25; alpha_OF = 0.0000074" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ASP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "o-brien-fleming-type-spend-at-a-quarter-of-the-information" .

hemo:7000873 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "O'Brien-Fleming type spend at half the information"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At t of 0.5 the cumulative spend is about 0.001525, still a small share of the 0.025 budget, as in the article's four-look example." ;
    hemo:9000009 "O'Brien-Fleming type spend at half the information" ;
    hemo:9000014 "alpha = 0.025; t = 0.5; alpha_OF = 0.001525" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ASP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "o-brien-fleming-type-spend-at-half-the-information" .

hemo:7000874 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Pocock type spend measured on the information scale"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "t is the information fraction, deaths observed over deaths planned for a survival outcome, not the share of calendar time or of patients recruited." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pocock type spend measured on the information scale" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ASP-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pocock-type-spend-measured-on-the-information-scale" .

hemo:7000875 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Pocock type boundaries solved from the joint normal distribution"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The function fixes how much error is available, not the critical values. Apart from the first look, where the critical value is Phi^-1(1 minus alpha_P(t_1)), the boundaries are solved numerically from the joint distribution of the test statistics." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pocock type boundaries solved from the joint normal distribution" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ASP-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pocock-type-boundaries-solved-from-the-joint-normal-distribution" .

hemo:7000876 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Pocock type cumulative spend in one cell"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000282 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Excel uses LN, the natural logarithm, and EXP(1) for e, on named cells holding the one-sided level and the information fraction." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pocock type cumulative spend in one cell" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ASP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pocock-type-cumulative-spend-in-one-cell" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=Alpha*LN(1+(EXP(1)-1)*InfoFraction)" .

hemo:7000877 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Pocock type spend matches the gsDesign output"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compute the spend at half the information with a one-sided level of 0.025. Expected result: TRUE, matching the 0.01550286 printed in the gsDesign technical manual." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pocock type spend matches the gsDesign output" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ASP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pocock-type-spend-matches-the-gsdesign-output" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND(0.025*LN(1+(EXP(1)-1)*0.5),8)=0.01550286" .

hemo:7000878 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "First Pocock type boundary from the spend"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Convert the spend at a quarter of the information into the first critical value. Expected result: TRUE, matching the 2.368 in the article's table and the gsDesign manual." ;
    hemo:9000009 "First Pocock type boundary from the spend" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ASP-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "first-pocock-type-boundary-from-the-spend" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND(NORM.S.INV(1-0.025*LN(1+(EXP(1)-1)*0.25)),3)=2.368" .

hemo:7000879 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Pocock type spend at a quarter of the information"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a one-sided level of 0.025 and t of 0.25, the natural logarithm of 1.42957 is 0.35737, so the cumulative spend is 0.025 times 0.35737, about 0.008934. The first boundary is then 2.368." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pocock type spend at a quarter of the information" ;
    hemo:9000014 "alpha = 0.025; t = 0.25; alpha_P = 0.008934" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ASP-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pocock-type-spend-at-a-quarter-of-the-information" .

hemo:7000880 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Pocock type spend at half the information"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At t of 0.5 the cumulative spend is about 0.015503, already more than half of the 0.025 budget." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pocock type spend at half the information" ;
    hemo:9000014 "alpha = 0.025; t = 0.5; alpha_P = 0.015503" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ASP-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pocock-type-spend-at-half-the-information" .

hemo:7000881 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Spending function chosen before the first look"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Only the spending function has to be fixed in advance; the number and timing of looks may vary. Choosing analysis times in response to accumulating results can still inflate the type I error." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Spending function chosen before the first look" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ASP-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "spending-function-chosen-before-the-first-look" .

hemo:7000882 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Increment is a probability of first crossing"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The increment is the probability, under the null hypothesis, that the trial first crosses a boundary at analysis k. The critical value that achieves it depends on the earlier boundaries and the correlation between looks, so it is not the normal quantile of the increment." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Increment is a probability of first crossing" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ASP-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "increment-is-a-probability-of-first-crossing" .

hemo:7000883 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Alpha spending increment from two cumulative spends"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000283 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the cumulative spend at this look and at the previous look in named cells, Excel returns the increment. At the first look the previous spend cell holds 0." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Alpha spending increment from two cumulative spends" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ASP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "alpha-spending-increment-from-two-cumulative-spends" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SpendThisLook-SpendPrevLook" .

hemo:7000884 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Alpha spending increments sum to the overall level"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the increments for every planned look in a range named Increments, check that none is negative and that they sum to alpha. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Alpha spending increments sum to the overall level" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ASP-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "alpha-spending-increments-sum-to-the-overall-level" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(MIN(Increments)>=0,ABS(SUM(Increments)-Alpha)<1E-9)" .

hemo:7000885 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Second look increment under O'Brien-Fleming type spending"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With cumulative spends of about 0.001525 at t of 0.5 and 0.0000074 at t of 0.25, the O'Brien-Fleming type plan may use about 0.001518 at the second look." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Second look increment under O'Brien-Fleming type spending" ;
    hemo:9000014 "alpha_k = 0.001525; alpha_(k-1) = 0.0000074; Delta_alpha_k = 0.001518" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ASP-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "second-look-increment-under-o-brien-fleming-type-spending" .

hemo:7000886 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Second look increment under Pocock type spending"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With cumulative spends of 0.015503 at t of 0.5 and 0.008934 at t of 0.25, the Pocock type plan may use 0.006569 at the second look, less than at the first." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Second look increment under Pocock type spending" ;
    hemo:9000014 "alpha_k = 0.015503; alpha_(k-1) = 0.008934; Delta_alpha_k = 0.006569" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ASP-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "second-look-increment-under-pocock-type-spending" .

hemo:7000887 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal allocation and log-rank information of d/4"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Patients are randomised 1:1, so the information is about one quarter of the deaths and the standard error of the log hazard ratio is about 2 divided by the square root of d_k. A lower hazard ratio favours the experimental arm." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equal allocation and log-rank information of d/4" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ASP-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equal-allocation-and-log-rank-information-of-d-4" .

hemo:7000888 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Proportional hazards for the stopping hazard ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The conversion assumes proportional hazards. With a delayed treatment effect the hazard ratio thresholds, and the chance of stopping at early looks, can mislead, although type I error control is unaffected." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Proportional hazards for the stopping hazard ratio" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ASP-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "proportional-hazards-for-the-stopping-hazard-ratio" .

hemo:7000889 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Stopping hazard ratio from a critical value in one cell"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000284 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Excel returns the hazard ratio threshold from named cells holding the critical value and the number of deaths at the analysis." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Stopping hazard ratio from a critical value in one cell" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ASP-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "stopping-hazard-ratio-from-a-critical-value-in-one-cell" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=EXP(-2*CritValue/SQRT(Deaths))" .

hemo:7000890 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Stopping hazard ratio converts back to the critical value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Convert the threshold back into a critical value. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows that LOG was used instead of LN or that patients rather than deaths were entered." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Stopping hazard ratio converts back to the critical value" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ASP-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "stopping-hazard-ratio-converts-back-to-the-critical-value" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(-LN(HRThreshold)*SQRT(Deaths)/2-CritValue)<1E-9" .

hemo:7000891 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Second O'Brien-Fleming type look at 200 deaths"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a critical value of 2.963 at 200 deaths, 2 times 2.963 is 5.926, divided by the square root of 200, 14.142, gives 0.4190, and exp(-0.4190) is about 0.658. An observed hazard ratio of 0.658 or lower crosses the boundary." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Second O'Brien-Fleming type look at 200 deaths" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c_k = 2.963; d_k = 200; HR_k = 0.658" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ASP-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "second-o-brien-fleming-type-look-at-200-deaths" .

hemo:7000892 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "First Pocock type look at 100 deaths"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a critical value of 2.368 at 100 deaths the trial stops only if the observed hazard ratio is about 0.623 or lower." ;
    hemo:9000009 "First Pocock type look at 100 deaths" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c_k = 2.368; d_k = 100; HR_k = 0.623" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ASP-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "first-pocock-type-look-at-100-deaths" .

hemo:7000893 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Final O'Brien-Fleming type analysis at 400 deaths"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At the final analysis the critical value of 2.014 at 400 deaths corresponds to an observed hazard ratio of about 0.818." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Final O'Brien-Fleming type analysis at 400 deaths" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c_k = 2.014; d_k = 400; HR_k = 0.818" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ASP-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "final-o-brien-fleming-type-analysis-at-400-deaths" .

hemo:7000894 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "First annuity instalment paid at treatment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each instalment falls at the start of a year and the first is paid at the point of treatment, as in the annuity-due set-up of Van Dyck et al. With payments at the end of each year the ordinary annuity form applies instead, and it gives a larger instalment for the same price and rate." ;
    hemo:9000009 "First annuity instalment paid at treatment" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "first-annuity-instalment-paid-at-treatment" .

hemo:7000895 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Fixed financing rate and equal annuity instalments"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The financing rate is fixed for the whole schedule and every instalment is the same size. The rate is a commercial term set in negotiation rather than by HTA methods guidance, and reflects the cost to whoever finances the deferral of waiting for the money and of the risk that later payments are not made." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fixed financing rate and equal annuity instalments" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fixed-financing-rate-and-equal-annuity-instalments" .

hemo:7000896 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Annuity-due instalment with Excel PMT"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000285 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Excel PMT with a type argument of 1 places each payment at the start of the period. The price is entered as a negative present value so that the instalment is returned as a positive amount. The explicit form =Price*FinRate/((1-(1+FinRate)^(-NInstal))*(1+FinRate)) gives the same result for any financing rate above zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Annuity-due instalment with Excel PMT" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "annuity-due-instalment-with-excel-pmt" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=PMT(FinRate,NInstal,-Price,0,1)" .

hemo:7000897 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Instalments at the financing rate reproduce the therapy price"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Discount the instalments at the financing rate with Excel PV, payments at the start of each year. The Instalment cell holds the unrounded PMT result, not a value rounded to whole pounds. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows a wrong type argument or a rate entered as a percentage, for example 5 instead of 0.05." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Instalments at the financing rate reproduce the therapy price" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "instalments-at-the-financing-rate-reproduce-the-therapy-price" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(PV(FinRate,NInstal,-Instalment,0,1)-Price)<1E-6" .

hemo:7000898 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "£1 million therapy paid in five instalments at 5% financing"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A one-off therapy priced at £1,000,000 is paid in five yearly instalments, the first at treatment, at a financing rate of 5%. The instalment is about £219,976 and the nominal total is about £1,099,880, about 10% more than the upfront price, as in the article's worked example." ;
    hemo:9000009 "£1 million therapy paid in five instalments at 5% financing" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P = 1000000; i = 0.05; n = 5; A = 219976" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "1-million-therapy-paid-in-five-instalments-at-5-financing" .

hemo:7000899 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "€1 million advanced therapy over five years at a 3% bond rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Van Dyck et al. price an advanced therapy at €1 million paid with five annuities. At a 3% bond rate the instalment due at the start of each year is about €211,995, and at a 10% corporate bond rate it rises to about €239,816." ;
    hemo:9000009 "€1 million advanced therapy over five years at a 3% bond rate" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P = 1000000; i = 0.03; n = 5; A = 211995" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "1-million-advanced-therapy-over-five-years-at-a-3-bond-rate" .

hemo:7000900 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Every fixed annuity instalment is paid"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every instalment falls due once the patient is treated, whatever happens to the benefit, as in a fixed annuity. When later payments depend on continued response, the expected present value HE-FM-AP-003 applies instead." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Every fixed annuity instalment is paid" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "every-fixed-annuity-instalment-is-paid" .

hemo:7000901 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Yearly discounting of instalments from the date of treatment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Year 0 is the date of treatment and its payment is not discounted, and later payments are discounted once a year at a constant rate r. The NICE manual (PMG36) sets 3.5% a year for costs and health effects in the reference case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Yearly discounting of instalments from the date of treatment" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "yearly-discounting-of-instalments-from-the-date-of-treatment" .

hemo:7000902 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Present value of fixed instalments with Excel PV"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000286 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Excel PV with a type argument of 1 discounts payments made at the start of each period. The instalment is entered as a negative payment so that the present value is returned as a positive amount." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Present value of fixed instalments with Excel PV" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "present-value-of-fixed-instalments-with-excel-pv" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=PV(DiscRate,NInstal,-Instalment,0,1)" .

hemo:7000903 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Closed form matches the year-by-year discounted instalments"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the years 0 to n minus 1 in a range named YearIndex, compare the closed form with the sum of the discounted instalments. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows that the first payment was discounted or that type 0 was used." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Closed form matches the year-by-year discounted instalments" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "closed-form-matches-the-year-by-year-discounted-instalments" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(PV(DiscRate,NInstal,-Instalment,0,1)-SUMPRODUCT(Instalment/(1+DiscRate)^YearIndex))<1E-6" .

hemo:7000904 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Five £219,976 instalments discounted at 3.5%"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Five instalments of £219,976 discounted at 3.5% have a present value of about £1,027,965, about 2.8% more than the £1 million upfront price, because the 5% financing rate exceeds the evaluation rate. The article's £1,027,948 uses discount factors rounded to four decimal places." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Five £219,976 instalments discounted at 3.5%" ;
    hemo:9000014 "A = 219976; r = 0.035; n = 5; PV = 1027965" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "five-219-976-instalments-discounted-at-3-5" .

hemo:7000905 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Interest-free split of a £1 million therapy over five years"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Five payments of £200,000 with no financing charge have a present value at 3.5% of about £934,616, about 6.5% below the upfront price, so the split works as a hidden price reduction." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Interest-free split of a £1 million therapy over five years" ;
    hemo:9000014 "A = 200000; r = 0.035; n = 5; PV = 934616" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "interest-free-split-of-a-1-million-therapy-over-five-years" .

hemo:7000906 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Outcomes-based payments stop once response is lost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each later instalment is due only if the patient meets the response criterion on that date, and payments stop when the criterion is not met, so S_t does not rise from one year to the next. Contract rules decide what happens for patients who die, move to another payer or are lost to follow-up." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Outcomes-based payments stop once response is lost" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AP-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "outcomes-based-payments-stop-once-response-is-lost" .

hemo:7000907 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Outcomes-based instalment fixed at the start"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The instalment A is agreed at the start and is not recalculated when payments stop, so the manufacturer bears the cost of the instalments that are not paid." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Outcomes-based instalment fixed at the start" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AP-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "outcomes-based-instalment-fixed-at-the-start" .

hemo:7000908 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected present value of outcomes-based instalments with SUMPRODUCT"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000287 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the probabilities for years 0 to T in a range named PayProb and the matching years in a range named YearIndex, SUMPRODUCT weights each discounted instalment by the probability that it is payable." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected present value of outcomes-based instalments with SUMPRODUCT" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-present-value-of-outcomes-based-instalments-with-sumproduct" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(PayProb,Instalment/(1+DiscRate)^YearIndex)" .

hemo:7000909 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Outcomes-based value no higher than the fixed schedule"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With every probability between 0 and 1, the expected present value is at most the present value of the same instalments paid in full, and equals it when every probability is 1. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Outcomes-based value no higher than the fixed schedule" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "outcomes-based-value-no-higher-than-the-fixed-schedule" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(PayProb,Instalment/(1+DiscRate)^YearIndex)<=PV(DiscRate,NInstal,-Instalment,0,1)+1E-6" .

hemo:7000910 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Outcomes-based payment at treatment is certain"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The first probability, for the payment made at treatment, equals 1. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Outcomes-based payment at treatment is certain" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "outcomes-based-payment-at-treatment-is-certain" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=INDEX(PayProb,1)=1" .

hemo:7000911 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Outcomes-based £1 million schedule with falling response"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With instalments of £219,976 discounted at 3.5%, and probabilities of 0.90, 0.85, 0.80 and 0.75 that the patient is alive and still responding at years 1 to 4, the expected present value is about £888,304, about 11% below the upfront price. The article's £888,285 uses rounded discount factors. The reduction reflects payments avoided for patients whose benefit ends, and those patients also contribute fewer QALYs, so it is not a price discount." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Outcomes-based £1 million schedule with falling response" ;
    hemo:9000014 "T = 4; A = 219976; r = 0.035; t = [0,1,2,3,4]; S_t = [1,0.90,0.85,0.80,0.75]; EPV = 888303.82" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AP-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "outcomes-based-1-million-schedule-with-falling-response" .

hemo:7000912 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Asymptotically normal estimator at the sample size used"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The sampling distribution of the estimator is close to normal at the sample size available. For a difference in arithmetic means this follows from the central limit theorem whatever the shape of patient-level costs, provided observations are independent with finite variance. A long right tail in a small trial slows the approach to normality." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Asymptotically normal estimator at the sample size used" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AN-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "asymptotically-normal-estimator-at-the-sample-size-used" .

hemo:7000913 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Standard error estimated on the right basis for the Wald interval"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The standard error reflects how the data were generated: clustered observations, as in a cluster-randomised trial, need cluster-robust standard errors, and a model-based standard error from a misspecified model is unreliable. The interval describes sampling uncertainty in one dataset only, not the choice of data source, model structure or extrapolation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Standard error estimated on the right basis for the Wald interval" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AN-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "standard-error-estimated-on-the-right-basis-for-the-wald-interval" .

hemo:7000914 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Wald interval limits from an estimate and its standard error"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000288 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the estimate in a cell named Estimate and its standard error in StdError, the two formulas return the lower and upper limits. NORM.S.INV(0.975) returns 1.959964, which rounds to 1.96." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Wald interval limits from an estimate and its standard error" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AN-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "wald-interval-limits-from-an-estimate-and-its-standard-error" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=Estimate-NORM.S.INV(0.975)*StdError; =Estimate+NORM.S.INV(0.975)*StdError" .

hemo:7000915 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Wald interval is centred on the estimate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The midpoint of the two limits equals the estimate. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows that different multipliers or standard errors were used for the two limits." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Wald interval is centred on the estimate" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AN-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "wald-interval-is-centred-on-the-estimate" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((WaldLower+WaldUpper)/2-Estimate)<1E-9*MAX(1,ABS(Estimate))" .

hemo:7000916 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Wald interval width equals twice the critical value times the standard error"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The distance between the limits is twice NORM.S.INV(0.975) times the standard error. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows a different multiplier or a standard deviation entered in place of the standard error elsewhere in the sheet." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Wald interval width equals twice the critical value times the standard error" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AN-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "wald-interval-width-equals-twice-the-critical-value-times-the-standard-error" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((WaldUpper-WaldLower)-2*NORM.S.INV(0.975)*StdError)<1E-9*MAX(1,StdError)" .

hemo:7000917 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Difference in mean cost of £2,000 with a standard error of £600"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative trial, 250 patients per arm and a per-patient cost standard deviation of £6,708 in each arm give a standard error of about £600 for the cost difference. The approximate 95% interval for the £2,000 difference runs from £824 to £3,176." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Difference in mean cost of £2,000 with a standard error of £600" ;
    hemo:9000014 "theta_hat = 2000; SE = 600; z = 1.96; L = 824; U = 3176" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AN-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "difference-in-mean-cost-of-2-000-with-a-standard-error-of-600" .

hemo:7000918 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Incremental net benefit of £1,000 with a standard error of about £1,342"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The same rule applied to the article's incremental net benefit at £30,000 per QALY, with a standard error of about £1,342, gives an interval from about -£1,630 to £3,630, as in HE-FM-AN-004." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Incremental net benefit of £1,000 with a standard error of about £1,342" ;
    hemo:9000014 "theta_hat = 1000; SE = 1341.6408; z = 1.96; L = -1629.62; U = 3629.62" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AN-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "incremental-net-benefit-of-1-000-with-a-standard-error-of-about-1-342" .

hemo:7000919 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Zero standard error collapses the Wald interval"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a standard error of zero both limits equal the estimate, a limiting case that checks the implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Zero standard error collapses the Wald interval" ;
    hemo:9000014 "theta_hat = 2000; SE = 0; z = 1.96; L = 2000; U = 2000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AN-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "zero-standard-error-collapses-the-wald-interval" .

hemo:7000920 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Effect difference well away from zero for the delta method"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The incremental effect is large relative to its standard error. When its sampling distribution has positive density at zero, the ratio estimator has no finite mean or variance in any finite sample, so the delta-method variance describes only the limiting distribution and the symmetric interval can be a poor guide in trials of realistic size." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Effect difference well away from zero for the delta method" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AN-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "effect-difference-well-away-from-zero-for-the-delta-method" .

hemo:7000921 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Non-zero cost and effect differences in the relative variance form"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Both estimated differences are non-zero, because the relative form of the variance divides by each of them." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Non-zero cost and effect differences in the relative variance form" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AN-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "non-zero-cost-and-effect-differences-in-the-relative-variance-form" .

hemo:7000922 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Delta-method ICER limits in two cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000289 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With named cells DeltaCost, DeltaEffect, SeCost, SeEffect, CovCostEffect and ZValue, the two formulas return the lower and upper limits." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Delta-method ICER limits in two cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AN-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "delta-method-icer-limits-in-two-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=DeltaCost/DeltaEffect-ZValue*ABS(DeltaCost/DeltaEffect)*SQRT(SeCost^2/DeltaCost^2+SeEffect^2/DeltaEffect^2-2*CovCostEffect/(DeltaCost*DeltaEffect)); =DeltaCost/DeltaEffect+ZValue*ABS(DeltaCost/DeltaEffect)*SQRT(SeCost^2/DeltaCost^2+SeEffect^2/DeltaEffect^2-2*CovCostEffect/(DeltaCost*DeltaEffect))" .

hemo:7000923 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Delta-method standard error matches the equivalent variance form"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The squared delta-method standard error, held in DeltaSE, equals the cost variance plus the squared ICER times the effect variance, less twice the ICER times the covariance, all divided by the squared effect difference. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows an error in the relative form, such as a missing square or a wrong sign on the covariance term." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Delta-method standard error matches the equivalent variance form" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AN-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "delta-method-standard-error-matches-the-equivalent-variance-form" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(DeltaSE^2-(SeCost^2+ICER^2*SeEffect^2-2*ICER*CovCostEffect)/DeltaEffect^2)<1E-9*DeltaSE^2" .

hemo:7000924 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Delta-method interval is centred on the ICER"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The midpoint of the two delta-method limits equals the ICER. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Delta-method interval is centred on the ICER" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AN-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "delta-method-interval-is-centred-on-the-icer" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((DeltaLower+DeltaUpper)/2-ICER)<1E-9*MAX(1,ABS(ICER))" .

hemo:7000925 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Delta-method interval for an ICER of £20,000 per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's trial, a cost difference of £2,000 and a QALY gain of 0.10, with standard errors of £600 and 0.04 and no correlation, give an ICER of £20,000 per QALY with a standard error of £10,000. The approximate 95% interval runs from £400 to £39,600 per QALY." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Delta-method interval for an ICER of £20,000 per QALY" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Delta_C = 2000; Delta_E = 0.10; SE_C = 600; SE_E = 0.04; Cov_CE = 0; z = 1.96; R = 20000; SE_R = 10000; R_L = 400; R_U = 39600" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AN-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "delta-method-interval-for-an-icer-of-20-000-per-qaly" .

hemo:7000926 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Delta-method interval with a QALY gain that is not significant"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the QALY standard error raised to 0.06 the effect difference is no longer significant at the 5% level, yet the delta method still returns a bounded interval of about -£6,296 to £46,296 per QALY. Fieller's method gives an unbounded set for the same data." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Delta-method interval with a QALY gain that is not significant" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Delta_C = 2000; Delta_E = 0.10; SE_C = 600; SE_E = 0.06; Cov_CE = 0; z = 1.96; R = 20000; SE_R = 13416.41; R_L = -6296.16; R_U = 46296.16" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AN-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "delta-method-interval-with-a-qaly-gain-that-is-not-significant" .

hemo:7000927 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Jointly normal cost and effect differences"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The two differences are approximately jointly normal, which the central limit theorem supports because each is a difference of averages. No normal approximation is applied to the ratio itself." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Jointly normal cost and effect differences" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AN-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "jointly-normal-cost-and-effect-differences" .

hemo:7000928 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Bounded Fieller interval only when a is positive"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The roots bound an interval only when a is positive, that is when the effect difference divided by its standard error exceeds z. When a is negative and the discriminant positive, the confidence set is every value below the smaller root together with every value above the larger root; when the discriminant is also negative, the set is the whole real line." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Bounded Fieller interval only when a is positive" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AN-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "bounded-fieller-interval-only-when-a-is-positive" .

hemo:7000929 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Fieller limits from three helper cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000290 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The first three formulas fill helper cells named QuadA, QuadB and QuadC; the last two return the lower and upper limits, which bound an interval only when QuadA is positive." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fieller limits from three helper cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AN-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fieller-limits-from-three-helper-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=DeltaEffect^2-ZValue^2*SeEffect^2; =-2*(DeltaCost*DeltaEffect-ZValue^2*CovCostEffect); =DeltaCost^2-ZValue^2*SeCost^2; =(-QuadB-SQRT(QuadB^2-4*QuadA*QuadC))/(2*QuadA); =(-QuadB+SQRT(QuadB^2-4*QuadA*QuadC))/(2*QuadA)" .

hemo:7000930 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Fieller limit solves the defining equation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At the lower limit the squared difference between the cost difference and the limit times the effect difference equals z squared times its variance. Expected result: TRUE; the same check applies to the upper limit." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fieller limit solves the defining equation" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AN-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fieller-limit-solves-the-defining-equation" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((DeltaCost-FiellerLower*DeltaEffect)^2/(ZValue^2*(SeCost^2+FiellerLower^2*SeEffect^2-2*FiellerLower*CovCostEffect))-1)<1E-9" .

hemo:7000931 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Bounded Fieller interval check"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Expected result: TRUE for the article's trial. FALSE means the effect difference is not significant at the chosen level and the two roots do not bound an interval." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Bounded Fieller interval check" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AN-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "bounded-fieller-interval-check" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=DeltaEffect^2>ZValue^2*SeEffect^2" .

hemo:7000932 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Fieller limits match the zero crossings of the net benefit interval"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At a threshold equal to the lower Fieller limit, the upper confidence limit of incremental net benefit is zero, as in the article's check at £7,017 per QALY. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fieller limits match the zero crossings of the net benefit interval" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AN-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fieller-limits-match-the-zero-crossings-of-the-net-benefit-interval" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(FiellerLower*DeltaEffect-DeltaCost+ZValue*SQRT(FiellerLower^2*SeEffect^2+SeCost^2-2*FiellerLower*CovCostEffect))<1E-6" .

hemo:7000933 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Fieller interval for the article's trial"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's figures the quadratic has a of about 0.0038534, b of -400 and c of 2,617,024, and its roots give a 95% interval of about £7,017 to £96,786 per QALY. The interval is asymmetric about the ICER of £20,000 per QALY." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fieller interval for the article's trial" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Delta_C = 2000; Delta_E = 0.10; SE_C = 600; SE_E = 0.04; Cov_CE = 0; z = 1.96; a = 0.0038534; b = -400; c = 2617024; R_L = 7016.89; R_U = 96786.47" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AN-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fieller-interval-for-the-article-s-trial" .

hemo:7000934 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No sampling uncertainty collapses the Fieller interval"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With both standard errors set to zero the discriminant is zero and both limits equal the ICER, here £2,000 divided by 0.5, a limiting case that checks the implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No sampling uncertainty collapses the Fieller interval" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Delta_C = 2000; Delta_E = 0.5; SE_C = 0; SE_E = 0; Cov_CE = 0; z = 1.96; a = 0.25; b = -2000; c = 4000000; R_L = 4000; R_U = 4000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AN-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-sampling-uncertainty-collapses-the-fieller-interval" .

hemo:7000935 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Normal approximation for a linear combination of mean differences"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "INB is a linear combination of two differences in means, so the central limit theorem applies to it directly and the approximation does not break down when Delta_E is close to zero. The probability describes sampling uncertainty in one dataset only." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Normal approximation for a linear combination of mean differences" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AN-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "normal-approximation-for-a-linear-combination-of-mean-differences" .

hemo:7000936 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Fixed, stated threshold for the net benefit interval"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "lambda is fixed by the analyst, and the interval and probability are recomputed at each threshold. Across a range of thresholds the probabilities trace the acceptability curve." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fixed, stated threshold for the net benefit interval" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AN-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fixed-stated-threshold-for-the-net-benefit-interval" .

hemo:7000937 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Net benefit interval and probability in five cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000291 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With named cells Threshold, DeltaEffect, DeltaCost, SeEffect, SeCost, CovCostEffect and ZValue, and the first two results named IncNB and SeINB, the formulas return INB, its standard error, the two limits and the probability that INB is positive." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net benefit interval and probability in five cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AN-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-benefit-interval-and-probability-in-five-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=Threshold*DeltaEffect-DeltaCost; =SQRT(Threshold^2*SeEffect^2+SeCost^2-2*Threshold*CovCostEffect); =IncNB-ZValue*SeINB; =IncNB+ZValue*SeINB; =NORM.S.DIST(IncNB/SeINB,TRUE)" .

hemo:7000938 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Probability of positive net benefit is one half at the ICER"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Evaluated at a threshold equal to the ICER, the probability that net benefit is positive is 0.5. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Probability of positive net benefit is one half at the ICER" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AN-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "probability-of-positive-net-benefit-is-one-half-at-the-icer" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(NORM.S.DIST((ICER*DeltaEffect-DeltaCost)/SQRT(ICER^2*SeEffect^2+SeCost^2-2*ICER*CovCostEffect),TRUE)-0.5)<1E-9" .

hemo:7000939 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Probability at the lower Fieller limit is 0.025"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Evaluated at a threshold equal to the lower Fieller limit, the probability that net benefit is positive is 0.025, up to rounding of z to 1.96. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Probability at the lower Fieller limit is 0.025" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AN-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "probability-at-the-lower-fieller-limit-is-0-025" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(NORM.S.DIST((FiellerLower*DeltaEffect-DeltaCost)/SQRT(FiellerLower^2*SeEffect^2+SeCost^2-2*FiellerLower*CovCostEffect),TRUE)-0.025)<1E-4" .

hemo:7000940 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Net benefit interval at £30,000 per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At an illustrative threshold of £30,000 per QALY, incremental net benefit is £1,000 with a variance of 1,800,000 and a standard error of about £1,342. The 95% interval runs from about -£1,630 to £3,630, which includes zero, and z_INB of about 0.745 gives a probability of about 0.77 that net benefit is positive." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net benefit interval at £30,000 per QALY" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 30000; Delta_E = 0.10; Delta_C = 2000; SE_E = 0.04; SE_C = 600; Cov_CE = 0; z = 1.96; INB = 1000; SE_INB = 1341.6408; INB_L = -1629.62; INB_U = 3629.62; z_INB = 0.7454" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AN-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-benefit-interval-at-30-000-per-qaly" .

hemo:7000941 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Threshold equal to the ICER gives zero net benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At a threshold equal to the ICER of £20,000 per QALY, incremental net benefit is zero, so z_INB is zero and the probability that net benefit is positive is one half, a limiting case that checks the implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Threshold equal to the ICER gives zero net benefit" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 20000; Delta_E = 0.10; Delta_C = 2000; SE_E = 0.04; SE_C = 600; Cov_CE = 0; z = 1.96; INB = 0; SE_INB = 1000; INB_L = -1960; INB_U = 1960; z_INB = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AN-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "threshold-equal-to-the-icer-gives-zero-net-benefit" .

hemo:7000942 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "EDE and Atkinson index computed at the same epsilon"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The EDE is computed at the epsilon for which the index is reported. Index values at different epsilon rest on different value judgements and are not compared with each other, so results are shown across a range of epsilon." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EDE and Atkinson index computed at the same epsilon" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ATK-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ede-and-atkinson-index-computed-at-the-same-epsilon" .

hemo:7000943 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Positive ratio-scale health in the Atkinson index"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Health is measured on a ratio scale with every value above zero, such as QALE in years. Negative values cannot be used, and an index of inequality in ill health is not the mirror image of an index of inequality in health." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Positive ratio-scale health in the Atkinson index" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ATK-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "positive-ratio-scale-health-in-the-atkinson-index" .

hemo:7000944 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Relative inequality concept of the Atkinson index"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The index measures relative inequality: multiplying every health value by the same factor leaves it unchanged, while adding the same number of years to everyone lowers it. The choice between relative and absolute measures, such as the Kolm index, is a value judgement, and the DCEA tutorial suggests calculating several measures when there is no clear choice." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Relative inequality concept of the Atkinson index" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ATK-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "relative-inequality-concept-of-the-atkinson-index" .

hemo:7000945 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Atkinson index from EDE and mean health in one cell"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000292 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the EDE in a cell named EDE and mean health in a cell named MeanHealth, both computed at the same epsilon and in the same unit, Excel returns the Atkinson index." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Atkinson index from EDE and mean health in one cell" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ATK-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "atkinson-index-from-ede-and-mean-health-in-one-cell" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1-EDE/MeanHealth" .

hemo:7000946 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Atkinson index lies between zero and one"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For any epsilon of zero or above the EDE cannot exceed the mean, so the index is at least 0 and below 1. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows an EDE computed with a negative epsilon, with the exponent entered as one plus epsilon, or from a different distribution than the mean." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Atkinson index lies between zero and one" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ATK-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "atkinson-index-lies-between-zero-and-one" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(AtkinsonIndex>=0,AtkinsonIndex<1)" .

hemo:7000947 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Atkinson index for programme U at epsilon of 2"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative two-group example, programme U gives QALE of 60.1 and 72.5 years in two equal-sized groups, a mean of 66.30 years and an EDE at epsilon of 2 of about 65.7202 years. The Atkinson index is about 0.00874, above the baseline value of about 0.00826, so U widens relative inequality." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Atkinson index for programme U at epsilon of 2" ;
    hemo:9000014 "h_EDE = 65.7202; h_bar = 66.3; A_epsilon = 0.008745" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ATK-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "atkinson-index-for-programme-u-at-epsilon-of-2" .

hemo:7000948 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Atkinson index for programme T at epsilon of 2"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Programme T gives QALE of 60.4 and 72.1 years, a mean of 66.25 years and an EDE at epsilon of 2 of about 65.7334 years. The Atkinson index is about 0.00780, below the baseline value of about 0.00826, so T narrows relative inequality." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Atkinson index for programme T at epsilon of 2" ;
    hemo:9000014 "h_EDE = 65.7334; h_bar = 66.25; A_epsilon = 0.00780" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ATK-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "atkinson-index-for-programme-t-at-epsilon-of-2" .

hemo:7000949 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant relative inequality aversion in the Atkinson EDE"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The social welfare function is symmetric and additively separable, with constant relative inequality aversion, so scaling every health value by the same factor scales the EDE by that factor. Atkinson himself described the additively separable form as restrictive." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant relative inequality aversion in the Atkinson EDE" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ATK-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-relative-inequality-aversion-in-the-atkinson-ede" .

hemo:7000950 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal weight for each person or group in the Atkinson EDE"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each h_i counts once, so each value stands for one person or for a group of the same size as the others. Groups of unequal size are weighted by their population shares, as in the computational function HE-CF-ATK-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equal weight for each person or group in the Atkinson EDE" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ATK-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equal-weight-for-each-person-or-group-in-the-atkinson-ede" .

hemo:7000951 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Strictly positive health values in the Atkinson EDE"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "With epsilon above 1 the exponent one minus epsilon is negative, so a value of zero cannot be used, and negative values have no meaning in the formula. It applies to ratio-scale health such as QALE, not to net health gains or losses that can fall to zero or below." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Strictly positive health values in the Atkinson EDE" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ATK-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "strictly-positive-health-values-in-the-atkinson-ede" .

hemo:7000952 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Atkinson EDE for epsilon other than 1 with SUMPRODUCT"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000293 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With one health value per person or per equal-sized group in a range named Health and the aversion parameter in a cell named Epsilon, not equal to 1, Excel returns the EDE. SUMPRODUCT evaluates the power for each cell without an array entry." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Atkinson EDE for epsilon other than 1 with SUMPRODUCT" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ATK-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "atkinson-ede-for-epsilon-other-than-1-with-sumproduct" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(SUMPRODUCT(Health^(1-Epsilon))/COUNT(Health))^(1/(1-Epsilon))" .

hemo:7000953 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Atkinson EDE lies between the lowest value and the mean"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For epsilon of zero or above the EDE is no lower than the least healthy value and no higher than the mean. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows the exponent entered as one plus epsilon, a negative epsilon, or values from another range." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Atkinson EDE lies between the lowest value and the mean" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ATK-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "atkinson-ede-lies-between-the-lowest-value-and-the-mean" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(EDE>=MIN(Health)-1E-9,EDE<=AVERAGE(Health)+1E-9)" .

hemo:7000954 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Atkinson EDE at epsilon of zero equals the mean"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With epsilon set to 0 the formula returns the arithmetic mean, the ranking of a conventional analysis of total health. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Atkinson EDE at epsilon of zero equals the mean" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ATK-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "atkinson-ede-at-epsilon-of-zero-equals-the-mean" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((SUMPRODUCT(Health^(1-0))/COUNT(Health))^(1/(1-0))-AVERAGE(Health))<1E-9" .

hemo:7000955 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Atkinson EDE for programme U at epsilon of 2"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:1000037 hemo:7000956,
        hemo:7000957,
        hemo:7000958 ;
    hemo:1000038 hemo:7000959 ;
    hemo:2000008 0.00005 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Programme U gives QALE of 60.1 and 72.5 years in two equal-sized groups. At epsilon of 2 the EDE is the harmonic mean of the two values, about 65.7202 years, against a mean of 66.30." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Atkinson EDE for programme U at epsilon of 2" ;
    hemo:9000014 "h_i = [60.1,72.5]; n = 2; epsilon = 2; h_EDE = 65.7202" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ATK-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "atkinson-ede-for-programme-u-at-epsilon-of-2" .

hemo:7000956 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "h_i in the test Atkinson EDE for programme U at epsilon of 2"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6001019 ;
    hemo:9000014 "[60.1, 72.5]" .

