hemo:7003278 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "All deaths in an age group placed at its midpoint"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each death counts as if it occurred at the midpoint of its age group, as in the CDC method, so the result depends on the age grouping of the death data." ;
    hemo:9000009 "All deaths in an age group placed at its midpoint" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-YLL-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "all-deaths-in-an-age-group-placed-at-its-midpoint" .

hemo:7003279 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "No potential years counted at or above the cut-off age"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Deaths at or above the cut-off count zero whatever the remaining life expectancy at that age, so the total reflects early deaths only." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No potential years counted at or above the cut-off age" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-YLL-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-potential-years-counted-at-or-above-the-cut-off-age" .

hemo:7003280 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Potential years of life lost for one age group from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000603 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the deaths in Deaths, the cut-off age in CutOff and the midpoint of the age group in AgeMid, the formula returns the potential years of life lost for the group, held in PYLLGroup." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Potential years of life lost for one age group from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-YLL-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "potential-years-of-life-lost-for-one-age-group-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=Deaths*MAX(CutOff-AgeMid,0)" .

hemo:7003281 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Potential years of life lost never negative and no more than deaths times the cut-off"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "CutOff is above zero, AgeMid is zero or more and PYLLGroup lies between zero and Deaths times CutOff, and PYLLGroup equals Deaths times MAX(CutOff minus AgeMid, 0). Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows the MAX omitted, which gives minus 750 years for 100 deaths placed at 77.5 with a cut-off of 70, or the lower bound of the age group used in place of AgeMid, which gives 1,380 in place of 1,340 for 20 deaths at ages 1 to 4." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Potential years of life lost never negative and no more than deaths times the cut-off" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-YLL-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "potential-years-of-life-lost-never-negative-and-no-more-than-deaths-times-the-cut-off" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(CutOff>0,AgeMid>=0,PYLLGroup>=0,PYLLGroup<=Deaths*CutOff,ABS(PYLLGroup-Deaths*MAX(CutOff-AgeMid,0))<1E-9)" .

hemo:7003282 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Twenty child deaths before a cut-off age of 70"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Twenty deaths at ages 1 to 4, placed at a midpoint of 3, give 1,340 potential years of life lost before age 70, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Twenty child deaths before a cut-off age of 70" ;
    hemo:9000014 "N_a = 20; C = 70; m_a = 3; PYLL_a = 1340" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-YLL-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "twenty-child-deaths-before-a-cut-off-age-of-70" .

hemo:7003283 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Fifty deaths at ages 40 to 44 before a cut-off age of 70"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Fifty deaths at ages 40 to 44, placed at 42.5, give 1,375 potential years of life lost before age 70, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fifty deaths at ages 40 to 44 before a cut-off age of 70" ;
    hemo:9000014 "N_a = 50; C = 70; m_a = 42.5; PYLL_a = 1375" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-YLL-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fifty-deaths-at-ages-40-to-44-before-a-cut-off-age-of-70" .

hemo:7003284 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "One hundred deaths at ages 75 to 79 above a cut-off age of 70"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Deaths placed at 77.5 are above the cut-off and count nothing, so the article's three age groups give 2,715 potential years of life lost against 5,169.1 YLLs under the GBD 2010 standard." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One hundred deaths at ages 75 to 79 above a cut-off age of 70" ;
    hemo:9000014 "N_a = 100; C = 70; m_a = 77.5; PYLL_a = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-YLL-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-hundred-deaths-at-ages-75-to-79-above-a-cut-off-age-of-70" .

hemo:7003285 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "One hundred deaths at ages 75 to 79 before a cut-off age of 85"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the cut-off of 85 that the CDC's 1992 report also used, the same 100 deaths count 750 potential years of life lost (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One hundred deaths at ages 75 to 79 before a cut-off age of 85" ;
    hemo:9000014 "N_a = 100; C = 85; m_a = 77.5; PYLL_a = 750" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-YLL-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-hundred-deaths-at-ages-75-to-79-before-a-cut-off-age-of-85" .

hemo:7003286 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Continuous discounting from the time of death without age weighting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each standard year is discounted continuously at a constant rate from the time of death, and the age weights of the original DALY are left out, as in the article and in Larson's equation 1." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Continuous discounting from the time of death without age weighting" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-YLL-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "continuous-discounting-from-the-time-of-death-without-age-weighting" .

hemo:7003287 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Discounted years of life lost for evaluation and historical comparison only"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "GBD from 2010 onwards and WHO since 2012 do not discount YLLs, so the formula reproduces the original DALY convention or an economic evaluation that discounts health; current burden estimates use HE-FM-YLL-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Discounted years of life lost for evaluation and historical comparison only" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-YLL-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "discounted-years-of-life-lost-for-evaluation-and-historical-comparison-only" .

hemo:7003288 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Discounted years of life lost per death and per group from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000604 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With DiscRate, StdLE and Deaths named, the first formula returns the discounted years per death, held in YLLDeath, and the second the discounted total for the group." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Discounted years of life lost per death and per group from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-YLL-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "discounted-years-of-life-lost-per-death-and-per-group-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(1-EXP(-DiscRate*StdLE))/DiscRate; =Deaths*YLLDeath" .

hemo:7003289 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Discounted years of life lost per death between its lower and upper bounds"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "DiscRate is above 0 and below 1, and YLLDeath lies between StdLE times EXP(minus DiscRate times StdLE) and StdLE. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows the rate entered as 3 for 3 per cent, or the sign of the exponent dropped, which gives minus 11.66 years for a death with 10 remaining years." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Discounted years of life lost per death between its lower and upper bounds" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-YLL-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "discounted-years-of-life-lost-per-death-between-its-lower-and-upper-bounds" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(DiscRate>0,DiscRate<1,YLLDeath<=StdLE,YLLDeath>=StdLE*EXP(-DiscRate*StdLE))" .

hemo:7003290 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Larson's continuous-time years of life lost reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The formula with a rate of 0.03 and 10 remaining years returns Larson's 8.6394 to four decimal places. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows discrete annual discounting, which gives 8.786." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Larson's continuous-time years of life lost reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-YLL-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "larson-s-continuous-time-years-of-life-lost-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((1-EXP(-0.03*10))/0.03-8.6394)<0.00005" .

hemo:7003291 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Larson's death with 10 years of remaining life expectancy at 3 per cent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "One death with 10 remaining years discounted continuously at 3 per cent counts 8.6394 years, Larson's continuous-time value, against 8.786 with discrete annual discounting." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Larson's death with 10 years of remaining life expectancy at 3 per cent" ;
    hemo:9000014 "r = 0.03; L = 10; N = 1; YLL_death = 8.6394; YLL_d = 8.6394" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-YLL-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "larson-s-death-with-10-years-of-remaining-life-expectancy-at-3-per-cent" .

hemo:7003292 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Death removing 80 standard years discounted at 3 per cent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A death that removes 80 standard years counts about 30.3 years at 3 per cent, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Death removing 80 standard years discounted at 3 per cent" ;
    hemo:9000014 "r = 0.03; L = 80; N = 1; YLL_death = 30.3094; YLL_d = 30.3094" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-YLL-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "death-removing-80-standard-years-discounted-at-3-per-cent" .

hemo:7003293 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Twenty child deaths at ages 1 to 4 discounted at 3 per cent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each death counts about 30.62 years in place of 83.63, and the 20 deaths 612.4 in place of 1,672.6, a cut of 63 per cent, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Twenty child deaths at ages 1 to 4 discounted at 3 per cent" ;
    hemo:9000014 "r = 0.03; L = 83.63; N = 20; YLL_death = 30.6214; YLL_d = 612.43" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-YLL-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "twenty-child-deaths-at-ages-1-to-4-discounted-at-3-per-cent" .

hemo:7003294 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "One hundred deaths at ages 75 to 79 discounted at 3 per cent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each death counts about 10.66 years in place of 12.85, a cut of 17 per cent, so a child death counts 2.9 times an older death against 6.5 times undiscounted. With 1,224.5 from the 50 deaths at ages 40 to 44, the discounted total is 2,903.2, or 2,902.9 in the article, which rounds each death to two decimals first." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One hundred deaths at ages 75 to 79 discounted at 3 per cent" ;
    hemo:9000014 "r = 0.03; L = 12.85; N = 100; YLL_death = 10.6630; YLL_d = 1066.3" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-YLL-012" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-hundred-deaths-at-ages-75-to-79-discounted-at-3-per-cent" .

hemo:7003295 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Both increments of the utility-cost ratio from one comparison"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Delta_Q and Delta_C come from the same comparator, population and time horizon and are both discounted, with incremental defined as the value for the proposed treatment minus the value for the comparator. Each is an expected value: across probabilistic draws or subgroups the costs and QALYs are averaged first and the ratio is formed last, as PHARMAC requires when aggregating subgroups." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Both increments of the utility-cost ratio from one comparison" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ICUR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "both-increments-of-the-utility-cost-ratio-from-one-comparison" .

hemo:7003296 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Quadrant fixes how an incremental utility-cost ratio is read"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The ratio is undefined when Delta_C is zero and carries no direction of its own. PHARMAC notes that in the two trade-off cases, where the ratio is positive, its value can inform a choice. When the intervention gains QALYs at extra cost, a higher IUCR is better; when it saves money but loses QALYs, a positive IUCR counts QALYs lost per one million saved and a lower value is better. A negative IUCR marks dominance one way or the other, so the signs of Delta_Q and Delta_C are reported with it." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Quadrant fixes how an incremental utility-cost ratio is read" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ICUR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "quadrant-fixes-how-an-incremental-utility-cost-ratio-is-read" .

hemo:7003297 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Incremental utility-cost ratio from named cells in Excel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000605 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the incremental QALYs in a cell named DeltaQ and the incremental cost in DeltaC, Excel returns the QALYs gained per one million units of spending, or #N/A when the incremental cost is zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Incremental utility-cost ratio from named cells in Excel" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ICUR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "incremental-utility-cost-ratio-from-named-cells-in-excel" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(DeltaC=0,NA(),DeltaQ/DeltaC*1000000)" .

hemo:7003298 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Utility-cost ratio inverts back to the cost per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "One million divided by the IUCR equals the ICUR of HE-FM-CUA-001 from the same increments; with the article's figures, 1,000,000 / 47.619 = 21,000 to the nearest unit. Expected result: TRUE when IUCR is the unrounded cell from HE-IM-ICUR-001. FALSE shows that the multiplier or the order of the increments differs between the two figures." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Utility-cost ratio inverts back to the cost per QALY" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ICUR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "utility-cost-ratio-inverts-back-to-the-cost-per-qaly" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(1000000/IUCR-DeltaC/DeltaQ)<1E-6" .

hemo:7003299 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Utility-cost ratio against the threshold agrees with net monetary benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For a QALY gain at extra cost, the IUCR exceeds 1,000,000 divided by the threshold Lambda exactly when the incremental net monetary benefit of HE-FM-NMB-002 is positive. At GBP 25,000 per QALY the cut-off is 1,000,000 / 25,000 = 40 QALYs per million; the article's IUCR of 47.619 is above it and its net monetary benefit of GBP 1,600 is positive. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows a result outside the north-east quadrant, where this rule does not apply, or a ratio taken the wrong way up." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Utility-cost ratio against the threshold agrees with net monetary benefit" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ICUR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "utility-cost-ratio-against-the-threshold-agrees-with-net-monetary-benefit" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(DeltaQ>0,DeltaC>0,(DeltaQ/DeltaC*1000000>1000000/Lambda)=(Lambda*DeltaQ-DeltaC>0))" .

hemo:7003300 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Utility-cost ratio reproduces the article's example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's increments of 0.40 QALYs and GBP 8,400 return 47.619 QALYs per GBP 1 million to three decimal places. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Utility-cost ratio reproduces the article's example" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ICUR-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "utility-cost-ratio-reproduces-the-article-s-example" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND(0.40/8400*1000000,3)=47.619" .

hemo:7003301 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Utility-cost ratio for the new treatment against usual care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative new treatment adds 0.40 QALYs at an extra GBP 8,400 per patient against usual care, an ICUR of GBP 21,000 per QALY. The IUCR is about 47.619 QALYs gained per GBP 1 million of extra spending, the article's figure of about 47.6." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Utility-cost ratio for the new treatment against usual care" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Delta_Q = 0.40; Delta_C = 8400; IUCR = 47.619" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ICUR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "utility-cost-ratio-for-the-new-treatment-against-usual-care" .

hemo:7003302 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Utility-cost ratio against a dominated comparator"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Against the article's dominated older drug the new treatment adds 0.52 QALYs at an extra GBP 6,600, an ICUR of about GBP 12,692 per QALY. The IUCR rises to about 78.788 QALYs per GBP 1 million, so the wrong comparator flatters the result in both forms." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Utility-cost ratio against a dominated comparator" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Delta_Q = 0.52; Delta_C = 6600; IUCR = 78.788" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ICUR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "utility-cost-ratio-against-a-dominated-comparator" .

hemo:7003303 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Utility-cost ratio with a QALY gain of 0.05"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Holding the incremental cost at GBP 8,400 and cutting the QALY gain to 0.05, as in the article's table, gives an ICUR of GBP 168,000 per QALY and an IUCR of about 5.952 QALYs per GBP 1 million. The ICUR is eight times its base value and the IUCR one eighth of it." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Utility-cost ratio with a QALY gain of 0.05" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Delta_Q = 0.05; Delta_C = 8400; IUCR = 5.952" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ICUR-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "utility-cost-ratio-with-a-qaly-gain-of-0-05" .

hemo:7003304 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Utility-cost ratio for a QALY loss at extra cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a QALY change of minus 0.05 at the same extra cost, the north-west case in the article's table, the ICUR is minus GBP 168,000 per QALY and the IUCR about minus 5.952. The negative value records that the intervention is dominated, not that it buys QALYs cheaply." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Utility-cost ratio for a QALY loss at extra cost" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Delta_Q = -0.05; Delta_C = 8400; IUCR = -5.952" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ICUR-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "utility-cost-ratio-for-a-qaly-loss-at-extra-cost" .

hemo:7003305 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Same arm order and outcome in every trial of a pairwise meta-analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Drug A is group 1 (a, n1) and drug B is group 2 (c, n2) in every trial, the outcome has the same definition and time point throughout, and each randomised patient appears once in the total of the arm to which they were allocated. The log risk ratios then share one direction, so a negative pooled value favours drug A whichever trial it came from." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same arm order and outcome in every trial of a pairwise meta-analysis" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PWMA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-arm-order-and-outcome-in-every-trial-of-a-pairwise-meta-analysis" .

hemo:7003306 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Inverse-variance use of the trial log risk ratio in a pairwise meta-analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The pair (y, v) feeds the inverse-variance method, which gives each trial a weight of one over its variance. When events are few the Cochrane Handbook generally prefers the Mantel-Haenszel method for a fixed-effect analysis, and with rare events it advises against inverse-variance methods, including DerSimonian and Laird. At event rates below 1% the Peto one-step odds ratio method was the least biased and most powerful, under the conditions the Handbook states." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Inverse-variance use of the trial log risk ratio in a pairwise meta-analysis" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PWMA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "inverse-variance-use-of-the-trial-log-risk-ratio-in-a-pairwise-meta-analysis" .

hemo:7003307 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Zero-cell rule for the log risk ratio in a pairwise meta-analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Both a and c are above zero. Where a zero makes the log or the variance impossible to compute, RevMan adds 0.5 to all four cells of that trial, so each arm total rises by 1. A trial with no events in either arm, or with every patient in both arms having the event, has an undefined risk ratio in RevMan and is left out; the Handbook describes excluding trials with no events in either arm as standard practice. The Handbook notes that the fixed correction biases the trial estimate towards no difference and over-estimates its variance." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Zero-cell rule for the log risk ratio in a pairwise meta-analysis" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PWMA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "zero-cell-rule-for-the-log-risk-ratio-in-a-pairwise-meta-analysis" .

hemo:7003308 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Risk ratio, log risk ratio and variance of one pairwise meta-analysis trial in Excel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000606 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the counts in cells named EventsA, PatientsA, EventsB and PatientsB, Excel 365 spills the risk ratio, the log risk ratio and its variance into three adjacent cells. LN is the natural log." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Risk ratio, log risk ratio and variance of one pairwise meta-analysis trial in Excel" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-PWMA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "risk-ratio-log-risk-ratio-and-variance-of-one-pairwise-meta-analysis-trial-in-excel" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LET(riskA,EventsA/PatientsA,riskB,EventsB/PatientsB,HSTACK(riskA/riskB,LN(riskA/riskB),1/EventsA+1/EventsB-1/PatientsA-1/PatientsB))" .

hemo:7003309 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Trial-by-row log risk ratios with the zero-cell rule for a pairwise meta-analysis in Excel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000606 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With one trial per row, drug A events in B2, drug A patients in C2, drug B events in D2 and drug B patients in E2, the formula in F2 spills y into F2 and v into G2 and is copied down. It adds 0.5 to every cell, and so 1 to each arm total, when either arm has no events, and returns #N/A when neither arm has events or every patient in both arms has the event. The standard errors that HE-CF-ADMA-001 takes are SQRT of column G." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Trial-by-row log risk ratios with the zero-cell rule for a pairwise meta-analysis in Excel" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-PWMA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "trial-by-row-log-risk-ratios-with-the-zero-cell-rule-for-a-pairwise-meta-analysis-in-excel" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LET(eA,B2,nA,C2,eB,D2,nB,E2,k,IF(OR(eA=0,eB=0),0.5,0),IF(OR(eA+eB=0,AND(eA=nA,eB=nB)),NA(),HSTACK(LN(((eA+k)/(nA+2*k))/((eB+k)/(nB+2*k))),1/(eA+k)+1/(eB+k)-1/(nA+2*k)-1/(nB+2*k))))" .

hemo:7003310 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Pairwise meta-analysis trial 1 log risk ratio and variance reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Trial 1 of the article, 30 of 200 against 45 of 200, returns a log risk ratio of -0.4055 to four decimal places and a variance of 0.04556 to five. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pairwise meta-analysis trial 1 log risk ratio and variance reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PWMA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pairwise-meta-analysis-trial-1-log-risk-ratio-and-variance-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ROUND(LN((30/200)/(45/200)),4)=-0.4055,ROUND(1/30+1/45-1/200-1/200,5)=0.04556)" .

hemo:7003311 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Pairwise meta-analysis log risk ratio variance matches its per-arm form"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The variance also equals the sum over the two arms of one minus the risk, divided by the number of events, so a computed v in a cell named LogRRVar can be checked against the counts. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows that the log odds ratio variance, with one over each of the four cells, or a standard error was entered where the log risk ratio variance belongs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pairwise meta-analysis log risk ratio variance matches its per-arm form" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PWMA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pairwise-meta-analysis-log-risk-ratio-variance-matches-its-per-arm-form" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(LogRRVar-((1-EventsA/PatientsA)/EventsA+(1-EventsB/PatientsB)/EventsB))<1E-12" .

hemo:7003312 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Four-trial fixed-effect pooled log risk ratio of the pairwise example reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Feeding the four trials' log risk ratios and variances into the fixed-effect estimate of HE-FM-ADMA-001 returns the article's pooled log risk ratio of -0.2071, a risk ratio of 0.813. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows a trial with its arms reversed or a variance that does not match the log risk ratio." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Four-trial fixed-effect pooled log risk ratio of the pairwise example reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PWMA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "four-trial-fixed-effect-pooled-log-risk-ratio-of-the-pairwise-example-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND((LN((30/200)/(45/200))/(1/30+1/45-1/200-1/200)+LN((60/500)/(70/500))/(1/60+1/70-1/500-1/500)+LN((12/100)/(25/100))/(1/12+1/25-1/100-1/100)+LN((90/800)/(96/800))/(1/90+1/96-1/800-1/800))/(1/(1/30+1/45-1/200-1/200)+1/(1/60+1/70-1/500-1/500)+1/(1/12+1/25-1/100-1/100)+1/(1/90+1/96-1/800-1/800)),4)=-0.2071" .

hemo:7003313 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Zero-cell corrected log risk ratio of the pairwise example reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The illustrative trial with 0 of 50 against 4 of 50, corrected to 0.5 of 51 against 4.5 of 51, returns a log risk ratio of -2.1972 and a variance of 2.1830 to four decimal places. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Zero-cell corrected log risk ratio of the pairwise example reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PWMA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "zero-cell-corrected-log-risk-ratio-of-the-pairwise-example-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ROUND(LN((0.5/51)/(4.5/51)),4)=-2.1972,ROUND(1/0.5+1/4.5-1/51-1/51,4)=2.183)" .

hemo:7003314 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Pairwise meta-analysis trial 1 log risk ratio, 30 of 200 against 45 of 200"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Trial 1 of the article's illustrative example admits 30 of 200 patients on drug A and 45 of 200 on drug B, risks of 0.15 and 0.225. The risk ratio is about 0.6667, the log risk ratio about -0.4055 and its variance about 0.04556, so the trial's fixed-effect weight is about 21.951, as in the article's table." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pairwise meta-analysis trial 1 log risk ratio, 30 of 200 against 45 of 200" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 30; n1 = 200; c = 45; n2 = 200; RR = 0.6667; y = -0.4055; v = 0.04556" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PWMA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pairwise-meta-analysis-trial-1-log-risk-ratio-30-of-200-against-45-of-200" .

hemo:7003315 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Pairwise meta-analysis trial 2 log risk ratio, 60 of 500 against 70 of 500"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Trial 2 admits 60 of 500 patients on drug A and 70 of 500 on drug B, risks of 0.12 and 0.14. The risk ratio is about 0.8571, the log risk ratio about -0.1542 and its variance about 0.02695, a weight of about 37.102." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pairwise meta-analysis trial 2 log risk ratio, 60 of 500 against 70 of 500" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 60; n1 = 500; c = 70; n2 = 500; RR = 0.8571; y = -0.1542; v = 0.02695" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PWMA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pairwise-meta-analysis-trial-2-log-risk-ratio-60-of-500-against-70-of-500" .

hemo:7003316 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Pairwise meta-analysis trial 3 log risk ratio, 12 of 100 against 25 of 100"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Trial 3, the smallest, admits 12 of 100 patients on drug A and 25 of 100 on drug B. The risk ratio is 0.48, the log risk ratio about -0.7340 and its variance about 0.10333, the largest benefit and the smallest weight, about 9.677, of the four trials." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pairwise meta-analysis trial 3 log risk ratio, 12 of 100 against 25 of 100" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 12; n1 = 100; c = 25; n2 = 100; RR = 0.4800; y = -0.7340; v = 0.10333" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PWMA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pairwise-meta-analysis-trial-3-log-risk-ratio-12-of-100-against-25-of-100" .

hemo:7003317 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Pairwise meta-analysis trial 4 log risk ratio, 90 of 800 against 96 of 800"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Trial 4, the largest, admits 90 of 800 patients on drug A and 96 of 800 on drug B. The risk ratio is 0.9375, the log risk ratio about -0.0645 and its variance about 0.01903, a weight of about 52.555, 43.3% of the fixed-effect total in the article. Pooling the four (y, v) pairs with HE-FM-ADMA-001 gives the article's fixed-effect log risk ratio of -0.2071, a risk ratio of 0.813." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pairwise meta-analysis trial 4 log risk ratio, 90 of 800 against 96 of 800" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 90; n1 = 800; c = 96; n2 = 800; RR = 0.9375; y = -0.0645; v = 0.01903" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PWMA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pairwise-meta-analysis-trial-4-log-risk-ratio-90-of-800-against-96-of-800" .

hemo:7003318 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Pairwise meta-analysis trial with no events on drug A after the 0.5 correction"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An illustrative small trial has no admissions among 50 patients on drug A and 4 among 50 on drug B, so the log and one over a cannot be computed. Adding 0.5 to all four cells, as RevMan does, gives 0.5 events in 51 patients and 4.5 in 51. The corrected risk ratio is about 0.1111, the log risk ratio about -2.1972 and its variance about 2.1830, a weight below 0.5 against about 52.555 for trial 4." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pairwise meta-analysis trial with no events on drug A after the 0.5 correction" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 0.5; n1 = 51; c = 4.5; n2 = 51; RR = 0.1111; y = -2.1972; v = 2.1830" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PWMA-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pairwise-meta-analysis-trial-with-no-events-on-drug-a-after-the-0-5-correction" .

hemo:7003319 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Binary variable and large samples for the Wagstaff bounds"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The variable takes only the values 0 and 1, and mu is a proportion strictly between 0 and 1. The World Bank guide gives the bounds mu minus 1 and 1 minus mu for large samples; with the mid-rank (i minus 0.5)/N of HE-FM-HINQ-005 and a whole number of people with the value 1, the bounds are reached exactly when those people are the poorest or the richest (computed here for illustration). The bounds do not apply to a variable that is not binary." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Binary variable and large samples for the Wagstaff bounds" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CIX-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "binary-variable-and-large-samples-for-the-wagstaff-bounds" .

hemo:7003320 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Same sample, weights and ranking for the index and mean in the Wagstaff normalisation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "C and mu come from the same sample, survey weights and living standards ranking, and the normalisation rescales the index without changing its sign. For a variable bounded by a and b, Erreygers and Van Ourti give the generalised Wagstaff index as (b minus a) times mu times C, divided by (b minus mu) times (mu minus a), which reduces to C divided by 1 minus mu when a is 0 and b is 1. Like the Erreygers index of HE-FM-CIX-002, it passes their mirror and cardinal invariance tests." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same sample, weights and ranking for the index and mean in the Wagstaff normalisation" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CIX-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-sample-weights-and-ranking-for-the-index-and-mean-in-the-wagstaff-normalisation" .

hemo:7003321 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Wagstaff normalised concentration index and binary bounds in Excel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000607 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the standard index in a cell named ConcIndex and the mean of the binary variable in MeanBinary, the three formulas return the lower bound, the upper bound and the normalised index, or #N/A when the mean is not strictly between 0 and 1." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Wagstaff normalised concentration index and binary bounds in Excel" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CIX-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "wagstaff-normalised-concentration-index-and-binary-bounds-in-excel" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MeanBinary-1; =1-MeanBinary; =IF(OR(MeanBinary<=0,MeanBinary>=1),NA(),ConcIndex/(1-MeanBinary))" .

hemo:7003322 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Wagstaff normalised index reaches minus 1 when all illness is in the poorest fifth"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "If everyone in the poorest quintile is ill and no one else is, the mean is 0.2 and the standard index is minus 0.8, equal to its lower bound, so the normalised index must be minus 1 (computed here for illustration). Expected result: W of minus 1, with bounds of minus 0.8 and 0.8. Dividing by the mean in place of 1 minus the mean gives minus 4." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Wagstaff normalised index reaches minus 1 when all illness is in the poorest fifth" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CIX-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "wagstaff-normalised-index-reaches-minus-1-when-all-illness-is-in-the-poorest-fifth" ;
    hemo:9000024 "C = -0.8; mu = 0.2; C_min = -0.8; C_max = 0.8; W = -1" .

hemo:7003323 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Wagstaff normalised index reaches 1 when all illness is in the richest fifth"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "If everyone in the richest quintile is ill and no one else is, the standard index is 0.8, its upper bound, and the normalised index must be 1 (computed here for illustration). Expected result: W of 1. Entering the mean as 20 per cent in place of 0.2 gives about minus 0.0421, small and with the sign reversed." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Wagstaff normalised index reaches 1 when all illness is in the richest fifth" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CIX-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "wagstaff-normalised-index-reaches-1-when-all-illness-is-in-the-richest-fifth" ;
    hemo:9000024 "C = 0.8; mu = 0.2; C_min = -0.8; C_max = 0.8; W = 1" .

hemo:7003324 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Wagstaff normalised index of limiting illness across five income quintiles"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative example the share of adults with a limiting long-term illness falls from 0.40 in the poorest quintile to 0.10 in the richest, with a mean of 0.24 and a standard index of minus 0.25. The feasible range is minus 0.76 to 0.76, and dividing by 0.76 gives a normalised index of about minus 0.3289, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Wagstaff normalised index of limiting illness across five income quintiles" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = -0.25; mu = 0.24; C_min = -0.76; C_max = 0.76; W = -0.3289" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CIX-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "wagstaff-normalised-index-of-limiting-illness-across-five-income-quintiles" .

hemo:7003325 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Wagstaff normalised index of being free of limiting illness"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The share without limiting illness, 0.60 in the poorest quintile rising to 0.90 in the richest, has a mean of 0.76 and a standard index of 0.06 divided by 0.76, about 0.0789. Its feasible range is only minus 0.24 to 0.24, and the normalised index is about 0.3289, the exact mirror of the illness value, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Wagstaff normalised index of being free of limiting illness" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = 0.078947; mu = 0.76; C_min = -0.24; C_max = 0.24; W = 0.3289" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CIX-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "wagstaff-normalised-index-of-being-free-of-limiting-illness" .

hemo:7003326 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Wagstaff normalised immunisation index at 50 per cent coverage"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In an illustrative country where half of children are fully immunised and the standard index is 0.10, the feasible range is minus 0.5 to 0.5 and the normalised index is 0.2 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Wagstaff normalised immunisation index at 50 per cent coverage" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = 0.10; mu = 0.5; C_min = -0.5; C_max = 0.5; W = 0.2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CIX-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "wagstaff-normalised-immunisation-index-at-50-per-cent-coverage" .

hemo:7003327 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Wagstaff normalised immunisation index at 90 per cent coverage"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At 90 per cent coverage the same standard index of 0.10 is the largest value the data allow, since the feasible range is minus 0.1 to 0.1, so the normalised index is 1 and every unimmunised child is poorer than every immunised child. The two standard indices are equal while the normalised indices differ fivefold (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Wagstaff normalised immunisation index at 90 per cent coverage" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = 0.10; mu = 0.9; C_min = -0.1; C_max = 0.1; W = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CIX-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "wagstaff-normalised-immunisation-index-at-90-per-cent-coverage" .

hemo:7003328 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Fixed bounds of the measurement scale for the Erreygers correction"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The variable has a known lower bound a and upper bound b set by its measurement scale, such as 0 and 1 for a binary indicator or 0 and 100 for a score, and not by the smallest and largest values observed in the sample. C and mu are computed with the same sample, weights and living standards ranking." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fixed bounds of the measurement scale for the Erreygers correction" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CIX-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fixed-bounds-of-the-measurement-scale-for-the-erreygers-correction" .

hemo:7003329 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Value judgement behind choosing the Erreygers correction"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Erreygers and Van Ourti show that the corrected index and the generalised Wagstaff index both pass the mirror and cardinal invariance tests and that further value judgements are needed to choose between them. Wagstaff replied that the standard index reflects a commitment to relative inequality, whereas the corrected index ends up as a measure of absolute inequality, so the choice of index is reported with the results." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Value judgement behind choosing the Erreygers correction" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CIX-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "value-judgement-behind-choosing-the-erreygers-correction" .

hemo:7003330 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Erreygers corrected concentration index in Excel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000608 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the standard index in ConcIndex, the mean in MeanH and the scale bounds in LowerBound and UpperBound, the first formula returns the corrected index; the second returns the same value from the covariance of health and fractional rank held in CovHR. Both return #N/A when the upper bound is not above the lower bound." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Erreygers corrected concentration index in Excel" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CIX-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "erreygers-corrected-concentration-index-in-excel" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(UpperBound<=LowerBound,NA(),4*MeanH*ConcIndex/(UpperBound-LowerBound)); =IF(UpperBound<=LowerBound,NA(),8*CovHR/(UpperBound-LowerBound))" .

hemo:7003331 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Erreygers corrected index unchanged when illness is recorded as 0 or 100"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Recording limiting illness as 0 or 100 multiplies the mean by 100, to 24, and leaves the standard index at minus 0.25; with b of 100 the corrected index stays at minus 0.24, the cardinal invariance property (computed here for illustration). Expected result: E of minus 0.24. A value of minus 24 shows the range of the scale left out." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Erreygers corrected index unchanged when illness is recorded as 0 or 100" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CIX-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "erreygers-corrected-index-unchanged-when-illness-is-recorded-as-0-or-100" ;
    hemo:9000024 "C = -0.25; mu = 24; a = 0; b = 100; E = -0.24" .

hemo:7003332 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Erreygers corrected index reaches minus 1 at a mean of one half"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "If the poorer half of the population is all ill and the richer half all well, the mean is 0.5 and the standard index sits at its lower bound of minus 0.5, so the corrected index is minus 1, its most extreme value for a binary variable (computed here for illustration). Expected result: E of minus 1. A value beyond minus 1 shows a wrong scale range or a mean outside the bounds." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Erreygers corrected index reaches minus 1 at a mean of one half" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CIX-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "erreygers-corrected-index-reaches-minus-1-at-a-mean-of-one-half" ;
    hemo:9000024 "C = -0.5; mu = 0.5; a = 0; b = 1; E = -1" .

hemo:7003333 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Erreygers corrected index of limiting illness across five income quintiles"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's example the mean prevalence is 0.24 and the standard index minus 0.25, so with a of 0 and b of 1 the corrected index is minus 0.24, which equals 8 times the covariance of minus 0.03, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Erreygers corrected index of limiting illness across five income quintiles" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = -0.25; mu = 0.24; a = 0; b = 1; E = -0.24" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CIX-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "erreygers-corrected-index-of-limiting-illness-across-five-income-quintiles" .

hemo:7003334 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Erreygers corrected index of being free of limiting illness"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For the share without limiting illness the mean is 0.76, the standard index about 0.0789 and the covariance 0.03, so the corrected index is 0.24, the exact mirror of the illness value, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Erreygers corrected index of being free of limiting illness" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = 0.078947; mu = 0.76; a = 0; b = 1; E = 0.24" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CIX-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "erreygers-corrected-index-of-being-free-of-limiting-illness" .

hemo:7003335 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Erreygers corrected immunisation index at 50 per cent coverage"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For the illustrative country with half of children immunised and a standard index of 0.10, the corrected index is 0.2, the same as the Wagstaff index in HE-EX-CIX-003, because the two corrections coincide when the mean of a binary variable is 0.5 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Erreygers corrected immunisation index at 50 per cent coverage" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = 0.10; mu = 0.5; a = 0; b = 1; E = 0.2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CIX-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "erreygers-corrected-immunisation-index-at-50-per-cent-coverage" .

hemo:7003336 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Erreygers corrected immunisation index at 90 per cent coverage"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At 90 per cent coverage the same standard index of 0.10 gives a corrected index of 0.36, against a Wagstaff index of 1 in HE-EX-CIX-004. Both corrections place the second country above the first, but they disagree on how far apart the two are (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Erreygers corrected immunisation index at 90 per cent coverage" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = 0.10; mu = 0.9; a = 0; b = 1; E = 0.36" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CIX-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "erreygers-corrected-immunisation-index-at-90-per-cent-coverage" .

hemo:7003337 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Same case definition and timing in both prevalence ratio groups"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Both groups use the same case definition and the same timing, either point prevalence at one date or period prevalence over the same interval, and both prevalences come from one cross-sectional sample. A ratio of prevalences measured at different times or with different definitions mixes the exposure with the measurement." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same case definition and timing in both prevalence ratio groups" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-case-definition-and-timing-in-both-prevalence-ratio-groups" .

hemo:7003338 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Prevalence ratio reflects onset and duration together"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Prevalence carries both incidence and the duration of the condition, so a PR above 1 can arise because exposed people develop the condition more often, because they live with it for longer, or because unexposed people with the condition die or recover sooner. Reading the PR as a ratio of risks of becoming ill needs further assumptions about duration and survival." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Prevalence ratio reflects onset and duration together" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "prevalence-ratio-reflects-onset-and-duration-together" .

hemo:7003339 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Prevalence ratio from the four table cells in Excel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000609 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the counts in named cells ExposedCases (a), ExposedFree (b), UnexposedCases (c) and UnexposedFree (d), Excel returns the PR, or #N/A when no unexposed person has the condition." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Prevalence ratio from the four table cells in Excel" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-PR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "prevalence-ratio-from-the-four-table-cells-in-excel" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(UnexposedCases=0,NA(),(ExposedCases/(ExposedCases+ExposedFree))/(UnexposedCases/(UnexposedCases+UnexposedFree)))" .

hemo:7003340 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Prevalence ratio within its ceiling of one over the unexposed prevalence"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Because the exposed prevalence cannot exceed 1, the PR cannot exceed 1 / P_0; with the article's unexposed prevalence of 0.15 the ceiling is about 6.67. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows a count entered in the wrong cell or prevalences taken over different denominators." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Prevalence ratio within its ceiling of one over the unexposed prevalence" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "prevalence-ratio-within-its-ceiling-of-one-over-the-unexposed-prevalence" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=PrevRatio<=1/(UnexposedCases/(UnexposedCases+UnexposedFree))+1E-12" .

hemo:7003341 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Prevalence ratio reproduces the article's survey figure"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's survey counts (180, 420, 210, 1190) in the four named cells, PrevRatio rounds to 2.00. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Prevalence ratio reproduces the article's survey figure" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "prevalence-ratio-reproduces-the-article-s-survey-figure" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND(PrevRatio,2)=2" .

hemo:7003342 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Prevalence ratio of 2.00 in the article's health survey"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A survey of 2,000 adults finds the condition in 180 of 600 exposed adults and 210 of 1,400 unexposed adults. The prevalences are 0.30 and 0.15, so the PR is 2.00 and the prevalence difference is 15 percentage points." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Prevalence ratio of 2.00 in the article's health survey" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 180; b = 420; c = 210; d = 1190; P_1 = 0.30; P_0 = 0.15; PR = 2.00" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "prevalence-ratio-of-2-00-in-the-article-s-health-survey" .

hemo:7003343 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Prevalence ratio when both prevalences are low"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the condition in 12 of 600 exposed adults and 14 of 1,400 unexposed adults, the prevalences are 0.02 and 0.01 and the PR is again 2.00. HE-EX-PR-005 shows that the prevalence odds ratio is then close to the PR." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Prevalence ratio when both prevalences are low" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 12; b = 588; c = 14; d = 1386; P_1 = 0.02; P_0 = 0.01; PR = 2.00" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "prevalence-ratio-when-both-prevalences-are-low" .

hemo:7003344 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Prevalence ratio of 1 when the two prevalences are equal"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the condition in 60 of 600 exposed adults and 140 of 1,400 unexposed adults, both prevalences are 0.10 and the PR is 1.00, a limiting case that checks the implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Prevalence ratio of 1 when the two prevalences are equal" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 60; b = 540; c = 140; d = 1260; P_1 = 0.10; P_0 = 0.10; PR = 1.00" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PR-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "prevalence-ratio-of-1-when-the-two-prevalences-are-equal" .

hemo:7003345 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Crude ratios from one table in the odds ratio conversion"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The identity holds exactly for crude ratios from the same two-by-two table. Adjusted estimates from a log-binomial model and a logistic model fitted to the same data need not satisfy it, because controlling for confounding is not equivalent for the two measures." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Crude ratios from one table in the odds ratio conversion" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PR-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "crude-ratios-from-one-table-in-the-odds-ratio-conversion" .

hemo:7003346 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Implied exposed prevalence below one in the odds ratio conversion"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The product of the PR and P_0 must be below 1, otherwise the odds among the exposed are undefined. A PR from one population combined with an unexposed prevalence from another can break this condition." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Implied exposed prevalence below one in the odds ratio conversion" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PR-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "implied-exposed-prevalence-below-one-in-the-odds-ratio-conversion" .

hemo:7003347 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Prevalence odds ratio from a prevalence ratio in Excel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000610 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the PR in a cell named PrevRatio and the unexposed prevalence in PrevUnexposed, Excel returns the POR." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Prevalence odds ratio from a prevalence ratio in Excel" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-PR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "prevalence-odds-ratio-from-a-prevalence-ratio-in-excel" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=PrevRatio*(1-PrevUnexposed)/(1-PrevRatio*PrevUnexposed)" .

hemo:7003348 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Odds ratio conversion matches the table cross-product"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With PrevRatio and PrevUnexposed computed from the same four named cells, the converted POR equals the cross-product of the table. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows that the PR or the unexposed prevalence came from a different table or was adjusted." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Odds ratio conversion matches the table cross-product" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PR-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "odds-ratio-conversion-matches-the-table-cross-product" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ExposedCases*UnexposedFree/(ExposedFree*UnexposedCases)-PrevRatio*(1-PrevUnexposed)/(1-PrevRatio*PrevUnexposed))<1E-9" .

hemo:7003349 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Prevalence odds ratio lies further from 1 than the prevalence ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the converted POR in a cell named PrevOddsRatio, it exceeds the PR when the PR is above 1, falls below it when the PR is below 1 and equals 1 when the PR is 1. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Prevalence odds ratio lies further from 1 than the prevalence ratio" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PR-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "prevalence-odds-ratio-lies-further-from-1-than-the-prevalence-ratio" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(PrevRatio>1,PrevOddsRatio>PrevRatio,IF(PrevRatio<1,PrevOddsRatio<PrevRatio,ABS(PrevOddsRatio-1)<1E-12))" .

hemo:7003350 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Prevalence odds ratio when the condition is common"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's PR of 2.00 and unexposed prevalence of 0.15, the exposed prevalence is 0.30 and the POR is about 2.4286, about 21% above the PR. The cross-product of the survey table, 214,200 divided by 88,200, gives the same value." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Prevalence odds ratio when the condition is common" ;
    hemo:9000014 "PR = 2.00; P_0 = 0.15; P_1 = 0.30; POR = 2.4286" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PR-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "prevalence-odds-ratio-when-the-condition-is-common" .

hemo:7003351 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Prevalence odds ratio when both prevalences are low"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a PR of 2.00 and an unexposed prevalence of 0.01, the exposed prevalence is 0.02 and the POR is about 2.0204, so the two measures nearly agree, as the article shows for prevalences of 2% and 1%." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Prevalence odds ratio when both prevalences are low" ;
    hemo:9000014 "PR = 2.00; P_0 = 0.01; P_1 = 0.02; POR = 2.0204" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PR-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "prevalence-odds-ratio-when-both-prevalences-are-low" .

hemo:7003352 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Prevalence odds ratio at a prevalence ratio of 1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When the prevalences are equal the conversion factor is 1, so a PR of 1 gives a POR of 1 at any unexposed prevalence, a limiting case that checks the implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Prevalence odds ratio at a prevalence ratio of 1" ;
    hemo:9000014 "PR = 1; P_0 = 0.15; P_1 = 0.15; POR = 1.0000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PR-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "prevalence-odds-ratio-at-a-prevalence-ratio-of-1" .

hemo:7003353 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Unconfounded prevalence ratio in the attributable fraction"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The PR is read as the causal effect of the exposure on prevalence. Jo notes that the formula holds only where other factors do not affect the association, and that uncontrolled confounding usually biases the ratio, and so the attributable fraction, upwards." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Unconfounded prevalence ratio in the attributable fraction" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PR-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "unconfounded-prevalence-ratio-in-the-attributable-fraction" .

hemo:7003354 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Exposure share and prevalence ratio from the costed population"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The exposed share e describes the population whose costs are attributed, and the PR is assumed to hold in that population. The fraction applies to the annual costs of prevalent cases over the same period, in line with a prevalence-based cost-of-illness study." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Exposure share and prevalence ratio from the costed population" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PR-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "exposure-share-and-prevalence-ratio-from-the-costed-population" .

hemo:7003355 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Attributable fraction from a prevalence ratio in Excel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000611 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the exposed share in a cell named ExposedShare and the PR in PrevRatio, Excel returns the attributable fraction of prevalent cases." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Attributable fraction from a prevalence ratio in Excel" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-PR-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "attributable-fraction-from-a-prevalence-ratio-in-excel" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ExposedShare*(PrevRatio-1)/(1+ExposedShare*(PrevRatio-1))" .

hemo:7003356 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Attributable fraction equals the excess share of prevalent cases"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With OverallPrev computed as ExposedShare*PrevExposed+(1-ExposedShare)*PrevUnexposed and PrevRatio as PrevExposed/PrevUnexposed, the fraction equals (P minus P_0) / P. With the article's figures both forms give 0.2857. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Attributable fraction equals the excess share of prevalent cases" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PR-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "attributable-fraction-equals-the-excess-share-of-prevalent-cases" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ExposedShare*(PrevRatio-1)/(1+ExposedShare*(PrevRatio-1))-(OverallPrev-PrevUnexposed)/OverallPrev)<1E-9" .

hemo:7003357 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Attributable fraction reproduces the article's local figure"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With 0.40 in ExposedShare and 2.00 in PrevRatio, the formula returns a PAF of 0.2857 to four decimal places. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Attributable fraction reproduces the article's local figure" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PR-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "attributable-fraction-reproduces-the-article-s-local-figure" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND(ExposedShare*(PrevRatio-1)/(1+ExposedShare*(PrevRatio-1)),4)=0.2857" .

hemo:7003358 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Attributable fraction for a local population with 40% exposed"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's local health economy 40% of 500,000 adults are exposed and the PR is 2.00, so the PAF is about 0.2857. Of 105,000 prevalent cases, 30,000 are attributable, the same as the excess among the exposed, and at an illustrative GBP 1,200 per case the attributable annual cost through HE-FM-COI-002 is GBP 36.0 million." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Attributable fraction for a local population with 40% exposed" ;
    hemo:9000014 "e = 0.40; PR = 2.00; PAF = 0.2857" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PR-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "attributable-fraction-for-a-local-population-with-40-exposed" .

hemo:7003359 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Attributable fraction when the whole population is exposed"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When everyone is exposed, the PAF reduces to (PR minus 1) / PR, so a PR of 2.00 attributes half of prevalent cases to the exposure, a limiting case that checks the implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Attributable fraction when the whole population is exposed" ;
    hemo:9000014 "e = 1; PR = 2.00; PAF = 0.5000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PR-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "attributable-fraction-when-the-whole-population-is-exposed" .

hemo:7003360 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Attributable fraction at a prevalence ratio of 1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A PR of 1 attributes no prevalent cases to the exposure whatever the exposed share." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Attributable fraction at a prevalence ratio of 1" ;
    hemo:9000014 "e = 0.40; PR = 1; PAF = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PR-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "attributable-fraction-at-a-prevalence-ratio-of-1" .

hemo:7003361 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Prevalence ratio transportable to the target population"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The PR measured in the source population is assumed to hold in the target population. Where age, deprivation or case-finding differ, the transported ratio belongs in the sensitivity analysis." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Prevalence ratio transportable to the target population" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PR-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "prevalence-ratio-transportable-to-the-target-population" .

hemo:7003362 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Overall prevalence and exposed share from one population and period"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "P and e describe the same population at the same time and use the case definition behind the PR, so the recovered subgroup prevalences reproduce the overall prevalence when recombined." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Overall prevalence and exposed share from one population and period" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PR-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "overall-prevalence-and-exposed-share-from-one-population-and-period" .

hemo:7003363 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Subgroup prevalences and exposed cases in Excel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000612 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With named cells OverallPrev, ExposedShare, PrevRatio and Adults, the three formulas return the unexposed prevalence, the exposed prevalence and the number of exposed people with the condition." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Subgroup prevalences and exposed cases in Excel" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-PR-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "subgroup-prevalences-and-exposed-cases-in-excel" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=OverallPrev/(1+ExposedShare*(PrevRatio-1)); =PrevRatio*OverallPrev/(1+ExposedShare*(PrevRatio-1)); =Adults*ExposedShare*PrevRatio*OverallPrev/(1+ExposedShare*(PrevRatio-1))" .

hemo:7003364 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Recovered subgroup prevalences recombine to the overall prevalence"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the recovered prevalences in PrevExposed and PrevUnexposed, weighting them by the exposed share returns the overall prevalence. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Recovered subgroup prevalences recombine to the overall prevalence" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PR-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "recovered-subgroup-prevalences-recombine-to-the-overall-prevalence" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ExposedShare*PrevExposed+(1-ExposedShare)*PrevUnexposed-OverallPrev)<1E-9" .

hemo:7003365 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Recovered exposed prevalence does not exceed one"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The exposed prevalence recovered with the PR must not exceed 1. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows that the PR from the source population is too large for the overall prevalence and exposed share of the target area." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Recovered exposed prevalence does not exceed one" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PR-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "recovered-exposed-prevalence-does-not-exceed-one" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=PrevRatio*OverallPrev/(1+ExposedShare*(PrevRatio-1))<=1" .

hemo:7003366 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Subgroup prevalences in the article's neighbouring area"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An area of 200,000 adults has an overall prevalence of 21.75% and 45% exposed. With the survey PR of 2.00 the unexposed prevalence is 0.15 and the exposed prevalence 0.30, so 27,000 exposed adults have the condition, 62% of the area's 43,500 prevalent cases." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Subgroup prevalences in the article's neighbouring area" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P = 0.2175; e = 0.45; PR = 2.00; N = 200000; P_0 = 0.15; P_1 = 0.30; n_1 = 27000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PR-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "subgroup-prevalences-in-the-article-s-neighbouring-area" .

hemo:7003367 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Subgroup prevalences when the whole population is exposed"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When everyone is exposed the overall prevalence is the exposed prevalence, so an overall prevalence of 0.30 with a PR of 2.00 returns 0.30 for the exposed and 0.15 for the unexposed, and all 60,000 prevalent cases are exposed." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Subgroup prevalences when the whole population is exposed" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P = 0.30; e = 1; PR = 2.00; N = 200000; P_0 = 0.15; P_1 = 0.30; n_1 = 60000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PR-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "subgroup-prevalences-when-the-whole-population-is-exposed" .

hemo:7003368 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Subgroup prevalences when no one is exposed"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When no one is exposed the overall prevalence is the unexposed prevalence and the count of exposed cases is zero, a limiting case that checks the implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Subgroup prevalences when no one is exposed" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P = 0.15; e = 0; PR = 2.00; N = 200000; P_0 = 0.15; P_1 = 0.30; n_1 = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PR-012" ;
    hemo:9000023 "subgroup-prevalences-when-no-one-is-exposed" .

hemo:7003369 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Price enters the incremental cost linearly in an EJP calculation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The acquisition cost per patient is the unit price times U, and neither Delta_E nor Delta_C_other changes with the price. Where price affects other model quantities, the price is iterated in the model until the ICER equals the threshold, as the article describes, instead of using the closed form." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Price enters the incremental cost linearly in an EJP calculation" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EJP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "price-enters-the-incremental-cost-linearly-in-an-ejp-calculation" .

hemo:7003370 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Inputs of an EJP from one comparator, perspective and discount rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Delta_E, Delta_C_other and U come from the same comparator, population, perspective and time horizon, with costs and health effects discounted at the same rate, 3.5% a year in NICE's reference case (PMG36 section 4.5.1), so later units count for less. Delta_E is above zero; the formula is not read for a medicine that loses QALYs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Inputs of an EJP from one comparator, perspective and discount rate" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EJP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "inputs-of-an-ejp-from-one-comparator-perspective-and-discount-rate" .

hemo:7003371 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "EJP compared with the net price paid"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The EJP is compared with the price the payer would actually pay, after patient access schemes and other commercial arrangements, which NICE's reference case uses (PMG36 section 4.4.4), not with the list price." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EJP compared with the net price paid" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EJP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ejp-compared-with-the-net-price-paid" .

hemo:7003372 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "EJP per unit from named threshold, QALY, cost and unit cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000613 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the threshold in a cell named Threshold, the incremental QALYs in DeltaE, the other incremental cost in DeltaCOther and the discounted units per patient in Units, Excel returns the EJP per unit." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EJP per unit from named threshold, QALY, cost and unit cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-EJP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ejp-per-unit-from-named-threshold-qaly-cost-and-unit-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(Threshold*DeltaE-DeltaCOther)/Units" .

hemo:7003373 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Per-patient EJP from named threshold, QALY and cost cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000613 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The same named cells without the division by Units return the per-patient EJP." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Per-patient EJP from named threshold, QALY and cost cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-EJP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "per-patient-ejp-from-named-threshold-qaly-and-cost-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=Threshold*DeltaE-DeltaCOther" .

hemo:7003374 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "ICER at Drug X's EJP equals the threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Costing Drug X at GBP 740 per pack gives an incremental cost of GBP 20,000 per patient and, with 0.80 QALYs, an ICER of GBP 25,000 per QALY under HE-FM-ICER-001, the threshold used. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows Delta_C_other added instead of subtracted, which gives GBP 860 per pack and an ICER of GBP 28,750 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "ICER at Drug X's EJP equals the threshold" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EJP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "icer-at-drug-x-s-ejp-equals-the-threshold" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((740*25+1500)/0.80-25000)<1E-6" .

hemo:7003375 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Net monetary benefit of Drug X changes sign at the EJP"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At GBP 25,000 per QALY the incremental net monetary benefit of HE-FM-NMB-002 is GBP 25 per patient at GBP 739 per pack, zero at the EJP of GBP 740 and minus GBP 25 at GBP 741. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows a price that is not the EJP for these inputs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net monetary benefit of Drug X changes sign at the EJP" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EJP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-monetary-benefit-of-drug-x-changes-sign-at-the-ejp" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(25000*0.80-(739*25+1500)>0,ABS(25000*0.80-(740*25+1500))<1E-6,25000*0.80-(741*25+1500)<0)" .

hemo:7003376 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "EJP reproduces the article's Drug X prices at both thresholds"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's inputs return GBP 740 per pack at GBP 25,000 per QALY and GBP 1,060 at GBP 35,000. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EJP reproduces the article's Drug X prices at both thresholds" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EJP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ejp-reproduces-the-article-s-drug-x-prices-at-both-thresholds" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ROUND((25000*0.80-1500)/25,2)=740,ROUND((35000*0.80-1500)/25,2)=1060)" .

hemo:7003377 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "EJP is negative exactly when other costs exceed the value of the QALY gain"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The sign of the EJP follows the comparison of Delta_C_other with lambda times Delta_E: negative for a gain of 0.05 QALYs and positive for 0.80, with GBP 1,500 of other costs. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows the sign of Delta_C_other reversed." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EJP is negative exactly when other costs exceed the value of the QALY gain" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EJP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ejp-is-negative-exactly-when-other-costs-exceed-the-value-of-the-qaly-gain" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(((25000*0.05-1500)/25<0)=(1500>25000*0.05),((25000*0.80-1500)/25<0)=(1500>25000*0.80))" .

hemo:7003378 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "EJP for Drug X per pack at 25,000 pounds per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative Drug X the model gives 0.80 incremental QALYs, GBP 1,500 of other incremental costs (GBP 4,000 of extra monitoring and administration less GBP 2,500 of avoided hospital care) and 25 discounted packs per patient. At GBP 25,000 per QALY the per-patient EJP is GBP 18,500 and the EJP is GBP 740 per pack." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EJP for Drug X per pack at 25,000 pounds per QALY" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 25000; Delta_E = 0.80; Delta_C_other = 1500; U = 25; EJP_patient = 18500; EJP = 740" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EJP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ejp-for-drug-x-per-pack-at-25-000-pounds-per-qaly" .

hemo:7003379 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "EJP for Drug X per pack at 35,000 pounds per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At GBP 35,000 per QALY the same inputs give a per-patient EJP of GBP 26,500 and an EJP of GBP 1,060 per pack, as in the article; only the threshold has changed." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EJP for Drug X per pack at 35,000 pounds per QALY" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 35000; Delta_E = 0.80; Delta_C_other = 1500; U = 25; EJP_patient = 26500; EJP = 1060" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EJP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ejp-for-drug-x-per-pack-at-35-000-pounds-per-qaly" .

hemo:7003380 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "EJP for Drug X when treatment lasts 30 packs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "If patients stayed on Drug X for 30 discounted packs with the same QALY gain, the per-patient EJP would stay at GBP 18,500 but the EJP per pack would fall to about GBP 616.67, because the same value is spread over more units." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EJP for Drug X when treatment lasts 30 packs" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 25000; Delta_E = 0.80; Delta_C_other = 1500; U = 30; EJP_patient = 18500; EJP = 616.67" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EJP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ejp-for-drug-x-when-treatment-lasts-30-packs" .

hemo:7003381 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Probabilistic EJP for Drug X from mean PSA outputs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "If probabilistic analysis gave a mean QALY gain of 0.76 and a mean other cost of GBP 1,700, with 25 packs, the price at which expected incremental net benefit is zero would be GBP 692 per pack, as in the article. Because net benefit is linear in price, the formula applied to the means of the probabilistic outputs gives this price; in a non-linear model it differs from the EJP computed at mean inputs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Probabilistic EJP for Drug X from mean PSA outputs" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 25000; Delta_E = 0.76; Delta_C_other = 1700; U = 25; EJP_patient = 17300; EJP = 692" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EJP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "probabilistic-ejp-for-drug-x-from-mean-psa-outputs" .

hemo:7003382 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Per-patient EJP for Drug X with one unit per patient"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Entering 1 for U returns the per-patient EJP as the price: GBP 18,500 at GBP 25,000 per QALY, the most the payer could spend on Drug X for each patient." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Per-patient EJP for Drug X with one unit per patient" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 25000; Delta_E = 0.80; Delta_C_other = 1500; U = 1; EJP_patient = 18500; EJP = 18500" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EJP-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "per-patient-ejp-for-drug-x-with-one-unit-per-patient" .

hemo:7003383 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Negative EJP when other costs exceed the value of the QALY gain"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a QALY gain of only 0.05 and the same GBP 1,500 of other incremental costs, the value of the gain at GBP 25,000 per QALY is GBP 1,250, so the per-patient EJP is minus GBP 250 and the EJP minus GBP 10 per pack (computed here for illustration). Mladsi and colleagues call this the zero-price conundrum: costs the medicine cannot offset, not a modelling error." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Negative EJP when other costs exceed the value of the QALY gain" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 25000; Delta_E = 0.05; Delta_C_other = 1500; U = 25; EJP_patient = -250; EJP = -10" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EJP-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "negative-ejp-when-other-costs-exceed-the-value-of-the-qaly-gain" .

hemo:7003384 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Same model results at both ends of the EJP range"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Only the threshold differs between the two prices; Delta_E, Delta_C_other and U are the same model results. Lower and higher QALY gain scenarios are produced by repeating the calculation with each scenario's inputs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same model results at both ends of the EJP range" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EJP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-model-results-at-both-ends-of-the-ejp-range" .

hemo:7003385 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Thresholds for the EJP range checked at source"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The thresholds are those the payer applies at the time of the analysis. NICE's manual, updated in March 2026, uses GBP 25,000 and GBP 35,000 per QALY, and GBP 100,000 per QALY for highly specialised technologies (PMG36 sections 6.3.4 and 6.3.8)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Thresholds for the EJP range checked at source" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EJP-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "thresholds-for-the-ejp-range-checked-at-source" .

hemo:7003386 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "EJP at the lower and upper thresholds in two cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000614 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With named cells ThresholdLow, ThresholdHigh, DeltaE, DeltaCOther and Units, the two formulas return the EJP per unit at each end of the range." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EJP at the lower and upper thresholds in two cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-EJP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ejp-at-the-lower-and-upper-thresholds-in-two-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(ThresholdLow*DeltaE-DeltaCOther)/Units; =(ThresholdHigh*DeltaE-DeltaCOther)/Units" .

hemo:7003387 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Width of the EJP range equals the threshold gap times the QALY gain per unit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For Drug X the range from GBP 740 to GBP 1,060 is GBP 320 wide, which equals the GBP 10,000 gap between the thresholds times 0.80 divided by 25. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows a different U or Delta_C_other used at the two ends." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Width of the EJP range equals the threshold gap times the QALY gain per unit" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EJP-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "width-of-the-ejp-range-equals-the-threshold-gap-times-the-qaly-gain-per-unit" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((35000*0.80-1500)/25-(25000*0.80-1500)/25-(35000-25000)*0.80/25)<1E-9" .

hemo:7003388 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "EJP range reproduces the article's scenario table"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The lower and higher QALY gains of 0.60 and 1.00 return GBP 540 and GBP 780, and GBP 940 and GBP 1,340, per pack at the two thresholds. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EJP range reproduces the article's scenario table" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EJP-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ejp-range-reproduces-the-article-s-scenario-table" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ROUND((25000*0.60-1500)/25,2)=540,ROUND((35000*0.60-1500)/25,2)=780,ROUND((25000*1.00-1500)/25,2)=940,ROUND((35000*1.00-1500)/25,2)=1340)" .

hemo:7003389 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Each 0.1 QALY moves the Drug X EJP by 100 and 140 pounds"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Raising the QALY gain from 0.80 to 0.90 raises the EJP by GBP 100 per pack at GBP 25,000 per QALY and by GBP 140 at GBP 35,000, as the article states. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Each 0.1 QALY moves the Drug X EJP by 100 and 140 pounds" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EJP-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "each-0-1-qaly-moves-the-drug-x-ejp-by-100-and-140-pounds" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS((25000*0.90-1500)/25-(25000*0.80-1500)/25-100)<1E-6,ABS((35000*0.90-1500)/25-(35000*0.80-1500)/25-140)<1E-6)" .

hemo:7003390 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Drug X EJP range in the base case"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's base-case inputs Drug X has an EJP of GBP 740 per pack at GBP 25,000 per QALY and GBP 1,060 at GBP 35,000." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Drug X EJP range in the base case" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda_L = 25000; lambda_U = 35000; Delta_E = 0.80; Delta_C_other = 1500; U = 25; EJP_L = 740; EJP_U = 1060" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EJP-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "drug-x-ejp-range-in-the-base-case" .

hemo:7003391 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Drug X EJP range in the lower QALY gain scenario"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's lower QALY gain scenario the QALY gain is 0.60, and the EJP falls to GBP 540 and GBP 780 per pack." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Drug X EJP range in the lower QALY gain scenario" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda_L = 25000; lambda_U = 35000; Delta_E = 0.60; Delta_C_other = 1500; U = 25; EJP_L = 540; EJP_U = 780" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EJP-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "drug-x-ejp-range-in-the-lower-qaly-gain-scenario" .

hemo:7003392 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Drug X EJP range in the higher QALY gain scenario"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's higher QALY gain scenario the QALY gain is 1.00, and the EJP rises to GBP 940 and GBP 1,340 per pack." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Drug X EJP range in the higher QALY gain scenario" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda_L = 25000; lambda_U = 35000; Delta_E = 1.00; Delta_C_other = 1500; U = 25; EJP_L = 940; EJP_U = 1340" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EJP-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "drug-x-ejp-range-in-the-higher-qaly-gain-scenario" .

hemo:7003393 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Severity weight multiplies the QALY gain only"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The weight applies to the incremental QALYs, not to the costs. It is set from the QALY shortfall of the population for which the medicine will be used, with discounting at the reference-case rate, and the shortfall measure that implies the greater severity sets the weight (PMG36 sections 6.2.16 to 6.2.18)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Severity weight multiplies the QALY gain only" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EJP-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "severity-weight-multiplies-the-qaly-gain-only" .

hemo:7003394 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Severity-weighted EJP from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000615 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the weight in a cell named SeverityWeight and the other inputs in Threshold, DeltaE, DeltaCOther and Units, Excel returns the severity-weighted EJP per unit." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Severity-weighted EJP from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-EJP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "severity-weighted-ejp-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(SeverityWeight*Threshold*DeltaE-DeltaCOther)/Units" .

hemo:7003395 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Severity weight of 1 returns the unweighted EJP"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a weight of 1 the weighted formula returns the unweighted price of GBP 740 per pack for Drug X, and a weight of 1.7 returns GBP 1,300. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Severity weight of 1 returns the unweighted EJP" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EJP-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "severity-weight-of-1-returns-the-unweighted-ejp" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ROUND((1*25000*0.80-1500)/25,2)=740,ROUND((1.7*25000*0.80-1500)/25,2)=1300)" .

hemo:7003396 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Severity-weighted EJP reproduces the article's 900 pounds"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A weight of 1.2 with the article's inputs returns GBP 900 per pack. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE at GBP 888 shows the weight applied to the unweighted EJP instead of the QALY gain." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Severity-weighted EJP reproduces the article's 900 pounds" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EJP-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "severity-weighted-ejp-reproduces-the-article-s-900-pounds" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND((1.2*25000*0.80-1500)/25,2)=900" .

hemo:7003397 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Severity weight raises the EJP by the extra value of the QALY gain per unit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For Drug X the weight of 1.2 raises the EJP by GBP 160 per pack, which is 0.2 times GBP 25,000 times 0.80, divided by 25. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Severity weight raises the EJP by the extra value of the QALY gain per unit" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EJP-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "severity-weight-raises-the-ejp-by-the-extra-value-of-the-qaly-gain-per-unit" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((1.2*25000*0.80-1500)/25-(25000*0.80-1500)/25-(1.2-1)*25000*0.80/25)<1E-9" .

hemo:7003398 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Drug X EJP with a severity weight of 1.2"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a severity weight of 1.2 Drug X's EJP at GBP 25,000 per QALY rises from GBP 740 to GBP 900 per pack, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Drug X EJP with a severity weight of 1.2" ;
    hemo:9000014 "w = 1.2; lambda = 25000; Delta_E = 0.80; Delta_C_other = 1500; U = 25; EJP_w = 900" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EJP-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "drug-x-ejp-with-a-severity-weight-of-1-2" .

hemo:7003399 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Drug X EJP with a severity weight of 1.7"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the higher weight of 1.7 the EJP would be GBP 1,300 per pack (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Drug X EJP with a severity weight of 1.7" ;
    hemo:9000014 "w = 1.7; lambda = 25000; Delta_E = 0.80; Delta_C_other = 1500; U = 25; EJP_w = 1300" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EJP-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "drug-x-ejp-with-a-severity-weight-of-1-7" .

hemo:7003400 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Drug X EJP with no severity weight"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A weight of 1 returns the unweighted EJP of GBP 740 per pack, a limiting case that checks the implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Drug X EJP with no severity weight" ;
    hemo:9000014 "w = 1; lambda = 25000; Delta_E = 0.80; Delta_C_other = 1500; U = 25; EJP_w = 740" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EJP-012" ;
    hemo:9000023 "drug-x-ejp-with-no-severity-weight" .

hemo:7003401 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Final-visit values missing completely at random for the LOCF mixture mean"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Whether a final-visit value is missing does not depend on the earlier value or on the true final value, so the carried-forward values are a random draw from the earlier visit and the missing values a random draw from the final visit. Lachin shows the same mean whether tau is fixed or random. When dropout is not random, the same mixture holds only with the unobservable means of the dropouts' own earlier and final values (Lachin 2016, equation 3), so the bias cannot be computed from the data." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Final-visit values missing completely at random for the LOCF mixture mean" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-LOCF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "final-visit-values-missing-completely-at-random-for-the-locf-mixture-mean" .

hemo:7003402 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "All imputed values carried from one earlier visit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every participant with a missing final value was last observed at the same earlier visit, as in Lachin's two-visit model and the article's example. Where dropouts were last seen at different visits, each group carries its own earlier mean and the formula is applied to each group in turn." ;
    hemo:9000009 "All imputed values carried from one earlier visit" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-LOCF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "all-imputed-values-carried-from-one-earlier-visit" .

hemo:7003403 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "LOCF mixture mean and its bias from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000616 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the fraction imputed in a cell named Tau, the earlier-visit mean in MuLast and the true final mean in MuEnd, the first formula, in a cell named MuTilde, returns the analysed mean and the second, in a cell named BiasMu, its bias." ;
    hemo:9000009 "LOCF mixture mean and its bias from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-LOCF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "locf-mixture-mean-and-its-bias-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=Tau*MuLast+(1-Tau)*MuEnd; =Tau*(MuLast-MuEnd)" .

hemo:7003404 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Lachin's single-arm LOCF means reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The mixture formula returns Lachin's Table 1 values of 0.3 for case 3 and 0.4 for case 6. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Lachin's single-arm LOCF means reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-LOCF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "lachin-s-single-arm-locf-means-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(0.3*1+0.7*0-0.3)<1E-9,ABS(0.3*(-1)+0.7*1-0.4)<1E-9)" .

hemo:7003405 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "LOCF bias cell equals the mixture mean minus the true final mean"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "BiasMu equals MuTilde minus MuEnd and also tau times the change between the visits; for the article's treatment arm both are 0.016. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows the weights tau and one minus tau swapped in MuTilde, whenever tau is not one half and the two means differ, or the bias entered with the wrong sign." ;
    hemo:9000009 "LOCF bias cell equals the mixture mean minus the true final mean" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-LOCF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "locf-bias-cell-equals-the-mixture-mean-minus-the-true-final-mean" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(BiasMu-(MuTilde-MuEnd))<1E-9,ABS(BiasMu-Tau*(MuLast-MuEnd))<1E-9)" .

hemo:7003406 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No LOCF bias when the mean does not change after dropout"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the same mean of 0.6 at both visits the analysed mean is 0.6 for any fraction imputed, here one half. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No LOCF bias when the mean does not change after dropout" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-LOCF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-locf-bias-when-the-mean-does-not-change-after-dropout" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(0.5*0.6+(1-0.5)*0.6-0.6)<1E-9" .

hemo:7003407 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "LOCF mean in the treatment arm with 40% dropout after six months"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative trial, 40% of the treatment arm stop after the 6-month visit, where the mean utility is 0.68, against a true 12-month mean of 0.64. LOCF returns an analysed 12-month mean of 0.656, an upward bias of 0.016 in a deteriorating condition." ;
    hemo:9000009 "LOCF mean in the treatment arm with 40% dropout after six months" ;
    hemo:9000014 "tau = 0.4; mu_last = 0.68; mu_end = 0.64; mu_tilde = 0.656; bias_mu = 0.016" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-LOCF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "locf-mean-in-the-treatment-arm-with-40-dropout-after-six-months" .

hemo:7003408 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "LOCF mean in the control arm with 10% dropout after six months"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the control arm 10% stop after the 6-month visit, where the mean is 0.64, against a true 12-month mean of 0.58. The analysed mean is 0.586, an upward bias of 0.006, smaller than in the treatment arm because fewer values are imputed." ;
    hemo:9000009 "LOCF mean in the control arm with 10% dropout after six months" ;
    hemo:9000014 "tau = 0.1; mu_last = 0.64; mu_end = 0.58; mu_tilde = 0.586; bias_mu = 0.006" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-LOCF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "locf-mean-in-the-control-arm-with-10-dropout-after-six-months" .

hemo:7003409 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "LOCF mean when the earlier visit shows a decline that later reverses"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Lachin's case 6: 30% of final values are imputed from an earlier visit with a mean change from baseline of minus 1, while the true final mean change is 1. The analysed mean change is diluted to 0.4, a bias of minus 0.6." ;
    hemo:9000009 "LOCF mean when the earlier visit shows a decline that later reverses" ;
    hemo:9000014 "tau = 0.3; mu_last = -1; mu_end = 1; mu_tilde = 0.4; bias_mu = -0.6" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-LOCF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "locf-mean-when-the-earlier-visit-shows-a-decline-that-later-reverses" .

hemo:7003410 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "LOCF mean with no true change at the final visit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Lachin's case 3: the true final mean change is 0 but the earlier mean is 1, so with 30% imputed the analysed mean is 0.3. In Lachin's setting, with n = 100 and variance 20 at both visits, a one-sided test of no change at the 0.05 level then rejects with probability 0.164 instead of the nominal 0.05." ;
    hemo:9000009 "LOCF mean with no true change at the final visit" ;
    hemo:9000014 "tau = 0.3; mu_last = 1; mu_end = 0; mu_tilde = 0.3; bias_mu = 0.3" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-LOCF-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "locf-mean-with-no-true-change-at-the-final-visit" .

hemo:7003411 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Dropout completely at random within each arm for the LOCF difference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Within each arm, missingness does not depend on the earlier or the final value, although the fraction missing may differ between arms, for example because one treatment is less well tolerated. The arms are independent and each arm's dropouts were last observed at the same earlier visit." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Dropout completely at random within each arm for the LOCF difference" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-LOCF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "dropout-completely-at-random-within-each-arm-for-the-locf-difference" .

hemo:7003412 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Arm means taken from complete follow-up for the LOCF bias"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "mu_T_end and mu_C_end are the means that complete follow-up would give, which the analyst cannot observe in a real trial. The formula is used to show the direction and size of the bias under stated scenarios, not to correct an LOCF estimate." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Arm means taken from complete follow-up for the LOCF bias" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-LOCF-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "arm-means-taken-from-complete-follow-up-for-the-locf-bias" .

hemo:7003413 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "LOCF between-arm difference and its bias from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000617 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With named cells TauT, TauC, MuTLast, MuTEnd, MuCLast and MuCEnd, the first formula, in a cell named DeltaTilde, returns the analysed difference and the second, in a cell named BiasDelta, its bias." ;
    hemo:9000009 "LOCF between-arm difference and its bias from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-LOCF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "locf-between-arm-difference-and-its-bias-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(TauT*MuTLast+(1-TauT)*MuTEnd)-(TauC*MuCLast+(1-TauC)*MuCEnd); =TauT*(MuTLast-MuTEnd)-TauC*(MuCLast-MuCEnd)" .

hemo:7003414 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "LOCF difference bias cell agrees with the analysed minus the true difference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "BiasDelta equals DeltaTilde minus the true difference, MuTEnd minus MuCEnd, and also the treatment arm's error minus the control arm's; for the article's inputs both are 0.010. Expected result: TRUE. FALSE shows an arm's error entered with the wrong sign." ;
    hemo:9000009 "LOCF difference bias cell agrees with the analysed minus the true difference" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-LOCF-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "locf-difference-bias-cell-agrees-with-the-analysed-minus-the-true-difference" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(BiasDelta-(DeltaTilde-(MuTEnd-MuCEnd)))<1E-9,ABS(BiasDelta-(TauT*(MuTLast-MuTEnd)-TauC*(MuCLast-MuCEnd)))<1E-9)" .

hemo:7003415 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal LOCF fractions reduce to Lachin's weighted difference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With 30% imputed in both arms of Lachin's case 8, the general formula gives the same analysed difference as tau times the earlier difference plus one minus tau times the final difference, 1.3. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equal LOCF fractions reduce to Lachin's weighted difference" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-LOCF-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equal-locf-fractions-reduce-to-lachin-s-weighted-difference" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(((0.3*1+0.7*1)-(0.3*(-1)+0.7*0))-(0.3*(1-(-1))+0.7*(1-0)))<1E-9" .

hemo:7003416 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No LOCF bias when neither arm changes after dropout"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "If each arm's mean is the same at the earlier and final visits, here 0.68 and 0.64, the bias is zero whatever the two dropout fractions. Expected result: TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No LOCF bias when neither arm changes after dropout" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-LOCF-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-locf-bias-when-neither-arm-changes-after-dropout" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(0.4*(0.68-0.68)-0.1*(0.64-0.64))<1E-9" .

hemo:7003417 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "LOCF overstates a widening utility difference under four-fold dropout"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative trial, 40% of the treatment arm and 10% of the control arm are imputed from 6-month means of 0.68 and 0.64, against true 12-month means of 0.64 and 0.58. The true difference is 0.06 but LOCF returns 0.07, an overstatement of 0.010, about 17%, although the true difference widens over the second half-year." ;
    hemo:9000009 "LOCF overstates a widening utility difference under four-fold dropout" ;
    hemo:9000014 "tau_T = 0.4; tau_C = 0.1; mu_T_last = 0.68; mu_T_end = 0.64; mu_C_last = 0.64; mu_C_end = 0.58; Delta_tilde = 0.07; Delta = 0.06; bias_Delta = 0.01" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-LOCF-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "locf-overstates-a-widening-utility-difference-under-four-fold-dropout" .

hemo:7003418 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "LOCF understates the same utility difference when the control arm drops out more"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Reversing the dropout fractions, 10% in the treatment arm and 40% in the control arm, with the same means, gives an analysed difference of 0.04 against the true 0.06, an understatement of 0.02 (computed here for illustration). The direction of the bias depends on who leaves." ;
    hemo:9000009 "LOCF understates the same utility difference when the control arm drops out more" ;
    hemo:9000014 "tau_T = 0.1; tau_C = 0.4; mu_T_last = 0.68; mu_T_end = 0.64; mu_C_last = 0.64; mu_C_end = 0.58; Delta_tilde = 0.04; Delta = 0.06; bias_Delta = -0.02" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-LOCF-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "locf-understates-the-same-utility-difference-when-the-control-arm-drops-out-more" .

hemo:7003419 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal LOCF dropout with a larger earlier difference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Lachin's Table 2 case 8: 30% of each arm imputed, a true final difference of 1 and an earlier difference of 2. The analysed difference is 1.3, biased upwards because the earlier difference exceeds the final one." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equal LOCF dropout with a larger earlier difference" ;
    hemo:9000014 "tau_T = 0.3; tau_C = 0.3; mu_T_last = 1; mu_T_end = 1; mu_C_last = -1; mu_C_end = 0; Delta_tilde = 1.3; Delta = 1; bias_Delta = 0.3" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-LOCF-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equal-locf-dropout-with-a-larger-earlier-difference" .

hemo:7003420 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal LOCF dropout with an earlier difference of the opposite sign"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Lachin's Table 2 case 5: with 30% of each arm imputed, an earlier difference of minus 1 and a true final difference of 1 give an analysed difference of 0.4, biased downwards by 0.6." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equal LOCF dropout with an earlier difference of the opposite sign" ;
    hemo:9000014 "tau_T = 0.3; tau_C = 0.3; mu_T_last = 1; mu_T_end = 1; mu_C_last = 2; mu_C_end = 0; Delta_tilde = 0.4; Delta = 1; bias_Delta = -0.6" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-LOCF-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equal-locf-dropout-with-an-earlier-difference-of-the-opposite-sign" .

hemo:7003421 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One constant annual rate across a level discounted stream"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The same rate r applies in every year, the constant or exponential model that Attema and colleagues describe as the one most used by social planners. The Green Book steps its rate down after year 30, so under that guidance a longer stream needs each year's factor built from the rates in force and the closed form no longer applies." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One constant annual rate across a level discounted stream" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DISC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-constant-annual-rate-across-a-level-discounted-stream" .

hemo:7003422 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Each year's amount counted at the end of the year"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each year's amount is credited at the end of that year, as for the QALYs in the article's worked example. Counting at the start of each year moves every factor one period earlier and multiplies the total by (1 + r), as in HE-FM-DISC-002, so the convention is stated and kept the same for every option compared." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Each year's amount counted at the end of the year" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DISC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "each-year-s-amount-counted-at-the-end-of-the-year" .

hemo:7003423 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant prices for a discounted cost stream"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "A cost stream is expressed in real terms, with general inflation removed first, because the Green Book rate is a real rate and adding the inflation rate to it gives a mathematically incorrect result." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant prices for a discounted cost stream" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DISC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-prices-for-a-discounted-cost-stream" .

hemo:7003424 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "End-of-year discounted level stream with Excel PV"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000618 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the rate in DiscRate, the number of years in Years and the yearly amount in YearlyAmount, Excel PV returns the total. The minus sign is needed because PV reports a payment as a negative value. The second formula is the same closed form written out." ;
    hemo:9000009 "End-of-year discounted level stream with Excel PV" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-DISC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "end-of-year-discounted-level-stream-with-excel-pv" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=PV(DiscRate,Years,-YearlyAmount); =YearlyAmount*(1-(1+DiscRate)^-Years)/DiscRate" .

hemo:7003425 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "End-of-year closed form agrees with the factor-by-factor sum"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Enter the yearly amounts in a range named Stream and the year numbers 1 to T beside them in a range named YearIndex, then compare the sum of each amount times its factor with the closed form. The expected result is TRUE. FALSE shows a year index that starts at 0 or a year missing from the range." ;
    hemo:9000009 "End-of-year closed form agrees with the factor-by-factor sum" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DISC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "end-of-year-closed-form-agrees-with-the-factor-by-factor-sum" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(SUMPRODUCT(Stream,1/(1+DiscRate)^YearIndex)-YearlyAmount*(1-(1+DiscRate)^-Years)/DiscRate)<1E-9" .

hemo:7003426 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "End-of-year discounted total between its two bounds"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For a positive rate and amount the discounted total lies below the undiscounted total q times T and above q times T times the factor for the final year. The expected result is TRUE. FALSE shows the rate entered as 3.5 instead of 0.035 or a sign error in the exponent." ;
    hemo:9000009 "End-of-year discounted total between its two bounds" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DISC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "end-of-year-discounted-total-between-its-two-bounds" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(DiscTotal<YearlyAmount*Years,DiscTotal>YearlyAmount*Years/(1+DiscRate)^Years)" .

hemo:7003427 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Thirty years of 0.8 QALYs counted at year end and discounted at 3.5 per cent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:1000037 hemo:7003428,
        hemo:7003429,
        hemo:7003430 ;
    hemo:1000038 hemo:7003431 ;
    hemo:2000008 0.00005 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An extra 0.8 QALYs in each of 30 years, counted at the end of each year, is worth about 14.71 discounted QALYs at 3.5% against 24 undiscounted. The 30 factors sum to 18.3920 and the factor for year 30 is about 0.3563, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Thirty years of 0.8 QALYs counted at year end and discounted at 3.5 per cent" ;
    hemo:9000014 "q = 0.8; r = 0.035; T = 30; Q = 14.7136" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DISC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "thirty-years-of-0-8-qalys-counted-at-year-end-and-discounted-at-3-5-per-cent" .

hemo:7003428 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "q (lower case) in the test Thirty years of 0.8 QALYs counted at year end and discounted at 3.5 per cent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6002622 ;
    rheo:2000001 0.8 .

hemo:7003429 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "r in the test Thirty years of 0.8 QALYs counted at year end and discounted at 3.5 per cent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6002623 ;
    rheo:2000001 0.035 .

hemo:7003430 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "T in the test Thirty years of 0.8 QALYs counted at year end and discounted at 3.5 per cent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6002624 ;
    rheo:2000001 30.0 .

hemo:7003431 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "Q (capital) in the test Thirty years of 0.8 QALYs counted at year end and discounted at 3.5 per cent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6002621 ;
    rheo:2000001 14.7136 .

hemo:7003432 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Thirty years of 0.8 QALYs counted at year end and discounted at 1.5 per cent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:1000037 hemo:7003433,
        hemo:7003434,
        hemo:7003435 ;
    hemo:1000038 hemo:7003436 ;
    hemo:2000008 0.00005 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At 1.5% the same gain is worth about 19.21 discounted QALYs, since the 30 factors sum to 24.0158, as in the article's table for the NICE non-reference-case rate." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Thirty years of 0.8 QALYs counted at year end and discounted at 1.5 per cent" ;
    hemo:9000014 "q = 0.8; r = 0.015; T = 30; Q = 19.2127" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DISC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "thirty-years-of-0-8-qalys-counted-at-year-end-and-discounted-at-1-5-per-cent" .

hemo:7003433 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "q (lower case) in the test Thirty years of 0.8 QALYs counted at year end and discounted at 1.5 per cent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6002622 ;
    rheo:2000001 0.8 .

hemo:7003434 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "r in the test Thirty years of 0.8 QALYs counted at year end and discounted at 1.5 per cent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6002623 ;
    rheo:2000001 0.015 .

