    hemo:1000039 hemo:6002850 ;
    rheo:2000001 1460000.0 .

hemo:7003976 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "ENBS in the test ENBS of the article's 600-patient trial with a discounted population of 8,000"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6002848 ;
    rheo:2000001 940000.0 .

hemo:7003977 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "ENBS of a 600-patient trial with 2,000 people a year for five years at 3.5 per cent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:1000037 hemo:7003978,
        hemo:7003979 ;
    hemo:1000038 hemo:7003980 ;
    hemo:2000008 0.005 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With an illustrative 2,000 people a year facing the decision for five years and later cohorts discounted at 3.5% a year, HE-FM-EVSI-004 gives a discounted population of about 9,346 and a population EVSI of about GBP 2,803,848 for the per-person EVSI of GBP 300. Against the same study cost of GBP 1,460,000, ENBS is about GBP 1,343,848 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "ENBS of a 600-patient trial with 2,000 people a year for five years at 3.5 per cent" ;
    hemo:9000014 "EVSI_pop = 2803847.53; C_n = 1460000; ENBS = 1343847.53" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ENBS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "enbs-of-a-600-patient-trial-with-2-000-people-a-year-for-five-years-at-3-5-per-cent" .

hemo:7003978 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "EVSI_pop in the test ENBS of a 600-patient trial with 2,000 people a year for five years at 3.5 per cent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6002849 ;
    rheo:2000001 2803847.529999999795109 .

hemo:7003979 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "C_n in the test ENBS of a 600-patient trial with 2,000 people a year for five years at 3.5 per cent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6002850 ;
    rheo:2000001 1460000.0 .

hemo:7003980 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "ENBS in the test ENBS of a 600-patient trial with 2,000 people a year for five years at 3.5 per cent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6002848 ;
    rheo:2000001 1343847.53000000002794 .

hemo:7003981 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Negative ENBS of a 100-patient trial for a small population"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:1000037 hemo:7003982,
        hemo:7003983 ;
    hemo:1000038 hemo:7003984 ;
    hemo:2000008 0.5 ;
    hemo:9000008 "If only 1,000 discounted people would follow the result, the article's 100-patient trial, with a per-person EVSI of GBP 100, has a population EVSI of GBP 100,000 against a cost of GBP 410,000, so ENBS is minus GBP 310,000 and the design is not worthwhile (illustrative population). The negative value applies to this design only." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Negative ENBS of a 100-patient trial for a small population" ;
    hemo:9000014 "EVSI_pop = 100000; C_n = 410000; ENBS = -310000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ENBS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "negative-enbs-of-a-100-patient-trial-for-a-small-population" .

hemo:7003982 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "EVSI_pop in the test Negative ENBS of a 100-patient trial for a small population"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6002849 ;
    rheo:2000001 100000.0 .

hemo:7003983 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "C_n in the test Negative ENBS of a 100-patient trial for a small population"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6002850 ;
    rheo:2000001 410000.0 .

hemo:7003984 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "ENBS in the test Negative ENBS of a 100-patient trial for a small population"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6002848 ;
    rheo:2000001 -310000.0 .

hemo:7003985 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Participants on the option expected to be better lose nothing"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Participants allocated to the option with the higher expected net benefit receive what they would have received outside the study, and Fenwick and colleagues note that those in the optimal arm benefit while the study is conducted. The loss for the other arm is valued with the current expected incremental net benefit, on the same scale and threshold as EVSI." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Participants on the option expected to be better lose nothing" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ENBS-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "participants-on-the-option-expected-to-be-better-lose-nothing" .

hemo:7003986 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Stated perspective for the cost of a study"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Fenwick and colleagues state that the total cost is commonly determined from a societal perspective, although it may also be considered from the perspective of the sponsor of the study. The fixed and variable costs are those of the design as specified, so a change in follow-up or endpoints changes them." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Stated perspective for the cost of a study" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ENBS-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "stated-perspective-for-the-cost-of-a-study" .

hemo:7003987 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected cost of a two-arm study in one Excel cell"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000659 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the fixed cost in FixedCost, the cost per participant in CostPerParticipant, the sample size in SampleSize, the share on the inferior option in InferiorShare and the expected incremental net benefit in ExpectedINB, the cell adds the three components. ABS keeps the participant loss positive when standard care is expected to be better." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected cost of a two-arm study in one Excel cell" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ENBS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-cost-of-a-two-arm-study-in-one-excel-cell" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=FixedCost+CostPerParticipant*SampleSize+InferiorShare*SampleSize*ABS(ExpectedINB)" .

hemo:7003988 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Study cost at least the fixed plus variable cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The participant loss can only add to the cost, so the total is at least the fixed cost plus the variable cost. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a negative expected incremental net benefit entered without ABS, which turns the loss into a gain." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Study cost at least the fixed plus variable cost" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ENBS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "study-cost-at-least-the-fixed-plus-variable-cost" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=StudyCost>=FixedCost+CostPerParticipant*SampleSize" .

hemo:7003989 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's 100, 300 and 1,000-patient trial costs reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's table gives totals of GBP 410,000, GBP 830,000 and GBP 2,300,000 for these designs. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the participant loss applied to every participant or left out." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's 100, 300 and 1,000-patient trial costs reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ENBS-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-100-300-and-1-000-patient-trial-costs-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(200000+2000*100+0.5*100*200-410000)<0.5,ABS(200000+2000*300+0.5*300*200-830000)<0.5,ABS(200000+2000*1000+0.5*1000*200-2300000)<0.5)" .

hemo:7003990 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Cost of the article's 600-patient trial with equal allocation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative study costs GBP 200,000 fixed plus GBP 2,000 per participant, and with equal allocation 300 participants receive standard care and forgo the expected incremental net monetary benefit of GBP 200 each, a loss of GBP 60,000. The total is GBP 1,460,000." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cost of the article's 600-patient trial with equal allocation" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C_F = 200000; c_V = 2000; n = 600; s_inf = 0.5; INB = 200; C_n = 1460000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ENBS-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cost-of-the-article-s-600-patient-trial-with-equal-allocation" .

hemo:7003991 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Cost of the article's 1,000-patient trial with equal allocation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For the article's largest design the variable cost is GBP 2,000,000 and the participant loss GBP 100,000, so the total is GBP 2,300,000." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cost of the article's 1,000-patient trial with equal allocation" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C_F = 200000; c_V = 2000; n = 1000; s_inf = 0.5; INB = 200; C_n = 2300000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ENBS-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cost-of-the-article-s-1-000-patient-trial-with-equal-allocation" .

hemo:7003992 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Cost of a 600-participant study with no participant on the inferior option"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When no participant receives the option expected to be inferior, for example a follow-up study of patients on the option expected to be best (illustrative), the loss term is zero and the cost is the fixed and variable cost alone, GBP 1,400,000." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cost of a 600-participant study with no participant on the inferior option" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C_F = 200000; c_V = 2000; n = 600; s_inf = 0; INB = 200; C_n = 1400000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ENBS-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cost-of-a-600-participant-study-with-no-participant-on-the-inferior-option" .

hemo:7003993 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "New treatment withheld outside the study under only in research"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Fenwick and colleagues explain that restricting a technology to people in research avoids committing irrecoverable costs, while patients outside the study would not have access to it and could miss the best treatment. McKenna and Claxton separate the effect of the research decision on patients enrolled, on those not enrolled and on those treated after the trial reports." ;
    hemo:9000009 "New treatment withheld outside the study under only in research" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ENBS-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "new-treatment-withheld-outside-the-study-under-only-in-research" .

hemo:7003994 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Population EVSI held fixed across access arrangements"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's example keeps population EVSI the same under both arrangements. Fenwick and colleagues adjust the beneficiary population for the time the study takes and for patients enrolled when the reimbursement decision is delayed, so a full analysis also recomputes EVSI_pop for each design." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Population EVSI held fixed across access arrangements" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ENBS-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "population-evsi-held-fixed-across-access-arrangements" .

hemo:7003995 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "ENBS under only in research in one Excel cell"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000660 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With population EVSI in PopulationEVSI, the study cost in StudyCost, the number waiting in WaitingPatients and the expected incremental net benefit in ExpectedINB, MAX sets the waiting cost to zero when the new treatment is not expected to be better." ;
    hemo:9000009 "ENBS under only in research in one Excel cell" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ENBS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "enbs-under-only-in-research-in-one-excel-cell" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=PopulationEVSI-StudyCost-WaitingPatients*MAX(ExpectedINB,0)" .

hemo:7003996 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Smaller trial efficient under only in research in the article"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Under only in research the 300-patient trial has the higher ENBS, GBP 570,000 against GBP 540,000. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the waiting cost left out, since without it the 600-patient trial is ahead by GBP 170,000." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Smaller trial efficient under only in research in the article" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ENBS-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "smaller-trial-efficient-under-only-in-research-in-the-article" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(1600000-830000-1000*MAX(200,0))>(2400000-1460000-2000*MAX(200,0))" .

hemo:7003997 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "ENBS under only in research not above ENBS with approval"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Waiting can only lower the value of a design, so ENBS under only in research is at most population EVSI less the study cost. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a negative expected incremental net benefit entered without MAX, which turns the waiting cost into a gain." ;
    hemo:9000009 "ENBS under only in research not above ENBS with approval" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ENBS-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "enbs-under-only-in-research-not-above-enbs-with-approval" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ENBSOnlyInResearch<=PopulationEVSI-StudyCost" .

hemo:7003998 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's 600-patient trial under only in research"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative example 2,000 people treated outside the 600-patient trial while it runs miss GBP 200 each, adding GBP 400,000 to the cost. ENBS falls to GBP 540,000." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's 600-patient trial under only in research" ;
    hemo:9000014 "EVSI_pop = 2400000; C_n = 1460000; N_w = 2000; INB = 200; ENBS_w = 540000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ENBS-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-600-patient-trial-under-only-in-research" .

hemo:7003999 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's 300-patient trial under only in research"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The 300-patient trial reports sooner, with 1,000 people waiting, so its ENBS becomes GBP 570,000 and the smaller trial is now efficient, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's 300-patient trial under only in research" ;
    hemo:9000014 "EVSI_pop = 1600000; C_n = 830000; N_w = 1000; INB = 200; ENBS_w = 570000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ENBS-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-300-patient-trial-under-only-in-research" .

hemo:7004000 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's 600-patient trial under approval with research"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With approval with research nobody waits, N_w is zero and the formula returns the ENBS of GBP 940,000 from the article's table, a limiting case that checks the implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's 600-patient trial under approval with research" ;
    hemo:9000014 "EVSI_pop = 2400000; C_n = 1460000; N_w = 0; INB = 200; ENBS_w = 940000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ENBS-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-600-patient-trial-under-approval-with-research" .

hemo:7004001 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "EVPI and EVSI on the same population and threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Population EVPI and population EVSI use the same beneficiary population, time horizon, discount rate and threshold. Otherwise EVSI can appear to exceed EVPI and the ceiling no longer holds." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EVPI and EVSI on the same population and threshold" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ENBS-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "evpi-and-evsi-on-the-same-population-and-threshold" .

hemo:7004002 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Same study cost in the ceiling as in the ENBS"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "C_n is the full expected cost used for ENBS, including the participant loss of HE-FM-ENBS-002. Using the fixed cost alone gives a weaker screen that rules out fewer designs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same study cost in the ceiling as in the ENBS" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ENBS-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-study-cost-in-the-ceiling-as-in-the-enbs" .

hemo:7004003 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Population EVPI ceiling on ENBS in Excel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000661 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With population EVPI in PopulationEVPI and the expected study cost in StudyCost, the cell returns the ceiling. A value of zero or below rules the design out." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Population EVPI ceiling on ENBS in Excel" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ENBS-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "population-evpi-ceiling-on-enbs-in-excel" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=PopulationEVPI-StudyCost" .

hemo:7004004 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "ENBS of a design within its population EVPI ceiling"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The ENBS of any design is at most population EVPI less its cost, GBP 940,000 against GBP 1,740,000 for the article's 600-patient trial. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows population EVSI above population EVPI, from simulation error or from different populations or thresholds in the two calculations." ;
    hemo:9000009 "ENBS of a design within its population EVPI ceiling" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ENBS-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "enbs-of-a-design-within-its-population-evpi-ceiling" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ENBS<=PopulationEVPI-StudyCost" .

hemo:7004005 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Population EVPI ceiling for the article's 600-patient trial"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Per-person EVPI of GBP 400 across 8,000 discounted people gives population EVPI of GBP 3,200,000. Less the cost of GBP 1,460,000 the ceiling is GBP 1,740,000, above the trial's ENBS of GBP 940,000." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Population EVPI ceiling for the article's 600-patient trial" ;
    hemo:9000014 "EVPI_pop = 3200000; C_n = 1460000; ENBS_ub = 1740000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ENBS-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "population-evpi-ceiling-for-the-article-s-600-patient-trial" .

hemo:7004006 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Study costing more than population EVPI"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An illustrative study costing GBP 3,500,000 against the article's population EVPI of GBP 3,200,000 has a ceiling of minus GBP 300,000, so it cannot be worthwhile whatever its EVSI." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Study costing more than population EVPI" ;
    hemo:9000014 "EVPI_pop = 3200000; C_n = 3500000; ENBS_ub = -300000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ENBS-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "study-costing-more-than-population-evpi" .

hemo:7004007 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Independent samples for the two arms of a net health benefit interval"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The two arms are separate samples of patients, so costs and effects are uncorrelated across arms, while each arm's own costs and effects may be correlated, which r_1 and r_0 carry. Stinnett and Mullahy state that this is reasonable when the two interventions are observed in different samples. A paired or crossover design needs the covariance of the paired differences instead." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Independent samples for the two arms of a net health benefit interval" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-NBA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "independent-samples-for-the-two-arms-of-a-net-health-benefit-interval" .

hemo:7004008 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Normal approximation for the net health benefit estimator"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The interval relies on the Central Limit Theorem. Stinnett and Mullahy give a rule of thumb of 30 or more observations, and the estimator is exactly normal in finite samples when costs and effects are jointly multivariate normal. They describe a nonparametric bootstrap interval as the alternative, the safer choice when arms are small and costs heavily skewed, and the bootstrap variance of incremental net monetary benefit is HE-FM-BSE-003." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Normal approximation for the net health benefit estimator" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-NBA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "normal-approximation-for-the-net-health-benefit-estimator" .

hemo:7004009 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Threshold fixed for each net health benefit interval"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Lambda is a stated policy value, not estimated from the trial, so it adds no sampling variance. Stinnett and Mullahy recommend repeating the analysis over a range of lambda values and reporting net health benefit as a function of lambda." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Threshold fixed for each net health benefit interval" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-NBA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "threshold-fixed-for-each-net-health-benefit-interval" .

hemo:7004010 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Interval for incremental net health benefit from per-arm named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000662 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the arm means in MeanEffect1, MeanEffect0, MeanCost1 and MeanCost0, the standard deviations in SdEffect1, SdCost1, SdEffect0 and SdCost0, the correlations in Corr1 and Corr0, the arm sizes in ArmSize1 and ArmSize0, the threshold in Threshold and the critical value in ZValue, the first formula gives the estimate (cell INHB) and the second its standard error (cell SeINHB). The third and fourth give the limits, and the last gives the probability that net health benefit is positive." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Interval for incremental net health benefit from per-arm named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-NBA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "interval-for-incremental-net-health-benefit-from-per-arm-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MeanEffect1-MeanEffect0-(MeanCost1-MeanCost0)/Threshold; =SQRT((SdEffect1^2+SdCost1^2/Threshold^2-2*Corr1*SdEffect1*SdCost1/Threshold)/ArmSize1+(SdEffect0^2+SdCost0^2/Threshold^2-2*Corr0*SdEffect0*SdCost0/Threshold)/ArmSize0); =INHB-ZValue*SeINHB; =INHB+ZValue*SeINHB; =NORM.S.DIST(INHB/SeINHB,TRUE)" .

hemo:7004011 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Health-scale standard error times lambda equals the money-scale standard error"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Multiplying SE_INHB by lambda must give the standard error of incremental net monetary benefit in HE-FM-AN-004, with SE_E squared equal to s_E1 squared over n_1 plus s_E0 squared over n_0, SE_C squared built the same way from the cost standard deviations, and Cov_CE equal to r_1 s_E1 s_C1 over n_1 plus r_0 s_E0 s_C0 over n_0. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a correlation term entered with the wrong sign or lambda squared left out of a cost term." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health-scale standard error times lambda equals the money-scale standard error" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-NBA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-scale-standard-error-times-lambda-equals-the-money-scale-standard-error" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(Threshold*SeINHB-SQRT(Threshold^2*(SdEffect1^2/ArmSize1+SdEffect0^2/ArmSize0)+SdCost1^2/ArmSize1+SdCost0^2/ArmSize0-2*Threshold*(Corr1*SdEffect1*SdCost1/ArmSize1+Corr0*SdEffect0*SdCost0/ArmSize0)))<1E-6" .

hemo:7004012 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Net health benefit standard error at a very large threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "As lambda grows without limit the cost terms vanish and the standard error tends to that of the difference in mean effects alone, a limiting case that checks the implementation. Setting lambda to 1E12 in the standard error formula gives that value. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the cost variance multiplied by lambda squared instead of divided by it." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net health benefit standard error at a very large threshold" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-NBA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-health-benefit-standard-error-at-a-very-large-threshold" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(SQRT((SdEffect1^2+SdCost1^2/1E12^2-2*Corr1*SdEffect1*SdCost1/1E12)/ArmSize1+(SdEffect0^2+SdCost0^2/1E12^2-2*Corr0*SdEffect0*SdCost0/1E12)/ArmSize0)-SQRT(SdEffect1^2/ArmSize1+SdEffect0^2/ArmSize0))<1E-6" .

hemo:7004013 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative trial standard error reproduced at GBP 25,000 per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The illustrative trial's figures entered directly reproduce the standard error of the first worked example. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows an arithmetic slip in the variance terms, such as squaring the correlation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative trial standard error reproduced at GBP 25,000 per QALY" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-NBA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-trial-standard-error-reproduced-at-gbp-25-000-per-qaly" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(SQRT((0.25^2+3000^2/25000^2-2*0.3*0.25*3000/25000)/150+(0.25^2+2500^2/25000^2-2*0.2*0.25*2500/25000)/150)-0.028449)<0.000001" .

hemo:7004014 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative two-arm trial judged at GBP 25,000 per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative arms of 150 patients each have mean QALYs of 0.80 and 0.70, mean costs of GBP 6,000 and GBP 4,500, standard deviations of 0.25 QALYs in both arms and of GBP 3,000 and GBP 2,500 for cost, and within-arm correlations of 0.3 and 0.2. At GBP 25,000 per QALY, one of the two values PMG36 section 4.2.16 asks for, the estimate is 0.04 QALYs per patient with a 95% interval of about minus 0.0158 to 0.0958, so the interval includes zero. The z statistic of 1.406 corresponds to a probability of about 0.92 that net health benefit is positive." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative two-arm trial judged at GBP 25,000 per QALY" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 25000; E_1 = 0.80; E_0 = 0.70; C_1 = 6000; C_0 = 4500; s_E1 = 0.25; s_C1 = 3000; r_1 = 0.3; n_1 = 150; s_E0 = 0.25; s_C0 = 2500; r_0 = 0.2; n_0 = 150; z = 1.96; INHB = 0.04; Var_INHB = 0.00080933; SE_INHB = 0.028449; L_INHB = -0.015760; U_INHB = 0.095760; z_INHB = 1.4060" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NBA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-two-arm-trial-judged-at-gbp-25-000-per-qaly" .

hemo:7004015 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative two-arm trial judged at GBP 35,000 per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The same illustrative trial at GBP 35,000 per QALY, the second PMG36 value, gives an estimate of about 0.0571 QALYs per patient with a 95% interval of about 0.0023 to 0.1120, which now excludes zero. The standard error falls slightly because the cost terms are divided by a larger lambda." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative two-arm trial judged at GBP 35,000 per QALY" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 35000; E_1 = 0.80; E_0 = 0.70; C_1 = 6000; C_0 = 4500; s_E1 = 0.25; s_C1 = 3000; r_1 = 0.3; n_1 = 150; s_E0 = 0.25; s_C0 = 2500; r_0 = 0.2; n_0 = 150; z = 1.96; INHB = 0.057143; Var_INHB = 0.00078299; SE_INHB = 0.027982; L_INHB = 0.002298; U_INHB = 0.111988; z_INHB = 2.0421" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NBA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-two-arm-trial-judged-at-gbp-35-000-per-qaly" .

hemo:7004016 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Non-zero effect difference in the quadrant identity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The identity divides by Delta_E. When the effects are equal the ICER is undefined but INMB is simply minus Delta_C, so INMB is computed directly from HE-FM-NMB-002 and the identity is used to read a reported ICER, not to replace that calculation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Non-zero effect difference in the quadrant identity" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-NBA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "non-zero-effect-difference-in-the-quadrant-identity" .

hemo:7004017 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One subtraction direction for the ratio and the net benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Delta_C, Delta_E and R are all new option minus comparator, as in HE-AS-NMB-003. Reversing the direction of both differences leaves R unchanged but reverses the sign of INMB, which is why R alone cannot show which option is favoured." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One subtraction direction for the ratio and the net benefit" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-NBA-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-subtraction-direction-for-the-ratio-and-the-net-benefit" .

hemo:7004018 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Incremental net monetary benefit from a reported ICER and effect difference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000663 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the ICER in ICER, the effect difference in IncrementalEffect and the threshold in Threshold, the first formula returns INMB and the second the decision, which reads the same way in every quadrant." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Incremental net monetary benefit from a reported ICER and effect difference" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-NBA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "incremental-net-monetary-benefit-from-a-reported-icer-and-effect-difference" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IncrementalEffect*(Threshold-ICER); =IF(IncrementalEffect*(Threshold-ICER)>0,\"Adopt new option\",\"Keep comparator\")" .

hemo:7004019 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "ICER rule with the inequality reversed for a health loss"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "INMB is positive exactly when R is below lambda for a health gain and exactly when R is above lambda for a health loss, for any non-zero Delta_E and R not equal to lambda. It extends HE-VT-NMB-003, which covers a health gain only. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the ICER rule applied without reversing the inequality when Delta_E is negative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "ICER rule with the inequality reversed for a health loss" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-NBA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "icer-rule-with-the-inequality-reversed-for-a-health-loss" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(Threshold*IncrementalEffect-IncrementalCost>0)=IF(IncrementalEffect>0,IncrementalCost/IncrementalEffect<Threshold,IncrementalCost/IncrementalEffect>Threshold)" .

hemo:7004020 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Four-quadrant sign check of the ICER identity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For the four worked examples at GBP 20,000 per QALY, the sign of INMB computed directly equals the sign of Delta_E times the sign of lambda minus R. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a slip in one of the quadrant cases, most often a sign error in a negative difference." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Four-quadrant sign check of the ICER identity" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-NBA-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "four-quadrant-sign-check-of-the-icer-identity" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(SIGN(20000*0.14-3000)=SIGN(0.14)*SIGN(20000-3000/0.14),SIGN(20000*(-0.02)-1000)=SIGN(-0.02)*SIGN(20000-1000/(-0.02)),SIGN(20000*0.05-(-500))=SIGN(0.05)*SIGN(20000-(-500)/0.05),SIGN(20000*(-0.1)-(-1000))=SIGN(-0.1)*SIGN(20000-(-1000)/(-0.1)))" .

hemo:7004021 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "North-east quadrant with the ratio just above the threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Option C in draw 3 of the article's illustrative example costs GBP 3,000 more and gains 0.14 QALYs. R is about GBP 21,429 per QALY, above GBP 20,000, and INMB is minus GBP 200, as in the article's table." ;
    hemo:9000009 "North-east quadrant with the ratio just above the threshold" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Delta_C = 3000; Delta_E = 0.14; lambda = 20000; R = 21428.57; INMB = -200.00" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NBA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "north-east-quadrant-with-the-ratio-just-above-the-threshold" .

hemo:7004022 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "North-west quadrant with a negative ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Option B in draw 4 of the article's illustrative example costs GBP 1,000 more and loses 0.02 QALYs. R is minus GBP 50,000 per QALY and INMB is minus GBP 1,400, as in the article, so here the negative ratio marks a worse option." ;
    hemo:9000009 "North-west quadrant with a negative ratio" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Delta_C = 1000; Delta_E = -0.02; lambda = 20000; R = -50000; INMB = -1400" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NBA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "north-west-quadrant-with-a-negative-ratio" .

hemo:7004023 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "South-east quadrant with a negative ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An illustrative option saves GBP 500 and gains 0.05 QALYs. R is minus GBP 10,000 per QALY, the same sign as in the north-west example, yet INMB is GBP 1,500 and the option dominates its comparator." ;
    hemo:9000009 "South-east quadrant with a negative ratio" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Delta_C = -500; Delta_E = 0.05; lambda = 20000; R = -10000; INMB = 1500" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NBA-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "south-east-quadrant-with-a-negative-ratio" .

hemo:7004024 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "South-west quadrant with a positive ratio below the threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An illustrative option saves GBP 1,000 and loses 0.10 QALYs. R is GBP 10,000 per QALY, below the threshold, but each QALY given up saves only GBP 10,000 against the GBP 20,000 it is worth, so INMB is minus GBP 1,000 and the comparator is kept." ;
    hemo:9000009 "South-west quadrant with a positive ratio below the threshold" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Delta_C = -1000; Delta_E = -0.10; lambda = 20000; R = 10000; INMB = -1000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NBA-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "south-west-quadrant-with-a-positive-ratio-below-the-threshold" .

hemo:7004025 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Treatment indicator coded 1 for the new option"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "T_i is 1 for patients given the new option and 0 for the comparator, so beta is new option minus comparator. Reversed coding reverses the sign of beta." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Treatment indicator coded 1 for the new option" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-NBA-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "treatment-indicator-coded-1-for-the-new-option" .

hemo:7004026 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One threshold per net-benefit regression"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The dependent variable changes with lambda, so the regression is re-run at each threshold. Hoch and colleagues use repeated regressions over willingness-to-pay values to show how the finding changes with lambda." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One threshold per net-benefit regression" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-NBA-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-threshold-per-net-benefit-regression" .

hemo:7004027 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Unadjusted coefficient equals the difference in arm means"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The equality of beta with the difference in arm mean net benefit holds for a regression on the treatment indicator alone. With baseline covariates added, beta is an adjusted incremental net benefit, and the arm-mean calculator returns the unadjusted value only." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Unadjusted coefficient equals the difference in arm means" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-NBA-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "unadjusted-coefficient-equals-the-difference-in-arm-means" .

hemo:7004028 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Net-benefit regression with Excel LINEST"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000664 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With patient effects in Effect, patient costs in Cost and the indicator in TreatFlag, one row per patient, the first formula returns beta and the second its standard error from the LINEST statistics table. The array expression Threshold*Effect-Cost forms each patient's net benefit inside the function. LINEST pools the residual variance of the two arms, so its standard error equals lambda times SE_INHB of HE-FM-NBA-001 only when the arms are the same size or share one variance, and otherwise the per-arm form is the one to report." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net-benefit regression with Excel LINEST" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-NBA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-benefit-regression-with-excel-linest" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=INDEX(LINEST(Threshold*Effect-Cost,TreatFlag),1); =INDEX(LINEST(Threshold*Effect-Cost,TreatFlag,TRUE,TRUE),2,1)" .

hemo:7004029 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Net-benefit regression coefficient equals the difference in arm mean net benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The LINEST slope equals lambda times the difference in mean effect minus the difference in mean cost. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the indicator coded 1 for the comparator or patients missing from one of the ranges." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net-benefit regression coefficient equals the difference in arm mean net benefit" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-NBA-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-benefit-regression-coefficient-equals-the-difference-in-arm-mean-net-benefit" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(INDEX(LINEST(Threshold*Effect-Cost,TreatFlag),1)-(Threshold*(AVERAGEIFS(Effect,TreatFlag,1)-AVERAGEIFS(Effect,TreatFlag,0))-(AVERAGEIFS(Cost,TreatFlag,1)-AVERAGEIFS(Cost,TreatFlag,0))))<1E-6" .

hemo:7004030 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Net-benefit regression coefficient is zero at the ICER"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the ICER of the arm means in a cell named ICER, the regression at that threshold returns a coefficient of zero. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows net benefit formed with the cost and effect columns swapped or a different threshold." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net-benefit regression coefficient is zero at the ICER" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-NBA-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-benefit-regression-coefficient-is-zero-at-the-icer" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(INDEX(LINEST(ICER*Effect-Cost,TreatFlag),1))<1E-6" .

hemo:7004031 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Net-benefit regression coefficient at a zero threshold equals minus the cost difference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At a threshold of zero the coefficient equals minus the difference in mean cost. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the effect column still entering the dependent variable." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net-benefit regression coefficient at a zero threshold equals minus the cost difference" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-NBA-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-benefit-regression-coefficient-at-a-zero-threshold-equals-minus-the-cost-difference" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(INDEX(LINEST(-Cost,TreatFlag),1)+AVERAGEIFS(Cost,TreatFlag,1)-AVERAGEIFS(Cost,TreatFlag,0))<1E-6" .

hemo:7004032 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Net-benefit regression for the illustrative trial at GBP 20,000 per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Using the arm means of the illustrative trial in HE-EX-NBA-001, mean net benefit is GBP 10,000 in the new-option arm and GBP 9,500 in the comparator arm, so the intercept is GBP 9,500 and the treatment coefficient is GBP 500." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net-benefit regression for the illustrative trial at GBP 20,000 per QALY" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 20000; E_1 = 0.80; C_1 = 6000; E_0 = 0.70; C_0 = 4500; NB_1 = 10000; NB_0 = 9500; alpha = 9500; beta = 500" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NBA-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-benefit-regression-for-the-illustrative-trial-at-gbp-20-000-per-qaly" .

hemo:7004033 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Net-benefit regression at a threshold equal to the ICER"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At GBP 15,000 per QALY, the ICER of the illustrative trial, both arms have mean net benefit of GBP 6,000 and the treatment coefficient is zero, the check Hoch and colleagues recommend." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net-benefit regression at a threshold equal to the ICER" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 15000; E_1 = 0.80; C_1 = 6000; E_0 = 0.70; C_0 = 4500; NB_1 = 6000; NB_0 = 6000; alpha = 6000; beta = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NBA-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-benefit-regression-at-a-threshold-equal-to-the-icer" .

hemo:7004034 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Net-benefit regression at a zero threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At a threshold of zero each patient's net benefit is minus the cost, so the coefficient is minus GBP 1,500, minus the difference in mean cost, a limiting case Hoch and colleagues also describe." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net-benefit regression at a zero threshold" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 0; E_1 = 0.80; C_1 = 6000; E_0 = 0.70; C_0 = 4500; NB_1 = -6000; NB_0 = -4500; alpha = -4500; beta = -1500" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NBA-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-benefit-regression-at-a-zero-threshold" .

hemo:7004035 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Neighbours taken from the finished cost-effectiveness frontier"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Strategies i and j are the adjacent strategies on the frontier after dominated strategies, ties included (HE-FM-DOM-002), and extendedly dominated strategies have been removed (HE-CF-CEF-001), so Q_i is below Q_k, Q_k is below Q_j and costs rise in the same order. Neighbours taken from a list that still holds a dominated strategy give slopes against the wrong comparator and a wrong range." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Neighbours taken from the finished cost-effectiveness frontier" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CEF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "neighbours-taken-from-the-finished-cost-effectiveness-frontier" .

hemo:7004036 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "End strategies of the cost-effectiveness frontier"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "For the cheapest frontier strategy lambda_in is replaced by zero, since at a value of zero the cheapest strategy has the highest net monetary benefit. For the most effective strategy lambda_out has no upper limit, since as λ grows without limit the strategy with the most QALYs has the highest net monetary benefit (Suen and Goldhaber-Fiebert, section III of the proof)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "End strategies of the cost-effectiveness frontier" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CEF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "end-strategies-of-the-cost-effectiveness-frontier" .

hemo:7004037 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected values for one population on the frontier"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Costs and QALYs are expected totals per person for the same population, perspective, time horizon and price year. The range describes the choice on expected values and does not show the probability that the choice is cost effective, which the acceptability frontier of HE-FM-CEAC-003 shows." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected values for one population on the frontier" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CEF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-values-for-one-population-on-the-frontier" .

hemo:7004038 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Threshold range check and frontier choice from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000665 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The first formula returns TRUE when the value in ValuePerQALY lies within the range of the strategy in CostStrategy and QALYStrategy, with its frontier neighbours in CostLower, QALYLower, CostHigher and QALYHigher. The second returns the chosen strategy from a finished frontier, with names in FrontierNames and ICERs in FrontierICERs sorted by cost and the cheapest strategy's ICER entered as 0. MATCH with match type 1 finds the largest ICER not above the value, which needs the ICERs to rise." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Threshold range check and frontier choice from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CEF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "threshold-range-check-and-frontier-choice-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ValuePerQALY>=(CostStrategy-CostLower)/(QALYStrategy-QALYLower),ValuePerQALY<=(CostHigher-CostStrategy)/(QALYHigher-QALYStrategy)); =INDEX(FrontierNames,MATCH(ValuePerQALY,FrontierICERs,1))" .

hemo:7004039 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Frontier choice from the ICERs agrees with the highest net monetary benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compare the net monetary benefit of the frontier strategy picked by its ICERs with the highest net monetary benefit over all strategies, with all costs in Costs and QALYs in QALYs, and the frontier in FrontierCosts, FrontierQALYs and FrontierICERs, with the cheapest strategy's ICER entered as 0 as in HE-IM-CEF-001. It needs Excel for Microsoft 365, or entry as an array formula in older versions. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows an unfinished frontier, an ICER taken against a strategy that is not adjacent, or a missing strategy." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Frontier choice from the ICERs agrees with the highest net monetary benefit" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CEF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "frontier-choice-from-the-icers-agrees-with-the-highest-net-monetary-benefit" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(MAX(ValuePerQALY*QALYs-Costs)-INDEX(ValuePerQALY*FrontierQALYs-FrontierCosts,MATCH(ValuePerQALY,FrontierICERs,1)))<0.01" .

hemo:7004040 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Slopes rise along the finished cost-effectiveness frontier in one cell"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the cheapest frontier strategy in row 2 and the sequential ICERs of the other frontier strategies in cells F3 to F8, a frontier of seven strategies, the formula counts places where an ICER is lower than the one before it. For a frontier of another length both ranges end at the last ICER, since an empty cell counts as zero and would give FALSE. It checks the whole column at once, where HE-VT-EXD-001 checks one row at a time. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows that an extendedly dominated strategy remains, so some ranges are empty or overlap." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Slopes rise along the finished cost-effectiveness frontier in one cell" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CEF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "slopes-rise-along-the-finished-cost-effectiveness-frontier-in-one-cell" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(--(F4:F8<F3:F7))=0" .

hemo:7004041 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's frontier slopes of GBP 20,000, 30,000 and 60,000 per QALY reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The slopes between R0 and R1, R1 and R4, and R4 and R5 round to GBP 20,000, GBP 30,000 and GBP 60,000 per QALY and rise. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a slope taken against a strategy that is not adjacent on the frontier, such as R4 against R3." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's frontier slopes of GBP 20,000, 30,000 and 60,000 per QALY reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CEF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-frontier-slopes-of-gbp-20-000-30-000-and-60-000-per-qaly-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ROUND((4000-2000)/(5.1-5),0)=20000,ROUND((10000-4000)/(5.3-5.1),0)=30000,ROUND((19000-10000)/(5.45-5.3),0)=60000)" .

hemo:7004042 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Rehabilitation programme R1 chosen at GBP 25,000 per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative rehabilitation example R1 (GBP 4,000, 5.10 QALYs) lies on the frontier between usual care R0 (GBP 2,000, 5.00 QALYs) and R4 (GBP 10,000, 5.30 QALYs). The segment entering R1 has a slope of GBP 20,000 per QALY and the segment leaving it GBP 30,000, so R1 is chosen at GBP 25,000, where its net monetary benefit of GBP 123,500 is the highest of the six options in the article's table." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Rehabilitation programme R1 chosen at GBP 25,000 per QALY" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C_i = 2000; Q_i = 5.00; C_k = 4000; Q_k = 5.10; C_j = 10000; Q_j = 5.30; lambda = 25000; lambda_in = 20000; lambda_out = 30000; best = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CEF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "rehabilitation-programme-r1-chosen-at-gbp-25-000-per-qaly" .

hemo:7004043 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Rehabilitation programme R4 chosen at GBP 35,000 per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "R4 lies on the frontier between R1 and R5 (GBP 19,000, 5.45 QALYs), with slopes of GBP 30,000 and GBP 60,000 per QALY into and out of it, so it is chosen at GBP 35,000, where its net monetary benefit of GBP 175,500 is the highest in the article's table. The figures are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Rehabilitation programme R4 chosen at GBP 35,000 per QALY" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C_i = 4000; Q_i = 5.10; C_k = 10000; Q_k = 5.30; C_j = 19000; Q_j = 5.45; lambda = 35000; lambda_in = 30000; lambda_out = 60000; best = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CEF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "rehabilitation-programme-r4-chosen-at-gbp-35-000-per-qaly" .

hemo:7004044 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Empty threshold range for the extendedly dominated programme R3"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Applied to R3 (GBP 9,000, 5.20 QALYs) with R1 and R4 either side, the slope into R3 is GBP 50,000 per QALY and the slope out of it GBP 10,000, so no value per QALY satisfies both conditions. At GBP 30,000, the switching value between R1 and R4, R3 is not chosen, and the same result holds at every value, as the article's line test shows. R3 is extendedly dominated, since D_M of HE-FM-EXD-001 is GBP 40,000 per QALY." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Empty threshold range for the extendedly dominated programme R3" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C_i = 4000; Q_i = 5.10; C_k = 9000; Q_k = 5.20; C_j = 10000; Q_j = 5.30; lambda = 30000; lambda_in = 50000; lambda_out = 10000; best = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CEF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "empty-threshold-range-for-the-extendedly-dominated-programme-r3" .

hemo:7004045 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Chosen strategy found among the complete set of strategies"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Strategy s has the highest net monetary benefit at λ among every strategy in the analysis. If L_k comes out negative for some k, s was not the highest and the choice has to be corrected first." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Chosen strategy found among the complete set of strategies" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CEF-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "chosen-strategy-found-among-the-complete-set-of-strategies" .

hemo:7004046 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Stated value per QALY for the net monetary benefit given up"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "L_k depends on λ and is reported with it. It is a per-person amount on expected values, and the same comparison made in QALYs divides it by λ, which gives the net health benefit form that PMG36 asks for." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Stated value per QALY for the net monetary benefit given up" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CEF-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "stated-value-per-qaly-for-the-net-monetary-benefit-given-up" .

hemo:7004047 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Net monetary benefit given up against the best of all strategies"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000666 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the costs of all strategies in Costs, their QALYs in QALYs, the value in ValuePerQALY and the strategy assessed in CostStrategy and QALYStrategy, the formula subtracts the strategy's net monetary benefit from the highest one. SUMPRODUCT makes older versions of Excel evaluate the array inside MAX without array entry." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net monetary benefit given up against the best of all strategies" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CEF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-monetary-benefit-given-up-against-the-best-of-all-strategies" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(MAX(ValuePerQALY*QALYs-Costs))-(ValuePerQALY*QALYStrategy-CostStrategy)" .

hemo:7004048 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Net monetary benefit given up equals the incremental net monetary benefit of the frontier choice"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The value in NMBGivenUp equals λ times the QALY gain of the chosen strategy over strategy k, less its extra cost, as in HE-FM-NMB-002. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the subtraction made the other way round, which gives a negative amount." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net monetary benefit given up equals the incremental net monetary benefit of the frontier choice" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CEF-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-monetary-benefit-given-up-equals-the-incremental-net-monetary-benefit-of-the-frontier-choice" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(NMBGivenUp-(ValuePerQALY*(QALYChosen-QALYStrategy)-(CostChosen-CostStrategy)))<0.01" .

hemo:7004049 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Net monetary benefit given up at the switching value equals the blend saving"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For the article's R3, the amount given up at GBP 30,000 per QALY against R1 equals R3's cost less the cost of the half-and-half blend of R1 and R4. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows that the value used is not the slope of the segment beneath R3." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net monetary benefit given up at the switching value equals the blend saving" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CEF-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-monetary-benefit-given-up-at-the-switching-value-equals-the-blend-saving" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND((30000*5.1-4000)-(30000*5.2-9000),0)=ROUND(9000-(0.5*4000+0.5*10000),0)" .

hemo:7004050 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Extendedly dominated programme R3 gives up net monetary benefit at both NICE values"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Across the six strategies of the article, R3's net monetary benefit is below the highest at GBP 25,000 and at GBP 35,000 per QALY. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a strategy list or a cost entered wrongly." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Extendedly dominated programme R3 gives up net monetary benefit at both NICE values" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CEF-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "extendedly-dominated-programme-r3-gives-up-net-monetary-benefit-at-both-nice-values" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(MAX(25000*{5;5.1;5.08;5.2;5.3;5.45}-{2000;4000;5500;9000;10000;19000})-(25000*5.2-9000)>0,MAX(35000*{5;5.1;5.08;5.2;5.3;5.45}-{2000;4000;5500;9000;10000;19000})-(35000*5.2-9000)>0)" .

hemo:7004051 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Extendedly dominated programme R3 against R1 at GBP 25,000 per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At GBP 25,000 per QALY the frontier choice in the article's illustrative example is R1, with a net monetary benefit of GBP 123,500 per person against GBP 121,000 for R3, so R3 gives up GBP 2,500 per person." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Extendedly dominated programme R3 against R1 at GBP 25,000 per QALY" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 25000; Q_s = 5.10; C_s = 4000; Q_k = 5.20; C_k = 9000; NMB_s = 123500; NMB_k = 121000; L_k = 2500" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CEF-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "extendedly-dominated-programme-r3-against-r1-at-gbp-25-000-per-qaly" .

hemo:7004052 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Programme R3 at the GBP 30,000 switching value of the frontier"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At GBP 30,000 per QALY, the slope of the segment from R1 to R4 beneath R3, R1 and R4 tie at GBP 149,000 and R3 reaches GBP 147,000, as in the article. The gap of GBP 2,000 is the smallest R3 ever gives up and equals the saving of the half-and-half blend of R1 and R4 in the article's line test, GBP 9,000 against GBP 7,000." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Programme R3 at the GBP 30,000 switching value of the frontier" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 30000; Q_s = 5.10; C_s = 4000; Q_k = 5.20; C_k = 9000; NMB_s = 149000; NMB_k = 147000; L_k = 2000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CEF-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "programme-r3-at-the-gbp-30-000-switching-value-of-the-frontier" .

hemo:7004053 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Frontier programme R5 outside its threshold range at GBP 35,000 per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "R5 is on the frontier but is chosen only above GBP 60,000 per QALY. At GBP 35,000 the choice is R4, with GBP 175,500 per person against GBP 171,750 for R5, so R5 gives up GBP 3,750. A place on the frontier does not by itself make a strategy the choice." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Frontier programme R5 outside its threshold range at GBP 35,000 per QALY" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 35000; Q_s = 5.30; C_s = 10000; Q_k = 5.45; C_k = 19000; NMB_s = 175500; NMB_k = 171750; L_k = 3750" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CEF-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "frontier-programme-r5-outside-its-threshold-range-at-gbp-35-000-per-qaly" .

hemo:7004054 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Chosen programme R4 measured against itself at GBP 35,000 per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With k equal to the chosen strategy the amount given up is zero, a limiting case that checks the implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Chosen programme R4 measured against itself at GBP 35,000 per QALY" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 35000; Q_s = 5.30; C_s = 10000; Q_k = 5.30; C_k = 10000; NMB_s = 175500; NMB_k = 175500; L_k = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CEF-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "chosen-programme-r4-measured-against-itself-at-gbp-35-000-per-qaly" .

hemo:7004055 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Benefit on an approximately cardinal scale for the efficiency frontier"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "IQWiG 6.1 (section 4.6.1) transfers the benefit, if required, into an approximately cardinally scaled measure and plots it in a positive value range (section 4.6.3), so that differences in benefit can be divided by differences in cost." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Benefit on an approximately cardinal scale for the efficiency frontier" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CEF-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "benefit-on-an-approximately-cardinal-scale-for-the-efficiency-frontier" .

hemo:7004056 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Last segment drawn with absolute and extended dominance applied"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Intervention i and intervention j are the last two interventions on a frontier built with absolute and extended dominance. IQWiG 6.1 (section 4.6.2) notes that without extended dominance the stepped absolute frontier no longer gives a gradient that reads as the reciprocal of a willingness to pay." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Last segment drawn with absolute and extended dominance applied" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CEF-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "last-segment-drawn-with-absolute-and-extended-dominance-applied" .

hemo:7004057 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Price judged by the version 6.1 extension of the last segment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The extension assumes that the gradient of the last segment represents the reciprocal of the current willingness to pay and that efficiency in the therapeutic area should not deteriorate (IQWiG 6.1, section 4.1.9). Version 8.0 keeps the frontier for evaluations outside §35b SGB V but no longer extends the last segment." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Price judged by the version 6.1 extension of the last segment" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CEF-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "price-judged-by-the-version-6-1-extension-of-the-last-segment" .

hemo:7004058 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Net cost on the extended last segment of an efficiency frontier from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000667 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the last two frontier interventions in CostPrev, BenefitPrev, CostLast and BenefitLast and the new intervention's benefit in BenefitNew, the first formula returns the gradient and the second the net cost per patient on the extension." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net cost on the extended last segment of an efficiency frontier from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CEF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-cost-on-the-extended-last-segment-of-an-efficiency-frontier-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(BenefitLast-BenefitPrev)/(CostLast-CostPrev); =CostLast+(BenefitNew-BenefitLast)/((BenefitLast-BenefitPrev)/(CostLast-CostPrev))" .

hemo:7004059 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Efficiency frontier gradient is the reciprocal of the segment ICER"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The gradient in a cell named Gradient multiplied by the incremental cost per unit of benefit of the last segment gives 1. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the axes swapped, so that cost per benefit was entered as the gradient." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Efficiency frontier gradient is the reciprocal of the segment ICER" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CEF-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "efficiency-frontier-gradient-is-the-reciprocal-of-the-segment-icer" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(Gradient*(CostLast-CostPrev)/(BenefitLast-BenefitPrev)-1)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004060 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "New intervention at the extended net cost keeps the last segment's ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At the net cost in MaxNetCost the new intervention has the same incremental cost per unit of benefit against the last frontier intervention as the last segment itself. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the extension drawn from the wrong segment." ;
    hemo:9000009 "New intervention at the extended net cost keeps the last segment's ratio" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CEF-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "new-intervention-at-the-extended-net-cost-keeps-the-last-segment-s-ratio" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((MaxNetCost-CostLast)/(BenefitNew-BenefitLast)-(CostLast-CostPrev)/(BenefitLast-BenefitPrev))<0.01" .

hemo:7004061 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "New intervention judged on an illustrative efficiency frontier in euros"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "On an illustrative frontier the last segment joins an intervention with net costs of EUR 8,000 and 40 benefit points to one with EUR 14,000 and 55 points, a gradient of 0.0025 points per euro, or EUR 400 per point. A new intervention with 61 points lies on the extended segment at a net cost of EUR 16,400 per patient." ;
    hemo:9000009 "New intervention judged on an illustrative efficiency frontier in euros" ;
    hemo:9000014 "B_i = 40; K_i = 8000; B_j = 55; K_j = 14000; B_new = 61; g = 0.0025; lambda_g = 400; K_max = 16400" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CEF-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "new-intervention-judged-on-an-illustrative-efficiency-frontier-in-euros" .

hemo:7004062 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "New intervention with no added benefit over the last frontier intervention"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With 55 points, the same benefit as the last intervention on the frontier, the net cost on the extension equals that intervention's EUR 14,000, a limiting case that checks the implementation. The figures are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "New intervention with no added benefit over the last frontier intervention" ;
    hemo:9000014 "B_i = 40; K_i = 8000; B_j = 55; K_j = 14000; B_new = 55; g = 0.0025; lambda_g = 400; K_max = 14000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CEF-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "new-intervention-with-no-added-benefit-over-the-last-frontier-intervention" .

hemo:7004063 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Net gains after the displaced health in each group"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each g value is the gross gain less the health displaced elsewhere in the system in that group. The article's example spreads 0.10 years per person evenly across both groups, the base case that the NICE support document on health inequalities sets for the distribution of health opportunity costs, with gradients towards more deprived groups explored as scenarios." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net gains after the displaced health in each group" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EET-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-gains-after-the-displaced-health-in-each-group" .

hemo:7004064 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Two options of equal cost from one budget in the health forgone for equity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The two options draw on the same budget, so the comparison is between health outcomes alone. Where costs differ, the displaced health of each option is computed at its own cost before the g values are formed, and the difference then includes the extra opportunity cost." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two options of equal cost from one budget in the health forgone for equity" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EET-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-options-of-equal-cost-from-one-budget-in-the-health-forgone-for-equity" .

hemo:7004065 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Population shares sum to one and gains are per person"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The shares s_worse and s_better add to 1 and each g is a gain per person within its group, so H_max and H_fair are per person of the whole population. Multiplying by the population size gives total health forgone." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Population shares sum to one and gains are per person" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EET-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "population-shares-sum-to-one-and-gains-are-per-person" .

hemo:7004066 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Health forgone for equity from named share and gain cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000668 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the shares in ShareWorse and ShareBetter and the net gains in GainWorseMax, GainBetterMax, GainWorseFair and GainBetterFair, the first formula returns the health forgone, held in a cell named HealthForgone, and the second its share of the health-maximising gain." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health forgone for equity from named share and gain cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-EET-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-forgone-for-equity-from-named-share-and-gain-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(ShareWorse*GainWorseMax+ShareBetter*GainBetterMax)-(ShareWorse*GainWorseFair+ShareBetter*GainBetterFair); =HealthForgone/(ShareWorse*GainWorseMax+ShareBetter*GainBetterMax)" .

hemo:7004067 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Health forgone equals the share-weighted sum of group differences"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The result cell HealthForgone equals the sum over the two groups of population share times the group's gain under the health-maximising option less its gain under the fairer option, 0.4 times minus 0.40 plus 0.6 times 0.35, or 0.05 in the article's example. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a result cell built from unweighted group averages, which gives minus 0.025 and reads as if F gained more health." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health forgone equals the share-weighted sum of group differences" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EET-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-forgone-equals-the-share-weighted-sum-of-group-differences" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(HealthForgone-(ShareWorse*(GainWorseMax-GainWorseFair)+ShareBetter*(GainBetterMax-GainBetterFair)))<0.000001" .

hemo:7004068 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Population shares in the health forgone for equity add to one"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The two shares add to 1 within rounding, so H_max and H_fair are means per person. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows group counts or percentages entered in place of shares, for example 40 and 60, which multiply the health forgone a hundredfold." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Population shares in the health forgone for equity add to one" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EET-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "population-shares-in-the-health-forgone-for-equity-add-to-one" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ShareWorse+ShareBetter-1)<0.000001" .

hemo:7004069 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Health forgone equals the excess switching weight times the extra gain to the worse-off group"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the switching weight of HE-FM-WE-004 for the two options, computed from the share-weighted group gains, held in SwitchWeight, the result cell HealthForgone equals SwitchWeight less 1 times ShareWorse times the worse-off group's extra gain under the fairer option, 0.3125 times 0.16, or 0.05 years, in the article's example. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a result cell built from unweighted group averages, minus 0.025, or a switching weight taken from per-person gains without the shares, 0.875, which gives minus 0.02." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health forgone equals the excess switching weight times the extra gain to the worse-off group" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EET-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-forgone-equals-the-excess-switching-weight-times-the-extra-gain-to-the-worse-off-group" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(HealthForgone-(SwitchWeight-1)*ShareWorse*(GainWorseFair-GainWorseMax))<0.000001" .

hemo:7004070 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Health forgone choosing programme F over programme E"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative example the more deprived group is 40% of the population. Programme E gives net gains of 0.10 and 0.60 years and programme F 0.50 and 0.25 years. The mean net gains are 0.40 years under E and 0.35 under F, so choosing F forgoes 0.05 years per person, one-eighth of E's net gain." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health forgone choosing programme F over programme E" ;
    hemo:9000014 "s_worse = 0.4; s_better = 0.6; g_worse_max = 0.10; g_better_max = 0.60; g_worse_fair = 0.50; g_better_fair = 0.25; H_max = 0.40; H_fair = 0.35; H_forgone = 0.05; f_forgone = 0.125" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EET-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-forgone-choosing-programme-f-over-programme-e" .

hemo:7004071 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No health forgone when the fairer option matches total health"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An illustrative fairer option that gives 0.40 years per person to both groups has the same mean gain as programme E, so no health is forgone and choosing it involves no equity-efficiency trade-off. This is the limiting case of the win-win cell of the health equity impact plane." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No health forgone when the fairer option matches total health" ;
    hemo:9000014 "s_worse = 0.4; s_better = 0.6; g_worse_max = 0.10; g_better_max = 0.60; g_worse_fair = 0.40; g_better_fair = 0.40; H_max = 0.4; H_fair = 0.4; H_forgone = 0; f_forgone = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EET-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-health-forgone-when-the-fairer-option-matches-total-health" .

hemo:7004072 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Weights taken at baseline health for the implied aversion"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The switching weight of HE-FM-WE-004 is one linear weight on every QALY to the worse-off group (HE-AS-WE-008), while the Atkinson weight applies to small gains at the stated health levels (HE-AS-ATK-009). Using baseline health gives the approximation, while solving the equally distributed equivalent health of the two options for the tie gives the exact value, about 2.35 in the article's example against 2.38 from this formula. Asaria and colleagues locate such a switch by comparing EDE health across levels of inequality aversion." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Weights taken at baseline health for the implied aversion" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EET-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "weights-taken-at-baseline-health-for-the-implied-aversion" .

hemo:7004073 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Ordered positive health levels and a weight of at least 1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "h_better is above h_worse and both are above zero, so the logarithm of the ratio is positive. The switching weight exceeds 1 when the fairer option gives less total health and more to the worse-off group, and a weight of at least 1 then gives an aversion of zero or above, the range of the Atkinson parameter (HE-VR-ATK-014)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ordered positive health levels and a weight of at least 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EET-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ordered-positive-health-levels-and-a-weight-of-at-least-1" .

hemo:7004074 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Relative inequality concept of the implied aversion"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The Atkinson parameter measures aversion to relative inequality (HE-AS-ATK-003). An absolute index such as the Kolm index, which Asaria and colleagues report beside it, gives a different parameter for the same break-even weight, and this formula does not convert between them." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Relative inequality concept of the implied aversion" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EET-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "relative-inequality-concept-of-the-implied-aversion" .

hemo:7004075 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Implied inequality aversion from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000669 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the switching weight in SwitchWeight and baseline health in HealthBetter and HealthWorse, the formula returns the implied aversion, held in a cell named ImpliedEpsilon. Excel LN is the natural logarithm, although any base gives the same ratio." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Implied inequality aversion from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-EET-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "implied-inequality-aversion-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LN(SwitchWeight)/LN(HealthBetter/HealthWorse)" .

hemo:7004076 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Atkinson weight at the implied aversion returns the switching weight"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Raising the health ratio to the power ImpliedEpsilon, as in HE-FM-ATK-004, returns SwitchWeight. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows an inverted health ratio, which gives an aversion of about minus 2.38 and a weight of about 0.76 in the article's example." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Atkinson weight at the implied aversion returns the switching weight" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EET-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "atkinson-weight-at-the-implied-aversion-returns-the-switching-weight" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((HealthBetter/HealthWorse)^ImpliedEpsilon-SwitchWeight)<0.000001" .

hemo:7004077 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Implied aversion recovers the English median from the published weight"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With SwitchWeight set to 6.95, the implied weight in Table 1 of Robson and colleagues, and HealthBetter and HealthWorse set to their initial 74 and 62 years, ImpliedEpsilon is within 0.01 of their median of 10.95. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the wrong health levels, for example 66 in place of 62 gives about 16.95." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Implied aversion recovers the English median from the published weight" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EET-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "implied-aversion-recovers-the-english-median-from-the-published-weight" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ImpliedEpsilon-10.95)<0.01" .

hemo:7004078 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Implied inequality aversion for programmes E and F"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative example the switching weight of HE-FM-WE-004, with the share-weighted net gains, is 0.21 divided by 0.16, or 1.3125. With baseline quality-adjusted life expectancy of 74 and 66 years it gives 0.2719 divided by 0.1144, or about 2.38. The weight of an Atkinson function with aversion above this value favours F at baseline health." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Implied inequality aversion for programmes E and F" ;
    hemo:9000014 "w_star = 1.3125; h_better = 74; h_worse = 66; epsilon_star = 2.3768" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EET-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "implied-inequality-aversion-for-programmes-e-and-f" .

hemo:7004079 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Inverting the implied weight of the English elicitation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Robson and colleagues report an implied weight of 6.95 at initial quality-adjusted life expectancy of 62 years for the poorest fifth and 74 for the richest fifth. Inverting it returns about 10.96, against their median Atkinson parameter of 10.95, with the gap due to the weight being rounded to two decimals." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Inverting the implied weight of the English elicitation" ;
    hemo:9000014 "w_star = 6.95; h_better = 74; h_worse = 62; epsilon_star = 10.96" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EET-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "inverting-the-implied-weight-of-the-english-elicitation" .

hemo:7004080 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Zero implied aversion when the switching weight is 1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When the two options tie under equal weights, so that no health is forgone and the switching weight is 1, the implied aversion is zero, the health-maximising rule." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Zero implied aversion when the switching weight is 1" ;
    hemo:9000014 "w_star = 1; h_better = 74; h_worse = 66; epsilon_star = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EET-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "zero-implied-aversion-when-the-switching-weight-is-1" .

hemo:7004081 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Stay probability unchanged across the cycles of one Markov state visit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The probability p_ii is the same in every cycle that the person spends in the state, as in a time-homogeneous model, which Sonnenberg and Beck call a Markov chain. In a time-inhomogeneous model the stay probability can change with model time, and the probability of staying at least d cycles becomes the product of the stay probabilities of the cycles concerned, so the geometric form holds only while they are equal." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Stay probability unchanged across the cycles of one Markov state visit" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MKA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "stay-probability-unchanged-across-the-cycles-of-one-markov-state-visit" .

hemo:7004082 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Cycle of entry counted in the length of a Markov state visit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The count d includes the cycle of entry, so the shortest stay is one cycle, as in the article. A trace counted at the end of each cycle from model start credits a person who starts in the state with p_ii/(1-p_ii) cycles, one fewer, which gives the article's 0.88/0.12 = 7.333 event-free years; the counting weights of a trace are HE-FM-CSIM-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cycle of entry counted in the length of a Markov state visit" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MKA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cycle-of-entry-counted-in-the-length-of-a-markov-state-visit" .

hemo:7004083 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One uninterrupted visit described by the geometric Markov sojourn"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The distribution describes a single visit. A state that can be left and entered again has several episodes, each with this distribution, and HE-FM-MKCH-002 gives the expected number of episodes and the total time across them." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One uninterrupted visit described by the geometric Markov sojourn" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MKA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-uninterrupted-visit-described-by-the-geometric-markov-sojourn" .

hemo:7004084 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Geometric Markov sojourn probabilities and median stay from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000670 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the stay probability in StayProb and the stay length in StayCycles, the four formulas return f_d, G_d, F_d and the median stay as a whole number of cycles, held in a cell named MedianStay. ROUNDUP to no decimals gives the smallest whole number at or above d_star, and subtracting 1E-9 first stops rounding noise in LN from pushing an exact whole number up by one." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Geometric Markov sojourn probabilities and median stay from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-MKA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "geometric-markov-sojourn-probabilities-and-median-stay-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=StayProb^(StayCycles-1)*(1-StayProb); =StayProb^(StayCycles-1); =1-StayProb^StayCycles; =ROUNDUP(LN(0.5)/LN(StayProb)-1E-9,0)" .

hemo:7004085 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Fitted memoryless post-event state reproduces the tunnel model's five-year survival"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The probability of a stay of at least six cycles in the memoryless state, 0.90613 to the power 5, equals the tunnel model's five-year post-event survival, 0.75 times 0.95 to the power 4, to four decimal places, as the article's calibration requires. Expected result TRUE. Raising 0.90613 to the power 6, which counts the entry cycle as a survived year, gives about 0.5535 and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fitted memoryless post-event state reproduces the tunnel model's five-year survival" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MKA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fitted-memoryless-post-event-state-reproduces-the-tunnel-model-s-five-year-survival" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(0.90613^5-0.75*0.95^4)<0.0001" .

hemo:7004086 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Median stay cell satisfies the first-crossing rule for a geometric Markov sojourn"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The value in MedianStay is the median when the probability of having left reaches one half by that cycle but not by the cycle before. Expected result TRUE. For StayProb = 0.90613 a cell that rounds d_star down to 7 gives FALSE, since only 49.8% have left by cycle 7, and so does a mean of 10.65 rounded to 11, since 62.7% have left by cycle 10." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Median stay cell satisfies the first-crossing rule for a geometric Markov sojourn" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MKA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "median-stay-cell-satisfies-the-first-crossing-rule-for-a-geometric-markov-sojourn" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(1-StayProb^MedianStay>=0.5,1-StayProb^(MedianStay-1)<0.5)" .

hemo:7004087 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Six-cycle stay in the article's memoryless post-event state"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's memoryless model the post-event state keeps 0.90613 of its members each year, a death probability of 0.09387. A stay of at least six cycles, the entry year and five survived years, has probability 0.90613 to the power 5, about 0.6109, the five-year post-event survival of 0.61088 to which the state was fitted. A stay of exactly six cycles has probability about 0.0573 and about 0.4465 of stays have ended within six cycles, while d_star is about 7.03, so the median stay is 8 cycles (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Six-cycle stay in the article's memoryless post-event state" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_ii = 0.90613; d = 6; f_d = 0.0573; G_d = 0.6109; F_d = 0.4465; d_star = 7.0319" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MKA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "six-cycle-stay-in-the-article-s-memoryless-post-event-state" .

hemo:7004088 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "One-cycle stay in the article's memoryless post-event state"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A stay of exactly one cycle, the cycle of entry only, has probability 1 minus p_ii, 0.09387. Applied to the 10% of the cohort with an event in year one it gives the article's 0.0094 of the cohort dying in year two, against 0.025 with the tunnel state, whose first post-event death probability is 0.25. At d = 1 the limiting values are G_d = 1 and F_d equal to f_d." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One-cycle stay in the article's memoryless post-event state" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_ii = 0.90613; d = 1; f_d = 0.0939; G_d = 1; F_d = 0.0939; d_star = 7.0319" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MKA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-cycle-stay-in-the-article-s-memoryless-post-event-state" .

hemo:7004089 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Median and six-cycle stay in the article's event-free state under standard care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The event-free state under standard care keeps 0.88 a year. Its d_star is about 5.42, so the median stay is 6 cycles, against a mean of 1 divided by 0.12, about 8.333 cycles, from HE-FM-MKCH-002. About 53.6% of stays have ended within six cycles (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Median and six-cycle stay in the article's event-free state under standard care" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_ii = 0.88; d = 6; f_d = 0.0633; G_d = 0.5277; F_d = 0.5356; d_star = 5.4223" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MKA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "median-and-six-cycle-stay-in-the-article-s-event-free-state-under-standard-care" .

hemo:7004090 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Residual stay measured on the clock that starts at entry to the Markov state"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The counts a, b and d run from entry to the state, not from model start. The Markov assumption allows the probabilities to follow model time, as p_ij(t) in the article, but not time since entry; Alarid-Escudero and colleagues call the second state-residence time dependence, which a cycle-specific matrix cannot carry when people enter the state at different times." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Residual stay measured on the clock that starts at entry to the Markov state" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MKA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "residual-stay-measured-on-the-clock-that-starts-at-entry-to-the-markov-state" .

hemo:7004091 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One homogeneous group followed from entry for a Markov memorylessness check"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Comparing exit probabilities across durations as a test needs survival measured from entry in a group that the state is meant to represent. The ISPOR-SMDM report requires states to be homogeneous with respect to observed and unobserved characteristics, so a pattern by duration in pooled data can also come from differences between people within the state, which the state definitions then need to carry." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One homogeneous group followed from entry for a Markov memorylessness check" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MKA-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-homogeneous-group-followed-from-entry-for-a-markov-memorylessness-check" .

hemo:7004092 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Residual stay and per-cycle exit probability of a geometric Markov sojourn in Excel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000671 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the stay probability in StayProb, the cycles already spent in ElapsedCycles, the further cycles in ExtraCycles and the cycle of the stay in StayCycles, the formulas return R_ab and h_d, the second held in a cell named ExitAtDuration. Filling the second down a column of durations returns the same value in every row." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Residual stay and per-cycle exit probability of a geometric Markov sojourn in Excel" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-MKA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "residual-stay-and-per-cycle-exit-probability-of-a-geometric-markov-sojourn-in-excel" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=StayProb^(ElapsedCycles+ExtraCycles)/StayProb^ElapsedCycles; =StayProb^(StayCycles-1)*(1-StayProb)/StayProb^(StayCycles-1)" .

hemo:7004093 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-cycle survival of the article's memoryless state passes the residual-stay check"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A survival curve for time since entry is memoryless over its first two cycles when survival at two cycles divided by survival at one equals survival at one. The memoryless model's values, 0.90613 and 0.821072 from its cohort trace, pass to four decimal places. Expected result TRUE. The tunnel model's 0.75 and 0.7125 give a ratio of 0.95 against 0.75 and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-cycle survival of the article's memoryless state passes the residual-stay check" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MKA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-cycle-survival-of-the-article-s-memoryless-state-passes-the-residual-stay-check" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(0.821072/0.90613-0.90613)<0.0001" .

hemo:7004094 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Per-cycle exit probability cell equals one minus the stay probability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The value in ExitAtDuration, computed by dividing the probability of a stay of exactly StayCycles cycles by the probability of reaching that cycle, equals 1 minus StayProb at any duration. Expected result TRUE. A cell that omits the division returns f_d instead, about 0.0771 at the third cycle for StayProb = 0.90613, and gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Per-cycle exit probability cell equals one minus the stay probability" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MKA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "per-cycle-exit-probability-cell-equals-one-minus-the-stay-probability" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ExitAtDuration-(1-StayProb))<1E-12" .

hemo:7004095 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Five further cycles after one cycle in the article's memoryless post-event state"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A person who has spent one cycle in the memoryless post-event state stays at least five more with probability about 0.6109, and anyone still in the state in its third cycle leaves in that cycle with probability 0.0939, the same as in the first." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Five further cycles after one cycle in the article's memoryless post-event state" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_ii = 0.90613; a = 1; b = 5; d = 3; R_ab = 0.6109; h_d = 0.0939" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MKA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "five-further-cycles-after-one-cycle-in-the-article-s-memoryless-post-event-state" .

hemo:7004096 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Five further cycles after ten cycles in the article's memoryless post-event state"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Someone who entered ten cycles earlier has the same 0.6109 chance of five more cycles and the same exit probability of 0.0939 in the twelfth cycle, so two people in the state face one future whether they arrived one cycle ago or ten, as the article states." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Five further cycles after ten cycles in the article's memoryless post-event state" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_ii = 0.90613; a = 10; b = 5; d = 12; R_ab = 0.6109; h_d = 0.0939" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MKA-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "five-further-cycles-after-ten-cycles-in-the-article-s-memoryless-post-event-state" .

hemo:7004097 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Total exit rate constant over time since entry to the Markov state"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Lambda is the sum of all cause-specific exit rates, as in the competing-hazards split of HE-FM-TP-004, and stays the same however long the person has been in the state. An exit hazard that rises or falls with time since entry, such as a Weibull hazard with a shape parameter other than 1, is not memoryless and needs tunnel states or a semi-Markov model." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Total exit rate constant over time since entry to the Markov state" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MKA-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "total-exit-rate-constant-over-time-since-entry-to-the-markov-state" .

hemo:7004098 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Exit rate and times in one unit for the exponential Markov sojourn"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Lambda per year is used with tau, s and t_med in years. Moving to months divides lambda by 12 and multiplies the times by 12, which leaves every probability unchanged." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Exit rate and times in one unit for the exponential Markov sojourn" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MKA-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "exit-rate-and-times-in-one-unit-for-the-exponential-markov-sojourn" .

hemo:7004099 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Exponential residual stay and median sojourn from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000672 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the total exit rate in ExitRate, the time already spent in TimeInState and the further time in ExtraTime, the formulas return S_res and t_med, the second held in a cell named MedianTime." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Exponential residual stay and median sojourn from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-MKA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "exponential-residual-stay-and-median-sojourn-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=EXP(-ExitRate*(TimeInState+ExtraTime))/EXP(-ExitRate*TimeInState); =LN(2)/ExitRate" .

hemo:7004100 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Median sojourn cell returns exponential survival of one half"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Survival at the value in MedianTime, exp(minus ExitRate times MedianTime), equals one half. Expected result TRUE. A cell holding half the mean, 0.5 divided by ExitRate, gives survival of about 0.607 and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Median sojourn cell returns exponential survival of one half" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MKA-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "median-sojourn-cell-returns-exponential-survival-of-one-half" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(EXP(-ExitRate*MedianTime)-0.5)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004101 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Exponential survival at 0.05 a year passes the residual-stay check"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Exponential survival at 0.05 a year gives the same chance of 10 further years after 10 years as from entry, about 0.6065. Expected result TRUE. Replacing it with Weibull survival exp(minus (0.05 t) to the power 1.5), whose hazard rises with time in the state, gives about 0.5239 against 0.7022 and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Exponential survival at 0.05 a year passes the residual-stay check" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MKA-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "exponential-survival-at-0-05-a-year-passes-the-residual-stay-check" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(EXP(-0.05*20)/EXP(-0.05*10)-EXP(-0.05*10))<1E-12" .

hemo:7004102 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's memoryless post-event state as an exponential sojourn"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The stay probability 0.90613 corresponds to a constant death rate of about 0.098572 a year, minus log 0.90613 by HE-FM-TP-002. After one year in the state the chance of five more is about 0.6109, as from entry, and half of all stays have ended by about 7.03 years, against a mean of 1 divided by 0.098572, about 10.14 years (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's memoryless post-event state as an exponential sojourn" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 0.098572; tau = 1; s = 5; S_res = 0.6109; t_med = 7.0319" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MKA-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-memoryless-post-event-state-as-an-exponential-sojourn" .

hemo:7004103 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Sonnenberg and Beck's constant haemorrhage rate of 0.05 a year as an exponential sojourn"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Sonnenberg and Beck note that a constant haemorrhage rate of 0.05 a year gives an average time to haemorrhage of 1 divided by 0.05, 20 years. The median is about 13.86 years, and after 10 event-free years the chance of 10 more is about 0.6065, the same as for the first 10 years (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Sonnenberg and Beck's constant haemorrhage rate of 0.05 a year as an exponential sojourn" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 0.05; tau = 10; s = 10; S_res = 0.6065; t_med = 13.8629" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MKA-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "sonnenberg-and-beck-s-constant-haemorrhage-rate-of-0-05-a-year-as-an-exponential-sojourn" .

hemo:7004104 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One constant exit rate behind both Markov mean stays"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The cycle model and the continuous process share one constant total exit rate, so the stay probability of every cycle is exp(minus lambda Delta) and the comparison isolates the effect of counting whole cycles." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One constant exit rate behind both Markov mean stays" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MKA-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-constant-exit-rate-behind-both-markov-mean-stays" .

hemo:7004105 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Discrete Markov mean stay counted from the cycle of entry"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "D_mean counts the cycle of entry, as HE-FM-MKA-001 does. A trace counted at the end of each cycle from model start credits one cycle fewer, Delta times p_ii/(1-p_ii), and gives a gap close to minus one half instead." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Discrete Markov mean stay counted from the cycle of entry" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MKA-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "discrete-markov-mean-stay-counted-from-the-cycle-of-entry" .

hemo:7004106 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Discrete and continuous Markov mean stays and their counting gap from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000673 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the total exit rate in ExitRate and the cycle length in years in CycleYears, the four formulas return p_ii, D_mean, T_mean and gap_c, the last held in a cell named GapCycles." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Discrete and continuous Markov mean stays and their counting gap from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-MKA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "discrete-and-continuous-markov-mean-stays-and-their-counting-gap-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=EXP(-ExitRate*CycleYears); =CycleYears/(1-EXP(-ExitRate*CycleYears)); =1/ExitRate; =(CycleYears/(1-EXP(-ExitRate*CycleYears))-1/ExitRate)/CycleYears" .

hemo:7004107 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Counting gap cell lies above one half and below one cycle"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For any positive rate and cycle length the excess of the discrete mean over the continuous mean is more than half a cycle and less than one. Expected result TRUE. A cell that counts the stay at the end of each cycle, CycleYears times p_ii/(1-p_ii), gives about minus 0.49 for the article's rate in annual cycles and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Counting gap cell lies above one half and below one cycle" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MKA-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "counting-gap-cell-lies-above-one-half-and-below-one-cycle" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(GapCycles>0.5,GapCycles<1)" .

hemo:7004108 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Counting gap approaches half a cycle at a small exit rate per cycle"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With lambda times Delta equal to 0.001 the gap is within 0.001 of one half, about 0.50008. Expected result TRUE. Counting at the end of each cycle, which removes the cycle of entry, gives about minus 0.49992 and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Counting gap approaches half a cycle at a small exit rate per cycle" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MKA-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "counting-gap-approaches-half-a-cycle-at-a-small-exit-rate-per-cycle" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(1/(1-EXP(-0.001))-1/0.001-0.5)<0.001" .

hemo:7004109 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's memoryless post-event state in annual cycles against continuous time"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At a constant death rate of 0.098572 a year in annual cycles the stay probability is 0.90613 and the mean post-event stay is about 10.653 years, the article's 1 divided by q. The continuous mean is about 10.145 years, so the counting gap is about 0.508 of a cycle (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's memoryless post-event state in annual cycles against continuous time" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 0.098572; Delta = 1; p_ii = 0.90613; D_mean = 10.6531; T_mean = 10.1449; gap_c = 0.5082" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MKA-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-memoryless-post-event-state-in-annual-cycles-against-continuous-time" .

hemo:7004110 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's post-event death rate in monthly cycles against continuous time"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the same rate in monthly cycles, Delta of one twelfth of a year, the discrete mean falls to about 10.187 years and the gap to about 0.501 of a cycle, about 0.04 of a year, as the cycle shortens (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's post-event death rate in monthly cycles against continuous time" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 0.098572; Delta = 0.0833333; p_ii = 0.991819; D_mean = 10.1866; T_mean = 10.1449; gap_c = 0.5007" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MKA-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-post-event-death-rate-in-monthly-cycles-against-continuous-time" .

hemo:7004111 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "High exit rate of 1 a year in annual cycles against continuous time"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At an illustrative exit rate of 1 a year in annual cycles the stay probability is about 0.368, the discrete mean is about 1.582 years against 1 year in continuous time, and the gap rises to about 0.582 of a cycle, which shows that long cycles relative to the stay widen it (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "High exit rate of 1 a year in annual cycles against continuous time" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 1; Delta = 1; p_ii = 0.3679; D_mean = 1.582; T_mean = 1; gap_c = 0.582" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MKA-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "high-exit-rate-of-1-a-year-in-annual-cycles-against-continuous-time" .

hemo:7004112 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Groups cover the whole general population in a DCEA"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Baseline health and group sizes refer to the full general population, people who receive the programme and people who do not. Asaria and colleagues give two reasons, that concern about health inequality usually relates to the general population and that in a budget-constrained system the resources used by recipients displace care that anyone could have received. A group's gain and its share of the displaced health are therefore spread over every member of the group." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Groups cover the whole general population in a DCEA" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DCEA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "groups-cover-the-whole-general-population-in-a-dcea" .

hemo:7004113 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Opportunity cost shares add to one in a DCEA"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The shares o_1 and o_2 add to 1, so the groups together bear the whole of H_d. The tutorial apportions the opportunity cost equally to each individual, so each share equals the group's share of the population, and calls this convenient but not evidence based. The NICE support document on health inequalities keeps equal sharing in the base case, with slight and moderate gradients towards the more deprived groups as scenarios, and Love-Koh and colleagues estimate that 26% of the health effect of a change in English NHS spending falls on the most deprived fifth of the population and 14% on the least deprived fifth." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Opportunity cost shares add to one in a DCEA" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DCEA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "opportunity-cost-shares-add-to-one-in-a-dcea" .

hemo:7004114 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Health opportunity cost of a DCEA at a stated value of a QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "H_d comes from HE-FM-CET-002 at a stated value of a QALY, and the group results change with it. PMG36 sections 6.3.4 to 6.3.8 describe a range of GBP 25,000 to GBP 35,000 per QALY gained, and the NICE support document asks a DCEA to present results at several values within the range in those sections." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health opportunity cost of a DCEA at a stated value of a QALY" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DCEA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-opportunity-cost-of-a-dcea-at-a-stated-value-of-a-qaly" .

hemo:7004115 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Group health after a programme from named cells for two groups"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000674 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With cells named QALYGainWorse, QALYGainBetter, OCShareWorse, OCShareBetter, HealthOppCost, GroupSizeWorse, GroupSizeBetter, BaselineWorse and BaselineBetter, the first two formulas return health per person in the more and the less deprived group under the programme, held in cells named PostHealthWorse and PostHealthBetter, and the third returns the total net health benefit, held in NetHealthTotal. HealthOppCost is the result of HE-FM-CET-002." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Group health after a programme from named cells for two groups" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-DCEA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "group-health-after-a-programme-from-named-cells-for-two-groups" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=BaselineWorse+(QALYGainWorse-OCShareWorse*HealthOppCost)/GroupSizeWorse; =BaselineBetter+(QALYGainBetter-OCShareBetter*HealthOppCost)/GroupSizeBetter; =(QALYGainWorse-OCShareWorse*HealthOppCost)+(QALYGainBetter-OCShareBetter*HealthOppCost)" .

hemo:7004116 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Group net health benefits add up to the total gain less the opportunity cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With NetHealthTotal from HE-IM-DCEA-001, the total net health benefit equals the two QALY gains less HealthOppCost whenever the shares add to 1. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows shares that do not add to 1, for example 0.6 for each group in the article's example, which gives 360 QALYs in place of 600." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Group net health benefits add up to the total gain less the opportunity cost" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DCEA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "group-net-health-benefits-add-up-to-the-total-gain-less-the-opportunity-cost" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(NetHealthTotal-(QALYGainWorse+QALYGainBetter-HealthOppCost))<0.000001" .

hemo:7004117 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's group health under equal sharing reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's illustrative inputs and equal shares entered, PostHealthWorse rounds to 63.996 and PostHealthBetter to 72.016 years. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the whole opportunity cost subtracted from each group, which gives 63.984 and 72.004 years." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's group health under equal sharing reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DCEA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-group-health-under-equal-sharing-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ROUND(PostHealthWorse,4)=63.996,ROUND(PostHealthBetter,4)=72.016)" .

hemo:7004118 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's two groups with the opportunity cost shared equally per head"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative example two groups of 50,000 people have baseline QALE of 64 and 72 years and gain 400 and 1,400 QALYs, and the programme's GBP 30 million cost displaces 1,200 QALYs at GBP 25,000 per QALY. With each group bearing half, the more deprived group's net health benefit is minus 200 QALYs and the other group's 800, so health per person moves to 63.996 and 72.016 years, and total health rises by 600 QALYs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's two groups with the opportunity cost shared equally per head" ;
    hemo:9000014 "dQ_1 = 400; dQ_2 = 1400; o_1 = 0.5; o_2 = 0.5; H_d = 1200; h0_1 = 64; h0_2 = 72; N_1 = 50000; N_2 = 50000; NHB_1 = -200; NHB_2 = 800; h1_1 = 63.996; h1_2 = 72.016; NHB_tot = 600" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DCEA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-two-groups-with-the-opportunity-cost-shared-equally-per-head" .

hemo:7004119 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's two groups with 60% of the opportunity cost on the more deprived group"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Under the article's illustrative gradient the more deprived group bears 720 of the 1,200 displaced QALYs and the other group 480, so the net health benefits are minus 320 and 920 QALYs and health per person moves to 63.9936 and 72.0184 years. Total health still rises by 600 QALYs, while the gap between the groups widens to 8.0248 years against 8.020 under equal sharing." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's two groups with 60% of the opportunity cost on the more deprived group" ;
    hemo:9000014 "dQ_1 = 400; dQ_2 = 1400; o_1 = 0.6; o_2 = 0.4; H_d = 1200; h0_1 = 64; h0_2 = 72; N_1 = 50000; N_2 = 50000; NHB_1 = -320; NHB_2 = 920; h1_1 = 63.9936; h1_2 = 72.0184; NHB_tot = 600" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DCEA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-two-groups-with-60-of-the-opportunity-cost-on-the-more-deprived-group" .

hemo:7004120 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Cost-neutral programme with no health opportunity cost in a DCEA"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With H_d set to 0, each group's health moves by its own gain per person, to 64.008 and 72.028 years, and the total net health benefit equals the 1,800 QALYs gained. This is the limiting case in which the opportunity cost shares have no effect." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cost-neutral programme with no health opportunity cost in a DCEA" ;
    hemo:9000014 "dQ_1 = 400; dQ_2 = 1400; o_1 = 0.5; o_2 = 0.5; H_d = 0; h0_1 = 64; h0_2 = 72; N_1 = 50000; N_2 = 50000; NHB_1 = 400; NHB_2 = 1400; h1_1 = 64.008; h1_2 = 72.028; NHB_tot = 1800" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DCEA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cost-neutral-programme-with-no-health-opportunity-cost-in-a-dcea" .

hemo:7004121 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Programme and comparator EDE at the same inequality aversion"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Both EDEs use one epsilon, so their difference is a change in social welfare under one value judgement. Results are shown across a range of epsilon, as in Figure 4 of the DCEA tutorial, because the ranking can reverse as epsilon rises." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Programme and comparator EDE at the same inequality aversion" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DCEA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "programme-and-comparator-ede-at-the-same-inequality-aversion" .

hemo:7004122 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Same population and groups for both distributions in a DCEA comparison"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The two distributions cover the same people in the same groups with the same population shares, so N_pop and the group weights are common to both. EDE health is computed from levels of health such as QALE, every value above zero, and never from net health changes." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same population and groups for both distributions in a DCEA comparison" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DCEA-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-population-and-groups-for-both-distributions-in-a-dcea-comparison" .

hemo:7004123 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Distributions hold only unfair variation before the EDE is taken"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Variation judged fair, such as differences by sex in the tutorial, is removed before the EDE is computed, for example by direct standardisation. A different fairness judgement can give the same programme a different change in EDE health." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Distributions hold only unfair variation before the EDE is taken" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DCEA-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "distributions-hold-only-unfair-variation-before-the-ede-is-taken" .

hemo:7004124 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Change in EDE health and Atkinson index from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000675 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With MeanOption, MeanComparator, EDEOption and EDEComparator taken from HE-CF-ATK-001 at the same Epsilon, and the population in PopulationSize, the first formula returns the population change in EDE health, held in a cell named PopEDEChange, and the second the change in the Atkinson index." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Change in EDE health and Atkinson index from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-DCEA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "change-in-ede-health-and-atkinson-index-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(EDEOption-EDEComparator)*PopulationSize; =(1-EDEOption/MeanOption)-(1-EDEComparator/MeanComparator)" .

hemo:7004125 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Population EDE change at zero aversion equals the total net health benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With both EDEs computed at an Epsilon of 0, PopEDEChange from HE-IM-DCEA-002 equals NetHealthTotal of HE-IM-DCEA-001 within 0.001 QALYs. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the per-person change left unscaled, 0.006 against 600 QALYs, or EDEs computed at a non-zero epsilon, such as 484.3 at an epsilon of 2." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Population EDE change at zero aversion equals the total net health benefit" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DCEA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "population-ede-change-at-zero-aversion-equals-the-total-net-health-benefit" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(PopEDEChange-NetHealthTotal)<0.001" .

hemo:7004126 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's change in EDE health at an aversion of 2 reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's illustrative distributions under equal sharing and an Epsilon of 2, PopEDEChange rounds to 484.3 EDE QALYs. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows mean health used in place of EDE health, which gives 600, or the gradient distribution entered, which gives 456.0." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's change in EDE health at an aversion of 2 reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DCEA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-change-in-ede-health-at-an-aversion-of-2-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND(PopEDEChange,1)=484.3" .

hemo:7004127 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's programme at an inequality aversion of 2 with equal sharing"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At epsilon of 2 the EDE of two equal-sized groups is their harmonic mean, about 67.764706 years at baseline and 67.769549 years with funding and equal sharing. EDE health rises by 0.004843 years per person, 484.3 EDE QALYs across 100,000 people, against 600 QALYs of total health, and the Atkinson index rises by about 0.0000167, so relative inequality widens slightly." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's programme at an inequality aversion of 2 with equal sharing" ;
    hemo:9000014 "mu_0 = 68; mu_1 = 68.006; EDE_0 = 67.764706; EDE_1 = 67.769549; N_pop = 100000; d_mu = 0.006; d_EDE = 0.004843; d_P = 0.001157; dEDE_pop = 484.3; d_A = 0.0000167" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DCEA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-programme-at-an-inequality-aversion-of-2-with-equal-sharing" .

hemo:7004128 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's programme at the English median aversion with equal sharing"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At epsilon of 10.95, the public estimate cited in the tutorial's case study, EDE health rises from about 66.781476 to 66.781794 years, or 31.8 EDE QALYs across the area, so funding is still preferred when the displaced care is shared equally." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's programme at the English median aversion with equal sharing" ;
    hemo:9000014 "mu_0 = 68; mu_1 = 68.006; EDE_0 = 66.781476; EDE_1 = 66.781794; N_pop = 100000; d_mu = 0.006; d_EDE = 0.000318; d_P = 0.005682; dEDE_pop = 31.8; d_A = 0.0000820" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DCEA-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-programme-at-the-english-median-aversion-with-equal-sharing" .

hemo:7004129 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's programme at the English median aversion with the 60% gradient"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When the more deprived group bears 60% of the displaced health, EDE health at epsilon of 10.95 falls to about 66.780405 years, or minus 107.1 EDE QALYs across the area, so not funding is preferred at that aversion." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's programme at the English median aversion with the 60% gradient" ;
    hemo:9000014 "mu_0 = 68; mu_1 = 68.006; EDE_0 = 66.781476; EDE_1 = 66.780405; N_pop = 100000; d_mu = 0.006; d_EDE = -0.001071; d_P = 0.007071; dEDE_pop = -107.1; d_A = 0.0001024" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DCEA-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-programme-at-the-english-median-aversion-with-the-60-gradient" .

hemo:7004130 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Zero inequality aversion where EDE health equals mean health"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With epsilon of 0 each EDE equals its mean, so the population change in EDE health equals the 600 QALYs of total net health benefit and neither the inequality cost nor the Atkinson index changes. This limiting case checks the link to HE-FM-DCEA-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Zero inequality aversion where EDE health equals mean health" ;
    hemo:9000014 "mu_0 = 68; mu_1 = 68.006; EDE_0 = 68; EDE_1 = 68.006; N_pop = 100000; d_mu = 0.006; d_EDE = 0.006; d_P = 0; dEDE_pop = 600; d_A = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DCEA-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "zero-inequality-aversion-where-ede-health-equals-mean-health" .

hemo:7004131 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Comparator at the origin of the equity-efficiency impact plane"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Both changes are the programme less the same comparator, which sits at the origin of the plane, as in Figure 1 of Cookson and colleagues 2021. A different comparator moves every programme on the plane." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Comparator at the origin of the equity-efficiency impact plane" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DCEA-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "comparator-at-the-origin-of-the-equity-efficiency-impact-plane" .

hemo:7004132 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Inequality index oriented so that a higher value means more inequality"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The change d_I is positive when inequality widens. The Atkinson index meets this, and so does the absolute gap of HE-FM-HINQ-001 while the less deprived group remains the healthier. An index that falls as inequality widens is negated before it is entered." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Inequality index oriented so that a higher value means more inequality" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DCEA-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "inequality-index-oriented-so-that-a-higher-value-means-more-inequality" .

hemo:7004133 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Total health on the impact plane counted net of the opportunity cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The health axis counts the health displaced by the programme's cost, as in Cookson and colleagues 2017, whose vertical axis is the total health benefit less the health forgone by spending the same money on other programmes, and whose equity axis also allows for opportunity costs. The inequality change therefore uses the distributions of HE-FM-DCEA-001 after the shares of H_d." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Total health on the impact plane counted net of the opportunity cost" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DCEA-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "total-health-on-the-impact-plane-counted-net-of-the-opportunity-cost" .

hemo:7004134 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Points on an axis of the impact plane follow the dominance tie rule"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "A zero change on one axis with an improvement on the other is classified with the improving quadrant, as an option of equal cost and greater health dominates under HE-FM-DOM-001, and identical health and inequality give no quadrant. Both changes are rounded to the precision of the model before the signs are read, so that rounding noise does not decide the quadrant." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Points on an axis of the impact plane follow the dominance tie rule" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DCEA-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "points-on-an-axis-of-the-impact-plane-follow-the-dominance-tie-rule" .

hemo:7004135 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Impact plane quadrant label from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000676 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the change in total health in a cell named HealthChange and the change in the inequality index in InequalityChange, the formula returns the quadrant label, or no change at the origin, held in a cell named ImpactQuadrant. Rounding both inputs to the model's precision first keeps the tie branches meaningful." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Impact plane quadrant label from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-DCEA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "impact-plane-quadrant-label-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(AND(HealthChange>=0,InequalityChange<=0,OR(HealthChange>0,InequalityChange<0)),\"win-win\",IF(AND(HealthChange<=0,InequalityChange>=0,OR(HealthChange<0,InequalityChange>0)),\"lose-lose\",IF(AND(HealthChange>0,InequalityChange>0),\"win-lose\",IF(AND(HealthChange<0,InequalityChange<0),\"lose-win\",\"no change\"))))" .

hemo:7004136 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No change in total health with narrower inequality classified as win-win"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With HealthChange set to 0 and InequalityChange to minus 0.01, ImpactQuadrant from HE-IM-DCEA-003 returns win-win. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows strict inequalities in the win-win branch, which leave the case as no change." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No change in total health with narrower inequality classified as win-win" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DCEA-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-change-in-total-health-with-narrower-inequality-classified-as-win-win" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ImpactQuadrant=\"win-win\"" .

hemo:7004137 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's programme classified as win-lose on the impact plane"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With HealthChange set to 600 and InequalityChange to 0.020, the article's equal-sharing case, ImpactQuadrant returns win-lose. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the gap entered with its sign reversed, minus 0.020, which returns win-win." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's programme classified as win-lose on the impact plane" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DCEA-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-programme-classified-as-win-lose-on-the-impact-plane" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ImpactQuadrant=\"win-lose\"" .

hemo:7004138 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's programme on the gap between groups with equal sharing"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With equal sharing the article's programme raises total health by 600 QALYs and widens the gap between the groups by 0.020 years, to 8.020, so it lies in the win-lose quadrant, as the article states." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's programme on the gap between groups with equal sharing" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d_H = 600; d_I = 0.020; WW = 0; LL = 0; WL = 1; LW = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DCEA-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-programme-on-the-gap-between-groups-with-equal-sharing" .

hemo:7004139 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's programme on the Atkinson index at an aversion of 2"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the Atkinson index at epsilon of 2 as the inequality measure, the change is about 0.0000167 from HE-FM-DCEA-002, so the same programme stays in the win-lose quadrant." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's programme on the Atkinson index at an aversion of 2" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d_H = 600; d_I = 0.0000167; WW = 0; LL = 0; WL = 1; LW = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DCEA-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-programme-on-the-atkinson-index-at-an-aversion-of-2" .

hemo:7004140 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Programme with no change in total health that narrows inequality"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An illustrative programme that leaves total health unchanged and narrows the gap by 0.01 years is classified win-win under the tie rule of HE-FM-DOM-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Programme with no change in total health that narrows inequality" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d_H = 0; d_I = -0.01; WW = 1; LL = 0; WL = 0; LW = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DCEA-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "programme-with-no-change-in-total-health-that-narrows-inequality" .

hemo:7004141 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Programme with no change on either axis of the impact plane"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With both changes at zero no quadrant applies and every indicator is 0, the limiting case of the comparator itself." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Programme with no change on either axis of the impact plane" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d_H = 0; d_I = 0; WW = 0; LL = 0; WL = 0; LW = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DCEA-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "programme-with-no-change-on-either-axis-of-the-impact-plane" .

hemo:7004142 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Ramsey components added as real annual rates"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The formula sums the components, as paragraph 2.2 of the guidance states, and every rate is real, so the result applies only to values with general inflation removed (paragraph 2.19). Compounding the parts instead, (1 + delta)(1 + L)(1 + mu g) minus 1, gives about 3.535% with the Table 2.A values and is not the Treasury's form (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ramsey components added as real annual rates" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-TPR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ramsey-components-added-as-real-annual-rates" .

hemo:7004143 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Ramsey component values fixed for years 1 to 30 only"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Table 2.A gives the parameter values for years 1 to 30. For later years the guidance states the declining rates directly in Table 3.A and attributes the decline to uncertainty about the future values of the parameters (paragraph 3.1), without new component values, so the formula is not used to rebuild the 3.0% or 2.5% rates. Their discount factors are in HE-FM-TPR-003." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ramsey component values fixed for years 1 to 30 only" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-TPR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ramsey-component-values-fixed-for-years-1-to-30-only" .

hemo:7004144 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Health discount rate as a Green Book rule and not the NICE reference case"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Paragraphs 2.16 and 2.17 discount health and life effects at the health discount rate in every year of the appraisal, 1.5% for years 1 to 30 and the lower rates of Table 3.A after that, with all other social costs and benefits at the STPR. Section 4.5.1 of the NICE manual PMG36 discounts costs and health effects at the same 3.5% a year in the reference case, and section 4.5.3 allows 1.5% for both only when all of its criteria are met, so r_HD belongs to Treasury cost-benefit appraisal." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health discount rate as a Green Book rule and not the NICE reference case" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-TPR-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-discount-rate-as-a-green-book-rule-and-not-the-nice-reference-case" .

hemo:7004145 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Normative status of the Ramsey components of time preference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Attema and colleagues note that building a social rate from the Ramsey components assumes that every component, impatience included, is normatively relevant. The guidance cites Freeman, Groom and Spackman for a plausible range of 0.0% to 1.0% for pure time preference (paragraph 2.10), so the 0.5% value is a choice within that range." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Normative status of the Ramsey components of time preference" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-TPR-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "normative-status-of-the-ramsey-components-of-time-preference" .

hemo:7004146 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Green Book social time preference rate and health rate from named component cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000677 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With pure time preference in PureTimePref, catastrophic risk in CatastropheRisk, the elasticity in ElasticityMU and consumption growth in ConsGrowth, all entered as decimals, the first formula returns the STPR and the second the health discount rate. The tests below read the two results from cells named STPROut and HealthRateOut." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Green Book social time preference rate and health rate from named component cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-TPR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "green-book-social-time-preference-rate-and-health-rate-from-named-component-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=PureTimePref+CatastropheRisk+ElasticityMU*ConsGrowth; =PureTimePref+CatastropheRisk" .

hemo:7004147 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Green Book 3.5% and 1.5% reproduced in the result cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the Table 2.A values in the four input cells, the STPR cell holds 3.5% and the health rate cell 1.5%. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows catastrophic risk left out of both cells (2.5% and 0.5%) or growth entered as 2 in place of 0.02." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Green Book 3.5% and 1.5% reproduced in the result cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-TPR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "green-book-3-5-and-1-5-reproduced-in-the-result-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(STPROut-0.035)<1E-9,ABS(HealthRateOut-0.015)<1E-9)" .

hemo:7004148 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Gap between the Green Book STPR and health rate equals the wealth effect"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The STPR cell minus the health rate cell equals the elasticity times consumption growth, 2.0% with the Table 2.A values. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a health rate cell holding the STPR, which discounts health at 3.5%, or a typed rate that does not match the components." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Gap between the Green Book STPR and health rate equals the wealth effect" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-TPR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "gap-between-the-green-book-stpr-and-health-rate-equals-the-wealth-effect" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((STPROut-HealthRateOut)-ElasticityMU*ConsGrowth)<1E-12" .

hemo:7004149 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Green Book STPR less pure time preference matches the reduced rate in Table 3.B"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the Table 2.A values, the STPR cell minus pure time preference gives 3.0%, the reduced rate for years 1 to 30 in Table 3.B. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows an STPR cell built without catastrophic risk, which leaves 2.0%." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Green Book STPR less pure time preference matches the reduced rate in Table 3.B" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-TPR-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "green-book-stpr-less-pure-time-preference-matches-the-reduced-rate-in-table-3-b" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((STPROut-PureTimePref)-0.03)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004150 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Green Book 3.5% rate and 1.5% health rate from the Table 2.A values"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With pure time preference of 0.5%, catastrophic risk of 1.0%, an elasticity of 1.0 and growth of 2.0%, time preference is 1.5%, the wealth effect 2.0%, the STPR 3.5% and the health rate 1.5%, as in paragraphs 2.6 to 2.8 and 2.16 of the guidance." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Green Book 3.5% rate and 1.5% health rate from the Table 2.A values" ;
    hemo:9000014 "delta = 0.005; L = 0.01; mu = 1; g = 0.02; rho = 0.015; W_e = 0.02; STPR = 0.035; r_HD = 0.015" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-TPR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "green-book-3-5-rate-and-1-5-health-rate-from-the-table-2-a-values" .

hemo:7004151 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Green Book rate with pure time preference set to zero"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Removing pure time preference leaves 3.0%, the reduced rate for years 1 to 30 in Table 3.B used for sensitivity analysis beyond 50 years. The health output of 1.0% is computed here for illustration only, since Table 3.B gives no reduced health rate." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Green Book rate with pure time preference set to zero" ;
    hemo:9000014 "delta = 0; L = 0.01; mu = 1; g = 0.02; rho = 0.01; W_e = 0.02; STPR = 0.03; r_HD = 0.01" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-TPR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "green-book-rate-with-pure-time-preference-set-to-zero" .

hemo:7004152 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Green Book rate with the pooled elasticity of 1.5 reported by Groom and Maddison"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Paragraph 2.12 reports a pooled elasticity of 1.5 from Groom and Maddison, while the Green Book assumes 1.0. With 1.5 and the other Table 2.A values the wealth effect rises to 3.0% and the STPR to 4.5%, while the health rate stays at 1.5% because it has no wealth effect (illustrative sensitivity, computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Green Book rate with the pooled elasticity of 1.5 reported by Groom and Maddison" ;
    hemo:9000014 "delta = 0.005; L = 0.01; mu = 1.5; g = 0.02; rho = 0.015; W_e = 0.03; STPR = 0.045; r_HD = 0.015" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-TPR-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "green-book-rate-with-the-pooled-elasticity-of-1-5-reported-by-groom-and-maddison" .

hemo:7004153 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant annual rate over the delay between the two illnesses"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The equation applies the same factor b to every year between year 2 and year s, so r is the one constant rate that fits a single answer. Answers at several delays give one rate each, and TEMPUS reports that stationarity was violated and that hyperbolic models fitted its data better than the discounted utility model." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant annual rate over the delay between the two illnesses" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-TPR-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-annual-rate-over-the-delay-between-the-two-illnesses" .

hemo:7004154 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Days of ill health weighed by their number in the TEMPUS equation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The equation sets 20 days in year 2 against y days in year s by count alone, so y days of the illness are worth y times one day of it and the illness is the same at both dates. An answer that reflects a change in severity or circumstances over time is read as time preference." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Days of ill health weighed by their number in the TEMPUS equation" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-TPR-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "days-of-ill-health-weighed-by-their-number-in-the-tempus-equation" .

hemo:7004155 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Individual implied rate used for description and not as the social rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The rate describes one person's trade-off. TEMPUS warns that its implied rates should not be over-emphasised because the sample was unrepresentative, Attema and colleagues report average rates as high as 45% across studies, and the reference-case rate is set by guidance (PMG36 section 4.5.1)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Individual implied rate used for description and not as the social rate" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-TPR-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "individual-implied-rate-used-for-description-and-not-as-the-social-rate" .

hemo:7004156 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Implied annual rate from a TEMPUS open-ended answer in named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000678 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the days accepted in DaysLater and the year of the delayed illness in YearLater, the first formula returns the implied rate; name its cell ImpliedRateOut. The second uses RRI, available from Excel 2013, which returns the same rate as the growth rate that takes 20 to DaysLater over YearLater minus 2 periods." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Implied annual rate from a TEMPUS open-ended answer in named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-TPR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "implied-annual-rate-from-a-tempus-open-ended-answer-in-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(DaysLater/20)^(1/(YearLater-2))-1; =RRI(YearLater-2,20,DaysLater)" .

hemo:7004157 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "TEMPUS implied rate cell agrees with the RRI growth rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The result cell equals the rate RRI computes from the same answer by a separate route. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows an exponent of 1 over YearLater, the delay counted from now, which gives 4.66% in place of 9.54% for 24 days in year 4." ;
    hemo:9000009 "TEMPUS implied rate cell agrees with the RRI growth rate" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-TPR-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "tempus-implied-rate-cell-agrees-with-the-rri-growth-rate" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ImpliedRateOut-RRI(YearLater-2,20,DaysLater))<1E-12" .

hemo:7004158 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Sign of the TEMPUS implied rate follows the answer relative to 20 days"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The rate is positive when more than 20 days are accepted, zero at 20 days and negative below, the classification by positive, zero and negative rates that TEMPUS applies. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the ratio written as 20 over DaysLater, which flips the sign of any answer other than 20 days." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Sign of the TEMPUS implied rate follows the answer relative to 20 days" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-TPR-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "sign-of-the-tempus-implied-rate-follows-the-answer-relative-to-20-days" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SIGN(ImpliedRateOut)=SIGN(DaysLater-20)" .

hemo:7004159 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's three TEMPUS-style implied rates reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The answers of 24, 30 and 36 days in years 4, 9 and 13 give 0.0954, 0.0596 and 0.0549 to four decimal places, as in the article. Expected result TRUE. Writing the exponent as 1 over the year in place of 1 over the delay gives 0.0466 for the first answer and makes it FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's three TEMPUS-style implied rates reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-TPR-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-three-tempus-style-implied-rates-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS((24/20)^(1/2)-1-0.0954)<0.0001,ABS((30/20)^(1/7)-1-0.0596)<0.0001,ABS((36/20)^(1/11)-1-0.0549)<0.0001)" .

hemo:7004160 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "TEMPUS-style answer of 24 days in year 4"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative respondent accepts up to 24 days in year 4, a delay of 2 years, which implies about 9.54% a year." ;
    hemo:9000009 "TEMPUS-style answer of 24 days in year 4" ;
    hemo:9000014 "y = 24; s = 4; r = 0.0954" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-TPR-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "tempus-style-answer-of-24-days-in-year-4" .

hemo:7004161 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "TEMPUS-style answer of 30 days in year 9"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Accepting up to 30 days in year 9, a delay of 7 years, implies about 5.96% a year, as in the article's table (illustrative answer)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "TEMPUS-style answer of 30 days in year 9" ;
    hemo:9000014 "y = 30; s = 9; r = 0.0596" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-TPR-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "tempus-style-answer-of-30-days-in-year-9" .

hemo:7004162 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "TEMPUS-style answer of 36 days in year 13"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Accepting up to 36 days in year 13, a delay of 11 years, implies about 5.49% a year. Across the three answers the rate falls with delay, the pattern the article reads as a violation of stationarity (illustrative answers)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "TEMPUS-style answer of 36 days in year 13" ;
    hemo:9000014 "y = 36; s = 13; r = 0.0549" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-TPR-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "tempus-style-answer-of-36-days-in-year-13" .

hemo:7004163 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "TEMPUS-style answer of exactly 20 days gives zero time preference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A respondent who accepts no more than the original 20 days at any later date is indifferent to timing, so the implied rate is zero whatever the delay, a limiting case that checks an implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "TEMPUS-style answer of exactly 20 days gives zero time preference" ;
    hemo:9000014 "y = 20; s = 7; r = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-TPR-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "tempus-style-answer-of-exactly-20-days-gives-zero-time-preference" .

hemo:7004164 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "TEMPUS-style answer below 20 days gives negative time preference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Accepting at most 18 days in year 4 means the respondent would rather have the illness sooner, which implies a rate of about minus 5.13% a year (illustrative answer, computed here for illustration). Van der Pol and Cairns report that a substantial number of respondents had negative or zero rates." ;
    hemo:9000009 "TEMPUS-style answer below 20 days gives negative time preference" ;
    hemo:9000014 "y = 18; s = 4; r = -0.0513" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-TPR-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "tempus-style-answer-below-20-days-gives-negative-time-preference" .

hemo:7004165 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Whole appraisal years counted from year 0 under the stepped schedule"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Year 0 has a factor of 1 and a cost or benefit in year t is multiplied by the factor for that year, as paragraph 1.3 and the Annex A tables of the guidance set out. The bands end at year 125, the last year of Table 3.A. The Green Book main text states 2.50% for year 76 onwards with no end year, so for the standard STPR a longer horizon keeps r_3 beyond year 125, whereas Table 3.B steps the reduced rate down again after year 125, so the formula covers the reduced schedule only to year 125 and the code of HE-CF-TPR-001 covers its later bands." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Whole appraisal years counted from year 0 under the stepped schedule" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-TPR-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "whole-appraisal-years-counted-from-year-0-under-the-stepped-schedule" .

hemo:7004166 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Stepped decline from parameter uncertainty and not from individual impatience"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Paragraph 3.1 attributes the decline after year 30 to uncertainty about the future values of the STPR parameters. The schedule is a fixed social rule for long horizons, while the hyperbolic and quasi-hyperbolic forms of HE-FN-HYPD-001 describe how individuals weigh delays, so one is not evidence for the other." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Stepped decline from parameter uncertainty and not from individual impatience" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-TPR-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "stepped-decline-from-parameter-uncertainty-and-not-from-individual-impatience" .

hemo:7004167 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Reduced stepped schedule for horizons beyond 50 years"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Paragraph 3.4 asks for additional sensitivity analysis beyond 50 years with pure time preference excluded. Table 3.B gives 3.00%, 2.57% and 2.14% for the same bands to year 125, so the formula takes these rates. Factors built from the printed rates are 0.0001 above Table A.3 at each listed year after 30, for example 0.2480 against 0.2479 at year 50 (computed here), so the Annex A factors are the values to report." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Reduced stepped schedule for horizons beyond 50 years" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-TPR-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "reduced-stepped-schedule-for-horizons-beyond-50-years" .

hemo:7004168 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Stepped Green Book discount factor from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000679 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the year in YearT and the three band rates in RateNear, RateMid and RateFar, the cell returns the factor; name it DiscFactorOut for the tests. MIN and MAX count the years that fall in each band, and filling the formula down a column of years gives a factor table like Annex A." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Stepped Green Book discount factor from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-TPR-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "stepped-green-book-discount-factor-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(1+RateNear)^(-MIN(YearT,30))*(1+RateMid)^(-MAX(MIN(YearT,75)-30,0))*(1+RateFar)^(-MAX(YearT-75,0))" .

hemo:7004169 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Stepped Green Book factor for year 60 matches Table A.1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With YearT = 60 and the STPR rates 0.035, 0.03 and 0.025 in the rate cells, the result cell holds 0.1468 to four decimal places. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows 3.0% applied from year 0, which gives 0.1697, or 2.5% entered in RateNear and 3.5% in RateFar, which gives 0.1964." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Stepped Green Book factor for year 60 matches Table A.1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-TPR-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "stepped-green-book-factor-for-year-60-matches-table-a-1" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(YearT=60,ABS(DiscFactorOut-0.1468)<0.00005)" .

hemo:7004170 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Stepped Green Book factor not below the constant first-band factor"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When the later band rates are no higher than the first, the stepped factor is at least (1 + RateNear) to the power minus YearT. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a later rate entered above the first, for example 4.0% in RateMid, which gives 0.1098 for year 60 against 0.1269." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Stepped Green Book factor not below the constant first-band factor" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-TPR-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "stepped-green-book-factor-not-below-the-constant-first-band-factor" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=DiscFactorOut>=(1+RateNear)^(-YearT)-1E-12" .

hemo:7004171 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Stepped Green Book health factor for year 50 matches Table A.2"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Thirty years at 1.5% and twenty at 1.286% give 0.4955 to four decimal places. Expected result TRUE. Applying 1.286% to all 50 years gives 0.5279 and makes it FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Stepped Green Book health factor for year 50 matches Table A.2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-TPR-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "stepped-green-book-health-factor-for-year-50-matches-table-a-2" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((1+0.015)^(-30)*(1+0.01286)^(-20)-0.4955)<0.00005" .

