hemo:7004202 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal-sized groups each at its mean health in the isoelastic welfare"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each h_i is the health of one person, or of one of n groups of equal size whose members are all given the group's mean health, so inequality within a group is ignored. Groups of unequal size need population shares, as in the computational function HE-CF-ATK-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equal-sized groups each at its mean health in the isoelastic welfare" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-SWF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equal-sized-groups-each-at-its-mean-health-in-the-isoelastic-welfare" .

hemo:7004203 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Inequality aversion as a value judgement in the isoelastic welfare"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Epsilon states how much total health would be given up for a more equal spread, a social value judgement that comes from outside the clinical evidence. Asaria, Griffin and Cookson state that such judgements should be provided by the decision maker and that the sensitivity of conclusions to them should be explored, so W is reported over a range of epsilon values." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Inequality aversion as a value judgement in the isoelastic welfare" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-SWF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "inequality-aversion-as-a-value-judgement-in-the-isoelastic-welfare" .

hemo:7004204 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Isoelastic welfare and the NICE limits on inequality aversion functions"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "In NICE technology appraisal a distributional cost-effectiveness analysis (DCEA) is supporting evidence only (PMG36, section 4.12.3), and its results should not weight costs or benefits by the social characteristics of the people affected (section 4.12.7). The NICE support document on health inequalities states that this includes mathematical functions that use health inequality aversion parameters, so W at an epsilon above 0 falls outside the DCEA results reported to NICE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Isoelastic welfare and the NICE limits on inequality aversion functions" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-SWF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "isoelastic-welfare-and-the-nice-limits-on-inequality-aversion-functions" .

hemo:7004205 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Isoelastic social welfare of three groups from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000680 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the three groups' health in HealthFirstGroup, HealthSecondGroup and HealthThirdGroup and the parameter in InequalityAversion, not equal to 1, the formula returns W. The result cell is named IsoelasticWelfare." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Isoelastic social welfare of three groups from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-SWF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "isoelastic-social-welfare-of-three-groups-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(HealthFirstGroup^(1-InequalityAversion)+HealthSecondGroup^(1-InequalityAversion)+HealthThirdGroup^(1-InequalityAversion))/(1-InequalityAversion)" .

hemo:7004206 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Isoelastic welfare at epsilon of 2 matches the harmonic-mean EDE"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At epsilon of 2 the EDE health of HE-FM-ATK-002 is the harmonic mean, so W equals minus 3 divided by the harmonic mean of the three health values, computed here with HARMEAN and not from the result cell's formula. Expected result TRUE with InequalityAversion at 2. FALSE shows W computed without the divisor 1 minus epsilon, which gives plus 0.0442201 for option P, or InequalityAversion set to another value." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Isoelastic welfare at epsilon of 2 matches the harmonic-mean EDE" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-SWF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "isoelastic-welfare-at-epsilon-of-2-matches-the-harmonic-mean-ede" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(InequalityAversion=2,ABS(IsoelasticWelfare+3/HARMEAN(HealthFirstGroup,HealthSecondGroup,HealthThirdGroup))<1E-12)" .

hemo:7004207 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Isoelastic welfare at epsilon of 0 equals the total of group health"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When InequalityAversion is 0 the result cell must equal the sum of the three health values, the utilitarian case, and at other values the test passes without checking. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the exponent written as minus epsilon in place of 1 minus epsilon, which gives W of 3 for any health values at epsilon of 0." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Isoelastic welfare at epsilon of 0 equals the total of group health" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-SWF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "isoelastic-welfare-at-epsilon-of-0-equals-the-total-of-group-health" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=OR(InequalityAversion<>0,ABS(IsoelasticWelfare-(HealthFirstGroup+HealthSecondGroup+HealthThirdGroup))<1E-9)" .

hemo:7004208 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Isoelastic welfare of the targeted option at epsilon of 2"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative example, option T leaves three equal-sized groups with QALE of 61.0, 68.3 and 76.0 years. At epsilon of 2 each term is minus the reciprocal of health, and W is about minus 0.0441926, shown in the article multiplied by 1,000 as minus 44.1926." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Isoelastic welfare of the targeted option at epsilon of 2" ;
    hemo:9000014 "h_1 = 61; h_2 = 68.3; h_3 = 76; epsilon = 2; W = -0.0441926" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-SWF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "isoelastic-welfare-of-the-targeted-option-at-epsilon-of-2" .

hemo:7004209 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Isoelastic welfare of the population-wide option at epsilon of 2"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Option P, with QALE of 60.3, 68.5 and 76.7 years, has W of about minus 0.0442201 at epsilon of 2. This is below T's minus 0.0441926, so the isoelastic function prefers T although P has the larger total." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Isoelastic welfare of the population-wide option at epsilon of 2" ;
    hemo:9000014 "h_1 = 60.3; h_2 = 68.5; h_3 = 76.7; epsilon = 2; W = -0.0442201" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-SWF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "isoelastic-welfare-of-the-population-wide-option-at-epsilon-of-2" .

hemo:7004210 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Isoelastic welfare at epsilon of 0 equals total health"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At epsilon of 0 every term is the group's own health, so W for option P is its total of 205.5 years, the utilitarian limiting case in which P ranks first." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Isoelastic welfare at epsilon of 0 equals total health" ;
    hemo:9000014 "h_1 = 60.3; h_2 = 68.5; h_3 = 76.7; epsilon = 0; W = 205.5" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-SWF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "isoelastic-welfare-at-epsilon-of-0-equals-total-health" .

hemo:7004211 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Health above zero for logarithmic social welfare"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every h_i is above zero, because the logarithm of zero is undefined and W falls without limit as any group's health approaches zero. The groups are of equal size, as in HE-FM-SWF-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health above zero for logarithmic social welfare" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-SWF-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-above-zero-for-logarithmic-social-welfare" .

hemo:7004212 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Logarithmic social welfare with the Excel natural logarithm"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000681 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the three health values in the named cells used for HE-FM-SWF-001, the formula returns W in a cell named LogWelfare. Excel's LN is the natural logarithm, while LOG with one argument is base 10." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Logarithmic social welfare with the Excel natural logarithm" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-SWF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "logarithmic-social-welfare-with-the-excel-natural-logarithm" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LN(HealthFirstGroup)+LN(HealthSecondGroup)+LN(HealthThirdGroup)" .

hemo:7004213 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Logarithmic welfare matches the geometric-mean EDE"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Three times the natural logarithm of the geometric mean of the health values, which is the EDE health of HE-FM-ATK-003 and is computed here with GEOMEAN, equals the result cell. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows W built with LOG, base 10, which gives about 5.5008 for option P instead of 12.6661." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Logarithmic welfare matches the geometric-mean EDE" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-SWF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "logarithmic-welfare-matches-the-geometric-mean-ede" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(LogWelfare-3*LN(GEOMEAN(HealthFirstGroup,HealthSecondGroup,HealthThirdGroup)))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004214 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Logarithmic welfare is the limit of the isoelastic form near epsilon of 1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With 1 minus epsilon set to 0.000001, the isoelastic terms less the constant n/(1-epsilon) are (h_i^0.000001 minus 1) divided by 0.000001, and their sum is within 0.0001 of the result cell. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a base-10 logarithm in the result cell, which leaves a gap of about 7.17 for option P." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Logarithmic welfare is the limit of the isoelastic form near epsilon of 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-SWF-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "logarithmic-welfare-is-the-limit-of-the-isoelastic-form-near-epsilon-of-1" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(((HealthFirstGroup^0.000001-1)+(HealthSecondGroup^0.000001-1)+(HealthThirdGroup^0.000001-1))/0.000001-LogWelfare)<0.0001" .

hemo:7004215 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Logarithmic welfare of the population-wide option"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At epsilon of 1 option P in the article's illustrative example, with QALE of 60.3, 68.5 and 76.7 years, has W of about 12.6660676." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Logarithmic welfare of the population-wide option" ;
    hemo:9000014 "h_1 = 60.3; h_2 = 68.5; h_3 = 76.7; W = 12.6660676" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-SWF-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "logarithmic-welfare-of-the-population-wide-option" .

hemo:7004216 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Logarithmic welfare of the targeted option"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Option T, with 61.0, 68.3 and 76.0 years, has W of about 12.665517, slightly below P, so P still ranks first at epsilon of 1, as the article's switching point of about 1.23 implies." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Logarithmic welfare of the targeted option" ;
    hemo:9000014 "h_1 = 61; h_2 = 68.3; h_3 = 76; W = 12.665517" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-SWF-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "logarithmic-welfare-of-the-targeted-option" .

hemo:7004217 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Logarithmic welfare of the option for the worst off"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Option R, with 61.2, 67.6 and 75.6 years, has W of about 12.6532115, the lowest of the three at epsilon of 1." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Logarithmic welfare of the option for the worst off" ;
    hemo:9000014 "h_1 = 61.2; h_2 = 67.6; h_3 = 75.6; W = 12.6532115" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-SWF-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "logarithmic-welfare-of-the-option-for-the-worst-off" .

hemo:7004218 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Maximin welfare compares groups at their mean health"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each h_i is the mean health of a group, so the form protects the least healthy group on average and not its least healthy member, and the result depends on how the population is divided into groups." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Maximin welfare compares groups at their mean health" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-SWF-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "maximin-welfare-compares-groups-at-their-mean-health" .

hemo:7004219 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Maximin social welfare from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000682 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the named cells of HE-IM-SWF-001 the formula returns W in a cell named MaximinWelfare." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Maximin social welfare from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-SWF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "maximin-social-welfare-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MIN(HealthFirstGroup,HealthSecondGroup,HealthThirdGroup)" .

hemo:7004220 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Maximin welfare does not exceed EDE health"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The minimum is never above the EDE health at any finite epsilon, here the harmonic mean, which is the EDE at epsilon of 2 (HE-FM-ATK-002). Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows MAX in place of MIN, which gives 76.7 for option P against an EDE of about 67.84." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Maximin welfare does not exceed EDE health" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-SWF-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "maximin-welfare-does-not-exceed-ede-health" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MaximinWelfare<=HARMEAN(HealthFirstGroup,HealthSecondGroup,HealthThirdGroup)+1E-9" .

hemo:7004221 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Maximin ranking of the article's three options"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The minima are 61.2 for R, 61.0 for T and 60.3 for P, so R ranks first and P last. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows MAX in place of MIN, which reverses the order with 75.6, 76.0 and 76.7." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Maximin ranking of the article's three options" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-SWF-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "maximin-ranking-of-the-article-s-three-options" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(MIN(61.2,67.6,75.6)>MIN(61,68.3,76),MIN(61,68.3,76)>MIN(60.3,68.5,76.7))" .

hemo:7004222 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Maximin welfare of the option for the worst off"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Option R in the article's illustrative example gives the least healthy group 61.2 years, the highest minimum of the three options, so R ranks first under maximin." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Maximin welfare of the option for the worst off" ;
    hemo:9000014 "h_1 = 61.2; h_2 = 67.6; h_3 = 75.6; W = 61.2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-SWF-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "maximin-welfare-of-the-option-for-the-worst-off" .

hemo:7004223 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Maximin welfare of the population-wide option"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Option P gives the least healthy group 60.3 years and ranks last under maximin, although it has the largest total." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Maximin welfare of the population-wide option" ;
    hemo:9000014 "h_1 = 60.3; h_2 = 68.5; h_3 = 76.7; W = 60.3" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-SWF-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "maximin-welfare-of-the-population-wide-option" .

hemo:7004224 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal-sized homogeneous groups in the CES welfare"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Dolan and Tsuchiya take the two groups to be of equal size, homogeneous and alike in every attribute other than the one alpha reflects, with well-being measured on a cardinal scale on which death is zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equal-sized homogeneous groups in the CES welfare" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-SWF-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equal-sized-homogeneous-groups-in-the-ces-welfare" .

hemo:7004225 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Group weight and curvature as separate value judgements"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Alpha states how much one group's health counts at equal health, for example less for a group judged responsible for its poorer health, and r states aversion to inequality. Asaria and colleagues note that in the Dolan and Tsuchiya formulation the empirical values of the two parameters are difficult to identify separately." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Group weight and curvature as separate value judgements" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-SWF-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "group-weight-and-curvature-as-separate-value-judgements" .

hemo:7004226 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-group CES social welfare from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000683 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the health of the two groups in HealthGroupA and HealthGroupB, alpha in GroupWeightA and r in CurvatureR, the formula returns W in a cell named CESWelfare." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-group CES social welfare from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-SWF-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-group-ces-social-welfare-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(GroupWeightA*HealthGroupA^(-CurvatureR)+(1-GroupWeightA)*HealthGroupB^(-CurvatureR))^(-1/CurvatureR)" .

hemo:7004227 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "CES welfare lies within the two groups' health"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For alpha at least 0 and at most 1 and r of minus 1 or above, not 0, W is a weighted power mean and so is no lower than the smaller and no higher than the larger of the two health values. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the outer exponent written as 1/r instead of minus 1/r, which gives about 0.0153 for 60 and 72 years at r = 1." ;
    hemo:9000009 "CES welfare lies within the two groups' health" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-SWF-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ces-welfare-lies-within-the-two-groups-health" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(CESWelfare>=MIN(HealthGroupA,HealthGroupB)-1E-9,CESWelfare<=MAX(HealthGroupA,HealthGroupB)+1E-9)" .

hemo:7004228 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "CES welfare with equal weights equals the harmonic-mean EDE at r of 1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With GroupWeightA at 0.5 and CurvatureR at 1, W equals the harmonic mean of the two health values from HARMEAN, the EDE health of HE-FM-ATK-002 at epsilon of 2. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the outer exponent dropped, which leaves the mean of the reciprocals, about 0.0153, or other values of the weight or of r." ;
    hemo:9000009 "CES welfare with equal weights equals the harmonic-mean EDE at r of 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-SWF-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ces-welfare-with-equal-weights-equals-the-harmonic-mean-ede-at-r-of-1" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(GroupWeightA=0.5,CurvatureR=1,ABS(CESWelfare-HARMEAN(HealthGroupA,HealthGroupB))<1E-9)" .

hemo:7004229 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "CES welfare with equal weights and r of 1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With illustrative group health of 60 and 72 years, alpha = 0.5 and r = 1, W is about 65.4545 years, the harmonic mean of the two values and the EDE health at epsilon of 2." ;
    hemo:9000009 "CES welfare with equal weights and r of 1" ;
    hemo:9000014 "h_a = 60; h_b = 72; alpha = 0.5; r = 1; W = 65.4545" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-SWF-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ces-welfare-with-equal-weights-and-r-of-1" .

hemo:7004230 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "CES welfare at r of minus 1 equals mean health"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With r = -1 and equal weights W is the mean of the two groups, 66 years, the utilitarian case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "CES welfare at r of minus 1 equals mean health" ;
    hemo:9000014 "h_a = 60; h_b = 72; alpha = 0.5; r = -1; W = 66" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-SWF-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ces-welfare-at-r-of-minus-1-equals-mean-health" .

hemo:7004231 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "CES welfare at r of 10 approaches the minimum"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At r = 10 W falls to about 63.3508 years, nearer the 60 years of the less healthy group, as the function moves towards maximin." ;
    hemo:9000009 "CES welfare at r of 10 approaches the minimum" ;
    hemo:9000014 "h_a = 60; h_b = 72; alpha = 0.5; r = 10; W = 63.3508" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-SWF-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ces-welfare-at-r-of-10-approaches-the-minimum" .

hemo:7004232 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Small changes at the current point for the CES marginal rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The MRS is a derivative and holds for small gains at the stated health levels. As a programme narrows the gap the ratio h_b/h_a falls and the weight with it, which is why Asaria and colleagues note that fair innings equity weights need constant updating as the distribution of health changes." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Small changes at the current point for the CES marginal rate" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-SWF-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "small-changes-at-the-current-point-for-the-ces-marginal-rate" .

hemo:7004233 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Fair innings calibration of the CES contour"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "In Williams' calibration, as Asaria and colleagues describe it, alpha is 0.5, r takes a range of values, and the contour is fixed by an EDE of 70 QALYs together with current health. The slopes of its tangents at current health give the equity weights used in cost-effectiveness analysis." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fair innings calibration of the CES contour" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-SWF-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fair-innings-calibration-of-the-ces-contour" .

hemo:7004234 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "CES marginal rate and contour slope from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000684 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the named cells of HE-IM-SWF-004 the first formula returns the MRS in a cell named MarginalRateCES and the second the slope of the social indifference curve." ;
    hemo:9000009 "CES marginal rate and contour slope from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-SWF-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ces-marginal-rate-and-contour-slope-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(GroupWeightA/(1-GroupWeightA))*(HealthGroupB/HealthGroupA)^(1+CurvatureR); =-MarginalRateCES" .

hemo:7004235 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "CES marginal rate matches a numerical derivative of the welfare"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Central differences of 0.001 years in each group's health, applied to the CES welfare of HE-FM-SWF-004, give the ratio of marginal welfare without the closed form, and the result cell agrees to within 0.0001. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the health ratio inverted, which gives about 0.6944 instead of 1.44 for 60 and 72 years at r = 1, or the exponent r used in place of 1 + r, which gives 1.2." ;
    hemo:9000009 "CES marginal rate matches a numerical derivative of the welfare" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-SWF-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ces-marginal-rate-matches-a-numerical-derivative-of-the-welfare" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(MarginalRateCES-((GroupWeightA*(HealthGroupA+0.001)^(-CurvatureR)+(1-GroupWeightA)*HealthGroupB^(-CurvatureR))^(-1/CurvatureR)-(GroupWeightA*(HealthGroupA-0.001)^(-CurvatureR)+(1-GroupWeightA)*HealthGroupB^(-CurvatureR))^(-1/CurvatureR))/((GroupWeightA*HealthGroupA^(-CurvatureR)+(1-GroupWeightA)*(HealthGroupB+0.001)^(-CurvatureR))^(-1/CurvatureR)-(GroupWeightA*HealthGroupA^(-CurvatureR)+(1-GroupWeightA)*(HealthGroupB-0.001)^(-CurvatureR))^(-1/CurvatureR)))<0.0001" .

hemo:7004236 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article and survey values of the CES marginal rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Equal weights with r = 1 at 60 and 72 years give 1.44, and alpha of 0.31 at equal health gives 0.45 to two decimals, as Dolan and Tsuchiya report. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows alpha used as the weight in place of alpha/(1-alpha), which gives 0.31." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article and survey values of the CES marginal rate" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-SWF-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-and-survey-values-of-the-ces-marginal-rate" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ROUND((0.5/(1-0.5))*(72/60)^(1+1),2)=1.44,ROUND((0.31/(1-0.31))*(70/70)^(1+1),2)=0.45)" .

hemo:7004237 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "CES marginal rate with equal weights and r of 1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For illustrative group health of 60 and 72 years with alpha = 0.5 and r = 1, the MRS is 72/60 squared, 1.44, and the contour slope is minus 1.44, the same as the Atkinson relative weight at epsilon of 2 (HE-EX-ATK-009)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "CES marginal rate with equal weights and r of 1" ;
    hemo:9000014 "alpha = 0.5; h_a = 60; h_b = 72; r = 1; MRS = 1.44; slope = -1.44" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-SWF-012" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ces-marginal-rate-with-equal-weights-and-r-of-1" .

hemo:7004238 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "CES marginal rate at equal health with the Dolan and Tsuchiya median weight"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Dolan and Tsuchiya's median respondent was indifferent between saving 100 lives in group a, people who had not cared for their health, and 45 in group b, which gave alpha of 0.31. At equal health, here an illustrative 70 years in each group, the MRS is about 0.4493 for any r, the weight of about half (0.45) that they report." ;
    hemo:9000009 "CES marginal rate at equal health with the Dolan and Tsuchiya median weight" ;
    hemo:9000014 "alpha = 0.31; h_a = 70; h_b = 70; r = 1; MRS = 0.4493; slope = -0.4493" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-SWF-013" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ces-marginal-rate-at-equal-health-with-the-dolan-and-tsuchiya-median-weight" .

hemo:7004239 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "CES marginal rate at r of minus 1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With r = -1 and equal weights the MRS is 1 at any health levels and the contour is a straight line of slope minus 1, the utilitarian case in which a year of health counts the same in either group." ;
    hemo:9000009 "CES marginal rate at r of minus 1" ;
    hemo:9000014 "alpha = 0.5; h_a = 60; h_b = 72; r = -1; MRS = 1; slope = -1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-SWF-014" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ces-marginal-rate-at-r-of-minus-1" .

hemo:7004240 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Standard GBD constants for the age-weight function"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The constants are fixed conventions, beta = 0.04 and C = 0.1658 in the standard DALY parameters of the WHO environmental burden of disease guide. Larson reports that, with beta at 0.04, C was chosen so that global DALYs were the same with and without age weighting in the 1990 study, so C is a calibration to that study and not a measured preference. Murray's 1994 paper used 0.16243." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Standard GBD constants for the age-weight function" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AGW-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "standard-gbd-constants-for-the-age-weight-function" .

hemo:7004241 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Age weight as a continuous function of exact age"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Age x is continuous and measured in years from birth, so the weight on a whole year of life is the integral of W over that year, not W at the start of it. Murray chose a continuous function over a set of discrete weights for convenience, and the peak formula uses the derivative, which needs beta above zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Age weight as a continuous function of exact age" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AGW-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "age-weight-as-a-continuous-function-of-exact-age" .

hemo:7004242 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "GBD age weight and peak from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000685 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the constants in AgeWtConstC and AgeWtBeta and the age in AgeYears, the first formula returns the weight (a cell named AgeWeight), the second the peak age (PeakAge) and the third the peak weight (PeakWeight)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "GBD age weight and peak from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AGW-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "gbd-age-weight-and-peak-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AgeWtConstC*AgeYears*EXP(-AgeWtBeta*AgeYears); =1/AgeWtBeta; =AgeWtConstC/AgeWtBeta*EXP(-1)" .

hemo:7004243 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's GBD age weights at 25 and 70 reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With C = 0.1658 and beta = 0.04 the weights at 25 and 70 are 1.525 and 0.706 to three decimal places, as in the article. Expected result TRUE. Entering Murray's 0.16243 for C gives 1.494 and 0.691 and makes it FALSE, as does a positive exponent." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's GBD age weights at 25 and 70 reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AGW-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-gbd-age-weights-at-25-and-70-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(0.1658*25*EXP(-0.04*25)-1.525)<0.0005,ABS(0.1658*70*EXP(-0.04*70)-0.706)<0.0005)" .

hemo:7004244 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "GBD age weight at the computed peak age equals the peak weight"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The weight evaluated at the PeakAge cell equals the PeakWeight cell, which holds only when the peak age is 1/beta, because C x e to the minus beta x reaches C/(beta e) at that age alone. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a peak age entered as beta times 100, as 1/beta squared, or a peak weight without the factor e to the minus 1." ;
    hemo:9000009 "GBD age weight at the computed peak age equals the peak weight" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AGW-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "gbd-age-weight-at-the-computed-peak-age-equals-the-peak-weight" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(PeakWeight-AgeWtConstC*PeakAge*EXP(-AgeWtBeta*PeakAge))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004245 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "GBD age weight at age 25, the peak"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the standard constants a year lived at 25 carries a weight of about 1.525, 0.1658 times 25 times e to the minus 1, as in the article. Here x equals the peak age, so W equals W_peak." ;
    hemo:9000009 "GBD age weight at age 25, the peak" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = 0.1658; beta = 0.04; x = 25; W = 1.5249; x_peak = 25; W_peak = 1.5249" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AGW-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "gbd-age-weight-at-age-25-the-peak" .

hemo:7004246 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "GBD age weight at age 70"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A year lived at 70 carries a weight of about 0.706, less than half the weight at 25, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "GBD age weight at age 70" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = 0.1658; beta = 0.04; x = 70; W = 0.7058; x_peak = 25; W_peak = 1.5249" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AGW-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "gbd-age-weight-at-age-70" .

hemo:7004247 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "GBD age weight at age 5"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A year lived at 5 carries a weight of about 0.679, the figure the article uses for a death at 5." ;
    hemo:9000009 "GBD age weight at age 5" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = 0.1658; beta = 0.04; x = 5; W = 0.6787; x_peak = 25; W_peak = 1.5249" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AGW-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "gbd-age-weight-at-age-5" .

hemo:7004248 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "GBD age weight at birth"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At birth the weight is zero whatever the constants, a limiting case that checks an implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "GBD age weight at birth" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = 0.1658; beta = 0.04; x = 0; W = 0; x_peak = 25; W_peak = 1.5249" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AGW-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "gbd-age-weight-at-birth" .

hemo:7004249 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Age weight at age 25 with Murray's 1994 constant"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With C = 0.16243, the value in Murray's 1994 paper, the weight at 25 is about 1.494, some 2% below the standard 1.525 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Age weight at age 25 with Murray's 1994 constant" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = 0.16243; beta = 0.04; x = 25; W = 1.4939; x_peak = 25; W_peak = 1.4939" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AGW-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "age-weight-at-age-25-with-murray-s-1994-constant" .

hemo:7004250 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Undiscounted age-weighted years over a single span of standard years"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The death removes the years from a to a + L in full and each is weighted by its own age, with no discounting. L comes from the standard used, the GBD standard life table in burden estimates or, in the article's worked example, the illustrative years up to age 80." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Undiscounted age-weighted years over a single span of standard years" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AGW-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "undiscounted-age-weighted-years-over-a-single-span-of-standard-years" .

hemo:7004251 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Modulation constant fixed within one set of estimates"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "K is at least 0 and at most 1 and is the same for every death compared. Values other than 0 and 1 give a weighted mixture of the age-weighted and uniform totals, used in sensitivity analysis." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Modulation constant fixed within one set of estimates" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AGW-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "modulation-constant-fixed-within-one-set-of-estimates" .

hemo:7004252 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Undiscounted age-weighted years lost per death from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000686 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the constants in AgeWtModulation, AgeWtConstC and AgeWtBeta, the age at death in AgeAtDeath and the years lost in YearsLost, the cell (named YLLUndisc) returns the age-weighted years lost." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Undiscounted age-weighted years lost per death from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AGW-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "undiscounted-age-weighted-years-lost-per-death-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AgeWtModulation*AgeWtConstC/AgeWtBeta^2*((1+AgeWtBeta*AgeAtDeath)*EXP(-AgeWtBeta*AgeAtDeath)-(1+AgeWtBeta*(AgeAtDeath+YearsLost))*EXP(-AgeWtBeta*(AgeAtDeath+YearsLost)))+(1-AgeWtModulation)*YearsLost" .

hemo:7004253 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's undiscounted age-weighted years lost reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With C/beta squared of 103.625, deaths at 5 and 40 with 75 and 40 years lost give 84.07 and 36.66, as in the article. Expected result TRUE. Dividing C by beta instead of beta squared gives 3.36 and 1.47 and makes it FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's undiscounted age-weighted years lost reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AGW-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-undiscounted-age-weighted-years-lost-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(0.1658/0.04^2*((1+0.04*5)*EXP(-0.04*5)-(1+0.04*80)*EXP(-0.04*80))-84.07)<0.005,ABS(0.1658/0.04^2*((1+0.04*40)*EXP(-0.04*40)-(1+0.04*80)*EXP(-0.04*80))-36.66)<0.005)" .

hemo:7004254 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Undiscounted age-weighted years lost cell agrees with the article for a death at 5"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With AgeWtModulation 1, AgeWtConstC 0.1658, AgeWtBeta 0.04, AgeAtDeath 5 and YearsLost 75, the YLLUndisc cell equals 84.068, the article's 84.07 to three decimals, which the numerical integral of HE-CF-AGW-002 also returns. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows an error in the cell, such as the weight at the age of death times the years lost, which gives 50.90." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Undiscounted age-weighted years lost cell agrees with the article for a death at 5" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AGW-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "undiscounted-age-weighted-years-lost-cell-agrees-with-the-article-for-a-death-at-5" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(YLLUndisc-84.068)<0.001" .

hemo:7004255 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Undiscounted age-weighted years lost for a death at 5"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A death at 5 that removes the 75 years to an illustrative age of 80 loses about 84.07 age-weighted years, 103.625 times (0.98248 minus 0.17120), against 75 unweighted, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Undiscounted age-weighted years lost for a death at 5" ;
    hemo:9000014 "K = 1; C = 0.1658; beta = 0.04; a = 5; L = 75; YLL_aw = 84.0684" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AGW-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "undiscounted-age-weighted-years-lost-for-a-death-at-5" .

hemo:7004256 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Undiscounted age-weighted years lost for a death at 40"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A death at 40 that removes 40 years loses about 36.66 age-weighted years against 40 unweighted, because all its lost years fall after the peak, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Undiscounted age-weighted years lost for a death at 40" ;
    hemo:9000014 "K = 1; C = 0.1658; beta = 0.04; a = 40; L = 40; YLL_aw = 36.6552" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AGW-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "undiscounted-age-weighted-years-lost-for-a-death-at-40" .

hemo:7004257 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Undiscounted years lost for a death at 5 without age weights"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With K = 0 the result is the 75 standard years, a limiting case that returns HE-FM-YLL-001 for one death." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Undiscounted years lost for a death at 5 without age weights" ;
    hemo:9000014 "K = 0; C = 0.1658; beta = 0.04; a = 5; L = 75; YLL_aw = 75" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AGW-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "undiscounted-years-lost-for-a-death-at-5-without-age-weights" .

hemo:7004258 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Continuous discounting from the time of death at one rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each lost year at age x is discounted by e to the minus r (x minus a), counting from the death, at a constant continuous rate above zero. The formula divides by r, so the undiscounted case is computed with HE-FM-AGW-002, not by entering r = 0. The guide's rate of 0.03 is used directly as the continuous rate, as in HE-FM-YLL-003." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Continuous discounting from the time of death at one rate" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AGW-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "continuous-discounting-from-the-time-of-death-at-one-rate" .

hemo:7004259 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Age-weighted discounted DALYs as a historical convention"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The standard DALYs of the original GBD study and of subsequent WHO updates used 3% discounting and age weights for YLLs and YLDs. GBD 2010 dropped age weights, judging them hard to justify once the DALY was defined as a measure of health loss, and WHO adopted a 0% rate and no age weighting after consultations in 2012, so results from this formula are for comparison with those earlier estimates." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Age-weighted discounted DALYs as a historical convention" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AGW-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "age-weighted-discounted-dalys-as-a-historical-convention" .

hemo:7004260 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Age-weighted discounted years lost per death from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000687 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With AgeWtModulation, AgeWtConstC, AgeWtBeta, the continuous rate in DiscRate, the age at death in AgeAtDeath and the standard years in YearsLost, the cell (named YLLAgeWtDisc) returns the years lost for one death." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Age-weighted discounted years lost per death from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AGW-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "age-weighted-discounted-years-lost-per-death-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AgeWtModulation*AgeWtConstC*EXP(DiscRate*AgeAtDeath)/(DiscRate+AgeWtBeta)^2*(EXP(-(DiscRate+AgeWtBeta)*(YearsLost+AgeAtDeath))*(-(DiscRate+AgeWtBeta)*(YearsLost+AgeAtDeath)-1)-EXP(-(DiscRate+AgeWtBeta)*AgeAtDeath)*(-(DiscRate+AgeWtBeta)*AgeAtDeath-1))+(1-AgeWtModulation)/DiscRate*(1-EXP(-DiscRate*YearsLost))" .

hemo:7004261 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's age-weighted discounted years lost reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With K = 1 and r = 0.03, deaths at 5 and 40 with 75 and 40 years lost give 36.44 and 23.22, as in the article's table and as the numerical integral of HE-CF-AGW-002 returns. Expected result TRUE. Leaving out the factor e to the ra gives 31.36 for the death at 5 and makes it FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's age-weighted discounted years lost reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AGW-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-age-weighted-discounted-years-lost-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(0.1658*EXP(0.03*5)/0.07^2*(EXP(-0.07*80)*(-0.07*80-1)-EXP(-0.07*5)*(-0.07*5-1))-36.44)<0.005,ABS(0.1658*EXP(0.03*40)/0.07^2*(EXP(-0.07*80)*(-0.07*80-1)-EXP(-0.07*40)*(-0.07*40-1))-23.22)<0.005)" .

hemo:7004262 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Age-weighted discounted years lost approach the undiscounted value as the rate shrinks"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With r = 0.000001, K = 1 and a death at 5 with 75 years lost, the closed form is within 0.01 of the 84.068 of HE-FM-AGW-002, an independent formula. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the bracket terms in reverse order, which gives minus 84.07, or a squared term missing from the denominator." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Age-weighted discounted years lost approach the undiscounted value as the rate shrinks" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AGW-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "age-weighted-discounted-years-lost-approach-the-undiscounted-value-as-the-rate-shrinks" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(0.1658*EXP(0.000001*5)/(0.040001)^2*(EXP(-0.040001*80)*(-0.040001*80-1)-EXP(-0.040001*5)*(-0.040001*5-1))-84.068)<0.01" .

hemo:7004263 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Age-weighted discounted cell with K of zero equals the live discounted years lost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With AgeWtModulation set to 0 the YLLAgeWtDisc cell equals (1 minus e to the minus rL)/r of HE-FM-YLL-003 for the same rate and years. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows AgeWtModulation not set to 0, or a second term that does not discount, such as (1 minus K) times L." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Age-weighted discounted cell with K of zero equals the live discounted years lost" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AGW-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "age-weighted-discounted-cell-with-k-of-zero-equals-the-live-discounted-years-lost" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(AgeWtModulation=0,ABS(YLLAgeWtDisc-(1-EXP(-DiscRate*YearsLost))/DiscRate)<1E-9)" .

hemo:7004264 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Age-weighted discounted years lost for a death at 5"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A death at 5 with 75 years lost to an illustrative age of 80 counts about 36.44 years with age weights and 3% discounting, as in the article's table, against 29.82 with discounting alone." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Age-weighted discounted years lost for a death at 5" ;
    hemo:9000014 "K = 1; C = 0.1658; r = 0.03; beta = 0.04; a = 5; L = 75; YLL_awd = 36.4398" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AGW-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "age-weighted-discounted-years-lost-for-a-death-at-5" .

hemo:7004265 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Age-weighted discounted years lost for a death at 40"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A death at 40 with 40 years lost counts about 23.22 years, slightly below the 23.29 with discounting alone, as in the article's table." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Age-weighted discounted years lost for a death at 40" ;
    hemo:9000014 "K = 1; C = 0.1658; r = 0.03; beta = 0.04; a = 40; L = 40; YLL_awd = 23.218" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AGW-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "age-weighted-discounted-years-lost-for-a-death-at-40" .

hemo:7004266 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Discounted years lost for a death at 5 without age weights"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With K = 0 a death at 5 counts about 29.82 years, the value of HE-FM-YLL-003 for 75 years at 3%, a limiting case that checks the second term." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Discounted years lost for a death at 5 without age weights" ;
    hemo:9000014 "K = 0; C = 0.1658; r = 0.03; beta = 0.04; a = 5; L = 75; YLL_awd = 29.82" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AGW-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "discounted-years-lost-for-a-death-at-5-without-age-weights" .

hemo:7004267 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant disability weight over the case and discounting from onset"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The weight is the same throughout the case, and each year is age-weighted at its own age and discounted from the age of onset. WHO's 2026 methods document (section 2.3, HE-SR-AGW-008) notes that in the original DALY method the start time for discounting YLDs was the year of incidence, whereas for YLLs it was the year of death." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant disability weight over the case and discounting from onset" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AGW-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-disability-weight-over-the-case-and-discounting-from-onset" .

hemo:7004268 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Incidence perspective for age-weighted years lived with disability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "All of a case's future health loss is assigned to its year and age of onset, the GBD 1990 incidence perspective of HE-FM-YLD-001. Prevalence-based YLDs from GBD 2010 onwards carry no age weights or discounting (HE-FM-DWT-001)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Incidence perspective for age-weighted years lived with disability" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AGW-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "incidence-perspective-for-age-weighted-years-lived-with-disability" .

hemo:7004269 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Age-weighted discounted years lived with disability per case from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000688 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With DisabilityWt, AgeWtModulation, AgeWtConstC, AgeWtBeta, DiscRate, the age at onset in OnsetAge and the duration in Duration, the cell (named YLDAgeWtDisc) returns the years lived with disability for one case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Age-weighted discounted years lived with disability per case from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AGW-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "age-weighted-discounted-years-lived-with-disability-per-case-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=DisabilityWt*(AgeWtModulation*AgeWtConstC*EXP(DiscRate*OnsetAge)/(DiscRate+AgeWtBeta)^2*(EXP(-(DiscRate+AgeWtBeta)*(Duration+OnsetAge))*(-(DiscRate+AgeWtBeta)*(Duration+OnsetAge)-1)-EXP(-(DiscRate+AgeWtBeta)*OnsetAge)*(-(DiscRate+AgeWtBeta)*OnsetAge-1))+(1-AgeWtModulation)/DiscRate*(1-EXP(-DiscRate*Duration)))" .

hemo:7004270 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Age-weighted years lived with disability cell with K of zero matches Larson's value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With DisabilityWt 0.6, AgeWtModulation 0, DiscRate 0.03, OnsetAge 35 and Duration 10, the YLDAgeWtDisc cell equals Larson's 5.1836. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows an undiscounted second term, which gives 6, or the disability weight left out, which gives 8.6394." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Age-weighted years lived with disability cell with K of zero matches Larson's value" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AGW-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "age-weighted-years-lived-with-disability-cell-with-k-of-zero-matches-larson-s-value" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(AgeWtModulation=0,ABS(YLDAgeWtDisc-5.1836)<0.0001)" .

hemo:7004271 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Age-weighted years lived with disability for the case with onset at 35 reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With full age weights the case counts 6.9548 years, the value the numerical integral in HE-CF-AGW-002 also returns. Expected result TRUE. Entering the age at the end of the case, 45, as the duration gives about 15.86 and makes it FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Age-weighted years lived with disability for the case with onset at 35 reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AGW-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "age-weighted-years-lived-with-disability-for-the-case-with-onset-at-35-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(0.6*0.1658*EXP(0.03*35)/0.07^2*(EXP(-0.07*45)*(-0.07*45-1)-EXP(-0.07*35)*(-0.07*35-1))-6.9548)<0.0005" .

hemo:7004272 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Age-weighted discounted years lived with disability for a case with onset at 35"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An illustrative case with onset at 35, lasting 10 years at a weight of 0.6, counts about 6.955 years with full age weights and 3% discounting, 0.6 times 11.5913 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Age-weighted discounted years lived with disability for a case with onset at 35" ;
    hemo:9000014 "DW = 0.6; K = 1; C = 0.1658; r = 0.03; beta = 0.04; a = 35; L = 10; YLD_awd = 6.9548" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AGW-012" ;
    hemo:9000023 "age-weighted-discounted-years-lived-with-disability-for-a-case-with-onset-at-35" .

hemo:7004273 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Discounted years lived with disability for the same case without age weights"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With K = 0 the case counts 5.1836 years, Larson's discounted YLD for this case and the value of HE-FM-YLD-002, a limiting case that checks the second term." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Discounted years lived with disability for the same case without age weights" ;
    hemo:9000014 "DW = 0.6; K = 0; C = 0.1658; r = 0.03; beta = 0.04; a = 35; L = 10; YLD_awd = 5.1836" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AGW-013" ;
    hemo:9000023 "discounted-years-lived-with-disability-for-the-same-case-without-age-weights" .

hemo:7004274 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Normal interim posterior with known variance and outcomes observed at once"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each pair's incremental net monetary benefit is normal with known variance and the prior is normal, so the interim posterior is normal with mean mu_t and effective sample size n_t (equations 2 and 3 of Chick and colleagues), and the posterior mean after m more pairs is normal about mu_t with variance sigma_X squared over n_t times m over n_t plus m (their equation 6). Outcomes are taken to arrive at once and rewards are not discounted. With a delay, stopping still yields the outcomes of the pipeline patients, so the value of stopping in their equation 7 is itself an expectation over those outcomes, a case this formula does not cover." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Normal interim posterior with known variance and outcomes observed at once" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AVOI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "normal-interim-posterior-with-known-variance-and-outcomes-observed-at-once" .

hemo:7004275 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Online and offline learning in the interim reward"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "With d_on of 1 the reward of each trial pair, its incremental net benefit, counts in addition to the P patients treated after the decision, which Chick and colleagues call online learning. With d_on of 0 the outcomes of participants do not enter the reward, which they note traditional trials assume implicitly. The participants' opportunity loss of HE-FM-ENBS-002, applied to the remaining pairs in HE-CF-AVOI-001, values the same participants another way, charging each pair the net benefit forgone by the participant on the arm expected to be inferior, so an analysis uses one convention or the other and not both." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Online and offline learning in the interim reward" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AVOI-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "online-and-offline-learning-in-the-interim-reward" .

hemo:7004276 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One-stage look-ahead as a check on the optimal sequential rule"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The comparison values plans that take m more pairs and then stop, the KG* approach in which Chick and colleagues continue only if some feasible one-stage plan has a greater expected reward than stopping, set out in their section 4 for unknown sampling variance and applied here with the variance known. The optimal sequential policy is the best of all policies, which include every one-stage plan, so a positive Delta for some m means that the optimal policy also continues, while a Delta of zero or below for every m does not prove that stopping is optimal. Chick and colleagues note that the optimal policy may have ties and that these can be broken by sampling more, at no loss of expected reward." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One-stage look-ahead as a check on the optimal sequential rule" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AVOI-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-stage-look-ahead-as-a-check-on-the-optimal-sequential-rule" .

hemo:7004277 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "One-stage interim gain of continuing in Excel with named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000689 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs in cells named InterimMeanINB, SwitchCost, PopBenefiting, SamplingSDPair, RemainingPairs, InterimESS, CostPerPair and OnlineFlag, the first two formulas go in cells named GapINB and PrepostSDRemaining, and the third, in a cell named ContinueGainKG, returns Delta. NORM.S.DIST with FALSE gives the density and with TRUE the cumulative probability." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One-stage interim gain of continuing in Excel with named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AVOI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-stage-interim-gain-of-continuing-in-excel-with-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=InterimMeanINB-SwitchCost/PopBenefiting; =SamplingSDPair*SQRT(RemainingPairs/(InterimESS*(InterimESS+RemainingPairs))); =PopBenefiting*(PrepostSDRemaining*NORM.S.DIST(ABS(GapINB)/PrepostSDRemaining,FALSE)-ABS(GapINB)*(1-NORM.S.DIST(ABS(GapINB)/PrepostSDRemaining,TRUE)))-RemainingPairs*CostPerPair+OnlineFlag*RemainingPairs*InterimMeanINB" .

hemo:7004278 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Interim one-stage gain agrees with the signed unit normal loss form"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The result cell ContinueGainKG equals P times the expected positive part of the updated mean net of the threshold, written in the signed form a times Phi(a/s_m) plus s_m times phi(a/s_m), less P times max(a, 0), less the sampling cost, plus the online term. Expected result TRUE for either sign of a. FALSE shows the absolute value omitted from z, which at a posterior mean of minus GBP 1,500 credits the remaining pairs with about GBP 1,501 per patient instead of GBP 1.14." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Interim one-stage gain agrees with the signed unit normal loss form" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AVOI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "interim-one-stage-gain-agrees-with-the-signed-unit-normal-loss-form" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ContinueGainKG-(PopBenefiting*(GapINB*NORM.S.DIST(GapINB/PrepostSDRemaining,TRUE)+PrepostSDRemaining*NORM.S.DIST(GapINB/PrepostSDRemaining,FALSE))-PopBenefiting*MAX(GapINB,0)-RemainingPairs*CostPerPair+OnlineFlag*RemainingPairs*InterimMeanINB))<0.01" .

hemo:7004279 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's interim gain of GBP 2.62 million reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's inputs entered (posterior mean GBP 200, n_t of 125, 100 remaining pairs, sigma_X of GBP 10,000, 20,000 patients, GBP 4,000 a pair, no switching cost and offline learning), ContinueGainKG rounds to GBP 2,622,800 at the nearest hundred. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the prior effective sample size of 25 entered as InterimESS, which gives about GBP 11.96 million." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's interim gain of GBP 2.62 million reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AVOI-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-interim-gain-of-gbp-2-62-million-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND(ContinueGainKG,-2)=2622800" .

hemo:7004280 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Value of the remaining pairs not negative at an interim look"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Adding back the sampling cost and taking away the online term leaves P times EVSI_I, which cannot be negative. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the cumulative probability Phi(z) used where the upper tail belongs, which at a posterior mean of minus GBP 1,500 gives about minus GBP 1,481 per patient." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Value of the remaining pairs not negative at an interim look" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AVOI-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "value-of-the-remaining-pairs-not-negative-at-an-interim-look" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ContinueGainKG+RemainingPairs*CostPerPair-OnlineFlag*RemainingPairs*InterimMeanINB>=0" .

hemo:7004281 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's interim look with a posterior mean of GBP 200"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative interim look after 100 of 200 pairs has n_t of 125 pairs, sigma_X of GBP 10,000, 20,000 patients informed, GBP 4,000 a pair, no switching cost and offline learning. The remaining 100 pairs are worth about GBP 151.14 per patient, about GBP 3.02 million in all, and continuing beats stopping by about GBP 2.62 million, the article's figure." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's interim look with a posterior mean of GBP 200" ;
    hemo:9000014 "mu_t = 200; I = 0; P = 20000; sigma_X = 10000; m = 100; n_t = 125; c = 4000; d_on = 0; Q_z = 0.3686578386; a = 200; s_m = 596.284794; z = 0.33541; phi_z = 0.377121; EVSI_I = 151.14; Delta = 2622802" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AVOI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-interim-look-with-a-posterior-mean-of-gbp-200" .

hemo:7004282 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's interim look with a posterior mean of minus GBP 1,500"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the same illustrative inputs and the interim posterior mean fallen to minus GBP 1,500, the remaining pairs are worth about GBP 1.14 per patient, about GBP 22,772 in all, and the one-stage gain is about minus GBP 377,228, so this plan does not beat stopping, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's interim look with a posterior mean of minus GBP 1,500" ;
    hemo:9000014 "mu_t = -1500; I = 0; P = 20000; sigma_X = 10000; m = 100; n_t = 125; c = 4000; d_on = 0; Q_z = 0.0059418946; a = -1500; s_m = 596.284794; z = 2.515576; phi_z = 0.016857; EVSI_I = 1.14; Delta = -377228" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AVOI-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-interim-look-with-a-posterior-mean-of-minus-gbp-1-500" .

hemo:7004283 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Interim one-stage gain with an illustrative switching cost of GBP 2 million"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An illustrative switching cost of GBP 2 million, GBP 100 for each of the 20,000 patients informed, moves the adoption threshold to GBP 100, so a posterior mean of GBP 200 lies closer to it. The remaining pairs are then worth about GBP 191.22 per patient and the gain of continuing rises to about GBP 3.42 million (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Interim one-stage gain with an illustrative switching cost of GBP 2 million" ;
    hemo:9000014 "mu_t = 200; I = 2000000; P = 20000; sigma_X = 10000; m = 100; n_t = 125; c = 4000; d_on = 0; Q_z = 0.4334076426; a = 100; s_m = 596.284794; z = 0.167705; phi_z = 0.393371; EVSI_I = 191.22; Delta = 3424413" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AVOI-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "interim-one-stage-gain-with-an-illustrative-switching-cost-of-gbp-2-million" .

hemo:7004284 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Interim one-stage gain with online learning at a posterior mean of GBP 200"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Counting the expected rewards of the 100 further pairs under online learning adds 100 times GBP 200, GBP 20,000, to the article's interim case, giving about GBP 2.64 million (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Interim one-stage gain with online learning at a posterior mean of GBP 200" ;
    hemo:9000014 "mu_t = 200; I = 0; P = 20000; sigma_X = 10000; m = 100; n_t = 125; c = 4000; d_on = 1; Q_z = 0.3686578386; a = 200; s_m = 596.284794; z = 0.33541; phi_z = 0.377121; EVSI_I = 151.14; Delta = 2642802" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AVOI-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "interim-one-stage-gain-with-online-learning-at-a-posterior-mean-of-gbp-200" .

hemo:7004285 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Independent transmission across the contacts of one agent in a step"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each contact transmits independently of the others and with the same probability p, the condition of the discrete-time Reed-Frost chain binomial model described by Britton. Where contacts carry different risks, for example a household contact and a casual one, the same independence condition multiplies the separate escape probabilities, and a single p no longer applies." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Independent transmission across the contacts of one agent in a step" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ABM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "independent-transmission-across-the-contacts-of-one-agent-in-a-step" .

hemo:7004286 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Per-contact probability defined for the model step length"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "p refers to one contact within one model step. When the source reports a transmission rate per unit of contact time, the probability is formed with the constant-rate conversion HE-FM-TP-001; Britton writes the Reed-Frost contact probability in this way, as one minus the exponential of minus the contact rate to a specific individual multiplied by the infectious period. A probability measured over a different interval is rescaled with HE-FM-TP-003 before it enters the formula." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Per-contact probability defined for the model step length" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ABM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "per-contact-probability-defined-for-the-model-step-length" .

hemo:7004287 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Agent susceptible at the start of the infection step"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The formula applies to an agent who is susceptible at the start of the step. Infected agents follow their own rules in the same step, for example progression through a Markov chain of disease stages within each infected agent as in the case study of Chhatwal and He." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Agent susceptible at the start of the infection step" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ABM-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "agent-susceptible-at-the-start-of-the-infection-step" .

hemo:7004288 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Agent infection probability from named cells in Excel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000690 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the per-contact probability in a cell named ContactTransProb and the number of infectious contacts in InfectiousContacts, the formula returns the per-step probability of infection. The tests below name the result cell InfectionProbStep." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Agent infection probability from named cells in Excel" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ABM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "agent-infection-probability-from-named-cells-in-excel" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1-(1-ContactTransProb)^InfectiousContacts" .

hemo:7004289 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Agent infection probability within its single-contact and summed-risk bounds"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For one or more contacts the value in InfectionProbStep is at least the per-contact probability and at most the smaller of 1 and InfectiousContacts times ContactTransProb. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the escape probability reported instead of its complement, 0.729 for the article's inputs, or the per-contact probability raised to the power k and subtracted from 1, 0.999." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Agent infection probability within its single-contact and summed-risk bounds" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ABM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "agent-infection-probability-within-its-single-contact-and-summed-risk-bounds" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(InfectiousContacts>=1,InfectionProbStep>=ContactTransProb,InfectionProbStep<=MIN(1,InfectiousContacts*ContactTransProb))" .

hemo:7004290 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's daily infection probability of 0.271 in the result cell"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With ContactTransProb set to 0.10 and InfectiousContacts to 3, the result cell rounds to 0.271. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the result cell holding the sum of the contact risks, 0.3, or the per-contact probability entered as a percentage." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's daily infection probability of 0.271 in the result cell" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ABM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-daily-infection-probability-of-0-271-in-the-result-cell" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND(InfectionProbStep,3)=0.271" .

hemo:7004291 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's three contacts at a 0.10 transmission probability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative example a susceptible agent has three contacts with infectious agents in a day, each transmitting with probability 0.10. The chance of escaping all three is 0.9 cubed, or 0.729, so the daily probability of infection is 0.271." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's three contacts at a 0.10 transmission probability" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p = 0.10; k = 3; P_inf = 0.271" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ABM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-three-contacts-at-a-0-10-transmission-probability" .

hemo:7004292 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's programme cutting contacts to two a day"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative programme cuts the contacts to two a day, so the probability of infection becomes 1 minus 0.81, or 0.19. Removing one contact in three lowers the daily risk by 0.081, about 30% of 0.271, slightly less than the one third that the sum k times p would suggest." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's programme cutting contacts to two a day" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p = 0.10; k = 2; P_inf = 0.19" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ABM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-programme-cutting-contacts-to-two-a-day" .

hemo:7004293 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Single infectious contact as a limiting case of the agent infection probability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With one infectious contact the formula returns the per-contact probability itself, here an illustrative 0.10, which checks that P_inf and p are on the same per-step basis." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Single infectious contact as a limiting case of the agent infection probability" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p = 0.10; k = 1; P_inf = 0.1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ABM-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "single-infectious-contact-as-a-limiting-case-of-the-agent-infection-probability" .

hemo:7004294 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Ten contacts at the case study's per-contact probability of 0.005"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Chhatwal and He's case study uses a transmission probability per contact of 0.005. For an illustrative agent with 10 infectious contacts in one annual cycle the probability of infection is 1 minus 0.995 to the power 10, about 0.0489, close to the sum of 0.05 because each contact carries a small risk." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ten contacts at the case study's per-contact probability of 0.005" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p = 0.005; k = 10; P_inf = 0.0489" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ABM-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ten-contacts-at-the-case-study-s-per-contact-probability-of-0-005" .

hemo:7004295 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Strict inequality in the agent uniform draw comparison"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The agent is infected when U is strictly below P_inf. A tie has probability zero for a continuous uniform draw, and because Excel RAND returns values of at least 0 and below 1, the strict form never infects an agent whose P_inf is zero and always infects one whose P_inf is 1." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Strict inequality in the agent uniform draw comparison" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ABM-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "strict-inequality-in-the-agent-uniform-draw-comparison" .

hemo:7004296 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Synchronous update of all agents from start-of-step states"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every agent's P_inf is computed from the states at the start of the step, and all agents then change state together. Chhatwal and He state that agents update their characteristics in a certain order within each cycle and that the sequence of events within a cycle is important, so a model that updates agents one at a time states that order, because it changes who is infectious later in the same step." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Synchronous update of all agents from start-of-step states" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ABM-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "synchronous-update-of-all-agents-from-start-of-step-states" .

hemo:7004297 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Agent's own random stream kept across strategies"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each agent draws its random numbers from its own stream, set by a seed per agent, so the agent's draws do not change between strategies unless its own exposure changes. Chhatwal and He describe this per-agent seed for common random numbers, and Krijkamp and colleagues describe setting a seed number per individual in a microsimulation so that individuals are as similar as possible across intervention scenarios." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Agent's own random stream kept across strategies" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ABM-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "agent-s-own-random-stream-kept-across-strategies" .

hemo:7004298 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Agent infection update from a frozen uniform draw in Excel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000691 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the agent's draw in a cell named AgentUniform, generated by RAND and then pasted as a value so that it does not change on recalculation, and the inputs named as in HE-IM-ABM-001, the formula returns 1 if the agent is infected in the step and 0 otherwise. Using the same AgentUniform column in each strategy's sheet gives common random numbers." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Agent infection update from a frozen uniform draw in Excel" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ABM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "agent-infection-update-from-a-frozen-uniform-draw-in-excel" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(AgentUniform<1-(1-ContactTransProb)^InfectiousContacts,1,0)" .

hemo:7004299 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No agent infected under fewer contacts but not under more"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With one column of frozen draws shared by two strategies, a column named StateFewer holding each agent's update with two contacts and StateMore its update with three, no agent is infected with fewer contacts and not with more. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows that the two strategies drew fresh RAND values instead of sharing each agent's draw, which for the article's inputs happens to about 14% of agents." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No agent infected under fewer contacts but not under more" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ABM-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-agent-infected-under-fewer-contacts-but-not-under-more" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(--(StateFewer>StateMore))=0" .

hemo:7004300 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Share infected in a column of agent updates near the infection probability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For a column named AgentStates of updates from independent draws, all with the same p and k, the share infected lies within three binomial standard errors (HE-FM-BINOM-004) of the value in InfectionProbStep, which a correct column meets in about 997 of 1,000 sets of draws. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the comparison reversed, U above P_inf, which gives a share near 0.729 for the article's inputs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Share infected in a column of agent updates near the infection probability" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ABM-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "share-infected-in-a-column-of-agent-updates-near-the-infection-probability" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(AVERAGE(AgentStates)-InfectionProbStep)<=3*SQRT(InfectionProbStep*(1-InfectionProbStep)/COUNT(AgentStates))" .

hemo:7004301 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Agent with a draw of 0.25 infected with three contacts"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For an illustrative agent whose uniform draw in the step is 0.25, three contacts at 0.10 give P_inf of 0.271, so the draw is below P_inf and the agent is infected." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Agent with a draw of 0.25 infected with three contacts" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p = 0.10; k = 3; U = 0.25; P_inf = 0.271; X_next = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ABM-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "agent-with-a-draw-of-0-25-infected-with-three-contacts" .

hemo:7004302 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Same agent and draw escaping infection with two contacts"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the same illustrative draw of 0.25 kept by the agent's own stream, the programme's two contacts give P_inf of 0.19, which is below the draw, so the agent stays susceptible. Under common random numbers the programme averts this agent's infection, and every agent whose draw is at least 0.19 and below 0.271 is averted in the same way, a share of 0.081." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same agent and draw escaping infection with two contacts" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p = 0.10; k = 2; U = 0.25; P_inf = 0.19; X_next = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ABM-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-agent-and-draw-escaping-infection-with-two-contacts" .

hemo:7004303 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Agent with no infectious contacts never infected in the step"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With k equal to zero P_inf is zero, so no draw, even an illustrative 0.001, can infect the agent, a limiting case of the rule." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Agent with no infectious contacts never infected in the step" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p = 0.10; k = 0; U = 0.001; P_inf = 0; X_next = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ABM-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "agent-with-no-infectious-contacts-never-infected-in-the-step" .

hemo:7004304 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Independent runs with separate random number seeds"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each run uses its own random numbers, so the run results are independent. Morris and colleagues advise setting the random number seed once per repetition and using separate streams of random numbers when chunks of the simulation run in parallel. Agents interact within a run, so the run, not the agent, is the unit of replication." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Independent runs with separate random number seeds" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ABM-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "independent-runs-with-separate-random-number-seeds" .

hemo:7004305 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Pilot standard deviation taken as a conservative estimate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "s_run comes from an initial small set of runs and is taken on the high side. Morris and colleagues base their count on a conservative estimate from an initial small simulation run and state that it is revised if the standard deviation turns out larger. The count fixes the standard error only. An interval built from that standard error also treats the mean over runs as close to normal, which needs a reasonably large number of runs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pilot standard deviation taken as a conservative estimate" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ABM-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pilot-standard-deviation-taken-as-a-conservative-estimate" .

hemo:7004306 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Paired runs with common random numbers for an incremental outcome"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "For an incremental cost or QALY, s_run is the standard deviation of the paired differences between strategies within each run, computed with the same random numbers in both strategies (HE-AS-ABM-006). The spread of each strategy's own total ignores the positive correlation that common random numbers create and gives a larger count than needed." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Paired runs with common random numbers for an incremental outcome" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ABM-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "paired-runs-with-common-random-numbers-for-an-incremental-outcome" .

hemo:7004307 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Runs needed for a target Monte Carlo standard error in Excel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000692 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the pilot standard deviation in a cell named RunSD, computed with STDEV.S over the pilot run results, and the target in TargetMCSE, CEILING rounds the result up to a whole number of runs. ROUND to six decimals first stops floating-point residue, such as 1600.0000000000002, being rounded up to 1,601. The tests below name the result cell RunsRequired." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Runs needed for a target Monte Carlo standard error in Excel" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ABM-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "runs-needed-for-a-target-monte-carlo-standard-error-in-excel" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CEILING(ROUND((RunSD/TargetMCSE)^2,6),1)" .

hemo:7004308 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Runs required meet the target Monte Carlo standard error"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The standard error implied by RunsRequired, RunSD divided by its square root, is no larger than TargetMCSE, with a small tolerance for rounding in floating point. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the ratio of RunSD to TargetMCSE used without squaring, 30 runs for the illustrative GBP 300 and GBP 10, which gives a standard error of about GBP 54.8." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Runs required meet the target Monte Carlo standard error" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ABM-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "runs-required-meet-the-target-monte-carlo-standard-error" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=RunSD/SQRT(RunsRequired)<=TargetMCSE*(1+1E-9)" .

hemo:7004309 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Runs required are the smallest count meeting the target"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "One run fewer than RunsRequired gives a standard error above TargetMCSE, so the count is not padded. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the count doubled for safety, 1,800 for the illustrative inputs, or the squared standard deviation divided by the target without squaring the target, 9,000." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Runs required are the smallest count meeting the target" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ABM-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "runs-required-are-the-smallest-count-meeting-the-target" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=OR(RunsRequired=1,RunSD/SQRT(RunsRequired-1)>TargetMCSE)" .

hemo:7004310 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Morris and colleagues' standard deviation of 0.2 and target of 0.005"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the worked simulation study of Morris and colleagues the standard deviation of the estimates is taken as at most 0.2 and the Monte Carlo standard error of bias is required to be below 0.005, which gives 1,600 repetitions. The same arithmetic applies to a mean output of an agent-based model." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Morris and colleagues' standard deviation of 0.2 and target of 0.005" ;
    hemo:9000014 "s_run = 0.2; e_target = 0.005; R_req = 1600" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ABM-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "morris-and-colleagues-standard-deviation-of-0-2-and-target-of-0-005" .

hemo:7004311 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative incremental cost per person with a spread of GBP 300 across paired runs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For an illustrative agent-based model whose pilot runs give a standard deviation of GBP 300 in the incremental cost per person between strategies, a Monte Carlo standard error of GBP 10 needs 900 runs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative incremental cost per person with a spread of GBP 300 across paired runs" ;
    hemo:9000014 "s_run = 300; e_target = 10; R_req = 900" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ABM-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-incremental-cost-per-person-with-a-spread-of-gbp-300-across-paired-runs" .

hemo:7004312 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Halving the target standard error quadruples the agent-based model runs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Tightening the illustrative target to GBP 5 needs 3,600 runs, four times as many, because the standard error falls with the square root of the number of runs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Halving the target standard error quadruples the agent-based model runs" ;
    hemo:9000014 "s_run = 300; e_target = 5; R_req = 3600" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ABM-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "halving-the-target-standard-error-quadruples-the-agent-based-model-runs" .

hemo:7004313 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected share set before the agent-based model runs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "P_exp is a planning value from a pilot or an earlier study. When no value is available, 0.5 gives the largest count, since Morris and colleagues note that the worst-case standard error occurs at a share of 50%." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected share set before the agent-based model runs" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ABM-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-share-set-before-the-agent-based-model-runs" .

hemo:7004314 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Binary run result with independent runs for a share"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each run either shows the event or does not, and the runs are independent, with seeds set as in HE-AS-ABM-007, so the count of runs with the event is binomial." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Binary run result with independent runs for a share" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ABM-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "binary-run-result-with-independent-runs-for-a-share" .

hemo:7004315 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Runs needed for a target standard error of a share in Excel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000693 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the planning share in a cell named ExpectedShare and the target in TargetShareMCSE, CEILING rounds the count up to a whole number of runs, after ROUND to six decimals removes floating-point residue as in HE-IM-ABM-003. The tests below name the result cell RunsForShare." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Runs needed for a target standard error of a share in Excel" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ABM-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "runs-needed-for-a-target-standard-error-of-a-share-in-excel" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CEILING(ROUND(ExpectedShare*(1-ExpectedShare)/TargetShareMCSE^2,6),1)" .

hemo:7004316 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Runs for a share meet the target standard error"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The binomial standard error at RunsForShare is no larger than TargetShareMCSE, with a small tolerance for rounding in floating point. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the target used without squaring, 16 runs for the illustrative share of 0.2 and target of 0.01, which gives a standard error of 0.1." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Runs for a share meet the target standard error" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ABM-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "runs-for-a-share-meet-the-target-standard-error" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SQRT(ExpectedShare*(1-ExpectedShare)/RunsForShare)<=TargetShareMCSE*(1+1E-9)" .

hemo:7004317 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Runs for a share are the smallest count meeting the target"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "One run fewer than RunsForShare gives a standard error above TargetShareMCSE. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a count above the minimum for the stated share, for example the worst-case 2,500 runs for a target of 0.01 used with an expected share of 0.2." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Runs for a share are the smallest count meeting the target" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ABM-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "runs-for-a-share-are-the-smallest-count-meeting-the-target" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=OR(RunsForShare=1,SQRT(ExpectedShare*(1-ExpectedShare)/(RunsForShare-1))>TargetShareMCSE)" .

hemo:7004318 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Morris and colleagues' coverage of 95% with a target standard error of 0.5%"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Morris and colleagues' example of a coverage of 95% estimated with a Monte Carlo standard error of 0.5% needs 1,900 repetitions." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Morris and colleagues' coverage of 95% with a target standard error of 0.5%" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_exp = 0.95; e_share = 0.005; R_prop = 1900" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ABM-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "morris-and-colleagues-coverage-of-95-with-a-target-standard-error-of-0-5" .

hemo:7004319 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Worst-case share of 0.5 with a target standard error of 0.005"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At the worst-case share of 0.5 the same target needs 10,000 runs, the figure Morris and colleagues give." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Worst-case share of 0.5 with a target standard error of 0.005" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_exp = 0.5; e_share = 0.005; R_prop = 10000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ABM-012" ;
    hemo:9000023 "worst-case-share-of-0-5-with-a-target-standard-error-of-0-005" .

hemo:7004320 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative major outbreak share of 0.2 with a target standard error of 0.01"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For an illustrative agent-based model in which about 20% of runs are expected to produce a major outbreak, a standard error of 0.01 for that share needs 1,600 runs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative major outbreak share of 0.2 with a target standard error of 0.01" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_exp = 0.2; e_share = 0.01; R_prop = 1600" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ABM-013" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-major-outbreak-share-of-0-2-with-a-target-standard-error-of-0-01" .

hemo:7004321 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected survival based on fixed life-table rates matched to the patients"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "S_star_t comes from population mortality rates by age, sex and calendar year, which TSD 21 says are typically assumed to be fixed and known with no associated uncertainty. The general population must be exchangeable with the patients for other-cause mortality, which may need matching on smoking or other lifestyle characteristics, and for conditions with frequent comorbidity general population rates may be too low." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected survival based on fixed life-table rates matched to the patients" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BGM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-survival-based-on-fixed-life-table-rates-matched-to-the-patients" .

hemo:7004322 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Common time origin and attained age for the two survival curves"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Both survival probabilities start at the same time origin, and S_star_t accumulates the life-table rates at each patient's attained age, entry age plus elapsed time, up to t. For a cohort with a spread of ages or both sexes, S_star_t is the average of the individual expected survivals given by HE-FM-BGM-003." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Common time origin and attained age for the two survival curves" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BGM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "common-time-origin-and-attained-age-for-the-two-survival-curves" .

hemo:7004323 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Relative survival from named all-cause and expected survival cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000694 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With all-cause survival in AllCauseSurvival and expected survival at the same time and attained age in ExpectedSurvival, the cell named RelativeSurvival returns the ratio. Filled down a column of model times it gives the relative survival curve." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Relative survival from named all-cause and expected survival cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-BGM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "relative-survival-from-named-all-cause-and-expected-survival-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AllCauseSurvival/ExpectedSurvival" .

hemo:7004324 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Relative survival cell matches the article's cure-model value at 10 years"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With AllCauseSurvival set to 0.386674 and ExpectedSurvival to 0.860708, the article's values at 10 years, the cell RelativeSurvival agrees within 0.00001 with relative survival computed directly from the cure fraction and the excess hazard, 0.40 plus 0.60 times exp(minus 2.5). Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the ratio inverted, which gives 2.2259, or all-cause survival left undivided, 0.3867." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Relative survival cell matches the article's cure-model value at 10 years" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BGM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "relative-survival-cell-matches-the-article-s-cure-model-value-at-10-years" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(RelativeSurvival-(0.4+0.6*EXP(-0.25*10)))<0.00001" .

hemo:7004325 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Relative survival above zero and not above one"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Checks that the cell RelativeSurvival is above zero and at most 1, which holds while the cumulative excess hazard is zero or above, so that modelled mortality has not on balance fallen below that of the general population, the expectation TSD 21 states for nearly all cases without ruling out exceptions. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows all-cause survival above expected survival, for example 0.90 against 0.860708, which gives 1.0457, or the ratio inverted; when no error is found it flags a group whose mortality is below that of the general population, which TSD 21 calls unlikely, and which then needs to be justified." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Relative survival above zero and not above one" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BGM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "relative-survival-above-zero-and-not-above-one" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(RelativeSurvival>0,RelativeSurvival<=1)" .

hemo:7004326 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Relative survival at 10 years in the article's illustrative cure model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative cure model (cure fraction 0.40, uncured excess hazard 0.25 a year, constant background hazard 0.015 a year), all-cause survival of 0.386674 at 10 years over expected survival of 0.860708 gives relative survival of 0.449251, the article's R(10)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Relative survival at 10 years in the article's illustrative cure model" ;
    hemo:9000014 "S_t = 0.386674; S_star_t = 0.860708; R_t = 0.449251" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BGM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "relative-survival-at-10-years-in-the-article-s-illustrative-cure-model" .

hemo:7004327 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Relative survival at 30 years close to the cure fraction"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At 30 years the same illustrative model gives 0.255263 over 0.637628, a relative survival of 0.400332, almost exactly the cure fraction of 0.40 because nearly all uncured patients have died, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Relative survival at 30 years close to the cure fraction" ;
    hemo:9000014 "S_t = 0.255263; S_star_t = 0.637628; R_t = 0.400332" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BGM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "relative-survival-at-30-years-close-to-the-cure-fraction" .

hemo:7004328 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Relative survival equal to 1 with no excess mortality"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When the patients die at the general-population rate the two curves are equal and relative survival is 1 at every time, a limiting case that checks an implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Relative survival equal to 1 with no excess mortality" ;
    hemo:9000014 "S_t = 0.860708; S_star_t = 0.860708; R_t = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BGM-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "relative-survival-equal-to-1-with-no-excess-mortality" .

hemo:7004329 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Background and disease deaths as independent competing risks within the cycle"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The two hazards add within the cycle (HE-FM-BTH-005) and act independently, and both probabilities are converted over the same cycle length (HE-FM-TP-001, or HE-FM-TP-003 from an annual life-table probability). Under those conditions the product form is exact and needs no ordering of the two risks within the cycle." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Background and disease deaths as independent competing risks within the cycle" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BGM-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "background-and-disease-deaths-as-independent-competing-risks-within-the-cycle" .

hemo:7004330 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Disease probability that excludes deaths already in the life table"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "p_dis is a disease-specific or excess probability, never an all-cause probability such as one taken from trial overall survival, which already contains background deaths. National life tables also include deaths from the modelled disease; the article notes that the overlap is small for rare conditions and that a cause-deleted life table is one remedy for common ones." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Disease probability that excludes deaths already in the life table" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BGM-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "disease-probability-that-excludes-deaths-already-in-the-life-table" .

hemo:7004331 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Background probability read at attained age in every cycle"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "p_bg changes with each cycle as the cohort ages. Sonnenberg and Beck look up the age-specific rate for each cycle in a table, which HE-CF-BGM-001 does for an age-banded life table." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Background probability read at attained age in every cycle" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BGM-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "background-probability-read-at-attained-age-in-every-cycle" .

hemo:7004332 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "All-cause cycle death probability from named background and disease cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000695 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the cycle's background probability in BackgroundDeathProb and the disease probability in DiseaseDeathProb, the first formula returns the all-cause probability, held in AllCauseDeathProb. The second fills BackgroundDeathProb from a constant background rate per year in BackgroundRate and a cycle length in years in CycleYears (HE-FM-TP-001); DiseaseDeathProb is filled the same way from ExcessRate." ;
    hemo:9000009 "All-cause cycle death probability from named background and disease cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-BGM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "all-cause-cycle-death-probability-from-named-background-and-disease-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1-(1-BackgroundDeathProb)*(1-DiseaseDeathProb); =1-EXP(-BackgroundRate*CycleYears)" .

hemo:7004333 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "All-cause cycle probability equals the summed-hazard probability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With BackgroundDeathProb and DiseaseDeathProb converted from BackgroundRate and ExcessRate over CycleYears, the cell AllCauseDeathProb equals 1 minus exp of minus the summed rate times the cycle length. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the two probabilities added, which gives 0.236087 in place of 0.232794 for the article's annual rates." ;
    hemo:9000009 "All-cause cycle probability equals the summed-hazard probability" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BGM-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "all-cause-cycle-probability-equals-the-summed-hazard-probability" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(AllCauseDeathProb-(1-EXP(-(BackgroundRate+ExcessRate)*CycleYears)))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004334 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "All-cause cycle probability bounded by its background and disease components"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Checks that AllCauseDeathProb is at least the larger of the two component probabilities and at most their sum. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the two probabilities multiplied together, 0.003293 for the article's annual rates." ;
    hemo:9000009 "All-cause cycle probability bounded by its background and disease components" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BGM-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "all-cause-cycle-probability-bounded-by-its-background-and-disease-components" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(AllCauseDeathProb>=MAX(BackgroundDeathProb,DiseaseDeathProb),AllCauseDeathProb<=BackgroundDeathProb+DiseaseDeathProb)" .

hemo:7004335 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Annual cycle with the article's background and excess hazards"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's illustrative constant background hazard of 0.015 a year and excess hazard of 0.25 a year, an annual cycle gives p_bg of 0.014888 and p_dis of 0.221199 (HE-FM-TP-001), and an all-cause probability of 0.232794, the same as 1 minus exp(minus 0.265) from the summed hazard." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Annual cycle with the article's background and excess hazards" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_bg = 0.014888; p_dis = 0.221199; p_all = 0.232794" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BGM-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "annual-cycle-with-the-article-s-background-and-excess-hazards" .

hemo:7004336 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Sonnenberg and Beck starting background rate with an illustrative disease probability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Sonnenberg and Beck's starting background rate of 0.0036 a year, for a 43-year-old man, gives p_bg of 0.003594 for an annual cycle. With an illustrative disease probability of 0.05 the all-cause probability is 0.053414 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Sonnenberg and Beck starting background rate with an illustrative disease probability" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_bg = 0.003594; p_dis = 0.05; p_all = 0.053414" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BGM-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "sonnenberg-and-beck-starting-background-rate-with-an-illustrative-disease-probability" .

hemo:7004337 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No disease mortality leaves the background death probability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When the disease adds no risk the all-cause probability equals the background probability, a limiting case that checks an implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No disease mortality leaves the background death probability" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_bg = 0.014888; p_dis = 0; p_all = 0.014888" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BGM-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-disease-mortality-leaves-the-background-death-probability" .

hemo:7004338 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Group shares fixed at model start"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "w_f is the share at time zero, as each subject carries the weight 1/N in TSD 21 equation 18. The share of women among survivors at time t, w_f S_f divided by S_star, follows from the formula and is not entered." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Group shares fixed at model start" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BGM-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "group-shares-fixed-at-model-start" .

hemo:7004339 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Group survival and hazard from each group's own life table"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "S_f, S_m, h_f and h_m come from life tables for each sex, which ONS constructs separately for males and females, at each group's attained age at time t, with S_f and S_m accumulated from model start. The rates are fixed and known, as for HE-FM-BGM-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Group survival and hazard from each group's own life table" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BGM-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "group-survival-and-hazard-from-each-group-s-own-life-table" .

hemo:7004340 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Marginal expected survival and background hazard from named group cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000696 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the female share in FemaleShare, the group survivals in FemaleExpSurv and MaleExpSurv and the group hazards in FemaleBgHazard and MaleBgHazard, the first formula returns the value held in MarginalExpSurv and the second the value held in MarginalBgHazard. Filled down a column of model times with lookups on attained age, they give the cohort's background curve." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Marginal expected survival and background hazard from named group cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-BGM-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "marginal-expected-survival-and-background-hazard-from-named-group-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=FemaleShare*FemaleExpSurv+(1-FemaleShare)*MaleExpSurv; =(FemaleShare*FemaleExpSurv*FemaleBgHazard+(1-FemaleShare)*MaleExpSurv*MaleBgHazard)/MarginalExpSurv" .

hemo:7004341 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Marginal background hazard within the two group hazards"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Checks that MarginalBgHazard is at least the smaller and at most the larger of the two group hazards, since it is a weighted average. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the division by MarginalExpSurv left out, which gives 0.0185 in the first worked example, or the two hazards added, 0.050." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Marginal background hazard within the two group hazards" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BGM-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "marginal-background-hazard-within-the-two-group-hazards" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(MarginalBgHazard>=MIN(FemaleBgHazard,MaleBgHazard),MarginalBgHazard<=MAX(FemaleBgHazard,MaleBgHazard))" .

hemo:7004342 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Marginal background hazard reproduces the illustrative mixed-sex value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of the first worked example the cell MarginalBgHazard is 0.024667 to six decimal places. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a fixed equal-weight blend of the hazards, 0.025, which ignores the shift in sex mix among survivors." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Marginal background hazard reproduces the illustrative mixed-sex value" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BGM-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "marginal-background-hazard-reproduces-the-illustrative-mixed-sex-value" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(MarginalBgHazard-0.024667)<0.000001" .

hemo:7004343 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative mixed-sex cohort in which men die sooner"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative values at a point some years into the model, with half the cohort female at the start, expected survival of 0.80 for women and 0.70 for men, and background hazards of 0.020 and 0.030 a year, give a marginal expected survival of 0.75 and a marginal hazard of about 0.024667, below the fixed blend of 0.025 because women now make up about 53% of survivors." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative mixed-sex cohort in which men die sooner" ;
    hemo:9000014 "w_f = 0.5; S_f = 0.80; S_m = 0.70; h_f = 0.020; h_m = 0.030; S_star = 0.75; h_star = 0.024667" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BGM-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-mixed-sex-cohort-in-which-men-die-sooner" .

hemo:7004344 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Marginal background hazard at model start"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At time zero both survivals are 1, so the marginal hazard is the simple weighted blend, 0.025 for the same hazards, a limiting case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Marginal background hazard at model start" ;
    hemo:9000014 "w_f = 0.5; S_f = 1; S_m = 1; h_f = 0.020; h_m = 0.030; S_star = 1; h_star = 0.025" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BGM-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "marginal-background-hazard-at-model-start" .

hemo:7004345 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Mixed-sex cohort that is 60% female at the start"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With 60% of the cohort female at the start and the same illustrative survivals and hazards, the marginal expected survival is 0.76 and the marginal hazard about 0.023684." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Mixed-sex cohort that is 60% female at the start" ;
    hemo:9000014 "w_f = 0.6; S_f = 0.80; S_m = 0.70; h_f = 0.020; h_m = 0.030; S_star = 0.76; h_star = 0.023684" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BGM-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "mixed-sex-cohort-that-is-60-female-at-the-start" .

hemo:7004346 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Closed homogeneously mixing SIR population behind beta over gamma"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The population is closed over the outbreak, mixes homogeneously and gains lasting immunity after recovery, as in section 2.1 of van den Driessche. Van den Driessche writes transmission by mass action, which gives R_0 = beta S_0/gamma; with beta defined per infectious person in a fully susceptible population, as in the article, S_0 is absorbed into beta and the formula reduces to beta/gamma. Where births and natural deaths matter the denominator becomes d + gamma, the form given in the same section." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Closed homogeneously mixing SIR population behind beta over gamma" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BRN-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "closed-homogeneously-mixing-sir-population-behind-beta-over-gamma" .

hemo:7004347 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant recovery rate implying an exponential infectious period"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "A constant recovery rate gamma means the time infectious is exponentially distributed with mean 1/gamma, so D is a mean and individual durations vary. The probability of recovering within a model cycle of length t is 1 minus exp(minus gamma t) by the rate conversion of HE-FN-TP-001, not gamma times t. Beta and gamma are in the same time unit, so their ratio has none, and Delamater and colleagues describe R_0 as cases per case, not a rate." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant recovery rate implying an exponential infectious period" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BRN-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-recovery-rate-implying-an-exponential-infectious-period" .

hemo:7004348 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "SIR basic reproduction number from named rate cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000697 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the transmission rate in TransRate and the recovery rate in RecoveryRate, the first cell returns the mean infectious period, named InfectiousPeriod, and the second returns R_0 in a cell named BasicRepNumber. Names such as R0 or R1 are not used because Excel reads them as cell references." ;
    hemo:9000009 "SIR basic reproduction number from named rate cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-BRN-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "sir-basic-reproduction-number-from-named-rate-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1/RecoveryRate; =TransRate*InfectiousPeriod" .

hemo:7004349 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "SIR basic reproduction number cell consistent with both rates"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The held R_0 cell times the recovery rate returns the transmission rate, and the infectious period times the recovery rate returns 1. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows R_0 computed as beta times gamma, 0.03 instead of 3 for the article's rates, or the infectious period taken as the inverse of the transmission rate." ;
    hemo:9000009 "SIR basic reproduction number cell consistent with both rates" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BRN-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "sir-basic-reproduction-number-cell-consistent-with-both-rates" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(BasicRepNumber*RecoveryRate-TransRate)<1E-9,ABS(InfectiousPeriod*RecoveryRate-1)<1E-9)" .

hemo:7004350 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "SIR threshold sign agrees with the comparison of beta and gamma"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "R_0 in BasicRepNumber is above 1 exactly when the transmission rate exceeds the recovery rate, the condition van den Driessche obtains from the sign of beta S_0 minus gamma. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows R_0 computed upside down as gamma/beta, about 0.333 for the article's rates." ;
    hemo:9000009 "SIR threshold sign agrees with the comparison of beta and gamma" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BRN-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "sir-threshold-sign-agrees-with-the-comparison-of-beta-and-gamma" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(BasicRepNumber>1)=(TransRate>RecoveryRate)" .

hemo:7004351 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's illustrative infection with beta of 0.30 and gamma of 0.10 per day"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An infection that causes 0.30 new infections per infectious person per day and has a recovery rate of 0.10 per day has a mean infectious period of 10 days and R_0 of 3, the illustrative values of the article's worked example." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's illustrative infection with beta of 0.30 and gamma of 0.10 per day" ;
    hemo:9000014 "gamma = 0.10; beta = 0.30; D = 10; R_0 = 3" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BRN-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-illustrative-infection-with-beta-of-0-30-and-gamma-of-0-10-per-day" .

hemo:7004352 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "SIR basic reproduction number with an influenza-like infectious period of five days"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Van den Driessche gives a mean infectious time of about 5 days for influenza, a recovery rate of 0.2 per day. With an illustrative transmission rate of 0.26 per day, R_0 is 1.3." ;
    hemo:9000009 "SIR basic reproduction number with an influenza-like infectious period of five days" ;
    hemo:9000014 "gamma = 0.2; beta = 0.26; D = 5; R_0 = 1.3" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BRN-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "sir-basic-reproduction-number-with-an-influenza-like-infectious-period-of-five-days" .

hemo:7004353 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "SIR basic reproduction number at the threshold when beta equals gamma"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With transmission and recovery both at 0.25 per day each case replaces itself exactly once and R_0 is 1, the boundary at which van den Driessche's sharp threshold sits, a limiting case for checking an implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "SIR basic reproduction number at the threshold when beta equals gamma" ;
    hemo:9000014 "gamma = 0.25; beta = 0.25; D = 4; R_0 = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BRN-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "sir-basic-reproduction-number-at-the-threshold-when-beta-equals-gamma" .

hemo:7004354 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Homogeneous mixing and few infectious people for the effective reproduction number"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Everyone meets everyone else at the same rate, and the infectious proportion is negligible apart from the one infectious person introduced, the conditions under which the WHO guide states R_t = R_0 x_t. In a structured population the susceptible share of each group scales the matching row of the next-generation matrix of HE-FM-BRN-005 instead." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Homogeneous mixing and few infectious people for the effective reproduction number" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BRN-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "homogeneous-mixing-and-few-infectious-people-for-the-effective-reproduction-number" .

hemo:7004355 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Immune people fully protected for the effective reproduction number"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "People counted as immune cannot be infected or transmit, and people counted as susceptible are fully susceptible, as with an all-or-nothing vaccine. A leaky vaccine that partly reduces each vaccinated person's risk does not divide the population into immune and susceptible people, so x_t then has to be read as average relative susceptibility, and the later course of the epidemic differs from the all-or-nothing case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Immune people fully protected for the effective reproduction number" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BRN-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "immune-people-fully-protected-for-the-effective-reproduction-number" .

hemo:7004356 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Effective reproduction number from R_0 and the susceptible share in named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000698 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With R_0 in BasicRepNumber and the susceptible proportion in SusceptibleShare, the first cell returns R_t, held in a cell named EffRepNumber. The second returns the susceptible proportion left by an all-or-nothing vaccine with coverage in Coverage and effectiveness in VaccineEff." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Effective reproduction number from R_0 and the susceptible share in named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-BRN-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "effective-reproduction-number-from-r-0-and-the-susceptible-share-in-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=BasicRepNumber*SusceptibleShare; =1-Coverage*VaccineEff" .

hemo:7004357 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's effective reproduction numbers at 50% and 80% coverage reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With R_0 of 3 and a 90% effective vaccine, coverage of 50% and 80% gives R_t of 1.65 and 0.84. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows coverage used as the immune share without the effectiveness, which gives 1.5 and 0.6." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's effective reproduction numbers at 50% and 80% coverage reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BRN-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-effective-reproduction-numbers-at-50-and-80-coverage-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(3*(1-0.5*0.9)-1.65)<1E-9,ABS(3*(1-0.8*0.9)-0.84)<1E-9)" .

hemo:7004358 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Effective reproduction number below one exactly when the susceptible share is below 1/R_0"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The held R_t cell is below 1 exactly when SusceptibleShare is below 1/R_0, the threshold susceptible proportion x* of the WHO guide. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the immune share used in place of the susceptible share, for example at the article's 80% coverage, where the wrong value 3 times 0.72, or 2.16, is above 1 while the susceptible share 0.28 is below one third." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Effective reproduction number below one exactly when the susceptible share is below 1/R_0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BRN-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "effective-reproduction-number-below-one-exactly-when-the-susceptible-share-is-below-1-r-0" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(EffRepNumber<1)=(SusceptibleShare<1/BasicRepNumber)" .

hemo:7004359 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's effective reproduction number at 50% coverage with a 90% effective vaccine"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's example, 50% coverage with a vaccine that fully protects 90% of those vaccinated leaves 55% of the population susceptible, so R_t is 3 times 0.55, or 1.65, and an epidemic can still occur." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's effective reproduction number at 50% coverage with a 90% effective vaccine" ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_0 = 3; x_t = 0.55; R_t = 1.65" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BRN-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-effective-reproduction-number-at-50-coverage-with-a-90-effective-vaccine" .

hemo:7004360 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's effective reproduction number at 80% coverage"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At 80% coverage the protected share is 72% and the susceptible share 28%, so R_t is 0.84, below 1, and a major epidemic cannot occur in the article's dynamic model." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's effective reproduction number at 80% coverage" ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_0 = 3; x_t = 0.28; R_t = 0.84" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BRN-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-effective-reproduction-number-at-80-coverage" .

hemo:7004361 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Effective reproduction number equal to R_0 in a fully susceptible population"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With x_t of 1 the effective and basic reproduction numbers coincide, a limiting case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Effective reproduction number equal to R_0 in a fully susceptible population" ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_0 = 3; x_t = 1; R_t = 3" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BRN-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "effective-reproduction-number-equal-to-r-0-in-a-fully-susceptible-population" .

hemo:7004362 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Homogeneous mixing and an R_0 above one for the herd immunity threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The formula assumes homogeneous mixing and R_0 above 1; for R_0 of 1 or less H is zero or negative and no vaccination is needed to prevent sustained spread. The WHO guide reports that crude estimates of R_0 and of the critical proportion are overestimates if transmission is greatest among young people and declines with age, and underestimates if transmission increases with age, so a structured model is used where mixing is uneven." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Homogeneous mixing and an R_0 above one for the herd immunity threshold" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BRN-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "homogeneous-mixing-and-an-r-0-above-one-for-the-herd-immunity-threshold" .

hemo:7004363 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Lasting all-or-nothing protection given before exposure for the critical coverage"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each vaccinated person is either fully and lastingly protected, with probability E, or not protected at all, and is vaccinated before exposure. The WHO guide notes that the simple formula implicitly assumes vaccination at birth or as soon as maternal immunity has waned, and that actual coverage must exceed the critical effective immunisation level because protection is usually imperfect. E is effectiveness against infection, since protection against disease alone does not stop a vaccinated person from transmitting." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Lasting all-or-nothing protection given before exposure for the critical coverage" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BRN-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "lasting-all-or-nothing-protection-given-before-exposure-for-the-critical-coverage" .

hemo:7004364 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Herd immunity threshold and critical coverage from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000699 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With R_0 in BasicRepNumber and effectiveness in VaccineEff, the first cell returns H, named HerdThreshold, and the second returns V_c, named CriticalCoverage. The third flags a target that a vaccine of that effectiveness cannot reach." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Herd immunity threshold and critical coverage from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-BRN-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "herd-immunity-threshold-and-critical-coverage-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1-1/BasicRepNumber; =HerdThreshold/VaccineEff; =IF(CriticalCoverage>1,\"not reachable\",\"reachable\")" .

hemo:7004365 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Coverage at the critical level brings the effective reproduction number to one"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Vaccinating the proportion held in CriticalCoverage with a vaccine of effectiveness VaccineEff leaves R_0 times the susceptible share equal to 1. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows V_c computed as H times E, giving R_t of 1.38 for the article's inputs, or the threshold H used as the coverage, giving 1.2." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Coverage at the critical level brings the effective reproduction number to one" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BRN-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "coverage-at-the-critical-level-brings-the-effective-reproduction-number-to-one" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(BasicRepNumber*(1-CriticalCoverage*VaccineEff)-1)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004366 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "WHO Table 7 measles critical proportions reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "R_0 of 9.3 and 15.6 give critical proportions that round to 89% and 94%, the measles row of Table 7 in the WHO guide. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows 1/R_0 used in place of 1 minus 1/R_0, which gives 11% and 6%." ;
    hemo:9000009 "WHO Table 7 measles critical proportions reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BRN-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "who-table-7-measles-critical-proportions-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ROUND(1-1/9.3,2)=0.89,ROUND(1-1/15.6,2)=0.94)" .

hemo:7004367 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's herd immunity threshold and critical coverage for R_0 of 3 and a 90% effective vaccine"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With R_0 of 3, two thirds of the population must be immune, and a vaccine that fully protects 90% of those vaccinated needs coverage of about 74.1%, as in step 2 of the article's worked example." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's herd immunity threshold and critical coverage for R_0 of 3 and a 90% effective vaccine" ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_0 = 3; E = 0.9; H = 0.6667; V_c = 0.7407" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BRN-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-herd-immunity-threshold-and-critical-coverage-for-r-0-of-3-and-a-90-effective-vaccine" .

hemo:7004368 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Masterson and colleagues' critical coverage for R_0 of 4 and a 90% effective vaccine"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Masterson and colleagues report that at R_0 of 4 the herd immunity threshold is 75% and that more than 80% of a population needs vaccinating with a vaccine of 90% effectiveness; the formula gives about 83.3%." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Masterson and colleagues' critical coverage for R_0 of 4 and a 90% effective vaccine" ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_0 = 4; E = 0.9; H = 0.75; V_c = 0.8333" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BRN-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "masterson-and-colleagues-critical-coverage-for-r-0-of-4-and-a-90-effective-vaccine" .

hemo:7004369 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Measles critical proportion at the lower WHO estimate of R_0"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With R_0 of 9.3, the lower end of the measles range in Table 7 of the WHO guide, and effectiveness set to 1 so that V_c is the critical effective immunisation level, both values are about 0.892, which the table rounds to 89%." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Measles critical proportion at the lower WHO estimate of R_0" ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_0 = 9.3; E = 1; H = 0.8925; V_c = 0.8925" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BRN-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "measles-critical-proportion-at-the-lower-who-estimate-of-r-0" .

hemo:7004370 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Measles at the upper WHO estimate of R_0 with a 90% effective vaccine"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At R_0 of 15.6 the threshold is about 0.936, the 94% of Table 7, and a vaccine that protects 90% of recipients would need coverage of about 104% (computed here for illustration), so that vaccine alone cannot reach the threshold in this simple model." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Measles at the upper WHO estimate of R_0 with a 90% effective vaccine" ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_0 = 15.6; E = 0.9; H = 0.9359; V_c = 1.0399" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BRN-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "measles-at-the-upper-who-estimate-of-r-0-with-a-90-effective-vaccine" .

hemo:7004371 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Early exponential growth in a fully susceptible population for the growth-rate relation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The growth rate is measured while almost everyone is susceptible and new infections grow at a steady exponential rate, so the result is R_0; measured later it is an effective reproduction number. Diekmann, Heesterbeek and Roberts prove for compartmental models that R_0 is above 1 if and only if r is above zero and equals 1 if and only if r is zero, so the sign of r fixes the side of the threshold whatever the shape of the generation interval." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Early exponential growth in a fully susceptible population for the growth-rate relation" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BRN-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "early-exponential-growth-in-a-fully-susceptible-population-for-the-growth-rate-relation" .

hemo:7004372 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Generation interval shape chosen to match the natural history"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Wallinga and Lipsitch show that the different equations differ only in the assumed shape of the generation interval distribution, so the shape is chosen from data on the infection, such as an observed generation interval distribution. The two-stage form takes the latent and infectious periods as independent and exponentially distributed, with constant infectiousness during the infectious stage, and r, T_E and T_I share one time unit." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Generation interval shape chosen to match the natural history" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BRN-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "generation-interval-shape-chosen-to-match-the-natural-history" .

hemo:7004373 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Reproduction number from the growth rate in named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000700 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the growth rate in GrowthRate and the mean latent and infectious periods in LatentPeriod and InfectiousPeriod, the first three cells return the exponential, two-stage and fixed-interval estimates, held in RepExp, RepTwoStage and RepDelta. The fourth converts a doubling time in DoublingTime to the growth rate." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Reproduction number from the growth rate in named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-BRN-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "reproduction-number-from-the-growth-rate-in-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1+GrowthRate*(LatentPeriod+InfectiousPeriod); =(1+GrowthRate*LatentPeriod)*(1+GrowthRate*InfectiousPeriod); =EXP(GrowthRate*(LatentPeriod+InfectiousPeriod)); =LN(2)/DoublingTime" .

hemo:7004374 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Growth-rate estimates ordered by the spread of the generation interval"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For a growth rate of zero or above the exponential estimate in RepExp is no larger than the two-stage estimate in RepTwoStage, which is no larger than the fixed-interval bound in RepDelta. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the two-stage form computed with the whole mean generation interval in each factor, 9 instead of 3.96 at r of 0.2 and T_c of 10 days, above the bound of 7.39." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Growth-rate estimates ordered by the spread of the generation interval" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BRN-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "growth-rate-estimates-ordered-by-the-spread-of-the-generation-interval" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(RepExp<=RepTwoStage+1E-12,RepTwoStage<=RepDelta+1E-12)" .

hemo:7004375 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's doubling time, R_0 and fixed-interval bound reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A growth rate of 0.20 per day gives a doubling time of 3.47 days, R_0 of 3 with a 10-day exponential interval and 7.39 with a fixed 10-day interval. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the base-10 logarithm used for the doubling time, 1.51 days instead of 3.47." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's doubling time, R_0 and fixed-interval bound reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BRN-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-doubling-time-r-0-and-fixed-interval-bound-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ROUND(LN(2)/0.2,2)=3.47,ROUND(1+0.2*10,4)=3,ROUND(EXP(0.2*10),2)=7.39)" .

hemo:7004376 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's growth rate of 0.20 per day with a 10-day exponential generation interval"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's infection grows at 0.20 per day with a 10-day mean infectious period and no latent stage, so the exponential and two-stage forms both return R_0 of 3, while a fixed 10-day interval would imply about 7.39, the upper bound in step 1 of the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's growth rate of 0.20 per day with a 10-day exponential generation interval" ;
    hemo:9000014 "r = 0.2; T_E = 0; T_I = 10; T_c = 10; R_exp = 3; R_seir = 3; R_delta = 7.389" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BRN-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-growth-rate-of-0-20-per-day-with-a-10-day-exponential-generation-interval" .

hemo:7004377 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Growth rate of 0.20 per day with an illustrative four-day latent and six-day infectious period"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Splitting the same 10-day mean generation interval into a 4-day latent and a 6-day infectious stage (illustrative values) raises the two-stage estimate to 3.96, above the exponential value of 3 and below the fixed-interval bound of about 7.39, because the generation interval is less dispersed." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Growth rate of 0.20 per day with an illustrative four-day latent and six-day infectious period" ;
    hemo:9000014 "r = 0.2; T_E = 4; T_I = 6; T_c = 10; R_exp = 3; R_seir = 3.96; R_delta = 7.389" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BRN-012" ;
    hemo:9000023 "growth-rate-of-0-20-per-day-with-an-illustrative-four-day-latent-and-six-day-infectious-period" .

hemo:7004378 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Zero growth rate returns a reproduction number of one for every interval shape"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With no growth every form returns 1, the threshold value, whatever the generation interval, a limiting case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Zero growth rate returns a reproduction number of one for every interval shape" ;
    hemo:9000014 "r = 0; T_E = 4; T_I = 6; T_c = 10; R_exp = 1; R_seir = 1; R_delta = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BRN-013" ;
    hemo:9000023 "zero-growth-rate-returns-a-reproduction-number-of-one-for-every-interval-shape" .

hemo:7004379 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Next-generation matrix evaluated at the disease-free state with non-negative entries"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "K is computed with everyone susceptible, so each entry is an expected count of new cases over the whole infectious period of one case and is zero or above; van den Driessche notes that F is entrywise non-negative and V a non-singular M-matrix, so the inverse of V and K are non-negative. Columns index the group of the infector and rows the group of the new case, as in the definition of Diekmann, Heesterbeek and Roberts. A transposed matrix has the same eigenvalues and so the same R_0, but its dominant eigenvector, the distribution of cases over groups, differs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Next-generation matrix evaluated at the disease-free state with non-negative entries" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BRN-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "next-generation-matrix-evaluated-at-the-disease-free-state-with-non-negative-entries" .

hemo:7004380 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-group dominant eigenvalue from a named matrix"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000701 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the 2 by 2 next-generation matrix in a range named NextGenMatrix, columns for the infector's group and rows for the new case's group, the cell returns R_0, held in a cell named DominantEig. It uses (k_11 minus k_22)^2 plus 4 k_12 k_21 under the square root, which equals T_K^2 minus 4 D_K and avoids small negative values from rounding." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-group dominant eigenvalue from a named matrix" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-BRN-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-group-dominant-eigenvalue-from-a-named-matrix" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(INDEX(NextGenMatrix,1,1)+INDEX(NextGenMatrix,2,2)+SQRT((INDEX(NextGenMatrix,1,1)-INDEX(NextGenMatrix,2,2))^2+4*INDEX(NextGenMatrix,1,2)*INDEX(NextGenMatrix,2,1)))/2" .

hemo:7004381 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-group R_0 solves the characteristic equation and is the dominant root"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The held value in DominantEig satisfies lambda^2 minus T_K lambda plus D_K = 0 and is at least the larger diagonal entry, a lower bound on the spectral radius of a non-negative matrix. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the smaller root taken, about 0.032 for the article's matrix, or the mean of the diagonal, 0.65." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-group R_0 solves the characteristic equation and is the dominant root" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BRN-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-group-r-0-solves-the-characteristic-equation-and-is-the-dominant-root" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(DominantEig^2-(INDEX(NextGenMatrix,1,1)+INDEX(NextGenMatrix,2,2))*DominantEig+INDEX(NextGenMatrix,1,1)*INDEX(NextGenMatrix,2,2)-INDEX(NextGenMatrix,1,2)*INDEX(NextGenMatrix,2,1))<1E-9,DominantEig>=MAX(INDEX(NextGenMatrix,1,1),INDEX(NextGenMatrix,2,2)))" .

hemo:7004382 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's two-group R_0 of 1.268 reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The trace 1.3 and determinant 0.04 give 1.268 to three decimals. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the square root term subtracted, which returns the smaller eigenvalue 0.032." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's two-group R_0 of 1.268 reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BRN-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-two-group-r-0-of-1-268-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND((1.3+SQRT(1.3^2-4*0.04))/2,3)=1.268" .

hemo:7004383 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's illustrative two-group next-generation matrix"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For the article's matrix with rows (0.8, 1.2) and (0.3, 0.5) the trace is 1.3 and the determinant 0.04, so R_0 is about 1.268. Neither diagonal entry reaches 1, yet transmission between the groups takes the system above the threshold." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's illustrative two-group next-generation matrix" ;
    hemo:9000014 "k_11 = 0.8; k_12 = 1.2; k_21 = 0.3; k_22 = 0.5; T_K = 1.3; D_K = 0.04; R_0 = 1.2685" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BRN-014" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-illustrative-two-group-next-generation-matrix" .

hemo:7004384 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Rank-one two-group next-generation matrix whose R_0 equals the trace"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Rows (1.2, 0.6) and (0.8, 0.4), an illustrative matrix with proportional columns, have a determinant of zero, so R_0 equals the trace, 1.6, the case Diekmann, Heesterbeek and Roberts single out for 2 by 2 matrices." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Rank-one two-group next-generation matrix whose R_0 equals the trace" ;
    hemo:9000014 "k_11 = 1.2; k_12 = 0.6; k_21 = 0.8; k_22 = 0.4; T_K = 1.6; D_K = 0; R_0 = 1.6" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BRN-015" ;
    hemo:9000023 "rank-one-two-group-next-generation-matrix-whose-r-0-equals-the-trace" .

hemo:7004385 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Host and vector next-generation matrix with a zero diagonal"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With no direct transmission within either population, an illustrative 4 host infections per infected vector and 0.5 vector infections per infected host give R_0 of the square root of 2, about 1.414, the geometric mean form that van den Driessche describes for a vector-host model." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Host and vector next-generation matrix with a zero diagonal" ;
    hemo:9000014 "k_11 = 0; k_12 = 4; k_21 = 0.5; k_22 = 0; T_K = 0; D_K = -2; R_0 = 1.4142" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BRN-016" ;
    hemo:9000023 "host-and-vector-next-generation-matrix-with-a-zero-diagonal" .

hemo:7004386 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Exchange rate quoted as home currency per unit of foreign currency"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "e_i is home currency per unit of foreign currency. The GLOBE Model final rule expresses its rates as local currency units per US dollar and converts net sales to US dollars by dividing by them, so a rate in that form enters as 1 divided by the published figure." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Exchange rate quoted as home currency per unit of foreign currency" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BOC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "exchange-rate-quoted-as-home-currency-per-unit-of-foreign-currency" .

hemo:7004387 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Rate period stated and applied to every price from one country"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The rate is an average over a stated period and the same rate converts every price from one country. The PMPRB Guidelines in force from 1 January 2026 convert filed prices into Canadian dollars with the simple average of 36 monthly average noon spot exchange rates published by the Bank of Canada, and the GLOBE Model final rule uses one rate per reference country for each quarter, rounded to 3 decimal places." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Rate period stated and applied to every price from one country" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BOC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "rate-period-stated-and-applied-to-every-price-from-one-country" .

hemo:7004388 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Basket price conversion from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000702 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the foreign price in ForeignPrice and a rate in home currency per foreign unit in HomePerForeignRate, the first formula returns the value held in ConvertedPrice. The second applies when the rate is published as foreign units per home unit, held in ForeignPerHomeRate. The third uses the PMPRB style average of 36 monthly rates in the range MonthlyRates, each in home currency per foreign unit." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Basket price conversion from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-BOC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "basket-price-conversion-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ForeignPrice*HomePerForeignRate; =ForeignPrice/ForeignPerHomeRate; =ForeignPrice*AVERAGE(MonthlyRates)" .

hemo:7004389 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "GLOBE reference country A conversion reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With ForeignPrice set to 120 and HomePerForeignRate to 1.25, the cell ConvertedPrice equals 120 divided by the published GLOBE rate of 0.800. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the published rate of 0.800 entered as HomePerForeignRate, which gives 96 in place of 150." ;
    hemo:9000009 "GLOBE reference country A conversion reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BOC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "globe-reference-country-a-conversion-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ConvertedPrice-120/0.8)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004390 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Turner and colleagues' method 2 currency step reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With ForeignPrice set to 2360037 and HomePerForeignRate to 1/21935, the cell ConvertedPrice is within 0.01 of US$107.59. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the rate of the costing year, 1/15994, used in place of that of the base year, which gives 147.56." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Turner and colleagues' method 2 currency step reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BOC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "turner-and-colleagues-method-2-currency-step-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ConvertedPrice-107.59)<0.01" .

hemo:7004391 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "GLOBE reference country A rate of 0.800 applied to an illustrative price"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:1000037 hemo:7004392,
        hemo:7004393 ;
    hemo:1000038 hemo:7004394 ;
    hemo:2000008 0.5 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The GLOBE Model final rule's illustrative reference country A has an exchange rate of 0.800 local currency units per US dollar, so e_i is 1.25 US dollars per unit. An illustrative price of 120 in that currency converts to 150 US dollars, the same as dividing 120 by 0.800." ;
    hemo:9000009 "GLOBE reference country A rate of 0.800 applied to an illustrative price" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_i = 120; e_i = 1.25; c_i = 150" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BOC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "globe-reference-country-a-rate-of-0-800-applied-to-an-illustrative-price" .

hemo:7004392 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "p_i in the test GLOBE reference country A rate of 0.800 applied to an illustrative price"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6003127 ;
    rheo:2000001 120.0 .

hemo:7004393 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "e_i in the test GLOBE reference country A rate of 0.800 applied to an illustrative price"@en ;
