hemo:7004402 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "c_i in the test Basket price already in the home currency"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6003126 ;
    rheo:2000001 95.0 .

hemo:7004403 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "PPP factors from one dataset and one year"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "PPP_s and PPP_t come from the same dataset and the same year, the price year of C_s. The CCEMG-EPPI-Centre Cost Converter page notes that IMF and OECD PPP values differ for OECD countries in all price years, so the dataset is reported with the result." ;
    hemo:9000009 "PPP factors from one dataset and one year" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BOC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ppp-factors-from-one-dataset-and-one-year" .

hemo:7004404 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "GDP purchasing power parity as a general price level"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The World Bank factor is for the level of GDP, so it reflects prices across the whole economy rather than health care alone. The CCEMG-EPPI-Centre Cost Converter page names health care specific, technology specific and episode specific PPPs as alternatives where the data allow." ;
    hemo:9000009 "GDP purchasing power parity as a general price level" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BOC-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "gdp-purchasing-power-parity-as-a-general-price-level" .

hemo:7004405 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "PPP conversion of a cost from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000703 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the cost in CostSourceCurrency and the two factors in PPPSource and PPPTarget, the cell CostTargetPPP returns the converted cost. The cross rate PPPTarget/PPPSource can be held in its own cell and reused for every cost from the same country and year." ;
    hemo:9000009 "PPP conversion of a cost from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-BOC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ppp-conversion-of-a-cost-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CostSourceCurrency/PPPSource*PPPTarget" .

hemo:7004406 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "PPP conversion reverses to the source cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Converting the result back with the two factors swapped returns the original cost. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the ratio inverted in CostTargetPPP; with the first worked example's inputs the round trip then gives about 1306.12 in place of 1000." ;
    hemo:9000009 "PPP conversion reverses to the source cost" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BOC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ppp-conversion-reverses-to-the-source-cost" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(CostTargetPPP*PPPSource/PPPTarget-CostSourceCurrency)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004407 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative PPP conversion reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the first worked example's inputs CostTargetPPP is 875. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the source factor in the numerator and the target factor in the denominator, which gives about 1142.86." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative PPP conversion reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BOC-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-ppp-conversion-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(CostTargetPPP-875)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004408 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative cost converted at two PPP factors"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:1000037 hemo:7004409,
        hemo:7004410,
        hemo:7004411 ;
    hemo:1000038 hemo:7004412 ;
    hemo:2000008 0.5 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A cost of 1,000 in a source currency with an illustrative factor of 0.8 per international dollar is 1,250 international dollars, which at a target factor of 0.7 is 875 in the target currency (illustrative values)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative cost converted at two PPP factors" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C_s = 1000; PPP_s = 0.8; PPP_t = 0.7; C_t = 875" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BOC-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-cost-converted-at-two-ppp-factors" .

hemo:7004409 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "C_s in the test Illustrative cost converted at two PPP factors"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6003130 ;
    rheo:2000001 1000.0 .

hemo:7004410 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "PPP_s in the test Illustrative cost converted at two PPP factors"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6003131 ;
    rheo:2000001 0.8 .

hemo:7004411 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "PPP_t in the test Illustrative cost converted at two PPP factors"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6003132 ;
    rheo:2000001 0.7 .

hemo:7004412 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "C_t in the test Illustrative cost converted at two PPP factors"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6003129 ;
    rheo:2000001 875.0 .

hemo:7004413 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Cost converted into international dollars"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:1000037 hemo:7004414,
        hemo:7004415,
        hemo:7004416 ;
    hemo:1000038 hemo:7004417 ;
    hemo:2000008 0.5 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With PPP_t set to 1, the base of the international dollar, the same cost becomes 1,250 international dollars, the division by the PPP rate that the WHO applies to local tobacco prices." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cost converted into international dollars" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C_s = 1000; PPP_s = 0.8; PPP_t = 1; C_t = 1250" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BOC-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cost-converted-into-international-dollars" .

hemo:7004414 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "C_s in the test Cost converted into international dollars"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6003130 ;
    rheo:2000001 1000.0 .

hemo:7004415 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "PPP_s in the test Cost converted into international dollars"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6003131 ;
    rheo:2000001 0.8 .

hemo:7004416 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "PPP_t in the test Cost converted into international dollars"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6003132 ;
    rheo:2000001 1.0 .

hemo:7004417 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "C_t in the test Cost converted into international dollars"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6003129 ;
    rheo:2000001 1250.0 .

hemo:7004418 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Same country as source and target at purchasing power parity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:1000037 hemo:7004419,
        hemo:7004420,
        hemo:7004421 ;
    hemo:1000038 hemo:7004422 ;
    hemo:2000008 0.5 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When the two factors are equal the cost is unchanged, a limiting case that checks an implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same country as source and target at purchasing power parity" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C_s = 1000; PPP_s = 0.8; PPP_t = 0.8; C_t = 1000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BOC-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-country-as-source-and-target-at-purchasing-power-parity" .

hemo:7004419 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "C_s in the test Same country as source and target at purchasing power parity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6003130 ;
    rheo:2000001 1000.0 .

hemo:7004420 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "PPP_s in the test Same country as source and target at purchasing power parity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6003131 ;
    rheo:2000001 0.8 .

hemo:7004421 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "PPP_t in the test Same country as source and target at purchasing power parity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6003132 ;
    rheo:2000001 0.8 .

hemo:7004422 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "C_t in the test Same country as source and target at purchasing power parity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000039 hemo:6003129 ;
    rheo:2000001 1000.0 .

hemo:7004423 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Same product and price type across the basket"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every c_i is for the same strength and dosage form, as in the PMPRB definition of the highest international price, and for the same price type. Kanavos and colleagues report that ex-factory prices are mostly used and that pricing authorities rely mostly on list prices rather than transaction prices, so confidential discounts are not reflected." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same product and price type across the basket" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BOC-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-product-and-price-type-across-the-basket" .

hemo:7004424 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "All five basket countries priced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each of the five countries has a price. The article treats a missing price as informative, since it may reflect a delayed launch, so a country without a price is dropped from the count, as HE-CF-BOC-002 does, never entered as a zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "All five basket countries priced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BOC-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "all-five-basket-countries-priced" .

hemo:7004425 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Basket aggregates from a named range of converted prices"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000704 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the five converted prices in the range BasketPrices, the four formulas return the values held in BasketMean, BasketLowest, BasketHighest and BasketMedian. AVERAGE, MIN, MAX and MEDIAN skip blank cells, so a country without a price is left blank, never set to zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Basket aggregates from a named range of converted prices" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-BOC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "basket-aggregates-from-a-named-range-of-converted-prices" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AVERAGE(BasketPrices); =MIN(BasketPrices); =MAX(BasketPrices); =MEDIAN(BasketPrices)" .

hemo:7004426 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's Basket A aggregates reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With Basket A in BasketPrices the mean is 105, the lowest price 80 and the highest 140. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a range that misses the country priced at 140, which gives a mean of 96.25 and a highest price of 110." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's Basket A aggregates reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BOC-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-basket-a-aggregates-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(BasketMean-105)<1E-9,BasketLowest=80,BasketHighest=140)" .

hemo:7004427 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No zero entered for a missing basket price"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Checks that every number in BasketPrices is above zero, so a country without a price has been left blank. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a zero entered for the unlaunched country of the article's launch-sequencing case, which would pull the mean of Basket B to 77." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No zero entered for a missing basket price" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BOC-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-zero-entered-for-a-missing-basket-price" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=COUNT(BasketPrices)=COUNTIF(BasketPrices,\">0\")" .

hemo:7004428 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's Basket A mean, lowest and highest prices"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Basket A of the article, 80, 95, 100, 110 and 140, has a mean of 105, a lowest price of 80 and a highest price of 140 (illustrative prices already in the home currency)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's Basket A mean, lowest and highest prices" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c_1 = 80; c_2 = 95; c_3 = 100; c_4 = 110; c_5 = 140; P_mean = 105; P_low = 80; P_high = 140" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BOC-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-basket-a-mean-lowest-and-highest-prices" .

hemo:7004429 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's Basket B mean, lowest and highest prices"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Basket B drops the country priced at 140 and adds one priced at 70, so the mean falls to 91, the lowest price to 70 and the highest to 110, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's Basket B mean, lowest and highest prices" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c_1 = 70; c_2 = 80; c_3 = 95; c_4 = 100; c_5 = 110; P_mean = 91; P_low = 70; P_high = 110" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BOC-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-basket-b-mean-lowest-and-highest-prices" .

hemo:7004430 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Basket with one common price"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When all five countries list the same converted price the three aggregates are equal, a limiting case that checks an implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Basket with one common price" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c_1 = 100; c_2 = 100; c_3 = 100; c_4 = 100; c_5 = 100; P_mean = 100; P_low = 100; P_high = 100" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BOC-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "basket-with-one-common-price" .

hemo:7004431 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Proposed price and basket prices on one basis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "P_prop and P_ref are for the same strength, dosage form and price type and in the same currency after HE-FM-BOC-001, so the margin compares like with like." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Proposed price and basket prices on one basis" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BOC-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "proposed-price-and-basket-prices-on-one-basis" .

hemo:7004432 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Price exactly at the basket ceiling accepted"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "A price equal to the ceiling gives D = 0 and is accepted. This follows the wording of each trigger (derived here), since the article's rule cuts prices above 1.25 times the average, the MHLW slide adjusts prices exceeding 1.25 times the foreign average price and the PMPRB Guidelines send prices above the HIP threshold to an In-Depth Review, and none of these texts addresses an exact tie." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Price exactly at the basket ceiling accepted" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BOC-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "price-exactly-at-the-basket-ceiling-accepted" .

hemo:7004433 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Basket ceiling and margin from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000705 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the multiplier in CeilingMultiplier, the reference price in ReferencePrice and the proposed price in ProposedPrice, the first formula returns BasketCeiling and the second CeilingMargin. The third returns TRUE when the price is above the ceiling." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Basket ceiling and margin from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-BOC-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "basket-ceiling-and-margin-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CeilingMultiplier*ReferencePrice; =ProposedPrice-BasketCeiling; =CeilingMargin>0" .

hemo:7004434 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's Basket A ceiling reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With CeilingMultiplier set to 1.25 and ReferencePrice to 105, BasketCeiling is 131.25. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the multiplier entered as 125 for a percentage, which gives 13125, or Basket A's lowest price of 80 used as the reference, which gives 100." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's Basket A ceiling reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BOC-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-basket-a-ceiling-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(BasketCeiling-131.25)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004435 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Margin sign matches the ceiling comparison"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "CeilingMargin is positive exactly when ProposedPrice is above BasketCeiling. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the margin computed as the ceiling minus the price, which for Basket B gives minus 16.25 while the price is above the ceiling." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Margin sign matches the ceiling comparison" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BOC-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "margin-sign-matches-the-ceiling-comparison" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(CeilingMargin>0)=(ProposedPrice>BasketCeiling)" .

hemo:7004436 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's Basket A ceiling at 1.25 times the mean"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Basket A's mean of 105 gives a ceiling of 131.25, so the proposed price of 130 is accepted with 1.25 to spare, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's Basket A ceiling at 1.25 times the mean" ;
    hemo:9000014 "k = 1.25; P_ref = 105; P_prop = 130; T = 131.25; D = -1.25" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BOC-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-basket-a-ceiling-at-1-25-times-the-mean" .

hemo:7004437 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's Basket B ceiling at 1.25 times the mean"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Basket B's mean of 91 gives a ceiling of 113.75, so the same proposed price is 16.25 above it and is cut, as in the article. The change of basket alone moves the ceiling by 17.5." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's Basket B ceiling at 1.25 times the mean" ;
    hemo:9000014 "k = 1.25; P_ref = 91; P_prop = 130; T = 113.75; D = 16.25" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BOC-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-basket-b-ceiling-at-1-25-times-the-mean" .

hemo:7004438 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Highest-price test with a multiplier of one"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Under a PMPRB style test with k = 1 on Basket A's highest price of 140, the proposed price of 130 is 10 below the ceiling and would not trigger an in-depth review (illustrative prices)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Highest-price test with a multiplier of one" ;
    hemo:9000014 "k = 1; P_ref = 140; P_prop = 130; T = 140; D = -10" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BOC-012" ;
    hemo:9000023 "highest-price-test-with-a-multiplier-of-one" .

hemo:7004439 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Adjustment limited to the pricing methods named by the MHLW"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The adjustment applies to the cost calculation method and to the similar efficacy comparison method for products without similar pharmacological actions, as described in 2021. Later revisions were not checked at a primary source." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Adjustment limited to the pricing methods named by the MHLW" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BOC-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "adjustment-limited-to-the-pricing-methods-named-by-the-mhlw" .

hemo:7004440 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Foreign average price trimmed before the adjustment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "P_fap follows the slide's trimming rules. With two or more prices and the highest more than 2.5 times the lowest, the average excludes the highest; with three or more prices and the highest more than twice the average of the others, the highest is counted as twice that average. The slide does not say which rule applies when both conditions hold, so P_fap is entered here as a given value." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Foreign average price trimmed before the adjustment" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-BOC-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "foreign-average-price-trimmed-before-the-adjustment" .

hemo:7004441 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Japanese foreign average price adjustment with nested IF"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000706 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With CalculatedPrice and ForeignAveragePrice named, the formula writes the slide's two branches with IF in classic Excel and the result is held in AdjustedPrice." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Japanese foreign average price adjustment with nested IF" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-BOC-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "japanese-foreign-average-price-adjustment-with-nested-if" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(CalculatedPrice>1.25*ForeignAveragePrice,(CalculatedPrice/ForeignAveragePrice/3+5/6)*ForeignAveragePrice,IF(CalculatedPrice<0.75*ForeignAveragePrice,MIN((CalculatedPrice/ForeignAveragePrice/3+1/2)*ForeignAveragePrice,2*CalculatedPrice),CalculatedPrice))" .

hemo:7004442 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Branch form agrees with the min and max form"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "AdjustedPrice, built with IF, equals the single-line min and max form for the same inputs. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the cap at twice the calculated value left out of AdjustedPrice; for a calculated price of 20 and a foreign average of 105 the cell then gives about 59.17 against 40." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Branch form agrees with the min and max form" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BOC-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "branch-form-agrees-with-the-min-and-max-form" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(AdjustedPrice-MIN(MAX(CalculatedPrice,MIN(CalculatedPrice/3+ForeignAveragePrice/2,2*CalculatedPrice)),CalculatedPrice/3+5*ForeignAveragePrice/6))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004443 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Reduction of a price of 140 against a foreign average of 105 reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With CalculatedPrice set to 140 and ForeignAveragePrice to 105, AdjustedPrice is 134.1667 to four decimal places. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the price cut to the threshold of 131.25, or the raise branch used, which gives about 99.17." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Reduction of a price of 140 against a foreign average of 105 reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-BOC-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "reduction-of-a-price-of-140-against-a-foreign-average-of-105-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(AdjustedPrice-134.1667)<0.0001" .

hemo:7004444 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Calculated price above 1.25 times the foreign average"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With an illustrative foreign average of 105, Basket A's mean, a calculated price of 140 exceeds 131.25 and is reduced to 140/3 plus 87.5, about 134.17 (illustrative values, computed here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Calculated price above 1.25 times the foreign average" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_calc = 140; P_fap = 105; P_adj = 134.1667" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BOC-013" ;
    hemo:9000023 "calculated-price-above-1-25-times-the-foreign-average" .

hemo:7004445 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Calculated price within the band around the foreign average"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A calculated price of 130 lies between 78.75 and 131.25 and is unchanged (illustrative values)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Calculated price within the band around the foreign average" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_calc = 130; P_fap = 105; P_adj = 130" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BOC-014" ;
    hemo:9000023 "calculated-price-within-the-band-around-the-foreign-average" .

hemo:7004446 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Calculated price below 0.75 times the foreign average"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A calculated price of 60 is below 78.75 and is raised to 20 plus 52.5, that is 72.5, within the cap of 120 (illustrative values, computed here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Calculated price below 0.75 times the foreign average" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_calc = 60; P_fap = 105; P_adj = 72.5" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BOC-015" ;
    hemo:9000023 "calculated-price-below-0-75-times-the-foreign-average" .

hemo:7004447 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Raise limited to twice the calculated price"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A calculated price of 20 would be raised to about 59.17 by the formula but is capped at twice the calculated value, 40 (illustrative values, computed here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Raise limited to twice the calculated price" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_calc = 20; P_fap = 105; P_adj = 40" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-BOC-016" ;
    hemo:9000023 "raise-limited-to-twice-the-calculated-price" .

hemo:7004448 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant death probability within each stratum run to absorption"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each stratum is a two-state Alive and Dead cohort with the same death probability in every cycle, run until everyone has died and counted at the start of each cycle less half a cycle, as Sonnenberg and Beck describe for a matrix solution. Where probabilities change with age the stratum totals come from the trace of HE-CF-CSIM-001 and the closed form does not apply." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant death probability within each stratum run to absorption" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-COHM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-death-probability-within-each-stratum-run-to-absorption" .

hemo:7004449 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Fixed and exhaustive stratum shares at model start"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The two strata are defined by characteristics known at the start, do not overlap and together make up the cohort, so their shares are w and 1 minus w; every member of a stratum has its death probability, the homogeneity within states that the ISPOR-SMDM report asks for." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fixed and exhaustive stratum shares at model start" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-COHM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fixed-and-exhaustive-stratum-shares-at-model-start" .

hemo:7004450 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Undiscounted life expectancy in the averaged-cohort gap"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Nothing is discounted, as in the article's example. With discounting each stratum total comes from HE-FM-MKCH-001 or HE-CF-MKCH-001 and the gap no longer has the closed form given in HE-FM-COHM-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Undiscounted life expectancy in the averaged-cohort gap" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-COHM-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "undiscounted-life-expectancy-in-the-averaged-cohort-gap" .

hemo:7004451 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Averaged-cohort life-expectancy gap from named stratum cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000707 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the first stratum's share in ShareLow and the two death probabilities in DeathProbLow and DeathProbHigh, the four formulas return q_bar in a cell named MeanDeathProb, L_strat in LifeExpStrat, L_avg in LifeExpAvg and gap_L in GapLifeYears." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Averaged-cohort life-expectancy gap from named stratum cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-COHM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "averaged-cohort-life-expectancy-gap-from-named-stratum-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ShareLow*DeathProbLow+(1-ShareLow)*DeathProbHigh; =ShareLow*(1/DeathProbLow-0.5)+(1-ShareLow)*(1/DeathProbHigh-0.5); =1/MeanDeathProb-0.5; =LifeExpStrat-LifeExpAvg" .

hemo:7004452 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Averaged-cohort gap cell matches its closed form"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "GapLifeYears is compared with w(1 minus w)(q_1 minus q_2) squared divided by q_1 q_2 q_bar, computed from the three inputs without either life expectancy. Expected result TRUE. With the article's inputs, a LifeExpStrat cell that leaves out the half cycle while LifeExpAvg keeps it gives a gap of 5.8333 against 5.3333 and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Averaged-cohort gap cell matches its closed form" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-COHM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "averaged-cohort-gap-cell-matches-its-closed-form" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(GapLifeYears-ShareLow*(1-ShareLow)*(DeathProbLow-DeathProbHigh)^2/(DeathProbLow*DeathProbHigh*(ShareLow*DeathProbLow+(1-ShareLow)*DeathProbHigh)))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004453 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Averaged-cohort gap is never negative"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Because 1/q is convex, the share-weighted mean of the stratum life expectancies is never below the life expectancy at the mean probability. Expected result TRUE. A gap cell written as LifeExpAvg minus LifeExpStrat gives minus 5.3333 for the article's inputs and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Averaged-cohort gap is never negative" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-COHM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "averaged-cohort-gap-is-never-negative" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=GapLifeYears>=0" .

hemo:7004454 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's standard-care strata give a gap of 16/3 years"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With ShareLow at 0.5, DeathProbLow at 0.05 and DeathProbHigh at 0.25, GapLifeYears is 16/3, about 5.3333. Expected result TRUE. Averaging the annual death rates instead of the probabilities gives a mean rate of about 0.1695, a probability of about 0.1559, a LifeExpAvg of about 5.9143 and a gap of about 5.5857, and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's standard-care strata give a gap of 16/3 years" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-COHM-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-standard-care-strata-give-a-gap-of-16-3-years" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(GapLifeYears-16/3)<0.0001" .

hemo:7004455 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's risk groups under standard care run separately and averaged"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative population is split equally between annual death probabilities of 0.05 and 0.25. Run separately and weighted, life expectancy is 11.5 years, while one cohort at the mean probability of 0.15 gives 6.17 years, so the averaged cohort falls 5.33 years short, 46% of the stratified value, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's risk groups under standard care run separately and averaged" ;
    hemo:9000014 "w = 0.5; q_1 = 0.05; q_2 = 0.25; q_bar = 0.15; L_strat = 11.5; L_avg = 6.1667; gap_L = 5.3333" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-COHM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-risk-groups-under-standard-care-run-separately-and-averaged" .

hemo:7004456 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's risk groups with treatment run separately and averaged"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the treatment the article's probabilities fall to 0.04 and 0.20, with life expectancies of 24.5 and 4.5 years. The stratified mean is 14.5 years (computed here for illustration) against 7.83 years for one cohort at 0.12, a gap of 6.67 years." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's risk groups with treatment run separately and averaged" ;
    hemo:9000014 "w = 0.5; q_1 = 0.04; q_2 = 0.20; q_bar = 0.12; L_strat = 14.5; L_avg = 7.8333; gap_L = 6.6667" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-COHM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-risk-groups-with-treatment-run-separately-and-averaged" .

hemo:7004457 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal stratum death probabilities leave no averaged-cohort gap"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When both strata have an annual death probability of 0.15 the averaged cohort is exact and the gap is zero, a limiting case that checks an implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equal stratum death probabilities leave no averaged-cohort gap" ;
    hemo:9000014 "w = 0.5; q_1 = 0.15; q_2 = 0.15; q_bar = 0.15; L_strat = 6.1667; L_avg = 6.1667; gap_L = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-COHM-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equal-stratum-death-probabilities-leave-no-averaged-cohort-gap" .

hemo:7004458 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Averaged-cohort gap with 80% of the cohort at low risk"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With illustrative shares of 80% at 0.05 and 20% at 0.25 the mean probability is 0.09, the stratified life expectancy 16.3 years and the averaged cohort 10.61 years, a gap of about 5.69 years (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Averaged-cohort gap with 80% of the cohort at low risk" ;
    hemo:9000014 "w = 0.8; q_1 = 0.05; q_2 = 0.25; q_bar = 0.09; L_strat = 16.3; L_avg = 10.6111; gap_L = 5.6889" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-COHM-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "averaged-cohort-gap-with-80-of-the-cohort-at-low-risk" .

hemo:7004459 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Flows held constant within each step and steps short enough to keep compartments non-negative"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The Euler step treats every flow as constant over the step, as the article does with its one-day steps. The step has to be short relative to the time people spend in the compartment, so that F_out times dt stays below x_now plus the inflows over the step; a longer step can return a negative count. Pitman and colleagues note that the Euler method often used in spreadsheet models is less accurate than other numerical techniques." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Flows held constant within each step and steps short enough to keep compartments non-negative" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CMPM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "flows-held-constant-within-each-step-and-steps-short-enough-to-keep-compartments-non-negative" .

hemo:7004460 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Euler step of one compartment from named flow cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000708 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the start count in CompNow, the total inflow and outflow rates in InflowRate and OutflowRate, the external inflow and outflow in BirthInflow and DeathOutflow and the step length in StepLength, the first cell returns the count at the end of the step, held in a cell named CompNext. The second flags a step long enough to make the compartment negative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Euler step of one compartment from named flow cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CMPM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "euler-step-of-one-compartment-from-named-flow-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CompNow+(InflowRate-OutflowRate+BirthInflow-DeathOutflow)*StepLength; =IF(CompNext<0,\"step too long\",\"ok\")" .

hemo:7004461 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's day-1 infectious count reproduced in the held compartment cell"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's day-1 inputs for the infectious compartment entered (100 at the start, inflow 29.7, outflow 10, no births or deaths, a step of 1 day), the held cell CompNext returns 119.7. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the outflow added instead of subtracted, which gives 139.7, or the inflow left out, which gives 90." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's day-1 infectious count reproduced in the held compartment cell" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CMPM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-day-1-infectious-count-reproduced-in-the-held-compartment-cell" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(CompNext-119.7)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004462 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's infectious compartment after day 1 without vaccination"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative closed population, 29.7 new infections enter the infectious compartment on day 1 and 10 recoveries leave it, with no births or deaths, so the compartment ends the day with 119.7 people against 100 at the start." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's infectious compartment after day 1 without vaccination" ;
    hemo:9000014 "x_now = 100; F_in = 29.7; F_out = 10; b = 0; d = 0; dt = 1; x_next = 119.7" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CMPM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-infectious-compartment-after-day-1-without-vaccination" .

hemo:7004463 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative susceptible compartment of an open population with births and deaths"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With illustrative births of 2 a day entering the susceptible compartment and deaths of 0.4 a day leaving it, the article's 29.7 day-1 infections leave 9,871.9 of the 9,900 susceptibles, against 9,870.3 in the closed population." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative susceptible compartment of an open population with births and deaths" ;
    hemo:9000014 "x_now = 9900; F_in = 0; F_out = 29.7; b = 2; d = 0.4; dt = 1; x_next = 9871.9" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CMPM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-susceptible-compartment-of-an-open-population-with-births-and-deaths" .

hemo:7004464 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Compartment with no flows left unchanged by an Euler step"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With every flow at zero the compartment keeps its count whatever the step length, a limiting case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Compartment with no flows left unchanged by an Euler step" ;
    hemo:9000014 "x_now = 500; F_in = 0; F_out = 0; b = 0; d = 0; dt = 7; x_next = 500" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CMPM-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "compartment-with-no-flows-left-unchanged-by-an-euler-step" .

hemo:7004465 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Homogeneous mixing and contacts that do not grow with population size"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every susceptible person is equally likely to meet every infectious person, and the number of contacts per person does not depend on population size, the standard incidence that Hethcote judges more realistic for human diseases than simple mass action. Mixing that differs between groups is handled by HE-FM-CMPM-003. lambda and beta share one time unit." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Homogeneous mixing and contacts that do not grow with population size" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CMPM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "homogeneous-mixing-and-contacts-that-do-not-grow-with-population-size" .

hemo:7004466 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Force of infection read as a rate and converted to a probability only through the hazard"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "lambda is a rate, so lambda times dt is close to the probability of infection in a step only when it is small. Under a constant hazard the probability is 1 minus exp(minus lambda dt) by HE-FM-TP-001, which the article applies to its day 1, with lambda of 0.003 per day giving 0.0029955 and 29.66 infections among 9,900 susceptibles rather than 29.7." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Force of infection read as a rate and converted to a probability only through the hazard" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CMPM-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "force-of-infection-read-as-a-rate-and-converted-to-a-probability-only-through-the-hazard" .

hemo:7004467 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Force of infection and new infection flow from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000709 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the transmission rate in TransRate, the numbers infectious and susceptible in InfectiousNow and SusceptibleNow and the population in PopTotal, the first cell returns the force of infection, held in ForceInf, and the second the flow of new infections, held in NewInfRate." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Force of infection and new infection flow from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CMPM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "force-of-infection-and-new-infection-flow-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=TransRate*InfectiousNow/PopTotal; =ForceInf*SusceptibleNow" .

hemo:7004468 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's day-1 force of infection and new infections reproduced in the held cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's day-1 inputs entered (beta 0.3, 100 infectious, 9,900 susceptible, population 10,000), ForceInf holds 0.003 and NewInfRate holds 29.7. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the mass action form without division by the population, which gives a force of 30 and 297,000 infections a day, or the susceptible count used as the denominator, which gives about 0.00303 and 30.0." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's day-1 force of infection and new infections reproduced in the held cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CMPM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-day-1-force-of-infection-and-new-infections-reproduced-in-the-held-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(ForceInf-0.003)<1E-12,ABS(NewInfRate-29.7)<1E-9)" .

hemo:7004469 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's day-1 force of infection and new infections without vaccination"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With beta of 0.3 per day, 100 infectious people in an illustrative population of 10,000 and 9,900 susceptibles, the force of infection is 0.003 per day and new infections are 29.7 a day, the article's day-1 figures." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's day-1 force of infection and new infections without vaccination" ;
    hemo:9000014 "beta = 0.3; I = 100; N = 10000; S = 9900; lambda = 0.003; F_SI = 29.7" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CMPM-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-day-1-force-of-infection-and-new-infections-without-vaccination" .

hemo:7004470 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's day-2 force of infection without a programme"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "After day 1 the article has 119.7 infectious and 9,870.3 susceptible people, so the force of infection rises to 0.003591 per day and new infections to about 35.44 a day." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's day-2 force of infection without a programme" ;
    hemo:9000014 "beta = 0.3; I = 119.7; N = 10000; S = 9870.3; lambda = 0.003591; F_SI = 35.4442" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CMPM-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-day-2-force-of-infection-without-a-programme" .

hemo:7004471 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No infectious people gives no force of infection"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With nobody infectious the force of infection and the flow of new infections are zero whatever the transmission rate, a limiting case for checking an implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No infectious people gives no force of infection" ;
    hemo:9000014 "beta = 0.3; I = 0; N = 10000; S = 9900; lambda = 0; F_SI = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CMPM-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-infectious-people-gives-no-force-of-infection" .

hemo:7004472 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Random mixing within each group and a transmission matrix fixed over the period"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "People within a group are alike and mix at random, so only the infectious proportion of each group matters, and the matrix of beta_ab stays fixed over the period modelled. Pitman and colleagues ask published models to tabulate the mixing matrix and give the type of mixing assumed. Rows index the group being infected and columns the group of the infectious person, so beta_12 and beta_21 are different entries." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Random mixing within each group and a transmission matrix fixed over the period" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CMPM-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "random-mixing-within-each-group-and-a-transmission-matrix-fixed-over-the-period" .

hemo:7004473 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-group force of infection on group 1 from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000710 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the transmission rates in TransWithin1 and TransFrom2To1, the infectious counts in Infectious1 and Infectious2 and the group sizes in PopGroup1 and PopGroup2, the cell returns the force of infection on group 1, held in ForceGroup1. Swapping the group indices in a second cell gives the force on group 2." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-group force of infection on group 1 from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CMPM-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-group-force-of-infection-on-group-1-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=TransWithin1*Infectious1/PopGroup1+TransFrom2To1*Infectious2/PopGroup2" .

hemo:7004474 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-group force of infection under random mixing equals the single-group value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the random-mixing inputs entered (0.06 and 0.24, 40 of 2,000 and 60 of 8,000 infectious), ForceGroup1 holds 0.003. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the infectious counts divided by the whole population instead of the group sizes, which gives 0.00168, or the two group terms added without their transmission rates." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-group force of infection under random mixing equals the single-group value" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CMPM-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-group-force-of-infection-under-random-mixing-equals-the-single-group-value" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ForceGroup1-0.003)<1E-12" .

hemo:7004475 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative children and adults with mostly within-group transmission"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative inputs. In a group of 2,000 children with 40 infectious and a group of 8,000 adults with 60 infectious, a within-child transmission rate of 0.5 and an adult-to-child rate of 0.2 per day give a force of infection on children of 0.01 plus 0.0015, or 0.0115 per day." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative children and adults with mostly within-group transmission" ;
    hemo:9000014 "beta_11 = 0.5; beta_12 = 0.2; I_1 = 40; N_1 = 2000; I_2 = 60; N_2 = 8000; lambda_1 = 0.0115" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CMPM-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-children-and-adults-with-mostly-within-group-transmission" .

hemo:7004476 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Random mixing reduces the two-group force of infection to the single-group form"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With beta of 0.3 and beta_1b set to 0.3 times N_b/N, that is 0.06 and 0.24, the same groups give lambda_1 of 0.003, the single-group value 0.3 times 100/10,000 of the article's day 1, a limiting case (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Random mixing reduces the two-group force of infection to the single-group form" ;
    hemo:9000014 "beta_11 = 0.06; beta_12 = 0.24; I_1 = 40; N_1 = 2000; I_2 = 60; N_2 = 8000; lambda_1 = 0.003" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CMPM-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "random-mixing-reduces-the-two-group-force-of-infection-to-the-single-group-form" .

hemo:7004477 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Closed population with lasting immunity and an exponential infectious period in the SIR step"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "There are no births, deaths or migration over the period, recovery gives lasting immunity, and the constant recovery rate gamma implies an exponentially distributed infectious period with mean 1/gamma, the waiting time that Hethcote attaches to terms such as gamma I. Open populations add the external flows b and d of HE-FM-CMPM-001 to each compartment." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Closed population with lasting immunity and an exponential infectious period in the SIR step" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CMPM-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "closed-population-with-lasting-immunity-and-an-exponential-infectious-period-in-the-sir-step" .

hemo:7004478 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Both SIR flows computed from start-of-step counts before any compartment is updated"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "F and G are computed from S and I at the start of the step and then applied together. With gamma dt or lambda dt close to 1 the step moves most of a compartment at once and can overshoot, so the step length is chosen short enough that a shorter step leaves the results of interest unchanged." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Both SIR flows computed from start-of-step counts before any compartment is updated" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CMPM-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "both-sir-flows-computed-from-start-of-step-counts-before-any-compartment-is-updated" .

hemo:7004479 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "SIR Euler step from named compartment and rate cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000711 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the rates in TransRate and RecoveryRate, the population in PopTotal, the step in StepLength and the start counts in SusNow, InfNow and RecNow, the five cells return the new infections (NewInfections), the recoveries (NewRecoveries) and the end-of-step counts SusNext, InfNext and RecNext. Copying the block down one row per step with each row's end counts as the next row's start counts runs the model forward." ;
    hemo:9000009 "SIR Euler step from named compartment and rate cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CMPM-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "sir-euler-step-from-named-compartment-and-rate-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=TransRate*SusNow*InfNow/PopTotal*StepLength; =RecoveryRate*InfNow*StepLength; =SusNow-NewInfections; =InfNow+NewInfections-NewRecoveries; =RecNow+NewRecoveries" .

hemo:7004480 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Population conserved across the three SIR compartments after a step"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The held end-of-step counts SusNext, InfNext and RecNext add up to the start counts. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the infections for the infectious compartment recomputed from the already updated susceptibles, which for the article's day 1 gives a total of 9,999.9109, or a flow added to one compartment but not taken from the other." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Population conserved across the three SIR compartments after a step" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CMPM-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "population-conserved-across-the-three-sir-compartments-after-a-step" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(SusNext+InfNext+RecNext-(SusNow+InfNow+RecNow))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004481 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's day-1 SIR compartments reproduced in the held cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's day-1 inputs entered, SusNext, InfNext and RecNext hold 9,870.3, 119.7 and 10. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the mean infectious period of 10 days entered as the recovery rate, which gives 1,000 recoveries and minus 870.3 infectious people." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's day-1 SIR compartments reproduced in the held cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CMPM-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-day-1-sir-compartments-reproduced-in-the-held-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(SusNext-9870.3)<1E-9,ABS(InfNext-119.7)<1E-9,ABS(RecNext-10)<1E-9)" .

hemo:7004482 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's day-1 SIR step without vaccination"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative population of 10,000 with 9,900 susceptible and 100 infectious people, beta of 0.3 and gamma of 0.1 per day has 29.7 new infections and 10 recoveries on day 1, leaving 9,870.3 susceptible, 119.7 infectious and 10 recovered, still 10,000 in all." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's day-1 SIR step without vaccination" ;
    hemo:9000014 "beta = 0.3; gamma = 0.1; N = 10000; dt = 1; S = 9900; I = 100; R = 0; F = 29.7; G = 10; S_next = 9870.3; I_next = 119.7; R_next = 10" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CMPM-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-day-1-sir-step-without-vaccination" .

hemo:7004483 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's day-1 SIR step after 2,000 susceptible people are vaccinated"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A perfect vaccine given to 2,000 susceptible people moves them to the immune compartment before day 1, so R starts at 2,000. Day-1 infections fall to 23.7, 6.0 fewer than without the programme, and the compartments become 7,876.3, 113.7 and 2,010." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's day-1 SIR step after 2,000 susceptible people are vaccinated" ;
    hemo:9000014 "beta = 0.3; gamma = 0.1; N = 10000; dt = 1; S = 7900; I = 100; R = 2000; F = 23.7; G = 10; S_next = 7876.3; I_next = 113.7; R_next = 2010" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CMPM-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-day-1-sir-step-after-2-000-susceptible-people-are-vaccinated" .

hemo:7004484 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Zero transmission rate leaves recoveries as the only flow"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With beta set to zero no one is infected and the infectious compartment only empties, the check the article lists for verifying a compartmental model, a limiting case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Zero transmission rate leaves recoveries as the only flow" ;
    hemo:9000014 "beta = 0; gamma = 0.1; N = 10000; dt = 1; S = 9900; I = 100; R = 0; F = 0; G = 10; S_next = 9900; I_next = 90; R_next = 10" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CMPM-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "zero-transmission-rate-leaves-recoveries-as-the-only-flow" .

hemo:7004485 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Both runs share parameters and differ only in their compartment counts"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The runs share beta, N and dt and differ only in the counts at the start of the step, as when a perfect vaccine moves people into the immune compartment. A_ind is zero or above whenever the programme has not raised the infectious count (derived from the simplified form). The static count takes lambda_b from the no-programme run at the same step, as the article does, so it changes over time but not with the programme; the WHO guide and Jit and Brisson describe the force of infection of a static model as constant, which is the case of a no-programme run whose force does not change. Pitman and colleagues note that indirect effects can also raise complications through a higher age at infection, as for hepatitis A and varicella, which a count of infections does not show." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Both runs share parameters and differ only in their compartment counts" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CMPM-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "both-runs-share-parameters-and-differ-only-in-their-compartment-counts" .

hemo:7004486 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Static and dynamic infections averted in one step from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000712 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With TransRate, PopTotal and StepLength as before and the start-of-step counts of the two runs in SusBase, InfBase, SusProg and InfProg, the four cells return ForceBase, AvertStatic, AvertDynamic and AvertIndirect." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Static and dynamic infections averted in one step from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CMPM-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "static-and-dynamic-infections-averted-in-one-step-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=TransRate*InfBase/PopTotal; =ForceBase*(SusBase-SusProg)*StepLength; =TransRate*(SusBase*InfBase-SusProg*InfProg)/PopTotal*StepLength; =AvertDynamic-AvertStatic" .

hemo:7004487 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Indirect infections averted equal the programme susceptibles times the fall in infectious people"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The held cell AvertIndirect equals beta times SusProg times (InfBase minus InfProg) times the step over PopTotal, the simplified form. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the static count computed with the programme's own force of infection, which for the article's day 2 gives AvertIndirect of about 1.7767 against 1.4177." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Indirect infections averted equal the programme susceptibles times the fall in infectious people" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CMPM-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "indirect-infections-averted-equal-the-programme-susceptibles-times-the-fall-in-infectious-people" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(AvertIndirect-TransRate*SusProg*(InfBase-InfProg)/PopTotal*StepLength)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004488 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's day-2 infections averted counted statically and dynamically"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "On day 2 the article's no-programme run has 9,870.3 susceptible and 119.7 infectious people, and the programme run 7,876.3 and 113.7. The static count averts about 7.16 infections and the dynamic count about 8.58, so about 1.42 are indirect, the article's figures." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's day-2 infections averted counted statically and dynamically" ;
    hemo:9000014 "beta = 0.3; N = 10000; dt = 1; S_b = 9870.3; I_b = 119.7; S_p = 7876.3; I_p = 113.7; lambda_b = 0.003591; A_stat = 7.1605; A_dyn = 8.5782; A_ind = 1.4177" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CMPM-012" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-day-2-infections-averted-counted-statically-and-dynamically" .

hemo:7004489 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's day-1 counts agree when both runs start with the same infectious count"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "On day 1 both runs start with 100 infectious people, so the static and dynamic counts both avert 6.0 infections and the indirect effect is zero, a limiting case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's day-1 counts agree when both runs start with the same infectious count" ;
    hemo:9000014 "beta = 0.3; N = 10000; dt = 1; S_b = 9900; I_b = 100; S_p = 7900; I_p = 100; lambda_b = 0.003; A_stat = 6; A_dyn = 6; A_ind = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CMPM-013" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-day-1-counts-agree-when-both-runs-start-with-the-same-infectious-count" .

hemo:7004490 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Fixed basket measuring pure price change in the consumer price index"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Only prices change between the base period and time t, and the basket is held fixed in composition, quantity and as far as possible quality (section 3.3 of the ONS technical manual). The manual quotes Eurostat's description of the harmonised index as a pure price index and states that it is not a cost of living index (section 2.4.1.2). Section 13.11 defines a cost of living index by the minimum cost of reaching base-period utility, states that because of this stress on minimum cost it will usually give a lower rate of inflation, and describes consumers switching towards goods and services that are becoming relatively cheaper." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fixed basket measuring pure price change in the consumer price index" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CPI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fixed-basket-measuring-pure-price-change-in-the-consumer-price-index" .

hemo:7004491 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Household spending scope of the consumer price index basket"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The basket covers prices paid by households. Section 2.5.5 of the manual states that typically no account is taken of services free at the point of consumption, even if consumers have paid for them indirectly through taxes or National Insurance contributions, and that NHS prescription and dental charges are included but not the full economic cost. An index built this way does not track the cost to the NHS of providing care." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Household spending scope of the consumer price index basket" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CPI-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "household-spending-scope-of-the-consumer-price-index-basket" .

hemo:7004492 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Fixed-basket form valid within one annual link of the CPI"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The basket and its weights are fixed only within one link, so the formula describes a within-year index. Section 12.4.2 of the manual states that a chain-linked index spanning more than one year cannot be represented as the ratio of basket prices or as a weighted average of price relatives, so longer series are built with HE-FM-CPI-003." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fixed-basket form valid within one annual link of the CPI" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CPI-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fixed-basket-form-valid-within-one-annual-link-of-the-cpi" .

hemo:7004493 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Lowe fixed-basket CPI from named price and quantity ranges"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000713 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With base-period prices in PriceBase, time t prices in PriceCurrent and weight reference quantities in QtyWeightRef, three ranges of equal length with one row per item, the formula returns the index; name its cell LoweIndexOut. A helper column named PriceRelative holds each current price divided by its base price and is read by the tests." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Lowe fixed-basket CPI from named price and quantity ranges" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CPI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "lowe-fixed-basket-cpi-from-named-price-and-quantity-ranges" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=100*SUMPRODUCT(PriceCurrent,QtyWeightRef)/SUMPRODUCT(PriceBase,QtyWeightRef)" .

hemo:7004494 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Lowe CPI cell agrees with the share-weighted average of price relatives"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The result cell equals 100 times the average of the price relatives weighted by each item's share of the basket cost at base-period prices, the second form in section 3.3 of the manual, computed by a separate route. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows base and current prices swapped in the result cell, which gives 92.1053 in place of 108.5714 for the worked example, or an unweighted mean of the relatives, which gives 107.5." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Lowe CPI cell agrees with the share-weighted average of price relatives" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CPI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "lowe-cpi-cell-agrees-with-the-share-weighted-average-of-price-relatives" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(LoweIndexOut-100*SUMPRODUCT(PriceBase*QtyWeightRef/SUMPRODUCT(PriceBase,QtyWeightRef),PriceRelative))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004495 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Lowe CPI cell lies within the range of its price relatives"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A weighted average of relatives with positive weights cannot fall below the smallest relative or exceed the largest, so the result is at least 100 times the smallest and at most 100 times the largest (derived from the weighted-average form). Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the factor 100 left out, 1.0857 for the worked example, or base and current prices swapped, 92.1053 against relatives of 105 and 110." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Lowe CPI cell lies within the range of its price relatives" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CPI-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "lowe-cpi-cell-lies-within-the-range-of-its-price-relatives" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(LoweIndexOut>=100*MIN(PriceRelative)-1E-9,LoweIndexOut<=100*MAX(PriceRelative)+1E-9)" .

hemo:7004496 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Lowe index of an illustrative two-item CPI basket"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An illustrative basket holds 50 units of an item priced 2.00 in the base period and 2.20 now, and 4 units of an item priced 10.00 and 10.50. The basket costs 140 at base prices and 152 at current prices, so the index is 108.5714. It lies between the item relatives of 110 and 105 and nearer the first, which accounts for 100 of the 140 of base-period spending." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Lowe index of an illustrative two-item CPI basket" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p1_0 = 2; p2_0 = 10; p1_t = 2.2; p2_t = 10.5; q1_b = 50; q2_b = 4; E_0 = 140; E_t = 152; I_0t = 108.5714" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CPI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "lowe-index-of-an-illustrative-two-item-cpi-basket" .

hemo:7004497 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Lowe index when every CPI basket price rises by the same 5%"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When every price rises by the same proportion the quantities cancel and the index is 100 times that proportion, 105 for an illustrative rise of 5%, a limiting case that checks an implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Lowe index when every CPI basket price rises by the same 5%" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p1_0 = 2; p2_0 = 10; p1_t = 2.1; p2_t = 10.5; q1_b = 50; q2_b = 4; E_0 = 140; E_t = 147; I_0t = 105" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CPI-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "lowe-index-when-every-cpi-basket-price-rises-by-the-same-5" .

hemo:7004498 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal weights for the prices in a Jevons elementary aggregate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "At the lowest level of aggregation detailed weights are not available, so the manual uses unweighted formulae that treat all the products as equally important, each price taking a weight equal to the reciprocal of the number of prices in the stratum (section 3.4). Differences in spending between the outlets or varieties priced are not reflected." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equal weights for the prices in a Jevons elementary aggregate" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CPI-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equal-weights-for-the-prices-in-a-jevons-elementary-aggregate" .

hemo:7004499 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Matched products with positive prices in a Jevons elementary aggregate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each relative compares the same product at both dates. The manual states that if a product has no price matching one in the base month, that price must be excluded from the calculation or a quality adjustment made (section 3.4). The geometric mean also needs every price to be above zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Matched products with positive prices in a Jevons elementary aggregate" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CPI-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "matched-products-with-positive-prices-in-a-jevons-elementary-aggregate" .

hemo:7004500 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Jevons elementary aggregate from named matched price ranges"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000714 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With base-month prices in ElemPriceBase and month t prices of the same products in ElemPriceCurrent, a helper column named ElemRelative holds each current price divided by its base price, and GEOMEAN, a classic Excel function, returns the Jevons index; name its cell JevonsOut." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Jevons elementary aggregate from named matched price ranges" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CPI-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "jevons-elementary-aggregate-from-named-matched-price-ranges" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=GEOMEAN(ElemRelative)" .

hemo:7004501 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Jevons cell agrees with the ratio of geometric mean prices"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The result cell equals the geometric mean of current prices divided by that of base prices, the equivalent form given in section 3.4 of the manual. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows an arithmetic mean of the relatives in the result cell, 1 in place of 0.996655 for the worked example." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Jevons cell agrees with the ratio of geometric mean prices" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CPI-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "jevons-cell-agrees-with-the-ratio-of-geometric-mean-prices" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(JevonsOut-GEOMEAN(ElemPriceCurrent)/GEOMEAN(ElemPriceBase))<1E-12" .

hemo:7004502 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Jevons cell reproduces the illustrative three-price example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the worked example prices in the two ranges, the result cell holds 0.996655 to six decimal places. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the Carli arithmetic mean of relatives, 1, or the Dutot ratio of average prices, 0.957143, in the result cell." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Jevons cell reproduces the illustrative three-price example" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CPI-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "jevons-cell-reproduces-the-illustrative-three-price-example" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(JevonsOut-0.996655)<0.0000005" .

hemo:7004503 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Jevons aggregate of three illustrative matched prices"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative prices of 1.00, 2.00 and 4.00 in the base month and 1.10, 2.00 and 3.60 now give relatives of 1.1, 1 and 0.9. Their geometric mean is 0.996655, a fall of about 0.33%, while their arithmetic mean is exactly 1 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Jevons aggregate of three illustrative matched prices" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p1_0 = 1; p2_0 = 2; p3_0 = 4; p1_t = 1.1; p2_t = 2; p3_t = 3.6; J = 0.996655" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CPI-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "jevons-aggregate-of-three-illustrative-matched-prices" .

hemo:7004504 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Jevons aggregate when all matched prices rise by 2%"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Equal relatives of 1.02 give a Jevons index of 1.02 whatever the price levels, a limiting case that checks an implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Jevons aggregate when all matched prices rise by 2%" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p1_0 = 1; p2_0 = 2; p3_0 = 4; p1_t = 1.02; p2_t = 2.04; p3_t = 4.08; J = 1.02" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CPI-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "jevons-aggregate-when-all-matched-prices-rise-by-2" .

hemo:7004505 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "December level and links from one CPI series and class"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each link compares prices within its own period, the January index against the previous December and later months against January (section 3.6), so the December level and both links come from the same series and class. The CPIH, CPI and RPI differ in coverage and methodology (ONS QMI), so a CPI link chained onto a CPIH level belongs to neither series." ;
    hemo:9000009 "December level and links from one CPI series and class" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CPI-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "december-level-and-links-from-one-cpi-series-and-class" .

hemo:7004506 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Chain-linked CPI series not a fixed basket over several years"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Section 12.4.2 of the manual states that a chain-linked index spanning more than one year cannot be represented as the ratio of basket prices or as a weighted average of price relatives, because the weights are not constant and the list of items is not fixed. A ratio of two chained levels therefore measures price change through a sequence of baskets." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Chain-linked CPI series not a fixed basket over several years" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CPI-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "chain-linked-cpi-series-not-a-fixed-basket-over-several-years" .

hemo:7004507 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Chain-linked CPI level from named December and link cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000715 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the December level in IndexDec, the January link in LinkJan and the link for the month in LinkMonth, the cell returns the chained level; name it ChainedIndexOut." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Chain-linked CPI level from named December and link cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CPI-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "chain-linked-cpi-level-from-named-december-and-link-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IndexDec*LinkJan/100*LinkMonth/100" .

hemo:7004508 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Unchaining the chained CPI level reproduces the month link"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Dividing the result by the chained January level, IndexDec times LinkJan over 100, and multiplying by 100 returns the link for the month, the unchaining step that section 11.6 of the manual applies to February to December. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the links added as point changes, 121.1 for the illustrative values, which unchains to 101.32 in place of 101.5." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Unchaining the chained CPI level reproduces the month link" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CPI-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "unchaining-the-chained-cpi-level-reproduces-the-month-link" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(100*ChainedIndexOut/(IndexDec*LinkJan/100)-LinkMonth)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004509 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "RPI July 1987 chaining reproduces the 4.4% change in the manual"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With 394.5, 100 and 101.8 in the input cells, the change of the result against 384.7 for July 1986 rounds to 4.4%, as section 11.2 reports. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the link added as 1.8 points, which gives 3.0%, or the division by 100 left out." ;
    hemo:9000009 "RPI July 1987 chaining reproduces the 4.4% change in the manual" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CPI-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "rpi-july-1987-chaining-reproduces-the-4-4-change-in-the-manual" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(IndexDec=394.5,LinkJan=100,LinkMonth=101.8,ROUND(100*(ChainedIndexOut/384.7-1),1)=4.4)" .

hemo:7004510 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "RPI July 1987 chained onto the January 1974 reference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Section 11.2 of the manual chains the RPI for July 1987, 101.8 on January 1987 = 100, onto the January 1987 level of 394.5 on January 1974 = 100. Entered with L_Jan = 100 for a series chained once a year, the chained level is 401.601 (computed here, since the manual prints only the change), and against 384.7 for July 1986 it gives the change of 4.4% that the manual reports." ;
    hemo:9000009 "RPI July 1987 chained onto the January 1974 reference" ;
    hemo:9000014 "I_Dec = 394.5; L_Jan = 100; L_t = 101.8; I_t = 401.601" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CPI-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "rpi-july-1987-chained-onto-the-january-1974-reference" .

hemo:7004511 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-link CPI chaining with illustrative December and link values"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An illustrative December level of 120.0, a January link of 99.6 and a link of 101.5 for March give a chained March level of 121.3128 (illustrative inputs, computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-link CPI chaining with illustrative December and link values" ;
    hemo:9000014 "I_Dec = 120; L_Jan = 99.6; L_t = 101.5; I_t = 121.3128" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CPI-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-link-cpi-chaining-with-illustrative-december-and-link-values" .

hemo:7004512 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Chained CPI level for January with the second link at 100"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For January the second link is 100 by definition, so the chained level is the December level times the January link over 100, 119.52 with the illustrative values, a limiting case that checks an implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Chained CPI level for January with the second link at 100" ;
    hemo:9000014 "I_Dec = 120; L_Jan = 99.6; L_t = 100; I_t = 119.52" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CPI-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "chained-cpi-level-for-january-with-the-second-link-at-100" .

hemo:7004513 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant compound rate behind an average annual CPI inflation rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The result is the one constant annual rate that takes I_1 to I_2 over n months with compounding, so it carries no information on how inflation varied within the period (a reading of the section 11.3 equation)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant compound rate behind an average annual CPI inflation rate" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CPI-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-compound-rate-behind-an-average-annual-cpi-inflation-rate" .

hemo:7004514 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Average annual CPI rate taken over a year or more"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Section 11.3 of the manual warns that the equation may produce misleading results for one or two months of change, because the month-to-month change includes a seasonal component and some prices change only once a year, and it prefers a comparison between different years' annual average indices, or at least between the same month in different years." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Average annual CPI rate taken over a year or more" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CPI-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "average-annual-cpi-rate-taken-over-a-year-or-more" .

hemo:7004515 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Both CPI values from one series and reference period"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "I_1 and I_2 come from the same series on the same reference, for example D7BT on 2015 = 100. The CPIH, CPI and RPI differ in both coverage and methodology (ONS QMI), so a rate computed from two different series is the inflation rate of neither." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Both CPI values from one series and reference period" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CPI-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "both-cpi-values-from-one-series-and-reference-period" .

hemo:7004516 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Average annual CPI inflation rate from two named index cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000716 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the earlier index in IndexEarlier, the later index in IndexLater and the months between them in MonthsApart, the cell returns the rate as a decimal; name it AvgRateOut and format it as a percentage." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Average annual CPI inflation rate from two named index cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CPI-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "average-annual-cpi-inflation-rate-from-two-named-index-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(IndexLater/IndexEarlier)^(12/MonthsApart)-1" .

hemo:7004517 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Average annual CPI rate compounds back to the index ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compounding one plus the result over MonthsApart divided by 12 years reproduces the ratio of the two index values. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the simple average of the total change in the result cell, 0.0541 in place of 0.0502 for the financial-year example, which compounds to 1.2347 against a ratio of 1.2165." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Average annual CPI rate compounds back to the index ratio" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CPI-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "average-annual-cpi-rate-compounds-back-to-the-index-ratio" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((1+AvgRateOut)^(MonthsApart/12)-IndexLater/IndexEarlier)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004518 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "January 2019 CPIH annual rate reproduced to one decimal place"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With 104.5, 106.4 and 12 in the input cells, 100 times the result rounds to 1.8, as section 11.2 of the manual reports. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the two index cells swapped, which gives minus 1.8." ;
    hemo:9000009 "January 2019 CPIH annual rate reproduced to one decimal place" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CPI-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "january-2019-cpih-annual-rate-reproduced-to-one-decimal-place" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(IndexEarlier=104.5,IndexLater=106.4,MonthsApart=12,ROUND(100*AvgRateOut,1)=1.8)" .

hemo:7004519 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Annual CPIH inflation rate for January 2019 from the manual"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The CPIH stood at 104.5 in January 2018 and 106.4 in January 2019. With n = 12 the formula gives 0.0182, the 1.8% annual inflation rate that section 11.2 reports, a limiting case where the average equals the 12-month change." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Annual CPIH inflation rate for January 2019 from the manual" ;
    hemo:9000014 "I_1 = 104.5; I_2 = 106.4; n = 12; g = 0.0182" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CPI-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "annual-cpih-inflation-rate-for-january-2019-from-the-manual" .

hemo:7004520 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Average annual CPIH inflation over the manual's 28-month example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Section 11.2 reports a change of 4.1% in the CPIH between April 2015 (99.9) and August 2017 (104.0). Over those 28 months the average annual rate is 0.0174, about 1.7% a year (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Average annual CPIH inflation over the manual's 28-month example" ;
    hemo:9000014 "I_1 = 99.9; I_2 = 104; n = 28; g = 0.0174" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CPI-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "average-annual-cpih-inflation-over-the-manual-s-28-month-example" .

hemo:7004521 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Average annual CPI inflation between the article's two financial years"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's D7BT averages are 108.242 for 2019/20 and 131.675 for 2023/24, four years or 48 months apart, so the average annual CPI rate is 0.0502, about 5.0% a year (computed here for illustration). The averages themselves are built in HE-CF-CPI-002." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Average annual CPI inflation between the article's two financial years" ;
    hemo:9000014 "I_1 = 108.242; I_2 = 131.675; n = 48; g = 0.0502" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CPI-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "average-annual-cpi-inflation-between-the-article-s-two-financial-years" .

hemo:7004522 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Independent programmes each measured against the comparator it would replace"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each Delta_C_i and Delta_E_i is measured against the comparator that programme would replace, on one perspective, price year and health measure. Where a programme comes in mutually exclusive versions for the same patients, the versions enter at their sequential ratios along the cost-effectiveness frontier (HE-FM-CEA-001, HE-CF-CEF-001), each against the next cheaper frontier option, as in the WHO-CHOICE malaria table that Marseille and colleagues reproduce, where each option after the first is compared with the next cheapest." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Independent programmes each measured against the comparator it would replace" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CELT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "independent-programmes-each-measured-against-the-comparator-it-would-replace" .

hemo:7004523 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Positive increments in ascending ICER order, with ties and cost-saving programmes"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Programmes are numbered in ascending order of ICER and Delta_C_i and Delta_E_i are both above zero, so x_i is defined. A programme that costs no more than its comparator and gives no less health, and is strictly better on one of the two, dominates it under HE-FM-DOM-001, ties included, and is adopted outside the table with any saving added to B. Programmes with equal ICERs buy the same health per unit of currency, so their order leaves total health unchanged when they are divisible (derived), while for whole programmes the order can matter (HE-FM-CELT-003)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Positive increments in ascending ICER order, with ties and cost-saving programmes" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CELT-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "positive-increments-in-ascending-icer-order-with-ties-and-cost-saving-programmes" .

hemo:7004524 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Divisible programmes at a constant cost per unit of health with no interdependence"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The marginal programme can be part-funded, each extra unit of currency buys the same health within a programme, and funding one programme leaves the costs and effects of the others unchanged. These are the article's conditions for the greedy rule to give the best allocation, in line with the scalability condition of Marseille and colleagues, and they are the restrictive assumptions that Stinnett and Paltiel relax with mathematical programming for returns to scale, partial and complete indivisibility and programme interdependence." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Divisible programmes at a constant cost per unit of health with no interdependence" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CELT-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "divisible-programmes-at-a-constant-cost-per-unit-of-health-with-no-interdependence" .

hemo:7004525 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Funded share and health of one league-table row from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000717 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the budget in Budget, the cumulative incremental cost of the programmes ranked above in CumCostAbove and the programme's incremental cost and QALYs in IncCost and IncQALY, the first formula returns the share, held in a cell named FundedShare, the second the health bought, held in FundedHealth, and the third the cumulative cost for the next row." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Funded share and health of one league-table row from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CELT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "funded-share-and-health-of-one-league-table-row-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MIN(1,MAX(0,(Budget-CumCostAbove)/IncCost)); =FundedShare*IncQALY; =CumCostAbove+IncCost" .

hemo:7004526 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Spending on a league-table programme equals the smaller of its cost and the budget left"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The share in FundedShare times IncCost equals the smaller of the programme's incremental cost and the budget left, never below zero, computed without the share. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a share formula without the floor at zero, which for the article's programme N below D gives a share of about minus 0.83 and spending of minus GBP 250,000 against zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Spending on a league-table programme equals the smaller of its cost and the budget left" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CELT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "spending-on-a-league-table-programme-equals-the-smaller-of-its-cost-and-the-budget-left" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(FundedShare*IncCost-MIN(IncCost,MAX(0,Budget-CumCostAbove)))<0.01" .

hemo:7004527 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's part-funded share of programme D reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With Budget set to 900,000, CumCostAbove to 750,000, IncCost to 400,000 and IncQALY to 20, FundedShare is 0.375 and FundedHealth 7.5 QALYs, as in step 1 of the article. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the cumulative cost including D itself, GBP 1,150,000, entered as the cost above D, which gives a share of zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's part-funded share of programme D reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CELT-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-part-funded-share-of-programme-d-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(FundedShare-0.375)<0.000001,ABS(FundedHealth-7.5)<0.000001)" .

hemo:7004528 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Programme A at the top of the article's league table funded in full"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative table a health authority has GBP 900,000 for four independent programmes A, B, C and D, each compared with no programme. A heads the table at GBP 5,000 per QALY, nothing is spent above it and its GBP 200,000 fits, so it is funded in full for 40 QALYs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Programme A at the top of the article's league table funded in full" ;
    hemo:9000014 "B = 900000; S_prev = 0; Delta_C_i = 200000; Delta_E_i = 40; x_i = 1; h_i = 40; S_i = 200000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CELT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "programme-a-at-the-top-of-the-article-s-league-table-funded-in-full" .

hemo:7004529 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Marginal programme D part-funded with the GBP 150,000 left"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "After A, B and C, which cost GBP 750,000 in all, GBP 150,000 is left for D, which costs GBP 400,000, so 0.375 of D is funded for 7.5 QALYs, as in step 1 of the article. The cumulative cost passed on is GBP 1,150,000, the last figure in the article's table." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Marginal programme D part-funded with the GBP 150,000 left" ;
    hemo:9000014 "B = 900000; S_prev = 750000; Delta_C_i = 400000; Delta_E_i = 20; x_i = 0.375; h_i = 7.5; S_i = 1150000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CELT-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "marginal-programme-d-part-funded-with-the-gbp-150-000-left" .

hemo:7004530 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Programme N at its true incremental ratio below D left unfunded"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Entered at its true ratio of GBP 30,000 per QALY against the service M it would replace, the article's programme N sits below D with an incremental cost of GBP 300,000 and a gain of 10 QALYs. The cost of the programmes above it, GBP 1,150,000, already exceeds the budget, so its share is zero, a limiting case for the floor at zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Programme N at its true incremental ratio below D left unfunded" ;
    hemo:9000014 "B = 900000; S_prev = 1150000; Delta_C_i = 300000; Delta_E_i = 10; x_i = 0; h_i = 0; S_i = 1450000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CELT-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "programme-n-at-its-true-incremental-ratio-below-d-left-unfunded" .

hemo:7004531 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Ratios ascending and positive with funding flags from the budget fill"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The ratios r_1, r_2, r_3 and r_4 rise in that order and are above zero, and the flags come from the fill of HE-FM-CELT-001 at the stated budget, so the largest product is the ratio of the last programme funded. A programme that dominates its comparator is adopted outside the table (HE-AS-CELT-002) and takes no ratio here." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ratios ascending and positive with funding flags from the budget fill" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CELT-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ratios-ascending-and-positive-with-funding-flags-from-the-budget-fill" .

hemo:7004532 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Budget that binds on the league table for the critical ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The critical ratio measures the opportunity cost only while the budget is exhausted. When B exceeds the cost of every programme in the table, all are funded and money is left over, so lambda_c is still the ratio of the least cost-effective programme adopted but an extra unit of budget buys no health within the table (derived). When B exactly exhausts a programme, lambda_c is that programme's ratio, while the next unit of budget would buy health at the ratio of the programme below it (HE-ER-CELT-003)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Budget that binds on the league table for the critical ratio" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CELT-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "budget-that-binds-on-the-league-table-for-the-critical-ratio" .

hemo:7004533 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Critical ratio from funded shares and ICERs in named ranges"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000718 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the funded shares of the fill in a column range named FundedShares and the programmes' ICERs, in ascending order, in ProgICERs, the formula returns the largest ICER among programmes with a share above zero, held in a cell named CriticalRatio. SUMPRODUCT makes older versions of Excel evaluate the array inside MAX without array entry." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Critical ratio from funded shares and ICERs in named ranges" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CELT-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "critical-ratio-from-funded-shares-and-icers-in-named-ranges" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(MAX((FundedShares>0)*ProgICERs))" .

hemo:7004534 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Critical ratio agrees with a lookup of the budget in the cumulative costs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the cumulative incremental cost of the programmes above each row in an ascending range named CumCostsAbove, starting at 0, MATCH with match type 1 finds the last row whose programmes above cost less than the budget, the row of the last programme funded, and INDEX returns its ICER. Taking a millionth of a unit off the budget keeps a programme that the budget exactly exhausts as the last one funded. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a result cell that takes the largest ICER in the table whatever the budget, GBP 20,000 against GBP 12,500 at a budget of GBP 700,000, or the ratio of the first programme not funded in full, GBP 20,000 against GBP 12,500 at a budget of GBP 750,000." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Critical ratio agrees with a lookup of the budget in the cumulative costs" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CELT-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "critical-ratio-agrees-with-a-lookup-of-the-budget-in-the-cumulative-costs" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(CriticalRatio-INDEX(ProgICERs,MATCH(Budget-0.000001,CumCostsAbove,1)))<0.01" .

hemo:7004535 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Critical ratio of GBP 20,000 per QALY at the article's budget of GBP 900,000"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With GBP 900,000 all four of the article's illustrative programmes receive a share, D in part, so the critical ratio is D's GBP 20,000 per QALY, as in step 1 of the article. An extra GBP 100,000 would then buy 5 more QALYs of D, which has GBP 250,000 still unfunded (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Critical ratio of GBP 20,000 per QALY at the article's budget of GBP 900,000" ;
    hemo:9000014 "f_1 = 1; f_2 = 1; f_3 = 1; f_4 = 1; r_1 = 5000; r_2 = 10000; r_3 = 12500; r_4 = 20000; lambda_c = 20000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CELT-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "critical-ratio-of-gbp-20-000-per-qaly-at-the-article-s-budget-of-gbp-900-000" .

hemo:7004536 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Critical ratio of GBP 12,500 per QALY with a budget of GBP 700,000"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With an illustrative budget of GBP 700,000, C receives 0.8 of its cost and D nothing, so the critical ratio falls to C's GBP 12,500 per QALY. Marseille and colleagues note that the length of the list of interventions deemed cost effective varies with the budget, which is how a league table combines cost-effectiveness with affordability." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Critical ratio of GBP 12,500 per QALY with a budget of GBP 700,000" ;
    hemo:9000014 "f_1 = 1; f_2 = 1; f_3 = 1; f_4 = 0; r_1 = 5000; r_2 = 10000; r_3 = 12500; r_4 = 20000; lambda_c = 12500" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CELT-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "critical-ratio-of-gbp-12-500-per-qaly-with-a-budget-of-gbp-700-000" .

hemo:7004537 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Critical ratio when only the first programme of the league table is funded"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With an illustrative GBP 150,000 only 0.75 of A is funded, so the critical ratio is A's GBP 5,000 per QALY, a limiting case with a single flag set." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Critical ratio when only the first programme of the league table is funded" ;
    hemo:9000014 "f_1 = 1; f_2 = 0; f_3 = 0; f_4 = 0; r_1 = 5000; r_2 = 10000; r_3 = 12500; r_4 = 20000; lambda_c = 5000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CELT-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "critical-ratio-when-only-the-first-programme-of-the-league-table-is-funded" .

hemo:7004538 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Whole programmes only in the indivisible league-table fill"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each programme is funded at its full incremental cost or not at all, the case Dakin and Tsiachristas describe as programmes that cannot be partially adopted. Programmes are numbered in ascending order of ICER with both increments above zero, and programmes with equal ICERs are taken in the order given, which can change the result." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Whole programmes only in the indivisible league-table fill" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CELT-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "whole-programmes-only-in-the-indivisible-league-table-fill" .

hemo:7004539 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "A whole programme that does not fit is passed over"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "When a programme's cost exceeds the budget left, the fill passes over it and tests the next, which agrees with the article's step 3, where neither Q nor R fits after P. The pass-over is the convention of this package; none of the cited sources states which variant a league table of whole programmes uses. Stopping at the first programme that does not fit is the other convention and can buy less health (HE-EX-CELT-009). A programme whose cost equals the budget left fits, since the test is at most B." ;
    hemo:9000009 "A whole programme that does not fit is passed over" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CELT-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "a-whole-programme-that-does-not-fit-is-passed-over" .

hemo:7004540 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Whole-programme league-table fill from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000719 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the budget in Budget and the three programmes' incremental costs and QALYs, in ICER order, in CostFirst, CostSecond, CostThird, QALYFirst, QALYSecond and QALYThird, the first three formulas, held in cells named FundFirst, FundSecond and FundThird, return 1 when that programme is funded, the double minus turning TRUE or FALSE into 1 or 0. The fourth returns the health, held in WholeHealth, and the fifth the budget left, held in UnspentBudget." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Whole-programme league-table fill from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CELT-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "whole-programme-league-table-fill-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=--(CostFirst<=Budget); =--(FundFirst*CostFirst+CostSecond<=Budget); =--(FundFirst*CostFirst+FundSecond*CostSecond+CostThird<=Budget); =FundFirst*QALYFirst+FundSecond*QALYSecond+FundThird*QALYThird; =Budget-(FundFirst*CostFirst+FundSecond*CostSecond+FundThird*CostThird)" .

hemo:7004541 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No whole programme passed over would fit in the budget left unspent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The budget left is zero or above, and every programme not funded costs more than it, so no programme that was passed over would still fit. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a fill that stops at the first programme that does not fit, which in HE-EX-CELT-009 leaves GBP 400,000 unspent with S, at GBP 300,000, unfunded." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No whole programme passed over would fit in the budget left unspent" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CELT-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-whole-programme-passed-over-would-fit-in-the-budget-left-unspent" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(UnspentBudget>=0,OR(FundSecond=1,UnspentBudget<CostSecond),OR(FundThird=1,UnspentBudget<CostThird))" .

hemo:7004542 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's whole-programme result of 60 QALYs reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's P, Q and R and a budget of GBP 1,000,000, only P is funded and the health is 60 QALYs, as in step 3 of the article. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows each programme tested against the whole budget in place of the budget left, which funds all three for 140 QALYs and spends GBP 1,600,000." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's whole-programme result of 60 QALYs reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CELT-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-whole-programme-result-of-60-qalys-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(FundFirst=1,FundSecond=0,FundThird=0,ABS(WholeHealth-60)<0.000001)" .

hemo:7004543 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's whole-programme case with P funded and Q and R left out"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In step 3 of the article a second authority has GBP 1,000,000 for whole programmes. P (GBP 600,000, 60 QALYs) heads the table at GBP 10,000 per QALY and is funded, after which neither Q nor R (GBP 500,000 and 40 QALYs each) fits in the GBP 400,000 left, so the fill buys 60 QALYs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's whole-programme case with P funded and Q and R left out" ;
    hemo:9000014 "B = 1000000; Delta_C_1 = 600000; Delta_E_1 = 60; Delta_C_2 = 500000; Delta_E_2 = 40; Delta_C_3 = 500000; Delta_E_3 = 40; y_1 = 1; y_2 = 0; y_3 = 0; H_w = 60; U_w = 400000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CELT-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-whole-programme-case-with-p-funded-and-q-and-r-left-out" .

hemo:7004544 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Whole programme that exactly uses the budget left"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With an illustrative budget of GBP 1,100,000, Q exactly fits in the GBP 500,000 left after P, a limiting case for the test at most B, and the fill buys 100 QALYs with nothing left." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Whole programme that exactly uses the budget left" ;
    hemo:9000014 "B = 1100000; Delta_C_1 = 600000; Delta_E_1 = 60; Delta_C_2 = 500000; Delta_E_2 = 40; Delta_C_3 = 500000; Delta_E_3 = 40; y_1 = 1; y_2 = 1; y_3 = 0; H_w = 100; U_w = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CELT-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "whole-programme-that-exactly-uses-the-budget-left" .

hemo:7004545 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Passing over a whole programme that does not fit to fund a smaller one below it"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Replacing R with an illustrative programme S (GBP 300,000, 15 QALYs, GBP 20,000 per QALY), Q does not fit after P but S does, so the fill buys 75 QALYs and leaves GBP 100,000. Stopping at Q would buy 60 QALYs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Passing over a whole programme that does not fit to fund a smaller one below it" ;
    hemo:9000014 "B = 1000000; Delta_C_1 = 600000; Delta_E_1 = 60; Delta_C_2 = 500000; Delta_E_2 = 40; Delta_C_3 = 300000; Delta_E_3 = 15; y_1 = 1; y_2 = 0; y_3 = 1; H_w = 75; U_w = 100000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CELT-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "passing-over-a-whole-programme-that-does-not-fit-to-fund-a-smaller-one-below-it" .

hemo:7004546 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Ties at the threshold count as adopted under the fixed ratio rule"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "A programme whose ICER equals lambda has an incremental net monetary benefit of zero and is adopted, following the wording of Sacristán and colleagues that its ICER does not exceed the threshold. The article phrases the rule as adopting what lies below the threshold, and the two readings differ only for such ties. Both increments are above zero, so the test agrees with the ICER rule of HE-AS-NMB-004." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ties at the threshold count as adopted under the fixed ratio rule" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CELT-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ties-at-the-threshold-count-as-adopted-under-the-fixed-ratio-rule" .

hemo:7004547 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Critical ratio as the threshold at which the two rules agree for divisible programmes"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "For divisible programmes the two rules agree at lambda equal to the critical ratio of HE-FM-CELT-002, where the marginal programme's incremental net monetary benefit is zero, so any share of it is consistent with the threshold. The budget then lies at or above the implied budget with ties left out and at or below the implied budget with ties counted in, while the budget binds (derived), which HE-VT-CELT-007 checks." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Critical ratio as the threshold at which the two rules agree for divisible programmes" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CELT-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "critical-ratio-as-the-threshold-at-which-the-two-rules-agree-for-divisible-programmes" .

hemo:7004548 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Budget implied by a threshold from ranges of incremental costs and QALYs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000720 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the incremental costs in a column range named IncCosts, the incremental QALYs in IncQALYs and the threshold in ThresholdValue, the formula returns the implied budget, held in a cell named ImpliedBudget." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Budget implied by a threshold from ranges of incremental costs and QALYs" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CELT-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "budget-implied-by-a-threshold-from-ranges-of-incremental-costs-and-qalys" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(--(IncCosts<=ThresholdValue*IncQALYs),IncCosts)" .

hemo:7004549 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Implied budget agrees with a sum over programmes whose ICER does not exceed the threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the programmes' ICERs in ProgICERs, SUMIF adds the incremental costs of those at or below the threshold, which matches ImpliedBudget while every incremental health gain is above zero. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a result cell built with a strict less-than test, which at GBP 20,000 per QALY drops D and gives GBP 750,000 against GBP 1,150,000." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Implied budget agrees with a sum over programmes whose ICER does not exceed the threshold" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CELT-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "implied-budget-agrees-with-a-sum-over-programmes-whose-icer-does-not-exceed-the-threshold" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ImpliedBudget-SUMIF(ProgICERs,\"<=\"&ThresholdValue,IncCosts))<0.01" .

hemo:7004550 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Budget bounded by the implied budgets without and with ties at the critical ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With CriticalRatio from HE-IM-CELT-002, the budget is at least the cost of the programmes strictly below the critical ratio and at most the cost with ties counted in, GBP 750,000 and GBP 1,150,000 against GBP 900,000 in the article's table. It holds while the budget binds. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a value in CriticalRatio that is not the critical ratio, for example GBP 15,000, where both sums are GBP 750,000, or GBP 25,000, where both are GBP 1,150,000." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Budget bounded by the implied budgets without and with ties at the critical ratio" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CELT-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "budget-bounded-by-the-implied-budgets-without-and-with-ties-at-the-critical-ratio" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(SUMPRODUCT(--(IncCosts<CriticalRatio*IncQALYs),IncCosts)<=Budget,SUMPRODUCT(--(IncCosts<=CriticalRatio*IncQALYs),IncCosts)>=Budget)" .

hemo:7004551 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Implied budget of GBP 750,000 at a threshold of GBP 15,000 per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's illustrative programmes A, B, C and D, a threshold of GBP 15,000 per QALY adopts A, B and C and implies a budget of GBP 750,000, GBP 150,000 below the GBP 900,000 available." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Implied budget of GBP 750,000 at a threshold of GBP 15,000 per QALY" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 15000; Delta_C_1 = 200000; Delta_E_1 = 40; Delta_C_2 = 300000; Delta_E_2 = 30; Delta_C_3 = 250000; Delta_E_3 = 20; Delta_C_4 = 400000; Delta_E_4 = 20; a_1 = 1; a_2 = 1; a_3 = 1; a_4 = 0; B_lambda = 750000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CELT-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "implied-budget-of-gbp-750-000-at-a-threshold-of-gbp-15-000-per-qaly" .

hemo:7004552 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Implied budget at a threshold equal to the critical ratio of GBP 20,000 per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At GBP 20,000 per QALY D's ICER equals the threshold, so D is adopted as a tie and the implied budget is GBP 1,150,000, GBP 250,000 above the budget. The two rules coincide here only with D part-funded." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Implied budget at a threshold equal to the critical ratio of GBP 20,000 per QALY" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 20000; Delta_C_1 = 200000; Delta_E_1 = 40; Delta_C_2 = 300000; Delta_E_2 = 30; Delta_C_3 = 250000; Delta_E_3 = 20; Delta_C_4 = 400000; Delta_E_4 = 20; a_1 = 1; a_2 = 1; a_3 = 1; a_4 = 1; B_lambda = 1150000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CELT-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "implied-budget-at-a-threshold-equal-to-the-critical-ratio-of-gbp-20-000-per-qaly" .

hemo:7004553 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Zero implied budget at a threshold below every ratio in the table"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At an illustrative GBP 4,000 per QALY, below A's GBP 5,000, no programme is adopted and the implied budget is zero, a limiting case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Zero implied budget at a threshold below every ratio in the table" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 4000; Delta_C_1 = 200000; Delta_E_1 = 40; Delta_C_2 = 300000; Delta_E_2 = 30; Delta_C_3 = 250000; Delta_E_3 = 20; Delta_C_4 = 400000; Delta_E_4 = 20; a_1 = 0; a_2 = 0; a_3 = 0; a_4 = 0; B_lambda = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CELT-012" ;
    hemo:9000023 "zero-implied-budget-at-a-threshold-below-every-ratio-in-the-table" .

hemo:7004554 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Both national value sets of the constant and N3 main-effects form"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each set is scored by HE-FM-EQ3L-001, a constant for any problem, a decrement for each level above 1 and at most one N3 decrement, so a set reported without the constant or without N3 enters zero for it. The Zimbabwe set of Jelsma and colleagues has a constant and ten level decrements and no N3 term, since their model with N3 valued moderate problems on several dimensions below extreme problems and was discarded. A set with count terms, such as the D1 form of HE-FM-CSVS-003, is not split this way." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Both national value sets of the constant and N3 main-effects form" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CSVS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "both-national-value-sets-of-the-constant-and-n3-main-effects-form" .

hemo:7004555 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Same profile pair, levels and coefficient signs under both value sets"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Both gains are for the same starting and end profiles, read in the order mobility, self-care, usual activities, pain or discomfort and anxiety or depression. Every coefficient is a positive decrement (HE-AS-EQ3L-004), so a relieved decrement is positive for a dimension that improves. Full health 11111 is fixed at 1 through I = 0 under both sets, the convention of HE-FM-EQ3L-001 and the treatment Rand-Hendriksen and colleagues describe for the US D1 tariff, in which the value of 11111 is given and not predicted. Jelsma and colleagues do not state this for the Zimbabwe set, whose model predicts 0.90 for 11111 (their Table 6), so a Zimbabwe gain into 11111 rests on this convention (HE-ER-CSVS-002)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same profile pair, levels and coefficient signs under both value sets" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CSVS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-profile-pair-levels-and-coefficient-signs-under-both-value-sets" .

hemo:7004556 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Cross-set gap in a utility gain from named constant, relieved decrement and N3 cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000721 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the two constants in ConstSetA and ConstSetB, the any-problem indicators of the two states in AnyProblemBefore and AnyProblemAfter, the five relieved decrements of each set in the ranges RelievedSetA and RelievedSetB, the N3 decrements in SevereTermSetA and SevereTermSetB and the N3 indicators in SevereBefore and SevereAfter, the first two formulas return the gains held in GainSetA and GainSetB and the third the gap held in GainGapAcrossSets." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cross-set gap in a utility gain from named constant, relieved decrement and N3 cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CSVS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cross-set-gap-in-a-utility-gain-from-named-constant-relieved-decrement-and-n3-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ConstSetA*(AnyProblemBefore-AnyProblemAfter)+SUM(RelievedSetA)+SevereTermSetA*(SevereBefore-SevereAfter); =ConstSetB*(AnyProblemBefore-AnyProblemAfter)+SUM(RelievedSetB)+SevereTermSetB*(SevereBefore-SevereAfter); =GainSetA-GainSetB" .

hemo:7004557 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Cross-set gain gap matches the gap computed from the four scored utilities"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's sets A and B entered, and the four utilities of 21232 and 11121 scored separately with HE-IM-EQ3L-001 into UtilBeforeSetA (0.35), UtilAfterSetA (0.80), UtilBeforeSetB (0.49) and UtilAfterSetB (0.88), the cell GainGapAcrossSets agrees with the difference of the two utility gains. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a relieved pain decrement entered as the full level 3 decrement (0.39 and 0.25 in place of 0.27 and 0.18), which gives a gap of 0.11 in place of 0.06." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cross-set gain gap matches the gap computed from the four scored utilities" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CSVS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cross-set-gain-gap-matches-the-gap-computed-from-the-four-scored-utilities" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(GainGapAcrossSets-((UtilAfterSetA-UtilBeforeSetA)-(UtilAfterSetB-UtilBeforeSetB)))<0.0005" .

hemo:7004558 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "UK and Zimbabwe gain gap for 21232 and 11121 rebuilt from published coefficients"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the UK tariff as set A and the Zimbabwe set as set B entered for the move out of 21232 into 11121, the cell GainGapAcrossSets equals the gap rebuilt directly from the UK coefficients of HE-FM-EQ3L-002 and the Zimbabwe coefficients of Jelsma and colleagues, 0.328. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the UK N3 decrement left out of SevereTermSetA, which gives 0.059." ;
    hemo:9000009 "UK and Zimbabwe gain gap for 21232 and 11121 rebuilt from published coefficients" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CSVS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "uk-and-zimbabwe-gain-gap-for-21232-and-11121-rebuilt-from-published-coefficients" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(GainGapAcrossSets-(((1-0.081-0.123)-(1-0.081-0.069-0.036-0.386-0.071-0.269))-((1-0.1-0.067)-(1-0.1-0.056-0.043-0.302-0.046))))<0.0005" .

hemo:7004559 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's illustrative sets A and B for the move out of 21232 into 11121"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's illustrative decrements, which are not taken from any published set, relieving moderate mobility and usual activities problems, moving pain down one level to level 2 and relieving moderate anxiety gains 0.45 under set A and 0.39 under set B. The constant cancels and neither set has an N3 term. Of the gap of 0.06, pain contributes 0.09 (0.27 against 0.18), anxiety 0.01, while mobility (minus 0.03) and usual activities (minus 0.01) pull the other way, so pain alone carries more than the whole gap (computed here), in line with the article's reading that set B gives severe pain a smaller decrement." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's illustrative sets A and B for the move out of 21232 into 11121" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c_A = 0.08; c_B = 0.05; I_0 = 1; I_1 = 1; v_A_MO = 0.07; v_A_SC = 0; v_A_UA = 0.04; v_A_PD = 0.27; v_A_AD = 0.07; v_B_MO = 0.10; v_B_SC = 0; v_B_UA = 0.05; v_B_PD = 0.18; v_B_AD = 0.06; gamma_A = 0; gamma_B = 0; N3_0 = 1; N3_1 = 0; g_A = 0.45; g_B = 0.39; dg = 0.06" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CSVS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-illustrative-sets-a-and-b-for-the-move-out-of-21232-into-11121" .

hemo:7004560 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "UK MVH and Zimbabwe EQ-5D-3L sets for the move out of 21232 into 11121"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Scoring the article's two states with the UK tariff of HE-FM-EQ3L-002 (set A) gives 0.088 and 0.796, a gain of 0.708, and with the Zimbabwe coefficients of Jelsma and colleagues (set B) gives 0.453 and 0.833, a gain of 0.380. Of the gap of 0.328, the UK N3 decrement of 0.269 carries about 82%, and the four dimension differences together only 0.059, so the two sets differ on this move mainly because one of them charges extra for any level 3 problem (computed here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "UK MVH and Zimbabwe EQ-5D-3L sets for the move out of 21232 into 11121" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c_A = 0.081; c_B = 0.100; I_0 = 1; I_1 = 1; v_A_MO = 0.069; v_A_SC = 0; v_A_UA = 0.036; v_A_PD = 0.263; v_A_AD = 0.071; v_B_MO = 0.056; v_B_SC = 0; v_B_UA = 0.043; v_B_PD = 0.235; v_B_AD = 0.046; gamma_A = 0.269; gamma_B = 0; N3_0 = 1; N3_1 = 0; g_A = 0.708; g_B = 0.380; dg = 0.328" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CSVS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "uk-mvh-and-zimbabwe-eq-5d-3l-sets-for-the-move-out-of-21232-into-11121" .

hemo:7004561 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "UK MVH and Zimbabwe EQ-5D-3L sets for a return to full health out of 21121"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When the end state is 11111 the constant no longer cancels, and 11111 is fixed at 1 under both sets (HE-AS-CSVS-002), not at the 0.90 that the Zimbabwe model predicts. Under the UK tariff the gain is 0.081 plus 0.069 plus 0.123, or 0.273, and under the Zimbabwe set 0.100 plus 0.056 plus 0.067, or 0.223. The gap of 0.050 is the sum of a constant term of minus 0.019, a mobility term of 0.013 and a pain term of 0.056 (computed here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "UK MVH and Zimbabwe EQ-5D-3L sets for a return to full health out of 21121" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c_A = 0.081; c_B = 0.100; I_0 = 1; I_1 = 0; v_A_MO = 0.069; v_A_SC = 0; v_A_UA = 0; v_A_PD = 0.123; v_A_AD = 0; v_B_MO = 0.056; v_B_SC = 0; v_B_UA = 0; v_B_PD = 0.067; v_B_AD = 0; gamma_A = 0.269; gamma_B = 0; N3_0 = 0; N3_1 = 0; g_A = 0.273; g_B = 0.223; dg = 0.050" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CSVS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "uk-mvh-and-zimbabwe-eq-5d-3l-sets-for-a-return-to-full-health-out-of-21121" .

hemo:7004562 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Only the value set differs between the two QALY gains"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The two gains come from the same model, comparator, time horizon, discounting and incremental cost, and the utilities alone are rescored, the kind of comparison Navega Biz and colleagues make when they map the utility values used in NICE appraisal models to the EQ-5D-5L. If costs also change, r_I has to be computed from the two ICERs directly." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Only the value set differs between the two QALY gains" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CSVS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "only-the-value-set-differs-between-the-two-qaly-gains" .

hemo:7004563 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Both QALY gains positive for the ICER change"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "r_I is a change in the cost per QALY gained only while dQ_A and dQ_B are both above zero. A value of r_Q below minus 1 means the gain changes sign under set B, and the result is then classified with the dominance rule of HE-FM-DOM-001 (ties included) or compared by net health benefit (HE-FM-CET-002), not by the ratio." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Both QALY gains positive for the ICER change" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CSVS-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "both-qaly-gains-positive-for-the-icer-change" .

hemo:7004564 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Relative QALY and ICER changes from two named QALY gain cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000722 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the QALY gain under the reference set in QALYGainSetA and under the alternative set in QALYGainSetB, the first formula returns the value held in QALYChangeRel and the second the value held in ICERChangeRel." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Relative QALY and ICER changes from two named QALY gain cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CSVS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "relative-qaly-and-icer-changes-from-two-named-qaly-gain-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=QALYGainSetB/QALYGainSetA-1; =QALYGainSetA/QALYGainSetB-1" .

hemo:7004565 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "ICER change cell reproduces the article's two ICERs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With QALYGainSetA at 0.90 and QALYGainSetB at 0.78, the cell ICERChangeRel equals the ratio of the article's ICERs, GBP 26,923.08 and GBP 23,333.33 per QALY, less 1, within 0.0001. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the ICER change taken as minus the QALY change, 0.1333 in place of 0.1538." ;
    hemo:9000009 "ICER change cell reproduces the article's two ICERs" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CSVS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "icer-change-cell-reproduces-the-article-s-two-icers" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ICERChangeRel-(26923.08/23333.33-1))<0.0001" .

hemo:7004566 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "QALY and ICER changes are reciprocal for one decision"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For any pair of positive QALY gains, one plus QALYChangeRel times one plus ICERChangeRel equals 1. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the ICER change entered as the negative of the QALY change, which for the article's example gives a product of 0.9822." ;
    hemo:9000009 "QALY and ICER changes are reciprocal for one decision" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CSVS-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "qaly-and-icer-changes-are-reciprocal-for-one-decision" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((1+QALYChangeRel)*(1+ICERChangeRel)-1)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004567 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's illustrative QALY gains of 0.90 and 0.78 under sets A and B"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's treatment gains 0.90 QALYs under set A and 0.78 under set B over 2 years. The QALY gain falls by 13.33% and the ICER rises by 15.38%, matching the article's GBP 23,333 and GBP 26,923 per QALY for an extra cost of GBP 21,000. Net health benefit at GBP 25,000 per QALY is 0.06 under set A and minus 0.06 under set B, a fall of 0.12 equal to the fall in the QALY gain." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's illustrative QALY gains of 0.90 and 0.78 under sets A and B" ;
    hemo:9000014 "dQ_A = 0.90; dQ_B = 0.78; r_Q = -0.1333; r_I = 0.1538" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CSVS-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-illustrative-qaly-gains-of-0-90-and-0-78-under-sets-a-and-b" .

hemo:7004568 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "UK MVH and Zimbabwe sets for 2 years out of 21232 and in 11121"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Two years in 11121 in place of 21232 gain 1.416 QALYs under the UK tariff and 0.760 under the Zimbabwe set (HE-FM-CSVS-001 gains of 0.708 and 0.380). Rescoring with the Zimbabwe set cuts the QALY gain by 46.33% and, for an unchanged cost, raises the ICER by 86.32% (computed here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "UK MVH and Zimbabwe sets for 2 years out of 21232 and in 11121" ;
    hemo:9000014 "dQ_A = 1.416; dQ_B = 0.760; r_Q = -0.4633; r_I = 0.8632" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CSVS-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "uk-mvh-and-zimbabwe-sets-for-2-years-out-of-21232-and-in-11121" .

hemo:7004569 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative single appraisal whose QALY gain rises by 12.5% under a new value set"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An illustrative decision with 1 incremental QALY under the old set and 1.125 under the new one has its ICER fall by 11.11%. The pair 12.5% and 11.1% matches the mean changes Navega Biz and colleagues report for 17 oncology decisions whose utilities were mapped to the EQ-5D-5L with the new UK value set, but the identity holds exactly only for one decision (derived), and group means need not follow it." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative single appraisal whose QALY gain rises by 12.5% under a new value set" ;
    hemo:9000014 "dQ_A = 1; dQ_B = 1.125; r_Q = 0.125; r_I = -0.1111" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CSVS-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-single-appraisal-whose-qaly-gain-rises-by-12-5-under-a-new-value-set" .

hemo:7004570 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Counts beyond the first dimension and no linear I2 term"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "D1, I2 and I3 count dimensions beyond the first, so a state with one problem carries none of the count terms and full health 11111 scores 1. Following Rand-Hendriksen and colleagues, the US model was fitted on disutilities, 1 minus the time trade-off value, and the linear I2 term was not statistically significant and was left out of the final model, which is why only its square appears." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Counts beyond the first dimension and no linear I2 term" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CSVS-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "counts-beyond-the-first-dimension-and-no-linear-i2-term" .

hemo:7004571 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Coefficient signs as estimated, entered as disutility coefficients"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the published US tariff as tabulated by Rand-Hendriksen and colleagues, the D1, I3 and I3 squared terms raise the value of a state and the I2 squared term lowers it. In this subtracting form a_D1, a_I3 and a_I32 are therefore entered as negative numbers and a_I22 as a positive one. The published US coefficients are not entered here because the coefficient table of Shaw and colleagues 2005 was not checked at text level; the examples use illustrative values with the same sign pattern." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Coefficient signs as estimated, entered as disutility coefficients" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CSVS-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "coefficient-signs-as-estimated-entered-as-disutility-coefficients" .

hemo:7004572 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Equivalence of the D1 form and a constant form"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Rand-Hendriksen and colleagues show that the D1 model and a model with a constant give identical values for all 242 states other than full health when the constant equals minus a_D1 and every level decrement is shifted by a_D1, with the I2 squared, I3 and I3 squared coefficients unchanged. The D1 form is a reparameterisation, so the difference from a constant and N3 set lies in the count terms, not in the D1 term." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equivalence of the D1 form and a constant form" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CSVS-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equivalence-of-the-d1-form-and-a-constant-form" .

hemo:7004573 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Count-based D1 value set utility from named levels, decrements and coefficients"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000723 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the five reported levels in a row range named Levels, the decrements at those levels in a range named DecrementsAtLevels and the four count coefficients in CoefExtraDims, CoefModerateSq, CoefExtreme and CoefExtremeSq, the first two formulas fill the cells CountModerate and CountExtreme and the third returns the utility held in UtilityDOneForm." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Count-based D1 value set utility from named levels, decrements and coefficients" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CSVS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "count-based-d1-value-set-utility-from-named-levels-decrements-and-coefficients" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=COUNTIF(Levels,2); =COUNTIF(Levels,3); =1-SUM(DecrementsAtLevels)-CoefExtraDims*MAX(CountModerate+CountExtreme-1,0)-CoefModerateSq*MAX(CountModerate-1,0)^2-CoefExtreme*MAX(CountExtreme-1,0)-CoefExtremeSq*MAX(CountExtreme-1,0)^2" .

hemo:7004574 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "D1 form equals the shifted constant form for state 22222"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With Levels at 22222 and the illustrative coefficients, the cell UtilityDOneForm equals the constant form of Rand-Hendriksen and colleagues, a constant of 0.12 (minus a_D1), five level 2 decrements shifted to 0.03 and the same I2 squared term, 0.57. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows D1 counted over all five dimensions with problems, which gives 0.69, or I2 counted without dropping the first dimension, which gives 0.48." ;
    hemo:9000009 "D1 form equals the shifted constant form for state 22222" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CSVS-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "d1-form-equals-the-shifted-constant-form-for-state-22222" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(UtilityDOneForm-(1-0.12-5*0.03-0.01*16))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004575 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "D1 form gives exactly 1 for full health 11111"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With Levels at 11111 the decrements and counts are all zero, and the floors at zero keep every count term out, so the cell UtilityDOneForm is 1. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows D1, I2 and I3 all left without their floors at zero, which with the illustrative coefficients gives 0.79 (D1 alone unfloored gives 0.88)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "D1 form gives exactly 1 for full health 11111" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CSVS-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "d1-form-gives-exactly-1-for-full-health-11111" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(UtilityDOneForm-1)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004576 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative D1 value set score for EQ-5D-3L state 21111 with one problem"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative coefficients, a level 2 decrement of 0.15 and a level 3 decrement of 0.50 on every dimension, a_D1 of minus 0.12, a_I22 of 0.01, a_I3 of minus 0.10 and a_I32 of minus 0.02, are used; they are not the published US values. With one problem every count is zero, so the utility is 1 less the mobility decrement, 0.85." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative D1 value set score for EQ-5D-3L state 21111 with one problem" ;
    hemo:9000014 "beta_MO = 0.15; beta_SC = 0; beta_UA = 0; beta_PD = 0; beta_AD = 0; n_2 = 1; n_3 = 0; a_D1 = -0.12; a_I22 = 0.01; a_I3 = -0.10; a_I32 = -0.02; D_1 = 0; I_2 = 0; I_3 = 0; U = 0.85" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CSVS-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-d1-value-set-score-for-eq-5d-3l-state-21111-with-one-problem" .

hemo:7004577 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative D1 value set score for EQ-5D-3L state 22222"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the same illustrative coefficients, five moderate problems cost 0.75 in level decrements, D1 of 4 gives back 0.48 and I2 of 4 costs 0.16 through its square, so the utility is 0.57." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative D1 value set score for EQ-5D-3L state 22222" ;
    hemo:9000014 "beta_MO = 0.15; beta_SC = 0.15; beta_UA = 0.15; beta_PD = 0.15; beta_AD = 0.15; n_2 = 5; n_3 = 0; a_D1 = -0.12; a_I22 = 0.01; a_I3 = -0.10; a_I32 = -0.02; D_1 = 4; I_2 = 4; I_3 = 0; U = 0.57" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CSVS-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-d1-value-set-score-for-eq-5d-3l-state-22222" .

hemo:7004578 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative D1 value set score for EQ-5D-3L state 22331 with mixed levels"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the same illustrative coefficients, two moderate and two extreme problems cost 1.30 in level decrements. D1 is 3, I2 and I3 are each 1, and the count terms add 0.36 minus 0.01 plus 0.10 plus 0.02, giving 0.17." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative D1 value set score for EQ-5D-3L state 22331 with mixed levels" ;
    hemo:9000014 "beta_MO = 0.15; beta_SC = 0.15; beta_UA = 0.50; beta_PD = 0.50; beta_AD = 0; n_2 = 2; n_3 = 2; a_D1 = -0.12; a_I22 = 0.01; a_I3 = -0.10; a_I32 = -0.02; D_1 = 3; I_2 = 1; I_3 = 1; U = 0.17" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CSVS-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-d1-value-set-score-for-eq-5d-3l-state-22331-with-mixed-levels" .

hemo:7004579 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative D1 value set score for the worst EQ-5D-3L state 33333"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the same illustrative coefficients, five extreme problems cost 2.50 in level decrements, and D1 of 4 and I3 of 4 give back 0.48, 0.40 and 0.32, so the utility is minus 0.30, a state below dead." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative D1 value set score for the worst EQ-5D-3L state 33333" ;
    hemo:9000014 "beta_MO = 0.50; beta_SC = 0.50; beta_UA = 0.50; beta_PD = 0.50; beta_AD = 0.50; n_2 = 0; n_3 = 5; a_D1 = -0.12; a_I22 = 0.01; a_I3 = -0.10; a_I32 = -0.02; D_1 = 4; I_2 = 0; I_3 = 4; U = -0.30" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CSVS-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-d1-value-set-score-for-the-worst-eq-5d-3l-state-33333" .

hemo:7004580 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Symmetric test statistic for halving the two-sided p-value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The p-value comes from a symmetric z or t test at the same threshold as the decision, so the one-sided tail is half the two-sided value. Hoch and colleagues note that the p-value of a parametric analysis rests on a distributional assumption and that bootstrapping may be preferred when the data do not appear normal. HEMA Report 2 notes that near the ICER the two-option error probability can be higher or lower than 50% when the distribution of incremental net health benefit is markedly skewed." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Symmetric test statistic for halving the two-sided p-value" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DCU-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "symmetric-test-statistic-for-halving-the-two-sided-p-value" .

hemo:7004581 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Two options and a probability reading of the p-value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "There are two options and the one-sided p-value is read as the probability that the chosen option is not the better one, the reading Hoch and colleagues give for the acceptability curve from net benefit regression. With three or more options the error probability is read from the draws (HE-FM-CEAC-003)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two options and a probability reading of the p-value" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DCU-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-options-and-a-probability-reading-of-the-p-value" .

hemo:7004582 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Error probability from a two-sided p-value in one cell"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000724 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the two-sided p-value of incremental net benefit, or of the treatment coefficient in a net benefit regression, in a cell named PValueTwoSided, this formula in a cell named ErrProbPValue returns the error probability." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Error probability from a two-sided p-value in one cell" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-DCU-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "error-probability-from-a-two-sided-p-value-in-one-cell" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=PValueTwoSided/2" .

hemo:7004583 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Error probability from the p-value matches the normal tail of the z statistic"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For a z test with the statistic of incremental net benefit in a cell named ZStatINB, the value in ErrProbPValue equals the upper normal tail at the absolute statistic. The tolerance of 0.0001 allows a p-value and a statistic rounded to five and four decimals. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the two-sided p-value left unhalved, which for z of about 0.7454 holds about 0.45606 instead of 0.22803." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Error probability from the p-value matches the normal tail of the z statistic" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DCU-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "error-probability-from-the-p-value-matches-the-normal-tail-of-the-z-statistic" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ErrProbPValue-(1-NORM.S.DIST(ABS(ZStatINB),TRUE)))<1E-4" .

hemo:7004584 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Error probability from the p-value not above one half"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The value in ErrProbPValue is at most 0.5. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows one minus the one-sided p-value entered instead, about 0.77197 in the GBP 30,000 per QALY example, which is the probability that the chosen option is the better one." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Error probability from the p-value not above one half" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DCU-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "error-probability-from-the-p-value-not-above-one-half" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ErrProbPValue<=0.5" .

hemo:7004585 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Error probability from the p-value at GBP 30,000 per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At the illustrative GBP 30,000 per QALY of HE-EX-AN-008, incremental net benefit of GBP 1,000 with a standard error of about GBP 1,342 has a two-sided z test p-value of about 0.45606, so the error probability of adopting is about 0.22803, one minus the 0.772 probability that net benefit is positive." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Error probability from the p-value at GBP 30,000 per QALY" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_two = 0.45606; P_error_p = 0.22803" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DCU-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "error-probability-from-the-p-value-at-gbp-30-000-per-qaly" .

hemo:7004586 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Error probability from the p-value at a threshold equal to the ICER"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At a threshold equal to the ICER the estimate is zero, the two-sided p-value is 1 and the error probability is 0.5, a limiting case that checks the implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Error probability from the p-value at a threshold equal to the ICER" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_two = 1; P_error_p = 0.5" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DCU-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "error-probability-from-the-p-value-at-a-threshold-equal-to-the-icer" .

hemo:7004587 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Joint normality of the correlated PSA pair on the scale of estimation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The two parameters are jointly normal on the scale on which they were estimated, for example the log scale of a rate and a hazard ratio from one regression. The ISPOR-SMDM task force states that the covariance matrix defines the uncertainty in regression coefficients and that multivariate normality is appropriate for the regression's linear predictor. Values on the natural scale are found by back-transforming each draw, and their means then follow the log-normal mean in HE-FM-EV-004, not the exponential of mu_1 or mu_2." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Joint normality of the correlated PSA pair on the scale of estimation" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PSA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "joint-normality-of-the-correlated-psa-pair-on-the-scale-of-estimation" .

hemo:7004588 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Correlation strictly inside minus 1 and 1 with a stated basis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "rho is above minus 1 and below 1, so L_22 is above zero and the covariance matrix is positive definite, the condition under which Owen states that a lower triangular Cholesky factor exists. At rho equal to 1 or minus 1 the second parameter is fixed by the first. rho comes from the joint estimation where it exists, and PMG36 section 4.7.19 asks for evidence on correlation to be reflected in the probabilistic analysis and for the assumptions made about correlations to be presented clearly." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Correlation strictly inside minus 1 and 1 with a stated basis" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PSA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "correlation-strictly-inside-minus-1-and-1-with-a-stated-basis" .

hemo:7004589 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One fresh pair of standard normal values per PSA draw shared by all options"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "z_1 and z_2 are independent standard normal values, drawn afresh for each PSA draw and never reused across draws. The pair x_1 and x_2 found from them enters the model for every option in that draw, as the article requires of each complete parameter set." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One fresh pair of standard normal values per PSA draw shared by all options" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PSA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-fresh-pair-of-standard-normal-values-per-psa-draw-shared-by-all-options" .

hemo:7004590 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Correlated pair of PSA draws from named cells in Excel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000725 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the means in cells named MeanLogRate and MeanLogHR, the standard deviations in SDLogRate and SDLogHR and the correlation in CorrRateHR, the first two formulas give the Cholesky entries in cells named CholOffDiag and CholDiagSecond. StdNormalFirst and StdNormalSecond each hold NORM.S.INV(RAND()), pasted as values when the draw must be kept, and the last two formulas return the draws in cells named DrawLogRate and DrawLogHR. Filling the four draw cells down one row per PSA draw gives the columns used in the computational function below." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Correlated pair of PSA draws from named cells in Excel" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-PSA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "correlated-pair-of-psa-draws-from-named-cells-in-excel" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CorrRateHR*SDLogHR; =SDLogHR*SQRT(1-CorrRateHR^2); =MeanLogRate+SDLogRate*StdNormalFirst; =MeanLogHR+CholOffDiag*StdNormalFirst+CholDiagSecond*StdNormalSecond" .

hemo:7004591 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Covariance matrix rebuilt from the two-parameter Cholesky entries"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "s_1 times CholOffDiag equals the covariance rho s_1 s_2, and CholOffDiag squared plus CholDiagSecond squared equals the variance of the second parameter, the check of Sigma minus L L transposed that Owen advises before a factor is used. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows CholDiagSecond computed without the square root, 0.15 for the illustrative inputs, which rebuilds a variance of 0.0325 against 0.04, or CholOffDiag set to rho itself, which gives a covariance of minus 0.05 against minus 0.01." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Covariance matrix rebuilt from the two-parameter Cholesky entries" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PSA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "covariance-matrix-rebuilt-from-the-two-parameter-cholesky-entries" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(SDLogRate*CholOffDiag-CorrRateHR*SDLogRate*SDLogHR)<1E-12,ABS(CholOffDiag^2+CholDiagSecond^2-SDLogHR^2)<1E-12)" .

hemo:7004592 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Second Cholesky draw equal to its conditional normal form given the first"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The draw in DrawLogHR equals the conditional mean of the second parameter given DrawLogRate, mu_2 plus rho s_2 / s_1 times the deviation of the first draw from its mean, plus s_2 times the square root of one minus rho squared times StdNormalSecond, the conditional distribution Owen lists for the multivariate normal. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the upper triangular factor used without transposing, which for the first example gives a log rate of about minus 1.3363 and a log hazard ratio of about minus 0.1365 instead of minus 1.2863 and minus 0.2365, or StdNormalSecond used in both terms, which gives about minus 0.1865." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Second Cholesky draw equal to its conditional normal form given the first" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PSA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "second-cholesky-draw-equal-to-its-conditional-normal-form-given-the-first" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(DrawLogHR-(MeanLogHR+CorrRateHR*SDLogHR/SDLogRate*(DrawLogRate-MeanLogRate)+SDLogHR*SQRT(1-CorrRateHR^2)*StdNormalSecond))<1E-12" .

hemo:7004593 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Neine and Curran's entry of 0.866 reproduced in the second Cholesky draw"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With both means set to 0, both standard deviations to 1, CorrRateHR to 0.5 and both standard normal cells to 1, DrawLogHR rounds to 1.366, the sum of Neine and Curran's Cholesky entries 0.5 and 0.866. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the square root left out of CholDiagSecond, which gives 1.25, or the factor transposed, which gives about 0.866." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Neine and Curran's entry of 0.866 reproduced in the second Cholesky draw" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PSA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "neine-and-curran-s-entry-of-0-866-reproduced-in-the-second-cholesky-draw" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND(DrawLogHR,3)=1.366" .

hemo:7004594 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative log baseline rate and log hazard ratio with a correlation of minus 0.5"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An illustrative log annual mortality rate with mean minus 1.3862944 (a rate of 0.25) and standard deviation 0.10 is drawn with an illustrative log hazard ratio with mean minus 0.2231436 (a hazard ratio of 0.8) and standard deviation 0.20, at an illustrative correlation of minus 0.5. For standard normal values of 1 and 0.5 the Cholesky entries are minus 0.1 and about 0.1732051, so the draw is a log rate of minus 1.2862944 (a rate of about 0.2763) and a log hazard ratio of about minus 0.2365411 (a hazard ratio of about 0.7894)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative log baseline rate and log hazard ratio with a correlation of minus 0.5" ;
    hemo:9000014 "rho = -0.5; s_2 = 0.20; mu_1 = -1.3862944; s_1 = 0.10; z_1 = 1; mu_2 = -0.2231436; z_2 = 0.5; L_21 = -0.1; L_22 = 0.1732051; x_1 = -1.2862944; x_2 = -0.2365411" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PSA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-log-baseline-rate-and-log-hazard-ratio-with-a-correlation-of-minus-0-5" .

hemo:7004595 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Neine and Curran's correlation of 0.5 on the standard normal scale"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For two standardised parameters with a correlation of 0.5, the second column of the Cholesky matrix printed by Neine and Curran has entries 0.5 and 0.866, the values of L_21 and L_22 here. Standard normal values of 1 and 1 give a first draw of 1 and a second of about 1.3660254." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Neine and Curran's correlation of 0.5 on the standard normal scale" ;
    hemo:9000014 "rho = 0.5; s_2 = 1; mu_1 = 0; s_1 = 1; z_1 = 1; mu_2 = 0; z_2 = 1; L_21 = 0.5; L_22 = 0.8660254; x_1 = 1; x_2 = 1.3660254" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PSA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "neine-and-curran-s-correlation-of-0-5-on-the-standard-normal-scale" .

hemo:7004596 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Zero correlation as the limiting case of the Cholesky PSA step"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of the first example but rho equal to zero, L_21 is zero and L_22 equals s_2, so the log hazard ratio draw of about minus 0.1231436 depends on z_2 alone. This limiting case is the usual independent draw." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Zero correlation as the limiting case of the Cholesky PSA step" ;
    hemo:9000014 "rho = 0; s_2 = 0.20; mu_1 = -1.3862944; s_1 = 0.10; z_1 = 1; mu_2 = -0.2231436; z_2 = 0.5; L_21 = 0; L_22 = 0.2; x_1 = -1.2862944; x_2 = -0.1231436" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PSA-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "zero-correlation-as-the-limiting-case-of-the-cholesky-psa-step" .

hemo:7004597 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Two options with the comparator as zero reference in the expected loss"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The decision is between one new option and one comparator, and every draw holds the new option's incremental net health benefit at the same threshold, so the comparator's net benefit is zero in every draw. The five-draw form is written out for the calculator; the Excel and R forms take any number of draws, and with three or more options each option's loss is the mean of the best net benefit in each draw less its own, equation 3 of Alarid-Escudero and colleagues." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two options with the comparator as zero reference in the expected loss" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PSA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-options-with-the-comparator-as-zero-reference-in-the-expected-loss" .

hemo:7004598 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Risk-neutral choice by the smaller expected loss"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The decision maker is risk neutral, so the option chosen on current evidence is the one with the highest expected net benefit, which Alarid-Escudero and colleagues show is the one with the lowest expected loss. Both losses are conditional on the uncertainties inside the probabilistic analysis, as HEMA Report 2 notes for its own example." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Risk-neutral choice by the smaller expected loss" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PSA-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "risk-neutral-choice-by-the-smaller-expected-loss" .

hemo:7004599 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Draws with zero incremental net health benefit add no expected loss"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "A draw in which the incremental net health benefit is exactly zero adds no loss to either option, since neither choice forgoes anything. The probability that the new option is cost-effective counts such a draw as not cost-effective, because HE-FM-CEAC-001 uses a strict inequality, so ties change that probability but not either expected loss." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Draws with zero incremental net health benefit add no expected loss" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PSA-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "draws-with-zero-incremental-net-health-benefit-add-no-expected-loss" .

hemo:7004600 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected loss of each option from a column of incremental net health benefit draws"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000726 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With one incremental net health benefit per draw in a range named IncNHB, the first formula returns the expected loss from adopting the new option in a cell named ExpLossNew and the second the expected loss from keeping the comparator in ExpLossComp. SUMPRODUCT needs no array entry, and the range can hold any number of draws." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected loss of each option from a column of incremental net health benefit draws" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-PSA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-loss-of-each-option-from-a-column-of-incremental-net-health-benefit-draws" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT((IncNHB<0)*(0-IncNHB))/COUNT(IncNHB); =SUMPRODUCT((IncNHB>0)*IncNHB)/COUNT(IncNHB)" .

hemo:7004601 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Difference of the two expected losses equals mean incremental net health benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "ExpLossComp less ExpLossNew equals the plain mean of IncNHB. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a loss averaged over the losing draws only, which for the article's draws gives an adoption loss of 1.4 / 4 = 0.35 and a difference of 0.13 instead of 0.2." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Difference of the two expected losses equals mean incremental net health benefit" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PSA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "difference-of-the-two-expected-losses-equals-mean-incremental-net-health-benefit" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((ExpLossComp-ExpLossNew)-AVERAGE(IncNHB))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004602 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Smaller expected loss equals the two-option EVPI of the same draws"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The smaller of ExpLossNew and ExpLossComp equals the two-option EVPI of HE-FM-EVPI-002, the mean of the positive parts of IncNHB less the positive part of its mean. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the sign kept in the adoption loss, which for the article's draws gives minus 0.28 against an EVPI of 0.28." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Smaller expected loss equals the two-option EVPI of the same draws" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PSA-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "smaller-expected-loss-equals-the-two-option-evpi-of-the-same-draws" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(MIN(ExpLossNew,ExpLossComp)-(SUMPRODUCT((IncNHB>0)*IncNHB)/COUNT(IncNHB)-MAX(0,AVERAGE(IncNHB))))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004603 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's expected losses of 0.28 and 0.48 QALYs in the result cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's five draws in IncNHB, ExpLossNew rounds to 0.28 and ExpLossComp to 0.48. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the two formulas swapped, which puts 0.48 in ExpLossNew and 0.28 in ExpLossComp." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's expected losses of 0.28 and 0.48 QALYs in the result cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PSA-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-expected-losses-of-0-28-and-0-48-qalys-in-the-result-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ROUND(ExpLossNew,2)=0.28,ROUND(ExpLossComp,2)=0.48)" .

hemo:7004604 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's five draws at GBP 25,000 per QALY as expected losses"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative example the incremental net health benefit in the five draws is 2.4, minus 0.1, minus 0.1, minus 0.6 and minus 0.6 QALYs. Adopting the new treatment loses 1.4 / 5 = 0.28 QALYs per patient on average and keeping usual care loses 2.4 / 5 = 0.48, so the difference of 0.2 QALYs is the expected net health benefit, as the article states. The smaller loss, 0.28, is the article's per-patient EVPI." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's five draws at GBP 25,000 per QALY as expected losses" ;
    hemo:9000014 "INHB_1 = 2.4; INHB_2 = -0.1; INHB_3 = -0.1; INHB_4 = -0.6; INHB_5 = -0.6; EL_new = 0.28; EL_comp = 0.48; D_EL = 0.2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PSA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-five-draws-at-gbp-25-000-per-qaly-as-expected-losses" .

hemo:7004605 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's five draws at GBP 35,000 per QALY as expected losses"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At GBP 35,000 per QALY, the higher value in PMG36 section 4.2.16, the fixed cost of GBP 15,000 is worth about 0.4285714 QALYs, so the same survival draws give incremental net health benefits of about 2.5714286, 0.0714286, 0.0714286, minus 0.4285714 and minus 0.4285714 QALYs. The expected loss from adopting falls to about 0.1714286 and that from keeping usual care rises to about 0.5428572 QALYs per patient. These figures are computed here from the article's draws." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's five draws at GBP 35,000 per QALY as expected losses" ;
    hemo:9000014 "INHB_1 = 2.5714286; INHB_2 = 0.0714286; INHB_3 = 0.0714286; INHB_4 = -0.4285714; INHB_5 = -0.4285714; EL_new = 0.1714286; EL_comp = 0.5428572; D_EL = 0.3714286" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PSA-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-five-draws-at-gbp-35-000-per-qaly-as-expected-losses" .

hemo:7004606 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No expected loss from adopting when every draw favours the new option"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In five illustrative draws with incremental net health benefits of 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.1 and 0.3 QALYs the new option is better in every draw, so the expected loss from adopting it is zero and the expected loss from keeping the comparator equals the mean of 0.2 QALYs. This limiting case has no decision uncertainty and an EVPI of zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No expected loss from adopting when every draw favours the new option" ;
    hemo:9000014 "INHB_1 = 0.1; INHB_2 = 0.2; INHB_3 = 0.3; INHB_4 = 0.1; INHB_5 = 0.3; EL_new = 0; EL_comp = 0.2; D_EL = 0.2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PSA-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-expected-loss-from-adopting-when-every-draw-favours-the-new-option" .

hemo:7004607 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Average hours per use and one hourly value for every patient hour"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each use of the service takes the same average travel, waiting and care time, and every hour is valued at the same w whatever the patient would otherwise have done. Yabroff and colleagues used a single median wage in their base case so that each person's time counted equally; HE-FM-PTC-003 values time by employment status instead. Round-trip travel applies to every use, and waiting is added only where the care time does not already include it, which in the same study meant office and outpatient visits, chemotherapy and radiation therapy, but not emergency visits, hospital stays or ambulatory surgery, which were timed by the interval between arrival or admission and discharge." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Average hours per use and one hourly value for every patient hour" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PTC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "average-hours-per-use-and-one-hourly-value-for-every-patient-hour" .

hemo:7004608 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Service-based hours leave out preparation, home recovery and administrative time"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Counting only the time attached to services misses preparation at home, recovery at home and time spent on insurance matters, which Yabroff and colleagues suggest may be best estimated by survey, micro-costing or time-and-motion methods, so C_T built this way tends to understate patient time. The time of relatives who travel or wait with the patient is carer time and is costed separately." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Service-based hours leave out preparation, home recovery and administrative time" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PTC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "service-based-hours-leave-out-preparation-home-recovery-and-administrative-time" .

hemo:7004609 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One period and one price year for service counts and the value of time"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "n counts uses within one stated period, a year for the time cost per patient-year, and w is in the price year of the other costs. A value from another year is moved with HE-FM-COST-002, as Yabroff and colleagues adjusted their home production value to 2011 dollars, and time costs in later years of a model are discounted with the other costs (HE-FN-DISC-001)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One period and one price year for service counts and the value of time" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PTC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-period-and-one-price-year-for-service-counts-and-the-value-of-time" .

hemo:7004610 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Patient time cost per visit and per year from named hour cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000727 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the travel, waiting and care hours of one visit in TravelHours, WaitHours and CareHours, the hourly value in HourlyValue and the uses in the period in VisitsPerYear, the three formulas fill HoursPerVisit, TimeCostPerVisit and PatientTimeCost in turn. Times recorded in minutes are divided by 60 before entry." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Patient time cost per visit and per year from named hour cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-PTC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "patient-time-cost-per-visit-and-per-year-from-named-hour-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=TravelHours+WaitHours+CareHours; =HourlyValue*HoursPerVisit; =VisitsPerYear*TimeCostPerVisit" .

hemo:7004611 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Patient time cost cell reproduces the article's infusion figure"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With VisitsPerYear 26, TravelHours 1.0, WaitHours 0.5, CareHours 3.0 and HourlyValue 15, the cell PatientTimeCost equals the article's 117 hours at GBP 15 an hour, GBP 1,755. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows travel and waiting left out of HoursPerVisit, which gives 1,170, or the time cost per visit not multiplied by the visits, which gives 67.5." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Patient time cost cell reproduces the article's infusion figure" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PTC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "patient-time-cost-cell-reproduces-the-article-s-infusion-figure" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(PatientTimeCost-117*15)<0.005" .

hemo:7004612 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Hours per visit held in hours and not in minutes"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Checks that HoursPerVisit is above zero and at most 24, so that one visit takes some time and no more than a day; nights in hospital are counted by HE-CF-PTC-001. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the infusion visit entered in minutes as 60, 30 and 180, which gives 270, or a visit with no time entered, which gives 0." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Hours per visit held in hours and not in minutes" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PTC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "hours-per-visit-held-in-hours-and-not-in-minutes" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(HoursPerVisit>0,HoursPerVisit<=24)" .

hemo:7004613 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Infusion visits valued at GBP 15 an hour in the patient time cost example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's hypothetical comparison an infusion needs 26 hospital visits a year, each with 1.0 hour of travel, 0.5 hours of waiting and 3.0 hours in the chair. At an illustrative GBP 15 an hour each visit takes 4.5 hours worth GBP 67.50, and the 117 hours a year are worth GBP 1,755, the article's figure." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Infusion visits valued at GBP 15 an hour in the patient time cost example" ;
    hemo:9000014 "n = 26; t_trav = 1.0; t_wait = 0.5; t_care = 3.0; w = 15; h_v = 4.5; c_v = 67.5; C_T = 1755" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PTC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "infusion-visits-valued-at-gbp-15-an-hour-in-the-patient-time-cost-example" .

hemo:7004614 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Oral treatment clinic visits valued at GBP 15 an hour"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The oral treatment in the same hypothetical comparison needs 12 clinic visits a year, each with 1.0 hour of travel, 0.5 hours of waiting and a 0.5 hour consultation. Each visit takes 2.0 hours worth GBP 30, and the 24 hours a year are worth GBP 360, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Oral treatment clinic visits valued at GBP 15 an hour" ;
    hemo:9000014 "n = 12; t_trav = 1.0; t_wait = 0.5; t_care = 0.5; w = 15; h_v = 2.0; c_v = 30; C_T = 360" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PTC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "oral-treatment-clinic-visits-valued-at-gbp-15-an-hour" .

hemo:7004615 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Infusion visits valued at GBP 7 an hour"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At the article's lower illustrative value of GBP 7 an hour the infusion's 4.5-hour visit is worth GBP 31.50 and the year's 117 hours GBP 819, the article's figure for the lower value of time." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Infusion visits valued at GBP 7 an hour" ;
    hemo:9000014 "n = 26; t_trav = 1.0; t_wait = 0.5; t_care = 3.0; w = 7; h_v = 4.5; c_v = 31.5; C_T = 819" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PTC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "infusion-visits-valued-at-gbp-7-an-hour" .

hemo:7004616 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "QALY gain unchanged when patient time is costed"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "dE is the same in both ratios, so patient time enters only the cost side. The article assumes that the QALY gain already captures any effect of treatment on health-related quality of life, so only the hours spent in care are costed; Kim and colleagues note an open question over whether lost productivity from illness is already captured in QALYs, and the same care applies to time spent unwell. The ratio reading needs dE above zero, and g_ICER is read only when ICER_H is above zero, since a health service ICER of zero leaves it undefined and a negative one reverses its sign. With dE of zero the ICER is undefined and the comparison uses HE-FM-NMB-002, or the dominance rule HE-FM-DOM-001 with its treatment of ties." ;
    hemo:9000009 "QALY gain unchanged when patient time is costed" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PTC-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "qaly-gain-unchanged-when-patient-time-is-costed" .

hemo:7004617 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Perspective that counts patient time in the incremental cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "ICER_S belongs to a limited societal perspective, which Kim and colleagues define as adding patient time, patient transport, unpaid carer time and productivity loss to the costs of the health care sector perspective; this formula adds only patient time, and the other items have their own records. The Second Panel on Cost-Effectiveness in Health and Medicine recommends a health care sector and a societal reference case with an impact inventory, which Breslau and colleagues describe as listing patient-time costs. The NICE manual sets the reference-case perspective on costs as the NHS and PSS (PMG36 sections 4.2.7 and 4.2.9), and its chapter on economic evaluation does not mention patients' own time, so on that perspective ICER_S lies outside the NICE reference case, an inference the article also draws." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Perspective that counts patient time in the incremental cost" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PTC-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "perspective-that-counts-patient-time-in-the-incremental-cost" .

hemo:7004618 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "ICER with incremental patient time from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000728 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the two options' time costs in TimeCostNew and TimeCostComparator, the QALY gain in QALYGain and the health service ICER in HealthServiceICER, the three formulas fill TimeCostIncrement, SocietalICER and ICERRiseShare in turn." ;
    hemo:9000009 "ICER with incremental patient time from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-PTC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "icer-with-incremental-patient-time-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=TimeCostNew-TimeCostComparator; =HealthServiceICER+TimeCostIncrement/QALYGain; =(SocietalICER-HealthServiceICER)/HealthServiceICER" .

hemo:7004619 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "ICER with patient time matches the article's limited societal value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With TimeCostNew 1,755, TimeCostComparator 360, QALYGain 0.10 and HealthServiceICER 15,000, the cell SocietalICER equals the article's direct calculation, GBP 1,500 plus GBP 1,395 over 0.10, which is GBP 28,950 per QALY. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the new option's whole time cost added in place of the increment, which gives 32,550, or the increment added without dividing by the QALY gain, which gives 16,395." ;
    hemo:9000009 "ICER with patient time matches the article's limited societal value" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PTC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "icer-with-patient-time-matches-the-article-s-limited-societal-value" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(SocietalICER-(1500+1755-360)/0.10)<0.005" .

hemo:7004620 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Proportional ICER rise equals incremental time cost over incremental health service cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The rise as a share of the health service ICER equals the incremental time cost over the incremental health service cost, HealthServiceICER times QALYGain, a derived identity that checks ICERRiseShare by a second route. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the rise divided by SocietalICER in place of HealthServiceICER, which gives 0.4819 for the first worked example in place of 0.93." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Proportional ICER rise equals incremental time cost over incremental health service cost" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PTC-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "proportional-icer-rise-equals-incremental-time-cost-over-incremental-health-service-cost" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ICERRiseShare-TimeCostIncrement/(HealthServiceICER*QALYGain))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004621 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Infusion against oral treatment with patient time at GBP 15 an hour"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's hypothetical infusion costs the health service GBP 1,500 more than the oral treatment and gains 0.10 QALYs, an ICER of GBP 15,000 per QALY (HE-FM-ICER-001). Patient time costs of GBP 1,755 and GBP 360 (HE-EX-PTC-001 and HE-EX-PTC-002) give an incremental time cost of GBP 1,395, an ICER of GBP 28,950 per QALY with patient time and a rise of 93%, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Infusion against oral treatment with patient time at GBP 15 an hour" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C_T_new = 1755; C_T_comp = 360; dE = 0.10; ICER_H = 15000; dC_T = 1395; ICER_S = 28950; g_ICER = 0.93" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PTC-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "infusion-against-oral-treatment-with-patient-time-at-gbp-15-an-hour" .

hemo:7004622 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Infusion against oral treatment with patient time at GBP 7 an hour"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At GBP 7 an hour the time costs are GBP 819 and GBP 168 (HE-EX-PTC-003 for the infusion), the incremental time cost is GBP 651 and the ICER with patient time is GBP 21,510 per QALY, a rise of GBP 6,510 as in the article, or 43.4% of the health service ICER (computed here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Infusion against oral treatment with patient time at GBP 7 an hour" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C_T_new = 819; C_T_comp = 168; dE = 0.10; ICER_H = 15000; dC_T = 651; ICER_S = 21510; g_ICER = 0.434" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PTC-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "infusion-against-oral-treatment-with-patient-time-at-gbp-7-an-hour" .

hemo:7004623 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal patient time for both options leaves the ICER unchanged"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When both options take the same patient time, here an illustrative GBP 360 each, the incremental time cost is zero and the ICER keeps its health service value, a limiting case that checks an implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equal patient time for both options leaves the ICER unchanged" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C_T_new = 360; C_T_comp = 360; dE = 0.10; ICER_H = 15000; dC_T = 0; ICER_S = 15000; g_ICER = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PTC-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equal-patient-time-for-both-options-leaves-the-icer-unchanged" .

hemo:7004624 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Employment status held fixed over the costing period"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "E_i is 1 or 0 for the whole period. In their sensitivity analysis Yabroff and colleagues used actual hourly wages for survey participants employed for wages and USD 5.32 an hour, the value of home production adjusted to 2011 dollars, for those not employed for wages. A person who works for part of the period needs the hours split by status, and a group share in place of E_i gives the bias described in HE-ER-PTC-006." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Employment status held fixed over the costing period" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PTC-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "employment-status-held-fixed-over-the-costing-period" .

hemo:7004625 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Wage as the proxy for the value a patient places on time"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The wage stands for the value a person places on time, which Russell argues is a reasonable proxy, adding that wage data are well measured and readily available; this formula uses a person's own wage when employed for wages, as in the sensitivity analysis of Yabroff and colleagues. Each hour is valued once whatever the patient gave up. Tranmer and colleagues review the human capital method, the friction cost method and the Washington Panel approach for patient and carer time and find the methods of valuation inconsistent, so the values chosen are stated and varied in sensitivity analysis." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Wage as the proxy for the value a patient places on time" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PTC-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "wage-as-the-proxy-for-the-value-a-patient-places-on-time" .

hemo:7004626 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Patient time cost by employment status from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000729 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the indicator in EmployedFlag, the own wage in OwnHourlyWage, the home production value in HomeProductionValue and the hours in PersonHours, the first formula fills PersonTimeValue and the second PersonTimeCost. Filled down one row per person, AVERAGE over the PersonTimeCost column gives the mean time cost of the group." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Patient time cost by employment status from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-PTC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "patient-time-cost-by-employment-status-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=EmployedFlag*OwnHourlyWage+(1-EmployedFlag)*HomeProductionValue; =PersonHours*PersonTimeValue" .

hemo:7004627 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Person-level time cost uses home production when not employed"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With EmployedFlag 0, OwnHourlyWage 22, HomeProductionValue 5.32 and PersonHours 55.1, the cell PersonTimeCost equals 55.1 hours at USD 5.32, USD 293.13. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the indicator ignored and the own wage applied, which gives 1,212.20, or the median wage of 16.57 applied, which gives 913.01." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Person-level time cost uses home production when not employed" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PTC-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "person-level-time-cost-uses-home-production-when-not-employed" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(PersonTimeCost-55.1*5.32)<0.005" .

hemo:7004628 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Employment indicator for patient time is 0 or 1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Checks that EmployedFlag holds 0 or 1, as the person-level formula requires. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a group share such as 0.3 entered in place of a person's status, the error described in HE-ER-PTC-006." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Employment indicator for patient time is 0 or 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PTC-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "employment-indicator-for-patient-time-is-0-or-1" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=OR(EmployedFlag=0,EmployedFlag=1)" .

hemo:7004629 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Person not employed for wages valued at home production"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative hours of 55.1 a year, the adjusted mean for cancer survivors aged 65 and over in Yabroff and colleagues, for a person not employed for wages and so with no own wage, valued at USD 5.32 an hour, give USD 293.13 a year (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Person not employed for wages valued at home production" ;
    hemo:9000014 "E_i = 0; w_i = 0; w_hp = 5.32; h_i = 55.1; v_i = 5.32; C_T_i = 293.13" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PTC-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "person-not-employed-for-wages-valued-at-home-production" .

hemo:7004630 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Employed person valued at an own hourly wage"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An employed person with 30.2 hours a year, the adjusted mean for survivors aged 18 to 64 in the same study, and an illustrative own wage of USD 22.00 an hour has a time cost of USD 664.40 a year." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Employed person valued at an own hourly wage" ;
    hemo:9000014 "E_i = 1; w_i = 22; w_hp = 5.32; h_i = 30.2; v_i = 22; C_T_i = 664.4" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PTC-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "employed-person-valued-at-an-own-hourly-wage" .

hemo:7004631 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Own wage equal to the median wage reproduces the single-wage time cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With an own wage equal to the study's median wage of USD 16.57 the 55.1 hours are worth USD 913.01, close to the USD 912.96 that Table 4 of the study reports for survivors aged 65 and over in the base case, the gap coming from the rounding of the hours (agreement computed here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Own wage equal to the median wage reproduces the single-wage time cost" ;
    hemo:9000014 "E_i = 1; w_i = 16.57; w_hp = 5.32; h_i = 55.1; v_i = 16.57; C_T_i = 913.01" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PTC-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "own-wage-equal-to-the-median-wage-reproduces-the-single-wage-time-cost" .

hemo:7004632 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "EQ-5D-5L level decrements zero at level 1 and ordered by level"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "No decrement applies at level 1, and on each dimension b_2, b_3, b_4 and b_5 are zero or positive and no smaller than the coefficient of the level below. A set that broke this order would score a worse level on one dimension above a better one with the other dimensions unchanged. Devlin and colleagues built the order into the England model by estimating the level 2 parameters first and adding terms that can only be non-negative for each worse level, and the England coefficients keep it on all five dimensions, for example 0.058, 0.076, 0.207 and 0.274 for mobility." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EQ-5D-5L level decrements zero at level 1 and ordered by level" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EQ5L-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "eq-5d-5l-level-decrements-zero-at-level-1-and-ordered-by-level" .

hemo:7004633 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Whole-number EQ-5D-5L levels with five labels per dimension"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The level is a whole number 1 to 5 read from the respondent's answer. The labels in the England value set table are slight, moderate and severe for levels 2 to 4, with unable as the level 5 label for mobility, self-care and usual activities and extreme for pain or discomfort and anxiety or depression. The min and max indicators are exact only for whole numbers, so a mean level or a missing answer has to be resolved before scoring." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Whole-number EQ-5D-5L levels with five labels per dimension" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EQ5L-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "whole-number-eq-5d-5l-levels-with-five-labels-per-dimension" .

hemo:7004634 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "EQ-5D-5L decrement of one dimension independent of the other dimensions"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The decrement on one dimension does not depend on the levels reported on the others, the main-effects structure of the 20-parameter model that Devlin and colleagues selected for England after testing alternative models for interaction effects between dimensions and levels. A set with interaction terms needs its own scoring rule. Summing the five lookups and subtracting the total from 1, less a constant where the set reports one, is HE-FM-EQ3L-001 with the N3 decrement gamma set to zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EQ-5D-5L decrement of one dimension independent of the other dimensions" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EQ5L-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "eq-5d-5l-decrement-of-one-dimension-independent-of-the-other-dimensions" .

hemo:7004635 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "EQ-5D-5L dimension decrement with CHOOSE from named level coefficients"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000730 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the reported level in a cell named LevelReported and the four coefficients in cells named DecrementSlight, DecrementModerate, DecrementSevere and DecrementWorst, CHOOSE returns 0 for level 1 and the matching coefficient otherwise. The result goes in a cell named DimensionDecrement." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EQ-5D-5L dimension decrement with CHOOSE from named level coefficients" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-EQ5L-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "eq-5d-5l-dimension-decrement-with-choose-from-named-level-coefficients" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CHOOSE(LevelReported,0,DecrementSlight,DecrementModerate,DecrementSevere,DecrementWorst)" .

hemo:7004636 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "EQ-5D-5L dimension decrement cell matches an independent lookup at level 4"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the England mobility coefficients entered and LevelReported set to 4, the cell DimensionDecrement agrees with the fourth entry of the England mobility row looked up with INDEX, 0.207. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the level read off by one, as when the leading 0 is left out of CHOOSE, which returns 0.274 for level 4." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EQ-5D-5L dimension decrement cell matches an independent lookup at level 4" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EQ5L-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "eq-5d-5l-dimension-decrement-cell-matches-an-independent-lookup-at-level-4" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(DimensionDecrement-INDEX({0,0.058,0.076,0.207,0.274},LevelReported))<0.000001" .

hemo:7004637 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "EQ-5D-5L level coefficients ordered on one dimension"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Checks that the four coefficients entered for a dimension are not negative and do not fall as the level rises. Expected result TRUE for every dimension of the England set. FALSE shows coefficients entered in the wrong cells, for example the England mobility values 0.076 and 0.207 swapped between DecrementModerate and DecrementSevere." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EQ-5D-5L level coefficients ordered on one dimension" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EQ5L-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "eq-5d-5l-level-coefficients-ordered-on-one-dimension" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(DecrementSlight>=0,DecrementModerate>=DecrementSlight,DecrementSevere>=DecrementModerate,DecrementWorst>=DecrementSevere)" .

hemo:7004638 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "England EQ-5D-5L mobility decrement at the severe level"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the England mobility coefficients of Devlin and colleagues 2018, a respondent reporting severe problems in walking about, level 4, takes the level 4 coefficient 0.207 and none of the others." ;
    hemo:9000009 "England EQ-5D-5L mobility decrement at the severe level" ;
    hemo:9000014 "b_2 = 0.058; b_3 = 0.076; b_4 = 0.207; b_5 = 0.274; l_d = 4; beta_d = 0.207" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EQ5L-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "england-eq-5d-5l-mobility-decrement-at-the-severe-level" .

hemo:7004639 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "England EQ-5D-5L pain or discomfort decrement at the extreme level"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Extreme pain or discomfort, level 5, takes the England coefficient 0.335, the largest single decrement in the set." ;
    hemo:9000009 "England EQ-5D-5L pain or discomfort decrement at the extreme level" ;
    hemo:9000014 "b_2 = 0.063; b_3 = 0.084; b_4 = 0.276; b_5 = 0.335; l_d = 5; beta_d = 0.335" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EQ5L-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "england-eq-5d-5l-pain-or-discomfort-decrement-at-the-extreme-level" .

hemo:7004640 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "EQ-5D-5L dimension with no problems carries no decrement"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At level 1 every indicator is zero, so the decrement is zero whatever the coefficients, shown here with the England anxiety or depression coefficients. This limiting case checks that full health on a dimension costs nothing." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EQ-5D-5L dimension with no problems carries no decrement" ;
    hemo:9000014 "b_2 = 0.078; b_3 = 0.104; b_4 = 0.285; b_5 = 0.289; l_d = 1; beta_d = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EQ5L-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "eq-5d-5l-dimension-with-no-problems-carries-no-decrement" .

hemo:7004641 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "England EQ-5D-5L coefficients as printed in Table 2 of Devlin and colleagues"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The decrements are the central estimates in Table 2, the mean coefficients of Bayesian regressions of a hybrid model of time trade-off and discrete choice data with three latent groups, reported after the latent class adjustment. Mobility 0.058, 0.076, 0.207 and 0.274, self-care 0.050, 0.080, 0.164 and 0.203, usual activities 0.050, 0.063, 0.162 and 0.184, pain or discomfort 0.063, 0.084, 0.276 and 0.335 and anxiety or depression 0.078, 0.104, 0.285 and 0.289, for levels 2 to 5. The table's constant row is 1.000, the value from which the decrements are subtracted." ;
    hemo:9000009 "England EQ-5D-5L coefficients as printed in Table 2 of Devlin and colleagues" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EQ5L-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "england-eq-5d-5l-coefficients-as-printed-in-table-2-of-devlin-and-colleagues" .

hemo:7004642 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Whole-number levels in the order of the EQ-5D-5L profile for the England set"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The levels are entered as whole numbers 1 to 5 in the profile order mobility, self-care, usual activities, pain or discomfort and anxiety or depression, so state 23245 has slight mobility problems and extreme anxiety or depression. The min and max indicators are exact only for those values." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Whole-number levels in the order of the EQ-5D-5L profile for the England set" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EQ5L-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "whole-number-levels-in-the-order-of-the-eq-5d-5l-profile-for-the-england-set" .

hemo:7004643 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "England EQ-5D-5L set outside the NICE reference case"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Section 4.3.16 of PMG36 still states that NICE does not recommend the Devlin value set and asks for five-level responses to be mapped onto the three-level value set in reference-case analyses. NICE's interim methods statement PMG51 of 27 August 2026, which the manual's notice says takes precedence until the manual is updated, makes the EQ-5D-5L and the UK value set of Rowen and colleagues 2026 NICE's preferred approach for adults and says that its position statement on the England set no longer applies. The UK coefficients are not formalised here, so this formula serves studies that report the England set and not a NICE reference case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "England EQ-5D-5L set outside the NICE reference case" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EQ5L-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "england-eq-5d-5l-set-outside-the-nice-reference-case" .

hemo:7004644 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Values for the England EQ-5D-5L set mostly predicted by the model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every value comes from the fitted model, including the 86 states that respondents valued by time trade-off, about 2.8% of the 3,125 (HE-FM-EQ5L-003). The quality assurance by Hernández Alava and colleagues 2020 reported significant limitations in the valuation data and the statistical analysis behind the set, which the authors of the set disputed in a reply (van Hout and colleagues 2020), so results scored with it are best reported with the set named." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Values for the England EQ-5D-5L set mostly predicted by the model" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EQ5L-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "values-for-the-england-eq-5d-5l-set-mostly-predicted-by-the-model" .

hemo:7004645 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "England EQ-5D-5L value set from five named level cells with CHOOSE"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000731 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the five levels in cells named LevelMob, LevelSelf, LevelUsual, LevelPain and LevelAnx, each CHOOSE picks the England decrement for the level reported, 0 for level 1. The result goes in a cell named EnglandUtility, and rounding it to 3 decimals with ROUND matches the published values." ;
    hemo:9000009 "England EQ-5D-5L value set from five named level cells with CHOOSE" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-EQ5L-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "england-eq-5d-5l-value-set-from-five-named-level-cells-with-choose" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1-CHOOSE(LevelMob,0,0.058,0.076,0.207,0.274)-CHOOSE(LevelSelf,0,0.050,0.080,0.164,0.203)-CHOOSE(LevelUsual,0,0.050,0.063,0.162,0.184)-CHOOSE(LevelPain,0,0.063,0.084,0.276,0.335)-CHOOSE(LevelAnx,0,0.078,0.104,0.285,0.289)" .

hemo:7004646 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "England EQ-5D-5L cell reproduces the Table 2 value of state 23245"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With LevelMob 2, LevelSelf 3, LevelUsual 2, LevelPain 4 and LevelAnx 5, the cell EnglandUtility rounds to 0.247, the value worked in Table 2. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the profile read in reverse order, 54232, which gives 0.350, or a decrement taken from the wrong level." ;
    hemo:9000009 "England EQ-5D-5L cell reproduces the Table 2 value of state 23245" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EQ5L-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "england-eq-5d-5l-cell-reproduces-the-table-2-value-of-state-23245" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND(EnglandUtility,3)=0.247" .

hemo:7004647 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "England EQ-5D-5L cell gives minus 0.285 for the worst state"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With all five level cells set to 5, the cell EnglandUtility rounds to minus 0.285 and lies below zero. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a transcription error in a level 5 coefficient, for example the anxiety or depression severe value 0.285 entered in place of the extreme value 0.289, which gives minus 0.281." ;
    hemo:9000009 "England EQ-5D-5L cell gives minus 0.285 for the worst state" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EQ5L-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "england-eq-5d-5l-cell-gives-minus-0-285-for-the-worst-state" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ROUND(EnglandUtility,3)=-0.285,EnglandUtility<0)" .

hemo:7004648 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "England EQ-5D-5L value of full health 11111"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With every level at 1 no indicator is set and no decrement applies, so the England set gives 1, the limiting case at the top of the scale." ;
    hemo:9000009 "England EQ-5D-5L value of full health 11111" ;
    hemo:9000014 "l_MO = 1; l_SC = 1; l_UA = 1; l_PD = 1; l_AD = 1; U = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EQ5L-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "england-eq-5d-5l-value-of-full-health-11111" .

hemo:7004649 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "England EQ-5D-5L value of state 23245 as worked in Table 2"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The table's own example subtracts the mobility slight decrement 0.058, the self-care moderate decrement 0.080, the usual activities slight decrement 0.050, the pain or discomfort severe decrement 0.276 and the anxiety or depression extreme decrement 0.289 from 1, giving 0.247." ;
    hemo:9000009 "England EQ-5D-5L value of state 23245 as worked in Table 2" ;
    hemo:9000014 "l_MO = 2; l_SC = 3; l_UA = 2; l_PD = 4; l_AD = 5; U = 0.247" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EQ5L-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "england-eq-5d-5l-value-of-state-23245-as-worked-in-table-2" .

hemo:7004650 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "England EQ-5D-5L value of the mild state 21111"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Slight problems in walking about and nothing else cost the mobility level 2 decrement 0.058, giving 0.942, the value listed for 21111 in Table 3 of Devlin and colleagues." ;
    hemo:9000009 "England EQ-5D-5L value of the mild state 21111" ;
    hemo:9000014 "l_MO = 2; l_SC = 1; l_UA = 1; l_PD = 1; l_AD = 1; U = 0.942" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EQ5L-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "england-eq-5d-5l-value-of-the-mild-state-21111" .

hemo:7004651 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "England EQ-5D-5L value of the mild state 12111"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Slight problems washing or dressing alone cost the self-care level 2 decrement 0.050, giving 0.950, the highest value below full health, which state 11211 shares." ;
    hemo:9000009 "England EQ-5D-5L value of the mild state 12111" ;
    hemo:9000014 "l_MO = 1; l_SC = 2; l_UA = 1; l_PD = 1; l_AD = 1; U = 0.950" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EQ5L-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "england-eq-5d-5l-value-of-the-mild-state-12111" .

hemo:7004652 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "England EQ-5D-5L value of the worst state 55555"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Level 5 on every dimension costs 0.274, 0.203, 0.184, 0.335 and 0.289, 1.285 in all, giving minus 0.285, the minimum of the England set and a state valued as worse than dead." ;
    hemo:9000009 "England EQ-5D-5L value of the worst state 55555" ;
    hemo:9000014 "l_MO = 5; l_SC = 5; l_UA = 5; l_PD = 5; l_AD = 5; U = -0.285" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EQ5L-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "england-eq-5d-5l-value-of-the-worst-state-55555" .

hemo:7004653 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Every combination of EQ-5D-5L levels counted as a state"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each dimension is answered independently, so every combination of levels is a possible profile, and the count leaves out dead, which anchors the scale and is not a profile. Devlin and colleagues give the count as 3,125, 5 to the power 5, against 243, 3 to the power 5, for the three-level version." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Every combination of EQ-5D-5L levels counted as a state" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EQ5L-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "every-combination-of-eq-5d-5l-levels-counted-as-a-state" .

hemo:7004654 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Share of EQ-5D-5L states valued counts the time trade-off design only"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "n_v counts the distinct states in the time trade-off design. Choice tasks in a hybrid design cover other states, which are not counted here, so f_v is the share of states with a directly observed value on the utility scale." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Share of EQ-5D-5L states valued counts the time trade-off design only" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-EQ5L-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "share-of-eq-5d-5l-states-valued-counts-the-time-trade-off-design-only" .

hemo:7004655 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "EQ-5D state count from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000732 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the levels per dimension in a cell named LevelsPerDimension and the number of dimensions in DimensionCount, the formula returns S in a cell named StateCount." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EQ-5D state count from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-EQ5L-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "eq-5d-state-count-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LevelsPerDimension^DimensionCount" .

hemo:7004656 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "EQ-5D main-effects decrement count from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000732 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the same two cells, the formula returns P in a cell named DecrementCount." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EQ-5D main-effects decrement count from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-EQ5L-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "eq-5d-main-effects-decrement-count-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=DimensionCount*(LevelsPerDimension-1)" .

hemo:7004657 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Share of EQ-5D states valued from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000732 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the number of states in the time trade-off design in a cell named StatesValued, the formula returns f_v in a cell named ShareValued." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Share of EQ-5D states valued from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-EQ5L-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "share-of-eq-5d-states-valued-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=StatesValued/StateCount" .

hemo:7004658 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "EQ-5D-5L state count and England design share reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With LevelsPerDimension 5, DimensionCount 5 and StatesValued 86, StateCount is 3,125 and ShareValued rounds to 0.0275. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the count taken as levels times dimensions, 25, which makes the share 3.44." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EQ-5D-5L state count and England design share reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EQ5L-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "eq-5d-5l-state-count-and-england-design-share-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(StateCount=3125,ROUND(ShareValued,4)=0.0275)" .

hemo:7004659 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "EQ-5D-5L decrement count matches the England 20-parameter model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the same inputs, DecrementCount equals the 20 parameters of the England model and ShareValued is below the 3% that the quality assurance reported for the England time trade-off tasks. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows level 1 counted as a decrement, which gives 25." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EQ-5D-5L decrement count matches the England 20-parameter model" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-EQ5L-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "eq-5d-5l-decrement-count-matches-the-england-20-parameter-model" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(DecrementCount=20,ShareValued<0.03)" .

hemo:7004660 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "EQ-5D-5L state count and the England time trade-off design"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Five dimensions with five levels describe 3,125 states and need 20 decrements in a main-effects set. The England study valued 86 states by time trade-off, a share of 0.0275, under 3% of the states." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EQ-5D-5L state count and the England time trade-off design" ;
    hemo:9000014 "L = 5; K = 5; n_v = 86; S = 3125; P = 20; f_v = 0.0275" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EQ5L-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "eq-5d-5l-state-count-and-the-england-time-trade-off-design" .

hemo:7004661 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "EQ-5D-5L state count and the UK time trade-off design"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The UK valuation study of Rowen and colleagues 2026 used composite time trade-off for 102 states, a share of 0.0326 of the 3,125." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EQ-5D-5L state count and the UK time trade-off design" ;
    hemo:9000014 "L = 5; K = 5; n_v = 102; S = 3125; P = 20; f_v = 0.0326" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EQ5L-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "eq-5d-5l-state-count-and-the-uk-time-trade-off-design" .

hemo:7004662 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "EQ-5D-3L state count and the UK MVH design for comparison"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Three levels on five dimensions give 243 states and 10 level decrements. The UK three-level study valued 42 states by time trade-off (HE-SR-EQ3L-001), a share of 0.1728, about six times the England five-level share." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EQ-5D-3L state count and the UK MVH design for comparison" ;
    hemo:9000014 "L = 3; K = 5; n_v = 42; S = 243; P = 10; f_v = 0.1728" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-EQ5L-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "eq-5d-3l-state-count-and-the-uk-mvh-design-for-comparison" .

hemo:7004663 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Restricted means of both arms taken at one follow-up time"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Both restricted means run from time 0 to the same tau, no later than the last follow-up observed in either arm, the restriction time of HE-FM-ADMC-003, and both means over the model horizon start at time 0 and run to the same horizon." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Restricted means of both arms taken at one follow-up time" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-LYG-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "restricted-means-of-both-arms-taken-at-one-follow-up-time" .

hemo:7004664 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Model horizon means from extrapolations that follow the trial data"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The means over the model horizon come from curves that agree with the observed data up to tau, so that each is its restricted mean plus the area after tau. TSD 14 section 2 states that the base case should use extrapolation of the fitted distribution and that results based only on the observed data can show the importance of the extrapolated period in the mean; phi expresses that importance as a share of the gain, a reading derived here that TSD 14 does not state." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Model horizon means from extrapolations that follow the trial data" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-LYG-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "model-horizon-means-from-extrapolations-that-follow-the-trial-data" .

hemo:7004665 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Within-trial share of life years gained read as a share only for a positive gain"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "phi is at least 0 and at most 1 when the gain over the horizon is positive and neither the within-trial gain nor the gain after follow-up is negative. If the curves cross after follow-up the within-trial gain can exceed the gain over the horizon, phi rises above 1 and dLY_beyond is negative (HE-EX-LYG-007), and phi is not defined when dLY_life is 0." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Within-trial share of life years gained read as a share only for a positive gain" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-LYG-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "within-trial-share-of-life-years-gained-read-as-a-share-only-for-a-positive-gain" .

hemo:7004666 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Excel cells for the within-trial share of life years gained"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000733 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the restricted means in RMSTNew and RMSTStd and the means over the model horizon in MeanSurvNew and MeanSurvStd, the four formulas return the within-trial gain in RMSTGain, the gain over the horizon in LifetimeLYGain, the share in WithinTrialShare and the gain after follow-up in GainBeyondTrial." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Excel cells for the within-trial share of life years gained" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-LYG-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "excel-cells-for-the-within-trial-share-of-life-years-gained" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=RMSTNew-RMSTStd; =MeanSurvNew-MeanSurvStd; =RMSTGain/LifetimeLYGain; =LifetimeLYGain-RMSTGain" .

hemo:7004667 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's two-year follow-up holds 6/13 of the life years gained"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With RMSTNew at 1.55, RMSTStd at 1.25, MeanSurvNew at 2.15 and MeanSurvStd at 1.5, WithinTrialShare is compared with 0.30 divided by 0.65, which is 6/13 or about 0.4615. Expected result TRUE. A share taken within one arm, the new treatment's restricted mean over its mean over the horizon (1 minus its tail share in HE-FM-ADMC-004), gives 0.7209 and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's two-year follow-up holds 6/13 of the life years gained" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-LYG-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-two-year-follow-up-holds-6-13-of-the-life-years-gained" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(WithinTrialShare-6/13)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004668 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Life years gained after follow-up equal the difference of the two tail areas"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "GainBeyondTrial is compared with the area after tau in the new arm, MeanSurvNew minus RMSTNew, less the area after tau in the comparator, MeanSurvStd minus RMSTStd, a route that uses neither gain cell. Expected result TRUE. A GainBeyondTrial cell that takes only the new arm's tail gives 0.60 against 0.35 for the article's figures and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Life years gained after follow-up equal the difference of the two tail areas" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-LYG-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "life-years-gained-after-follow-up-equal-the-difference-of-the-two-tail-areas" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(GainBeyondTrial-((MeanSurvNew-RMSTNew)-(MeanSurvStd-RMSTStd)))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004669 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's two-year follow-up against the life years gained over the horizon"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's step 6 follow-up ends at year 2, where the restricted means are 1.55 and 1.25 life years against means of 2.15 and 1.50 over the horizon, all from illustrative curves. The within-trial gain of 0.30 is 46.2% of the gain of 0.65, so 0.35 life years, more than half, lie after follow-up, as the article states." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's two-year follow-up against the life years gained over the horizon" ;
    hemo:9000014 "RMST_1 = 1.55; RMST_0 = 1.25; LY_1 = 2.15; LY_0 = 1.5; dRMST = 0.3; dLY_life = 0.65; phi = 0.4615; dLY_beyond = 0.35" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-LYG-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-two-year-follow-up-against-the-life-years-gained-over-the-horizon" .

hemo:7004670 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Late survival gain largely unseen at two years of follow-up"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative curves with the same gain of 0.65 life years arising later, standard care at 0.90, 0.70, 0.40, 0.10 and 0 and the new treatment at 0.90, 0.75, 0.65, 0.45 and 0 at the ends of years 1 to 5, have trapezoid restricted means of 1.775 and 1.75 at two years and means of 3.25 and 2.60 over the horizon, so a two-year trial sees 0.025 of the 0.65 life years gained, 3.8%, and 0.625 lie after follow-up (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Late survival gain largely unseen at two years of follow-up" ;
    hemo:9000014 "RMST_1 = 1.775; RMST_0 = 1.75; LY_1 = 3.25; LY_0 = 2.6; dRMST = 0.025; dLY_life = 0.65; phi = 0.0385; dLY_beyond = 0.625" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-LYG-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "late-survival-gain-largely-unseen-at-two-years-of-follow-up" .

hemo:7004671 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Survival curves crossing after follow-up give a within-trial share above 1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With illustrative means of 2.0 and 1.85 over the horizon, the comparator's longer tail cuts the gain from 0.30 within follow-up to 0.15 over the horizon, so phi is 2 and the gain after follow-up is negative (computed here for illustration), the case set aside in HE-AS-LYG-007." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Survival curves crossing after follow-up give a within-trial share above 1" ;
    hemo:9000014 "RMST_1 = 1.55; RMST_0 = 1.25; LY_1 = 2.0; LY_0 = 1.85; dRMST = 0.3; dLY_life = 0.15; phi = 2; dLY_beyond = -0.15" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-LYG-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "survival-curves-crossing-after-follow-up-give-a-within-trial-share-above-1" .

hemo:7004672 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Cobb-Douglas class with constant returns in medical care and own time"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Grossman assumes the gross investment function is homogeneous of degree one in medical care and own time and, for estimation, a member of the Cobb-Douglas class, so the elasticity of substitution between the two inputs is one and the cost share of medical care, 1-K, is constant. Grossman notes that with an elasticity of substitution other than one K would depend on W and P. The closed forms of HE-FM-HPF-002 and HE-FM-HPF-003 would then not apply (derived here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cobb-Douglas class with constant returns in medical care and own time" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HPF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cobb-douglas-class-with-constant-returns-in-medical-care-and-own-time" .

hemo:7004673 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Factor-neutral schooling effect and a suppressed intercept in gross investment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "One more year of E raises the marginal products of M and TH by the same percentage, Grossman's Hicks or factor-neutrality assumption, so schooling changes the gross investment obtained from given inputs but gives no reason to change the ratio of care to time. Grossman writes the estimating form with its intercept suppressed, left out of the equation as shown, so the level of the efficiency factor is not pinned down. Because health capital is measured in abstract units, the formula absorbs the intercept by counting E from the level at which the factor is 1 (a normalisation made here), so only differences in E between people or years carry meaning. Allocative efficiency, in which schooling improves knowledge of the true effects of inputs and so the choice of inputs, is a separate channel that this formula leaves out." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Factor-neutral schooling effect and a suppressed intercept in gross investment" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HPF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "factor-neutral-schooling-effect-and-a-suppressed-intercept-in-gross-investment" .

hemo:7004674 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Medical care as the single purchased health input with no joint production"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "M stands for the purchased health inputs, as Grossman associates the market goods input in the gross investment function with medical care, and the formula rules out joint production, in which inputs such as cigarette smoking have negative marginal products in health but are bought as inputs into other commodities. Grossman notes that the model keeps its structure if the main purchased input is something other than medical care." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Medical care as the single purchased health input with no joint production" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HPF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "medical-care-as-the-single-purchased-health-input-with-no-joint-production" .

hemo:7004675 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Cobb-Douglas gross investment in health from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000734 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the schooling parameter in SchoolingEffect, the years above the reference level in SchoolingYears, the inputs in MedicalCare and OwnTime and the time share in TimeShare, the formula fills GrossInvestCD." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cobb-Douglas gross investment in health from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-HPF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cobb-douglas-gross-investment-in-health-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=EXP(SchoolingEffect*SchoolingYears)*MedicalCare^(1-TimeShare)*OwnTime^TimeShare" .

hemo:7004676 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Cobb-Douglas gross investment cell reproduces the article's 20 units"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With MedicalCare 10, OwnTime 40, TimeShare 0.5, SchoolingEffect 0.03 and SchoolingYears 0, the cell GrossInvestCD equals the square root of 10 times 40, the article's 20 units. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the exponents left out, which gives 400, or total years of schooling such as 12 entered in place of years above the reference level, which gives 28.67." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cobb-Douglas gross investment cell reproduces the article's 20 units" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HPF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cobb-douglas-gross-investment-cell-reproduces-the-article-s-20-units" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(GrossInvestCD-SQRT(10*40))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004677 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Time share and inputs in valid ranges for Cobb-Douglas gross investment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Checks that TimeShare is above 0 and below 1 and that MedicalCare and OwnTime are above 0, as the log form of the equation requires. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the time share entered as a percentage, 50, or an input of zero, for which the logarithm is undefined." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Time share and inputs in valid ranges for Cobb-Douglas gross investment" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HPF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "time-share-and-inputs-in-valid-ranges-for-cobb-douglas-gross-investment" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(TimeShare>0,TimeShare<1,MedicalCare>0,OwnTime>0)" .

hemo:7004678 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Least-cost inputs of 10 units of care and 40 hours in the health production example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative person uses 10 units of medical care and 40 hours of own time with K of 0.5 and schooling at the reference level, so I is the square root of 400, 20 units of health capital. The figures are illustrative and not drawn from any study." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Least-cost inputs of 10 units of care and 40 hours in the health production example" ;
    hemo:9000014 "rho_H = 0.03; E = 0; M = 10; TH = 40; K = 0.5; I = 20" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HPF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "least-cost-inputs-of-10-units-of-care-and-40-hours-in-the-health-production-example" .

hemo:7004679 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Same outlay spent on 15 units of care and 20 hours in the health production example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Spending the same 800 GBP on 15 units of care and 20 hours, the article's alternative mix, gives the square root of 300, 17.32 units, about 13% less than the least-cost mix of HE-EX-HPF-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same outlay spent on 15 units of care and 20 hours in the health production example" ;
    hemo:9000014 "rho_H = 0.03; E = 0; M = 15; TH = 20; K = 0.5; I = 17.32" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HPF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-outlay-spent-on-15-units-of-care-and-20-hours-in-the-health-production-example" .

hemo:7004680 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "One more year of schooling with the same inputs in the health production example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With one more year of schooling at rho_H of 0.03 the same 10 units of care and 40 hours give exp(0.03) times 20, 20.61 units, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One more year of schooling with the same inputs in the health production example" ;
    hemo:9000014 "rho_H = 0.03; E = 1; M = 10; TH = 40; K = 0.5; I = 20.61" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HPF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-more-year-of-schooling-with-the-same-inputs-in-the-health-production-example" .

hemo:7004681 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Input prices fixed and the outlay spent on care and own time"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "P and W do not depend on how much care or time the person uses, as Grossman assumes, and the whole outlay C goes on medical care and on own time priced at the wage, the two prices in Grossman's condition (2-10). Time lost to illness is not an input and does not enter C." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Input prices fixed and the outlay spent on care and own time" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HPF-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "input-prices-fixed-and-the-outlay-spent-on-care-and-own-time" .

hemo:7004682 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Interior least-cost mix with both inputs used"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Both inputs are bought in positive amounts, which the Cobb-Douglas form ensures for K greater than 0 and less than 1, and the split is the same whether the person fixes the outlay or the gross investment (derived here). The least-cost condition settles how gross investment is produced; how much to produce is set by the optimality condition HE-FM-DFH-002." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Interior least-cost mix with both inputs used" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HPF-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "interior-least-cost-mix-with-both-inputs-used" .

hemo:7004683 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Least-cost split of an outlay between care and own time from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000735 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the outlay in Outlay, the price of care in PriceCare, the wage in Wage, the time share in TimeShare and the schooling inputs in SchoolingEffect and SchoolingYears, the six formulas fill CareUnits, TimeHours, GrossInvestSplit, MargProdCare, MargProdTime and MargProdRatio in turn." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Least-cost split of an outlay between care and own time from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-HPF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "least-cost-split-of-an-outlay-between-care-and-own-time-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(1-TimeShare)*Outlay/PriceCare; =TimeShare*Outlay/Wage; =EXP(SchoolingEffect*SchoolingYears)*CareUnits^(1-TimeShare)*TimeHours^TimeShare; =(1-TimeShare)*GrossInvestSplit/CareUnits; =TimeShare*GrossInvestSplit/TimeHours; =MargProdCare/MargProdTime" .

hemo:7004684 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Ratio of marginal products equals the price ratio at the least-cost split"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compares MargProdRatio with PriceCare over Wage, Grossman's least-cost condition. Expected result TRUE for any outlay, prices and time share. FALSE shows equal quantities of care and time bought, 16 of each for the article's 800 GBP, which gives a ratio of 1 against 4, or the shares swapped when K is 0.25, which gives 5 units and 60 hours and a ratio of 36 against 4." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ratio of marginal products equals the price ratio at the least-cost split" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HPF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ratio-of-marginal-products-equals-the-price-ratio-at-the-least-cost-split" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(MargProdRatio-PriceCare/Wage)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004685 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Least-cost split gives 15 units of care and 20 hours when K is 0.25"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With Outlay 800, PriceCare 40, Wage 10 and TimeShare 0.25, CareUnits equals 0.75 times 800 over 40, 15 units, and TimeHours equals 0.25 times 800 over 10, 20 hours. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows TimeShare used as the share of care, which gives 5 units and 60 hours, or the outlay divided by the sum of the two prices, which gives 16 of each." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Least-cost split gives 15 units of care and 20 hours when K is 0.25" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HPF-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "least-cost-split-gives-15-units-of-care-and-20-hours-when-k-is-0-25" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(CareUnits-15)<1E-9,ABS(TimeHours-20)<1E-9)" .

hemo:7004686 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's 800 GBP split between 10 units of care and 40 hours"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With an outlay of 800 GBP, a price of care of 40 GBP, a wage of 10 GBP and K of 0.5, half the outlay buys 10 units of care and half buys 40 hours, giving 20 units of gross investment. The marginal products are 1 and 0.25, a ratio of 4 equal to the price ratio of 40 to 10, as in the article. The figures are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's 800 GBP split between 10 units of care and 40 hours" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = 800; P = 40; W = 10; K = 0.5; rho_H = 0.03; E = 0; M = 10; TH = 40; I = 20; MP_M = 1; MP_TH = 0.25; R_MP = 4" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HPF-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-800-gbp-split-between-10-units-of-care-and-40-hours" .

hemo:7004687 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Time share of 0.25 makes the article's alternative mix least-cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For an illustrative person whose own time is a quarter of the cost of gross investment, K of 0.25, the same 800 GBP at the same prices buys 15 units of care and 20 hours, the mix that the article shows is not least-cost when K is 0.5. Gross investment is 16.12 units and the ratio of marginal products is again 4 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Time share of 0.25 makes the article's alternative mix least-cost" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = 800; P = 40; W = 10; K = 0.25; rho_H = 0.03; E = 0; M = 15; TH = 20; I = 16.12; MP_M = 0.806; MP_TH = 0.2015; R_MP = 4" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HPF-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "time-share-of-0-25-makes-the-article-s-alternative-mix-least-cost" .

hemo:7004688 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Older person's 1,000 GBP split between 12.5 units of care and 50 hours"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's older person spends 1,000 GBP to hold the stock at 100 with depreciation of 0.25, which buys 12.5 units of care and 50 hours and so 25 units of gross investment, the investment that HE-FM-DFH-001 requires. The figures are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Older person's 1,000 GBP split between 12.5 units of care and 50 hours" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = 1000; P = 40; W = 10; K = 0.5; rho_H = 0.03; E = 0; M = 12.5; TH = 50; I = 25; MP_M = 1; MP_TH = 0.25; R_MP = 4" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HPF-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "older-person-s-1-000-gbp-split-between-12-5-units-of-care-and-50-hours" .

hemo:7004689 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant returns and fixed prices make the unit cost equal marginal cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "pi does not depend on I only because the function has constant returns and P and W do not change with the amounts used, the conditions under which Grossman states that average cost is constant and equal to marginal cost, so C is proportional to I. Ehrlich and Chuma, as Grossman reports, introduce rising marginal cost of investment. C would then rise faster than I (derived here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant returns and fixed prices make the unit cost equal marginal cost" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HPF-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-returns-and-fixed-prices-make-the-unit-cost-equal-marginal-cost" .

hemo:7004690 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Schooling lowers the unit cost of gross investment by the efficiency factor"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "With factor-neutral efficiency the least-cost ratio of care to time is the same at every E, and a unit of gross investment needs the inputs divided by exp(rho_H) for each extra year, so pi falls in the same proportion. Grossman's equation (3-10) gives the percentage form, a fall in pi of rho_H per unit of E, and his 1972 example states that education raising both marginal products by 3 percent would reduce the price of gross investment by 3 percent; the exponential factor is the exact form under the log-linear equation (5-3) (derived here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Schooling lowers the unit cost of gross investment by the efficiency factor" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HPF-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "schooling-lowers-the-unit-cost-of-gross-investment-by-the-efficiency-factor" .

hemo:7004691 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Prices of the period in which the gross investment is produced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "P and W are the prices of the period in which the gross investment is made, so pi computed with period t-1 prices is the pi_(t-1) of HE-FM-DFH-002. C is the cost within that period; costs of later periods are discounted with HE-FN-DISC-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Prices of the period in which the gross investment is produced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HPF-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "prices-of-the-period-in-which-the-gross-investment-is-produced" .

hemo:7004692 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Unit cost of gross investment and outlay for a target from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000736 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With PriceCare, Wage, TimeShare, SchoolingEffect and SchoolingYears as in HE-IM-HPF-002 and the target in TargetInvest, the first formula fills UnitCostInvest and the second OutlayForTarget." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Unit cost of gross investment and outlay for a target from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-HPF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "unit-cost-of-gross-investment-and-outlay-for-a-target-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=EXP(-SchoolingEffect*SchoolingYears)*(PriceCare/(1-TimeShare))^(1-TimeShare)*(Wage/TimeShare)^TimeShare; =UnitCostInvest*TargetInvest" .

hemo:7004693 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Unit cost cell equals the article's outlay over gross investment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With PriceCare 40, Wage 10, TimeShare 0.5 and SchoolingYears 0, UnitCostInvest equals the article's 800 GBP over 20 units, 40 GBP. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the share terms left out, so that the formula returns 40 to the power 0.5 times 10 to the power 0.5, which is 20, or the two prices added, which gives 50." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Unit cost cell equals the article's outlay over gross investment" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HPF-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "unit-cost-cell-equals-the-article-s-outlay-over-gross-investment" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(UnitCostInvest-800/20)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004694 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Unit cost equals the price of care over its marginal product"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compares UnitCostInvest with PriceCare over MargProdCare from HE-IM-HPF-002 on the same inputs, Grossman's equation (2-10), in which the marginal cost of gross investment equals the price of an input over its marginal product. Expected result TRUE for any outlay, prices, time share and schooling. FALSE shows the sign of the schooling term reversed, which with SchoolingYears 1 gives 41.22 against 38.82, or the share terms left out, which gives 20 against 40." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Unit cost equals the price of care over its marginal product" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HPF-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "unit-cost-equals-the-price-of-care-over-its-marginal-product" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(UnitCostInvest-PriceCare/MargProdCare)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004695 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Outlay for 20 units falls to 776.36 GBP with one more year of schooling"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With TargetInvest 20, PriceCare 40, Wage 10, TimeShare 0.5, SchoolingEffect 0.03 and SchoolingYears 1, OutlayForTarget equals 800 GBP divided by exp(0.03), the article's 776.36 GBP. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the schooling factor multiplied in place of divided, which gives 824.36, or the linear reading 800 times 0.97, which gives 776.00." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Outlay for 20 units falls to 776.36 GBP with one more year of schooling" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HPF-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "outlay-for-20-units-falls-to-776-36-gbp-with-one-more-year-of-schooling" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(OutlayForTarget-800/EXP(0.03))<0.005" .

hemo:7004696 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Unit cost of 40 GBP in the health production example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a price of care of 40 GBP, a wage of 10 GBP, K of 0.5 and schooling at the reference level, the unit cost is 2 times the square root of 40 times 10, 40 GBP, so 20 units of gross investment cost 800 GBP, the article's C / I = 800 / 20 = 40." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Unit cost of 40 GBP in the health production example" ;
    hemo:9000014 "rho_H = 0.03; E = 0; P = 40; W = 10; K = 0.5; I = 20; pi = 40; C = 800" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HPF-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "unit-cost-of-40-gbp-in-the-health-production-example" .

hemo:7004697 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Outlay for 20 units of gross investment after one more year of schooling"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "One more year of schooling at rho_H of 0.03 divides the unit cost by exp(0.03), to 38.8178 GBP, so holding gross investment at 20 costs 776.36 GBP, the article's figure, about 24 GBP less." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Outlay for 20 units of gross investment after one more year of schooling" ;
    hemo:9000014 "rho_H = 0.03; E = 1; P = 40; W = 10; K = 0.5; I = 20; pi = 38.8178; C = 776.36" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HPF-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "outlay-for-20-units-of-gross-investment-after-one-more-year-of-schooling" .

hemo:7004698 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Outlay for 25 units of gross investment for the older person"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's older person needs 25 units of gross investment to hold the stock at 100 with depreciation of 0.25 (HE-FM-DFH-001), which at 40 GBP a unit costs 1,000 GBP." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Outlay for 25 units of gross investment for the older person" ;
    hemo:9000014 "rho_H = 0.03; E = 0; P = 40; W = 10; K = 0.5; I = 25; pi = 40; C = 1000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HPF-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "outlay-for-25-units-of-gross-investment-for-the-older-person" .

hemo:7004699 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Unit cost of gross investment when own time is a quarter of the cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the illustrative K of 0.25 of HE-EX-HPF-005 the unit cost is 49.6323 GBP, so 20 units cost 992.65 GBP and 800 GBP buys 800 / 49.6323 = 16.12 units, the gross investment of HE-EX-HPF-005 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Unit cost of gross investment when own time is a quarter of the cost" ;
    hemo:9000014 "rho_H = 0.03; E = 0; P = 40; W = 10; K = 0.25; I = 20; pi = 49.6323; C = 992.65" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HPF-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "unit-cost-of-gross-investment-when-own-time-is-a-quarter-of-the-cost" .

hemo:7004700 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Remaining claims probability below every specific attachment point compared"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The three levels carry all the probability of claims that can reach the attachment point. The remaining probability, 1 minus p_1 minus p_2 minus p_3, sits at claims at or below A and adds nothing to E_R or M2_R, although it still counts in the variance through the squared mean. In the article's example 0.990 of members claim less than USD 50,000, so the formula holds for attachment points of USD 50,000 or more; a lower attachment point needs the claims below USD 50,000 written out as further levels." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Remaining claims probability below every specific attachment point compared" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ATTP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "remaining-claims-probability-below-every-specific-attachment-point-compared" .

hemo:7004701 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Cap at or above the specific attachment point and full reimbursement entered as 1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "U is at least A, so U minus A is the layer limit of HE-FM-ASL-001, and a contract with no cap is entered with U at least C_3. The coinsurance rate c is the share paid inside the layer, as 45 CFR 153.20 defines the coinsurance rate and 45 CFR 153.230(c) applies it, and is entered at least 0 and at most 1." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cap at or above the specific attachment point and full reimbursement entered as 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ATTP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cap-at-or-above-the-specific-attachment-point-and-full-reimbursement-entered-as-1" .

hemo:7004702 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One covered person and one contract year on the specific contract's own basis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "C is one person's eligible claims for one contract year, counted on the contract's basis. The NAIC white paper notes that, unless a provision or endorsement extends cover, a stop-loss policy reimburses only claims incurred and paid in the same policy year, and that an insurer may set a higher laser specific attachment point for individuals with known high-cost conditions, so a lasered person is computed with that person's own A. Claims outside the contract year or the covered services are not in C." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One covered person and one contract year on the specific contract's own basis" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ATTP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-covered-person-and-one-contract-year-on-the-specific-contract-s-own-basis" .

hemo:7004703 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Specific stop-loss reimbursement mean and variance from a claims table in Excel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000737 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The three claim levels are in B2 to B4, named ClaimLevels, and their probabilities in C2 to C4, named ClaimProbs, with named cells AttachPoint, ClaimCap and CoinsRate. The first formula, entered in D2 and filled down to D4, gives each level's reimbursement in the range named LevelPayments. The next three formulas return the expected reimbursement in a cell named ExpReimb, the variance in VarReimb and the standard deviation in SDReimb. SUMPRODUCT needs no array entry, and further claim levels can be added as rows." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Specific stop-loss reimbursement mean and variance from a claims table in Excel" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ATTP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "specific-stop-loss-reimbursement-mean-and-variance-from-a-claims-table-in-excel" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CoinsRate*MAX(MIN(B2,ClaimCap)-AttachPoint,0); =SUMPRODUCT(ClaimProbs,LevelPayments); =SUMPRODUCT(ClaimProbs,LevelPayments^2)-ExpReimb^2; =SQRT(VarReimb)" .

hemo:7004704 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Specific reimbursement variance matches the central-moment route"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The value in VarReimb equals the probability-weighted squared deviation of each level's payment from ExpReimb plus the remaining probability, the members who claim nothing from the layer, times ExpReimb squared. The tolerance is relative to the variance. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the squared mean left unsubtracted, 236,250,000 against 235,440,000 at USD 50,000, or the squared deviations summed over the three claim levels only, 234,638,100 at USD 50,000, which leaves out the 99% of members who receive nothing." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Specific reimbursement variance matches the central-moment route" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ATTP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "specific-reimbursement-variance-matches-the-central-moment-route" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(VarReimb-(SUMPRODUCT(ClaimProbs,(LevelPayments-ExpReimb)^2)+(1-SUM(ClaimProbs))*ExpReimb^2))<1E-6*MAX(VarReimb,1)" .

hemo:7004705 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's USD 550 mean and USD 408,898 group spread at a USD 100,000 specific attachment point"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's three claim levels and probabilities, AttachPoint at 100000, ClaimCap at 500000 and CoinsRate at 1, ExpReimb rounds to 550 and SDReimb times the square root of 1,000 rounds to 408,898. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a rule that pays the whole claim once it passes the attachment point, which gives a mean of 950, or the retained part min(C, A) put in place of the excess, which gives 0.006 times 75,000 plus 0.003 times 100,000 plus 0.001 times 100,000, a mean of 850." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's USD 550 mean and USD 408,898 group spread at a USD 100,000 specific attachment point" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ATTP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-usd-550-mean-and-usd-408-898-group-spread-at-a-usd-100-000-specific-attachment-point" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ROUND(ExpReimb,0)=550,ROUND(SDReimb*SQRT(1000),0)=408898)" .

hemo:7004706 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Specific attachment point of USD 50,000 in the article's illustrative claims distribution"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative distribution has annual claims of USD 75,000, USD 150,000 and USD 500,000 with probabilities 0.006, 0.003 and 0.001, full reimbursement and no cap (U entered as USD 500,000). At an attachment point of USD 50,000 the expected reimbursement is USD 900 per member and the variance 235,440,000 dollars squared, as in the article, with a standard deviation of about USD 15,344 per member. For 1,000 independent members HE-FM-ASL-003 gives a standard deviation of total reimbursement of about USD 485,222, the article's figure." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Specific attachment point of USD 50,000 in the article's illustrative claims distribution" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 1; p_1 = 0.006; C_1 = 75000; U = 500000; A = 50000; p_2 = 0.003; C_2 = 150000; p_3 = 0.001; C_3 = 500000; E_R = 900; M2_R = 236250000; V_R = 235440000; SD_R = 15344.05" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ATTP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "specific-attachment-point-of-usd-50-000-in-the-article-s-illustrative-claims-distribution" .

hemo:7004707 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Specific attachment point of USD 100,000 in the article's illustrative claims distribution"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At USD 100,000 the USD 75,000 level no longer reaches the layer. The expected reimbursement is USD 550 per member and the standard deviation about USD 12,930, which for 1,000 independent members gives about USD 408,898, the article's figure." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Specific attachment point of USD 100,000 in the article's illustrative claims distribution" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 1; p_1 = 0.006; C_1 = 75000; U = 500000; A = 100000; p_2 = 0.003; C_2 = 150000; p_3 = 0.001; C_3 = 500000; E_R = 550; M2_R = 167500000; V_R = 167197500; SD_R = 12930.49" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ATTP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "specific-attachment-point-of-usd-100-000-in-the-article-s-illustrative-claims-distribution" .

hemo:7004708 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Specific attachment point of USD 200,000 in the article's illustrative claims distribution"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At USD 200,000 only the USD 500,000 level reaches the layer. The expected reimbursement is USD 300 per member and the standard deviation about USD 9,482, about USD 299,850 for 1,000 members. The mean is a third of its value at USD 50,000, while the standard deviation is still about 62% of it, the slower fall the article describes." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Specific attachment point of USD 200,000 in the article's illustrative claims distribution" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 1; p_1 = 0.006; C_1 = 75000; U = 500000; A = 200000; p_2 = 0.003; C_2 = 150000; p_3 = 0.001; C_3 = 500000; E_R = 300; M2_R = 90000000; V_R = 89910000; SD_R = 9482.09" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ATTP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "specific-attachment-point-of-usd-200-000-in-the-article-s-illustrative-claims-distribution" .

hemo:7004709 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Layer between USD 50,000 and USD 200,000 given up by raising the specific attachment point"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The layer the employer takes back by raising the attachment point is the same formula with A at USD 50,000 and the cap U at USD 200,000. Its expected reimbursement is USD 600 per member, the difference of USD 900 and USD 300, and its standard deviation about USD 7,476, about USD 236,411 for 1,000 members, the article's figure. The ratio of standard deviation to mean for the group is about 0.39 against about 1.00 for the layer above USD 200,000." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Layer between USD 50,000 and USD 200,000 given up by raising the specific attachment point" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 1; p_1 = 0.006; C_1 = 75000; U = 200000; A = 50000; p_2 = 0.003; C_2 = 150000; p_3 = 0.001; C_3 = 500000; E_R = 600; M2_R = 56250000; V_R = 55890000; SD_R = 7475.96" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ATTP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "layer-between-usd-50-000-and-usd-200-000-given-up-by-raising-the-specific-attachment-point" .

hemo:7004710 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Coinsurance and cap of the 2015 ACA reinsurance parameters on the illustrative claims distribution"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The 2015 benefit year parameters of the ACA transitional reinsurance programme after HHS lowered the attachment point, USD 45,000 with a USD 250,000 cap and a 50% coinsurance rate as set, are applied here to the article's illustrative claims distribution for illustration only. Because USD 45,000 is below the USD 50,000 that HE-AS-ATTP-001 requires, the example also takes the 0.990 of members who claim less than USD 50,000 to claim no more than USD 45,000; any of them with claims above USD 45,000 would add to the figures. Each level pays half of its claims between USD 45,000 and USD 250,000, so the expected reimbursement is USD 350 per member and the standard deviation about USD 4,472." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Coinsurance and cap of the 2015 ACA reinsurance parameters on the illustrative claims distribution" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 0.5; p_1 = 0.006; C_1 = 75000; U = 250000; A = 45000; p_2 = 0.003; C_2 = 150000; p_3 = 0.001; C_3 = 500000; E_R = 350; M2_R = 20125000; V_R = 20002500; SD_R = 4472.42" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ATTP-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "coinsurance-and-cap-of-the-2015-aca-reinsurance-parameters-on-the-illustrative-claims-distribution" .

hemo:7004711 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Specific attachment point at the highest claim level as a limiting case"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the attachment point at USD 500,000, the highest claim level, no claim exceeds it, so the expected reimbursement, the second moment and the variance are all zero. This limiting case checks the implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Specific attachment point at the highest claim level as a limiting case" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 1; p_1 = 0.006; C_1 = 75000; U = 500000; A = 500000; p_2 = 0.003; C_2 = 150000; p_3 = 0.001; C_3 = 500000; E_R = 0; M2_R = 0; V_R = 0; SD_R = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ATTP-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "specific-attachment-point-at-the-highest-claim-level-as-a-limiting-case" .

hemo:7004712 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Uniform claims inflation with the specific attachment point fixed in money"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every claim level rises by the same factor 1 plus g, the simple case that Lee 1988 and Miccolis 1977 analyse, while the attachment point keeps its money value, as a contract or regulatory amount does until it is changed. A contract with an index clause that raises the attachment point with claims is a different case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Uniform claims inflation with the specific attachment point fixed in money" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ATTP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "uniform-claims-inflation-with-the-specific-attachment-point-fixed-in-money" .

hemo:7004713 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Claims below the three levels stay below the specific attachment point after inflation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The remaining probability sits at claims that stay at or below A after inflation. In the article's example claims below USD 50,000 stay below USD 55,000 after 10% inflation and the USD 75,000 level becomes USD 82,500, so at an attachment point of USD 100,000 only the two upper levels pay; at USD 50,000 the formula would need the claims below USD 50,000 written out." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Claims below the three levels stay below the specific attachment point after inflation" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ATTP-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "claims-below-the-three-levels-stay-below-the-specific-attachment-point-after-inflation" .

hemo:7004714 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Uncapped layer, positive inflation and a layer that pays before inflation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The layer has no cap, g is above zero and E_0 is above zero, so both ratios are defined. With a cap, part of the inflated claims falls above it; for an illustrative layer between USD 100,000 and USD 150,000 on the article's distribution, 10% inflation leaves the expected reimbursement unchanged at USD 200 per member (derived), because both upper claim levels already exhaust the layer." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Uncapped layer, positive inflation and a layer that pays before inflation" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ATTP-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "uncapped-layer-positive-inflation-and-a-layer-that-pays-before-inflation" .

hemo:7004715 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected excess layer reimbursement before and after uniform claims inflation in Excel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000738 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With ClaimLevels and ClaimProbs as in HE-IM-ATTP-001, the attachment point in AttachPoint and the inflation rate in a cell named ClaimsInflation, the four formulas return the expected reimbursement before inflation in a cell named ExpReimbBase, after inflation in ExpReimbInflated, the proportional rise in LayerRise and its ratio to the inflation rate in InflationMultiple. The comparisons inside SUMPRODUCT return 1 or 0 and keep only the levels above the attachment point." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected excess layer reimbursement before and after uniform claims inflation in Excel" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ATTP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-excess-layer-reimbursement-before-and-after-uniform-claims-inflation-in-excel" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(ClaimProbs,(ClaimLevels-AttachPoint)*(ClaimLevels>AttachPoint)); =SUMPRODUCT(ClaimProbs,((1+ClaimsInflation)*ClaimLevels-AttachPoint)*((1+ClaimsInflation)*ClaimLevels>AttachPoint)); =ExpReimbInflated/ExpReimbBase-1; =LayerRise/ClaimsInflation" .

hemo:7004716 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Inflated excess layer reimbursement matches the deflated attachment point route"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The value in ExpReimbInflated equals 1 plus the inflation rate times the expected excess of the uninflated claims over the attachment point divided by 1 plus the inflation rate, the identity derived from equation 17 of Miccolis 1977. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the attachment point inflated together with the claims, or the inflation rate applied to the uninflated excess, both of which give 605 against 645 at USD 100,000." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Inflated excess layer reimbursement matches the deflated attachment point route" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ATTP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "inflated-excess-layer-reimbursement-matches-the-deflated-attachment-point-route" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ExpReimbInflated-(1+ClaimsInflation)*SUMPRODUCT(ClaimProbs,(ClaimLevels-AttachPoint/(1+ClaimsInflation))*(ClaimLevels>AttachPoint/(1+ClaimsInflation))))<1E-6" .

hemo:7004717 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's 17.3% rise in expected reimbursement at a USD 100,000 specific attachment point"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's claims distribution, AttachPoint at 100000 and ClaimsInflation at 0.1, LayerRise rounds to 0.173 and is at least the inflation rate. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the attachment point raised with the claims, a rise of 0.100, or the rise divided by the inflated value, about 0.147." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's 17.3% rise in expected reimbursement at a USD 100,000 specific attachment point" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ATTP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-17-3-rise-in-expected-reimbursement-at-a-usd-100-000-specific-attachment-point" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ROUND(LayerRise,3)=0.173,LayerRise>=ClaimsInflation)" .

hemo:7004718 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Ten per cent claims inflation at a USD 100,000 specific attachment point"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's step 4. With every claim level 10% higher the USD 150,000 level becomes USD 165,000 and the USD 500,000 level USD 550,000, so the expected reimbursement rises from USD 550 to USD 645 per member, about 17.3%, about 1.73 times the rate of claims inflation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ten per cent claims inflation at a USD 100,000 specific attachment point" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_1 = 0.006; C_1 = 75000; A = 100000; p_2 = 0.003; C_2 = 150000; p_3 = 0.001; C_3 = 500000; g = 0.1; E_0 = 550; E_g = 645; rise_E = 0.1727; mult_E = 1.7273" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ATTP-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ten-per-cent-claims-inflation-at-a-usd-100-000-specific-attachment-point" .

hemo:7004719 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Ten per cent claims inflation at a USD 200,000 specific attachment point"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At USD 200,000 only the top level pays, and 10% inflation raises its excess from USD 300,000 to USD 350,000, so the expected reimbursement rises from USD 300 to USD 350 per member, about 16.7%. This figure is computed here from the article's distribution and is not in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ten per cent claims inflation at a USD 200,000 specific attachment point" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_1 = 0.006; C_1 = 75000; A = 200000; p_2 = 0.003; C_2 = 150000; p_3 = 0.001; C_3 = 500000; g = 0.1; E_0 = 300; E_g = 350; rise_E = 0.1667; mult_E = 1.6667" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ATTP-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ten-per-cent-claims-inflation-at-a-usd-200-000-specific-attachment-point" .

hemo:7004720 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Claims inflation with a zero attachment point as a limiting case"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With an illustrative distribution in which every member claims USD 10,000, USD 20,000 or USD 50,000 with probabilities 0.5, 0.3 and 0.2 and the attachment point set to zero, the layer is the whole claim and grows at exactly the inflation rate, so the ratio is 1. This limiting case checks the implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Claims inflation with a zero attachment point as a limiting case" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_1 = 0.5; C_1 = 10000; A = 0; p_2 = 0.3; C_2 = 20000; p_3 = 0.2; C_3 = 50000; g = 0.1; E_0 = 21000; E_g = 23100; rise_E = 0.1; mult_E = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ATTP-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "claims-inflation-with-a-zero-attachment-point-as-a-limiting-case" .

hemo:7004721 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant hazards within each response group for the bias factor"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each death rate is events over person-time and estimates a constant hazard, as in HE-FM-HR-005. With hazards that change over follow-up the extended Cox model is needed, and the closed form of F describes only the crude rate ratio." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant hazards within each response group for the bias factor" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-LMA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-hazards-within-each-response-group-for-the-bias-factor" .

hemo:7004722 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "No deaths in the pre-response person-time of responders"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "A responder cannot die before responding, so every death before a response counts in d_N and d_R holds only deaths after the response. This is what makes the event counts equal in the naive and time-split ratios." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No deaths in the pre-response person-time of responders" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-LMA-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-deaths-in-the-pre-response-person-time-of-responders" .

hemo:7004723 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Response time known for every responder in the person-time split"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Splitting person-time needs each responder's response time. Where response is assessed only at set visits, such as the article's six-month assessment, the assessment time is used, and I is the person-time responders spent before it." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Response time known for every responder in the person-time split" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-LMA-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "response-time-known-for-every-responder-in-the-person-time-split" .

hemo:7004724 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Naive and time-split responder rate ratios and the bias factor from named person-time"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000739 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the counts and person-time in cells named DeathsResp, PersonTimeResp, ImmortalTime, DeathsNonResp and PersonTimeNonResp, the three formulas return RR_naive in a cell named RateRatioNaive, RR_split in RateRatioSplit and F in BiasFactorRR." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Naive and time-split responder rate ratios and the bias factor from named person-time" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-LMA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "naive-and-time-split-responder-rate-ratios-and-the-bias-factor-from-named-person-time" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(DeathsResp/(PersonTimeResp+ImmortalTime))/(DeathsNonResp/PersonTimeNonResp); =(DeathsResp/PersonTimeResp)/(DeathsNonResp/(PersonTimeNonResp+ImmortalTime)); =RateRatioNaive/RateRatioSplit" .

hemo:7004725 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Rate-ratio bias factor cell matches its person-time closed form"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "BiasFactorRR is compared with PT_R PT_N divided by (PT_R + I)(PT_N + I), computed from person-time alone without the death counts. Expected result TRUE. A BiasFactorRR cell written as RateRatioSplit / RateRatioNaive returns 1.5124 for the article-based inputs and FALSE, and halving the cell also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Rate-ratio bias factor cell matches its person-time closed form" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-LMA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "rate-ratio-bias-factor-cell-matches-its-person-time-closed-form" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(BiasFactorRR-PersonTimeResp*PersonTimeNonResp/((PersonTimeResp+ImmortalTime)*(PersonTimeNonResp+ImmortalTime)))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004726 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Time-split rate ratio equals the naive ratio corrected by the closed-form factor"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "RateRatioSplit is compared with RateRatioNaive divided by the closed form of F. Expected result TRUE. A split that removes the immortal time from responders without adding it to the side without a response returns 0.4577 against 0.5349 for the article-based inputs and FALSE, and doubling RateRatioSplit also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Time-split rate ratio equals the naive ratio corrected by the closed-form factor" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-LMA-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "time-split-rate-ratio-equals-the-naive-ratio-corrected-by-the-closed-form-factor" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(RateRatioSplit-RateRatioNaive*(PersonTimeResp+ImmortalTime)*(PersonTimeNonResp+ImmortalTime)/(PersonTimeResp*PersonTimeNonResp))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004727 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Rate-ratio bias factor within its derived bounds and equal to 1 without immortal time"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a = I / PT_R and b = I / PT_N, BiasFactorRR is at most 1 / (1 + a + b) and at least 1 minus a minus b (bounds derived here and checked over a grid of person-time in Python), and both bounds equal 1 when ImmortalTime is zero. Expected result TRUE. For the article-based inputs the bounds are 0.6836 and 0.5372, so the inverted factor of 1.5124, a doubled cell of 1.3224 and a halved cell of 0.3306 all give FALSE, as does any value other than 1 when ImmortalTime is zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Rate-ratio bias factor within its derived bounds and equal to 1 without immortal time" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-LMA-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "rate-ratio-bias-factor-within-its-derived-bounds-and-equal-to-1-without-immortal-time" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(BiasFactorRR<=1/(1+ImmortalTime/PersonTimeResp+ImmortalTime/PersonTimeNonResp)+1E-12,BiasFactorRR>=1-ImmortalTime/PersonTimeResp-ImmortalTime/PersonTimeNonResp-1E-12)" .

hemo:7004728 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's cohort with illustrative timing of deaths and responses"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Using the article's counts with timing assumptions added here for illustration (response at the six-month assessment, early deaths at three months on average and later deaths one year after the landmark on average), responders contribute 35 person-years before response and 119 after it, with 21 deaths, and patients without a response contribute 207.5 person-years with 80 deaths. The naive rate ratio is 0.354 and the time-split ratio 0.535, so F is 0.661 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's cohort with illustrative timing of deaths and responses" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d_R = 21; PT_R = 119; I = 35; d_N = 80; PT_N = 207.5; RR_naive = 0.3537; RR_split = 0.5349; F = 0.6612" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-LMA-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-cohort-with-illustrative-timing-of-deaths-and-responses" .

hemo:7004729 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No pre-response person-time leaves no bias in the responder rate ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the same deaths and person-time but no immortal time, as when status is known at entry, both ratios are 0.4577 and F is 1, a limiting case that checks an implementation (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No pre-response person-time leaves no bias in the responder rate ratio" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d_R = 21; PT_R = 119; I = 0; d_N = 80; PT_N = 207.5; RR_naive = 0.4577; RR_split = 0.4577; F = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-LMA-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-pre-response-person-time-leaves-no-bias-in-the-responder-rate-ratio" .

hemo:7004730 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Rate-ratio bias factor with 50 person-years of immortal time"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With illustrative inputs of 20 deaths in 200 person-years after response, 50 person-years before it, and 60 deaths in 300 person-years among patients who never responded, the naive ratio is 0.4 and the time-split ratio 0.5833, so F is 0.6857 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Rate-ratio bias factor with 50 person-years of immortal time" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d_R = 20; PT_R = 200; I = 50; d_N = 60; PT_N = 300; RR_naive = 0.4; RR_split = 0.5833; F = 0.6857" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-LMA-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "rate-ratio-bias-factor-with-50-person-years-of-immortal-time" .

hemo:7004731 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Normal outcomes with a common standard deviation behind the Hedges' correction"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Hedges derived the bias of d and its correction for outcomes that are normally distributed in each group with the same standard deviation in both, the model of his Theorem 1, under which d scaled by the square root of n_1 n_2 over (n_1 + n_2) follows a noncentral t distribution. Outside that model the factor still shrinks d towards zero, but the result is not shown to be unbiased." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Normal outcomes with a common standard deviation behind the Hedges' correction" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HEDG-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "normal-outcomes-with-a-common-standard-deviation-behind-the-hedges-correction" .

hemo:7004732 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Degrees of freedom of the standard deviation actually used in the Hedges' correction"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Hedges sets m to the comparator group size less 1 when d is standardised by the control group standard deviation, as in Glass's estimator, and to the total sample size less 2 when the pooled standard deviation is used. This formula takes the pooled standard deviation of HE-FM-CD-001, so m is n_1 plus n_2 less 2." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Degrees of freedom of the standard deviation actually used in the Hedges' correction" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HEDG-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "degrees-of-freedom-of-the-standard-deviation-actually-used-in-the-hedges-correction" .

hemo:7004733 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Algebraic approximation in place of the exact gamma-function factor for Hedges' g"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The algebraic factor is slightly larger than the exact one. Hedges reports a maximum error of 0.007 at m equal to 2, an error within 0.00033 for m of 10 or more and an error no larger than 0.000015 for m above 50. The exact factor, which the site calculator cannot evaluate, is computed in HE-CF-HEDG-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Algebraic approximation in place of the exact gamma-function factor for Hedges' g" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HEDG-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "algebraic-approximation-in-place-of-the-exact-gamma-function-factor-for-hedges-g" .

hemo:7004734 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Hedges' g in three cells from Cohen's d and the two group sizes"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000740 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With Cohen's d in the cell named CohensD (the output cell of HE-IM-CD-001) and the group sizes in cells named SizeIntervention and SizeComparator, the three formulas return the degrees of freedom in a cell named DegFreedom, the correction factor in CorrectionJ and Hedges' g in HedgesG." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Hedges' g in three cells from Cohen's d and the two group sizes" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-HEDG-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "hedges-g-in-three-cells-from-cohen-s-d-and-the-two-group-sizes" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SizeIntervention+SizeComparator-2; =1-3/(4*DegFreedom-1); =CorrectionJ*CohensD" .

hemo:7004735 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "HedgesG cell within Hedges' stated error of the exact gamma-function correction"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For 10 or more degrees of freedom, the model's HedgesG cell is compared with CohensD multiplied by the exact factor, computed in the test with GAMMALN from the group sizes, with a tolerance of 0.0004 times the size of CohensD, since Hedges bounds the error of the approximation at 0.00033 in that range. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the total sample size used in place of the degrees of freedom, which for the article's trial gives a factor of about 0.9655 and a g of about 0.8078 against the exact 0.8048, or the correction left out." ;
    hemo:9000009 "HedgesG cell within Hedges' stated error of the exact gamma-function correction" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HEDG-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "hedgesg-cell-within-hedges-stated-error-of-the-exact-gamma-function-correction" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(HedgesG-CohensD*EXP(GAMMALN((SizeIntervention+SizeComparator-2)/2)-GAMMALN((SizeIntervention+SizeComparator-3)/2))/SQRT((SizeIntervention+SizeComparator-2)/2))<=0.0004*ABS(CohensD)" .

hemo:7004736 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article Hedges' g of 0.805 reproduced in the HedgesG cell"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Enter the article's trial, CohensD 0.8366 with SizeIntervention 10 and SizeComparator 12. The model's HedgesG cell then rounds to the article's 0.805. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the correction applied twice, which gives about 0.774, the total sample size used in place of the degrees of freedom, about 0.808, or the correction left out, about 0.837." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article Hedges' g of 0.805 reproduced in the HedgesG cell" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HEDG-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-hedges-g-of-0-805-reproduced-in-the-hedgesg-cell" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND(HedgesG,3)=0.805" .

hemo:7004737 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Correction factor cell equal to the total-sample form of Hedges' J"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The model's CorrectionJ cell equals 1 minus 3 over (4N minus 9), computed in the test from the two group sizes, the form in the Review Manager algorithms. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows DegFreedom set to the total sample size, which for the article's trial gives about 0.965517 against 0.962025, or to the total less 1, about 0.963855." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Correction factor cell equal to the total-sample form of Hedges' J" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HEDG-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "correction-factor-cell-equal-to-the-total-sample-form-of-hedges-j" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(CorrectionJ-(1-3/(4*(SizeIntervention+SizeComparator)-9)))<1E-12" .

hemo:7004738 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article rehabilitation trial with 10 and 12 patients corrected to Hedges' g"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative trial from the article, with means of 62 and 54, standard deviations of 9 and 10 and 10 and 12 patients, which give a d of about 0.8366 by HE-FM-CD-001. With 20 degrees of freedom J is 1 minus 3 over 79, about 0.962025, and g is about 0.8048, shown as 0.962 and 0.805 in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article rehabilitation trial with 10 and 12 patients corrected to Hedges' g" ;
    hemo:9000014 "n_1 = 10; n_2 = 12; d = 0.8366; m = 20; J = 0.962025; g = 0.8048" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HEDG-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-rehabilitation-trial-with-10-and-12-patients-corrected-to-hedges-g" .

hemo:7004739 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Fifty patients per arm shrink the Hedges' correction below 1%"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Computed here for illustration with the same d of 0.8366 and 50 patients in each arm. J is about 0.992327, the article's 0.992, and g is about 0.8302, a reduction of under 1%." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fifty patients per arm shrink the Hedges' correction below 1%" ;
    hemo:9000014 "n_1 = 50; n_2 = 50; d = 0.8366; m = 98; J = 0.992327; g = 0.8302" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HEDG-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fifty-patients-per-arm-shrink-the-hedges-correction-below-1" .

hemo:7004740 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No difference in means gives a Hedges' g of zero"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a d of 0 and 30 patients per arm, J is about 0.987013 and g is 0, a limiting case. The correction rescales d and never changes its sign." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No difference in means gives a Hedges' g of zero" ;
    hemo:9000014 "n_1 = 30; n_2 = 30; d = 0; m = 58; J = 0.987013; g = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HEDG-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-difference-in-means-gives-a-hedges-g-of-zero" .

hemo:7004741 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Large-sample approximation to the variance of Hedges' g"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The expression is a large-sample approximation with the observed d standing in for the unknown population effect, which the What Works Clearinghouse handbook notes makes every such formula an approximation. Hedges' own normal approximation uses delta squared over 2m in the second term, and his Theorem 2 gives the exact variance of the corrected estimate. For the article's trial, with delta set to 0.8048, the exact variance is about 0.2067 against 0.1844 here and 0.1995 for Hedges' approximation (computed here and confirmed by simulation), so in very small trials this standard error is somewhat too small." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Large-sample approximation to the variance of Hedges' g" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HEDG-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "large-sample-approximation-to-the-variance-of-hedges-g" .

hemo:7004742 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Independent groups with a pooled standard deviation for the variance of Hedges' g"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The two groups are independent and d uses the pooled standard deviation, as in HE-FM-CD-001. Crossover trials, change scores and cluster-randomised trials need other variance formulas, and the What Works Clearinghouse handbook gives separate formulas for cluster-level assignment." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Independent groups with a pooled standard deviation for the variance of Hedges' g" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HEDG-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "independent-groups-with-a-pooled-standard-deviation-for-the-variance-of-hedges-g" .

hemo:7004743 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Variance and standard error of Hedges' g in three cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000741 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the named cells of HE-IM-HEDG-001, the first formula returns the variance of d in a cell named VarianceD, the second the variance of g in VarianceG and the third the standard error in StdErrG." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Variance and standard error of Hedges' g in three cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-HEDG-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "variance-and-standard-error-of-hedges-g-in-three-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(SizeIntervention+SizeComparator)/(SizeIntervention*SizeComparator)+CohensD^2/(2*(SizeIntervention+SizeComparator)); =CorrectionJ^2*VarianceD; =SQRT(VarianceG)" .

hemo:7004744 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Standard error of Hedges' g at zero effect equal to J times the group-size term"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For this check, set CohensD to 0; the group sizes can be anything. The model's StdErrG cell must then equal CorrectionJ times the square root of 1/SizeIntervention plus 1/SizeComparator, a different route to the same limiting value. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows J left unsquared in VarianceG, which for 30 patients per arm gives about 0.2565 against 0.2548, or the variance entered in StdErrG." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Standard error of Hedges' g at zero effect equal to J times the group-size term" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HEDG-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "standard-error-of-hedges-g-at-zero-effect-equal-to-j-times-the-group-size-term" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(StdErrG-CorrectionJ*SQRT(1/SizeIntervention+1/SizeComparator))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004745 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article variance of 0.184 and standard error of 0.429 for Hedges' g reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Enter the article's trial, CohensD 0.8366 with SizeIntervention 10 and SizeComparator 12. VarianceG then rounds to 0.184 and StdErrG to 0.429. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows J left out, which gives a standard error of about 0.446, or twice the degrees of freedom in the second denominator in place of twice the total sample size, about 0.431." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article variance of 0.184 and standard error of 0.429 for Hedges' g reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HEDG-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-variance-of-0-184-and-standard-error-of-0-429-for-hedges-g-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ROUND(VarianceG,3)=0.184,ROUND(StdErrG,3)=0.429)" .

hemo:7004746 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article rehabilitation trial variance and standard error of Hedges' g"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "From the article's trial, V_d is about 0.1992, V_g about 0.1844 and SE_g about 0.4294, shown as 0.199, 0.184 and 0.429 in the article. HE-FM-AN-001 with z of 1.96 then gives an interval of about minus 0.037 to 1.646 around g of 0.8048, the article's minus 0.04 to 1.65, which includes no effect." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article rehabilitation trial variance and standard error of Hedges' g" ;
    hemo:9000014 "n_1 = 10; n_2 = 12; d = 0.8366; J = 0.962025; V_d = 0.1992; V_g = 0.1844; SE_g = 0.4294" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HEDG-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-rehabilitation-trial-variance-and-standard-error-of-hedges-g" .

hemo:7004747 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Fifty patients per arm narrow the standard error of Hedges' g"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Computed here for illustration with the same d and 50 patients in each arm, V_d is about 0.0435, V_g about 0.0428 and SE_g about 0.2070, under half the standard error of the 22-patient trial." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fifty patients per arm narrow the standard error of Hedges' g" ;
    hemo:9000014 "n_1 = 50; n_2 = 50; d = 0.8366; J = 0.992327; V_d = 0.0435; V_g = 0.0428; SE_g = 0.2070" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HEDG-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fifty-patients-per-arm-narrow-the-standard-error-of-hedges-g" .

hemo:7004748 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Zero effect leaves only the group-size term in the variance of Hedges' g"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With d equal to 0 and 30 patients per arm, V_d is 60 over 900, about 0.0667, V_g about 0.0649 and SE_g about 0.2548, a limiting case in which the effect term vanishes." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Zero effect leaves only the group-size term in the variance of Hedges' g" ;
    hemo:9000014 "n_1 = 30; n_2 = 30; d = 0; J = 0.987013; V_d = 0.0667; V_g = 0.0649; SE_g = 0.2548" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HEDG-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "zero-effect-leaves-only-the-group-size-term-in-the-variance-of-hedges-g" .

hemo:7004749 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Hedges' adjusted g as the standardised mean difference of Cochrane reviews"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The Cochrane Handbook states that the standardised mean difference used in Cochrane reviews is Hedges' adjusted g, with a pooled standard deviation in the denominator that assumes similar standard deviations in the two groups. Review Manager computes g with the factor 1 minus 3 over (4N minus 9), the same factor as HE-FM-HEDG-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Hedges' adjusted g as the standardised mean difference of Cochrane reviews" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HEDG-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "hedges-adjusted-g-as-the-standardised-mean-difference-of-cochrane-reviews" .

hemo:7004750 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Inverse-variance weights from the Review Manager standard error"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Review Manager weights each study by the reciprocal of the square of this standard error in its inverse-variance methods. Mixing it with SE_g of HE-FM-HEDG-002 in one analysis gives studies of the same size slightly different weights." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Inverse-variance weights from the Review Manager standard error" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HEDG-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "inverse-variance-weights-from-the-review-manager-standard-error" .

hemo:7004751 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Review Manager standard error of Hedges' g in two cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000742 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the named cells of HE-IM-HEDG-001, the first formula returns the total sample size in a cell named TotalSize and the second the Review Manager standard error in StdErrRevMan." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Review Manager standard error of Hedges' g in two cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-HEDG-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "review-manager-standard-error-of-hedges-g-in-two-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SizeIntervention+SizeComparator; =SQRT(TotalSize/(SizeIntervention*SizeComparator)+HedgesG^2/(2*(TotalSize-3.94)))" .

hemo:7004752 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article trial standard error of 0.449 under Review Manager"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Enter the article's trial, CohensD 0.8366 with SizeIntervention 10 and SizeComparator 12, so that HedgesG is about 0.8048. The model's StdErrRevMan cell then rounds to 0.449. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows 3.94 left out of the denominator, which gives about 0.445, or the result multiplied by CorrectionJ, about 0.432." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article trial standard error of 0.449 under Review Manager" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HEDG-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-trial-standard-error-of-0-449-under-review-manager" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND(StdErrRevMan,3)=0.449" .

hemo:7004753 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Review Manager standard error at zero effect equal to the group-size term"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For this check, set CohensD to 0 so that HedgesG is 0. The model's StdErrRevMan cell must then equal the square root of 1/SizeIntervention plus 1/SizeComparator. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows TotalSize used alone in the first term, which for 30 patients per arm gives about 7.75 against 0.2582, or the cell left as a variance, about 0.0667." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Review Manager standard error at zero effect equal to the group-size term" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HEDG-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "review-manager-standard-error-at-zero-effect-equal-to-the-group-size-term" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(StdErrRevMan-SQRT(1/SizeIntervention+1/SizeComparator))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004754 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article rehabilitation trial under the Review Manager standard error"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Computed here for illustration with the article's g of 0.8048, 10 and 12 patients. SE_RM is about 0.4486, about 4.5% above the 0.4294 of HE-FM-HEDG-002, the small-trial difference the article mentions." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article rehabilitation trial under the Review Manager standard error" ;
    hemo:9000014 "n_1 = 10; n_2 = 12; g = 0.8048; N = 22; SE_RM = 0.4486" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HEDG-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-rehabilitation-trial-under-the-review-manager-standard-error" .

hemo:7004755 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Fifty patients per arm bring the two standard errors of Hedges' g together"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Computed here for illustration with g of 0.8302 and 50 patients per arm. SE_RM is about 0.2088 against 0.2070 from HE-FM-HEDG-002, a gap under 1%." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fifty patients per arm bring the two standard errors of Hedges' g together" ;
    hemo:9000014 "n_1 = 50; n_2 = 50; g = 0.8302; N = 100; SE_RM = 0.2088" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HEDG-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fifty-patients-per-arm-bring-the-two-standard-errors-of-hedges-g-together" .

hemo:7004756 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Zero Hedges' g reduces the Review Manager standard error to the group-size term"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With g equal to 0 and 30 patients per arm, SE_RM is the square root of 60 over 900, about 0.2582, a limiting case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Zero Hedges' g reduces the Review Manager standard error to the group-size term" ;
    hemo:9000014 "n_1 = 30; n_2 = 30; g = 0; N = 60; SE_RM = 0.2582" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HEDG-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "zero-hedges-g-reduces-the-review-manager-standard-error-to-the-group-size-term" .

hemo:7004757 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Normal outcomes and a known population effect for the expected bias of d"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The result holds under Hedges' Theorem 1, normal outcomes with a common variance in both groups. delta is a population value, so the formula shows how far d overstates a given effect on average; applied to an observed d it would not give g, which is d times J (HE-FM-HEDG-001)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Normal outcomes and a known population effect for the expected bias of d" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HEDG-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "normal-outcomes-and-a-known-population-effect-for-the-expected-bias-of-d" .

hemo:7004758 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Degrees of freedom set by the standard deviation in the expected bias of d"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "m is the number of degrees of freedom of the standard deviation used, as in HE-AS-HEDG-002. Hedges' own example of five subjects per group with 4 degrees of freedom corresponds to the control group standard deviation; with the pooled standard deviation the same trial has 8 degrees of freedom." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Degrees of freedom set by the standard deviation in the expected bias of d" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HEDG-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "degrees-of-freedom-set-by-the-standard-deviation-in-the-expected-bias-of-d" .

hemo:7004759 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected value and bias of Cohen's d in three cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000743 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the degrees of freedom in a cell named DegFreedomSD and the population standardised difference in TrueEffect, the formulas return the factor in CorrectionJBias, the expected value of d in ExpectedD and its bias in BiasD." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected value and bias of Cohen's d in three cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-HEDG-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-value-and-bias-of-cohen-s-d-in-three-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1-3/(4*DegFreedomSD-1); =TrueEffect/CorrectionJBias; =ExpectedD-TrueEffect" .

hemo:7004760 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected Cohen's d within Hedges' stated error of the exact mean"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For 10 or more degrees of freedom the model's ExpectedD cell is compared with TrueEffect divided by the exact factor, computed in the test with GAMMALN, with a tolerance of 0.0004 times the size of TrueEffect. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows J used as a multiplier instead of a divisor, which for 20 degrees of freedom and a TrueEffect of 0.8 gives about 0.7696 against 0.8316." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected Cohen's d within Hedges' stated error of the exact mean" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HEDG-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-cohen-s-d-within-hedges-stated-error-of-the-exact-mean" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ExpectedD-TrueEffect/(EXP(GAMMALN(DegFreedomSD/2)-GAMMALN((DegFreedomSD-1)/2))/SQRT(DegFreedomSD/2)))<=0.0004*ABS(TrueEffect)" .

hemo:7004761 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Hedges' overstatement of 25 percent at four degrees of freedom"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Set DegFreedomSD to 4 and TrueEffect to 0.5. The model's BiasD cell divided by TrueEffect then rounds to 0.25, Hedges' approximately 25 percent. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the bias taken as delta times one minus J, which gives 0.2, or J used as a multiplier, which gives minus 0.2." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Hedges' overstatement of 25 percent at four degrees of freedom" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HEDG-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "hedges-overstatement-of-25-percent-at-four-degrees-of-freedom" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND(BiasD/TrueEffect,2)=0.25" .

hemo:7004762 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Hedges' example of five subjects per group with four degrees of freedom"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Hedges states that with five subjects per group and 4 degrees of freedom d overestimates delta by approximately 25 percent. With an illustrative delta of 0.5 the approximate J of 0.8 gives a mean d of 0.625, an overstatement of 0.125; the exact factor 0.79788 of Hedges' Table I gives about 0.6267, about 25.3% (computed here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Hedges' example of five subjects per group with four degrees of freedom" ;
    hemo:9000014 "m = 4; delta = 0.5; J = 0.8; E_d = 0.625; Bias_d = 0.125" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HEDG-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "hedges-example-of-five-subjects-per-group-with-four-degrees-of-freedom" .

hemo:7004763 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Twenty degrees of freedom as in the article's trial"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Computed here for illustration with 20 degrees of freedom, the article's trial, and an illustrative delta of 0.8. The mean d is about 0.8316, an overstatement of about 0.0316 or 3.9%, which the factor J of about 0.962 removes on average." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Twenty degrees of freedom as in the article's trial" ;
    hemo:9000014 "m = 20; delta = 0.8; J = 0.962025; E_d = 0.8316; Bias_d = 0.0316" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HEDG-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "twenty-degrees-of-freedom-as-in-the-article-s-trial" .

hemo:7004764 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Zero population effect gives no expected bias in Cohen's d"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With delta equal to 0 the mean of d is 0 at any number of degrees of freedom, a limiting case, because the bias is proportional to delta." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Zero population effect gives no expected bias in Cohen's d" ;
    hemo:9000014 "m = 20; delta = 0; J = 0.962025; E_d = 0; Bias_d = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HEDG-012" ;
    hemo:9000023 "zero-population-effect-gives-no-expected-bias-in-cohen-s-d" .

hemo:7004765 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One rate paid on every unit with no displacement of private uptake"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The subsidy is paid at the same rate on every unit, so the Q_p units that people would have taken up anyway attract it as well as the induced units. Inframarginal users keep their uptake unchanged, so T is a pure transfer to them and no private activity is displaced. OMB Circular A-94 asks analyses to identify how far a policy such as a subsidy programme promotes substitutes for activities that would occur without it, and to record either the displaced activities as costs or only the incremental gains as benefits." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One rate paid on every unit with no displacement of private uptake" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PIGS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-rate-paid-on-every-unit-with-no-displacement-of-private-uptake" .

hemo:7004766 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Uptake under the subsidy taken from the demand response"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Q_u is the uptake when the price people face falls by s, as in HE-AS-EXT-006, so with the linear demand of HE-FM-EXT-002 it equals Q_p plus s / b. Q_u must exceed Q_p for c_n to be defined; a subsidy that induces no extra uptake is a transfer only." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Uptake under the subsidy taken from the demand response" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PIGS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "uptake-under-the-subsidy-taken-from-the-demand-response" .

hemo:7004767 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Pigouvian subsidy bill and inframarginal transfer from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000744 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the rate in SubsidyRate and the uptakes without and with the subsidy in PrivateUptake and SubsidisedUptake, the four formulas fill SubsidyBill, InframarginalTransfer, InducedUnitsBill and BillPerInducedUnit in turn." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pigouvian subsidy bill and inframarginal transfer from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-PIGS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pigouvian-subsidy-bill-and-inframarginal-transfer-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SubsidyRate*SubsidisedUptake; =SubsidyRate*PrivateUptake; =SubsidyRate*(SubsidisedUptake-PrivateUptake); =SubsidyBill/(SubsidisedUptake-PrivateUptake)" .

hemo:7004768 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Pigouvian subsidy bill splits into the inframarginal transfer and the induced-unit bill"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The cell SubsidyBill equals InframarginalTransfer plus InducedUnitsBill, which are computed from the two uptakes separately. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the bill computed on the private uptake, which gives 720 against 864 in the article's example, or a bill that has been doubled, 1,728." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pigouvian subsidy bill splits into the inframarginal transfer and the induced-unit bill" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PIGS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pigouvian-subsidy-bill-splits-into-the-inframarginal-transfer-and-the-induced-unit-bill" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(SubsidyBill-(InframarginalTransfer+InducedUnitsBill))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004769 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Bill per induced unit reproduces the lentils figure of USD 100"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With SubsidyRate 10, PrivateUptake 9 and SubsidisedUptake 10, the cell BillPerInducedUnit equals the USD 100 per new immunisation of Goodkin-Gold and colleagues. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the bill divided by all users, which gives 10, or only the inframarginal transfer divided by the induced units, which gives 90." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Bill per induced unit reproduces the lentils figure of USD 100" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PIGS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "bill-per-induced-unit-reproduces-the-lentils-figure-of-usd-100" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(BillPerInducedUnit-100)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004770 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Treatment subsidy of GBP 12 on 72 thousand courses"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative example a subsidy of GBP 12 a course raises uptake by 12 thousand courses a year, to 72 thousand. The bill is GBP 864,000 a year, of which GBP 720,000 goes to people who would have been treated anyway and GBP 144,000 is paid on the 12 thousand extra courses, the article's figures. Each extra course costs the budget GBP 72 (computed here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Treatment subsidy of GBP 12 on 72 thousand courses" ;
    hemo:9000014 "s = 12; Q_p = 60; Q_u = 72; B = 864; T = 720; B_n = 144; c_n = 72" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PIGS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "treatment-subsidy-of-gbp-12-on-72-thousand-courses" .

hemo:7004771 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Lentils incentive with nine inframarginal children per marginal child"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Goodkin-Gold and colleagues suppose an incentive worth USD 10 per child immunised and nine inframarginal children for every marginal child, round numbers chosen for ease of analysis. Per ten children paid, nine would have been immunised anyway, so USD 100 is paid out for each new immunisation induced, their figure." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Lentils incentive with nine inframarginal children per marginal child" ;
    hemo:9000014 "s = 10; Q_p = 9; Q_u = 10; B = 100; T = 90; B_n = 10; c_n = 100" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PIGS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "lentils-incentive-with-nine-inframarginal-children-per-marginal-child" .

hemo:7004772 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No inframarginal users leaves the bill per induced unit at the rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "If nobody would take up the activity without the subsidy, every unit is induced, the transfer is zero and the bill per induced unit equals the rate of GBP 12, a limiting case computed here for illustration." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No inframarginal users leaves the bill per induced unit at the rate" ;
    hemo:9000014 "s = 12; Q_p = 0; Q_u = 12; B = 144; T = 0; B_n = 144; c_n = 12" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PIGS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-inframarginal-users-leaves-the-bill-per-induced-unit-at-the-rate" .

hemo:7004773 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Linear curves and full pass-through of the subsidy"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Marginal private and external benefit are linear and marginal cost constant over the range of uptake between Q_p and Q_p plus dQ, as in HE-AS-EXT-004, and the subsidy lowers the price people face by s, as in HE-AS-EXT-006. Goodkin-Gold and colleagues rely on the same pass-through of a subsidy by competitive firms. Their steady-state external benefit of a vaccine rises with coverage, so the falling linear benefit here is an illustration, as the article says." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Linear curves and full pass-through of the subsidy" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PIGS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "linear-curves-and-full-pass-through-of-the-subsidy" .

hemo:7004774 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One marginal excess burden on every pound of the bill"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each pound of the bill is raised at the same marginal excess burden lambda, the assumption of HE-AS-DWL-010, and the bill itself is a transfer. OMB Circular A-94 sets 25 cents per dollar of revenue for a supplementary analysis, the article uses an illustrative 20p, and lambda of zero corresponds to Pigou's proviso that the funds be raised by a mere transfer." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One marginal excess burden on every pound of the bill" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PIGS-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-marginal-excess-burden-on-every-pound-of-the-bill" .

hemo:7004775 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Targeting that is exact and costless for the targeted net welfare"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "W_T supposes that the subsidy reaches only the people induced to take up the activity, at no cost of targeting, as in the article's case of a subsidy confined to the extra 12 thousand courses. It is a bound on what targeting can achieve; Goodkin-Gold and colleagues argue for subsidies targeted even very imprecisely to people or areas with low take-up." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Targeting that is exact and costless for the targeted net welfare" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PIGS-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "targeting-that-is-exact-and-costless-for-the-targeted-net-welfare" .

hemo:7004776 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Net welfare of a Pigouvian subsidy from named curve cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000745 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the curve parameters in DemandIntercept, DemandSlope, MargCost, ExtIntercept and ExtSlope, the marginal excess burden in ExcessBurden and the rate in SubsidyRate, the formulas fill PrivateQty, MebAtPrivateQty, InducedUnits, SurplusGain, NetWelfareUniform and NetWelfareTargeted in turn." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net welfare of a Pigouvian subsidy from named curve cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-PIGS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-welfare-of-a-pigouvian-subsidy-from-named-curve-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(DemandIntercept-MargCost)/DemandSlope; =ExtIntercept-ExtSlope*PrivateQty; =SubsidyRate/DemandSlope; =MebAtPrivateQty*InducedUnits-0.5*(DemandSlope+ExtSlope)*InducedUnits^2; =SurplusGain-ExcessBurden*SubsidyRate*(PrivateQty+InducedUnits); =SurplusGain-ExcessBurden*SubsidyRate*InducedUnits" .

hemo:7004777 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Welfare gain of a Pigouvian subsidy equals the trapezoid under the social gap"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With linear curves the cell SurplusGain equals half the induced units times the sum of the gaps between marginal social benefit and marginal cost at Q_p and at Q_p plus dQ, computed from the curve cells. Expected result TRUE; in the first worked example both give 90. FALSE shows the quadratic term written with 0.25 in place of 0.5, which gives 135, or a doubled gain, 180." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Welfare gain of a Pigouvian subsidy equals the trapezoid under the social gap" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PIGS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "welfare-gain-of-a-pigouvian-subsidy-equals-the-trapezoid-under-the-social-gap" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(SurplusGain-0.5*InducedUnits*(MebAtPrivateQty+(DemandIntercept+ExtIntercept-MargCost-(DemandSlope+ExtSlope)*(PrivateQty+InducedUnits))))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004778 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Net loss of GBP 82,800 at the Pigouvian rate reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's curves, ExcessBurden 0.2 and SubsidyRate 12, the cell NetWelfareUniform equals the gain of 90 less 0.2 times the bill of 12 times 72. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the excess burden charged only on the induced courses, which gives 61.2, or the bill subtracted as a cost as well, which gives minus 946.8." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net loss of GBP 82,800 at the Pigouvian rate reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PIGS-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-loss-of-gbp-82-800-at-the-pigouvian-rate-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(NetWelfareUniform-(90-0.2*12*72))<1E-6" .

hemo:7004779 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Pigouvian rate of GBP 12 with a 20p excess burden"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's illustrative curves MPB = 100 minus Q, MC = 40 and MEB = 30 minus 0.25Q, a uniform subsidy of GBP 12 gains GBP 90,000 a year but the excess burden of the GBP 864,000 bill at 20p a pound is GBP 172,800, a net loss of GBP 82,800. Confined to the 12 thousand extra courses the net gain is GBP 61,200, the article's figures." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pigouvian rate of GBP 12 with a 20p excess burden" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 100; b = 1; c = 40; m = 30; g = 0.25; lambda = 0.2; s = 12; Q_p = 60; d_0 = 15; dQ = 12; S_g = 90; W = -82.8; W_T = 61.2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PIGS-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pigouvian-rate-of-gbp-12-with-a-20p-excess-burden" .

hemo:7004780 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Uniform subsidy of GBP 1.82 with a 20p excess burden"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At the article's best uniform rate of about GBP 1.82 a course the net gain is about GBP 2,700 a year, as in the article; the gain from the 1.82 thousand induced courses is GBP 25,230 and the excess burden of the bill GBP 22,502 (computed here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Uniform subsidy of GBP 1.82 with a 20p excess burden" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 100; b = 1; c = 40; m = 30; g = 0.25; lambda = 0.2; s = 1.82; Q_p = 60; d_0 = 15; dQ = 1.82; S_g = 25.22975; W = 2.7273; W_T = 24.5673" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PIGS-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "uniform-subsidy-of-gbp-1-82-with-a-20p-excess-burden" .

hemo:7004781 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Subsidy set at the pre-subsidy external benefit of GBP 15 with costless funds"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With lambda of zero, a subsidy of GBP 15, the external benefit at 60 thousand courses, raises uptake to 75 thousand and gains GBP 84,375 a year, short of the GBP 90,000 at the Pigouvian rate of HE-FM-EXT-002 by the article's overshoot loss of GBP 5,625." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Subsidy set at the pre-subsidy external benefit of GBP 15 with costless funds" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 100; b = 1; c = 40; m = 30; g = 0.25; lambda = 0; s = 15; Q_p = 60; d_0 = 15; dQ = 15; S_g = 84.375; W = 84.375; W_T = 84.375" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PIGS-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "subsidy-set-at-the-pre-subsidy-external-benefit-of-gbp-15-with-costless-funds" .

hemo:7004782 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Steeper demand halves the uptake response to a GBP 12 subsidy"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With illustrative curves MPB = 100 minus 2Q, MC = 40 and MEB = 30 minus 0.5Q, private uptake is 30 thousand and the Pigouvian rate of HE-FM-EXT-002 is again GBP 12, but it induces only 6 thousand courses. The gain is GBP 45,000 and the excess burden of the GBP 432,000 bill at 20p a pound GBP 86,400, a net loss of GBP 41,400, or a net gain of GBP 30,600 if confined to the induced courses (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Steeper demand halves the uptake response to a GBP 12 subsidy" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 100; b = 2; c = 40; m = 30; g = 0.5; lambda = 0.2; s = 12; Q_p = 30; d_0 = 15; dQ = 6; S_g = 45; W = -41.4; W_T = 30.6" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PIGS-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "steeper-demand-halves-the-uptake-response-to-a-gbp-12-subsidy" .

hemo:7004783 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Subsidy constrained to be zero or above"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "s_opt is floored at zero, so the formula never returns a tax on the activity; Goodkin-Gold and colleagues likewise constrain the subsidy to be nonnegative. When d_0 is at most lambda times b times Q_p the best uniform subsidy is zero and W_opt is zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Subsidy constrained to be zero or above" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PIGS-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "subsidy-constrained-to-be-zero-or-above" .

hemo:7004784 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Concave net welfare with an interior optimum"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "With b above zero, g and lambda zero or above, b + g + 2 lambda b is positive and W is a concave quadratic in s, so the first-order condition gives the maximum. The optimum is read within the range where the linear curves of HE-FM-PIGS-002 hold." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Concave net welfare with an interior optimum" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PIGS-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "concave-net-welfare-with-an-interior-optimum" .

hemo:7004785 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Best uniform Pigouvian subsidy from named curve cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000746 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the curve parameters in DemandIntercept, DemandSlope, MargCost, ExtIntercept and ExtSlope and the marginal excess burden in ExcessBurden, the formulas fill PrivateQty, MebAtPrivateQty, BestUniformSubsidy and BestNetWelfare in turn." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Best uniform Pigouvian subsidy from named curve cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-PIGS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "best-uniform-pigouvian-subsidy-from-named-curve-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(DemandIntercept-MargCost)/DemandSlope; =ExtIntercept-ExtSlope*PrivateQty; =MAX(0,DemandSlope*(MebAtPrivateQty-ExcessBurden*DemandSlope*PrivateQty)/(DemandSlope+ExtSlope+2*ExcessBurden*DemandSlope)); =MAX(0,MebAtPrivateQty-ExcessBurden*DemandSlope*PrivateQty)^2/(2*(DemandSlope+ExtSlope+2*ExcessBurden*DemandSlope))" .

hemo:7004786 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Best net welfare equals net welfare evaluated at the best uniform subsidy"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The cell BestNetWelfare, from the closed form, equals W of HE-FM-PIGS-002 evaluated at the rate held in BestUniformSubsidy. Expected result TRUE; for the article's curves both give 2.7273. FALSE shows a doubled BestUniformSubsidy, at which W is 0, or the factor 2 dropped from 2 lambda b in both cells, which gives 3.1034 against 2.6754." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Best net welfare equals net welfare evaluated at the best uniform subsidy" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PIGS-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "best-net-welfare-equals-net-welfare-evaluated-at-the-best-uniform-subsidy" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(BestNetWelfare-(MebAtPrivateQty*BestUniformSubsidy/DemandSlope-0.5*(DemandSlope+ExtSlope)*(BestUniformSubsidy/DemandSlope)^2-ExcessBurden*BestUniformSubsidy*(PrivateQty+BestUniformSubsidy/DemandSlope)))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004787 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Best uniform subsidy of 3 over 1.65 reproduced for the article's curves"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's curves and ExcessBurden 0.2, the cell BestUniformSubsidy equals the article's 3 / 1.65. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the excess burden charged only on the inframarginal transfer, which gives 2.4, or only on the induced units, which gives 9.0909." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Best uniform subsidy of 3 over 1.65 reproduced for the article's curves" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PIGS-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "best-uniform-subsidy-of-3-over-1-65-reproduced-for-the-article-s-curves" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(BestUniformSubsidy-3/1.65)<0.00001" .

hemo:7004788 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Costless funds return the Pigouvian rate of HE-FM-EXT-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With ExcessBurden set to 0, the cell BestUniformSubsidy equals the Pigouvian subsidy of HE-FM-EXT-002, m minus g times the efficient quantity, computed from the curve cells; for the article's curves both give 12. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows ExtSlope left out of the denominator, which gives 15, or a doubled subsidy, 24." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Costless funds return the Pigouvian rate of HE-FM-EXT-002" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PIGS-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "costless-funds-return-the-pigouvian-rate-of-he-fm-ext-002" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(BestUniformSubsidy-(ExtIntercept-ExtSlope*(DemandIntercept+ExtIntercept-MargCost)/(DemandSlope+ExtSlope)))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004789 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Best uniform treatment subsidy of GBP 1.82 with a 20p excess burden"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's illustrative curves and an excess burden of 20p a pound, the best uniform subsidy is 3 / 1.65, about GBP 1.82 a course, and the net gain about GBP 2,700 a year, the article's figures, against the Pigouvian rate of GBP 12." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Best uniform treatment subsidy of GBP 1.82 with a 20p excess burden" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 100; b = 1; c = 40; m = 30; g = 0.25; lambda = 0.2; Q_p = 60; d_0 = 15; s_opt = 1.8182; W_opt = 2.7273" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PIGS-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "best-uniform-treatment-subsidy-of-gbp-1-82-with-a-20p-excess-burden" .

hemo:7004790 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Costless public funds return the Pigouvian subsidy of GBP 12"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With lambda of zero the best uniform subsidy is GBP 12, the Pigouvian rate of HE-FM-EXT-002 for the article's curves, and the net welfare GBP 90,000 a year, the deadweight loss that subsidy removes, a limiting case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Costless public funds return the Pigouvian subsidy of GBP 12" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 100; b = 1; c = 40; m = 30; g = 0.25; lambda = 0; Q_p = 60; d_0 = 15; s_opt = 12; W_opt = 90" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PIGS-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "costless-public-funds-return-the-pigouvian-subsidy-of-gbp-12" .

hemo:7004791 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Excess burden of 25 cents per pound leaves no worthwhile uniform subsidy"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At the OMB supplementary rate of 25 cents per unit of revenue, applied to the article's curves for illustration, the external benefit of GBP 15 at 60 thousand courses equals 0.25 times 60, so the best uniform subsidy and its net gain are both zero (computed here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Excess burden of 25 cents per pound leaves no worthwhile uniform subsidy" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 100; b = 1; c = 40; m = 30; g = 0.25; lambda = 0.25; Q_p = 60; d_0 = 15; s_opt = 0; W_opt = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PIGS-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "excess-burden-of-25-cents-per-pound-leaves-no-worthwhile-uniform-subsidy" .

hemo:7004792 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Additive shift on imputed QALYs of those lost to follow-up"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The shift is an offset added to the QALYs of those lost, the delta parameter of the pattern-mixture approach that Leurent and colleagues describe, applied after imputation under missing at random. Because a fraction p of an arm is shifted by delta, the arm mean moves by p times delta in every imputed dataset, and Rubin's rules average the point estimates, so the pooled QALY gain moves by the same amount (derived). With covariates in the imputation model the base-case gain is the multiple imputation estimate, which can differ from the difference in observed means; the article's example takes the two to be equal." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Additive shift on imputed QALYs of those lost to follow-up" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-LTFU-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "additive-shift-on-imputed-qalys-of-those-lost-to-follow-up" .

hemo:7004793 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Incremental cost unchanged by the loss to follow-up shift"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Only QALYs are shifted and Delta_C and lambda are fixed, as in the article's example, where costs come from complete linked hospital records. When missing costs are also shifted, as in the extension that Leurent and colleagues report in section 4.1 of their tutorial, the incremental cost moves with the assumption and the tipping point is found by repeating the analysis over a grid of shifts on the incremental net monetary benefit (HE-FM-NMB-002), as HE-CF-LTFU-001 does for QALYs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Incremental cost unchanged by the loss to follow-up shift" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-LTFU-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "incremental-cost-unchanged-by-the-loss-to-follow-up-shift" .

hemo:7004794 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Positive denominator and the tie at the loss to follow-up tipping point"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The denominator, p_T minus r times p_C, is above zero. That holds for a shift confined to the new treatment arm whenever anyone in that arm is lost, and for a common shift only when the new treatment arm loses the larger share. With a common shift and equal shares lost the QALY gain does not move and there is no tipping point, and with a smaller share lost from the new treatment arm a common worsening raises the gain (derived). At delta equal to delta_star the delta-adjusted ICER equals lambda and the incremental net health benefit of HE-FM-CET-002 is zero. The package reports that boundary value as the tipping point and does not classify the tie as acceptance or rejection." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Positive denominator and the tie at the loss to follow-up tipping point" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-LTFU-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "positive-denominator-and-the-tie-at-the-loss-to-follow-up-tipping-point" .

hemo:7004795 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Excel tipping-point shift for loss to follow-up from arm summaries"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000747 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the incremental cost in a cell named DeltaCost, the threshold in ThresholdQALY, the base-case QALY gain in GainObserved, the proportions lost in LostShareNew and LostShareUsual and the shift ratio r in ShiftRatio, this formula in a cell named TippingShift returns the tipping-point shift, or #N/A when the denominator is not above zero and no tipping point exists." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Excel tipping-point shift for loss to follow-up from arm summaries" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-LTFU-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "excel-tipping-point-shift-for-loss-to-follow-up-from-arm-summaries" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(LostShareNew-ShiftRatio*LostShareUsual<=0,NA(),(DeltaCost/ThresholdQALY-GainObserved)/(LostShareNew-ShiftRatio*LostShareUsual))" .

hemo:7004796 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Delta-adjusted ICER equals the threshold at the loss to follow-up tipping point"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The ICER recomputed at the shift held in TippingShift, the incremental cost divided by the base-case gain plus the shifted share times TippingShift, equals ThresholdQALY within GBP 0.50. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a TippingShift that leaves out the division by the proportion lost, which with the article's inputs holds -0.025 and gives an ICER of about GBP 15,789 instead of GBP 20,000." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Delta-adjusted ICER equals the threshold at the loss to follow-up tipping point" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-LTFU-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "delta-adjusted-icer-equals-the-threshold-at-the-loss-to-follow-up-tipping-point" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(DeltaCost/(GainObserved+(LostShareNew-ShiftRatio*LostShareUsual)*TippingShift)-ThresholdQALY)<0.5" .

hemo:7004797 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Loss to follow-up tipping point reproduces the article's minus 0.125"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's inputs (DeltaCost 1,500, ThresholdQALY 20,000, GainObserved 0.10, LostShareNew 0.2, LostShareUsual 0.1 and ShiftRatio 0) the value in TippingShift is -0.125. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows ShiftRatio left at 1, the same shift in both arms, which returns -0.25." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Loss to follow-up tipping point reproduces the article's minus 0.125" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-LTFU-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "loss-to-follow-up-tipping-point-reproduces-the-article-s-minus-0-125" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(TippingShift+0.125)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004798 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Loss to follow-up tipping point with the shift in the new treatment arm only"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative trial has an incremental cost of GBP 1,500, observed mean QALYs of 0.78 and 0.68, and 20% lost from the new treatment arm against 10% from usual care. At an illustrative GBP 20,000 per QALY the gain must stay above 0.075, and with the shift in the new treatment arm only the tipping point is -0.125, as in the article. The conclusion holds unless those lost from the new treatment arm are worse off than those observed by more than 0.125 QALYs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Loss to follow-up tipping point with the shift in the new treatment arm only" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Delta_C = 1500; lambda = 20000; H_d = 0.075; Delta_E_obs = 0.1; p_T = 0.2; p_C = 0.1; r = 0; delta_star = -0.125" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-LTFU-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "loss-to-follow-up-tipping-point-with-the-shift-in-the-new-treatment-arm-only" .

hemo:7004799 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Loss to follow-up tipping point with the same shift in both arms"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the same illustrative inputs and the same shift in both arms, the gain moves by only 0.1 times the shift, the difference between the 20% and 10% lost, so the tipping point is -0.25 (computed here for illustration). This matches step 1 of the article, where a common shift of -0.10 lowers the gain by only 0.01." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Loss to follow-up tipping point with the same shift in both arms" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Delta_C = 1500; lambda = 20000; H_d = 0.075; Delta_E_obs = 0.1; p_T = 0.2; p_C = 0.1; r = 1; delta_star = -0.25" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-LTFU-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "loss-to-follow-up-tipping-point-with-the-same-shift-in-both-arms" .

hemo:7004800 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Loss to follow-up tipping point at a threshold equal to the observed ICER"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When the threshold is set at the observed ICER of GBP 15,000 per QALY, the gain needed equals the observed gain of 0.10 and the tipping point is zero, a limiting case in which any worsening for those lost from the new treatment arm changes the conclusion (illustrative)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Loss to follow-up tipping point at a threshold equal to the observed ICER" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Delta_C = 1500; lambda = 15000; H_d = 0.1; Delta_E_obs = 0.1; p_T = 0.2; p_C = 0.1; r = 0; delta_star = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-LTFU-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "loss-to-follow-up-tipping-point-at-a-threshold-equal-to-the-observed-icer" .

hemo:7004801 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Same national unit costs and cost basis for both providers' indices"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Both expected costs price the same case groups at the same national mean unit costs, and each actual cost is on the same basis as its expected cost. In the National Cost Collection the expected cost is the organisation's activity multiplied by the national mean unit cost and the index queries run on data adjusted by the market forces factor, so a crude cost set against that index is taken on the same adjusted basis." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same national unit costs and cost basis for both providers' indices" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CMAD-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-national-unit-costs-and-cost-basis-for-both-providers-indices" .

hemo:7004802 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Index ratio read as like for like only under proportional cost deviations"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "RI compares two indices, each standardised to its own provider's mix. When each provider's unit cost is the same multiple of the national cost in every group, its index is 100 times that multiple and RI equals the ratio of directly standardised costs for any common mix (derived here), as in the article, where both give 1.105. Otherwise the two ratios can differ. Shahian and Normand state that indirectly standardised outcomes should not be used to compare one institution directly with another unless the case-mix distributions overlap reasonably, so the direct standardisation of HE-FM-INEQ-001 on the pooled mix is the check." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Index ratio read as like for like only under proportional cost deviations" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CMAD-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "index-ratio-read-as-like-for-like-only-under-proportional-cost-deviations" .

hemo:7004803 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Ties in the crude or the adjusted cost ranking"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "rev is 1 only when the product of rho minus 1 and RI minus 1 is negative. Equal crude costs per case, rho equal to 1, or equal indices, RI equal to 1, give a product of 0 and are not counted as a reversal, a convention of this formula." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ties in the crude or the adjusted cost ranking" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CMAD-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ties-in-the-crude-or-the-adjusted-cost-ranking" .

hemo:7004804 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Crude, mix and index ratios with a reversal flag from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000748 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With each provider's actual cost, cases and expected cost in cells named ActualCostFirst, CasesFirst, ExpectedCostFirst, ActualCostSecond, CasesSecond and ExpectedCostSecond, the four formulas return rho in a cell named CrudeCostRatio, mu in MixRatio, RI in IndexRatio and the flag, TRUE for a reversal, in RankReversal." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Crude, mix and index ratios with a reversal flag from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CMAD-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "crude-mix-and-index-ratios-with-a-reversal-flag-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(ActualCostFirst/CasesFirst)/(ActualCostSecond/CasesSecond); =(ExpectedCostFirst/CasesFirst)/(ExpectedCostSecond/CasesSecond); =CrudeCostRatio/MixRatio; =(CrudeCostRatio-1)*(IndexRatio-1)<0" .

hemo:7004805 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Index ratio cell matches the ratio of the two providers' cost indices"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each provider's index is held in its own cell, IndexFirst as =100*ActualCostFirst/ExpectedCostFirst and IndexSecond in the same way, the form of HE-FM-CWT-003 multiplied by 100, and IndexRatio must equal their ratio. Expected result TRUE. An IndexRatio written as CrudeCostRatio times MixRatio gives about 0.2944 for the article's hospitals against 1.1053, and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Index ratio cell matches the ratio of the two providers' cost indices" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CMAD-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "index-ratio-cell-matches-the-ratio-of-the-two-providers-cost-indices" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(IndexRatio-IndexFirst/IndexSecond)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004806 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's hospitals give a reversal and an index ratio of 105 over 95"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's inputs RankReversal is TRUE and IndexRatio equals 105 / 95, the ratio of the two indices in the article. Expected result TRUE. An IndexRatio written as MixRatio divided by CrudeCostRatio gives about 0.9048, the flag turns FALSE and the test returns FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's hospitals give a reversal and an index ratio of 105 over 95" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CMAD-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-hospitals-give-a-reversal-and-an-index-ratio-of-105-over-95" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(RankReversal,ABS(IndexRatio-105/95)<1E-6)" .

hemo:7004807 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Reversal flag agrees with the derived between condition"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "RankReversal is compared with the derived condition that rho lies strictly above mu and below 1, or strictly below mu and above 1. Expected result TRUE. A flag written with MixRatio in place of IndexRatio returns FALSE for the article's hospitals, where the condition holds, and the test returns FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Reversal flag agrees with the derived between condition" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CMAD-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "reversal-flag-agrees-with-the-derived-between-condition" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=RankReversal=OR(AND(CrudeCostRatio>MixRatio,CrudeCostRatio<1),AND(CrudeCostRatio<MixRatio,CrudeCostRatio>1))" .

hemo:7004808 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's two hospitals reverse after indirect standardisation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative example Hospital A costs 3,360,000 GBP for 1,000 cases against an expected 3,200,000 GBP, and Hospital B 5,890,000 GBP for 1,000 cases against an expected 6,200,000 GBP. A's crude cost per case is 57% of B's, but its expected cost per case is only 52% of B's, so rho lies between mu and 1 and the ranking reverses. The index ratio is 105 / 95, about 1.105, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's two hospitals reverse after indirect standardisation" ;
    hemo:9000014 "A_1 = 3360000; n_1 = 1000; E_1 = 3200000; A_2 = 5890000; n_2 = 1000; E_2 = 6200000; rho = 0.570458; mu = 0.516129; RI = 1.105263; rev = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CMAD-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-two-hospitals-reverse-after-indirect-standardisation" .

hemo:7004809 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Crude cost advantage larger than the case-mix advantage survives adjustment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An illustrative Hospital C has B's mix and costs 20% above the national mean in both groups, 7,440,000 GBP for 1,000 cases against an expected 6,200,000 GBP. A's crude cost per case is 45% of C's, below the mix ratio of 52%, so A stays the cheaper provider after adjustment, with indices of 105 and 120 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Crude cost advantage larger than the case-mix advantage survives adjustment" ;
    hemo:9000014 "A_1 = 3360000; n_1 = 1000; E_1 = 3200000; A_2 = 7440000; n_2 = 1000; E_2 = 6200000; rho = 0.451613; mu = 0.516129; RI = 0.875; rev = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CMAD-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "crude-cost-advantage-larger-than-the-case-mix-advantage-survives-adjustment" .

hemo:7004810 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Same case mix cannot reverse a crude cost ranking"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An illustrative second hospital has A's mix and costs 5% below the national mean in both groups, 3,040,000 GBP against an expected 3,200,000 GBP. Then mu is 1, the index ratio equals the crude ratio and no reversal is possible, a limiting case that checks an implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same case mix cannot reverse a crude cost ranking" ;
    hemo:9000014 "A_1 = 3360000; n_1 = 1000; E_1 = 3200000; A_2 = 3040000; n_2 = 1000; E_2 = 3200000; rho = 1.105263; mu = 1; RI = 1.105263; rev = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CMAD-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-case-mix-cannot-reverse-a-crude-cost-ranking" .

hemo:7004811 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Both tests read on every person under the either-positive rule"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each person has both tests and the combined result is negative only when both are negative, the parallel interpretation of Gardner and colleagues. A pathway in which every person negative on the first test then has the second gives the same both-negative probabilities (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Both tests read on every person under the either-positive rule" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-NLR-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "both-tests-read-on-every-person-under-the-either-positive-rule" .

hemo:7004812 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Conditional covariances inside their valid limits"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "C_pos and C_neg must keep every joint probability of the two results a valid probability. Wang, Lin and Nelson give the upper limits min(Se_1, Se_2) minus Se_1 Se_2 and min(Sp_1, Sp_2) minus Sp_1 Sp_2; at the upper limit of C_pos the more sensitive test is negative only when the other is too (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Conditional covariances inside their valid limits" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-NLR-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "conditional-covariances-inside-their-valid-limits" .

hemo:7004813 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Accuracy and covariances from people who had both tests"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Se_1, Se_2, Sp_1, Sp_2 and the covariances come from the same people tested with both tests against one reference standard, as in the cross-classification of HE-CF-NLR-001. Two separate accuracy studies give the single-test values but no covariance, and setting the covariances to zero is then an assumption, not an estimate." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Accuracy and covariances from people who had both tests" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-NLR-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "accuracy-and-covariances-from-people-who-had-both-tests" .

hemo:7004814 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Joint negative likelihood ratio of two tests from named accuracies and covariances"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000749 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the two sensitivities in SensFirst and SensSecond, the two specificities in SpecFirst and SpecSecond and the covariances in CovCases and CovNonCases, the formulas return the combined sensitivity, specificity and negative likelihood ratio, held in SensParallel, SpecParallel and JointNegLR." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Joint negative likelihood ratio of two tests from named accuracies and covariances" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-NLR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "joint-negative-likelihood-ratio-of-two-tests-from-named-accuracies-and-covariances" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1-(1-SensFirst)*(1-SensSecond)-CovCases; =SpecFirst*SpecSecond+CovNonCases; =(1-SensParallel)/SpecParallel" .

hemo:7004815 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Joint negative likelihood ratio agrees with the both-positive route"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The both-negative probabilities can also be built from the both-positive cells of Wang, Lin and Nelson, 1 minus SensFirst minus SensSecond plus (SensFirst SensSecond + CovCases) among people with the condition and the same with the false positive rates among people without it, and JointNegLR equals their ratio. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the covariances entered with the wrong sign, which with the third worked example's inputs gives about minus 0.0429 instead of 0.0556." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Joint negative likelihood ratio agrees with the both-positive route" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-NLR-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "joint-negative-likelihood-ratio-agrees-with-the-both-positive-route" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(JointNegLR/((1-SensFirst-SensSecond+SensFirst*SensSecond+CovCases)/(1-(1-SpecFirst)-(1-SpecSecond)+(1-SpecFirst)*(1-SpecSecond)+CovNonCases))-1)<1E-9" .

hemo:7004816 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Joint negative likelihood ratio equals the product of single ratios plus a dependence term"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "JointNegLR equals the product of the two single-test ratios of HE-FM-DXA-002 plus (CovCases minus that product times CovNonCases) / (SpecFirst SpecSecond + CovNonCases) (derived), so with both covariances zero it is the product. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the single-test ratios multiplied while covariances are present, 0.0125 instead of about 0.0556 with the third worked example's inputs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Joint negative likelihood ratio equals the product of single ratios plus a dependence term" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-NLR-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "joint-negative-likelihood-ratio-equals-the-product-of-single-ratios-plus-a-dependence-term" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(JointNegLR-(1-SensFirst)/SpecFirst*(1-SensSecond)/SpecSecond-(CovCases-(1-SensFirst)/SpecFirst*(1-SensSecond)/SpecSecond*CovNonCases)/(SpecFirst*SpecSecond+CovNonCases))<1E-9" .

hemo:7004817 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Either-positive rule at least as sensitive and no more specific than each test"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With covariances inside the limits of Wang, Lin and Nelson, SensParallel is at least the larger single sensitivity and at most 1, and SpecParallel is at most the smaller single specificity and at least 0 (derived). Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a covariance above its limit, for example CovCases of 0.06 with the worked example's tests, which gives SensParallel of 0.935, below the first test's 0.95." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Either-positive rule at least as sensitive and no more specific than each test" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-NLR-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "either-positive-rule-at-least-as-sensitive-and-no-more-specific-than-each-test" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(SensParallel>=MAX(SensFirst,SensSecond)-1E-12,SensParallel<=1,SpecParallel<=MIN(SpecFirst,SpecSecond)+1E-12,SpecParallel>=0)" .

hemo:7004818 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Joint negative likelihood ratio of two conditionally independent tests"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's triage test, with sensitivity 0.95 and specificity 0.50, combined with an illustrative second test of sensitivity 0.90 and specificity 0.80 gives Se_par of 0.995 and Sp_par of 0.40, so LR_neg_12 is 0.005 / 0.40 = 0.0125, the product of the single-test ratios 0.10 and 0.125." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Joint negative likelihood ratio of two conditionally independent tests" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Se_1 = 0.95; Se_2 = 0.9; Sp_1 = 0.5; Sp_2 = 0.8; C_pos = 0; C_neg = 0; Se_par = 0.995; Sp_par = 0.4; LR_neg_12 = 0.0125" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NLR-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "joint-negative-likelihood-ratio-of-two-conditionally-independent-tests" .

hemo:7004819 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Joint negative likelihood ratio with dependence among people with the condition"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With an illustrative covariance of 0.02 among people with the condition, inside its upper limit of 0.045, both tests miss 2.5% of cases instead of 0.5%, Se_par is 0.975 and LR_neg_12 is 0.025 / 0.40 = 0.0625, five times the product. At a 30% pre-test probability the residual risk is about 2.61% against about 0.53% under independence." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Joint negative likelihood ratio with dependence among people with the condition" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Se_1 = 0.95; Se_2 = 0.9; Sp_1 = 0.5; Sp_2 = 0.8; C_pos = 0.02; C_neg = 0; Se_par = 0.975; Sp_par = 0.4; LR_neg_12 = 0.0625" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NLR-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "joint-negative-likelihood-ratio-with-dependence-among-people-with-the-condition" .

hemo:7004820 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Joint negative likelihood ratio with dependence in both groups"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Adding an illustrative covariance of 0.05 among people without the condition, inside its upper limit of 0.10, raises Sp_par to 0.45 and lowers LR_neg_12 to 0.025 / 0.45, about 0.0556, still about 4.4 times the product." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Joint negative likelihood ratio with dependence in both groups" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Se_1 = 0.95; Se_2 = 0.9; Sp_1 = 0.5; Sp_2 = 0.8; C_pos = 0.02; C_neg = 0.05; Se_par = 0.975; Sp_par = 0.45; LR_neg_12 = 0.0556" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NLR-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "joint-negative-likelihood-ratio-with-dependence-in-both-groups" .

hemo:7004821 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Trial adverse event counts to a per-cycle probability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every patient is followed for the whole period, the event rate is constant over follow-up, patients leave the at-risk pool after a first episode, and the adverse event is the only exit from the state. With competing exits such as death or progression the rates are converted jointly, as in HE-FM-TP-004." .

hemo:7004825 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-AER-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000750 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=1-EXP(LN(1-PatientsWithEvent/PatientsAtRisk)*CycleLength/FollowUp)` All three steps in one cell, with the counts in PatientsWithEvent and PatientsAtRisk and the two lengths, in the same time unit, in CycleLength and FollowUp. The equivalent form =1-(1-PatientsWithEvent/PatientsAtRisk)^(CycleLength/FollowUp) gives the same result." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1-EXP(LN(1-PatientsWithEvent/PatientsAtRisk)*CycleLength/FollowUp)" .

hemo:7004826 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-AER-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000750 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`ae_cycle_prob <- function(n_event, n_risk, follow_up, cycle_length) { p_trial <- n_event / n_risk; ae_rate <- -log(1-p_trial) / follow_up; 1-exp(-ae_rate * cycle_length) }` Vectorised, so one call can return the cycle probabilities for every arm or event type held in parallel vectors." ;
    hemo:9000024 "ae_cycle_prob <- function(n_event, n_risk, follow_up, cycle_length) { p_trial <- n_event / n_risk; ae_rate <- -log(1-p_trial) / follow_up; 1-exp(-ae_rate * cycle_length) }" .

hemo:7004827 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-AER-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000750 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def ae_cycle_prob(n_event, n_risk, follow_up, cycle_length): p_trial = n_event / n_risk; ae_rate = -math.log(1-p_trial) / follow_up; return 1-math.exp(-ae_rate * cycle_length)` Uses the math module, where math.log is the natural logarithm; replacing math with numpy gives the vectorised form." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def ae_cycle_prob(n_event, n_risk, follow_up, cycle_length): p_trial = n_event / n_risk; ae_rate = -math.log(1-p_trial) / follow_up; return 1-math.exp(-ae_rate * cycle_length)" .

hemo:7004830 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of AIC, AIC differences and Akaike weights for candidate survival curves"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "All candidates are fitted by maximum likelihood to identical data, and the set is specified before fitting. The weights are conditional on the set: adding or removing a model changes every weight. Subtracting the minimum before exponentiating also avoids exponentials of large negative numbers, which underflow to zero in floating-point arithmetic when AIC values run into the thousands." .

hemo:7004833 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-AIC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000751 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=2*C2-2*B2` in D2, `=D2-MIN($D$2:$D$7)` in E2 and `=EXP(-E2/2)/SUMPRODUCT(EXP(-$E$2:$E$7/2))` in F2, each filled down to row 7. With one model per row, log-likelihoods in B2:B7 and parameter counts in C2:C7, columns D, E and F return AIC, the AIC difference and the Akaike weight." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=2*C2-2*B2" .

hemo:7004834 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-AIC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000751 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`akaike_table <- function(loglik, k) { aic <- 2*k-2*loglik; delta <- aic-min(aic); w <- exp(-delta/2)/sum(exp(-delta/2)); data.frame(aic, delta, w) }` Vectorised over models; `akaike_table(c(-612.4, -605.1, -606.0, -603.9, -603.2, -602.8), c(1, 2, 2, 2, 2, 3))` reproduces the six-curve example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "akaike_table <- function(loglik, k) { aic <- 2*k-2*loglik; delta <- aic-min(aic); w <- exp(-delta/2)/sum(exp(-delta/2)); data.frame(aic, delta, w) }" .

hemo:7004835 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-AIC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000751 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def akaike_table(loglik, k): aic = [2*ki-2*li for li, ki in zip(loglik, k)]; m = min(aic); delta = [a-m for a in aic]; e = [math.exp(-d/2) for d in delta]; s = sum(e); return aic, delta, [x/s for x in e]` Uses the math module and returns the three lists in the order of the inputs." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def akaike_table(loglik, k): aic = [2*ki-2*li for li, ki in zip(loglik, k)]; m = min(aic); delta = [a-m for a in aic]; e = [math.exp(-d/2) for d in delta]; s = sum(e); return aic, delta, [x/s for x in e]" .

hemo:7004838 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Alpha spent and stopping hazard ratio at a group sequential look"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Deaths measure information, with 1:1 allocation and proportional hazards, and the one-sided level is halved inside the O'Brien-Fleming type quantile. The O'Brien-Fleming type spend at a fraction of 0 is taken as 0." .

hemo:7004842 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ASP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000752 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=Deaths/PlannedDeaths; =2-2*NORM.S.DIST(NORM.S.INV(1-Alpha/2)/SQRT(Deaths/PlannedDeaths),TRUE); =Alpha*LN(1+(EXP(1)-1)*Deaths/PlannedDeaths); =SpendOF-IF(PrevDeaths=0,0,2-2*NORM.S.DIST(NORM.S.INV(1-Alpha/2)/SQRT(PrevDeaths/PlannedDeaths),TRUE)); =SpendP-Alpha*LN(1+(EXP(1)-1)*PrevDeaths/PlannedDeaths); =EXP(-2*CritValue/SQRT(Deaths))` Six cells returning t_k, alpha_OF_k (named SpendOF), alpha_P_k (named SpendP), inc_OF_k, inc_P_k and HR_k, from named cells Deaths, PrevDeaths, PlannedDeaths, Alpha and CritValue. The IF avoids a division by zero at the first look." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=Deaths/PlannedDeaths; =2-2*NORM.S.DIST(NORM.S.INV(1-Alpha/2)/SQRT(Deaths/PlannedDeaths),TRUE); =Alpha*LN(1+(EXP(1)-1)*Deaths/PlannedDeaths); =SpendOF-IF(PrevDeaths=0,0,2-2*NORM.S.DIST(NORM.S.INV(1-Alpha/2)/SQRT(PrevDeaths/PlannedDeaths),TRUE)); =SpendP-Alpha*LN(1+(EXP(1)-1)*PrevDeaths/PlannedDeaths); =EXP(-2*CritValue/SQRT(Deaths))" .

hemo:7004843 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ASP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000752 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`alpha_spend_look <- function(d_k, d_prev, D, alpha, c_k) { of <- function(t) ifelse(t == 0, 0, 2-2*pnorm(qnorm(1-alpha/2)/sqrt(t))); po <- function(t) alpha*log(1+(exp(1)-1)*t); t_k <- d_k/D; t_prev <- d_prev/D; data.frame(t_k = t_k, alpha_OF_k = of(t_k), alpha_P_k = po(t_k), inc_OF_k = of(t_k)-of(t_prev), inc_P_k = po(t_k)-po(t_prev), HR_k = exp(-2*c_k/sqrt(d_k))) }` Base R only; vectorised, so vectors of deaths and critical values give one row per look. Exact boundaries for later looks come from gsDesign, for example gsDesign(k = 4, test.type = 1, sfu = sfLDOF)." ;
    hemo:9000024 "alpha_spend_look <- function(d_k, d_prev, D, alpha, c_k) { of <- function(t) ifelse(t == 0, 0, 2-2*pnorm(qnorm(1-alpha/2)/sqrt(t))); po <- function(t) alpha*log(1+(exp(1)-1)*t); t_k <- d_k/D; t_prev <- d_prev/D; data.frame(t_k = t_k, alpha_OF_k = of(t_k), alpha_P_k = po(t_k), inc_OF_k = of(t_k)-of(t_prev), inc_P_k = po(t_k)-po(t_prev), HR_k = exp(-2*c_k/sqrt(d_k))) }" .

hemo:7004844 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ASP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000752 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def alpha_spend_look(d_k, d_prev, D, alpha, c_k): N = NormalDist(); z = N.inv_cdf(1-alpha/2); of = lambda t: 0.0 if t == 0 else 2-2*N.cdf(z/math.sqrt(t)); po = lambda t: alpha*math.log(1+(math.e-1)*t); t_k, t_prev = d_k/D, d_prev/D; return t_k, of(t_k), po(t_k), of(t_k)-of(t_prev), po(t_k)-po(t_prev), math.exp(-2*c_k/math.sqrt(d_k))` Needs import math and from statistics import NormalDist (standard library). Returns t_k, alpha_OF_k, alpha_P_k, inc_OF_k, inc_P_k and HR_k for one look." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def alpha_spend_look(d_k, d_prev, D, alpha, c_k): N = NormalDist(); z = N.inv_cdf(1-alpha/2); of = lambda t: 0.0 if t == 0 else 2-2*N.cdf(z/math.sqrt(t)); po = lambda t: alpha*math.log(1+(math.e-1)*t); t_k, t_prev = d_k/D, d_prev/D; return t_k, of(t_k), po(t_k), of(t_k)-of(t_prev), po(t_k)-po(t_prev), math.exp(-2*c_k/math.sqrt(d_k))" .

hemo:7004847 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Therapy price to annuity instalment, nominal total and present value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Instalments are equal, fixed and paid at the start of each year, the first at treatment, and every instalment is paid once the patient is treated. For an outcomes-based schedule the present value is replaced by the expected present value HE-FM-AP-003." .

hemo:7004851 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-AP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000753 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=PMT(FinRate,NInstal,-Price,0,1); =NInstal*Instalment; =PV(DiscRate,NInstal,-Instalment,0,1)` Three cells that return A, T_nom and PV, with the instalment cell named Instalment. With a type argument of 1, PMT and PV place payments at the start of each year, and they return P divided by n and n times A when a rate is zero." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=PMT(FinRate,NInstal,-Price,0,1); =NInstal*Instalment; =PV(DiscRate,NInstal,-Instalment,0,1)" .

hemo:7004852 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-AP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000753 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`annuity_schedule <- function(P, n, i, r) { A <- if (i == 0) P / n else P * i / ((1-(1+i)^(-n)) * (1+i)); pv <- if (r == 0) n * A else A * (1-(1+r)^(-n)) * (1+r) / r; c(instalment = A, nominal_total = n * A, present_value = pv) }` Returns a named vector, with separate branches for zero rates." ;
    hemo:9000024 "annuity_schedule <- function(P, n, i, r) { A <- if (i == 0) P / n else P * i / ((1-(1+i)^(-n)) * (1+i)); pv <- if (r == 0) n * A else A * (1-(1+r)^(-n)) * (1+r) / r; c(instalment = A, nominal_total = n * A, present_value = pv) }" .

hemo:7004853 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-AP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000753 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def annuity_schedule(P, n, i, r): A = P / n if i == 0 else P * i / ((1-(1+i)**(-n)) * (1+i)); pv = n * A if r == 0 else A * (1-(1+r)**(-n)) * (1+r) / r; return A, n * A, pv` Returns the instalment, the nominal total and the present value as a tuple, with no imports needed." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def annuity_schedule(P, n, i, r): A = P / n if i == 0 else P * i / ((1-(1+i)**(-n)) * (1+i)); pv = n * A if r == 0 else A * (1-(1+r)**(-n)) * (1+r) / r; return A, n * A, pv" .

hemo:7004856 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Delta-method, Fieller and net benefit intervals from trial summary statistics"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The cost and effect differences are approximately jointly normal and come from the same patients, so their covariance is estimated from the trial, and the threshold is fixed. The three intervals describe sampling uncertainty in one dataset only." .

hemo:7004859 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-AN-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000754 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=IF(QuadA>0,(-QuadB-SQRT(QuadB^2-4*QuadA*QuadC))/(2*QuadA),IF(QuadB^2-4*QuadA*QuadC>0,\"two unbounded pieces\",\"whole line\"))` Returns the lower Fieller limit from the helper cells QuadA, QuadB and QuadC of HE-FM-AN-003, or a description of the confidence set when QuadA is not positive. The other outputs use the Excel formulas under HE-FM-AN-002 and HE-FM-AN-004." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(QuadA>0,(-QuadB-SQRT(QuadB^2-4*QuadA*QuadC))/(2*QuadA),IF(QuadB^2-4*QuadA*QuadC>0,\"two unbounded pieces\",\"whole line\"))" .

hemo:7004860 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-AN-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000754 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`an_intervals <- function(dC, dE, seC, seE, lambda, covCE=0, z=1.96) { r <- dC/dE; seR <- abs(r)*sqrt(seC^2/dC^2+seE^2/dE^2-2*covCE/(dC*dE)); a <- dE^2-z^2*seE^2; b <- -2*(dC*dE-z^2*covCE); cc <- dC^2-z^2*seC^2; disc <- b^2-4*a*cc; roots <- if (disc >= 0) sort((-b+c(-1,1)*sqrt(disc))/(2*a)) else c(NA,NA); fieller <- if (a > 0) roots else if (disc > 0) c(-Inf,roots[1],roots[2],Inf) else c(-Inf,Inf); inb <- lambda*dE-dC; seN <- sqrt(lambda^2*seE^2+seC^2-2*lambda*covCE); list(delta=r+c(-1,1)*z*seR, fieller=fieller, inb=inb+c(-1,1)*z*seN, p_pos=pnorm(inb/seN)) }` Returns the delta-method and net benefit limits, the probability that net benefit is positive and the Fieller set: two limits when it is bounded, four values marking two unbounded pieces, or minus to plus infinity for the whole line." ;
    hemo:9000024 "an_intervals <- function(dC, dE, seC, seE, lambda, covCE=0, z=1.96) { r <- dC/dE; seR <- abs(r)*sqrt(seC^2/dC^2+seE^2/dE^2-2*covCE/(dC*dE)); a <- dE^2-z^2*seE^2; b <- -2*(dC*dE-z^2*covCE); cc <- dC^2-z^2*seC^2; disc <- b^2-4*a*cc; roots <- if (disc >= 0) sort((-b+c(-1,1)*sqrt(disc))/(2*a)) else c(NA,NA); fieller <- if (a > 0) roots else if (disc > 0) c(-Inf,roots[1],roots[2],Inf) else c(-Inf,Inf); inb <- lambda*dE-dC; seN <- sqrt(lambda^2*seE^2+seC^2-2*lambda*covCE); list(delta=r+c(-1,1)*z*seR, fieller=fieller, inb=inb+c(-1,1)*z*seN, p_pos=pnorm(inb/seN)) }" .

hemo:7004861 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-AN-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000754 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def an_intervals(dC, dE, seC, seE, lam, covCE=0.0, z=1.96): r = dC/dE; seR = abs(r)*math.sqrt(seC**2/dC**2+seE**2/dE**2-2*covCE/(dC*dE)); a = dE**2-z**2*seE**2; b = -2*(dC*dE-z**2*covCE); c = dC**2-z**2*seC**2; disc = b*b-4*a*c; roots = sorted([(-b-math.sqrt(disc))/(2*a), (-b+math.sqrt(disc))/(2*a)]) if disc >= 0 else None; inb = lam*dE-dC; seN = math.sqrt(lam**2*seE**2+seC**2-2*lam*covCE); return {'delta': (r-z*seR, r+z*seR), 'fieller': roots if a > 0 else (('outside', roots) if roots else 'whole line'), 'inb': (inb-z*seN, inb+z*seN), 'p_pos': statistics.NormalDist().cdf(inb/seN)}` Requires the math and statistics modules; the Fieller entry is the bounded interval, the two roots outside which the set lies, or the whole line." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def an_intervals(dC, dE, seC, seE, lam, covCE=0.0, z=1.96): r = dC/dE; seR = abs(r)*math.sqrt(seC**2/dC**2+seE**2/dE**2-2*covCE/(dC*dE)); a = dE**2-z**2*seE**2; b = -2*(dC*dE-z**2*covCE); c = dC**2-z**2*seC**2; disc = b*b-4*a*c; roots = sorted([(-b-math.sqrt(disc))/(2*a), (-b+math.sqrt(disc))/(2*a)]) if disc >= 0 else None; inb = lam*dE-dC; seN = math.sqrt(lam**2*seE**2+seC**2-2*lam*covCE); return {'delta': (r-z*seR, r+z*seR), 'fieller': roots if a > 0 else (('outside', roots) if roots else 'whole line'), 'inb': (inb-z*seN, inb+z*seN), 'p_pos': statistics.NormalDist().cdf(inb/seN)}" .

hemo:7004864 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Annual event rate to a model-cycle transition probability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The event rate is constant across the year, so every cycle within the year has the same probability, and a month counts as one twelfth of a year. Where the rate changes with age or time since treatment, the function is applied cycle by cycle with the rate for that period." .

hemo:7004868 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-TP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000755 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=1-EXP(-AnnualRate*CycleMonths/12)` With the annual rate in a cell named AnnualRate and the cycle length in months in a cell named CycleMonths, the formula returns the cycle probability." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1-EXP(-AnnualRate*CycleMonths/12)" .

hemo:7004869 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-TP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000755 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`cycle_prob <- function(annual_rate, cycle_months) 1-exp(-annual_rate * cycle_months / 12)` Vectorised, so annual_rate may hold one rate per age band or per cycle and the result is a vector of cycle probabilities." ;
    hemo:9000024 "cycle_prob <- function(annual_rate, cycle_months) 1-exp(-annual_rate * cycle_months / 12)" .

hemo:7004870 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-TP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000755 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def cycle_prob(annual_rate, cycle_months): return 1-math.exp(-annual_rate * cycle_months / 12)` Uses the math module for a single value; replacing math.exp with numpy.exp gives the vectorised form." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def cycle_prob(annual_rate, cycle_months): return 1-math.exp(-annual_rate * cycle_months / 12)" .

hemo:7004873 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Annual probability to a model-cycle transition probability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The annual probability arises from a constant rate within the year, and only one event leads out of the starting state. With competing exits the cause-specific rates are separated first, as in HE-FM-TP-004." .

hemo:7004876 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-TP-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000756 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=1-EXP(LN(1-AnnualProb)*CycleMonths/12)` Both steps in one cell, with the annual probability in AnnualProb and the cycle length in months in CycleMonths. The equivalent form =1-(1-AnnualProb)^(CycleMonths/12) gives the same result." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1-EXP(LN(1-AnnualProb)*CycleMonths/12)" .

hemo:7004877 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-TP-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000756 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`cycle_prob_from_annual <- function(annual_prob, cycle_months) { annual_rate <- -log(1-annual_prob); 1-exp(-annual_rate * cycle_months / 12) }` Two steps that return the cycle probability; the intermediate rate can be kept for reporting." ;
    hemo:9000024 "cycle_prob_from_annual <- function(annual_prob, cycle_months) { annual_rate <- -log(1-annual_prob); 1-exp(-annual_rate * cycle_months / 12) }" .

hemo:7004878 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-TP-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000756 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def cycle_prob_from_annual(annual_prob, cycle_months): annual_rate = -math.log(1-annual_prob); return 1-math.exp(-annual_rate * cycle_months / 12)` Two steps with the math module, where math.log is the natural logarithm." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def cycle_prob_from_annual(annual_prob, cycle_months): annual_rate = -math.log(1-annual_prob); return 1-math.exp(-annual_rate * cycle_months / 12)" .

hemo:7004880 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Atkinson index from subgroup health levels and population shares"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Everyone in a subgroup is given the subgroup's mean health, so inequality within subgroups is ignored and only variation between the subgroups that define unfair inequality counts. Shares sum to 1 after normalisation." .

hemo:7004883 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ATK-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000757 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=1-IF(Epsilon=1,EXP(SUMPRODUCT(Share,LN(Health))/SUM(Share)),(SUMPRODUCT(Share,Health^(1-Epsilon))/SUM(Share))^(1/(1-Epsilon)))/(SUMPRODUCT(Share,Health)/SUM(Share))` With subgroup health in a range named Health, population shares or counts in a range named Share and the parameter in Epsilon, the formula returns the Atkinson index; the part inside IF on its own returns the EDE." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1-IF(Epsilon=1,EXP(SUMPRODUCT(Share,LN(Health))/SUM(Share)),(SUMPRODUCT(Share,Health^(1-Epsilon))/SUM(Share))^(1/(1-Epsilon)))/(SUMPRODUCT(Share,Health)/SUM(Share))" .

hemo:7004884 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ATK-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000757 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`atkinson_groups <- function(h, s, eps) { s <- s/sum(s); m <- sum(s*h); ede <- if (eps == 1) exp(sum(s*log(h))) else sum(s*h^(1-eps))^(1/(1-eps)); c(mean = m, ede = ede, index = 1-ede/m) }` Returns the mean, the EDE and the index; applying it over a vector of epsilon values with sapply gives the table across a range of aversion." ;
    hemo:9000024 "atkinson_groups <- function(h, s, eps) { s <- s/sum(s); m <- sum(s*h); ede <- if (eps == 1) exp(sum(s*log(h))) else sum(s*h^(1-eps))^(1/(1-eps)); c(mean = m, ede = ede, index = 1-ede/m) }" .

hemo:7004885 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ATK-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000757 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def atkinson_groups(h, s, eps): t = sum(s); m = sum(si*hi for si, hi in zip(s, h))/t; ede = math.exp(sum(si*math.log(hi) for si, hi in zip(s, h))/t) if eps == 1 else (sum(si*hi**(1-eps) for si, hi in zip(s, h))/t)**(1/(1-eps)); return m, ede, 1-ede/m` Uses the math module, where math.log is the natural logarithm." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def atkinson_groups(h, s, eps): t = sum(s); m = sum(si*hi for si, hi in zip(s, h))/t; ede = math.exp(sum(si*math.log(hi) for si, hi in zip(s, h))/t) if eps == 1 else (sum(si*hi**(1-eps) for si, hi in zip(s, h))/t)**(1/(1-eps)); return m, ede, 1-ede/m" .

hemo:7004888 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of AUC from predicted risks and observed outcomes"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The outcome is binary and observed for everyone, with no censoring, and each person contributes one score. Pairs within the same outcome group are not compared." .

hemo:7004892 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-AUC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000758 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=SUMPRODUCT((Outcome=1)*(COUNTIFS(Outcome,0,Score,\"<\"&Score)+0.5*COUNTIFS(Outcome,0,Score,Score)))/(COUNTIF(Outcome,1)*COUNTIF(Outcome,0))` With the scores in a range named Score and the outcomes, coded 1 or 0, in a range of the same length named Outcome, the formula counts for each person with the outcome the non-cases with a lower score plus half those with the same score, and divides by the number of pairs." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT((Outcome=1)*(COUNTIFS(Outcome,0,Score,\"<\"&Score)+0.5*COUNTIFS(Outcome,0,Score,Score)))/(COUNTIF(Outcome,1)*COUNTIF(Outcome,0))" .

hemo:7004893 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-AUC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000758 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`auc_pairs <- function(s, y) { d <- outer(s[y == 1], s[y == 0], \"-\"); (sum(d > 0) + 0.5 * sum(d == 0)) / length(d) }` The outer function forms the n_1 by n_0 matrix of score differences, so length(d) is the number of pairs. Memory grows with n_1 times n_0, so very large samples use the equivalent Wilcoxon rank form instead." ;
    hemo:9000024 "auc_pairs <- function(s, y) { d <- outer(s[y == 1], s[y == 0], \"-\"); (sum(d > 0) + 0.5 * sum(d == 0)) / length(d) }" .

hemo:7004894 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-AUC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000758 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def auc_pairs(s, y): cases = [a for a, o in zip(s, y) if o == 1]; controls = [b for b, o in zip(s, y) if o == 0]; return sum((a > b) + 0.5 * (a == b) for a in cases for b in controls) / (len(cases) * len(controls))` Standard library only; each comparison adds as 0 or 1." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def auc_pairs(s, y): cases = [a for a, o in zip(s, y) if o == 1]; controls = [b for b, o in zip(s, y) if o == 0]; return sum((a > b) + 0.5 * (a == b) for a in cases for b in controls) / (len(cases) * len(controls))" .

hemo:7004897 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of DerSimonian and Laird meta-analysis from study estimates and standard errors"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each estimate is approximately normal on the pooling scale with a known variance equal to the square of its standard error, and the studies estimate different but related effects. I-squared is returned as 0 when Q does not exceed k-1, which also avoids a division by zero when Q is 0. The confidence interval uses 1.96 and treats tau2 as known; the prediction interval in HE-FM-ADMA-006 needs a t quantile." .

hemo:7004900 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ADMA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000759 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=SUMPRODUCT(B2:B4/C2:C4^2)/SUMPRODUCT(1/C2:C4^2)` in F2, `=SQRT(1/SUMPRODUCT(1/C2:C4^2))` in F3, `=SUMPRODUCT((B2:B4-F2)^2/C2:C4^2)` in F4, `=IF(F4>COUNT(B2:B4)-1,(F4-(COUNT(B2:B4)-1))/F4*100,0)` in F5, `=MAX(0,(F4-(COUNT(B2:B4)-1))/(SUMPRODUCT(1/C2:C4^2)-SUMPRODUCT(1/C2:C4^4)/SUMPRODUCT(1/C2:C4^2)))` in F6, `=SUMPRODUCT(B2:B4/(C2:C4^2+F6))/SUMPRODUCT(1/(C2:C4^2+F6))` in F7, `=SQRT(1/SUMPRODUCT(1/(C2:C4^2+F6)))` in F8, `=F7-1.96*F8` in F9 and `=F7+1.96*F8` in F10. With one study per row, estimates in B2:B4 and standard errors in C2:C4, cells F2 to F10 return theta_F, SE_F, Q, I2, tau2, theta_R, SE_R and the two confidence limits; extending the ranges adds studies." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(B2:B4/C2:C4^2)/SUMPRODUCT(1/C2:C4^2)" .

hemo:7004901 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ADMA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000759 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`dl_meta <- function(y, se) { v <- se^2; w <- 1/v; k <- length(y); th_f <- sum(w*y)/sum(w); q <- sum(w*(y-th_f)^2); i2 <- if (q > k-1) 100*(q-(k-1))/q else 0; tau2 <- max(0, (q-(k-1))/(sum(w)-sum(w^2)/sum(w))); ws <- 1/(v+tau2); th_r <- sum(ws*y)/sum(ws); se_r <- sqrt(1/sum(ws)); c(theta_F = th_f, SE_F = sqrt(1/sum(w)), Q = q, I2 = i2, tau2 = tau2, theta_R = th_r, SE_R = se_r, CI_low_R = th_r-1.96*se_r, CI_high_R = th_r+1.96*se_r) }` Vectorised over studies; `dl_meta(c(-0.60, -0.05, -0.40), c(0.20, 0.10, 0.25))` reproduces the article's example, and exp() of the estimates and limits gives hazard ratios." ;
    hemo:9000024 "dl_meta <- function(y, se) { v <- se^2; w <- 1/v; k <- length(y); th_f <- sum(w*y)/sum(w); q <- sum(w*(y-th_f)^2); i2 <- if (q > k-1) 100*(q-(k-1))/q else 0; tau2 <- max(0, (q-(k-1))/(sum(w)-sum(w^2)/sum(w))); ws <- 1/(v+tau2); th_r <- sum(ws*y)/sum(ws); se_r <- sqrt(1/sum(ws)); c(theta_F = th_f, SE_F = sqrt(1/sum(w)), Q = q, I2 = i2, tau2 = tau2, theta_R = th_r, SE_R = se_r, CI_low_R = th_r-1.96*se_r, CI_high_R = th_r+1.96*se_r) }" .

hemo:7004902 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ADMA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000759 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def dl_meta(y, se): v = [s**2 for s in se]; w = [1/x for x in v]; k = len(y); sw = sum(w); th_f = sum(a*b for a, b in zip(w, y))/sw; q = sum(a*(b-th_f)**2 for a, b in zip(w, y)); i2 = 100*(q-(k-1))/q if q > k-1 else 0.0; tau2 = max(0.0, (q-(k-1))/(sw-sum(a*a for a in w)/sw)); ws = [1/(x+tau2) for x in v]; th_r = sum(a*b for a, b in zip(ws, y))/sum(ws); se_r = math.sqrt(1/sum(ws)); return th_f, math.sqrt(1/sw), q, i2, tau2, th_r, se_r, th_r-1.96*se_r, th_r+1.96*se_r` Uses the math module and plain lists, and returns the nine outputs in the order listed above." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def dl_meta(y, se): v = [s**2 for s in se]; w = [1/x for x in v]; k = len(y); sw = sum(w); th_f = sum(a*b for a, b in zip(w, y))/sw; q = sum(a*(b-th_f)**2 for a, b in zip(w, y)); i2 = 100*(q-(k-1))/q if q > k-1 else 0.0; tau2 = max(0.0, (q-(k-1))/(sw-sum(a*a for a in w)/sw)); ws = [1/(x+tau2) for x in v]; th_r = sum(a*b for a, b in zip(ws, y))/sum(ws); se_r = math.sqrt(1/sum(ws)); return th_f, math.sqrt(1/sw), q, i2, tau2, th_r, se_r, th_r-1.96*se_r, th_r+1.96*se_r" .

hemo:7004905 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of AR(1) QALY variance and between-arm standard error for any visit schedule"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Utilities share a common variance at every visit, the correlation depends only on elapsed time, and patients are independent with complete utility data. The standard error assumes equal variances and equal arm sizes, as in the article's example. phi has to be above zero because elapsed times can be fractions of a year." .

hemo:7004909 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-AR1-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000760 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=Sigma2*SUMPRODUCT(B2:B4*TRANSPOSE(B2:B4)*Phi^ABS(A2:A4-TRANSPOSE(A2:A4)))` With visit times in years in A2:A4, weights in B2:B4 from `=(A3-A2)/2` in B2, `=(A4-A2)/2` in B3 and `=(A4-A3)/2` in B4, and named cells Sigma2 and Phi, the formula returns Var_Q in a cell named VarQ; `=SQRT(2*VarQ/PatientsPerArm)` returns SE. It needs dynamic-array Excel (Microsoft 365 or Excel 2021); earlier versions need Ctrl+Shift+Enter. Extending the ranges handles more visits, with each middle weight equal to half the distance between its neighbours." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=Sigma2*SUMPRODUCT(B2:B4*TRANSPOSE(B2:B4)*Phi^ABS(A2:A4-TRANSPOSE(A2:A4)))" .

hemo:7004910 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-AR1-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000760 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`qaly_var_ar1 <- function(t, sigma2, phi, n) { k <- length(t); w <- (c(t[-1], t[k])-c(t[1], t[-k]))/2; v <- sigma2 * sum(outer(w, w) * phi^abs(outer(t, t, \"-\"))); c(var_q = v, se = sqrt(2*v/n)) }` `qaly_var_ar1(c(0, 0.5, 1), 0.04, 0.49, 100)` returns 0.03145 and about 0.02508." ;
    hemo:9000024 "qaly_var_ar1 <- function(t, sigma2, phi, n) { k <- length(t); w <- (c(t[-1], t[k])-c(t[1], t[-k]))/2; v <- sigma2 * sum(outer(w, w) * phi^abs(outer(t, t, \"-\"))); c(var_q = v, se = sqrt(2*v/n)) }" .

hemo:7004911 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-AR1-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000760 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def qaly_var_ar1(t, sigma2, phi, n): k = len(t); w = [(t[min(j+1, k-1)]-t[max(j-1, 0)])/2 for j in range(k)]; v = sigma2*sum(w[a]*w[b]*phi**abs(t[a]-t[b]) for a in range(k) for b in range(k)); return w, v, math.sqrt(2*v/n)` Uses the math module and returns the weights, Var_Q and SE." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def qaly_var_ar1(t, sigma2, phi, n): k = len(t); w = [(t[min(j+1, k-1)]-t[max(j-1, 0)])/2 for j in range(k)]; v = sigma2*sum(w[a]*w[b]*phi**abs(t[a]-t[b]) for a in range(k) for b in range(k)); return w, v, math.sqrt(2*v/n)" .

hemo:7004914 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Average length of stay from admission and discharge dates"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "A stay is the unit stated for the analysis (episode, provider spell or continuous inpatient spell), and its dates have already been linked on that basis. No leave days or other deductions apply; where a convention deducts days, as the NHS Payment Scheme and the Australian definition do, the deduction is made to L_i before averaging." .

hemo:7004917 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ALOS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000761 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=SUMPRODUCT((DischDates>=PeriodStart)*(DischDates<=PeriodEnd)*(DischDates-AdmitDates))/SUMPRODUCT((DischDates>=PeriodStart)*(DischDates<=PeriodEnd))` With admission and discharge dates in equal-sized ranges named AdmitDates and DischDates, and the period limits in PeriodStart and PeriodEnd, the formula returns the ALOS. Excel stores dates as day numbers, so a subtraction gives days." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT((DischDates>=PeriodStart)*(DischDates<=PeriodEnd)*(DischDates-AdmitDates))/SUMPRODUCT((DischDates>=PeriodStart)*(DischDates<=PeriodEnd))" .

hemo:7004918 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ALOS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000761 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`alos <- function(admit, disch, p_start, p_end) { keep <- disch >= p_start & disch <= p_end; mean(as.numeric(disch[keep]-admit[keep])) }` Takes vectors of class Date; as.numeric turns the date differences into days." ;
    hemo:9000024 "alos <- function(admit, disch, p_start, p_end) { keep <- disch >= p_start & disch <= p_end; mean(as.numeric(disch[keep]-admit[keep])) }" .

hemo:7004919 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ALOS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000761 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def alos(admit, disch, p_start, p_end): stays = [(d-a).days for a, d in zip(admit, disch) if p_start <= d <= p_end]; return sum(stays) / len(stays)` Takes lists of datetime.date values; the .days attribute gives the length of each stay." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def alos(admit, disch, p_start, p_end): stays = [(d-a).days for a, d in zip(admit, disch) if p_start <= d <= p_end]; return sum(stays) / len(stays)" .

hemo:7004922 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Hourly arrival overflow probability and expected cover cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Arrivals within each period follow a Poisson process with a constant hourly rate, each hour is treated separately and backlog carried from one hour to the next is ignored, which a full simulation would capture. Adding backlog would be expected to widen the gap between steady and peaked demand, because queues build up during the peak." .

hemo:7004926 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ARRP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000762 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=CostPerHour*HoursAtRate*(1-POISSON.DIST(Capacity,HourlyRate,TRUE))` With named cells HourlyRate, Capacity, HoursAtRate and CostPerHour, the formula returns the expected overflow cost for one rate period; one row per period and a SUM give the daily total." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CostPerHour*HoursAtRate*(1-POISSON.DIST(Capacity,HourlyRate,TRUE))" .

hemo:7004927 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ARRP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000762 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`overflow_cost <- function(rate, cap, hours, cost) cost * hours * ppois(cap, rate, lower.tail = FALSE)` Vectorised over rate and hours, so `sum(overflow_cost(c(3, 1), 4, c(12, 12), 150))` returns the Model B daily cost of about 339.11." ;
    hemo:9000024 "overflow_cost <- function(rate, cap, hours, cost) cost * hours * ppois(cap, rate, lower.tail = FALSE)" .

hemo:7004928 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ARRP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000762 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def overflow_cost(rate, cap, hours, cost): return cost * hours * (1-sum(rate**n * math.exp(-rate) / math.factorial(n) for n in range(cap + 1)))` Uses the math module; `scipy.stats.poisson.sf(cap, rate)` gives the same overflow probability." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def overflow_cost(rate, cap, hours, cost): return cost * hours * (1-sum(rate**n * math.exp(-rate) / math.factorial(n) for n in range(cap + 1)))" .

hemo:7004931 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Antithetic pairs of exponential survival times with a pair-mean standard error"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Survival times are exponential with a constant hazard and are sampled by the inverse distribution function, so U and 1-U give mirrored quantiles. Each patient has its own random number, used for the same purpose in both runs. Survival time falls as U rises, so it is monotone in U and the within-pair correlation is negative." .

hemo:7004935 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ANTV-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000763 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=-LN(A2)/Hazard` in B2, `=-LN(1-A2)/Hazard` in C2 and `=(B2+C2)/2` in D2, filled down to row 501, with `=RAND()` in A2:A501 and the hazard in a cell named Hazard. `=AVERAGE(D2:D501)` returns mu_anti and `=STDEV.S(D2:D501)/SQRT(COUNT(D2:D501))` returns SE_anti for 500 pairs. Pasting the uniform numbers as values fixes the draws so that the run can be reproduced." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=-LN(A2)/Hazard" .

hemo:7004936 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ANTV-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000763 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`anti_exp <- function(u, h) { t1 <- -log(u)/h; t2 <- -log(1-u)/h; pm <- (t1+t2)/2; c(mu_anti = mean(pm), se_anti = sd(pm)/sqrt(length(pm))) }` `anti_exp(c(0.20, 0.90), 0.1)` returns about 10.601 and 1.438, and `anti_exp(runif(500), 0.1)` runs 500 pairs." ;
    hemo:9000024 "anti_exp <- function(u, h) { t1 <- -log(u)/h; t2 <- -log(1-u)/h; pm <- (t1+t2)/2; c(mu_anti = mean(pm), se_anti = sd(pm)/sqrt(length(pm))) }" .

hemo:7004937 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ANTV-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000763 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def anti_exp(u, h): pm = [(-math.log(x)/h-math.log(1-x)/h)/2 for x in u]; mu = sum(pm)/len(pm); return pm, mu, statistics.stdev(pm)/math.sqrt(len(pm))` Uses the math and statistics modules, returns the pair means, the estimate and its standard error, and needs at least two pairs." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def anti_exp(u, h): pm = [(-math.log(x)/h-math.log(1-x)/h)/2 for x in u]; mu = sum(pm)/len(pm); return pm, mu, statistics.stdev(pm)/math.sqrt(len(pm))" .

hemo:7004941 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Final attack rate by iteration and infections averted by pre-epidemic vaccination"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The final size relation applies (HE-AS-ATR-009): a large closed population, lasting immunity, a negligible seed and homogeneous mixing. Everyone is susceptible before the programme, vaccination is completed before the epidemic starts and the vaccine gives complete protection, so s_0 = 1 minus V. When R_0 s_0 is 1 or less the epidemic cannot grow and the function returns a final attack rate of 0." .

hemo:7004944 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ATR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000764 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=IF(R0Value*(1-Coverage)<=1,0,REDUCE(1-Coverage,SEQUENCE(2000),LAMBDA(z,k,(1-Coverage)*(1-EXP(-R0Value*z)))))` Returns z_V in Excel 365 from named cells R0Value and Coverage; with Coverage set to 0 the same formula returns z_0. Infections averted then follow from `=Population*(FinalSizeNone-FinalSizeProgramme)`." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(R0Value*(1-Coverage)<=1,0,REDUCE(1-Coverage,SEQUENCE(2000),LAMBDA(z,k,(1-Coverage)*(1-EXP(-R0Value*z)))))" .

hemo:7004945 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ATR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000764 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`final_size <- function(R0, s0, n = 2000) if (R0*s0 <= 1) 0 else Reduce(function(z, k) s0*(1-exp(-R0*z)), seq_len(n), s0)` and `averted <- function(R0, V, N) N*(final_size(R0, 1)-final_size(R0, 1-V))` Two thousand updates are ample unless R_0 s_0 is very close to 1, where the iteration slows and more updates are needed." ;
    hemo:9000024 "final_size <- function(R0, s0, n = 2000) if (R0*s0 <= 1) 0 else Reduce(function(z, k) s0*(1-exp(-R0*z)), seq_len(n), s0)" .

hemo:7004946 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ATR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000764 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def final_size(R0, s0, n=2000): return 0.0 if R0*s0 <= 1 else functools.reduce(lambda z, k: s0*(1-math.exp(-R0*z)), range(n), s0)` and `def averted(R0, V, N): return N*(final_size(R0, 1)-final_size(R0, 1-V))` Uses the functools and math modules." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def final_size(R0, s0, n=2000): return 0.0 if R0*s0 <= 1 else functools.reduce(lambda z, k: s0*(1-math.exp(-R0*z)), range(n), s0)" .

hemo:7004950 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Plan design and spending distribution to actuarial value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Everyone in a group has the same allowed cost, the plan has one deductible, one coinsurance rate and one out-of-pocket maximum with no copayments, and spending does not change with the design. The federal calculator instead uses finer spending buckets and metal-level continuance tables that allow for induced demand." .

hemo:7004954 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-AV-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000765 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=LET(t,Allowed-(1-Coins)*(Allowed>Deductible)*(Allowed-Deductible),s,t-(t>OOPMax)*(t-OOPMax),SUMPRODUCT(People,Allowed-s)/SUMPRODUCT(People,Allowed))` With the people per group in People and the allowed cost per person in Allowed (ranges of the same size) and the plan terms in Deductible, Coins and OOPMax, the formula returns the actuarial value in Excel 2021 or Microsoft 365. Inside LET, t is the deductible plus coinsurance before the cap and s is the capped member payment for each group." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LET(t,Allowed-(1-Coins)*(Allowed>Deductible)*(Allowed-Deductible),s,t-(t>OOPMax)*(t-OOPMax),SUMPRODUCT(People,Allowed-s)/SUMPRODUCT(People,Allowed))" .

hemo:7004955 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-AV-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000765 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`actuarial_value <- function(n, x, D, c, M) { s <- pmin(M, pmin(x, D) + c*pmax(0, x-D)); sum(n*(x-s)) / sum(n*x) }` Vectorised over the groups with pmin and pmax; n and x are numeric vectors of the same length." ;
    hemo:9000024 "actuarial_value <- function(n, x, D, c, M) { s <- pmin(M, pmin(x, D) + c*pmax(0, x-D)); sum(n*(x-s)) / sum(n*x) }" .

hemo:7004956 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-AV-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000765 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def actuarial_value(n, x, D, c, M): s = [min(M, min(xi, D) + c*max(0, xi-D)) for xi in x]; return sum(ni*(xi-si) for ni, xi, si in zip(n, x, s)) / sum(ni*xi for ni, xi in zip(n, x))` Takes lists of group sizes and allowed costs and needs no imports." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def actuarial_value(n, x, D, c, M): s = [min(M, min(xi, D) + c*max(0, xi-D)) for xi in x]; return sum(ni*(xi-si) for ni, xi, si in zip(n, x, s)) / sum(ni*xi for ni, xi in zip(n, x))" .

hemo:7004959 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Group size and attachment factor to expected aggregate stop-loss payment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Members' net claims are independent with a common mean and standard deviation, total claims are approximately normal, and the policy has no maximum aggregate payment. Skewed claims and common shocks make the result understate the expected payment, most of all for small groups." .

hemo:7004963 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ASL-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000766 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=LET(es,Members*MeanMember,sdv,SDMember*SQRT(Members),zv,(AttachFactor*es-es)/sdv,sdv*(NORM.S.DIST(zv,FALSE)-zv*(1-NORM.S.DIST(zv,TRUE))))` With named cells Members, MeanMember, SDMember and AttachFactor, the formula returns the expected payment in Excel 2021 or Microsoft 365; dividing by Members*MeanMember gives the share." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LET(es,Members*MeanMember,sdv,SDMember*SQRT(Members),zv,(AttachFactor*es-es)/sdv,sdv*(NORM.S.DIST(zv,FALSE)-zv*(1-NORM.S.DIST(zv,TRUE))))" .

hemo:7004964 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ASL-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000766 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`agg_sl_expected <- function(n, mu, sigma, a) { es <- n*mu; sdv <- sigma*sqrt(n); z <- (a*es-es)/sdv; sdv*(dnorm(z)-z*(1-pnorm(z))) }` Uses dnorm and pnorm from base R and is vectorised over n, so one call covers several group sizes." ;
    hemo:9000024 "agg_sl_expected <- function(n, mu, sigma, a) { es <- n*mu; sdv <- sigma*sqrt(n); z <- (a*es-es)/sdv; sdv*(dnorm(z)-z*(1-pnorm(z))) }" .

hemo:7004965 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ASL-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000766 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def agg_sl_expected(n, mu, sigma, a): es = n*mu; sdv = sigma*math.sqrt(n); z = (a*es-es)/sdv; nd = statistics.NormalDist(); return sdv*(nd.pdf(z)-z*(1-nd.cdf(z)))` Needs the standard library modules math and statistics only." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def agg_sl_expected(n, mu, sigma, a): es = n*mu; sdv = sigma*math.sqrt(n); z = (a*es-es)/sdv; nd = statistics.NormalDist(); return sdv*(nd.pdf(z)-z*(1-nd.cdf(z)))" .

hemo:7004968 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Insurer annual figures to medical loss ratio rebate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "One year of fully credible experience, with no risk adjustment, risk corridor, reinsurance or fraud recovery adjustment. The reported ratio aggregates three years of data under 45 CFR 158.220 and adds a credibility adjustment where one is due, which this function leaves out." .

hemo:7004972 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ACR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000767 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=LET(m,ROUND((Claims+QualityImp)/(Premium-Taxes),3),MAX(0,(Premium-Taxes)*(Standard-m)))` With the reported items in named cells Premium, Taxes, Claims and QualityImp and the market standard in Standard, the formula returns the rebate in Excel 2021 or Microsoft 365. Inside LET, m is the rounded ratio, which can also be shown in its own cell with the ROUND part alone." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LET(m,ROUND((Claims+QualityImp)/(Premium-Taxes),3),MAX(0,(Premium-Taxes)*(Standard-m)))" .

hemo:7004973 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ACR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000767 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`mlr_rebate <- function(premium, taxes, claims, qi, standard) { m <- round((claims + qi) / (premium-taxes), 3); c(mlr = m, rebate = max(0, (premium-taxes) * (standard-m))) }` Returns the rounded ratio and the rebate for one issuer; pmax in place of max vectorises it across issuers." ;
    hemo:9000024 "mlr_rebate <- function(premium, taxes, claims, qi, standard) { m <- round((claims + qi) / (premium-taxes), 3); c(mlr = m, rebate = max(0, (premium-taxes) * (standard-m))) }" .

hemo:7004974 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ACR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000767 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def mlr_rebate(premium, taxes, claims, qi, standard): m = round((claims + qi) / (premium-taxes), 3); return m, max(0, (premium-taxes) * (standard-m))` Returns the rounded ratio and the rebate and needs no imports." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def mlr_rebate(premium, taxes, claims, qi, standard): m = round((claims + qi) / (premium-taxes), 3); return m, max(0, (premium-taxes) * (standard-m))" .

hemo:7004977 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of New benefit costing from base use to gross premium in actuarial analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each member uses either no unit or one unit of the benefit in the year, with probability f, so f is at most 1 and sigma follows the two-point formula. Members' costs are independent, and the loadings are defined as in HE-FM-ACTA-002. For benefits used more than once a year, sigma is estimated from claims data and passed to HE-FM-ACTA-003 directly." .

hemo:7004981 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ACTA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000768 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=(BaseRate/1000*(1+Uplift)*Allowed*(1-Coins)+FixedLoad)/(1-PropLoad)` Returns the gross premium in one cell from named cells BaseRate, Uplift, Allowed, Coins, FixedLoad and PropLoad. The net premium is `=BaseRate/1000*(1+Uplift)*Allowed*(1-Coins)`, and the pooled standard deviation is `=Allowed*(1-Coins)*SQRT(UseRate*(1-UseRate))/SQRT(Members)` with the per-member rate in UseRate." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(BaseRate/1000*(1+Uplift)*Allowed*(1-Coins)+FixedLoad)/(1-PropLoad)" .

hemo:7004982 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ACTA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000768 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`cost_benefit <- function(r_base, m, a, c, beta, gamma, n) { f <- r_base / 1000 * (1 + m); u <- a * (1-c); p <- f * u; g <- (p + beta) / (1-gamma); sigma <- u * sqrt(f * (1-f)); list(f = f, u = u, P = p, G = g, T = n * p, R_inc = n * g, sigma = sigma, SD_N = sigma / sqrt(n)) }` Returns a named list; the arithmetic is vectorised, so r_base or m may hold several scenarios at once." ;
    hemo:9000024 "cost_benefit <- function(r_base, m, a, c, beta, gamma, n) { f <- r_base / 1000 * (1 + m); u <- a * (1-c); p <- f * u; g <- (p + beta) / (1-gamma); sigma <- u * sqrt(f * (1-f)); list(f = f, u = u, P = p, G = g, T = n * p, R_inc = n * g, sigma = sigma, SD_N = sigma / sqrt(n)) }" .

hemo:7004983 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ACTA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000768 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def cost_benefit(r_base, m, a, c, beta, gamma, n): f = r_base / 1000 * (1 + m); u = a * (1-c); p = f * u; g = (p + beta) / (1-gamma); sigma = u * math.sqrt(f * (1-f)); return dict(f=f, u=u, P=p, G=g, T=n * p, R_inc=n * g, sigma=sigma, SD_N=sigma / math.sqrt(n))` Uses the math module for single values." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def cost_benefit(r_base, m, a, c, beta, gamma, n): f = r_base / 1000 * (1 + m); u = a * (1-c); p = f * u; g = (p + beta) / (1-gamma); sigma = u * math.sqrt(f * (1-f)); return dict(f=f, u=u, P=p, G=g, T=n * p, R_inc=n * g, sigma=sigma, SD_N=sigma / math.sqrt(n))" .

hemo:7004986 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of IPW incremental net monetary benefit from patient-level data"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The same propensity score weights apply to a patient's cost and QALYs, and ignorability and overlap hold as in HE-FM-ATE-003. The vectors are aligned so that row i of each refers to the same patient." .

hemo:7004989 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ATE-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000769 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=ValuePerQALY*(SUMPRODUCT(Treated,QALYs/Propensity)-SUMPRODUCT(1-Treated,QALYs/(1-Propensity)))/COUNT(Treated)-(SUMPRODUCT(Treated,Costs/Propensity)-SUMPRODUCT(1-Treated,Costs/(1-Propensity)))/COUNT(Treated)` With one row per patient in named ranges Treated, Propensity, Costs and QALYs and the value per QALY in a cell named ValuePerQALY, the formula returns the incremental net monetary benefit; the two bracketed terms divided by COUNT(Treated) are the incremental QALYs and cost." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ValuePerQALY*(SUMPRODUCT(Treated,QALYs/Propensity)-SUMPRODUCT(1-Treated,QALYs/(1-Propensity)))/COUNT(Treated)-(SUMPRODUCT(Treated,Costs/Propensity)-SUMPRODUCT(1-Treated,Costs/(1-Propensity)))/COUNT(Treated)" .

hemo:7004990 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ATE-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000769 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`ipw_inmb <- function(t, e, cost, qaly, lambda) { d_c <- mean(t*cost/e)-mean((1-t)*cost/(1-e)); d_q <- mean(t*qaly/e)-mean((1-t)*qaly/(1-e)); c(d_c = d_c, d_q = d_q, inmb = lambda*d_q-d_c) }` Vectorised over patients; mean() divides each weighted sum by n." ;
    hemo:9000024 "ipw_inmb <- function(t, e, cost, qaly, lambda) { d_c <- mean(t*cost/e)-mean((1-t)*cost/(1-e)); d_q <- mean(t*qaly/e)-mean((1-t)*qaly/(1-e)); c(d_c = d_c, d_q = d_q, inmb = lambda*d_q-d_c) }" .

hemo:7004991 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ATE-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000769 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def ipw_inmb(t, e, cost, qaly, lam): t, e, cost, qaly = (np.asarray(v, dtype=float) for v in (t, e, cost, qaly)); d_c = np.mean(t*cost/e)-np.mean((1-t)*cost/(1-e)); d_q = np.mean(t*qaly/e)-np.mean((1-t)*qaly/(1-e)); return d_c, d_q, lam*d_q-d_c` Uses numpy imported as np; the function returns the incremental cost, incremental QALYs and incremental net monetary benefit." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def ipw_inmb(t, e, cost, qaly, lam): t, e, cost, qaly = (np.asarray(v, dtype=float) for v in (t, e, cost, qaly)); d_c = np.mean(t*cost/e)-np.mean((1-t)*cost/(1-e)); d_q = np.mean(t*qaly/e)-np.mean((1-t)*qaly/(1-e)); return d_c, d_q, lam*d_q-d_c" .

hemo:7004994 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Survival and mean survival from a piecewise-constant bathtub hazard schedule"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The hazard is constant within each interval and the final hazard holds for life. Mean survival is undiscounted, and time runs from the procedure for the whole cohort." .

hemo:7004998 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BTH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000770 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=D2*EXP(-C2*(B2-A2))` and `=IF(B2=\"\",D2/C2,D2*(1-EXP(-C2*(B2-A2)))/C2)` With one interval per row from row 2, start in column A, end in column B (blank for the open interval), hazard in column C and survival at the start in column D (1 in D2, then D3 equal to E2 and so on), the first formula in column E returns survival at the end and the second in column F the life-years; `=SUM(F2:F5)` returns mean survival for four intervals." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=D2*EXP(-C2*(B2-A2))" .

hemo:7004999 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BTH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000770 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`piecewise_surv <- function(cuts, h) { L <- diff(c(0, cuts)); k <- seq_along(L); S <- exp(-cumsum(h[k] * L)); S0 <- c(1, S); A <- c(S0[k] * (1-exp(-h[k] * L)) / h[k], S0[length(S0)] / h[length(h)]); list(S = S, A = A, mean = sum(A)) }` Returns a list with survival at the cut points, the life-years per interval and the mean; `piecewise_surv(c(0.25, 5, 15), c(0.40, 0.05, 0.08, 0.20))$mean` gives 10.578368." ;
    hemo:9000024 "piecewise_surv <- function(cuts, h) { L <- diff(c(0, cuts)); k <- seq_along(L); S <- exp(-cumsum(h[k] * L)); S0 <- c(1, S); A <- c(S0[k] * (1-exp(-h[k] * L)) / h[k], S0[length(S0)] / h[length(h)]); list(S = S, A = A, mean = sum(A)) }" .

hemo:7005000 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BTH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000770 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def piecewise_surv(cuts, h): L = [b-a for a, b in zip([0]+cuts, cuts)]; S = [math.exp(-x) for x in itertools.accumulate(hj*Lj for hj, Lj in zip(h, L))]; S0 = [1]+S; A = [s*(1-math.exp(-hj*Lj))/hj for s, hj, Lj in zip(S0, h, L)]+[S0[-1]/h[-1]]; return S, A, sum(A)` Uses the math and itertools modules and returns survival, life-years and the mean; `piecewise_surv([0.25, 5, 15], [0.40, 0.05, 0.08, 0.20])[2]` gives 10.578368." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def piecewise_surv(cuts, h): L = [b-a for a, b in zip([0]+cuts, cuts)]; S = [math.exp(-x) for x in itertools.accumulate(hj*Lj for hj, Lj in zip(h, L))]; S0 = [1]+S; A = [s*(1-math.exp(-hj*Lj))/hj for s, hj, Lj in zip(S0, h, L)]+[S0[-1]/h[-1]]; return S, A, sum(A)" .

hemo:7005003 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Threshold, patience and outside option to a negotiated price per patient"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "One course per patient, money payoffs that move one for one with the price, complete information and alternating offers with fixed discount factors, with the manufacturer's alternating-offers share used as its Nash bargaining weight, as in the article's worked example. The outside option ends the negotiation for good and leaves a price the manufacturer accepts." .

hemo:7005007 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BARG-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000771 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=MIN(ReservationPrice+(1-PayerDelta)/(1-MfrDelta*PayerDelta)*(MfrFirst+(1-MfrFirst)*MfrDelta)*(Threshold*IncQALYs-OtherIncCost-ReservationPrice),Threshold*IncQALYs-OtherIncCost-PayerOutsideOption)` With named cells for each input and MfrFirst set to 1 or 0, the formula returns the negotiated price; (NegotiatedPrice+OtherIncCost)/IncQALYs then gives the cost per QALY." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MIN(ReservationPrice+(1-PayerDelta)/(1-MfrDelta*PayerDelta)*(MfrFirst+(1-MfrFirst)*MfrDelta)*(Threshold*IncQALYs-OtherIncCost-ReservationPrice),Threshold*IncQALYs-OtherIncCost-PayerOutsideOption)" .

hemo:7005008 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BARG-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000771 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`negotiated_price <- function(k, d_e, d_co, res, delta_m, delta_p, m_first, v_p) { p_bar <- k*d_e-d_co; s_m <- (1-delta_p)/(1-delta_m*delta_p)*(m_first+(1-m_first)*delta_m); p_neg <- pmin(res+s_m*(p_bar-res), p_bar-v_p); data.frame(p_bar = p_bar, s_m = s_m, p_neg = p_neg, icer = (p_neg+d_co)/d_e, nmb_p = k*d_e-d_co-p_neg) }` Vectorised, so a column of thresholds or outside option values returns one row per scenario." ;
    hemo:9000024 "negotiated_price <- function(k, d_e, d_co, res, delta_m, delta_p, m_first, v_p) { p_bar <- k*d_e-d_co; s_m <- (1-delta_p)/(1-delta_m*delta_p)*(m_first+(1-m_first)*delta_m); p_neg <- pmin(res+s_m*(p_bar-res), p_bar-v_p); data.frame(p_bar = p_bar, s_m = s_m, p_neg = p_neg, icer = (p_neg+d_co)/d_e, nmb_p = k*d_e-d_co-p_neg) }" .

hemo:7005009 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BARG-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000771 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def negotiated_price(k, d_e, d_co, res, delta_m, delta_p, m_first, v_p): p_bar = k*d_e-d_co; s_m = (1-delta_p)/(1-delta_m*delta_p)*(m_first+(1-m_first)*delta_m); p_neg = min(res+s_m*(p_bar-res), p_bar-v_p); return p_bar, s_m, p_neg, (p_neg+d_co)/d_e, k*d_e-d_co-p_neg` Returns the maximum price, the manufacturer's share, the negotiated price, the cost per QALY and the payer's net monetary benefit for one scenario." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def negotiated_price(k, d_e, d_co, res, delta_m, delta_p, m_first, v_p): p_bar = k*d_e-d_co; s_m = (1-delta_p)/(1-delta_m*delta_p)*(m_first+(1-m_first)*delta_m); p_neg = min(res+s_m*(p_bar-res), p_bar-v_p); return p_bar, s_m, p_neg, (p_neg+d_co)/d_e, k*d_e-d_co-p_neg" .

hemo:7005012 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Partial pooling of a basket trial table and its tumour-type mix response"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "As in HE-FM-BSKT-001, mu and tau are fixed rather than estimated, the baskets are exchangeable and the sampling variance is evaluated at the common mean. The shares cover the whole population expected to be treated." .

hemo:7005015 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BSKT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000772 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=SUMPRODUCT(Shares,(BetweenSD^2/(BetweenSD^2+CommonMean*(1-CommonMean)/Patients))*Responders/Patients+(1-BetweenSD^2/(BetweenSD^2+CommonMean*(1-CommonMean)/Patients))*CommonMean)` With one row per basket in named ranges Responders, Patients and Shares, and the common mean and between-basket standard deviation in cells named CommonMean and BetweenSD, the formula returns R_pp. The expression after Shares, entered row by row, returns each p_tilde_j." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(Shares,(BetweenSD^2/(BetweenSD^2+CommonMean*(1-CommonMean)/Patients))*Responders/Patients+(1-BetweenSD^2/(BetweenSD^2+CommonMean*(1-CommonMean)/Patients))*CommonMean)" .

hemo:7005016 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BSKT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000772 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`pool_baskets <- function(y, n, mu, tau, share) { w <- tau^2/(tau^2+mu*(1-mu)/n); p <- w*y/n+(1-w)*mu; list(p_tilde = p, R_pp = sum(share*p)) }` Vectorised over baskets; the three vectors must hold the baskets in the same order." ;
    hemo:9000024 "pool_baskets <- function(y, n, mu, tau, share) { w <- tau^2/(tau^2+mu*(1-mu)/n); p <- w*y/n+(1-w)*mu; list(p_tilde = p, R_pp = sum(share*p)) }" .

hemo:7005017 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BSKT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000772 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def pool_baskets(y, n, mu, tau, share): y, n, share = (np.asarray(v, dtype=float) for v in (y, n, share)); w = tau**2/(tau**2+mu*(1-mu)/n); p = w*y/n+(1-w)*mu; return p, float(np.sum(share*p))` Uses numpy imported as np; returns the partially pooled responses and the mix response." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def pool_baskets(y, n, mu, tau, share): y, n, share = (np.asarray(v, dtype=float) for v in (y, n, share)); w = tau**2/(tau**2+mu*(1-mu)/n); p = w*y/n+(1-w)*mu; return p, float(np.sum(share*p))" .

hemo:7005020 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Two-sector Baumol scenario from a starting share of hours"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Labour is the only cost, both sectors pay the same wage, productivity grows at constant rates, total hours are unchanged and the ratio of health output to other output is held fixed, as in the article's illustrative example." .

hemo:7005024 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BAUM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000773 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=((1+WageGrowth)/(1+GrowthHealth))^Years/((1+WageGrowth)/(1+GrowthOther))^Years; =ShareHealth0/(1-ShareHealth0)*RelCostT; =HoursRatioT/(1+HoursRatioT); =(1-ShareHealthT)/(1-ShareHealth0)*(1+GrowthOther)^Years; =ShareHealthT/ShareHealth0*(1+GrowthHealth)^Years` Five cells that return R_T, x_T, s_hT, q_o and q_h, with the first three outputs named RelCostT, HoursRatioT and ShareHealthT." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=((1+WageGrowth)/(1+GrowthHealth))^Years/((1+WageGrowth)/(1+GrowthOther))^Years; =ShareHealth0/(1-ShareHealth0)*RelCostT; =HoursRatioT/(1+HoursRatioT); =(1-ShareHealthT)/(1-ShareHealth0)*(1+GrowthOther)^Years; =ShareHealthT/ShareHealth0*(1+GrowthHealth)^Years" .

hemo:7005025 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BAUM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000773 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`baumol_scenario <- function(s_h0, g_o, g_h, g_w, T) { C_h <- ((1+g_w)/(1+g_h))^T; C_o <- ((1+g_w)/(1+g_o))^T; R_T <- C_h/C_o; x_T <- s_h0/(1-s_h0)*R_T; s_hT <- x_T/(1+x_T); c(C_h = C_h, C_o = C_o, R_T = R_T, x_T = x_T, s_hT = s_hT, q_o = (1-s_hT)/(1-s_h0)*(1+g_o)^T, q_h = s_hT/s_h0*(1+g_h)^T) }` Returns a named vector of the outputs." ;
    hemo:9000024 "baumol_scenario <- function(s_h0, g_o, g_h, g_w, T) { C_h <- ((1+g_w)/(1+g_h))^T; C_o <- ((1+g_w)/(1+g_o))^T; R_T <- C_h/C_o; x_T <- s_h0/(1-s_h0)*R_T; s_hT <- x_T/(1+x_T); c(C_h = C_h, C_o = C_o, R_T = R_T, x_T = x_T, s_hT = s_hT, q_o = (1-s_hT)/(1-s_h0)*(1+g_o)^T, q_h = s_hT/s_h0*(1+g_h)^T) }" .

hemo:7005026 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BAUM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000773 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def baumol_scenario(s_h0, g_o, g_h, g_w, T): C_h = ((1+g_w)/(1+g_h))**T; C_o = ((1+g_w)/(1+g_o))**T; R_T = C_h/C_o; x_T = s_h0/(1-s_h0)*R_T; s_hT = x_T/(1+x_T); return C_h, C_o, R_T, x_T, s_hT, (1-s_hT)/(1-s_h0)*(1+g_o)**T, s_hT/s_h0*(1+g_h)**T` Returns the outputs as a tuple, with no imports needed." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def baumol_scenario(s_h0, g_o, g_h, g_w, T): C_h = ((1+g_w)/(1+g_h))**T; C_o = ((1+g_w)/(1+g_o))**T; R_T = C_h/C_o; x_T = s_h0/(1-s_h0)*R_T; s_hT = x_T/(1+x_T); return C_h, C_o, R_T, x_T, s_hT, (1-s_hT)/(1-s_h0)*(1+g_o)**T, s_hT/s_h0*(1+g_h)**T" .

hemo:7005029 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Health inequality indices from group mean health and population shares"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The groups have a natural socioeconomic order, each group is given its mean health so only inequality between groups is measured, and the slope index comes from a straight line fitted by population-weighted least squares." .

hemo:7005034 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-HINQ-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000774 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=SUM(B$2:B2)-B2/2` With the shares in B2:B6, summing to 1, this formula filled down beside them gives the midpoint ranks, named Rank, with health in a range named Health and shares in Share. The slope index is then `=SUMPRODUCT(Share,(Health-SUMPRODUCT(Share,Health))*(Rank-0.5))/SUMPRODUCT(Share,(Rank-0.5)^2)` and the concentration index `=2*SUMPRODUCT(Share,(Health-SUMPRODUCT(Share,Health))*(Rank-0.5))/SUMPRODUCT(Share,Health)`." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUM(B$2:B2)-B2/2" .

hemo:7005035 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-HINQ-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000774 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`health_ineq <- function(h, f) { f <- f/sum(f); r <- cumsum(f)-f/2; mu <- sum(f*h); cv <- sum(f*(h-mu)*(r-0.5)); vr <- sum(f*(r-0.5)^2); sii <- cv/vr; a <- mu-sii/2; c(mean = mu, SII = sii, RII_ratio = (a+sii)/a, RII_mean = sii/mu, C = 2*cv/mu) }` Returns the mean and the four indices for one set of groups; applying it to each programme's group means gives a comparison table." ;
    hemo:9000024 "health_ineq <- function(h, f) { f <- f/sum(f); r <- cumsum(f)-f/2; mu <- sum(f*h); cv <- sum(f*(h-mu)*(r-0.5)); vr <- sum(f*(r-0.5)^2); sii <- cv/vr; a <- mu-sii/2; c(mean = mu, SII = sii, RII_ratio = (a+sii)/a, RII_mean = sii/mu, C = 2*cv/mu) }" .

hemo:7005036 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-HINQ-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000774 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def health_ineq(h, f): w = [x/sum(f) for x in f]; r = [sum(w[:j])+w[j]/2 for j in range(len(w))]; mu = sum(a*b for a, b in zip(w, h)); cv = sum(a*(b-mu)*(c-0.5) for a, b, c in zip(w, h, r)); vr = sum(a*(c-0.5)**2 for a, c in zip(w, r)); sii = cv/vr; al = mu-sii/2; return {'mean': mu, 'SII': sii, 'RII_ratio': (al+sii)/al, 'RII_mean': sii/mu, 'C': 2*cv/mu}` Plain Python with no imports; the shares are rescaled to sum to 1 inside the function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def health_ineq(h, f): w = [x/sum(f) for x in f]; r = [sum(w[:j])+w[j]/2 for j in range(len(w))]; mu = sum(a*b for a, b in zip(w, h)); cv = sum(a*(b-mu)*(c-0.5) for a, b, c in zip(w, h, r)); vr = sum(a*(c-0.5)**2 for a, c in zip(w, r)); sii = cv/vr; al = mu-sii/2; return {'mean': mu, 'SII': sii, 'RII_ratio': (al+sii)/al, 'RII_mean': sii/mu, 'C': 2*cv/mu}" .

hemo:7005039 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Kakwani index from group shares of income and health payments"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Groups are ranked by ability to pay, not by the payment, and the curves are straight lines between group points, so inequality within groups does not enter. Payments are attributed by their assumed economic incidence." .

hemo:7005043 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-VEQ-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000775 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=LET(lx,SCAN(0,IncShare,LAMBDA(a,b,a+b)),lp,SCAN(0,PayShare,LAMBDA(a,b,a+b)),SUMPRODUCT(Pop,2*lx-IncShare)-SUMPRODUCT(Pop,2*lp-PayShare))` With the group population shares in a range named Pop and the income and payment shares in IncShare and PayShare, poorest group first, SCAN builds the cumulative shares and the formula returns the Kakwani index. SCAN and LAMBDA need Excel for Microsoft 365 or Excel 2024." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LET(lx,SCAN(0,IncShare,LAMBDA(a,b,a+b)),lp,SCAN(0,PayShare,LAMBDA(a,b,a+b)),SUMPRODUCT(Pop,2*lx-IncShare)-SUMPRODUCT(Pop,2*lp-PayShare))" .

hemo:7005044 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-VEQ-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000775 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`kakwani_grouped <- function(f, sx, sp) { conc <- function(s) 1-sum(f * (2*cumsum(s)-s)); conc(sp)-conc(sx) }` The inner function returns one minus the trapezoid sum for one curve; calling conc(sx) alone gives the Gini coefficient." ;
    hemo:9000024 "kakwani_grouped <- function(f, sx, sp) { conc <- function(s) 1-sum(f * (2*cumsum(s)-s)); conc(sp)-conc(sx) }" .

hemo:7005045 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-VEQ-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000775 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def kakwani_grouped(f, sx, sp): conc = lambda s: 1-sum(fi * (2*L-si) for fi, L, si in zip(f, itertools.accumulate(s), s)); return conc(sp)-conc(sx)` Uses itertools.accumulate for the cumulative shares, so the itertools module must be imported." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def kakwani_grouped(f, sx, sp): conc = lambda s: 1-sum(fi * (2*L-si) for fi, L, si in zip(f, itertools.accumulate(s), s)); return conc(sp)-conc(sx)" .

hemo:7005048 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Horizontal inequity index from group shares, actual use and need-expected use"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Groups are ranked by income or another living standards measure, and each group is represented by its mean, so only inequality between groups enters. When mu and muX are equal, as with linear standardisation, HI also equals the concentration index of the need-standardised group means." .

hemo:7005052 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-HEQ-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000776 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=LET(ranks,SCAN(0,Pop,LAMBDA(a,b,a+b))-Pop/2,2*SUMPRODUCT(Pop,Actual,ranks)/SUMPRODUCT(Pop,Actual)-2*SUMPRODUCT(Pop,Expected,ranks)/SUMPRODUCT(Pop,Expected))` With population shares summing to 1 in a range named Pop and the group means of actual and need-expected use in Actual and Expected, poorest group first, SCAN builds the midpoint ranks and the formula returns the horizontal inequity index; the minus 1 in each concentration index cancels. SCAN and LAMBDA need Excel for Microsoft 365 or Excel 2024." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LET(ranks,SCAN(0,Pop,LAMBDA(a,b,a+b))-Pop/2,2*SUMPRODUCT(Pop,Actual,ranks)/SUMPRODUCT(Pop,Actual)-2*SUMPRODUCT(Pop,Expected,ranks)/SUMPRODUCT(Pop,Expected))" .

hemo:7005053 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-HEQ-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000776 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`hi_grouped <- function(f, m, mx) { f <- f/sum(f); r <- cumsum(f)-f/2; conc <- function(y) 2*sum(f*y*r)/sum(f*y)-1; c(C_M = conc(m), C_N = conc(mx), HI = conc(m)-conc(mx)) }` Returns the two concentration indices and the horizontal inequity index; the shares are rescaled to sum to 1 inside the function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "hi_grouped <- function(f, m, mx) { f <- f/sum(f); r <- cumsum(f)-f/2; conc <- function(y) 2*sum(f*y*r)/sum(f*y)-1; c(C_M = conc(m), C_N = conc(mx), HI = conc(m)-conc(mx)) }" .

hemo:7005054 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-HEQ-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000776 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def hi_grouped(f, m, mx): w = [x/sum(f) for x in f]; r = [sum(w[:t])+w[t]/2 for t in range(len(w))]; conc = lambda y: 2*sum(a*b*c for a, b, c in zip(w, y, r))/sum(a*b for a, b in zip(w, y))-1; return {'C_M': conc(m), 'C_N': conc(mx), 'HI': conc(m)-conc(mx)}` Plain Python with no imports; the shares are rescaled to sum to 1 inside the function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def hi_grouped(f, m, mx): w = [x/sum(f) for x in f]; r = [sum(w[:t])+w[t]/2 for t in range(len(w))]; conc = lambda y: 2*sum(a*b*c for a, b, c in zip(w, y, r))/sum(a*b for a, b in zip(w, y))-1; return {'C_M': conc(m), 'C_N': conc(mx), 'HI': conc(m)-conc(mx)}" .

hemo:7005057 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Grid posterior over the between-study standard deviation in a Bayesian meta-analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each estimate is normal with known variance s_i^2, the true effects are exchangeable draws from N(mu, tau^2) and mu has a flat prior, which gives the same results to the decimals shown as the N(0, 100^2) prior used in the article. The prior for tau places a negligible share of its probability above tmax: none for U(0, 2), and well under 0.1% for the Turner mortality prior. Grid points are the midpoints (j-0.5)*tmax/J, so tau = 0 is never evaluated." .

hemo:7005060 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BMA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000777 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=(ROW()-1.5)*0.0001` in A2, `=SUMPRODUCT(1/(StudySE^2+A2^2))` in B2, `=SUMPRODUCT(StudyEst/(StudySE^2+A2^2))/B2` in C2, `=0.5*SUMPRODUCT(LN(1/(StudySE^2+A2^2))-(StudyEst-C2)^2/(StudySE^2+A2^2))-0.5*LN(B2)` in D2, `=-LN(A2)-(2*LN(A2)+3.95)^2/(2*1.34^2)` in E2 (or 0 for the U(0, 2) prior), `=D2+E2` in F2, `=EXP(F2-$L$1)` in G2, `=G2/$L$2` in H2, `=H2` in I2 and `=I2+H3` in I3, `=H2*NORM.S.DIST(-C2*SQRT(B2),TRUE)` in J2, with `=MAX(F2:F20001)` in L1, `=SUM(G2:G20001)` in L2, `=INDEX(A2:A20001,MATCH(TRUE,INDEX(I2:I20001>=0.5,0),0))` in L3 and `=SUM(J2:J20001)` in L4. With the trial estimates in a range named StudyEst and the standard errors in StudySE, rows 2 to 20001 hold the grid of 20000 values of tau from 0 to 2 (I3 is filled down to I20001, the other columns from row 2), L3 returns tau_med and L4 returns P_mu_neg." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(ROW()-1.5)*0.0001" .

hemo:7005061 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BMA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000777 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`bma_grid <- function(y, s, logprior, J = 20000, tmax = 2) { tau <- (seq_len(J)-0.5)*tmax/J; W <- sapply(tau, function(t) sum(1/(s^2+t^2))); m <- sapply(tau, function(t) sum(y/(s^2+t^2)))/W; lp <- sapply(seq_len(J), function(j) { w <- 1/(s^2+tau[j]^2); 0.5*sum(log(w)-w*(y-m[j])^2) })-0.5*log(W)+logprior(tau); post <- exp(lp-max(lp)); post <- post/sum(post); c(tau_med = tau[which(cumsum(post) >= 0.5)[1]], P_mu_neg = sum(post*pnorm(-m*sqrt(W)))) }` Vectorised over the grid; `bma_grid(c(-0.45, -0.10, -0.60, 0.05), c(0.20, 0.15, 0.30, 0.25), function(t) 0*t)` reproduces analysis A and `function(t) -log(t)-(2*log(t)+3.95)^2/(2*1.34^2)` as the third argument reproduces analysis B." ;
    hemo:9000024 "bma_grid <- function(y, s, logprior, J = 20000, tmax = 2) { tau <- (seq_len(J)-0.5)*tmax/J; W <- sapply(tau, function(t) sum(1/(s^2+t^2))); m <- sapply(tau, function(t) sum(y/(s^2+t^2)))/W; lp <- sapply(seq_len(J), function(j) { w <- 1/(s^2+tau[j]^2); 0.5*sum(log(w)-w*(y-m[j])^2) })-0.5*log(W)+logprior(tau); post <- exp(lp-max(lp)); post <- post/sum(post); c(tau_med = tau[which(cumsum(post) >= 0.5)[1]], P_mu_neg = sum(post*pnorm(-m*sqrt(W)))) }" .

hemo:7005062 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BMA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000777 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def bma_grid(y, s, logprior, J=20000, tmax=2.0): tau = [(j+0.5)*tmax/J for j in range(J)]; W = [sum(1/(si**2+t**2) for si in s) for t in tau]; m = [sum(yi/(si**2+t**2) for yi, si in zip(y, s))/Wj for t, Wj in zip(tau, W)]; lp = [0.5*sum(math.log(1/(si**2+t**2))-(yi-mj)**2/(si**2+t**2) for yi, si in zip(y, s))-0.5*math.log(Wj)+logprior(t) for t, Wj, mj in zip(tau, W, m)]; top = max(lp); post = [math.exp(v-top) for v in lp]; tot = sum(post); post = [p/tot for p in post]; tau_med = next(t for t, c in zip(tau, itertools.accumulate(post)) if c >= 0.5); P_mu_neg = sum(p*0.5*math.erfc(mj*math.sqrt(Wj)/math.sqrt(2)) for p, mj, Wj in zip(post, m, W)); return tau_med, P_mu_neg` Uses the math and itertools modules and plain lists; math.erfc gives the normal tail probability, and logprior is a function of a single value of tau, for example `lambda t: 0.0`." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def bma_grid(y, s, logprior, J=20000, tmax=2.0): tau = [(j+0.5)*tmax/J for j in range(J)]; W = [sum(1/(si**2+t**2) for si in s) for t in tau]; m = [sum(yi/(si**2+t**2) for yi, si in zip(y, s))/Wj for t, Wj in zip(tau, W)]; lp = [0.5*sum(math.log(1/(si**2+t**2))-(yi-mj)**2/(si**2+t**2) for yi, si in zip(y, s))-0.5*math.log(Wj)+logprior(t) for t, Wj, mj in zip(tau, W, m)]; top = max(lp); post = [math.exp(v-top) for v in lp]; tot = sum(post); post = [p/tot for p in post]; tau_med = next(t for t, c in zip(tau, itertools.accumulate(post)) if c >= 0.5); P_mu_neg = sum(p*0.5*math.erfc(mj*math.sqrt(Wj)/math.sqrt(2)) for p, mj, Wj in zip(post, m, W)); return tau_med, P_mu_neg" .

hemo:7005065 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Bed turnover rate from daily available bed counts"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each daily count uses one stated definition of an available bed, the same one that defines the beds whose discharges are counted in D. A day on which a ward is closed carries the reduced count, not a blank." .

hemo:7005068 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BTO-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000778 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=Discharges/AVERAGE(DailyBeds)` With one row per day of the period in a range named DailyBeds and deaths and discharges in a cell named Discharges, the formula returns the turnover rate." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=Discharges/AVERAGE(DailyBeds)" .

hemo:7005069 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BTO-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000778 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`turnover_rate <- function(daily_beds, discharges) discharges / mean(daily_beds)` Takes a numeric vector of daily bed counts; rep(c(400, 340), c(292, 73)) builds the first worked example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "turnover_rate <- function(daily_beds, discharges) discharges / mean(daily_beds)" .

hemo:7005070 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BTO-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000778 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def turnover_rate(daily_beds, discharges): return discharges / (sum(daily_beds) / len(daily_beds))` Takes a list of daily bed counts, for example [400] * 292 + [340] * 73." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def turnover_rate(daily_beds, discharges): return discharges / (sum(daily_beds) / len(daily_beds))" .

hemo:7005073 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Reported mean and standard error to a fitted beta with its interval and a PSA draw"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The quantity is bounded by zero and one, s is the standard error of the mean rather than a patient-level standard deviation, and a beta matched on two moments represents the evidence. Draws for different parameters use independent uniform numbers unless a correlation is modelled." .

hemo:7005077 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BETA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000779 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=IF(StdErr^2<MeanProb*(1-MeanProb),MeanProb*(MeanProb*(1-MeanProb)/StdErr^2-1),NA())` returns alpha in a cell named AlphaParam, and the same formula with (1-MeanProb) as the first factor returns beta in BetaParam. Then `=BETA.INV(0.025,AlphaParam,BetaParam)` and `=BETA.INV(0.975,AlphaParam,BetaParam)` return the interval, and `=BETA.INV(RAND(),AlphaParam,BetaParam)` one PSA draw." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(StdErr^2<MeanProb*(1-MeanProb),MeanProb*(MeanProb*(1-MeanProb)/StdErr^2-1),NA())" .

hemo:7005078 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BETA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000779 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`fit_beta <- function(mu, s, u = 0.5) { k <- mu * (1-mu) / s^2; if (k <= 1) stop(\"no beta distribution has this mean and standard error\"); a <- mu * (k-1); b <- (1-mu) * (k-1); c(alpha = a, beta = b, q_lo = qbeta(0.025, a, b), q_hi = qbeta(0.975, a, b), p_u = qbeta(u, a, b)) }` Base R only; `fit_beta(0.20, 0.05, runif(1))` returns the fit with one PSA draw." ;
    hemo:9000024 "fit_beta <- function(mu, s, u = 0.5) { k <- mu * (1-mu) / s^2; if (k <= 1) stop(\"no beta distribution has this mean and standard error\"); a <- mu * (k-1); b <- (1-mu) * (k-1); c(alpha = a, beta = b, q_lo = qbeta(0.025, a, b), q_hi = qbeta(0.975, a, b), p_u = qbeta(u, a, b)) }" .

hemo:7005079 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BETA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000779 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def fit_beta(mu, s, u=0.5): k = mu*(1-mu)/s**2; assert k > 1, \"no beta distribution has this mean and standard error\"; a, b = mu*(k-1), (1-mu)*(k-1); return dict(alpha=a, beta=b, q_lo=stats.beta.ppf(0.025, a, b), q_hi=stats.beta.ppf(0.975, a, b), p_u=stats.beta.ppf(u, a, b))` Needs `from scipy import stats`; stats.beta.ppf is the inverse cumulative beta." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def fit_beta(mu, s, u=0.5): k = mu*(1-mu)/s**2; assert k > 1, \"no beta distribution has this mean and standard error\"; a, b = mu*(k-1), (1-mu)*(k-1); return dict(alpha=a, beta=b, q_lo=stats.beta.ppf(0.025, a, b), q_hi=stats.beta.ppf(0.975, a, b), p_u=stats.beta.ppf(u, a, b))" .

hemo:7005082 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Sequential incremental benefit-cost comparison of mutually exclusive options"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The options are mutually exclusive and are all described with the same classification of savings, transfers and costs. Where two options have the same cost, the one with the larger benefits is placed first and the other cannot replace it." .

hemo:7005086 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BCRX-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000780 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=REDUCE(1,SEQUENCE(ROWS(Costs)-1,1,2),LAMBDA(best,k,IF(INDEX(Costs,k)>INDEX(Costs,best),IF((INDEX(Benefits,k)-INDEX(Benefits,best))/(INDEX(Costs,k)-INDEX(Costs,best))>1,k,best),best)))` In Excel 365, with the options sorted by ascending cost (ties with larger benefits first) in ranges named Benefits and Costs, the formula returns the row of the chosen option within the ranges." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=REDUCE(1,SEQUENCE(ROWS(Costs)-1,1,2),LAMBDA(best,k,IF(INDEX(Costs,k)>INDEX(Costs,best),IF((INDEX(Benefits,k)-INDEX(Benefits,best))/(INDEX(Costs,k)-INDEX(Costs,best))>1,k,best),best)))" .

hemo:7005087 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BCRX-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000780 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`choose_option <- function(pvb, pvc) { o <- order(pvc, -pvb); d <- o[1]; for (j in o[-1]) if (pvc[j] > pvc[d] && (pvb[j]-pvb[d]) / (pvc[j]-pvc[d]) > 1) d <- j; d }` Returns the position of the chosen option in the input vectors, which need not be sorted." ;
    hemo:9000024 "choose_option <- function(pvb, pvc) { o <- order(pvc, -pvb); d <- o[1]; for (j in o[-1]) if (pvc[j] > pvc[d] && (pvb[j]-pvb[d]) / (pvc[j]-pvc[d]) > 1) d <- j; d }" .

hemo:7005088 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BCRX-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000780 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def choose_option(pvb, pvc): return functools.reduce(lambda d, j: j if pvc[j] > pvc[d] and (pvb[j]-pvb[d]) / (pvc[j]-pvc[d]) > 1 else d, sorted(range(len(pvc)), key=lambda j: (pvc[j], -pvb[j])))` Uses functools.reduce over the options sorted by cost and returns the index of the chosen option." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def choose_option(pvb, pvc): return functools.reduce(lambda d, j: j if pvc[j] > pvc[d] and (pvb[j]-pvb[d]) / (pvc[j]-pvc[d]) > 1 else d, sorted(range(len(pvc)), key=lambda j: (pvc[j], -pvb[j])))" .

hemo:7005091 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of BIC, BIC differences and posterior model probabilities for candidate survival curves"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "All candidates are fitted by maximum likelihood to identical data with one count, and every model has the same prior probability. Working from the differences rather than the raw BIC values keeps the exponentials within floating-point range when BIC values run into the thousands." .

hemo:7005094 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BIC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000781 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=-2*B2+C2*LN($G$1)` in D2, `=D2-MIN($D$2:$D$5)` in E2 and `=EXP(-E2/2)/SUMPRODUCT(EXP(-$E$2:$E$5/2))` in F2, each filled down to row 5. With one model per row, log-likelihoods in B2:B5, parameter counts in C2:C5 and the penalty count in G1, columns D, E and F return BIC, the BIC difference and the posterior probability." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=-2*B2+C2*LN($G$1)" .

hemo:7005095 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BIC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000781 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`bic_table <- function(loglik, k, count) { bic <- -2*loglik+k*log(count); delta <- bic-min(bic); p <- exp(-delta/2)/sum(exp(-delta/2)); data.frame(bic, delta, p) }` Vectorised over models; `bic_table(c(-530, -524, -525.5, -521.3), c(1, 2, 2, 3), 120)` reproduces the event-count example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "bic_table <- function(loglik, k, count) { bic <- -2*loglik+k*log(count); delta <- bic-min(bic); p <- exp(-delta/2)/sum(exp(-delta/2)); data.frame(bic, delta, p) }" .

hemo:7005096 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BIC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000781 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def bic_table(loglik, k, count): bic = [-2*li+ki*math.log(count) for li, ki in zip(loglik, k)]; m = min(bic); delta = [b-m for b in bic]; e = [math.exp(-d/2) for d in delta]; s = sum(e); return bic, delta, [x/s for x in e]` Uses the math module, where math.log is the natural logarithm, and returns the three lists in the order of the inputs." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def bic_table(loglik, k, count): bic = [-2*li+ki*math.log(count) for li, ki in zip(loglik, k)]; m = min(bic); delta = [b-m for b in bic]; e = [math.exp(-d/2) for d in delta]; s = sum(e); return bic, delta, [x/s for x in e]" .

hemo:7005099 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Bootstrap standard error and normal interval from a vector of replicates"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The replicates come from resampling that copies the trial design, and the statistic behaves like an average, so a normal interval is a reasonable first summary. Efron and Tibshirani note that this standard interval is sometimes good and sometimes not, and it does not suit the incremental cost-effectiveness ratio, whose variance may be undefined." .

hemo:7005102 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BSE-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000782 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=STDEV.S(Replicates)` in a cell named SE, with `=Estimate-ZValue*SE` and `=Estimate+ZValue*SE` for the limits. With the replicate values in a range named Replicates, the original estimate in Estimate and the critical value in ZValue, the three cells return the standard error and the interval." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=STDEV.S(Replicates)" .

hemo:7005103 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BSE-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000782 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`boot_se_interval <- function(reps, estimate, z = 1.96) { se <- sd(reps); c(se = se, lower = estimate-z*se, upper = estimate+z*se) }` The R function sd uses the divisor B minus 1, so no further correction is needed; `boot_se_interval(c(600, -1400, 2600, 1100, 100), 500)` reproduces the first example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "boot_se_interval <- function(reps, estimate, z = 1.96) { se <- sd(reps); c(se = se, lower = estimate-z*se, upper = estimate+z*se) }" .

hemo:7005104 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BSE-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000782 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def boot_se_interval(reps, estimate, z=1.96): se = statistics.stdev(reps); return se, estimate-z*se, estimate+z*se` Uses the statistics module, whose stdev function also uses the divisor B minus 1." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def boot_se_interval(reps, estimate, z=1.96): se = statistics.stdev(reps); return se, estimate-z*se, estimate+z*se" .

hemo:7005107 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Ideal paired bootstrap standard error of incremental net benefit from patient-level data"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Patients are resampled in cost and effect pairs within each arm with the original arm sizes, and the arms are resampled independently. The ideal value carries no simulation noise; a Monte Carlo run with B replicates approximates it." .

hemo:7005110 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BSE-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000783 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=SQRT(DEVSQ(Lambda*QALYsT-CostsT)/COUNT(CostsT)^2+DEVSQ(Lambda*QALYsU-CostsU)/COUNT(CostsU)^2)` With costs and QALYs in ranges named CostsT, QALYsT, CostsU and QALYsU and the threshold in Lambda, the cell, named SEINMB, returns the ideal paired standard error; Excel versions without dynamic arrays need it entered as an array formula. `=Lambda*(AVERAGE(QALYsT)-AVERAGE(QALYsU))-(AVERAGE(CostsT)-AVERAGE(CostsU))` returns INMB." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SQRT(DEVSQ(Lambda*QALYsT-CostsT)/COUNT(CostsT)^2+DEVSQ(Lambda*QALYsU-CostsU)/COUNT(CostsU)^2)" .

hemo:7005111 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BSE-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000783 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`ideal_se_inmb <- function(cT, eT, cU, eU, lambda) { nbT <- lambda*eT-cT; nbU <- lambda*eU-cU; ss <- function(x) sum((x-mean(x))^2); c(inmb = mean(nbT)-mean(nbU), se = sqrt(ss(nbT)/length(nbT)^2+ss(nbU)/length(nbU)^2)) }` Returns INMB and its ideal paired standard error for any arm sizes." ;
    hemo:9000024 "ideal_se_inmb <- function(cT, eT, cU, eU, lambda) { nbT <- lambda*eT-cT; nbU <- lambda*eU-cU; ss <- function(x) sum((x-mean(x))^2); c(inmb = mean(nbT)-mean(nbU), se = sqrt(ss(nbT)/length(nbT)^2+ss(nbU)/length(nbU)^2)) }" .

hemo:7005112 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BSE-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000783 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def ideal_se_inmb(cT, eT, cU, eU, lam): nbT = [lam*e-c for c, e in zip(cT, eT)]; nbU = [lam*e-c for c, e in zip(cU, eU)]; ss = lambda x: sum((v-sum(x)/len(x))**2 for v in x); return sum(nbT)/len(nbT)-sum(nbU)/len(nbU), math.sqrt(ss(nbT)/len(nbT)**2+ss(nbU)/len(nbU)**2)` Uses the math module; the threshold argument is named lam because lambda is a reserved word in Python." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def ideal_se_inmb(cT, eT, cU, eU, lam): nbT = [lam*e-c for c, e in zip(cT, eT)]; nbU = [lam*e-c for c, e in zip(cU, eU)]; ss = lambda x: sum((v-sum(x)/len(x))**2 for v in x); return sum(nbT)/len(nbT)-sum(nbU)/len(nbU), math.sqrt(ss(nbT)/len(nbT)**2+ss(nbU)/len(nbU)**2)" .

hemo:7005114 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Brier score, Murphy partition and scaled score from predicted risks and outcomes"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Grouping by identical predicted values makes the partition exact. With continuous risks every patient forms a group of one, the resolution term then equals the uncertainty term and the partition says nothing useful, so continuous risks are binned before the partition is read, and the bins are reported." .

hemo:7005117 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BRIER-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000784 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=SUMXMY2(Predicted,Outcome)/COUNT(Predicted)` returns the score from patient-level columns named Predicted and Outcome, and `=1-(SUMXMY2(Predicted,Outcome)/COUNT(Predicted))/(AVERAGE(Outcome)*(1-AVERAGE(Outcome)))` returns the scaled score. The partition terms come from a grouped table, as in HE-IM-BRIER-002." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMXMY2(Predicted,Outcome)/COUNT(Predicted)" .

hemo:7005118 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BRIER-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000784 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`brier_partition <- function(p, y) { n <- length(y); obar <- mean(y); nk <- tapply(y, p, length); ok <- tapply(y, p, mean); fk <- as.numeric(names(nk)); bs <- mean((p-y)^2); rel <- sum(nk*(fk-ok)^2)/n; res <- sum(nk*(ok-obar)^2)/n; unc <- obar*(1-obar); c(bs = bs, rel = rel, res = res, unc = unc, scaled = 1-bs/unc) }` tapply groups the outcomes by distinct predicted value; the example vectors above can be pasted into R as written." ;
    hemo:9000024 "brier_partition <- function(p, y) { n <- length(y); obar <- mean(y); nk <- tapply(y, p, length); ok <- tapply(y, p, mean); fk <- as.numeric(names(nk)); bs <- mean((p-y)^2); rel <- sum(nk*(fk-ok)^2)/n; res <- sum(nk*(ok-obar)^2)/n; unc <- obar*(1-obar); c(bs = bs, rel = rel, res = res, unc = unc, scaled = 1-bs/unc) }" .

hemo:7005119 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BRIER-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000784 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def brier_partition(p, y): n = len(y); obar = sum(y)/n; g = {f: [yi for pi, yi in zip(p, y) if pi == f] for f in set(p)}; bs = sum((pi-yi)**2 for pi, yi in zip(p, y))/n; rel = sum(len(v)*(f-sum(v)/len(v))**2 for f, v in g.items())/n; res = sum(len(v)*(sum(v)/len(v)-obar)**2 for v in g.values())/n; unc = obar*(1-obar); return bs, rel, res, unc, 1-bs/unc` Plain Python with no imports; the dictionary g holds the outcomes for each distinct predicted value." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def brier_partition(p, y): n = len(y); obar = sum(y)/n; g = {f: [yi for pi, yi in zip(p, y) if pi == f] for f in set(p)}; bs = sum((pi-yi)**2 for pi, yi in zip(p, y))/n; rel = sum(len(v)*(f-sum(v)/len(v))**2 for f, v in g.items())/n; res = sum(len(v)*(sum(v)/len(v)-obar)**2 for v in g.values())/n; unc = obar*(1-obar); return bs, rel, res, unc, 1-bs/unc" .

hemo:7005122 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Exact binomial probability, cumulative probability and percentiles of an event count"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The binomial conditions hold: a fixed number of patients followed for the whole period, the same probability for each and independent outcomes. The percentiles describe chance variation in the count at a fixed p, not uncertainty about p, which is described by a beta distribution." .

hemo:7005126 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BINOM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000785 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=BINOM.DIST(Events,Patients,Prob,FALSE)` returns P_r and `=BINOM.DIST(Events,Patients,Prob,TRUE)` returns F_r. `=BINOM.INV(Patients,Prob,0.025)` and `=BINOM.INV(Patients,Prob,0.975)` return the percentiles, each the smallest count whose cumulative probability is at least the level." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=BINOM.DIST(Events,Patients,Prob,FALSE)" .

hemo:7005127 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BINOM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000785 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`binom_summary <- function(n, p, r, a_lo = 0.025, a_hi = 0.975) c(P_r = dbinom(r, n, p), F_r = pbinom(r, n, p), q_lo = qbinom(a_lo, n, p), q_hi = qbinom(a_hi, n, p))` Base R; qbinom uses the same smallest-count definition as BINOM.INV." ;
    hemo:9000024 "binom_summary <- function(n, p, r, a_lo = 0.025, a_hi = 0.975) c(P_r = dbinom(r, n, p), F_r = pbinom(r, n, p), q_lo = qbinom(a_lo, n, p), q_hi = qbinom(a_hi, n, p))" .

hemo:7005128 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BINOM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000785 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def binom_summary(n, p, r, a_lo=0.025, a_hi=0.975): return dict(P_r=stats.binom.pmf(r, n, p), F_r=stats.binom.cdf(r, n, p), q_lo=stats.binom.ppf(a_lo, n, p), q_hi=stats.binom.ppf(a_hi, n, p))` Needs `from scipy import stats`; stats.binom.ppf returns the smallest count whose cumulative probability is at least the level." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def binom_summary(n, p, r, a_lo=0.025, a_hi=0.975): return dict(P_r=stats.binom.pmf(r, n, p), F_r=stats.binom.cdf(r, n, p), q_lo=stats.binom.ppf(a_lo, n, p), q_hi=stats.binom.ppf(a_hi, n, p))" .

hemo:7005131 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Likelihood ratio test p-value and critical value from two fitted models"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The models are nested, the null value is not on the boundary of the parameter space and the sample is large, so that Wilks' theorem applies. Non-nested survival curves are compared with AIC and BIC instead." .

hemo:7005135 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CHISQ-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000786 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=CHISQ.DIST.RT(2*(LogLik1-LogLik0),Params1-Params0)` returns the p-value and `=CHISQ.INV.RT(Alpha,Params1-Params0)` the critical value, with the log-likelihoods in LogLik1 and LogLik0 and the parameter counts in Params1 and Params0." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CHISQ.DIST.RT(2*(LogLik1-LogLik0),Params1-Params0)" .

hemo:7005136 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CHISQ-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000786 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`lr_test <- function(ll0, ll1, q0, q1, alpha = 0.05) { lr <- 2 * (ll1-ll0); df <- q1-q0; c(LR = lr, df = df, p_value = pchisq(lr, df, lower.tail = FALSE), x_crit = qchisq(alpha, df, lower.tail = FALSE)) }` Base R; `lr_test(-612.8, -608.3, 1, 2)` returns the step 1 result." ;
    hemo:9000024 "lr_test <- function(ll0, ll1, q0, q1, alpha = 0.05) { lr <- 2 * (ll1-ll0); df <- q1-q0; c(LR = lr, df = df, p_value = pchisq(lr, df, lower.tail = FALSE), x_crit = qchisq(alpha, df, lower.tail = FALSE)) }" .

hemo:7005137 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CHISQ-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000786 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def lr_test(ll0, ll1, q0, q1, alpha=0.05): lr = 2 * (ll1-ll0); df = q1-q0; return dict(LR=lr, df=df, p_value=stats.chi2.sf(lr, df), x_crit=stats.chi2.isf(alpha, df))` Needs `from scipy import stats`; chi2.sf is the right-tail probability and chi2.isf its inverse." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def lr_test(ll0, ll1, q0, q1, alpha=0.05): lr = 2 * (ll1-ll0); df = q1-q0; return dict(LR=lr, df=df, p_value=stats.chi2.sf(lr, df), x_crit=stats.chi2.isf(alpha, df))" .

hemo:7005140 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Kaplan-Meier curve, restricted mean and tail share from entry months, event times and a data cut-off"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "All censoring is administrative and unrelated to prognosis, the arm has at least one event, a patient censored at an event time is counted at risk for it, and the hazard after the last observed follow-up is constant. Results are undiscounted." .

hemo:7005143 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ADMC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000787 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=CutOff-B2`, `=IF(C2=\"\",D2,MIN(C2,D2))` and `=IF(AND(C2<>\"\",C2<=D2),1,0)` With one patient per row from row 2, entry month in column B and event time in column C (blank when no event was seen), these give the censoring time in column D, the observed time in column E and the event flag in column F. With column E named ObservedRange and column F named EventRange, for each distinct event time in column H, `=COUNTIF(ObservedRange,\">=\"&H2)` and `=COUNTIFS(ObservedRange,H2,EventRange,1)` give the number at risk and the deaths, and the survival column is `=1-J2/I2` in the first row and `=K2*(1-J3/I3)` copied down." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CutOff-B2" .

hemo:7005144 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ADMC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000787 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`admin_km <- function(entry, event, cutoff, h = NULL) { C <- cutoff-entry; Y <- pmin(event, C); delta <- as.numeric(event <= C); tj <- sort(unique(Y[delta == 1])); nj <- sapply(tj, function(t) sum(Y >= t)); dj <- sapply(tj, function(t) sum(Y == t & delta == 1)); S <- cumprod(1-dj/nj); tau <- max(Y); RMST <- sum(c(1, S) * diff(c(0, tj, tau))); if (is.null(h)) h <- sum(delta)/sum(Y); tail <- S[length(S)]/h; list(C = C, Y = Y, delta = delta, t = tj, S = S, RMST = RMST, h = h, tail = tail, share = tail/(RMST+tail)) }` Event times for patients alive at the cut-off are entered as Inf; `admin_km(c(0, 2, 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24), c(10, Inf, 20, 6, Inf, 14, Inf, Inf), 36)[[\"RMST\"]]` gives 21.7." ;
    hemo:9000024 "admin_km <- function(entry, event, cutoff, h = NULL) { C <- cutoff-entry; Y <- pmin(event, C); delta <- as.numeric(event <= C); tj <- sort(unique(Y[delta == 1])); nj <- sapply(tj, function(t) sum(Y >= t)); dj <- sapply(tj, function(t) sum(Y == t & delta == 1)); S <- cumprod(1-dj/nj); tau <- max(Y); RMST <- sum(c(1, S) * diff(c(0, tj, tau))); if (is.null(h)) h <- sum(delta)/sum(Y); tail <- S[length(S)]/h; list(C = C, Y = Y, delta = delta, t = tj, S = S, RMST = RMST, h = h, tail = tail, share = tail/(RMST+tail)) }" .

hemo:7005145 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ADMC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000787 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def admin_km(entry, event, cutoff, h=None): C = [cutoff-e for e in entry]; Y = [min(t, c) for t, c in zip(event, C)]; delta = [int(t <= c) for t, c in zip(event, C)]; tj = sorted({y for y, x in zip(Y, delta) if x}); S = list(itertools.accumulate([1-sum(x for y, x in zip(Y, delta) if y == t)/sum(y >= t for y in Y) for t in tj], operator.mul)); tau = max(Y); RMST = sum(s*(b-a) for s, a, b in zip([1]+S, [0]+tj, tj+[tau])); h = h if h is not None else sum(delta)/sum(Y); tail = S[-1]/h; return C, Y, delta, tj, S, RMST, h, tail, tail/(RMST+tail)` Uses the math, itertools and operator modules, with math.inf for patients alive at the cut-off; the sixth value returned for the article's arm is 21.7." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def admin_km(entry, event, cutoff, h=None): C = [cutoff-e for e in entry]; Y = [min(t, c) for t, c in zip(event, C)]; delta = [int(t <= c) for t, c in zip(event, C)]; tj = sorted({y for y, x in zip(Y, delta) if x}); S = list(itertools.accumulate([1-sum(x for y, x in zip(Y, delta) if y == t)/sum(y >= t for y in Y) for t in tj], operator.mul)); tau = max(Y); RMST = sum(s*(b-a) for s, a, b in zip([1]+S, [0]+tj, tj+[tau])); h = h if h is not None else sum(delta)/sum(Y); tail = S[-1]/h; return C, Y, delta, tj, S, RMST, h, tail, tail/(RMST+tail)" .

hemo:7005148 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Community-rated pool from group counts and expected costs to premium, cross-subsidies and fund balance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "All members belong to one pool or rating cell, the premium carries no loadings, and the fund uses the same risk groups, counts and premium as the pool. The counts are those who buy at the resulting premium; the function does not model who leaves." .

hemo:7005152 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CRAT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000788 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=SUMPRODUCT(Members,ExpectedCost)/SUM(Members)` in a cell named Premium returns the premium; `=Premium-ExpectedCost` filled down beside each group gives the cross-subsidies, `=ExpectedCost-Premium` the transfers, and `=SUMPRODUCT(Members,ExpectedCost-Premium)` the fund balance." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(Members,ExpectedCost)/SUM(Members)" .

hemo:7005153 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CRAT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000788 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`community_pool <- function(n, cost) { P <- sum(n*cost)/sum(n); s <- P-cost; tr <- cost-P; list(premium = P, cross_subsidy = s, transfer = tr, fund_balance = sum(n*tr)) }` Vectorised over the risk groups; `community_pool(c(800, 200), c(1000, 6000))` returns the article's pool." ;
    hemo:9000024 "community_pool <- function(n, cost) { P <- sum(n*cost)/sum(n); s <- P-cost; tr <- cost-P; list(premium = P, cross_subsidy = s, transfer = tr, fund_balance = sum(n*tr)) }" .

hemo:7005154 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CRAT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000788 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def community_pool(n, cost): P = sum(a*b for a, b in zip(n, cost))/sum(n); s = [P-c for c in cost]; tr = [c-P for c in cost]; return {'premium': P, 'cross_subsidy': s, 'transfer': tr, 'fund_balance': sum(a*b for a, b in zip(n, tr))}` Plain Python with no imports; lists hold one value per risk group." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def community_pool(n, cost): P = sum(a*b for a, b in zip(n, cost))/sum(n); s = [P-c for c in cost]; tr = [c-P for c in cost]; return {'premium': P, 'cross_subsidy': s, 'transfer': tr, 'fund_balance': sum(a*b for a, b in zip(n, tr))}" .

hemo:7005157 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Cohort trace run with rewards accumulated under a counting rule"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The transition matrix is constant across cycles and the discount factor for cycle t is 1/(1+d)^t from t = 0. For probabilities that change with age or time, the R version takes a list of matrices by replacing P with P_list[[k]] inside Reduce. Each state is homogeneous, so a subgroup with different risks needs its own run." .

hemo:7005162 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CSIM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000789 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=MMULT(PreviousRow,TransitionMatrix)` Copied down one row per cycle from the starting row, this builds the trace; `=SUMPRODUCT(CycleWeights,DiscountFactors,MMULT(Trace,StateRewards))` then returns Y, as under HE-FM-CSIM-001." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MMULT(PreviousRow,TransitionMatrix)" .

hemo:7005163 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CSIM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000789 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`cohort_run <- function(m0, P, r, n_cycles, rule=\"half\", disc=0) { if (rule == \"simpson\" && n_cycles %% 2 != 0) stop(\"Simpson rule needs an even number of cycles\"); M <- do.call(rbind, Reduce(function(x, k) x %*% P, seq_len(n_cycles), matrix(m0, nrow=1), accumulate=TRUE)); w <- switch(rule, end=c(0, rep(1, n_cycles)), start=c(rep(1, n_cycles), 0), half=c(0.5, rep(1, n_cycles-1), 0.5), simpson=c(1, ifelse(seq_len(n_cycles-1) %% 2 == 1, 4, 2), 1)/3); delta <- 1/(1+disc)^(0:n_cycles); list(trace=M, Y=sum(w*delta*(M %*% r))) }` Returns a list; the total is read with [[\"Y\"]] and the trace with [[\"trace\"]]." ;
    hemo:9000024 "cohort_run <- function(m0, P, r, n_cycles, rule=\"half\", disc=0) { if (rule == \"simpson\" && n_cycles %% 2 != 0) stop(\"Simpson rule needs an even number of cycles\"); M <- do.call(rbind, Reduce(function(x, k) x %*% P, seq_len(n_cycles), matrix(m0, nrow=1), accumulate=TRUE)); w <- switch(rule, end=c(0, rep(1, n_cycles)), start=c(rep(1, n_cycles), 0), half=c(0.5, rep(1, n_cycles-1), 0.5), simpson=c(1, ifelse(seq_len(n_cycles-1) %% 2 == 1, 4, 2), 1)/3); delta <- 1/(1+disc)^(0:n_cycles); list(trace=M, Y=sum(w*delta*(M %*% r))) }" .

hemo:7005164 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CSIM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000789 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def cohort_run(m0, P, r, n_cycles, rule=\"half\", disc=0.0): assert not (rule == \"simpson\" and n_cycles % 2), \"Simpson rule needs an even number of cycles\"; P = np.asarray(P, float); M = np.vstack([np.asarray(m0, float) @ np.linalg.matrix_power(P, t) for t in range(n_cycles+1)]); w = {\"end\": [0]+[1]*n_cycles, \"start\": [1]*n_cycles+[0], \"half\": [0.5]+[1]*(n_cycles-1)+[0.5], \"simpson\": [1/3]+[4/3 if t % 2 else 2/3 for t in range(1, n_cycles)]+[1/3]}[rule]; delta = (1+disc)**-np.arange(n_cycles+1.0); return M, float(np.sum(np.asarray(w)*delta*(M @ np.asarray(r, float))))` Requires numpy imported as np; returns the trace and the total." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def cohort_run(m0, P, r, n_cycles, rule=\"half\", disc=0.0): assert not (rule == \"simpson\" and n_cycles % 2), \"Simpson rule needs an even number of cycles\"; P = np.asarray(P, float); M = np.vstack([np.asarray(m0, float) @ np.linalg.matrix_power(P, t) for t in range(n_cycles+1)]); w = {\"end\": [0]+[1]*n_cycles, \"start\": [1]*n_cycles+[0], \"half\": [0.5]+[1]*(n_cycles-1)+[0.5], \"simpson\": [1/3]+[4/3 if t % 2 else 2/3 for t in range(1, n_cycles)]+[1/3]}[rule]; delta = (1+disc)**-np.arange(n_cycles+1.0); return M, float(np.sum(np.asarray(w)*delta*(M @ np.asarray(r, float))))" .

hemo:7005167 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Trial summary data to an illustrative QALY gain through Cohen's d"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The function uses the uncorrected d. The article applies Hedges' small-sample correction first and rounds the result to 0.54, which gives 0.0186 QALYs instead of the 0.0188 below; with a pooled estimate from a meta-analysis, the pooled standardised difference would replace d. The logistic conversion is an approximation, nothing is discounted within the duration, and each external input carries its own uncertainty." .

hemo:7005170 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CD-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000790 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=1.814*CohensD` in a cell named LogOR; `=ComparatorResp*EXP(LogOR)/(1-ComparatorResp+ComparatorResp*EXP(LogOR))` in a cell named InterventionResp; `=(InterventionResp-ComparatorResp)*(UtilResp-UtilNon)*Duration` in a cell named QALYGain. CohensD and PooledSD come from the Excel cells of HE-FM-CD-001, and ComparatorResp, UtilResp, UtilNon and Duration are named input cells." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1.814*CohensD" .

hemo:7005171 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CD-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000790 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`cd_qaly_gain <- function(m1, m2, sd1, sd2, n1, n2, p0, u_resp, u_non, years=1) { sp <- sqrt(((n1-1)*sd1^2+(n2-1)*sd2^2)/(n1+n2-2)); d <- (m1-m2)/sp; or <- exp(1.814*d); p1 <- p0*or/(1-p0+p0*or); list(sp=sp, d=d, or=or, p1=p1, qaly=(p1-p0)*(u_resp-u_non)*years) }` Returns a list of the intermediate values and the QALY gain, read with [[\"qaly\"]]." ;
    hemo:9000024 "cd_qaly_gain <- function(m1, m2, sd1, sd2, n1, n2, p0, u_resp, u_non, years=1) { sp <- sqrt(((n1-1)*sd1^2+(n2-1)*sd2^2)/(n1+n2-2)); d <- (m1-m2)/sp; or <- exp(1.814*d); p1 <- p0*or/(1-p0+p0*or); list(sp=sp, d=d, or=or, p1=p1, qaly=(p1-p0)*(u_resp-u_non)*years) }" .

hemo:7005172 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CD-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000790 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def cd_qaly_gain(m1, m2, sd1, sd2, n1, n2, p0, u_resp, u_non, years=1.0): sp = math.sqrt(((n1-1)*sd1**2+(n2-1)*sd2**2)/(n1+n2-2)); d = (m1-m2)/sp; odds_ratio = math.exp(1.814*d); p1 = p0*odds_ratio/(1-p0+p0*odds_ratio); return {\"sp\": sp, \"d\": d, \"or\": odds_ratio, \"p1\": p1, \"qaly\": (p1-p0)*(u_resp-u_non)*years}` Requires the math module; returns a dictionary of the intermediate values and the QALY gain." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def cd_qaly_gain(m1, m2, sd1, sd2, n1, n2, p0, u_resp, u_non, years=1.0): sp = math.sqrt(((n1-1)*sd1**2+(n2-1)*sd2**2)/(n1+n2-2)); d = (m1-m2)/sp; odds_ratio = math.exp(1.814*d); p1 = p0*odds_ratio/(1-p0+p0*odds_ratio); return {\"sp\": sp, \"d\": d, \"or\": odds_ratio, \"p1\": p1, \"qaly\": (p1-p0)*(u_resp-u_non)*years}" .

hemo:7005175 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Case weights, hospital casemix, case payment and cost ratio from costing data"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The hospital's cases are grouped on the same classification and coding rules as the costing sample, each case receives one basic payment with no outlier or add-on payments, and the reference cost of one unit of weight is the sample mean c_bar." .

hemo:7005178 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CWT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000791 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=SUMPRODUCT(SampleCases,SampleMeanCost)/SUM(SampleCases)` in a cell named RefUnitCost; `=SampleMeanCost/RefUnitCost` beside each group, giving the range GroupWeights; `=SUMPRODUCT(HospitalCases,GroupWeights)` in a cell named Casemix; then `=Casemix/SUM(HospitalCases)` for the average weight, `=Casemix*BaseRate*HospitalAdj` for the payment and `=HospitalCost/(Casemix*RefUnitCost)` for the cost ratio. All ranges are the same size and in the same group order." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(SampleCases,SampleMeanCost)/SUM(SampleCases)" .

hemo:7005179 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CWT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000791 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`case_weights <- function(n_k, cbar_k, n_hg, B, a_h, C_h) { cbar <- sum(n_k * cbar_k) / sum(n_k); w <- cbar_k / cbar; cm <- sum(n_hg * w); list(cbar = cbar, w = w, cm = cm, cmi = cm / sum(n_hg), pay = cm * B * a_h, ratio = C_h / (cm * cbar)) }` Returns a list; for example case_weights(c(600, 300, 100), c(2000, 5000, 13000), c(200, 50, 10), 4000, 1, 858000)[[\"ratio\"]] gives 1.1." ;
    hemo:9000024 "case_weights <- function(n_k, cbar_k, n_hg, B, a_h, C_h) { cbar <- sum(n_k * cbar_k) / sum(n_k); w <- cbar_k / cbar; cm <- sum(n_hg * w); list(cbar = cbar, w = w, cm = cm, cmi = cm / sum(n_hg), pay = cm * B * a_h, ratio = C_h / (cm * cbar)) }" .

hemo:7005180 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CWT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000791 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def case_weights(n_k, cbar_k, n_hg, B, a_h, C_h): cbar = sum(n * c for n, c in zip(n_k, cbar_k)) / sum(n_k); w = [c / cbar for c in cbar_k]; cm = sum(n * x for n, x in zip(n_hg, w)); return {\"cbar\": cbar, \"w\": w, \"cm\": cm, \"cmi\": cm / sum(n_hg), \"pay\": cm * B * a_h, \"ratio\": C_h / (cm * cbar)}` Takes lists in the same group order and returns a dictionary of the outputs." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def case_weights(n_k, cbar_k, n_hg, B, a_h, C_h): cbar = sum(n * c for n, c in zip(n_k, cbar_k)) / sum(n_k); w = [c / cbar for c in cbar_k]; cm = sum(n * x for n, x in zip(n_hg, w)); return {\"cbar\": cbar, \"w\": w, \"cm\": cm, \"cmi\": cm / sum(n_hg), \"pay\": cm * B * a_h, \"ratio\": C_h / (cm * cbar)}" .

hemo:7005183 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Proportion of days covered from a same-drug fill history with overlap shifting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "All fills are of the same drug (the same active ingredient), the dispensing record is complete, each fill's supply starts on its dispensing day and the patient finishes the earlier supply before starting a refill. For a class measure with different drugs, overlaps between drugs are not shifted and the union of covered days is counted instead." .

hemo:7005186 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-PDC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000792 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=MAX(B3,D2+C2)` With dispensing days in column B and days' supply in column C from row 2, sorted by date, D2 holds `=B2` and this formula, filled down from D3, gives the shifted starts. E2 holds `=MAX(0,MIN(C2,EndDay-D2+1))`, filled down, and `=SUM(E2:E6)/(EndDay-B2+1)` returns the PDC for five fills." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MAX(B3,D2+C2)" .

hemo:7005187 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-PDC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000792 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`pdc <- function(fill_day, supply, end_day, shift = TRUE) { o <- order(fill_day); f <- fill_day[o]; s <- supply[o]; if (shift) { a <- f; for (i in seq_along(f)[-1]) a[i] <- max(f[i], a[i-1]+s[i-1]); covered <- sum(pmax(0, pmin(s, end_day-a+1))) } else { days <- unique(unlist(mapply(function(x, y) x:(x+y-1), f, s, SIMPLIFY = FALSE))); covered <- sum(days <= end_day) }; covered/(end_day-f[1]+1) }` Returns 0.8333 for the article's fills and 0.7444 with shift = FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000024 "pdc <- function(fill_day, supply, end_day, shift = TRUE) { o <- order(fill_day); f <- fill_day[o]; s <- supply[o]; if (shift) { a <- f; for (i in seq_along(f)[-1]) a[i] <- max(f[i], a[i-1]+s[i-1]); covered <- sum(pmax(0, pmin(s, end_day-a+1))) } else { days <- unique(unlist(mapply(function(x, y) x:(x+y-1), f, s, SIMPLIFY = FALSE))); covered <- sum(days <= end_day) }; covered/(end_day-f[1]+1) }" .

hemo:7005188 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-PDC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000792 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def pdc(fill_day, supply, end_day): import itertools; arr = list(itertools.accumulate(sorted(zip(fill_day, supply)), lambda p, c: (max(c[0], p[0]+p[1]), c[1]))); return sum(max(0, min(s, end_day-a+1)) for a, s in arr)/(end_day-arr[0][0]+1)` Each element of arr is a shifted start and its supply. The merged-overlap version is `len({d for f, s in zip(fill_day, supply) for d in range(f, f+s) if d <= end_day})/(end_day-min(fill_day)+1)`." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def pdc(fill_day, supply, end_day): import itertools; arr = list(itertools.accumulate(sorted(zip(fill_day, supply)), lambda p, c: (max(c[0], p[0]+p[1]), c[1]))); return sum(max(0, min(s, end_day-a+1)) for a, s in arr)/(end_day-arr[0][0]+1)" .

hemo:7005191 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Medication possession ratio variants from a fill history and window"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The fills are of one medicine, the dispensing record is complete and dispensed supply is taken as directed. The refill-interval ratios need at least two fills on different days." .

hemo:7005194 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-MPR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000793 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=SUMIFS(Supplies,FillDays,\"<=\"&EndDay)/(EndDay-MIN(FillDays)+1)` With dispensing days in a range named FillDays, days' supply in a range named Supplies and the end day in a cell named EndDay, this returns the fixed-interval MPR. The refill-interval MPR is `=SUM(Supplies)/(MAX(FillDays)-MIN(FillDays)+INDEX(Supplies,MATCH(MAX(FillDays),FillDays,0)))` for fills up to the end day." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMIFS(Supplies,FillDays,\"<=\"&EndDay)/(EndDay-MIN(FillDays)+1)" .

hemo:7005195 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-MPR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000793 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`mpr <- function(fill_day, supply, end_day) { k <- fill_day <= end_day; o <- order(fill_day[k]); f <- fill_day[k][o]; s <- supply[k][o]; n <- length(f); fixed <- sum(s)/(end_day-f[1]+1); c(fixed = fixed, refill = sum(s)/(f[n]-f[1]+s[n]), mprm = (sum(s)-s[n])/(f[n]-f[1]), capped = min(fixed, 1)) }` Returns 1.1667, 1.0553, 1.0651 and 1 for the article's fills." ;
    hemo:9000024 "mpr <- function(fill_day, supply, end_day) { k <- fill_day <= end_day; o <- order(fill_day[k]); f <- fill_day[k][o]; s <- supply[k][o]; n <- length(f); fixed <- sum(s)/(end_day-f[1]+1); c(fixed = fixed, refill = sum(s)/(f[n]-f[1]+s[n]), mprm = (sum(s)-s[n])/(f[n]-f[1]), capped = min(fixed, 1)) }" .

hemo:7005196 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-MPR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000793 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def mpr(fill_day, supply, end_day): fs = sorted((f, s) for f, s in zip(fill_day, supply) if f <= end_day); tot = sum(s for _, s in fs); fixed = tot/(end_day-fs[0][0]+1); return {\"fixed\": fixed, \"refill\": tot/(fs[-1][0]-fs[0][0]+fs[-1][1]), \"mprm\": (tot-fs[-1][1])/(fs[-1][0]-fs[0][0]), \"capped\": min(fixed, 1)}` With a single fill the MPRm divides by zero, so such patients are handled separately." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def mpr(fill_day, supply, end_day): fs = sorted((f, s) for f, s in zip(fill_day, supply) if f <= end_day); tot = sum(s for _, s in fs); fixed = tot/(end_day-fs[0][0]+1); return {\"fixed\": fixed, \"refill\": tot/(fs[-1][0]-fs[0][0]+fs[-1][1]), \"mprm\": (tot-fs[-1][1])/(fs[-1][0]-fs[0][0]), \"capped\": min(fixed, 1)}" .

hemo:7005199 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Begg test from study effect estimates and standard errors"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "No tied standardised effects or tied variances, so the null variance needs no tie correction; a tied pair counts as neither concordant nor discordant, and with ties the variance is only approximate. With fewer than 10 studies, or studies of similar size, the Cochrane Handbook advises against tests for funnel plot asymmetry; it does not name the rank correlation test, so applying this to the Begg test is an inference." .

hemo:7005201 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BEGG-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000794 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=SUMPRODUCT(SIGN((StdEffects-TRANSPOSE(StdEffects))*(SEs-TRANSPOSE(SEs))))/2` With the estimates in a range named Effects, the standard errors in SEs, the weights `=1/SEs^2` in Weights, the pooled estimate `=SUMPRODUCT(Effects,Weights)/SUM(Weights)` in PooledEffect and the standardised effects `=(Effects-PooledEffect)/SQRT(SEs^2-1/SUM(Weights))` in StdEffects, this formula returns Kendall's score S with each pair counted once (the full matrix counts each pair twice). In versions before Excel 365 the range formulas are entered as array formulas." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(SIGN((StdEffects-TRANSPOSE(StdEffects))*(SEs-TRANSPOSE(SEs))))/2" .

hemo:7005202 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BEGG-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000794 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`begg_test <- function(effect, se) { v <- se^2; W <- sum(1/v); pooled <- sum(effect/v)/W; t <- (effect-pooled)/sqrt(v-1/W); K <- length(v); S <- sum(sign(outer(t, t, \"-\")*outer(v, v, \"-\")))/2; z <- S/sqrt(K*(K-1)*(2*K+5)/18); c(tau = S/(K*(K-1)/2), z = z, p = 2*pnorm(-abs(z))) }` Returns tau -0.511, z -2.06 and p 0.040 for the article's trials; with t and v computed as in the function, `cor.test(t, v, method = \"kendall\")` gives the same tau with the exact p-value of about 0.047." ;
    hemo:9000024 "begg_test <- function(effect, se) { v <- se^2; W <- sum(1/v); pooled <- sum(effect/v)/W; t <- (effect-pooled)/sqrt(v-1/W); K <- length(v); S <- sum(sign(outer(t, t, \"-\")*outer(v, v, \"-\")))/2; z <- S/sqrt(K*(K-1)*(2*K+5)/18); c(tau = S/(K*(K-1)/2), z = z, p = 2*pnorm(-abs(z))) }" .

hemo:7005203 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BEGG-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000794 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def begg_test(effect, se): import math, itertools; v = [s**2 for s in se]; W = sum(1/x for x in v); pooled = sum(e/x for e, x in zip(effect, v))/W; t = [(e-pooled)/math.sqrt(x-1/W) for e, x in zip(effect, v)]; K = len(v); S = sum(((t[i]-t[j])*(v[i]-v[j]) > 0)-((t[i]-t[j])*(v[i]-v[j]) < 0) for i, j in itertools.combinations(range(K), 2)); z = S/math.sqrt(K*(K-1)*(2*K+5)/18); return S/(K*(K-1)/2), z, math.erfc(abs(z)/math.sqrt(2))` Returns (-0.5111, -2.0572, 0.0397) for the article's trials." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def begg_test(effect, se): import math, itertools; v = [s**2 for s in se]; W = sum(1/x for x in v); pooled = sum(e/x for e, x in zip(effect, v))/W; t = [(e-pooled)/math.sqrt(x-1/W) for e, x in zip(effect, v)]; K = len(v); S = sum(((t[i]-t[j])*(v[i]-v[j]) > 0)-((t[i]-t[j])*(v[i]-v[j]) < 0) for i, j in itertools.combinations(range(K), 2)); z = S/math.sqrt(K*(K-1)*(2*K+5)/18); return S/(K*(K-1)/2), z, math.erfc(abs(z)/math.sqrt(2))" .

hemo:7005206 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Hazard ratio, interval and cycle probabilities from event counts and person-time"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Both hazards are constant over follow-up and over the modelled cycles, so the exponential model holds in each arm and the hazard ratio is constant. Only one event leads out of the starting state; with competing exits the cause-specific rates are separated first." .

hemo:7005209 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-HR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000795 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=(TreatmentEvents/TreatmentPersonTime)/(ComparatorEvents/ComparatorPersonTime)` returns the hazard ratio in RateHR; `=EXP(LN(RateHR)-1.96*SQRT(1/TreatmentEvents+1/ComparatorEvents))` and the same with a plus sign return the limits in LowerHR and UpperHR; with the cycle length in years in a cell named CycleYears, `=1-EXP(-ComparatorEvents/ComparatorPersonTime*CycleYears)` returns the comparator probability in CompProb; and `=1-(1-CompProb)^RateHR` returns the treatment probability." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(TreatmentEvents/TreatmentPersonTime)/(ComparatorEvents/ComparatorPersonTime)" .

hemo:7005210 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-HR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000795 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`hr_counts <- function(d_T, PT_T, d_C, PT_C, u, z = 1.96) { h_T <- d_T/PT_T; h_C <- d_C/PT_C; HR <- h_T/h_C; se <- sqrt(1/d_T+1/d_C); p_C <- 1-exp(-h_C*u); c(HR = HR, SE_beta = se, HR_L = exp(log(HR)-z*se), HR_U = exp(log(HR)+z*se), p_C = p_C, p_T = 1-(1-p_C)^HR) }` Returns 0.7, 0.155839, 0.515757, 0.950059, 0.095163 and 0.067606 for the first example; given a vector argument, such as several cycle lengths, it returns each output that depends on it once per element (p_C1, p_C2 and so on) in one named vector." ;
    hemo:9000024 "hr_counts <- function(d_T, PT_T, d_C, PT_C, u, z = 1.96) { h_T <- d_T/PT_T; h_C <- d_C/PT_C; HR <- h_T/h_C; se <- sqrt(1/d_T+1/d_C); p_C <- 1-exp(-h_C*u); c(HR = HR, SE_beta = se, HR_L = exp(log(HR)-z*se), HR_U = exp(log(HR)+z*se), p_C = p_C, p_T = 1-(1-p_C)^HR) }" .

hemo:7005211 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-HR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000795 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def hr_counts(d_T, PT_T, d_C, PT_C, u, z=1.96): import math; h_T = d_T/PT_T; h_C = d_C/PT_C; HR = h_T/h_C; se = math.sqrt(1/d_T+1/d_C); p_C = 1-math.exp(-h_C*u); return {\"HR\": HR, \"SE_beta\": se, \"HR_L\": math.exp(math.log(HR)-z*se), \"HR_U\": math.exp(math.log(HR)+z*se), \"p_C\": p_C, \"p_T\": 1-(1-p_C)**HR}` Returns 0.7, 0.155839, 0.515757, 0.950059, 0.095163 and 0.067606 for the first example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def hr_counts(d_T, PT_T, d_C, PT_C, u, z=1.96): import math; h_T = d_T/PT_T; h_C = d_C/PT_C; HR = h_T/h_C; se = math.sqrt(1/d_T+1/d_C); p_C = 1-math.exp(-h_C*u); return {\"HR\": HR, \"SE_beta\": se, \"HR_L\": math.exp(math.log(HR)-z*se), \"HR_U\": math.exp(math.log(HR)+z*se), \"p_C\": p_C, \"p_T\": 1-(1-p_C)**HR}" .

hemo:7005214 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Markov cycle probabilities and survival trace from a Weibull cumulative hazard"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The Weibull curve holds over the whole horizon in the TSD 21 parameterisation, the event is the only exit from the state, time runs from model start, and the hazard ratio is constant and comes from the same parametric model fitted with treatment as a covariate." .

hemo:7005217 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CUMH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000796 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With Lambda, Gamma and CycleLen named and cycle numbers 0, 1, 2 and so on in column A from A2, `=Lambda*(A2*CycleLen)^Gamma` in B2 filled down gives H, `=EXP(-B2)` in C2 gives survival, `=1-EXP(-(B3-B2))` in D3 gives the control probability and `=1-EXP(-HazardRatio*(B3-B2))` in E3 the treated probability." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=Lambda*(A2*CycleLen)^Gamma" .

hemo:7005218 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CUMH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000796 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`cumhaz_cycles <- function(lambda, gamma, Delta, n_cyc, HR = 1) { t <- (0:n_cyc)*Delta; H <- lambda*t^gamma; dH <- diff(H); list(H = H[-1], S = exp(-H[-1]), dH = dH, p = 1-exp(-dH), p_T = 1-exp(-HR*dH)) }` Returns p of 0.095163, 0.167101, 0.210829, 0.244507, 0.272422 and 0.296481 and a final S of 0.229996 for the first example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "cumhaz_cycles <- function(lambda, gamma, Delta, n_cyc, HR = 1) { t <- (0:n_cyc)*Delta; H <- lambda*t^gamma; dH <- diff(H); list(H = H[-1], S = exp(-H[-1]), dH = dH, p = 1-exp(-dH), p_T = 1-exp(-HR*dH)) }" .

hemo:7005219 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CUMH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000796 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def cumhaz_cycles(lam, gam, Delta, n_cyc, HR=1): import math; H = [lam*(k*Delta)**gam for k in range(n_cyc+1)]; dH = [H[k]-H[k-1] for k in range(1, n_cyc+1)]; return {\"H\": H[1:], \"S\": [math.exp(-h) for h in H[1:]], \"p\": [1-math.exp(-d) for d in dH], \"p_T\": [1-math.exp(-HR*d) for d in dH]}` Returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def cumhaz_cycles(lam, gam, Delta, n_cyc, HR=1): import math; H = [lam*(k*Delta)**gam for k in range(n_cyc+1)]; dH = [H[k]-H[k-1] for k in range(1, n_cyc+1)]; return {\"H\": H[1:], \"S\": [math.exp(-h) for h in H[1:]], \"p\": [1-math.exp(-d) for d in dH], \"p_T\": [1-math.exp(-HR*d) for d in dH]}" .

hemo:7005222 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Feldstein and Gruber deadweight loss reduction across insurance units"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Constant cost, constant elasticity over the range of prices, out-of-pocket spending below any cap under both plans, and the change in risk bearing valued separately." .

hemo:7005225 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-DWL-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000797 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With spending in column B and coinsurance in column C from row 2, and NewCoins and Elasticity named, `=B2*(C2/NewCoins)^Elasticity` in D2 gives E_1 and `=(B2-D2)*(1-NewCoins)+0.5*(NewCoins-C2)*(B2-D2)` in E2 the reduction; `=AVERAGE(E2:E1000)` averages it." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=B2*(C2/NewCoins)^Elasticity" .

hemo:7005226 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-DWL-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000797 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`fg_dwl <- function(E_0, P_0, P_1, eta) { E_1 <- E_0*(P_0/P_1)^eta; d <- (E_0-E_1)*(1-P_1)+0.5*(P_1-P_0)*(E_0-E_1); list(E_1 = E_1, dDWL = d, mean_dDWL = mean(d)) }` Returns reductions of 84.8054, 339.2218 and 1356.8871 and a mean of 593.6381 for the first example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "fg_dwl <- function(E_0, P_0, P_1, eta) { E_1 <- E_0*(P_0/P_1)^eta; d <- (E_0-E_1)*(1-P_1)+0.5*(P_1-P_0)*(E_0-E_1); list(E_1 = E_1, dDWL = d, mean_dDWL = mean(d)) }" .

hemo:7005227 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-DWL-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000797 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def fg_dwl(E_0, P_0, P_1, eta): E_1 = [e*(p/P_1)**eta for e, p in zip(E_0, P_0)]; d = [(e-f)*(1-P_1)+0.5*(P_1-p)*(e-f) for e, f, p in zip(E_0, E_1, P_0)]; return {\"E_1\": E_1, \"dDWL\": d, \"mean_dDWL\": sum(d)/len(d)}` Returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def fg_dwl(E_0, P_0, P_1, eta): E_1 = [e*(p/P_1)**eta for e, p in zip(E_0, P_0)]; d = [(e-f)*(1-P_1)+0.5*(P_1-p)*(e-f) for e, f, p in zip(E_0, E_1, P_0)]; return {\"E_1\": E_1, \"dDWL\": d, \"mean_dDWL\": sum(d)/len(d)}" .

hemo:7005230 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Maxdiff and sequential probabilities for every best and worst pair in a choice set"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Logit choice with a common error scale for the set; best and worst come from the same set; under the sequential model the worst choice is made from the items left after the best is removed." .

hemo:7005233 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BWS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000798 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the set's utilities in a column range named SetUtils, `=EXP(SetUtils-TRANSPOSE(SetUtils))*(1-MUNIT(ROWS(SetUtils)))` spills the maxdiff numerators, held in a range named MaxDiffNum, and `=MaxDiffNum/SUM(MaxDiffNum)` the maxdiff matrix; `=EXP(SetUtils)/SUM(EXP(SetUtils))*EXP(-TRANSPOSE(SetUtils))/(SUM(EXP(-SetUtils))-EXP(-SetUtils))*(1-MUNIT(ROWS(SetUtils)))` spills the sequential matrix." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=EXP(SetUtils-TRANSPOSE(SetUtils))*(1-MUNIT(ROWS(SetUtils)))" .

hemo:7005234 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BWS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000798 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`bw_probs <- function(V) { J <- length(V); N <- exp(outer(V, V, \"-\")); diag(N) <- 0; P_seq <- outer(1:J, 1:J, function(b, w) exp(V[b])/sum(exp(V))*exp(-V[w])/(sum(exp(-V))-exp(-V[b]))); diag(P_seq) <- 0; list(P_max = N/sum(N), P_seq = P_seq) }` Returns 0.254022 and 0.230476 in row 1, column 4 for the first example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "bw_probs <- function(V) { J <- length(V); N <- exp(outer(V, V, \"-\")); diag(N) <- 0; P_seq <- outer(1:J, 1:J, function(b, w) exp(V[b])/sum(exp(V))*exp(-V[w])/(sum(exp(-V))-exp(-V[b]))); diag(P_seq) <- 0; list(P_max = N/sum(N), P_seq = P_seq) }" .

hemo:7005235 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BWS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000798 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def bw_probs(V): import math; J = len(V); D = sum(math.exp(V[j]-V[k]) for j in range(J) for k in range(J) if j != k); Sp = sum(math.exp(v) for v in V); Sn = sum(math.exp(-v) for v in V); return {\"P_max\": [[math.exp(V[b]-V[w])/D if b != w else 0 for w in range(J)] for b in range(J)], \"P_seq\": [[math.exp(V[b])/Sp*math.exp(-V[w])/(Sn-math.exp(-V[b])) if b != w else 0 for w in range(J)] for b in range(J)]}` Returns the same matrices as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def bw_probs(V): import math; J = len(V); D = sum(math.exp(V[j]-V[k]) for j in range(J) for k in range(J) if j != k); Sp = sum(math.exp(v) for v in V); Sn = sum(math.exp(-v) for v in V); return {\"P_max\": [[math.exp(V[b]-V[w])/D if b != w else 0 for w in range(J)] for b in range(J)], \"P_seq\": [[math.exp(V[b])/Sp*math.exp(-V[w])/(Sn-math.exp(-V[b])) if b != w else 0 for w in range(J)] for b in range(J)]}" .

hemo:7005238 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Interim look with posterior and predictive probabilities and the stopping decision"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Normal prior and normal estimates with known standard errors, independent stages, a final analysis that pools the prior and both stages, and the efficacy rule checked before the futility rule." .

hemo:7005241 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BAD-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000799 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The HE-FM-BAD-001 and HE-FM-BAD-002 formulas with ZFinal set to `=NORM.S.INV(0.975)`, then `=IF(PostProb>0.99,\"stop: conclusive benefit\",IF(PredProb<0.1,\"stop: futility\",\"continue\"))` for the decision." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=NORM.S.INV(0.975)" .

hemo:7005242 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BAD-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000799 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`interim_look <- function(m_0, sd_0, y_1, se_1, se_2, c_eff = 0.99, c_final = 0.975, pp_fut = 0.10) { v_0 <- sd_0^2; v_1 <- 1/(1/v_0+1/se_1^2); m_1 <- v_1*(m_0/v_0+y_1/se_1^2); P_post <- pnorm(m_1/sqrt(v_1)); v_f <- 1/(1/v_1+1/se_2^2); y2_crit <- se_2^2*(qnorm(c_final)/sqrt(v_f)-m_1/v_1); PP <- 1-pnorm((y2_crit-m_1)/sqrt(v_1+se_2^2)); decision <- if (P_post > c_eff) \"stop: conclusive benefit\" else if (PP < pp_fut) \"stop: futility\" else \"continue\"; list(m_1 = m_1, sd_1 = sqrt(v_1), P_post = P_post, y2_crit = y2_crit, PP = PP, decision = decision) }` Returns 1034.48, 742.78, 0.9181, 1104.44, 0.4745 and continue for the first example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "interim_look <- function(m_0, sd_0, y_1, se_1, se_2, c_eff = 0.99, c_final = 0.975, pp_fut = 0.10) { v_0 <- sd_0^2; v_1 <- 1/(1/v_0+1/se_1^2); m_1 <- v_1*(m_0/v_0+y_1/se_1^2); P_post <- pnorm(m_1/sqrt(v_1)); v_f <- 1/(1/v_1+1/se_2^2); y2_crit <- se_2^2*(qnorm(c_final)/sqrt(v_f)-m_1/v_1); PP <- 1-pnorm((y2_crit-m_1)/sqrt(v_1+se_2^2)); decision <- if (P_post > c_eff) \"stop: conclusive benefit\" else if (PP < pp_fut) \"stop: futility\" else \"continue\"; list(m_1 = m_1, sd_1 = sqrt(v_1), P_post = P_post, y2_crit = y2_crit, PP = PP, decision = decision) }" .

hemo:7005243 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BAD-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000799 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def interim_look(m_0, sd_0, y_1, se_1, se_2, c_eff=0.99, c_final=0.975, pp_fut=0.10): from statistics import NormalDist; N = NormalDist(); v_0 = sd_0**2; v_1 = 1/(1/v_0+1/se_1**2); m_1 = v_1*(m_0/v_0+y_1/se_1**2); P_post = N.cdf(m_1/v_1**0.5); v_f = 1/(1/v_1+1/se_2**2); y2_crit = se_2**2*(N.inv_cdf(c_final)/v_f**0.5-m_1/v_1); PP = 1-N.cdf((y2_crit-m_1)/(v_1+se_2**2)**0.5); decision = \"stop: conclusive benefit\" if P_post > c_eff else (\"stop: futility\" if PP < pp_fut else \"continue\"); return {\"m_1\": m_1, \"sd_1\": v_1**0.5, \"P_post\": P_post, \"y2_crit\": y2_crit, \"PP\": PP, \"decision\": decision}` Returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def interim_look(m_0, sd_0, y_1, se_1, se_2, c_eff=0.99, c_final=0.975, pp_fut=0.10): from statistics import NormalDist; N = NormalDist(); v_0 = sd_0**2; v_1 = 1/(1/v_0+1/se_1**2); m_1 = v_1*(m_0/v_0+y_1/se_1**2); P_post = N.cdf(m_1/v_1**0.5); v_f = 1/(1/v_1+1/se_2**2); y2_crit = se_2**2*(N.inv_cdf(c_final)/v_f**0.5-m_1/v_1); PP = 1-N.cdf((y2_crit-m_1)/(v_1+se_2**2)**0.5); decision = \"stop: conclusive benefit\" if P_post > c_eff else (\"stop: futility\" if PP < pp_fut else \"continue\"); return {\"m_1\": m_1, \"sd_1\": v_1**0.5, \"P_post\": P_post, \"y2_crit\": y2_crit, \"PP\": PP, \"decision\": decision}" .

hemo:7005246 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Lee and Liu predictive probability from responses so far"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Binary responses, one response rate, a beta prior and a fixed maximum sample size, with every enrolled patient's response known." .

hemo:7005249 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BAD-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000800 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With i = 0 to m in column A from A2 and X, N, NMax, P0, A0 and B0 named, `=COMBIN(NMax-N,A2)*EXP(GAMMALN(A0+X+A2)+GAMMALN(B0+NMax-X-A2)-GAMMALN(A0+B0+NMax)-GAMMALN(A0+X)-GAMMALN(B0+N-X)+GAMMALN(A0+B0+N))` in B2 gives the weight, `=1-BETA.DIST(P0,A0+X+A2,B0+NMax-X-A2,TRUE)` in C2 the final posterior probability, and `=SUMPRODUCT(B2:B19*(C2:C19>ThetaT))` the predictive probability for m = 17." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=COMBIN(NMax-N,A2)*EXP(GAMMALN(A0+X+A2)+GAMMALN(B0+NMax-X-A2)-GAMMALN(A0+B0+NMax)-GAMMALN(A0+X)-GAMMALN(B0+N-X)+GAMMALN(A0+B0+N))" .

hemo:7005250 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BAD-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000800 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`pp_leeliu <- function(x, n, N_max, p_0, theta_T, a_0 = 1, b_0 = 1) { m <- N_max-n; i <- 0:m; w <- choose(m, i)*exp(lbeta(a_0+x+i, b_0+n-x+m-i)-lbeta(a_0+x, b_0+n-x)); B <- 1-pbeta(p_0, a_0+x+i, b_0+N_max-x-i); list(w = w, B = B, PP = sum(w*(B > theta_T))) }` Returns a PP of 0.565559 for Lee and Liu's example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "pp_leeliu <- function(x, n, N_max, p_0, theta_T, a_0 = 1, b_0 = 1) { m <- N_max-n; i <- 0:m; w <- choose(m, i)*exp(lbeta(a_0+x+i, b_0+n-x+m-i)-lbeta(a_0+x, b_0+n-x)); B <- 1-pbeta(p_0, a_0+x+i, b_0+N_max-x-i); list(w = w, B = B, PP = sum(w*(B > theta_T))) }" .

hemo:7005251 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BAD-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000800 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def pp_leeliu(x, n, N_max, p_0, theta_T, a_0=1, b_0=1): from scipy.stats import betabinom, beta; m = N_max-n; w = [betabinom.pmf(i, m, a_0+x, b_0+n-x) for i in range(m+1)]; B = [beta.sf(p_0, a_0+x+i, b_0+N_max-x-i) for i in range(m+1)]; return {\"w\": w, \"B\": B, \"PP\": sum(wi*(Bi > theta_T) for wi, Bi in zip(w, B))}` Returns the same values as the R function (uses SciPy)." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def pp_leeliu(x, n, N_max, p_0, theta_T, a_0=1, b_0=1): from scipy.stats import betabinom, beta; m = N_max-n; w = [betabinom.pmf(i, m, a_0+x, b_0+n-x) for i in range(m+1)]; B = [beta.sf(p_0, a_0+x+i, b_0+N_max-x-i) for i in range(m+1)]; return {\"w\": w, \"B\": B, \"PP\": sum(wi*(Bi > theta_T) for wi, Bi in zip(w, B))}" .

hemo:7005254 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of New quantities of several products from an own and cross price elasticity matrix"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Constant elasticities over the price range, income and other prices unchanged, and an elasticity matrix estimated for the same population and price measure; insignificant cross terms are kept or set to zero as a stated structural choice." .

hemo:7005257 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-XPE-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000801 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the elasticity matrix in a range named Elasticities, current and new prices in vertical ranges OldPrices and NewPrices and current quantities in OldQty, `=OldQty*EXP(MMULT(Elasticities,LN(NewPrices/OldPrices)))` returns the new quantities as a dynamic array (Excel 365)." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=OldQty*EXP(MMULT(Elasticities,LN(NewPrices/OldPrices)))" .

hemo:7005258 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-XPE-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000801 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`xpe_matrix <- function(Q_0, P_0, P_1, E) { Q_1 <- Q_0*exp(as.vector(E %*% log(P_1/P_0))); list(Q_1 = Q_1, pct = Q_1/Q_0-1) }` Returns new quantities of 2796.9008 and 7012.5496 for the first example, with E entered as `matrix(c(-0.8824, 0.10, 0.3846, -0.30), 2, byrow = TRUE)`." ;
    hemo:9000024 "xpe_matrix <- function(Q_0, P_0, P_1, E) { Q_1 <- Q_0*exp(as.vector(E %*% log(P_1/P_0))); list(Q_1 = Q_1, pct = Q_1/Q_0-1) }" .

hemo:7005259 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-XPE-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000801 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def xpe_matrix(Q_0, P_0, P_1, E): r = [math.log(b/a) for a, b in zip(P_0, P_1)]; Q_1 = [q*math.exp(sum(e*x for e, x in zip(row, r))) for q, row in zip(Q_0, E)]; return {\"Q_1\": Q_1, \"pct\": [b/a-1 for a, b in zip(Q_0, Q_1)]}` Needs `import math`; returns the same values as the R function, with E as a list of rows." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def xpe_matrix(Q_0, P_0, P_1, E): r = [math.log(b/a) for a, b in zip(P_0, P_1)]; Q_1 = [q*math.exp(sum(e*x for e, x in zip(row, r))) for q, row in zip(Q_0, E)]; return {\"Q_1\": Q_1, \"pct\": [b/a-1 for a, b in zip(Q_0, Q_1)]}" .

hemo:7005262 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Group-time effects and event-time averages from cohort mean outcomes"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Parallel trends between each cohort and the never-treated units, no anticipation, treatment that stays on once adopted, and cohort means measured on the same scale in every period." .

hemo:7005265 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-DID-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000802 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With cohort means in rows 2 and 3 and never-treated means in row 4, years in B1:F1 and each cohort's base-period column found by MATCH, `=(D2-INDEX($B2:$F2,MATCH($H2-1,$B$1:$F$1,0)))-(D$4-INDEX($B$4:$F$4,MATCH($H2-1,$B$1:$F$1,0)))` gives ATT(g,t) for the cohort whose adoption year is in H2 and the period in column D; the size-weighted average at each e uses SUMPRODUCT of effects and cohort sizes divided by SUM of the sizes observed at that e." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(D2-INDEX($B2:$F2,MATCH($H2-1,$B$1:$F$1,0)))-(D$4-INDEX($B$4:$F$4,MATCH($H2-1,$B$1:$F$1,0)))" .

hemo:7005266 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-DID-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000802 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`att_gt <- function(Y, years, g, n) { N <- Y[nrow(Y), ]; out <- NULL; for (k in seq_along(g)) { b <- which(years == g[k]-1); for (j in which(years >= g[k])) out <- rbind(out, data.frame(g = g[k], t = years[j], e = years[j]-g[k], n = n[k], ATT = (Y[k, j]-Y[k, b])-(N[j]-N[b]))) }; list(att = out, event = sapply(split(out, out$e), function(d) sum(d$n*d$ATT)/sum(d$n))) }` With `Y <- rbind(c(60, 59, 55, 53, 51), c(55, 54, 53, 50, 49), c(50, 49, 48, 47, 46))`, `att_gt(Y, 2021:2025, c(2023, 2024), c(3, 2))` returns the effects and event-time averages of the first example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "att_gt <- function(Y, years, g, n) { N <- Y[nrow(Y), ]; out <- NULL; for (k in seq_along(g)) { b <- which(years == g[k]-1); for (j in which(years >= g[k])) out <- rbind(out, data.frame(g = g[k], t = years[j], e = years[j]-g[k], n = n[k], ATT = (Y[k, j]-Y[k, b])-(N[j]-N[b]))) }; list(att = out, event = sapply(split(out, out$e), function(d) sum(d$n*d$ATT)/sum(d$n))) }" .

hemo:7005267 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-DID-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000802 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def att_gt(Y, years, g, n): N = Y[-1]; att = [(gk, t, t-gk, nk, (Y[k][j]-Y[k][years.index(gk-1)])-(N[j]-N[years.index(gk-1)])) for k, (gk, nk) in enumerate(zip(g, n)) for j, t in enumerate(years) if t >= gk]; ev = {e: sum(r[3]*r[4] for r in att if r[2] == e)/sum(r[3] for r in att if r[2] == e) for e in sorted({r[2] for r in att})}; return {\"att\": att, \"event\": ev}` Returns the same values as the R function, with Y as a list of rows." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def att_gt(Y, years, g, n): N = Y[-1]; att = [(gk, t, t-gk, nk, (Y[k][j]-Y[k][years.index(gk-1)])-(N[j]-N[years.index(gk-1)])) for k, (gk, nk) in enumerate(zip(g, n)) for j, t in enumerate(years) if t >= gk]; ev = {e: sum(r[3]*r[4] for r in att if r[2] == e)/sum(r[3] for r in att if r[2] == e) for e in sorted({r[2] for r in att})}; return {\"att\": att, \"event\": ev}" .

hemo:7005270 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Decision curve from predicted risks and outcomes over a threshold grid"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "One validation sample with known outcomes for every patient (no censoring), patients classed positive when their risk is at or above the threshold, and the threshold range set before the curve is read." .

hemo:7005273 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-DCA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000803 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With risks in A2:A101, outcomes in B2:B101 and a threshold in D2, `=COUNTIFS(A$2:A$101,\">=\"&D2,B$2:B$101,1)/COUNT(B$2:B$101)-COUNTIFS(A$2:A$101,\">=\"&D2,B$2:B$101,0)/COUNT(B$2:B$101)*D2/(1-D2)` in E2 gives the model's net benefit and `=AVERAGE(B$2:B$101)-(1-AVERAGE(B$2:B$101))*D2/(1-D2)` in F2 the net benefit of intervening in everyone; fill down for a column of thresholds." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=COUNTIFS(A$2:A$101,\">=\"&D2,B$2:B$101,1)/COUNT(B$2:B$101)-COUNTIFS(A$2:A$101,\">=\"&D2,B$2:B$101,0)/COUNT(B$2:B$101)*D2/(1-D2)" .

hemo:7005274 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-DCA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000803 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`dca <- function(risk, y, p_t) { n <- length(y); sapply(p_t, function(p) { pos <- risk >= p; w <- p/(1-p); m <- sum(pos & y == 1)/n-sum(pos & y == 0)/n*w; a <- mean(y)-(1-mean(y))*w; c(p_t = p, NB_model = m, NB_all = a, NB_none = 0, avoided = (m-a)/w*100) }) }` With `risk <- rep(c(0.10, 0.30, 0.60), c(50, 30, 20))` and `y <- c(rep(1, 4), rep(0, 46), rep(1, 9), rep(0, 21), rep(1, 13), rep(0, 7))`, `dca(risk, y, c(0.15, 0.25, 0.40))` returns the values of the first example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "dca <- function(risk, y, p_t) { n <- length(y); sapply(p_t, function(p) { pos <- risk >= p; w <- p/(1-p); m <- sum(pos & y == 1)/n-sum(pos & y == 0)/n*w; a <- mean(y)-(1-mean(y))*w; c(p_t = p, NB_model = m, NB_all = a, NB_none = 0, avoided = (m-a)/w*100) }) }" .

hemo:7005275 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-DCA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000803 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def dca(risk, y, p_t): n = len(y); pi = sum(y)/n; return [{\"p_t\": p, \"NB_model\": m, \"NB_all\": a, \"NB_none\": 0, \"avoided\": (m-a)*(1-p)/p*100} for p in p_t for m, a in [(sum(v for r, v in zip(risk, y) if r >= p)/n-sum(1-v for r, v in zip(risk, y) if r >= p)/n*p/(1-p), pi-(1-pi)*p/(1-p))]]` Returns the same values as the R function for the same inputs." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def dca(risk, y, p_t): n = len(y); pi = sum(y)/n; return [{\"p_t\": p, \"NB_model\": m, \"NB_all\": a, \"NB_none\": 0, \"avoided\": (m-a)*(1-p)/p*100} for p in p_t for m, a in [(sum(v for r, v in zip(risk, y) if r >= p)/n-sum(1-v for r, v in zip(risk, y) if r >= p)/n*p/(1-p), pi-(1-pi)*p/(1-p))]]" .

hemo:7005278 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Comorbidity-adjusted and cause-attributed YLDs for any number of independent conditions"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Independent occurrence within the population group, multiplicative combination of weights, apportioning in proportion to stand-alone weights, and prevalence over one year with no age weighting or discounting." .

hemo:7005281 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-DWT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000804 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For any number of conditions, the total is `=Population*(1-PRODUCT(1-PrevRange*WeightRange))`, with the population in a cell named Population, (array formula in older versions); cause attribution needs one row per combination of conditions, with its probability from PRODUCT of p or 1 minus p, its combined weight from 1 minus PRODUCT of 1 minus the weights present, and each condition's share from SUMPRODUCT." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=Population*(1-PRODUCT(1-PrevRange*WeightRange))" .

hemo:7005282 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-DWT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000804 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`yld_comorb <- function(N, p, DW) { K <- length(p); S <- as.matrix(expand.grid(rep(list(0:1), K))); pr <- apply(S, 1, function(s) prod(ifelse(s == 1, p, 1-p))); dw <- apply(S, 1, function(s) 1-prod(1-DW*s)); sh <- t(apply(S, 1, function(s) if (sum(s) == 0) 0*s else DW*s/sum(DW*s))); list(total = N*sum(pr*dw), by_condition = N*colSums(pr*dw*sh), unadjusted = N*sum(p*DW)) }` `yld_comorb(10000, c(0.10, 0.05), c(0.10, 0.30))` returns 248.5, 99.625 and 148.875, and 250 unadjusted." ;
    hemo:9000024 "yld_comorb <- function(N, p, DW) { K <- length(p); S <- as.matrix(expand.grid(rep(list(0:1), K))); pr <- apply(S, 1, function(s) prod(ifelse(s == 1, p, 1-p))); dw <- apply(S, 1, function(s) 1-prod(1-DW*s)); sh <- t(apply(S, 1, function(s) if (sum(s) == 0) 0*s else DW*s/sum(DW*s))); list(total = N*sum(pr*dw), by_condition = N*colSums(pr*dw*sh), unadjusted = N*sum(p*DW)) }" .

hemo:7005283 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-DWT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000804 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def yld_comorb(N, p, DW): S = list(itertools.product([0, 1], repeat=len(p))); pr = [math.prod(q if b else 1-q for q, b in zip(p, s)) for s in S]; dw = [1-math.prod(1-d*b for d, b in zip(DW, s)) for s in S]; by = [N*sum(w*c*d/sum(x*b for x, b in zip(DW, s)) for w, c, s in zip(pr, dw, S) if s[k]) for k, d in enumerate(DW)]; return {\"total\": N*sum(w*c for w, c in zip(pr, dw)), \"by_condition\": by, \"unadjusted\": N*sum(q*d for q, d in zip(p, DW))}` Needs `import itertools, math` (Python 3.8 or later for math.prod); returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def yld_comorb(N, p, DW): S = list(itertools.product([0, 1], repeat=len(p))); pr = [math.prod(q if b else 1-q for q, b in zip(p, s)) for s in S]; dw = [1-math.prod(1-d*b for d, b in zip(DW, s)) for s in S]; by = [N*sum(w*c*d/sum(x*b for x, b in zip(DW, s)) for w, c, s in zip(pr, dw, S) if s[k]) for k, d in enumerate(DW)]; return {\"total\": N*sum(w*c for w, c in zip(pr, dw)), \"by_condition\": by, \"unadjusted\": N*sum(q*d for q, d in zip(p, DW))}" .

hemo:7005286 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of K-means clustering with within-cluster sum of squares and silhouettes"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Quantitative variables scaled as intended before clustering, squared Euclidean distance for assignment and a fixed K; the result depends on the starts, so in practice several starts are run and the lowest W(K) kept." .

hemo:7005289 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CLU-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000805 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For two clusters with centre cells CentreA and CentreB (each a row of the variables) and a unit's row Profile, `=IF(SUMXMY2(Profile,CentreA)<=SUMXMY2(Profile,CentreB),\"A\",\"B\")` assigns the unit; each centre is then recomputed with AVERAGEIF over the assignment column, and the two steps are repeated until no assignment changes." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(SUMXMY2(Profile,CentreA)<=SUMXMY2(Profile,CentreB),\"A\",\"B\")" .

hemo:7005290 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CLU-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000805 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`kmeans_lloyd <- function(X, starts, iter = 100) { X <- as.matrix(X); C <- as.matrix(starts); K <- nrow(C); for (it in 1:iter) { D <- sapply(1:K, function(k) colSums((t(X)-C[k, ])^2)); cl <- max.col(-D, ties.method = \"first\"); C1 <- t(sapply(1:K, function(k) colMeans(X[cl == k, , drop = FALSE]))); if (all(abs(C1-C) < 1e-12)) break; C <- C1 }; W_K <- sum((X-C[cl, ])^2); W_1 <- sum((t(X)-colMeans(X))^2); E <- as.matrix(dist(X)); sil <- sapply(1:nrow(X), function(i) { own <- setdiff(which(cl == cl[i]), i); if (!length(own)) return(0); a <- mean(E[i, own]); b <- min(sapply(setdiff(unique(cl), cl[i]), function(k) mean(E[i, cl == k]))); (b-a)/max(a, b) }); list(cluster = cl, centres = C, W_K = W_K, R_K = 1-W_K/W_1, sil = sil, sil_mean = mean(sil)) }` Base R only; `kmeans_lloyd(rbind(c(0,1), c(1,2), c(2,0), c(5,4), c(6,6), c(4,5)), rbind(c(2,0), c(5,4)))` returns W_K = 8, R_K = 0.8571 and sil_mean = 0.6573. The base function `kmeans(X, centers = starts, algorithm = \"Lloyd\")` gives the same clusters." ;
    hemo:9000024 "kmeans_lloyd <- function(X, starts, iter = 100) { X <- as.matrix(X); C <- as.matrix(starts); K <- nrow(C); for (it in 1:iter) { D <- sapply(1:K, function(k) colSums((t(X)-C[k, ])^2)); cl <- max.col(-D, ties.method = \"first\"); C1 <- t(sapply(1:K, function(k) colMeans(X[cl == k, , drop = FALSE]))); if (all(abs(C1-C) < 1e-12)) break; C <- C1 }; W_K <- sum((X-C[cl, ])^2); W_1 <- sum((t(X)-colMeans(X))^2); E <- as.matrix(dist(X)); sil <- sapply(1:nrow(X), function(i) { own <- setdiff(which(cl == cl[i]), i); if (!length(own)) return(0); a <- mean(E[i, own]); b <- min(sapply(setdiff(unique(cl), cl[i]), function(k) mean(E[i, cl == k]))); (b-a)/max(a, b) }); list(cluster = cl, centres = C, W_K = W_K, R_K = 1-W_K/W_1, sil = sil, sil_mean = mean(sil)) }" .

hemo:7005291 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CLU-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000805 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def kmeans_lloyd(X, C, it=100): K = len(C); sq = lambda u, v: sum((a-b)**2 for a, b in zip(u, v)); cl = [min(range(K), key=lambda k: sq(x, C[k])) for x in X]; C1 = [[sum(x[j] for x, c in zip(X, cl) if c == k)/cl.count(k) for j in range(len(X[0]))] for k in range(K)]; m = [sum(col)/len(X) for col in zip(*X)]; own = lambda i: [j for j in range(len(X)) if cl[j] == cl[i] and j != i]; a = [sum(math.dist(X[i], X[j]) for j in own(i))/len(own(i)) if own(i) else 0 for i in range(len(X))]; b = [min(sum(math.dist(X[i], X[j]) for j in range(len(X)) if cl[j] == k)/cl.count(k) for k in set(cl) if k != cl[i]) for i in range(len(X))]; sil = [(bi-ai)/max(ai, bi) if own(i) else 0 for i, (ai, bi) in enumerate(zip(a, b))]; return kmeans_lloyd(X, C1, it-1) if C1 != C and it > 0 else {\"cluster\": cl, \"centres\": C, \"W_K\": sum(sq(x, C[c]) for x, c in zip(X, cl)), \"R_K\": 1-sum(sq(x, C[c]) for x, c in zip(X, cl))/sum(sq(x, m) for x in X), \"sil\": sil, \"sil_mean\": sum(sil)/len(X)}` Needs `import math`; returns the same values as the R function, with clusters numbered from 0 and X and the starts as lists of rows." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def kmeans_lloyd(X, C, it=100): K = len(C); sq = lambda u, v: sum((a-b)**2 for a, b in zip(u, v)); cl = [min(range(K), key=lambda k: sq(x, C[k])) for x in X]; C1 = [[sum(x[j] for x, c in zip(X, cl) if c == k)/cl.count(k) for j in range(len(X[0]))] for k in range(K)]; m = [sum(col)/len(X) for col in zip(*X)]; own = lambda i: [j for j in range(len(X)) if cl[j] == cl[i] and j != i]; a = [sum(math.dist(X[i], X[j]) for j in own(i))/len(own(i)) if own(i) else 0 for i in range(len(X))]; b = [min(sum(math.dist(X[i], X[j]) for j in range(len(X)) if cl[j] == k)/cl.count(k) for k in set(cl) if k != cl[i]) for i in range(len(X))]; sil = [(bi-ai)/max(ai, bi) if own(i) else 0 for i, (ai, bi) in enumerate(zip(a, b))]; return kmeans_lloyd(X, C1, it-1) if C1 != C and it > 0 else {\"cluster\": cl, \"centres\": C, \"W_K\": sum(sq(x, C[c]) for x, c in zip(X, cl)), \"R_K\": 1-sum(sq(x, C[c]) for x, c in zip(X, cl))/sum(sq(x, m) for x in X), \"sil\": sil, \"sil_mean\": sum(sil)/len(X)}" .

hemo:7005294 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Least-squares part-worths, fit and relative importance from a ratings design"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Ratings on an interval scale, an additive model without interactions and at least as many profiles as parameters; with several respondents the model is fitted per respondent or to pooled data with stated weights." .

hemo:7005297 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CJA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000806 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the ratings in Ratings and the dummy codes in an eight-by-three range AttributeCodes, `=LINEST(Ratings,AttributeCodes,TRUE,TRUE)` returns the part-worths in reverse column order with the intercept last in the first row, and R squared in the first cell of the third row: `=INDEX(LINEST(Ratings,AttributeCodes,TRUE,TRUE),3,1)`." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LINEST(Ratings,AttributeCodes,TRUE,TRUE)" .

hemo:7005298 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CJA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000806 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`partworths <- function(X, y, attr) { fit <- lm.fit(cbind(1, as.matrix(X)), y); b <- fit$coefficients; R2 <- 1-sum(fit$residuals^2)/sum((y-mean(y))^2); rng <- tapply(b[-1], factor(attr, levels = unique(attr)), function(v) max(0, v)-min(0, v)); list(beta = b, R2 = R2, range = rng, RI = rng/sum(rng)) }` Base R only; with `X <- cbind(W = c(0,1,0,1,0,1,0,1), L = c(0,0,1,1,0,0,1,1), N = c(0,0,0,0,1,1,1,1))` and `y <- c(6,3,8,4,5,2,6,4)`, `partworths(X, y, c(\"wait\", \"location\", \"lead\"))` returns the first example's values." ;
    hemo:9000024 "partworths <- function(X, y, attr) { fit <- lm.fit(cbind(1, as.matrix(X)), y); b <- fit$coefficients; R2 <- 1-sum(fit$residuals^2)/sum((y-mean(y))^2); rng <- tapply(b[-1], factor(attr, levels = unique(attr)), function(v) max(0, v)-min(0, v)); list(beta = b, R2 = R2, range = rng, RI = rng/sum(rng)) }" .

hemo:7005299 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CJA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000806 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def partworths(X, y, attr): Z = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(y)), np.asarray(X, float)]); y = np.asarray(y, float); b = np.linalg.lstsq(Z, y, rcond=None)[0]; r = y-Z@b; R2 = 1-r@r/((y-y.mean())@(y-y.mean())); rng = {k: max(0, *b[1:][np.array(attr) == k])-min(0, *b[1:][np.array(attr) == k]) for k in dict.fromkeys(attr)}; return {\"beta\": b, \"R2\": R2, \"range\": rng, \"RI\": {k: v/sum(rng.values()) for k, v in rng.items()}}` Needs `import numpy as np`; returns the same values as the R function, with X as a list of rows." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def partworths(X, y, attr): Z = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(y)), np.asarray(X, float)]); y = np.asarray(y, float); b = np.linalg.lstsq(Z, y, rcond=None)[0]; r = y-Z@b; R2 = 1-r@r/((y-y.mean())@(y-y.mean())); rng = {k: max(0, *b[1:][np.array(attr) == k])-min(0, *b[1:][np.array(attr) == k]) for k in dict.fromkeys(attr)}; return {\"beta\": b, \"R2\": R2, \"range\": rng, \"RI\": {k: v/sum(rng.values()) for k, v in rng.items()}}" .

hemo:7005302 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Transition probability matrix from an intensity matrix by the matrix exponential"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Constant intensities over t (time-homogeneous Markov process); the series of t Q divided by 2 to the power m converges quickly when that scaled matrix is small, which the defaults ensure for annual rates of the size used in health economic models." .

hemo:7005306 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CONT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000807 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Excel has no matrix exponential. With Q in a three-by-three range IntensityQ and the step h = t/1024 in StepH, `=MUNIT(3)+StepH*IntensityQ+MMULT(StepH*IntensityQ,StepH*IntensityQ)/2` gives a one-step matrix A; applying `=MMULT(A,A)` ten times in turn gives P(t) to within about 1E-7 for the examples here." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MUNIT(3)+StepH*IntensityQ+MMULT(StepH*IntensityQ,StepH*IntensityQ)/2" .

hemo:7005307 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CONT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000807 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`expm_ss <- function(Q, t, m = 10, terms = 12) { A <- Q*t/2^m; P <- diag(nrow(Q)); Tk <- diag(nrow(Q)); for (k in 1:terms) { Tk <- Tk %*% A/k; P <- P+Tk }; for (j in 1:m) P <- P %*% P; P }` Base R only; `expm_ss(rbind(c(-0.35, 0.30, 0.05), c(0, -0.40, 0.40), c(0, 0, 0)), 1)` returns the first example's matrix. The Matrix package's `expm()` gives the same result." ;
    hemo:9000024 "expm_ss <- function(Q, t, m = 10, terms = 12) { A <- Q*t/2^m; P <- diag(nrow(Q)); Tk <- diag(nrow(Q)); for (k in 1:terms) { Tk <- Tk %*% A/k; P <- P+Tk }; for (j in 1:m) P <- P %*% P; P }" .

hemo:7005308 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CONT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000807 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def expm_ss(Q, t, m=10, terms=12): n = len(Q); mul = lambda A, B: [[sum(A[i][k]*B[k][j] for k in range(n)) for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]; A = [[q*t/2**m for q in row] for row in Q]; I = [[float(i == j) for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]; T = list(itertools.accumulate(range(1, terms+1), lambda T, k: [[v/k for v in row] for row in mul(T, A)], initial=I)); P = [[sum(Tk[i][j] for Tk in T) for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]; return functools.reduce(lambda P, _: mul(P, P), range(m), P)` Needs `import itertools, functools`; returns the same matrices as the R function, with Q as a list of rows. `scipy.linalg.expm(t*Q)` gives the same result." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def expm_ss(Q, t, m=10, terms=12): n = len(Q); mul = lambda A, B: [[sum(A[i][k]*B[k][j] for k in range(n)) for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]; A = [[q*t/2**m for q in row] for row in Q]; I = [[float(i == j) for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]; T = list(itertools.accumulate(range(1, terms+1), lambda T, k: [[v/k for v in row] for row in mul(T, A)], initial=I)); P = [[sum(Tk[i][j] for Tk in T) for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]; return functools.reduce(lambda P, _: mul(P, P), range(m), P)" .

hemo:7005311 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Bang and Tsiatis weighted mean cost from patient-level censored data"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Censoring independent of costs and survival, follow-up truncated at a limit with patients alive at the limit counted as complete, and distinct times; with ties the order of deaths and censorings must be stated." .

hemo:7005314 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-DCEN-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000808 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With patients sorted by time in rows 2 to 6, status in column B and the number at risk in column D (=COUNT(B$2:B$6)-ROW()+2), censoring survival in C2 is 1 and `=C2*(1-(1-B2)/D2)` filled down from C3 gives each later value; the weighted mean is `=SUMPRODUCT(B2:B6,E2:E6/C2:C6)/COUNT(E2:E6)` with costs in column E." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=C2*(1-(1-B2)/D2)" .

hemo:7005315 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-DCEN-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000808 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`bt_cost <- function(time, status, cost) { o <- order(time); st <- status[o]; A <- cost[o]; N <- length(A); Sc <- cumprod(c(1, 1-(1-st)/(N:1)))[1:N]; w <- st/Sc; list(S_c = Sc, w = w, mu = sum(w*A)/N, naive = mean(A), complete = mean(A[st == 1])) }` Base R only; `bt_cost(c(0.5, 1, 1.5, 2, 3), c(1, 0, 1, 0, 1), c(6000, 5000, 18000, 9000, 12000))` returns mu = 12400." ;
    hemo:9000024 "bt_cost <- function(time, status, cost) { o <- order(time); st <- status[o]; A <- cost[o]; N <- length(A); Sc <- cumprod(c(1, 1-(1-st)/(N:1)))[1:N]; w <- st/Sc; list(S_c = Sc, w = w, mu = sum(w*A)/N, naive = mean(A), complete = mean(A[st == 1])) }" .

hemo:7005316 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-DCEN-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000808 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def bt_cost(time, status, cost): d = sorted(zip(time, status, cost)); N = len(d); Sc = list(itertools.accumulate([1-(1-st)/(N-i) for i, (t, st, c) in enumerate(d)], operator.mul, initial=1))[:N]; w = [st/s for (t, st, c), s in zip(d, Sc)]; return {\"S_c\": Sc, \"w\": w, \"mu\": sum(wi*c for wi, (t, st, c) in zip(w, d))/N, \"naive\": sum(c for t, st, c in d)/N, \"complete\": sum(c for t, st, c in d if st)/sum(st for t, st, c in d)}` Needs `import itertools, operator`; returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def bt_cost(time, status, cost): d = sorted(zip(time, status, cost)); N = len(d); Sc = list(itertools.accumulate([1-(1-st)/(N-i) for i, (t, st, c) in enumerate(d)], operator.mul, initial=1))[:N]; w = [st/s for (t, st, c), s in zip(d, Sc)]; return {\"S_c\": Sc, \"w\": w, \"mu\": sum(wi*c for wi, (t, st, c) in zip(w, d))/N, \"naive\": sum(c for t, st, c in d)/N, \"complete\": sum(c for t, st, c in d if st)/sum(st for t, st, c in d)}" .

hemo:7005319 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Gaussian process posterior and expected improvement over candidate values from any number of runs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Squared exponential kernel with fixed parameters; the best value in expected improvement is the lowest observed loss without noise and the lowest posterior mean at the evaluated values with noise." .

hemo:7005323 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BOPT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000809 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For one parameter, with runs in RunPoints and RunLosses, a candidate in Cand and PriorMean, SFVar, LengthScale and NoiseVar named, KMat is `=SFVar*EXP(-(RunPoints-TRANSPOSE(RunPoints))^2/(2*LengthScale^2))+NoiseVar*MUNIT(ROWS(RunPoints))`, KVec is `=SFVar*EXP(-(Cand-RunPoints)^2/(2*LengthScale^2))`, PostMean is `=PriorMean+SUMPRODUCT(MMULT(MINVERSE(KMat),KVec),RunLosses-PriorMean)`, PostVar is `=SFVar-SUMPRODUCT(KVec,MMULT(MINVERSE(KMat),KVec))`, and expected improvement follows HE-FM-BOPT-004 with NORM.S.DIST." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SFVar*EXP(-(RunPoints-TRANSPOSE(RunPoints))^2/(2*LengthScale^2))+NoiseVar*MUNIT(ROWS(RunPoints))" .

hemo:7005324 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BOPT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000809 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`gp_ei <- function(X, y, cand, mu0, sf2, ell, noise = 0) { X <- as.matrix(X); C <- as.matrix(cand); k <- function(A, B) sf2*exp(-pmax(outer(rowSums(A^2), rowSums(B^2), \"+\")-2*A %*% t(B), 0)/(2*ell^2)); K <- k(X, X)+diag(noise+1e-6, nrow(X)); ks <- k(C, X); m <- as.vector(mu0+ks %*% solve(K, y-mu0)); s <- sqrt(pmax(sf2-rowSums(ks*t(solve(K, t(ks)))), 0)); best <- if (noise > 0) min(mu0+k(X, X) %*% solve(K, y-mu0)) else min(y); z <- (best-m)/pmax(s, 1e-12); data.frame(mean = m, sd = s, ei = ifelse(s > 0, (best-m)*pnorm(z)+s*dnorm(z), pmax(best-m, 0))) }` Base R only; `gp_ei(c(0.01, 0.06), c(20.4382, 16.1385), seq(0.02, 0.05, by = 0.005), 20, 100, 0.02)` returns the first example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "gp_ei <- function(X, y, cand, mu0, sf2, ell, noise = 0) { X <- as.matrix(X); C <- as.matrix(cand); k <- function(A, B) sf2*exp(-pmax(outer(rowSums(A^2), rowSums(B^2), \"+\")-2*A %*% t(B), 0)/(2*ell^2)); K <- k(X, X)+diag(noise+1e-6, nrow(X)); ks <- k(C, X); m <- as.vector(mu0+ks %*% solve(K, y-mu0)); s <- sqrt(pmax(sf2-rowSums(ks*t(solve(K, t(ks)))), 0)); best <- if (noise > 0) min(mu0+k(X, X) %*% solve(K, y-mu0)) else min(y); z <- (best-m)/pmax(s, 1e-12); data.frame(mean = m, sd = s, ei = ifelse(s > 0, (best-m)*pnorm(z)+s*dnorm(z), pmax(best-m, 0))) }" .

hemo:7005325 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BOPT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000809 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def gp_ei(X, y, cand, mu0, sf2, ell, noise=0.0): y = np.asarray(y, float); X = np.asarray(X, float).reshape(len(y), -1); C = np.asarray(cand, float).reshape(len(cand), -1); k = lambda A, B: sf2*np.exp(-((A[:, None, :]-B[None, :, :])**2).sum(-1)/(2*ell**2)); K = k(X, X)+(noise+1e-6)*np.eye(len(y)); ks = k(C, X); m = mu0+ks@np.linalg.solve(K, y-mu0); s = np.sqrt(np.maximum(sf2-(ks*np.linalg.solve(K, ks.T).T).sum(1), 0)); best = (mu0+k(X, X)@np.linalg.solve(K, y-mu0)).min() if noise > 0 else y.min(); z = (best-m)/np.maximum(s, 1e-12); Phi = np.array([0.5*math.erfc(-u/math.sqrt(2)) for u in z]); return {\"mean\": m, \"sd\": s, \"ei\": np.where(s > 0, (best-m)*Phi+s*np.exp(-z**2/2)/math.sqrt(2*math.pi), np.maximum(best-m, 0))}` Needs `import math` and `import numpy as np`; returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def gp_ei(X, y, cand, mu0, sf2, ell, noise=0.0): y = np.asarray(y, float); X = np.asarray(X, float).reshape(len(y), -1); C = np.asarray(cand, float).reshape(len(cand), -1); k = lambda A, B: sf2*np.exp(-((A[:, None, :]-B[None, :, :])**2).sum(-1)/(2*ell**2)); K = k(X, X)+(noise+1e-6)*np.eye(len(y)); ks = k(C, X); m = mu0+ks@np.linalg.solve(K, y-mu0); s = np.sqrt(np.maximum(sf2-(ks*np.linalg.solve(K, ks.T).T).sum(1), 0)); best = (mu0+k(X, X)@np.linalg.solve(K, y-mu0)).min() if noise > 0 else y.min(); z = (best-m)/np.maximum(s, 1e-12); Phi = np.array([0.5*math.erfc(-u/math.sqrt(2)) for u in z]); return {\"mean\": m, \"sd\": s, \"ei\": np.where(s > 0, (best-m)*Phi+s*np.exp(-z**2/2)/math.sqrt(2*math.pi), np.maximum(best-m, 0))}" .

hemo:7005327 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Simple exponential smoothing through a series with its sum of squared errors and next forecast"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "No clear trend or seasonal pattern; the starting level is given or estimated with alpha." .

hemo:7005330 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-DFC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000810 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the series in Observed (rows 2 onwards of column B) and Alpha and InitLevel named, put `=InitLevel` in C2 and `=Alpha*B2+(1-Alpha)*C2` in C3, filled down one row beyond the data; the last cell is the next forecast (NextValue) and `=SUMXMY2(Observed,C2:C7)` the SSE for six observations. Solver can minimise the SSE over Alpha and InitLevel." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=InitLevel" .

hemo:7005331 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-DFC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000810 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`ses_run <- function(y, alpha, l0) { f <- Reduce(function(l, v) alpha*v+(1-alpha)*l, y, accumulate = TRUE, init = l0); n <- length(y); e <- y-f[1:n]; list(fitted = f[1:n], errors = e, SSE = sum(e^2), next_forecast = f[n+1]) }` Base R only; `ses_run(c(1980, 2040, 2010, 2100, 2060, 2120), 0.3, 2000)` returns the first example, fitted values in input order." ;
    hemo:9000024 "ses_run <- function(y, alpha, l0) { f <- Reduce(function(l, v) alpha*v+(1-alpha)*l, y, accumulate = TRUE, init = l0); n <- length(y); e <- y-f[1:n]; list(fitted = f[1:n], errors = e, SSE = sum(e^2), next_forecast = f[n+1]) }" .

hemo:7005332 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-DFC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000810 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def ses_run(y, alpha, l0): f = list(itertools.accumulate(y, lambda l, v: alpha*v+(1-alpha)*l, initial=l0)); e = [v-a for v, a in zip(y, f)]; return {\"fitted\": f[:-1], \"errors\": e, \"SSE\": sum(x*x for x in e), \"next_forecast\": f[-1]}` Needs `import itertools`; returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def ses_run(y, alpha, l0): f = list(itertools.accumulate(y, lambda l, v: alpha*v+(1-alpha)*l, initial=l0)); e = [v-a for v, a in zip(y, f)]; return {\"fitted\": f[:-1], \"errors\": e, \"SSE\": sum(x*x for x in e), \"next_forecast\": f[-1]}" .

hemo:7005334 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Net monetary benefit of two triage tests over a prevalence range and the break-even prevalence"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Sensitivity and specificity are the same at every prevalence, held fixed purely for illustration as in the article; the consequences of each result are as in HE-FM-DXA-005." .

hemo:7005337 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-DXA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000811 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With prevalences in PrevGrid and the inputs named SensA, SpecA, CostA, SensB, SpecB, CostB, GainTP and LossFP, `=PrevGrid*SensA*GainTP-(1-PrevGrid)*(1-SpecA)*LossFP-CostA` and the same for test B spill the two columns, and `=((SpecB-SpecA)*LossFP+CostA-CostB)/(GainTP*(SensA-SensB)+LossFP*(SpecB-SpecA))` returns the break-even prevalence, held in BreakEven." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=PrevGrid*SensA*GainTP-(1-PrevGrid)*(1-SpecA)*LossFP-CostA" .

hemo:7005338 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-DXA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000811 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`nmb_two_tests <- function(p, seA, spA, cA, seB, spB, cB, G, L) { nA <- p*seA*G-(1-p)*(1-spA)*L-cA; nB <- p*seB*G-(1-p)*(1-spB)*L-cB; den <- G*(seA-seB)+L*(spB-spA); data.frame(p = p, nmb_A = nA, nmb_B = nB, best = ifelse(nA >= nB, \"A\", \"B\"), p_even = if (den != 0) ((spB-spA)*L+cA-cB)/den else NA) }` Base R only; `nmb_two_tests(c(0.01, 0.02, 0.05), 0.96, 0.76, 40, 0.80, 0.96, 40, 21600, 400)` returns the first example, rows in input order." ;
    hemo:9000024 "nmb_two_tests <- function(p, seA, spA, cA, seB, spB, cB, G, L) { nA <- p*seA*G-(1-p)*(1-spA)*L-cA; nB <- p*seB*G-(1-p)*(1-spB)*L-cB; den <- G*(seA-seB)+L*(spB-spA); data.frame(p = p, nmb_A = nA, nmb_B = nB, best = ifelse(nA >= nB, \"A\", \"B\"), p_even = if (den != 0) ((spB-spA)*L+cA-cB)/den else NA) }" .

hemo:7005339 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-DXA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000811 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def nmb_two_tests(p, seA, spA, cA, seB, spB, cB, G, L): nA = [q*seA*G-(1-q)*(1-spA)*L-cA for q in p]; nB = [q*seB*G-(1-q)*(1-spB)*L-cB for q in p]; den = G*(seA-seB)+L*(spB-spA); return {\"p\": list(p), \"nmb_A\": nA, \"nmb_B\": nB, \"best\": [\"A\" if a >= b else \"B\" for a, b in zip(nA, nB)], \"p_even\": ((spB-spA)*L+cA-cB)/den if den else None}` Returns the same values as the R function, in input order." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def nmb_two_tests(p, seA, spA, cA, seB, spB, cB, G, L): nA = [q*seA*G-(1-q)*(1-spA)*L-cA for q in p]; nB = [q*seB*G-(1-q)*(1-spB)*L-cB for q in p]; den = G*(seA-seB)+L*(spB-spA); return {\"p\": list(p), \"nmb_A\": nA, \"nmb_B\": nB, \"best\": [\"A\" if a >= b else \"B\" for a, b in zip(nA, nB)], \"p_even\": ((spB-spA)*L+cA-cB)/den if den else None}" .

hemo:7005341 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Left-truncated Kaplan-Meier curve and restricted mean from entry, exit and event records"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Entry and event times are independent given that the event comes after entry; risk sets near the origin may be small or empty, so the curve should be read only where the risk set has support." .

hemo:7005344 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-LTR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000812 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With records in EntryTimes, ExitTimes and Status and the distinct event times in EventCol, `=COUNTIFS(EntryTimes,\"<\"&EventCol,ExitTimes,\">=\"&EventCol)` gives the risk sets (RiskCol), `=COUNTIFS(ExitTimes,EventCol,Status,1)` the deaths (DeathCol), a running product of 1 minus DeathCol / RiskCol the survival column, and the restricted mean is the sum of each survival level times the months it lasts up to tau." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=COUNTIFS(EntryTimes,\"<\"&EventCol,ExitTimes,\">=\"&EventCol)" .

hemo:7005345 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-LTR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000812 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`lt_km <- function(entry, stop, status, tau) { tj <- sort(unique(stop[status == 1])); n <- sapply(tj, function(t) sum(entry < t & stop >= t)); d <- sapply(tj, function(t) sum(stop == t & status == 1)); S <- cumprod(1-d/n); k <- tj <= tau; w <- diff(c(0, tj[k], tau)); list(time = tj, n_risk = n, n_event = d, surv = S, rmst = sum(c(1, S[k])*w)) }` Base R only; `lt_km(c(0, 0, 0, 12, 12, 20), c(6, 18, 30, 24, 36, 33), c(1, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1), 36)` returns the first example. With the survival package, `survfit(Surv(entry, stop, status) ~ 1)` gives the same curve." ;
    hemo:9000024 "lt_km <- function(entry, stop, status, tau) { tj <- sort(unique(stop[status == 1])); n <- sapply(tj, function(t) sum(entry < t & stop >= t)); d <- sapply(tj, function(t) sum(stop == t & status == 1)); S <- cumprod(1-d/n); k <- tj <= tau; w <- diff(c(0, tj[k], tau)); list(time = tj, n_risk = n, n_event = d, surv = S, rmst = sum(c(1, S[k])*w)) }" .

hemo:7005346 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-LTR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000812 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def lt_km(entry, stop, status, tau): tj = sorted({x for x, s in zip(stop, status) if s == 1}); n = [sum(1 for l, x in zip(entry, stop) if l < t <= x) for t in tj]; d = [sum(1 for x, s in zip(stop, status) if x == t and s == 1) for t in tj]; S = list(itertools.accumulate([1-a/b for a, b in zip(d, n)], operator.mul)); k = [t for t in tj if t <= tau]; w = [b-a for a, b in zip([0]+k, k+[tau])]; return {\"time\": tj, \"n_risk\": n, \"n_event\": d, \"surv\": S, \"rmst\": sum(s*v for s, v in zip([1]+S[:len(k)], w))}` Needs `import itertools, operator`; returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def lt_km(entry, stop, status, tau): tj = sorted({x for x, s in zip(stop, status) if s == 1}); n = [sum(1 for l, x in zip(entry, stop) if l < t <= x) for t in tj]; d = [sum(1 for x, s in zip(stop, status) if x == t and s == 1) for t in tj]; S = list(itertools.accumulate([1-a/b for a, b in zip(d, n)], operator.mul)); k = [t for t in tj if t <= tau]; w = [b-a for a, b in zip([0]+k, k+[tau])]; return {\"time\": tj, \"n_risk\": n, \"n_event\": d, \"surv\": S, \"rmst\": sum(s*v for s, v in zip([1]+S[:len(k)], w))}" .

hemo:7005348 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Provider payment rules compared by chosen stay, net benefit and margin"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "alpha is constant and positive, cost per unit is constant, and the payer's rules differ only in P and r; cost-reduction effort and patient selection are left out, as in the article." .

hemo:7005351 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CTH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000813 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the rules in ranges FixedPart and ShareGrid and the parameters named BenIntercept, BenSlope, UnitCost and AgencyIndex, `=IF(BenIntercept-(1-ShareGrid)*UnitCost/AgencyIndex>0,(BenIntercept-(1-ShareGrid)*UnitCost/AgencyIndex)/BenSlope,0)` spills the stays into StayGrid (IF rather than MAX, because MAX would collapse the column to one value), `=BenIntercept*StayGrid-BenSlope*StayGrid^2/2-UnitCost*StayGrid` spills the net benefits into NetGrid and `=FixedPart+ShareGrid*UnitCost*StayGrid-UnitCost*StayGrid` the margins into MarginGrid." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(BenIntercept-(1-ShareGrid)*UnitCost/AgencyIndex>0,(BenIntercept-(1-ShareGrid)*UnitCost/AgencyIndex)/BenSlope,0)" .

hemo:7005352 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CTH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000813 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`pay_rules <- function(P, r, b0, b1, c, alpha) { q <- pmax(0, (b0-(1-r)*c/alpha)/b1); B <- b0*q-b1*q^2/2; C <- c*q; pay <- P+r*c*q; data.frame(P = P, r = r, q = q, benefit = B, cost = C, net = B-C, payment = pay, margin = pay-C) }` Base R only; `pay_rules(c(3200, 0, 1600), c(0, 1, 0.5), 1200, 100, 400, 0.5)` returns the first example, rows in input order." ;
    hemo:9000024 "pay_rules <- function(P, r, b0, b1, c, alpha) { q <- pmax(0, (b0-(1-r)*c/alpha)/b1); B <- b0*q-b1*q^2/2; C <- c*q; pay <- P+r*c*q; data.frame(P = P, r = r, q = q, benefit = B, cost = C, net = B-C, payment = pay, margin = pay-C) }" .

hemo:7005353 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CTH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000813 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def pay_rules(P, r, b0, b1, c, alpha): q = [max(0.0, (b0-(1-s)*c/alpha)/b1) for s in r]; B = [b0*x-b1*x*x/2 for x in q]; C = [c*x for x in q]; pay = [p+s*c*x for p, s, x in zip(P, r, q)]; return {\"P\": list(P), \"r\": list(r), \"q\": q, \"benefit\": B, \"cost\": C, \"net\": [b-k for b, k in zip(B, C)], \"payment\": pay, \"margin\": [y-k for y, k in zip(pay, C)]}` Returns the same values as the R function, in input order." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def pay_rules(P, r, b0, b1, c, alpha): q = [max(0.0, (b0-(1-s)*c/alpha)/b1) for s in r]; B = [b0*x-b1*x*x/2 for x in q]; C = [c*x for x in q]; pay = [p+s*c*x for p, s, x in zip(P, r, q)]; return {\"P\": list(P), \"r\": list(r), \"q\": q, \"benefit\": B, \"cost\": C, \"net\": [b-k for b, k in zip(B, C)], \"payment\": pay, \"margin\": [y-k for y, k in zip(pay, C)]}" .

hemo:7005355 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Budget-neutral conversion factor and fee changes for a schedule of services"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Volumes are fixed and the whole adjustment passes through the conversion factor, as in HE-FM-CVF-003; CMS's own estimate uses prior-year utilisation and several sequential adjustments." .

hemo:7005358 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CVF-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000814 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the ranges Volumes, OldRVUs and NewRVUs and the cells OldCF, BNMult and AdjCF of HE-FM-CVF-003, `=OldRVUs*OldCF` and `=NewRVUs*AdjCF` spill the fees before and after into OldFees and NewFees, and `=100*(NewFees/OldFees-1)` spills the percentage changes." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=OldRVUs*OldCF" .

hemo:7005359 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CVF-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000814 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`bn_schedule <- function(q, R_old, R_new, cf_old) { bn <- sum(q*R_old)/sum(q*R_new); cf_new <- cf_old*bn; f0 <- R_old*cf_old; f1 <- R_new*cf_new; list(bn = bn, cf_new = cf_new, fees = data.frame(fee_old = f0, fee_new = f1, pct_change = 100*(f1/f0-1)), spend_old = sum(q*f0), spend_new = sum(q*f1)) }` Base R only; `bn_schedule(c(600, 400), c(2.00, 4.00), c(2.00, 3.95), 33.00)` returns the first example, services in input order." ;
    hemo:9000024 "bn_schedule <- function(q, R_old, R_new, cf_old) { bn <- sum(q*R_old)/sum(q*R_new); cf_new <- cf_old*bn; f0 <- R_old*cf_old; f1 <- R_new*cf_new; list(bn = bn, cf_new = cf_new, fees = data.frame(fee_old = f0, fee_new = f1, pct_change = 100*(f1/f0-1)), spend_old = sum(q*f0), spend_new = sum(q*f1)) }" .

hemo:7005360 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CVF-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000814 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def bn_schedule(q, R_old, R_new, cf_old): bn = sum(a*b for a, b in zip(q, R_old))/sum(a*b for a, b in zip(q, R_new)); cf_new = cf_old*bn; f0 = [x*cf_old for x in R_old]; f1 = [x*cf_new for x in R_new]; return {\"bn\": bn, \"cf_new\": cf_new, \"fee_old\": f0, \"fee_new\": f1, \"pct_change\": [100*(b/a-1) for a, b in zip(f0, f1)], \"spend_old\": sum(n*f for n, f in zip(q, f0)), \"spend_new\": sum(n*f for n, f in zip(q, f1))}` Returns the same values as the R function, in input order." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def bn_schedule(q, R_old, R_new, cf_old): bn = sum(a*b for a, b in zip(q, R_old))/sum(a*b for a, b in zip(q, R_new)); cf_new = cf_old*bn; f0 = [x*cf_old for x in R_old]; f1 = [x*cf_new for x in R_new]; return {\"bn\": bn, \"cf_new\": cf_new, \"fee_old\": f0, \"fee_new\": f1, \"pct_change\": [100*(b/a-1) for a, b in zip(f0, f1)], \"spend_old\": sum(n*f for n, f in zip(q, f0)), \"spend_new\": sum(n*f for n, f in zip(q, f1))}" .

hemo:7005362 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Validation table and flagged share to corrected prevalence, database PPV and flagged-cohort cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Sensitivity and specificity transfer unchanged from the validation sample to the database, and cost depends only on true status, as in the article." .

hemo:7005365 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CRV-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000815 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the counts named TruePos, FalseNeg, TrueNeg and FalsePos, `=TruePos/(TruePos+FalseNeg)` and `=TrueNeg/(TrueNeg+FalsePos)` return AlgSens and AlgSpec, the formula of HE-FM-CRV-001 returns TruePrev from FlagShare, `=AlgSens*TruePrev/(AlgSens*TruePrev+(1-AlgSpec)*(1-TruePrev))` returns the database PPV into PPVFlag, and the formulas of HE-FM-CRV-002 return FlagMeanCost and FlagCostBias." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=TruePos/(TruePos+FalseNeg)" .

hemo:7005366 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CRV-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000815 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`cv_cost <- function(TP, FN, TN, FP, a, C1, C0, N) { se <- TP/(TP+FN); sp <- TN/(TN+FP); ppv_v <- TP/(TP+FP); p <- min(1, max(0, (a+sp-1)/(se+sp-1))); ppv_d <- se*p/(se*p+(1-sp)*(1-p)); cost_d <- ppv_d*C1+(1-ppv_d)*C0; list(se = se, sp = sp, ppv_valid = ppv_v, prev = p, ppv_db = ppv_d, cost_valid = ppv_v*C1+(1-ppv_v)*C0, cost_db = cost_d, flagged_per_case = a/p, total_flagged = N*a*cost_d, total_cases = N*p*C1) }` Base R only; `cv_cost(180, 20, 760, 40, 0.067, 6000, 1000, 100000)` returns the first example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "cv_cost <- function(TP, FN, TN, FP, a, C1, C0, N) { se <- TP/(TP+FN); sp <- TN/(TN+FP); ppv_v <- TP/(TP+FP); p <- min(1, max(0, (a+sp-1)/(se+sp-1))); ppv_d <- se*p/(se*p+(1-sp)*(1-p)); cost_d <- ppv_d*C1+(1-ppv_d)*C0; list(se = se, sp = sp, ppv_valid = ppv_v, prev = p, ppv_db = ppv_d, cost_valid = ppv_v*C1+(1-ppv_v)*C0, cost_db = cost_d, flagged_per_case = a/p, total_flagged = N*a*cost_d, total_cases = N*p*C1) }" .

hemo:7005367 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CRV-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000815 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def cv_cost(TP, FN, TN, FP, a, C1, C0, N): se = TP/(TP+FN); sp = TN/(TN+FP); ppv_v = TP/(TP+FP); p = min(1.0, max(0.0, (a+sp-1)/(se+sp-1))); ppv_d = se*p/(se*p+(1-sp)*(1-p)); cost_d = ppv_d*C1+(1-ppv_d)*C0; return {\"se\": se, \"sp\": sp, \"ppv_valid\": ppv_v, \"prev\": p, \"ppv_db\": ppv_d, \"cost_valid\": ppv_v*C1+(1-ppv_v)*C0, \"cost_db\": cost_d, \"flagged_per_case\": a/p if p > 0 else float(\"inf\"), \"total_flagged\": N*a*cost_d, \"total_cases\": N*p*C1}` Returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def cv_cost(TP, FN, TN, FP, a, C1, C0, N): se = TP/(TP+FN); sp = TN/(TN+FP); ppv_v = TP/(TP+FP); p = min(1.0, max(0.0, (a+sp-1)/(se+sp-1))); ppv_d = se*p/(se*p+(1-sp)*(1-p)); cost_d = ppv_d*C1+(1-ppv_d)*C0; return {\"se\": se, \"sp\": sp, \"ppv_valid\": ppv_v, \"prev\": p, \"ppv_db\": ppv_d, \"cost_valid\": ppv_v*C1+(1-ppv_v)*C0, \"cost_db\": cost_d, \"flagged_per_case\": a/p if p > 0 else float(\"inf\"), \"total_flagged\": N*a*cost_d, \"total_cases\": N*p*C1}" .

hemo:7005369 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Gamma deviance residuals, unit deviances and model deviance for a set of patient costs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Costs are positive and the fitted means come from the gamma model being checked, with prior weights of 1." .

hemo:7005372 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-DEVR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000816 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the costs in the range CostVec and the fitted means in FitVec, the array formula `=2*((CostVec-FitVec)/FitVec-LN(CostVec/FitVec))` returns the unit deviances into UnitDevVec, `=SIGN(CostVec-FitVec)*SQRT(UnitDevVec)` the deviance residuals into DevResVec, `=SUM(UnitDevVec)` the model deviance and `=XMATCH(ABS(DevResVec),SORT(ABS(DevResVec),,-1))` the ranks (Excel 365)." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=2*((CostVec-FitVec)/FitVec-LN(CostVec/FitVec))" .

hemo:7005373 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-DEVR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000816 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`gamma_dev <- function(y, mu = rep(mean(y), length(y))) { d <- 2*((y-mu)/mu-log(y/mu)); r <- sign(y-mu)*sqrt(d); list(table = data.frame(y = y, mu = mu, pearson = (y-mu)/mu, unit_dev = d, dev_res = r, rank_abs_dev = rank(-abs(r))), deviance = sum(d), sum_dev_res = sum(r)) }` Base R only; `gamma_dev(c(1000, 2000, 6000))` returns the article example in input order." ;
    hemo:9000024 "gamma_dev <- function(y, mu = rep(mean(y), length(y))) { d <- 2*((y-mu)/mu-log(y/mu)); r <- sign(y-mu)*sqrt(d); list(table = data.frame(y = y, mu = mu, pearson = (y-mu)/mu, unit_dev = d, dev_res = r, rank_abs_dev = rank(-abs(r))), deviance = sum(d), sum_dev_res = sum(r)) }" .

hemo:7005374 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-DEVR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000816 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def gamma_dev(y, mu=None): mu = mu or [sum(y)/len(y)]*len(y); d = [2*((a-m)/m-math.log(a/m)) for a, m in zip(y, mu)]; r = [math.copysign(math.sqrt(x), a-m) if a != m else 0.0 for x, a, m in zip(d, y, mu)]; return {\"y\": list(y), \"mu\": list(mu), \"pearson\": [(a-m)/m for a, m in zip(y, mu)], \"unit_dev\": d, \"dev_res\": r, \"deviance\": sum(d), \"sum_dev_res\": sum(r)}` Needs `import math`; returns the same values as the R function apart from the ranks." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def gamma_dev(y, mu=None): mu = mu or [sum(y)/len(y)]*len(y); d = [2*((a-m)/m-math.log(a/m)) for a, m in zip(y, mu)]; r = [math.copysign(math.sqrt(x), a-m) if a != m else 0.0 for x, a, m in zip(d, y, mu)]; return {\"y\": list(y), \"mu\": list(mu), \"pearson\": [(a-m)/m for a, m in zip(y, mu)], \"unit_dev\": d, \"dev_res\": r, \"deviance\": sum(d), \"sum_dev_res\": sum(r)}" .

hemo:7005376 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Exponential survival fit with martingale and deviance residuals for every patient"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "One record per patient and a constant hazard over follow-up; for another parametric model the fitted cumulative hazard replaces rate x time." .

hemo:7005379 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-DEVR-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000817 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With times in TimeVec and indicators in EventVec, `=SUM(EventVec)/SUM(TimeVec)` returns ExpRate, `=ExpRate*TimeVec` the cumulative hazards into CumHazVec, `=EventVec-CumHazVec` the martingale residuals into MartVec and `=SIGN(MartVec)*SQRT(-2*(MartVec+EventVec*LN(CumHazVec)))` the deviance residuals into DevVec; LN(CumHazVec) equals LN(EventVec minus MartVec)." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUM(EventVec)/SUM(TimeVec)" .

hemo:7005380 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-DEVR-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000817 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`exp_dev <- function(time, event) { lam <- sum(event)/sum(time); H <- lam*time; M <- event-H; r <- sign(M)*sqrt(-2*(M+ifelse(event == 1, log(H), 0))); list(rate = lam, table = data.frame(time = time, event = event, cumhaz = H, mart = M, dev_res = r), sum_mart = sum(M), deviance = sum(r^2)) }` Base R only; `exp_dev(c(1, 3, 5, 11), c(1, 1, 0, 1))` returns the article example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "exp_dev <- function(time, event) { lam <- sum(event)/sum(time); H <- lam*time; M <- event-H; r <- sign(M)*sqrt(-2*(M+ifelse(event == 1, log(H), 0))); list(rate = lam, table = data.frame(time = time, event = event, cumhaz = H, mart = M, dev_res = r), sum_mart = sum(M), deviance = sum(r^2)) }" .

hemo:7005381 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-DEVR-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000817 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def exp_dev(time, event): lam = sum(event)/sum(time); H = [lam*t for t in time]; M = [e-x for e, x in zip(event, H)]; r = [math.copysign(math.sqrt(-2*(m+(math.log(x) if e == 1 else 0.0))), m) for m, x, e in zip(M, H, event)]; return {\"rate\": lam, \"cumhaz\": H, \"mart\": M, \"dev_res\": r, \"sum_mart\": sum(M), \"deviance\": sum(v*v for v in r)}` Needs `import math`; returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def exp_dev(time, event): lam = sum(event)/sum(time); H = [lam*t for t in time]; M = [e-x for e, x in zip(event, H)]; r = [math.copysign(math.sqrt(-2*(m+(math.log(x) if e == 1 else 0.0))), m) for m, x, e in zip(M, H, event)]; return {\"rate\": lam, \"cumhaz\": H, \"mart\": M, \"dev_res\": r, \"sum_mart\": sum(M), \"deviance\": sum(v*v for v in r)}" .

hemo:7005383 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Distribution-based yardsticks and reliable change classification for a cohort of paired scores"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "One SEM applies to every patient and both measurements, with independent normal errors; the shares describe individual change, not a difference between arms." .

hemo:7005385 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-DMID-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000818 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the scores in ScoreT1Vec and ScoreT2Vec, SEMVal and SDiffVal from HE-FM-DMID-002 and HE-FM-DMID-003, the array formula `=(ScoreT2Vec-ScoreT1Vec)/SDiffVal` returns the indices into RCVec, `=AVERAGE(--(RCVec>1.96))` the share with reliable gain and `=AVERAGE(--(RCVec<-1.96))` the share with reliable loss." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(ScoreT2Vec-ScoreT1Vec)/SDiffVal" .

hemo:7005386 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-DMID-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000818 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`rel_change <- function(x1, x2, sd0, rel, z = 1.96) { sem <- sd0*sqrt(1-rel); sdiff <- sqrt(2)*sem; rc <- (x2-x1)/sdiff; list(half_sd = 0.5*sd0, es_small = 0.2*sd0, sem = sem, sdiff = sdiff, mdc = z*sdiff, table = data.frame(x1 = x1, x2 = x2, change = x2-x1, rc = rc, reliable_gain = rc > z, reliable_loss = rc < -z), share_gain = mean(rc > z), share_loss = mean(rc < -z)) }` Base R only; `rel_change(c(0.60, 0.50, 0.70, 0.40, 0.55), c(0.75, 0.80, 0.65, 0.70, 0.75), 0.20, 0.80)` returns the example in input order." ;
    hemo:9000024 "rel_change <- function(x1, x2, sd0, rel, z = 1.96) { sem <- sd0*sqrt(1-rel); sdiff <- sqrt(2)*sem; rc <- (x2-x1)/sdiff; list(half_sd = 0.5*sd0, es_small = 0.2*sd0, sem = sem, sdiff = sdiff, mdc = z*sdiff, table = data.frame(x1 = x1, x2 = x2, change = x2-x1, rc = rc, reliable_gain = rc > z, reliable_loss = rc < -z), share_gain = mean(rc > z), share_loss = mean(rc < -z)) }" .

hemo:7005387 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-DMID-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000818 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def rel_change(x1, x2, sd0, rel, z=1.96): sem = sd0*math.sqrt(1-rel); sdiff = math.sqrt(2)*sem; rc = [(b-a)/sdiff for a, b in zip(x1, x2)]; return {\"half_sd\": 0.5*sd0, \"es_small\": 0.2*sd0, \"sem\": sem, \"sdiff\": sdiff, \"mdc\": z*sdiff, \"change\": [b-a for a, b in zip(x1, x2)], \"rc\": rc, \"reliable_gain\": [v > z for v in rc], \"reliable_loss\": [v < -z for v in rc], \"share_gain\": sum(v > z for v in rc)/len(rc), \"share_loss\": sum(v < -z for v in rc)/len(rc)}` Needs `import math`; returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def rel_change(x1, x2, sd0, rel, z=1.96): sem = sd0*math.sqrt(1-rel); sdiff = math.sqrt(2)*sem; rc = [(b-a)/sdiff for a, b in zip(x1, x2)]; return {\"half_sd\": 0.5*sd0, \"es_small\": 0.2*sd0, \"sem\": sem, \"sdiff\": sdiff, \"mdc\": z*sdiff, \"change\": [b-a for a, b in zip(x1, x2)], \"rc\": rc, \"reliable_gain\": [v > z for v in rc], \"reliable_loss\": [v < -z for v in rc], \"share_gain\": sum(v > z for v in rc)/len(rc), \"share_loss\": sum(v < -z for v in rc)/len(rc)}" .

hemo:7005389 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Combined utility, QALY gain and cost per QALY under three combination rules for any number of conditions"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "One shared baseline, no measured utilities for combined states, a constant decrement over dur and no deaths, as in the article." .

hemo:7005392 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-DISU-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000819 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With NeitherUtil named and the single-condition utilities in the range SingleUtils, `=NeitherUtil-SUMPRODUCT(NeitherUtil-SingleUtils)` returns the additive value, `=NeitherUtil*PRODUCT(SingleUtils/NeitherUtil)` the multiplicative value (entered as an array formula in older versions) and `=MIN(NeitherUtil,SingleUtils)` the minimum, held in UAddAll, UMultAll and UMinAll; the same formulas on the range without the last condition give the values without it, and HE-FM-DISU-003 gives the gains." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=NeitherUtil-SUMPRODUCT(NeitherUtil-SingleUtils)" .

hemo:7005393 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-DISU-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000819 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`combine_util <- function(U0, U, dur, cost) { k <- length(U); f <- function(u) c(additive = U0-sum(U0-u), multiplicative = U0*prod(u/U0), minimum = min(c(U0, u))); w <- f(U); wo <- f(U[-k]); g <- (wo-w)*dur; data.frame(rule = names(w), combined = w, without_last = wo, qaly_gain = g, cost_per_qaly = ifelse(g > 0, cost/g, NA), row.names = NULL) }` Base R only; `combine_util(0.80, c(0.70, 0.60), 5, 20000)` returns the article example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "combine_util <- function(U0, U, dur, cost) { k <- length(U); f <- function(u) c(additive = U0-sum(U0-u), multiplicative = U0*prod(u/U0), minimum = min(c(U0, u))); w <- f(U); wo <- f(U[-k]); g <- (wo-w)*dur; data.frame(rule = names(w), combined = w, without_last = wo, qaly_gain = g, cost_per_qaly = ifelse(g > 0, cost/g, NA), row.names = NULL) }" .

hemo:7005394 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-DISU-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000819 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def combine_util(U0, U, dur, cost): f = lambda u: {\"additive\": U0-sum(U0-x for x in u), \"multiplicative\": U0*math.prod(x/U0 for x in u), \"minimum\": min([U0]+list(u))}; w = f(U); wo = f(U[:-1]); g = {k: (wo[k]-w[k])*dur for k in w}; return {k: {\"combined\": w[k], \"without_last\": wo[k], \"qaly_gain\": g[k], \"cost_per_qaly\": cost/g[k] if g[k] > 0 else None} for k in w}` Needs `import math` (Python 3.8 or later for math.prod); returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def combine_util(U0, U, dur, cost): f = lambda u: {\"additive\": U0-sum(U0-x for x in u), \"multiplicative\": U0*math.prod(x/U0 for x in u), \"minimum\": min([U0]+list(u))}; w = f(U); wo = f(U[:-1]); g = {k: (wo[k]-w[k])*dur for k in w}; return {k: {\"combined\": w[k], \"without_last\": wo[k], \"qaly_gain\": g[k], \"cost_per_qaly\": cost/g[k] if g[k] > 0 else None} for k in w}" .

hemo:7005396 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Dose ladder with Emax responses, shared and whole-vial course costs and incremental cost per extra responder"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Placebo, at zero drug cost, is the starting point of the frontier; other costs are left out, and a dose is dominated when another dose or a blend of two gives more responders for no more cost (HE-FM-DOM-002, HE-FM-EXD-001)." .

hemo:7005399 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-DOSE-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000820 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With doses in DoseGrid and the parameters named as in HE-FM-DOSE-001, `=PlaceboResp+MaxEffect*DoseGrid^HillCoef/(ED50Dose^HillCoef+DoseGrid^HillCoef)` returns the responses, `=DoseGrid*VialPrice/VialMg*AdminsPerCourse` the shared-vial costs and `=ROUNDUP(DoseGrid/VialMg,0)*VialPrice*AdminsPerCourse` the whole-vial costs; after sorting by cost and deleting dominated doses, with the frontier costs in FrontCost and extra responders in FrontExtra, `=(FrontCost-VSTACK(0,DROP(FrontCost,-1)))/(FrontExtra-VSTACK(0,DROP(FrontExtra,-1)))` returns the incremental ratios into FrontICER and `=FrontCost/FrontExtra` the average ratios into FrontAvg (Excel 365)." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=PlaceboResp+MaxEffect*DoseGrid^HillCoef/(ED50Dose^HillCoef+DoseGrid^HillCoef)" .

hemo:7005400 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-DOSE-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000820 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`dose_ladder <- function(dose, E0, Emax, ED50, h, vial_mg, vial_price, n_admin) { resp <- E0+Emax*dose^h/(ED50^h+dose^h); gain <- resp-E0; fr <- function(cost) { icer <- rep(NA, length(dose)); c0 <- 0; g0 <- 0; repeat { cand <- which(gain > g0); if (length(cand) == 0) break; ic <- (cost[cand]-c0)/(gain[cand]-g0); j <- cand[order(ic, -gain[cand])[1]]; icer[j] <- min(ic); c0 <- cost[j]; g0 <- gain[j] }; data.frame(dose = dose, response = resp, extra = gain, cost = cost, average = cost/gain, icer = icer) }; list(sharing = fr(dose*vial_price/vial_mg*n_admin), whole_vials = fr(ceiling(dose/vial_mg)*vial_price*n_admin)) }` Base R only; `dose_ladder(c(100, 150, 200), 0.20, 0.40, 100, 2, 100, 300, 12)` returns both tables in input order." ;
    hemo:9000024 "dose_ladder <- function(dose, E0, Emax, ED50, h, vial_mg, vial_price, n_admin) { resp <- E0+Emax*dose^h/(ED50^h+dose^h); gain <- resp-E0; fr <- function(cost) { icer <- rep(NA, length(dose)); c0 <- 0; g0 <- 0; repeat { cand <- which(gain > g0); if (length(cand) == 0) break; ic <- (cost[cand]-c0)/(gain[cand]-g0); j <- cand[order(ic, -gain[cand])[1]]; icer[j] <- min(ic); c0 <- cost[j]; g0 <- gain[j] }; data.frame(dose = dose, response = resp, extra = gain, cost = cost, average = cost/gain, icer = icer) }; list(sharing = fr(dose*vial_price/vial_mg*n_admin), whole_vials = fr(ceiling(dose/vial_mg)*vial_price*n_admin)) }" .

hemo:7005401 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-DOSE-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000820 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def dose_ladder(dose, E0, Emax, ED50, h, vial_mg, vial_price, n_admin): resp = [E0+Emax*d**h/(ED50**h+d**h) for d in dose]; gain = [r-E0 for r in resp]; fr = lambda c, c0, g0: (lambda cand: [] if not cand else (lambda j: [(j, (c[j]-c0)/(gain[j]-g0))]+fr(c, c[j], gain[j]))(min(cand, key=lambda i: ((c[i]-c0)/(gain[i]-g0), -gain[i]))))([i for i in range(len(c)) if gain[i] > g0]); costs = {\"sharing\": [d*vial_price/vial_mg*n_admin for d in dose], \"whole_vials\": [math.ceil(d/vial_mg)*vial_price*n_admin for d in dose]}; return {k: {\"dose\": list(dose), \"response\": resp, \"extra\": gain, \"cost\": c, \"average\": [x/g for x, g in zip(c, gain)], \"icer\": [dict(fr(c, 0.0, 0.0)).get(i) for i in range(len(dose))]} for k, c in costs.items()}` Needs `import math`; returns the same values as the R function, with None for a dominated dose." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def dose_ladder(dose, E0, Emax, ED50, h, vial_mg, vial_price, n_admin): resp = [E0+Emax*d**h/(ED50**h+d**h) for d in dose]; gain = [r-E0 for r in resp]; fr = lambda c, c0, g0: (lambda cand: [] if not cand else (lambda j: [(j, (c[j]-c0)/(gain[j]-g0))]+fr(c, c[j], gain[j]))(min(cand, key=lambda i: ((c[i]-c0)/(gain[i]-g0), -gain[i]))))([i for i in range(len(c)) if gain[i] > g0]); costs = {\"sharing\": [d*vial_price/vial_mg*n_admin for d in dose], \"whole_vials\": [math.ceil(d/vial_mg)*vial_price*n_admin for d in dose]}; return {k: {\"dose\": list(dose), \"response\": resp, \"extra\": gain, \"cost\": c, \"average\": [x/g for x, g in zip(c, gain)], \"icer\": [dict(fr(c, 0.0, 0.0)).get(i) for i in range(len(dose))]} for k, c in costs.items()}" .

hemo:7005403 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Discounted cost per QALY of several programmes' yearly streams under a list of cost and health rate pairs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each amount falls at a whole-year point and year 0 is the start of treatment; all programmes are compared with the same comparator and independently of each other, so the lowest ratio describes value under each pair, not an incremental ranking of mutually exclusive options." .

hemo:7005406 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-DDIS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000821 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With one programme's streams in rows named CostRow and QALYRow and the years 0, 1, 2 and so on in YearRow, `=SUMPRODUCT(CostRow,(1+RateC)^-YearRow)/SUMPRODUCT(QALYRow,(1+RateH)^-YearRow)` returns its ratio; copied across pairs and programmes it fills the table, and `=MIN(...)` over a column picks the lowest." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(CostRow,(1+RateC)^-YearRow)/SUMPRODUCT(QALYRow,(1+RateH)^-YearRow)" .

hemo:7005407 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-DDIS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000821 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`dd_ratios <- function(cost, qaly, rates) { yr <- 0:(ncol(cost)-1); do.call(rbind, lapply(seq_len(nrow(rates)), function(k) { dc <- as.vector(cost %*% (1+rates[k, 1])^-yr); dq <- as.vector(qaly %*% (1+rates[k, 2])^-yr); data.frame(r_C = rates[k, 1], r_H = rates[k, 2], programme = rownames(cost), disc_cost = dc, disc_qaly = dq, ratio = dc/dq, lowest = dc/dq == min(dc/dq)) })) }` Base R only; `dd_ratios(rbind(A = c(12000, rep(0, 9), 12000, rep(0, 10)), B = c(20000, rep(0, 20))), rbind(A = c(rep(0, 20), 1.2), B = c(0, 0.8, rep(0, 19))), rbind(c(0.035, 0.035), c(0.015, 0.015), c(0.03, 0.015)))` returns the first example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "dd_ratios <- function(cost, qaly, rates) { yr <- 0:(ncol(cost)-1); do.call(rbind, lapply(seq_len(nrow(rates)), function(k) { dc <- as.vector(cost %*% (1+rates[k, 1])^-yr); dq <- as.vector(qaly %*% (1+rates[k, 2])^-yr); data.frame(r_C = rates[k, 1], r_H = rates[k, 2], programme = rownames(cost), disc_cost = dc, disc_qaly = dq, ratio = dc/dq, lowest = dc/dq == min(dc/dq)) })) }" .

hemo:7005408 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-DDIS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000821 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def dd_ratios(cost, qaly, rates, names=None): names = names or [f\"P{i+1}\" for i in range(len(cost))]; disc = lambda rows, r: [sum(v/(1+r)**t for t, v in enumerate(row)) for row in rows]; tab = [(rc, rh, disc(cost, rc), disc(qaly, rh)) for rc, rh in rates]; return [{\"r_C\": rc, \"r_H\": rh, \"programme\": n, \"disc_cost\": a, \"disc_qaly\": b, \"ratio\": a/b, \"lowest\": a/b == min(x/y for x, y in zip(dc, dq))} for rc, rh, dc, dq in tab for n, a, b in zip(names, dc, dq)]` Needs no imports; called with the same streams as lists, the rate pairs as tuples and names [\"A\", \"B\"], it returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def dd_ratios(cost, qaly, rates, names=None): names = names or [f\"P{i+1}\" for i in range(len(cost))]; disc = lambda rows, r: [sum(v/(1+r)**t for t, v in enumerate(row)) for row in rows]; tab = [(rc, rh, disc(cost, rc), disc(qaly, rh)) for rc, rh in rates]; return [{\"r_C\": rc, \"r_H\": rh, \"programme\": n, \"disc_cost\": a, \"disc_qaly\": b, \"ratio\": a/b, \"lowest\": a/b == min(x/y for x, y in zip(dc, dq))} for rc, rh, dc, dq in tab for n, a, b in zip(names, dc, dq)]" .

hemo:7005410 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Dirichlet row from transition counts and a prior with beta-marginal intervals, correlations and a sampled row"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The counts describe one starting state over the model cycle, structural zeros have been left out, and the uniform numbers are independent, one per destination, in each iteration." .

hemo:7005413 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-DIRI-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000822 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the posterior parameters in AlphaVec and the uniform numbers in UVec, `=GAMMA.INV(UVec,AlphaVec,1)` returns the gamma quantiles into GammaVec and `=GammaVec/SUM(GammaVec)` the sampled row into RowVec; `=AlphaVec/SUM(AlphaVec)` gives MeanVec, and `=BETA.INV(0.025,AlphaVec,SUM(AlphaVec)-AlphaVec)` and the same with 0.975 give LowerVec and UpperVec. In a probabilistic analysis UVec holds RAND() in each cell." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=GAMMA.INV(UVec,AlphaVec,1)" .

hemo:7005414 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-DIRI-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000822 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`dir_row <- function(n, a = 0, u = NULL, level = 0.95) { alpha <- n+a; if (any(alpha <= 0)) stop(\"every parameter must be above zero: add a prior or leave out a structural zero\"); a0 <- sum(alpha); v <- alpha*(a0-alpha)/(a0^2*(a0+1)); cv <- -outer(alpha, alpha)/(a0^2*(a0+1)); diag(cv) <- v; q <- (1-level)/2; out <- list(alpha = alpha, mean = alpha/a0, sd = sqrt(v), corr = cv/sqrt(outer(v, v)), lower = qbeta(q, alpha, a0-alpha), upper = qbeta(1-q, alpha, a0-alpha)); if (!is.null(u)) { g <- qgamma(u, alpha, 1); out$gamma <- g; out$row <- g/sum(g) }; out }` Base R only; `dir_row(c(70, 20, 10), 0, c(0.30, 0.80, 0.50))` returns the first example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "dir_row <- function(n, a = 0, u = NULL, level = 0.95) { alpha <- n+a; if (any(alpha <= 0)) stop(\"every parameter must be above zero: add a prior or leave out a structural zero\"); a0 <- sum(alpha); v <- alpha*(a0-alpha)/(a0^2*(a0+1)); cv <- -outer(alpha, alpha)/(a0^2*(a0+1)); diag(cv) <- v; q <- (1-level)/2; out <- list(alpha = alpha, mean = alpha/a0, sd = sqrt(v), corr = cv/sqrt(outer(v, v)), lower = qbeta(q, alpha, a0-alpha), upper = qbeta(1-q, alpha, a0-alpha)); if (!is.null(u)) { g <- qgamma(u, alpha, 1); out$gamma <- g; out$row <- g/sum(g) }; out }" .

hemo:7005415 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-DIRI-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000822 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def dir_row(n, a=0, u=None, level=0.95): alpha = [x+y for x, y in zip(n, a if isinstance(a, list) else [a]*len(n))]; assert min(alpha) > 0, \"every parameter must be above zero\"; a0 = sum(alpha); v = [x*(a0-x)/(a0**2*(a0+1)) for x in alpha]; k = range(len(alpha)); cov = [[v[i] if i == j else -alpha[i]*alpha[j]/(a0**2*(a0+1)) for j in k] for i in k]; q = (1-level)/2; g = [stats.gamma.ppf(x, s) for x, s in zip(u, alpha)] if u else None; return {\"alpha\": alpha, \"mean\": [x/a0 for x in alpha], \"sd\": [math.sqrt(x) for x in v], \"corr\": [[cov[i][j]/math.sqrt(v[i]*v[j]) for j in k] for i in k], \"lower\": [stats.beta.ppf(q, x, a0-x) for x in alpha], \"upper\": [stats.beta.ppf(1-q, x, a0-x) for x in alpha], \"gamma\": g, \"row\": [x/sum(g) for x in g] if g else None}` Needs `import math` and `from scipy import stats`; returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def dir_row(n, a=0, u=None, level=0.95): alpha = [x+y for x, y in zip(n, a if isinstance(a, list) else [a]*len(n))]; assert min(alpha) > 0, \"every parameter must be above zero\"; a0 = sum(alpha); v = [x*(a0-x)/(a0**2*(a0+1)) for x in alpha]; k = range(len(alpha)); cov = [[v[i] if i == j else -alpha[i]*alpha[j]/(a0**2*(a0+1)) for j in k] for i in k]; q = (1-level)/2; g = [stats.gamma.ppf(x, s) for x, s in zip(u, alpha)] if u else None; return {\"alpha\": alpha, \"mean\": [x/a0 for x in alpha], \"sd\": [math.sqrt(x) for x in v], \"corr\": [[cov[i][j]/math.sqrt(v[i]*v[j]) for j in k] for i in k], \"lower\": [stats.beta.ppf(q, x, a0-x) for x in alpha], \"upper\": [stats.beta.ppf(1-q, x, a0-x) for x in alpha], \"gamma\": g, \"row\": [x/sum(g) for x in g] if g else None}" .

hemo:7005417 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of ROC threshold for a minimal important change from patients' change scores and anchor classification"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "A patient reaches a cut-off when the change is at least the cut-off, and the anchor classification is fixed before any cut-off is tried; the result carries the bias noted by Terwee and colleagues when the share improved is far from one half." .

hemo:7005419 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-MCID-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000823 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With change scores in ChangeVec, improved flags (1 or 0) in ImprovedVec and a cut-off in CutVal, `=SUMPRODUCT(--(ChangeVec>=CutVal),ImprovedVec)/SUM(ImprovedVec)` returns sensitivity and `=SUMPRODUCT(--(ChangeVec<CutVal),1-ImprovedVec)/(COUNT(ImprovedVec)-SUM(ImprovedVec))` specificity; copied over the cut-offs in CutVec they fill MisclassVec and YoudenVec, and `=INDEX(CutVec,MATCH(MIN(MisclassVec),MisclassVec,0))` returns the threshold into ROCCut." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(--(ChangeVec>=CutVal),ImprovedVec)/SUM(ImprovedVec)" .

hemo:7005420 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-MCID-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000823 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`roc_cut <- function(change, improved, cuts = sort(unique(change))) { se <- sapply(cuts, function(c) mean(change[improved] >= c)); sp <- sapply(cuts, function(c) mean(change[!improved] < c)); m <- (1-se)+(1-sp); list(table = data.frame(cut = cuts, sens = se, spec = sp, misclass = m, youden = se+sp-1), cutoff = cuts[which.min(m)]) }` Base R only; `roc_cut(c(rep(2, 10), rep(4, 8), rep(6, 12), rep(8, 15), rep(10, 45), rep(0, 80), rep(4, 14), rep(6, 8), rep(8, 5), rep(10, 3)), rep(c(TRUE, FALSE), c(90, 110)), c(4, 6, 8, 10))` returns the example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "roc_cut <- function(change, improved, cuts = sort(unique(change))) { se <- sapply(cuts, function(c) mean(change[improved] >= c)); sp <- sapply(cuts, function(c) mean(change[!improved] < c)); m <- (1-se)+(1-sp); list(table = data.frame(cut = cuts, sens = se, spec = sp, misclass = m, youden = se+sp-1), cutoff = cuts[which.min(m)]) }" .

hemo:7005421 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-MCID-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000823 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def roc_cut(change, improved, cuts=None): cuts = cuts or sorted(set(change)); imp = [c for c, i in zip(change, improved) if i]; oth = [c for c, i in zip(change, improved) if not i]; rows = [{\"cut\": c, \"sens\": sum(x >= c for x in imp)/len(imp), \"spec\": sum(x < c for x in oth)/len(oth)} for c in cuts]; [r.update(misclass=2-r[\"sens\"]-r[\"spec\"], youden=r[\"sens\"]+r[\"spec\"]-1) for r in rows]; return {\"table\": rows, \"cutoff\": min(rows, key=lambda r: r[\"misclass\"])[\"cut\"]}` Needs no imports; returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def roc_cut(change, improved, cuts=None): cuts = cuts or sorted(set(change)); imp = [c for c, i in zip(change, improved) if i]; oth = [c for c, i in zip(change, improved) if not i]; rows = [{\"cut\": c, \"sens\": sum(x >= c for x in imp)/len(imp), \"spec\": sum(x < c for x in oth)/len(oth)} for c in cuts]; [r.update(misclass=2-r[\"sens\"]-r[\"spec\"], youden=r[\"sens\"]+r[\"spec\"]-1) for r in rows]; return {\"table\": rows, \"cutoff\": min(rows, key=lambda r: r[\"misclass\"])[\"cut\"]}" .

hemo:7005423 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of N-th root of a transition matrix of any size by eigendecomposition with validity and reproduction checks"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "P is diagonalisable (for example, distinct eigenvalues) and one shorter-cycle matrix applies throughout the original interval. The function returns the principal root; when any check fails, Chhatwal and colleagues approximate it with the closest stochastic matrix, which this function does not do." .

hemo:7005427 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-TMAT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000824 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`mat_root <- function(P, n, tol = 1e-9) { e <- eigen(P); R <- e$vectors %*% diag(as.complex(e$values)^(1/n)) %*% solve(e$vectors); real <- all(abs(Im(R)) < tol); R <- Re(R); Rn <- diag(nrow(P)); for (k in seq_len(n)) Rn <- Rn %*% R; list(root = R, real = real, nonnegative = all(R > -tol), rows_sum_to_one = all(abs(rowSums(R)-1) < tol), reproduction_error = max(abs(Rn-P))) }` Base R only; `mat_root(rbind(c(0.70, 0.20, 0.10), c(0, 0.60, 0.40), c(0, 0, 1)), 2)` returns the first example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "mat_root <- function(P, n, tol = 1e-9) { e <- eigen(P); R <- e$vectors %*% diag(as.complex(e$values)^(1/n)) %*% solve(e$vectors); real <- all(abs(Im(R)) < tol); R <- Re(R); Rn <- diag(nrow(P)); for (k in seq_len(n)) Rn <- Rn %*% R; list(root = R, real = real, nonnegative = all(R > -tol), rows_sum_to_one = all(abs(rowSums(R)-1) < tol), reproduction_error = max(abs(Rn-P))) }" .

hemo:7005428 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-TMAT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000824 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def mat_root(P, n, tol=1e-9): P = np.asarray(P, float); w, V = np.linalg.eig(P); R = V @ np.diag(w.astype(complex)**(1/n)) @ np.linalg.inv(V); real = bool(np.all(abs(R.imag) < tol)); R = R.real; return {\"root\": R, \"real\": real, \"nonnegative\": bool(np.all(R > -tol)), \"rows_sum_to_one\": bool(np.all(abs(R.sum(axis=1)-1) < tol)), \"reproduction_error\": float(abs(np.linalg.matrix_power(R, n)-P).max())}` Needs `import numpy as np`; returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def mat_root(P, n, tol=1e-9): P = np.asarray(P, float); w, V = np.linalg.eig(P); R = V @ np.diag(w.astype(complex)**(1/n)) @ np.linalg.inv(V); real = bool(np.all(abs(R.imag) < tol)); R = R.real; return {\"root\": R, \"real\": real, \"nonnegative\": bool(np.all(R > -tol)), \"rows_sum_to_one\": bool(np.all(abs(R.sum(axis=1)-1) < tol)), \"reproduction_error\": float(abs(np.linalg.matrix_power(R, n)-P).max())}" .

hemo:7005430 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Yearly incidence-based and prevalence-based YLDs from a history of incident cases"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every case lasts exactly L years with no deaths and contributes one full person-year in each of them; the weight is the same for every case and year." .

hemo:7005433 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-YLD-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000825 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`yld_series <- function(inc, L, DW, r = 0) { yrs <- L:length(inc); f <- if (r > 0) (1-exp(-r*L))/r else L; data.frame(year = yrs, incident = inc[yrs], yld_inc = inc[yrs]*DW*f, yld_prev = sapply(yrs, function(y) DW*sum(inc[(y-L+1):y]))) }` Base R only; `yld_series(c(rep(100, 10), rep(50, 10)), 10, 0.20)` returns the first example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "yld_series <- function(inc, L, DW, r = 0) { yrs <- L:length(inc); f <- if (r > 0) (1-exp(-r*L))/r else L; data.frame(year = yrs, incident = inc[yrs], yld_inc = inc[yrs]*DW*f, yld_prev = sapply(yrs, function(y) DW*sum(inc[(y-L+1):y]))) }" .

hemo:7005434 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-YLD-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000825 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def yld_series(inc, L, DW, r=0): f = (1-math.exp(-r*L))/r if r > 0 else L; return [{\"year\": y+1, \"incident\": inc[y], \"yld_inc\": inc[y]*DW*f, \"yld_prev\": DW*sum(inc[y-L+1:y+1])} for y in range(L-1, len(inc))]` Needs `import math`; returns the same values as the R function, with years numbered from 1." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def yld_series(inc, L, DW, r=0): f = (1-math.exp(-r*L))/r if r > 0 else L; return [{\"year\": y+1, \"incident\": inc[y], \"yld_inc\": inc[y]*DW*f, \"yld_prev\": DW*sum(inc[y-L+1:y+1])} for y in range(L-1, len(inc))]" .

hemo:7005436 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Expanded tunnel transition matrix from death probabilities by cycle since entry, with its cohort trace"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Death is the only exit from the tunnel other than moving along it, every entrant comes from the origin state and a repeat event does not restart the tunnel. Death probabilities by duration may come from a survival curve for time since entry with HE-FM-CUMH-002, the clock restarting on entry." .

hemo:7005440 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-TUN-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000826 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=MMULT(PrevTraceRow,ExpandedMatrix)` Entered across the cells of a trace row (as an array formula in older versions) with the expanded matrix laid out as in the article's table: each tunnel row holds 1 minus its death probability one column to the right of the diagonal and its death probability in the Dead column, and the L row holds its stay probability on the diagonal. The average is HE-FM-TUN-003." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MMULT(PrevTraceRow,ExpandedMatrix)" .

hemo:7005441 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-TUN-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000826 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`tunnel_model <- function(p_entry, q_origin, q_dur, m0, n_cycles) { K1 <- length(q_dur); n <- K1+2; P <- matrix(0, n, n); P[1, 1] <- 1-p_entry-q_origin; P[1, 2] <- p_entry; P[1, n] <- q_origin; for (k in 1:K1) { P[k+1, min(k+2, K1+1)] <- 1-q_dur[k]; P[k+1, n] <- q_dur[k] }; P[n, n] <- 1; tr <- matrix(0, n_cycles+1, n); tr[1, ] <- m0; for (t in 1:n_cycles) tr[t+1, ] <- tr[t, ] %*% P; occ <- tr[, 2:(K1+1), drop = FALSE]; list(P = P, trace = tr, q_bar = as.vector(occ %*% q_dur) / rowSums(occ)) }` Base R only; row t+1 of the trace and element t+1 of q_bar belong to cycle t." ;
    hemo:9000024 "tunnel_model <- function(p_entry, q_origin, q_dur, m0, n_cycles) { K1 <- length(q_dur); n <- K1+2; P <- matrix(0, n, n); P[1, 1] <- 1-p_entry-q_origin; P[1, 2] <- p_entry; P[1, n] <- q_origin; for (k in 1:K1) { P[k+1, min(k+2, K1+1)] <- 1-q_dur[k]; P[k+1, n] <- q_dur[k] }; P[n, n] <- 1; tr <- matrix(0, n_cycles+1, n); tr[1, ] <- m0; for (t in 1:n_cycles) tr[t+1, ] <- tr[t, ] %*% P; occ <- tr[, 2:(K1+1), drop = FALSE]; list(P = P, trace = tr, q_bar = as.vector(occ %*% q_dur) / rowSums(occ)) }" .

hemo:7005442 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-TUN-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000826 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def tunnel_model(p_entry, q_origin, q_dur, m0, n_cycles): q_dur = np.asarray(q_dur, float); K1 = len(q_dur); n = K1+2; P = np.zeros((n, n)); P[0, 0] = 1-p_entry-q_origin; P[0, 1] = p_entry; P[0, n-1] = q_origin; k = np.arange(K1); P[k+1, np.minimum(k+2, K1)] = 1-q_dur; P[k+1, n-1] = q_dur; P[n-1, n-1] = 1; tr = np.vstack([np.asarray(m0, float) @ np.linalg.matrix_power(P, t) for t in range(n_cycles+1)]); occ = tr[:, 1:K1+1]; tot = occ.sum(axis=1); return {\"P\": P, \"trace\": tr, \"q_bar\": np.divide(occ @ q_dur, tot, out=np.full(len(tot), np.nan), where=tot > 0)}` Needs `import numpy as np`; returns the same values as the R function, with index t for cycle t." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def tunnel_model(p_entry, q_origin, q_dur, m0, n_cycles): q_dur = np.asarray(q_dur, float); K1 = len(q_dur); n = K1+2; P = np.zeros((n, n)); P[0, 0] = 1-p_entry-q_origin; P[0, 1] = p_entry; P[0, n-1] = q_origin; k = np.arange(K1); P[k+1, np.minimum(k+2, K1)] = 1-q_dur; P[k+1, n-1] = q_dur; P[n-1, n-1] = 1; tr = np.vstack([np.asarray(m0, float) @ np.linalg.matrix_power(P, t) for t in range(n_cycles+1)]); occ = tr[:, 1:K1+1]; tot = occ.sum(axis=1); return {\"P\": P, \"trace\": tr, \"q_bar\": np.divide(occ @ q_dur, tot, out=np.full(len(tot), np.nan), where=tot > 0)}" .

hemo:7005445 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Partitioned survival occupancy, crossing check, life years and QALYs from PFS and OS curves on a time grid"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Survival changes close to linearly between grid times, so the trapezoid areas approach the exact areas as the grid gets finer. Utilities are constant within each state and death carries zero. Discounting uses the factor 1/(1+d) raised to the midpoint time of each interval." .

hemo:7005450 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-PSM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000827 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=C2-B2` and `=1-C2` filled down give the progressed and dead shares, with time in column A, PFS in column B and OS in column C over rows 2 to 602; `=SUMPRODUCT(--(B2:B602>C2:C602))` gives n_cross into a cell named ncross; `=SUMPRODUCT((C2:C601+C3:C602)/2*(A3:A602-A2:A601)/(1+DiscRate)^((A2:A601+A3:A602)/2))` gives LY; and `=IF(ncross>0,NA(),SUMPRODUCT(((B2:B601+B3:B602)/2*UtilPF+((C2:C601-B2:B601)+(C3:C602-B3:B602))/2*UtilPD)*(A3:A602-A2:A601)/(1+DiscRate)^((A2:A601+A3:A602)/2)))` gives QALYs, with the utilities in UtilPF and UtilPD and the annual rate in DiscRate." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=C2-B2" .

hemo:7005451 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-PSM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000827 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`psm_qaly <- function(t, s_pfs, s_os, u_pf, u_pd, d = 0) { n_cross <- sum(s_pfs > s_os); k <- seq_along(t)[-1]; w <- diff(t)/(1+d)^((t[k-1]+t[k])/2); pd <- s_os-s_pfs; a_pf <- sum(w*(s_pfs[k-1]+s_pfs[k])/2); a_pd <- sum(w*(pd[k-1]+pd[k])/2); list(P_PD = pd, P_D = 1-s_os, n_cross = n_cross, LY = a_pf+a_pd, QALY = if (n_cross > 0) NA else u_pf*a_pf+u_pd*a_pd) }` Vectorised over the grid; `psm_qaly(0:3, exp(-(0:3)), exp(-0.5*(0:3)), 0.75, 0.60)$QALY` gives about 1.105801 from unrounded curve values." ;
    hemo:9000024 "psm_qaly <- function(t, s_pfs, s_os, u_pf, u_pd, d = 0) { n_cross <- sum(s_pfs > s_os); k <- seq_along(t)[-1]; w <- diff(t)/(1+d)^((t[k-1]+t[k])/2); pd <- s_os-s_pfs; a_pf <- sum(w*(s_pfs[k-1]+s_pfs[k])/2); a_pd <- sum(w*(pd[k-1]+pd[k])/2); list(P_PD = pd, P_D = 1-s_os, n_cross = n_cross, LY = a_pf+a_pd, QALY = if (n_cross > 0) NA else u_pf*a_pf+u_pd*a_pd) }" .

hemo:7005452 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-PSM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000827 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def psm_qaly(t, s_pfs, s_os, u_pf, u_pd, d=0): t, sp, so = (np.asarray(x, float) for x in (t, s_pfs, s_os)); n_cross = int((sp > so).sum()); w = np.diff(t)/(1+d)**((t[:-1]+t[1:])/2); pd = so-sp; a_pf = (w*(sp[:-1]+sp[1:])/2).sum(); a_pd = (w*(pd[:-1]+pd[1:])/2).sum(); return pd, 1-so, n_cross, a_pf+a_pd, (None if n_cross else u_pf*a_pf+u_pd*a_pd)` Uses numpy imported as np and returns the progressed and dead shares, n_cross, LY and QALYs." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def psm_qaly(t, s_pfs, s_os, u_pf, u_pd, d=0): t, sp, so = (np.asarray(x, float) for x in (t, s_pfs, s_os)); n_cross = int((sp > so).sum()); w = np.diff(t)/(1+d)**((t[:-1]+t[1:])/2); pd = so-sp; a_pf = (w*(sp[:-1]+sp[1:])/2).sum(); a_pd = (w*(pd[:-1]+pd[1:])/2).sum(); return pd, 1-so, n_cross, a_pf+a_pd, (None if n_cross else u_pf*a_pf+u_pd*a_pd)" .

hemo:7005455 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Expected discounted rewards of a time-homogeneous absorbing Markov chain by matrix inversion"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Q and the rewards are the same in every cycle, the discount rate is constant, membership is counted at the start of each cycle with the first cycle undiscounted, and the half-cycle subtraction removes half a cycle of the starting reward only, as Sonnenberg and Beck describe for a matrix solution. A model whose probabilities change with age needs the cohort trace of HE-CF-CSIM-001." .

hemo:7005460 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-MKCH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000828 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With Q in TransientQ, the starting distribution as a row in StartRow, the rewards as a column in RewardCol, the rate in DiscRate, the cycle length in CycleYears and TRUE or FALSE in HalfCycle, Excel 365 returns the value: `=INDEX(MMULT(MMULT(StartRow,MINVERSE(MUNIT(ROWS(TransientQ))-TransientQ/(1+DiscRate)^CycleYears)),RewardCol)-IF(HalfCycle,0.5*MMULT(StartRow,RewardCol),0),1,1)`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=INDEX(MMULT(MMULT(StartRow,MINVERSE(MUNIT(ROWS(TransientQ))-TransientQ/(1+DiscRate)^CycleYears)),RewardCol)-IF(HalfCycle,0.5*MMULT(StartRow,RewardCol),0),1,1)" .

hemo:7005461 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-MKCH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000828 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`mc_value <- function(Q, m0, u, r = 0, Delta = 1, half_cycle = FALSE) { Q <- as.matrix(Q); d <- (1+r)^Delta; converges <- max(Mod(eigen(Q/d, only.values = TRUE)$values)) < 1; substochastic <- all(Q >= 0) && all(rowSums(Q) <= 1+1e-12); Nd <- solve(diag(nrow(Q))-Q/d); value <- drop(m0 %*% Nd %*% u); if (half_cycle) value <- value-0.5*sum(m0*u); list(N_disc = Nd, value = value, substochastic = substochastic, converges = converges) }` Base R only; `mc_value(rbind(c(0.85, 0.10), c(0.20, 0.70)), c(1, 0), c(0.85, 0.60), r = 0.035)` returns 8.5007." ;
    hemo:9000024 "mc_value <- function(Q, m0, u, r = 0, Delta = 1, half_cycle = FALSE) { Q <- as.matrix(Q); d <- (1+r)^Delta; converges <- max(Mod(eigen(Q/d, only.values = TRUE)$values)) < 1; substochastic <- all(Q >= 0) && all(rowSums(Q) <= 1+1e-12); Nd <- solve(diag(nrow(Q))-Q/d); value <- drop(m0 %*% Nd %*% u); if (half_cycle) value <- value-0.5*sum(m0*u); list(N_disc = Nd, value = value, substochastic = substochastic, converges = converges) }" .

hemo:7005462 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-MKCH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000828 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def mc_value(Q, m0, u, r=0.0, Delta=1.0, half_cycle=False): Q = np.asarray(Q, float); d = (1+r)**Delta; converges = bool(max(abs(np.linalg.eigvals(Q/d))) < 1); substochastic = bool((Q >= 0).all() and (Q.sum(axis=1) <= 1+1e-12).all()); Nd = np.linalg.inv(np.eye(len(Q))-Q/d); value = float(np.asarray(m0) @ Nd @ np.asarray(u)); value -= 0.5*float(np.dot(m0, u)) if half_cycle else 0.0; return {\"N_disc\": Nd, \"value\": value, \"substochastic\": substochastic, \"converges\": converges}` Needs `import numpy as np`; returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def mc_value(Q, m0, u, r=0.0, Delta=1.0, half_cycle=False): Q = np.asarray(Q, float); d = (1+r)**Delta; converges = bool(max(abs(np.linalg.eigvals(Q/d))) < 1); substochastic = bool((Q >= 0).all() and (Q.sum(axis=1) <= 1+1e-12).all()); Nd = np.linalg.inv(np.eye(len(Q))-Q/d); value = float(np.asarray(m0) @ Nd @ np.asarray(u)); value -= 0.5*float(np.dot(m0, u)) if half_cycle else 0.0; return {\"N_disc\": Nd, \"value\": value, \"substochastic\": substochastic, \"converges\": converges}" .

hemo:7005464 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Discounted life years and QALYs from a mixture cure model by numerical integration"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The midpoint of each step stands for the whole step; with a step of 0.01 years the error is below 0.00001 QALYs for these inputs. The horizon is long enough for the cohort to have died. The background hazard is constant here; with life tables, exp(minus mu t) is replaced by background survival built from age-specific hazards. The Excel form needs Excel 365 or 2021 (LET and SEQUENCE)." .

hemo:7005468 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CRM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000829 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=LET(t,SEQUENCE(Horizon/StepYrs,1,StepYrs/2,StepYrs),SUMPRODUCT(StepYrs*EXP(-(BgHazard+LN(1+DiscRate))*t)*(CureFrac*UtilCured+(1-CureFrac)*UtilUncured*EXP(-((t/WeibScale)^WeibShape)))))` With pi, mu, k, b, u_C, u_U, d, T_h and dt in the named cells CureFrac, BgHazard, WeibShape, WeibScale, UtilCured, UtilUncured, DiscRate, Horizon and StepYrs, the formula builds the grid of step midpoints and returns discounted QALYs, held in CureQALY." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LET(t,SEQUENCE(Horizon/StepYrs,1,StepYrs/2,StepYrs),SUMPRODUCT(StepYrs*EXP(-(BgHazard+LN(1+DiscRate))*t)*(CureFrac*UtilCured+(1-CureFrac)*UtilUncured*EXP(-((t/WeibScale)^WeibShape)))))" .

hemo:7005469 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CRM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000829 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`cure_qaly <- function(pi, mu, k, b, u_c, u_u, d, horizon = 300, dt = 0.01) { t <- seq(dt/2, horizon, by = dt); sum(dt * exp(-(mu + log(1+d)) * t) * (pi * u_c + (1-pi) * u_u * exp(-(t/b)^k))) }` In R the power is applied before the minus sign, so exp(-(t/b)^k) is correct as written." ;
    hemo:9000024 "cure_qaly <- function(pi, mu, k, b, u_c, u_u, d, horizon = 300, dt = 0.01) { t <- seq(dt/2, horizon, by = dt); sum(dt * exp(-(mu + log(1+d)) * t) * (pi * u_c + (1-pi) * u_u * exp(-(t/b)^k))) }" .

hemo:7005470 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CRM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000829 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def cure_qaly(pi, mu, k, b, u_c, u_u, d, horizon=300, dt=0.01): t = np.arange(dt/2, horizon, dt); return float(np.sum(dt * np.exp(-(mu + np.log(1+d)) * t) * (pi * u_c + (1-pi) * u_u * np.exp(-(t/b)**k))))` Needs numpy imported as np; t holds the step midpoints." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def cure_qaly(pi, mu, k, b, u_c, u_u, d, horizon=300, dt=0.01): t = np.arange(dt/2, horizon, dt); return float(np.sum(dt * np.exp(-(mu + np.log(1+d)) * t) * (pi * u_c + (1-pi) * u_u * np.exp(-(t/b)**k))))" .

hemo:7005472 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Expected event probability and deterministic bias from the mean and standard deviation of an uncertain hazard"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The hazard is constant over the horizon in each draw, and its uncertainty is represented by a gamma distribution matched to the reported mean and standard deviation. Another distribution with the same mean and standard deviation would give a somewhat different expected probability." .

hemo:7005475 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-EV-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000830 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=1-(1+(HazSD^2/HazMean)*Horizon)^(-(HazMean^2/HazSD^2))` returns the expected probability from named cells HazMean, HazSD and Horizon, held in ExpProbCF; `=(1-EXP(-HazMean*Horizon))-ExpProbCF` gives the bias in probability, held in GapProb, and GapProb multiplied by CostPerEvent and by CohortSize gives the cost and event gaps." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1-(1+(HazSD^2/HazMean)*Horizon)^(-(HazMean^2/HazSD^2))" .

hemo:7005476 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-EV-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000830 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`ev_gamma_hazard <- function(h_mean, h_sd, t, c_event, n_cohort = 1000) { k <- h_mean^2 / h_sd^2; s <- h_sd^2 / h_mean; p_det <- 1-exp(-h_mean * t); p_bar <- 1-(1 + s * t)^(-k); p_approx <- p_det-0.5 * t^2 * exp(-h_mean * t) * h_sd^2; list(k = k, s = s, p_det = p_det, p_bar = p_bar, p_approx = p_approx, gap_events = n_cohort * (p_det-p_bar), gap_cost = c_event * (p_det-p_bar)) }` Base R only; vectorised over t, so a vector of horizons traces the gap over time." ;
    hemo:9000024 "ev_gamma_hazard <- function(h_mean, h_sd, t, c_event, n_cohort = 1000) { k <- h_mean^2 / h_sd^2; s <- h_sd^2 / h_mean; p_det <- 1-exp(-h_mean * t); p_bar <- 1-(1 + s * t)^(-k); p_approx <- p_det-0.5 * t^2 * exp(-h_mean * t) * h_sd^2; list(k = k, s = s, p_det = p_det, p_bar = p_bar, p_approx = p_approx, gap_events = n_cohort * (p_det-p_bar), gap_cost = c_event * (p_det-p_bar)) }" .

hemo:7005477 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-EV-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000830 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def ev_gamma_hazard(h_mean, h_sd, t, c_event, n_cohort=1000): k = h_mean**2 / h_sd**2; s = h_sd**2 / h_mean; p_det = 1-math.exp(-h_mean * t); p_bar = 1-(1 + s * t)**(-k); p_approx = p_det-0.5 * t**2 * math.exp(-h_mean * t) * h_sd**2; return {\"k\": k, \"s\": s, \"p_det\": p_det, \"p_bar\": p_bar, \"p_approx\": p_approx, \"gap_events\": n_cohort * (p_det-p_bar), \"gap_cost\": c_event * (p_det-p_bar)}` Needs `import math`; returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def ev_gamma_hazard(h_mean, h_sd, t, c_event, n_cohort=1000): k = h_mean**2 / h_sd**2; s = h_sd**2 / h_mean; p_det = 1-math.exp(-h_mean * t); p_bar = 1-(1 + s * t)**(-k); p_approx = p_det-0.5 * t**2 * math.exp(-h_mean * t) * h_sd**2; return {\"k\": k, \"s\": s, \"p_det\": p_det, \"p_bar\": p_bar, \"p_approx\": p_approx, \"gap_events\": n_cohort * (p_det-p_bar), \"gap_cost\": c_event * (p_det-p_bar)}" .

hemo:7005480 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Total years of life lost for a cause from deaths by age group and a named standard life table"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every death in an age group counts the tabulated value for that group, the standard applies to both sexes, and no discounting or age weighting is applied, as in HE-FM-YLL-001." .

hemo:7005483 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-YLL-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000831 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=SUMPRODUCT(Deaths,INDEX(StdTable,0,MATCH(StdName,StdNames,0)))` With deaths for the 20 age groups in a column named Deaths, the Table 2.1 values in a 20-row range named StdTable with one column per standard, the names of the standards in a header row named StdNames and the chosen name in StdName, the formula returns the total. Each group's YLLs divided by the total give the shares." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(Deaths,INDEX(StdTable,0,MATCH(StdName,StdNames,0)))" .

hemo:7005484 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-YLL-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000831 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`yll_cause <- function(deaths, std) { yll <- deaths * std[names(deaths)]; list(total = sum(yll), share = yll / sum(yll)) }` Takes a named vector of deaths and a named vector holding one column of Table 2.1, for example `c(a1to4 = 83.63, a40to44 = 44.23, a75to79 = 12.85)` for GBD 2010, and matches them by age group." ;
    hemo:9000024 "yll_cause <- function(deaths, std) { yll <- deaths * std[names(deaths)]; list(total = sum(yll), share = yll / sum(yll)) }" .

hemo:7005485 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-YLL-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000831 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def yll_cause(deaths, std): yll = {a: n * std[a] for a, n in deaths.items()}; total = sum(yll.values()); return total, {a: v / total for a, v in yll.items()}` Takes dictionaries of deaths and standard values keyed by age group and returns the total and the shares." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def yll_cause(deaths, std): yll = {a: n * std[a] for a, n in deaths.items()}; total = sum(yll.values()); return total, {a: v / total for a, v in yll.items()}" .

hemo:7005488 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Incremental cost-utility and utility-cost ratios with quadrant from arm-level costs and QALYs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Both arms share the comparator, population, horizon, price year and discount rate, and draws are matched pairs from one probabilistic run. The quadrant label is returned because the ratios alone do not show whether a result is a trade-off or a case of dominance." .

hemo:7005492 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ICUR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000832 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=LET(dC,AVERAGE(Cost1)-AVERAGE(Cost0),dQ,AVERAGE(QALY1)-AVERAGE(QALY0),HSTACK(IF(dQ=0,NA(),dC/dQ),IF(dC=0,NA(),dQ/dC*1000000),IF(OR(dC=0,dQ=0),\"on an axis\",IF(dC>0,\"north\",\"south\")&\"-\"&IF(dQ>0,\"east\",\"west\"))))` Excel 365; Cost1, Cost0, QALY1 and QALY0 may be single cells or matched columns of draws, and the result spills ICUR, IUCR and the quadrant into three cells." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LET(dC,AVERAGE(Cost1)-AVERAGE(Cost0),dQ,AVERAGE(QALY1)-AVERAGE(QALY0),HSTACK(IF(dQ=0,NA(),dC/dQ),IF(dC=0,NA(),dQ/dC*1000000),IF(OR(dC=0,dQ=0),\"on an axis\",IF(dC>0,\"north\",\"south\")&\"-\"&IF(dQ>0,\"east\",\"west\"))))" .

hemo:7005493 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ICUR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000832 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`icur_iucr <- function(C_1, C_0, Q_1, Q_0, scale = 1e6) { dC <- mean(C_1)-mean(C_0); dQ <- mean(Q_1)-mean(Q_0); quadrant <- if (dC == 0 || dQ == 0) \"on an axis\" else paste0(if (dC > 0) \"north\" else \"south\", \"-\", if (dQ > 0) \"east\" else \"west\"); list(Delta_C = dC, Delta_Q = dQ, ICUR = if (dQ != 0) dC/dQ else NA, IUCR = if (dC != 0) dQ/dC*scale else NA, quadrant = quadrant) }` Base R; `icur_iucr(24600, 16200, 6.42, 6.02)` returns an ICUR of 21000 and an IUCR of 47.619." ;
    hemo:9000024 "icur_iucr <- function(C_1, C_0, Q_1, Q_0, scale = 1e6) { dC <- mean(C_1)-mean(C_0); dQ <- mean(Q_1)-mean(Q_0); quadrant <- if (dC == 0 || dQ == 0) \"on an axis\" else paste0(if (dC > 0) \"north\" else \"south\", \"-\", if (dQ > 0) \"east\" else \"west\"); list(Delta_C = dC, Delta_Q = dQ, ICUR = if (dQ != 0) dC/dQ else NA, IUCR = if (dC != 0) dQ/dC*scale else NA, quadrant = quadrant) }" .

hemo:7005494 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ICUR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000832 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def icur_iucr(C_1, C_0, Q_1, Q_0, scale=1e6): dC = float(np.mean(C_1))-float(np.mean(C_0)); dQ = float(np.mean(Q_1))-float(np.mean(Q_0)); quadrant = \"on an axis\" if dC == 0 or dQ == 0 else (\"north\" if dC > 0 else \"south\")+\"-\"+(\"east\" if dQ > 0 else \"west\"); return {\"Delta_C\": dC, \"Delta_Q\": dQ, \"ICUR\": dC/dQ if dQ != 0 else None, \"IUCR\": dQ/dC*scale if dC != 0 else None, \"quadrant\": quadrant}` Needs `import numpy as np`; returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def icur_iucr(C_1, C_0, Q_1, Q_0, scale=1e6): dC = float(np.mean(C_1))-float(np.mean(C_0)); dQ = float(np.mean(Q_1))-float(np.mean(Q_0)); quadrant = \"on an axis\" if dC == 0 or dQ == 0 else (\"north\" if dC > 0 else \"south\")+\"-\"+(\"east\" if dQ > 0 else \"west\"); return {\"Delta_C\": dC, \"Delta_Q\": dQ, \"ICUR\": dC/dQ if dQ != 0 else None, \"IUCR\": dQ/dC*scale if dC != 0 else None, \"quadrant\": quadrant}" .

hemo:7005496 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Standard, Wagstaff and Erreygers concentration indices from group values, shares and scale bounds"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Groups are ranked by living standards from poorest to richest, each group carries its mean so only between-group inequality is measured, a and b are fixed by the measurement scale, and mu lies strictly between a and b for the Wagstaff index." .

hemo:7005500 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CIX-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000833 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=LET(h,Health,f,Share/SUM(Share),rk,SCAN(0,f,LAMBDA(acc,x,acc+x))-f/2,mu,SUMPRODUCT(f,h),cv,SUMPRODUCT(f,(h-mu)*(rk-0.5)),HSTACK(2*cv/mu,2*(UpperBound-LowerBound)*cv/((UpperBound-mu)*(mu-LowerBound)),8*cv/(UpperBound-LowerBound)))` Excel 365; with group means in a column named Health, shares or weights in Share and the scale bounds in LowerBound and UpperBound, the formula spills C, W and E into three cells." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LET(h,Health,f,Share/SUM(Share),rk,SCAN(0,f,LAMBDA(acc,x,acc+x))-f/2,mu,SUMPRODUCT(f,h),cv,SUMPRODUCT(f,(h-mu)*(rk-0.5)),HSTACK(2*cv/mu,2*(UpperBound-LowerBound)*cv/((UpperBound-mu)*(mu-LowerBound)),8*cv/(UpperBound-LowerBound)))" .

hemo:7005501 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CIX-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000833 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`ci_bounded <- function(h, f, a = 0, b = 1) { f <- f/sum(f); r <- cumsum(f)-f/2; mu <- sum(f*h); cv <- sum(f*(h-mu)*(r-0.5)); mu_s <- a+b-mu; c(C = 2*cv/mu, W = 2*(b-a)*cv/((b-mu)*(mu-a)), E = 8*cv/(b-a), C_s = -2*cv/mu_s, W_s = -2*(b-a)*cv/((b-mu_s)*(mu_s-a)), E_s = -8*cv/(b-a)) }` Base R; `ci_bounded(c(0.40, 0.30, 0.25, 0.15, 0.10), rep(0.2, 5))` returns C of minus 0.25, W of minus 0.3289 and E of minus 0.24." ;
    hemo:9000024 "ci_bounded <- function(h, f, a = 0, b = 1) { f <- f/sum(f); r <- cumsum(f)-f/2; mu <- sum(f*h); cv <- sum(f*(h-mu)*(r-0.5)); mu_s <- a+b-mu; c(C = 2*cv/mu, W = 2*(b-a)*cv/((b-mu)*(mu-a)), E = 8*cv/(b-a), C_s = -2*cv/mu_s, W_s = -2*(b-a)*cv/((b-mu_s)*(mu_s-a)), E_s = -8*cv/(b-a)) }" .

hemo:7005502 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CIX-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000833 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def ci_bounded(h, f, a=0, b=1): w = [x/sum(f) for x in f]; r = [sum(w[:j])+w[j]/2 for j in range(len(w))]; mu = sum(p*x for p, x in zip(w, h)); cv = sum(p*(x-mu)*(q-0.5) for p, x, q in zip(w, h, r)); mu_s = a+b-mu; return {'C': 2*cv/mu, 'W': 2*(b-a)*cv/((b-mu)*(mu-a)), 'E': 8*cv/(b-a), 'C_s': -2*cv/mu_s, 'W_s': -2*(b-a)*cv/((b-mu_s)*(mu_s-a)), 'E_s': -8*cv/(b-a)}` Plain Python with no imports; returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def ci_bounded(h, f, a=0, b=1): w = [x/sum(f) for x in f]; r = [sum(w[:j])+w[j]/2 for j in range(len(w))]; mu = sum(p*x for p, x in zip(w, h)); cv = sum(p*(x-mu)*(q-0.5) for p, x, q in zip(w, h, r)); mu_s = a+b-mu; return {'C': 2*cv/mu, 'W': 2*(b-a)*cv/((b-mu)*(mu-a)), 'E': 8*cv/(b-a), 'C_s': -2*cv/mu_s, 'W_s': -2*(b-a)*cv/((b-mu_s)*(mu_s-a)), 'E_s': -8*cv/(b-a)}" .

hemo:7005505 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Attributable annual cost of prevalent cases from survey counts and a prevalence ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The survey prevalences and their ratio hold in the local population, the ratio is causal and unconfounded, and each prevalent case costs the same in both groups. The result is an annual cost of prevalent cases, not a lifetime cost of new cases." .

hemo:7005509 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-PR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000834 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=LET(pExp,ExposedCases/(ExposedCases+ExposedFree),pUnexp,UnexposedCases/(UnexposedCases+UnexposedFree),ratio,pExp/pUnexp,frac,ExposedShare*(ratio-1)/(1+ExposedShare*(ratio-1)),cases,Adults*(ExposedShare*pExp+(1-ExposedShare)*pUnexp),HSTACK(ratio,frac,cases,frac*cases,frac*cases*CostPerCase))` Excel 365; with the survey counts, Adults, ExposedShare and CostPerCase in named cells, the result spills PR, PAF, M, A and C_A into five cells." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LET(pExp,ExposedCases/(ExposedCases+ExposedFree),pUnexp,UnexposedCases/(UnexposedCases+UnexposedFree),ratio,pExp/pUnexp,frac,ExposedShare*(ratio-1)/(1+ExposedShare*(ratio-1)),cases,Adults*(ExposedShare*pExp+(1-ExposedShare)*pUnexp),HSTACK(ratio,frac,cases,frac*cases,frac*cases*CostPerCase))" .

hemo:7005510 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-PR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000834 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`pr_attributable_cost <- function(a, b, c, d, N, e, k) { P_1 <- a/(a+b); P_0 <- c/(c+d); PR <- P_1/P_0; PAF <- e*(PR-1)/(1+e*(PR-1)); M <- N*(e*P_1+(1-e)*P_0); A <- PAF*M; list(PR = PR, PAF = PAF, M = M, A = A, C_A = A*k) }` Base R; `pr_attributable_cost(180, 420, 210, 1190, 500000, 0.40, 1200)` returns a PR of 2, 30000 attributable cases and a cost of 3.6e+07." ;
    hemo:9000024 "pr_attributable_cost <- function(a, b, c, d, N, e, k) { P_1 <- a/(a+b); P_0 <- c/(c+d); PR <- P_1/P_0; PAF <- e*(PR-1)/(1+e*(PR-1)); M <- N*(e*P_1+(1-e)*P_0); A <- PAF*M; list(PR = PR, PAF = PAF, M = M, A = A, C_A = A*k) }" .

hemo:7005511 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-PR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000834 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def pr_attributable_cost(a, b, c, d, N, e, k): P_1 = a/(a+b); P_0 = c/(c+d); PR = P_1/P_0; PAF = e*(PR-1)/(1+e*(PR-1)); M = N*(e*P_1+(1-e)*P_0); A = PAF*M; return {\"PR\": PR, \"PAF\": PAF, \"M\": M, \"A\": A, \"C_A\": A*k}` Plain Python with no imports; returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def pr_attributable_cost(a, b, c, d, N, e, k): P_1 = a/(a+b); P_0 = c/(c+d); PR = P_1/P_0; PAF = e*(PR-1)/(1+e*(PR-1)); M = N*(e*P_1+(1-e)*P_0); A = PAF*M; return {\"PR\": PR, \"PAF\": PAF, \"M\": M, \"A\": A, \"C_A\": A*k}" .

hemo:7005514 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Probabilistic economically justifiable price at a stated probability of cost-effectiveness"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Price enters each draw's incremental cost linearly as P times U_i, and the draws come from one probabilistic run with the price held out. A draw counts as cost-effective when its incremental net benefit is zero or above (for a draw with a positive QALY gain, an ICER at or below lambda); with continuous draws this matches the strict rule of HE-FM-CEAC-001 except at ties. Where price changes other model quantities, the probabilistic analysis is repeated at several prices, as the article describes, and the share of cost-effective draws is read at each." .

hemo:7005517 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-EJP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000835 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=(Threshold*AVERAGE(DeltaE)-AVERAGE(DeltaCOther))/AVERAGE(Units)` returns EJP_mean and `=LARGE((Threshold*DeltaE-DeltaCOther)/Units,ROUNDUP(Alpha*ROWS(DeltaE)-1E-9,0))` returns EJP_alpha, with DeltaE, DeltaCOther and Units as matched columns of draws (Units may be one cell) and Threshold and Alpha as single cells. Excel 365 evaluates the array inside LARGE directly; earlier versions need it entered as an array formula. The small offset stops a product such as 0.7 times 10 being rounded up to 8." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(Threshold*AVERAGE(DeltaE)-AVERAGE(DeltaCOther))/AVERAGE(Units)" .

hemo:7005518 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-EJP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000835 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`ejp_psa <- function(dE, dC_other, U, lambda, alpha) { ejp_i <- (lambda*dE-dC_other)/U; k <- ceiling(alpha*length(dE)-1e-9); list(EJP_mean = (lambda*mean(dE)-mean(dC_other))/mean(U), EJP_alpha = sort(ejp_i, decreasing = TRUE)[k]) }` Base R; `ejp_psa(c(0.56, 0.66, 0.76, 0.86, 0.96), c(2100, 1900, 1700, 1500, 1300), 25, 25000, 0.8)` returns 692 and 584." ;
    hemo:9000024 "ejp_psa <- function(dE, dC_other, U, lambda, alpha) { ejp_i <- (lambda*dE-dC_other)/U; k <- ceiling(alpha*length(dE)-1e-9); list(EJP_mean = (lambda*mean(dE)-mean(dC_other))/mean(U), EJP_alpha = sort(ejp_i, decreasing = TRUE)[k]) }" .

hemo:7005519 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-EJP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000835 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def ejp_psa(dE, dC_other, U, lam, alpha): dE, dC_other, U = (np.asarray(x, dtype=float) for x in (dE, dC_other, U)); ejp_i = (lam*dE-dC_other)/U; k = math.ceil(alpha*dE.size-1e-9); return {\"EJP_mean\": float((lam*dE.mean()-dC_other.mean())/U.mean()), \"EJP_alpha\": float(np.sort(ejp_i)[::-1][k-1])}` Needs `import math` and `import numpy as np`; lam is the threshold, because lambda is reserved in Python. Returns the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def ejp_psa(dE, dC_other, U, lam, alpha): dE, dC_other, U = (np.asarray(x, dtype=float) for x in (dE, dC_other, U)); ejp_i = (lam*dE-dC_other)/U; k = math.ceil(alpha*dE.size-1e-9); return {\"EJP_mean\": float((lam*dE.mean()-dC_other.mean())/U.mean()), \"EJP_alpha\": float(np.sort(ejp_i)[::-1][k-1])}" .

hemo:7005522 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Incremental QALYs and ICER after LOCF from arm utility means and dropout fractions"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Dropout after the 6-month visit is completely at random within each arm, everyone who stops was alive and last observed at 6 months, and costs are complete. Mean utilities at baseline and 6 months are observed for everyone, so the imputation changes only the 12-month means." .

hemo:7005526 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-LOCF-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000836 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=IncCost/(0.25*(UtilT0+2*UtilT6+(TauT*UtilT6+(1-TauT)*UtilT12))-0.25*(UtilC0+2*UtilC6+(TauC*UtilC6+(1-TauC)*UtilC12)))` With the mean utilities in cells named UtilT0 to UtilC12, the dropout fractions in TauT and TauC and dC in IncCost, the formula returns ICER_LOCF; setting TauT and TauC to 0 returns ICER_full." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IncCost/(0.25*(UtilT0+2*UtilT6+(TauT*UtilT6+(1-TauT)*UtilT12))-0.25*(UtilC0+2*UtilC6+(TauC*UtilC6+(1-TauC)*UtilC12)))" .

hemo:7005527 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-LOCF-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000836 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`locf_icer <- function(u_T, u_C, tau_T, tau_C, dC, t = c(0, 0.5, 1)) { K <- length(t); w <- (c(diff(t), 0) + c(0, diff(t))) / 2; aT <- sum(w * u_T); aC <- sum(w * u_C); bT <- aT + w[K] * tau_T * (u_T[K-1]-u_T[K]); bC <- aC + w[K] * tau_C * (u_C[K-1]-u_C[K]); c(dQ_full = aT-aC, dQ_LOCF = bT-bC, ICER_full = dC / (aT-aC), ICER_LOCF = dC / (bT-bC)) }` Takes each arm's mean utilities at visit times t, in years, and carries the second-last visit forward into the last for the stated fractions." ;
    hemo:9000024 "locf_icer <- function(u_T, u_C, tau_T, tau_C, dC, t = c(0, 0.5, 1)) { K <- length(t); w <- (c(diff(t), 0) + c(0, diff(t))) / 2; aT <- sum(w * u_T); aC <- sum(w * u_C); bT <- aT + w[K] * tau_T * (u_T[K-1]-u_T[K]); bC <- aC + w[K] * tau_C * (u_C[K-1]-u_C[K]); c(dQ_full = aT-aC, dQ_LOCF = bT-bC, ICER_full = dC / (aT-aC), ICER_LOCF = dC / (bT-bC)) }" .

hemo:7005528 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-LOCF-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000836 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def locf_icer(u_T, u_C, tau_T, tau_C, dC, t=(0, 0.5, 1)): K = len(t); w = [((t[k]-t[k-1]) if k > 0 else 0) / 2 + ((t[k+1]-t[k]) if k < K-1 else 0) / 2 for k in range(K)]; aT = sum(wk * uk for wk, uk in zip(w, u_T)); aC = sum(wk * uk for wk, uk in zip(w, u_C)); bT = aT + w[-1] * tau_T * (u_T[-2]-u_T[-1]); bC = aC + w[-1] * tau_C * (u_C[-2]-u_C[-1]); return {'dQ_full': aT-aC, 'dQ_LOCF': bT-bC, 'ICER_full': dC / (aT-aC), 'ICER_LOCF': dC / (bT-bC)}` The same steps in plain Python for any visit schedule." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def locf_icer(u_T, u_C, tau_T, tau_C, dC, t=(0, 0.5, 1)): K = len(t); w = [((t[k]-t[k-1]) if k > 0 else 0) / 2 + ((t[k+1]-t[k]) if k < K-1 else 0) / 2 for k in range(K)]; aT = sum(wk * uk for wk, uk in zip(w, u_T)); aC = sum(wk * uk for wk, uk in zip(w, u_C)); bT = aT + w[-1] * tau_T * (u_T[-2]-u_T[-1]); bC = aC + w[-1] * tau_C * (u_C[-2]-u_C[-1]); return {'dQ_full': aT-aC, 'dQ_LOCF': bT-bC, 'ICER_full': dC / (aT-aC), 'ICER_LOCF': dC / (bT-bC)}" .

hemo:7005531 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for discounted QALYs and cost per QALY of a one-off treatment under a chosen timing convention"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The whole incremental cost falls in year 0, the gain is the same in every year and one rate applies to costs and QALYs, as in the NICE reference case. With s set to 1 the first year's gain is counted at year 0 and is not discounted." .

hemo:7005572 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-DISC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000837 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=OneOffCost/(YearlyQALYs*SUM((1+DiscRate)^-(SEQUENCE(Years)-StartFlag)))` Returns the discounted cost per QALY from named cells, with StartFlag holding 0 or 1. SEQUENCE needs Excel 365 or Excel 2021." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=OneOffCost/(YearlyQALYs*SUM((1+DiscRate)^-(SEQUENCE(Years)-StartFlag)))" .

hemo:7005573 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-DISC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000837 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`disc_icer <- function(cost0, q, years, rate, start = 0) { a <- sum((1+rate)^-((1:years)-start)); q_d <- q * a; icer_d <- cost0 / q_d; icer_0 <- cost0 / (q * years); c(A = a, Q_d = q_d, ICER_d = icer_d, ICER_0 = icer_0, rise = icer_d / icer_0-1) }` Returns a named vector, and works at a zero rate." ;
    hemo:9000024 "disc_icer <- function(cost0, q, years, rate, start = 0) { a <- sum((1+rate)^-((1:years)-start)); q_d <- q * a; icer_d <- cost0 / q_d; icer_0 <- cost0 / (q * years); c(A = a, Q_d = q_d, ICER_d = icer_d, ICER_0 = icer_0, rise = icer_d / icer_0-1) }" .

hemo:7005574 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-DISC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000837 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def disc_icer(cost0, q, years, rate, start=0): a = sum((1+rate)**-(t-start) for t in range(1, years+1)); q_d = q*a; icer_d = cost0/q_d; icer_0 = cost0/(q*years); return a, q_d, icer_d, icer_0, icer_d/icer_0-1` Plain Python with no imports, returning a tuple in the order of the outputs." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def disc_icer(cost0, q, years, rate, start=0): a = sum((1+rate)**-(t-start) for t in range(1, years+1)); q_d = q*a; icer_d = cost0/q_d; icer_0 = cost0/(q*years); return a, q_d, icer_d, icer_0, icer_d/icer_0-1" .

hemo:7005577 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for annualised capital cost per unit of service from several capital inputs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every input is annualised at the evaluation's discount rate with end-of-year sums, each input serves only the evaluated service, and the volume is the same in every year. A shared asset is first split between services as described on the Capital Cost page." .

hemo:7005580 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ANLS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000838 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=SUMPRODUCT((Price-Resale*(1+Rate)^-Life)/((1-(1+Rate)^-Life)/Rate))/Volume` With one row per capital input in ranges named Price, Resale and Life, the rate in Rate and the annual volume in Volume, the formula returns the annualised capital cost per unit. `=SUMPRODUCT((Price-Resale)/Life)/Volume` gives the straight-line figure." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT((Price-Resale*(1+Rate)^-Life)/((1-(1+Rate)^-Life)/Rate))/Volume" .

hemo:7005581 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ANLS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000838 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`capital_cost_per_unit <- function(K, S, n, r, Q) { E <- (K-S*(1+r)^(-n))/((1-(1+r)^(-n))/r); c(E_total = sum(E), c_unit = sum(E)/Q, c_SL = sum((K-S)/n)/Q) }` Vectorised over K, S and n, with one element per capital input." ;
    hemo:9000024 "capital_cost_per_unit <- function(K, S, n, r, Q) { E <- (K-S*(1+r)^(-n))/((1-(1+r)^(-n))/r); c(E_total = sum(E), c_unit = sum(E)/Q, c_SL = sum((K-S)/n)/Q) }" .

hemo:7005582 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ANLS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000838 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def capital_cost_per_unit(K, S, n, r, Q): E = [(k-s*(1+r)**(-m))/((1-(1+r)**(-m))/r) for k, s, m in zip(K, S, n)]; return sum(E), sum(E)/Q, sum((k-s)/m for k, s, m in zip(K, S, n))/Q` Takes lists of prices, resale values and lives and returns the total annual charge, the cost per unit and the straight-line cost per unit." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def capital_cost_per_unit(K, S, n, r, Q): E = [(k-s*(1+r)**(-m))/((1-(1+r)**(-m))/r) for k, s, m in zip(K, S, n)]; return sum(E), sum(E)/Q, sum((k-s)/m for k, s, m in zip(K, S, n))/Q" .

hemo:7005585 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for the yearly return on capital and price recovered in an annualised charge"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The input has no resale value, the charge falls at the end of each year and the return is calculated at the rate used to annualise the charge. Over the n years the returns sum to n times E less K and the recovered parts sum to K." .

hemo:7005588 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ANLS-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000839 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=-IPMT(DiscountRate,Year,UsefulLife,CapitalPrice)` returns the return R_t for the year in the cell named Year, and `=-PPMT(DiscountRate,Year,UsefulLife,CapitalPrice)` returns the recovered part P_t. The two sum to the charge from PMT in every year." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=-IPMT(DiscountRate,Year,UsefulLife,CapitalPrice)" .

hemo:7005589 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ANLS-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000839 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`capital_schedule <- function(K, r, n) { E <- K*r/(1-(1+r)^(-n)); t <- 1:n; B <- K*(1+r)^(t-1)-E*((1+r)^(t-1)-1)/r; data.frame(year = t, balance = B, return_on_capital = r*B, recovered = E-r*B) }` Returns one row per year of use." ;
    hemo:9000024 "capital_schedule <- function(K, r, n) { E <- K*r/(1-(1+r)^(-n)); t <- 1:n; B <- K*(1+r)^(t-1)-E*((1+r)^(t-1)-1)/r; data.frame(year = t, balance = B, return_on_capital = r*B, recovered = E-r*B) }" .

hemo:7005590 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ANLS-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000839 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def capital_schedule(K, r, n): E = K*r/(1-(1+r)**(-n)); B = [K*(1+r)**(t-1)-E*((1+r)**(t-1)-1)/r for t in range(1, n+1)]; return [(t, b, r*b, E-r*b) for t, b in enumerate(B, 1)]` Returns a list of tuples of year, balance, return and recovered part." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def capital_schedule(K, r, n): E = K*r/(1-(1+r)**(-n)); B = [K*(1+r)**(t-1)-E*((1+r)**(t-1)-1)/r for t in range(1, n+1)]; return [(t, b, r*b, E-r*b) for t, b in enumerate(B, 1)]" .

hemo:7005593 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for a baseline-risk adjusted odds ratio, events avoided and ICER from a centred meta-regression"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The meta-regression was fitted on the logit scale with the true baseline log-odds as covariate, centred at x_bar, and the decision population's risk lies inside the range of the trials. Costs and QALYs accrue within one period with no discounting, as in the article's illustrative example, and C_net and Q_gain are positive." .

hemo:7005596 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-MREG-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000840 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=LN(B1/(1-B1))` in B9, `=B2+B3*(B9-B4)` in B10, `=EXP(B10)` in B11, `=B1/(1-B1)*B11/(1+B1/(1-B1)*B11)` in B12, `=1000*(B1-B12)` in B13, `=1000*B5-B13*B6` in B14, `=B13*B7` in B15 and `=B14/B15` in B16. With p_0, d, b, x_bar, c_tx, s_ev and q_ev in cells B1 to B7, cells B9 to B16 return z, theta_z, OR_z, p_1, N_av, C_net, Q_gain and the ICER." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LN(B1/(1-B1))" .

hemo:7005597 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-MREG-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000840 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`mr_adjust <- function(p0, d, b, xbar, c_tx, s_ev, q_ev) { z <- log(p0/(1-p0)); th <- d+b*(z-xbar); or_z <- exp(th); o0 <- p0/(1-p0); p1 <- o0*or_z/(1+o0*or_z); n_av <- 1000*(p0-p1); c_net <- 1000*c_tx-n_av*s_ev; q_gain <- n_av*q_ev; c(z = z, theta_z = th, OR_z = or_z, p_1 = p1, N_av = n_av, C_net = c_net, Q_gain = q_gain, ICER = c_net/q_gain) }` Vectorised over p0, so a vector of baseline risks returns the adjusted results for each subgroup; `mr_adjust(0.05, -0.40, 0.30, -1.70, 300, 2000, 0.5)` reproduces the article's example." ;
    hemo:9000024 "mr_adjust <- function(p0, d, b, xbar, c_tx, s_ev, q_ev) { z <- log(p0/(1-p0)); th <- d+b*(z-xbar); or_z <- exp(th); o0 <- p0/(1-p0); p1 <- o0*or_z/(1+o0*or_z); n_av <- 1000*(p0-p1); c_net <- 1000*c_tx-n_av*s_ev; q_gain <- n_av*q_ev; c(z = z, theta_z = th, OR_z = or_z, p_1 = p1, N_av = n_av, C_net = c_net, Q_gain = q_gain, ICER = c_net/q_gain) }" .

hemo:7005598 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-MREG-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000840 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def mr_adjust(p0, d, b, xbar, c_tx, s_ev, q_ev): z = math.log(p0/(1-p0)); th = d+b*(z-xbar); or_z = math.exp(th); o0 = p0/(1-p0); p1 = o0*or_z/(1+o0*or_z); n_av = 1000*(p0-p1); c_net = 1000*c_tx-n_av*s_ev; q_gain = n_av*q_ev; return z, th, or_z, p1, n_av, c_net, q_gain, c_net/q_gain` Uses the math module, where math.log is the natural logarithm, and returns the eight outputs in the order listed above." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def mr_adjust(p0, d, b, xbar, c_tx, s_ev, q_ev): z = math.log(p0/(1-p0)); th = d+b*(z-xbar); or_z = math.exp(th); o0 = p0/(1-p0); p1 = o0*or_z/(1+o0*or_z); n_av = 1000*(p0-p1); c_net = 1000*c_tx-n_av*s_ev; q_gain = n_av*q_ev; return z, th, or_z, p1, n_av, c_net, q_gain, c_net/q_gain" .

hemo:7005601 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for a yearly stream weighted by the simple hyperbola against constant-rate discounting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The calculation is descriptive, showing how an individual with simple hyperbolic preferences would weigh the stream. A reference-case evaluation for NICE uses the constant 3.5% a year of PMG36 section 4.5.1, which is the `Q_E` column. Both sums count the first amount one period ahead." .

hemo:7005605 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-HYPD-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000841 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=YearlyAmount*SUM(1/(1+HypAlpha*SEQUENCE(Years)))/(YearlyAmount*SUM((1+HypAlpha)^-SEQUENCE(Years)))` Returns the ratio from named cells; the numerator alone is `Q_H` and the denominator alone `Q_E`. SEQUENCE needs Excel 365 or Excel 2021." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=YearlyAmount*SUM(1/(1+HypAlpha*SEQUENCE(Years)))/(YearlyAmount*SUM((1+HypAlpha)^-SEQUENCE(Years)))" .

hemo:7005606 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-HYPD-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000841 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`hyp_stream <- function(q, years, alpha) { t <- 1:years; s_h <- sum(1/(1+alpha*t)); s_e <- sum((1+alpha)^-t); c(S_H = s_h, S_E = s_e, Q_H = q*s_h, Q_E = q*s_e, ratio = s_h/s_e) }` Returns a named vector and works at alpha = 0." ;
    hemo:9000024 "hyp_stream <- function(q, years, alpha) { t <- 1:years; s_h <- sum(1/(1+alpha*t)); s_e <- sum((1+alpha)^-t); c(S_H = s_h, S_E = s_e, Q_H = q*s_h, Q_E = q*s_e, ratio = s_h/s_e) }" .

hemo:7005607 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-HYPD-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000841 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def hyp_stream(q, years, alpha): s_h = sum(1/(1+alpha*t) for t in range(1, years+1)); s_e = sum((1+alpha)**-t for t in range(1, years+1)); return s_h, s_e, q*s_h, q*s_e, s_h/s_e` Plain Python with no imports, returning a tuple in the order of the outputs." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def hyp_stream(q, years, alpha): s_h = sum(1/(1+alpha*t) for t in range(1, years+1)); s_e = sum((1+alpha)**-t for t in range(1, years+1)); return s_h, s_e, q*s_h, q*s_e, s_h/s_e" .

hemo:7005610 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for a smaller-sooner against larger-later plan check with commitment thresholds under a chosen discount form"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The amounts are fixed, the weights are seen from today and the later outcome arrives one period after the sooner one. The calculator line below follows the quasi-hyperbolic path, which gives constant-rate weights when beta = 1; the R and Python code cover all four forms." .

hemo:7005615 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-HYPD-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000842 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=AND(LaterAmount*QHBeta*QHDelta-SoonerAmount<0,LaterAmount*QHBeta*QHDelta^2-SoonerAmount*QHBeta*QHDelta>0)` Returns TRUE when a quasi-hyperbolic plan reverses; `=MAX(SoonerAmount/(QHBeta*QHDelta)-LaterAmount,0)` returns the reward threshold." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(LaterAmount*QHBeta*QHDelta-SoonerAmount<0,LaterAmount*QHBeta*QHDelta^2-SoonerAmount*QHBeta*QHDelta>0)" .

hemo:7005616 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-HYPD-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000842 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`plan_check <- function(a, b, form, alpha = 0, beta = 1, delta = 1, s_max = 120) { w <- switch(form, exp = function(t) delta^t, hyp = function(t) 1/(1+alpha*t), gen = function(t) (1+alpha*t)^(-beta/alpha), qh = function(t) ifelse(t == 0, 1, beta*delta^t)); m0 <- b*w(1)-a*w(0); m1 <- b*w(2)-a*w(1); s <- 0:s_max; ok <- which(b*w(s+1) > a*w(s)); list(w_1 = w(1), w_2 = w(2), M_0 = m0, M_1 = m1, reversal = m0 < 0 & m1 > 0, P = max(-m0, 0), R = max(-m0/w(1), 0), s_star = if (length(ok)) s[ok[1]] else NA) }` Returns a named list." ;
    hemo:9000024 "plan_check <- function(a, b, form, alpha = 0, beta = 1, delta = 1, s_max = 120) { w <- switch(form, exp = function(t) delta^t, hyp = function(t) 1/(1+alpha*t), gen = function(t) (1+alpha*t)^(-beta/alpha), qh = function(t) ifelse(t == 0, 1, beta*delta^t)); m0 <- b*w(1)-a*w(0); m1 <- b*w(2)-a*w(1); s <- 0:s_max; ok <- which(b*w(s+1) > a*w(s)); list(w_1 = w(1), w_2 = w(2), M_0 = m0, M_1 = m1, reversal = m0 < 0 & m1 > 0, P = max(-m0, 0), R = max(-m0/w(1), 0), s_star = if (length(ok)) s[ok[1]] else NA) }" .

hemo:7005617 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-HYPD-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000842 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def plan_check(a, b, form, alpha=0.0, beta=1.0, delta=1.0, s_max=120): w = {'exp': lambda t: delta**t, 'hyp': lambda t: 1/(1+alpha*t), 'gen': lambda t: (1+alpha*t)**(-beta/alpha) if t else 1.0, 'qh': lambda t: beta*delta**t if t else 1.0}[form]; m0 = b*w(1)-a*w(0); m1 = b*w(2)-a*w(1); s_star = next((s for s in range(s_max+1) if b*w(s+1) > a*w(s)), None); return w(1), w(2), m0, m1, m0 < 0 < m1, max(-m0, 0), max(-m0/w(1), 0), s_star` Plain Python with no imports." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def plan_check(a, b, form, alpha=0.0, beta=1.0, delta=1.0, s_max=120): w = {'exp': lambda t: delta**t, 'hyp': lambda t: 1/(1+alpha*t), 'gen': lambda t: (1+alpha*t)**(-beta/alpha) if t else 1.0, 'qh': lambda t: beta*delta**t if t else 1.0}[form]; m0 = b*w(1)-a*w(0); m1 = b*w(2)-a*w(1); s_star = next((s for s in range(s_max+1) if b*w(s+1) > a*w(s)), None); return w(1), w(2), m0, m1, m0 < 0 < m1, max(-m0, 0), max(-m0/w(1), 0), s_star" .

hemo:7005620 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for per-person and population EVSI of a two-arm trial under normal incremental net benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Incremental net benefit is normal a priori and the trial estimates it with normal error, the two arms are independent with equal numbers, and the choice is between the two arms' options. The trial informs incremental net benefit directly; a trial that informs only some parameters needs the variance explained by them, and its EVSI is bounded by their EVPPI (HE-FM-VOI-003)." .

hemo:7005624 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-EVSI-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000843 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=LET(pvar,PriorSDINB^2,nzero,(SDArmA^2+SDArmB^2)/pvar,spre,SQRT(pvar*PatientsPerArm/(PatientsPerArm+nzero)),zval,ABS(PriorMeanINB)/spre,spre*(NORM.S.DIST(zval,FALSE)-zval*(1-NORM.S.DIST(zval,TRUE))))` With named cells PriorMeanINB, PriorSDINB, SDArmA, SDArmB and PatientsPerArm, the formula returns per-person EVSI in Excel 2021 or Microsoft 365. Multiplying it by `AnnualPop*PV(DiscountRate,DecisionYears,-1,0,1)` gives population EVSI." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LET(pvar,PriorSDINB^2,nzero,(SDArmA^2+SDArmB^2)/pvar,spre,SQRT(pvar*PatientsPerArm/(PatientsPerArm+nzero)),zval,ABS(PriorMeanINB)/spre,spre*(NORM.S.DIST(zval,FALSE)-zval*(1-NORM.S.DIST(zval,TRUE))))" .

hemo:7005625 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-EVSI-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000843 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`evsi_normal <- function(m0, sd0, sd_A, sd_B, n, N, Y, r) { L <- function(s) { z <- abs(m0)/s; s*(dnorm(z)-z*(1-pnorm(z))) }; n0 <- (sd_A^2+sd_B^2)/sd0^2; s <- sqrt(sd0^2*n/(n+n0)); e <- L(s); data.frame(n = n, EVSI = e, EVPI = L(sd0), share = e/L(sd0), EVSI_pop = e*N*(1+r)*(1-(1+r)^(-Y))/r) }` Vectorised over n, so one call traces EVSI against trial size." ;
    hemo:9000024 "evsi_normal <- function(m0, sd0, sd_A, sd_B, n, N, Y, r) { L <- function(s) { z <- abs(m0)/s; s*(dnorm(z)-z*(1-pnorm(z))) }; n0 <- (sd_A^2+sd_B^2)/sd0^2; s <- sqrt(sd0^2*n/(n+n0)); e <- L(s); data.frame(n = n, EVSI = e, EVPI = L(sd0), share = e/L(sd0), EVSI_pop = e*N*(1+r)*(1-(1+r)^(-Y))/r) }" .

hemo:7005626 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-EVSI-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000843 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def evsi_normal(m0, sd0, sd_A, sd_B, n, N, Y, r): nd = statistics.NormalDist(); L = lambda s: s*(nd.pdf(abs(m0)/s)-abs(m0)/s*(1-nd.cdf(abs(m0)/s))); n0 = (sd_A**2+sd_B**2)/sd0**2; s = math.sqrt(sd0**2*n/(n+n0)); e = L(s); return e, L(sd0), e/L(sd0), e*N*(1+r)*(1-(1+r)**(-Y))/r` Needs the standard library modules math and statistics only, and returns EVSI, EVPI, the share and population EVSI." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def evsi_normal(m0, sd0, sd_A, sd_B, n, N, Y, r): nd = statistics.NormalDist(); L = lambda s: s*(nd.pdf(abs(m0)/s)-abs(m0)/s*(1-nd.cdf(abs(m0)/s))); n0 = (sd_A**2+sd_B**2)/sd0**2; s = math.sqrt(sd0**2*n/(n+n0)); e = L(s); return e, L(sd0), e/L(sd0), e*N*(1+r)*(1-(1+r)**(-Y))/r" .

hemo:7005629 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for weighted and unweighted population net health benefit across several groups"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each group carries one weight, the weights act on the incremental QALYs only, and the displaced health is unweighted. The weights are set beforehand, for example from the severity band rule HE-FM-ASF-003." .

hemo:7005633 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-EQWA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000844 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=SUMPRODUCT(GroupSize,EquityWeight,QALYGainPerPatient)-SUMPRODUCT(GroupSize,CostPerPatient)/QALYValue` With one row per group in ranges named GroupSize, EquityWeight, QALYGainPerPatient and CostPerPatient, and the value of a QALY in QALYValue, the formula returns the weighted population net health benefit. `=SUMPRODUCT(GroupSize,QALYGainPerPatient)-SUMPRODUCT(GroupSize,CostPerPatient)/QALYValue` gives the unweighted value, and `=SUMPRODUCT(GroupSize,CostPerPatient)/SUMPRODUCT(GroupSize,EquityWeight,QALYGainPerPatient)` the weighted ICER." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(GroupSize,EquityWeight,QALYGainPerPatient)-SUMPRODUCT(GroupSize,CostPerPatient)/QALYValue" .

hemo:7005634 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-EQWA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000844 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`eq_nhb <- function(N, w, dE, dC, lambda) { Eu <- sum(N*dE); Ew <- sum(N*w*dE); C <- sum(N*dC); Hd <- C/lambda; c(E_u = Eu, E_w = Ew, C_tot = C, H_d = Hd, NHB_u = Eu-Hd, NHB_w = Ew-Hd, ICER_u = C/Eu, ICER_w = C/Ew) }` Vectorised over N, w, dE and dC, with one element per group." ;
    hemo:9000024 "eq_nhb <- function(N, w, dE, dC, lambda) { Eu <- sum(N*dE); Ew <- sum(N*w*dE); C <- sum(N*dC); Hd <- C/lambda; c(E_u = Eu, E_w = Ew, C_tot = C, H_d = Hd, NHB_u = Eu-Hd, NHB_w = Ew-Hd, ICER_u = C/Eu, ICER_w = C/Ew) }" .

hemo:7005635 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-EQWA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000844 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def eq_nhb(N, w, dE, dC, lam): Eu = sum(n*e for n, e in zip(N, dE)); Ew = sum(n*v*e for n, v, e in zip(N, w, dE)); C = sum(n*c for n, c in zip(N, dC)); Hd = C/lam; return {\"E_u\": Eu, \"E_w\": Ew, \"C_tot\": C, \"H_d\": Hd, \"NHB_u\": Eu-Hd, \"NHB_w\": Ew-Hd, \"ICER_u\": C/Eu, \"ICER_w\": C/Ew}` Takes lists with one element per group and the value of a QALY, and returns a dictionary of the outputs." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def eq_nhb(N, w, dE, dC, lam): Eu = sum(n*e for n, e in zip(N, dE)); Ew = sum(n*v*e for n, v, e in zip(N, w, dE)); C = sum(n*c for n, c in zip(N, dC)); Hd = C/lam; return {\"E_u\": Eu, \"E_w\": Ew, \"C_tot\": C, \"H_d\": Hd, \"NHB_u\": Eu-Hd, \"NHB_w\": Ew-Hd, \"ICER_u\": C/Eu, \"ICER_w\": C/Ew}" .

hemo:7005638 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for DALYs averted and cost per DALY averted from deaths, disability and duration in two scenarios"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Deaths occur at the start and at one age, survivors live exactly T years at one weight and then recover fully, and deaths and disability are both discounted continuously from time zero with no age weighting, as in HE-FM-DALY-004." .

hemo:7005641 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-DALY-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000845 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=CostDiff/((DeathsComp-DeathsProg)*IF(DiscRate=0,StdLE,(1-EXP(-DiscRate*StdLE))/DiscRate)+((CohortSize-DeathsComp)*DWComp-(CohortSize-DeathsProg)*DWProg)*IF(DiscRate=0,DurationYrs,(1-EXP(-DiscRate*DurationYrs))/DiscRate))` Returns the cost per DALY averted from named cells and takes a zero rate as the undiscounted case. It uses no LET, so it runs in any current version of Excel." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CostDiff/((DeathsComp-DeathsProg)*IF(DiscRate=0,StdLE,(1-EXP(-DiscRate*StdLE))/DiscRate)+((CohortSize-DeathsComp)*DWComp-(CohortSize-DeathsProg)*DWProg)*IF(DiscRate=0,DurationYrs,(1-EXP(-DiscRate*DurationYrs))/DiscRate))" .

hemo:7005642 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-DALY-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000845 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`daly_cea <- function(N, D0, D1, DW0, DW1, Tdur, L, dC, r = 0) { fL <- if (r > 0) (1-exp(-r*L))/r else L; fT <- if (r > 0) (1-exp(-r*Tdur))/r else Tdur; d0 <- D0*fL+(N-D0)*DW0*fT; d1 <- D1*fL+(N-D1)*DW1*fT; av <- d0-d1; c(DALY_0 = d0, DALY_1 = d1, DALY_av = av, share_YLL = (D0-D1)*fL/av, CPD = dC/av) }` Base R, returning a named vector; `daly_cea(200, 20, 8, 0.3, 0.1, 10, 45, 444000)` gives the undiscounted case." ;
    hemo:9000024 "daly_cea <- function(N, D0, D1, DW0, DW1, Tdur, L, dC, r = 0) { fL <- if (r > 0) (1-exp(-r*L))/r else L; fT <- if (r > 0) (1-exp(-r*Tdur))/r else Tdur; d0 <- D0*fL+(N-D0)*DW0*fT; d1 <- D1*fL+(N-D1)*DW1*fT; av <- d0-d1; c(DALY_0 = d0, DALY_1 = d1, DALY_av = av, share_YLL = (D0-D1)*fL/av, CPD = dC/av) }" .

hemo:7005643 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-DALY-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000845 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def daly_cea(N, D0, D1, DW0, DW1, T, L, dC, r=0): fL = (1-math.exp(-r*L))/r if r > 0 else L; fT = (1-math.exp(-r*T))/r if r > 0 else T; d0 = D0*fL+(N-D0)*DW0*fT; d1 = D1*fL+(N-D1)*DW1*fT; av = d0-d1; return d0, d1, av, (D0-D1)*fL/av, dC/av` Needs `import math` and returns a tuple in the order of the outputs." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def daly_cea(N, D0, D1, DW0, DW1, T, L, dC, r=0): fL = (1-math.exp(-r*L))/r if r > 0 else L; fT = (1-math.exp(-r*T))/r if r > 0 else T; d0 = D0*fL+(N-D0)*DW0*fT; d1 = D1*fL+(N-D1)*DW1*fT; av = d0-d1; return d0, d1, av, (D0-D1)*fL/av, dC/av" .

hemo:7005646 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for scoring an EQ-5D-3L profile under any constant and N3 coefficient set"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The value set has the constant and N3 form with no other interaction terms. Sets built differently, such as the US D1 model of Shaw and colleagues 2005, need their own scoring rule. Coefficients printed as negative numbers are entered without their signs." .

hemo:7005650 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-EQ3L-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000846 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=1-Const*(SUM(Levels)>5)-SUMPRODUCT((Levels=2)*Level2Dec+(Levels=3)*Level3Dec)-CoefN3*(MAX(Levels)=3)` With the five levels in a row range named Levels, the five level 2 decrements in a range named Level2Dec and the five level 3 decrements in Level3Dec, of the same shape, and the constant and N3 coefficients in Const and CoefN3, the formula returns U." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1-Const*(SUM(Levels)>5)-SUMPRODUCT((Levels=2)*Level2Dec+(Levels=3)*Level3Dec)-CoefN3*(MAX(Levels)=3)" .

hemo:7005651 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-EQ3L-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000846 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`eq3l_utility <- function(s, cf) { l <- as.integer(strsplit(as.character(s), \"\")[[1]]); dims <- c(\"MO\", \"SC\", \"UA\", \"PD\", \"AD\"); dec <- sum(sapply(1:5, function(d) if (l[d] == 1) 0 else cf[[paste0(dims[d], l[d])]])); 1-cf[[\"c0\"]]*any(l > 1)-dec-cf[[\"N3\"]]*any(l == 3) }` Takes the profile as a string and a named vector with elements c0, MO2 to AD3 and N3. With `uk <- c(c0 = 0.081, MO2 = 0.069, MO3 = 0.314, SC2 = 0.104, SC3 = 0.214, UA2 = 0.036, UA3 = 0.094, PD2 = 0.123, PD3 = 0.386, AD2 = 0.071, AD3 = 0.236, N3 = 0.269)`, `v <- sapply(apply(expand.grid(rep(list(1:3), 5)), 1, paste, collapse = \"\"), eq3l_utility, cf = uk)` scores all 243 states." ;
    hemo:9000024 "eq3l_utility <- function(s, cf) { l <- as.integer(strsplit(as.character(s), \"\")[[1]]); dims <- c(\"MO\", \"SC\", \"UA\", \"PD\", \"AD\"); dec <- sum(sapply(1:5, function(d) if (l[d] == 1) 0 else cf[[paste0(dims[d], l[d])]])); 1-cf[[\"c0\"]]*any(l > 1)-dec-cf[[\"N3\"]]*any(l == 3) }" .

hemo:7005652 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-EQ3L-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000846 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def eq3l_utility(s, cf): l = [int(c) for c in str(s)]; dec = sum(cf[d + str(x)] for d, x in zip([\"MO\", \"SC\", \"UA\", \"PD\", \"AD\"], l) if x > 1); return 1-cf[\"c0\"]*any(x > 1 for x in l)-dec-cf[\"N3\"]*any(x == 3 for x in l)` Takes the profile as a string or integer and a dictionary with keys c0, MO2 to AD3 and N3. With `uk = {\"c0\": 0.081, \"MO2\": 0.069, \"MO3\": 0.314, \"SC2\": 0.104, \"SC3\": 0.214, \"UA2\": 0.036, \"UA3\": 0.094, \"PD2\": 0.123, \"PD3\": 0.386, \"AD2\": 0.071, \"AD3\": 0.236, \"N3\": 0.269}`, `from itertools import product; v = [eq3l_utility(\"\".join(map(str, p)), uk) for p in product((1, 2, 3), repeat=5)]` scores all 243 states." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def eq3l_utility(s, cf): l = [int(c) for c in str(s)]; dec = sum(cf[d + str(x)] for d, x in zip([\"MO\", \"SC\", \"UA\", \"PD\", \"AD\"], l) if x > 1); return 1-cf[\"c0\"]*any(x > 1 for x in l)-dec-cf[\"N3\"]*any(x == 3 for x in l)" .

hemo:7005655 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for EVPI, error probability and population EVPI from a three-option table of draws"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The draws are equally likely, the option with the highest mean net benefit is chosen on current evidence, the same number of patients faces the decision in each year, counted at the start of each year, and per-person EVPI applies unchanged to every cohort." .

hemo:7005658 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-EVPI-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000847 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=SUMPRODUCT(RowMax-INDEX(NB,0,MATCH(MAX(ColMeans),ColMeans,0)))/ROWS(NB)` returns per-person EVPI from a table named NB with one column per option, a helper column RowMax and a helper row ColMeans. `=SUMPRODUCT(--(RowMax>INDEX(NB,0,MATCH(MAX(ColMeans),ColMeans,0))))/ROWS(NB)` returns the error probability, and `=EVPI*Patients*(1-(1+Rate)^-Years)*(1+Rate)/Rate` population EVPI with cells named EVPI, Patients, Rate and Years." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(RowMax-INDEX(NB,0,MATCH(MAX(ColMeans),ColMeans,0)))/ROWS(NB)" .

hemo:7005659 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-EVPI-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000847 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`evpi_table <- function(NB, I, Y, r, lambda) { d <- which.max(colMeans(NB)); L <- apply(NB, 1, max)-NB[, d]; N <- I*sum((1+r)^-(0:(Y-1))); c(EVPI = mean(L), P_err = mean(L > 0), EVPI_pop = mean(L)*N, EVPI_popM = lambda*mean(L)*N) }` Takes a matrix with one row per draw and one column per option." ;
    hemo:9000024 "evpi_table <- function(NB, I, Y, r, lambda) { d <- which.max(colMeans(NB)); L <- apply(NB, 1, max)-NB[, d]; N <- I*sum((1+r)^-(0:(Y-1))); c(EVPI = mean(L), P_err = mean(L > 0), EVPI_pop = mean(L)*N, EVPI_popM = lambda*mean(L)*N) }" .

hemo:7005660 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-EVPI-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000847 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def evpi_table(NB, I, Y, r, lam): m = [sum(c)/len(NB) for c in zip(*NB)]; d = m.index(max(m)); L = [max(row)-row[d] for row in NB]; N = I*sum((1+r)**-t for t in range(Y)); e = sum(L)/len(L); return e, sum(x > 0 for x in L)/len(L), e*N, lam*e*N` Takes a list of rows, one per draw, each with one net benefit per option." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def evpi_table(NB, I, Y, r, lam): m = [sum(c)/len(NB) for c in zip(*NB)]; d = m.index(max(m)); L = [max(row)-row[d] for row in NB]; N = I*sum((1+r)**-t for t in range(Y)); e = sum(L)/len(L); return e, sum(x > 0 for x in L)/len(L), e*N, lam*e*N" .

hemo:7005663 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for regression-based EVPPI and its EVPI bound from a probabilistic sample table"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The runs are equally weighted draws from the joint distribution of all parameters. The regression is flexible enough to give the conditional mean of each incremental net benefit given the parameters of interest, so interactions among them are entered as columns of `X`." .

hemo:7005667 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-EVPP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000848 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=LET(g,TREND(RawInc2,ThetaI),AVERAGE(IF(g>0,g,0))-MAX(AVERAGE(g),0))` Returns the estimate for two options from the raw incremental net benefits in RawInc2 and the regressor columns in ThetaI. `=AVERAGE(IF(RawInc2>0,RawInc2,0))-MAX(AVERAGE(RawInc2),0)` returns the EVPI from the same runs. LET needs Excel 365 or Excel 2021." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LET(g,TREND(RawInc2,ThetaI),AVERAGE(IF(g>0,g,0))-MAX(AVERAGE(g),0))" .

hemo:7005668 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-EVPP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000848 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`evppi_reg <- function(X, NB, tol = 1e-6) { X <- as.data.frame(X); B <- NB-NB[, 1]; G <- sapply(1:ncol(B), function(d) { y <- B[, d]; if (all(y == 0)) y else fitted(lm(y ~ ., data = X)) }); evppi <- mean(apply(G, 1, max))-max(colMeans(G)); evpi <- mean(apply(B, 1, max))-max(colMeans(B)); c(EVPPI = evppi, EVPI = evpi, ok = as.numeric(evppi >= -tol && evppi <= evpi + tol)) }` Base R only. For a generalised additive model, the lm call is replaced by a gam call from the mgcv package." ;
    hemo:9000024 "evppi_reg <- function(X, NB, tol = 1e-6) { X <- as.data.frame(X); B <- NB-NB[, 1]; G <- sapply(1:ncol(B), function(d) { y <- B[, d]; if (all(y == 0)) y else fitted(lm(y ~ ., data = X)) }); evppi <- mean(apply(G, 1, max))-max(colMeans(G)); evpi <- mean(apply(B, 1, max))-max(colMeans(B)); c(EVPPI = evppi, EVPI = evpi, ok = as.numeric(evppi >= -tol && evppi <= evpi + tol)) }" .

hemo:7005669 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-EVPP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000848 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`import numpy as np` then `def evppi_reg(X, NB, tol=1e-6): B = NB-NB[:, [0]]; Z = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(B)), X]); G = Z @ np.linalg.lstsq(Z, B, rcond=None)[0]; evppi = G.max(axis=1).mean()-G.mean(axis=0).max(); evpi = B.max(axis=1).mean()-B.mean(axis=0).max(); return evppi, evpi, int(-tol <= evppi <= evpi+tol)` NumPy arrays in, a tuple out in the order of the outputs." ;
    hemo:9000024 "import numpy as np" .

hemo:7005672 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for the efficient sample size over a grid of study designs by ENBS"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "One beneficiary population applies to every design, the article's simplification. Where larger designs report later, N and Y are entered separately for each design or `N_w` carries the difference. Per-person EVSI rises with sample size but stays below per-person EVPI while costs grow with each participant, so ENBS typically rises to a peak and then falls, but the code evaluates the whole grid and does not rely on that shape." .

hemo:7005677 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ENBS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000849 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=IF(MAX(ENBSRange)>0,INDEX(SampleSize,MATCH(MAX(ENBSRange),ENBSRange,0)),\"none\")` Returns the efficient sample size, where SampleSize holds the candidate sizes in column A and ENBSRange the ENBS of each design, filled down from `=B2*AnnualPopulation*(1+DiscountRate)*(1-(1+DiscountRate)^(-Years))/DiscountRate-(FixedCost+CostPerParticipant*A2+InferiorShare*A2*ABS(ExpectedINB))-C2*MAX(ExpectedINB,0)` with the per-person EVSI of each design in column B and the number waiting in column C. Both formulas work in any current version of Excel." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(MAX(ENBSRange)>0,INDEX(SampleSize,MATCH(MAX(ENBSRange),ENBSRange,0)),\"none\")" .

hemo:7005678 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ENBS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000849 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`enbs_grid <- function(n, evsi, N, Y, r, cf, cv, s_inf, inb, wait = 0) { D <- (1+r)*(1-(1+r)^(-Y))/r; e <- evsi*N*D-(cf+cv*n+s_inf*n*abs(inb))-wait*max(inb, 0); k <- which.max(e); list(D = D, ENBS = e, n_star = if (e[k] > 0) n[k] else NA, ENBS_max = e[k]) }` Returns a list, with `wait` a single value or one value for each design." ;
    hemo:9000024 "enbs_grid <- function(n, evsi, N, Y, r, cf, cv, s_inf, inb, wait = 0) { D <- (1+r)*(1-(1+r)^(-Y))/r; e <- evsi*N*D-(cf+cv*n+s_inf*n*abs(inb))-wait*max(inb, 0); k <- which.max(e); list(D = D, ENBS = e, n_star = if (e[k] > 0) n[k] else NA, ENBS_max = e[k]) }" .

hemo:7005679 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ENBS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000849 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def enbs_grid(n, evsi, N, Y, r, cf, cv, s_inf, inb, wait=None): wait = wait or [0]*len(n); D = (1+r)*(1-(1+r)**(-Y))/r; e = [v*N*D-(cf+cv*m+s_inf*m*abs(inb))-w*max(inb, 0) for v, m, w in zip(evsi, n, wait)]; k = e.index(max(e)); return D, e, (n[k] if e[k] > 0 else None), e[k]` Plain Python with no imports, returning the discount factor, the list of ENBS values, the efficient sample size or None, and the largest ENBS." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def enbs_grid(n, evsi, N, Y, r, cf, cv, s_inf, inb, wait=None): wait = wait or [0]*len(n); D = (1+r)*(1-(1+r)**(-Y))/r; e = [v*N*D-(cf+cv*m+s_inf*m*abs(inb))-w*max(inb, 0) for v, m, w in zip(evsi, n, wait)]; k = e.index(max(e)); return D, e, (n[k] if e[k] > 0 else None), e[k]" .

hemo:7005682 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for incremental net benefit on the money and health scales from per-arm trial summaries"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The arms are independent samples with costs and effects correlated within each arm, the normal approximation holds, and lambda is fixed." .

hemo:7005685 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-NBA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000850 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=NORM.S.DIST((Threshold*(MeanEffect1-MeanEffect0)-(MeanCost1-MeanCost0))/SQRT(Threshold^2*(SdEffect1^2/ArmSize1+SdEffect0^2/ArmSize0)+SdCost1^2/ArmSize1+SdCost0^2/ArmSize0-2*Threshold*(Corr1*SdEffect1*SdCost1/ArmSize1+Corr0*SdEffect0*SdCost0/ArmSize0)),TRUE)` Returns the probability that net benefit is positive from the named cells of HE-IM-NBA-001. The other outputs use the formulas of HE-IM-NBA-001 and HE-FM-AN-004." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=NORM.S.DIST((Threshold*(MeanEffect1-MeanEffect0)-(MeanCost1-MeanCost0))/SQRT(Threshold^2*(SdEffect1^2/ArmSize1+SdEffect0^2/ArmSize0)+SdCost1^2/ArmSize1+SdCost0^2/ArmSize0-2*Threshold*(Corr1*SdEffect1*SdCost1/ArmSize1+Corr0*SdEffect0*SdCost0/ArmSize0)),TRUE)" .

hemo:7005686 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-NBA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000850 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`nb_arms <- function(e1, e0, c1, c0, sdE1, sdC1, r1, n1, sdE0, sdC0, r0, n0, lambda, z = 1.96) { dE <- e1-e0; dC <- c1-c0; vE <- sdE1^2/n1+sdE0^2/n0; vC <- sdC1^2/n1+sdC0^2/n0; cv <- r1*sdE1*sdC1/n1+r0*sdE0*sdC0/n0; inmb <- lambda*dE-dC; se <- sqrt(lambda^2*vE+vC-2*lambda*cv); list(R = dC/dE, INMB = inmb, SE_INMB = se, M = inmb+c(-1,1)*z*se, INHB = inmb/lambda, SE_INHB = se/lambda, H = (inmb+c(-1,1)*z*se)/lambda, z_NB = inmb/se, P_pos = pnorm(inmb/se)) }` Returns a named list with both scales." ;
    hemo:9000024 "nb_arms <- function(e1, e0, c1, c0, sdE1, sdC1, r1, n1, sdE0, sdC0, r0, n0, lambda, z = 1.96) { dE <- e1-e0; dC <- c1-c0; vE <- sdE1^2/n1+sdE0^2/n0; vC <- sdC1^2/n1+sdC0^2/n0; cv <- r1*sdE1*sdC1/n1+r0*sdE0*sdC0/n0; inmb <- lambda*dE-dC; se <- sqrt(lambda^2*vE+vC-2*lambda*cv); list(R = dC/dE, INMB = inmb, SE_INMB = se, M = inmb+c(-1,1)*z*se, INHB = inmb/lambda, SE_INHB = se/lambda, H = (inmb+c(-1,1)*z*se)/lambda, z_NB = inmb/se, P_pos = pnorm(inmb/se)) }" .

hemo:7005687 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-NBA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000850 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def nb_arms(e1, e0, c1, c0, sdE1, sdC1, r1, n1, sdE0, sdC0, r0, n0, lam, z=1.96): dE = e1-e0; dC = c1-c0; vE = sdE1**2/n1+sdE0**2/n0; vC = sdC1**2/n1+sdC0**2/n0; cv = r1*sdE1*sdC1/n1+r0*sdE0*sdC0/n0; inmb = lam*dE-dC; se = math.sqrt(lam**2*vE+vC-2*lam*cv); return {'R': dC/dE, 'INMB': inmb, 'SE_INMB': se, 'M': (inmb-z*se, inmb+z*se), 'INHB': inmb/lam, 'SE_INHB': se/lam, 'H': ((inmb-z*se)/lam, (inmb+z*se)/lam), 'z_NB': inmb/se, 'P_pos': statistics.NormalDist().cdf(inmb/se)}` Requires the math and statistics modules." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def nb_arms(e1, e0, c1, c0, sdE1, sdC1, r1, n1, sdE0, sdC0, r0, n0, lam, z=1.96): dE = e1-e0; dC = c1-c0; vE = sdE1**2/n1+sdE0**2/n0; vC = sdC1**2/n1+sdC0**2/n0; cv = r1*sdE1*sdC1/n1+r0*sdE0*sdC0/n0; inmb = lam*dE-dC; se = math.sqrt(lam**2*vE+vC-2*lam*cv); return {'R': dC/dE, 'INMB': inmb, 'SE_INMB': se, 'M': (inmb-z*se, inmb+z*se), 'INHB': inmb/lam, 'SE_INHB': se/lam, 'H': ((inmb-z*se)/lam, (inmb+z*se)/lam), 'z_NB': inmb/se, 'P_pos': statistics.NormalDist().cdf(inmb/se)}" .

hemo:7005690 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for the cost-effectiveness frontier by removing dominated and extendedly dominated strategies"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Costs and QALYs are expected values per person on one basis. Equal cost with more QALYs, or equal QALYs at lower cost, counts as dominance (HE-AS-DOM-001). Exact duplicates are merged before the call (HE-AS-DOM-004), since the R version would return a missing ratio and the Python version would stop on a division by zero. A strategy whose ratio equals the next one lies on a segment and is kept (HE-AS-EXD-003). The R and Python versions compare ratios with a tolerance of 0.000001, so that equal ratios held in floating point, such as 1.1 minus 1.0 QALYs, are not read as a fall." .

hemo:7005694 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CEF-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000851 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=IF((C3-C2)/(D3-D2)>(C4-C3)/(D4-D3),\"Remove\",\"Keep\")` With the strategies left after the dominance flag of HE-IM-DOM-002 sorted by cost in rows 2 onwards, costs in column C and QALYs in column D, the formula in row 3, filled down to the second-last strategy, flags the strategy in its own row when its ratio is higher than the next one. Excel has no built-in loop for the repeated removal, so the first flagged row is deleted and the check repeated until, with the ratios in column F and the ranges ending at the last ratio, `=SUMPRODUCT(--(F4:F8<F3:F7))=0` (HE-VT-CEF-002) returns TRUE." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF((C3-C2)/(D3-D2)>(C4-C3)/(D4-D3),\"Remove\",\"Keep\")" .

hemo:7005695 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CEF-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000851 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`cef_frontier <- function(name, C, Q) { o <- order(C, -Q); name <- name[o]; C <- C[o]; Q <- Q[o]; dom <- sapply(seq_along(C), function(k) any(C <= C[k] & Q >= Q[k] & (C < C[k] | Q > Q[k]))); name <- name[!dom]; C <- C[!dom]; Q <- Q[!dom]; repeat { r <- diff(C)/diff(Q); k <- which(diff(r) < -1e-6); if (length(k) == 0) break; k <- k[1]+1; name <- name[-k]; C <- C[-k]; Q <- Q[-k] }; data.frame(strategy = name, cost = C, qaly = Q, icer = c(NA, diff(C)/diff(Q))) }` Returns one row per frontier strategy. `f$strategy[max(which(c(0, f$icer[-1]) <= 25000))]` then returns the choice at GBP 25,000 per QALY from the result f." ;
    hemo:9000024 "cef_frontier <- function(name, C, Q) { o <- order(C, -Q); name <- name[o]; C <- C[o]; Q <- Q[o]; dom <- sapply(seq_along(C), function(k) any(C <= C[k] & Q >= Q[k] & (C < C[k] | Q > Q[k]))); name <- name[!dom]; C <- C[!dom]; Q <- Q[!dom]; repeat { r <- diff(C)/diff(Q); k <- which(diff(r) < -1e-6); if (length(k) == 0) break; k <- k[1]+1; name <- name[-k]; C <- C[-k]; Q <- Q[-k] }; data.frame(strategy = name, cost = C, qaly = Q, icer = c(NA, diff(C)/diff(Q))) }" .

hemo:7005696 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CEF-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000851 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def cef_frontier(name, C, Q): pts = sorted(zip(C, [-q for q in Q], name)); pts = [(c, -nq, n) for c, nq, n in pts]; pts = [p for p in pts if not any(o[0] <= p[0] and o[1] >= p[1] and (o[0] < p[0] or o[1] > p[1]) for o in pts)]; return _cef_exd(pts)` with the helper `def _cef_exd(pts): r = [(b[0]-a[0])/(b[1]-a[1]) for a, b in zip(pts, pts[1:])]; k = next((m+1 for m in range(len(r)-1) if r[m] > r[m+1]+1e-6), None); return [(p[2], p[0], p[1], ([None]+r)[i]) for i, p in enumerate(pts)] if k is None else _cef_exd(pts[:k]+pts[k+1:])` Plain Python with no imports, returning tuples of name, cost, QALYs and ICER." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def cef_frontier(name, C, Q): pts = sorted(zip(C, [-q for q in Q], name)); pts = [(c, -nq, n) for c, nq, n in pts]; pts = [p for p in pts if not any(o[0] <= p[0] and o[1] >= p[1] and (o[0] < p[0] or o[1] > p[1]) for o in pts)]; return _cef_exd(pts)" .

hemo:7005699 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for the one-pass net monetary benefit search for the cost-effectiveness frontier"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Where two strategies tie for the highest net monetary benefit at a grid value, the R and Python versions keep the one with more QALYs, so an equal-cost strategy with fewer QALYs is not reported at a value of zero. The Excel version takes the first maximum, so dominated strategies, ties included, are screened out first with HE-IM-DOM-002. A strategy lying exactly on a segment ties with its neighbours at one value only, so whether it is reported depends on rounding in floating point (with QALYs of 1.0, 1.1, 1.2 and 1.3 at costs of 1,000 to 4,000 the second strategy is reported and the third is not), where the elimination function HE-CF-CEF-001 keeps both. Strategies whose ratios exceed `lambda_max` are not reported, so the most effective strategy appears only when the grid reaches past the last ratio on the frontier." .

hemo:7005704 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CEF-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000852 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=LET(g,TOCOL((Costs-TRANSPOSE(Costs))/(QALYs-TRANSPOSE(QALYs)),3),grid,SORT(UNIQUE(VSTACK(0,MaxValue,FILTER(g,(g>0)*(g<=MaxValue),0)))),UNIQUE(MAP(grid,LAMBDA(l,INDEX(StrategyNames,MATCH(MAX(l*QALYs-Costs),l*QALYs-Costs,0))))))` With names, costs and QALYs in column ranges StrategyNames, Costs and QALYs and the largest value in MaxValue, the formula builds the grid, dropping the errors from equal QALYs and returning only 0 and MaxValue when no pairwise ratio is in range, and spills the frontier strategies. It needs Excel for Microsoft 365." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LET(g,TOCOL((Costs-TRANSPOSE(Costs))/(QALYs-TRANSPOSE(QALYs)),3),grid,SORT(UNIQUE(VSTACK(0,MaxValue,FILTER(g,(g>0)*(g<=MaxValue),0)))),UNIQUE(MAP(grid,LAMBDA(l,INDEX(StrategyNames,MATCH(MAX(l*QALYs-Costs),l*QALYs-Costs,0))))))" .

hemo:7005705 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CEF-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000852 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`cef_nmb <- function(name, C, Q, lambda_max) { g <- outer(C, C, \"-\")/outer(Q, Q, \"-\"); grid <- sort(unique(c(0, g[is.finite(g) & g > 0 & g <= lambda_max], lambda_max))); win <- sapply(grid, function(l) { v <- l*Q-C; k <- which(v == max(v)); k[which.max(Q[k])] }); name[unique(win)] }` Returns the frontier strategies in the order in which they win as the value rises, which is cost order." ;
    hemo:9000024 "cef_nmb <- function(name, C, Q, lambda_max) { g <- outer(C, C, \"-\")/outer(Q, Q, \"-\"); grid <- sort(unique(c(0, g[is.finite(g) & g > 0 & g <= lambda_max], lambda_max))); win <- sapply(grid, function(l) { v <- l*Q-C; k <- which(v == max(v)); k[which.max(Q[k])] }); name[unique(win)] }" .

hemo:7005706 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CEF-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000852 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def cef_nmb(name, C, Q, lambda_max): n = len(C); grid = sorted({0, lambda_max} | {(C[j]-C[i])/(Q[j]-Q[i]) for i in range(n) for j in range(n) if Q[j] != Q[i] and 0 < (C[j]-C[i])/(Q[j]-Q[i]) <= lambda_max}); win = [max(range(n), key=lambda k: (l*Q[k]-C[k], Q[k])) for l in grid]; return [name[k] for k in dict.fromkeys(win)]` Plain Python with no imports." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def cef_nmb(name, C, Q, lambda_max): n = len(C); grid = sorted({0, lambda_max} | {(C[j]-C[i])/(Q[j]-Q[i]) for i in range(n) for j in range(n) if Q[j] != Q[i] and 0 < (C[j]-C[i])/(Q[j]-Q[i]) <= lambda_max}); win = [max(range(n), key=lambda k: (l*Q[k]-C[k], Q[k])) for l in grid]; return [name[k] for k in dict.fromkeys(win)]" .

hemo:7005709 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for health forgone by each option against the health-maximising option"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "All options draw on the same budget, and each gain is net of the health displaced in that group. Two options with the same mean gain both forgo zero, and since neither dominates the other on cost and health under HE-FM-DOM-001 the choice between them rests on equity alone." .

hemo:7005712 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-EET-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000853 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=SUMPRODUCT(ShareGroup,INDEX(GainMatrix,0,COLUMN()-MIN(COLUMN(GainMatrix))+1))` With one row per group, shares in a column range named ShareGroup and net gains in a range named GainMatrix with one column per option, the formula entered in each cell of a helper row named MeanGainRow, set in the same columns as GainMatrix, returns that option's mean gain, the COLUMN terms giving the option number. `=MAX(MeanGainRow)-INDEX(MeanGainRow,COLUMN()-MIN(COLUMN(GainMatrix))+1)` entered in each cell of a helper row named ForgoneRow, in the same columns, then returns the health that option forgoes." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(ShareGroup,INDEX(GainMatrix,0,COLUMN()-MIN(COLUMN(GainMatrix))+1))" .

hemo:7005713 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-EET-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000853 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`eq_forgone <- function(s, G) { s <- s/sum(s); H <- colSums(s*G); list(H = H, H_top = max(H), forgone = max(H)-H) }` Takes a vector of shares and a matrix of gains with one row per group and one column per option, and normalises the shares so counts can be entered." ;
    hemo:9000024 "eq_forgone <- function(s, G) { s <- s/sum(s); H <- colSums(s*G); list(H = H, H_top = max(H), forgone = max(H)-H) }" .

hemo:7005714 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-EET-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000853 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def eq_forgone(s, G): t = sum(s); H = [sum(si*row[k] for si, row in zip(s, G))/t for k in range(len(G[0]))]; top = max(H); return H, top, [top-h for h in H]` Takes a list of shares and a list of rows of gains, one row per group and one entry per option." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def eq_forgone(s, G): t = sum(s); H = [sum(si*row[k] for si, row in zip(s, G))/t for k in range(len(G[0]))]; top = max(H); return H, top, [top-h for h in H]" .

hemo:7005717 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Per-cycle exit probabilities by time since entry from a survival curve checked against one constant exit probability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Survival runs on the clock that starts at entry to the state, in one group followed from entry, at whole cycles of the model's cycle length. Censored follow-up is handled before this step, for example by reading the values off a Kaplan-Meier curve. The fitted constant matches survival at the last cycle only." .

hemo:7005721 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-MKA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000854 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With survival at entry, 1, in B1 and survival at the end of cycles 1 to K in B2 downwards (named SurvByCycle), put `=1-B2/B1` in C2 and fill down to give the per-cycle values (named ExitByCycle). The constant is `=1-INDEX(SurvByCycle,ROWS(SurvByCycle))^(1/ROWS(SurvByCycle))` in a cell named ConstExit, the largest gap is `=SUMPRODUCT(MAX(ABS(ExitByCycle-ConstExit)))`, which SUMPRODUCT evaluates as an array in classic Excel without array entry, and the mean stay is `=1/ConstExit`." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1-B2/B1" .

hemo:7005722 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-MKA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000854 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`exit_check <- function(S, tol = 0.001) { K <- length(S); h <- 1-S/c(1, S[-K]); q_c <- 1-S[K]^(1/K); dev <- max(abs(h-q_c)); list(h = h, q_c = q_c, dev = dev, D_c = 1/q_c, memoryless = dev <= tol) }` Base R only; `exit_check(c(0.75, 0.7125, 0.676875, 0.643031, 0.61088))` returns a `dev` of 0.156131 and `memoryless` FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000024 "exit_check <- function(S, tol = 0.001) { K <- length(S); h <- 1-S/c(1, S[-K]); q_c <- 1-S[K]^(1/K); dev <- max(abs(h-q_c)); list(h = h, q_c = q_c, dev = dev, D_c = 1/q_c, memoryless = dev <= tol) }" .

hemo:7005723 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-MKA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000854 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def exit_check(S, tol=0.001): K = len(S); prev = [1.0]+list(S[:-1]); h = [1-s/p for s, p in zip(S, prev)]; q_c = 1-S[-1]**(1/K); dev = max(abs(x-q_c) for x in h); return h, q_c, dev, 1/q_c, dev <= tol` Plain Python with no imports, returning the outputs in the order listed." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def exit_check(S, tol=0.001): K = len(S); prev = [1.0]+list(S[:-1]); h = [1-s/p for s, p in zip(S, prev)]; q_c = 1-S[-1]**(1/K); dev = max(abs(x-q_c) for x in h); return h, q_c, dev, 1/q_c, dev <= tol" .

hemo:7005726 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for the critical inequality aversion at which two options give equal EDE health"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "D changes sign once within the bracket, as in the article's example, where funding gains total health and widens inequality. Bisection returns only one root when there are several, so D is checked on a coarse grid across the bracket before the search, a step the code below leaves to the analyst. Every health value is above zero, and the geometric mean of HE-FM-ATK-003 replaces the power mean at epsilon of 1." .

hemo:7005730 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-DCEA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000855 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With one row per group in ranges named GroupShare, HealthOptionA and HealthOptionB, the shares adding to 1, and the bracket ends in cells named BracketLow and BracketHigh, a helper table holds the lower end in column A, the upper end in column B, the midpoint in column C and the difference in column D. Row 2 has `=BracketLow`, `=BracketHigh`, `=(A2+B2)/2` and `=SUMPRODUCT(GroupShare,HealthOptionA^(1-C2))^(1/(1-C2))-SUMPRODUCT(GroupShare,HealthOptionB^(1-C2))^(1/(1-C2))`, and row 3 has `=IF(SIGN(D2)=SIGN(DiffAtLow),C2,A2)` and `=IF(SIGN(D2)=SIGN(DiffAtLow),B2,C2)` in columns A and B with the formulas of columns C and D copied down from row 2, where DiffAtLow holds the same difference at BracketLow. Row 3 is then copied down to row 41, and after these 40 rows the last midpoint, held in CritEpsilon, is within about 1E-10 of the root for a bracket 30 wide. The table has no geometric-mean branch, so a midpoint of exactly 1 would return #DIV/0!, which never happens with bracket ends 0 and 30. The table uses classic functions only." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=BracketLow" .

hemo:7005731 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-DCEA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000855 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`crit_eps <- function(s, hA, hB, lo = 0, hi = 30, tol = 1e-10) { s <- s/sum(s); ede <- function(h, e) if (abs(1-e) < 1e-9) exp(sum(s*log(h))) else sum(s*h^(1-e))^(1/(1-e)); f <- function(e) ede(hA, e)-ede(hB, e); fl <- f(lo); if (fl*f(hi) > 0) return(NA); while (hi-lo > tol) { m <- (lo+hi)/2; fm <- f(m); if (sign(fm) == sign(fl)) { lo <- m; fl <- fm } else hi <- m }; (lo+hi)/2 }` Takes vectors with one element per group, so `crit_eps(c(0.5, 0.5), c(63.996, 72.016), c(64, 72))` returns about 11.7557." ;
    hemo:9000024 "crit_eps <- function(s, hA, hB, lo = 0, hi = 30, tol = 1e-10) { s <- s/sum(s); ede <- function(h, e) if (abs(1-e) < 1e-9) exp(sum(s*log(h))) else sum(s*h^(1-e))^(1/(1-e)); f <- function(e) ede(hA, e)-ede(hB, e); fl <- f(lo); if (fl*f(hi) > 0) return(NA); while (hi-lo > tol) { m <- (lo+hi)/2; fm <- f(m); if (sign(fm) == sign(fl)) { lo <- m; fl <- fm } else hi <- m }; (lo+hi)/2 }" .

hemo:7005732 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-DCEA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000855 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def crit_eps(s, hA, hB, lo=0.0, hi=30.0, tol=1e-10): t = sum(s); w = [x/t for x in s]; ede = lambda h, e: math.exp(sum(a*math.log(b) for a, b in zip(w, h))) if abs(1-e) < 1e-9 else sum(a*b**(1-e) for a, b in zip(w, h))**(1/(1-e)); f = lambda e: ede(hA, e)-ede(hB, e); return None if f(lo)*f(hi) > 0 else _bisect(f, lo, hi, tol)` with the helper `def _bisect(f, lo, hi, tol): m = (lo+hi)/2; return m if hi-lo <= tol else (_bisect(f, m, hi, tol) if (f(m) > 0) == (f(lo) > 0) else _bisect(f, lo, m, tol))`. Needs `import math` only, takes lists with one element per group and returns None when D has the same sign at both ends of the bracket." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def crit_eps(s, hA, hB, lo=0.0, hi=30.0, tol=1e-10): t = sum(s); w = [x/t for x in s]; ede = lambda h, e: math.exp(sum(a*math.log(b) for a, b in zip(w, h))) if abs(1-e) < 1e-9 else sum(a*b**(1-e) for a, b in zip(w, h))**(1/(1-e)); f = lambda e: ede(hA, e)-ede(hB, e); return None if f(lo)*f(hi) > 0 else _bisect(f, lo, hi, tol)" .

hemo:7005735 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for a level yearly stream under the stepped Green Book schedule and the reduced schedule without pure time preference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "All rates are above zero for the closed form, which the R and Python code do not need. Any amount in year 0 is added undiscounted. Table 3.B gives no reduced health rate, so for a health stream the code is run with the health rates 0.015, 0.01286 and 0.01071 for the standard total only. The R and Python code take any list of band ends and rates, including the six reduced bands of Table 3.B that run past year 125." .

hemo:7005739 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-TPR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000856 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=(1-(1+RateNear)^(-MIN(Horizon,30)))/RateNear+(1+RateNear)^(-30)*(1-(1+RateMid)^(-MAX(MIN(Horizon,75)-30,0)))/RateMid+(1+RateNear)^(-30)*(1+RateMid)^(-45)*(1-(1+RateFar)^(-MAX(Horizon-75,0)))/RateFar` Returns `A_S` from named cells; the same formula on the reduced rates returns `A_Z`. With years 1 to 125 in a column named YearCol and the HE-FM-TPR-003 factors in a column named FactorStd, `=StreamAmount*SUMPRODUCT((YearCol<=Horizon)*FactorStd)` returns `PV_S` without the closed form." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(1-(1+RateNear)^(-MIN(Horizon,30)))/RateNear+(1+RateNear)^(-30)*(1-(1+RateMid)^(-MAX(MIN(Horizon,75)-30,0)))/RateMid+(1+RateNear)^(-30)*(1+RateMid)^(-45)*(1-(1+RateFar)^(-MAX(Horizon-75,0)))/RateFar" .

hemo:7005740 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-TPR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000856 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`stepped_pv <- function(q, n_years, ends, rates) { t <- 1:n_years; r_t <- sapply(t, function(k) rates[which(k <= ends)[1]]); df <- cumprod(1/(1+r_t)); c(A = sum(df), PV = q*sum(df), DF_T = df[n_years]) }` Returns a named vector; the wealth transfer is `stepped_pv(1000, 100, c(30, 75, 125), c(0.03, 0.0257, 0.0214))[\"PV\"]-stepped_pv(1000, 100, c(30, 75, 125), c(0.035, 0.03, 0.025))[\"PV\"]`. Use `Inf` as the last band end for an open-ended band." ;
    hemo:9000024 "stepped_pv <- function(q, n_years, ends, rates) { t <- 1:n_years; r_t <- sapply(t, function(k) rates[which(k <= ends)[1]]); df <- cumprod(1/(1+r_t)); c(A = sum(df), PV = q*sum(df), DF_T = df[n_years]) }" .

hemo:7005741 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-TPR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000856 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def stepped_pv(q, n_years, ends, rates): r_t = [next(r for e, r in zip(ends, rates) if k <= e) for k in range(1, n_years+1)]; f = 1.0; df = [(f := f/(1+x)) for x in r_t]; return sum(df), q*sum(df), df[-1]` Plain Python 3.8 or later with no imports, returning the sum of factors, the present value and the last year's factor; use `float('inf')` as the last band end for an open-ended band." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def stepped_pv(q, n_years, ends, rates): r_t = [next(r for e, r in zip(ends, rates) if k <= e) for k in range(1, n_years+1)]; f = 1.0; df = [(f := f/(1+x)) for x in r_t]; return sum(df), q*sum(df), df[-1]" .

hemo:7005744 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for ranking allocations by utilitarian, isoelastic and maximin health social welfare"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Groups are of equal size and every member of a group has the group's health, as in HE-FM-SWF-001. Tied options are all reported as first, because the three forms give no rule to break a tie." .

hemo:7005748 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-SWF-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000857 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With one option per row, the row's group health values in columns B to D and the parameter in InequalityAversion, `=IF(InequalityAversion=1,SUMPRODUCT(LN(B2:D2)),SUMPRODUCT(B2:D2^(1-InequalityAversion))/(1-InequalityAversion))` returns the isoelastic welfare, `=SUM(B2:D2)` the total and `=MIN(B2:D2)` the maximin value, each filled down. With these columns named IsoelasticByOption, TotalByOption and MaximinByOption and the option names in OptionLabels, `=INDEX(OptionLabels,MATCH(MAX(IsoelasticByOption),IsoelasticByOption,0))` returns the preferred option. MATCH returns only the first of tied options, so `=COUNTIF(IsoelasticByOption,MAX(IsoelasticByOption))` is checked for a value above 1." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(InequalityAversion=1,SUMPRODUCT(LN(B2:D2)),SUMPRODUCT(B2:D2^(1-InequalityAversion))/(1-InequalityAversion))" .

hemo:7005749 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-SWF-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000857 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`swf_rank <- function(H, epsilon) { U <- rowSums(H); M <- apply(H, 1, min); if (epsilon == 1) { W <- rowSums(log(H)); K <- W } else { L <- (1-epsilon)*log(M)+log(rowSums((H/M)^(1-epsilon)))-log(abs(1-epsilon)); W <- sign(1-epsilon)*exp(L); K <- sign(1-epsilon)*L }; best <- function(v) rownames(H)[abs(v-max(v)) <= 1e-12*max(1, abs(max(v)))]; list(U = U, W = W, M = M, best_U = best(U), best_W = best(K), best_M = best(M)) }` H is a matrix with one named row per option and one column per group; K is the log-scale ranking key, which keeps its order when W underflows." ;
    hemo:9000024 "swf_rank <- function(H, epsilon) { U <- rowSums(H); M <- apply(H, 1, min); if (epsilon == 1) { W <- rowSums(log(H)); K <- W } else { L <- (1-epsilon)*log(M)+log(rowSums((H/M)^(1-epsilon)))-log(abs(1-epsilon)); W <- sign(1-epsilon)*exp(L); K <- sign(1-epsilon)*L }; best <- function(v) rownames(H)[abs(v-max(v)) <= 1e-12*max(1, abs(max(v)))]; list(U = U, W = W, M = M, best_U = best(U), best_W = best(K), best_M = best(M)) }" .

hemo:7005750 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-SWF-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000857 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`import math` then `def swf_rank(H, eps): s = math.copysign(1, 1-eps); U = [sum(r) for r in H]; M = [min(r) for r in H]; K = [sum(math.log(x) for x in r) if eps == 1 else s*((1-eps)*math.log(min(r))+math.log(sum((x/min(r))**(1-eps) for x in r))-math.log(abs(1-eps))) for r in H]; W = [k if eps == 1 else s*math.exp(s*k) for k in K]; best = lambda v: [i for i, x in enumerate(v) if abs(x-max(v)) <= 1e-12*max(1, abs(max(v)))]; return {\"U\": U, \"W\": W, \"M\": M, \"best_U\": best(U), \"best_W\": best(K), \"best_M\": best(M)}` Takes a list of options, each a list of group health values, and returns the outputs with the positions of the preferred options." ;
    hemo:9000024 "import math" .

hemo:7005753 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for the ages at which the GBD age weight crosses a chosen level"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The weight function is HE-FM-AGW-001 with beta above zero, so it rises to a single peak and then falls, and each side has at most one crossing. An age where the weight equals the level exactly is counted inside the span." .

hemo:7005756 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-AGW-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000858 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a column of ages 0, 0.01 and onwards to 100 named AgeGrid, `=INDEX(AgeGrid,MATCH(TRUE,INDEX(AgeWtConstC*AgeGrid*EXP(-AgeWtBeta*AgeGrid)>=1,0),0))` returns the lower crossing and `=LOOKUP(2,1/(AgeWtConstC*AgeGrid*EXP(-AgeWtBeta*AgeGrid)>=1),AgeGrid)` the upper crossing, both to the nearest 0.01 year and inside the span. Both work in classic Excel without array entry." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=INDEX(AgeGrid,MATCH(TRUE,INDEX(AgeWtConstC*AgeGrid*EXP(-AgeWtBeta*AgeGrid)>=1,0),0))" .

hemo:7005757 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-AGW-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000858 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`agw_cross <- function(C = 0.1658, beta = 0.04, level = 1) { w <- function(x) C*x*exp(-beta*x)-level; if (C/(beta*exp(1)) < level) return(NULL); c(x_lo = uniroot(w, c(0, 1/beta), tol = 1e-10)$root, x_hi = uniroot(w, c(1/beta, 50/beta), tol = 1e-10)$root) }` Returns the two crossing ages." ;
    hemo:9000024 "agw_cross <- function(C = 0.1658, beta = 0.04, level = 1) { w <- function(x) C*x*exp(-beta*x)-level; if (C/(beta*exp(1)) < level) return(NULL); c(x_lo = uniroot(w, c(0, 1/beta), tol = 1e-10)$root, x_hi = uniroot(w, c(1/beta, 50/beta), tol = 1e-10)$root) }" .

hemo:7005758 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-AGW-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000858 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def agw_cross(C=0.1658, beta=0.04, level=1.0): w = lambda x: C*x*math.exp(-beta*x)-level; return (brentq(w, 0.0, 1/beta, xtol=1e-12), brentq(w, 1/beta, 50/beta, xtol=1e-12)) if C/(beta*math.e) >= level else None` Needs `import math` and `from scipy.optimize import brentq`." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def agw_cross(C=0.1658, beta=0.04, level=1.0): w = lambda x: C*x*math.exp(-beta*x)-level; return (brentq(w, 0.0, 1/beta, xtol=1e-12), brentq(w, 1/beta, 50/beta, xtol=1e-12)) if C/(beta*math.e) >= level else None" .

hemo:7005761 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for age-weighted discounted years lost per death or case with a numerical integral check"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Loss runs at a constant rate over the span from a to a + L and is discounted continuously from a. The calculator line follows the discounted path and needs r above zero; the code switches to HE-FM-AGW-002 at r = 0." .

hemo:7005766 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-AGW-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000859 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The closed form is the HE-FM-AGW-003 cell with OnsetAge in place of AgeAtDeath and Duration in place of YearsLost, times DisabilityWt. For the integral, fill a column named PanelIdx with 0, 1 and onwards to Panels (1,000, an even number) and use `=DisabilityWt*Duration/Panels/3*SUMPRODUCT((2+2*MOD(PanelIdx,2)-(PanelIdx=0)-(PanelIdx=Panels))*(AgeWtModulation*AgeWtConstC*(OnsetAge+PanelIdx*Duration/Panels)*EXP(-AgeWtBeta*(OnsetAge+PanelIdx*Duration/Panels))+1-AgeWtModulation)*EXP(-DiscRate*PanelIdx*Duration/Panels))`, which applies the Simpson weights 1, 4, 2 and so on to 4, 1 and multiplies by the disability weight, so it matches the closed-form cell for a case as well as for a death (DisabilityWt = 1)." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=DisabilityWt*Duration/Panels/3*SUMPRODUCT((2+2*MOD(PanelIdx,2)-(PanelIdx=0)-(PanelIdx=Panels))*(AgeWtModulation*AgeWtConstC*(OnsetAge+PanelIdx*Duration/Panels)*EXP(-AgeWtBeta*(OnsetAge+PanelIdx*Duration/Panels))+1-AgeWtModulation)*EXP(-DiscRate*PanelIdx*Duration/Panels))" .

hemo:7005767 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-AGW-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000859 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`agw_yll <- function(a, L, r, K = 1, C = 0.1658, beta = 0.04, DW = 1) { f <- function(x) (K*C*x*exp(-beta*x)+1-K)*exp(-r*(x-a)); num <- integrate(f, a, a+L, rel.tol = 1e-10)$value; s <- r+beta; g <- if (r > 0) K*C*exp(r*a)/s^2*(exp(-s*(L+a))*(-s*(L+a)-1)-exp(-s*a)*(-s*a-1))+(1-K)/r*(1-exp(-r*L)) else K*C/beta^2*((1+beta*a)*exp(-beta*a)-(1+beta*(a+L))*exp(-beta*(a+L)))+(1-K)*L; g0 <- if (r > 0) (1-exp(-r*L))/r else L; c(closed = DW*g, numerical = DW*num, diff = DW*abs(g-num), effect = g/g0) }` Returns a named vector." ;
    hemo:9000024 "agw_yll <- function(a, L, r, K = 1, C = 0.1658, beta = 0.04, DW = 1) { f <- function(x) (K*C*x*exp(-beta*x)+1-K)*exp(-r*(x-a)); num <- integrate(f, a, a+L, rel.tol = 1e-10)$value; s <- r+beta; g <- if (r > 0) K*C*exp(r*a)/s^2*(exp(-s*(L+a))*(-s*(L+a)-1)-exp(-s*a)*(-s*a-1))+(1-K)/r*(1-exp(-r*L)) else K*C/beta^2*((1+beta*a)*exp(-beta*a)-(1+beta*(a+L))*exp(-beta*(a+L)))+(1-K)*L; g0 <- if (r > 0) (1-exp(-r*L))/r else L; c(closed = DW*g, numerical = DW*num, diff = DW*abs(g-num), effect = g/g0) }" .

hemo:7005768 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-AGW-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000859 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def agw_yll(a, L, r, K=1.0, C=0.1658, beta=0.04, DW=1.0, n=1000): f = lambda x: (K*C*x*math.exp(-beta*x)+1-K)*math.exp(-r*(x-a)); h = L/n; num = h/3*sum((1 if i in (0, n) else 4 if i % 2 else 2)*f(a+i*h) for i in range(n+1)); s = r+beta; g = K*C*math.exp(r*a)/s**2*(math.exp(-s*(L+a))*(-s*(L+a)-1)-math.exp(-s*a)*(-s*a-1))+(1-K)/r*(1-math.exp(-r*L)) if r > 0 else K*C/beta**2*((1+beta*a)*math.exp(-beta*a)-(1+beta*(a+L))*math.exp(-beta*(a+L)))+(1-K)*L; g0 = (1-math.exp(-r*L))/r if r > 0 else L; return DW*g, DW*num, DW*abs(g-num), g/g0` Needs `import math`; n must be even." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def agw_yll(a, L, r, K=1.0, C=0.1658, beta=0.04, DW=1.0, n=1000): f = lambda x: (K*C*x*math.exp(-beta*x)+1-K)*math.exp(-r*(x-a)); h = L/n; num = h/3*sum((1 if i in (0, n) else 4 if i % 2 else 2)*f(a+i*h) for i in range(n+1)); s = r+beta; g = K*C*math.exp(r*a)/s**2*(math.exp(-s*(L+a))*(-s*(L+a)-1)-math.exp(-s*a)*(-s*a-1))+(1-K)/r*(1-math.exp(-r*L)) if r > 0 else K*C/beta**2*((1+beta*a)*math.exp(-beta*a)-(1+beta*(a+L))*math.exp(-beta*(a+L)))+(1-K)*L; g0 = (1-math.exp(-r*L))/r if r > 0 else L; return DW*g, DW*num, DW*abs(g-num), g/g0" .

hemo:7005771 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for the stop or continue decision at one interim look of a two-arm trial"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The prior and the pair observations are normal with known variance, outcomes are observed at once, there is no discounting and no switching cost. Fixed costs are treated as committed, as Forster and colleagues assume for ProFHER once the trial was commissioned, so only the remaining pairs and any further analysis are costed. The participant in each remaining pair on the arm expected to be inferior forgoes abs(mu_t), the participants' loss of HE-FM-ENBS-002 valued on the interim evidence; that valuation at an interim look is this package's application, since Fenwick and colleagues and Flight and colleagues value the loss on the information held before the study, and Flight and colleagues set it equal to the signed prior incremental net benefit, which equals its absolute value when that benefit is positive. Flight and colleagues note that the loss holds only if the new intervention cannot be used in practice before the trial ends and may be zero if it is potentially cost-effective and can be used while research is ongoing. It replaces the online learning term of HE-FM-AVOI-001 and is not added to it. A net gain of exactly zero gives go of 0, the strict inequality of the KG* rule. The check is one-stage, so a decision to continue also holds for the optimal sequential policy, while a decision to stop may come too early (HE-FM-AVOI-001)." .

hemo:7005775 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-AVOI-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000860 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=PriorESS+FirstStagePairs` in a cell named InterimESS, `=(PriorESS*DesignPriorMean+FirstStagePairs*FirstStageMean)/InterimESS` in InterimMeanINB, `=SamplingSDPair*SQRT(RemainingPairs/(InterimESS*(InterimESS+RemainingPairs)))` in PrepostSDRemaining, `=PrepostSDRemaining*NORM.S.DIST(ABS(InterimMeanINB)/PrepostSDRemaining,FALSE)-ABS(InterimMeanINB)*(1-NORM.S.DIST(ABS(InterimMeanINB)/PrepostSDRemaining,TRUE))` in EVSIRemaining and `=PopBenefiting*EVSIRemaining-RemainingPairs*CostPerPair-FinalAnalysisCost-RemainingPairs*ABS(InterimMeanINB)` in ContinueNetGain. For the scan, the sizes 1, 2 and onwards go in A2 downwards (named PairsGrid), B2 holds `=PopBenefiting*(SamplingSDPair*SQRT(A2/(InterimESS*(InterimESS+A2)))*NORM.S.DIST(ABS(InterimMeanINB)/(SamplingSDPair*SQRT(A2/(InterimESS*(InterimESS+A2)))),FALSE)-ABS(InterimMeanINB)*(1-NORM.S.DIST(ABS(InterimMeanINB)/(SamplingSDPair*SQRT(A2/(InterimESS*(InterimESS+A2)))),TRUE)))-A2*(CostPerPair+ABS(InterimMeanINB))-FinalAnalysisCost` filled down (named GainGrid), `=INDEX(PairsGrid,MATCH(MAX(GainGrid),GainGrid,0))` returns the best size and `=IF(MAX(GainGrid)>0,\"Continue\",\"Stop\")` the decision. Classic Excel functions only." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=PriorESS+FirstStagePairs" .

hemo:7005776 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-AVOI-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000860 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`interim_voi <- function(mu0, n0, xbar1, m1, M, sigmaX, P, cpair, Ca) { nt <- n0+m1; mut <- (n0*mu0+m1*xbar1)/nt; m <- 1:M; s <- sigmaX*sqrt(m/(nt*(nt+m))); z <- abs(mut)/s; evsi <- s*(dnorm(z)-z*(1-pnorm(z))); ng <- P*evsi-m*cpair-Ca-m*abs(mut); k <- which.max(ng); list(mu_t = mut, NG_all = ng[M], best_m = m[k], NG_best = ng[k], decision = if (ng[k] > 0) \"continue\" else \"stop\") }` Base R only; returns the interim mean, the net gain of all remaining pairs, the best one-stage size, its net gain and the decision." ;
    hemo:9000024 "interim_voi <- function(mu0, n0, xbar1, m1, M, sigmaX, P, cpair, Ca) { nt <- n0+m1; mut <- (n0*mu0+m1*xbar1)/nt; m <- 1:M; s <- sigmaX*sqrt(m/(nt*(nt+m))); z <- abs(mut)/s; evsi <- s*(dnorm(z)-z*(1-pnorm(z))); ng <- P*evsi-m*cpair-Ca-m*abs(mut); k <- which.max(ng); list(mu_t = mut, NG_all = ng[M], best_m = m[k], NG_best = ng[k], decision = if (ng[k] > 0) \"continue\" else \"stop\") }" .

hemo:7005777 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-AVOI-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000860 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def interim_voi(mu0, n0, xbar1, m1, M, sigmaX, P, cpair, Ca): nd = statistics.NormalDist(); nt = n0+m1; mut = (n0*mu0+m1*xbar1)/nt; ng = [(P*s*(nd.pdf(abs(mut)/s)-abs(mut)/s*(1-nd.cdf(abs(mut)/s)))-m*cpair-Ca-m*abs(mut), m) for m in range(1, M+1) for s in [sigmaX*math.sqrt(m/(nt*(nt+m)))]]; best = max(ng); return mut, ng[-1][0], best[1], best[0], 'continue' if best[0] > 0 else 'stop'` Needs the standard library modules math and statistics only, and returns the same five results as a tuple." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def interim_voi(mu0, n0, xbar1, m1, M, sigmaX, P, cpair, Ca): nd = statistics.NormalDist(); nt = n0+m1; mut = (n0*mu0+m1*xbar1)/nt; ng = [(P*s*(nd.pdf(abs(mut)/s)-abs(mut)/s*(1-nd.cdf(abs(mut)/s)))-m*cpair-Ca-m*abs(mut), m) for m in range(1, M+1) for s in [sigmaX*math.sqrt(m/(nt*(nt+m)))]]; best = max(ng); return mut, ng[-1][0], best[1], best[0], 'continue' if best[0] > 0 else 'stop'" .

hemo:7005780 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Seeded one-step agent simulation checked against the analytic infection probability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "All agents are susceptible at the start of the step, contacts transmit independently with the same probability, and each agent's stream is used only by that agent, as in the per-agent seed of Chhatwal and He. Separate runs take base seeds at least `N_agents` apart, because with seed + i runs with base seeds 2026 and 2027 reuse 9,999 of the same 10,000 agent streams and are not independent (HE-AS-ABM-007)." .

hemo:7005783 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ABM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000861 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "RAND cannot be seeded, so the draws are generated once and pasted as values. With one row per agent and three columns of draws in `B2:D10001`, `=IF(MIN(B2:D2)<ContactTransProb,1,0)` filled down gives a column named InfectedThree and `=IF(MIN(B2:C2)<ContactTransProb,1,0)` a column named InfectedTwo that shares the first two draws. `=AVERAGE(InfectedThree)` and `=AVERAGE(InfectedTwo)` return the simulated shares and `=AVERAGE(InfectedThree)-AVERAGE(InfectedTwo)` the share averted." ;
    hemo:9000024 "B2:D10001" .

hemo:7005784 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ABM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000861 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`abm_step <- function(N, p, k_3, k_2, seed) { x <- sapply(1:N, function(i) { set.seed(seed+i); u <- runif(k_3); c(any(u < p), any(u[1:k_2] < p)) }); P_3 <- 1-(1-p)^k_3; MCSE_3 <- sqrt(P_3*(1-P_3)/N); c(n_3 = sum(x[1, ]), n_2 = sum(x[2, ]), P_3 = P_3, MCSE_3 = MCSE_3, ok = as.numeric(abs(mean(x[1, ])-P_3) <= 3*MCSE_3), crn = sum(x[2, ] > x[1, ])) }` Run as `abm_step(10000, 0.10, 3, 2, 2026)`." ;
    hemo:9000024 "abm_step <- function(N, p, k_3, k_2, seed) { x <- sapply(1:N, function(i) { set.seed(seed+i); u <- runif(k_3); c(any(u < p), any(u[1:k_2] < p)) }); P_3 <- 1-(1-p)^k_3; MCSE_3 <- sqrt(P_3*(1-P_3)/N); c(n_3 = sum(x[1, ]), n_2 = sum(x[2, ]), P_3 = P_3, MCSE_3 = MCSE_3, ok = as.numeric(abs(mean(x[1, ])-P_3) <= 3*MCSE_3), crn = sum(x[2, ] > x[1, ])) }" .

hemo:7005785 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ABM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000861 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def abm_step(N, p, k_3, k_2, seed): import random, math; U = [[g.random() for _ in range(k_3)] for g in (random.Random(seed+i) for i in range(1, N+1))]; x_3 = [any(u < p for u in r) for r in U]; x_2 = [any(u < p for u in r[:k_2]) for r in U]; P_3 = 1-(1-p)**k_3; MCSE_3 = math.sqrt(P_3*(1-P_3)/N); return sum(x_3), sum(x_2), P_3, MCSE_3, int(abs(sum(x_3)/N-P_3) <= 3*MCSE_3), sum(b and not a for a, b in zip(x_3, x_2))` Run as `abm_step(10000, 0.10, 3, 2, 2026)`." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def abm_step(N, p, k_3, k_2, seed): import random, math; U = [[g.random() for _ in range(k_3)] for g in (random.Random(seed+i) for i in range(1, N+1))]; x_3 = [any(u < p for u in r) for r in U]; x_2 = [any(u < p for u in r[:k_2]) for r in U]; P_3 = 1-(1-p)**k_3; MCSE_3 = math.sqrt(P_3*(1-P_3)/N); return sum(x_3), sum(x_2), P_3, MCSE_3, int(abs(sum(x_3)/N-P_3) <= 3*MCSE_3), sum(b and not a for a, b in zip(x_3, x_2))" .

hemo:7005788 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for cycle-by-cycle background death probabilities from an age-banded life table"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The hazard is constant within each band, so the rescaling is exact. The band is read at the attained age at the start of each cycle; Sonnenberg and Beck describe small corrections to the lookup age, half a cycle for transitions in mid-cycle and half a year because a rate published for age x applies to people aged x to x + 1, which a model may add. The calculator line assumes the cohort starts in the first band and crosses into the second after a whole number of cycles; the R and Python code handle any number of bands. The values below are illustrative, not ONS figures." .

hemo:7005792 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BGM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000862 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the cycle number in column A from row 2, `=StartAge+(A2-1)*CycleYears` in column B gives the attained age, `=INDEX(BandQ,MATCH(B2,BandLower,1))` in column C the band's annual probability, `=1-(1-C2)^CycleYears` in column D the cycle probability, and `=E1*(1-D2)` in column E, with 1 in E1, the background survival. BandLower holds the lower ages in ascending order, as MATCH with match type 1 requires, and BandQ the matching probabilities." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=StartAge+(A2-1)*CycleYears" .

hemo:7005793 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BGM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000862 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`bg_probs <- function(a0, delta, n_cyc, band_lower, q_band) { age <- a0+(seq_len(n_cyc)-1)*delta; q <- q_band[findInterval(age, band_lower)]; p <- 1-(1-q)^delta; list(age = age, p_bg = p, S_bg = cumprod(1-p)) }` Returns the attained ages, the cycle probabilities and the running background survival; `bg_probs(63, 0.5, 6, c(60, 65, 70), c(0.010, 0.016, 0.026))` ends at 0.964418." ;
    hemo:9000024 "bg_probs <- function(a0, delta, n_cyc, band_lower, q_band) { age <- a0+(seq_len(n_cyc)-1)*delta; q <- q_band[findInterval(age, band_lower)]; p <- 1-(1-q)^delta; list(age = age, p_bg = p, S_bg = cumprod(1-p)) }" .

hemo:7005794 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BGM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000862 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def bg_probs(a0, delta, n_cyc, band_lower, q_band): ages = [a0+k*delta for k in range(n_cyc)]; q = [q_band[max(i for i, b in enumerate(band_lower) if b <= a)] for a in ages]; p = [1-(1-x)**delta for x in q]; s = list(accumulate((1-x for x in p), lambda u, v: u*v)); return ages, p, s` Needs `from itertools import accumulate`." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def bg_probs(a0, delta, n_cyc, band_lower, q_band): ages = [a0+k*delta for k in range(n_cyc)]; q = [q_band[max(i for i, b in enumerate(band_lower) if b <= a)] for a in ages]; p = [1-(1-x)**delta for x in q]; s = list(accumulate((1-x for x in p), lambda u, v: u*v)); return ages, p, s" .

hemo:7005797 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for R_0 as the spectral radius of a next-generation matrix of any size"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "K is computed at the disease-free state and is non-negative, so its spectral radius is a real eigenvalue with a non-negative eigenvector. Power iteration on K settles when that eigenvalue is strictly larger in modulus than all others. A host and vector matrix with a zero diagonal has eigenvalues R_0 and minus R_0, so the one-generation ratio swings, and the two-generation ratio, or iteration on K plus the identity matrix as in the Excel version, is used instead. Three generations in the calculator line give an approximation, while the R and Python routines are exact to rounding." .

hemo:7005802 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BRN-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000863 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Put K in a range named `NextGenMatrix` and a column of n ones as the starting vector. Select the n cells of the next column and enter `=(MMULT(NextGenMatrix,B2:B4)+B2:B4)/SUM(MMULT(NextGenMatrix,B2:B4)+B2:B4)` as an array formula with Ctrl+Shift+Enter, shown for three groups with the start vector in `B2:B4`, then copy it across 100 columns and name the last column `LastVector`. `=SUMPRODUCT(MMULT(NextGenMatrix,LastVector))/SUM(LastVector)` returns R_0, and LastVector itself holds the case shares, since every column is scaled to sum to 1. Adding the vector to its image is iteration on K plus the identity, which settles even for a host and vector matrix, and only classic Excel functions are used." ;
    hemo:9000024 "NextGenMatrix" .

hemo:7005803 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BRN-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000863 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`ngm_r0 <- function(K = NULL, Fm = NULL, Vm = NULL) { if (is.null(K)) K <- Fm %*% solve(Vm); ev <- eigen(K); i <- which.max(Mod(ev$values)); v <- Mod(ev$vectors[, i]); list(R0 = Mod(ev$values[i]), share = v/sum(v)) }` Takes a matrix K, or Fm and Vm, and returns a list." ;
    hemo:9000024 "ngm_r0 <- function(K = NULL, Fm = NULL, Vm = NULL) { if (is.null(K)) K <- Fm %*% solve(Vm); ev <- eigen(K); i <- which.max(Mod(ev$values)); v <- Mod(ev$vectors[, i]); list(R0 = Mod(ev$values[i]), share = v/sum(v)) }" .

hemo:7005804 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BRN-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000863 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def ngm_r0(K=None, Fm=None, Vm=None): K = np.array(K, float) if Fm is None else np.array(Fm, float) @ np.linalg.inv(np.array(Vm, float)); w, vec = np.linalg.eig(K); i = int(np.argmax(np.abs(w))); v = np.abs(vec[:, i].real); return float(np.abs(w[i])), v/v.sum()` Uses numpy imported as np and returns R_0 and the shares." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def ngm_r0(K=None, Fm=None, Vm=None): K = np.array(K, float) if Fm is None else np.array(Fm, float) @ np.linalg.inv(np.array(Vm, float)); w, vec = np.linalg.eig(K); i = int(np.argmax(np.abs(w))); v = np.abs(vec[:, i].real); return float(np.abs(w[i])), v/v.sum()" .

hemo:7005807 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for a foreign cost moved to a target price year and currency in either order"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each index is used in one base for both years. For studies in low- and middle-income countries Turner and colleagues recommend splitting a cost into tradable and nontradable resources where possible (their method 3, not computed here), and otherwise method 1 when tradable resources dominate and method 2 when nontradable resources dominate." .

hemo:7005811 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BOC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000864 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=CostOriginal*RateCostingYear*TargetIndexBase/TargetIndexOriginal` returns method 1 into CostMethodOne and `=CostOriginal*SourceIndexBase/SourceIndexOriginal*RateBaseYear` returns method 2 into CostMethodTwo. For a conversion at purchasing power parity RateBaseYear holds `=PPPTarget/PPPSource` for the base year." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CostOriginal*RateCostingYear*TargetIndexBase/TargetIndexOriginal" .

hemo:7005812 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BOC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000864 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`cost_two_orders <- function(C_a, I_a, I_b, e_a, e_b, J_a, J_b) { m1 <- C_a*e_a*J_b/J_a; m2 <- C_a*I_b/I_a*e_b; c(C_m1 = m1, C_m2 = m2, gap = m2/m1) }` The call `cost_two_orders(1e6, 1, 2.360037, 1/15994, 1/21935, 1, 1.1684)` returns 73.05, 107.59 and 1.4728 when rounded." ;
    hemo:9000024 "cost_two_orders <- function(C_a, I_a, I_b, e_a, e_b, J_a, J_b) { m1 <- C_a*e_a*J_b/J_a; m2 <- C_a*I_b/I_a*e_b; c(C_m1 = m1, C_m2 = m2, gap = m2/m1) }" .

hemo:7005813 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BOC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000864 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def cost_two_orders(C_a, I_a, I_b, e_a, e_b, J_a, J_b): m1 = C_a*e_a*J_b/J_a; m2 = C_a*I_b/I_a*e_b; return m1, m2, m2/m1` Plain Python with no imports." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def cost_two_orders(C_a, I_a, I_b, e_a, e_b, J_a, J_b): m1 = C_a*e_a*J_b/J_a; m2 = C_a*I_b/I_a*e_b; return m1, m2, m2/m1" .

hemo:7005816 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for a basket reference price and ceiling with countries that have no price"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "At least one country has a price, so `n` is 1 or more. A country without a price is a missing observation, not a price of zero, and the rule for missing prices is stated with the basket, as the article recommends. The prices are the article's illustrative prices, already in the home currency, so every `e_i` is 1." .

hemo:7005820 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-BOC-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000865 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the flags in ObservedFlags and the converted prices in ConvertedPrices, `=SUMPRODUCT(ObservedFlags,ConvertedPrices)/SUM(ObservedFlags)` returns the mean into BasketMeanObserved and `=CeilingMultiplier*BasketMeanObserved` the ceiling into BasketCeilingObserved. For the median a helper column holds `=IF(D2=1,C2,\"\")` for each country, with the flag in column D and the converted price in column C, and `=MEDIAN(HelperPrices)` over that column returns BasketMedianObserved, since MEDIAN skips text." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(ObservedFlags,ConvertedPrices)/SUM(ObservedFlags)" .

hemo:7005821 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-BOC-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000865 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`basket_ref <- function(p, e, d, k, proposed) { c_obs <- (p*e)[d == 1]; m <- mean(c_obs); list(n = length(c_obs), mean = m, median = median(c_obs), lowest = min(c_obs), highest = max(c_obs), ceiling = k*m, margin = proposed-k*m) }` Returns a named list." ;
    hemo:9000024 "basket_ref <- function(p, e, d, k, proposed) { c_obs <- (p*e)[d == 1]; m <- mean(c_obs); list(n = length(c_obs), mean = m, median = median(c_obs), lowest = min(c_obs), highest = max(c_obs), ceiling = k*m, margin = proposed-k*m) }" .

hemo:7005822 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-BOC-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000865 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def basket_ref(p, e, d, k, proposed): c = sorted(pi*ei for pi, ei, di in zip(p, e, d) if di == 1); n = len(c); m = sum(c)/n; med = (c[(n-1)//2]+c[n//2])/2; return n, m, med, c[0], c[-1], k*m, proposed-k*m` Plain Python with no imports, returning a tuple in the order of the outputs." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def basket_ref(p, e, d, k, proposed): c = sorted(pi*ei for pi, ei, di in zip(p, e, d) if di == 1); n = len(c); m = sum(c)/n; med = (c[(n-1)//2]+c[n//2])/2; return n, m, med, c[0], c[-1], k*m, proposed-k*m" .

hemo:7005825 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Two risk strata run as separate cohorts against one averaged cohort, with life expectancy, QALY gains and ICERs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each run is a two-state Alive and Dead cohort with annual cycles and a constant death probability, counted at the start of each cycle less half a cycle, with nothing discounted; the treatment multiplies the death probability by 1 minus e in every cycle and costs c once. The averaged cohort is run at q_bar and at (1 minus e) times q_bar, which equals the share-weighted mean of the treated probabilities. With 2,000 cycles the untraced tail is below 1E-33 of a year at the article's probabilities." .

hemo:7005829 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-COHM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000866 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Put the cycle numbers 0 to 1999 in a helper column named CycleIndex. With the probabilities in DeathProbLow and DeathProbHigh, the share in ShareLow, the cut in RiskCut, the utility in UtilityAlive and the cost in TreatCost, the trace life expectancy of the low-risk stratum without treatment is `=SUMPRODUCT((1-DeathProbLow)^CycleIndex)-0.5` in a cell named LifeExpLowStd and with treatment `=SUMPRODUCT((1-(1-RiskCut)*DeathProbLow)^CycleIndex)-0.5` in LifeExpLowTreat, with LifeExpHighStd and LifeExpHighTreat built the same way. The averaged cohort uses `=SUMPRODUCT((1-MeanDeathProb)^CycleIndex)-0.5` in LifeExpAvgStd and `=SUMPRODUCT((1-(1-RiskCut)*MeanDeathProb)^CycleIndex)-0.5` in LifeExpAvgTreat, with MeanDeathProb `=ShareLow*DeathProbLow+(1-ShareLow)*DeathProbHigh`. The stratified QALY gain is `=UtilityAlive*(ShareLow*(LifeExpLowTreat-LifeExpLowStd)+(1-ShareLow)*(LifeExpHighTreat-LifeExpHighStd))` in QALYGainStrat and its ICER `=TreatCost/QALYGainStrat` in ICERStrat; `=UtilityAlive*(LifeExpAvgTreat-LifeExpAvgStd)` in QALYGainAvg and `=TreatCost/QALYGainAvg` in ICERAvg give the averaged-cohort results, and `=ICERAvg/ICERStrat-1` the excess. SUMPRODUCT evaluates the power of the range as an array in classic Excel without array entry." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT((1-DeathProbLow)^CycleIndex)-0.5" .

hemo:7005830 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-COHM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000866 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`cohort_le <- function(q, n_cyc = 2000) { m <- (1-q)^(0:(n_cyc-1)); sum(m)-0.5 }; strat_vs_avg <- function(w, q, e, u, cost, n_cyc = 2000) { le0 <- sapply(q, cohort_le, n_cyc = n_cyc); le1 <- sapply((1-e)*q, cohort_le, n_cyc = n_cyc); q_bar <- sum(w*q); dQ_strat <- u*sum(w*(le1-le0)); dQ_avg <- u*(cohort_le((1-e)*q_bar, n_cyc)-cohort_le(q_bar, n_cyc)); c(LE_strat_0 = sum(w*le0), L_avg_0 = cohort_le(q_bar, n_cyc), dQ_strat = dQ_strat, dQ_avg = dQ_avg, ICER_strat = cost/dQ_strat, ICER_avg = cost/dQ_avg) }` Base R only; `strat_vs_avg(c(0.5, 0.5), c(0.05, 0.25), 0.2, 0.8, 20000)` returns 11.5, 6.1667, 2.4, 1.3333, 8333.33 and 15000, and the vector arguments take any number of strata." ;
    hemo:9000024 "cohort_le <- function(q, n_cyc = 2000) { m <- (1-q)^(0:(n_cyc-1)); sum(m)-0.5 }; strat_vs_avg <- function(w, q, e, u, cost, n_cyc = 2000) { le0 <- sapply(q, cohort_le, n_cyc = n_cyc); le1 <- sapply((1-e)*q, cohort_le, n_cyc = n_cyc); q_bar <- sum(w*q); dQ_strat <- u*sum(w*(le1-le0)); dQ_avg <- u*(cohort_le((1-e)*q_bar, n_cyc)-cohort_le(q_bar, n_cyc)); c(LE_strat_0 = sum(w*le0), L_avg_0 = cohort_le(q_bar, n_cyc), dQ_strat = dQ_strat, dQ_avg = dQ_avg, ICER_strat = cost/dQ_strat, ICER_avg = cost/dQ_avg) }" .

hemo:7005831 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-COHM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000866 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def cohort_le(q, n_cyc=2000): return sum((1-q)**t for t in range(n_cyc))-0.5` and `def strat_vs_avg(w, q, e, u, cost, n_cyc=2000): le0 = [cohort_le(x, n_cyc) for x in q]; le1 = [cohort_le((1-e)*x, n_cyc) for x in q]; q_bar = sum(a*b for a, b in zip(w, q)); dQ_strat = u*sum(a*(y1-y0) for a, y0, y1 in zip(w, le0, le1)); dQ_avg = u*(cohort_le((1-e)*q_bar, n_cyc)-cohort_le(q_bar, n_cyc)); return sum(a*y for a, y in zip(w, le0)), cohort_le(q_bar, n_cyc), dQ_strat, dQ_avg, cost/dQ_strat, cost/dQ_avg` Plain Python with no imports; `strat_vs_avg([0.5, 0.5], [0.05, 0.25], 0.2, 0.8, 20000)` returns the same six values." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def cohort_le(q, n_cyc=2000): return sum((1-q)**t for t in range(n_cyc))-0.5" .

hemo:7005835 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for an Euler run of a closed SIR model with and without vaccination counting infections averted statically and dynamically"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Closed, homogeneously mixing population with frequency-dependent transmission and lasting immunity, flows held constant within each step, and a perfect vaccine given before the first step. The static programme run keeps its own susceptible count but takes its force of infection from the no-programme run, so on the first step it agrees with the dynamic run. That force rises and falls with the no-programme epidemic but does not respond to the programme, the article's static count, whereas the WHO guide describes a static force of infection as constant. Costs attach to the infection flow only; costs per day spent in a compartment would be added in the same way, weighted by compartment counts as state rewards are in HE-FM-CSIM-001." .

hemo:7005838 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CMPM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000867 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Name the inputs `TransRate`, `RecoveryRate`, `PopTotal`, `StepLength`, `InitSus`, `InitInf`, `VaccCount`, `CostPerInf` and `DiscRateCont`. Row 2 holds step 0 and each later row one step. Column A holds the step number. No-programme run in B (S), C (I) and E (new infections), programme run in G (S), H (I) and I (new infections), static programme run in K (S) and L (new infections), and the discount factor in M. Row 2 starts `B2 =InitSus`, `C2 =InitInf`, `G2 =InitSus-VaccCount`, `H2 =InitInf`, `K2 =InitSus-VaccCount`. Each row computes `E2 =TransRate*B2*C2/PopTotal*StepLength`, `I2 =TransRate*G2*H2/PopTotal*StepLength`, `L2 =TransRate*C2/PopTotal*K2*StepLength`, `M2 =EXP(-DiscRateCont*A2*StepLength)`, and the next row `B3 =B2-E2`, `C3 =C2+E2-RecoveryRate*C2*StepLength`, `G3 =G2-I2`, `H3 =H2+I2-RecoveryRate*H2*StepLength`, `K3 =K2-L2`. With T steps in rows 2 to T+1, a cell named `AvertStaticTotal` holds `=SUM(E2:E201)-SUM(L2:L201)` and a cell named `AvertDynamicTotal` holds `=SUM(E2:E201)-SUM(I2:I201)` for 200 steps, and the discounted dynamic cost is `=CostPerInf*SUMPRODUCT(M2:M201,E2:E201-I2:I201)`. Only classic Excel functions are used." ;
    hemo:9000024 "TransRate" .

hemo:7005839 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CMPM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000867 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`sir_avert <- function(beta, gamma, N, S0, I0, V, steps, dt = 1, cinf = 0, rc = 0) { Sb <- S0; Ib <- I0; Sp <- S0-V; Ip <- I0; Ss <- S0-V; As <- 0; Ad <- 0; Cs <- 0; Cd <- 0; for (k in seq_len(steps)) { lb <- beta*Ib/N; Fb <- lb*Sb*dt; Fp <- beta*Ip/N*Sp*dt; Fs <- lb*Ss*dt; df <- exp(-rc*(k-1)*dt); As <- As+Fb-Fs; Ad <- Ad+Fb-Fp; Cs <- Cs+cinf*df*(Fb-Fs); Cd <- Cd+cinf*df*(Fb-Fp); Ib <- Ib+Fb-gamma*Ib*dt; Sb <- Sb-Fb; Ip <- Ip+Fp-gamma*Ip*dt; Sp <- Sp-Fp; Ss <- Ss-Fs }; c(A_s = As, A_d = Ad, A_i = Ad-As, C_s = Cs, C_d = Cd) }` Returns a named vector." ;
    hemo:9000024 "sir_avert <- function(beta, gamma, N, S0, I0, V, steps, dt = 1, cinf = 0, rc = 0) { Sb <- S0; Ib <- I0; Sp <- S0-V; Ip <- I0; Ss <- S0-V; As <- 0; Ad <- 0; Cs <- 0; Cd <- 0; for (k in seq_len(steps)) { lb <- beta*Ib/N; Fb <- lb*Sb*dt; Fp <- beta*Ip/N*Sp*dt; Fs <- lb*Ss*dt; df <- exp(-rc*(k-1)*dt); As <- As+Fb-Fs; Ad <- Ad+Fb-Fp; Cs <- Cs+cinf*df*(Fb-Fs); Cd <- Cd+cinf*df*(Fb-Fp); Ib <- Ib+Fb-gamma*Ib*dt; Sb <- Sb-Fb; Ip <- Ip+Fp-gamma*Ip*dt; Sp <- Sp-Fp; Ss <- Ss-Fs }; c(A_s = As, A_d = Ad, A_i = Ad-As, C_s = Cs, C_d = Cd) }" .

hemo:7005840 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CMPM-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000867 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def sir_avert(beta, gamma, N, S0, I0, V, steps, dt=1, cinf=0, rc=0): Sb, Ib, Sp, Ip, Ss = S0, I0, S0-V, I0, S0-V; As = Ad = Cs = Cd = 0.0` followed by the loop `for k in range(steps): lb = beta*Ib/N; Fb = lb*Sb*dt; Fp = beta*Ip/N*Sp*dt; Fs = lb*Ss*dt; df = math.exp(-rc*k*dt); As += Fb-Fs; Ad += Fb-Fp; Cs += cinf*df*(Fb-Fs); Cd += cinf*df*(Fb-Fp); Ib, Sb = Ib+Fb-gamma*Ib*dt, Sb-Fb; Ip, Sp = Ip+Fp-gamma*Ip*dt, Sp-Fp; Ss = Ss-Fs` and `return dict(A_s=As, A_d=Ad, A_i=Ad-As, C_s=Cs, C_d=Cd)`, with math imported, one statement per line and the loop body indented." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def sir_avert(beta, gamma, N, S0, I0, V, steps, dt=1, cinf=0, rc=0): Sb, Ib, Sp, Ip, Ss = S0, I0, S0-V, I0, S0-V; As = Ad = Cs = Cd = 0.0" .

hemo:7005843 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for a Lowe CPI from weight-reference expenditure with price-updated weights for a three-item basket"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "All prices are above zero and refer to matched items. The manual price-updates weights at each link, to the previous December for the January index and to January for February to December (section 3.5), so the base period here is the link month and a longer series is chained with HE-FM-CPI-003." .

hemo:7005846 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CPI-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000868 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=100*SUMPRODUCT(UpdatedExpenditure/SUM(UpdatedExpenditure),ItemPriceCurrent/ItemPriceBase)` Returns the index into a cell named CFIndexOut. ItemExpenditure, ItemPriceWeightRef, ItemPriceBase and ItemPriceCurrent are ranges with one row per item, and the helper column UpdatedExpenditure holds on each row the expenditure times the base price divided by the period b price, for example `=B2*D2/C2` filled down when ItemExpenditure, ItemPriceWeightRef and ItemPriceBase sit in columns B, C and D from row 2." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=100*SUMPRODUCT(UpdatedExpenditure/SUM(UpdatedExpenditure),ItemPriceCurrent/ItemPriceBase)" .

hemo:7005847 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CPI-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000868 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`lowe_index <- function(V_b, p_b, p_0, p_t) { u <- V_b*p_0/p_b; w <- u/sum(u); list(w = w, index = 100*sum(w*p_t/p_0)) }` Returns the weights and the index; `lowe_index(c(500, 300, 200), c(2, 5, 10), c(2.1, 5, 11), c(2.31, 5.25, 11))$index` gives 106.4593." ;
    hemo:9000024 "lowe_index <- function(V_b, p_b, p_0, p_t) { u <- V_b*p_0/p_b; w <- u/sum(u); list(w = w, index = 100*sum(w*p_t/p_0)) }" .

hemo:7005848 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CPI-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000868 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def lowe_index(V_b, p_b, p_0, p_t): u = [v*a/b for v, a, b in zip(V_b, p_0, p_b)]; w = [x/sum(u) for x in u]; return w, 100*sum(wi*t/a for wi, t, a in zip(w, p_t, p_0))` Plain Python 3 with no imports, returning the weights and the index." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def lowe_index(V_b, p_b, p_0, p_t): u = [v*a/b for v, a, b in zip(V_b, p_0, p_b)]; w = [x/sum(u) for x in u]; return w, 100*sum(wi*t/a for wi, t, a in zip(w, p_t, p_0))" .

hemo:7005851 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for monthly D7BT values to financial-year CPI averages, their ratio and the average annual rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "All 24 values come from one release of one series. ONS computes CPI averages from unrounded monthly indices (section 11.3), so averages of the published one-decimal values can differ slightly from ONS-computed averages." .

hemo:7005854 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CPI-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000869 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=AVERAGE(MonthsEarlier)` in a cell named FYAverageEarlier and `=AVERAGE(MonthsLater)` in FYAverageLater, where MonthsEarlier and MonthsLater each hold twelve monthly values; `=FYAverageLater/FYAverageEarlier` in IndexRatioOut; `=IndexRatioOut^(1/YearsApart)-1` in AvgRateCFOut, since 12 over 12 times YearsApart is 1 over YearsApart." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AVERAGE(MonthsEarlier)" .

hemo:7005855 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CPI-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000869 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`fy_rate <- function(a, b, Y) { A1 <- mean(a); A2 <- mean(b); R <- A2/A1; c(A1 = A1, A2 = A2, R = R, g = R^(12/(12*Y))-1) }` Returns the two averages, their ratio and the average annual rate for vectors of monthly values." ;
    hemo:9000024 "fy_rate <- function(a, b, Y) { A1 <- mean(a); A2 <- mean(b); R <- A2/A1; c(A1 = A1, A2 = A2, R = R, g = R^(12/(12*Y))-1) }" .

hemo:7005856 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CPI-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000869 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def fy_rate(a, b, Y): A1 = sum(a)/len(a); A2 = sum(b)/len(b); R = A2/A1; return A1, A2, R, R**(12/(12*Y))-1` Plain Python 3 with no imports." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def fy_rate(a, b, Y): A1 = sum(a)/len(a); A2 = sum(b)/len(b); R = A2/A1; return A1, A2, R, R**(12/(12*Y))-1" .

hemo:7005859 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for filling a fixed budget down a cost-effectiveness league table"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Programmes are independent and each is measured against the comparator it would replace (HE-AS-CELT-001). A programme that dominates its comparator under HE-FM-DOM-001 is adopted before the call and its saving added to `B`, since a negative incremental cost would give it a share of zero. Programmes with equal ICERs keep their input order, which changes nothing for divisible programmes but can change the result for whole ones. In whole mode a programme that does not fit is passed over (HE-AS-CELT-007), and the critical ratio returned is the ICER of the least cost-effective programme adopted." .

hemo:7005864 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CELT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000870 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With one row per programme from row 2, sorted by ICER with Data, Sort, incremental costs in column B, QALYs in column C and the ICER `=B2/C2` in column D, enter `=SUM(B$1:B1)` in E2 for the cost of the programmes above, the header in B1 counting as zero, `=MIN(1,MAX(0,(Budget-E2)/B2))` in F2 for the share and `=F2*C2` in G2 for the health, and fill down. For four programmes `=SUMPRODUCT(F2:F5,C2:C5)` returns the health and `=SUMPRODUCT(MAX((F2:F5>0)*D2:D5))` the critical ratio. For whole programmes the share in F2 becomes `=--(SUMPRODUCT(B$1:B1,F$1:F1)+B2<=Budget)`, which tests the cost of the programmes already funded." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=B2/C2" .

hemo:7005865 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CELT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000870 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`celt_fill <- function(name, Delta_C, Delta_E, B, whole = FALSE) { r <- Delta_C/Delta_E; o <- order(r); name <- name[o]; Delta_C <- Delta_C[o]; Delta_E <- Delta_E[o]; r <- r[o]; x <- numeric(length(r)); left <- B; for (i in seq_along(r)) { x[i] <- if (whole) as.numeric(Delta_C[i] <= left) else min(1, max(0, left/Delta_C[i])); left <- left-x[i]*Delta_C[i] }; list(table = data.frame(programme = name, icer = r, share = x), health = sum(x*Delta_E), spend = sum(x*Delta_C), unspent = left, critical_ratio = if (any(x > 0)) max(r[x > 0]) else NA) }` Programmes may be given in any order." ;
    hemo:9000024 "celt_fill <- function(name, Delta_C, Delta_E, B, whole = FALSE) { r <- Delta_C/Delta_E; o <- order(r); name <- name[o]; Delta_C <- Delta_C[o]; Delta_E <- Delta_E[o]; r <- r[o]; x <- numeric(length(r)); left <- B; for (i in seq_along(r)) { x[i] <- if (whole) as.numeric(Delta_C[i] <= left) else min(1, max(0, left/Delta_C[i])); left <- left-x[i]*Delta_C[i] }; list(table = data.frame(programme = name, icer = r, share = x), health = sum(x*Delta_E), spend = sum(x*Delta_C), unspent = left, critical_ratio = if (any(x > 0)) max(r[x > 0]) else NA) }" .

hemo:7005866 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CELT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000870 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def celt_fill(name, Delta_C, Delta_E, B, whole=False): o = sorted(range(len(Delta_C)), key=lambda i: Delta_C[i]/Delta_E[i]); x = _celt_shares([Delta_C[i] for i in o], B, whole); r = [Delta_C[i]/Delta_E[i] for i in o]; spend = sum(s*Delta_C[i] for s, i in zip(x, o)); return [(name[i], r[k], x[k]) for k, i in enumerate(o)], sum(s*Delta_E[i] for s, i in zip(x, o)), spend, B-spend, max([r[k] for k in range(len(o)) if x[k] > 0], default=None)` with the helper `def _celt_shares(c, left, whole): return [] if not c else (lambda s: [s] + _celt_shares(c[1:], left-s*c[0], whole))(float(c[0] <= left) if whole else min(1.0, max(0.0, left/c[0])))` Plain Python with no imports, returning the programmes as tuples of name, ICER and share, then the health, the spending, the budget left and the critical ratio." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def celt_fill(name, Delta_C, Delta_E, B, whole=False): o = sorted(range(len(Delta_C)), key=lambda i: Delta_C[i]/Delta_E[i]); x = _celt_shares([Delta_C[i] for i in o], B, whole); r = [Delta_C[i]/Delta_E[i] for i in o]; spend = sum(s*Delta_C[i] for s, i in zip(x, o)); return [(name[i], r[k], x[k]) for k, i in enumerate(o)], sum(s*Delta_E[i] for s, i in zip(x, o)), spend, B-spend, max([r[k] for k in range(len(o)) if x[k] > 0], default=None)" .

hemo:7005869 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for comparing the UK MVH and Zimbabwe EQ-5D-3L value sets state by state"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Both sets take the constant and N3 form of HE-FM-EQ3L-001, with the Zimbabwe N3 decrement zero. Full health 11111 is fixed at 1 under both, although Jelsma and colleagues' model predicts 0.90 for it." .

hemo:7005874 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CSVS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000871 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=1-UKConst*(SUM(Levels)>5)-SUMPRODUCT((Levels=2)*UKModerate+(Levels=3)*UKExtreme)-UKSevereTerm*(MAX(Levels)=3)` fills UKUtility, `=1-ZimConst*(SUM(Levels)>5)-SUMPRODUCT((Levels=2)*ZimModerate+(Levels=3)*ZimExtreme)` fills ZimUtility and `=UKUtility-ZimUtility` fills CrossSetGap. The five levels are in a row range named Levels, the level 2 and level 3 decrements of each set in row ranges of the same shape named UKModerate, UKExtreme, ZimModerate and ZimExtreme, and the constants and the UK N3 decrement in UKConst, ZimConst and UKSevereTerm." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1-UKConst*(SUM(Levels)>5)-SUMPRODUCT((Levels=2)*UKModerate+(Levels=3)*UKExtreme)-UKSevereTerm*(MAX(Levels)=3)" .

hemo:7005875 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CSVS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000871 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`uk <- c(c0 = 0.081, MO2 = 0.069, MO3 = 0.314, SC2 = 0.104, SC3 = 0.214, UA2 = 0.036, UA3 = 0.094, PD2 = 0.123, PD3 = 0.386, AD2 = 0.071, AD3 = 0.236, N3 = 0.269); zw <- c(c0 = 0.100, MO2 = 0.056, MO3 = 0.204, SC2 = 0.092, SC3 = 0.231, UA2 = 0.043, UA3 = 0.135, PD2 = 0.067, PD3 = 0.302, AD2 = 0.046, AD3 = 0.173, N3 = 0); score <- function(l, cf) { dims <- c(\"MO\", \"SC\", \"UA\", \"PD\", \"AD\"); dec <- sum(sapply(1:5, function(d) if (l[d] == 1) 0 else cf[[paste0(dims[d], l[d])]])); 1-cf[[\"c0\"]]*any(l > 1)-dec-cf[[\"N3\"]]*any(l == 3) }; g <- as.matrix(expand.grid(rep(list(1:3), 5))); vu <- apply(g, 1, score, cf = uk); vz <- apply(g, 1, score, cf = zw); du <- vu-vz; gu <- outer(vu, vu, \"-\"); gz <- outer(vz, vz, \"-\"); rev <- sum(gu*gz < 0)` gives the 243 differences in `du` and the number of ordered pairs whose direction reverses in `rev`." ;
    hemo:9000024 "uk <- c(c0 = 0.081, MO2 = 0.069, MO3 = 0.314, SC2 = 0.104, SC3 = 0.214, UA2 = 0.036, UA3 = 0.094, PD2 = 0.123, PD3 = 0.386, AD2 = 0.071, AD3 = 0.236, N3 = 0.269); zw <- c(c0 = 0.100, MO2 = 0.056, MO3 = 0.204, SC2 = 0.092, SC3 = 0.231, UA2 = 0.043, UA3 = 0.135, PD2 = 0.067, PD3 = 0.302, AD2 = 0.046, AD3 = 0.173, N3 = 0); score <- function(l, cf) { dims <- c(\"MO\", \"SC\", \"UA\", \"PD\", \"AD\"); dec <- sum(sapply(1:5, function(d) if (l[d] == 1) 0 else cf[[paste0(dims[d], l[d])]])); 1-cf[[\"c0\"]]*any(l > 1)-dec-cf[[\"N3\"]]*any(l == 3) }; g <- as.matrix(expand.grid(rep(list(1:3), 5))); vu <- apply(g, 1, score, cf = uk); vz <- apply(g, 1, score, cf = zw); du <- vu-vz; gu <- outer(vu, vu, \"-\"); gz <- outer(vz, vz, \"-\"); rev <- sum(gu*gz < 0)" .

hemo:7005876 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CSVS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000871 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`from itertools import product; uk = {\"c0\": 0.081, \"MO2\": 0.069, \"MO3\": 0.314, \"SC2\": 0.104, \"SC3\": 0.214, \"UA2\": 0.036, \"UA3\": 0.094, \"PD2\": 0.123, \"PD3\": 0.386, \"AD2\": 0.071, \"AD3\": 0.236, \"N3\": 0.269}; zw = {\"c0\": 0.100, \"MO2\": 0.056, \"MO3\": 0.204, \"SC2\": 0.092, \"SC3\": 0.231, \"UA2\": 0.043, \"UA3\": 0.135, \"PD2\": 0.067, \"PD3\": 0.302, \"AD2\": 0.046, \"AD3\": 0.173, \"N3\": 0}; score = lambda l, cf: 1-cf[\"c0\"]*any(x > 1 for x in l)-sum(cf[d + str(x)] for d, x in zip([\"MO\", \"SC\", \"UA\", \"PD\", \"AD\"], l) if x > 1)-cf[\"N3\"]*any(x == 3 for x in l); S = list(product((1, 2, 3), repeat=5)); du = [score(s, uk)-score(s, zw) for s in S]; rev = sum(1 for a in S for b in S if (score(b, uk)-score(a, uk))*(score(b, zw)-score(a, zw)) < 0)` gives the same differences and reversal count." ;
    hemo:9000024 "from itertools import product; uk = {\"c0\": 0.081, \"MO2\": 0.069, \"MO3\": 0.314, \"SC2\": 0.104, \"SC3\": 0.214, \"UA2\": 0.036, \"UA3\": 0.094, \"PD2\": 0.123, \"PD3\": 0.386, \"AD2\": 0.071, \"AD3\": 0.236, \"N3\": 0.269}; zw = {\"c0\": 0.100, \"MO2\": 0.056, \"MO3\": 0.204, \"SC2\": 0.092, \"SC3\": 0.231, \"UA2\": 0.043, \"UA3\": 0.135, \"PD2\": 0.067, \"PD3\": 0.302, \"AD2\": 0.046, \"AD3\": 0.173, \"N3\": 0}; score = lambda l, cf: 1-cf[\"c0\"]*any(x > 1 for x in l)-sum(cf[d + str(x)] for d, x in zip([\"MO\", \"SC\", \"UA\", \"PD\", \"AD\"], l) if x > 1)-cf[\"N3\"]*any(x == 3 for x in l); S = list(product((1, 2, 3), repeat=5)); du = [score(s, uk)-score(s, zw) for s in S]; rev = sum(1 for a in S for b in S if (score(b, uk)-score(a, uk))*(score(b, zw)-score(a, zw)) < 0)" .

hemo:7005879 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for a PSA run with correlated log baseline rate and log hazard ratio through a constant-rate survival model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The two log parameters are jointly normal, each draw uses a fresh pair of standard normal values, both options in a draw use the same drawn parameters, mortality is constant over time, the incremental cost is fixed and the draws are equally likely. The illustrative inputs below are not estimates for any real technology." .

hemo:7005882 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-PSA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000872 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With frozen standard normal pairs in columns A and B (each `=NORM.S.INV(RAND())` pasted as values) and the named inputs of HE-IM-PSA-001, `=MeanLogRate+SDLogRate*A2` and `=MeanLogHR+CholOffDiag*A2+CholDiagSecond*B2` filled down give columns C and D, named DrawLogRate and DrawLogHR, and `=QALYPerLifeYear*(EXP(-C2-D2)-EXP(-C2))-IncCost/Threshold` filled down in column E gives the column named DrawIncNHB. `=AVERAGE(DrawIncNHB)`, `=COUNTIF(DrawIncNHB,\">0\")/COUNT(DrawIncNHB)` and `=STDEV.S(DrawIncNHB)/SQRT(COUNT(DrawIncNHB))` return the expected value, the probability and the Monte Carlo standard error, and the formulas of HE-IM-PSA-002 applied to DrawIncNHB return the two expected losses." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=NORM.S.INV(RAND())" .

hemo:7005883 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-PSA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000872 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`psa_run <- function(N, mu, s, rho, q, dC, lambda, seed) { set.seed(seed); S <- matrix(c(s[1]^2, rho*s[1]*s[2], rho*s[1]*s[2], s[2]^2), 2); L <- t(chol(S)); Z <- matrix(rnorm(2*N), 2); B <- mu + L %*% Z; INHB <- q*(exp(-B[1, ]-B[2, ])-exp(-B[1, ]))-dC/lambda; c(ENHB = mean(INHB), P_CE = mean(INHB > 0), EL_new = mean(pmax(-INHB, 0)), EL_comp = mean(pmax(INHB, 0)), MCSE = sd(INHB)/sqrt(N), r = cor(B[1, ], B[2, ])) }` Run as `psa_run(10000, c(log(0.25), log(0.8)), c(0.1, 0.2), -0.5, 0.5, 15000, 25000, 2026)`, which returns an expected incremental net health benefit of about minus 0.0761 QALYs, a probability of 0.4136, expected losses of about 0.2342 and 0.1581, a Monte Carlo standard error of about 0.0049 and a sample correlation of about minus 0.5005. The exact expected value for these inputs, from the log-normal mean of HE-FM-EV-004 (derived here), is about minus 0.0722 QALYs, about 0.79 Monte Carlo standard errors from the R result." ;
    hemo:9000024 "psa_run <- function(N, mu, s, rho, q, dC, lambda, seed) { set.seed(seed); S <- matrix(c(s[1]^2, rho*s[1]*s[2], rho*s[1]*s[2], s[2]^2), 2); L <- t(chol(S)); Z <- matrix(rnorm(2*N), 2); B <- mu + L %*% Z; INHB <- q*(exp(-B[1, ]-B[2, ])-exp(-B[1, ]))-dC/lambda; c(ENHB = mean(INHB), P_CE = mean(INHB > 0), EL_new = mean(pmax(-INHB, 0)), EL_comp = mean(pmax(INHB, 0)), MCSE = sd(INHB)/sqrt(N), r = cor(B[1, ], B[2, ])) }" .

hemo:7005884 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-PSA-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000872 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def psa_run(N, mu, s, rho, q, dC, lam, seed): import random, math; g = random.Random(seed); L21 = rho*s[1]; L22 = s[1]*math.sqrt(1-rho**2); Z = [(g.gauss(0, 1), g.gauss(0, 1)) for _ in range(N)]; B = [(mu[0]+s[0]*a, mu[1]+L21*a+L22*b) for a, b in Z]; I = [q*(math.exp(-b0-b1)-math.exp(-b0))-dC/lam for b0, b1 in B]; m = sum(I)/N; sd = math.sqrt(sum((x-m)**2 for x in I)/(N-1)); return m, sum(x > 0 for x in I)/N, sum(max(-x, 0) for x in I)/N, sum(max(x, 0) for x in I)/N, sd/math.sqrt(N)` Run with the same inputs; Python's generator gives different draws for the same seed, an expected value of about minus 0.0811 QALYs, a probability of 0.4094 and a Monte Carlo standard error of about 0.0048." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def psa_run(N, mu, s, rho, q, dC, lam, seed): import random, math; g = random.Random(seed); L21 = rho*s[1]; L22 = s[1]*math.sqrt(1-rho**2); Z = [(g.gauss(0, 1), g.gauss(0, 1)) for _ in range(N)]; B = [(mu[0]+s[0]*a, mu[1]+L21*a+L22*b) for a, b in Z]; I = [q*(math.exp(-b0-b1)-math.exp(-b0))-dC/lam for b0, b1 in B]; m = sum(I)/N; sd = math.sqrt(sum((x-m)**2 for x in I)/(N-1)); return m, sum(x > 0 for x in I)/N, sum(max(-x, 0) for x in I)/N, sum(max(x, 0) for x in I)/N, sd/math.sqrt(N)" .

hemo:7005887 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for annual patient time cost from service counts with the Yabroff time estimates"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Minutes per use are the urban (MSA) estimates in Table 1 of Yabroff and colleagues, 51.6 for an office or outpatient visit at ages 18 to 64 (the table's row for visits other than outpatient surgery, chemotherapy and radiation therapy) and 168.8 for a chemotherapy visit, which include the 1992 NHIS waiting time of 30.19 minutes, with 35.2 minutes of round-trip travel per visit and hospital time as the number of nights times 960 minutes. The paper states that travel was added to all services; one round trip per hospital stay and none per night is the reading taken here. The counts below are illustrative, not survey figures, and the value of time is the study's base-case median wage of USD 16.57 an hour in 2011." .

hemo:7005891 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-PTC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000873 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With one row per service category, the counts in ServiceCount, the minutes per use in MinutesPerUse and the travel flags in TravelAdded, the travel minutes in TravelMinutes and the hourly value in HourlyValue, `=SUMPRODUCT(ServiceCount,MinutesPerUse+TravelMinutes*TravelAdded)/60` in AnnualPatientHours gives the hours and `=AnnualPatientHours*HourlyValue` in AnnualPatientTimeCost the cost. A hospital stay row has 0 minutes and a travel flag of 1, and a hospital night row has 960 minutes and a flag of 0." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(ServiceCount,MinutesPerUse+TravelMinutes*TravelAdded)/60" .

hemo:7005892 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-PTC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000873 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`ptc_annual <- function(n, m, a, m_trav, w) { h <- sum(n*(m+m_trav*a))/60; c(hours = h, cost = w*h) }` Called as `ptc_annual(c(4, 6, 1, 3), c(51.6, 168.8, 0, 960), c(1, 1, 1, 0), 35.2, 16.57)` it returns 74.7733 hours and a cost of 1238.99." ;
    hemo:9000024 "ptc_annual <- function(n, m, a, m_trav, w) { h <- sum(n*(m+m_trav*a))/60; c(hours = h, cost = w*h) }" .

hemo:7005893 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-PTC-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000873 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def ptc_annual(n, m, a, m_trav, w): h = sum(nk*(mk+m_trav*ak) for nk, mk, ak in zip(n, m, a))/60; return h, w*h` Called with the same lists it returns 74.7733 and 1238.99." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def ptc_annual(n, m, a, m_trav, w): h = sum(nk*(mk+m_trav*ak) for nk, mk, ak in zip(n, m, a))/60; return h, w*h" .

hemo:7005896 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for scoring an EQ-5D-5L profile under any 20-decrement value set"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The value set has main effects only, with at most a constant beyond the 20 decrements. A set with interaction terms, such as the N3 term of the UK three-level tariff in HE-FM-EQ3L-002, needs its own scoring rule." .

hemo:7005900 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-EQ5L-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000874 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=1-Const*(SUM(Levels)>5)-SUMPRODUCT((Levels=2)*DecSlight+(Levels=3)*DecModerate+(Levels=4)*DecSevere+(Levels=5)*DecWorst)` With the five levels in a row range named Levels and the level 2, 3, 4 and 5 decrements of the five dimensions in row ranges named DecSlight, DecModerate, DecSevere and DecWorst of the same shape, and the constant in a cell named Const, the formula returns U in a cell named ProfileUtility." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=1-Const*(SUM(Levels)>5)-SUMPRODUCT((Levels=2)*DecSlight+(Levels=3)*DecModerate+(Levels=4)*DecSevere+(Levels=5)*DecWorst)" .

hemo:7005901 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-EQ5L-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000874 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`eq5l_utility <- function(s, tab, c0 = 0) { l <- as.integer(strsplit(as.character(s), \"\")[[1]]); dec <- sum(sapply(1:5, function(d) if (l[d] == 1) 0 else tab[d, l[d]-1])); 1-c0*any(l > 1)-dec }` Takes the profile as a string and a 5 by 4 matrix of decrements with rows in profile order and columns for levels 2 to 5. With `eng <- rbind(c(0.058, 0.076, 0.207, 0.274), c(0.050, 0.080, 0.164, 0.203), c(0.050, 0.063, 0.162, 0.184), c(0.063, 0.084, 0.276, 0.335), c(0.078, 0.104, 0.285, 0.289))`, `v <- sapply(apply(expand.grid(rep(list(1:5), 5)), 1, paste, collapse = \"\"), eq5l_utility, tab = eng)` scores all 3,125 states, and `c(min(v), sum(v < 0))` returns minus 0.285 and 159." ;
    hemo:9000024 "eq5l_utility <- function(s, tab, c0 = 0) { l <- as.integer(strsplit(as.character(s), \"\")[[1]]); dec <- sum(sapply(1:5, function(d) if (l[d] == 1) 0 else tab[d, l[d]-1])); 1-c0*any(l > 1)-dec }" .

hemo:7005902 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-EQ5L-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000874 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def eq5l_utility(s, tab, c0=0): l = [int(c) for c in str(s)]; return 1-c0*any(x > 1 for x in l)-sum(tab[d][x-2] for d, x in enumerate(l) if x > 1)` Takes the profile as a string or integer and a list of five lists of four decrements in profile order. With `eng = [[0.058, 0.076, 0.207, 0.274], [0.050, 0.080, 0.164, 0.203], [0.050, 0.063, 0.162, 0.184], [0.063, 0.084, 0.276, 0.335], [0.078, 0.104, 0.285, 0.289]]`, `from itertools import product; v = [eq5l_utility(\"\".join(map(str, p)), eng) for p in product(range(1, 6), repeat=5)]` scores all 3,125 states, and `(round(min(v), 3), sum(x < 0 for x in v))` returns (-0.285, 159)." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def eq5l_utility(s, tab, c0=0): l = [int(c) for c in str(s)]; return 1-c0*any(x > 1 for x in l)-sum(tab[d][x-2] for d, x in enumerate(l) if x > 1)" .

hemo:7005905 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for the outlay and least-cost inputs that hold the health stock on a target path"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Depreciation is exogenous and applies to the stock at the start of the period, as in HE-FM-DFH-001, prices are fixed within each period, and the target path is set by the user, not derived from the optimality condition HE-FM-DFH-002. The path total is undiscounted at one unit cost; costs of later periods are discounted with HE-FN-DISC-001. The figures are the article's illustrative values." .

hemo:7005909 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-HPF-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000875 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For one period, `=MAX(TargetStock-(1-DeprRate)*HealthStock,0)` in RequiredInvest, `=UnitCostInvest*RequiredInvest` in HoldOutlay, `=(1-TimeShare)*HoldOutlay/PriceCare` in HoldCareUnits and `=TimeShare*HoldOutlay/Wage` in HoldTimeHours, with UnitCostInvest from HE-IM-HPF-003. For a path with one row per period in the ranges StartPath, DeprPath and TargetPath, where StartPath holds the starting stock in its first row and in each later row the previous row's end stock, the larger of that row's TargetPath and (1-DeprPath)*StartPath, `=UnitCostInvest*SUMPRODUCT((TargetPath-(1-DeprPath)*StartPath)*(TargetPath>(1-DeprPath)*StartPath))` in PathOutlay gives the undiscounted total outlay over the path with each period's required investment floored at zero." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MAX(TargetStock-(1-DeprRate)*HealthStock,0)" .

hemo:7005910 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-HPF-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000875 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`hold_path <- function(H0, d, H_target, P, W, K, rho, E) { n <- length(d); H_target <- rep_len(H_target, n); pi <- exp(-rho*E)*(P/(1-K))^(1-K)*(W/K)^K; H <- numeric(n); I_req <- numeric(n); h <- H0; for (t in seq_len(n)) { H[t] <- h; I_req[t] <- max(H_target[t]-(1-d[t])*h, 0); h <- (1-d[t])*h+I_req[t] }; C <- pi*I_req; data.frame(H = H, I_req = I_req, C = C, M = (1-K)*C/P, TH = K*C/W) }` Called as `hold_path(100, c(0.15, 0.20, 0.25), 100, 40, 10, 0.5, 0.03, 0)` it returns required gross investment of 15, 20 and 25 and outlays of 600, 800 and 1000, 2400 in total. Called as `hold_path(100, c(0.15, 0.15, 0.15), c(105, 80, 100), 40, 10, 0.5, 0.03, 0)` it carries 105 into the second period, where the depreciated stock of 89.25 is above the target and no investment is needed, and 89.25 into the third, which needs 24.1375 units and 965.50 GBP." ;
    hemo:9000024 "hold_path <- function(H0, d, H_target, P, W, K, rho, E) { n <- length(d); H_target <- rep_len(H_target, n); pi <- exp(-rho*E)*(P/(1-K))^(1-K)*(W/K)^K; H <- numeric(n); I_req <- numeric(n); h <- H0; for (t in seq_len(n)) { H[t] <- h; I_req[t] <- max(H_target[t]-(1-d[t])*h, 0); h <- (1-d[t])*h+I_req[t] }; C <- pi*I_req; data.frame(H = H, I_req = I_req, C = C, M = (1-K)*C/P, TH = K*C/W) }" .

hemo:7005911 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-HPF-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000875 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With `import math` and `from itertools import accumulate` (Python 3.8 or later), `def hold_path(H0, d, H_target, P, W, K, rho, E): pi = math.exp(-rho*E)*(P/(1-K))**(1-K)*(W/K)**K; H = list(accumulate(zip(d, H_target), lambda h, x: max(x[1], (1-x[0])*h), initial=H0)); req = [max(t-(1-dt)*h, 0) for h, dt, t in zip(H, d, H_target)]; return [(h, i, pi*i, (1-K)*pi*i/P, K*pi*i/W) for h, i in zip(H, req)]` Called as `hold_path(100, [0.15, 0.20, 0.25], [100, 100, 100], 40, 10, 0.5, 0.03, 0)` it returns outlays of 600, 800 and 1000, and with the second R call's inputs it returns the same starting stocks and outlays." ;
    hemo:9000024 "import math" .

hemo:7005914 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for specific stop-loss expected cost, spread and premium by attachment point from a claims distribution and group size"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Members' claims are independent and follow the same distribution, the remaining probability sits at claims below the attachment point, and the loading is a fixed share of expected reimbursement. Shared shocks make members' claims move together, and the true standard deviation of total reimbursement is then larger than `SD_T` (HE-AS-ASL-005)." .

hemo:7005918 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ATTP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000876 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With ClaimLevels, ClaimProbs, ClaimCap and CoinsRate as in HE-IM-ATTP-001, the number of members in a cell named Members, the loading in PremLoading, the capped claim levels `=MIN(B2,ClaimCap)` filled down in E2 to E4 and named CappedLevels, and attachment points in F2 to F4, the row formulas `=CoinsRate*SUMPRODUCT(ClaimProbs,(CappedLevels-F2)*(CappedLevels>F2))` in G2, `=CoinsRate^2*SUMPRODUCT(ClaimProbs,((CappedLevels-F2)*(CappedLevels>F2))^2)-G2^2` in H2, `=Members*G2` in I2, `=SQRT(H2)*SQRT(Members)` in J2, `=(1+PremLoading)*I2` in K2, `=J2/I2` in L2 and `=Members*SUMPRODUCT(ClaimProbs,--(ClaimLevels>F2))` in M2, filled down to row 4, give the article's table." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MIN(B2,ClaimCap)" .

hemo:7005919 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ATTP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000876 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`attp_grid <- function(p, C, A, U = Inf, cc = 1, n = 1000, theta = 0.3) { t(sapply(A, function(a) { x <- cc*pmax(pmin(C, U)-a, 0); E <- sum(p*x); V <- sum(p*x^2)-E^2; c(A = a, E_R = E, SD_R = sqrt(V), N_A = n*sum(p[C > a]), E_T = n*E, SD_T = sqrt(n*V), P_T = (1+theta)*n*E, CV_T = sqrt(n*V)/(n*E)) })) }` Run as `attp_grid(c(0.006, 0.003, 0.001), c(75000, 150000, 500000), c(50000, 100000, 200000))`, which returns totals of 900,000, 550,000 and 300,000 with standard deviations of about 485,222, 408,898 and 299,850." ;
    hemo:9000024 "attp_grid <- function(p, C, A, U = Inf, cc = 1, n = 1000, theta = 0.3) { t(sapply(A, function(a) { x <- cc*pmax(pmin(C, U)-a, 0); E <- sum(p*x); V <- sum(p*x^2)-E^2; c(A = a, E_R = E, SD_R = sqrt(V), N_A = n*sum(p[C > a]), E_T = n*E, SD_T = sqrt(n*V), P_T = (1+theta)*n*E, CV_T = sqrt(n*V)/(n*E)) })) }" .

hemo:7005920 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ATTP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000876 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def attp_grid(p, C, A, U=float(\"inf\"), c=1.0, n=1000, theta=0.3): import math; out = []; [out.append((a, E, math.sqrt(V), n*sum(pk for pk, ck in zip(p, C) if ck > a), n*E, math.sqrt(n*V), (1+theta)*n*E, math.sqrt(n*V)/(n*E))) for a in A for x in [[c*max(min(ck, U)-a, 0) for ck in C]] for E in [sum(pk*xk for pk, xk in zip(p, x))] for V in [sum(pk*xk**2 for pk, xk in zip(p, x))-E**2]]; return out` Run as `attp_grid([0.006, 0.003, 0.001], [75000, 150000, 500000], [50000, 100000, 200000])`, which returns the same figures as the R version." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def attp_grid(p, C, A, U=float(\"inf\"), c=1.0, n=1000, theta=0.3): import math; out = []; [out.append((a, E, math.sqrt(V), n*sum(pk for pk, ck in zip(p, C) if ck > a), n*E, math.sqrt(n*V), (1+theta)*n*E, math.sqrt(n*V)/(n*E))) for a in A for x in [[c*max(min(ck, U)-a, 0) for ck in C]] for E in [sum(pk*xk for pk, xk in zip(p, x))] for V in [sum(pk*xk**2 for pk, xk in zip(p, x))-E**2]]; return out" .

hemo:7005923 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Exact Hedges' correction factor by the log-gamma function with g and its variance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Normal outcomes with a common standard deviation in both independent groups and a pooled standard deviation, as in Hedges' Theorems 1 and 2. Putting g in place of the unknown delta in the exact variance is a plug-in choice made here, as Hedges does for his large-sample variance." .

hemo:7005926 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-HEDG-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000877 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=EXP(GAMMALN(DegFreedom/2)-GAMMALN((DegFreedom-1)/2))/SQRT(DegFreedom/2)` in a cell named ExactJ; `=CorrectionJ-ExactJ` in ApproxErrorJ; `=ExactJ*CohensD` in HedgesGExact; `=ExactJ^2*VarianceD` in VarianceGExact; `=SQRT(VarianceGExact)` in StdErrGExact; `=ExactJ^2*DegFreedom/(DegFreedom-2)*((SizeIntervention+SizeComparator)/(SizeIntervention*SizeComparator)+HedgesGExact^2)-HedgesGExact^2` in VarianceTheorem2. DegFreedom, CorrectionJ, CohensD, VarianceD, SizeIntervention and SizeComparator are the named cells of HE-IM-HEDG-001 and HE-IM-HEDG-002. GAMMALN is available in all Excel versions." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=EXP(GAMMALN(DegFreedom/2)-GAMMALN((DegFreedom-1)/2))/SQRT(DegFreedom/2)" .

hemo:7005927 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-HEDG-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000877 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`hedges_g_exact <- function(d, n1, n2) { m <- n1+n2-2; J_exact <- exp(lgamma(m/2)-lgamma((m-1)/2))/sqrt(m/2); J_approx <- 1-3/(4*m-1); nt <- n1*n2/(n1+n2); v_d <- (n1+n2)/(n1*n2)+d^2/(2*(n1+n2)); g <- J_exact*d; list(m=m, J_exact=J_exact, J_approx=J_approx, approx_error=J_approx-J_exact, g_exact=g, v_g_exact=J_exact^2*v_d, se_g_exact=sqrt(J_exact^2*v_d), v_theorem2=J_exact^2*m/(m-2)*(1/nt+g^2)-g^2) }` Returns a named list; `hedges_g_exact(0.8366, 10, 12)$J_exact` is about 0.9619445." ;
    hemo:9000024 "hedges_g_exact <- function(d, n1, n2) { m <- n1+n2-2; J_exact <- exp(lgamma(m/2)-lgamma((m-1)/2))/sqrt(m/2); J_approx <- 1-3/(4*m-1); nt <- n1*n2/(n1+n2); v_d <- (n1+n2)/(n1*n2)+d^2/(2*(n1+n2)); g <- J_exact*d; list(m=m, J_exact=J_exact, J_approx=J_approx, approx_error=J_approx-J_exact, g_exact=g, v_g_exact=J_exact^2*v_d, se_g_exact=sqrt(J_exact^2*v_d), v_theorem2=J_exact^2*m/(m-2)*(1/nt+g^2)-g^2) }" .

hemo:7005928 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-HEDG-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000877 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def hedges_g_exact(d, n1, n2): m = n1+n2-2; J_exact = math.exp(math.lgamma(m/2)-math.lgamma((m-1)/2))/math.sqrt(m/2); J_approx = 1-3/(4*m-1); nt = n1*n2/(n1+n2); v_d = (n1+n2)/(n1*n2)+d**2/(2*(n1+n2)); g = J_exact*d; return {\"m\": m, \"J_exact\": J_exact, \"J_approx\": J_approx, \"approx_error\": J_approx-J_exact, \"g_exact\": g, \"v_g_exact\": J_exact**2*v_d, \"se_g_exact\": math.sqrt(J_exact**2*v_d), \"v_theorem2\": J_exact**2*m/(m-2)*(1/nt+g**2)-g**2}` Requires the math module; returns a dictionary with the same values as the R function." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def hedges_g_exact(d, n1, n2): m = n1+n2-2; J_exact = math.exp(math.lgamma(m/2)-math.lgamma((m-1)/2))/math.sqrt(m/2); J_approx = 1-3/(4*m-1); nt = n1*n2/(n1+n2); v_d = (n1+n2)/(n1*n2)+d**2/(2*(n1+n2)); g = J_exact*d; return {\"m\": m, \"J_exact\": J_exact, \"J_approx\": J_approx, \"approx_error\": J_approx-J_exact, \"g_exact\": g, \"v_g_exact\": J_exact**2*v_d, \"se_g_exact\": math.sqrt(J_exact**2*v_d), \"v_theorem2\": J_exact**2*m/(m-2)*(1/nt+g**2)-g**2}" .

hemo:7005931 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for delta-adjusted QALY gain, ICER and tipping-point shift over a grid for loss to follow-up"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The shift is additive on the imputed QALYs of those lost and costs are not shifted (HE-AS-LTFU-001, HE-AS-LTFU-002). The interpolation is exact when the net health benefit is linear in the shift and an approximation otherwise (HE-FM-OWSA-002). The grid runs from zero towards worse outcomes, so it finds a tipping point only when the base case is cost-effective; for a base case that is not, the grid runs towards positive shifts." .

hemo:7005936 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-LTFU-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000878 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs in cells named ObsMeanNew, ObsMeanUsual, LostShareNew, LostShareUsual, ShiftRatio, DeltaCost and ThresholdQALY, put the shifts 0, -0.02 and so on to -0.30 in `A2:A17` (named GridShift), enter `=(ObsMeanNew+LostShareNew*A2)-(ObsMeanUsual+ShiftRatio*LostShareUsual*A2)` in B2 for the gain, `=DeltaCost/B2` in C2 for the ICER and `=B2-DeltaCost/ThresholdQALY` in D2 for the net health benefit, and fill down to row 17 (name `C2:C17` GridICER and `D2:D17` GridINHB). A cell named GridRowBelow holds `=MATCH(TRUE,INDEX(GridINHB<0,0),0)`, the first grid row with a negative net health benefit, and a cell named GridTipping holds `=INDEX(GridShift,GridRowBelow-1)-INDEX(GridINHB,GridRowBelow-1)*(INDEX(GridShift,GridRowBelow)-INDEX(GridShift,GridRowBelow-1))/(INDEX(GridINHB,GridRowBelow)-INDEX(GridINHB,GridRowBelow-1))`." ;
    hemo:9000024 "A2:A17" .

hemo:7005937 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-LTFU-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000878 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`ltfu_scan <- function(q, arm, lost, dC, lambda, r = 0, grid = seq(0, -0.3, by = -0.02)) { inhb <- sapply(grid, function(d) { qa <- q+d*lost*(arm == 1)+r*d*lost*(arm == 0); mean(qa[arm == 1])-mean(qa[arm == 0])-dC/lambda }); k <- which(diff(sign(inhb)) != 0)[1]; tip <- if (is.na(k)) NA else grid[k]-inhb[k]*(grid[k+1]-grid[k])/(inhb[k+1]-inhb[k]); list(table = data.frame(delta = grid, INHB = inhb, ICER = dC/(inhb+dC/lambda)), tipping = tip) }` Run on each imputed dataset when there are several and average the gains." ;
    hemo:9000024 "ltfu_scan <- function(q, arm, lost, dC, lambda, r = 0, grid = seq(0, -0.3, by = -0.02)) { inhb <- sapply(grid, function(d) { qa <- q+d*lost*(arm == 1)+r*d*lost*(arm == 0); mean(qa[arm == 1])-mean(qa[arm == 0])-dC/lambda }); k <- which(diff(sign(inhb)) != 0)[1]; tip <- if (is.na(k)) NA else grid[k]-inhb[k]*(grid[k+1]-grid[k])/(inhb[k+1]-inhb[k]); list(table = data.frame(delta = grid, INHB = inhb, ICER = dC/(inhb+dC/lambda)), tipping = tip) }" .

hemo:7005938 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-LTFU-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000878 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def ltfu_scan(q, arm, lost, dC, lam, r=0.0, grid=None): grid = grid or [-0.02*i for i in range(16)]; f = lambda d: (lambda qa: sum(x for x, a in zip(qa, arm) if a == 1)/arm.count(1)-sum(x for x, a in zip(qa, arm) if a == 0)/arm.count(0)-dC/lam)([x+d*l*(a == 1)+r*d*l*(a == 0) for x, a, l in zip(q, arm, lost)]); v = [f(d) for d in grid]; k = next((i for i in range(len(v)-1) if (v[i] > 0) != (v[i+1] > 0)), None); return list(zip(grid, v)), (None if k is None else grid[k]-v[k]*(grid[k+1]-grid[k])/(v[k+1]-v[k]))` Plain Python with no imports." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def ltfu_scan(q, arm, lost, dC, lam, r=0.0, grid=None): grid = grid or [-0.02*i for i in range(16)]; f = lambda d: (lambda qa: sum(x for x, a in zip(qa, arm) if a == 1)/arm.count(1)-sum(x for x, a in zip(qa, arm) if a == 0)/arm.count(0)-dC/lam)([x+d*l*(a == 1)+r*d*l*(a == 0) for x, a, l in zip(q, arm, lost)]); v = [f(d) for d in grid]; k = next((i for i in range(len(v)-1) if (v[i] > 0) != (v[i+1] > 0)), None); return list(zip(grid, v)), (None if k is None else grid[k]-v[k]*(grid[k+1]-grid[k])/(v[k+1]-v[k]))" .

hemo:7005941 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Provider cost indices, direct standardisation check and reversed pairs from group tables"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every provider's cases are grouped on the same classification, the national mean unit costs come from one collection on the same cost basis as the providers' unit costs, and ties are not counted as reversals." .

hemo:7005944 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CMAD-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000879 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With one provider per row from row 2, here rows 2 to 11 for ten providers, its cases in the two groups in columns B and C, its unit costs in D and E, and the national mean unit costs in `B13:C13` (named NationalUnitCost), row 2 holds `=SUMPRODUCT(B2:C2,D2:E2)` for the actual cost in F2, `=SUMPRODUCT(B2:C2,NationalUnitCost)` for the expected cost in G2, `=F2/SUM(B2:C2)` for the crude cost per case in H2 and `=100*F2/G2` for the index in I2, each filled down. The pooled shares are `=SUM(B$2:B$11)/SUM($B$2:$C$11)` in B14, filled right into C14 (named PooledShare), and `=SUMPRODUCT(D2:E2,PooledShare)` in J2, filled down, gives the directly standardised cost per case, with J2 and J3 named DirectCostFirst and DirectCostSecond. `=SUMPRODUCT(--(($H$2:$H$11-H2)*($I$2:$I$11-I2)<0))` in K2 and `=SUMPRODUCT(--(($H$2:$H$11-H2)*($J$2:$J$11-J2)<0))` in L2, filled down, count the providers whose order against row 2 differs, and `=SUM(K2:K11)/2` and `=SUM(L2:L11)/2` in cells named ReversedPairsIndex and ReversedPairsDirect count each reversed pair once. The ranges cover exactly the providers compared, and more groups add columns." ;
    hemo:9000024 "B13:C13" .

hemo:7005945 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CMAD-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000879 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`case_mix_compare <- function(n, u, cbar) { A <- rowSums(n*u); E <- as.vector(n %*% cbar); crude <- A/rowSums(n); I <- 100*A/E; s <- colSums(n)/sum(n); D <- as.vector(u %*% s); pr <- combn(nrow(n), 2); rev_I <- sum((crude[pr[1, ]]-crude[pr[2, ]])*(I[pr[1, ]]-I[pr[2, ]]) < 0); rev_D <- sum((crude[pr[1, ]]-crude[pr[2, ]])*(D[pr[1, ]]-D[pr[2, ]]) < 0); list(A = A, E = E, crude = crude, I = I, D = D, rev_I = rev_I, rev_D = rev_D) }` Base R only; `case_mix_compare(rbind(c(800, 200), c(300, 700)), rbind(c(2100, 8400), c(1900, 7600)), c(2000, 8000))` returns indices of 105 and 95, direct costs of 4935 and 4465 and one reversed pair for each method." ;
    hemo:9000024 "case_mix_compare <- function(n, u, cbar) { A <- rowSums(n*u); E <- as.vector(n %*% cbar); crude <- A/rowSums(n); I <- 100*A/E; s <- colSums(n)/sum(n); D <- as.vector(u %*% s); pr <- combn(nrow(n), 2); rev_I <- sum((crude[pr[1, ]]-crude[pr[2, ]])*(I[pr[1, ]]-I[pr[2, ]]) < 0); rev_D <- sum((crude[pr[1, ]]-crude[pr[2, ]])*(D[pr[1, ]]-D[pr[2, ]]) < 0); list(A = A, E = E, crude = crude, I = I, D = D, rev_I = rev_I, rev_D = rev_D) }" .

hemo:7005946 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CMAD-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000879 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def case_mix_compare(n, u, cbar): P = len(n); G = len(cbar); A = [sum(n[h][g]*u[h][g] for g in range(G)) for h in range(P)]; E = [sum(n[h][g]*cbar[g] for g in range(G)) for h in range(P)]; crude = [A[h]/sum(n[h]) for h in range(P)]; I = [100*A[h]/E[h] for h in range(P)]; tot = sum(sum(r) for r in n); s = [sum(n[h][g] for h in range(P))/tot for g in range(G)]; D = [sum(s[g]*u[h][g] for g in range(G)) for h in range(P)]; pairs = [(i, j) for i in range(P) for j in range(i+1, P)]; rev_I = sum((crude[i]-crude[j])*(I[i]-I[j]) < 0 for i, j in pairs); rev_D = sum((crude[i]-crude[j])*(D[i]-D[j]) < 0 for i, j in pairs); return {\"A\": A, \"E\": E, \"crude\": crude, \"I\": I, \"D\": D, \"rev_I\": rev_I, \"rev_D\": rev_D}` Plain Python with no imports, taking lists of lists in the same group order; the article's inputs return the same values as the R code." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def case_mix_compare(n, u, cbar): P = len(n); G = len(cbar); A = [sum(n[h][g]*u[h][g] for g in range(G)) for h in range(P)]; E = [sum(n[h][g]*cbar[g] for g in range(G)) for h in range(P)]; crude = [A[h]/sum(n[h]) for h in range(P)]; I = [100*A[h]/E[h] for h in range(P)]; tot = sum(sum(r) for r in n); s = [sum(n[h][g] for h in range(P))/tot for g in range(G)]; D = [sum(s[g]*u[h][g] for g in range(G)) for h in range(P)]; pairs = [(i, j) for i in range(P) for j in range(i+1, P)]; rev_I = sum((crude[i]-crude[j])*(I[i]-I[j]) < 0 for i, j in pairs); rev_D = sum((crude[i]-crude[j])*(D[i]-D[j]) < 0 for i, j in pairs); return {\"A\": A, \"E\": E, \"crude\": crude, \"I\": I, \"D\": D, \"rev_I\": rev_I, \"rev_D\": rev_D}" .

hemo:7005949 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for conditional covariances and the joint negative likelihood ratio of two tests from cross-classified counts"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The covariances are plug-in estimates of the quantities in the joint probabilities of Wang, Lin and Nelson, with the reference standard taken as correct (HE-AS-DXA-001). Sampling uncertainty is not computed." .

hemo:7005952 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-NLR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000880 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the counts in CasesTotal, NonCasesTotal, CasesNegFirst, CasesNegSecond, CasesNegBoth, NonCasesNegFirst, NonCasesNegSecond and NonCasesNegBoth, `=CasesNegBoth/CasesTotal-(CasesNegFirst/CasesTotal)*(CasesNegSecond/CasesTotal)` returns CovCasesEst, `=NonCasesNegBoth/NonCasesTotal-(NonCasesNegFirst/NonCasesTotal)*(NonCasesNegSecond/NonCasesTotal)` returns CovNonCasesEst, `=((CasesNegFirst/CasesTotal)*(CasesNegSecond/CasesTotal)+CovCasesEst)/((NonCasesNegFirst/NonCasesTotal)*(NonCasesNegSecond/NonCasesTotal)+CovNonCasesEst)` returns JointNegLRCounts and `=(CasesNegFirst/CasesTotal)/(NonCasesNegFirst/NonCasesTotal)*(CasesNegSecond/CasesTotal)/(NonCasesNegSecond/NonCasesTotal)` returns ProductNegLR." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CasesNegBoth/CasesTotal-(CasesNegFirst/CasesTotal)*(CasesNegSecond/CasesTotal)" .

hemo:7005953 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-NLR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000880 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`joint_lr_neg <- function(n_D, n_N, x_1D, x_2D, x_12D, y_1N, y_2N, y_12N) { Se_1 <- 1-x_1D/n_D; Se_2 <- 1-x_2D/n_D; Sp_1 <- y_1N/n_N; Sp_2 <- y_2N/n_N; C_pos <- x_12D/n_D-(1-Se_1)*(1-Se_2); C_neg <- y_12N/n_N-Sp_1*Sp_2; c(C_pos = C_pos, C_neg = C_neg, LR_neg_12 = ((1-Se_1)*(1-Se_2)+C_pos)/(Sp_1*Sp_2+C_neg), LR_prod = (1-Se_1)/Sp_1*(1-Se_2)/Sp_2) }` The call `round(joint_lr_neg(100, 200, 5, 10, 3, 100, 160, 90), 4)` returns 0.025, 0.05, 0.0667 and 0.0125." ;
    hemo:9000024 "joint_lr_neg <- function(n_D, n_N, x_1D, x_2D, x_12D, y_1N, y_2N, y_12N) { Se_1 <- 1-x_1D/n_D; Se_2 <- 1-x_2D/n_D; Sp_1 <- y_1N/n_N; Sp_2 <- y_2N/n_N; C_pos <- x_12D/n_D-(1-Se_1)*(1-Se_2); C_neg <- y_12N/n_N-Sp_1*Sp_2; c(C_pos = C_pos, C_neg = C_neg, LR_neg_12 = ((1-Se_1)*(1-Se_2)+C_pos)/(Sp_1*Sp_2+C_neg), LR_prod = (1-Se_1)/Sp_1*(1-Se_2)/Sp_2) }" .

hemo:7005954 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-NLR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000880 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def joint_lr_neg(n_D, n_N, x_1D, x_2D, x_12D, y_1N, y_2N, y_12N): Se_1 = 1-x_1D/n_D; Se_2 = 1-x_2D/n_D; Sp_1 = y_1N/n_N; Sp_2 = y_2N/n_N; C_pos = x_12D/n_D-(1-Se_1)*(1-Se_2); C_neg = y_12N/n_N-Sp_1*Sp_2; return C_pos, C_neg, ((1-Se_1)*(1-Se_2)+C_pos)/(Sp_1*Sp_2+C_neg), (1-Se_1)/Sp_1*(1-Se_2)/Sp_2` Plain Python with no imports; `joint_lr_neg(100, 200, 5, 10, 3, 100, 160, 90)` returns about 0.025, 0.05, 0.0667 and 0.0125." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def joint_lr_neg(n_D, n_N, x_1D, x_2D, x_12D, y_1N, y_2N, y_12N): Se_1 = 1-x_1D/n_D; Se_2 = 1-x_2D/n_D; Sp_1 = y_1N/n_N; Sp_2 = y_2N/n_N; C_pos = x_12D/n_D-(1-Se_1)*(1-Se_2); C_neg = y_12N/n_N-Sp_1*Sp_2; return C_pos, C_neg, ((1-Se_1)*(1-Se_2)+C_pos)/(Sp_1*Sp_2+C_neg), (1-Se_1)/Sp_1*(1-Se_2)/Sp_2" .

hemo:7005957 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal-sized groups in the Kolm mean"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each of the n values counts equally, as for people or equal-sized groups. Unequal groups need population shares in place of 1/n." .

hemo:7005958 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal increments read as a straight line"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "PMG36 table 6.2 gives weights between 1 and 3 in equal increments for 11 to 29 QALYs. HEMO reads this as a weight rising by 0.1 for each QALY from 10 to 30, which matches the table at every whole number." .

hemo:7005959 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant change in life satisfaction"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The change is the same for every person and every year, and is not discounted. A changing or discounted stream needs a sum over people and years." .

hemo:7005960 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Hours valued at the substitute's price"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each hour of care is valued at the price of a close market substitute, whatever the carer gave up." .

hemo:7005961 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Weights add to one"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The three criterion weights add to 1, so the overall value lies on the scale of the partial values." .

hemo:7005962 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "No other entries or exits"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every change in the stock is counted in the inflow or the outflow." .

hemo:7005963 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Same period and currency for spending and consumption"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Out-of-pocket spending and consumption cover the same period and are in the same currency." .

hemo:7005964 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant annual costs per patient"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The drug and displaced costs per patient-year are the same in every year of the projection." .

hemo:7005965 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Gross, not incremental"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The ratio does not subtract quits that would have happened without the service." .

hemo:7005966 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Comparison districts as the counterfactual"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "D_0 is measured in comparable districts without the programme, unadjusted." .

hemo:7005967 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Grants and spending over the same period"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The grants and the spending cover the same period." .

hemo:7005968 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Wastage counted against doses used up"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The denominator is doses given plus doses wasted, not doses received." .

hemo:7005969 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Prices and GDP in the same international currency"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The prices and GDP per capita are in one currency at purchasing power parity." .

