hemo:7006159 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Probit selection on the reported standard error with fixed selection parameters"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Publication depends on the study only through its reported standard error and a standard normal error delta, as in the selection equation Z = gamma_0 + gamma_1 / s + delta that Carpenter, Rücker and Schwarzer state with fixed gamma_0 and gamma_1. In a Copas sensitivity analysis the pair is not estimated from the published studies but fixed over a grid of assumed degrees of selection, because, as Huang and colleagues describe (HE-SR-COPAS-004), the conditional likelihood can peak on a very flat plateau. Dependence on the study's own result enters only through the correlation rho between delta and the sampling error, which this formula does not use." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Probit selection on the reported standard error with fixed selection parameters" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-COPAS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "probit-selection-on-the-reported-standard-error-with-fixed-selection-parameters" .

hemo:7006160 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Precision coefficient at zero or above in the Copas selection equation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "gamma_1 is at least zero, so a more precise study is at least as likely to be published as a less precise one; Huang and colleagues describe the term gamma_0 + gamma_1 / s as making studies of high precision more likely to be published (HE-SR-COPAS-004), and Duan and colleagues note that its sign is expected to be positive because larger studies are more likely to be accepted for publication. With gamma_1 equal to zero every study has probability Phi(gamma_0) whatever its size, and with gamma_1 above zero P rises towards 1 as s falls towards zero (derived). The standard error s is above zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Precision coefficient at zero or above in the Copas selection equation" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-COPAS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "precision-coefficient-at-zero-or-above-in-the-copas-selection-equation" .

hemo:7006161 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Standard error on the pooling scale in the Copas selection index"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "s is the standard error of the estimate on the scale used for pooling, the log odds ratio in the article, so that 1 / s is the precision in the selection equation. A standard error taken on another scale, such as the odds ratio itself, changes the index and the probability." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Standard error on the pooling scale in the Copas selection index" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-COPAS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "standard-error-on-the-pooling-scale-in-the-copas-selection-index" .

hemo:7006162 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Copas publication probability of one study from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000901 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the selection parameters in cells named SelGammaZero and SelGammaOne and the study's standard error in StudyStdErr, the first formula in a cell named PubIndex returns u and the second in a cell named PubProb returns the publication probability, using NORM.S.DIST with TRUE for the standard normal distribution function." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Copas publication probability of one study from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-COPAS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "copas-publication-probability-of-one-study-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SelGammaZero+SelGammaOne/StudyStdErr; =NORM.S.DIST(PubIndex,TRUE)" .

hemo:7006163 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Copas publication probability inverts to the selection index"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "NORM.S.INV of the value in PubProb returns the index gamma_0 + gamma_1 / s computed directly from the named inputs, within 1E-6. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a PubProb built on the variance s^2 in place of the standard error, which for the article's small trial (standard error 0.40) holds 0.7356 instead of 0.35, and for the large trial holds 1, where NORM.S.INV fails and IFERROR returns FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Copas publication probability inverts to the selection index" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-COPAS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "copas-publication-probability-inverts-to-the-selection-index" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IFERROR(ABS(NORM.S.INV(PubProb)-(SelGammaZero+SelGammaOne/StudyStdErr))<1E-6,FALSE)" .

hemo:7006164 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Copas publication probability of the article's large trial"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With SelGammaZero at -1.062, SelGammaOne at 0.2707 and StudyStdErr at 0.10, PubProb holds 0.95002 within 0.00001. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows gamma_1 multiplied by s instead of divided by it, which gives an index of -1.0349 and a probability of about 0.1504." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Copas publication probability of the article's large trial" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-COPAS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "copas-publication-probability-of-the-article-s-large-trial" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(AND(SelGammaZero=-1.062,SelGammaOne=0.2707,StudyStdErr=0.1),ABS(PubProb-0.95002)<0.00001,TRUE)" .

hemo:7006165 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Copas publication probability of a large trial in the moderate scenario"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's moderate scenario, gamma_0 = -1.062 and gamma_1 = 0.2707, a large trial with standard error 0.10 has index 2.707 minus 1.062, which is 1.645, and publication probability 0.95, the value the scenario was built from (illustrative inputs; Phi_u rounded to two decimals, as in the article)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Copas publication probability of a large trial in the moderate scenario" ;
    hemo:9000014 "gamma_0 = -1.062; gamma_1 = 0.2707; s = 0.10; u = 1.645; Phi_u = 0.95; P = 0.95" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-COPAS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "copas-publication-probability-of-a-large-trial-in-the-moderate-scenario" .

hemo:7006166 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Copas publication probability of a small trial in the moderate scenario"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the same selection parameters a small trial with standard error 0.40 has index 0.67675 minus 1.062, which is -0.38525, and publication probability 0.35, so about two in three such trials would stay unpublished (illustrative inputs)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Copas publication probability of a small trial in the moderate scenario" ;
    hemo:9000014 "gamma_0 = -1.062; gamma_1 = 0.2707; s = 0.40; u = -0.38525; Phi_u = 0.35; P = 0.35" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-COPAS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "copas-publication-probability-of-a-small-trial-in-the-moderate-scenario" .

hemo:7006167 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Copas publication probability at a very large standard error"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At a standard error of 1,000 the precision term is negligible, the index is -1.0617 and the probability is 0.1442, close to Phi(gamma_0) = 0.1441, the limiting case that defines gamma_0 (illustrative)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Copas publication probability at a very large standard error" ;
    hemo:9000014 "gamma_0 = -1.062; gamma_1 = 0.2707; s = 1000; u = -1.0617; Phi_u = 0.1442; P = 0.1442" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-COPAS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "copas-publication-probability-at-a-very-large-standard-error" .

hemo:7006168 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Selection parameters fixed when counting implied unpublished studies"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "M is a consequence of the assumed gamma_0 and gamma_1, not an estimate from the published studies. Huang and colleagues present it as a more interpretable translation of the two sensitivity parameters, and propose using information in clinical trial registries to estimate all the parameters of the model." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Selection parameters fixed when counting implied unpublished studies" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-COPAS-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "selection-parameters-fixed-when-counting-implied-unpublished-studies" .

hemo:7006169 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Studies in each Copas group share one standard error"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every published study in a group has the same standard error and so the same publication probability, as in the article's example with two large trials at 0.10 and ten small trials at 0.40. With distinct standard errors each study forms its own group with a count of 1." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Studies in each Copas group share one standard error" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-COPAS-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "studies-in-each-copas-group-share-one-standard-error" .

hemo:7006170 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Unpublished studies per published study in the Copas count"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "If studies of a given precision are published independently with probability P, the expected number left unpublished for each one published is (1 minus P) / P, the mean number of failures per success of a negative binomial count (derived). P must be above zero; as P falls towards zero, M grows without bound." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Unpublished studies per published study in the Copas count" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-COPAS-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "unpublished-studies-per-published-study-in-the-copas-count" .

hemo:7006171 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Copas implied unpublished studies for two standard error groups in Excel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000902 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the selection parameters in cells named GammaZeroGrp and GammaOneGrp, the group standard errors in StdErrGroupA and StdErrGroupB and the published counts in TrialsGroupA and TrialsGroupB, the five formulas fill cells named IndexGroupA, IndexGroupB, PubProbGroupA, PubProbGroupB and ExpectedUnpublished." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Copas implied unpublished studies for two standard error groups in Excel" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-COPAS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "copas-implied-unpublished-studies-for-two-standard-error-groups-in-excel" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=GammaZeroGrp+GammaOneGrp/StdErrGroupA; =GammaZeroGrp+GammaOneGrp/StdErrGroupB; =NORM.S.DIST(IndexGroupA,TRUE); =NORM.S.DIST(IndexGroupB,TRUE); =TrialsGroupA*(1-PubProbGroupA)/PubProbGroupA+TrialsGroupB*(1-PubProbGroupB)/PubProbGroupB" .

hemo:7006172 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Implied unpublished studies equal expected studies run less those published"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "ExpectedUnpublished equals n_1 / P_1 + n_2 / P_2, the expected number of studies run at each precision, less the n_1 + n_2 published, within 1E-9 (derived identity). Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows 1 / P used in place of (1 minus P) / P, which with the moderate scenario's inputs holds 30.67, the expected total including the twelve published trials, instead of 18.67." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Implied unpublished studies equal expected studies run less those published" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-COPAS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "implied-unpublished-studies-equal-expected-studies-run-less-those-published" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ExpectedUnpublished-(TrialsGroupA/PubProbGroupA+TrialsGroupB/PubProbGroupB-TrialsGroupA-TrialsGroupB))<1E-9" .

hemo:7006173 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Implied unpublished studies in the article's moderate scenario"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With GammaZeroGrp at -1.062, GammaOneGrp at 0.2707, standard errors of 0.10 and 0.40 and counts of 2 and 10, ExpectedUnpublished holds 18.6745 within 0.001 (the article's 18.68 uses probabilities of exactly 0.95 and 0.35). Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows all twelve trials given their average publication probability of 0.45, which returns 14.67." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Implied unpublished studies in the article's moderate scenario" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-COPAS-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "implied-unpublished-studies-in-the-article-s-moderate-scenario" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(AND(GammaZeroGrp=-1.062,GammaOneGrp=0.2707,StdErrGroupA=0.1,StdErrGroupB=0.4,TrialsGroupA=2,TrialsGroupB=10),ABS(ExpectedUnpublished-18.6745)<0.001,TRUE)" .

hemo:7006174 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Implied unpublished trials in the article's moderate scenario"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the moderate scenario's gamma_0 = -1.062 and gamma_1 = 0.2707, the two large trials (standard error 0.10) are published with probability 0.95 and the ten small trials (0.40) with probability 0.35. M is 0.105 + 18.571 = 18.68, the article's figure, so about 19 trials, nearly all small, would sit unpublished behind the twelve found (illustrative inputs; Phi values rounded to two decimals, as in the article)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Implied unpublished trials in the article's moderate scenario" ;
    hemo:9000014 "gamma_0 = -1.062; gamma_1 = 0.2707; s_1 = 0.10; s_2 = 0.40; n_1 = 2; n_2 = 10; u_1 = 1.645; u_2 = -0.38525; Phi_1 = 0.95; Phi_2 = 0.35; P_1 = 0.95; P_2 = 0.35; M = 18.68" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-COPAS-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "implied-unpublished-trials-in-the-article-s-moderate-scenario" .

hemo:7006175 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Implied unpublished trials in the article's severe scenario"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's severe scenario, probabilities of 0.90 and 0.15, corresponds to gamma_1 = 0.3091 and gamma_0 = -1.8091 (HE-CF-COPAS-001). M is 0.222 + 56.667 = 56.89, about 57 unpublished trials, as in the article's scenario table (illustrative inputs; Phi values rounded to two decimals)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Implied unpublished trials in the article's severe scenario" ;
    hemo:9000014 "gamma_0 = -1.8091; gamma_1 = 0.3091; s_1 = 0.10; s_2 = 0.40; n_1 = 2; n_2 = 10; u_1 = 1.2819; u_2 = -1.03635; Phi_1 = 0.90; Phi_2 = 0.15; P_1 = 0.90; P_2 = 0.15; M = 56.89" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-COPAS-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "implied-unpublished-trials-in-the-article-s-severe-scenario" .

hemo:7006176 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Implied unpublished trials with almost no selection"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With gamma_1 = 0 and gamma_0 = 3 every trial is published with probability 0.99865 whatever its size, and the twelve trials imply only 0.0162 unpublished ones. M reaches the zero of the article's no-selection row only in the limit as P reaches 1 (illustrative)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Implied unpublished trials with almost no selection" ;
    hemo:9000014 "gamma_0 = 3; gamma_1 = 0; s_1 = 0.10; s_2 = 0.40; n_1 = 2; n_2 = 10; u_1 = 3; u_2 = 3; Phi_1 = 0.99865; Phi_2 = 0.99865; P_1 = 0.99865; P_2 = 0.99865; M = 0.0162" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-COPAS-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "implied-unpublished-trials-with-almost-no-selection" .

hemo:7006177 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Pure investment model with a zero real-own rate of interest in the health stock demand"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Healthy time yields no utility directly and the cost of health capital reduces to delta because the real-own rate of interest, the interest rate r minus the rate of change in pi, is zero, the assumption Grossman states for his demand function for the stock of health. With a positive real-own rate the cost of capital is r minus pi_tilde plus delta, as in HE-FM-DFH-002, and Grossman's 1972 equation (4-2) keeps it inside the logarithm, so when r minus pi_tilde is positive a change in delta moves the cost of capital by a smaller proportion than delta itself (derived here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pure investment model with a zero real-own rate of interest in the health stock demand" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DDHC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pure-investment-model-with-a-zero-real-own-rate-of-interest-in-the-health-stock-demand" .

hemo:7006178 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant elasticity of the marginal efficiency schedule from Grossman's healthy-time function"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Grossman's healthy-time function h = 8,760 minus B H^(-C), with B and C positive constants, gives a log-linear marginal efficiency schedule whose elasticity is eps = 1/(1 + C), below 1 since C is positive, and he argues on this ground that eps is likely to be smaller than one. The formula needs only a constant positive eps. A value of 1 or more cannot come from that healthy-time function and serves as a counterfactual, as in the article's case of eps = 1.5." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant elasticity of the marginal efficiency schedule from Grossman's healthy-time function" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DDHC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-elasticity-of-the-marginal-efficiency-schedule-from-grossman-s-healthy-time-function" .

hemo:7006179 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Net investment small relative to depreciation for the gross investment ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "I_ratio treats gross investment as delta H, the identity of HE-FM-DFH-001 with the rate of net disinvestment small relative to the depreciation rate, the condition under which Grossman drops the net disinvestment term from his estimating equations. When the stock changes quickly within the period the ratio is an approximation (derived here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net investment small relative to depreciation for the gross investment ratio" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DDHC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-investment-small-relative-to-depreciation-for-the-gross-investment-ratio" .

hemo:7006180 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Health stock demand ratio and gross investment ratio from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000903 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the elasticity in MECElasticity, the wages in WageBefore and WageAfter, the marginal costs of gross investment in InvestCostBefore and InvestCostAfter and the depreciation rates in DeprRateBefore and DeprRateAfter, the first formula fills HealthStockRatio and the second GrossInvestRatio. InvestCostAfter can hold UnitCostInvest from HE-IM-HPF-003 computed at the new prices." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health stock demand ratio and gross investment ratio from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-DDHC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-stock-demand-ratio-and-gross-investment-ratio-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(WageAfter/WageBefore)^MECElasticity*(InvestCostAfter/InvestCostBefore)^(-MECElasticity)*(DeprRateAfter/DeprRateBefore)^(-MECElasticity); =(DeprRateAfter/DeprRateBefore)*HealthStockRatio" .

hemo:7006181 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Health stock ratio cell gives 0.707 when depreciation doubles with eps of 0.5"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With MECElasticity 0.5, WageBefore and WageAfter 10, InvestCostBefore and InvestCostAfter 40, DeprRateBefore 0.02 and DeprRateAfter 0.04, HealthStockRatio equals 1 over the square root of 2, the article's 0.707. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the sign of the depreciation exponent reversed, which gives 1.4142, or eps applied to the percentage change, 1 minus 0.5 times 100%, which gives 0.5, and a doubled cell (1.4142) or a halved cell (0.3536) also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health stock ratio cell gives 0.707 when depreciation doubles with eps of 0.5" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DDHC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-stock-ratio-cell-gives-0-707-when-depreciation-doubles-with-eps-of-0-5" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(HealthStockRatio-1/SQRT(2))<1E-9" .

hemo:7006182 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Gross investment ratio cell gives 1.414 when depreciation doubles with eps of 0.5"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of HE-VT-DDHC-001, GrossInvestRatio equals the square root of 2, twice the depreciation rate on a stock 1 over the square root of 2 as large, the article's 1.414. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the depreciation ratio left out, so that the cell repeats HealthStockRatio at 0.7071, and a doubled cell (2.8284) or a halved cell (0.7071) also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Gross investment ratio cell gives 1.414 when depreciation doubles with eps of 0.5" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DDHC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "gross-investment-ratio-cell-gives-1-414-when-depreciation-doubles-with-eps-of-0-5" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(GrossInvestRatio-SQRT(2))<1E-9" .

hemo:7006183 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Gross investment ratio cell equals 1 when eps is 1 and depreciation doubles"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With MECElasticity 1 and the other inputs of HE-VT-DDHC-001, the stock halves and GrossInvestRatio equals 1, the limiting case in which the depreciation elasticity of gross investment, 1 minus eps, is zero. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the depreciation ratio left out, which gives 0.5, and a doubled cell (2) or a halved cell (0.5) also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Gross investment ratio cell equals 1 when eps is 1 and depreciation doubles" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DDHC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "gross-investment-ratio-cell-equals-1-when-eps-is-1-and-depreciation-doubles" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(GrossInvestRatio-1)<1E-9" .

hemo:7006184 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Health stock ratio after a wage rise equals the wage ratio to the power (1-K) eps"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With MECElasticity 0.5, WageBefore 10, WageAfter 11, InvestCostBefore 40, InvestCostAfter holding UnitCostInvest of HE-IM-HPF-003 at PriceCare 40, Wage 11, TimeShare 0.5 and SchoolingYears 0 (41.95235, or 41.9524 typed to four decimals, which the tolerance of 1E-5 allows), and DeprRateBefore and DeprRateAfter both 0.04, HealthStockRatio equals 1.1 to the power 0.25, Grossman's wage elasticity of health (1-K) eps applied to the wage ratio. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows InvestCostAfter left at 40, so that the rise in the cost of own time is ignored, which gives 1.0488, and a doubled cell (2.0482) or a halved cell (0.5121) also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health stock ratio after a wage rise equals the wage ratio to the power (1-K) eps" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DDHC-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-stock-ratio-after-a-wage-rise-equals-the-wage-ratio-to-the-power-1-k-eps" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(HealthStockRatio-1.1^0.25)<1E-5" .

hemo:7006185 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Doubling of depreciation with eps of 0.5 in the derived demand worked example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative person has eps of 0.5, an unchanged wage and marginal cost of gross investment (10 and 40 here, illustrative) and a depreciation rate that doubles, 0.02 a year before and 0.04 after. The stock demanded falls to 1 over the square root of 2, about 29% less, while gross investment rises by about 41%, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Doubling of depreciation with eps of 0.5 in the derived demand worked example" ;
    hemo:9000014 "eps = 0.5; W_0 = 10; W_1 = 10; pi_0 = 40; pi_1 = 40; delta_0 = 0.02; delta_1 = 0.04; H_ratio = 0.7071; I_ratio = 1.4142" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DDHC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "doubling-of-depreciation-with-eps-of-0-5-in-the-derived-demand-worked-example" .

hemo:7006186 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Doubling of depreciation with eps of 1.5 in the derived demand worked example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's counterfactual eps of 1.5 and the same doubling of depreciation the stock demanded falls to 2 to the power -1.5, 0.3536, and gross investment to 0.7071, so investment falls by about 29%, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Doubling of depreciation with eps of 1.5 in the derived demand worked example" ;
    hemo:9000014 "eps = 1.5; W_0 = 10; W_1 = 10; pi_0 = 40; pi_1 = 40; delta_0 = 0.02; delta_1 = 0.04; H_ratio = 0.3536; I_ratio = 0.7071" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DDHC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "doubling-of-depreciation-with-eps-of-1-5-in-the-derived-demand-worked-example" .

hemo:7006187 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Unit MEC elasticity leaves gross investment unchanged when depreciation doubles"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With eps of 1 the stock demanded halves when depreciation doubles, so gross investment, the doubled rate times the halved stock, is unchanged, the limiting case between the article's two examples (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Unit MEC elasticity leaves gross investment unchanged when depreciation doubles" ;
    hemo:9000014 "eps = 1; W_0 = 10; W_1 = 10; pi_0 = 40; pi_1 = 40; delta_0 = 0.02; delta_1 = 0.04; H_ratio = 0.5; I_ratio = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DDHC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "unit-mec-elasticity-leaves-gross-investment-unchanged-when-depreciation-doubles" .

hemo:7006188 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Ten per cent wage rise with the marginal cost of gross investment taken from the unit cost record"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An illustrative wage rise to 11 from 10, with a price of care of 40, K of 0.5 and schooling at the reference level, raises the unit cost of HE-FM-HPF-003 to 41.9524 from 40. With eps of 0.5 and depreciation unchanged at 0.04 the stock demanded rises by 2.41%, which is 1.1 to the power (1-K) eps = 0.25, Grossman's wage elasticity of health, and gross investment rises in the same proportion (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ten per cent wage rise with the marginal cost of gross investment taken from the unit cost record" ;
    hemo:9000014 "eps = 0.5; W_0 = 10; W_1 = 11; pi_0 = 40; pi_1 = 41.9524; delta_0 = 0.04; delta_1 = 0.04; H_ratio = 1.0241; I_ratio = 1.0241" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DDHC-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ten-per-cent-wage-rise-with-the-marginal-cost-of-gross-investment-taken-from-the-unit-cost-record" .

hemo:7006189 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Cobb-Douglas gross investment with a unit elasticity of substitution in the derived demand for care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gross investment has the Cobb-Douglas form of HE-FM-HPF-001, so the elasticity of substitution between care and own time is one and K is constant. Grossman states that with an elasticity of substitution other than one the term K in the wage and price elasticities of care would be multiplied by it, and that K would then depend on W and P, so the coefficients would not be constant." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cobb-Douglas gross investment with a unit elasticity of substitution in the derived demand for care" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DDHC-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cobb-douglas-gross-investment-with-a-unit-elasticity-of-substitution-in-the-derived-demand-for-care" .

hemo:7006190 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Pure investment model, zero real-own rate and small net disinvestment in the derived demand for care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The formula keeps the conditions of HE-FM-DDHC-001, the pure investment model and a zero real-own rate of interest, and leaves out the term in the rate of net disinvestment, which Grossman states could be ignored if net disinvestment were small relative to the depreciation rate. The depreciation ratio covers both a rise with age and a difference in initial depreciation between people of the same age." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pure investment model, zero real-own rate and small net disinvestment in the derived demand for care" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DDHC-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pure-investment-model-zero-real-own-rate-and-small-net-disinvestment-in-the-derived-demand-for-care" .

hemo:7006191 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Money price of care and two inputs in the derived demand for care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Care has a money price P and medical care and own time are the only inputs. Grossman shows that where the money price is zero and care is rationed by time, so that its price is the wage times the hours needed, and a third input needs no time, the wage elasticity of care becomes (1-K)(eps minus sigma_MX), with K reinterpreted as the share of care and own time together in the cost of gross investment and sigma_MX the partial elasticity of substitution between care and that input, and can be zero or negative. This formula does not cover that case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Money price of care and two inputs in the derived demand for care" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DDHC-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "money-price-of-care-and-two-inputs-in-the-derived-demand-for-care" .

hemo:7006192 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Factor-neutral schooling in the derived demand for care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "One more year of schooling raises the marginal products of care and own time by the same rho_H, Grossman's factor-neutrality assumption, so it leaves the least-cost mix unchanged and changes care only through the health stock demanded and the inputs needed per unit of gross investment." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Factor-neutral schooling in the derived demand for care" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DDHC-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "factor-neutral-schooling-in-the-derived-demand-for-care" .

hemo:7006193 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Elasticities and ratio of the derived demand for medical care from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000904 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With MECElasticity, WageBefore, WageAfter, DeprRateBefore and DeprRateAfter as in HE-IM-DDHC-001, the time share in TimeShareCost, the schooling parameter in SchoolingEffect, the years of schooling in SchoolingBefore and SchoolingAfter and the prices of care in PriceCareBefore and PriceCareAfter, the seven formulas fill CareWageElast, CarePriceScale, CarePriceSubst, CarePriceElast, CareSchoolingEffect, CareDeprElast and CareDemandRatio." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Elasticities and ratio of the derived demand for medical care from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-DDHC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "elasticities-and-ratio-of-the-derived-demand-for-medical-care-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(1-TimeShareCost)*MECElasticity+TimeShareCost; =-(1-TimeShareCost)*MECElasticity; =-TimeShareCost; =CarePriceScale+CarePriceSubst; =SchoolingEffect*(MECElasticity-1); =1-MECElasticity; =EXP(CareWageElast*LN(WageAfter/WageBefore)+CarePriceElast*LN(PriceCareAfter/PriceCareBefore)+CareSchoolingEffect*(SchoolingAfter-SchoolingBefore)+CareDeprElast*LN(DeprRateAfter/DeprRateBefore))" .

hemo:7006194 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Care demand ratio cell gives 1.414 when depreciation doubles with eps of 0.5"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With MECElasticity 0.5, TimeShareCost 0.4, SchoolingEffect 0.06, wages 10, prices of care 40, schooling unchanged and DeprRateBefore 0.02 and DeprRateAfter 0.04, CareDemandRatio equals the square root of 2, the article's 1.414 and the gross investment ratio of HE-VT-DDHC-002. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows minus eps, the depreciation coefficient of the health stock, used in place of 1 minus eps, which gives 0.7071, and a doubled cell (2.8284) or a halved cell (0.7071) also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Care demand ratio cell gives 1.414 when depreciation doubles with eps of 0.5" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DDHC-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "care-demand-ratio-cell-gives-1-414-when-depreciation-doubles-with-eps-of-0-5" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(CareDemandRatio-SQRT(2))<1E-9" .

hemo:7006195 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Price elasticity cell of care equals minus 0.7 with eps of 0.5 and K of 0.4"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With MECElasticity 0.5 and TimeShareCost 0.4, CarePriceElast equals the hand value minus (0.6 times 0.5 plus 0.4), -0.7, as in the article. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the substitution part left out, which gives -0.3, or K and 1-K swapped, which gives -0.8, and a doubled cell (-1.4) or a halved cell (-0.35) also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Price elasticity cell of care equals minus 0.7 with eps of 0.5 and K of 0.4" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DDHC-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "price-elasticity-cell-of-care-equals-minus-0-7-with-eps-of-0-5-and-k-of-0-4" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(CarePriceElast+0.7)<1E-9" .

hemo:7006196 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Care demand ratio cell gives exp(-0.03) for one more year of schooling"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With MECElasticity 0.5, TimeShareCost 0.4, SchoolingEffect 0.06, SchoolingBefore 0, SchoolingAfter 1 and wages, prices and depreciation rates unchanged, CareDemandRatio equals exp(-0.03), 0.9704. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the schooling effect on health, rho_H eps, used for care, which gives 1.0305, and a doubled cell (1.9409) or a halved cell (0.4852) also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Care demand ratio cell gives exp(-0.03) for one more year of schooling" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DDHC-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "care-demand-ratio-cell-gives-exp-0-03-for-one-more-year-of-schooling" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(CareDemandRatio-EXP(-0.03))<1E-9" .

hemo:7006197 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Care demand ratio cell equals 1 when eps is 1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With MECElasticity 1, depreciation doubled, SchoolingAfter one year above SchoolingBefore and the wage and price of care unchanged, CareDemandRatio equals 1, the limiting case in which the schooling and depreciation coefficients of care are zero. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the depreciation elasticity written as eps in place of 1 minus eps, which gives 2, and a doubled cell (2) or a halved cell (0.5) also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Care demand ratio cell equals 1 when eps is 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DDHC-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "care-demand-ratio-cell-equals-1-when-eps-is-1" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(CareDemandRatio-1)<1E-9" .

hemo:7006198 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Doubling of depreciation with eps of 0.5 in the derived demand for care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's person with eps of 0.5, K of 0.4 and rho_H of 0.06 faces a doubling of depreciation, 0.02 a year before and 0.04 after, with the wage, the price of care (10 and 40 here, illustrative) and schooling unchanged. Care rises by the depreciation elasticity 0.5 times ln 2, 0.347, a ratio of 1.4142, the same 41% as gross investment in HE-EX-DDHC-001. The price elasticity is -0.7, made up of -0.3 through the lower health stock and -0.4 from substitution towards own time, and the schooling effect is -0.03 a year, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Doubling of depreciation with eps of 0.5 in the derived demand for care" ;
    hemo:9000014 "eps = 0.5; K = 0.4; rho_H = 0.06; W_0 = 10; W_1 = 10; P_0 = 40; P_1 = 40; E_0 = 0; E_1 = 0; delta_0 = 0.02; delta_1 = 0.04; e_MW = 0.7; e_MP_scale = -0.3; e_MP_subst = -0.4; e_MP = -0.7; s_ME = -0.03; e_Mdelta = 0.5; M_ratio = 1.4142" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DDHC-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "doubling-of-depreciation-with-eps-of-0-5-in-the-derived-demand-for-care" .

hemo:7006199 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "One more year of schooling in the derived demand for care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With eps of 0.5 and rho_H of 0.06 one more year of schooling changes care by rho_H (eps minus 1) = -0.03, the article's fall of 3% in log terms. The exact ratio is exp(-0.03) = 0.9704, a fall of 2.96%, while the health stock demanded rises by rho_H eps = 0.03 (computed here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One more year of schooling in the derived demand for care" ;
    hemo:9000014 "eps = 0.5; K = 0.4; rho_H = 0.06; W_0 = 10; W_1 = 10; P_0 = 40; P_1 = 40; E_0 = 0; E_1 = 1; delta_0 = 0.02; delta_1 = 0.02; e_MW = 0.7; e_MP_scale = -0.3; e_MP_subst = -0.4; e_MP = -0.7; s_ME = -0.03; e_Mdelta = 0.5; M_ratio = 0.9704" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DDHC-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-more-year-of-schooling-in-the-derived-demand-for-care" .

hemo:7006200 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Doubling of depreciation with eps of 1.5 in the derived demand for care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's counterfactual eps of 1.5 the depreciation elasticity of care is -0.5, so doubling depreciation cuts care to 0.7071, a fall of about 29%, as in the article. The price elasticity is -1.3, of which -0.9 is the scale part, and the schooling effect turns positive at 0.03 a year (computed here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Doubling of depreciation with eps of 1.5 in the derived demand for care" ;
    hemo:9000014 "eps = 1.5; K = 0.4; rho_H = 0.06; W_0 = 10; W_1 = 10; P_0 = 40; P_1 = 40; E_0 = 0; E_1 = 0; delta_0 = 0.02; delta_1 = 0.04; e_MW = 1.3; e_MP_scale = -0.9; e_MP_subst = -0.4; e_MP = -1.3; s_ME = 0.03; e_Mdelta = -0.5; M_ratio = 0.7071" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DDHC-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "doubling-of-depreciation-with-eps-of-1-5-in-the-derived-demand-for-care" .

hemo:7006201 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Ten per cent rise in the price of care in the derived demand for care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With eps of 0.5 and K of 0.4 an illustrative rise in the price of care to 44 from 40, with nothing else changed, gives 1.1 to the power -0.7, a ratio of 0.9355, a fall of 6.45% in care demanded (computed here for illustration). This is HE-FM-XPE-003 with own-price elasticity -0.7." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ten per cent rise in the price of care in the derived demand for care" ;
    hemo:9000014 "eps = 0.5; K = 0.4; rho_H = 0.06; W_0 = 10; W_1 = 10; P_0 = 40; P_1 = 44; E_0 = 0; E_1 = 0; delta_0 = 0.02; delta_1 = 0.02; e_MW = 0.7; e_MP_scale = -0.3; e_MP_subst = -0.4; e_MP = -0.7; s_ME = -0.03; e_Mdelta = 0.5; M_ratio = 0.9355" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DDHC-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ten-per-cent-rise-in-the-price-of-care-in-the-derived-demand-for-care" .

hemo:7006202 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Unit MEC elasticity in the derived demand for care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With eps of 1 the schooling and depreciation effects on care vanish and the price elasticity is -1, so a doubling of depreciation together with one more year of schooling leaves care unchanged, a limiting case that checks an implementation (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Unit MEC elasticity in the derived demand for care" ;
    hemo:9000014 "eps = 1; K = 0.4; rho_H = 0.06; W_0 = 10; W_1 = 10; P_0 = 40; P_1 = 40; E_0 = 0; E_1 = 1; delta_0 = 0.02; delta_1 = 0.04; e_MW = 1; e_MP_scale = -0.6; e_MP_subst = -0.4; e_MP = -1; s_ME = 0; e_Mdelta = 0; M_ratio = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DDHC-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "unit-mec-elasticity-in-the-derived-demand-for-care" .

hemo:7006203 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Net receivables and net revenue on the same basis in days in accounts receivable"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "AR and R are both stated net, as in HFMA FM-1, which takes the net patient receivable on the balance sheet over average daily net patient service revenue. Gross receivables go over gross revenue and net over net, never one mixed with the other, because gross figures are valued at list charges that most payers do not pay in full." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net receivables and net revenue on the same basis in days in accounts receivable" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DAR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-receivables-and-net-revenue-on-the-same-basis-in-days-in-accounts-receivable" .

hemo:7006204 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Most recent three months and actual calendar days in the days in accounts receivable denominator"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "For FM-1, r_bar is the daily average of net patient service revenue over the most recent three months, using the actual number of calendar days in those months, so the three months ending 30 June use 91 days. A period of another length gives a figure that is not comparable with the HFMA measure." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Most recent three months and actual calendar days in the days in accounts receivable denominator" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DAR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "most-recent-three-months-and-actual-calendar-days-in-the-days-in-accounts-receivable-denominator" .

hemo:7006205 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Revenue earned claim by claim for days in accounts receivable"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The formula assumes revenue that turns into receivables claim by claim, under fee-for-service or case-based payment. HFMA excludes capitation and premium revenue related to value-based or risk-based payer contracts from both the receivables and the revenue. Where a provider is paid fixed sums on account, as an NHS trust is paid one twelfth of its expected annual contract value each month under the NHS Standard Contract unless another proportion is agreed, the figure reflects contract timing rather than collection." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Revenue earned claim by claim for days in accounts receivable" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DAR-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "revenue-earned-claim-by-claim-for-days-in-accounts-receivable" .

hemo:7006206 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Steady daily revenue when days in accounts receivable are read as a collection period"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "DAR equals the time a unit of revenue waits for cash only when daily revenue is steady, the steady-state reading of Little's law in HE-FM-DES-003 (derived). With every dollar collected exactly 55 days after it is earned, the FM-1 figure is 55.0 at constant revenue and 56.96 when daily revenue grows by 0.2% a day (computed here for illustration), because the trailing three-month average understates current daily revenue. Growth raises the figure only while the collection lag is shorter than the 91-day window, and with every dollar collected after 120 days the same growth gives 116.69 (derived). HFMA describes FM-1 as a trending indicator." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Steady daily revenue when days in accounts receivable are read as a collection period" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DAR-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "steady-daily-revenue-when-days-in-accounts-receivable-are-read-as-a-collection-period" .

hemo:7006207 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Net days in accounts receivable from named receivables, revenue and days"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000905 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With net patient receivables in a cell named NetReceivables, net patient service revenue for the period in NetPatientRevenue and its calendar days in DaysInPeriod, the first formula returns r_bar in a cell named AvgDailyNetRevenue and the second returns DAR in a cell named NetDaysInAR. For the article's quarter the cells hold 55.0, 91.0 and 91 and return 1.0 and 55.0." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net days in accounts receivable from named receivables, revenue and days" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-DAR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-days-in-accounts-receivable-from-named-receivables-revenue-and-days" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=NetPatientRevenue/DaysInPeriod; =NetReceivables/AvgDailyNetRevenue" .

hemo:7006208 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Net days in accounts receivable cell matches the receivables turnover route"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "NetDaysInAR is compared with the period's days divided by receivables turnover, revenue over receivables, a route that does not use the AvgDailyNetRevenue cell. Expected result TRUE. A NetDaysInAR cell written with a fixed 90 days in place of DaysInPeriod returns 54.40 against 55.0 for the article's quarter and gives FALSE, a cell written upside down as AvgDailyNetRevenue/NetReceivables returns 0.018 and gives FALSE, and doubling or halving the cell also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net days in accounts receivable cell matches the receivables turnover route" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DAR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-days-in-accounts-receivable-cell-matches-the-receivables-turnover-route" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(NetDaysInAR-DaysInPeriod/(NetPatientRevenue/NetReceivables))<1E-9" .

hemo:7006209 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Net days in accounts receivable cell equals the revenue-weighted mean of payer group days"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's three payer groups in cells named DailyRevPublic, DailyRevCommercial and DailyRevPatient (0.50, 0.30 and 0.20) and GroupDaysPublic, GroupDaysCommercial and GroupDaysPatient (35, 60 and 97.5), and NetDaysInAR computed from the totals of 55.0 and 91.0 over 91 days, the cell is compared with the revenue-weighted mean of the group days, the identity of HE-FM-CRAT-001. Expected result TRUE. An unweighted mean of the group days, 64.17, gives FALSE, as do a doubled cell of 110 and a halved cell of 27.5." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net days in accounts receivable cell equals the revenue-weighted mean of payer group days" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DAR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-days-in-accounts-receivable-cell-equals-the-revenue-weighted-mean-of-payer-group-days" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(NetDaysInAR-(DailyRevPublic*GroupDaysPublic+DailyRevCommercial*GroupDaysCommercial+DailyRevPatient*GroupDaysPatient)/(DailyRevPublic+DailyRevCommercial+DailyRevPatient))<1E-9" .

hemo:7006210 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Days in accounts receivable for the article's hospital quarter"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative hospital, paid mainly per claim, earns net patient service revenue of USD 91.0 million in the quarter April to June (91 days) and holds net patient receivables of USD 55.0 million at 30 June. Average daily net revenue is USD 1.0 million, so the hospital holds 55.0 days of net revenue as receivables. Money is in USD millions." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Days in accounts receivable for the article's hospital quarter" ;
    hemo:9000014 "AR = 55; R = 91; D = 91; r_bar = 1; DAR = 55" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DAR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "days-in-accounts-receivable-for-the-article-s-hospital-quarter" .

hemo:7006211 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Days in accounts receivable of the patient balance payer group"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Applied to the patient balance group of the article's table, with daily net revenue of USD 0.20 million, or USD 18.2 million over the 91 days, and USD 19.5 million in receivables, the group's own figure is 97.5 days. The public payer group (17.5 over 0.50) gives 35.0 days and commercial insurers (18.0 over 0.30) 60.0 days in the same way. Money is in USD millions." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Days in accounts receivable of the patient balance payer group" ;
    hemo:9000014 "AR = 19.5; R = 18.2; D = 91; r_bar = 0.2; DAR = 97.5" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DAR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "days-in-accounts-receivable-of-the-patient-balance-payer-group" .

hemo:7006212 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Days in accounts receivable after a shift in payer mix alone"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's step 3 the public payer's share of the USD 1.0 million daily revenue falls to 40% and the patient share rises to 30%, with every group collecting exactly as fast as before. Group receivables become 14.0, 18.0 and 29.25, USD 61.25 million in all, and the overall figure rises to 61.25 days with no change in billing, the revenue-weighted mean of the unchanged group days, the weighted-mean algebra of HE-FM-CRAT-001 with daily revenue in place of member numbers. Money is in USD millions." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Days in accounts receivable after a shift in payer mix alone" ;
    hemo:9000014 "AR = 61.25; R = 91; D = 91; r_bar = 1; DAR = 61.25" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DAR-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "days-in-accounts-receivable-after-a-shift-in-payer-mix-alone" .

hemo:7006213 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Days in accounts receivable after collection shortens to 45 days"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's step 4 better coding and fewer denials bring net days down to 45 at the same revenue, so receivables fall to USD 45.0 million and USD 10.0 million of cash is released, r_bar times the fall of 10 days. At an illustrative 6% a year the annual saving in carrying cost is USD 0.6 million, i times the released balance, the same rate-times-balance product as R_t = r * B_t in HE-CF-ANLS-002; alternatively, held as cash with daily cash operating expenses of USD 0.95 million, it adds about 10.5 days cash on hand. The two uses are alternatives, not additions. Money is in USD millions." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Days in accounts receivable after collection shortens to 45 days" ;
    hemo:9000014 "AR = 45; R = 91; D = 91; r_bar = 1; DAR = 45" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DAR-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "days-in-accounts-receivable-after-collection-shortens-to-45-days" .

hemo:7006214 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Reflectively measured constructs for the HTMT ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Henseler and colleagues state that the HTMT-based criteria assume reflectively measured constructs, in which each indicator reflects its construct, and that for formative indicators neither the within-construct nor the between-construct correlations indicate discriminant validity." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Reflectively measured constructs for the HTMT ratio" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DISV-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "reflectively-measured-constructs-for-the-htmt-ratio" .

hemo:7006215 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Indicator scores coded in one direction before the HTMT ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Eq. 6 averages the correlations as they stand, so every indicator is coded in the same direction, as in the article's example where higher scores mean better health throughout. A reverse-coded indicator turns its correlations negative, and the product under the square root can then be negative (derived here; see HE-ER-DISV-001)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Indicator scores coded in one direction before the HTMT ratio" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DISV-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "indicator-scores-coded-in-one-direction-before-the-htmt-ratio" .

hemo:7006216 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Correlations from the same respondents for the HTMT ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Henseler and colleagues note that the multitrait-multimethod approach from which the ratio is derived requires the constructs to be measured on the same respondents, so all six correlations come from one sample, such as the 200 patients of the article's example." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Correlations from the same respondents for the HTMT ratio" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DISV-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "correlations-from-the-same-respondents-for-the-htmt-ratio" .

hemo:7006217 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Cut-off rule and ties for the HTMT ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The authors conclude a lack of discriminant validity when the ratio is higher than the cut-off, so under that wording a value equal to 0.85 or 0.90 is not flagged. They call the 0.85 criterion the most conservative and regard a more liberal one, such as 0.90, as warranted for constructs that are conceptually different but hard to distinguish empirically. The cut-off is fixed before the data are seen, in line with the article's prespecified hypotheses." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cut-off rule and ties for the HTMT ratio" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-DISV-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cut-off-rule-and-ties-for-the-htmt-ratio" .

hemo:7006218 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "HTMT ratio and cut-off flags from six named correlations"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000906 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the four between-construct correlations in cells named HeteroCorrAA, HeteroCorrAB, HeteroCorrBA and HeteroCorrBB (the first letter for the indicator of construct i and the second for that of construct j, A the first indicator and B the second) and the within-construct correlations in MonoCorrI and MonoCorrJ, the formulas return rbar_ij in a cell named MeanHeteroCorr, HTMT_ij in HTMTRatio, and TRUE in FlagStrictCutOff or FlagLiberalCutOff when the ratio is above 0.85 or above 0.90." ;
    hemo:9000009 "HTMT ratio and cut-off flags from six named correlations" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-DISV-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "htmt-ratio-and-cut-off-flags-from-six-named-correlations" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(HeteroCorrAA+HeteroCorrAB+HeteroCorrBA+HeteroCorrBB)/4; =MeanHeteroCorr/SQRT(MonoCorrI*MonoCorrJ); =HTMTRatio>0.85; =HTMTRatio>0.9" .

hemo:7006219 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "HTMT ratio cell reproduces the article's 0.494"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's inputs, HTMTRatio is compared with the article's value of 0.494 at a tolerance of 0.0005. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a denominator taken as the arithmetic mean of 0.64 and 0.52, which gives 0.4914, or the square root left out, which gives 0.8564, and doubling the cell (0.9881) or halving it (0.2470) also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "HTMT ratio cell reproduces the article's 0.494" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DISV-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "htmt-ratio-cell-reproduces-the-article-s-0-494" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(HTMTRatio-0.494)<0.0005" .

hemo:7006220 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "HTMT ratio cell satisfies the squared identity with its inputs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "HTMTRatio squared times MonoCorrI times MonoCorrJ is compared with the square of the mean of the four between-construct inputs, computed again from the input cells, and the sign of HTMTRatio with the sign of their sum. Expected result TRUE. With the article's inputs a cell that divides by the arithmetic mean of the within-construct correlations (0.4914) gives FALSE, as do a doubled cell (0.9881), a halved cell (0.2470) and a cell with its sign reversed (-0.494)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "HTMT ratio cell satisfies the squared identity with its inputs" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-DISV-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "htmt-ratio-cell-satisfies-the-squared-identity-with-its-inputs" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(HTMTRatio^2*MonoCorrI*MonoCorrJ-((HeteroCorrAA+HeteroCorrAB+HeteroCorrBA+HeteroCorrBB)/4)^2)<1E-9,SIGN(HTMTRatio)=SIGN(HeteroCorrAA+HeteroCorrAB+HeteroCorrBA+HeteroCorrBB))" .

hemo:7006221 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's physical functioning and mood example of the HTMT ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative correlation matrix for 200 patients treats the questionnaire and the clinician rating as two indicators of physical functioning (construct i) and of mood (construct j). The four between-trait correlations 0.45, 0.18, 0.21 and 0.30 average 0.285, the within-trait correlations 0.64 and 0.52 have a geometric mean of 0.5769, and the ratio is 0.494, far below 0.85, as in the article. All inputs are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's physical functioning and mood example of the HTMT ratio" ;
    hemo:9000014 "r_i1j1 = 0.45; r_i1j2 = 0.18; r_i2j1 = 0.21; r_i2j2 = 0.30; r_i1i2 = 0.64; r_j1j2 = 0.52; rbar_ij = 0.285; HTMT_ij = 0.494" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DISV-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-physical-functioning-and-mood-example-of-the-htmt-ratio" .

hemo:7006222 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal correlations give an HTMT ratio of 1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With all six correlations set to 0.60 the indicators correlate as strongly across the constructs as within them, so rbar_ij is 0.60 and HTMT_ij is 1, the limiting case in which the indicators give no evidence that the constructs differ (illustrative inputs)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equal correlations give an HTMT ratio of 1" ;
    hemo:9000014 "r_i1j1 = 0.6; r_i1j2 = 0.6; r_i2j1 = 0.6; r_i2j2 = 0.6; r_i1i2 = 0.6; r_j1j2 = 0.6; rbar_ij = 0.6; HTMT_ij = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DISV-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equal-correlations-give-an-htmt-ratio-of-1" .

hemo:7006223 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "HTMT ratio that lies above one cut-off and below the other"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative between-construct correlations of 0.62, 0.58, 0.60 and 0.64 with within-construct correlations of 0.70 for each construct give rbar_ij of 0.61 and HTMT_ij of 0.8714, which is flagged under the 0.85 cut-off and not under 0.90, so the conclusion depends on the cut-off fixed in advance (computed here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "HTMT ratio that lies above one cut-off and below the other" ;
    hemo:9000014 "r_i1j1 = 0.62; r_i1j2 = 0.58; r_i2j1 = 0.60; r_i2j2 = 0.64; r_i1i2 = 0.70; r_j1j2 = 0.70; rbar_ij = 0.61; HTMT_ij = 0.8714" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-DISV-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "htmt-ratio-that-lies-above-one-cut-off-and-below-the-other" .

hemo:7006224 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant output elasticities in the Cobb-Douglas hospital production function"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each output elasticity is a constant that does not depend on how much of each input is used, Sielska and Nojszewska's equation (10), so RTS is the same for a small and a large provider, and the elasticity of substitution is fixed at 1 (their equation (13); HE-AS-HPF-001 states the same for the household function). A provider whose returns to scale differ with its size cannot be described by one set of Cobb-Douglas elasticities. A more flexible form, such as the translog function that Sielska and Nojszewska also estimated, is needed for that." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant output elasticities in the Cobb-Douglas hospital production function" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HCPF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-output-elasticities-in-the-cobb-douglas-hospital-production-function" .

hemo:7006225 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Positive inputs and elasticities with technology held fixed in the hospital scale formula"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "L, K, A and s are above zero, and alpha and beta are above zero, the condition Sielska and Nojszewska give for positive marginal products. The scale change moves the provider along one function with A unchanged; a change in technology or organisation shifts the whole function, as the article describes, and appears as a change in A rather than in RTS." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Positive inputs and elasticities with technology held fixed in the hospital scale formula" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HCPF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "positive-inputs-and-elasticities-with-technology-held-fixed-in-the-hospital-scale-formula" .

hemo:7006226 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Returns to scale measured over the inputs included in the estimated hospital function"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "RTS describes the response of activity to the inputs that appear in the function. Sielska and Nojszewska estimated two-factor and three-factor functions, each using some of beds, doctors, nurses, materials, electricity and outsourced services. A sum below 1 for two included inputs therefore describes scaling those two, not every resource the hospital uses (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Returns to scale measured over the inputs included in the estimated hospital function" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HCPF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "returns-to-scale-measured-over-the-inputs-included-in-the-estimated-hospital-function" .

hemo:7006227 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Cobb-Douglas hospital output and returns to scale from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000907 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the productivity parameter in CDProductivity, the inputs in LabourInput and CapitalInput, the elasticities in LabourElasticity and CapitalElasticity and the scale factor in ScaleFactor, the four formulas fill CDOutput, ReturnsToScale, ScaledOutput and ScaleOutputRatio in turn. ScaledOutput recomputes output from the scaled inputs, so ScaleOutputRatio does not use ReturnsToScale." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cobb-Douglas hospital output and returns to scale from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-HCPF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cobb-douglas-hospital-output-and-returns-to-scale-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CDProductivity*LabourInput^LabourElasticity*CapitalInput^CapitalElasticity; =LabourElasticity+CapitalElasticity; =CDProductivity*(ScaleFactor*LabourInput)^LabourElasticity*(ScaleFactor*CapitalInput)^CapitalElasticity; =ScaledOutput/CDOutput" .

hemo:7006228 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Scaled output ratio equals the scale factor to the power of the summed elasticities"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compares ScaleOutputRatio, which the implementation forms from the scaled inputs, with ScaleFactor raised to the sum of LabourElasticity and CapitalElasticity. Expected result TRUE for any positive inputs, elasticities and scale factor. FALSE shows only labour scaled, which with the inputs of HE-EX-HCPF-001 gives 1.001992 against 1.006789, or the scale factor applied to output instead of to the inputs, which gives 1.01." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Scaled output ratio equals the scale factor to the power of the summed elasticities" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HCPF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "scaled-output-ratio-equals-the-scale-factor-to-the-power-of-the-summed-elasticities" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ScaleOutputRatio-ScaleFactor^(LabourElasticity+CapitalElasticity))<1E-9" .

hemo:7006229 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Sielska and Nojszewska elasticities give returns to scale of 0.68 in the hospital function"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With LabourElasticity 0.2, CapitalElasticity 0.48 and ScaleFactor 1.01, ReturnsToScale is 0.68 and ScaleOutputRatio is 1.006789, a 0.68% rise in patient-days for a 1% rise in both inputs. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the elasticities multiplied instead of added, which gives 0.096, or the scale factor entered as 0.01 for 1%, which gives a ratio of 0.0437." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Sielska and Nojszewska elasticities give returns to scale of 0.68 in the hospital function" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HCPF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "sielska-and-nojszewska-elasticities-give-returns-to-scale-of-0-68-in-the-hospital-function" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(ReturnsToScale-0.68)<1E-9,ABS(ScaleOutputRatio-1.006789)<5E-7)" .

hemo:7006230 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant returns limiting case reproduces 20 units and doubles to 40 in the hospital function"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With CDProductivity 1, LabourInput 10, CapitalInput 40, both elasticities 0.5 and ScaleFactor 2, CDOutput equals the square root of 10 times 40, 20 units, the value of HE-EX-HPF-001, and ScaledOutput equals 40. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the exponents left out, which gives a CDOutput of 400, or only labour scaled, which gives a ScaledOutput of 28.28." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant returns limiting case reproduces 20 units and doubles to 40 in the hospital function" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HCPF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-returns-limiting-case-reproduces-20-units-and-doubles-to-40-in-the-hospital-function" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(CDOutput-SQRT(10*40))<1E-9,ABS(ScaledOutput-2*SQRT(10*40))<1E-9)" .

hemo:7006231 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Sielska and Nojszewska elasticities with a 1% rise in nurses and electricity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the output elasticities that Sielska and Nojszewska estimated for 94 Polish county hospitals, 0.2 for nurse full-time equivalents and 0.48 for electricity costs, RTS is 0.68, so a 1% rise in both inputs raises patient-days by 0.68%, less than 1%, as the article states. The productivity parameter of 2,000, the 100 nurse FTEs and the electricity input of 500 are illustrative and not taken from the study, and g_s does not depend on them." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Sielska and Nojszewska elasticities with a 1% rise in nurses and electricity" ;
    hemo:9000014 "A = 2000; L = 100; K = 500; alpha = 0.2; beta = 0.48; s = 1.01; Q = 99205; RTS = 0.68; Q_s = 99879; g_s = 1.006789" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HCPF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "sielska-and-nojszewska-elasticities-with-a-1-rise-in-nurses-and-electricity" .

hemo:7006232 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Doubling nurses and electricity with returns to scale of 0.68"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the same illustrative hospital, doubling both inputs multiplies patient-days by 2 to the power 0.68, 1.6021, a rise of 60.2% rather than 100% (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Doubling nurses and electricity with returns to scale of 0.68" ;
    hemo:9000014 "A = 2000; L = 100; K = 500; alpha = 0.2; beta = 0.48; s = 2; Q = 99205; RTS = 0.68; Q_s = 158941; g_s = 1.6021" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HCPF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "doubling-nurses-and-electricity-with-returns-to-scale-of-0-68" .

hemo:7006233 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant returns limiting case of the hospital scale formula"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With elasticities of 0.5 each, the inputs of HE-EX-HPF-001 read as 10 units of labour and 40 of capital and A of 1, output is 20, the value HE-FM-HPF-001 gives, RTS is 1 and doubling both inputs doubles output, a limiting case (illustrative)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant returns limiting case of the hospital scale formula" ;
    hemo:9000014 "A = 1; L = 10; K = 40; alpha = 0.5; beta = 0.5; s = 2; Q = 20; RTS = 1; Q_s = 40; g_s = 2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HCPF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-returns-limiting-case-of-the-hospital-scale-formula" .

hemo:7006234 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Linear QALY model behind the time trade-off rule"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The rule holds under the linear QALY model, with the utility of life duration linear in years, full health valued at 1 and immediate death at 0, together with expected utility, the conditions under which Lipman, Brouwer and Attema state that time trade-off and standard gamble weights are often derived. Their study corrects the weights for loss aversion, probability weighting and nonlinear utility of life duration, and the corrected weights are not reproduced here." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Linear QALY model behind the time trade-off rule" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-UTIL-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "linear-qaly-model-behind-the-time-trade-off-rule" .

hemo:7006235 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Chronic state judged at least as good as immediate death"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The state lasts T_x years and is followed by death, and the respondent judges it at least as good as immediate death, so T_y is at least 0 and at most T_x. Torrance describes the use of the techniques for both chronic and temporary states and for states considered better and worse than death, and the rule here covers only the chronic state judged at least as good as immediate death." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Chronic state judged at least as good as immediate death" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-UTIL-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "chronic-state-judged-at-least-as-good-as-immediate-death" .

hemo:7006236 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One respondent's value and the public valuation in the NICE reference case"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The rule gives the value of one respondent for one state. PMG36 section 4.3.4 bases the valuation of health-related quality of life on public preferences from a representative sample of the UK population using a choice-based method, so reference-case utilities summarise many respondents' answers, usually through a value set such as HE-FM-EQ3L-002." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One respondent's value and the public valuation in the NICE reference case" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-UTIL-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-respondent-s-value-and-the-public-valuation-in-the-nice-reference-case" .

hemo:7006237 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Time trade-off utility from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000908 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the two durations in cells named TTOStateYears and TTOFullHealthYears, the formula returns U_TTO in a cell named TTOUtility." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Time trade-off utility from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-UTIL-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "time-trade-off-utility-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=TTOFullHealthYears/TTOStateYears" .

hemo:7006238 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Time trade-off utility gives equal QALYs to the two sides of the question"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "TTOUtility times TTOStateYears, the QALYs of the state side under HE-FM-QALY-001, is compared with the QALYs of the full-health side, TTOFullHealthYears times 1. Expected result TRUE. For the inputs of the first worked example, a TTOUtility cell written as TTOStateYears divided by TTOFullHealthYears returns 1.3333 and FALSE, and doubling the cell to 1.5 or halving it to 0.375 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Time trade-off utility gives equal QALYs to the two sides of the question" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-UTIL-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "time-trade-off-utility-gives-equal-qalys-to-the-two-sides-of-the-question" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(TTOUtility*TTOStateYears-1*TTOFullHealthYears)<1E-9" .

hemo:7006239 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Time trade-off utility cell matches the first worked example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of the first worked example (TTOStateYears 10, TTOFullHealthYears 7.5), TTOUtility is compared with the hand value 0.75. Expected result TRUE. A TTOUtility cell holding the share of time given up returns 0.25 and FALSE, the inverted ratio returns 1.3333 and FALSE, and doubling the cell to 1.5 or halving it to 0.375 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Time trade-off utility cell matches the first worked example" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-UTIL-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "time-trade-off-utility-cell-matches-the-first-worked-example" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(TTOUtility-0.75)<1E-9" .

hemo:7006240 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative time trade-off answer for one chronic state"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A respondent judges 7.5 years in full health equal to 10 years in a health state, each followed by death. The time trade-off utility of the state is 0.75. The inputs are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative time trade-off answer for one chronic state" ;
    hemo:9000014 "T_x = 10; T_y = 7.5; U_TTO = 0.75" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-UTIL-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-time-trade-off-answer-for-one-chronic-state" .

hemo:7006241 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Limiting case of a state judged as good as full health"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A respondent who gives up no time, judging 10 years in full health equal to 10 years in the state, values the state as full health. This limiting case, with illustrative durations of 10 years, checks an implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Limiting case of a state judged as good as full health" ;
    hemo:9000014 "T_x = 10; T_y = 10; U_TTO = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-UTIL-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "limiting-case-of-a-state-judged-as-good-as-full-health" .

hemo:7006242 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Limiting case of a state judged as bad as immediate death"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A respondent who would give up all 10 years in the state, judging it equal to immediate death, values it at 0, the lower end of the range the rule can return. The inputs are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Limiting case of a state judged as bad as immediate death" ;
    hemo:9000014 "T_x = 10; T_y = 0; U_TTO = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-UTIL-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "limiting-case-of-a-state-judged-as-bad-as-immediate-death" .

hemo:7006247 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Copas selection parameters and implied unpublished studies from two assumed publication probabilities"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "`P_a` is at least `P_b`, so `gamma_1` is zero or above (HE-AS-COPAS-002); both probabilities are above 0 and below 1, so the quantiles are finite; the two standard errors differ; published studies at each standard error share the probability (HE-AS-COPAS-005)." .

hemo:7006251 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-COPAS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000909 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the probabilities in cells named PubProbA and PubProbB, the standard errors in StdErrA and StdErrB and the counts in TrialsA and TrialsB, a cell named GammaOneCF holds `=(NORM.S.INV(PubProbA)-NORM.S.INV(PubProbB))/(1/StdErrA-1/StdErrB)`, a cell named GammaZeroCF holds `=NORM.S.INV(PubProbB)-GammaOneCF/StdErrB` and a cell named ImpliedUnpubCF holds `=TrialsA*(1-PubProbA)/PubProbA+TrialsB*(1-PubProbB)/PubProbB`. For a grid of scenarios put one pair of probabilities per row and fill the same formulas down with relative references." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(NORM.S.INV(PubProbA)-NORM.S.INV(PubProbB))/(1/StdErrA-1/StdErrB)" .

hemo:7006252 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-COPAS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000909 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`copas_scenario <- function(Pa, Pb, sa, sb, na, nb) { g1 <- (qnorm(Pa)-qnorm(Pb))/(1/sa-1/sb); g0 <- qnorm(Pb)-g1/sb; M <- na*(1-Pa)/Pa+nb*(1-Pb)/Pb; data.frame(Pa = Pa, Pb = Pb, gamma1 = g1, gamma0 = g0, M = M) }` Run `copas_scenario(c(0.95, 0.90), c(0.35, 0.15), 0.10, 0.40, 2, 10)` for the article's two selection scenarios; it returns gamma1 of 0.2707 and 0.3091, gamma0 of -1.0620 and -1.8091 and M of 18.677 and 56.889." ;
    hemo:9000024 "copas_scenario <- function(Pa, Pb, sa, sb, na, nb) { g1 <- (qnorm(Pa)-qnorm(Pb))/(1/sa-1/sb); g0 <- qnorm(Pb)-g1/sb; M <- na*(1-Pa)/Pa+nb*(1-Pb)/Pb; data.frame(Pa = Pa, Pb = Pb, gamma1 = g1, gamma0 = g0, M = M) }" .

hemo:7006253 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-COPAS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000909 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def copas_scenario(Pa, Pb, sa, sb, na, nb): from statistics import NormalDist; q = NormalDist().inv_cdf; return [(pa, pb, (q(pa)-q(pb))/(1/sa-1/sb), q(pb)-(q(pa)-q(pb))/(1/sa-1/sb)/sb, na*(1-pa)/pa+nb*(1-pb)/pb) for pa, pb in zip(Pa, Pb)]` Standard library only; `copas_scenario([0.95, 0.90], [0.35, 0.15], 0.10, 0.40, 2, 10)` returns the same rows as R." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def copas_scenario(Pa, Pb, sa, sb, na, nb): from statistics import NormalDist; q = NormalDist().inv_cdf; return [(pa, pb, (q(pa)-q(pb))/(1/sa-1/sb), q(pb)-(q(pa)-q(pb))/(1/sa-1/sb)/sb, na*(1-pa)/pa+nb*(1-pb)/pb) for pa, pb in zip(Pa, Pb)]" .

hemo:7006256 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of HFMA net days in accounts receivable from three monthly net revenues and their calendar days"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Monthly revenue and the receivables are both net, with capitation and premium revenue excluded, as in HE-FM-DAR-001, and the three months end with the month whose closing balance is AR." .

hemo:7006259 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-DAR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000910 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`=EOMONTH(ReportMonth,0)-EOMONTH(ReportMonth,-3)` in QuarterCalendarDays and `=NetReceivables/(SUM(MonthNetRevenue)/QuarterCalendarDays)` in QuarterNetDaysInAR, with the first day of the last month reported in a cell named ReportMonth, the three monthly revenues in a range named MonthNetRevenue and the receivables in NetReceivables. For ReportMonth 1 June 2026 the first formula returns 91, the days of April, May and June." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=EOMONTH(ReportMonth,0)-EOMONTH(ReportMonth,-3)" .

hemo:7006260 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-DAR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000910 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`fm1_days <- function(ar, rev, last_month) { first <- seq(as.Date(last_month), by = \"-2 months\", length.out = 2)[2]; end <- seq(as.Date(last_month), by = \"month\", length.out = 2)[2]; d <- as.numeric(difftime(end, first, units = \"days\")); c(D = d, r_bar = sum(rev) / d, DAR = ar / (sum(rev) / d)) }` Called as `fm1_days(55, c(28, 31, 32), \"2026-06-01\")` it returns D = 91, r_bar = 1 and DAR = 55, and as `fm1_days(55, c(30, 30, 31), \"2026-03-01\")` it returns D = 90 and DAR = 54.3956." ;
    hemo:9000024 "fm1_days <- function(ar, rev, last_month) { first <- seq(as.Date(last_month), by = \"-2 months\", length.out = 2)[2]; end <- seq(as.Date(last_month), by = \"month\", length.out = 2)[2]; d <- as.numeric(difftime(end, first, units = \"days\")); c(D = d, r_bar = sum(rev) / d, DAR = ar / (sum(rev) / d)) }" .

hemo:7006261 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-DAR-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000910 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With `import calendar`, `def fm1_days(ar, rev, year, last_month): d = sum(calendar.monthrange(year + (last_month-k-1)//12, (last_month-k-1) % 12 + 1)[1] for k in (2, 1, 0)); return d, sum(rev)/d, ar/(sum(rev)/d)` Called as `fm1_days(55, [28, 31, 32], 2026, 6)` it returns (91, 1.0, 55.0), and as `fm1_days(55, [30, 30, 31], 2026, 3)` it returns 90 days and 54.3956 days." ;
    hemo:9000024 "import calendar" .

hemo:7006264 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for HTMT ratios of every construct pair from an indicator correlation matrix"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "As in HE-AS-DISV-001, HE-AS-DISV-002 and HE-AS-DISV-003, the constructs are reflectively measured, every indicator is coded in the same direction and all correlations come from the same respondents. In the Excel version the indicators of each construct sit in adjacent rows and columns of the matrix. A construct with one indicator has no within-construct correlation, and its ratio is undefined." .

hemo:7006267 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-DISV-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000911 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the full matrix in a range named ItemCorr, the position of the first indicator of construct i in StartI and the number of its indicators in CountI, and likewise StartJ and CountJ, enter `=AVERAGE(OFFSET(ItemCorr,StartI-1,StartJ-1,CountI,CountJ))` in a cell named MeanHeteroBlock, `=(SUM(OFFSET(ItemCorr,StartI-1,StartI-1,CountI,CountI))-CountI)/(CountI*(CountI-1))` in MeanMonoBlockI, `=(SUM(OFFSET(ItemCorr,StartJ-1,StartJ-1,CountJ,CountJ))-CountJ)/(CountJ*(CountJ-1))` in MeanMonoBlockJ and `=MeanHeteroBlock/SQRT(MeanMonoBlockI*MeanMonoBlockJ)` in HTMTBlock. The within-construct formula removes the ones on the diagonal and counts each pair twice, which its divisor allows for. OFFSET is available in all Excel versions." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AVERAGE(OFFSET(ItemCorr,StartI-1,StartJ-1,CountI,CountJ))" .

hemo:7006268 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-DISV-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000911 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`htmt_matrix <- function(R, groups) { g <- unique(groups); k <- length(g); mono <- sapply(g, function(a) { B <- R[groups == a, groups == a, drop = FALSE]; mean(B[upper.tri(B)]) }); H <- matrix(NA, k, k, dimnames = list(g, g)); for (a in 1:k) for (b in 1:k) if (a != b) H[a, b] <- mean(R[groups == g[a], groups == g[b]])/sqrt(mono[a]*mono[b]); list(htmt = H, flag85 = H > 0.85, flag90 = H > 0.90) }` With Table 5 as `R` and `groups <- rep(c(\"ACSI\", \"CUEX\", \"PERQ\", \"PERV\"), c(3, 3, 3, 2))`, `round(htmt_matrix(R, groups)$htmt[\"CUEX\", \"PERV\"], 4)` is 0.5326." ;
    hemo:9000024 "htmt_matrix <- function(R, groups) { g <- unique(groups); k <- length(g); mono <- sapply(g, function(a) { B <- R[groups == a, groups == a, drop = FALSE]; mean(B[upper.tri(B)]) }); H <- matrix(NA, k, k, dimnames = list(g, g)); for (a in 1:k) for (b in 1:k) if (a != b) H[a, b] <- mean(R[groups == g[a], groups == g[b]])/sqrt(mono[a]*mono[b]); list(htmt = H, flag85 = H > 0.85, flag90 = H > 0.90) }" .

hemo:7006269 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-DISV-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000911 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def htmt_matrix(R, groups): labs = list(dict.fromkeys(groups)); idx = {a: [n for n, x in enumerate(groups) if x == a] for a in labs}; mono = {a: sum(R[p][q] for p in idx[a] for q in idx[a] if p < q)/(len(idx[a])*(len(idx[a])-1)/2) for a in labs}; return {(a, b): sum(R[p][q] for p in idx[a] for q in idx[b])/(len(idx[a])*len(idx[b]))/math.sqrt(mono[a]*mono[b]) for k, a in enumerate(labs) for b in labs[k+1:]}` Requires the math module; `R` is a list of rows and the result for the pair (\"CUEX\", \"PERV\") is about 0.5326." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def htmt_matrix(R, groups): labs = list(dict.fromkeys(groups)); idx = {a: [n for n, x in enumerate(groups) if x == a] for a in labs}; mono = {a: sum(R[p][q] for p in idx[a] for q in idx[a] if p < q)/(len(idx[a])*(len(idx[a])-1)/2) for a in labs}; return {(a, b): sum(R[p][q] for p in idx[a] for q in idx[b])/(len(idx[a])*len(idx[b]))/math.sqrt(mono[a]*mono[b]) for k, a in enumerate(labs) for b in labs[k+1:]}" .

