hemo:7006289 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Second-order approximation of the risk premium in the Phelps loss"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The risk term keeps only the variance term of a Taylor series expansion of expected utility about mean spending, which Phelps notes replicates Pratt's result that the risk premium is proportional to the variance. Applied to the utility of income net of the retained spending, the risk term is minus the correction of HE-FM-EV-002 (one half of the second derivative of utility times C squared times sigma squared) divided by the first derivative of utility, which converts a loss of expected utility into money (derived). Skewness and kurtosis terms are dropped. Phelps finds that for hospital care, with the skewness and kurtosis of the RAND spending data, the third and fourth order terms are of the same order of magnitude as the variance term, while for the other types of care they are vanishingly small." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Second-order approximation of the risk premium in the Phelps loss" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MHHI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "second-order-approximation-of-the-risk-premium-in-the-phelps-loss" .

hemo:7006290 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Straight-line demand with one elasticity at the full price in the Phelps loss"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Demand for the insured care is a straight line over the price range, the elasticity is evaluated at the full-price demand and one elasticity eps applies across illnesses, so that Phelps's expected term E(pm eta) equals minus eps times mu. The general constant-slope form of the triangle and its own assumptions are HE-FM-MH-002 and HE-AS-MH-004." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Straight-line demand with one elasticity at the full price in the Phelps loss" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MHHI-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "straight-line-demand-with-one-elasticity-at-the-full-price-in-the-phelps-loss" .

hemo:7006291 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Fair premium, a single coinsurance rate and no income effect of the premium in the Phelps loss"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The coinsurance rate is the only choice parameter of the policy, the loading charged by the insurer is left out, and the income-price interaction of paying the premium in advance is ignored. Phelps reports in his appendix that for all medical care, at a budget share of 0.05, that ignored term is about one fifth of the welfare triangle, and that for components of care other than hospital care the relevant budget share is on the order of 0.01." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fair premium, a single coinsurance rate and no income effect of the premium in the Phelps loss" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MHHI-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fair-premium-a-single-coinsurance-rate-and-no-income-effect-of-the-premium-in-the-phelps-loss" .

hemo:7006292 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Induced care valued by the demand curve in the Phelps loss"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The second term counts every unit of insurance-induced care as worth what the demand curve says, so all of it below its cost is a loss. HE-AS-MH-001 records that Nyman's access motive and behavioural hazard reject this reading for part of the extra use, and the article notes that on Nyman's argument the induced-demand term is then too large and the optimal rate from HE-FM-MHHI-002 too high (a smaller second term lowers C_star, derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Induced care valued by the demand curve in the Phelps loss" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MHHI-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "induced-care-valued-by-the-demand-curve-in-the-phelps-loss" .

hemo:7006293 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Phelps welfare loss from named coinsurance and spending cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000920 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the coinsurance rate in PhelpsCoinsRate, the risk aversion coefficient in RiskAversionCoef, the standard deviation and mean of uninsured spending in SpendSD and MeanSpendFullPrice and the absolute elasticity in PriceElasticityAbs, the three formulas return the risk premium in RiskPremiumLoss, the induced-demand loss in MoralHazardLoss and their sum in PhelpsWelfareLoss." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Phelps welfare loss from named coinsurance and spending cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-MHHI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "phelps-welfare-loss-from-named-coinsurance-and-spending-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=0.5*RiskAversionCoef*SpendSD^2*PhelpsCoinsRate^2; =0.5*(1-PhelpsCoinsRate)^2*PriceElasticityAbs*MeanSpendFullPrice; =RiskPremiumLoss+MoralHazardLoss" .

hemo:7006294 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Phelps welfare loss equals its minimum plus the curvature term"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Because the loss is a quadratic in C with curvature r sigma squared plus eps mu, it equals the minimum loss at C_star plus one half of that curvature times the squared distance of C from C_star (derived, checked on a grid). The test recomputes this from the input cells and compares it with PhelpsWelfareLoss. Expected result TRUE. For the third worked example a PhelpsWelfareLoss cell that uses the standard deviation in place of the variance returns 46.23 and FALSE, and doubling the cell to 96.15 or halving it to 24.04 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Phelps welfare loss equals its minimum plus the curvature term" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MHHI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "phelps-welfare-loss-equals-its-minimum-plus-the-curvature-term" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(PhelpsWelfareLoss-(0.5*RiskAversionCoef*SpendSD^2*PriceElasticityAbs*MeanSpendFullPrice/(RiskAversionCoef*SpendSD^2+PriceElasticityAbs*MeanSpendFullPrice)+0.5*(RiskAversionCoef*SpendSD^2+PriceElasticityAbs*MeanSpendFullPrice)*(PhelpsCoinsRate-PriceElasticityAbs*MeanSpendFullPrice/(RiskAversionCoef*SpendSD^2+PriceElasticityAbs*MeanSpendFullPrice))^2))<1E-6" .

hemo:7006295 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Phelps welfare loss matches the hospital example at its optimal rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of the third worked example (PhelpsCoinsRate 0.0384615, RiskAversionCoef 0.0001, SpendSD 5000, PriceElasticityAbs 0.1, MeanSpendFullPrice 1000), PhelpsWelfareLoss is compared with the article's hand value of 48.08. Expected result TRUE. A cell that leaves C unsquared in the risk term returns 94.30 and FALSE, and doubling the cell to 96.15 or halving it to 24.04 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Phelps welfare loss matches the hospital example at its optimal rate" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MHHI-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "phelps-welfare-loss-matches-the-hospital-example-at-its-optimal-rate" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(PhelpsWelfareLoss-48.08)<0.005" .

hemo:7006296 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Full insurance of the illustrative hospital risk"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative hospital care has expected spending of USD 1,000, a standard deviation of USD 5,000 and an absolute elasticity of 0.1, with r = 0.0001 per USD. With full insurance (C = 0) the risk premium is zero and only the induced-demand loss remains, 0.5 times 100, giving 50 USD per person per year." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Full insurance of the illustrative hospital risk" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = 0; r = 0.0001; sigma = 5000; eps = 0.1; mu = 1000; RP = 0; MH = 50; WL = 50" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MHHI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "full-insurance-of-the-illustrative-hospital-risk" .

hemo:7006297 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No insurance of the illustrative hospital risk"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With no insurance (C = 1) there is no induced use and the whole loss is the risk premium, 0.5 times 0.0001 times 25,000,000, giving 1,250 USD per person per year, as in the article. The inputs are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No insurance of the illustrative hospital risk" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = 1; r = 0.0001; sigma = 5000; eps = 0.1; mu = 1000; RP = 1250; MH = 0; WL = 1250" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MHHI-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-insurance-of-the-illustrative-hospital-risk" .

hemo:7006298 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative hospital risk at its optimal coinsurance rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At the optimal rate 100 / 2,600 = 0.0384615 from HE-FM-MHHI-002, the risk premium is 1.85 and the induced-demand loss 46.23, a total of 48.08 USD per person per year, as in the article. This equals one half of 2,500 times 100 over 2,600, the minimum loss the article states (derived by substituting C_star into the formula). The inputs are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative hospital risk at its optimal coinsurance rate" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = 0.0384615; r = 0.0001; sigma = 5000; eps = 0.1; mu = 1000; RP = 1.85; MH = 46.23; WL = 48.08" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MHHI-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-hospital-risk-at-its-optimal-coinsurance-rate" .

hemo:7006299 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative outpatient risk at its optimal coinsurance rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative outpatient visits have expected spending and a standard deviation of USD 500 each and an absolute elasticity of 0.3. At the optimal rate 150 / 175 = 0.857143 the risk premium is 9.18 and the induced-demand loss 1.53, a total of 10.71 USD per person per year, below the 75 of full insurance and the 12.5 of no insurance." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative outpatient risk at its optimal coinsurance rate" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = 0.857143; r = 0.0001; sigma = 500; eps = 0.3; mu = 500; RP = 9.18; MH = 1.53; WL = 10.71" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MHHI-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-outpatient-risk-at-its-optimal-coinsurance-rate" .

hemo:7006300 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Interior minimum of the Phelps welfare loss"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "With r above zero and a downward-sloping demand curve, Phelps shows that the optimal rate lies strictly above 0 and below 1 and that the second-order condition holds. He sets aside cases in which the variance of the risk changes with C, through C altering the probabilities of illnesses or the shapes of the demand curves." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Interior minimum of the Phelps welfare loss" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MHHI-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "interior-minimum-of-the-phelps-welfare-loss" .

hemo:7006301 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Same approximations as the Phelps welfare loss"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The rate inherits HE-AS-MHHI-001 to HE-AS-MHHI-004, a second-order risk premium, straight-line demand with one elasticity at the full price, a fair premium with a single rate, and induced care valued by the demand curve. Phelps reports that the optimal hospital rate is little different at the second and fourth order approximations of risk." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same approximations as the Phelps welfare loss" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MHHI-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-approximations-as-the-phelps-welfare-loss" .

hemo:7006302 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Risk aversion measured in the units of the spending figures"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "r is per unit of the currency in which mu and sigma are measured. Phelps takes r of about 0.0001 per dollar, corresponding to relative risk aversion of about 2 at an income of USD 20,000, and treats 0.00005 to 0.0002 as the range of most of the literature." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Risk aversion measured in the units of the spending figures" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MHHI-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "risk-aversion-measured-in-the-units-of-the-spending-figures" .

hemo:7006303 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Phelps optimal coinsurance rate from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000921 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the absolute elasticity in PriceElasticityAbs, mean spending at the full price in MeanSpendFullPrice, the risk aversion coefficient in RiskAversionCoef and the standard deviation of spending in SpendSD, the formula returns the optimal rate in OptimalCoinsRate." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Phelps optimal coinsurance rate from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-MHHI-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "phelps-optimal-coinsurance-rate-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=PriceElasticityAbs*MeanSpendFullPrice/(PriceElasticityAbs*MeanSpendFullPrice+RiskAversionCoef*SpendSD^2)" .

hemo:7006304 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Optimal coinsurance rate satisfies the Phelps first-order condition"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At the optimum the marginal risk premium r sigma squared times C equals the marginal induced-demand loss (1 minus C) times eps mu, Phelps's equation (5a) with the elasticity as an absolute value. The test compares the two at OptimalCoinsRate with a tolerance of 0.001 times r sigma squared plus eps mu, which accepts the rate typed as 0.038 (gap 1.2 against 2.6 for the hospital inputs). Expected result TRUE. For the illustrative hospital inputs, doubling the cell to 0.0769 or halving it to 0.0192 leaves a gap of 100 or 50 and gives FALSE (a halved cell is caught whenever C_star exceeds 0.002, derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Optimal coinsurance rate satisfies the Phelps first-order condition" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MHHI-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "optimal-coinsurance-rate-satisfies-the-phelps-first-order-condition" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(RiskAversionCoef*SpendSD^2*OptimalCoinsRate-(1-OptimalCoinsRate)*PriceElasticityAbs*MeanSpendFullPrice)<0.001*(RiskAversionCoef*SpendSD^2+PriceElasticityAbs*MeanSpendFullPrice)" .

hemo:7006305 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Optimal coinsurance rate matches the Phelps hospital base case"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With Phelps's hospital inputs (PriceElasticityAbs 0.15, MeanSpendFullPrice 500, RiskAversionCoef 0.0001, SpendSD 3150), OptimalCoinsRate is compared with his reported 0.07. Expected result TRUE. A cell using the standard deviation in place of the variance returns 0.9958 and FALSE, a negative elasticity returns minus 0.0818 and FALSE, and doubling the cell to 0.1405 or halving it to 0.0351 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Optimal coinsurance rate matches the Phelps hospital base case" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MHHI-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "optimal-coinsurance-rate-matches-the-phelps-hospital-base-case" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(OptimalCoinsRate-0.07)<0.005" .

hemo:7006306 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Optimal rate for the illustrative hospital risk"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For the article's illustrative hospital care, eps mu is 0.1 times 1,000 = 100 and r sigma squared is 0.0001 times 25,000,000 = 2,500, so the optimal rate is 100 / 2,600 = 0.038, about 4 per cent. The inputs are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Optimal rate for the illustrative hospital risk" ;
    hemo:9000014 "eps = 0.1; mu = 1000; r = 0.0001; sigma = 5000; C_star = 0.038" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MHHI-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "optimal-rate-for-the-illustrative-hospital-risk" .

hemo:7006307 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Optimal rate for the illustrative outpatient risk"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For the article's illustrative outpatient visits, eps mu is 0.3 times 500 = 150 and r sigma squared is 0.0001 times 250,000 = 25, so the optimal rate is 150 / 175 = 0.857, about 86 per cent. The inputs are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Optimal rate for the illustrative outpatient risk" ;
    hemo:9000014 "eps = 0.3; mu = 500; r = 0.0001; sigma = 500; C_star = 0.857" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MHHI-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "optimal-rate-for-the-illustrative-outpatient-risk" .

hemo:7006308 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Phelps base case for hospital care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Phelps's Table 1 base case for hospital care, with RAND-based mean spending of 500, elasticity 0.15 and variance 3,150 squared at r = 0.0001, gives an optimal rate of 0.07, the value he reports." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Phelps base case for hospital care" ;
    hemo:9000014 "eps = 0.15; mu = 500; r = 0.0001; sigma = 3150; C_star = 0.07" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MHHI-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "phelps-base-case-for-hospital-care" .

hemo:7006309 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Phelps base case for physician care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Phelps's Table 1 base case for physician care, with mean spending of 500, elasticity 0.3 and variance 1,250 squared at r = 0.0001, gives an optimal rate of 0.49." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Phelps base case for physician care" ;
    hemo:9000014 "eps = 0.3; mu = 500; r = 0.0001; sigma = 1250; C_star = 0.49" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MHHI-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "phelps-base-case-for-physician-care" .

hemo:7006310 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Physician care with doubled risk aversion"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Doubling r to 0.0002 for the same physician care lowers the optimal rate to 0.32, the value in Phelps's Table 2, which shows the rate for intermediate types of care moving with the risk aversion assumed." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Physician care with doubled risk aversion" ;
    hemo:9000014 "eps = 0.3; mu = 500; r = 0.0002; sigma = 1250; C_star = 0.32" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MHHI-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "physician-care-with-doubled-risk-aversion" .

hemo:7006311 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Whole programmes, one budget and independent additive gains in the 0-1 funding problem"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each programme is funded in full or not at all, the budget is the only constraint, and each programme's cost and QALY gain are the same whatever else is funded, so the cost and QALYs of a package are the sums over its programmes, as in the article's binary form. Crown and colleagues give infectious disease as a case where investments interact, since vaccination may leave fewer cases to treat, and such interactions need extra variables or constraints (HE-ER-IPH-003). Costs and QALY gains are fixed numbers, the deterministic case that Crown and colleagues contrast with stochastic optimisation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Whole programmes, one budget and independent additive gains in the 0-1 funding problem" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-IPH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "whole-programmes-one-budget-and-independent-additive-gains-in-the-0-1-funding-problem" .

hemo:7006312 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Positive costs and QALY gains on one basis in the 0-1 funding problem"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every c_i and q_i is above zero and all are measured against current care on one perspective, price year, discount rate and budget period, with c_i the draw on the budget in that period. A programme that costs no more than current care, gives no less health and is strictly better on at least one of the two dominates it under HE-FM-DOM-001 and is funded before the search with any saving added to B, as HE-AS-CELT-002 does for the league table." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Positive costs and QALY gains on one basis in the 0-1 funding problem" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-IPH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "positive-costs-and-qaly-gains-on-one-basis-in-the-0-1-funding-problem" .

hemo:7006313 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Ties and comparison inputs for the integer optimum"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "H_star is a single number, but Crown and colleagues note that two or more decisions may yield the same optimum, so the package behind it need not be unique, and HE-CF-IPH-001 reports the first optimal package in its enumeration order, a convention of this package. H_rule must be the health of a package that fits the same budget, so G_rule is zero or above, and H_div must be the divisible fill at the same inputs, which is never below H_star, so L_ind is zero or above (derived, with no exception in a check of 200,000 random four-programme cases)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ties and comparison inputs for the integer optimum" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-IPH-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ties-and-comparison-inputs-for-the-integer-optimum" .

hemo:7006314 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Integer optimum of four whole programmes from a package matrix in named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000922 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the four programmes' costs in a column range named ProgCosts (B2 to B5), their QALY gains in ProgQALYs (C2 to C5) and the budget in Budget, the first formula, entered in E2 and filled to H17, builds a range named PackageMatrix whose row k holds the binary digits of k less 1, one column per programme, so its 16 rows are every package. In each row I2 holds the package cost, J2 its QALYs and K2 its QALYs when it fits and zero when it does not, each filled down to row 17, and the cell named IntegerOptimum takes the largest value in K2 to K17. With the health of the league table of whole programmes (WholeHealth of HE-IM-CELT-003, extended to four rows) in RuleHealth and the divisible fill of HE-CF-CELT-001 in DivisibleHealth, the last two formulas return the cells named GainOverRule and IndivisibilityLoss. MMULT of a one-row package and a four-row column of costs or QALYs is a single value, so it needs no array entry in an ordinary cell. MMULT returns #VALUE! when any cell of ProgCosts or ProgQALYs is blank or holds text, so every programme needs a number in both ranges." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Integer optimum of four whole programmes from a package matrix in named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-IPH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "integer-optimum-of-four-whole-programmes-from-a-package-matrix-in-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MOD(INT((ROW()-ROW($E$2))/2^(COLUMN()-COLUMN($E$2))),2); =MMULT(E2:H2,ProgCosts); =MMULT(E2:H2,ProgQALYs); =(I2<=Budget)*J2; =MAX(K2:K17); =IntegerOptimum-RuleHealth; =DivisibleHealth-IntegerOptimum" .

hemo:7006315 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's integer optimum of 375 QALYs and its two gaps reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's costs of 6, 5, 5 and 2 in ProgCosts, QALYs of 240, 190, 185 and 50 in ProgQALYs, Budget at 10, RuleHealth at 290 and DivisibleHealth at 392, IntegerOptimum is 375, GainOverRule 85 and IndivisibilityLoss 17, as in the article. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a K column without the budget test, which returns 665 for the package of all four, an IntegerOptimum cell holding the league-table result of 290, and a doubled cell of 750 or a halved cell of 187.5." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's integer optimum of 375 QALYs and its two gaps reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-IPH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-integer-optimum-of-375-qalys-and-its-two-gaps-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(IntegerOptimum-375)<0.000001,ABS(GainOverRule-85)<0.000001,ABS(IndivisibilityLoss-17)<0.000001)" .

hemo:7006316 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No package within the budget beats the integer optimum and one package attains it"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Computed from PackageMatrix, ProgCosts and ProgQALYs without the helper columns, no package whose cost is at most Budget has more QALYs than IntegerOptimum, and at least one such package has exactly that many. Expected result TRUE. For the article's inputs FALSE shows the league-table result of 290 (B plus C fits and gives 375), the divisible 392 or a doubled 750 (no package fits and gives that much), and a halved 187.5." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No package within the budget beats the integer optimum and one package attains it" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-IPH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-package-within-the-budget-beats-the-integer-optimum-and-one-package-attains-it" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(SUMPRODUCT((MMULT(PackageMatrix,ProgCosts)<=Budget)*(MMULT(PackageMatrix,ProgQALYs)>IntegerOptimum+0.000001))=0,SUMPRODUCT((MMULT(PackageMatrix,ProgCosts)<=Budget)*(ABS(MMULT(PackageMatrix,ProgQALYs)-IntegerOptimum)<0.000001))>=1)" .

hemo:7006317 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's four programmes and GBP 10 million budget with the optimum B plus C"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative programmes A, B, C and D cost GBP 6, 5, 5 and 2 million and gain 240, 190, 185 and 50 QALYs, with GBP 10 million to spend, and money is in GBP millions. Eight packages fit besides funding nothing, and B plus C, which uses the whole budget, gives the most, 375 QALYs. The pass-over league table of HE-FM-CELT-003, with a fourth row added, funds A and then D for 290 QALYs, so G_rule is 85, about 29% more health for the same budget, and the divisible fill of HE-FM-CELT-001 funds A and 0.8 of B for 392 QALYs, so L_ind is 17, as in steps 3 to 5 of the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's four programmes and GBP 10 million budget with the optimum B plus C" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c_1 = 6; c_2 = 5; c_3 = 5; c_4 = 2; q_1 = 240; q_2 = 190; q_3 = 185; q_4 = 50; B = 10; H_rule = 290; H_div = 392; H_star = 375; G_rule = 85; L_ind = 17" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-IPH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-four-programmes-and-gbp-10-million-budget-with-the-optimum-b-plus-c" .

hemo:7006318 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Integer optimum when the league table of whole programmes is already optimal at GBP 12 million"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With an illustrative budget of GBP 12 million for the same programmes, the best package is A plus B, GBP 11 million for 430 QALYs, ahead of B plus C plus D at 425. The same four-row league table funds A and B and then finds that neither C nor D fits, so it reaches the same 430 and G_rule is zero, while the divisible fill adds 0.2 of C for 467 QALYs, so 37 QALYs are still lost to indivisibility (computed here for illustration). Money is in GBP millions." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Integer optimum when the league table of whole programmes is already optimal at GBP 12 million" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c_1 = 6; c_2 = 5; c_3 = 5; c_4 = 2; q_1 = 240; q_2 = 190; q_3 = 185; q_4 = 50; B = 12; H_rule = 430; H_div = 467; H_star = 430; G_rule = 0; L_ind = 37" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-IPH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "integer-optimum-when-the-league-table-of-whole-programmes-is-already-optimal-at-gbp-12-million" .

hemo:7006319 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Integer optimum when the budget covers every programme"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With an illustrative GBP 18 million every programme fits, the package of all four costs GBP 18 million and buys 665 QALYs, and the league table and the divisible fill reach the same total, so both gaps are zero, a limiting case in which the budget does not bind. Money is in GBP millions." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Integer optimum when the budget covers every programme" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c_1 = 6; c_2 = 5; c_3 = 5; c_4 = 2; q_1 = 240; q_2 = 190; q_3 = 185; q_4 = 50; B = 18; H_rule = 665; H_div = 665; H_star = 665; G_rule = 0; L_ind = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-IPH-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "integer-optimum-when-the-budget-covers-every-programme" .

hemo:7006320 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Both plant capacity scores from one sample under constant returns to scale"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "E_F and E_V come from the same sample of units, with the same outputs, inputs and output orientation and a constant returns to scale frontier, as Valdmanis and colleagues restrict the plant capacity analysis. Only the treatment of the variable inputs differs. Removing the constraints on variable inputs cannot lower the largest attainable expansion of outputs, so E_F is at most E_V and PI is above zero and at most 1 (derived, and checked here on 1,200 unit scores from random data sets solved as linear programmes)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Both plant capacity scores from one sample under constant returns to scale" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CAPU-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "both-plant-capacity-scores-from-one-sample-under-constant-returns-to-scale" .

hemo:7006321 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Fixed and variable inputs classified before the plant capacity analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The inputs that cannot change in the short run are named in advance. Valdmanis and colleagues class beds, doctors, nurses and other staff as fixed, because the ministry sets them, and allowance, drug and other operating expenditure as variable. PI measures unused capacity given that classification, and a different split of the same inputs gives a different value." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fixed and variable inputs classified before the plant capacity analysis" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CAPU-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fixed-and-variable-inputs-classified-before-the-plant-capacity-analysis" .

hemo:7006322 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Proportional output expansion against best observed practice"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "All outputs are expanded in the same proportion, as in the output-based programmes of Valdmanis and colleagues, and capacity output is the best performance the frontier finds among comparable units (HE-AS-EFF-001), not an engineering maximum. Outputs are measured on the same basis for every unit, with inpatient care weighted for case mix as in that study." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Proportional output expansion against best observed practice" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CAPU-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "proportional-output-expansion-against-best-observed-practice" .

hemo:7006323 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Plant capacity utilisation from two named efficiency cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000923 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the score from the fixed-input programme in a cell named OutputEfficiencyFixedInputs and the score from the all-input programme in a cell named OutputEfficiencyAllInputs, the formula returns PI in a cell named PlantCapacityUtilisation. The scores themselves come from the two linear programmes, solved with a data envelopment analysis tool or the Excel Solver. A blank OutputEfficiencyAllInputs cell is treated as 0 and the formula returns #DIV/0!." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Plant capacity utilisation from two named efficiency cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CAPU-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "plant-capacity-utilisation-from-two-named-efficiency-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=OutputEfficiencyFixedInputs/OutputEfficiencyAllInputs" .

hemo:7006324 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Plant capacity utilisation cell matches the first worked example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of the first worked example (OutputEfficiencyFixedInputs 0.76, OutputEfficiencyAllInputs 0.80), PlantCapacityUtilisation is compared with the hand value 0.95. Expected result TRUE. A cell written with the ratio inverted returns 1.0526 and FALSE, and doubling the cell to 1.9 or halving it to 0.475 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Plant capacity utilisation cell matches the first worked example" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CAPU-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "plant-capacity-utilisation-cell-matches-the-first-worked-example" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(PlantCapacityUtilisation-0.95)<0.0005" .

hemo:7006325 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Plant capacity utilisation equals 1 when the two efficiency scores are equal"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With OutputEfficiencyFixedInputs and OutputEfficiencyAllInputs both set to 0.88, the limiting case of the third worked example, PlantCapacityUtilisation is compared with 1. Expected result TRUE. A cell that returns OutputEfficiencyFixedInputs alone gives 0.88 and FALSE, and doubling the cell to 2 or halving it to 0.5 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Plant capacity utilisation equals 1 when the two efficiency scores are equal" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CAPU-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "plant-capacity-utilisation-equals-1-when-the-two-efficiency-scores-are-equal" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(PlantCapacityUtilisation-1)<0.0005" .

hemo:7006326 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative hospital with technical inefficiency and little unused plant capacity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A hospital produces 80% of the frontier output for its inputs, so E_V is 0.80, and 76% of the output its fixed inputs could support with variable inputs unrestricted, so E_F is 0.76. Plant capacity utilisation is 0.76 divided by 0.80, or 0.95, so only 5% of capacity output is unused once its technical inefficiency is set aside, in line with the article's point that a hospital can lack technical efficiency while having little spare capacity. The scores are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative hospital with technical inefficiency and little unused plant capacity" ;
    hemo:9000014 "E_F = 0.76; E_V = 0.80; PI = 0.95" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CAPU-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-hospital-with-technical-inefficiency-and-little-unused-plant-capacity" .

hemo:7006327 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative technically efficient hospital with idle plant"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A hospital on the all-input frontier, with E_V equal to 1, reaches only 75% of the output its beds and staff could support, so E_F is 0.75 and PI is 0.75. The hospital is technically efficient, yet a quarter of its plant capacity output is unused. The scores are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative technically efficient hospital with idle plant" ;
    hemo:9000014 "E_F = 0.75; E_V = 1; PI = 0.75" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CAPU-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-technically-efficient-hospital-with-idle-plant" .

hemo:7006328 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Limiting case of full plant capacity utilisation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When freeing the variable inputs allows no further expansion, the two scores are equal, here both 0.88, and PI is 1. The hospital is technically inefficient, but once on the frontier it could not raise its outputs in proportion without more fixed inputs. The scores are illustrative and the case checks an implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Limiting case of full plant capacity utilisation" ;
    hemo:9000014 "E_F = 0.88; E_V = 0.88; PI = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CAPU-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "limiting-case-of-full-plant-capacity-utilisation" .

hemo:7006329 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Same country, year and currency in both parts of the GDP share"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "CHE and GDP refer to the same country and calendar year and are both in national currency at current prices, as in the GHED, where both are held in million current national currency units. Under this condition the currency cancels and no conversion is needed; a share built from spending in one year and GDP in another, or from parts in different currencies, is not the indicator." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same country, year and currency in both parts of the GDP share" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HGDP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-country-year-and-currency-in-both-parts-of-the-gdp-share" .

hemo:7006330 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Current health expenditure counted on the health accounts boundary"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The numerator follows the System of Health Accounts 2011 boundary that the OECD and WHO use, the final consumption of health goods and services, including spending by all financing arrangements on medical services and goods, population health and prevention programmes and administration of the health system. The GHED builds it as government and compulsory schemes plus voluntary schemes plus household out-of-pocket payment plus rest-of-the-world schemes plus unspecified schemes, and WHO states that capital expenditure on health is not included." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Current health expenditure counted on the health accounts boundary" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HGDP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "current-health-expenditure-counted-on-the-health-accounts-boundary" .

hemo:7006331 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One database and one release for a comparison of GDP shares"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "All countries and years in a comparison come from one database and one release. The GHED notes that WHO adjusts and estimates national figures and that country data may still differ in definitions, data collection methods, population coverage and estimation methods, and the most recent year is provisional in the OECD data and preliminary in the GHED. The OECD keeps GDP as the denominator for all countries, including Ireland and Luxembourg where it judges gross national income a possibly more meaningful base." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One database and one release for a comparison of GDP shares" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-HGDP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-database-and-one-release-for-a-comparison-of-gdp-shares" .

hemo:7006332 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Health spending share of GDP from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000924 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With current health expenditure in a cell named HealthExpenditureCurrent and GDP for the same year and currency in a cell named GrossDomesticProduct, the formula returns S in a cell named HealthSpendingShareGDP as a plain number of percentage points. Keep the cell in General or Number format, since the Percentage format multiplies the display by a further 100. A blank or zero GDP cell returns #DIV/0!." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health spending share of GDP from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-HGDP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-spending-share-of-gdp-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=HealthExpenditureCurrent/GrossDomesticProduct*100" .

hemo:7006333 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Health spending share of GDP cell matches the United Kingdom 2019 example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of HE-EX-HGDP-001 (HealthExpenditureCurrent 222935, GrossDomesticProduct 2233921), HealthSpendingShareGDP is compared with the hand value 9.98 at a tolerance of 0.005. Expected result TRUE. A cell that leaves out the factor of 100 returns 0.0998 and FALSE, the inverted ratio returns 1002.05 and FALSE, and doubling the cell to 19.96 or halving it to 4.99 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health spending share of GDP cell matches the United Kingdom 2019 example" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HGDP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-spending-share-of-gdp-cell-matches-the-united-kingdom-2019-example" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(HealthSpendingShareGDP-9.98)<0.005" .

hemo:7006334 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Health spending share of GDP cell matches the GHED published share for Norway in 2022"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of HE-EX-HGDP-004 (HealthExpenditureCurrent 453400, GrossDomesticProduct 5732905), HealthSpendingShareGDP is compared with the share the GHED publishes for Norway in 2022 in its che_gdp column, 7.9087, at a tolerance of 0.0001. Expected result TRUE. Dividing the 2022 spending by the 2021 GDP of 4323931 returns 10.4858 and FALSE, and doubling the cell to 15.8175 or halving it to 3.9544 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health spending share of GDP cell matches the GHED published share for Norway in 2022" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-HGDP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-spending-share-of-gdp-cell-matches-the-ghed-published-share-for-norway-in-2022" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(HealthSpendingShareGDP-7.9087)<0.0001" .

hemo:7006335 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "United Kingdom health spending share of GDP in 2019"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "GHED figures as the article gives them, in million pounds at current prices, for the United Kingdom in 2019. Current health expenditure of 222,935 million over GDP of 2,233,921 million gives 9.98% of GDP." ;
    hemo:9000009 "United Kingdom health spending share of GDP in 2019" ;
    hemo:9000014 "CHE = 222935; GDP = 2233921; S = 9.98" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HGDP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "united-kingdom-health-spending-share-of-gdp-in-2019" .

hemo:7006336 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "United Kingdom health spending share of GDP in 2020"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "GHED figures as the article gives them for the United Kingdom in 2020, when spending rose and GDP fell. Current health expenditure of 254,603 million pounds over GDP of 2,103,486 million pounds gives 12.10% of GDP, against 9.98% in 2019 (HE-EX-HGDP-001)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "United Kingdom health spending share of GDP in 2020" ;
    hemo:9000014 "CHE = 254603; GDP = 2103486; S = 12.10" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HGDP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "united-kingdom-health-spending-share-of-gdp-in-2020" .

hemo:7006337 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Norway health spending share of GDP in 2021"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "GHED figures as the article gives them, in million kroner at current prices, for Norway in 2021. Current health expenditure of 424,362 million over GDP of 4,323,931 million gives 9.81% of GDP." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Norway health spending share of GDP in 2021" ;
    hemo:9000014 "CHE = 424362; GDP = 4323931; S = 9.81" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HGDP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "norway-health-spending-share-of-gdp-in-2021" .

hemo:7006338 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Norway health spending share of GDP in 2022"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "GHED figures as the article gives them for Norway in 2022, when nominal GDP grew much faster than health spending. Current health expenditure of 453,400 million kroner over GDP of 5,732,905 million kroner gives 7.91% of GDP, below the 9.81% of 2021 (HE-EX-HGDP-003) although spending in kroner rose." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Norway health spending share of GDP in 2022" ;
    hemo:9000014 "CHE = 453400; GDP = 5732905; S = 7.91" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-HGDP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "norway-health-spending-share-of-gdp-in-2022" .

hemo:7006339 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Endemic SIR steady state with births balancing deaths behind the infection risk"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The infection follows the single-group SIR model used by Bauch and Earn, with births and deaths at the same rate mu, lifelong immunity after recovery and a constant population, and has reached the endemic steady state, at which coverage equals uptake; Goodkin-Gold and colleagues use a model of the same form with a vaccinated compartment and a non-fatal disease. Bauch and Earn note that the birth rate and the infectious period drop out of pi_p, so R_0 and v are the only inputs. The formula does not cover waning immunity, age-structured mixing, seasonal transmission or deaths from the infection." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Endemic SIR steady state with births balancing deaths behind the infection risk" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-VEXT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "endemic-sir-steady-state-with-births-balancing-deaths-behind-the-infection-risk" .

hemo:7006340 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Fully protective vaccination at birth and coverage below one for the endemic infection risk"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each vaccinated newborn is fully and lastingly protected, and no child is infected before vaccination, the simplification Bauch and Earn state. v is at least 0 and below 1, since at a coverage of 1 no child is unvaccinated and pi is undefined, and R_0 is above 1. At or above H = 1 minus 1/R_0 the formula returns 0, the case the appendix of Bauch and Earn states (pi_p = 0 for p at or above p_crit). For an all-or-nothing vaccine that protects a share theta of those vaccinated, Goodkin-Gold and colleagues write the immunised share theta Q/mu in place of v, so v is then entered as theta times coverage, with theta for example from HE-FM-ATR-004." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fully protective vaccination at birth and coverage below one for the endemic infection risk" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-VEXT-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fully-protective-vaccination-at-birth-and-coverage-below-one-for-the-endemic-infection-risk" .

hemo:7006341 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Endemic infection risk and infections per birth from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000925 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the share vaccinated at birth in a cell named CoverageAtBirth and the basic reproduction number in BasicRepNum, the first formula returns pi in a cell named LifetimeInfRisk and the second returns n in a cell named InfectionsPerBirth. MAX returns 0 at or above the threshold; CoverageAtBirth must be below 1, since a value of 1 makes the division return #DIV/0!. For the article's inputs 5 and 0.6 the cells return 0.5 and 0.2." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Endemic infection risk and infections per birth from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-VEXT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "endemic-infection-risk-and-infections-per-birth-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MAX(0,1-1/(BasicRepNum*(1-CoverageAtBirth))); =(1-CoverageAtBirth)*LifetimeInfRisk" .

hemo:7006342 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Endemic infection risk cell satisfies the susceptible balance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Below the threshold the steady state requires R_0(1 minus v)(1 minus pi) = 1, the susceptible balance with the escape probability 1 minus pi, a route that does not take the reciprocal used in the LifetimeInfRisk cell; at or above the threshold the cell must hold 0. Expected result TRUE. At the article's inputs 5 and 0.6, a doubled cell of 1.0 gives 0 against 1 and a halved cell of 0.25 gives 1.5, both FALSE, a cell that ignores coverage and holds 1 minus 1/R_0, or 0.8, gives 0.4 and FALSE, and at a coverage of 0.9 a cell written without MAX holds minus 1 and gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Endemic infection risk cell satisfies the susceptible balance" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-VEXT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "endemic-infection-risk-cell-satisfies-the-susceptible-balance" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(CoverageAtBirth<1-1/BasicRepNum,ABS(BasicRepNum*(1-CoverageAtBirth)*(1-LifetimeInfRisk)-1)<1E-9,LifetimeInfRisk=0)" .

hemo:7006343 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Infections per birth cell equals the unvaccinated share less the endemic susceptible share"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "InfectionsPerBirth is compared with max(0, 1 minus v minus 1/R_0), the unvaccinated share less the endemic susceptible share, a route that does not use the LifetimeInfRisk cell. Expected result TRUE. At the article's inputs 5 and 0.6 the cell holds 0.2; doubled to 0.4 or halved to 0.1 it gives FALSE, and a cell holding pi itself, 0.5, gives FALSE. Run the test below the threshold, since at or above it both sides are 0." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Infections per birth cell equals the unvaccinated share less the endemic susceptible share" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-VEXT-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "infections-per-birth-cell-equals-the-unvaccinated-share-less-the-endemic-susceptible-share" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(InfectionsPerBirth-MAX(0,1-CoverageAtBirth-1/BasicRepNum))<1E-9" .

hemo:7006344 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's endemic infection risk of an unvaccinated child at 60% coverage"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative endemic infection has R_0 of 5, so H is 0.8 by HE-FM-BRN-003. At the voluntary coverage of 0.6 an unvaccinated child's lifetime risk is 1 minus 1/(5 × 0.4), or 0.5, and infections per birth are 0.4 × 0.5 = 0.2, that is 200 per 1,000 births, the same as the unvaccinated share 0.4 less the equilibrium susceptible share 0.2. The inputs are invented, as the article says." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's endemic infection risk of an unvaccinated child at 60% coverage" ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_0 = 5; v = 0.6; pi = 0.5; n = 0.2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-VEXT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-endemic-infection-risk-of-an-unvaccinated-child-at-60-coverage" .

hemo:7006345 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's endemic infection risk of an unvaccinated child at 75% coverage under free provision"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With free provision the article's coverage rises to 0.75. The unvaccinated child's risk falls to 1 minus 1/(5 × 0.25), or 0.2, and infections per birth to 0.25 × 0.2 = 0.05, the article's 50 infections per 1,000 births, equal to 0.8 less 0.75. Illustrative inputs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's endemic infection risk of an unvaccinated child at 75% coverage under free provision" ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_0 = 5; v = 0.75; pi = 0.2; n = 0.05" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-VEXT-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-endemic-infection-risk-of-an-unvaccinated-child-at-75-coverage-under-free-provision" .

hemo:7006346 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Endemic infection risk of an unvaccinated child with no vaccination"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With no vaccination every child is unvaccinated, the lifetime risk is 1 minus 1/5, or 0.8, and infections per birth are also 0.8, the article's 800 infections per 1,000 births in step 3. This risk with no vaccination is the pi_0 of Bauch and Earn. Illustrative inputs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Endemic infection risk of an unvaccinated child with no vaccination" ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_0 = 5; v = 0; pi = 0.8; n = 0.8" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-VEXT-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "endemic-infection-risk-of-an-unvaccinated-child-with-no-vaccination" .

hemo:7006347 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Endemic infection risk of an unvaccinated child at the herd immunity threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At the article's threshold coverage of 0.8 the expression 1 minus 1/(R_0(1 minus v)) reaches 0, so an unvaccinated child is not infected and no infections remain, the limiting case at which the infection dies out in the steady state. Any coverage above 0.8 also returns 0. Illustrative inputs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Endemic infection risk of an unvaccinated child at the herd immunity threshold" ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_0 = 5; v = 0.8; pi = 0; n = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-VEXT-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "endemic-infection-risk-of-an-unvaccinated-child-at-the-herd-immunity-threshold" .

hemo:7006348 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Same loss per infection for the family and for society in the vaccination benefit split"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "L is one money value of an infection for the family deciding and for society, as in the article, and families are risk neutral, as Goodkin-Gold and colleagues assume. Where the family values the loss differently from society, MPB takes the family's value and MEB society's, and their sum is no longer L (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same loss per infection for the family and for society in the vaccination benefit split" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-VEXT-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-loss-per-infection-for-the-family-and-for-society-in-the-vaccination-benefit-split" .

hemo:7006349 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Marginal benefits read at one steady-state coverage of a fully protective vaccine"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "MPB and MEB are the steady-state effect of one more vaccination per birth at coverage v of a fully protective vaccine, comparing steady states with the transition and discounting ignored, as in the article and in the limiting case without discounting that Goodkin-Gold and colleagues study; with an all-or-nothing vaccine of efficacy theta they scale all three benefits by theta. Boulier, Datta and Goldfarb report from a standard epidemiological model that the size of the externality is not necessarily monotonic in the number vaccinated, vaccine efficacy or infectiousness and that the marginal externality can exceed one case prevented among the unvaccinated, so the closed form here holds only for this endemic model." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Marginal benefits read at one steady-state coverage of a fully protective vaccine" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-VEXT-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "marginal-benefits-read-at-one-steady-state-coverage-of-a-fully-protective-vaccine" .

hemo:7006350 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Private and external benefit of one vaccination from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000926 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With CoverageAtBirth, BasicRepNum and the loss per infection in LossPerInfection, and LifetimeInfRisk computed as in HE-IM-VEXT-001, the first formula returns MPB in a cell named PrivateBenefitVacc and the second returns MEB in a cell named ExternalBenefitVacc; the IF returns 0 at or above the threshold without evaluating the division. For the article's inputs 5, 0.75 and 500 the cells return 100 and 400." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Private and external benefit of one vaccination from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-VEXT-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "private-and-external-benefit-of-one-vaccination-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=LossPerInfection*LifetimeInfRisk; =IF(CoverageAtBirth<1-1/BasicRepNum,LossPerInfection/(BasicRepNum*(1-CoverageAtBirth)),0)" .

hemo:7006351 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Private and external benefit cells add to the loss per infection below the threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Below the threshold PrivateBenefitVacc plus ExternalBenefitVacc must equal LossPerInfection, the constant marginal social benefit of Goodkin-Gold and colleagues, and at or above it both must be 0. Expected result TRUE. At the article's inputs 5, 0.75 and 500 the cells hold 100 and 400; doubling the external cell to 800 or halving it to 200 gives FALSE, an external cell written as L times pi, 100, gives FALSE, and at a coverage of 0.9 an external cell without the zero branch holds 1,000 and gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Private and external benefit cells add to the loss per infection below the threshold" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-VEXT-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "private-and-external-benefit-cells-add-to-the-loss-per-infection-below-the-threshold" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(PrivateBenefitVacc+ExternalBenefitVacc-IF(CoverageAtBirth<1-1/BasicRepNum,LossPerInfection,0))<1E-6" .

hemo:7006352 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's free-provision split of a vaccination's benefit reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With BasicRepNum 5, CoverageAtBirth 0.75 and LossPerInfection 500, the article's free-provision case, the cells must hold GBP 100 of private benefit and GBP 400 of external benefit. Expected result TRUE. Doubling or halving either cell gives FALSE, and cells that read the external share at the 60% coverage, 250 and 250, give FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's free-provision split of a vaccination's benefit reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-VEXT-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-free-provision-split-of-a-vaccination-s-benefit-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(PrivateBenefitVacc-100)<1E-6,ABS(ExternalBenefitVacc-400)<1E-6)" .

hemo:7006353 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Private and external benefit of a vaccination at the article's 60% coverage"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At the article's voluntary coverage of 0.6, with R_0 of 5 and a loss of GBP 500 per infection, the unvaccinated risk is 0.5, so the vaccinated child captures GBP 250 and unvaccinated children receive the other GBP 250, which is 500/(5 × 0.4). The two add to the GBP 500 marginal social benefit the article compares with the GBP 250 cost. Illustrative inputs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Private and external benefit of a vaccination at the article's 60% coverage" ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_0 = 5; v = 0.6; L = 500; pi = 0.5; MPB = 250; MEB = 250" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-VEXT-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "private-and-external-benefit-of-a-vaccination-at-the-article-s-60-coverage" .

hemo:7006354 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Private and external benefit of a vaccination at the article's 75% coverage"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At 75% coverage the unvaccinated risk is 0.2, so the vaccinated child captures GBP 100, exactly the GBP 100 of time and travel the parent still bears under free provision, and unvaccinated children receive GBP 400, which is 500/(5 × 0.25). The external share has risen from 0.5 to 0.8, as in the article's interpretation. Illustrative inputs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Private and external benefit of a vaccination at the article's 75% coverage" ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_0 = 5; v = 0.75; L = 500; pi = 0.2; MPB = 100; MEB = 400" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-VEXT-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "private-and-external-benefit-of-a-vaccination-at-the-article-s-75-coverage" .

hemo:7006355 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No private or external benefit from a vaccination at the herd immunity threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At the threshold coverage of 0.8 no infections remain, so one more vaccination prevents nothing and both benefits are 0, the reason the article's 200 vaccinations above the threshold under a universal requirement add GBP 50,000 of cost and no benefit. Illustrative inputs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No private or external benefit from a vaccination at the herd immunity threshold" ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_0 = 5; v = 0.8; L = 500; pi = 0; MPB = 0; MEB = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-VEXT-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-private-or-external-benefit-from-a-vaccination-at-the-herd-immunity-threshold" .

hemo:7006356 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Families compare expected loss with private cost at the steady-state infection risk"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every family has the same information, the same L and c, and acts on the steady-state risk pi, as Bauch and Earn assume when all individuals are given the same information and use it in the same way to assess risks; Goodkin-Gold and colleagues take homogeneous, risk-neutral consumers in the steady state without discounting. At v_vol families are indifferent, so v_vol is the share that vaccinates rather than each family's choice. Bauch and Earn read the risks as perceived risks, so a change in perceived cost, such as during a vaccine scare, moves v_vol." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Families compare expected loss with private cost at the steady-state infection risk" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-VEXT-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "families-compare-expected-loss-with-private-cost-at-the-steady-state-infection-risk" .

hemo:7006357 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "No voluntary vaccination once the cost ratio reaches the risk with no vaccination"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "c is zero or above and L above zero. Bauch and Earn show that the equilibrium is never to vaccinate when r is at least pi_0, the risk with no vaccination, which is 1 minus 1/R_0 by their Eq. 15 at p of 0 (derived); Goodkin-Gold and colleagues reach the same case when R_0 is at most 1/(1 minus c/(theta H)). The term max(1 minus r, 1/R_0) returns 0 in that case, and returns Eq. 17 of Bauch and Earn otherwise." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No voluntary vaccination once the cost ratio reaches the risk with no vaccination" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-VEXT-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-voluntary-vaccination-once-the-cost-ratio-reaches-the-risk-with-no-vaccination" .

hemo:7006358 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Voluntary vaccination coverage from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000927 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the family's private cost in a cell named PrivateCostVacc, the loss per infection in LossPerInfection and R_0 in BasicRepNum, the first formula returns r in a cell named CostLossRatio and the second returns v_vol in a cell named VoluntaryCoverage. For the article's inputs 250, 500 and 5 the cells return 0.5 and 0.6, and with 100 in place of 250 they return 0.2 and 0.75." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Voluntary vaccination coverage from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-VEXT-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "voluntary-vaccination-coverage-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=PrivateCostVacc/LossPerInfection; =1-1/(BasicRepNum*MAX(1-CostLossRatio,1/BasicRepNum))" .

hemo:7006359 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Unvaccinated risk at the voluntary coverage cell equals the cost ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At an interior equilibrium the unvaccinated child's risk at the coverage in VoluntaryCoverage, 1 minus 1/(R_0(1 minus v_vol)), must equal CostLossRatio, the indifference condition of Bauch and Earn, and in the corner case the cell must hold 0. Expected result TRUE. At the article's inputs 250, 500 and 5 the cell holds 0.6; doubled to 1.2 the risk computes as 2 and halved to 0.3 as 0.714, both FALSE, a cell of 0.6 when the private cost is 100 gives a risk of 0.5 against 0.2 and FALSE, and a cell of minus 1 from Eq. 17 used alone at a cost of 450 gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Unvaccinated risk at the voluntary coverage cell equals the cost ratio" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-VEXT-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "unvaccinated-risk-at-the-voluntary-coverage-cell-equals-the-cost-ratio" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF(CostLossRatio<1-1/BasicRepNum,ABS(1-1/(BasicRepNum*(1-VoluntaryCoverage))-CostLossRatio)<1E-9,VoluntaryCoverage=0)" .

hemo:7006360 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's free-provision voluntary coverage reproduced"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With PrivateCostVacc 100, LossPerInfection 500 and BasicRepNum 5, the article's free-provision case, VoluntaryCoverage must hold 0.75. Expected result TRUE. A doubled cell of 1.5 or a halved cell of 0.375 gives FALSE, and a cell computed with the full resource cost of 250 in place of the private cost returns 0.6 and gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's free-provision voluntary coverage reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-VEXT-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-free-provision-voluntary-coverage-reproduced" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(VoluntaryCoverage-0.75)<1E-9" .

hemo:7006361 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Voluntary coverage when parents bear the article's full GBP 250 cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's example each vaccination costs the parent GBP 250 and an infection costs GBP 500, so r is 0.5 and uptake settles where the unvaccinated risk is 0.5, at 1 minus 1/(5 × 0.5), a coverage of 0.6, twenty percentage points short of the 0.8 threshold. Illustrative inputs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Voluntary coverage when parents bear the article's full GBP 250 cost" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 250; L = 500; r = 0.5; R_0 = 5; v_vol = 0.6" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-VEXT-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "voluntary-coverage-when-parents-bear-the-article-s-full-gbp-250-cost" .

hemo:7006362 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Voluntary coverage under free provision with GBP 100 of time and travel"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When the health service pays the GBP 150 for the vaccine and its delivery, parents still bear GBP 100 of time and travel, so r is 0.2 and coverage settles at 1 minus 1/(5 × 0.8), or 0.75, the article's step 4, five percentage points short of the threshold. Illustrative inputs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Voluntary coverage under free provision with GBP 100 of time and travel" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 100; L = 500; r = 0.2; R_0 = 5; v_vol = 0.75" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-VEXT-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "voluntary-coverage-under-free-provision-with-gbp-100-of-time-and-travel" .

hemo:7006363 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Voluntary coverage reaches the threshold only at zero private cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With no private cost at all r is 0 and v_vol equals the threshold of 0.8, the limiting case in which an unvaccinated child's risk has to fall to zero before families stop vaccinating (derived). Any positive private cost leaves coverage below 0.8. Illustrative inputs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Voluntary coverage reaches the threshold only at zero private cost" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 0; L = 500; r = 0; R_0 = 5; v_vol = 0.8" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-VEXT-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "voluntary-coverage-reaches-the-threshold-only-at-zero-private-cost" .

hemo:7006364 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No voluntary vaccination when the cost ratio exceeds the risk with no vaccination"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "If the private cost were GBP 450 against a loss of GBP 500, r of 0.9 would exceed the risk with no vaccination of 0.8, so no family vaccinates, the pure never-vaccinate equilibrium of Bauch and Earn. Eq. 17 used alone would return minus 1. Illustrative inputs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No voluntary vaccination when the cost ratio exceeds the risk with no vaccination" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 450; L = 500; r = 0.9; R_0 = 5; v_vol = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-VEXT-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-voluntary-vaccination-when-the-cost-ratio-exceeds-the-risk-with-no-vaccination" .

hemo:7006365 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Poisson arrivals at a constant rate in the six-bed loss model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Patients arrive one at a time and independently at a constant mean rate lambda, as de Bruin and colleagues assume, and the formula describes the steady state reached when that rate has held long enough. Green reports that the Poisson process represents unscheduled arrivals to intensive care units, obstetrics units and emergency departments well; booked elective admissions and weekday patterns break the assumption and call for a time-dependent model or simulation. A loss system reaches a steady state at any load, unlike a queue, because refused patients do not accumulate (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Poisson arrivals at a constant rate in the six-bed loss model" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CAPP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "poisson-arrivals-at-a-constant-rate-in-the-six-bed-loss-model" .

hemo:7006366 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Refused patients leave the six-bed unit and do not wait"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "A patient who finds all six beds occupied is refused and leaves the unit, which in practice means diversion to another hospital or admission to another ward, and does not wait or come back later. If patients who find the unit full wait for a bed instead, the measure that matters is the probability of delay of the M/M/s model in Green's equation 11-1, not P_block." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Refused patients leave the six-bed unit and do not wait" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CAPP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "refused-patients-leave-the-six-bed-unit-and-do-not-wait" .

hemo:7006367 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Any length of stay distribution with the same mean in the six-bed loss model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "De Bruin and colleagues note that the loss model is insensitive to the length of stay distribution and valid for general service times, so only the mean stay L_bar enters. Stays are independent of each other and of the arrival process." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Any length of stay distribution with the same mean in the six-bed loss model" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CAPP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "any-length-of-stay-distribution-with-the-same-mean-in-the-six-bed-loss-model" .

hemo:7006368 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Six interchangeable staffed beds and arrivals counted as demand"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "All six beds can take any patient of the unit and no extra bed is opened when the unit is full, and lambda counts every patient who needs a bed, refused patients included. Hospital systems usually record only admissions, so de Bruin and colleagues estimate lambda from the average number of occupied beds, which the hospital system does record, by solving their expression (iii) numerically." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Six interchangeable staffed beds and arrivals counted as demand" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-CAPP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "six-interchangeable-staffed-beds-and-arrivals-counted-as-demand" .

hemo:7006369 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Six-bed Erlang loss probability, refusals and occupancy from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000928 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the arrival rate per day in a cell named ArrivalsPerDay and the mean stay in days in MeanStay, the four formulas return the offered load in a cell named OfferedLoad, the refusal probability in BlockProbSixBeds, the refusals a year in RefusalsPerYear and the achieved occupancy in AchievedOccupancy. For de Bruin and colleagues' coronary care unit, with 3.52 and 1.694, they return 5.96288, 0.2623, 337.02 and 0.7331." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Six-bed Erlang loss probability, refusals and occupancy from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-CAPP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "six-bed-erlang-loss-probability-refusals-and-occupancy-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ArrivalsPerDay*MeanStay; =(OfferedLoad^6/720)/(1+OfferedLoad+OfferedLoad^2/2+OfferedLoad^3/6+OfferedLoad^4/24+OfferedLoad^5/120+OfferedLoad^6/720); =365*ArrivalsPerDay*BlockProbSixBeds; =OfferedLoad*(1-BlockProbSixBeds)/6" .

hemo:7006370 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Six-bed refusal probability matches the Poisson ratio route"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "BlockProbSixBeds is compared with the Poisson probability of exactly 6 divided by the Poisson probability of 6 or fewer, at a mean equal to the offered load, a route through POISSON.DIST that does not use the term-by-term sum (the two are equal because the factor exp(-a) cancels, derived). Expected result TRUE. A cell written as the Poisson overflow 1-POISSON.DIST(6,OfferedLoad,TRUE) returns 0.3877 for the coronary care unit against 0.2623 and gives FALSE, and doubling or halving the cell also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Six-bed refusal probability matches the Poisson ratio route" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CAPP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "six-bed-refusal-probability-matches-the-poisson-ratio-route" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(BlockProbSixBeds-POISSON.DIST(6,OfferedLoad,FALSE)/POISSON.DIST(6,OfferedLoad,TRUE))<1E-9" .

hemo:7006371 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Six-bed achieved occupancy matches admitted patients times stay"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "AchievedOccupancy times six beds times 365 days, the occupied bed-days a year, is compared with the patients admitted in a year, annual arrivals less RefusalsPerYear, times MeanStay, which is Little's law of HE-FM-DES-003 applied to the admitted patients. Expected result TRUE. A cell that leaves out the refusals, OfferedLoad/6, returns 0.9938 for the coronary care unit against 0.7331 and gives FALSE, and doubling or halving the cell also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Six-bed achieved occupancy matches admitted patients times stay" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CAPP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "six-bed-achieved-occupancy-matches-admitted-patients-times-stay" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(AchievedOccupancy*6*365-(365*ArrivalsPerDay-RefusalsPerYear)*MeanStay)<1E-6" .

hemo:7006372 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Coronary care unit cells reproduce de Bruin and colleagues' Table 7"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With ArrivalsPerDay 3.52 and MeanStay 1.694, BlockProbSixBeds and AchievedOccupancy agree with the 26.2% of refused admissions and the 73.3% occupancy in de Bruin and colleagues' Table 7 to within 0.0005. Expected result TRUE. Entering the roughly 2.60 patients a day actually admitted as ArrivalsPerDay returns 0.1470 and 0.6262 and gives FALSE, and doubling or halving either cell also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Coronary care unit cells reproduce de Bruin and colleagues' Table 7" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-CAPP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "coronary-care-unit-cells-reproduce-de-bruin-and-colleagues-table-7" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(BlockProbSixBeds-0.262)<0.0005,ABS(AchievedOccupancy-0.733)<0.0005)" .

hemo:7006373 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "De Bruin and colleagues' six-bed coronary care unit in 2006"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "De Bruin and colleagues' coronary care unit had 6 operational beds in 2006, an estimated 3.52 arrivals a day and a mean stay of 1.694 days, so the offered load is 5.96 beds. The formula refuses 26.2% of arrivals and the unit runs at 73.3% occupancy, the refused fraction and occupancy rate in their Table 7; that is about 337 refused patients a year (computed here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "De Bruin and colleagues' six-bed coronary care unit in 2006" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 3.52; L_bar = 1.694; a = 5.96288; P_block = 0.2623; R = 337.02; o_ach = 0.7331" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CAPP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "de-bruin-and-colleagues-six-bed-coronary-care-unit-in-2006" .

hemo:7006374 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Six-bed unit held to 5% refused admissions"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative inputs of 1.48 arrivals a day and a mean stay of 2 days give an offered load of 2.96 beds, at which the six-bed unit refuses 5.0% of arrivals, about 27 a year, and runs at 46.9% occupancy, below the 50% that de Bruin and colleagues give for a small unit held to 5% refused admissions." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Six-bed unit held to 5% refused admissions" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 1.48; L_bar = 2; a = 2.96; P_block = 0.05; R = 27; o_ach = 0.4687" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CAPP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "six-bed-unit-held-to-5-refused-admissions" .

hemo:7006375 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Overloaded six-bed unit with five times the load it has beds for"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative inputs of 6 arrivals a day and a mean stay of 5 days give an offered load of 30 beds. The unit is almost always full, with achieved occupancy of 96.2%, and refuses 80.8% of arrivals, about 1,769 patients a year. As the load grows the achieved occupancy approaches 1 and P_block approaches 1 minus 6 / a (derived), a limiting case in which a full unit says nothing about how many patients it turns away." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Overloaded six-bed unit with five times the load it has beds for" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 6; L_bar = 5; a = 30; P_block = 0.8076; R = 1768.62; o_ach = 0.9621" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-CAPP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "overloaded-six-bed-unit-with-five-times-the-load-it-has-beds-for" .

hemo:7006376 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Linear partial value function on the MCDA criterion range"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Equal changes in performance anywhere in the range are equally valued, the linear case that the first ISPOR task force appendix assumes for its criteria A and B. For its criterion C, where a change near the bottom of the range is valued more than the same change near the top, the appendix uses a nonlinear partial value function, which it says could be developed by the bisection method, and this formula does not apply." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Linear partial value function on the MCDA criterion range" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MCDH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "linear-partial-value-function-on-the-mcda-criterion-range" .

hemo:7006377 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Performance inside the fixed range of the MCDA criterion"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "x lies in the range whose ends are x_worst and x_best, so v is at least 0 and at most 100; a level outside the range returns a score below 0 or above 100 (derived). For criteria reused across decisions, as in HTA, the second ISPOR report asks for a range covering the best and worst performance that could realistically occur, a global or fixed scale, rather than the range of the technologies in hand." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Performance inside the fixed range of the MCDA criterion" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MCDH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "performance-inside-the-fixed-range-of-the-mcda-criterion" .

hemo:7006378 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Interval meaning of a partial value score in MCDA"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The score is an interval scale whose zero is the bottom of the range. For a range of 6 (scored 0) to 10 (scored 100) with a linear partial value function, the second ISPOR report states that going from 6 to 8 is as good as going from 8 to 10, but not that 10 is twice as good as 8." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Interval meaning of a partial value score in MCDA" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MCDH-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "interval-meaning-of-a-partial-value-score-in-mcda" .

hemo:7006379 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Excel linear partial value score for an MCDA criterion"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000929 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the performance in a cell named PerformanceLevel and the range ends in cells named WorstPerformance and BestPerformance, the formula returns the score in a cell named PartialValueScore. For the article's technology A the cells hold 0.6, 0 and 0.8 and the formula returns 75. For a lower-is-better criterion the higher level goes in WorstPerformance and the formula is unchanged. A range with equal ends, or two blank end cells, returns #DIV/0!." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Excel linear partial value score for an MCDA criterion" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-MCDH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "excel-linear-partial-value-score-for-an-mcda-criterion" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=100*(PerformanceLevel-WorstPerformance)/(BestPerformance-WorstPerformance)" .

hemo:7006380 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Partial value score cell matches the distance from the best end of the range"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "PartialValueScore is compared with a route that measures from the best end of the range, 100 less 100 times (BestPerformance less PerformanceLevel) over (BestPerformance less WorstPerformance), which does not reuse the cell's own expression. Expected result TRUE. With the ISPOR appendix's criterion B (PerformanceLevel 8, WorstPerformance 10, BestPerformance 5), a cell written with the two ends swapped returns 60 in place of 40 and gives FALSE; for the article's technology A, doubling the cell to 150 or halving it to 37.5 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Partial value score cell matches the distance from the best end of the range" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MCDH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "partial-value-score-cell-matches-the-distance-from-the-best-end-of-the-range" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(PartialValueScore-(100-100*(BestPerformance-PerformanceLevel)/(BestPerformance-WorstPerformance)))<1E-6" .

hemo:7006381 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Health gain score of technology A in the MCDA in HTA example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative example, health gain runs over a fixed range of 0 to 0.8 QALYs per patient with a linear value function. Technology A gains 0.6 QALYs and scores 75; technology B, with 0.4 QALYs, scores 50 in the same way." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health gain score of technology A in the MCDA in HTA example" ;
    hemo:9000014 "x = 0.6; x_worst = 0; x_best = 0.8; v = 75" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MCDH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-gain-score-of-technology-a-in-the-mcda-in-hta-example" .

hemo:7006382 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Lower-is-better criterion score in the ISPOR MCDA appendix"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The first ISPOR task force appendix scores its criterion B, on which lower performance is better, over a range of 5 to 10 bb, so 10 bb is the worst level and 5 bb the best. Alternative 1, at 8 bb, scores 40 and alternative 2, at 6.5 bb, scores 70, as in the appendix's Table A2, and in its scenario analysis 6 bb scores 80. Its criterion A, on which higher is better, runs over 65 to 90 aa and gives 80 for 85 aa and 32 for 73 aa." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Lower-is-better criterion score in the ISPOR MCDA appendix" ;
    hemo:9000014 "x = 8; x_worst = 10; x_best = 5; v = 40" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MCDH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "lower-is-better-criterion-score-in-the-ispor-mcda-appendix" .

hemo:7006383 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Health gain score at the bottom of the range for technology C"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Technology C in the article has no health gain, the bottom of the 0 to 0.8 QALY range, and scores 0, the limiting case. A score of 0 marks the worst end of the chosen range, not the absence of value on the criterion." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health gain score at the bottom of the range for technology C" ;
    hemo:9000014 "x = 0; x_worst = 0; x_best = 0.8; v = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MCDH-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-gain-score-at-the-bottom-of-the-range-for-technology-c" .

hemo:7006384 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Preference independence of criteria in the additive MCDA value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "How much stakeholders care about performance on one criterion does not depend on performance on the others, the preference independence that the second ISPOR report requires of an additive model. It names separate criteria for health gain and severity as a likely failure, since the preference for a gain in health may depend on baseline health, and warns that an additive model can then give a positive value to an alternative that generates no health gain." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Preference independence of criteria in the additive MCDA value" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MCDH-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "preference-independence-of-criteria-in-the-additive-mcda-value" .

hemo:7006385 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Swing points judged over the ranges of the partial value scores"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each criterion's points value the swing over the same range on which its partial value scores run, so the weights are scaling constants showing the rate at which changes in one criterion compensate changes in another. The second ISPOR report finds this condition best met by swing weighting and decompositional methods, and notes that direct rating tends to give flatter weights." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Swing points judged over the ranges of the partial value scores" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MCDH-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "swing-points-judged-over-the-ranges-of-the-partial-value-scores" .

hemo:7006386 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Positive total of swing points in the MCDA weights"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The points are zero or above with a positive total. Only their ratios matter, because multiplying every point by the same constant leaves each weight unchanged (derived), so the 100-point anchor of the elicitation need not appear among the inputs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Positive total of swing points in the MCDA weights" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MCDH-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "positive-total-of-swing-points-in-the-mcda-weights" .

hemo:7006387 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Non-overlapping MCDA criteria without double counting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "No two criteria measure the same consequence. The second ISPOR report gives cost-effectiveness included as a criterion alongside cost or effectiveness criteria as an example of double counting, which would enter one consequence twice in V." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Non-overlapping MCDA criteria without double counting" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MCDH-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "non-overlapping-mcda-criteria-without-double-counting" .

hemo:7006388 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Excel swing weights and additive overall value for three MCDA criteria"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000930 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Swing points go in cells named SwingPointsFirst, SwingPointsSecond and SwingPointsThird and the partial value scores in ValueFirstCriterion, ValueSecondCriterion and ValueThirdCriterion. The first three formulas return the weights in cells named WeightFirstCriterion, WeightSecondCriterion and WeightThirdCriterion and the last returns the overall value in a cell named OverallValue; for the article's technology A they return 0.5, 0.3, 0.2 and 72.5. With many criteria, =SUMPRODUCT(SwingPointsRow,PartialValueRow)/SUM(SwingPointsRow) gives the same total from a row of points and a row of scores of equal length." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Excel swing weights and additive overall value for three MCDA criteria" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-MCDH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "excel-swing-weights-and-additive-overall-value-for-three-mcda-criteria" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SwingPointsFirst/(SwingPointsFirst+SwingPointsSecond+SwingPointsThird); =SwingPointsSecond/(SwingPointsFirst+SwingPointsSecond+SwingPointsThird); =SwingPointsThird/(SwingPointsFirst+SwingPointsSecond+SwingPointsThird); =WeightFirstCriterion*ValueFirstCriterion+WeightSecondCriterion*ValueSecondCriterion+WeightThirdCriterion*ValueThirdCriterion" .

hemo:7006389 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "MCDA overall value cell matches the points-weighted mean of the scores"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "OverallValue is compared with the scores weighted by the swing points and divided by the total points, a route that does not use the weight cells. Expected result TRUE. For the article's technology A, a cell that multiplies the scores by the raw points without dividing returns 14,500 and gives FALSE, an unweighted mean of the three scores returns 75 and gives FALSE, and doubling the cell to 145 or halving it to 36.25 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "MCDA overall value cell matches the points-weighted mean of the scores" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MCDH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "mcda-overall-value-cell-matches-the-points-weighted-mean-of-the-scores" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(OverallValue-(SwingPointsFirst*ValueFirstCriterion+SwingPointsSecond*ValueSecondCriterion+SwingPointsThird*ValueThirdCriterion)/(SwingPointsFirst+SwingPointsSecond+SwingPointsThird))<1E-6" .

hemo:7006390 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "MCDA swing weight cells add to one"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The three weight cells are added and compared with 1. Expected result TRUE. Weights written as points over 100 in place of points over their total give 1, 0.6 and 0.4 for the article's points, a sum of 2, and FALSE; doubling WeightFirstCriterion to 1 or halving it to 0.25 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "MCDA swing weight cells add to one" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MCDH-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "mcda-swing-weight-cells-add-to-one" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(WeightFirstCriterion+WeightSecondCriterion+WeightThirdCriterion-1)<1E-9" .

hemo:7006391 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Overall value of technology A in the MCDA in HTA example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative swing points are 100 for health gain, 60 for severity and 40 for strength of evidence, a total of 200, so the weights are 0.50, 0.30 and 0.20. Technology A scores 75 on health gain, 50 for a moderate condition and 100 for high-strength evidence, an overall value of 37.5 plus 15 plus 20, or 72.5. With the same weights technology B (50, 100 and 50) gives 65 and technology C (0, 100 and 100) gives 50." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Overall value of technology A in the MCDA in HTA example" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_1 = 100; p_2 = 60; p_3 = 40; w_1 = 0.5; w_2 = 0.3; w_3 = 0.2; v_1 = 75; v_2 = 50; v_3 = 100; V = 72.5" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MCDH-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "overall-value-of-technology-a-in-the-mcda-in-hta-example" .

hemo:7006392 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Technology C first at a health gain weight of 0.25 in the MCDA example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's stability check the health gain weight falls to 0.25 while severity and evidence share the rest in the ratio 60 to 40, weights of 0.25, 0.45 and 0.30, entered here as points of 25, 45 and 30. Technology C, with no health gain, then scores 75, ahead of B at 72.5 and A at 71.25 computed in the same way." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Technology C first at a health gain weight of 0.25 in the MCDA example" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_1 = 25; p_2 = 45; p_3 = 30; w_1 = 0.25; w_2 = 0.45; w_3 = 0.3; v_1 = 0; v_2 = 100; v_3 = 100; V = 75" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MCDH-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "technology-c-first-at-a-health-gain-weight-of-0-25-in-the-mcda-example" .

hemo:7006393 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Three-way tie of the MCDA example at a health gain weight of two sevenths"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Points of 2, 3 and 2 give weights of 2/7, 3/7 and 2/7, a health gain weight of about 0.29 with severity and evidence still in the ratio 60 to 40. Technology A then scores 500/7, about 71.43, and B (50, 100 and 50) and C (0, 100 and 100) score the same, the tie that the article reports at w = 2/7 (checked here with exact fractions). Below this weight C ranks first." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Three-way tie of the MCDA example at a health gain weight of two sevenths" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_1 = 2; p_2 = 3; p_3 = 2; w_1 = 0.2857; w_2 = 0.4286; w_3 = 0.2857; v_1 = 75; v_2 = 50; v_3 = 100; V = 71.4286" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MCDH-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "three-way-tie-of-the-mcda-example-at-a-health-gain-weight-of-two-sevenths" .

hemo:7006394 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "ISPOR MCDA appendix alternative 1 with unrounded swing weights"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The first ISPOR task force appendix gives 60, 100 and 80 points to its criteria A, B and C, and its alternative 1 scores 80, 40 and 65. Unrounded weights of 0.25, 0.4167 and 0.3333 give an overall value of 58.33; the appendix rounds the weights to 0.25, 0.42 and 0.33 and prints 58.25. Its alternative 2 (32, 70 and 55) gives 55.5 against the printed 55.55, so alternative 1 ranks first either way." ;
    hemo:9000009 "ISPOR MCDA appendix alternative 1 with unrounded swing weights" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_1 = 60; p_2 = 100; p_3 = 80; w_1 = 0.25; w_2 = 0.4167; w_3 = 0.3333; v_1 = 80; v_2 = 40; v_3 = 65; V = 58.3333" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MCDH-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ispor-mcda-appendix-alternative-1-with-unrounded-swing-weights" .

hemo:7006395 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Two cost types with constant marginal cost in the screening check"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The supplier's cost is theta q with theta equal to theta_L or theta_H, as in (2.1) and (2.2) of Laffont and Martimort, with the fixed cost set to zero as the authors do after (2.6). A supplier with more than two types, or with a cost that is not linear in output, needs the constraints between every pair of types." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two cost types with constant marginal cost in the screening check" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MECH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-cost-types-with-constant-marginal-cost-in-the-screening-check" .

hemo:7006396 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Outside option of zero for both supplier types"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The participation constraints (2.11) and (2.12) compare each rent with zero, the authors' normalisation of the status quo utility level. A supplier with a positive outside option U_0 needs each rent to be at least U_0, which HE-CF-MECH-001 handles." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Outside option of zero for both supplier types" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MECH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "outside-option-of-zero-for-both-supplier-types" .

hemo:7006397 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Weak inequalities and indifferent types in the screening check"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Definition 2.1 of Laffont and Martimort requires each type to weakly prefer its own contract, so a slack of exactly zero passes and an indifferent type is taken to choose the contract intended for it. The authors note that strict preference can be obtained at an arbitrarily small cost to the purchaser." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Weak inequalities and indifferent types in the screening check" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MECH-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "weak-inequalities-and-indifferent-types-in-the-screening-check" .

hemo:7006398 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Observable and enforceable quantity and transfer"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Laffont and Martimort take the quantity and the transfer to be observable and verifiable by a third party, so each contract can be enforced as written. Only the supplier's cost is hidden." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Observable and enforceable quantity and transfer" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MECH-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "observable-and-enforceable-quantity-and-transfer" .

hemo:7006399 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Excel screening menu check with rents and incentive slacks"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000931 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the menu in cells named TransferLow, OutputLow, TransferHigh and OutputHigh and the two unit costs in CostLow and CostHigh, the formulas return the rents in cells named RentLow and RentHigh, the incentive slacks in IcSlackLow and IcSlackHigh, and TRUE in MenuFeasible when all four are at least zero. The ROUND to six decimals stops a binding constraint failing on floating-point residue when costs such as 4.6 are typed." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Excel screening menu check with rents and incentive slacks" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-MECH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "excel-screening-menu-check-with-rents-and-incentive-slacks" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=TransferLow-CostLow*OutputLow; =TransferHigh-CostHigh*OutputHigh; =RentLow-(TransferHigh-CostLow*OutputHigh); =RentHigh-(TransferLow-CostHigh*OutputLow); =AND(ROUND(IcSlackLow,6)>=0,ROUND(IcSlackHigh,6)>=0,ROUND(RentLow,6)>=0,ROUND(RentHigh,6)>=0)" .

hemo:7006400 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Low-cost slack at the first-best menu equals the mimicking gain"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the first-best menu of HE-EX-MECH-002 typed in, RentLow is zero and IcSlackLow is compared with minus (CostHigh minus CostLow) times OutputHigh, the gain that (2.18) of Laffont and Martimort gives a low-cost type that mimics when the high-cost rent is zero, at a tolerance of 0.005. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a slack that evaluates the high-cost contract at the high cost, which gives 0, and doubling the IcSlackLow cell (minus 200) or halving it (minus 50) also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Low-cost slack at the first-best menu equals the mimicking gain" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MECH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "low-cost-slack-at-the-first-best-menu-equals-the-mimicking-gain" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(RentLow)<0.005,ABS(IcSlackLow+(CostHigh-CostLow)*OutputHigh)<0.005)" .

hemo:7006401 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Second-best menu is feasible with the high-cost slack set by the output gap"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the second-best menu of HE-EX-MECH-001 typed in, MenuFeasible is TRUE, IcSlackLow is zero and IcSlackHigh equals (CostHigh minus CostLow) times (OutputLow minus OutputHigh), the slack of 40 implied by the authors' check of the omitted incentive constraint after Proposition 2.1 (derived), at a tolerance of 0.005. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a low-cost transfer of 480 that leaves out the rent (IcSlackLow minus 80, MenuFeasible FALSE), and doubling the IcSlackHigh cell (80) or halving it (20) also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Second-best menu is feasible with the high-cost slack set by the output gap" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MECH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "second-best-menu-is-feasible-with-the-high-cost-slack-set-by-the-output-gap" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(MenuFeasible,ABS(IcSlackLow)<0.005,ABS(IcSlackHigh-(CostHigh-CostLow)*(OutputLow-OutputHigh))<0.005)" .

hemo:7006402 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Second-best procurement menu passes every screening constraint"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative figures. A commissioner buys elective procedures from a provider whose unit cost is GBP 4,000 or GBP 5,000 and offers 120 procedures for GBP 560,000 or 80 procedures for GBP 400,000, the second-best menu of HE-EX-MECH-004. The low-cost type earns a rent of 80 (GBP 80,000) and would earn the same 80 on the other contract, so its incentive constraint binds; the high-cost type earns no rent and would lose 40 by taking the larger contract. All four constraints hold." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Second-best procurement menu passes every screening constraint" ;
    hemo:9000014 "t_L = 560; q_L = 120; t_H = 400; q_H = 80; theta_L = 4; theta_H = 5; U_L = 80; U_H = 0; IC_L = 0; IC_H = 40; feasible = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MECH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "second-best-procurement-menu-passes-every-screening-constraint" .

hemo:7006403 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "First-best procurement menu fails the low-cost type's incentive constraint"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative figures. Paying each type exactly its cost for its efficient output, 120 procedures for GBP 480,000 or 100 for GBP 500,000, leaves both types no rent and satisfies both participation constraints. The low-cost type would earn 100 (GBP 100,000) by taking the high-cost contract, which is (5 minus 4) x 100, so IC_L is minus 100 and the menu is not incentive feasible, the failure Laffont and Martimort show for their first-best contracts." ;
    hemo:9000009 "First-best procurement menu fails the low-cost type's incentive constraint" ;
    hemo:9000014 "t_L = 480; q_L = 120; t_H = 500; q_H = 100; theta_L = 4; theta_H = 5; U_L = 0; U_H = 0; IC_L = -100; IC_H = 120; feasible = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MECH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "first-best-procurement-menu-fails-the-low-cost-type-s-incentive-constraint" .

hemo:7006404 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Pooling contract passes the screening constraints with a rent to the low-cost type"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative figures. Offering both types the same contract, 100 procedures for GBP 500,000, satisfies both incentive constraints trivially, a pooling contract in the authors' terms. The high-cost type's participation constraint is the one that binds, and the low-cost type keeps a rent of 100 (GBP 100,000)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pooling contract passes the screening constraints with a rent to the low-cost type" ;
    hemo:9000014 "t_L = 500; q_L = 100; t_H = 500; q_H = 100; theta_L = 4; theta_H = 5; U_L = 100; U_H = 0; IC_L = 0; IC_H = 0; feasible = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MECH-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pooling-contract-passes-the-screening-constraints-with-a-rent-to-the-low-cost-type" .

hemo:7006405 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Linear marginal value of output in the second-best menu"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The purchaser's value is S(q) = a q minus b q^2 / 2, so the marginal value a minus b q falls in a straight line, with a above theta_H so that both first-best outputs are positive. This functional form is chosen here for a closed form; Laffont and Martimort require only S' above zero, S'' below zero and S(0) = 0, and (2.26) and (2.28) hold for any such S." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Linear marginal value of output in the second-best menu" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MECH-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "linear-marginal-value-of-output-in-the-second-best-menu" .

hemo:7006406 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Risk-neutral purchaser offering the menu after the supplier learns its cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The purchaser maximises expected S(q) less the transfer with the prior nu at least 0 and below 1, offers the menu once the supplier knows its type (the timing of Figure 2.1 of Laffont and Martimort), and both sides are committed to the contract. The supplier is risk neutral." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Risk-neutral purchaser offering the menu after the supplier learns its cost" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MECH-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "risk-neutral-purchaser-offering-the-menu-after-the-supplier-learns-its-cost" .

hemo:7006407 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Zero outside option and no fixed cost in the second-best menu"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Both types have a status quo utility of zero and no fixed cost, the setting of Proposition 2.1. With these, and a positive solution of (2.28), serving the high-cost type is better than shutting it out (the remark in section 2.7.3 of Laffont and Martimort, which holds for any value function with S'' below zero; checked here for the linear form on a grid of 777 cases); a positive outside option is handled in HE-CF-MECH-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Zero outside option and no fixed cost in the second-best menu" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-MECH-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "zero-outside-option-and-no-fixed-cost-in-the-second-best-menu" .

hemo:7006408 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Excel second-best two-type menu from a linear marginal value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000932 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the intercept and slope of the marginal value in cells named ValueIntercept and ValueSlope, the unit costs in CostLowSB and CostHighSB and the probability of the low-cost type in ProbLowSB, the formulas return OutputLowSB, VirtualCostSB, OutputHighSB, RentLowSB, TransferLowSB and TransferHighSB in that order." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Excel second-best two-type menu from a linear marginal value" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-MECH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "excel-second-best-two-type-menu-from-a-linear-marginal-value" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(ValueIntercept-CostLowSB)/ValueSlope; =CostHighSB+ProbLowSB/(1-ProbLowSB)*(CostHighSB-CostLowSB); =MAX(0,(ValueIntercept-VirtualCostSB)/ValueSlope); =(CostHighSB-CostLowSB)*OutputHighSB; =CostLowSB*OutputLowSB+RentLowSB; =CostHighSB*OutputHighSB" .

hemo:7006409 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Second-best rent equals the low-cost type's gain from the high-cost contract"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of HE-EX-MECH-004, RentLowSB is compared with TransferHighSB less CostLowSB times OutputHighSB, what the low-cost type would earn on the high-cost contract, since its incentive constraint binds at the optimum ((2.24) of Laffont and Martimort), at a tolerance of 0.005. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a rent computed on the low-cost type's own output, 1 x 120 = 120, and doubling the RentLowSB cell (160) or halving it (40) also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Second-best rent equals the low-cost type's gain from the high-cost contract" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MECH-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "second-best-rent-equals-the-low-cost-type-s-gain-from-the-high-cost-contract" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(RentLowSB-(TransferHighSB-CostLowSB*OutputHighSB))<0.005" .

hemo:7006410 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "High-cost output satisfies the second-best first-order condition"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of HE-EX-MECH-004, where OutputHighSB is above zero, (1 minus nu) times marginal value less the high cost at OutputHighSB is compared with nu times the cost gap, the form of (2.27) of Laffont and Martimort, at a tolerance of 0.005. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows nu used in place of nu / (1 minus nu), which gives an output of 90 and a difference of minus 0.25, and doubling the OutputHighSB cell (160) or halving it (40) also gives FALSE. The test does not apply when OutputHighSB is zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "High-cost output satisfies the second-best first-order condition" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MECH-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "high-cost-output-satisfies-the-second-best-first-order-condition" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((1-ProbLowSB)*(ValueIntercept-ValueSlope*OutputHighSB-CostHighSB)-ProbLowSB*(CostHighSB-CostLowSB))<0.005" .

hemo:7006411 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "High-cost output returns to first best when the low-cost type is absent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of HE-EX-MECH-005 (ProbLowSB = 0), OutputHighSB is compared with the first-best output (ValueIntercept minus CostHighSB) / ValueSlope, 100, at a tolerance of 0.005. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a virtual cost with the cost gap added whatever the prior, which gives 80, and doubling the OutputHighSB cell (200) or halving it (50) also gives FALSE. With nu = 0.5 the test is FALSE by design, since the output is then distorted." ;
    hemo:9000009 "High-cost output returns to first best when the low-cost type is absent" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-MECH-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "high-cost-output-returns-to-first-best-when-the-low-cost-type-is-absent" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(OutputHighSB-(ValueIntercept-CostHighSB)/ValueSlope)<0.005" .

hemo:7006412 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Second-best menu for elective procedures with equal priors"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative figures. The first procedure is worth GBP 10,000 to the commissioner and each further one GBP 50 less; the provider's unit cost is GBP 4,000 or GBP 5,000 with equal probability. The low-cost type produces its first-best 120 procedures. The virtual cost of the high-cost type is 5 + 1 x 1 = 6, so it produces 80 rather than its first-best 100, and the low-cost type's rent is 1 x 80 = 80 (GBP 80,000). The transfers are GBP 560,000 and GBP 400,000." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Second-best menu for elective procedures with equal priors" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 10; b = 0.05; theta_L = 4; theta_H = 5; nu = 0.5; q_L = 120; theta_v = 6; q_H = 80; U_L = 80; t_L = 560; t_H = 400" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MECH-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "second-best-menu-for-elective-procedures-with-equal-priors" .

hemo:7006413 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Second-best menu when the low-cost type is believed absent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Limiting case with the same illustrative figures and nu = 0. The virtual cost equals the high cost, so the high-cost type produces its first-best 100 procedures and the formula leaves the low-cost type a rent of 100 (GBP 100,000) on an output it is believed never to supply." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Second-best menu when the low-cost type is believed absent" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 10; b = 0.05; theta_L = 4; theta_H = 5; nu = 0; q_L = 120; theta_v = 5; q_H = 100; U_L = 100; t_L = 580; t_H = 500" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MECH-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "second-best-menu-when-the-low-cost-type-is-believed-absent" .

hemo:7006414 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Second-best menu that shuts out the high-cost type"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative figures as above with nu = 0.9. The virtual cost is 5 + 9 x 1 = 14, above the value of the first procedure, so (2.28) has no positive solution, the high-cost type is offered nothing and the low-cost type is paid its cost of GBP 480,000 with no rent, the shut-down case of Laffont and Martimort." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Second-best menu that shuts out the high-cost type" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 10; b = 0.05; theta_L = 4; theta_H = 5; nu = 0.9; q_L = 120; theta_v = 14; q_H = 0; U_L = 0; t_L = 480; t_H = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-MECH-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "second-best-menu-that-shuts-out-the-high-cost-type" .

hemo:7006415 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for the best package of whole programmes by enumerating every package within the budget"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Programmes are whole, independent and additive on one basis (HE-AS-IPH-001, HE-AS-IPH-002), and the required programmes together cost no more than `B`; when they cost more, no package qualifies and the R and Python versions return NULL or None. Among packages with equal QALYs the first in enumeration order is returned, the package whose binary number, with programme 1 as the lowest digit, is smallest (HE-AS-IPH-003). The `H_must` line assumes `c_4` is at most `B`." .

hemo:7006419 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-IPH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000933 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "On the sheet of HE-IM-IPH-001, with a column range named MustFund (D2 to D5) holding 1 for each required programme and 0 otherwise, enter `=MMULT(E2:H2,MustFund)` in L2 for the number of required programmes in the package and `=(I2<=Budget)*(L2=SUM(MustFund))*J2` in M2, and fill both down to row 17. Then `=MAX(M2:M17)` in a cell named ForcedOptimum gives the best total with the requirement, `=MATCH(ForcedOptimum,M2:M17,0)` in BestPackageRow gives the row of PackageMatrix that holds the package, the first such row when packages tie, and `=IntegerOptimum-ForcedOptimum` in EquityRuleCost gives the QALYs forgone. MustFund needs a 0 typed for each programme not required, since MMULT returns #VALUE! when a cell is blank. When the required programmes cost more than Budget no package qualifies, and the sheet then gives a ForcedOptimum of 0, a BestPackageRow of 1 (the empty package) and an EquityRuleCost equal to IntegerOptimum, so a ForcedOptimum of 0 with a programme required reads as no qualifying package. For more than four programmes PackageMatrix needs 2^n rows and n columns." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MMULT(E2:H2,MustFund)" .

hemo:7006420 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-IPH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000933 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`ip_best <- function(name, cost, qaly, B, must = character(0)) { n <- length(cost); best <- NULL; hb <- -Inf; feas <- 0; for (k in 0:(2^n-1)) { x <- as.numeric(bitwAnd(k, 2^(0:(n-1))) > 0); if (sum(x*cost) <= B && all(x[name %in% must] == 1)) { feas <- feas+1; h <- sum(x*qaly); if (h > hb) { hb <- h; best <- x } } }; if (is.null(best)) return(NULL); list(package = name[best == 1], cost = sum(best*cost), qalys = hb, qualifying = feas) }` The strict comparison keeps the first optimal package, and `bitwAnd` limits n to 31." ;
    hemo:9000024 "ip_best <- function(name, cost, qaly, B, must = character(0)) { n <- length(cost); best <- NULL; hb <- -Inf; feas <- 0; for (k in 0:(2^n-1)) { x <- as.numeric(bitwAnd(k, 2^(0:(n-1))) > 0); if (sum(x*cost) <= B && all(x[name %in% must] == 1)) { feas <- feas+1; h <- sum(x*qaly); if (h > hb) { hb <- h; best <- x } } }; if (is.null(best)) return(NULL); list(package = name[best == 1], cost = sum(best*cost), qalys = hb, qualifying = feas) }" .

hemo:7006421 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-IPH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000933 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def ip_best(name, cost, qaly, B, must=()): pk = [[(k >> j) & 1 for j in range(len(cost))] for k in range(2**len(cost))]; ok = [x for x in pk if sum(a*c for a, c in zip(x, cost)) <= B and all(x[name.index(m)] for m in must)]; best = max(ok, key=lambda x: sum(a*q for a, q in zip(x, qaly)), default=None); return None if best is None else ([nm for nm, a in zip(name, best) if a], sum(a*c for a, c in zip(best, cost)), sum(a*q for a, q in zip(best, qaly)), len(ok))` Plain Python with no imports; `max` returns the first optimal package in enumeration order." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def ip_best(name, cost, qaly, B, must=()): pk = [[(k >> j) & 1 for j in range(len(cost))] for k in range(2**len(cost))]; ok = [x for x in pk if sum(a*c for a, c in zip(x, cost)) <= B and all(x[name.index(m)] for m in must)]; best = max(ok, key=lambda x: sum(a*q for a, q in zip(x, qaly)), default=None); return None if best is None else ([nm for nm, a in zip(name, best) if a], sum(a*c for a, c in zip(best, cost)), sum(a*q for a, q in zip(best, qaly)), len(ok))" .

hemo:7006424 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for Erlang B refusals, beds for a refusal target and cost-minimising beds"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The assumptions of HE-FM-CAPP-001 hold for every number of beds (HE-AS-CAPP-001 to HE-AS-CAPP-004). Each staffed bed costs c_b a year whether used or not and each refusal costs c_r, both constant, the article's illustrative convention; Gorunescu and colleagues balance the cost of empty beds against the cost of delayed patients instead; that convention would change the cost line of the code here but not the recursion (derived)." .

hemo:7006428 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-CAPP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000934 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With named cells ArrivalsPerDayCF, MeanStayCF, BlockTarget, BedCost and RefusalCost, enter the bed numbers 0 to 199 in `A2:A201` and name the range BedCount. Enter 1 in B2 and `=ArrivalsPerDayCF*MeanStayCF*B2/(A3+ArrivalsPerDayCF*MeanStayCF*B2)` in B3, fill it down to `B201` and name `B2:B201` ErlangBColumn. Enter `=365*ArrivalsPerDayCF*B2` in C2 and `=BedCost*A2+RefusalCost*C2` in D2, fill both down and name them RefusalsColumn and TotalCostColumn. A cell named BedsForTarget holds `=INDEX(BedCount,MATCH(TRUE,INDEX(ErlangBColumn<=BlockTarget,0),0))` and a cell named CostMinBeds holds `=INDEX(BedCount,MATCH(MIN(TotalCostColumn),TotalCostColumn,0))`. With a number of beds in BedsInPlan, `=ArrivalsPerDayCF*MeanStayCF*(1-INDEX(ErlangBColumn,BedsInPlan+1))/BedsInPlan` returns its achieved occupancy. The INDEX form with a column argument of 0 makes MATCH read the comparison as an array without array entry, so the formulas work in every Excel version; wards above 199 beds need longer columns." ;
    hemo:9000024 "A2:A201" .

hemo:7006429 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-CAPP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000934 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`erlang_ward <- function(lambda, L, Nmax = 200, target = 0.05, cb = 150000, cr = 10000) { a <- lambda*L; B <- numeric(Nmax+1); B[1] <- 1; for (k in 1:Nmax) B[k+1] <- a*B[k]/(k+a*B[k]); N <- 0:Nmax; R <- 365*lambda*B; occ <- ifelse(N > 0, a*(1-B)/N, NA); TC <- cb*N+cr*R; list(table = data.frame(N = N, B = B, R = R, occ = occ), beds_target = N[which(B <= target)[1]], beds_costmin = N[which.min(TC)]) }` Called as `w <- erlang_ward(20, 4, target = 0.01)` it gives `w$table$B[96]` of 0.01137, `w$table$R[95:97]` of 99.69, 82.99 and 68.51, `w$beds_costmin` of 95 and `w$beds_target` of 96, and `erlang_ward(2, 4, target = 0.01)$beds_target` is 15." ;
    hemo:9000024 "erlang_ward <- function(lambda, L, Nmax = 200, target = 0.05, cb = 150000, cr = 10000) { a <- lambda*L; B <- numeric(Nmax+1); B[1] <- 1; for (k in 1:Nmax) B[k+1] <- a*B[k]/(k+a*B[k]); N <- 0:Nmax; R <- 365*lambda*B; occ <- ifelse(N > 0, a*(1-B)/N, NA); TC <- cb*N+cr*R; list(table = data.frame(N = N, B = B, R = R, occ = occ), beds_target = N[which(B <= target)[1]], beds_costmin = N[which.min(TC)]) }" .

hemo:7006430 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-CAPP-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000934 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def erlang_ward(lam, L, nmax=200, target=0.05, cb=150000, cr=10000): a = lam*L; B = [1.0]; [B.append(a*B[-1]/(k+a*B[-1])) for k in range(1, nmax+1)]; R = [365*lam*b for b in B]; tc = [cb*n+cr*r for n, r in enumerate(R)]; return B, R, next(n for n, b in enumerate(B) if b <= target), min(range(nmax+1), key=tc.__getitem__)` Standard library only; `erlang_ward(20, 4, target=0.01)` returns B[95] of 0.01137, beds for the target of 96 and cost-minimising beds of 95, and `erlang_ward(20, 4, cr=5000)[3]` is 89." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def erlang_ward(lam, L, nmax=200, target=0.05, cb=150000, cr=10000): a = lam*L; B = [1.0]; [B.append(a*B[-1]/(k+a*B[-1])) for k in range(1, nmax+1)]; R = [365*lam*b for b in B]; tc = [cb*n+cr*r for n, r in enumerate(R)]; return B, R, next(n for n, b in enumerate(B) if b <= target), min(range(nmax+1), key=tc.__getitem__)" .

hemo:7006434 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for MCDA totals, ranking and a health gain weight scan for three technologies"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The assumptions of HE-FM-MCDH-001 and HE-FM-MCDH-002 hold, with a linear partial value function on health gain and the scores on the other two criteria already on their 0 to 100 scales. The weight scan keeps the ratio of the severity and evidence points fixed, as the article's stability check does." .

hemo:7006437 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-MCDH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000935 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With health gains in cells named HealthGainTechA, HealthGainTechB and HealthGainTechC, the range ends in HealthGainWorst and HealthGainBest, and points in SwingPointsHealth, SwingPointsSeverity and SwingPointsEvidence (a row named SwingPointsRow), `=100*(HealthGainTechA-HealthGainWorst)/(HealthGainBest-HealthGainWorst)` returns HealthScoreTechA. With that score beside SeverityScoreTechA and EvidenceScoreTechA in a row named ScoresTechA, `=SUMPRODUCT(SwingPointsRow,ScoresTechA)/SUM(SwingPointsRow)` returns CFValueTechA, and B and C follow in the same way; `=MAX(CFValueTechA,CFValueTechB,CFValueTechC)` returns TopValue. `=(SwingPointsSeverity*SeverityScoreTechA+SwingPointsEvidence*EvidenceScoreTechA)/(SwingPointsSeverity+SwingPointsEvidence)` returns OtherBlendTechA, and `=(OtherBlendTechC-OtherBlendTechA)/((HealthScoreTechA-OtherBlendTechA)-(HealthScoreTechC-OtherBlendTechC))` returns SwitchWeightAC, the health gain weight at which A and C tie, or #DIV/0! when their slopes are equal. For the scan, a helper column named WeightGrid holds the weights 0, 0.01 and so on up to 1, and beside it `=WeightGrid*HealthScoreTechA+(1-WeightGrid)*OtherBlendTechA` filled down gives technology A's overall value at each weight, with one such column per technology." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=100*(HealthGainTechA-HealthGainWorst)/(HealthGainBest-HealthGainWorst)" .

hemo:7006438 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-MCDH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000935 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`mcda3 <- function(x, s, e, lo, hi, p) { v <- 100*(x-lo)/(hi-lo); w <- p/sum(p); V <- w[1]*v+w[2]*s+w[3]*e; o <- (p[2]*s+p[3]*e)/(p[2]+p[3]); g <- seq(0, 1, by = 0.01); top <- sapply(g, function(a) which.max(a*v+(1-a)*o)); tie <- outer(1:3, 1:3, function(i, j) (o[j]-o[i])/((v[i]-o[i])-(v[j]-o[j]))); list(v = v, w = w, V = V, rank = rank(-V), top = top, tie = tie) }` Called as `mcda3(c(0.6, 0.4, 0), c(50, 100, 100), c(100, 50, 100), 0, 0.8, c(100, 60, 40))` it returns v = 75, 50, 0, V = 72.5, 65, 50, ranks 1, 2 and 3, tie weights of 0.2857 for every pair (NaN on the diagonal, and Inf or NaN for a pair with equal slopes) and technology 3 (C) first at the grid weights up to 0.28 and technology 1 (A) from 0.29; on an exact tie which.max returns the first technology listed." ;
    hemo:9000024 "mcda3 <- function(x, s, e, lo, hi, p) { v <- 100*(x-lo)/(hi-lo); w <- p/sum(p); V <- w[1]*v+w[2]*s+w[3]*e; o <- (p[2]*s+p[3]*e)/(p[2]+p[3]); g <- seq(0, 1, by = 0.01); top <- sapply(g, function(a) which.max(a*v+(1-a)*o)); tie <- outer(1:3, 1:3, function(i, j) (o[j]-o[i])/((v[i]-o[i])-(v[j]-o[j]))); list(v = v, w = w, V = V, rank = rank(-V), top = top, tie = tie) }" .

hemo:7006439 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-MCDH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000935 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def mcda3(x, s, e, lo, hi, p): v = [100*(xi-lo)/(hi-lo) for xi in x]; w = [pj/sum(p) for pj in p]; V = [w[0]*v[i]+w[1]*s[i]+w[2]*e[i] for i in range(3)]; o = [(p[1]*s[i]+p[2]*e[i])/(p[1]+p[2]) for i in range(3)]; tie = {(i, j): ((o[j]-o[i])/((v[i]-o[i])-(v[j]-o[j])) if (v[i]-o[i]) != (v[j]-o[j]) else None) for i in range(3) for j in range(i+1, 3)}; top = [max(range(3), key=lambda i: g/100*v[i]+(1-g/100)*o[i]) for g in range(101)]; return v, w, V, tie, top` Called as `mcda3([0.6, 0.4, 0], [50, 100, 100], [100, 50, 100], 0, 0.8, [100, 60, 40])` it returns v = [75.0, 50.0, 0.0], V = [72.5, 65.0, 50.0], tie weights of 0.2857 for the pairs (0, 1), (0, 2) and (1, 2) (None for a pair with equal slopes), and index 2 (C) first at grid steps 0 to 28 and index 0 (A) from step 29." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def mcda3(x, s, e, lo, hi, p): v = [100*(xi-lo)/(hi-lo) for xi in x]; w = [pj/sum(p) for pj in p]; V = [w[0]*v[i]+w[1]*s[i]+w[2]*e[i] for i in range(3)]; o = [(p[1]*s[i]+p[2]*e[i])/(p[1]+p[2]) for i in range(3)]; tie = {(i, j): ((o[j]-o[i])/((v[i]-o[i])-(v[j]-o[j])) if (v[i]-o[i]) != (v[j]-o[j]) else None) for i in range(3) for j in range(i+1, 3)}; top = [max(range(3), key=lambda i: g/100*v[i]+(1-g/100)*o[i]) for g in range(101)]; return v, w, V, tie, top" .

hemo:7006442 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for the second-best menu against shut-down and complete information with an outside option"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "As in HE-AS-MECH-005 to HE-AS-MECH-007, with S(q) = a q minus b q^2 / 2, a above theta_H and nu at least 0 and below 1, except that U_0 may be positive. Serving each type is assumed worthwhile under complete information (S_L minus theta_L q_L and S_FB minus theta_H q_FB both at least U_0), so `V_FB` serves both types. At a tie (2.32) holds with equality and the code reports shut-down, as the source's weak inequality does; the purchaser's payoff is the same either way." .

hemo:7006445 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-MECH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000936 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With inputs in cells named ValueInterceptCF, ValueSlopeCF, CostLowCF, CostHighCF, ProbLowCF and OutsideOption, enter `=(ValueInterceptCF-CostLowCF)/ValueSlopeCF` in OutputLowCF, `=(ValueInterceptCF-CostHighCF)/ValueSlopeCF` in OutputFirstBestHigh, `=MAX(0,(ValueInterceptCF-(CostHighCF+ProbLowCF/(1-ProbLowCF)*(CostHighCF-CostLowCF)))/ValueSlopeCF)` in OutputHighCF, `=ValueInterceptCF*OutputLowCF-ValueSlopeCF*OutputLowCF^2/2` in SurplusLow and the same form for OutputFirstBestHigh and OutputHighCF in SurplusFirstBestHigh and SurplusSecondBestHigh, then `=ProbLowCF*(SurplusLow-CostLowCF*OutputLowCF-OutsideOption)+(1-ProbLowCF)*(SurplusFirstBestHigh-CostHighCF*OutputFirstBestHigh-OutsideOption)` in PayoffFirstBest, `=ProbLowCF*(SurplusLow-CostLowCF*OutputLowCF-OutsideOption-(CostHighCF-CostLowCF)*OutputHighCF)+(1-ProbLowCF)*(SurplusSecondBestHigh-CostHighCF*OutputHighCF-OutsideOption)` in PayoffSecondBest, `=ProbLowCF*(SurplusLow-CostLowCF*OutputLowCF-OutsideOption)` in PayoffShutDown, `=MAX(PayoffSecondBest,PayoffShutDown)` in BestPayoff, `=PayoffShutDown>=PayoffSecondBest` in ShutDownChosen and `=PayoffFirstBest-BestPayoff` in CostOfHiddenCost." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(ValueInterceptCF-CostLowCF)/ValueSlopeCF" .

hemo:7006446 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-MECH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000936 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`screen_menu <- function(a, b, thL, thH, nu, U0) { qL <- (a-thL)/b; qFB <- (a-thH)/b; tv <- thH+nu/(1-nu)*(thH-thL); qSB <- max(0, (a-tv)/b); S <- function(q) a*q-b*q^2/2; VFB <- nu*(S(qL)-thL*qL-U0)+(1-nu)*(S(qFB)-thH*qFB-U0); VSB <- nu*(S(qL)-thL*qL-U0-(thH-thL)*qSB)+(1-nu)*(S(qSB)-thH*qSB-U0); Vsh <- nu*(S(qL)-thL*qL-U0); c(qSB = qSB, VFB = VFB, VSB = VSB, Vshut = Vsh, Vstar = max(VSB, Vsh), shut = as.numeric(Vsh >= VSB), loss = VFB-max(VSB, Vsh)) }` `screen_menu(10, 0.05, 4, 5, 0.5, 0)` returns qSB 80, VFB 305, VSB 260, Vshut 180, Vstar 260, shut 0 and loss 45, and `sapply(seq(0, 250, 10), function(u) screen_menu(10, 0.05, 4, 5, 0.5, u)[\"shut\"])` first returns 1 at U_0 = 160." ;
    hemo:9000024 "screen_menu <- function(a, b, thL, thH, nu, U0) { qL <- (a-thL)/b; qFB <- (a-thH)/b; tv <- thH+nu/(1-nu)*(thH-thL); qSB <- max(0, (a-tv)/b); S <- function(q) a*q-b*q^2/2; VFB <- nu*(S(qL)-thL*qL-U0)+(1-nu)*(S(qFB)-thH*qFB-U0); VSB <- nu*(S(qL)-thL*qL-U0-(thH-thL)*qSB)+(1-nu)*(S(qSB)-thH*qSB-U0); Vsh <- nu*(S(qL)-thL*qL-U0); c(qSB = qSB, VFB = VFB, VSB = VSB, Vshut = Vsh, Vstar = max(VSB, Vsh), shut = as.numeric(Vsh >= VSB), loss = VFB-max(VSB, Vsh)) }" .

hemo:7006447 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-MECH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000936 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def screen_menu(a, b, thL, thH, nu, U0): S = lambda q: a*q-b*q**2/2; qL = (a-thL)/b; qFB = (a-thH)/b; tv = thH+nu/(1-nu)*(thH-thL); qSB = max(0.0, (a-tv)/b); VFB = nu*(S(qL)-thL*qL-U0)+(1-nu)*(S(qFB)-thH*qFB-U0); VSB = nu*(S(qL)-thL*qL-U0-(thH-thL)*qSB)+(1-nu)*(S(qSB)-thH*qSB-U0); Vsh = nu*(S(qL)-thL*qL-U0); Vst = max(VSB, Vsh); return dict(qSB=qSB, VFB=VFB, VSB=VSB, Vshut=Vsh, Vstar=Vst, shut=int(Vsh >= VSB), loss=VFB-Vst)` `screen_menu(10, 0.05, 4, 5, 0.5, 200)` returns VFB 105, VSB 60, Vshut 80, Vstar 80, shut 1 and loss 25 (to floating point), and `min(u for u in range(0, 260, 10) if screen_menu(10, 0.05, 4, 5, 0.5, u)['shut'])` is 160." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def screen_menu(a, b, thL, thH, nu, U0): S = lambda q: a*q-b*q**2/2; qL = (a-thL)/b; qFB = (a-thH)/b; tv = thH+nu/(1-nu)*(thH-thL); qSB = max(0.0, (a-tv)/b); VFB = nu*(S(qL)-thL*qL-U0)+(1-nu)*(S(qFB)-thH*qFB-U0); VSB = nu*(S(qL)-thL*qL-U0-(thH-thL)*qSB)+(1-nu)*(S(qSB)-thH*qSB-U0); Vsh = nu*(S(qL)-thL*qL-U0); Vst = max(VSB, Vsh); return dict(qSB=qSB, VFB=VFB, VSB=VSB, Vshut=Vsh, Vstar=Vst, shut=int(Vsh >= VSB), loss=VFB-Vst)" .

hemo:7006450 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Two effort levels and an indicator linked to effort only through its probability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The agent chooses between a high and a low effort, the principal sees only whether a verifiable indicator is met, and the indicator is met with probability p_H after high effort and p_L after low effort, with p_H above p_L, as in section 4.2 of Laffont and Martimort. The payment can depend on the indicator and on nothing else about the agent's work. With p_H equal to p_L the indicator says nothing about effort, no bonus induces the high effort and the formula divides by zero; with p_H below p_L it returns a negative bonus and does not apply (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two effort levels and an indicator linked to effort only through its probability" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PAP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-effort-levels-and-an-indicator-linked-to-effort-only-through-its-probability" .

hemo:7006451 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Risk-neutral parties and a floor on payments to the agent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Both parties are risk neutral and a payment to the agent can fall no lower than minus ell, the limited liability constraints (4.17) and (4.18) of Laffont and Martimort. The practice in the article cannot be paid less than nothing, so ell = 0. With a risk-averse agent the trade-off is instead between insurance and efficiency, section 4.5 of the same text, which this formula does not cover. The agent's outside option is zero, and at b_star its expected payoff from the high effort is R, never below zero, so it accepts the contract (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Risk-neutral parties and a floor on payments to the agent" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PAP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "risk-neutral-parties-and-a-floor-on-payments-to-the-agent" .

hemo:7006452 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Weak incentive constraint and an indifferent agent at the smallest bonus"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The incentive constraint, p_H b minus c at least p_L b, is a weak inequality, as (4.3) and (4.15) of Laffont and Martimort are, and their Definition 4.1 calls a contract that satisfies (4.3) and (4.4), with equality allowed, incentive feasible, which the text defines as inducing a positive effort. At b_star the agent is indifferent and is taken to make the high effort. Remark 2 of the same chapter notes that other transfers can give the agent a strict preference for effort, so a principal that wants a margin against the tie pays slightly more than b_star." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Weak incentive constraint and an indifferent agent at the smallest bonus" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PAP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "weak-incentive-constraint-and-an-indifferent-agent-at-the-smallest-bonus" .

hemo:7006453 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One task and an indicator the agent cannot move without the effort"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The agent has one task and can change the chance that the indicator is met only through its effort. The article describes multitasking, where a bonus on a measured task draws effort away from unmeasured ones, and gaming such as exception reporting, which raises the recorded indicator without the review. Gaming of this kind raises p_L and with it the rent, since R rises with p_L for fixed c and p_H (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One task and an indicator the agent cannot move without the effort" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-PAP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-task-and-an-indicator-the-agent-cannot-move-without-the-effort" .

hemo:7006454 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Excel bonus, fee, expected payment and rent for a binary effort choice"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000945 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the cost of the high effort in a cell named ReviewCost, the two probabilities in ProbMetThorough and ProbMetCursory and the liability limit in LiabilityLimit, the four formulas return the bonus in a cell named BonusPerIndicator, the fee in FeeCharged, the expected payment in ExpectedPayment and the rent in PracticeRent. N() turns a blank LiabilityLimit into 0; without it MIN would ignore the blank cell, return ProbMetCursory times BonusPerIndicator as the fee and report no rent. Equal probabilities return #DIV/0! in every cell." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Excel bonus, fee, expected payment and rent for a binary effort choice" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-PAP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "excel-bonus-fee-expected-payment-and-rent-for-a-binary-effort-choice" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ReviewCost/(ProbMetThorough-ProbMetCursory); =MIN(N(LiabilityLimit),ProbMetCursory*BonusPerIndicator); =ProbMetThorough*BonusPerIndicator-FeeCharged; =ExpectedPayment-ReviewCost" .

hemo:7006455 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Bonus and rent cells match the diabetes review example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of HE-EX-PAP-001 (ReviewCost 30, ProbMetThorough 0.70, ProbMetCursory 0.45, LiabilityLimit 0), BonusPerIndicator is compared with the hand value 120 and PracticeRent with 54. Expected result TRUE. A bonus written as ReviewCost/ProbMetThorough gives 42.86 and a rent of 0, and FALSE. Doubling the bonus cell to 240 or halving it to 60, or doubling the rent cell to 108 or halving it to 27, also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Bonus and rent cells match the diabetes review example" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PAP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "bonus-and-rent-cells-match-the-diabetes-review-example" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(BonusPerIndicator-120)<0.005,ABS(PracticeRent-54)<0.005)" .

hemo:7006456 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Incentive constraint holds with equality at the bonus cell"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For any inputs with ProbMetThorough above ProbMetCursory, the extra expected bonus from the high effort, the difference in probabilities times BonusPerIndicator, is compared with ReviewCost. Expected result TRUE. With the inputs of HE-EX-PAP-001, doubling the bonus cell leaves a gap of 30 and halving it a gap of minus 15, and a bonus of ReviewCost/ProbMetThorough leaves a gap of minus 19.29, all FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Incentive constraint holds with equality at the bonus cell" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PAP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "incentive-constraint-holds-with-equality-at-the-bonus-cell" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((ProbMetThorough-ProbMetCursory)*BonusPerIndicator-ReviewCost)<0.005" .

hemo:7006457 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Fee equal to the chance earnings leaves the expected payment at cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of HE-EX-PAP-003 (ReviewCost 30, ProbMetThorough 0.70, ProbMetCursory 0.45, LiabilityLimit 54), ExpectedPayment is compared with ReviewCost and PracticeRent with zero, where ell equals p_L b_star and the rent (4.21) of the second case of Proposition 4.2 of Laffont and Martimort falls to zero. Expected result TRUE. A fee cell that ignores LiabilityLimit and returns 0 gives an expected payment of 84 and FALSE, and doubling ExpectedPayment to 60 or halving it to 15 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fee equal to the chance earnings leaves the expected payment at cost" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PAP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fee-equal-to-the-chance-earnings-leaves-the-expected-payment-at-cost" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(ExpectedPayment-ReviewCost)<0.005,ABS(PracticeRent)<0.005)" .

hemo:7006458 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Rent equals the chance earnings less the fee with a partial fee"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of HE-EX-PAP-004 (LiabilityLimit 20, other inputs as HE-EX-PAP-001), PracticeRent, built as the expected payment less the cost, is compared with what the practice would earn without the review, ProbMetCursory times BonusPerIndicator, less the fee, the rent of (4.21). Expected result TRUE. Doubling the rent cell to 68 or halving it to 17 gives FALSE, and a rent cell built as ExpectedPayment alone returns 64 and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Rent equals the chance earnings less the fee with a partial fee" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-PAP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "rent-equals-the-chance-earnings-less-the-fee-with-a-partial-fee" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(PracticeRent-(ProbMetCursory*BonusPerIndicator-FeeCharged))<0.005" .

hemo:7006459 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Weak quality indicator for a diabetes annual review"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative example. A purchaser wants a general practice to carry out a thorough annual review for each patient with diabetes, costing the practice 30 pounds of staff time, and can pay only on an indicator met for 70% of patients after a thorough review and 45% without one. The practice cannot be paid less than nothing. The bonus is 120 pounds per patient meeting the indicator, the expected payment 84 pounds and the rent 54 pounds per patient, what the practice would earn by chance without any review. For a list of 200 patients the purchaser expects to pay 16,800 pounds for reviews costing 6,000 pounds, a rent of 10,800 pounds." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Weak quality indicator for a diabetes annual review" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 30; p_H = 0.70; p_L = 0.45; ell = 0; b_star = 120; F = 0; E_pay = 84; R = 54" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PAP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "weak-quality-indicator-for-a-diabetes-annual-review" .

hemo:7006460 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Sharper audited indicator for the diabetes annual review"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative second case. An audited measure is met for 90% of patients after a thorough review and 20% without one, and the practice still cannot be paid less than nothing. The bonus falls to 42.86 pounds, the expected payment to 38.57 pounds and the rent to 8.57 pounds per patient, or 1,714 pounds for a list of 200. The weak indicator of HE-EX-PAP-001 costs the purchaser about six times as much in rent, because p_H / p_L is 4.5 rather than 1.56." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Sharper audited indicator for the diabetes annual review" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 30; p_H = 0.90; p_L = 0.20; ell = 0; b_star = 42.86; F = 0; E_pay = 38.57; R = 8.57" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PAP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "sharper-audited-indicator-for-the-diabetes-annual-review" .

hemo:7006461 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Fixed fee of 54 pounds removes the practice rent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative aside. If the purchaser could also charge the practice a fixed fee of 54 pounds per patient, the weak indicator and the same 120 pound bonus still buy the review. The practice receives 66 pounds when the indicator is met and pays 54 pounds when it is not, the transfers (4.12) and (4.13) of Laffont and Martimort, which at this liability limit coincide with (4.19) and (4.20), so the expected payment equals the cost of the review and the rent is zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Fixed fee of 54 pounds removes the practice rent" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 30; p_H = 0.70; p_L = 0.45; ell = 54; b_star = 120; F = 54; E_pay = 30; R = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PAP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "fixed-fee-of-54-pounds-removes-the-practice-rent" .

hemo:7006462 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Practice able to pay only 20 pounds per patient"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative inputs. With the weak indicator and a practice that can be charged at most 20 pounds per patient, the fee is 20 pounds, the expected payment 64 pounds and the rent 34 pounds, the 54 pounds of HE-EX-PAP-001 less the fee, as (4.21) of Laffont and Martimort gives for a liability limit below p_L b_star." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Practice able to pay only 20 pounds per patient" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 30; p_H = 0.70; p_L = 0.45; ell = 20; b_star = 120; F = 20; E_pay = 64; R = 34" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PAP-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "practice-able-to-pay-only-20-pounds-per-patient" .

hemo:7006463 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Limiting case of an indicator never met without the review"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative inputs. When the indicator is met for 90% of patients after a thorough review and never without one, the bonus is 33.33 pounds, the expected payment equals the 30 pound cost and the rent is zero even though the practice cannot be charged a fee, because nothing is paid for indicators met by chance. The case checks an implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Limiting case of an indicator never met without the review" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 30; p_H = 0.90; p_L = 0; ell = 0; b_star = 33.33; F = 0; E_pay = 30; R = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-PAP-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "limiting-case-of-an-indicator-never-met-without-the-review" .

hemo:7006464 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One-shot simultaneous game with two players and two strategies each"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The two players choose at the same time without seeing each other's choice, each knows both players' payoffs, and the payoffs are those of a game in matrix form, as in section 5.1.1 of Tadelis. When one player sees the other's choice before moving, the game is sequential and is solved by backward induction under the Game Theory concept, as in step 5 of the article's example." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One-shot simultaneous game with two players and two strategies each" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-NASH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-shot-simultaneous-game-with-two-players-and-two-strategies-each" .

hemo:7006465 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Each player's payoffs compared only with that player's own payoffs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The row player's comparisons use only u_jk and the column player's only v_jk, so the two players' payoffs need not share a unit. The check says nothing about which cell is best for both players or for the health system, which needs a separate valuation of each cell, as in step 4 of the article's example." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Each player's payoffs compared only with that player's own payoffs" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-NASH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "each-player-s-payoffs-compared-only-with-that-player-s-own-payoffs" .

hemo:7006466 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Ties count as best replies in the weak equilibrium test"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Definition 5.1 of Tadelis requires the equilibrium payoff to be at least the payoff from any alternative, so a player that does exactly as well with either strategy against the other's choice is playing a best reply with both, and the cell passes NE_jk. Definition 7 of Slantchev uses a strict inequality, so the same cell fails S_jk and is a weak equilibrium. The tie rule of cost-effectiveness dominance (HE-FM-DOM-001) is a different rule and is not used here." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ties count as best replies in the weak equilibrium test" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-NASH-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ties-count-as-best-replies-in-the-weak-equilibrium-test" .

hemo:7006467 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Excel best-reply check of the four cells of a 2 x 2 game"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000946 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The row player's first and second strategies are Up and Down and the column player's are Left and Right. With the row player's payoffs in cells named PayoffAUpLeft, PayoffAUpRight, PayoffADownLeft and PayoffADownRight and the column player's in PayoffBUpLeft, PayoffBUpRight, PayoffBDownLeft and PayoffBDownRight, the first four formulas go in EquilibriumUpLeft, EquilibriumUpRight, EquilibriumDownLeft and EquilibriumDownRight and the last four in StrictUpLeft, StrictUpRight, StrictDownLeft and StrictDownRight. Each returns TRUE or FALSE, which Excel counts as 1 or 0 in arithmetic." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Excel best-reply check of the four cells of a 2 x 2 game" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-NASH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "excel-best-reply-check-of-the-four-cells-of-a-2-x-2-game" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(PayoffAUpLeft>=PayoffADownLeft,PayoffBUpLeft>=PayoffBUpRight); =AND(PayoffAUpRight>=PayoffADownRight,PayoffBUpRight>=PayoffBUpLeft); =AND(PayoffADownLeft>=PayoffAUpLeft,PayoffBDownLeft>=PayoffBDownRight); =AND(PayoffADownRight>=PayoffAUpRight,PayoffBDownRight>=PayoffBDownLeft); =AND(PayoffAUpLeft>PayoffADownLeft,PayoffBUpLeft>PayoffBUpRight); =AND(PayoffAUpRight>PayoffADownRight,PayoffBUpRight>PayoffBUpLeft); =AND(PayoffADownLeft>PayoffAUpLeft,PayoffBDownLeft>PayoffBDownRight); =AND(PayoffADownRight>PayoffAUpRight,PayoffBDownRight>PayoffBDownLeft)" .

hemo:7006468 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Angioplasty game equilibria are the two one-investor cells and both are strict"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the payoffs of HE-EX-NASH-001 typed in, the four equilibrium cells and the strict cells of the two one-investor cells are compared with the result of step 1 of the article. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a check that uses the row player's payoffs for both players, which still passes the DownLeft cell but only as a weak equilibrium (StrictDownLeft FALSE), and a cell formula that always returns FALSE also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Angioplasty game equilibria are the two one-investor cells and both are strict" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-NASH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "angioplasty-game-equilibria-are-the-two-one-investor-cells-and-both-are-strict" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(NOT(EquilibriumUpLeft),EquilibriumUpRight,EquilibriumDownLeft,NOT(EquilibriumDownRight),StrictUpRight,StrictDownLeft)" .

hemo:7006469 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No cell of matching pennies passes the best-reply check"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the payoffs of HE-EX-NASH-003 typed in, all four equilibrium cells are FALSE, as Slantchev states for matching pennies. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a check that tests only the row player's comparison, which passes the UpLeft and DownRight cells, where the column player would gain 2 by switching." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No cell of matching pennies passes the best-reply check" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-NASH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-cell-of-matching-pennies-passes-the-best-reply-check" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(NOT(EquilibriumUpLeft),NOT(EquilibriumUpRight),NOT(EquilibriumDownLeft),NOT(EquilibriumDownRight))" .

hemo:7006470 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Break-even angioplasty game has three weak and no strict pure equilibria"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the payoffs of HE-EX-NASH-002 typed in, the three cells with at least one investor pass the weak test and fail the strict one, and the DownRight cell fails both, as in step 6 of the article. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a weak test written with strict inequalities, which finds no pure equilibrium, and a strict test written with inequalities that allow ties, which marks all three cells strict." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Break-even angioplasty game has three weak and no strict pure equilibria" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-NASH-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "break-even-angioplasty-game-has-three-weak-and-no-strict-pure-equilibria" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(EquilibriumUpLeft,EquilibriumUpRight,EquilibriumDownLeft,NOT(EquilibriumDownRight),NOT(StrictUpLeft),NOT(StrictUpRight),NOT(StrictDownLeft))" .

hemo:7006471 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Two hospitals deciding on one angioplasty service have two strict pure equilibria"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative game, with Invest as each hospital's first strategy and Stay out as its second, payoffs in GBP million a year. (Invest, Stay out) and (Stay out, Invest) pass both comparisons strictly. (Invest, Invest) fails because each hospital gains 2 by staying out, and (Stay out, Stay out) fails because each gains 4 by investing, as in step 1 of the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two hospitals deciding on one angioplasty service have two strict pure equilibria" ;
    hemo:9000014 "u_11 = -2; u_12 = 4; u_21 = 0; u_22 = 0; v_11 = -2; v_12 = 0; v_21 = 4; v_22 = 0; NE_11 = 0; NE_12 = 1; NE_21 = 1; NE_22 = 0; S_11 = 0; S_12 = 1; S_21 = 1; S_22 = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NASH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-hospitals-deciding-on-one-angioplasty-service-have-two-strict-pure-equilibria" .

hemo:7006472 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Angioplasty game in which duplication breaks even has three weak pure equilibria"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Step 6 of the article, with each hospital earning 0 in place of a loss of 2 when both invest. The three cells with at least one investor pass the weak test, and none passes the strict test, because in each of them one hospital does exactly as well by switching." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Angioplasty game in which duplication breaks even has three weak pure equilibria" ;
    hemo:9000014 "u_11 = 0; u_12 = 4; u_21 = 0; u_22 = 0; v_11 = 0; v_12 = 0; v_21 = 4; v_22 = 0; NE_11 = 1; NE_12 = 1; NE_21 = 1; NE_22 = 0; S_11 = 0; S_12 = 0; S_21 = 0; S_22 = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NASH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "angioplasty-game-in-which-duplication-breaks-even-has-three-weak-pure-equilibria" .

hemo:7006473 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Matching pennies has no pure-strategy equilibrium"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Slantchev's matching pennies game (Figure 18), in which the row player wins 1 when the two choices match and the column player wins 1 when they differ. In every cell one player gains 2 by switching, so no cell passes. The game's only equilibrium is mixed (HE-EX-NASH-007)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Matching pennies has no pure-strategy equilibrium" ;
    hemo:9000014 "u_11 = 1; u_12 = -1; u_21 = -1; u_22 = 1; v_11 = -1; v_12 = 1; v_21 = 1; v_22 = -1; NE_11 = 0; NE_12 = 0; NE_21 = 0; NE_22 = 0; S_11 = 0; S_12 = 0; S_21 = 0; S_22 = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NASH-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "matching-pennies-has-no-pure-strategy-equilibrium" .

hemo:7006474 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Hi-lo coordination game has two strict pure equilibria"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The Hi-lo game the article takes from the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, in which matching on the first option pays 10 each, matching on the second pays 1 each and mismatching pays nothing. Both matched cells pass strictly, so the test alone cannot say which will occur." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Hi-lo coordination game has two strict pure equilibria" ;
    hemo:9000014 "u_11 = 10; u_12 = 0; u_21 = 0; u_22 = 1; v_11 = 10; v_12 = 0; v_21 = 0; v_22 = 1; NE_11 = 1; NE_12 = 0; NE_21 = 0; NE_22 = 1; S_11 = 1; S_12 = 0; S_21 = 0; S_22 = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NASH-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "hi-lo-coordination-game-has-two-strict-pure-equilibria" .

hemo:7006475 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected payoffs and independent mixing in the 2 x 2 mixed equilibrium"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Nash defines mixed strategies as probability distributions over the pure strategies, with payoffs measured as expectations, so each payoff is a value whose expectation the player maximises, not only a ranking. The two players randomise independently of each other, as when each hospital decides without knowing the other's choice." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected payoffs and independent mixing in the 2 x 2 mixed equilibrium" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-NASH-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-payoffs-and-independent-mixing-in-the-2-x-2-mixed-equilibrium" .

hemo:7006476 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "No payoff ties against a fixed choice of the other player"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The formula assumes a_1, a_2, b_1 and b_2 are all non-zero, so that no player does equally well with both strategies against one of the other's strategies. With such a tie, as in the break-even angioplasty game of HE-EX-NASH-002 where a_1 is 0, equilibria can form a continuum that the formula does not describe (computed here), and a_1 + a_2 of zero, which occurs when one strategy pays the same amount more against both columns, leaves q_star undefined." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No payoff ties against a fixed choice of the other player" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-NASH-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-payoff-ties-against-a-fixed-choice-of-the-other-player" .

hemo:7006477 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Each player mixes over both of its strategies"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The formula gives the completely mixed equilibrium, in which both strategies of each player are used. In a 2 x 2 game any mixture of two strategies uses both, as Slantchev notes for the simplified card game, so the only other equilibria are the pure ones of HE-FM-NASH-001 and, with ties, the partly mixed ones of HE-AS-NASH-005." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Each player mixes over both of its strategies" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-NASH-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "each-player-mixes-over-both-of-its-strategies" .

hemo:7006478 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Excel mixed equilibrium probabilities of a 2 x 2 game"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000947 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the payoffs in the cells named in HE-IM-NASH-001, the formulas return GapALeft (a_1), GapARight (a_2), MixProbBLeft (q_star), GapBUp (b_1), GapBDown (b_2), MixProbAUp (p_star) and InteriorMixed in that order. The IF returns #N/A in place of #DIV/0! when a gap sum is zero, and InteriorMixed then also returns #N/A, since AND passes an error on." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Excel mixed equilibrium probabilities of a 2 x 2 game" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-NASH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "excel-mixed-equilibrium-probabilities-of-a-2-x-2-game" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=PayoffAUpLeft-PayoffADownLeft; =PayoffADownRight-PayoffAUpRight; =IF(GapALeft+GapARight=0,NA(),GapARight/(GapALeft+GapARight)); =PayoffBUpLeft-PayoffBUpRight; =PayoffBDownRight-PayoffBDownLeft; =IF(GapBUp+GapBDown=0,NA(),GapBDown/(GapBUp+GapBDown)); =AND(MixProbBLeft>0,MixProbBLeft<1,MixProbAUp>0,MixProbAUp<1)" .

hemo:7006479 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Row player indifferent at the column player's mixed probability in the angioplasty game"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the payoffs of HE-EX-NASH-005 typed in, the row player's expected payoff from investing at the probability in MixProbBLeft is compared with its expected payoff from staying out, at a tolerance of 1E-6. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the probability doubled (1.3333), which gives a gap of 4, or halved (0.3333), which gives a gap of 2, and a probability taken as a_1 / (a_1 + a_2), one third, which gives a gap of 2." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Row player indifferent at the column player's mixed probability in the angioplasty game" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-NASH-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "row-player-indifferent-at-the-column-player-s-mixed-probability-in-the-angioplasty-game" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((MixProbBLeft*PayoffAUpLeft+(1-MixProbBLeft)*PayoffAUpRight)-(MixProbBLeft*PayoffADownLeft+(1-MixProbBLeft)*PayoffADownRight))<1E-6" .

hemo:7006480 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of the sexes mixed probabilities equal Slantchev's two thirds and one third"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the payoffs of HE-EX-NASH-006 typed in, InteriorMixed is TRUE, MixProbAUp is compared with two thirds and MixProbBLeft with one third, Slantchev's equilibrium, at a tolerance of 0.0005. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows each probability computed from the player's own payoffs, which swaps them, and doubling MixProbAUp (1.3333) or halving it (0.3333) also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Battle of the sexes mixed probabilities equal Slantchev's two thirds and one third" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-NASH-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "battle-of-the-sexes-mixed-probabilities-equal-slantchev-s-two-thirds-and-one-third" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(InteriorMixed,ABS(MixProbAUp-2/3)<0.0005,ABS(MixProbBLeft-1/3)<0.0005)" .

hemo:7006481 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No completely mixed equilibrium when investing is dominant"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the payoffs of HE-EX-NASH-008 typed in, InteriorMixed is FALSE and MixProbBLeft equals four thirds, at a tolerance of 0.0005. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows an interior check that tests only that both values are above 0, which returns TRUE, and doubling MixProbBLeft (2.6667) or halving it (0.6667) also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No completely mixed equilibrium when investing is dominant" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-NASH-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-completely-mixed-equilibrium-when-investing-is-dominant" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(NOT(InteriorMixed),ABS(MixProbBLeft-4/3)<0.0005)" .

hemo:7006482 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Mixed equilibrium of the angioplasty investment game"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Step 2 of the article. Hospital A is indifferent when -2q + 4(1 minus q) equals 0, so hospital B invests with probability 4/6, and by symmetry A invests with the same probability. Both values are above 0 and below 1, so the game has a completely mixed equilibrium besides its two pure ones (HE-EX-NASH-001)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Mixed equilibrium of the angioplasty investment game" ;
    hemo:9000014 "u_11 = -2; u_12 = 4; u_21 = 0; u_22 = 0; v_11 = -2; v_12 = 0; v_21 = 4; v_22 = 0; a_1 = -2; a_2 = -4; q_star = 0.6667; b_1 = -2; b_2 = -4; p_star = 0.6667; interior = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NASH-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "mixed-equilibrium-of-the-angioplasty-investment-game" .

hemo:7006483 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Mixed equilibrium of the battle of the sexes in which each probability comes from the other's payoffs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Slantchev's battle of the sexes (Figure 20). The row player is indifferent when 2q equals 1 minus q, so the column player chooses its first strategy with probability one third, and the column player is indifferent when p equals 2(1 minus p), so the row player chooses its first strategy with probability two thirds, Slantchev's equilibrium. Each player puts the higher probability on the strategy it prefers." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Mixed equilibrium of the battle of the sexes in which each probability comes from the other's payoffs" ;
    hemo:9000014 "u_11 = 2; u_12 = 0; u_21 = 0; u_22 = 1; v_11 = 1; v_12 = 0; v_21 = 0; v_22 = 2; a_1 = 2; a_2 = 1; q_star = 0.3333; b_1 = 1; b_2 = 2; p_star = 0.6667; interior = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NASH-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "mixed-equilibrium-of-the-battle-of-the-sexes-in-which-each-probability-comes-from-the-other-s-payoffs" .

hemo:7006484 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Mixed equilibrium of matching pennies"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Slantchev's matching pennies, the game of HE-EX-NASH-003 with no pure equilibrium. Each player mixes with probability one half, the unique equilibrium Slantchev gives." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Mixed equilibrium of matching pennies" ;
    hemo:9000014 "u_11 = 1; u_12 = -1; u_21 = -1; u_22 = 1; v_11 = -1; v_12 = 1; v_21 = 1; v_22 = -1; a_1 = 2; a_2 = 2; q_star = 0.5; b_1 = -2; b_2 = -2; p_star = 0.5; interior = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NASH-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "mixed-equilibrium-of-matching-pennies" .

hemo:7006485 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Angioplasty game in which two services both profit has no mixed equilibrium"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Illustrative variant of the article's game in which each hospital earns 1 when both invest. Investing then pays more whatever the other does, the formula returns 1.3333 for each player, which is not a probability, and the only equilibrium is the pure cell (Invest, Invest), which HE-FM-NASH-001 finds." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Angioplasty game in which two services both profit has no mixed equilibrium" ;
    hemo:9000014 "u_11 = 1; u_12 = 4; u_21 = 0; u_22 = 0; v_11 = 1; v_12 = 0; v_21 = 4; v_22 = 0; a_1 = 1; a_2 = -4; q_star = 1.3333; b_1 = 1; b_2 = -4; p_star = 1.3333; interior = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-NASH-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "angioplasty-game-in-which-two-services-both-profit-has-no-mixed-equilibrium" .

hemo:7006486 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Identical users whose use lowers the value of every unit by the same amount"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every user faces the same a, c and k, and the value of each unit falls in a straight line with total use, so user i's net value is q_i (a less c less k Q). This is the payoff of Falk, Fehr and Fischbacher, e plus a x_i less b x_i times total appropriation, with their a equal to a less c here and their b equal to k, and of Delaney and Jacobson with beta equal to a, alpha to c and gamma to k. Users with different values or costs need the asymmetric first-order conditions and do not share one q_E." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Identical users whose use lowers the value of every unit by the same amount" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-TOTC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "identical-users-whose-use-lowers-the-value-of-every-unit-by-the-same-amount" .

hemo:7006487 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Independent one-shot choices by selfish users with an interior equilibrium"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Users choose independently and at the same time and each cares only about their own net value, the setting of the standard game and Proposition 1 of Falk, Fehr and Fischbacher. No communication or binding agreement between users is allowed for. Use is continuous and a exceeds c, so q_E is above zero; with a at or below c no unit is worth taking and the formula does not apply. In the experiments Ostrom reviews, allowing users to communicate reduced overharvesting, so the equilibrium describes unmanaged open access and not a governed commons." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Independent one-shot choices by selfish users with an interior equilibrium" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-TOTC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "independent-one-shot-choices-by-selfish-users-with-an-interior-equilibrium" .

hemo:7006488 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One period with no benefit of use to non-users"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "All values are in one currency for one season, with no carry-over of resistance to later seasons and no benefit of treatment to people other than the patient. The article notes that transmission benefits, future seasons and differences in need can change the answer, and Porco and colleagues found that more antibiotic use benefits society in some circumstances." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One period with no benefit of use to non-users" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-TOTC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-period-with-no-benefit-of-use-to-non-users" .

hemo:7006489 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Equilibrium use, rent and dissipated share of a shared resource in six Excel cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000948 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With inputs in cells named ValuePerUnitFull, CostPerUnit, LossPerUnitUsed and NumberOfUsers, the six formulas fill cells named EquilibriumUsePerUser, EquilibriumTotalUse, EquilibriumRent, OptimalTotalUse, OptimalRent and RentShareDissipated, in that order. A blank LossPerUnitUsed cell is read as 0 and returns #DIV/0!. A blank NumberOfUsers cell is read as 0 and returns zero total use and a dissipated share of 1, so the cell must hold a whole number of at least 1." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equilibrium use, rent and dissipated share of a shared resource in six Excel cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-TOTC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equilibrium-use-rent-and-dissipated-share-of-a-shared-resource-in-six-excel-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(ValuePerUnitFull-CostPerUnit)/(LossPerUnitUsed*(NumberOfUsers+1)); =NumberOfUsers*EquilibriumUsePerUser; =EquilibriumTotalUse*(ValuePerUnitFull-CostPerUnit-LossPerUnitUsed*EquilibriumTotalUse); =(ValuePerUnitFull-CostPerUnit)/(2*LossPerUnitUsed); =OptimalTotalUse*(ValuePerUnitFull-CostPerUnit-LossPerUnitUsed*OptimalTotalUse); =1-EquilibriumRent/OptimalRent" .

hemo:7006490 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Each user's first-order condition holds at the equilibrium use of the shared resource"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of the first worked example, the value of a unit less its cost, less k times total use and less k times the user's own use is compared with zero, relative to a less c. Expected result TRUE. Doubling EquilibriumUsePerUser to 3,200 (and the total with it) leaves minus 80 and FALSE, and halving it to 800 leaves 40 and FALSE. A cell that omits the user's own share, (a less c) / (k N), gives 2,000 per user and minus 20 and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Each user's first-order condition holds at the equilibrium use of the shared resource" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-TOTC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "each-user-s-first-order-condition-holds-at-the-equilibrium-use-of-the-shared-resource" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ValuePerUnitFull-CostPerUnit-LossPerUnitUsed*EquilibriumTotalUse-LossPerUnitUsed*EquilibriumUsePerUser)<1E-6*(ValuePerUnitFull-CostPerUnit)" .

hemo:7006491 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Total use and rent of the shared antibiotic match the article for four prescribers"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of the first worked example, EquilibriumTotalUse is compared with the article's 6,400 courses and EquilibriumRent with its 102,400 pounds. Expected result TRUE. Doubling the total use cell to 12,800 or halving it to 3,200 gives FALSE, as does a rent cell doubled to 204,800 or halved to 51,200." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Total use and rent of the shared antibiotic match the article for four prescribers" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-TOTC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "total-use-and-rent-of-the-shared-antibiotic-match-the-article-for-four-prescribers" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(EquilibriumTotalUse-6400)<0.5,ABS(EquilibriumRent-102400)<0.5)" .

hemo:7006492 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Dissipated share of the rent matches its closed form in the number of users"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "RentShareDissipated is compared with ((N less 1) / (N plus 1)) squared, the closed form derived from the chain, which uses only NumberOfUsers. Expected result TRUE for any valid inputs; with the first worked example both sides are 0.36. Doubling the cell to 0.72 or halving it to 0.18 gives FALSE, and a share taken as V_E / V_s instead of 1 less that ratio gives 0.64 and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Dissipated share of the rent matches its closed form in the number of users" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-TOTC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "dissipated-share-of-the-rent-matches-its-closed-form-in-the-number-of-users" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(RentShareDissipated-((NumberOfUsers-1)/(NumberOfUsers+1))^2)<1E-9" .

hemo:7006493 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Equilibrium use equals the joint optimum for a single user of the shared resource"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With NumberOfUsers set to 1 and the other inputs of the second worked example, EquilibriumTotalUse is compared with OptimalTotalUse, the limiting case in which the only user bears the whole loss. Expected result TRUE. Doubling the equilibrium cell to 8,000 or halving it to 2,000 gives FALSE, as does doubling or halving OptimalTotalUse." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equilibrium use equals the joint optimum for a single user of the shared resource" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-TOTC-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equilibrium-use-equals-the-joint-optimum-for-a-single-user-of-the-shared-resource" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(EquilibriumTotalUse-OptimalTotalUse)<1E-6*OptimalTotalUse" .

hemo:7006494 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Four prescribers sharing one antibiotic in the article's example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's illustrative values, a course worth 100 pounds at full effectiveness, a cost of 20 pounds and a loss of 0.01 pounds per course for each course prescribed, four prescribers each choose 1,600 courses, 6,400 in all. Each course is then worth 16 pounds net and the rent is 102,400 pounds, against 160,000 pounds at the joint optimum of 4,000 courses, so 36% of the attainable value is lost, as the article reports." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Four prescribers sharing one antibiotic in the article's example" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 100; c = 20; k = 0.01; N = 4; q_E = 1600; Q_E = 6400; v_E = 16; V_E = 102400; Q_s = 4000; V_s = 160000; d_E = 0.36" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-TOTC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "four-prescribers-sharing-one-antibiotic-in-the-article-s-example" .

hemo:7006495 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Single steward of the antibiotic as the limiting case with no dissipation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With one prescriber, or one steward choosing for the region, the equilibrium is the joint optimum of 4,000 courses, each worth 40 pounds net, and none of the 160,000 pounds is lost, the first row of the article's table. The values are illustrative and the case checks an implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Single steward of the antibiotic as the limiting case with no dissipation" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 100; c = 20; k = 0.01; N = 1; q_E = 4000; Q_E = 4000; v_E = 40; V_E = 160000; Q_s = 4000; V_s = 160000; d_E = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-TOTC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "single-steward-of-the-antibiotic-as-the-limiting-case-with-no-dissipation" .

hemo:7006496 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Ninety-nine prescribers sharing the antibiotic near open access"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With 99 prescribers each chooses 80 courses, 7,920 in all, close to the open-access limit of 8,000. Each course is worth 0.80 pounds net, the rent is 6,336 pounds and 96.04% of the attainable value is lost, the last row of the article's table. The values are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ninety-nine prescribers sharing the antibiotic near open access" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 100; c = 20; k = 0.01; N = 99; q_E = 80; Q_E = 7920; v_E = 0.8; V_E = 6336; Q_s = 4000; V_s = 160000; d_E = 0.9604" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-TOTC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ninety-nine-prescribers-sharing-the-antibiotic-near-open-access" .

hemo:7006497 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Eight appropriators in the laboratory resource of Walker and colleagues"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Falk, Fehr and Fischbacher restate the laboratory design of Walker and colleagues with a revenue of 23 per token less 0.25 times total tokens, a cost of 5 per token and eight players. The prediction is 8 tokens each and 64 in all against a social optimum of 36, as the chapter states. The net value per token is 2, the group rent 128 against 324 at the optimum, so 60.49% of the attainable rent is lost (derived from the printed values)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Eight appropriators in the laboratory resource of Walker and colleagues" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 23; c = 5; k = 0.25; N = 8; q_E = 8; Q_E = 64; v_E = 2; V_E = 128; Q_s = 36; V_s = 324; d_E = 0.6049" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-TOTC-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "eight-appropriators-in-the-laboratory-resource-of-walker-and-colleagues" .

hemo:7006498 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Uniform per-unit fee passed in full to every user's cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The same fee is charged on every unit and each user treats it as part of the cost of a unit, so the first-order condition becomes a less c less t_s less k Q less k q_i equal to zero. Delaney and Jacobson pay their subsidy on each token not used, which changes a user's payoff by a lump sum less the subsidy times own use, so it acts on choices as a fee per unit of use (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Uniform per-unit fee passed in full to every user's cost" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-TOTC-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "uniform-per-unit-fee-passed-in-full-to-every-user-s-cost" .

hemo:7006499 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Revenue not returned in proportion to each user's own use"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The fee changes choices only if its revenue does not come back to each user in proportion to that user's use. Revenue returned as a fixed sum, or spent on conservation as Giubilini proposes for an antibiotic tax, leaves the incentive in place, while a rebate per unit used cancels it (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Revenue not returned in proportion to each user's own use" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-TOTC-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "revenue-not-returned-in-proportion-to-each-user-s-own-use" .

hemo:7006500 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Users, values and loss of value as in the equilibrium formula"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The users are identical, choose independently and care only about their own net value, a exceeds c, k is above zero and N is a whole number of at least 1, as in HE-AS-TOTC-001 and HE-AS-TOTC-002. The fee assumes the authority knows a, c, k and N; with a misjudged k or N the fee misses the optimum." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Users, values and loss of value as in the equilibrium formula" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-TOTC-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "users-values-and-loss-of-value-as-in-the-equilibrium-formula" .

hemo:7006501 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Corrective fee, use under the fee and revenue in four Excel cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000949 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With inputs in cells named ValuePerUnitFull, CostPerUnit, LossPerUnitUsed and NumberOfUsers, the four formulas fill cells named CorrectiveFeePerUnit, UsePerUserWithFee, TotalUseWithFee and FeeRevenue, in that order. A blank NumberOfUsers cell is read as 0 and the fee returns #DIV/0!, and a blank LossPerUnitUsed cell gives #DIV/0! in the use cell." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Corrective fee, use under the fee and revenue in four Excel cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-TOTC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "corrective-fee-use-under-the-fee-and-revenue-in-four-excel-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=(ValuePerUnitFull-CostPerUnit)*(NumberOfUsers-1)/(2*NumberOfUsers); =(ValuePerUnitFull-CostPerUnit-CorrectiveFeePerUnit)/(LossPerUnitUsed*(NumberOfUsers+1)); =NumberOfUsers*UsePerUserWithFee; =CorrectiveFeePerUnit*TotalUseWithFee" .

hemo:7006502 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Total use under the corrective fee equals the joint optimum of the shared resource"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "TotalUseWithFee is compared with (a less c) / (2k) computed from the inputs, at a tolerance relative to (a less c) / k. Expected result TRUE; with the first worked example both are 4,000. Doubling the total cell to 8,000 or halving it to 2,000 gives FALSE. A fee cell doubled to 60 gives 1,600 courses and FALSE, and a fee cell halved to 15 gives 5,200 and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Total use under the corrective fee equals the joint optimum of the shared resource" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-TOTC-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "total-use-under-the-corrective-fee-equals-the-joint-optimum-of-the-shared-resource" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(TotalUseWithFee-(ValuePerUnitFull-CostPerUnit)/(2*LossPerUnitUsed))<1E-6*(ValuePerUnitFull-CostPerUnit)/LossPerUnitUsed" .

hemo:7006503 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Corrective fee equals the loss imposed on the other users at the optimum"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "CorrectiveFeePerUnit is compared with k times (N less 1) times UsePerUserWithFee, the loss one more unit imposes on the other users' units when use is at the optimum. Expected result TRUE; with the first worked example both are 30. A fee cell doubled to 60 gives use of 400 per user, a loss of 12 and FALSE, and a fee cell halved to 15 gives 1,300 per user, a loss of 39 and FALSE. A fee set at the whole external cost k Q_s, 40, gives 800 per user, a loss of 24 and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Corrective fee equals the loss imposed on the other users at the optimum" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-TOTC-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "corrective-fee-equals-the-loss-imposed-on-the-other-users-at-the-optimum" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(CorrectiveFeePerUnit-LossPerUnitUsed*(NumberOfUsers-1)*UsePerUserWithFee)<1E-6*(ValuePerUnitFull-CostPerUnit)" .

hemo:7006504 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Corrective fee and revenue match the article for four prescribers"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of the first worked example, CorrectiveFeePerUnit is compared with the article's 30 pounds and FeeRevenue with its 120,000 pounds. Expected result TRUE. Doubling the fee cell to 60 or halving it to 15 gives FALSE, as does a revenue cell doubled to 240,000 or halved to 60,000." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Corrective fee and revenue match the article for four prescribers" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-TOTC-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "corrective-fee-and-revenue-match-the-article-for-four-prescribers" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(CorrectiveFeePerUnit-30)<0.005,ABS(FeeRevenue-120000)<0.5)" .

hemo:7006505 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Charge of 30 pounds per course for four prescribers in the article's example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's illustrative values the fee is 30 pounds per course, the loss one more course imposes on the other three prescribers' 3,000 courses at the optimum. Each prescriber then chooses 1,000 courses, 4,000 in all, the joint optimum, and the 120,000 pounds raised is a transfer, as the article states." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Charge of 30 pounds per course for four prescribers in the article's example" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 100; c = 20; k = 0.01; N = 4; t_s = 30; q_t = 1000; Q_t = 4000; R_t = 120000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-TOTC-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "charge-of-30-pounds-per-course-for-four-prescribers-in-the-article-s-example" .

hemo:7006506 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Pigouvian subsidy in the three-person laboratory game of Delaney and Jacobson"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Delaney and Jacobson set a starting return of 0.36 dollars per token, an outside return of zero, a fall of 0.01 dollars per token for each token in the resource and groups of three. The formula gives their subsidy of 0.12 dollars, 6 tokens each and a group total of 18, the social optimum, as the paper reports. R_t is the fee equivalent. The paper pays its subsidy on tokens kept out of the resource, 12 of the group's 30, so it pays out 1.44 dollars, a lump-sum difference that leaves choices unchanged (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pigouvian subsidy in the three-person laboratory game of Delaney and Jacobson" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 0.36; c = 0; k = 0.01; N = 3; t_s = 0.12; q_t = 6; Q_t = 18; R_t = 2.16" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-TOTC-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pigouvian-subsidy-in-the-three-person-laboratory-game-of-delaney-and-jacobson" .

hemo:7006507 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No corrective fee needed for a single user of the shared resource"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With one prescriber the fee is zero, use is the joint optimum of 4,000 courses and no revenue is raised, since the only user already bears the whole loss. The values are illustrative and the case checks an implementation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No corrective fee needed for a single user of the shared resource" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 100; c = 20; k = 0.01; N = 1; t_s = 0; q_t = 4000; Q_t = 4000; R_t = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-TOTC-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-corrective-fee-needed-for-a-single-user-of-the-shared-resource" .

hemo:7006508 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Corrective fee for ninety-nine prescribers close to the whole external cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With 99 prescribers the fee is 39.596 pounds per course, close to the 40 pounds of the whole marginal external cost at the optimum, the t_star of HE-FM-PIGT-001 with b and g equal to 0.01 and m equal to zero. Each prescriber chooses 40.404 courses, 4,000 in all, and 158,383.84 pounds is raised. The values are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Corrective fee for ninety-nine prescribers close to the whole external cost" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = 100; c = 20; k = 0.01; N = 99; t_s = 39.596; q_t = 40.404; Q_t = 4000; R_t = 158383.84" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-TOTC-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "corrective-fee-for-ninety-nine-prescribers-close-to-the-whole-external-cost" .

hemo:7006509 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Independent uniform private costs on a common range in a reverse auction"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each supplier's unit cost is an independent draw from the same uniform distribution on the interval [c_low, c_high] and is known only to that supplier, the independent private-values setting of Vickrey described in section 2.2 of the 2020 Nobel scientific background and the uniform case of Klemperer's Appendix D. Costs that are correlated, such as a shared input price, or drawn from different ranges for an incumbent and an entrant, break the closed form." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Independent uniform private costs on a common range in a reverse auction" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AUCT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "independent-uniform-private-costs-on-a-common-range-in-a-reverse-auction" .

hemo:7006510 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Truthful second-price bidding with no binding price ceiling and at least two bidders"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each supplier bids its own cost, its dominant strategy under the second-price rule (the mirror for a reverse auction of the statement in section 2.2 of the Nobel background that each bidder's dominant strategy is to bid her value). One supplier wins the whole contract, there is no reserve price below c_high that could bind, and N is at least 2 so that a second-lowest bid exists." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Truthful second-price bidding with no binding price ceiling and at least two bidders" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AUCT-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "truthful-second-price-bidding-with-no-binding-price-ceiling-and-at-least-two-bidders" .

hemo:7006511 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Risk-neutral symmetric suppliers for the first-price equality of the expected price"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The statement that the first-price rule yields the same expected price rests on revenue equivalence, which section 2.2 of the Nobel background reports for risk-neutral, ex-ante symmetric bidders, and which Vickrey proved first for a uniform distribution of values. The second-price result for E_p does not need risk neutrality, since Klemperer's section 5 (Risk Aversion) states that risk aversion has no effect on a bidder's optimal second-price strategy." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Risk-neutral symmetric suppliers for the first-price equality of the expected price" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AUCT-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "risk-neutral-symmetric-suppliers-for-the-first-price-equality-of-the-expected-price" .

hemo:7006512 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Excel expected lowest cost, price and margin of a uniform-cost reverse auction"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000950 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the cost range in cells named CostLowest and CostHighest and the number of bidders in NumberOfBidders, the formulas return the expected lowest cost in a cell named ExpLowestCost, the expected price in ExpWinningPrice and the winner's margin in WinnerMargin, in that order." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Excel expected lowest cost, price and margin of a uniform-cost reverse auction" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AUCT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "excel-expected-lowest-cost-price-and-margin-of-a-uniform-cost-reverse-auction" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CostLowest+(CostHighest-CostLowest)/(NumberOfBidders+1); =CostLowest+2*(CostHighest-CostLowest)/(NumberOfBidders+1); =ExpWinningPrice-ExpLowestCost" .

hemo:7006513 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected price of GBP 3.50 reproduced for three suppliers"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With CostLowest 2, CostHighest 5 and NumberOfBidders 3, ExpWinningPrice is compared with the article's hand value of 3.50 at a tolerance of 0.005. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a denominator of N in place of N + 1, which gives 4.00, and doubling the ExpWinningPrice cell (7.00) or halving it (1.75) also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected price of GBP 3.50 reproduced for three suppliers" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AUCT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-price-of-gbp-3-50-reproduced-for-three-suppliers" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ExpWinningPrice-3.5)<0.005" .

hemo:7006514 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Second-price expected price equals the expected first-price winning bid"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For any inputs, ExpWinningPrice is compared with the expected winning bid of the first-price rule, the bid of HE-FM-AUCT-002 at the expected lowest cost, ExpLowestCost plus (CostHighest minus ExpLowestCost) / NumberOfBidders, at a tolerance of 0.005; with five bidders both are 3.00. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows Klemperer's selling weight (N minus 1) / (N + 1) applied from c_low, which gives 4.00 with five bidders, and doubling the ExpWinningPrice cell (6.00) or halving it (1.50) also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Second-price expected price equals the expected first-price winning bid" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AUCT-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "second-price-expected-price-equals-the-expected-first-price-winning-bid" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ExpWinningPrice-(ExpLowestCost+(CostHighest-ExpLowestCost)/NumberOfBidders))<0.005" .

hemo:7006515 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Winner's margin equals the expected gap above the lowest possible cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The expected gaps between successive ordered uniform costs are all (c_high minus c_low) / (N + 1) (derived from Klemperer's fact), so WinnerMargin is compared with ExpLowestCost minus CostLowest at a tolerance of 0.005; with three bidders both are 0.75. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a margin taken as the price less c_low, 1.50, and doubling the WinnerMargin cell (1.50) or halving it (0.375) also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Winner's margin equals the expected gap above the lowest possible cost" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AUCT-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "winner-s-margin-equals-the-expected-gap-above-the-lowest-possible-cost" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(WinnerMargin-(ExpLowestCost-CostLowest))<0.005" .

hemo:7006516 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Three suppliers with unit costs uniform on [2.00, 5.00] GBP per pack"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative tender. The expected lowest cost is 2.00 plus 3.00 / 4, which is 2.75, the expected price is 2.00 plus 6.00 / 4, which is 3.50, and the winner's expected margin is 0.75 per pack, GBP 750,000 on 1,000,000 packs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Three suppliers with unit costs uniform on [2.00, 5.00] GBP per pack" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c_low = 2; c_high = 5; N = 3; E_c1 = 2.75; E_p = 3.5; M_w = 0.75" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AUCT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "three-suppliers-with-unit-costs-uniform-on-2-00-5-00-gbp-per-pack" .

hemo:7006517 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Two suppliers bidding for the same uniform-cost tender"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's evaluation section. With only two bidders the expected price rises to GBP 4.00 per pack and the winner's expected margin to GBP 1.00." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two suppliers bidding for the same uniform-cost tender" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c_low = 2; c_high = 5; N = 2; E_c1 = 3; E_p = 4; M_w = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AUCT-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-suppliers-bidding-for-the-same-uniform-cost-tender" .

hemo:7006518 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Five suppliers bidding for the same uniform-cost tender"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's evaluation section. Five bidders bring the expected price down to GBP 3.00 per pack, with an expected margin of GBP 0.50." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Five suppliers bidding for the same uniform-cost tender" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c_low = 2; c_high = 5; N = 5; E_c1 = 2.5; E_p = 3; M_w = 0.5" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AUCT-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "five-suppliers-bidding-for-the-same-uniform-cost-tender" .

hemo:7006519 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Ninety-nine suppliers approach the lowest possible cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Limiting case with illustrative inputs. With 99 bidders the expected price is 2.06 and the margin 0.03 per pack, close to c_low and zero, since the gap (c_high minus c_low) / (N + 1), which E_c1 adds to c_low once and E_p twice, shrinks as N grows (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Ninety-nine suppliers approach the lowest possible cost" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c_low = 2; c_high = 5; N = 99; E_c1 = 2.03; E_p = 2.06; M_w = 0.03" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AUCT-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "ninety-nine-suppliers-approach-the-lowest-possible-cost" .

hemo:7006520 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Symmetric risk-neutral equilibrium in a first-price tender with uniform costs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "All suppliers are risk neutral, draw their costs independently from the same uniform range, know only their own cost and use the same increasing bidding rule, the setting of Klemperer's Appendix D. Klemperer's section 5 (Risk Aversion) states that risk aversion makes bidders bid more aggressively in first-price auctions, which in a tender means bids nearer to cost (the mirror, derived), and suppliers whose costs come from different ranges are not symmetric, so the bid formula then no longer holds." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Symmetric risk-neutral equilibrium in a first-price tender with uniform costs" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AUCT-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "symmetric-risk-neutral-equilibrium-in-a-first-price-tender-with-uniform-costs" .

hemo:7006521 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Single winner paid its own bid with at least two bidders and no binding ceiling"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "One supplier wins the whole contract at its bid, N is at least 2 and no price ceiling below c_high binds. With continuous costs two suppliers tie with probability zero, so tie-breaking does not change the expected values. At c = c_high the bid equals the cost and the margin is zero (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Single winner paid its own bid with at least two bidders and no binding ceiling" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AUCT-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "single-winner-paid-its-own-bid-with-at-least-two-bidders-and-no-binding-ceiling" .

hemo:7006522 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Procurement bid derived from the printed selling bid by mirroring the cost range"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The procurement bid is derived here from Klemperer's selling bid by mapping each cost c to the value c_low + c_high minus c and each bid back the same way, which turns v_low + ((n minus 1) / n) (v minus v_low) into c + (c_high minus c) / N. The identity held in 11,514 grid cases, a best-response grid search with step 0.0005 matched the bid to within 0.0002 for N of 2, 3, 5 and 8, and a seeded simulation matched E_b (Python checks here)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Procurement bid derived from the printed selling bid by mirroring the cost range" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-AUCT-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "procurement-bid-derived-from-the-printed-selling-bid-by-mirroring-the-cost-range" .

hemo:7006523 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Excel first-price equilibrium bid and expected winning bid with uniform costs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000951 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the supplier's cost in a cell named BidderCost, the cost range in CostLowestFP and CostHighestFP and the number of bidders in BiddersFP, the formulas return the bid in a cell named EquilibriumBid, the expected lowest cost in ExpLowestCostFP and the expected winning bid in ExpWinningBid, in that order. For several suppliers, put their costs in a column and copy the first formula down." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Excel first-price equilibrium bid and expected winning bid with uniform costs" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-AUCT-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "excel-first-price-equilibrium-bid-and-expected-winning-bid-with-uniform-costs" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=BidderCost+(CostHighestFP-BidderCost)/BiddersFP; =CostLowestFP+(CostHighestFP-CostLowestFP)/(BiddersFP+1); =ExpLowestCostFP+(CostHighestFP-ExpLowestCostFP)/BiddersFP" .

hemo:7006524 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "First-price bid equals Klemperer's selling bid on the mirrored cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "EquilibriumBid is compared with the procurement mirror of Klemperer's printed bid, c_low + c_high minus (c_low + ((N minus 1) / N) (c_high minus c)), at a tolerance of 0.005; for a cost of 2.60 both are 3.40. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a bid equal to cost (2.60) or one shaded over the full range, c + (c_high minus c_low) / N, which gives 3.60, and doubling the EquilibriumBid cell (6.80) or halving it (1.70) also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "First-price bid equals Klemperer's selling bid on the mirrored cost" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AUCT-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "first-price-bid-equals-klemperer-s-selling-bid-on-the-mirrored-cost" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(EquilibriumBid-(CostLowestFP+CostHighestFP-(CostLowestFP+(BiddersFP-1)/BiddersFP*(CostHighestFP-BidderCost))))<0.005" .

hemo:7006525 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected first-price winning bid equals the expected second-price price"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "ExpWinningBid is compared with the expected second-lowest cost c_low + 2 (c_high minus c_low) / (N + 1) of HE-FM-AUCT-001, the revenue-equivalence result, at a tolerance of 0.005; with three bidders both are 3.50. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the expected winning bid shaded over the full range, 2.75 + 3.00 / 3, which gives 3.75, and doubling the ExpWinningBid cell (7.00) or halving it (1.75) also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected first-price winning bid equals the expected second-price price" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-AUCT-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-first-price-winning-bid-equals-the-expected-second-price-price" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ExpWinningBid-(CostLowestFP+2*(CostHighestFP-CostLowestFP)/(BiddersFP+1)))<0.005" .

hemo:7006526 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Lowest-cost supplier at GBP 2.60 bids GBP 3.40 in the article's draw"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's one draw, illustrative. With three suppliers and costs uniform on the interval [2.00, 5.00] GBP per pack, the supplier with cost 2.60 adds 2.40 / 3 and bids 3.40, which wins. The expected winning bid is 3.50, equal to the second-price expected price of HE-EX-AUCT-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Lowest-cost supplier at GBP 2.60 bids GBP 3.40 in the article's draw" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 2.6; c_high = 5; c_low = 2; N = 3; b = 3.4; E_c1 = 2.75; E_b = 3.5" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AUCT-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "lowest-cost-supplier-at-gbp-2-60-bids-gbp-3-40-in-the-article-s-draw" .

hemo:7006527 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Middle-cost supplier at GBP 3.20 bids GBP 3.80 in the article's draw"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The same draw, illustrative. The supplier with cost 3.20 adds 1.80 / 3 and bids 3.80, which loses to 3.40." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Middle-cost supplier at GBP 3.20 bids GBP 3.80 in the article's draw" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 3.2; c_high = 5; c_low = 2; N = 3; b = 3.8; E_c1 = 2.75; E_b = 3.5" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AUCT-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "middle-cost-supplier-at-gbp-3-20-bids-gbp-3-80-in-the-article-s-draw" .

hemo:7006528 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Highest-cost supplier at GBP 4.40 bids GBP 4.60 in the article's draw"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The same draw, illustrative. The supplier with cost 4.40 adds 0.60 / 3 and bids 4.60. A supplier closer to c_high shades its bid less, since fewer rival costs can lie above its own." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Highest-cost supplier at GBP 4.40 bids GBP 4.60 in the article's draw" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 4.4; c_high = 5; c_low = 2; N = 3; b = 4.6; E_c1 = 2.75; E_b = 3.5" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AUCT-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "highest-cost-supplier-at-gbp-4-40-bids-gbp-4-60-in-the-article-s-draw" .

hemo:7006529 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Supplier at the highest possible cost bids its cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Limiting case with the article's range. A supplier whose cost equals c_high wins with probability zero, since every rival's cost lies below its own almost surely, and its equilibrium bid is its cost of 5.00 with no margin (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Supplier at the highest possible cost bids its cost" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 5; c_high = 5; c_low = 2; N = 3; b = 5; E_c1 = 2.75; E_b = 3.5" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-AUCT-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "supplier-at-the-highest-possible-cost-bids-its-cost" .

hemo:7006530 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Track rates for performance years from 2024 in the shared savings payment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The sharing rate and performance payment limit are those of 42 CFR 425.605(d)(1)(i) to (v), paragraphs (A)(4) and (B), for the BASIC levels and 425.610(d)(4) and (e) for ENHANCED, which apply to performance years beginning on or after 1 January 2024. Earlier performance years used other rules, such as rates set on quality performance under 425.502 up to 2020, and are not covered. For agreement periods from 2027 an ACO with fewer than 5,000 assigned beneficiaries in a benchmark year may face an alternative, lower payment limit under 425.605(i)(1)(ii) or 425.610(l)(3), which the formula does not include." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Track rates for performance years from 2024 in the shared savings payment" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ACO-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "track-rates-for-performance-years-from-2024-in-the-shared-savings-payment" .

hemo:7006531 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Benchmark and spending on one assigned population and basis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "B and E are per capita amounts for the same assigned beneficiaries and the same performance year, with the benchmark risk-adjusted and both measured on Parts A and B fee-for-service claims excluding IME and DSH payments, as 42 CFR 425.605(a) and 425.610(a) set out. Total benchmark expenditure is the per capita updated benchmark times assigned beneficiary person years, 425.605(i)(1)(i)(A), so N is in person years." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Benchmark and spending on one assigned population and basis" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ACO-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "benchmark-and-spending-on-one-assigned-population-and-basis" .

hemo:7006532 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Qualification at the minimum savings rate and the low revenue route"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "A savings ratio equal to the MSR qualifies, since the rule pays an ACO that qualifies by meeting or exceeding the MSR (42 CFR 425.605(d)(1)(v)(B)(1), 425.610(e)(1)). The ACO must also meet the quality performance standard or its alternative and keep its eligibility, 425.605(c)(2). The 425.605(h) route applies only to a BASIC ACO that is a low revenue ACO, has at least 5,000 assigned beneficiaries, is in an agreement period beginning on or after 1 January 2024, has spending below its benchmark and, for agreement periods from 2027, has at least 5,000 assigned beneficiaries in each benchmark year, 425.605(h)(1); it is entered as MSR = 0 with s halved." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Qualification at the minimum savings rate and the low revenue route" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ACO-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "qualification-at-the-minimum-savings-rate-and-the-low-revenue-route" .

hemo:7006533 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Shared savings payment of an accountable care organisation in one cell"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000952 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With named cells Benchmark, Spending, Beneficiaries, SharingRate, QualityFactor, MinSavingsRate and PayLimitShare, the formula returns the payment in a cell named SharedSavingsPayment. A blank Benchmark is treated as 0 and the formula returns #DIV/0!. For the 42 CFR 425.605(h) low revenue route, enter MinSavingsRate as 0 and SharingRate as half the level's rate." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Shared savings payment of an accountable care organisation in one cell" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ACO-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "shared-savings-payment-of-an-accountable-care-organisation-in-one-cell" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF((Benchmark-Spending)/Benchmark>=MinSavingsRate,MIN(SharingRate*QualityFactor*Beneficiaries*(Benchmark-Spending),PayLimitShare*Beneficiaries*Benchmark),0)" .

hemo:7006534 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Shared savings payment cell matches the Level E worked example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of the first worked example (Benchmark 12,000, Spending 11,640, Beneficiaries 20,000, SharingRate 0.5, QualityFactor 1, MinSavingsRate 0.02, PayLimitShare 0.1), SharedSavingsPayment is compared with the hand value of USD 3,600,000. Expected result TRUE. A cell that treats the MSR as a deductible and shares only the savings above 2% returns 1,200,000 and FALSE, and doubling the cell to 7,200,000 or halving it to 1,800,000 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Shared savings payment cell matches the Level E worked example" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ACO-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "shared-savings-payment-cell-matches-the-level-e-worked-example" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(SharedSavingsPayment-3600000)<1" .

hemo:7006535 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Shared savings payment equals the final sharing rate on all savings or the payment limit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When the savings ratio reaches MinSavingsRate and the final sharing rate times total savings reaches the payment limit, SharedSavingsPayment must equal the limit; when it does not reach the limit, SharedSavingsPayment divided by total savings must equal SharingRate times QualityFactor; below the MSR it must be 0. Expected result TRUE for every worked example. In the first worked example the deductible reading gives a ratio of 0.1667 instead of 0.5 and FALSE, a cell that always returns the limit gives 3.3333 and FALSE, and the cell doubled or halved gives 1.0 or 0.25 and FALSE. In the ENHANCED example a cell that caps savings before sharing returns 36,000,000, a cell with no limit returns 54,000,000 and the cell doubled or halved returns 96,000,000 or 24,000,000, each FALSE. With Spending equal to Benchmark and MinSavingsRate 0 the ratio branch divides by zero and the test returns #DIV/0!." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Shared savings payment equals the final sharing rate on all savings or the payment limit" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ACO-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "shared-savings-payment-equals-the-final-sharing-rate-on-all-savings-or-the-payment-limit" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF((Benchmark-Spending)/Benchmark<MinSavingsRate,SharedSavingsPayment=0,IF(SharingRate*QualityFactor*Beneficiaries*(Benchmark-Spending)>=PayLimitShare*Beneficiaries*Benchmark,ABS(SharedSavingsPayment-PayLimitShare*Beneficiaries*Benchmark)<1,ABS(SharedSavingsPayment/(Beneficiaries*(Benchmark-Spending))-SharingRate*QualityFactor)<1E-6))" .

hemo:7006536 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Shared savings of the illustrative Level E accountable care organisation with 3% savings"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's illustrative BASIC Level E ACO has 20,000 assigned beneficiaries, a benchmark of USD 12,000 per beneficiary and an MSR of 2.0%. Spending of USD 11,640 saves 360 per beneficiary, a ratio of 0.03 that clears the MSR. With the quality standard met, half of the USD 7.2 million savings is paid, USD 3.6 million, well under the payment limit of USD 24 million, as in the article's case 1." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Shared savings of the illustrative Level E accountable care organisation with 3% savings" ;
    hemo:9000014 "B = 12000; E = 11640; N = 20000; s = 0.5; q = 1; MSR = 0.02; c_P = 0.1; g_S = 0.03; S_raw = 3600000; PPL = 24000000; P = 3600000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACO-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "shared-savings-of-the-illustrative-level-e-accountable-care-organisation-with-3-savings" .

hemo:7006537 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Shared savings of the illustrative Level E accountable care organisation meeting only the alternative standard"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The same ACO meets only the alternative quality performance standard with a quality score of 70%, so the sharing rate of 0.5 is multiplied by 0.7. The payment falls to USD 2.52 million, the figure in the article's case 1." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Shared savings of the illustrative Level E accountable care organisation meeting only the alternative standard" ;
    hemo:9000014 "B = 12000; E = 11640; N = 20000; s = 0.5; q = 0.7; MSR = 0.02; c_P = 0.1; g_S = 0.03; S_raw = 2520000; PPL = 24000000; P = 2520000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACO-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "shared-savings-of-the-illustrative-level-e-accountable-care-organisation-meeting-only-the-alternative-standard" .

hemo:7006538 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative Level E accountable care organisation with savings below the minimum savings rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Spending of USD 11,820 saves 180 per beneficiary, a ratio of 0.015 below the 2.0% MSR, so nothing is paid although the shared amount before the gate would be USD 1.8 million, as in the article's case 2." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative Level E accountable care organisation with savings below the minimum savings rate" ;
    hemo:9000014 "B = 12000; E = 11820; N = 20000; s = 0.5; q = 1; MSR = 0.02; c_P = 0.1; g_S = 0.015; S_raw = 1800000; PPL = 24000000; P = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACO-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-level-e-accountable-care-organisation-with-savings-below-the-minimum-savings-rate" .

hemo:7006539 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative low revenue Level E accountable care organisation paid at half the sharing rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "If the ACO of the previous example is a low revenue ACO that meets the other conditions of 42 CFR 425.605(h), its savings below the benchmark are shared at half the Level E rate, 0.25, with no MSR test. The payment is USD 900,000, the article's figure for case 2." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative low revenue Level E accountable care organisation paid at half the sharing rate" ;
    hemo:9000014 "B = 12000; E = 11820; N = 20000; s = 0.25; q = 1; MSR = 0; c_P = 0.1; g_S = 0.015; S_raw = 900000; PPL = 24000000; P = 900000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACO-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-low-revenue-level-e-accountable-care-organisation-paid-at-half-the-sharing-rate" .

hemo:7006540 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative ENHANCED accountable care organisation at its performance payment limit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An illustrative ENHANCED ACO with the same population, benchmark and MSR spends USD 8,400, saving 30% of the benchmark. Three quarters of the USD 72 million savings is USD 54 million, above the 20% payment limit of USD 48 million, so the payment is USD 48 million. The limit binds whenever the savings ratio exceeds c_P divided by s q, 0.2667 here and 0.20 for BASIC Levels C to E at full quality (derived, checked on a grid of 600 savings ratios)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative ENHANCED accountable care organisation at its performance payment limit" ;
    hemo:9000014 "B = 12000; E = 8400; N = 20000; s = 0.75; q = 1; MSR = 0.02; c_P = 0.2; g_S = 0.3; S_raw = 54000000; PPL = 48000000; P = 48000000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACO-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-enhanced-accountable-care-organisation-at-its-performance-payment-limit" .

hemo:7006541 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-sided BASIC levels for performance years from 2024"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The ACO is in BASIC Level C, D or E; Levels A and B are one-sided and share no losses. The shared loss rate and limits are those of 42 CFR 425.605(d)(1)(iii) to (v), paragraphs (C) and (D). The Level E revenue-based percentage is the 8 percent of 42 CFR 414.1415(c)(3)(i)(A). That paragraph reads \"For QP Performance Periods beginning in 2023\", and the CY 2023 physician fee schedule final rule that wrote it removed the earlier 2024 end date so that the 8 percent standard applies to every QP performance period from 2023 on (HE-SR-ACO-008). Levels C and D use 2% and 4%, which 425.605 states directly." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-sided BASIC levels for performance years from 2024" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ACO-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-sided-basic-levels-for-performance-years-from-2024" .

hemo:7006542 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Adjustments to shared losses outside the formula"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The reduction for extreme and uncontrollable circumstances, applied after the shared loss rate and the loss recoupment limit under 42 CFR 425.605(f), and the alternative benchmark-based limit for agreement periods from 2027 with fewer than 5,000 assigned beneficiaries in a benchmark year, 425.605(i)(2)(ii), are not included. A loss ratio equal to the MLR counts, since the rule requires spending above the benchmark by at least the MLR (425.605(b)(4))." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Adjustments to shared losses outside the formula" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ACO-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "adjustments-to-shared-losses-outside-the-formula" .

hemo:7006543 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Shared losses of a two-sided BASIC accountable care organisation in one cell"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000953 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With named cells Benchmark, Spending, Beneficiaries, LossRate, MinLossRate, RevLimitShare, ParticipantRevenue and BmkLimitShare, the formula returns the amount owed in a cell named SharedLossBasic. A blank Benchmark returns #DIV/0!; a blank ParticipantRevenue is treated as 0, which sets the limit to 0 and returns 0." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Shared losses of a two-sided BASIC accountable care organisation in one cell" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ACO-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "shared-losses-of-a-two-sided-basic-accountable-care-organisation-in-one-cell" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF((Spending-Benchmark)/Benchmark>=MinLossRate,MIN(LossRate*Beneficiaries*(Spending-Benchmark),MIN(RevLimitShare*ParticipantRevenue,BmkLimitShare*Beneficiaries*Benchmark)),0)" .

hemo:7006544 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "BASIC shared losses cell matches the Level D worked example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of the first Level D worked example, SharedLossBasic is compared with the hand value of USD 2,160,000. Expected result TRUE. A cell that applies the loss rate only to the excess above the 2% MLR returns 720,000 and FALSE, and doubling the cell to 4,320,000 or halving it to 1,080,000 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "BASIC shared losses cell matches the Level D worked example" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ACO-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "basic-shared-losses-cell-matches-the-level-d-worked-example" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(SharedLossBasic-2160000)<1" .

hemo:7006545 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "BASIC shared losses equal the loss rate on the whole excess or the recoupment limit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "When the loss ratio reaches MinLossRate and the loss rate times the total excess reaches the lesser of the two limits, SharedLossBasic must equal that limit; otherwise SharedLossBasic divided by the total excess must equal LossRate; below the MLR it must be 0. Expected result TRUE for every worked example. In the Level C example a cell that uses the revenue-based limit alone returns 4,000,000 and a cell with no limit returns 7,200,000, both FALSE; in the revenue-limited Level D example a cell with no limit returns 2,160,000 and the cell doubled to 3,200,000 or halved to 800,000 gives FALSE. With Spending equal to Benchmark, MinLossRate 0 and a positive ParticipantRevenue the ratio branch divides by zero and the test returns #DIV/0!." ;
    hemo:9000009 "BASIC shared losses equal the loss rate on the whole excess or the recoupment limit" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ACO-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "basic-shared-losses-equal-the-loss-rate-on-the-whole-excess-or-the-recoupment-limit" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=IF((Spending-Benchmark)/Benchmark<MinLossRate,SharedLossBasic=0,IF(LossRate*Beneficiaries*(Spending-Benchmark)>=MIN(RevLimitShare*ParticipantRevenue,BmkLimitShare*Beneficiaries*Benchmark),ABS(SharedLossBasic-MIN(RevLimitShare*ParticipantRevenue,BmkLimitShare*Beneficiaries*Benchmark))<1,ABS(SharedLossBasic/(Beneficiaries*(Spending-Benchmark))-LossRate)<1E-6))" .

hemo:7006546 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative Level D accountable care organisation with a 3% overspend"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's case 3 figures (20,000 beneficiaries, benchmark USD 12,000, spending USD 12,360, MLR 2.0%) are placed in Level D with an illustrative participant revenue of USD 100 million. The 3% overspend clears the MLR and 30% of the USD 7.2 million excess is USD 2.16 million. The revenue-based limit of USD 4 million is below the benchmark-based USD 4.8 million, so the limit is USD 4 million and does not bind. The result equals the article's Level E figure, since the loss rate is the same." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative Level D accountable care organisation with a 3% overspend" ;
    hemo:9000014 "B = 12000; E = 12360; N = 20000; r_L = 0.3; MLR = 0.02; rho_R = 0.04; Rev = 100000000; rho_B = 0.02; g_L = 0.03; L_raw = 2160000; LRL = 4000000; SL = 2160000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACO-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-level-d-accountable-care-organisation-with-a-3-overspend" .

hemo:7006547 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative Level D accountable care organisation held by the revenue-based limit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With participant revenue of USD 40 million, an illustrative figure for an ACO whose participants bill a small share of its beneficiaries' spending, the revenue-based limit is 4% of 40 million, USD 1.6 million. The ACO repays USD 1.6 million instead of USD 2.16 million." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative Level D accountable care organisation held by the revenue-based limit" ;
    hemo:9000014 "B = 12000; E = 12360; N = 20000; r_L = 0.3; MLR = 0.02; rho_R = 0.04; Rev = 40000000; rho_B = 0.02; g_L = 0.03; L_raw = 2160000; LRL = 1600000; SL = 1600000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACO-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-level-d-accountable-care-organisation-held-by-the-revenue-based-limit" .

hemo:7006548 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative Level D accountable care organisation with an overspend below the minimum loss rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Spending of USD 12,180 exceeds the benchmark by 1.5%, below the 2.0% MLR, so nothing is owed although 30% of the excess would be USD 1.08 million." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative Level D accountable care organisation with an overspend below the minimum loss rate" ;
    hemo:9000014 "B = 12000; E = 12180; N = 20000; r_L = 0.3; MLR = 0.02; rho_R = 0.04; Rev = 100000000; rho_B = 0.02; g_L = 0.015; L_raw = 1080000; LRL = 4000000; SL = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACO-008" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-level-d-accountable-care-organisation-with-an-overspend-below-the-minimum-loss-rate" .

hemo:7006549 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative Level C accountable care organisation held by the benchmark-based limit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In Level C, with illustrative participant revenue of USD 200 million and spending 10% above the benchmark at USD 13,200, 30% of the USD 24 million excess is USD 7.2 million. The revenue-based limit of 2% of revenue, USD 4 million, exceeds 1% of the USD 240 million benchmark, so the limit is USD 2.4 million and the ACO repays that amount." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative Level C accountable care organisation held by the benchmark-based limit" ;
    hemo:9000014 "B = 12000; E = 13200; N = 20000; r_L = 0.3; MLR = 0.02; rho_R = 0.02; Rev = 200000000; rho_B = 0.01; g_L = 0.1; L_raw = 7200000; LRL = 2400000; SL = 2400000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACO-009" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-level-c-accountable-care-organisation-held-by-the-benchmark-based-limit" .

hemo:7006550 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative Level E accountable care organisation with the article case 3 overspend"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's case 3 in Level E itself, with an illustrative participant revenue of USD 100 million. Thirty percent of the USD 7.2 million excess is USD 2.16 million. The revenue-based limit is 8% of USD 100 million, USD 8 million, below the benchmark-based ceiling of 4% of USD 240 million, USD 9.6 million, the figure the article gives, so the limit is USD 8 million and does not bind." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative Level E accountable care organisation with the article case 3 overspend" ;
    hemo:9000014 "B = 12000; E = 12360; N = 20000; r_L = 0.3; MLR = 0.02; rho_R = 0.08; Rev = 100000000; rho_B = 0.04; g_L = 0.03; L_raw = 2160000; LRL = 8000000; SL = 2160000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACO-014" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-level-e-accountable-care-organisation-with-the-article-case-3-overspend" .

hemo:7006551 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "ENHANCED loss rate rule for performance years from 2024"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The rate follows 42 CFR 425.610(f)(4). For an ACO that meets the quality performance standard or the alternative standard, 1 minus the product of 75 percent and its quality score, not above 75% and not below 40%; for one that meets neither, 75%. Earlier performance years used other rules, 1 minus the final sharing rate up to 2020 and a quotient of MIPS Quality points in 2021 and 2022, and are not covered." ;
    hemo:9000009 "ENHANCED loss rate rule for performance years from 2024" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ACO-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "enhanced-loss-rate-rule-for-performance-years-from-2024" .

hemo:7006552 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "ENHANCED loss recoupment limit and adjustments outside the formula"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Losses are limited to 15% of the updated benchmark times assigned beneficiary person years (42 CFR 425.610(g) and (l)(2)). The reduction for extreme and uncontrollable circumstances under 425.610(i) and the alternative limit for agreement periods from 2027 with fewer than 5,000 assigned beneficiaries in a benchmark year, 425.610(l)(3), are not included. A loss ratio equal to the MLR counts (425.610(b)(3))." ;
    hemo:9000009 "ENHANCED loss recoupment limit and adjustments outside the formula" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ACO-007" ;
    hemo:9000023 "enhanced-loss-recoupment-limit-and-adjustments-outside-the-formula" .

hemo:7006553 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "ENHANCED shared loss rate and shared losses in two cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000954 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With named cells QualityScore, Benchmark, Spending, Beneficiaries and MinLossRate, the first formula returns the loss rate in a cell named EnhancedLossRate and the second the amount owed in a cell named SharedLossEnhanced. A blank QualityScore is treated as 0 and gives the 75% rate." ;
    hemo:9000009 "ENHANCED shared loss rate and shared losses in two cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ACO-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "enhanced-shared-loss-rate-and-shared-losses-in-two-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MIN(MAX(1-0.75*QualityScore,0.4),0.75); =IF((Spending-Benchmark)/Benchmark>=MinLossRate,MIN(EnhancedLossRate*Beneficiaries*(Spending-Benchmark),0.15*Beneficiaries*Benchmark),0)" .

hemo:7006554 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "ENHANCED shared losses cell matches the 70% quality score example"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of the first ENHANCED worked example (QualityScore 0.7, Benchmark 12,000, Spending 12,360, Beneficiaries 20,000, MinLossRate 0.02), SharedLossEnhanced is compared with the hand value of USD 3,420,000. Expected result TRUE. A cell that takes the loss rate as 1 minus the 75% sharing rate, 0.25, returns 1,800,000 and FALSE, and doubling the cell to 6,840,000 or halving it to 1,710,000 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "ENHANCED shared losses cell matches the 70% quality score example" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ACO-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "enhanced-shared-losses-cell-matches-the-70-quality-score-example" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(SharedLossEnhanced-3420000)<1" .

hemo:7006555 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "ENHANCED loss rate cell matches the hand value at a 70% quality score"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With QualityScore 0.7, EnhancedLossRate is compared with the hand value 0.475, 1 minus 0.75 times 0.7. Expected result TRUE. A cell written as 1 minus the quality score gives 0.3, raised to the 0.4 floor, and FALSE, and doubling the cell to 0.95 or halving it to 0.2375 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "ENHANCED loss rate cell matches the hand value at a 70% quality score" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ACO-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "enhanced-loss-rate-cell-matches-the-hand-value-at-a-70-quality-score" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(EnhancedLossRate-0.475)<1E-9" .

hemo:7006556 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative ENHANCED accountable care organisation with a quality score of 70%"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's illustrative population (20,000 beneficiaries, benchmark USD 12,000, spending USD 12,360, MLR 2.0%) placed in ENHANCED and a quality score of 70%, the loss rate is 1 minus 0.525, or 0.475. The ACO repays 0.475 of the USD 7.2 million excess, USD 3.42 million, far below the 15% limit of USD 36 million." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative ENHANCED accountable care organisation with a quality score of 70%" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Q = 0.7; B = 12000; E = 12360; N = 20000; MLR = 0.02; r_E = 0.475; g_L = 0.03; SL_E = 3420000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACO-010" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-enhanced-accountable-care-organisation-with-a-quality-score-of-70" .

hemo:7006557 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative ENHANCED accountable care organisation at the 40% loss rate floor"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a quality score of 90%, 1 minus 0.675 gives 0.325, below the floor, so the loss rate is 0.40 and the ACO repays USD 2.88 million." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative ENHANCED accountable care organisation at the 40% loss rate floor" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Q = 0.9; B = 12000; E = 12360; N = 20000; MLR = 0.02; r_E = 0.4; g_L = 0.03; SL_E = 2880000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACO-011" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-enhanced-accountable-care-organisation-at-the-40-loss-rate-floor" .

hemo:7006558 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative ENHANCED accountable care organisation meeting neither quality standard"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An ACO that meets neither the quality performance standard nor the alternative standard shares losses at 75%, entered as Q = 0, and repays USD 5.4 million on the same overspend." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative ENHANCED accountable care organisation meeting neither quality standard" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Q = 0; B = 12000; E = 12360; N = 20000; MLR = 0.02; r_E = 0.75; g_L = 0.03; SL_E = 5400000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACO-012" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-enhanced-accountable-care-organisation-meeting-neither-quality-standard" .

hemo:7006559 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative ENHANCED accountable care organisation at its loss recoupment limit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With spending 25% above the benchmark at USD 15,000 and the 75% rate, the shared amount would be USD 45 million, above the 15% limit of USD 36 million, so the ACO repays USD 36 million." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative ENHANCED accountable care organisation at its loss recoupment limit" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Q = 0; B = 12000; E = 15000; N = 20000; MLR = 0.02; r_E = 0.75; g_L = 0.25; SL_E = 36000000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACO-013" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-enhanced-accountable-care-organisation-at-its-loss-recoupment-limit" .

hemo:7006560 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Catchment membership by a hard travel-time threshold in the two-step floating catchment area"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every practice within d_0 counts fully and every practice beyond it counts not at all, whatever the travel time inside the threshold, the dichotomous treatment that Wang 2012 calls the method's major limitation and that Luo and Wang describe as an artificial line between accessible and inaccessible physicians. A time exactly equal to d_0 counts as inside, as in the set {d_ij <= d_0} of the published formula. The threshold is a choice; Luo and Wang mapped Chicago at 30 minutes and tested thresholds of 20 to 50 minutes." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Catchment membership by a hard travel-time threshold in the two-step floating catchment area" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ACCS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "catchment-membership-by-a-hard-travel-time-threshold-in-the-two-step-floating-catchment-area" .

hemo:7006561 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "One travel-time matrix for both steps and residents placed at one point per neighbourhood"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The same travel time between a neighbourhood and a practice decides whose population the practice serves in step one and which practices the neighbourhood reaches in step two; Luo and Wang compute the matrix once and use it twice. Each neighbourhood's residents and each practice's GPs sit at one point, as Luo and Wang represent whole census tracts or ZIP code areas by their centroids. Every practice needs someone within d_0, or its supply ratio has a zero denominator (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One travel-time matrix for both steps and residents placed at one point per neighbourhood" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ACCS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-travel-time-matrix-for-both-steps-and-residents-placed-at-one-point-per-neighbourhood" .

hemo:7006562 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Potential access with equal weight for every resident in the two-step floating catchment area"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The score measures the opportunity to reach care, not its use, and every resident counts once in each denominator, so need per head and travel options are taken as the same everywhere (derived). Guagliardo reports that Luo and Wang planned to add an adjustment for differences in transport options between areas, and Wang 2012 separates this place accessibility from the accessibility of individuals." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Potential access with equal weight for every resident in the two-step floating catchment area" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ACCS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "potential-access-with-equal-weight-for-every-resident-in-the-two-step-floating-catchment-area" .

hemo:7006563 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-step floating catchment area scores for two practices and three neighbourhoods from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000955 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the GPs in cells named GPsPracticeX and GPsPracticeY, the populations in PopNbhdA, PopNbhdB and PopNbhdC, the travel times in TimeAtoX, TimeAtoY, TimeBtoX, TimeBtoY, TimeCtoX and TimeCtoY and the threshold in ThresholdMinutes, the five formulas return the supply ratios in cells named RatioPracticeX and RatioPracticeY and the scores in AccessNbhdA, AccessNbhdB and AccessNbhdC, in GPs per person; multiplying by 10,000 gives the article's units. Excel treats TRUE as 1 and FALSE as 0 in multiplication, so each comparison works as the catchment indicator. For the article's inputs they return 0.000266667, 0.000153846, 0.000266667, 0.000420513 and 0.000153846. A blank travel-time cell counts as 0 minutes in the comparison and so places that neighbourhood inside the catchment; with TimeCtoX blank, C joins X's catchment and B's score falls from 4.205 to 3.761 per 10,000 (checked in Excel)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-step floating catchment area scores for two practices and three neighbourhoods from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ACCS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-step-floating-catchment-area-scores-for-two-practices-and-three-neighbourhoods-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=GPsPracticeX/((TimeAtoX<=ThresholdMinutes)*PopNbhdA+(TimeBtoX<=ThresholdMinutes)*PopNbhdB+(TimeCtoX<=ThresholdMinutes)*PopNbhdC); =GPsPracticeY/((TimeAtoY<=ThresholdMinutes)*PopNbhdA+(TimeBtoY<=ThresholdMinutes)*PopNbhdB+(TimeCtoY<=ThresholdMinutes)*PopNbhdC); =(TimeAtoX<=ThresholdMinutes)*RatioPracticeX+(TimeAtoY<=ThresholdMinutes)*RatioPracticeY; =(TimeBtoX<=ThresholdMinutes)*RatioPracticeX+(TimeBtoY<=ThresholdMinutes)*RatioPracticeY; =(TimeCtoX<=ThresholdMinutes)*RatioPracticeX+(TimeCtoY<=ThresholdMinutes)*RatioPracticeY" .

hemo:7006564 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-step floating catchment area scores weighted by population return the total GPs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The population-weighted sum of AccessNbhdA, AccessNbhdB and AccessNbhdC is compared with the total GPs at the two practices. Luo and Wang and Wang 2012 state that the population-weighted mean of the scores equals total supply over total population; the property holds whenever every practice has someone within the threshold (derived). Expected result TRUE. With the article's inputs, doubling AccessNbhdB makes the sum 20.41 against 12 and halving it makes it 7.79, both FALSE, and a step one that divides each practice's GPs by its own neighbourhood's population only gives 41.33 and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-step floating catchment area scores weighted by population return the total GPs" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ACCS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-step-floating-catchment-area-scores-weighted-by-population-return-the-total-gps" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(PopNbhdA*AccessNbhdA+PopNbhdB*AccessNbhdB+PopNbhdC*AccessNbhdC-(GPsPracticeX+GPsPracticeY))<1E-9" .

hemo:7006565 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-step floating catchment area cells reproduce the article's scores per 10,000"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's inputs and a 30-minute threshold, the three score cells times 10,000 agree with the article's 2.667, 4.205 and 1.538 per 10,000, written here to four decimals, within 0.0001. Expected result TRUE. Bordered ratios in the cells (8.0, 0 and 6.667 per 10,000) give FALSE, and doubling or halving any one cell also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-step floating catchment area cells reproduce the article's scores per 10,000" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ACCS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-step-floating-catchment-area-cells-reproduce-the-article-s-scores-per-10-000" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(AccessNbhdA*10000-2.6667)<0.0001,ABS(AccessNbhdB*10000-4.2051)<0.0001,ABS(AccessNbhdC*10000-1.5385)<0.0001)" .

hemo:7006566 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's two practices and three neighbourhoods at a 30-minute threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative example neighbourhood A (10,000 people) contains practice X with 8 GPs, C (6,000) contains practice Y with 4 and B (20,000) has no practice; travel times to X and Y are 10 and 40 minutes for A, 20 and 25 for B and 45 and 15 for C. X's catchment holds A and B, so R_X is 8 / 30,000, or 2.667 per 10,000, and Y's holds B and C, so R_Y is 4 / 26,000, or 1.538 per 10,000. A scores 2.667, B 4.205 and C 1.538 per 10,000, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's two practices and three neighbourhoods at a 30-minute threshold" ;
    hemo:9000014 "S_X = 8; S_Y = 4; P_1 = 10000; P_2 = 20000; P_3 = 6000; d_1X = 10; d_1Y = 40; d_2X = 20; d_2Y = 25; d_3X = 45; d_3Y = 15; d_0 = 30; R_X = 0.000266667; R_Y = 0.000153846; A_1 = 0.000266667; A_2 = 0.000420513; A_3 = 0.000153846" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACCS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-two-practices-and-three-neighbourhoods-at-a-30-minute-threshold" .

hemo:7006567 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's consolidation with all 12 GPs at practice X"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "If practice Y closes and its 4 GPs move to X, R_X is 12 / 30,000, or 4.0 per 10,000, and R_Y is 0. A and B score 4.0 per 10,000 and C scores 0 because X is 45 minutes away, as in the article. The population-weighted mean stays at 3.333 per 10,000 while the spread widens, which HE-CF-ACCS-001 measures." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's consolidation with all 12 GPs at practice X" ;
    hemo:9000014 "S_X = 12; S_Y = 0; P_1 = 10000; P_2 = 20000; P_3 = 6000; d_1X = 10; d_1Y = 40; d_2X = 20; d_2Y = 25; d_3X = 45; d_3Y = 15; d_0 = 30; R_X = 0.0004; R_Y = 0; A_1 = 0.0004; A_2 = 0.0004; A_3 = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACCS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-consolidation-with-all-12-gps-at-practice-x" .

hemo:7006568 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-step floating catchment area with a threshold that every travel time meets"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With an illustrative threshold of 60 minutes every neighbourhood reaches both practices, so each catchment holds all 36,000 people and every neighbourhood scores 12 / 36,000, or 3.333 per 10,000, the GPs per head of the whole area (derived). It is the limit of Luo and Wang's finding that a larger threshold gives a smaller variance of the scores." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-step floating catchment area with a threshold that every travel time meets" ;
    hemo:9000014 "S_X = 8; S_Y = 4; P_1 = 10000; P_2 = 20000; P_3 = 6000; d_1X = 10; d_1Y = 40; d_2X = 20; d_2Y = 25; d_3X = 45; d_3Y = 15; d_0 = 60; R_X = 0.000222222; R_Y = 0.000111111; A_1 = 0.000333333; A_2 = 0.000333333; A_3 = 0.000333333" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACCS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-step-floating-catchment-area-with-a-threshold-that-every-travel-time-meets" .

hemo:7006569 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Power decay with one friction coefficient for every trip in the gravity accessibility index"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Travel time discounts both supply and demand by the same power, d^(-beta), with one beta for every pair. Wang 2012 notes that beta requires additional data and work to define and might be region specific; Luo and Wang tested seven values of 1.0 to 2.2, citing 1.85 from an earlier commuting study in the same area, and found larger variances of the scores at higher values. At a beta of 1 two GPs 20 minutes away count as much as one GP 10 minutes away, a trade-off that Luo and Wang note may be considered questionable, particularly by people outside the field of geography." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Power decay with one friction coefficient for every trip in the gravity accessibility index" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ACCS-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "power-decay-with-one-friction-coefficient-for-every-trip-in-the-gravity-accessibility-index" .

hemo:7006570 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Positive travel times in one unit for the gravity accessibility index"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every travel time must be above zero, because d^(-beta) is undefined at zero when beta is above zero (derived); a neighbourhood that contains a practice needs a positive time to it, such as the article's 10 minutes between A and X. All times are in one unit. Rescaling every time by the same factor leaves the scores unchanged, because the factor cancels between each numerator and the demand potential (derived, checked on a grid of 2,000 cases)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Positive travel times in one unit for the gravity accessibility index" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ACCS-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "positive-travel-times-in-one-unit-for-the-gravity-accessibility-index" .

hemo:7006571 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "No catchment edge, so every practice adds to every gravity accessibility score"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Every practice adds to every neighbourhood's score however far away it is, which removes the sharp edge of HE-FM-ACCS-001 but gives distant supply some weight. Luo and Wang found that the gravity form tends to give higher scores than the 2SFCA to areas with low accessibility and so could conceal local pockets of poor access. Like HE-FM-ACCS-001 it measures potential access for residents placed at one point per neighbourhood." ;
    hemo:9000009 "No catchment edge, so every practice adds to every gravity accessibility score" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ACCS-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "no-catchment-edge-so-every-practice-adds-to-every-gravity-accessibility-score" .

hemo:7006572 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Gravity accessibility scores for two practices and three neighbourhoods from named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000956 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs named as in the Excel implementation of HE-FM-ACCS-001 and the friction coefficient in a cell named FrictionBeta, the five formulas return the demand potentials in cells named DemandPotentialX and DemandPotentialY and the scores in GravityAccessA, GravityAccessB and GravityAccessC, in GPs per person. The brackets around the negative exponent keep Excel's order of operations right. For the article's inputs at FrictionBeta 1 they return 2133.333, 1450, 0.000443966, 0.000297845 and 0.000267241." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Gravity accessibility scores for two practices and three neighbourhoods from named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ACCS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "gravity-accessibility-scores-for-two-practices-and-three-neighbourhoods-from-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=PopNbhdA*TimeAtoX^(-FrictionBeta)+PopNbhdB*TimeBtoX^(-FrictionBeta)+PopNbhdC*TimeCtoX^(-FrictionBeta); =PopNbhdA*TimeAtoY^(-FrictionBeta)+PopNbhdB*TimeBtoY^(-FrictionBeta)+PopNbhdC*TimeCtoY^(-FrictionBeta); =GPsPracticeX*TimeAtoX^(-FrictionBeta)/DemandPotentialX+GPsPracticeY*TimeAtoY^(-FrictionBeta)/DemandPotentialY; =GPsPracticeX*TimeBtoX^(-FrictionBeta)/DemandPotentialX+GPsPracticeY*TimeBtoY^(-FrictionBeta)/DemandPotentialY; =GPsPracticeX*TimeCtoX^(-FrictionBeta)/DemandPotentialX+GPsPracticeY*TimeCtoY^(-FrictionBeta)/DemandPotentialY" .

hemo:7006573 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Gravity accessibility scores weighted by population return the total GPs"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The population-weighted sum of GravityAccessA, GravityAccessB and GravityAccessC is compared with the total GPs at the two practices, the property that Luo and Wang and Wang 2012 state for the gravity measure, which holds for any beta (derived algebra, the demand potentials cancel). Expected result TRUE. With the article's inputs at FrictionBeta 1, doubling GravityAccessB makes the sum 17.96 against 12 and halving it makes it 9.02, both FALSE, and scores computed without dividing by the demand potentials give 22,866.7 and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Gravity accessibility scores weighted by population return the total GPs" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ACCS-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "gravity-accessibility-scores-weighted-by-population-return-the-total-gps" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(PopNbhdA*GravityAccessA+PopNbhdB*GravityAccessB+PopNbhdC*GravityAccessC-(GPsPracticeX+GPsPracticeY))<1E-9" .

hemo:7006574 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Gravity accessibility cell for neighbourhood B at a friction coefficient of 1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's inputs and FrictionBeta 1, GravityAccessB times 10,000 equals the hand value 2.9784 per 10,000 (computed here as 8 / 20 / 2,133.33 plus 4 / 25 / 1,450) within 0.0001. Expected result TRUE. A FrictionBeta entered as minus 1 gives 3.0880 and FALSE, the 2SFCA value of 4.2051 gives FALSE, and doubling (5.9569) or halving (1.4892) the cell also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Gravity accessibility cell for neighbourhood B at a friction coefficient of 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ACCS-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "gravity-accessibility-cell-for-neighbourhood-b-at-a-friction-coefficient-of-1" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(GravityAccessB*10000-2.9784)<0.0001" .

hemo:7006575 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's practices and neighbourhoods under gravity decay with beta of 1"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's practices, populations and travel times and an illustrative beta of 1, the demand potentials are 2,133.3 for X and 1,450 for Y, and A, B and C score 4.440, 2.978 and 2.672 per 10,000 (computed here). The population-weighted mean is 3.333 per 10,000, as for the 2SFCA, but B now ranks below A, because B's practices are 20 and 25 minutes away while A's is 10 minutes away." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's practices and neighbourhoods under gravity decay with beta of 1" ;
    hemo:9000014 "S_X = 8; S_Y = 4; P_1 = 10000; P_2 = 20000; P_3 = 6000; d_1X = 10; d_1Y = 40; d_2X = 20; d_2Y = 25; d_3X = 45; d_3Y = 15; beta = 1; V_X = 2133.333; V_Y = 1450; A_1 = 0.000443966; A_2 = 0.000297845; A_3 = 0.000267241" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACCS-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-practices-and-neighbourhoods-under-gravity-decay-with-beta-of-1" .

hemo:7006576 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's practices and neighbourhoods under gravity decay with beta of 2"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With an illustrative beta of 2, A rises to 5.615 per 10,000, B falls to 2.293 and C rises to 2.997, so B and C change places, and the population-weighted variance of the scores rises to 2.066 (GPs per 10,000, squared) against 0.483 at a beta of 1 (computed here), in line with Luo and Wang's finding of larger variances at larger beta." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's practices and neighbourhoods under gravity decay with beta of 2" ;
    hemo:9000014 "S_X = 8; S_Y = 4; P_1 = 10000; P_2 = 20000; P_3 = 6000; d_1X = 10; d_1Y = 40; d_2X = 20; d_2Y = 25; d_3X = 45; d_3Y = 15; beta = 2; V_X = 152.963; V_Y = 64.917; A_1 = 0.000561513; A_2 = 0.000229339; A_3 = 0.000299683" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACCS-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-practices-and-neighbourhoods-under-gravity-decay-with-beta-of-2" .

hemo:7006577 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Gravity accessibility with no distance decay at beta of 0"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With beta of 0 every weight is 1, both demand potentials equal the 36,000 residents and every neighbourhood scores 12 / 36,000, or 3.333 per 10,000, the GPs per head of the whole area (derived). Travel time then plays no part, the same limit that HE-FM-ACCS-001 reaches with a threshold every travel time meets." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Gravity accessibility with no distance decay at beta of 0" ;
    hemo:9000014 "S_X = 8; S_Y = 4; P_1 = 10000; P_2 = 20000; P_3 = 6000; d_1X = 10; d_1Y = 40; d_2X = 20; d_2Y = 25; d_3X = 45; d_3Y = 15; beta = 0; V_X = 36000; V_Y = 36000; A_1 = 0.000333333; A_2 = 0.000333333; A_3 = 0.000333333" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACCS-006" ;
    hemo:9000023 "gravity-accessibility-with-no-distance-decay-at-beta-of-0" .

hemo:7006578 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Margin fixed in the protocol and not greater than the control effect"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "M is chosen and justified before the data are seen, as CPMP/EWP/482/99 section III and the EMEA 2005 margin guideline section 2 require, because a margin chosen after the results can always be made to give non-inferiority. ICH E10 section 1.5.1.1 states that the margin cannot be greater than the smallest effect the active control would reliably be expected to have against placebo in the planned trial, and the FDA guidance sets M2, the largest clinically acceptable loss, at or below M1. The EMEA 2005 guideline section 3 asks for a conservative margin when constancy of the control effect cannot be assured. In the article's illustrative example M1 is 0.22 and M2 is 0.10 on the risk difference scale." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Margin fixed in the protocol and not greater than the control effect" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ACTR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "margin-fixed-in-the-protocol-and-not-greater-than-the-control-effect" .

hemo:7006579 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Favourable outcome and test minus control orientation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "d is the test proportion minus the control proportion of a favourable outcome, so values above zero favour the test treatment, the orientation of ICH E9 section 3.3.2 (investigational product minus control) and of CPMP/EWP/482/99 section II.1. For an adverse outcome such as death, d is entered as the control risk minus the test risk, which is the ARR of HE-FM-ARR-001, and the same rule then applies (derived). M is entered as a positive number in both cases." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Favourable outcome and test minus control orientation" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ACTR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "favourable-outcome-and-test-minus-control-orientation" .

hemo:7006580 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Independent randomised arms large enough for the Wald interval"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The two arms are independent randomised groups, so the variance of d is the sum of the arm variances, and each arm is large enough, with its proportion away from 0 and 1, for the normal approximation of HE-FM-AN-001 to hold (compare HE-AS-BINOM-009 for one proportion). Newcombe (1998) found that computationally simpler intervals for a difference of independent proportions are prone to aberrations and poor coverage, and that the methods of Mee and of Miettinen and Nurminen and a method combining Wilson score intervals perform well, so a margin decision close to the boundary in a small trial is better checked with one of those intervals (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Independent randomised arms large enough for the Wald interval" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ACTR-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "independent-randomised-arms-large-enough-for-the-wald-interval" .

hemo:7006581 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-sided 95% interval and a strict inequality at the margin"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "z is 1.96, the two-sided 95% interval that CPMP/EWP/482/99 section II.4 assumes for all clinical trials whatever their objective, with a one-sided interval at 97.5% as its counterpart. The FDA guidance section III.C states that the two-sided 95% interval corresponds to a one-sided test size of 0.025, and ICH E9 section 5.5 prefers one-sided type I errors at half the two-sided level. The FDA guidance section III.A establishes non-inferiority when the upper bound for control minus test is less than M, and shows an effect over placebo when the lower bound is above 0, so a lower limit L_d exactly equal to minus M gives NI equal to 0 and L_d exactly equal to zero gives SUP equal to 0 (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-sided 95% interval and a strict inequality at the margin" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ACTR-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-sided-95-interval-and-a-strict-inequality-at-the-margin" .

hemo:7006582 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Risk difference interval and margin readings in named cells"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000957 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs in cells named CureProportionTest, CureProportionControl, ArmSizeTest, ArmSizeControl, CriticalValueZ and NonInferiorityMargin, the six formulas return, in order, cells named RiskDifference, StandardErrorRiskDifference, LowerLimitRiskDifference, UpperLimitRiskDifference, NonInferiorityShown and SuperiorityShown. CriticalValueZ can be entered as 1.96 or as =NORM.S.INV(0.975). A blank ArmSizeTest or ArmSizeControl is treated as 0 and the standard error returns #DIV/0!. A blank NonInferiorityMargin is also treated as 0 but raises no error, so NonInferiorityShown then silently returns the superiority reading." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Risk difference interval and margin readings in named cells" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ACTR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "risk-difference-interval-and-margin-readings-in-named-cells" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CureProportionTest-CureProportionControl; =SQRT(CureProportionTest*(1-CureProportionTest)/ArmSizeTest+CureProportionControl*(1-CureProportionControl)/ArmSizeControl); =RiskDifference-CriticalValueZ*StandardErrorRiskDifference; =RiskDifference+CriticalValueZ*StandardErrorRiskDifference; =IF(LowerLimitRiskDifference>-NonInferiorityMargin,1,0); =IF(LowerLimitRiskDifference>0,1,0)" .

hemo:7006583 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Lower limit of the risk difference interval matches the article"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of the first worked example (0.84, 0.86, 500, 500, 1.96), LowerLimitRiskDifference is compared with the article's minus 0.0642. Expected result TRUE. Doubling the cell to minus 0.1285 or halving it to minus 0.0321 gives FALSE, as do a cell that divides both arm variances by all 1,000 patients (minus 0.0513) and a cell built with z of 1.645 (minus 0.0571)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Lower limit of the risk difference interval matches the article" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ACTR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "lower-limit-of-the-risk-difference-interval-matches-the-article" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(LowerLimitRiskDifference+0.0642)<0.0005" .

hemo:7006584 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Non-inferiority cell agrees with the one-sided test against the margin"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compares NonInferiorityShown with the one-sided test route, the difference plus the margin divided by its standard error set against the critical value, which gives the same decision (derived). With the first worked example the statistic is 3.544 and both routes give 1. Expected result TRUE. A non-inferiority cell written without the minus sign on the margin returns 0 and FALSE, and doubling the cell to 2 or halving it to 0.5 also gives FALSE. With the second worked example (margin 0.05) the statistic is 1.329 and both routes give 0, while a cell that reads the point estimate instead of the lower limit returns 1 and FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Non-inferiority cell agrees with the one-sided test against the margin" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ACTR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "non-inferiority-cell-agrees-with-the-one-sided-test-against-the-margin" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=NonInferiorityShown=IF((RiskDifference+NonInferiorityMargin)/StandardErrorRiskDifference>CriticalValueZ,1,0)" .

hemo:7006585 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Superiority cell and lower limit for the illustrative superior treatment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of the fourth worked example (0.90, 0.84, 500, 500, 1.96, 0.10), SuperiorityShown is compared with 1 and LowerLimitRiskDifference with the hand value 0.0185. Expected result TRUE. Doubling or halving SuperiorityShown (2 or 0.5) gives FALSE, doubling or halving the lower limit (0.0370 or 0.0092) gives FALSE, and a lower limit built with z of 1.645 (0.0252) gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Superiority cell and lower limit for the illustrative superior treatment" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ACTR-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "superiority-cell-and-lower-limit-for-the-illustrative-superior-treatment" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(SuperiorityShown=1,ABS(LowerLimitRiskDifference-0.0185)<0.0005)" .

hemo:7006586 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's antibiotic trial read against a 10-point margin"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The article's hypothetical trial randomises 500 patients to each arm, with cure in 84% on test drug T and 86% on active control C, and a clinical margin M2 of 10 points. The difference is minus 0.02 with standard error 0.0226, and the 95% interval has a lower limit of minus 0.0642 and an upper limit of plus 0.0242. The lower limit lies above minus 0.10, so non-inferiority is shown, and it also lies above minus 0.22, the article's M1. The interval contains zero, so superiority is not shown. All figures are the article's illustrative inputs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article's antibiotic trial read against a 10-point margin" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_T = 0.84; p_C = 0.86; n_T = 500; n_C = 500; z = 1.96; M = 0.10; d = -0.02; SE_d = 0.0226; L_d = -0.0642; U_d = 0.0242; NI = 1; SUP = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACTR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-s-antibiotic-trial-read-against-a-10-point-margin" .

hemo:7006587 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Same trial against an illustrative 5-point margin"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the same results and an illustrative margin of 5 points, the lower limit of minus 0.0642 falls below minus 0.05, so non-inferiority is not shown, although the interval contains zero and the trial shows no significant difference. The margin is illustrative and lies below the article's M1 of 0.22." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Same trial against an illustrative 5-point margin" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_T = 0.84; p_C = 0.86; n_T = 500; n_C = 500; z = 1.96; M = 0.05; d = -0.02; SE_d = 0.0226; L_d = -0.0642; U_d = 0.0242; NI = 0; SUP = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACTR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "same-trial-against-an-illustrative-5-point-margin" .

hemo:7006588 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative larger trial that is non-inferior and statistically worse"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's cure proportions but 5,000 patients per arm, an illustrative size, the standard error falls to 0.0071 and the interval is minus 0.0340 at its lower end and minus 0.0060 at its upper end. Non-inferiority at the 10-point margin is shown, yet the whole interval lies below zero, so T is also statistically worse than C, the case the FDA guidance shows as result 6 of its Figure 2." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative larger trial that is non-inferior and statistically worse" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_T = 0.84; p_C = 0.86; n_T = 5000; n_C = 5000; z = 1.96; M = 0.10; d = -0.02; SE_d = 0.0071; L_d = -0.034; U_d = -0.006; NI = 1; SUP = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACTR-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-larger-trial-that-is-non-inferior-and-statistically-worse" .

hemo:7006589 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative test treatment shown non-inferior and superior"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "If T instead cured 90% against 84% on C, with 500 patients per arm, the difference is 0.06 with standard error 0.0212 and the interval is 0.0185 at its lower end and 0.1015 at its upper end. The lower limit lies above minus 0.10 and above zero, so both readings are shown, and CPMP/EWP/482/99 section IV.1 allows a non-inferiority trial to be read as showing superiority in this case. The cure proportions are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Illustrative test treatment shown non-inferior and superior" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_T = 0.90; p_C = 0.84; n_T = 500; n_C = 500; z = 1.96; M = 0.10; d = 0.06; SE_d = 0.0212; L_d = 0.0185; U_d = 0.1015; NI = 1; SUP = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ACTR-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "illustrative-test-treatment-shown-non-inferior-and-superior" .

hemo:7006590 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Stationary mortality and dependency prevalence behind a period figure"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Sullivan's method rests on a period life table, so it assumes constant age-specific death rates, a constant birth rate and no net migration, and it adds that age-specific dependency prevalence is constant over time; Imai and Soneji prove that under these four assumptions the estimator is unbiased and consistent. The result describes the rates prevailing now and is not a forecast for people now aged x. Mathers and Robine found by simulation that it could not detect a sudden change in disability transition rates but gave a good estimate of the multistate value when prevalence changed smoothly over the longer term." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Stationary mortality and dependency prevalence behind a period figure" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ALE-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "stationary-mortality-and-dependency-prevalence-behind-a-period-figure" .

hemo:7006591 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Bands cover all remaining life on one life table"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "l_x and every L_i come from the same life table on the same radix, the bands run without gaps from age x, and the last band is open-ended. Bands can have any widths; Imai and Soneji note that the result does not depend on the interval widths. If the last band stops at a fixed age, the result is a partial expectancy up to that age and not ALE_x." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Bands cover all remaining life on one life table" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ALE-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "bands-cover-all-remaining-life-on-one-life-table" .

hemo:7006592 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Prevalence is the share of the band dependent at the time of the survey"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each pi_i is the proportion of survey respondents in band i who are dependent when asked, a sample fraction, weighted where the survey design requires it. Imai and Soneji show that Sullivan's own 1971 estimator, the days a respondent spent disabled in the previous year divided by 365, measures something else and is not valid for the method. The survey covers the whole population of the band, including people in care homes and other institutions." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Prevalence is the share of the band dependent at the time of the survey" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ALE-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "prevalence-is-the-share-of-the-band-dependent-at-the-time-of-the-survey" .

hemo:7006593 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Dependency treated as a single yes or no state"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each surviving person in a band is either dependent in activities of daily living or not, under one definition held fixed across bands and over time. Independent years count fully and dependent years count zero, as in the other dichotomous health expectancies in the article; severity is not weighted, which is what separates the measure from HALE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Dependency treated as a single yes or no state" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-AS-ALE-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "dependency-treated-as-a-single-yes-or-no-state" .

hemo:7006594 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Active life expectancy from named life-table and prevalence ranges"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000958 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Person-years for the bands are in a column range named PersonYearsByBand, the dependency prevalence of the same bands in a range of the same size named DependentShareByBand, and the survivors at the starting age in a cell named SurvivorsAtStartAge. The formula, in a cell named ActiveLifeExpectancy, works for any number of bands because SUMPRODUCT evaluates the array 1 minus the prevalence without array entry. A blank prevalence cell is treated as 0, so that band counts as wholly active, and a blank SurvivorsAtStartAge returns #DIV/0!." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Active life expectancy from named life-table and prevalence ranges" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ALE-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "active-life-expectancy-from-named-life-table-and-prevalence-ranges" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(1-DependentShareByBand,PersonYearsByBand)/SurvivorsAtStartAge" .

hemo:7006595 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "Life expectancy, dependent years and active share cells for active life expectancy"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000958 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A cell named LifeExpectancyAtStartAge holds the formula shown, the person-years over the survivors. A cell named DependentYearsExpected holds `=LifeExpectancyAtStartAge-ActiveLifeExpectancy` and a cell named ActiveShareOfRemainingLife holds `=ActiveLifeExpectancy/LifeExpectancyAtStartAge`. Multiplying DependentYearsExpected by a cost per dependent year gives the expected care cost of HE-FM-COST-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Life expectancy, dependent years and active share cells for active life expectancy" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-IM-ALE-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "life-expectancy-dependent-years-and-active-share-cells-for-active-life-expectancy" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUM(PersonYearsByBand)/SurvivorsAtStartAge" .

hemo:7006596 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Active life expectancy cell matches the article baseline"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the baseline inputs of the first worked example (PersonYearsByBand 900,000, 650,000 and 300,000, DependentShareByBand 0.10, 0.25 and 0.50, SurvivorsAtStartAge 100,000), ActiveLifeExpectancy is compared with the article's 14.475 years. Expected result TRUE. A cell that weights the person-years by the prevalence in place of its complement returns 4.025 and FALSE, and doubling the cell to 28.95 or halving it to 7.2375 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Active life expectancy cell matches the article baseline" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ALE-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "active-life-expectancy-cell-matches-the-article-baseline" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ActiveLifeExpectancy-14.475)<0.0005" .

hemo:7006597 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Active life expectancy equals total less dependent person-years over survivors"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "For any inputs, ActiveLifeExpectancy is compared with the total person-years less the prevalence-weighted person-years, divided by the survivors, a second route to the same quantity. Expected result TRUE. With the baseline inputs a cell that applies the unweighted mean prevalence of 0.2833 to life expectancy returns 13.2583 and FALSE, and doubling the cell to 28.95 or halving it to 7.2375 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Active life expectancy equals total less dependent person-years over survivors" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ALE-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "active-life-expectancy-equals-total-less-dependent-person-years-over-survivors" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ActiveLifeExpectancy-(SUM(PersonYearsByBand)-SUMPRODUCT(DependentShareByBand,PersonYearsByBand))/SurvivorsAtStartAge)<0.0005" .

hemo:7006598 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Active life expectancy equals life expectancy when no one is dependent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With DependentShareByBand set to 0, 0 and 0 on the baseline life table, the limiting case of the fourth worked example, ActiveLifeExpectancy is compared with LifeExpectancyAtStartAge, 18.5 years. Expected result TRUE. A cell that returns the dependent years in place of the active years gives 0 and FALSE, and doubling the cell to 37 or halving it to 9.25 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Active life expectancy equals life expectancy when no one is dependent" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ALE-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "active-life-expectancy-equals-life-expectancy-when-no-one-is-dependent" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ActiveLifeExpectancy-LifeExpectancyAtStartAge)<0.0005" .

hemo:7006599 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Active share cell matches the article Scenario B"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000010 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the inputs of the third worked example (300,000 replaced by 400,000 in the last band), ActiveShareOfRemainingLife is compared with 0.7679, the article's 76.8%. Expected result TRUE. A cell that divides the dependent years by life expectancy returns 0.2321 and FALSE, and doubling the cell to 1.5359 or halving it to 0.384 also gives FALSE." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Active share cell matches the article Scenario B" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-VT-ALE-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "active-share-cell-matches-the-article-scenario-b" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ActiveShareOfRemainingLife-0.7679)<0.0001" .

hemo:7006600 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article baseline life table with dependency rising with age"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "In the article's illustrative period life table, 100,000 people reach 65 and live 900,000, 650,000 and 300,000 person-years in the three bands, with dependency of 10%, 25% and 50%. Life expectancy at 65 is 18.5 years, 4.025 years are expected to be lived dependent, and active life expectancy is 14.475 years, 78.2% of remaining life, as in the article. All inputs are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article baseline life table with dependency rising with age" ;
    hemo:9000014 "l_x = 100000; L_1 = 900000; L_2 = 650000; L_3 = 300000; pi_1 = 0.1; pi_2 = 0.25; pi_3 = 0.5; e_x = 18.5; ALE_x = 14.475; DLE_x = 4.025; s_A = 0.7824" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ALE-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-baseline-life-table-with-dependency-rising-with-age" .

hemo:7006601 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article Scenario A with prevention lowering dependency after 75"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A falls and reablement programme cuts dependency to 20% at 75 to 84 and 45% at 85 and over with mortality unchanged. Life expectancy stays at 18.5 years, dependent years fall to 3.55 and active life expectancy rises by 0.475 years to 14.95, an active share of 80.8%, as in the article. The inputs are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article Scenario A with prevention lowering dependency after 75" ;
    hemo:9000014 "l_x = 100000; L_1 = 900000; L_2 = 650000; L_3 = 300000; pi_1 = 0.1; pi_2 = 0.2; pi_3 = 0.45; e_x = 18.5; ALE_x = 14.95; DLE_x = 3.55; s_A = 0.8081" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ALE-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-scenario-a-with-prevention-lowering-dependency-after-75" .

hemo:7006602 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article Scenario B with longer survival after 85"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Mortality improves after 85, so the last band holds 400,000 person-years in place of 300,000, with prevalence unchanged. Life expectancy rises to 19.5 years and active life expectancy to 14.975, but dependent years rise to 4.525 and the active share falls to 76.8%, as in the article. At an illustrative GBP 30,000 per dependent year, HE-FM-COST-001 gives expected care costs of GBP 135,750 per person against GBP 120,750 at baseline. The inputs are illustrative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Article Scenario B with longer survival after 85" ;
    hemo:9000014 "l_x = 100000; L_1 = 900000; L_2 = 650000; L_3 = 400000; pi_1 = 0.1; pi_2 = 0.25; pi_3 = 0.5; e_x = 19.5; ALE_x = 14.975; DLE_x = 4.525; s_A = 0.7679" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ALE-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "article-scenario-b-with-longer-survival-after-85" .

hemo:7006603 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Limiting case with no dependency in any band"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With every pi_i set to 0 the formula returns life expectancy, 18.5 years on the baseline life table, with no dependent years and an active share of 1. The case checks an implementation against the life-table identity." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Limiting case with no dependency in any band" ;
    hemo:9000014 "l_x = 100000; L_1 = 900000; L_2 = 650000; L_3 = 300000; pi_1 = 0; pi_2 = 0; pi_3 = 0; e_x = 18.5; ALE_x = 18.5; DLE_x = 0; s_A = 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-EX-ALE-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "limiting-case-with-no-dependency-in-any-band" .

hemo:7006604 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for every equilibrium of a 2 x 2 game with the outcome probabilities and expected welfare of mixed play"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "As in HE-AS-NASH-001 to HE-AS-NASH-006. The count is complete only for games without the payoff ties of HE-AS-NASH-005; with a tie the code reports the pure cells and no mixed equilibrium, and a continuum of partly mixed equilibria can exist besides them (HE-ER-NASH-006). W is any value attached to the cells, such as the players' surpluses plus the patients' net health gain." .

hemo:7006607 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-NASH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000959 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the cells of HE-IM-NASH-001 and HE-IM-NASH-002 and the cell values in WelfareUpLeft, WelfareUpRight, WelfareDownLeft and WelfareDownRight, enter `=MixProbAUp*MixProbBLeft` in OutcomeUpLeft, `=MixProbAUp*(1-MixProbBLeft)` in OutcomeUpRight, `=(1-MixProbAUp)*MixProbBLeft` in OutcomeDownLeft, `=(1-MixProbAUp)*(1-MixProbBLeft)` in OutcomeDownRight, `=MixProbBLeft*PayoffAUpLeft+(1-MixProbBLeft)*PayoffAUpRight` in ExpectedPayoffA, `=MixProbAUp*PayoffBUpLeft+(1-MixProbAUp)*PayoffBDownLeft` in ExpectedPayoffB, `=OutcomeUpLeft*WelfareUpLeft+OutcomeUpRight*WelfareUpRight+OutcomeDownLeft*WelfareDownLeft+OutcomeDownRight*WelfareDownRight` in ExpectedWelfare, and `=EquilibriumUpLeft+EquilibriumUpRight+EquilibriumDownLeft+EquilibriumDownRight+IFERROR(InteriorMixed,FALSE)` in CountEquilibria, where Excel adds TRUE as 1." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=MixProbAUp*MixProbBLeft" .

hemo:7006608 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-NASH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000959 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`nash22 <- function(u, v, w) { pure <- outer(1:2, 1:2, Vectorize(function(j, k) u[j, k] >= u[3-j, k] && v[j, k] >= v[j, 3-k])); a1 <- u[1, 1]-u[2, 1]; a2 <- u[2, 2]-u[1, 2]; b1 <- v[1, 1]-v[1, 2]; b2 <- v[2, 2]-v[2, 1]; q <- if (a1+a2 == 0) NA else a2/(a1+a2); p <- if (b1+b2 == 0) NA else b2/(b1+b2); mixed <- !is.na(p) && !is.na(q) && p > 0 && p < 1 && q > 0 && q < 1; P <- if (mixed) outer(c(p, 1-p), c(q, 1-q)) else NA; list(pure = pure, p = p, q = q, mixed = mixed, P = P, EW = if (mixed) sum(P*w) else NA, count = sum(pure)+mixed) }` With `u <- matrix(c(-2, 0, 4, 0), 2); v <- matrix(c(-2, 4, 0, 0), 2); w <- matrix(c(7, 14, 14, 0), 2)`, `nash22(u, v, w)` returns pure cells (1, 2) and (2, 1), p and q 0.6667, mixed TRUE, EW 9.3333 and count 3. For the break-even game (`u[1, 1] <- 0; v[1, 1] <- 0`) it returns three pure cells, q 1, mixed FALSE, EW NA and count 3, and for a game with a gap sum of zero, such as `u <- matrix(c(4, 0, 4, 0), 2)`, q is NA and mixed FALSE without an error." ;
    hemo:9000024 "nash22 <- function(u, v, w) { pure <- outer(1:2, 1:2, Vectorize(function(j, k) u[j, k] >= u[3-j, k] && v[j, k] >= v[j, 3-k])); a1 <- u[1, 1]-u[2, 1]; a2 <- u[2, 2]-u[1, 2]; b1 <- v[1, 1]-v[1, 2]; b2 <- v[2, 2]-v[2, 1]; q <- if (a1+a2 == 0) NA else a2/(a1+a2); p <- if (b1+b2 == 0) NA else b2/(b1+b2); mixed <- !is.na(p) && !is.na(q) && p > 0 && p < 1 && q > 0 && q < 1; P <- if (mixed) outer(c(p, 1-p), c(q, 1-q)) else NA; list(pure = pure, p = p, q = q, mixed = mixed, P = P, EW = if (mixed) sum(P*w) else NA, count = sum(pure)+mixed) }" .

hemo:7006609 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-NASH-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000959 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def nash22(u, v, w): pure = [[u[j][k] >= u[1-j][k] and v[j][k] >= v[j][1-k] for k in (0, 1)] for j in (0, 1)]; a1, a2 = u[0][0]-u[1][0], u[1][1]-u[0][1]; b1, b2 = v[0][0]-v[0][1], v[1][1]-v[1][0]; q = a2/(a1+a2) if a1+a2 != 0 else None; p = b2/(b1+b2) if b1+b2 != 0 else None; mixed = p is not None and q is not None and 0 < p < 1 and 0 < q < 1; P = [[(p if j == 0 else 1-p)*(q if k == 0 else 1-q) for k in (0, 1)] for j in (0, 1)] if mixed else None; EW = sum(P[j][k]*w[j][k] for j in (0, 1) for k in (0, 1)) if mixed else None; return dict(pure=pure, p=p, q=q, mixed=mixed, P=P, EW=EW, count=sum(map(sum, pure))+int(mixed))` `nash22([[2, 0], [0, 1]], [[1, 0], [0, 2]], [[3, 0], [0, 3]])` returns pure [[True, False], [False, True]], p 0.6667, q 0.3333, mixed True, P [[0.2222, 0.4444], [0.1111, 0.2222]], EW 1.3333 and count 3 (to floating point). With the gap sum of zero of the R example q is None and mixed False." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def nash22(u, v, w): pure = [[u[j][k] >= u[1-j][k] and v[j][k] >= v[j][1-k] for k in (0, 1)] for j in (0, 1)]; a1, a2 = u[0][0]-u[1][0], u[1][1]-u[0][1]; b1, b2 = v[0][0]-v[0][1], v[1][1]-v[1][0]; q = a2/(a1+a2) if a1+a2 != 0 else None; p = b2/(b1+b2) if b1+b2 != 0 else None; mixed = p is not None and q is not None and 0 < p < 1 and 0 < q < 1; P = [[(p if j == 0 else 1-p)*(q if k == 0 else 1-q) for k in (0, 1)] for j in (0, 1)] if mixed else None; EW = sum(P[j][k]*w[j][k] for j in (0, 1) for k in (0, 1)) if mixed else None; return dict(pure=pure, p=p, q=q, mixed=mixed, P=P, EW=EW, count=sum(map(sum, pure))+int(mixed))" .

hemo:7006612 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for one tender under both pricing rules and a seeded check of the uniform-cost closed forms"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "As in HE-AS-AUCT-001 to HE-AS-AUCT-006. The calculator line is for exactly three suppliers; the code handles any N of 2 or more. Equal costs are allowed in the scalar line and give a price equal to that cost under the second-price rule." .

hemo:7006615 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-AUCT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000960 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With costs in cells named CostSupplierA, CostSupplierB and CostSupplierC, the range top in CostTopCF and the bidder count in BiddersCF, enter `=CostSupplierA+(CostTopCF-CostSupplierA)/BiddersCF` and the same for B and C in BidSupplierA, BidSupplierB and BidSupplierC, then `=MIN(BidSupplierA,BidSupplierB,BidSupplierC)` in PriceFirstPrice and `=MEDIAN(CostSupplierA,CostSupplierB,CostSupplierC)` in PriceSecondPrice, the second-lowest of three costs. For the simulation with three bidders and a range bottom in CostBottomCF, paste 100,000 rows of uniform draws as values (not live RAND) into the range `A2:C100001`, named UniformDraws, enter `=CostBottomCF+(CostTopCF-CostBottomCF)*A2` in E2 and copy it across to G2 and down, then `=SMALL(E2:G2,1)` in H2, `=SMALL(E2:G2,2)` in I2 and `=H2+(CostTopCF-H2)/BiddersCF` in J2, copied down. `=AVERAGE(H2:H100001)`, `=AVERAGE(I2:I100001)` and `=AVERAGE(J2:J100001)` give MeanLowestSim, MeanSecondPriceSim and MeanFirstPriceSim, and `=STDEV(I2:I100001)/SQRT(ROWS(UniformDraws))` gives SESecondPriceSim. More bidders need more draw and cost columns." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CostSupplierA+(CostTopCF-CostSupplierA)/BiddersCF" .

hemo:7006616 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-AUCT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000960 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`tender_sim <- function(cl, ch, N, S, seed) { if (N < 2 || ch < cl || S < 2) stop(\"needs N >= 2, c_high >= c_low and S >= 2\"); set.seed(seed); m <- matrix(runif(S*N, cl, ch), S, N); srt <- t(apply(m, 1, sort)); lo <- srt[, 1]; sp <- srt[, 2]; fp <- lo+(ch-lo)/N; c(lowest = mean(lo), second_price = mean(sp), first_price = mean(fp), closed = cl+2*(ch-cl)/(N+1)) }` `tender_sim(2, 5, 3, 100000, 2026)` returns 2.748819, 3.500529, 3.499212 and 3.5; `tender_sim(2, 5, 5, 100000, 2026)` returns 2.500546, 2.999899, 3.000437 and 3. With `cl` equal to `ch` every output is that cost, and N of 1 stops with the message." ;
    hemo:9000024 "tender_sim <- function(cl, ch, N, S, seed) { if (N < 2 || ch < cl || S < 2) stop(\"needs N >= 2, c_high >= c_low and S >= 2\"); set.seed(seed); m <- matrix(runif(S*N, cl, ch), S, N); srt <- t(apply(m, 1, sort)); lo <- srt[, 1]; sp <- srt[, 2]; fp <- lo+(ch-lo)/N; c(lowest = mean(lo), second_price = mean(sp), first_price = mean(fp), closed = cl+2*(ch-cl)/(N+1)) }" .

hemo:7006617 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-AUCT-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000960 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`import random` then `def tender_sim(cl, ch, N, S, seed): assert N >= 2 and ch >= cl and S >= 2, \"needs N >= 2, c_high >= c_low and S >= 2\"; rng = random.Random(seed); T = [sorted(rng.uniform(cl, ch) for _ in range(N)) for _ in range(S)]; return dict(lowest=sum(t[0] for t in T)/S, second_price=sum(t[1] for t in T)/S, first_price=sum(t[0]+(ch-t[0])/N for t in T)/S, closed=cl+2*(ch-cl)/(N+1))` Standard library only; `tender_sim(2, 5, 3, 100000, 2026)` returns lowest 2.74854, second_price 3.49587, first_price 3.49903 and closed 3.5; `tender_sim(2, 5, 5, 100000, 2026)` returns 2.49980, 2.99660, 2.99984 and 3.0. Equal `cl` and `ch` return that cost for every output, and N of 1 raises AssertionError." ;
    hemo:9000024 "import random" .

hemo:7006620 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for two-step floating catchment area scores, mean and spread for any number of sites"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The assumptions of HE-FM-ACCS-001 hold (HE-AS-ACCS-001 to HE-AS-ACCS-003). A provider with nobody within the threshold has no defined ratio; the R and Python code return NA or nan for it and leave it out of every score, and the Excel guard sets its ratio to 0, so in every version its capacity drops out and the supply check falls short by that capacity. The total population must be above zero." .

hemo:7006623 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ACCS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000961 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Name a block of m rows and n columns of travel times TravelTimes, a column of m populations PopColumn, a row of n capacities SupplyRow and the threshold ThresholdCF. In a row of n cells named CatchPopRow enter `=SUMPRODUCT((INDEX(TravelTimes,0,COLUMNS($A$1:A$1))<=ThresholdCF)*PopColumn)` and fill right; in a row of n cells named RatioRow enter `=IF(INDEX(CatchPopRow,COLUMNS($A$1:A$1))>0,INDEX(SupplyRow,COLUMNS($A$1:A$1))/INDEX(CatchPopRow,COLUMNS($A$1:A$1)),0)` and fill right. In a column of m cells named AccessColumn enter `=SUMPRODUCT((INDEX(TravelTimes,ROWS($A$1:A1),0)<=ThresholdCF)*RatioRow)` and fill down. A cell named MeanAccessCF holds `=SUMPRODUCT(PopColumn,AccessColumn)/SUM(PopColumn)` and a cell named VarAccessCF holds `=SUMPRODUCT(PopColumn,(AccessColumn-MeanAccessCF)^2)/SUM(PopColumn)`, both in GPs per person; multiply the mean by 10,000 and the variance by 10,000 squared for the article's units. INDEX with a row or column argument of 0 returns a whole column or row, and SUMPRODUCT evaluates the comparisons as arrays without array entry, so the formulas work in every Excel version." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT((INDEX(TravelTimes,0,COLUMNS($A$1:A$1))<=ThresholdCF)*PopColumn)" .

hemo:7006624 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ACCS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000961 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`fca2 <- function(S, P, D, d0, k = 10000) { D <- matrix(D, nrow = length(P)); W <- (D <= d0) * 1; dem <- colSums(W * P); R <- ifelse(dem > 0, S / dem, NA); A <- as.vector(W %*% ifelse(is.na(R), 0, R)); M <- sum(P * A) / sum(P); list(R = k * R, A = k * A, mean = k * M, var = k^2 * sum(P * (A-M)^2) / sum(P)) }` Called as `fca2(c(8, 4), c(10000, 20000, 6000), rbind(c(10, 40), c(20, 25), c(45, 15)), 30)` it returns R of 2.667 and 1.538, A of 2.667, 4.205 and 1.538, mean 3.333 and variance 1.083; with `c(12, 0)` the variance is 2.222. The `matrix` line keeps one row or one column of times a matrix." ;
    hemo:9000024 "fca2 <- function(S, P, D, d0, k = 10000) { D <- matrix(D, nrow = length(P)); W <- (D <= d0) * 1; dem <- colSums(W * P); R <- ifelse(dem > 0, S / dem, NA); A <- as.vector(W %*% ifelse(is.na(R), 0, R)); M <- sum(P * A) / sum(P); list(R = k * R, A = k * A, mean = k * M, var = k^2 * sum(P * (A-M)^2) / sum(P)) }" .

hemo:7006625 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ACCS-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000961 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def fca2(S, P, D, d0, k=10000): n, m = len(S), len(P); dem = [sum(P[i] for i in range(m) if D[i][j] <= d0) for j in range(n)]; R = [S[j] / dem[j] if dem[j] > 0 else float('nan') for j in range(n)]; A = [sum(R[j] for j in range(n) if D[i][j] <= d0 and dem[j] > 0) for i in range(m)]; T = sum(P); M = sum(p * a for p, a in zip(P, A)) / T; return [k * r for r in R], [k * a for a in A], k * M, k * k * sum(p * (a-M) ** 2 for p, a in zip(P, A)) / T` Standard library only; `fca2([8, 4], [10000, 20000, 6000], [[10, 40], [20, 25], [45, 15]], 30)` returns the same values as the R code, and a total population of 0 raises ZeroDivisionError, where the R code returns NaN for the mean and variance." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def fca2(S, P, D, d0, k=10000): n, m = len(S), len(P); dem = [sum(P[i] for i in range(m) if D[i][j] <= d0) for j in range(n)]; R = [S[j] / dem[j] if dem[j] > 0 else float('nan') for j in range(n)]; A = [sum(R[j] for j in range(n) if D[i][j] <= d0 and dem[j] > 0) for i in range(m)]; T = sum(P); M = sum(p * a for p, a in zip(P, A)) / T; return [k * r for r in R], [k * a for a in A], k * M, k * k * sum(p * (a-M) ** 2 for p, a in zip(P, A)) / T" .

hemo:7006628 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for gravity accessibility scores, mean and spread for any number of sites"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The assumptions of HE-FM-ACCS-002 hold (HE-AS-ACCS-004 to HE-AS-ACCS-006). Every travel time is above zero; the R code stops with a message and the Python code raises AssertionError when one is not, and Excel returns #DIV/0! for a zero or blank time when beta is above zero and #NUM! when beta is 0. The total population must be above zero; with every population at 0 the Python code raises ZeroDivisionError and the R code returns infinite scores and a NaN mean and variance." .

hemo:7006631 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ACCS-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000962 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With TravelTimes, PopColumn and SupplyRow laid out as for HE-CF-ACCS-001 and the coefficient in a cell named BetaCF, enter in a row of n cells named DemandRow `=SUMPRODUCT(PopColumn,INDEX(TravelTimes,0,COLUMNS($A$1:A$1))^(-BetaCF))` and fill right, and in a column of m cells named AccessColumnG `=SUMPRODUCT(SupplyRow/DemandRow,INDEX(TravelTimes,ROWS($A$1:A1),0)^(-BetaCF))` and fill down. A cell named MeanAccessG holds `=SUMPRODUCT(PopColumn,AccessColumnG)/SUM(PopColumn)` and a cell named VarAccessG holds `=SUMPRODUCT(PopColumn,(AccessColumnG-MeanAccessG)^2)/SUM(PopColumn)`, in GPs per person." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(PopColumn,INDEX(TravelTimes,0,COLUMNS($A$1:A$1))^(-BetaCF))" .

hemo:7006632 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ACCS-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000962 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`grav <- function(S, P, D, beta, k = 10000) { D <- matrix(D, nrow = length(P)); if (any(D <= 0)) stop(\"travel times must be above zero\"); W <- D^(-beta); V <- colSums(W * P); A <- as.vector(W %*% (S / V)); M <- sum(P * A) / sum(P); list(V = V, A = k * A, mean = k * M, var = k^2 * sum(P * (A-M)^2) / sum(P)) }` Called as `grav(c(8, 4), c(10000, 20000, 6000), rbind(c(10, 40), c(20, 25), c(45, 15)), 1)` it returns V of 2133.333 and 1450, A of 4.440, 2.978 and 2.672, mean 3.333 and variance 0.483." ;
    hemo:9000024 "grav <- function(S, P, D, beta, k = 10000) { D <- matrix(D, nrow = length(P)); if (any(D <= 0)) stop(\"travel times must be above zero\"); W <- D^(-beta); V <- colSums(W * P); A <- as.vector(W %*% (S / V)); M <- sum(P * A) / sum(P); list(V = V, A = k * A, mean = k * M, var = k^2 * sum(P * (A-M)^2) / sum(P)) }" .

hemo:7006633 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ACCS-002"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000962 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def grav(S, P, D, beta, k=10000): assert min(min(r) for r in D) > 0, 'travel times must be above zero'; n, m = len(S), len(P); V = [sum(P[i] * D[i][j] ** -beta for i in range(m)) for j in range(n)]; A = [sum(S[j] * D[i][j] ** -beta / V[j] for j in range(n)) for i in range(m)]; T = sum(P); M = sum(p * a for p, a in zip(P, A)) / T; return V, [k * a for a in A], k * M, k * k * sum(p * (a-M) ** 2 for p, a in zip(P, A)) / T` Standard library only; `grav([8, 4], [10000, 20000, 6000], [[10, 40], [20, 25], [45, 15]], 2)` returns A of 5.615, 2.293 and 2.997 and variance 2.066." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def grav(S, P, D, beta, k=10000): assert min(min(r) for r in D) > 0, 'travel times must be above zero'; n, m = len(S), len(P); V = [sum(P[i] * D[i][j] ** -beta for i in range(m)) for j in range(n)]; A = [sum(S[j] * D[i][j] ** -beta / V[j] for j in range(n)) for i in range(m)]; T = sum(P); M = sum(p * a for p, a in zip(P, A)) / T; return V, [k * a for a in A], k * M, k * k * sum(p * (a-M) ** 2 for p, a in zip(P, A)) / T" .

hemo:7006636 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000017 ;
    rdfs:label "Assumption of Computational function for Sullivan active life expectancy with its standard error over any number of age bands"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:9000008 "The assumptions of HE-FM-ALE-001 hold (HE-AS-ALE-001 to HE-AS-ALE-004). The number dependent in each band follows an independent binomial process estimated by the sample fraction, as Imai and Soneji describe the standard approach, so the variance reflects survey sampling only; the life table is treated as known, which Imai and Soneji report is typical in the literature because the death and population counts behind it are large. Simple random sampling is assumed; under a complex design the prevalence is a weighted average and design effects are not captured by this formula." .

hemo:7006639 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "excel implementation of HE-CF-ALE-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000963 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000019 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Name the band columns PersonYearsBandCF, DependentShareBandCF and SurveyRespondentsBandCF (one row per band, any number of rows) and the survivors cell SurvivorsAtStartAgeCF. A cell named SullivanALE holds `=SUMPRODUCT(1-DependentShareBandCF,PersonYearsBandCF)/SurvivorsAtStartAgeCF`, a cell named SullivanVariance holds `=SUMPRODUCT(DependentShareBandCF*(1-DependentShareBandCF)*PersonYearsBandCF^2/SurveyRespondentsBandCF)/SurvivorsAtStartAgeCF^2`, a cell named SullivanSE holds `=SQRT(SullivanVariance)`, and cells named SullivanLower and SullivanUpper hold `=SullivanALE-1.96*SullivanSE` and `=SullivanALE+1.96*SullivanSE`. SUMPRODUCT evaluates the array products without array entry, so the formulas work in every Excel version. A blank respondents cell returns #DIV/0! in SullivanVariance." ;
    hemo:9000024 "=SUMPRODUCT(1-DependentShareBandCF,PersonYearsBandCF)/SurvivorsAtStartAgeCF" .

hemo:7006640 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "r implementation of HE-CF-ALE-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000963 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000020 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`sullivan_ale <- function(lx, L, pi, N) { w <- L/lx; ale <- sum((1-pi)*w); le <- sum(w); v <- sum(w^2*pi*(1-pi)/N); se <- sqrt(v); list(ale = ale, le = le, dle = le-ale, share = ale/le, se = se, lower = ale-1.96*se, upper = ale+1.96*se) }` Called as `sullivan_ale(100000, c(900000, 650000, 300000), c(0.1, 0.25, 0.5), c(2000, 1500, 800))` it returns ale 14.475, le 18.5, dle 4.025, share 0.7824, se 0.1083 and limits 14.2626 and 14.6874. A band with N of 0 gives an infinite or undefined variance, so every N_i must be at least 1, and l_x must be positive." ;
    hemo:9000024 "sullivan_ale <- function(lx, L, pi, N) { w <- L/lx; ale <- sum((1-pi)*w); le <- sum(w); v <- sum(w^2*pi*(1-pi)/N); se <- sqrt(v); list(ale = ale, le = le, dle = le-ale, share = ale/le, se = se, lower = ale-1.96*se, upper = ale+1.96*se) }" .

hemo:7006641 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000014 ;
    rdfs:label "python implementation of HE-CF-ALE-001"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000004 hemo:5000963 ;
    hemo:1000040 hemo:4000021 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`def sullivan_ale(lx, L, pi, N): w = [x/lx for x in L]; ale = sum((1-p)*x for p, x in zip(pi, w)); le = sum(w); v = sum(x*x*p*(1-p)/n for x, p, n in zip(w, pi, N)); se = v**0.5; return ale, le, le-ale, ale/le, se, ale-1.96*se, ale+1.96*se` Standard library only; `sullivan_ale(100000, [900000, 650000, 300000], [0.1, 0.25, 0.5], [2000, 1500, 800])` returns 14.475, 18.5, 4.025, 0.7824, 0.1083, 14.2626 and 14.6874 (rounded). With every prevalence 0 it returns a standard error of 0 and active life expectancy equal to life expectancy; a respondent count of 0 or an empty list of bands raises ZeroDivisionError, so the inputs need at least one band, every N_i at least 1 and l_x above 0." ;
    hemo:9000024 "def sullivan_ale(lx, L, pi, N): w = [x/lx for x in L]; ale = sum((1-p)*x for p, x in zip(pi, w)); le = sum(w); v = sum(x*x*p*(1-p)/n for x, p, n in zip(w, pi, N)); se = v**0.5; return ale, le, le-ale, ale/le, se, ale-1.96*se, ale+1.96*se" .

