hemo:7007251 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's USD 200,000 specific attachment point (HE-CF-ATTP-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "One member is expected above it, expected reimbursement is USD 300,000 with a standard deviation of about USD 299,850, and the premium is USD 390,000, USD 780,000 less than at USD 50,000; the ratio of spread to mean is about 1.00. `c = 1; p_1 = 0.006; C_1 = 75000; p_2 = 0.003; C_2 = 150000; p_3 = 0.001; C_3 = 500000; n = 1000; theta = 0.3; A = 200000; U = 500000; E_R = 300; M2_R = 90000000; V_R = 89910000; SD_R = 9482.09; N_A = 1; E_T = 300000; SD_T = 299849.96; P_T = 390000; CV_T = 0.9995`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c = 1; p_1 = 0.006; C_1 = 75000; p_2 = 0.003; C_2 = 150000; p_3 = 0.001; C_3 = 500000; n = 1000; theta = 0.3; A = 200000; U = 500000; E_R = 300; M2_R = 90000000; V_R = 89910000; SD_R = 9482.09; N_A = 1; E_T = 300000; SD_T = 299849.96; P_T = 390000; CV_T = 0.9995" .

hemo:7007252 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's group standard deviations reproduced (HE-CF-ATTP-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's inputs, Members at 1000, CoinsRate at 1, ClaimCap at 500000 and attachment points of 50000, 100000 and 200000 in F2 to F4, J2 to J4 round to 485,222, 408,898 and 299,850 and I2 equals 900,000. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the squared mean left unsubtracted in column H, which gives about 486,056 in J2, or the per-member standard deviation multiplied by the number of members, about 15,344,054 in J2. Excel check `=AND(ROUND(J2,0)=485222,ROUND(J3,0)=408898,ROUND(J4,0)=299850,I2=900000)`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ROUND(J2,0)=485222,ROUND(J3,0)=408898,ROUND(J4,0)=299850,I2=900000)" .

hemo:7007253 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Layer between two attachment points by the difference route (HE-CF-ATTP-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the cap at or above the attachment point in F4, G2 less G4 equals the expected reimbursement of the layer between the attachment points in F2 and F4 computed directly with the higher one as a cap, the excess layer as the difference of two first-dollar expected values in equation 11 of Miccolis 1977. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the whole claim paid once it passes the attachment point, which gives a difference of 900 against 600, or G2 halved, which gives 150. Excel check `=ABS((G2-G4)-CoinsRate*SUMPRODUCT(ClaimProbs,(((ClaimLevels<F4)*ClaimLevels+(ClaimLevels>=F4)*F4)-F2)*(ClaimLevels>F2)))<1E-6`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS((G2-G4)-CoinsRate*SUMPRODUCT(ClaimProbs,(((ClaimLevels<F4)*ClaimLevels+(ClaimLevels>=F4)*F4)-F2)*(ClaimLevels>F2)))<1E-6" .

hemo:7007254 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article rehabilitation trial (HE-CF-HEDG-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With d of 0.8366 and 10 and 12 patients the calculator chain gives J of about 0.962025, g of about 0.8048 and SE_g of about 0.4294. The code gives J_exact of about 0.961945, so approx_error is about 0.000081, g_exact about 0.8048, se_g_exact about 0.4294 and v_theorem2 about 0.2067 against V_g of about 0.1844 (computed here). `d = 0.8366; n_1 = 10; n_2 = 12; m = 20; J = 0.962025; g = 0.8048; V_d = 0.1992; V_g = 0.1844; SE_g = 0.4294`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d = 0.8366; n_1 = 10; n_2 = 12; m = 20; J = 0.962025; g = 0.8048; V_d = 0.1992; V_g = 0.1844; SE_g = 0.4294" .

hemo:7007255 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Hedges' Table I at 20 degrees of freedom (HE-CF-HEDG-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With d of 1 and 11 patients per arm, m is 20 and J_exact is 0.96194, the value in Hedges' Table I, against the approximate 0.962025. `d = 1; n_1 = 11; n_2 = 11; m = 20; J = 0.962025; g = 0.9620; V_d = 0.2045; V_g = 0.1893; SE_g = 0.4351`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d = 1; n_1 = 11; n_2 = 11; m = 20; J = 0.962025; g = 0.9620; V_d = 0.2045; V_g = 0.1893; SE_g = 0.4351" .

hemo:7007256 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Hedges' Table I value at 20 degrees of freedom (HE-CF-HEDG-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With SizeIntervention and SizeComparator both 11, the model's ExactJ cell rounds to 0.96194, the value printed in Hedges' Table I. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the total sample size used in place of the degrees of freedom, which gives 0.96545, the Table I value for 22, or m/2 used in the denominator without its square root, about 0.3042. Excel check `=ROUND(ExactJ,5)=0.96194`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND(ExactJ,5)=0.96194" .

hemo:7007257 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Hedges' maximum approximation error at two degrees of freedom (HE-CF-HEDG-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With both group sizes 2, the model's ExactJ cell rounds to 0.56419, the Table I value, and ApproxErrorJ rounds to Hedges' maximum error of 0.007. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the two GAMMALN arguments swapped, which gives about 1.7725, or the error taken as exact less approximate, minus 0.007. Excel check `=AND(ROUND(ExactJ,5)=0.56419,ROUND(ApproxErrorJ,3)=0.007)`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ROUND(ExactJ,5)=0.56419,ROUND(ApproxErrorJ,3)=0.007)" .

hemo:7007258 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's step 1, shift of -0.10 in both arms (HE-CF-LTFU-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The arm means fall to 0.76 and 0.67, the gain is 0.09, the ICER is GBP 16,667 per QALY and the net health benefit at GBP 20,000 per QALY is 0.015 QALYs, as in the article; the common-shift tipping point is -0.25. `E_obs_T = 0.78; E_obs_C = 0.68; p_T = 0.2; p_C = 0.1; r = 1; delta = -0.1; Delta_C = 1500; lambda = 20000; E_T = 0.76; E_C = 0.67; Delta_E = 0.09; ICER = 16666.67; INHB = 0.015; delta_star = -0.25`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "E_obs_T = 0.78; E_obs_C = 0.68; p_T = 0.2; p_C = 0.1; r = 1; delta = -0.1; Delta_C = 1500; lambda = 20000; E_T = 0.76; E_C = 0.67; Delta_E = 0.09; ICER = 16666.67; INHB = 0.015; delta_star = -0.25" .

hemo:7007259 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's step 2, shift of -0.10 in the new treatment arm only (HE-CF-LTFU-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The means are 0.76 and 0.68, the gain is 0.08 and the ICER is GBP 18,750 per QALY, as in the article, with a tipping point of -0.125. `E_obs_T = 0.78; E_obs_C = 0.68; p_T = 0.2; p_C = 0.1; r = 0; delta = -0.1; Delta_C = 1500; lambda = 20000; E_T = 0.76; E_C = 0.68; Delta_E = 0.08; ICER = 18750; INHB = 0.005; delta_star = -0.125`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "E_obs_T = 0.78; E_obs_C = 0.68; p_T = 0.2; p_C = 0.1; r = 0; delta = -0.1; Delta_C = 1500; lambda = 20000; E_T = 0.76; E_C = 0.68; Delta_E = 0.08; ICER = 18750; INHB = 0.005; delta_star = -0.125" .

hemo:7007260 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "At the tipping point (HE-CF-LTFU-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "A shift of -0.125 in the new treatment arm only gives a gain of 0.075, an ICER equal to the threshold and a net health benefit of zero, a limiting case. `E_obs_T = 0.78; E_obs_C = 0.68; p_T = 0.2; p_C = 0.1; r = 0; delta = -0.125; Delta_C = 1500; lambda = 20000; E_T = 0.755; E_C = 0.68; Delta_E = 0.075; ICER = 20000; INHB = 0; delta_star = -0.125`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "E_obs_T = 0.78; E_obs_C = 0.68; p_T = 0.2; p_C = 0.1; r = 0; delta = -0.125; Delta_C = 1500; lambda = 20000; E_T = 0.755; E_C = 0.68; Delta_E = 0.075; ICER = 20000; INHB = 0; delta_star = -0.125" .

hemo:7007261 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Participant-level table, for the R and Python code (HE-CF-LTFU-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "An illustrative new treatment arm of five with QALYs 0.70, 0.80, 0.84 and 0.78 observed and one participant lost and imputed at 0.78, and a usual care arm of ten with 0.60, 0.65, 0.70, 0.72, 0.66, 0.70, 0.68, 0.71 and 0.70 observed and one imputed at 0.68, reproduce the article's means and shares lost. `ltfu_scan(q, arm, lost, 1500, 20000)` with the grid 0 to -0.30 in steps of 0.02 returns a tipping point of -0.125, and with `r = 1` it returns -0.25. The scalar form is the Math / Code line and HE-EX-LTFU-001." ;
    hemo:9000014 "r = 1" .

hemo:7007262 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Grid tipping point equals the closed form (HE-CF-LTFU-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's inputs the value in GridTipping equals TippingShift of HE-IM-LTFU-001 within 1E-9. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a grid that adds the shift to every participant in the new treatment arm rather than to those lost, which returns -0.025 instead of -0.125. Excel check `=ABS(GridTipping-TippingShift)<1E-9`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(GridTipping-TippingShift)<1E-9" .

hemo:7007263 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's step 2 ICER on the grid (HE-CF-LTFU-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's inputs and ShiftRatio 0, the sixth grid row, a shift of -0.10, holds an ICER of GBP 18,750 per QALY. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows ShiftRatio left at 1, which gives GBP 16,667 per QALY, the article's step 1. Excel check `=ABS(INDEX(GridICER,6)-18750)<0.01`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(INDEX(GridICER,6)-18750)<0.01" .

hemo:7007264 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's Hospitals A and B (HE-CF-CMAD-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "The indices are 105 and 95 and the directly standardised costs 4,935 GBP and 4,465 GBP, so both methods reverse the crude ranking, as in the article. `cbar_1 = 2000; cbar_2 = 8000; n_A1 = 800; n_A2 = 200; u_A1 = 2100; u_A2 = 8400; n_B1 = 300; n_B2 = 700; u_B1 = 1900; u_B2 = 7600; A_A = 3360000; A_B = 5890000; E_A = 3200000; E_B = 6200000; crude_A = 3360; crude_B = 5890; I_A = 105; I_B = 95; s_1 = 0.55; D_A = 4935; D_B = 4465; rev_I = 1; rev_D = 1`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "cbar_1 = 2000; cbar_2 = 8000; n_A1 = 800; n_A2 = 200; u_A1 = 2100; u_A2 = 8400; n_B1 = 300; n_B2 = 700; u_B1 = 1900; u_B2 = 7600; A_A = 3360000; A_B = 5890000; E_A = 3200000; E_B = 6200000; crude_A = 3360; crude_B = 5890; I_A = 105; I_B = 95; s_1 = 0.55; D_A = 4935; D_B = 4465; rev_I = 1; rev_D = 1" .

hemo:7007265 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Identical unit costs in every group (HE-CF-CMAD-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With illustrative unit costs of 2,200 GBP and 8,000 GBP at both hospitals the indices are 105 and about 100.97 and reverse the crude ranking, while the directly standardised costs are equal at 4,810 GBP, so the reversal comes from the mix weighting alone (computed here for illustration). `cbar_1 = 2000; cbar_2 = 8000; n_A1 = 800; n_A2 = 200; u_A1 = 2200; u_A2 = 8000; n_B1 = 300; n_B2 = 700; u_B1 = 2200; u_B2 = 8000; A_A = 3360000; A_B = 6260000; E_A = 3200000; E_B = 6200000; crude_A = 3360; crude_B = 6260; I_A = 105; I_B = 100.9677; s_1 = 0.55; D_A = 4810; D_B = 4810; rev_I = 1; rev_D = 0`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "cbar_1 = 2000; cbar_2 = 8000; n_A1 = 800; n_A2 = 200; u_A1 = 2200; u_A2 = 8000; n_B1 = 300; n_B2 = 700; u_B1 = 2200; u_B2 = 8000; A_A = 3360000; A_B = 6260000; E_A = 3200000; E_B = 6200000; crude_A = 3360; crude_B = 6260; I_A = 105; I_B = 100.9677; s_1 = 0.55; D_A = 4810; D_B = 4810; rev_I = 1; rev_D = 0" .

hemo:7007266 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's hospitals reverse under both methods (HE-CF-CMAD-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's inputs ReversedPairsIndex and ReversedPairsDirect are both 1. Expected result TRUE. Expected costs priced at each provider's own unit costs instead of NationalUnitCost give both indices 100, a tie that is not counted, and FALSE. Excel check `=AND(ReversedPairsIndex=1,ReversedPairsDirect=1)`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ReversedPairsIndex=1,ReversedPairsDirect=1)" .

hemo:7007267 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Identical unit costs give identical direct costs (HE-CF-CMAD-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the identical unit costs of the second worked example DirectCostFirst equals DirectCostSecond while ReversedPairsIndex is 1. Expected result TRUE. A direct standardisation taken on each provider's own mix returns the crude costs, 3,360 GBP and 6,260 GBP, and FALSE. Excel check `=AND(ABS(DirectCostFirst-DirectCostSecond)<1E-9,ReversedPairsIndex=1)`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(DirectCostFirst-DirectCostSecond)<1E-9,ReversedPairsIndex=1)" .

hemo:7007268 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Illustrative study with dependence in both groups (HE-CF-NLR-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Of 100 people with the condition, 5 are negative on the first test, 10 on the second and 3 on both; of 200 without it, 100, 160 and 90 (illustrative counts). The tests match those of the worked examples, the covariances are 0.025 and 0.05, and the joint ratio is about 0.0667, or 0.03 / 0.45, against a product of 0.0125. `n_D = 100; n_N = 200; x_1D = 5; x_2D = 10; x_12D = 3; y_1N = 100; y_2N = 160; y_12N = 90; Se_1 = 0.95; Se_2 = 0.9; Sp_1 = 0.5; Sp_2 = 0.8; C_pos = 0.025; C_neg = 0.05; LR_neg_12 = 0.0667; LR_prod = 0.0125`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "n_D = 100; n_N = 200; x_1D = 5; x_2D = 10; x_12D = 3; y_1N = 100; y_2N = 160; y_12N = 90; Se_1 = 0.95; Se_2 = 0.9; Sp_1 = 0.5; Sp_2 = 0.8; C_pos = 0.025; C_neg = 0.05; LR_neg_12 = 0.0667; LR_prod = 0.0125" .

hemo:7007269 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Counts with no dependence (HE-CF-NLR-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With 1,000 people in each group and both-negative counts of 5 and 400, equal to the products of the single-test rates, both covariances are 0 and the joint ratio equals the product, a limiting case. `n_D = 1000; n_N = 1000; x_1D = 50; x_2D = 100; x_12D = 5; y_1N = 500; y_2N = 800; y_12N = 400; Se_1 = 0.95; Se_2 = 0.9; Sp_1 = 0.5; Sp_2 = 0.8; C_pos = 0; C_neg = 0; LR_neg_12 = 0.0125; LR_prod = 0.0125`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "n_D = 1000; n_N = 1000; x_1D = 50; x_2D = 100; x_12D = 5; y_1N = 500; y_2N = 800; y_12N = 400; Se_1 = 0.95; Se_2 = 0.9; Sp_1 = 0.5; Sp_2 = 0.8; C_pos = 0; C_neg = 0; LR_neg_12 = 0.0125; LR_prod = 0.0125" .

hemo:7007270 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Joint ratio equals the observed both-negative ratio (HE-CF-NLR-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the first worked example's counts JointNegLRCounts equals the both-negative proportion among people with the condition divided by that among people without it, 0.03 / 0.45. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the independence product returned in place of the joint ratio, 0.0125 against about 0.0667, or the implementation cell doubled. Excel check `=ABS(JointNegLRCounts-(CasesNegBoth/CasesTotal)/(NonCasesNegBoth/NonCasesTotal))<1E-12`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(JointNegLRCounts-(CasesNegBoth/CasesTotal)/(NonCasesNegBoth/NonCasesTotal))<1E-12" .

hemo:7007271 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "No dependence gives the product (HE-CF-NLR-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the second worked example's counts both covariance cells are 0 and JointNegLRCounts equals ProductNegLR. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the covariance computed with the sensitivities in place of the false negative rates, 0.005 minus 0.855, about minus 0.85, or the joint ratio cell doubled. Excel check `=AND(ABS(CovCasesEst)<1E-12,ABS(CovNonCasesEst)<1E-12,ABS(JointNegLRCounts-ProductNegLR)<1E-12)`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(CovCasesEst)<1E-12,ABS(CovNonCasesEst)<1E-12,ABS(JointNegLRCounts-ProductNegLR)<1E-12)" .

hemo:7007272 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's moderate scenario (HE-CF-COPAS-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Probabilities of 0.95 for the large trials and 0.35 for the small ones give gamma_1 = 2.0302 / 7.5 = 0.2707 and gamma_0 = -1.062, as in the article's step 2, and 18.68 implied unpublished trials. `P_a = 0.95; P_b = 0.35; z_a = 1.6449; z_b = -0.3853; s_a = 0.10; s_b = 0.40; n_a = 2; n_b = 10; gamma_1 = 0.2707; gamma_0 = -1.062; M = 18.68`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_a = 0.95; P_b = 0.35; z_a = 1.6449; z_b = -0.3853; s_a = 0.10; s_b = 0.40; n_a = 2; n_b = 10; gamma_1 = 0.2707; gamma_0 = -1.062; M = 18.68" .

hemo:7007273 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's severe scenario (HE-CF-COPAS-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Probabilities of 0.90 and 0.15 give gamma_1 = 0.3091, gamma_0 = -1.8091 and 56.89 implied unpublished trials, the article's 56.9. `P_a = 0.90; P_b = 0.15; z_a = 1.2816; z_b = -1.0364; s_a = 0.10; s_b = 0.40; n_a = 2; n_b = 10; gamma_1 = 0.3091; gamma_0 = -1.8091; M = 56.89`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_a = 0.90; P_b = 0.15; z_a = 1.2816; z_b = -1.0364; s_a = 0.10; s_b = 0.40; n_a = 2; n_b = 10; gamma_1 = 0.3091; gamma_0 = -1.8091; M = 56.89" .

hemo:7007274 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal probabilities (HE-CF-COPAS-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With both probabilities at 0.5 the quantiles are zero, so gamma_1 and gamma_0 are zero, publication does not depend on precision, and each of the twelve published trials stands for one unpublished trial, a limiting case. `P_a = 0.5; P_b = 0.5; z_a = 0; z_b = 0; s_a = 0.10; s_b = 0.40; n_a = 2; n_b = 10; gamma_1 = 0; gamma_0 = 0; M = 12`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_a = 0.5; P_b = 0.5; z_a = 0; z_b = 0; s_a = 0.10; s_b = 0.40; n_a = 2; n_b = 10; gamma_1 = 0; gamma_0 = 0; M = 12" .

hemo:7007275 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Selection parameters reproduce both assumed probabilities (HE-CF-COPAS-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "NORM.S.DIST of the index built from GammaZeroCF and GammaOneCF at each standard error returns PubProbA and PubProbB, within 1E-9 in total. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows gamma_1 doubled or halved, or divided by the difference in standard errors instead of the difference in precisions, which gives gamma_1 = -6.767 and a probability of almost 0 at s_a. Excel check `=ABS(NORM.S.DIST(GammaZeroCF+GammaOneCF/StdErrA,TRUE)-PubProbA)+ABS(NORM.S.DIST(GammaZeroCF+GammaOneCF/StdErrB,TRUE)-PubProbB)<1E-9`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(NORM.S.DIST(GammaZeroCF+GammaOneCF/StdErrA,TRUE)-PubProbA)+ABS(NORM.S.DIST(GammaZeroCF+GammaOneCF/StdErrB,TRUE)-PubProbB)<1E-9" .

hemo:7007276 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's moderate scenario count (HE-CF-COPAS-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With PubProbA 0.95, PubProbB 0.35, TrialsA 2 and TrialsB 10, ImpliedUnpubCF holds 18.6767 within 0.001. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows 1 / P in place of (1 minus P) / P, which returns 30.6767. Excel check `=ABS(ImpliedUnpubCF-18.6767)<0.001`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ImpliedUnpubCF-18.6767)<0.001" .

hemo:7007277 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Quarter ending 30 June with rising monthly revenue (HE-CF-DAR-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With an illustrative split of the article's USD 91.0 million into 28.0, 31.0 and 32.0 for April, May and June, the quarter has 91 days, r_bar is 1.0 and DAR is 55.0, as in the article. June's own daily rate of 32.0 over 30 days, 1.0667, would give 51.56 days (computed here for illustration). Money is in USD millions. `AR = 55; R_1 = 28; R_2 = 31; R_3 = 32; d_1 = 30; d_2 = 31; d_3 = 30; D = 91; R = 91; r_bar = 1; DAR = 55`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "AR = 55; R_1 = 28; R_2 = 31; R_3 = 32; d_1 = 30; d_2 = 31; d_3 = 30; D = 91; R = 91; r_bar = 1; DAR = 55" .

hemo:7007278 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Quarter ending 31 March 2026 with the same revenue (HE-CF-DAR-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With illustrative revenue of 30.0, 30.0 and 31.0 for January, February and March 2026, the quarter has 90 days, so the same USD 91.0 million gives r_bar of 1.0111 and DAR of 54.40 (computed here for illustration). Money is in USD millions. `AR = 55; R_1 = 30; R_2 = 30; R_3 = 31; d_1 = 31; d_2 = 28; d_3 = 31; D = 90; R = 91; r_bar = 1.0111; DAR = 54.3956`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "AR = 55; R_1 = 30; R_2 = 30; R_3 = 31; d_1 = 31; d_2 = 28; d_3 = 31; D = 90; R = 91; r_bar = 1.0111; DAR = 54.3956" .

hemo:7007279 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Quarter ending 30 June (HE-CF-DAR-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With NetReceivables 55, MonthNetRevenue 28, 31 and 32 and ReportMonth 1 June 2026, QuarterNetDaysInAR equals the article's 55.0 days. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a fixed 90-day quarter, which gives 54.40, or June's revenue and days alone, which gives 51.56, and doubling or halving the cell also gives FALSE. Excel check `=ABS(QuarterNetDaysInAR-55)<1E-9`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(QuarterNetDaysInAR-55)<1E-9" .

hemo:7007280 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Quarter ending 31 March 2026 (HE-CF-DAR-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With NetReceivables 55, MonthNetRevenue 30, 30 and 31 and ReportMonth 1 March 2026, QuarterNetDaysInAR equals 55 times 90 over 91, 54.3956 days. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a fixed 91-day quarter, which gives 55.0, or a leap-year February of 29 days, which gives 55.0 too, and doubling or halving the cell also gives FALSE. Excel check `=ABS(QuarterNetDaysInAR-55*90/91)<1E-9`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(QuarterNetDaysInAR-55*90/91)<1E-9" .

hemo:7007281 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Henseler and colleagues' Table 5, customer expectations and perceived value (HE-CF-DISV-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the published item correlations of the three customer expectation items (construct i) and the two perceived value items (construct j), the between-construct mean is 0.2997, the within-construct means are 0.409 and 0.774, and the ratio is 0.5326, the 0.53 the authors report as their lowest value. `r_i1j1 = 0.373; r_i1j2 = 0.326; r_i2j1 = 0.359; r_i2j2 = 0.310; r_i3j1 = 0.230; r_i3j2 = 0.200; r_i1i2 = 0.574; r_i1i3 = 0.318; r_i2i3 = 0.335; r_j1j2 = 0.774; rbar_ij = 0.2997; rbar_ii = 0.409; rbar_jj = 0.774; HTMT_ij = 0.5326`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "r_i1j1 = 0.373; r_i1j2 = 0.326; r_i2j1 = 0.359; r_i2j2 = 0.310; r_i3j1 = 0.230; r_i3j2 = 0.200; r_i1i2 = 0.574; r_i1i3 = 0.318; r_i2i3 = 0.335; r_j1j2 = 0.774; rbar_ij = 0.2997; rbar_ii = 0.409; rbar_jj = 0.774; HTMT_ij = 0.5326" .

hemo:7007282 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Henseler and colleagues' Table 5, all six pairs, for the R and Python code (HE-CF-DISV-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Run on the full eleven-item matrix of Table 5 with three items each for ACSI, CUEX and PERQ and two for PERV, the functions return 0.6321 for ACSI and CUEX, 0.9516 for ACSI and PERQ, 0.8745 for ACSI and PERV, 0.7334 for CUEX and PERQ, 0.5326 for CUEX and PERV and 0.7608 for PERQ and PERV (computed here). Each rounds to the value in the authors' Table 6 (0.63, 0.95, 0.87, 0.73, 0.53 and 0.76), and the text reports two pairs above 0.85 (ACSI and PERQ, ACSI and PERV) and one above 0.90 (ACSI and PERQ). This example is for reading against the R and Python code, and the scalar line above runs on the site." .

hemo:7007283 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Table 5, customer expectations and perceived value (HE-CF-DISV-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With Table 5 in ItemCorr, StartI 4, CountI 3, StartJ 10 and CountJ 2, HTMTBlock rounds to 0.53, the value the authors report. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the diagonal ones kept in the within-construct means (0.606 and 0.887), which gives 0.4087, and a doubled cell (1.0652) or a halved cell (0.2663) also gives FALSE. Excel check `=ROUND(HTMTBlock,2)=0.53`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND(HTMTBlock,2)=0.53" .

hemo:7007284 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Table 5, customer satisfaction and perceived quality (HE-CF-DISV-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With StartI 1, CountI 3, StartJ 7 and CountJ 3, HTMTBlock rounds to 0.95, the highest value the authors report and above both cut-offs. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a matrix with only its lower triangle filled and zeros above the diagonal, which gives 0 because the block between the constructs then lies in the upper triangle (with those cells left blank AVERAGE finds no numbers and the check returns #DIV/0! in place of TRUE), and a doubled cell (1.9033) or a halved cell (0.4758) also gives FALSE. Excel check `=ROUND(HTMTBlock,2)=0.95`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ROUND(HTMTBlock,2)=0.95" .

hemo:7007285 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's equity rule with D required (HE-CF-IPH-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With GBP 10 million and D required because it serves the group with the worst health, the best package is A plus D for 290 QALYs, so the rule costs 85 QALYs, as in the article, and four packages qualify. Money is in GBP millions. `c_1 = 6; c_2 = 5; c_3 = 5; c_4 = 2; q_1 = 240; q_2 = 190; q_3 = 185; q_4 = 50; B = 10; H_star = 375; H_must = 290; E_must = 85`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c_1 = 6; c_2 = 5; c_3 = 5; c_4 = 2; q_1 = 240; q_2 = 190; q_3 = 185; q_4 = 50; B = 10; H_star = 375; H_must = 290; E_must = 85" .

hemo:7007286 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "D required at GBP 12 million (HE-CF-IPH-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With an illustrative GBP 12 million the best package is A plus B for 430 QALYs, and with D required it is B plus C plus D for 425, so the rule costs 5 QALYs (computed here for illustration). Money is in GBP millions. `c_1 = 6; c_2 = 5; c_3 = 5; c_4 = 2; q_1 = 240; q_2 = 190; q_3 = 185; q_4 = 50; B = 12; H_star = 430; H_must = 425; E_must = 5`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c_1 = 6; c_2 = 5; c_3 = 5; c_4 = 2; q_1 = 240; q_2 = 190; q_3 = 185; q_4 = 50; B = 12; H_star = 430; H_must = 425; E_must = 5" .

hemo:7007287 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's optimum from the R and Python code (HE-CF-IPH-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "`ip_best(c(\"A\", \"B\", \"C\", \"D\"), c(6, 5, 5, 2), c(240, 190, 185, 50), 10)` returns the package B and C, cost 10, 375 QALYs and 9 qualifying packages, and with `must = \"D\"` it returns A and D, cost 8, 290 QALYs and 4 qualifying packages. This example is for reading against the R and Python code; its calculator form is HE-EX-IPH-001." ;
    hemo:9000014 "must = \"D\"" .

hemo:7007288 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's equity rule (HE-CF-IPH-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With MustFund holding 0, 0, 0 and 1 for the article's inputs, ForcedOptimum is 290 and EquityRuleCost 85. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows an M column without the requirement test, which returns 375 and a cost of 0, and a doubled ForcedOptimum of 580 or a halved one of 145. Excel check `=AND(ABS(ForcedOptimum-290)<0.000001,ABS(EquityRuleCost-85)<0.000001)`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(ForcedOptimum-290)<0.000001,ABS(EquityRuleCost-85)<0.000001)" .

hemo:7007289 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Nothing required (HE-CF-IPH-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With MustFund all 0 the requirement test passes for every package, so ForcedOptimum equals IntegerOptimum, 375 for the article's inputs, a limiting case. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows an M column that tests L2 against 1 in place of SUM(MustFund), which leaves out the packages with no required programme and returns 0, and a doubled or halved ForcedOptimum. Excel check `=ABS(ForcedOptimum-IntegerOptimum)<0.000001`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ForcedOptimum-IntegerOptimum)<0.000001" .

hemo:7007290 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "De Bruin and colleagues' coronary care unit at GBP 10,000 a refusal (HE-CF-CAPP-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the unit's 3.52 arrivals a day and 1.694-day stay, illustrative costs of GBP 150,000 a bed and GBP 10,000 a refusal, the sixth bed cuts refusals by 122.7 a year, to 337.0, worth GBP 1.23 million against its GBP 150,000, so it pays; the code puts the cost minimum at 11 beds (computed here). `lambda = 3.52; L_bar = 1.694; c_b = 150000; c_r = 10000; a = 5.96288; B_1 = 0.8564; B_2 = 0.7186; B_3 = 0.5882; B_4 = 0.4672; B_5 = 0.3578; B_6 = 0.2623; R_5 = 459.70; R_6 = 337.02; S_6 = 1076836`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 3.52; L_bar = 1.694; c_b = 150000; c_r = 10000; a = 5.96288; B_1 = 0.8564; B_2 = 0.7186; B_3 = 0.5882; B_4 = 0.4672; B_5 = 0.3578; B_6 = 0.2623; R_5 = 459.70; R_6 = 337.02; S_6 = 1076836" .

hemo:7007291 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Same unit at GBP 1,000 a refusal (HE-CF-CAPP-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "At an illustrative GBP 1,000 a refusal the sixth bed saves GBP 122,684 of refusal costs, less than its GBP 150,000, and the code puts the cost minimum at 4 beds (computed here). `lambda = 3.52; L_bar = 1.694; c_b = 150000; c_r = 1000; a = 5.96288; B_1 = 0.8564; B_2 = 0.7186; B_3 = 0.5882; B_4 = 0.4672; B_5 = 0.3578; B_6 = 0.2623; R_5 = 459.70; R_6 = 337.02; S_6 = -27316`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda = 3.52; L_bar = 1.694; c_b = 150000; c_r = 1000; a = 5.96288; B_1 = 0.8564; B_2 = 0.7186; B_3 = 0.5882; B_4 = 0.4672; B_5 = 0.3578; B_6 = 0.2623; R_5 = 459.70; R_6 = 337.02; S_6 = -27316" .

hemo:7007292 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's acute medical unit, for reading against the code (HE-CF-CAPP-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With 20 arrivals a day and a 4.0-day stay, the offered load is 80 beds and 95 beds refuse 1.14% of arrivals, 83.0 patients a year, at 83.3% occupancy. Refusals are 99.7, 83.0 and 68.5 a year at 94, 95 and 96 beds, so at GBP 150,000 a bed and GBP 10,000 a refusal the 95th bed saves GBP 167,000 and the 96th GBP 145,000, and the cost minimum is 95 beds; at GBP 5,000 it is 89 and at GBP 20,000 it is 99. The unit one-tenth the size, 2 arrivals a day, refuses 12.2% with 10 beds, 88.8 patients a year, and needs 15 beds and 52.8% occupancy to hold refusals below 1%, all as in the article. This example is for reading against the R and Python code; the scalar lines above run on the site." .

hemo:7007293 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's acute medical unit at 95 beds (HE-CF-CAPP-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With ArrivalsPerDayCF 20 and MeanStayCF 4, the 96th cell of ErlangBColumn, the loss probability with 95 beds, holds 0.01137 within 0.00005. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the Poisson overflow at a mean of 80, 0.0446, in place of the recursion, a column that starts from 0 in B2, which returns 0 in every row, and a doubled (0.0227) or halved (0.0057) cell. Excel check `=ABS(INDEX(ErlangBColumn,96)-0.01137)<0.00005`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(INDEX(ErlangBColumn,96)-0.01137)<0.00005" .

hemo:7007294 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's cost-minimising beds (HE-CF-CAPP-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the same inputs, BedCost 150,000 and RefusalCost 10,000, CostMinBeds is 95, as in the article. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a refusal cost entered as 5,000, which gives 89, and a doubled (190) or halved (47.5) cell. Excel check `=CostMinBeds=95`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=CostMinBeds=95" .

hemo:7007295 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "De Bruin and colleagues' merged cardiac units (HE-CF-CAPP-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With ArrivalsPerDayCF 8.37 (3.52, 3.06 and 1.79 a day added), MeanStayCF 1.96, the weighted mean stay in their Table 7, and BlockTarget 0.05, BedsForTarget is 22, the beds they report for 5% refused admissions after merging, against 10, 12 and 7 (29 in all) for the three units apart. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a target typed as 5 in place of 0.05, which returns 0 beds, and a doubled (44) or halved (11) cell. Excel check `=BedsForTarget=22`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=BedsForTarget=22" .

hemo:7007296 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article technologies at swing points of 100, 60 and 40 (HE-CF-MCDH-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's illustrative inputs the health gain scores are 75, 50 and 0, the weights 0.5, 0.3 and 0.2 and the overall values 72.5, 65 and 50, so A ranks first. The scan finds that every pair ties at a health gain weight of 2/7, about 0.2857, with an overall value of about 71.43, and that C ranks first at every grid weight up to 0.28 and A from 0.29 (derived). `x_A = 0.6; x_B = 0.4; x_C = 0; x_worst = 0; x_best = 0.8; s_A = 50; s_B = 100; s_C = 100; e_A = 100; e_B = 50; e_C = 100; p_1 = 100; p_2 = 60; p_3 = 40; v_A = 75; v_B = 50; v_C = 0; w_1 = 0.5; w_2 = 0.3; w_3 = 0.2; V_A = 72.5; V_B = 65; V_C = 50; V_top = 72.5`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "x_A = 0.6; x_B = 0.4; x_C = 0; x_worst = 0; x_best = 0.8; s_A = 50; s_B = 100; s_C = 100; e_A = 100; e_B = 50; e_C = 100; p_1 = 100; p_2 = 60; p_3 = 40; v_A = 75; v_B = 50; v_C = 0; w_1 = 0.5; w_2 = 0.3; w_3 = 0.2; V_A = 72.5; V_B = 65; V_C = 50; V_top = 72.5" .

hemo:7007297 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Health gain weight of 0.25 (HE-CF-MCDH-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Points of 25, 45 and 30 keep severity and evidence in the ratio 60 to 40 and give C 75, B 72.5 and A 71.25, so C, with no health gain, ranks first. `x_A = 0.6; x_B = 0.4; x_C = 0; x_worst = 0; x_best = 0.8; s_A = 50; s_B = 100; s_C = 100; e_A = 100; e_B = 50; e_C = 100; p_1 = 25; p_2 = 45; p_3 = 30; v_A = 75; v_B = 50; v_C = 0; w_1 = 0.25; w_2 = 0.45; w_3 = 0.3; V_A = 71.25; V_B = 72.5; V_C = 75; V_top = 75`" ;
    hemo:9000014 "x_A = 0.6; x_B = 0.4; x_C = 0; x_worst = 0; x_best = 0.8; s_A = 50; s_B = 100; s_C = 100; e_A = 100; e_B = 50; e_C = 100; p_1 = 25; p_2 = 45; p_3 = 30; v_A = 75; v_B = 50; v_C = 0; w_1 = 0.25; w_2 = 0.45; w_3 = 0.3; V_A = 71.25; V_B = 72.5; V_C = 75; V_top = 75" .

hemo:7007298 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article technologies (HE-CF-MCDH-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's inputs, CFValueTechA equals 72.5. Expected result TRUE. A health gain score computed on the local range 0 to 0.6 makes the cell 85 and gives FALSE, raw points used without division make it 14,500 and give FALSE, and doubling or halving the cell also gives FALSE. Excel check `=ABS(CFValueTechA-72.5)<1E-6`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(CFValueTechA-72.5)<1E-6" .

hemo:7007299 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Tie weight of A and C (HE-CF-MCDH-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the article's inputs, SwitchWeightAC equals 2/7. Expected result TRUE. A blend that averages severity and evidence equally, ignoring the 60 to 40 ratio, makes A's blend 75 against C's 100 and the cell 0.25, which gives FALSE, and doubling or halving the cell also gives FALSE. Excel check `=ABS(SwitchWeightAC-2/7)<1E-6`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(SwitchWeightAC-2/7)<1E-6" .

hemo:7007300 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal priors, no outside option (HE-CF-MECH-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With `a = 10; b = 0.05; theta_L = 4; theta_H = 5; nu = 0.5; U_0 = 0` the outputs are `q_L = 120; q_FB = 100; theta_v = 6; q_SB = 80`, the values `S_L = 840; S_FB = 750; S_SB = 640`, and the payoffs `V_FB = 305; V_SB = 260; V_shut = 180; V_star = 260; shut = 0; loss = 45`. Of the loss of 45, 40 is the expected rent 0.5 x 1 x 80 and 5 is the expected efficiency loss 0.5 x (250 minus 240) on the high-cost type (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "V_FB = 305; V_SB = 260; V_shut = 180; V_star = 260; shut = 0; loss = 45" .

hemo:7007301 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Outside option of 200 (HE-CF-MECH-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With `U_0 = 200` and the other inputs as above, `V_FB = 105; V_SB = 60; V_shut = 80; V_star = 80; shut = 1; loss = 25`. The payoffs tie at U_0 = 160, where 240 less the rent term 80 equals U_0 in (2.32) (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "V_FB = 105; V_SB = 60; V_shut = 80; V_star = 80; shut = 1; loss = 25" .

hemo:7007302 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Equal priors, no outside option (HE-CF-MECH-001) (HE-CF-MECH-001 test ce7a1335454a)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "PayoffSecondBest is compared with PayoffFirstBest less the expected rent ProbLowCF x (CostHighCF minus CostLowCF) x OutputHighCF and less the expected efficiency loss on the high-cost type, (1 minus ProbLowCF) times the drop in SurplusFirstBestHigh minus CostHighCF x OutputFirstBestHigh to SurplusSecondBestHigh minus CostHighCF x OutputHighCF, at a tolerance of 0.005. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a payoff that leaves out the rent (300), and doubling the PayoffSecondBest cell (520) or halving it (130) also gives FALSE. Excel check `=ABS(PayoffSecondBest-(PayoffFirstBest-ProbLowCF*(CostHighCF-CostLowCF)*OutputHighCF-(1-ProbLowCF)*((SurplusFirstBestHigh-CostHighCF*OutputFirstBestHigh)-(SurplusSecondBestHigh-CostHighCF*OutputHighCF))))<0.005`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(PayoffSecondBest-(PayoffFirstBest-ProbLowCF*(CostHighCF-CostLowCF)*OutputHighCF-(1-ProbLowCF)*((SurplusFirstBestHigh-CostHighCF*OutputFirstBestHigh)-(SurplusSecondBestHigh-CostHighCF*OutputHighCF))))<0.005" .

hemo:7007303 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Outside option of 200 (HE-CF-MECH-001) (HE-CF-MECH-001 test 612b612c16cd)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "ShutDownChosen is TRUE and BestPayoff equals ProbLowCF times the low-cost type's surplus net of cost and outside option, 0.5 x (840 minus 480 minus 200) = 80, at a tolerance of 0.005. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the outside option left out of the payoffs, which keeps the high-cost type in (BestPayoff 260, ShutDownChosen FALSE), and doubling the BestPayoff cell (160) or halving it (40) also gives FALSE. Excel check `=AND(ShutDownChosen,ABS(BestPayoff-ProbLowCF*(SurplusLow-CostLowCF*OutputLowCF-OutsideOption))<0.005)`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ShutDownChosen,ABS(BestPayoff-ProbLowCF*(SurplusLow-CostLowCF*OutputLowCF-OutsideOption))<0.005)" .

hemo:7007304 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Angioplasty game (HE-CF-NASH-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the payoffs of HE-EX-NASH-001 and `W_11 = 7; W_12 = 14; W_21 = 14; W_22 = 0` (step 4 of the article) the chain gives `NE_12 = 1; NE_21 = 1; q_star = 0.6667; p_star = 0.6667; interior = 1; P_11 = 0.4444; P_12 = 0.2222; P_21 = 0.2222; P_22 = 0.1111; U_A = 0; U_B = 0; E_W = 9.3333`. Both hospitals invest four times in nine and neither one time in nine, and E_W is 84/9 against 14 at either pure equilibrium, as in the article. The code counts 3 equilibria." ;
    hemo:9000014 "NE_12 = 1; NE_21 = 1; q_star = 0.6667; p_star = 0.6667; interior = 1; P_11 = 0.4444; P_12 = 0.2222; P_21 = 0.2222; P_22 = 0.1111; U_A = 0; U_B = 0; E_W = 9.3333" .

hemo:7007305 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of the sexes (HE-CF-NASH-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the payoffs of HE-EX-NASH-006 and W equal to the sum of the two payoffs in each cell, `W_11 = 3; W_12 = 0; W_21 = 0; W_22 = 3`, the chain gives `NE_11 = 1; NE_22 = 1; q_star = 0.3333; p_star = 0.6667; interior = 1; P_11 = 0.2222; P_12 = 0.4444; P_21 = 0.1111; P_22 = 0.2222; U_A = 0.6667; U_B = 0.6667; E_W = 1.3333`. The cell probabilities and each player's expected payoff of two thirds are those Slantchev gives, and the players meet with probability 4/9." ;
    hemo:9000014 "NE_11 = 1; NE_22 = 1; q_star = 0.3333; p_star = 0.6667; interior = 1; P_11 = 0.2222; P_12 = 0.4444; P_21 = 0.1111; P_22 = 0.2222; U_A = 0.6667; U_B = 0.6667; E_W = 1.3333" .

hemo:7007306 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Angioplasty game (HE-CF-NASH-001) (HE-CF-NASH-001 test c4fca8f1c6f1)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "ExpectedWelfare is compared with 84/9, the article's E[W], at a tolerance of 0.0005. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the cells with exactly one investor counted once in place of twice, which gives 56/9 (6.2222), and doubling the ExpectedWelfare cell (18.6667) or halving it (4.6667) also gives FALSE. Excel check `=ABS(ExpectedWelfare-84/9)<0.0005`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(ExpectedWelfare-84/9)<0.0005" .

hemo:7007307 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of the sexes (HE-CF-NASH-001) (HE-CF-NASH-001 test fc057a0e60e5)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "CountEquilibria is 3, the four outcome probabilities add to 1 and OutcomeUpRight equals 4/9, Slantchev's probability that the row player plays its first strategy and the column player its second. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a count that leaves out the mixed equilibrium (2), and doubling OutcomeUpRight (0.8889) or halving it (0.2222) also gives FALSE. Excel check `=AND(CountEquilibria=3,ABS(OutcomeUpLeft+OutcomeUpRight+OutcomeDownLeft+OutcomeDownRight-1)<1E-9,ABS(OutcomeUpRight-4/9)<0.0005)`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(CountEquilibria=3,ABS(OutcomeUpLeft+OutcomeUpRight+OutcomeDownLeft+OutcomeDownRight-1)<1E-9,ABS(OutcomeUpRight-4/9)<0.0005)" .

hemo:7007308 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "The article's draw (HE-CF-AUCT-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With `c_1 = 2.60; c_2 = 3.20; c_3 = 4.40; c_high = 5; N = 3` the bids are `b_1 = 3.40; b_2 = 3.80; b_3 = 4.60`, so `p_FP = 3.40`, `p_SP = 3.20` and `d = 0.20`; the same supplier wins under both rules." ;
    hemo:9000014 "d = 0.20" .

hemo:7007309 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Seeded simulation, for reading against the R and Python code (HE-CF-AUCT-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000007 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With `c_low = 2; c_high = 5; N = 3; S = 100000` and seed 2026, Python's `random` module returns a mean lowest cost of 2.7485, a mean second-price price of 3.4959 and a mean first-price price of 3.4990, and R returns 2.7488, 3.5005 and 3.4992, against the closed forms 2.75 and 3.50. The standard error of the second-price mean is about 0.0021 at this S (derived from the variance of the second-lowest of three uniforms), so the two second-price means lie 1.95 and 0.25 standard errors from 3.50. The scalar worked example above is the one that runs in the calculator." ;
    hemo:9000014 "random" .

hemo:7007310 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "The article's draw (HE-CF-AUCT-001) (HE-CF-AUCT-001 test 50ff8e33d0b5)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With the costs 2.60, 3.20 and 4.40, a top of 5 and three bidders typed in, PriceFirstPrice is compared with 3.40 and PriceSecondPrice with 3.20, the article's hand values, at a tolerance of 0.005. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows a second-price rule that pays the lowest cost (2.60), and doubling either price cell (6.80 or 6.40) or halving it (1.70 or 1.60) also gives FALSE. Excel check `=AND(ABS(PriceFirstPrice-3.4)<0.005,ABS(PriceSecondPrice-3.2)<0.005)`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=AND(ABS(PriceFirstPrice-3.4)<0.005,ABS(PriceSecondPrice-3.2)<0.005)" .

hemo:7007311 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Simulation against the closed form (HE-CF-AUCT-001)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000036 hemo:4000009 ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a fixed table of draws, MeanSecondPriceSim is compared with the closed-form price CostBottomCF + 2 (CostTopCF minus CostBottomCF) / (BiddersCF + 1) and must lie within 4 standard errors. Expected result TRUE. FALSE shows the helper column taking the lowest cost in place of the second-lowest, which with S = 100000 sits about 410 of its own standard errors below 3.50, and doubling or halving the MeanSecondPriceSim cell also gives FALSE. Excel check `=ABS(MeanSecondPriceSim-(CostBottomCF+2*(CostTopCF-CostBottomCF)/(BiddersCF+1)))<4*SESecondPriceSim`" ;
    hemo:9000024 "=ABS(MeanSecondPriceSim-(CostBottomCF+2*(CostTopCF-CostBottomCF)/(BiddersCF+1)))<4*SESecondPriceSim" .

hemo:7007312 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000015 ;
    rdfs:label "Article's base case at 30 minutes (HE-CF-ACCS-001)"@en ;
