        hemo:6002948,
        hemo:6002950 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002945,
        hemo:6002949 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004090,
        hemo:7004091 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004093,
        hemo:7004094,
        hemo:7004095,
        hemo:7004096 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives the probability that a stay already longer than a cycles lasts more than a + b cycles, and the probability of leaving in cycle d of the stay among those still in the state in that cycle. Neither depends on the time already spent, since R_ab equals p_ii to the power b whatever a is and h_d equals 1 minus p_ii at every d, which is the memoryless property that Grinstead and Snell derive for the geometric distribution. The first part is the conditional survival ratio of HE-FM-LTR-001 applied to the geometric stay, and the second divides f_d by G_d of HE-FM-MKA-001. Applied to a survival curve for time since entry, the same ratios test the assumption, since only a memoryless curve has the same per-cycle exit probability at every duration; HE-CF-MKA-001 carries out that check, and HE-FM-CUMH-002 gives the per-cycle probabilities of a fitted curve." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Memoryless residual stay and constant per-cycle exit probability of a geometric Markov sojourn" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Alarid-Escudero F, Krijkamp E, Enns EA, Yang A, Hunink MGM, Pechlivanoglou P, Jalal H. A tutorial on time-dependent cohort state-transition models in R using a cost-effectiveness analysis example. Medical Decision Making. 2023;43(1) 21-41. Introduction, which separates dependence on time since the start of the simulation from dependence on time spent in a state, and states that because the cohort ages together a cycle-specific matrix handles the first, while parameters that depend on time since disease onset cannot be implemented on simulation time and need tunnel states. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X221121747",
        "Grinstead CM, Snell JL. Grinstead and Snell's Introduction to Probability. The CHANCE Project version of 4 July 2006, based on the 2nd edition published by the American Mathematical Society. Chapter 5, section 5.1, Example 5.1, page 186, which computes P(T > r + s | T > r) = q^(r+s)/q^r = q^s for a geometric waiting time, independent of the time r already served, and states that this property is called the memoryless property and is also obeyed by the exponential distribution. https://math.dartmouth.edu/~prob/prob/prob.pdf",
        "Siebert U, Alagoz O, Bayoumi AM, Jahn B, Owens DK, Cohen DJ, Kuntz KM. State-transition modeling: a report of the ISPOR-SMDM Modeling Good Research Practices Task Force-3. Value in Health. 2012;15(6) 812-820. Sections on defining states and heterogeneity with recommendation III-6, which require each state to be homogeneous with respect to both observed and unobserved characteristics that affect transition probabilities, and state that where prior states or time in the current state matter the relevant states should carry that history in their definition. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2012.06.014",
        "Williams C, Lewsey JD, Mackay DF, Briggs AH. Estimation of survival probabilities for use in cost-effectiveness analyses: a comparison of a multi-state modeling survival analysis approach with partitioned survival and Markov decision-analytic modeling. Medical Decision Making. 2017;37(4) 427-439. Section Multi-state Modeling, which fits a Cox state-arrival extended model for progression to death with a covariate for time in the previous state purely to help decide whether to accept the Markov assumption, finds evidence that the Markov property did not hold and moves to a semi-Markov approach; the Discussion suggests a semi-Markov model when power to detect a violation is insufficient and doubts remain. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X16670617" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Model-time probabilities used for time since entry to a Markov state. Applying the article's post-event death probabilities by model cycle, 0.25 in the first cycle and 0.05 after, gives someone who has the event in model year three a first post-event probability of 0.05 instead of 0.25. A model-time curve treats everyone as having entered at the start, which is right only if all enter together.",
        "Survival matched at one duration taken as proof of memorylessness. The article's single post-event state with a death probability of 0.09387 reproduces five-year survival of 0.61088 exactly, yet gives one-year post-event survival of about 0.906 against 0.75 and 10.653 post-event years per event against 16, a third less. In the article this overstates the life-years gained by about 0.64, or 15.7%, so agreement at one horizon does not show that time since entry can be ignored." ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_ab = p_ii^(a + b) / p_ii^a; h_d = p_ii^(d - 1) * (1 - p_ii) / p_ii^(d - 1)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-MKA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "memoryless-residual-stay-and-constant-per-cycle-exit-probability-of-a-geometric-markov-sojourn" .

hemo:5000674 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Group health after a programme net of the group's share of the health opportunity cost"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-DCEA-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000148 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004115 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002962,
        hemo:6002963,
        hemo:6002964,
        hemo:6002965,
        hemo:6002966,
        hemo:6002967,
        hemo:6002968,
        hemo:6002969,
        hemo:6002970,
        hemo:6002971,
        hemo:6002972,
        hemo:6002973,
        hemo:6002974,
        hemo:6002975 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002964,
        hemo:6002965,
        hemo:6002966,
        hemo:6002967,
        hemo:6002968,
        hemo:6002969,
        hemo:6002970,
        hemo:6002971,
        hemo:6002972 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002973,
        hemo:6002974,
        hemo:6002975 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004112,
        hemo:7004113,
        hemo:7004114 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004116,
        hemo:7004117,
        hemo:7004118,
        hemo:7004119,
        hemo:7004120 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Subtracts each group's share of the health opportunity cost from the QALYs the group gains, giving the group's net health benefit, and divides that by the number of people in the group to move the group's baseline health per person to its health under the programme. The health opportunity cost H_d is the incremental cost divided by the value of a QALY, the live record HE-FM-CET-002, and the shares o_1 and o_2 say who bears it. Because the shares add to 1, the total of the group net health benefits equals the total QALY gain less H_d, so the distribution of the opportunity cost changes who gains while total health stays the same. The line shows two groups, the more deprived group 1 and the less deprived group 2, and each further group adds a pair of terms of the same form." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Group health after a programme net of the group's share of the health opportunity cost" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Asaria M, Griffin S, Cookson R. Distributional cost-effectiveness analysis: a tutorial. Medical Decision Making. 2016;36(1):8-19. Section Estimating the distribution of health changes due to the interventions, which converts total costs into health opportunity costs at a threshold value, apportions them equally to each individual so that a subgroup with 10% of the population is allocated 360 of 3,600 QALYs, subtracts them from the subgroup's health gains to give its net health effect, and calls equal sharing convenient but not evidence based. Section Estimating the baseline health distribution, which requires the full general population. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X15583266",
        "Love-Koh J, Cookson R, Claxton K, Griffin S. Estimating social variation in the health effects of changes in health care expenditure. Medical Decision Making. 2020;40(2):170-182. Abstract, which estimates that 26% of the total health effect of a change in English NHS expenditure would accrue to the fifth of the population living in the most deprived areas, against 14% to the fifth living in the least deprived areas. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X20904360",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. Support document, health inequalities. Tools and resources of NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance, the manual (PMG36). London, NICE, 2022, last updated 13 October 2026; page last updated 13 May 2025. Section Distributional cost-effectiveness analysis methods, subsection Health opportunity cost and Table 2, which ask for health opportunity costs equally distributed between social groups in the base case, with slight (22%, 21%, 20%, 19% and 18%) and moderate (24%, 22%, 20%, 18% and 16%) gradients across five IMD groups starting with the most deprived, and for results at several values within the ranges in sections 6.3.4 to 6.3.8 of the manual; subsection Outputs, which asks for net health benefit for each IMD quintile. PMG36 sections 6.3.4 and 6.3.7 give the GBP 25,000 and GBP 35,000 points of that range. https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/resources/support-document-health-inequalities-15313210669/chapter/distributional-cost-effectiveness-analysis-methods" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Reading the equity impact of a DCEA from gross QALY gains. Before the opportunity cost is counted, the more deprived group in the article's example gains 400 QALYs, 0.008 years per person, yet after its equal share of the 1,200 displaced QALYs it loses 0.004 years per person. A programme whose benefits reach a disadvantaged group can still leave that group worse off once the displaced care is counted.",
        "Splitting the opportunity cost equally per group when groups differ in size. With illustrative groups of 20,000 and 80,000 people, sharing 1,200 displaced QALYs equally per head puts 240 on the smaller group, whereas a half and half split puts 600 on it. With a gain of 400 QALYs the smaller group's net health benefit is 160 QALYs under per-head sharing and minus 200 under the half split, so the error turns a gain into a loss." ;
    hemo:9000014 "NHB_1 = dQ_1 - o_1 * H_d; NHB_2 = dQ_2 - o_2 * H_d; h1_1 = h0_1 + NHB_1 / N_1; h1_2 = h0_2 + NHB_2 / N_2; NHB_tot = NHB_1 + NHB_2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-DCEA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "group-health-after-a-programme-net-of-the-group-s-share-of-the-health-opportunity-cost" .

hemo:5000675 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Change in mean health, EDE health and Atkinson index of a programme against its comparator"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-DCEA-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000148 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004124 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002976,
        hemo:6002977,
        hemo:6002978,
        hemo:6002979,
        hemo:6002980,
        hemo:6002981,
        hemo:6002982,
        hemo:6002983,
        hemo:6002984,
        hemo:6002985 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002977,
        hemo:6002978,
        hemo:6002980,
        hemo:6002981,
        hemo:6002984 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002982,
        hemo:6002983,
        hemo:6002985 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004121,
        hemo:7004122,
        hemo:7004123 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004125,
        hemo:7004126,
        hemo:7004127,
        hemo:7004128,
        hemo:7004129,
        hemo:7004130 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the mean health and the equally distributed equivalent health of the programme's distribution and of the comparator's, both at the same inequality aversion epsilon from the live records HE-FM-ATK-002 or HE-FM-ATK-003, or HE-CF-ATK-001 for groups of unequal size, and returns the change in mean health, the change in EDE health per person and across the population, the change in the inequality cost and the change in the Atkinson index. The inequality cost is mean health less EDE health, the health per person that a decision maker with that aversion would give up for full equality, so the change in EDE equals the change in mean health less the change in the inequality cost. A positive change in EDE health means the programme is preferred to the comparator at that epsilon. Each Atkinson index is HE-FM-ATK-001, and only the difference between the two is new here." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Change in mean health, EDE health and Atkinson index of a programme against its comparator" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Asaria M, Griffin S, Cookson R. Distributional cost-effectiveness analysis: a tutorial. Medical Decision Making. 2016;36(1):8-19. Section Analyzing tradeoffs between total health and health inequality using social welfare indices, which gives the Atkinson EDE with inequality aversion epsilon, states that with zero inequality aversion the EDE is mean health, reads mean less EDE health as the health per person one would sacrifice for full equality, and plots the difference in EDE health between two strategies across levels of inequality aversion in Figure 4. Section Comparing interventions in terms of total health and unfair health inequality, which compares total health and one or more inequality indices between an intervention and a comparator, and section Adjusting for social value judgments about fair and unfair sources of inequality, on direct standardisation. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X15583266",
        "Robson M, Asaria M, Cookson R, Tsuchiya A, Ali S. Eliciting the level of health inequality aversion in England. Health Economics. 2017;26(10):1328-1334. Table 1, which reports the median Atkinson epsilon of 10.95 elicited from the general public in England, the value used in two of the worked examples. https://doi.org/10.1002/hec.3430" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Applying the EDE formula to net health changes instead of health levels. The harmonic mean of the article's per-person changes of minus 0.004 and 0.016 years is about minus 0.0107 years, a figure with no meaning, because the EDE needs positive levels of health. The change in EDE health is the EDE of the programme's distribution less the EDE of the comparator's.",
        "Setting the per-person change in EDE health against a population total. At epsilon of 2 the change of 0.004843 years per person is 484.3 EDE QALYs across 100,000 people. Setting the per-person figure beside the 600 QALYs of total net health benefit suggests that inequality absorbs almost all the gain, when the inequality cost takes about 19% of it." ;
    hemo:9000014 "d_mu = mu_1 - mu_0; d_EDE = EDE_1 - EDE_0; d_P = d_mu - d_EDE; dEDE_pop = N_pop * d_EDE; d_A = (1 - EDE_1 / mu_1) - (1 - EDE_0 / mu_0)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-DCEA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "change-in-mean-health-ede-health-and-atkinson-index-of-a-programme-against-its-comparator" .

hemo:5000680 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Isoelastic Atkinson health social welfare of three equal-sized groups for epsilon other than 1"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-SWF-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000150 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004205 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003008,
        hemo:6003009,
        hemo:6003010,
        hemo:6003011,
        hemo:6003012 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003009,
        hemo:6003010,
        hemo:6003011,
        hemo:6003012 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003008 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004202,
        hemo:7004203,
        hemo:7004204 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004206,
        hemo:7004207,
        hemo:7004208,
        hemo:7004209,
        hemo:7004210 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Adds h_i^(1-epsilon)/(1-epsilon) over the groups, the welfare function Atkinson derived from constant relative inequality aversion with its two constants set to 0 and 1, which leaves every ranking unchanged. The line is written for the three groups of the article's example, and with n people or groups the sum has n terms. At epsilon of 0 W is total health, the utilitarian rule of maximising total QALYs; as epsilon rises the weight on a small gain to group i, h_i^(-epsilon), shifts priority to the less healthy (HE-FM-ATK-004), and the ranking approaches the maximin rule of HE-FM-SWF-003. Solving W = n h_EDE^(1-epsilon)/(1-epsilon) for h_EDE gives the EDE health of HE-FM-ATK-002, so W and EDE health rank allocations identically, and W has meaning only in comparisons made at the same epsilon." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Isoelastic Atkinson health social welfare of three equal-sized groups for epsilon other than 1" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Asaria M, Griffin S, Cookson R. Measuring health inequality in the context of cost-effectiveness analysis. In Health and Inequality. Research on Economic Inequality, volume 21. Emerald Group Publishing, 2013, pages 491-507. Author manuscript, section on the Atkinson social welfare index, which states that an epsilon of 0 represents the utilitarian view that only total health matters and that as epsilon approaches infinity the index represents the maximin view, and the passage after the Kolm index, which states that these social value judgements should be provided by the decision maker and that the sensitivity of conclusions to them should be explored. https://doi.org/10.1108/S1049-2585(2013)0000021024",
        "Atkinson AB. On the measurement of inequality. Journal of Economic Theory. 1970;2(3):244-263. Page 251, equation (5), which derives U(y) = A + B y^(1-epsilon)/(1-epsilon) for epsilon not equal to 1 and log y at epsilon of 1 from constant relative inequality aversion, with epsilon of zero or above for concavity, and page 257, which takes the welfare function as symmetric and additively separable, with epsilon of 0 ranking distributions solely by the total and the limit as epsilon tends to infinity giving the minimum. https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-0531(70)90039-6",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 13 October 2026. Chapter 4 Economic evaluation, section 4.12.3, which states that DCEA should only be used as supporting evidence of the potential for a technology to impact health inequalities, and section 4.12.7, which states that the results of the DCEA should not weigh the costs or benefits of a technology differently based on the social characteristics of the people affected by the recommendation. https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. Support document: health inequalities. Technology evaluation methods support document for PMG36, chapter Distributional cost-effectiveness analysis methods, page last updated 13 May 2025. Section Health inequality aversion weights, which states that the results of the DCEA should not weight the costs or benefits of a technology differently based on the social characteristics of the people affected, and that this includes using mathematical functions that use health inequality aversion parameters, and table 1, which states that health inequality aversion weights should not be applied to QALYs. https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/resources/support-document-health-inequalities-15313210669/chapter/distributional-cost-effectiveness-analysis-methods" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Comparing isoelastic welfare values across epsilon values. W for option P is about 49.5993 at epsilon of 0.5 and minus 0.0442201 at epsilon of 2. The fall reflects the change of scale alone, so W is compared only between allocations at one epsilon, and a comparison across epsilon values uses EDE health from HE-FM-ATK-002, which is in years of QALE.",
        "Dropping the divisor 1 minus epsilon from isoelastic welfare. For epsilon above 1 the divisor is negative, so leaving it out flips the sign of every term and reverses the ranking. In the article's example at epsilon of 2 the values become 0.0442201 for P, 0.0441926 for T and 0.0443603 for R, so R would appear best and T worst, the opposite of the correct order." ;
    hemo:9000014 "W = h_1^(1 - epsilon) / (1 - epsilon) + h_2^(1 - epsilon) / (1 - epsilon) + h_3^(1 - epsilon) / (1 - epsilon)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-SWF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "isoelastic-atkinson-health-social-welfare-of-three-equal-sized-groups-for-epsilon-other-than-1" .

hemo:5000681 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Logarithmic health social welfare of three equal-sized groups at epsilon of 1"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-SWF-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000150 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004212 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003013,
        hemo:6003014,
        hemo:6003015,
        hemo:6003016 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003014,
        hemo:6003015,
        hemo:6003016 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003013 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004211 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004213,
        hemo:7004214,
        hemo:7004215,
        hemo:7004216,
        hemo:7004217 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "At epsilon of 1 the isoelastic form of HE-FM-SWF-001 divides by zero, and Atkinson's welfare function becomes the natural logarithm, so W adds the logarithms of the groups' health. Once the constant n/(1-epsilon), which does not affect rankings, is taken away, HE-FM-SWF-001 tends to this form as epsilon tends to 1, so rankings change smoothly with epsilon through 1. The weight on a small gain to group i is 1/h_i, so a gain of 1% counts the same for every group. Dividing W by n and taking the exponential gives the geometric-mean EDE health of HE-FM-ATK-003. The function log is the natural logarithm." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Logarithmic health social welfare of three equal-sized groups at epsilon of 1" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Atkinson AB. On the measurement of inequality. Journal of Economic Theory. 1970;2(3):244-263. Page 251, equation (5), which gives U(y) = log_e(y) at epsilon of 1 under constant relative inequality aversion, the case in which the general form y^(1-epsilon)/(1-epsilon) is undefined. https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-0531(70)90039-6" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Excel LOG in place of LN for logarithmic welfare. LOG with one argument is base 10, so W for option P becomes about 5.5008 instead of 12.6661. The ranking is unchanged, but the EDE health exp(W/3) becomes about 6.26 years instead of 68.17.",
        "General isoelastic formula applied at epsilon close to 1. At epsilon of exactly 1 the formula of HE-FM-SWF-001 divides by zero. Entering 0.999 instead gives about 3,012.6929 for option P, which ranks correctly but differs from the logarithmic 12.6661 by the constant 3/(1-epsilon), here 3,000, plus a small remainder, so the two values cannot be set side by side." ;
    hemo:9000014 "W = log(h_1) + log(h_2) + log(h_3)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-SWF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "logarithmic-health-social-welfare-of-three-equal-sized-groups-at-epsilon-of-1" .

hemo:5000682 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Maximin health social welfare of three groups as the Rawlsian limit"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-SWF-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000150 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004219 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003017,
        hemo:6003018,
        hemo:6003019,
        hemo:6003020 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003018,
        hemo:6003019,
        hemo:6003020 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003017 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004218 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004220,
        hemo:7004221,
        hemo:7004222,
        hemo:7004223 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Sets social welfare equal to the health of the least healthy group, the Rawlsian or maximin form. Atkinson gives it as the limit of the isoelastic family as epsilon tends to infinity (HE-FM-SWF-001), and Dolan and Tsuchiya as the limit of the two-group CES form as r approaches infinity (HE-FM-SWF-004). Two allocations with the same minimum have the same W and are ranked as tied however the other groups fare, so a gain to a healthier group alone leaves W unchanged. The nested min written here returns the smallest of the three values." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Maximin health social welfare of three groups as the Rawlsian limit" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Atkinson AB. On the measurement of inequality. Journal of Economic Theory. 1970;2(3):244-263. Page 257, which states that the limiting case as epsilon tends to infinity gives the function min_i(y_i), which takes account only of transfers to the very lowest income group and is therefore not strictly concave. https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-0531(70)90039-6",
        "Brouwer WB, Culyer AJ, van Exel NJ, Rutten FF. Welfarism vs. extra-welfarism. Journal of Health Economics. 2008;27(2):325-338. Section 3 on extra-welfarism, which notes that Rawls explicitly excluded both health and health care from the list of primary goods. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhealeco.2007.07.003" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Expecting maximin welfare to credit gains to healthier groups. Raising the third group in option P from 76.7 to 80 years leaves W at 60.3, so a programme that helps only healthier groups scores no gain under maximin even though nobody is worse off.",
        "Reading maximin health welfare as Rawls's own theory. The maximin health form borrows Rawls's priority for the worst off, but Brouwer and colleagues note that Rawls explicitly excluded health and health care from his list of primary goods, so the health version is an analogy drawn from his work." ;
    hemo:9000014 "W = min(h_1, min(h_2, h_3))" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-SWF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "maximin-health-social-welfare-of-three-groups-as-the-rawlsian-limit" .

hemo:5000685 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "GBD age-weight function with its age and value of peak weight"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-AGW-001" ;
