        hemo:7004249 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives the weight on a year of life lived at age x as C times x times e to the minus beta x, the exponential form Murray used for the original DALY. The weight is zero at birth, rises through childhood and declines slowly after its peak, which falls where the slope C e to the minus beta x times (1 minus beta x) is zero, at the age 1/beta. The weight at the peak is C/(beta e). With the standard constants of 0.1658 and 0.04 the peak is at age 25 with a weight of about 1.525, as in the article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "GBD age-weight function with its age and value of peak weight" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Larson BA. Calculating disability-adjusted-life-years lost (DALYs) in discrete-time. Cost Effectiveness and Resource Allocation. 2013;11 article 18. Appendix, equation 5, which gives the age-weighting function as 0.1658 x e to the minus 0.04 x and states that, with beta = 0.04, C = 0.1658 was chosen so that global DALYs were the same with and without age weighting in the 1990 global burden of disease study. https://doi.org/10.1186/1478-7547-11-18",
        "Murray CJ. Quantifying the burden of disease, the technical basis for disability-adjusted life years. Bulletin of the World Health Organization. 1994;72(3) 429-445. Page 436, which gives continuous age-weighting functions of the form C x e to the minus beta x, states that only a narrow range of beta, about 0.03 to 0.05, gives reasonable age patterns, that beta = 0.04 was chosen by informal polling of the advisory board, that C is chosen so that unequal age weights do not change the global burden, that C equals 0.16243, and that altering beta would change the constant. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2486718/",
        "Prüss-Üstün A, Mathers C, Corvalán C, Woodward A. Introduction and methods, assessing the environmental burden of disease at national and local levels. Environmental Burden of Disease Series No. 1. Geneva, World Health Organization, 2003. Section 3.4, which gives the function used to model the relative age weights, C x e to the minus beta x, and the GBD choice to value a year in young adulthood more than one in old age or infancy, and section 3.5, whose DALY parameters state that standard age weights use beta = 0.04 and C = 0.1658. https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/42750" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Peak of the age weight read as the age of greatest loss per death. The weight peaks at 25, but a death at 25 loses fewer age-weighted years than a death in childhood, because the child's lost years pass through the whole high-weight span. Up to an illustrative age of 80 and without discounting, a death at 25 loses about 58.50 age-weighted years against 84.07 at age 5 and 85.88 at birth (HE-FM-AGW-002, computed here for illustration), the article's first misreading.",
        "Shape parameter changed without recalibrating the scaling constant. Murray notes that if beta is altered the constant C would necessarily change as well. Keeping C = 0.1658 with beta = 0.05 lowers every weight, and the undiscounted age-weighted loss from a death at 5 up to age 80 falls from 84.07 to about 58.49 (computed here for illustration), a change produced by the constant, not by the new shape." ;
    hemo:9000014 "W = C * x * exp(-beta * x); x_peak = 1 / beta; W_peak = C / beta * exp(-1)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-AGW-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "gbd-age-weight-function-with-its-age-and-value-of-peak-weight" .

hemo:5000686 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Undiscounted age-weighted years of life lost per death with a modulation constant"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-AGW-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000151 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004252 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003038,
        hemo:6003039,
        hemo:6003040,
        hemo:6003041,
        hemo:6003042,
        hemo:6003043 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003039,
        hemo:6003040,
        hemo:6003041,
        hemo:6003042,
        hemo:6003043 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003038 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004250,
        hemo:7004251 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004253,
        hemo:7004254,
        hemo:7004255,
        hemo:7004256,
        hemo:7004257 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Integrates the weight of HE-FM-AGW-001 over the L years lost from the age at death a, which gives C/beta squared times the difference of (1 + beta a) e to the minus beta a and the same term at a + L. The modulation constant K mixes this with the unweighted L years, so K = 1 is the article's undiscounted age-weighted YLL and K = 0 returns the L standard years of HE-FM-YLL-001 for one death. The (1 minus K) L term is not printed in the sources cited, which give the K = 1 integral and the discounted form with K. It is derived here as the limit of the second term of HE-FM-AGW-003 as r falls to zero, and the numerical integral of HE-CF-AGW-002 confirms it. The whole expression is the r = 0 case of HE-FM-AGW-003, which cannot be evaluated directly at r = 0 because it divides by r." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Undiscounted age-weighted years of life lost per death with a modulation constant" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Murray CJ. Quantifying the burden of disease, the technical basis for disability-adjusted life years. Bulletin of the World Health Organization. 1994;72(3) 429-445. Page 441, which writes the DALYs lost by one individual as the integral from a to a + L of D C x e to the minus beta x times e to the minus r (x minus a), with D the disability weight (1 for a death), a the age of onset and L the duration or time lost. Setting r to zero gives the undiscounted integral here. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2486718/",
        "Prüss-Üstün A, Mathers C, Corvalán C, Woodward A. Introduction and methods, assessing the environmental burden of disease at national and local levels. Environmental Burden of Disease Series No. 1. Geneva, World Health Organization, 2003. Section 3.5 and its DALY parameters, which define K as the age-weighting modulation constant, K = 0 for no age weights to 1 for full age weights. The guide gives K only in the discounted formula; the undiscounted (1 minus K) L term of HE-FM-AGW-002 is derived from it, not printed there. https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/42750" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Undiscounted age-weighted total treated as the standard GBD 1990 figure. The standard DALYs of the original GBD combined age weights with 3% discounting, so a death at 5 in the article's illustration counts 36.44 years (HE-FM-AGW-003), not 84.07. The undiscounted age-weighted value is a component for comparison, not the published convention.",
        "Weight at the age of death applied to every year lost. Multiplying L by the weight at the age of death, W(a) times L, ignores that the lost years are lived at later ages. For a death at 5 it gives 0.6787 times 75, about 50.90, instead of 84.07 (computed here for illustration), and turns the gain from age weighting for a child death into a loss." ;
    hemo:9000014 "YLL_aw = K * C / beta^2 * ((1 + beta * a) * exp(-beta * a) - (1 + beta * (a + L)) * exp(-beta * (a + L))) + (1 - K) * L" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-AGW-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "undiscounted-age-weighted-years-of-life-lost-per-death-with-a-modulation-constant" .

hemo:5000687 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Age-weighted continuously discounted years of life lost per death of the original DALY"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-AGW-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000151 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004260 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003044,
        hemo:6003045,
        hemo:6003046,
        hemo:6003047,
        hemo:6003048,
        hemo:6003049,
        hemo:6003050 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003045,
        hemo:6003046,
        hemo:6003047,
        hemo:6003048,
        hemo:6003049,
        hemo:6003050 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003044 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004258,
        hemo:7004259 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004261,
        hemo:7004262,
        hemo:7004263,
        hemo:7004264,
        hemo:7004265,
        hemo:7004266 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives the years of life lost for one death at age a with L standard years remaining, each year weighted by HE-FM-AGW-001 and discounted continuously at rate r from the time of death, in the form the WHO environmental burden of disease guide gives for a single death. The first term is the age-weighted integral, scaled by K, and the second term, with weight 1 minus K, is the continuously discounted years of HE-FM-YLL-003, so K = 0 returns that record and HE-FM-DISC-003 with an amount of 1 and its continuous rate rho set equal to r. With r = 0.03 and K = 1 it gives the YLL per death of the standard GBD 1990 DALY, which GBD 2010 and WHO later dropped. The undiscounted case is HE-FM-AGW-002." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Age-weighted continuously discounted years of life lost per death of the original DALY" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Murray CJ, Vos T, Lozano R, et al. Disability-adjusted life years (DALYs) for 291 diseases and injuries in 21 regions, 1990-2010, a systematic analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study 2010. The Lancet. 2012;380(9859) 2197-2223. Abstract, methods, which states that neither YLLs nor YLDs were age-weighted or discounted. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(12)61689-4",
        "Murray CJ. Quantifying the burden of disease, the technical basis for disability-adjusted life years. Bulletin of the World Health Organization. 1994;72(3) 429-445. Page 441, which gives the solution of the definite integral of D C x e to the minus beta x e to the minus r (x minus a) from a to a + L as the DALY formula for an individual, with r = 0.03, beta = 0.04 and C = 0.16243 in the specific form used. This is the K = 1 case of the guide's formula. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2486718/",
        "Prüss-Üstün A, Mathers C, Corvalán C, Woodward A. Introduction and methods, assessing the environmental burden of disease at national and local levels. Environmental Burden of Disease Series No. 1. Geneva, World Health Organization, 2003. Section 3.5, Calculation of DALYs with discounting and age weighting, pages 32 to 34, which gives YLL for a single death as K C e to the ra over (r + beta) squared times the bracketed difference at a + L and at a, plus (1 minus K)/r times (1 minus e to the minus rL), with a the age of death, L the standard life expectancy at that age, r usually 3%, beta = 0.04 and C = 0.1658. https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/42750",
        "World Health Organization. WHO methods and data sources for global burden of disease estimates 2000-2023. Global Health Estimates Technical Paper WHO/DSA/DDA/GHE/2026.3. Geneva, WHO, September 2026. Section 2, which states that the standard DALYs of the original GBD study and later WHO updates used 3% discounting and non-uniform age weights, and section 2.3, which states that GBD 2010 dropped age weights as hard to justify for a measure of health loss that in the original method the discounting of YLDs started at the year of incidence and that of YLLs at the year of death, and that WHO adopted a 0% discount rate and no age weighting after informal consultations in 2012. https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/gho-documents/global-health-estimates/ghe2023_daly_methods.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Age-weighted discounted values compared with later unweighted estimates. GBD from 2010 onwards and WHO after its 2012 decision use neither age weights nor discounting. Comparing the 36.44 years for a death at 5 in the article's illustration with an undiscounted 75 years from a later study attributes to a change in health a difference that is the change in convention, the article's second misreading.",
        "Factor e to the ra dropped from the age-weighted term. The age-weighted term is valued at the time of death, which the factor e to the ra restores after the integral is taken from birth. Without it a death at 5 counts about 31.36 years instead of 36.44, and a death at 40 about 7.0 instead of 23.22 (computed here for illustration), so the error grows with the age at death." ;
    hemo:9000014 "YLL_awd = K * C * exp(r * a) / (r + beta)^2 * (exp(-(r + beta) * (L + a)) * (-(r + beta) * (L + a) - 1) - exp(-(r + beta) * a) * (-(r + beta) * a - 1)) + (1 - K) / r * (1 - exp(-r * L))" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-AGW-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "age-weighted-continuously-discounted-years-of-life-lost-per-death-of-the-original-daly" .

hemo:5000688 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Age-weighted continuously discounted years lived with disability per incident case"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-AGW-004" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000151 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004269 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003051,
        hemo:6003052,
        hemo:6003053,
        hemo:6003054,
        hemo:6003055,
        hemo:6003056,
        hemo:6003057,
        hemo:6003058 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003052,
        hemo:6003053,
        hemo:6003054,
        hemo:6003055,
        hemo:6003056,
        hemo:6003057,
        hemo:6003058 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003051 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004267,
        hemo:7004268 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004270,
        hemo:7004271,
        hemo:7004272,
        hemo:7004273 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Applies the expression of HE-FM-AGW-003 to a case of a non-fatal condition, with a the age at onset and L the duration, and multiplies it by the disability weight, as the WHO environmental burden of disease guide gives for a single disabling event. With K = 0 it is the continuously discounted YLD per case of HE-FM-YLD-002, and multiplying by the incident cases gives the incidence-based total of HE-FN-YLD-001 under the original DALY conventions. Disability weights are on the Disability Weight page (HE-FN-DWT-001)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Age-weighted continuously discounted years lived with disability per incident case" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Larson BA. Calculating disability-adjusted-life-years lost (DALYs) in discrete-time. Cost Effectiveness and Resource Allocation. 2013;11 article 18. Equation 2, which gives the discounted YLD for 10 years at a weight of 0.60 and 3% as 0.6 times (1/0.03)(1 minus e to the minus 0.3), 5.1836, for a woman with onset at age 35 in the Fox-Rushby and Hanson example. https://doi.org/10.1186/1478-7547-11-18",
        "Prüss-Üstün A, Mathers C, Corvalán C, Woodward A. Introduction and methods, assessing the environmental burden of disease at national and local levels. Environmental Burden of Disease Series No. 1. Geneva, World Health Organization, 2003. Section 3.5, page 33, which obtains the YLD for a single disabling event by replacing the standard life expectancy in the YLL formula with the duration of disease and multiplying by the disability weight, with a the age of onset and L the duration of disability. https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/42750" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Age at the end of the case entered as its duration. L is the duration, not the age at which the case ends. Entering 45 for the case with onset at 35 counts 45 years of illness and gives about 15.86 years lived with disability instead of 6.955 (computed here for illustration).",
        "Disability weight applied to the age-weighted term only. The weight multiplies the whole expression. Leaving the (1 minus K) term unweighted at K = 0.5 gives about 7.80 years for the case with onset at 35 instead of 6.07 (computed here for illustration), and at K = 0 it counts the illness as if it were death." ;
    hemo:9000014 "YLD_awd = DW * (K * C * exp(r * a) / (r + beta)^2 * (exp(-(r + beta) * (L + a)) * (-(r + beta) * (L + a) - 1) - exp(-(r + beta) * a) * (-(r + beta) * a - 1)) + (1 - K) / r * (1 - exp(-r * L)))" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-AGW-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "age-weighted-continuously-discounted-years-lived-with-disability-per-incident-case" .

hemo:5000689 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "One-stage expected gain of continuing over stopping at an interim look with a switching cost"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-AVOI-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000152 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004277 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003059,
        hemo:6003060,
        hemo:6003061,
        hemo:6003062,
        hemo:6003063,
        hemo:6003064,
        hemo:6003065,
        hemo:6003066,
        hemo:6003067,
        hemo:6003068,
        hemo:6003069,
        hemo:6003070,
        hemo:6003071,
        hemo:6003072,
        hemo:6003073 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003060,
        hemo:6003062,
        hemo:6003063,
        hemo:6003064,
        hemo:6003065,
        hemo:6003067,
        hemo:6003069,
        hemo:6003070,
        hemo:6003073 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003059 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004274,
        hemo:7004275,
        hemo:7004276 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004278,
        hemo:7004279,
        hemo:7004280,
        hemo:7004281,
        hemo:7004282,
        hemo:7004283,
        hemo:7004284 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compares two expected rewards at an interim look in the model of Chick, Forster and Pertile, with outcomes observed at once and no discounting. Stopping now adopts the new technology only if P times the posterior mean exceeds the switching cost I, which is worth P times max(a, 0), with a the posterior mean less I divided by P. Continuing with m more pairs and then choosing is worth P times the expected positive part of a after updating, less the cost c of each pair, plus the participants' expected reward d_on times m times mu_t under online learning. The difference Delta is P times EVSI_I less the sampling cost plus that online term, where s_m is the preposterior standard deviation of HE-FM-EVSI-002 with n_t and m, and EVSI_I is the closed-form EVSI of HE-FM-EVSI-003 taken about the threshold I/P in place of zero. A positive Delta for some feasible m means that a one-stage plan beats stopping, the continuation condition of the KG* rule, which Chick and colleagues set out for unknown sampling variance (their section 4) and which here takes the normal distribution of their equation 6 as in their equation 14." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One-stage expected gain of continuing over stopping at an interim look with a switching cost" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Chick SE, Forster M, Pertile P. A Bayesian decision theoretic model of sequential experimentation with delayed response. Journal of the Royal Statistical Society Series B. 2017;79(5):1439-1462. Section 2.1, equation 4 and the text after it, where d_on of 1 includes the benefits to trial participants in addition to the P post-trial patients (online learning) and traditional trials set it to 0 implicitly. Section 2.2, where ties may be broken by picking the action that samples more, at no loss of expected reward. Section 3, equation 14, the Optimal Bayes One Stage policy, and section 4, equations 17 and 18, the KG* rule that continues if and only if a feasible one-stage plan has a greater expected reward than stopping, given there for unknown sampling variance with a Student t predictive distribution in place of the normal of equation 6. https://doi.org/10.1111/rssb.12225",
        "Chick SE, Forster M, Pertile P. A Bayesian decision theoretic model of sequential experimentation with delayed response. Journal of the Royal Statistical Society Series B. 2017;79(5):1439-1462. Section 2.1, equations 2 and 3, the normal posterior for the expected incremental net monetary benefit with effective sample size n0 plus the pairs observed. Section 2.1.1, equation 6, the distribution of the posterior mean still to be obtained, normal about the current mean with variance sigma_X squared over n_t times u over n_t plus u, and equation 7, the reward of stopping, with the new technology adopted when P times the posterior mean exceeds the switching cost I. Section 2.1.2, equation 8a, Bellman's comparison of that reward with the reward of one more allocation, less its cost c, plus the online reward. https://doi.org/10.1111/rssb.12225",
        "Flight L, Brennan A, Wilson I, Chick SE. A tutorial on value-based adaptive designs, could a value-based sequential 2-arm design have created more health economic value for the Big CACTUS trial. Value in Health. 2024;27(10):1328-1337. Case study step 2, where the EVSI of the value-based fixed design is calculated with the standard normal loss function method and ENBS is EVSI minus trial costs. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2024.06.004",
        "Welch C, Forster M, Ronaldson S, Keding A, Corbacho-Martín B, Tharmanathan P. The performance of a Bayesian value-based sequential clinical trial design in the presence of an equivocal cost-effectiveness signal, evidence from the HERO trial. BMC Medical Research Methodology. 2024;24:155. Methods, section on the Bayesian value-based model of a sequential clinical trial, where adoption of the new technology is recommended if P times the posterior mean exceeds the switching cost I, and the design stops the trial as soon as an extra pair of patients is not worth its additional cost. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12874-024-02248-9" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Design-stage prior mean used at the interim look. Keeping the prior mean of GBP 500 when the interim posterior mean has fallen to minus GBP 1,500 gives a one-stage gain of about GBP 0.94 million, a signal to continue, where the interim evidence gives about minus GBP 377,228, a signal to stop (computed here for illustration).",
        "Negative one-stage gain read as proof that stopping is optimal. A one-stage check looks only at plans that stop after m more pairs, so it can stop a trial too early. Flight and colleagues note that updating the EVSI with the interim data does not take account of all possible future interim analyses, and Chick and colleagues report the optimal sequential policy outperforming the one-stage policy in net gain where the prior mean lies in the sequential region.",
        "Prior effective sample size used in place of the interim effective sample size. Computing s_m with the prior n0 of 25 in place of n_t of 125 treats the 100 pairs already observed as if they had not been, so the remaining 100 pairs are credited with about GBP 618.11 per patient instead of GBP 151.14, and the gain of continuing with about GBP 11.96 million instead of GBP 2.62 million (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = mu_t - I / P; s_m = sigma_X * sqrt(m / (n_t * (n_t + m))); z = abs(a) / s_m; phi_z = exp(-z * z / 2) / sqrt(2 * 3.14159265); EVSI_I = s_m * (phi_z - z * Q_z); Delta = P * EVSI_I - m * c + d_on * m * mu_t" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-AVOI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-stage-expected-gain-of-continuing-over-stopping-at-an-interim-look-with-a-switching-cost" .

hemo:5000690 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Per-step infection probability of a susceptible agent from k infectious contacts"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ABM-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000153 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004288 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003074,
        hemo:6003075,
        hemo:6003076 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003075,
        hemo:6003076 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003074 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004285,
        hemo:7004286,
        hemo:7004287 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004289,
        hemo:7004290,
        hemo:7004291,
        hemo:7004292,
        hemo:7004293,
        hemo:7004294 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "A susceptible agent with k contacts with infectious agents in one step, each transmitting independently with probability p, escapes infection only if every contact fails, which happens with probability (1 minus p) to the power k, the binomial probability of no events in HE-FM-BINOM-002 with k in place of n. The probability of at least one transmission, P_inf, is the complement; the article writes it P. It rises with k but less than in proportion, because a second transmission to an agent already infected adds nothing, so the sum k times p overstates it and can exceed 1. With one contact the formula returns p and with no contacts it returns zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Per-step infection probability of a susceptible agent from k infectious contacts" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Britton T. Basic stochastic transmission models and their inference. arXiv preprint 1801.09594, 2018. Section 2.3, The Reed-Frost epidemic and chain-binomial models, which states that a susceptible individual escapes infection in the next generation with probability (1 minus p) raised to the number infected in the previous generation, that the probability of infection is the complementary probability, and that in the Reed-Frost epidemic an infective has independent infectious contacts with each other individual, each with probability p equal to one minus the exponential of minus beta times iota over n. https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.09594",
        "Chhatwal J, He T. Economic evaluations with agent-based modelling, an introduction. PharmacoEconomics. 2015;33(5):423-433. The caption of Figure 3, in which each infected agent can transmit the infection to other susceptible people with some probability per contact, section 5.4, in which an infected person could come into contact with several susceptible persons in the contact radius in each cycle, and Table 1 of the case study, which gives a transmission probability per contact of 0.005 and a cycle length of 1 year. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40273-015-0254-2" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Adding contact risks instead of combining escape probabilities. Summing the risks gives 3 times 0.10, or 0.30, for the article's agent instead of 0.271, about 11% too high, and for 20 such contacts gives 2.0, an impossible probability, where the formula gives about 0.878.",
        "Counting every contact rather than infectious contacts in k. If an agent meets 10 people of whom 3 are infectious and k is set to 10, the probability with p of 0.10 is about 0.651 instead of 0.271. k counts only contacts with agents who are infectious at the start of the step." ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_inf = 1 - (1 - p)^k" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ABM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "per-step-infection-probability-of-a-susceptible-agent-from-k-infectious-contacts" .

hemo:5000692 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Monte Carlo runs needed for a target standard error of a mean agent-based model outcome"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ABM-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000153 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004307 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003082,
        hemo:6003083,
        hemo:6003084 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003083,
        hemo:6003084 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003082 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004304,
        hemo:7004305,
        hemo:7004306 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004308,
        hemo:7004309,
        hemo:7004310,
        hemo:7004311,
        hemo:7004312 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Inverts the Monte Carlo standard error of a mean over independent runs, s_run divided by the square root of the number of runs (the form of HE-FM-ANTV-002 with one observation per run, with the standard deviation taken with divisor R minus 1 as in HE-FM-BSE-001), to give the number of runs R_req whose standard error equals a chosen target e_target. s_run is the standard deviation of the outcome across a pilot set of runs, for example of mean cost per person or, for an increment, of the paired difference between strategies run with common random numbers. The result is rounded up to a whole number of runs. Halving the target multiplies the runs by four." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Monte Carlo runs needed for a target standard error of a mean agent-based model outcome" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Chhatwal J, He T. Economic evaluations with agent-based modelling, an introduction. PharmacoEconomics. 2015;33(5):423-433. Section 2.5, which states that agent-based models are typically stochastic, that first-order uncertainty can be higher than in state-transition or discrete event models and can be reduced by running the model several times with different random numbers, and that the sufficient number of Monte Carlo runs depends on the structure, the number of parameters and the time horizon and can be assessed qualitatively or by evaluation of the variance in model outcomes. Section 5.4 and Table 1 report 5,000 runs of each scenario in the case study. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40273-015-0254-2",
        "Morris TP, White IR, Crowther MJ. Using simulation studies to evaluate statistical methods. Statistics in Medicine. 2019;38(11):2074-2102. Table 6, which gives the Monte Carlo standard error of estimated bias as the square root of the sum of squared deviations of the estimates from their mean divided by n_sim times (n_sim minus 1), and section 7.1, which takes the standard deviation of the estimates as at most 0.2 from an initial small simulation run, requires a Monte Carlo standard error of bias below 0.005 and concludes that 1,600 repetitions are needed. The checklist in Table 1 advises setting the random number seed once per repetition and using separate streams when running chunks in parallel. https://doi.org/10.1002/sim.8086" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Counting agents rather than runs as the replications. Dividing the spread of outcomes across the agents of one run by the square root of the number of agents, for example 5,000, treats the agents as independent when interaction makes their outcomes correlated, so the standard error is understated and too few runs are chosen. The standard error of a mean over runs comes from the spread across independent runs.",
        "Doubling runs to halve the Monte Carlo standard error. Doubling the illustrative 900 runs to 1,800 gives a standard error of about GBP 7.07, not GBP 5. Halving the standard error needs 3,600 runs." ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_req = (s_run / e_target)^2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ABM-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "monte-carlo-runs-needed-for-a-target-standard-error-of-a-mean-agent-based-model-outcome" .

hemo:5000695 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "All-cause cycle death probability from independent background and disease-specific death probabilities"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-BGM-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000154 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004332 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003091,
        hemo:6003092,
        hemo:6003093 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003092,
        hemo:6003093 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003091 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004329,
        hemo:7004330,
        hemo:7004331 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004333,
        hemo:7004334,
        hemo:7004335,
        hemo:7004336,
        hemo:7004337 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Combines the probability of dying of other causes in a cycle, read from the life table at the cohort's attained age, with the probability of dying of the disease in the same cycle. A person survives the cycle only by escaping both, so the all-cause probability is 1 minus the product of the two survival probabilities. When both come from constant rates over the same cycle with HE-FM-TP-001, the result equals the probability from the summed hazard of HE-FM-BTH-005, the total compound rate that Sonnenberg and Beck form by adding the excess rate to the baseline rate. HE-FM-TP-004 splits the result between background and disease death states where a model keeps them apart." ;
    hemo:9000009 "All-cause cycle death probability from independent background and disease-specific death probabilities" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Rutherford MJ, Lambert PC, Sweeting MJ, Pennington B, Crowther MJ, Abrams KR, Latimer NR. NICE DSU Technical Support Document 21: Flexible methods for survival analysis. Sheffield, Decision Support Unit, ScHARR, University of Sheffield; 2020 (updated March 2022). Section 3.7.2, equation 13, which breaks the all-cause mortality rate into background mortality, typically obtained from population mortality rates stratified by age, sex and calendar year, and an excess mortality rate; section 3.7.1 describes modelling disease-specific and other-cause mortality separately and combining them to give estimates of long-term all-cause survival. https://doi.org/10.15131/shef.data.33849118",
        "Sonnenberg FA, Beck JR. Markov models in medical decision making: a practical guide. Medical Decision Making. 1993;13(4):322-338. Section on assignment of probabilities (pages 335 and 336), which splits each state's mortality rate into a baseline rate that depends on age, sex and race and an excess rate from comorbid disease, looks the baseline rate up in a table of age-specific mortality rates for each cycle, adds the excess component to the baseline rate to give a total compound mortality rate, and uses the total rate to calculate the probability of death in the cycle; the section on conversion of rates to probabilities (pages 332 and 333) gives the constant-rate conversion. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X9301300409" ;
    hemo:9000011 "All-cause hazard ratio applied to background deaths. Applying a treatment hazard ratio to p_all also cuts background deaths, which the treatment cannot affect. With the article's annual rates and an illustrative hazard ratio of 0.70, the treated all-cause probability is 0.169311 when the ratio is applied to p_all through HE-FM-HR-004, against 0.173041 when it is applied to p_dis alone (computed here for illustration).",
        "Background and disease death probabilities added. Adding the two probabilities counts twice the people who would have died of both causes in the cycle. For the article's annual rates the error is small, 0.236087 against 0.232794, but with illustrative probabilities of 0.10 and 0.30 at older ages the sum is 0.40 against 0.37 (computed here for illustration).",
        "Life-table probability layered on an all-cause trial probability. Combining p_bg with a disease probability that already contains other-cause deaths counts background mortality twice. In the article's illustrative cure model life years to 30 years fall from 11.93 to 10.05, an understatement of 1.87 years, or 16%. A trial curve can take background mortality only as a floor, which HE-VT-BTH-008 checks." ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_all = 1 - (1 - p_bg) * (1 - p_dis)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-BGM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "all-cause-cycle-death-probability-from-independent-background-and-disease-specific-death-probabilities" .

hemo:5000701 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Basic reproduction number of a two-group next-generation matrix as its dominant eigenvalue"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-BRN-005" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000155 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004380 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003119,
        hemo:6003120,
        hemo:6003121,
        hemo:6003122,
        hemo:6003123,
        hemo:6003124,
        hemo:6003125 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003119,
        hemo:6003120,
        hemo:6003121,
        hemo:6003122 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003125 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004379 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004381,
        hemo:7004382,
        hemo:7004383,
        hemo:7004384,
        hemo:7004385 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "When infected people differ by group or stage, element k_ij of the next-generation matrix K is the expected number of new cases in group i caused by one newly infected person in group j, as Diekmann, Heesterbeek and Roberts define it, and R_0 is the dominant eigenvalue of K, the definition proposed by Diekmann, Heesterbeek and Metz. For a compartmental model van den Driessche writes K as F times the inverse of V, with F the rates of new infections and V the other transitions, and R_0 as its spectral radius. For two groups the dominant eigenvalue is the larger root of lambda^2 minus T_K lambda plus D_K = 0, with T_K the trace and D_K the determinant, and the term under the square root equals (k_11 minus k_22)^2 plus 4 k_12 k_21, which is never negative for a matrix of non-negative entries. When D_K is zero, as for a matrix of rank one, R_0 is the trace. Larger matrices are handled by HE-CF-BRN-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Basic reproduction number of a two-group next-generation matrix as its dominant eigenvalue" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Diekmann O, Heesterbeek JA, Metz JA. On the definition and the computation of the basic reproduction ratio R0 in models for infectious diseases in heterogeneous populations. Journal of Mathematical Biology. 1990;28(4):365-382. The abstract, which defines the expected number of secondary cases produced by a typical infected individual during its entire period of infectiousness in a completely susceptible population as the dominant eigenvalue of a positive linear operator. https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00178324",
        "Diekmann O, Heesterbeek JA, Roberts MG. The construction of next-generation matrices for compartmental epidemic models. Journal of the Royal Society Interface. 2010;7(47):873-885. Section 1, which defines element K_ij as the expected number of new cases with state-at-infection i generated by one individual just infected in state-at-infection j; section 2.1, equation 2.12, which obtains the dominant eigenvalue of a 2 by 2 matrix from its trace and determinant and notes that R_0 equals the trace when det K = 0; and section 3.2, which defines R_0 as the largest eigenvalue, R_0 = rho(K). https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2009.0386",
        "van den Driessche P, Watmough J. Reproduction numbers and sub-threshold endemic equilibria for compartmental models of disease transmission. Mathematical Biosciences. 2002;180:29-48. The abstract, which presents a precise definition of R_0 for a general compartmental model based on ordinary differential equations, shows that the disease-free equilibrium is locally asymptotically stable if R_0 is below 1 and unstable if it is above 1, and illustrates the definition with multigroup, staged progression and vector-host models. https://doi.org/10.1016/s0025-5564(02)00108-6",
        "van den Driessche P. Reproduction numbers of infectious disease models. Infectious Disease Modelling. 2017;2(3):288-303. Section 3, which splits the infected compartments into new infections F and other transitions V at the disease-free equilibrium, states that F is entrywise non-negative and V a non-singular M-matrix, that the (i, j) entry of F V^-1 is the expected number of secondary infections in compartment i produced by an individual introduced in compartment j and that R_0 = rho(F V^-1), with Theorem 1 linking R_0 below 1 to local stability; section 4.1, which states that herd immunity is applicable if a control strategy is aimed at all individuals and introduces type reproduction numbers for control aimed at one host population; and section 5, which gives the square root form for a vector-host model and notes that the square root is often omitted in the literature. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.idm.2017.06.002" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Largest diagonal entry of a next-generation matrix read as R_0. For the article's matrix the larger diagonal entry, 0.8, suggests that spread dies out, while R_0 is about 1.268 because cases pass between the groups, so judging each group on its own transmission misses the threshold crossing.",
        "Square root dropped from a host and vector reproduction number or used for a host coverage target. Van den Driessche notes that the literature often omits the square root for vector-host models, which keeps the threshold at 1 but changes the value, so the two forms cannot be compared across studies. In the illustrative host and vector matrix the product 2 would replace 1.414. Neither value gives a host coverage target through the threshold formula of HE-FM-BRN-003. Vaccinating a proportion p of hosts scales the host row of K by 1 minus p and gives R_0 of the square root of 2(1 minus p), which falls below 1 only when p exceeds 50%, while 1 minus 1/1.414 would suggest about 29.3% (computed here for illustration). Van den Driessche restricts herd immunity to control aimed at all individuals and uses type reproduction numbers for control aimed at one host type." ;
    hemo:9000014 "T_K = k_11 + k_22; D_K = k_11 * k_22 - k_12 * k_21; R_0 = (T_K + sqrt(T_K^2 - 4 * D_K)) / 2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-BRN-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "basic-reproduction-number-of-a-two-group-next-generation-matrix-as-its-dominant-eigenvalue" .

hemo:5000705 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Basket ceiling as a multiple of the reference price and the margin of a proposed price"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-BOC-004" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000156 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004433 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003141,
        hemo:6003142,
        hemo:6003143,
        hemo:6003144,
        hemo:6003145 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003142,
        hemo:6003143,
        hemo:6003144 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003145 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004431,
        hemo:7004432 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004434,
        hemo:7004435,
        hemo:7004436,
        hemo:7004437,
        hemo:7004438 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Multiplies a basket reference price by a multiplier k to give a ceiling and subtracts the ceiling from a proposed domestic price. A positive margin means the price is above the ceiling and is cut or reviewed, and a margin of zero or below means it is accepted. The article's illustrative rule takes k = 1.25 on the basket mean, modelled on Japan's foreign average price adjustment, whose adjusted price is HE-FM-BOC-005, while the PMPRB test is k = 1 on the highest international price. The reference price comes from HE-FM-BOC-003 or HE-CF-BOC-002." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Basket ceiling as a multiple of the reference price and the margin of a proposed price" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare (Abe Y). Japan's NHI Drug Price System. Presentation, 1 April 2021, hosted by the Pharmaceuticals and Medical Devices Agency. Slide headed Foreign Average Price Adjustment, part 1 (page 17 of the PDF), which defines the foreign average price as the average of prices in the US (Medicare or Medicaid), the UK, Germany and France and sets a reduction adjustment for prices exceeding 1.25 times the foreign average price and a raise adjustment for prices below 0.75 times it, for the cost calculation method and the similar efficacy comparison method for products without similar pharmacological actions. https://www.pmda.go.jp/files/000248690.pdf",
        "Patented Medicine Prices Review Board. Guidelines for PMPRB Staff, in effect 1 January 2026. Paragraph 50, under Initial Review, which states that patented medicines whose prices are above the HIP threshold are subject to an In-Depth Review. https://www.canada.ca/en/patented-medicine-prices-review/services/legislation/guidelines.html" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Basket ceiling read as a change in the value of the drug. The ceiling falls by 17.5, from 131.25 to 113.75, between Baskets A and B with no change in the drug or its evidence. A cost-effectiveness model that uses the ceiling as the drug price states the basket, the price type, the exchange-rate rule and the aggregation formula and tests other prices in sensitivity analysis, as the article recommends.",
        "Multiplier applied to the lowest basket price. Applying 1.25 to Basket A's lowest price of 80 gives a ceiling of 100 instead of 131.25 under the article's mean-based rule, so the proposed price of 130 would be cut by mistake (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "T = k * P_ref; D = P_prop - T" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-BOC-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "basket-ceiling-as-a-multiple-of-the-reference-price-and-the-margin-of-a-proposed-price" .

hemo:5000707 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Averaged-cohort gap in constant-risk life expectancy for two strata"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-COHM-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000157 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004451 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003149,
        hemo:6003150,
        hemo:6003151,
        hemo:6003152,
        hemo:6003153,
        hemo:6003154,
        hemo:6003155 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003149,
        hemo:6003150,
        hemo:6003151 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003155 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004448,
        hemo:7004449,
        hemo:7004450 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004452,
        hemo:7004453,
        hemo:7004454,
        hemo:7004455,
        hemo:7004456,
        hemo:7004457,
        hemo:7004458 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives the population mean life expectancy when each of two strata with constant per-cycle death probabilities q_1 and q_2 is run as its own cohort and weighted by its share, the life expectancy of one cohort run at the share-weighted mean probability, and the difference between them. Each stratum result is the article's L(q), 1/q minus one half, the one-state case of HE-FM-ABS-001 with the half-cycle subtraction of HE-CF-MKCH-001, and the weighting is that of HE-FM-ATE-001. The half cycle cancels in gap_L, which equals w(1 minus w)(q_1 minus q_2) squared divided by q_1 q_2 q_bar (derived here), so the gap is never negative and is zero only when the strata share one probability or one stratum holds the whole cohort. The averaged cohort therefore never overstates life expectancy in this model and understates it whenever both strata are present with different probabilities, the bias that TSD 15 section 3.1 describes for an outcome that is non-linear in a patient characteristic; HE-FM-EV-002 approximates the same gap by a second-order term, whereas this form is exact." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Averaged-cohort gap in constant-risk life expectancy for two strata" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Davis S, Stevenson M, Tappenden P, Wailoo A. NICE DSU Technical Support Document 15: Cost-effectiveness modelling using patient-level simulation. Sheffield, Decision Support Unit, ScHARR, University of Sheffield; April 2014. Section 3.1, model non-linearity with respect to heterogeneous patient characteristics (pages 14 and 15), which states that estimating outcomes for a cohort using only average characteristics gives a biased estimate of the average outcome across the population where a characteristic has a non-linear relationship with the outcomes, and that subgroup analysis may be used where outcomes within subgroups are reasonably homogeneous. The closed form of the gap is derived here and is not printed in TSD 15. https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD15_Patient-level_simulation.pdf",
        "Siebert U, Alagoz O, Bayoumi AM, Jahn B, Owens DK, Cohen DJ, Kuntz KM. State-transition modeling: a report of the ISPOR-SMDM Modeling Good Research Practices Task Force-3. Value in Health. 2012;15(6):812-820. Section Starting Cohort, which states that if outputs are wanted for a population-based starting cohort the model must be run multiple times, one for each stratum, and then aggregated across strata. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2012.06.014",
        "Sonnenberg FA, Beck JR. Markov models in medical decision making: a practical guide. Medical Decision Making. 1993;13(4):322-338. The Half-Cycle Correction (pages 329 and 330), which states that the fundamental matrix representation is equivalent to counting state membership at the beginning of each cycle and that the correction to a matrix solution is subtraction of one half cycle from the membership of each starting state. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X9301300409" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Averaged-cohort ICER taken as confirmed by the mean of stratum ICERs. With a one-off cost c, a constant utility u and a proportional cut e, each constant-risk stratum has an ICER of c(1 minus e)q divided by ue (derived here), proportional to q, so the ICER of one cohort at the mean probability equals the share-weighted mean of the stratum ICERs. In the article's example the averaged cohort's GBP 15,000 equals the simple mean of GBP 5,000 and GBP 25,000. The agreement is a property of this model and checks nothing, since both differ from the population ICER of GBP 8,333 per QALY that the share-weighted gains of HE-FM-ATE-001 give with HE-FM-ICER-001; HE-ER-CHN-002 shows why subgroup ICERs are not averaged.",
        "Death rates averaged in place of probabilities for an averaged cohort. Converting 0.05 and 0.25 to rates, averaging them and converting back gives a probability of about 0.156 and a life expectancy of about 5.91 years, still 49% below the stratified 11.5 years (computed here for illustration). Changing the scale on which the input is averaged leaves the bias in place; running the strata separately removes it.",
        "Expecting a proportional cut to gain most where death risk is highest. Run as separate cohorts, the article's strata gain 5.0 life years from a 20% cut at an annual death probability of 0.05 and 1.0 at 0.25, each gain the difference of two values of L(q), although the absolute risk reduction of HE-FM-ARR-002 in one cycle is 0.01 in the first stratum and 0.05 in the second. At a constant probability the gain equals e divided by (1 minus e) q (derived here), so at a fixed proportional cut it falls as baseline risk rises, and ranking strata by the one-cycle absolute reduction reverses their ranking by lifetime life years gained.",
        "Half cycle subtracted on one side of an averaged-cohort comparison. Setting a stratified result counted at the start of each cycle with no half-cycle subtraction, 12 years, against an averaged cohort that has it, 6.17 years, overstates the gap by half a cycle, 5.83 against 5.33 years. Both sides need the same counting weights, as in HE-FM-CSIM-001.",
        "Second-order approximation read as the averaged-cohort gap. Applying HE-FM-EV-002 to 1/q at the mean probability of 0.15, with a second derivative of about 592.6 and a variance of 0.01 across the two equal strata, gives a gap of about 2.96 years against the exact 5.33, 44% too small (computed here for illustration). With strata this far apart the higher-order terms are large, so the closed form or separate stratum runs give the estimate." ;
    hemo:9000014 "q_bar = w * q_1 + (1 - w) * q_2; L_strat = w * (1 / q_1 - 0.5) + (1 - w) * (1 / q_2 - 0.5); L_avg = 1 / q_bar - 0.5; gap_L = L_strat - L_avg" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-COHM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "averaged-cohort-gap-in-constant-risk-life-expectancy-for-two-strata" .

hemo:5000711 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "One Euler step of the closed SIR model with frequency-dependent transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CMPM-004" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000158 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004479 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003176,
        hemo:6003177,
        hemo:6003178,
        hemo:6003179,
        hemo:6003180,
        hemo:6003181,
        hemo:6003182,
        hemo:6003183,
        hemo:6003184,
        hemo:6003185,
        hemo:6003186,
        hemo:6003187 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003176,
        hemo:6003177,
        hemo:6003178,
        hemo:6003179,
        hemo:6003180,
        hemo:6003181,
        hemo:6003182 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003185,
        hemo:6003186,
        hemo:6003187 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004477,
        hemo:7004478 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004480,
        hemo:7004481,
        hemo:7004482,
        hemo:7004483,
        hemo:7004484 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Applies the compartment balance HE-FM-CMPM-001 to the susceptible, infectious and recovered compartments of Hethcote's classic SIR epidemic model, with new infections F from the force of infection of HE-FM-CMPM-002 and recoveries G at rate gamma, both computed from the counts at the start of the step. F leaves S and enters I, and G leaves I and enters R, so S_next plus I_next plus R_next equals S plus I plus R and the population is conserved at every step. Written as a cohort update the step has an S to I entry of beta I dt/N that changes with the infectious count, so the transition matrix differs at every step and the update is nonlinear in the state, whereas HE-FM-MM-001 applies one fixed matrix to a cohort that does not interact. Pitman and colleagues note that spreadsheet models often use this Euler method and that it is less accurate than other numerical techniques, so the step is kept short against the mean infectious period 1/gamma." ;
    hemo:9000009 "One Euler step of the closed SIR model with frequency-dependent transmission" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Hethcote HW. The mathematics of infectious diseases. SIAM Review. 2000;42(4):599-653. Section 2.3, The Classic Epidemic Model (pages 604 and 605), which gives the SIR model as dS/dt = minus beta I S/N, dI/dt = beta I S/N minus gamma I and dR/dt = gamma I in equation 2.1, with S(t) + I(t) + R(t) = N, states that it uses the standard incidence and has recovery at rate gamma I corresponding to an exponential waiting time, and that it has no births and deaths because the period is short; and section 2.1 (page 603), which states that terms such as gamma I correspond to exponentially distributed waiting times with mean 1/gamma. https://doi.org/10.1137/S0036144500371907",
        "Pitman R, Fisman D, Zaric GS, Postma M, Kretzschmar M, Edmunds J, Brisson M. Dynamic transmission modeling. A report of the ISPOR-SMDM Modeling Good Research Practices Task Force-5. Value in Health. 2012;15(6):828-834. Section Software Options, which states that in a spreadsheet environment the Euler method is often used to project the system of differential equations forward in time and that this method is not as accurate as other numerical techniques; and section Methodological uncertainty, which states that most dynamic transmission modelling has used system dynamics, in which transition between compartments is represented by differential equations. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2012.06.011" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Euler step too long for the speed of the epidemic. Euler steps lag the true solution while infections grow. With one-day steps the article's epidemic has 570.0 infectious people on day 10, about 13% fewer than the 653.9 given by steps of 0.0001 day, which is close to the exact solution, and two five-day steps give 389.6 (computed here for illustration). The error builds over the run and feeds every count and cost taken from it.",
        "Infections for the infectious compartment taken from already updated susceptibles. Updating S first and then computing the inflow to I from the new S mixes two time points. On the article's day 1 it moves 29.6109 people into I but 29.7 out of S, leaving 9,999.9109 people in the population, and the loss repeats at every step (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "F = beta * S * I / N * dt; G = gamma * I * dt; S_next = S - F; I_next = I + F - G; R_next = R + G" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CMPM-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "one-euler-step-of-the-closed-sir-model-with-frequency-dependent-transmission" .

hemo:5000712 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Infections averted in one step under a static and a dynamic force of infection"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CMPM-005" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000158 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004486 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003188,
        hemo:6003189,
        hemo:6003190,
        hemo:6003191,
        hemo:6003192,
        hemo:6003193,
        hemo:6003194,
        hemo:6003195,
        hemo:6003196,
        hemo:6003197,
        hemo:6003198 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003188,
        hemo:6003189,
        hemo:6003190,
        hemo:6003191,
        hemo:6003192,
        hemo:6003193,
        hemo:6003194 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003198 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004485 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004487,
        hemo:7004488,
        hemo:7004489 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compares one step of a programme run with the same step of the no-programme run. The static count applies the no-programme force of infection lambda_b to the programme's susceptibles, so infections averted are lambda_b times the fall in susceptibles, the direct effect only. The dynamic count recalculates the force of infection from the programme's own infectious count, as the WHO guide describes for a dynamic model, and the difference A_ind is the indirect effect of fewer infectious people, which simplifies to beta S_p (I_b minus I_p) dt/N (derived). The two counts agree in a step that both runs start with the same infectious count and diverge once the programme has lowered I. Summed over the article's two days this gives the article's comparison, and HE-CF-CMPM-001 runs longer horizons with the static run keeping its own susceptible count, and Jit and Brisson explain why against no intervention the dynamic count is usually the larger, so that a static estimate is conservative." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Infections averted in one step under a static and a dynamic force of infection" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Jit M, Brisson M. Modelling the epidemiology of infectious diseases for decision analysis. A primer. PharmacoEconomics. 2011;29(5):371-386. Section 3.1, Types of Models, which defines the force of infection as the instantaneous rate at which susceptible people become infected, constant in a static model and a function of the proportion infected in a dynamic model; and section 3.2, When to Use Transmission Dynamic Models, which states that a dynamic model will usually show an intervention as more cost effective than an equivalent static model, because it predicts more infections prevented, including those indirectly protected through herd immunity, and a faster fall in infections, and that a static model may therefore be sufficient to obtain a conservative estimate of cost effectiveness. https://doi.org/10.2165/11539960-000000000-00000",
        "Pitman R, Fisman D, Zaric GS, Postma M, Kretzschmar M, Edmunds J, Brisson M. Dynamic transmission modeling. A report of the ISPOR-SMDM Modeling Good Research Practices Task Force-5. Value in Health. 2012;15(6):828-834. The section on when a dynamic approach is appropriate, with best practice V-1, which calls for a dynamic model when an intervention has an impact on disease transmission in the target population, and its subsection Indirect effects of intervention programs, which states that indirect effects are not always beneficial because a higher average age at infection increases complication risk and mortality for many diseases, for example hepatitis A and varicella; and section Reporting Results and Informing Decision Making, which states that infections can be disaggregated according to whether they are directly or indirectly prevented. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2012.06.011",
        "World Health Organization. WHO guide for standardization of economic evaluations of immunization programmes, 2nd edition (WHO/IVB/19.10). Geneva, WHO, 2019. Section 6.3.1, Basic types of infectious disease models, which states that in a dynamic transmission model the force of infection can change over time, that a dynamic model recalculates it cyclically or continuously from the proportion of infectious people it projects at each point in time, that in a static model it remains constant and independent of the proportion of infectious people, and that dynamic models are typically solved as differential equations in continuous time, with discrete time intervals often applied for practical reasons. https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/329389" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Static count of infections averted reported as the whole effect of a programme. Over the article's two days the static count averts 13.16 infections and the dynamic count 14.58, so the static figure misses 1.42, about a tenth of the dynamic total, and at the article's GBP 40 per infection treated it values averted treatment costs at GBP 526 instead of GBP 583. The gap grows over the early generations of transmission, to about 2,024.6 infections by day 29 with the article's inputs, and then narrows to about 482.6 once both epidemics have ended (HE-CF-CMPM-001, computed here for illustration), so a static count from a short run understates the indirect effect by a different share from one taken over the whole epidemic." ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda_b = beta * I_b / N; A_stat = lambda_b * (S_b - S_p) * dt; A_dyn = beta * (S_b * I_b - S_p * I_p) / N * dt; A_ind = A_dyn - A_stat" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CMPM-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "infections-averted-in-one-step-under-a-static-and-a-dynamic-force-of-infection" .

hemo:5000713 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Lowe fixed-basket consumer price index of a two-item basket"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CPI-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000159 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004493 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003199,
        hemo:6003200,
        hemo:6003201,
        hemo:6003202,
        hemo:6003203,
        hemo:6003204,
        hemo:6003205,
        hemo:6003206,
        hemo:6003207 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003199,
        hemo:6003200,
        hemo:6003201,
        hemo:6003202,
        hemo:6003203,
        hemo:6003204 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003207 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004490,
        hemo:7004491,
        hemo:7004492 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004494,
        hemo:7004495,
        hemo:7004496,
        hemo:7004497 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Prices the same basket of quantities q_b, bought in the weight reference period b, at time t prices and at base-period prices, and expresses the first cost as a percentage of the second, the Laspeyres-type index of section 3.3 of the ONS technical manual. Because the UK quantities come from a period before the base period, the manual classes the CPI and CPIH as Lowe-type indices. The same index is a weighted arithmetic average of the price relatives p_t / p_0, each weighted by its share of the basket cost at base-period prices, the route HE-CF-CPI-001 follows from expenditure data. The formula is written out for two items so that the calculator runs, and the Excel and code forms take any number of items. A ratio of two index values is the input of HE-FM-COST-002." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Lowe fixed-basket consumer price index of a two-item basket" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Jones KC, Weatherly H, Birch S, Castelli A, Chalkley M, Dargan A, et al. Unit Costs of Health and Social Care 2024 Manual. Canterbury and York, Personal Social Services Research Unit (University of Kent) and Centre for Health Economics (University of York), 2025. Section 12.1.1, the NHS Cost Inflation Index (NHSCII), table 12.1.1, which gives annual increases in the NHSCII pay and prices series for NHS providers of 2.49% for 2020/2021, 2.58% for 2021/2022, 7.32% for 2022/2023 and 4.31% for 2023/2024, the last marked provisional. Their product, 1.1769, gives the GBP 2,942.25 in HE-ER-CPI-002 (computed in the article). https://doi.org/10.22024/UniKent/01.02.109563",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 13 October 2026. Chapter 4 economic evaluation, section 4.4.12, which states that when current costs are not available, costs from previous years should be adjusted using inflation indices appropriate to the cost perspective, such as the NHS cost inflation index and the PSS pay and prices index from the PSS Research Unit report on unit costs of health and social care, or the Office for National Statistics consumer price index. https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2",
        "Office for National Statistics. Consumer Prices Indices Technical Manual, 2019. Newport, Office for National Statistics, 2019, page last revised 26 March 2025. Section 2.5.5, expenditure items, which states that typically no account is taken of services free at the point of consumption, even if paid for indirectly through taxes or National Insurance contributions, and that NHS prescription and dental charges are included in the CPIH and CPI but not the full economic cost. Section 13.11, the cost of living, which defines the cost of living as the minimum cost at current prices of the base-period level of utility, states that because of the stress on minimum cost a cost of living index will usually give a lower rate of inflation than the consumer price inflation indices, and describes substitution towards goods and services that are becoming relatively cheaper. https://www.ons.gov.uk/economy/inflationandpriceindices/methodologies/consumerpricesindicestechnicalmanual2019",
        "Office for National Statistics. Consumer Prices Indices Technical Manual, 2019. Newport, Office for National Statistics, 2019, page last revised 26 March 2025. Section 3.3, index calculation, which gives the Laspeyres-type or fixed-basket index I_0,t = 100 x sum of p_i^t q_i^b over sum of p_i^0 q_i^b, its equivalent form as a weighted arithmetic average of price relatives with weights w_i^0;b = p_i^0 q_i^b over sum of p_i^0 q_i^b, and the statement that UK consumer price indices are Lowe-type indices with quantities taken from a period before the base period and price-updated to it. Section 2.4.1.2 states that the harmonised index is not a cost of living index, section 3.5 forms higher-level indices as weighted averages, and section 12.4.2 states that a chain-linked index spanning more than one year cannot be represented as a basket ratio or a weighted average of price relatives. A notice on the page dated 25 March 2026 states that some aspects of the manual are out of date and that it has been replaced by the ONS Consumer prices indices technical guidance (last revised 28 May 2026), whose article Higher-level aggregation and weights in consumer prices (last revised 25 March 2026) still gives aggregation as a Laspeyres-type weighted average with price-updated expenditure weights (sections 3 and 4). https://www.ons.gov.uk/economy/inflationandpriceindices/methodologies/consumerpricesindicestechnicalmanual2019" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Consumer price index ratio taken as the cost of NHS care. The CPI follows household payments and leaves out care free at the point of use, and PMG36 section 4.4.12 asks for inflation indices appropriate to the cost perspective. In the article's worked example, uprating GBP 2,500 in 2019/20 prices to 2023/24 prices with the CPI ratio through HE-FM-COST-002 gives GBP 3,041.25, about 3.4% more than the GBP 2,942.25 given by the NHS cost inflation index pay and prices series for NHS providers (HE-SR-CPI-010), whose 2023/24 increase is provisional.",
        "Current quantities in the numerator of a fixed-basket CPI. Pricing the current basket at current prices against the old basket at base prices measures the change in spending, a value index, and not price change. If the quantities in the worked example double, the result is 217.1429 in place of 108.5714 (computed here for illustration), although no price rose by more than 10%.",
        "Unweighted average of item relatives in place of CPI basket weights. Averaging the relatives of 110 and 105 without weights gives 107.5, while the basket in the worked example gives 108.5714 (computed here for illustration), because the first item accounts for 100 of the 140 of base-period spending. Above the elementary aggregate the manual combines indices as weighted averages (section 3.5)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "E_0 = p1_0 * q1_b + p2_0 * q2_b; E_t = p1_t * q1_b + p2_t * q2_b; I_0t = 100 * E_t / E_0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CPI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "lowe-fixed-basket-consumer-price-index-of-a-two-item-basket" .

hemo:5000715 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Chain-linked consumer price index from the December level and two annual links"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CPI-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000159 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004507 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003215,
        hemo:6003216,
        hemo:6003217,
        hemo:6003218 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003215,
        hemo:6003216,
        hemo:6003217 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003218 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004505,
        hemo:7004506 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004508,
        hemo:7004509,
        hemo:7004510,
        hemo:7004511,
        hemo:7004512 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Builds a long-run index level for month t of year y from the published level for December of year y minus 1 on the 2015 = 100 reference, the January index of year y on the previous December = 100, and the index for month t on January of year y = 100. Section 3.6 of the ONS technical manual sets out the two-link product for the CPI and CPIH, whose weights are updated twice a year, and prints it as the product of the three index values with no divisor. Each link is stated on a base of 100, so here each is divided by 100 before it multiplies the December level (derived from the link bases), as the manual's RPI chaining formulas in section 12.4.2 and its July 1987 example in section 11.2 divide the link by 100. For January itself L_t is 100. A series chained once a year, such as the RPI, is entered with L_Jan = 100 and the long-run level of its January link month in I_Dec. Ratios of chained levels such as the D7BT series are what HE-FM-COST-002 takes." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Chain-linked consumer price index from the December level and two annual links" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Office for National Statistics. Consumer Price Inflation (includes all 3 indices, CPIH, CPI and RPI) QMI. Newport, Office for National Statistics, last revised 25 March 2026. Section on methods used to create the data, which states that the indices are annually chain-linked with each year's series taking the value 100 in the most recent January, and the coherence section, which states that the CPIH, CPI and RPI differ in both coverage and methodology. https://www.ons.gov.uk/economy/inflationandpriceindices/methodologies/consumerpriceinflationincludesall3indicescpihcpiandrpiqmi",
        "Office for National Statistics. Consumer Prices Indices Technical Manual, 2019. Newport, Office for National Statistics, 2019, page last revised 26 March 2025. Section 3.6, chaining, which states that the CPIH and CPI are chain-linked twice every year, with a January index based on the previous December = 100 and a further index for February to December based on January = 100, and gives the chained class index as the product of the December level on 2015 = 100 and the two links, printed without a divisor of 100. Section 11.2 chains the RPI for July 1987 (101.8 on January 1987) to 394.5 on January 1974 and reports a change of 4.4% from 384.7 in July 1986. Section 11.6 unchains by dividing by the January (or previous December) index and multiplying by 100, and section 12.4.2 chains the RPI each January with the January link divided by 100. https://www.ons.gov.uk/economy/inflationandpriceindices/methodologies/consumerpricesindicestechnicalmanual2019" ;
    hemo:9000011 "CPI link movements added as index points. Adding the link movements of minus 0.4 and plus 1.5 points to the December level of 120.0 gives 121.1 in place of 121.3128 (computed here for illustration). Chaining is multiplicative, so a rise of 1.5% in the link is worth 1.8 points at a level of 120.",
        "CPI links multiplied without dividing by 100. Each link is on a base of 100. Multiplying 120.0 by 99.6 and 101.5 without the two divisions gives 1,213,128 in place of 121.3128 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "I_t = I_Dec * L_Jan / 100 * L_t / 100" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CPI-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "chain-linked-consumer-price-index-from-the-december-level-and-two-annual-links" .

hemo:5000716 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Average annual inflation rate between two consumer price index values"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CPI-004" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000159 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004516 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003219,
        hemo:6003220,
        hemo:6003221,
        hemo:6003222 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003219,
        hemo:6003220,
        hemo:6003221 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003222 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004513,
        hemo:7004514,
        hemo:7004515 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004517,
        hemo:7004518,
        hemo:7004519,
        hemo:7004520,
        hemo:7004521 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Turns the ratio of a later index value I_2 to an earlier value I_1, n months apart, into the constant annual rate that compounds to the same change, the equation section 11.3 of the ONS technical manual gives for an annual average inflation rate over any period other than a year. With n = 12 it is the change on the figure 12 months earlier, the annual inflation rate of section 11.2. The ratio I_2 / I_1 is the I_b / I_a of HE-FM-COST-002, which uprates a cost and is not repeated here. The financial-year averages that the article compares are built in HE-CF-CPI-002." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Average annual inflation rate between two consumer price index values" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Office for National Statistics. CPI index 00, all items, 2015=100 (series D7BT, dataset MM23). Newport, Office for National Statistics, release of 16 September 2026. Monthly values used here are 126.4 for January 2023, 132.2 for December 2023 and 131.5 for January 2024, with the April 2019 to March 2020 and April 2023 to March 2024 values used in HE-CF-CPI-002. https://www.ons.gov.uk/economy/inflationandpriceindices/timeseries/d7bt/mm23",
        "Office for National Statistics. Consumer Price Inflation (includes all 3 indices, CPIH, CPI and RPI) QMI. Newport, Office for National Statistics, last revised 25 March 2026. Coherence section, which states that the CPIH, CPI and RPI all measure the average change in the prices of consumer goods and services purchased in the UK but differ in both coverage and methodology. https://www.ons.gov.uk/economy/inflationandpriceindices/methodologies/consumerpriceinflationincludesall3indicescpihcpiandrpiqmi",
        "Office for National Statistics. Consumer Prices Indices Technical Manual, 2019. Newport, Office for National Statistics, 2019, page last revised 26 March 2025. Section 11.3, annual and quarterly averages, which defines the annual average as the arithmetic mean of the 12 monthly values, gives the annual average inflation rate over any period other than a year as (I_2 / I_1) to the power 12/n minus 1, with I_2 the index in the later month or year, I_1 the index in the earlier month or year and n the number of months in the period, and warns that this may produce misleading results for one or two months of change. The replacement ONS guidance article on how consumer prices outputs are published (last revised 28 May 2026, section 4, under annual and quarterly averages) gives the same definition, equation and warning. Section 11.2 reports the CPIH change of 4.1% between April 2015 (99.9) and August 2017 (104.0) and the annual inflation rate of 1.8% for January 2019 (106.4 against 104.5 in January 2018). https://www.ons.gov.uk/economy/inflationandpriceindices/methodologies/consumerpricesindicestechnicalmanual2019" ;
    hemo:9000011 "One month of CPI change annualised. D7BT fell from 132.2 in December 2023 to 131.5 in January 2024. Annualising that one month gives minus 6.2% a year, while the 12-month change to January 2024 was 4.0% (131.5 against 126.4), both computed here from the published rounded indices. Section 11.3 of the manual warns that one or two months of change may mislead.",
        "Total CPI change divided by the number of years. Dividing the 21.65% rise between the two financial-year averages by 4 gives 5.41% a year, against a compound average of 5.02% (computed here for illustration). Compounding the simple rate over four years gives a ratio of 1.2347 in place of 1.2165.",
        "Years entered in place of months in the average CPI rate. Entering n = 4 for four years in place of 48 months makes the exponent 3 and gives 80.0% a year for the financial-year example in place of 5.02% (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "g = (I_2 / I_1)^(12 / n) - 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CPI-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "average-annual-inflation-rate-between-two-consumer-price-index-values" .

hemo:5000717 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Funded share of a league-table programme from the budget left after the programmes ranked above it"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CELT-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000160 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004525 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003223,
        hemo:6003224,
        hemo:6003225,
        hemo:6003226,
        hemo:6003227,
        hemo:6003228,
        hemo:6003229 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003226,
        hemo:6003227,
        hemo:6003228,
        hemo:6003229 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003224,
        hemo:6003225 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004522,
        hemo:7004523,
        hemo:7004524 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004526,
        hemo:7004527,
        hemo:7004528,
        hemo:7004529,
        hemo:7004530 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Funds programme i, the i-th in ascending order of ICER, in full when the budget left after the programmes ranked above it covers its incremental cost, in part when it covers some of that cost, and not at all when nothing is left. The share x_i is the budget left, B less the cumulative cost S_prev of the programmes above, divided by Delta_C_i and held at least 0 and at most 1, and the health it buys is x_i times Delta_E_i. Applied down the table this is the league-table rule of Marseille and colleagues, which Sacristán and colleagues call the fixed budget rule and Dakin and Tsiachristas identify as the greedy algorithm for the knapsack problem, with the marginal programme part-funded as in the article's worked example. S_i carries the cumulative cost to the next row, and HE-CF-CELT-001 fills the whole table." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Funded share of a league-table programme from the budget left after the programmes ranked above it" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Dakin H, Tsiachristas A. Rationing in an era of multiple tight constraints: is cost-utility analysis still fit for purpose? Applied Health Economics and Health Policy. 2024;22(3):315-329. Section 2.2, headed Cost-effectiveness league tables, a greedy algorithm, which states that given one constraint the league table ranks proposed and current interventions by ICER and adopts interventions from the lowest ICER upwards until the budget is exhausted, equivalent to the greedy algorithm for the knapsack problem, which sorts items by the reciprocal of the ICER. Section 2.3 describes the alternative of Birch and Gafni, which adopts a proposed intervention only if current interventions can be discontinued to free enough funds to cover its cost while generating fewer QALYs than it does. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40258-023-00858-w",
        "Marseille E, Larson B, Kazi DS, Kahn JG, Rosen S. Thresholds for the cost-effectiveness of interventions: alternative approaches. Bulletin of the World Health Organization. 2015;93(2):118-124. Section League tables, which states that if all of the interventions considered are at least somewhat scalable they can be ranked into a league table by their ICERs, and that the approach rests on the principle that for any budget health outcomes are maximised if selection begins with the option with the lowest ICER and moves down the list, to successively higher ratios, until the budget is exhausted. Table 1, the WHO-CHOICE malaria table, compares each option after the first with the next cheapest intervention. https://doi.org/10.2471/BLT.14.138206" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Counting a programme's own cost in the cumulative cost above it. Using the cumulative cost through each programme in place of the cost above it moves every row's budget test down one programme. In the article's table C then receives 0.6 of its cost and D nothing, so GBP 650,000 is spent, GBP 250,000 is left over and the budget buys 82 QALYs in place of 97.5 (computed here for illustration).",
        "Entering a programme at its ratio against no treatment when it would replace a funded service. The article's programme N costs GBP 700,000 and gives 50 QALYs, GBP 14,000 per QALY against no treatment, which places it between C and D and ahead of D in the fill. It would replace the funded service M (GBP 400,000, 40 QALYs), so its incremental ratio from HE-FM-ICER-001 is GBP 30,000 per QALY and it belongs below D. Switching spends GBP 300,000 for 10 QALYs, while the same sum on D buys 15, so the error loses 5 QALYs, the incremental net health benefit of HE-FM-CET-002 at the GBP 20,000 critical ratio. The test of Birch and Gafni, as Dakin and Tsiachristas describe it, also rejects the switch, since freeing GBP 300,000 from the funded programmes gives up at least 19.5 QALYs, the funded part of D and GBP 150,000 of C, against the 10 gained (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "x_i = min(1, max(0, (B - S_prev) / Delta_C_i)); h_i = x_i * Delta_E_i; S_i = S_prev + Delta_C_i" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CELT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "funded-share-of-a-league-table-programme-from-the-budget-left-after-the-programmes-ranked-above-it" .

hemo:5000718 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Critical ratio of the fixed budget rule as the highest ICER among four funded programmes"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CELT-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000160 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004533 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003230,
        hemo:6003231,
        hemo:6003232,
        hemo:6003233,
        hemo:6003234,
        hemo:6003235,
        hemo:6003236,
        hemo:6003237,
        hemo:6003238 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003231,
        hemo:6003232,
        hemo:6003233,
        hemo:6003234,
        hemo:6003235,
        hemo:6003236,
        hemo:6003237,
        hemo:6003238 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003230 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004531,
        hemo:7004532 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004534,
        hemo:7004535,
        hemo:7004536,
        hemo:7004537 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Reads the critical ratio lambda_c, which Sacristán and colleagues define as the ICER of the least cost-effective intervention adopted, from four programmes in ascending ICER order. Each f_i is 1 when programme i receives a share above zero in HE-FM-CELT-001, in full or in part, and 0 otherwise, and each r_i is that programme's ICER from HE-FM-ICER-001, so the largest product is the ratio of the last programme funded. Sacristán and colleagues describe this ratio as the cost-effectiveness threshold that represents the opportunity cost of funding new programmes. While the marginal programme still has an unfunded part, one more unit of budget buys 1 divided by lambda_c units of health, the shadow price of the budget in the sense Dakin and Tsiachristas give, and lambda_c is then the threshold k of HE-FM-CET-001 for a small change in budget (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Critical ratio of the fixed budget rule as the highest ICER among four funded programmes" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Dakin H, Tsiachristas A. Rationing in an era of multiple tight constraints: is cost-utility analysis still fit for purpose? Applied Health Economics and Health Policy. 2024;22(3):315-329. Section 2.1, headed Constrained optimisation, which defines shadow prices as the change in outcome from a one unit increase in the constraint, and section 2.2 on the league table, which sorts items by the reciprocal of the ICER. The equality of the shadow price of the budget and the reciprocal of the critical ratio is derived here, not stated in the paper. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40258-023-00858-w",
        "Marseille E, Larson B, Kazi DS, Kahn JG, Rosen S. Thresholds for the cost-effectiveness of interventions: alternative approaches. Bulletin of the World Health Organization. 2015;93(2):118-124. Section League tables, which states that since the length of the list of interventions deemed cost effective varies according to the budget, league tables combine considerations of cost-effectiveness with affordability, and that the last, least cost-effective intervention in the table to be adopted is more likely to approximate society's willingness to pay for health benefits than the open-ended set of commitments implied by global thresholds. https://doi.org/10.2471/BLT.14.138206",
        "Sacristán JA, Abellán-Perpiñán JM, Dilla T, Soto J, Oliva J. Some reflections on the use of inappropriate comparators in CEA. Cost Effectiveness and Resource Allocation. 2020;18:29. Introduction, which states that under the fixed budget rule, or league table approach, interventions are ranked in increasing order of ICER and successively included until the budget is exhausted, and that the ICER of the least cost-effective intervention adopted indicates the critical ratio, a term they attribute to Weinstein and Zeckhauser 1973, or the cost-effectiveness threshold representing the opportunity cost of funding new programmes. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12962-020-00226-8" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Treating the critical ratio as fixed when the budget changes. In the article's table the critical ratio is GBP 12,500 per QALY at a budget of GBP 700,000 and GBP 20,000 at GBP 900,000. A ratio read at one budget and applied as a threshold after the budget changes adopts or rejects the wrong programmes, so it is recomputed with the fill at each budget.",
        "Using one critical ratio for budget cuts and increases at a row boundary. With a budget of GBP 750,000 that exactly exhausts C, a cut of GBP 50,000 loses 4 QALYs at C's GBP 12,500 per QALY, while an increase of GBP 50,000 gains only 2.5 QALYs at D's GBP 20,000 (computed here for illustration). At a row boundary the health lost from a cut and the health gained from an increase are read from the two neighbouring rows." ;
    hemo:9000014 "lambda_c = max(max(max(f_1 * r_1, f_2 * r_2), f_3 * r_3), f_4 * r_4)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CELT-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "critical-ratio-of-the-fixed-budget-rule-as-the-highest-icer-among-four-funded-programmes" .

hemo:5000719 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "League-table fill of three indivisible programmes funded whole or not at all"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CELT-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000160 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004540 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003239,
        hemo:6003240,
        hemo:6003241,
        hemo:6003242,
        hemo:6003243,
        hemo:6003244,
        hemo:6003245,
        hemo:6003246,
        hemo:6003247,
        hemo:6003248,
        hemo:6003249,
        hemo:6003250 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003244,
        hemo:6003245,
        hemo:6003246,
        hemo:6003247,
        hemo:6003248,
        hemo:6003249,
        hemo:6003250 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003242,
        hemo:6003243 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004538,
        hemo:7004539 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004541,
        hemo:7004542,
        hemo:7004543,
        hemo:7004544,
        hemo:7004545 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Funds whole programmes in ascending order of ICER, each only if its full incremental cost fits in the budget left after the programmes already funded, and otherwise passes over it to the next. Each y_i is 1 when programme i is funded and 0 otherwise, H_w is the health bought and U_w the budget left unspent. Dakin and Tsiachristas frame the allocation of healthcare resources as a 0-1 knapsack problem, in which each item is packed whole or not at all, and the league table as the greedy algorithm for it, and Stinnett and Paltiel show that mathematical programming handles partial and complete indivisibility. Following the order need not give the most health that whole programmes can buy (HE-ER-CELT-005). The best choice of whole programmes belongs to the Integer Programming article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "League-table fill of three indivisible programmes funded whole or not at all" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Dakin H, Tsiachristas A. Rationing in an era of multiple tight constraints: is cost-utility analysis still fit for purpose? Applied Health Economics and Health Policy. 2024;22(3):315-329. Section 2.1, headed Constrained optimisation, which frames the allocation of healthcare resources as a 0-1 knapsack problem of choosing items to maximise value without exceeding a weight constraint or packing more than one of each item, and notes that constrained optimisation may fail to identify the best solution when the constraints are flexible and programmes cannot be partially adopted. Section 2.2 describes the league table as the greedy algorithm for the knapsack problem. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40258-023-00858-w",
        "Stinnett AA, Paltiel AD. Mathematical programming for the efficient allocation of health care resources. Journal of Health Economics. 1996;15(5):641-653. Abstract, which states that previous methods for allocating health care resources subject to a budget constraint relied on unnecessarily restrictive assumptions, and that a general mathematical programming framework can accommodate returns to scale, partial and complete indivisibility and programme interdependence. https://doi.org/10.1016/s0167-6296(96)00493-6" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Part-funding an indivisible programme in the league table. Applying the divisible share of HE-FM-CELT-001 to step 3 of the article funds 0.8 of Q and reports 92 QALYs, the 60 of P plus 0.8 of Q's 40, a result that cannot be delivered when Q must be funded whole (computed here for illustration).",
        "Reading the whole-programme fill as the best choice of whole programmes. In step 3 of the article the fill buys 60 QALYs, while funding Q and R in place of P spends the same GBP 1,000,000 for 80 QALYs, 20 more. Following the order does not guarantee the best choice of whole programmes, the case integer programming is built for, and the mathematical programming framework of Stinnett and Paltiel handles complete indivisibility directly." ;
    hemo:9000014 "y_1 = (Delta_C_1 <= B); y_2 = (y_1 * Delta_C_1 + Delta_C_2 <= B); y_3 = (y_1 * Delta_C_1 + y_2 * Delta_C_2 + Delta_C_3 <= B); H_w = y_1 * Delta_E_1 + y_2 * Delta_E_2 + y_3 * Delta_E_3; U_w = B - (y_1 * Delta_C_1 + y_2 * Delta_C_2 + y_3 * Delta_C_3)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CELT-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "league-table-fill-of-three-indivisible-programmes-funded-whole-or-not-at-all" .

hemo:5000721 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
