        hemo:6003278,
        hemo:6003279,
        hemo:6003280,
        hemo:6003281,
        hemo:6003282,
        hemo:6003283,
        hemo:6003284,
        hemo:6003285 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003267 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004554,
        hemo:7004555 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004557,
        hemo:7004558,
        hemo:7004559,
        hemo:7004560,
        hemo:7004561 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Scores the same move between two EQ-5D-3L states under two national value sets of the constant and N3 form of HE-FM-EQ3L-001 and finds where the difference between the two gains comes from. Under each set the gain is the constant times the change in the any-problem indicator, plus the decrement relieved on each of the five dimensions, plus the N3 decrement times the change in the N3 indicator; this is the difference of two HE-FM-EQ3L-001 scores, written term by term (derived). Subtracting the two gains shows how much of the gap each dimension and each profile-wide term carries. When neither state is full health the constant cancels within each set, as HE-AS-EQ3L-009 notes, so the gap rests on the dimension weights and the N3 term alone." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cross-set gap in a utility gain split into constant, dimension and N3 terms" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Janssen B, Szende A, Ramos-Goñi JM. Data and methods. In Szende A, Janssen B, Cabases J, editors. Self-reported population health. An international perspective based on EQ-5D. Dordrecht, Springer, 2014, chapter 2. Table 2.2, which tabulates the time trade-off value sets of 13 countries as a constant, level 2 and level 3 dummies for each dimension and, for some sets, N3 or other terms; the Zimbabwe row (Jelsma 2003, random effects) prints the constant minus 0.100 and the same ten level coefficients as Jelsma and colleagues' Table 5, with no N3 entry. Open access. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-007-7596-1_2",
        "Jelsma J, Hansen K, De Weerdt W, De Cock P, Kind P. How do Zimbabweans value health states? Population Health Metrics. 2003;1(1):11. Results, Valuations subsection, Table 5 (main effects of the residual maximum likelihood linear mixed model on 12,663 observations), which gives the constant 0.900 and the level 2 and level 3 effects for mobility (minus 0.056, minus 0.204), self-care (minus 0.092, minus 0.231), usual activities (minus 0.043, minus 0.135), pain or discomfort (minus 0.067, minus 0.302) and anxiety or depression (minus 0.046, minus 0.173), the worked value of 0.315 for state 22331, 0.90 less 0.056, 0.092, 0.135 and 0.302, the text on the discarded N3 model and Table 6 with a predicted mean of 0.90 for 11111. Open access. https://doi.org/10.1186/1478-7954-1-11",
        "Rand-Hendriksen K, Augestad LA, Dahl FA. A critical re-evaluation of the regression model specification in the US D1 EQ-5D value function. Population Health Metrics. 2012;10(1):2. Methods, subsection Handling the constant and state 11111, which states that state 11111 was given a fixed value of 1 and death a fixed value of 0, and that the authors treat the value of 11111 as axiomatic and outside the scope of the tariff prediction, with the intercept a constant for the 242 other states. The paper concerns the US D1 tariff; applying the same fixed value to the Zimbabwe set is this package's convention, not a statement of Jelsma and colleagues. Open access. https://doi.org/10.1186/1478-7954-10-2" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Charging the Zimbabwe constant to full health in a gain to 11111. Jelsma and colleagues' Table 6 lists a predicted value of 0.90 for 11111, which is their constant 0.900 with no level effect subtracted (derived). Using that value for the end state of a return to full health out of 21121 gives a gain of 0.123 in place of 0.223 under the Zimbabwe set and inflates the UK and Zimbabwe gap to 0.150 in place of 0.050 (computed here). Fixing 11111 at 1, through I = 0, keeps every set on the scale anchored at full health.",
        "Reading a cross-set gain gap as a difference in dimension weights. Two sets can agree closely on every dimension and still give very different gains because of a profile-wide term. For the move out of 21232 into 11121 the four dimension differences between the UK and Zimbabwe sets add up to only 0.059, while the UK N3 decrement adds 0.269, so 82% of the gap of 0.328 comes from a term the Zimbabwe set does not have (computed here). Splitting the gap by term shows this before the gap is put down to national preferences about pain or mobility." ;
    hemo:9000014 "g_A = c_A * (I_0 - I_1) + v_A_MO + v_A_SC + v_A_UA + v_A_PD + v_A_AD + gamma_A * (N3_0 - N3_1); g_B = c_B * (I_0 - I_1) + v_B_MO + v_B_SC + v_B_UA + v_B_PD + v_B_AD + gamma_B * (N3_0 - N3_1); dg = g_A - g_B" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CSVS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cross-set-gap-in-a-utility-gain-split-into-constant-dimension-and-n3-terms" .

hemo:5000725 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-parameter Cholesky step for a correlated pair of normal PSA draws"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-PSA-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000163 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004590 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003307,
        hemo:6003308,
        hemo:6003309,
        hemo:6003310,
        hemo:6003311,
        hemo:6003312,
        hemo:6003313,
        hemo:6003314,
        hemo:6003315,
        hemo:6003316,
        hemo:6003317 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003308,
        hemo:6003309,
        hemo:6003312,
        hemo:6003313,
        hemo:6003314,
        hemo:6003316,
        hemo:6003317 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003311,
        hemo:6003315 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004587,
        hemo:7004588,
        hemo:7004589 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004591,
        hemo:7004592,
        hemo:7004593,
        hemo:7004594,
        hemo:7004595,
        hemo:7004596 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Draws two parameters that were estimated together, such as the log of a baseline event rate and a log hazard ratio from one survival regression, so that their correlation rho is kept. Two independent standard normal values z_1 and z_2 are combined through the lower triangular Cholesky factor of the 2 by 2 covariance matrix, whose entries are s_1, L_21 and L_22. The first parameter uses z_1 alone and the second takes a share of z_1 through L_21, which carries the correlation, and the rest through L_22. By algebra (derived here from the factor) the drawn pair has standard deviations s_1 and s_2 and covariance rho s_1 s_2, because s_1 times L_21 equals rho s_1 s_2 and L_21 squared plus L_22 squared equals s_2 squared. With rho equal to zero the step reduces to two independent normal draws." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-parameter Cholesky step for a correlated pair of normal PSA draws" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Briggs AH, Weinstein MC, Fenwick EA, Karnon J, Sculpher MJ, Paltiel AD. Model parameter estimation and uncertainty analysis, a report of the ISPOR-SMDM Modeling Good Research Practices Task Force Working Group-6. Medical Decision Making. 2012;32(5):722-732. Section Multivariate estimation and correlation, which states that the covariance matrix defines the uncertainty in regression coefficients and that multivariate normality is appropriate for the regression's linear predictor, and Best Practice VI-10, which states that correlation among parameters should be considered and that jointly estimated parameters, such as those from a regression analysis, have direct evidence on correlation that should be reflected in the analysis. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X12458348",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 13 October 2026. Section 4.7.19, which asks for evidence about the extent of correlation between individual parameters to be considered and reflected in the probabilistic analysis and for assumptions made about the correlations to be presented clearly. https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation",
        "Neine M, Curran D. An algorithm to generate correlated input-parameters to be used in probabilistic sensitivity analyses. Journal of Market Access & Health Policy. 2020;9(1):1857052. Methods, sections Correlation of parameters and Sampling algorithm, which give a correlation matrix with all correlations 0.5 for three variables and its Cholesky decomposition matrix, with rows (1, 0.5, 0.5), (0, 0.866, 0.2887) and (0, 0, 0.8165), state that multiplying independent variables by this matrix gives correlated variables with that correlation matrix, and describe simulating uncorrelated standard normal variables, multiplying them by the Cholesky matrix and transforming the result through the normal distribution function and the inverse beta, gamma or log-normal distribution function to give correlated non-normal PSA inputs. https://doi.org/10.1080/20016689.2020.1857052",
        "Owen AB. Monte Carlo theory, methods and examples. Stanford University, 2013. Chapter 5, Random vectors and objects. Section 5.2, Multivariate normal and t, which on page 8 samples X equal to mu plus C Z with Z standard normal and C C transposed equal to Sigma (equation 5.3) and lists the conditional normal distribution of one block given the other (property MVN-4), and on page 9 states that when Sigma is positive definite C can be the lower triangular L with L L transposed equal to Sigma, the Cholesky decomposition, that some implementations return the upper triangular R equal to L transposed and failing to transpose it would be an error that could be hard to detect from simulation output, and that it is good practice to compute Sigma minus C C transposed before using C in a simulation. https://artowen.su.domains/mc/Ch-randvectors.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Drawing jointly estimated parameters as if independent. Setting rho to zero for the illustrative log rate and log hazard ratio gives the log of the treated rate, their sum, a variance of 0.05 instead of 0.03 (derived), a standard deviation about 29% too large. For the inputs of the computational function below the same omission moves the exact expected incremental net health benefit (derived from the log-normal mean of HE-FM-EV-004) from about minus 0.072 to about minus 0.047 QALYs per person, because the model is not linear in the parameters.",
        "Using an upper triangular Cholesky factor without transposing it. R's chol returns the upper triangular factor R with R transposed times R equal to the covariance matrix, and Owen warns that failing to transpose it is an error hard to detect from simulation output. For the illustrative inputs mu plus R z gives draws with standard deviations of about 0.1414 and 0.1732 instead of 0.10 and 0.20 and a correlation of about minus 0.71 instead of minus 0.5 (derived from R times R transposed). The factor used must be the lower triangular L with L times L transposed equal to the covariance matrix." ;
    hemo:9000014 "L_21 = rho * s_2; L_22 = s_2 * sqrt(1 - rho^2); x_1 = mu_1 + s_1 * z_1; x_2 = mu_2 + L_21 * z_1 + L_22 * z_2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-PSA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-parameter-cholesky-step-for-a-correlated-pair-of-normal-psa-draws" .

hemo:5000726 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected loss of each of two options from five PSA draws of incremental net health benefit"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-PSA-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000163 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004600 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003318,
        hemo:6003319,
        hemo:6003320,
        hemo:6003321,
        hemo:6003322,
        hemo:6003323,
        hemo:6003324,
        hemo:6003325 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003319,
        hemo:6003320,
        hemo:6003321,
        hemo:6003322,
        hemo:6003323 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003325 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004597,
        hemo:7004598,
        hemo:7004599 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004601,
        hemo:7004602,
        hemo:7004603,
        hemo:7004604,
        hemo:7004605,
        hemo:7004606 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "With a new option and a comparator whose net health benefit is set to zero, the loss from adopting the new option in a draw is the incremental net health benefit forgone when it is negative, and the loss from keeping the comparator is the gain forgone when it is positive. EL_new and EL_comp average these losses over all five equally likely draws, the two-option case of the expected loss of each strategy in equation 3 of Alarid-Escudero and colleagues, whose expected loss curves give the loss of every option as HE-FM-CEAC-002 gives the probability of every option. For the option chosen on current evidence the loss is the mean opportunity loss of HE-FM-EVPI-001, so the smaller of the two equals the per-person EVPI of HE-FM-EVPI-001 and HE-FM-EVPI-002, and the larger is the figure that HE-ER-EVPI-001 warns must not be reported as EVPI. Their difference D_EL equals the mean incremental net health benefit, because the positive part of a number less the positive part of its negative is the number itself (derived, the two-option case of equation 4 of Alarid-Escudero and colleagues), so the option with the smaller expected loss is the option with the higher expected net health benefit (HE-FM-ENB-002)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected loss of each of two options from five PSA draws of incremental net health benefit" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Alarid-Escudero F, Enns EA, Kuntz KM, Michaud TL, Jalal H. \"Time traveling is just too dangerous\" but some methods are worth revisiting, the advantages of expected loss curves over cost-effectiveness acceptability curves and frontier. Value in Health. 2019;22(5):611-618. Section Definition and mathematical notation of expected losses, equations 3 and 4, which define the expected loss of strategy d as the mean over the N PSA samples of the net benefit of the best strategy in each sample less that of d, and state that for a risk-neutral decision maker the optimal strategy, the one with the highest expected benefit, is the one with the lowest expected loss, and that the expected loss of the optimal strategy equals the EVPI. The two-option form with the comparator at zero follows by setting the best net benefit in a draw to the positive part of the incremental net benefit. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2019.02.008",
        "Steuten L, Thavorn K, Cipriano LE, Guertin JR, McQueen B, Schmidler G, Sullivan S, Wailoo A, Sculpher M. Methods to analyze and present uncertainty in economic evaluation to support HTA decisions. HEMA Report 2. Health Economics Methods Advisory, 2026. Section 6.4, Standard outputs and their limitations (page 52), which states that expected loss shows the magnitude of the potential loss associated with decision uncertainty and should accompany the CEAC, and in its illustrative example gives an expected loss of 0.14 QALYs per patient from choosing the new medicine against 0.24 from choosing standard care, with the difference equal to the expected incremental net health benefit of 0.10 QALYs, while the probability alone would point to the option with the lower expected net benefit. https://hemamethods.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/HEMA-Report-2_For-Publication_100726.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Averaging an expected loss over the losing draws only. Dividing the adoption losses in the article's example, 1.4 QALYs in total, by the 4 draws in which adoption loses gives 0.35 QALYs, the loss when wrong of HE-FM-EVPI-003, not the expected loss of 0.28, the slip that HE-ER-EVPI-005 describes for EVPI. An expected loss is averaged over all draws, including those with no loss.",
        "Choosing the option most likely to be cost-effective instead of the smaller expected loss. In the article's example usual care is the better option in four draws out of five, but keeping it has an expected loss of 0.48 QALYs per patient against 0.28 for adopting the new treatment. Choosing by the probability of 0.8 gives up 0.2 QALYs per patient of expected net health benefit, the HEMA point that the probability alone can point to the option with the lower expected net benefit, and the trap HE-ER-ENB-001 shows in money terms." ;
    hemo:9000014 "EL_new = (max(-INHB_1, 0) + max(-INHB_2, 0) + max(-INHB_3, 0) + max(-INHB_4, 0) + max(-INHB_5, 0)) / 5; EL_comp = (max(INHB_1, 0) + max(INHB_2, 0) + max(INHB_3, 0) + max(INHB_4, 0) + max(INHB_5, 0)) / 5; D_EL = EL_comp - EL_new" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-PSA-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-loss-of-each-of-two-options-from-five-psa-draws-of-incremental-net-health-benefit" .

hemo:5000727 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Patient time cost per visit and per year from travel, waiting and care hours"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-PTC-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000164 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004610 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003326,
        hemo:6003327,
        hemo:6003328,
        hemo:6003329,
        hemo:6003330,
        hemo:6003331,
        hemo:6003332,
        hemo:6003333 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003329,
        hemo:6003330,
        hemo:6003331,
        hemo:6003332,
        hemo:6003333 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003326 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004607,
        hemo:7004608,
        hemo:7004609 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004611,
        hemo:7004612,
        hemo:7004613,
        hemo:7004614,
        hemo:7004615 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Adds the hours that one use of a service takes in round-trip travel, waiting and care, values them at the hourly value of time to give a time cost per visit, and multiplies by the number of uses in the period. With n counted over a year the result is the time cost per patient-year. The article's formula sums the same product over K service types; this formula is the term for one service type, and HE-CF-PTC-001 adds the terms for several types with published time estimates. It is HE-FM-COST-001 with a single resource, patient hours, whose unit cost is w." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Patient time cost per visit and per year from travel, waiting and care hours" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Russell LB. Completing costs: patients' time. Medical Care. 2009;47(7 Suppl 1):S89-S93. Abstract, which states that good costing practice measures all important uses of a resource and values it consistently with other resources, and that counts of formal medical services, already measured in most studies, can serve as the starting point for valuing patients' time even when other uses of time cannot be measured. https://doi.org/10.1097/MLR.0b013e31819bc077",
        "Yabroff KR, Davis WW, Lamont EB, Fahey A, Topor M, Brown ML, Warren JL. Patient time costs associated with cancer care. Journal of the National Cancer Institute. 2007;99(1):14-23. Abstract, which states that for each service category time estimates were combined with service frequency and an hourly value of patient time, and that net time costs for the initial phase of care were applied to national numbers of new cancers, giving 2.3 billion dollars in 2005; that national step is the bottom-up scaling of HE-FM-COI-001. https://doi.org/10.1093/jnci/djk001",
        "Yabroff KR, Guy GP Jr, Ekwueme DU, McNeel T, Rozjabek HM, Dowling E, Li C, Virgo KS. Annual patient time costs associated with medical care among cancer survivors in the United States. Medical Care. 2014;52(7):594-601. Methods, Measures subsection, which estimates round-trip travel, waiting and care time separately for each service category, adds travel time to all service time estimates and waiting time to office-based or hospital outpatient visits, chemotherapy and radiation therapy; the Analyses subsection, which applies time estimates for each type of service, summarises patient time and applies the hourly value of patient time; and the Discussion, which states that preparation time at home, recovery time at home and time on health insurance issues were not included and may be best estimated from survey or micro-costing and time-and-motion techniques. https://doi.org/10.1097/MLR.0000000000000151" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Patient time cost counting only the time in care. Counting only the chair or consultation time leaves out travel and waiting. For the article's infusion the hours fall to 78 against 117 and the time cost at GBP 15 an hour to GBP 1,170 against GBP 1,755, a third lower, and for the oral treatment, whose 0.5 hour consultation is a quarter of a 2.0 hour visit, the cost falls by three quarters, to GBP 90 (computed here for illustration).",
        "Same hours counted as patient time and as productivity cost. When the hours in care are taken from paid work and the analysis also values them as absence with HE-FM-ABSN-002, the same hour is counted twice. If all 117 infusion hours came out of paid work and were valued at GBP 15 under both headings, the time would add GBP 3,510 to costs in place of GBP 1,755 (computed here for illustration). The article treats this as a boundary rule, and each hour is counted under one heading only.",
        "Visit times in minutes entered as hours in the patient time cost. Entering 60, 30 and 180 minutes as hours makes each infusion visit 270 hours and the yearly time cost GBP 105,300 in place of GBP 1,755, 60 times too large (computed here for illustration). HE-VT-PTC-002 catches this." ;
    hemo:9000014 "h_v = t_trav + t_wait + t_care; c_v = w * h_v; C_T = n * c_v" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-PTC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "patient-time-cost-per-visit-and-per-year-from-travel-waiting-and-care-hours" .

hemo:5000730 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "EQ-5D-5L dimension decrement at the reported level from four level coefficients"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EQ5L-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000165 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004635 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003347,
        hemo:6003348,
        hemo:6003349,
        hemo:6003350,
        hemo:6003351,
        hemo:6003352 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003348,
        hemo:6003349,
        hemo:6003350,
        hemo:6003351,
        hemo:6003352 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003347 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004632,
        hemo:7004633,
        hemo:7004634 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004636,
        hemo:7004637,
        hemo:7004638,
        hemo:7004639,
        hemo:7004640 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Returns the decrement that a five-level value set attaches to the level reported on one dimension. Level 1 carries no decrement, and each of levels 2 to 5 has its own coefficient, which is the full decrement for that level and not a step to be added to the lower ones. The level indicators are written with min and max so that the site calculator picks exactly one coefficient for a whole-number level. Five such lookups, one per dimension, give the decrements beta_d of the article's additive form U(s) = 1 minus their sum, scored for a whole profile in HE-CF-EQ5L-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EQ-5D-5L dimension decrement at the reported level from four level coefficients" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Devlin NJ, Shah KK, Feng Y, Mulhern B, van Hout B. Valuing health-related quality of life: an EQ-5D-5L value set for England. Health Economics. 2018;27(1):7-22. Section 2.5, methods of analysis, which describes the least restrictive model as having a parameter for each decrement or step away from no problems (level 1) on each dimension, 20 parameters (4 levels times 5 dimensions), notes that alternative models were tested for interaction effects, states that in the England model the level 2 parameters were estimated first and the parameters for worse levels by adding quadratic terms that can only be non-negative, and gives unable as the level 5 label for mobility, self-care and usual activities and extreme for pain or discomfort and anxiety or depression; section 3.4 and Table 2, which select the 20-parameter model and print one decrement for each level of each dimension. https://doi.org/10.1002/hec.3564" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Adding the lower EQ-5D-5L level decrements to the reported one. Each coefficient in a five-level value set table is the whole decrement for its level. Adding the England mobility coefficients for levels 2, 3 and 4 for a severe mobility problem gives 0.341 instead of 0.207, and the England worked example for 23245 subtracts a single coefficient per dimension.",
        "Reading the EQ-5D-5L level one place too high. Treating the level as a position in the list of four coefficients, without the zero for level 1, picks the next coefficient up. For England mobility a severe problem then costs 0.274 instead of 0.207, a slight problem 0.076 instead of 0.058, and level 5 has no coefficient left, so CHOOSE returns an error value." ;
    hemo:9000014 "beta_d = b_2 * (min(l_d - 1, 1) - min(max(l_d - 2, 0), 1)) + b_3 * (min(max(l_d - 2, 0), 1) - min(max(l_d - 3, 0), 1)) + b_4 * (min(max(l_d - 3, 0), 1) - max(l_d - 4, 0)) + b_5 * max(l_d - 4, 0)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EQ5L-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "eq-5d-5l-dimension-decrement-at-the-reported-level-from-four-level-coefficients" .

hemo:5000731 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "England EQ-5D-5L value set of Devlin and colleagues from five reported levels"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EQ5L-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000165 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004645 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003353,
        hemo:6003354,
        hemo:6003355,
        hemo:6003356,
        hemo:6003357,
        hemo:6003358 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003354,
        hemo:6003355,
        hemo:6003356,
        hemo:6003357,
        hemo:6003358 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003353 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004641,
        hemo:7004642,
        hemo:7004643,
        hemo:7004644 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004646,
        hemo:7004647,
        hemo:7004648,
        hemo:7004649,
        hemo:7004650,
        hemo:7004651,
        hemo:7004652 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Scores a profile with the EQ-5D-5L value set for England published by Devlin and colleagues in 2018, 1 minus the Table 2 decrement for the level reported on each dimension, with no separate constant decrement. Each bracket is the lookup of HE-FM-EQ5L-001 with that dimension's England coefficients. The lowest value is minus 0.285 for 55555, the highest below full health is 0.950, and 159 of the 3,125 states score below zero. NICE does not use this set for reference-case analyses, and the set NICE now names is not formalised here." ;
    hemo:9000009 "England EQ-5D-5L value set of Devlin and colleagues from five reported levels" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Devlin NJ, Shah KK, Feng Y, Mulhern B, van Hout B. Valuing health-related quality of life: an EQ-5D-5L value set for England. Health Economics. 2018;27(1):7-22. Section 3.5 and Table 3, which list 159 of 3,125 states worse than dead in the five-level set against 833 of 3,125 under the crosswalk and 84 of 243 under the three-level set, and the values 0.922 for 11112, 0.937 for 11121, 0.950 for 11211, 0.950 for 12111 and 0.942 for 21111 in the five-level set, with 0.877 for 21111 under the crosswalk. https://doi.org/10.1002/hec.3564",
        "Devlin NJ, Shah KK, Feng Y, Mulhern B, van Hout B. Valuing health-related quality of life: an EQ-5D-5L value set for England. Health Economics. 2018;27(1):7-22. Table 2, an EQ-5D-5L value set for England, which prints the constant 1.000 and the decrements for slight, moderate, severe and unable or extreme problems on each dimension and works the value of state 23245 as 1 minus (0.058 + 0.080 + 0.050 + 0.276 + 0.289), 0.247; section 3.4, which states that the minimum value is minus 0.285 for 55555 and that 5.1% of the 3,125 states are valued as worse than dead. Open access. https://doi.org/10.1002/hec.3564",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. Interim methods statement: implementing the EQ-5D-5L value set (PMG51). Published 27 August 2026. Sections 1.6 and 1.7, which state that five-level data previously had to be mapped to 3L values because no suitable UK 5L value set was available, that a UK 5L value set has been published (Rowen et al. 2026), and that the UK 5L value set should be used to derive all utility values for adults in reference-case analyses; section 1.13, that 3L-based utility values should not be included in sensitivity or scenario analyses; section 1.16, that the position statement on the England value set no longer applies. https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg51/chapter/statement-on-using-the-eq-5d-5l-value-set",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 13 October 2026. Section 4.3.16, which states that NICE does not recommend using the EQ-5D-5L value set for England published by Devlin et al. (2018), that the 3L value set should be used for reference-case analyses with 5L descriptive data mapped onto it, and that the Decision Support Unit mapping function of Hernández Alava et al. 2017 should be used. The manual's overview page carries a notice that the interim methods statement takes precedence until the manual is updated and lists section 4.3.16 among those to be revised. https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2",
        "van Hout B, Janssen MF, Feng YS, Kohlmann T, Busschbach J, Golicki D, Lloyd A, Scalone L, Kind P, Pickard AS. Interim scoring for the EQ-5D-5L: mapping the EQ-5D-5L to EQ-5D-3L value sets. Value in Health. 2012;15(5):708-715. Abstract, which reports that five-level value sets based on directly elicited preferences were not yet available, that values for the five-level version were generated by a mapping (crosswalk) approach from data on 3,691 respondents who completed both versions, with a nonparametric model preferred, and that the range of crosswalk values is restricted to the range of the three-level value set used. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2012.02.008",
        "van Hout B, Mulhern B, Feng Y, Shah K, Devlin N. The EQ-5D-5L value set for England: response to the \"quality assurance\". Value in Health. 2020;23(5):649-655. Abstract, which describes a point-by-point response to the issues raised by the authors of the quality assurance paper, states that many of those points are misleading, suggest misunderstandings or are irrelevant, and concludes that the modelling approaches used address the characteristics of the data and give a reasonable representation of the average stated preferences of the general public in England. Abstract only. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2019.10.013" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Reading an EQ-5D-5L profile in reverse dimension order. The five digits run mobility, self-care, usual activities, pain or discomfort and anxiety or depression. Reading 23245 backwards scores 54232, 0.350 under the England set instead of 0.247, because the dimensions carry different decrements.",
        "Taking 0.950 as the England EQ-5D-5L value of state 21111. The abstract of Devlin and colleagues gives 0.950 for states 11211 and 21111, but Table 3 of the same paper lists 0.942 for 21111, which follows from the Table 2 decrement of 0.058 for slight mobility problems, and 0.950 for 11211 and 12111. Using 0.950 for 21111 overstates it by 0.008, and the other single slight-problem states are lower still, 0.937 for 11121 and 0.922 for 11112.",
        "Treating crosswalk values as the England EQ-5D-5L value set. The crosswalk of van Hout and colleagues 2012 maps five-level responses onto three-level values and is not a value set elicited for five-level states. Table 3 of Devlin and colleagues shows it giving 0.877 for 21111 against 0.942 in the England set, and 833 of the 3,125 states, 26.7%, below zero against 159, 5.1%, so mixing the two within one analysis changes utilities by far more than rounding." ;
    hemo:9000014 "U = 1 - (0.058 * (min(l_MO - 1, 1) - min(max(l_MO - 2, 0), 1)) + 0.076 * (min(max(l_MO - 2, 0), 1) - min(max(l_MO - 3, 0), 1)) + 0.207 * (min(max(l_MO - 3, 0), 1) - max(l_MO - 4, 0)) + 0.274 * max(l_MO - 4, 0)) - (0.050 * (min(l_SC - 1, 1) - min(max(l_SC - 2, 0), 1)) + 0.080 * (min(max(l_SC - 2, 0), 1) - min(max(l_SC - 3, 0), 1)) + 0.164 * (min(max(l_SC - 3, 0), 1) - max(l_SC - 4, 0)) + 0.203 * max(l_SC - 4, 0)) - (0.050 * (min(l_UA - 1, 1) - min(max(l_UA - 2, 0), 1)) + 0.063 * (min(max(l_UA - 2, 0), 1) - min(max(l_UA - 3, 0), 1)) + 0.162 * (min(max(l_UA - 3, 0), 1) - max(l_UA - 4, 0)) + 0.184 * max(l_UA - 4, 0)) - (0.063 * (min(l_PD - 1, 1) - min(max(l_PD - 2, 0), 1)) + 0.084 * (min(max(l_PD - 2, 0), 1) - min(max(l_PD - 3, 0), 1)) + 0.276 * (min(max(l_PD - 3, 0), 1) - max(l_PD - 4, 0)) + 0.335 * max(l_PD - 4, 0)) - (0.078 * (min(l_AD - 1, 1) - min(max(l_AD - 2, 0), 1)) + 0.104 * (min(max(l_AD - 2, 0), 1) - min(max(l_AD - 3, 0), 1)) + 0.285 * (min(max(l_AD - 3, 0), 1) - max(l_AD - 4, 0)) + 0.289 * max(l_AD - 4, 0))" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EQ5L-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "england-eq-5d-5l-value-set-of-devlin-and-colleagues-from-five-reported-levels" .

hemo:5000735 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Least-cost split of an outlay between medical care and own time for Cobb-Douglas gross investment"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-HPF-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000167 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004683 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003379,
        hemo:6003380,
        hemo:6003381,
        hemo:6003382,
        hemo:6003383,
        hemo:6003384,
        hemo:6003385,
        hemo:6003386,
        hemo:6003387,
        hemo:6003388,
        hemo:6003389,
        hemo:6003390 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003379,
        hemo:6003380,
        hemo:6003381,
        hemo:6003382,
        hemo:6003383,
        hemo:6003384 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003390 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004681,
        hemo:7004682 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004684,
        hemo:7004685,
        hemo:7004686,
        hemo:7004687,
        hemo:7004688 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Spends the share 1-K of the outlay on medical care and the share K on own time, the cost shares that Grossman gives for the Cobb-Douglas form of HE-FM-HPF-001, and returns the gross investment the outlay buys. The quantities follow from Grossman's least-cost condition (derived here), under which the ratio of the marginal product of care, (1-K) I / M, to that of time, K I / TH, equals the ratio of the price of care to the wage. The chain therefore also returns both marginal products and their ratio, so the condition can be checked against P / W. With the outlay fixed, a higher wage buys fewer hours and leaves the units of care unchanged, so the mix shifts towards care." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Least-cost split of an outlay between medical care and own time for Cobb-Douglas gross investment" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Grossman M. The Demand for Health: A Theoretical and Empirical Investigation. National Bureau of Economic Research, New York; 1972. Chapter 1, A Stock Approach to the Demand for Health, pages 1 to 10. Page 5, which states that equation (1-11) shows the condition for minimising the cost of producing a given quantity of gross investment, met when the change in gross investment from spending an additional dollar on medical care equals the change from spending an additional dollar on time, and that with a function homogeneous of degree one and prices independent of the level of the inputs average cost is constant and equal to marginal cost. https://www.nber.org/system/files/chapters/c3485/c3485.pdf",
        "Grossman M. The human capital model of the demand for health. NBER Working Paper 7078. National Bureau of Economic Research, Cambridge, MA; 1999. Published as The human capital model, in Culyer AJ, Newhouse JP, editors. Handbook of Health Economics. Vol 1A. Elsevier, Amsterdam; 2000. p. 347-408. Section 2.2, equation (2-10), which sets the marginal cost of gross investment equal to the price of medical care over the marginal product of medical care and to the wage over the marginal product of own time, and the text after it, which states that this is the condition for minimising the cost of producing a given quantity of gross investment; section 5.1, which states the least-cost equilibrium condition that the ratio of the marginal product of medical care to the marginal product of time must equal the ratio of the price of medical care to the wage rate, and gives 1-K as the share of medical care in the total cost of gross investment; section 3.2, which defines K as the fraction of the total cost accounted for by time. The quantities (1-K) C / P and K C / W are derived here from these statements and equation (5-3). https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w7078/w7078.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Equal quantities of care and time in place of the least-cost shares. Buying the same number of units of each input, 800 GBP over 50 GBP for 16 units of care and 16 hours in the article's example, gives 16 units of gross investment against 20, 20% less for the same outlay (computed here for illustration). The least-cost rule sets the spending shares to 1-K and K, not the quantities.",
        "Least-cost shares swapped when the time share is not one half. With K of 0.25 the least-cost mix spends three quarters of the outlay on care. Swapping the shares buys 5 units of care and 60 hours, which gives 9.31 units of gross investment against 16.12, 42% less (computed here for illustration). With the article's K of 0.5 the swap leaves the result unchanged, so HE-VT-HPF-004 tests the split at another K.",
        "Shortfall from a non-least-cost mix of care and time read as wasted inputs. The article's alternative mix of 15 units and 20 hours lies on the production function, so no input is wasted, yet it yields 17.32 units against 20 for the same 800 GBP. In the terms of HE-FM-EFF-002 the minimum cost of 17.32 units is 692.82 GBP, so cost efficiency and input-mix allocative efficiency are both 692.82 / 800 = 0.866 while technical efficiency is 1 (computed here for illustration). The remedy is a change of mix, not fewer inputs." ;
    hemo:9000014 "M = (1 - K) * C / P; TH = K * C / W; I = exp(rho_H * E) * M^(1 - K) * TH^K; MP_M = (1 - K) * I / M; MP_TH = K * I / TH; R_MP = MP_M / MP_TH" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-HPF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "least-cost-split-of-an-outlay-between-medical-care-and-own-time-for-cobb-douglas-gross-investment" .

hemo:5000736 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Unit cost of gross investment in health and the outlay for a target gross investment with schooling"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-HPF-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000167 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004692 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003391,
        hemo:6003392,
