        hemo:7004691 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004693,
        hemo:7004694,
        hemo:7004695,
        hemo:7004696,
        hemo:7004697,
        hemo:7004698,
        hemo:7004699 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Returns the cost of one unit of gross investment at the least-cost mix and the outlay needed for a target gross investment. For the Cobb-Douglas form of HE-FM-HPF-001 the unit cost is exp(-rho_H E) times (P / (1-K)) to the power 1-K times (W / K) to the power K, derived here by putting the least-cost split of HE-FM-HPF-002 into the production function. Because of constant returns and fixed prices it is both the average and the marginal cost of gross investment, the pi_(t-1) that HE-FM-DFH-002 takes as an input. One more year of schooling divides the unit cost by exp(rho_H), the exact form of Grossman's result that marginal cost falls by rho_H percent per unit of E, and a 1% rise in the wage raises it by about K percent." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Unit cost of gross investment in health and the outlay for a target gross investment with schooling" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Grossman M. The Demand for Health: A Theoretical and Empirical Investigation. National Bureau of Economic Research, New York; 1972. Chapter 2, The Shadow Price of Health, pages 11 to 30. Section 3, Market and Nonmarket Efficiency, page 22, which states that a 1 percent rise in W would increase marginal cost by K percent, and page 26, equation (2-17), which states that under factor neutrality education lowers average or marginal cost and that education raising the marginal products of medical care and own time by 3 percent would reduce the price of gross investment by 3 percent. https://www.nber.org/system/files/chapters/c3486/c3486.pdf",
        "Grossman M. The human capital model of the demand for health. NBER Working Paper 7078. National Bureau of Economic Research, Cambridge, MA; 1999. Published as The human capital model, in Culyer AJ, Newhouse JP, editors. Handbook of Health Economics. Vol 1A. Elsevier, Amsterdam; 2000. p. 347-408. Section 2.2, the text after equation (2-10), which states that with linear homogeneity and prices independent of the level of the inputs the average cost of gross investment is constant and equal to the marginal cost; section 3.2, which states that if K is the fraction of the total cost of gross investment accounted for by time a 1 percent rise in W would increase marginal cost by K percent, and equation (3-10), in which the percentage change in marginal cost per unit change in E equals minus rho_H because a rise in E reduces the inputs required for a given gross investment; section 2.4, which reports that Ehrlich and Chuma introduce rising marginal cost of investment. The closed-form unit cost with the share terms is derived here from equation (5-3) and the least-cost condition. https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w7078/w7078.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Linear reading of the schooling saving over several years. Reading rho_H E as a percentage cut in cost is close for one year, 776.00 GBP against 776.36 GBP for 20 units, but the gap widens as the change in E grows. For four extra years at 0.03 the outlay is 800 times exp(-0.12), 709.54 GBP, against 704.00 GBP from the linear reading (computed here for illustration).",
        "Price of medical care taken as the unit cost of gross investment. In the article's example the unit cost equals the price of care, 40 GBP, only because 2 times the square root of 40 times 10 happens to be 40. With a wage of 20 GBP and the same price of care the unit cost is 56.57 GBP, so using 40 GBP would understate it by 29% (computed here for illustration). HE-ER-DFH-004 makes the same point for the pure investment condition.",
        "Wage left out of the cost of producing health. Own time is an input, so the unit cost rises with the wage. Grossman states that a 1 percent rise in the wage raises the marginal cost of gross investment by K percent; in the article's example a 10% rise in the wage, to 11 GBP, raises the unit cost by 4.88%, to 41.95 GBP (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "pi = exp(-rho_H * E) * (P / (1 - K))^(1 - K) * (W / K)^K; C = pi * I" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-HPF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "unit-cost-of-gross-investment-in-health-and-the-outlay-for-a-target-gross-investment-with-schooling" .

hemo:5000737 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Mean and variance of specific stop-loss reimbursement over a three-level claims distribution"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ATTP-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000168 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004703 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003399,
        hemo:6003400,
        hemo:6003401,
        hemo:6003402,
        hemo:6003403,
        hemo:6003404,
        hemo:6003405,
        hemo:6003406,
        hemo:6003407,
        hemo:6003408,
        hemo:6003409,
        hemo:6003410,
        hemo:6003411 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003400,
        hemo:6003401,
        hemo:6003402,
        hemo:6003403,
        hemo:6003404,
        hemo:6003405,
        hemo:6003406,
        hemo:6003407,
        hemo:6003408 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003411 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004700,
        hemo:7004701,
        hemo:7004702 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004704,
        hemo:7004705,
        hemo:7004706,
        hemo:7004707,
        hemo:7004708,
        hemo:7004709,
        hemo:7004710,
        hemo:7004711 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives the expected reimbursement per covered person, its second moment, variance and standard deviation when the person's annual eligible claims take one of three levels C_1 to C_3 with probabilities p_1 to p_3, and the remaining probability sits at claims that never reach the attachment point. Each level's payment is the specific layer rule of HE-FM-ASL-001 with a layer limit of U minus A, times the coinsurance rate c, and E_R is the probability-weighted mean of HE-FN-EV-001 over those payments, the net premium per person for the layer before any loading, carried here because the variance needs it. V_R is the second moment less the squared mean, following the expected value and second moment of the excess of loss cost function in Miccolis 1977. The record works from the discrete distribution of the article's worked example; the normal closed form for an aggregate total is the separate record HE-FM-ASL-005. For a group of n independent members the expected total is n times E_R and its standard deviation SD_R times the square root of n, the rule of HE-FM-ASL-003, which the computational function below applies. Raising A by an amount that passes no claim level and stays below the cap lowers E_R by c times that amount times the probability that claims exceed the old attachment point (derived), so expected reimbursement falls steeply while, as the worked examples show, the standard deviation falls more slowly." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Mean and variance of specific stop-loss reimbursement over a three-level claims distribution" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Miccolis RS. On the theory of increased limits and excess of loss pricing. Proceedings of the Casualty Actuarial Society. 1977;64:27-59. Section Excess of Loss Coverage (page 34), equation 9, which defines the excess of loss cost function h(x; r, j) as zero for losses up to the retention r, the loss minus r up to r plus the maximum liability j, and j above that, and equation 10, which gives its expected value; and section Variance as a Measure of Risk (pages 42 to 44), which writes the variance of a cost function as its second moment less the square of its first moment and gives the second moment of the excess of loss cost function in equation 31. The article's attachment point A is the retention r and its cap U is r plus j; the coinsurance factor c and the three-level discrete form are applied here. https://www.casact.org/sites/default/files/database/proceed_proceed77_77027.pdf",
        "National Association of Insurance Commissioners. Stop Loss Insurance, Self-Funding and the ACA. White paper. 2015. Section II, which describes a specific attachment point, or retention level, that protects against claim severity for a single covered individual; section III, which describes the laser specific attachment point, a higher attachment point for individuals with preexisting high-cost conditions or other identified risk factors, and states that unless a provision or endorsement provides extended cover the policy reimburses only claims incurred and paid during the same policy year; and section V, which states that stop-loss premiums are developed from an actuary's determination of the expected losses of the self-funded group. https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/inline-files/SLI_SF.pdf",
        "US Code of Federal Regulations. Title 45, part 153 (Standards related to reinsurance, risk corridors, and HHS risk adjustment under the Affordable Care Act). eCFR text as at 1 October 2026, read 18 October 2026. Section 153.20 defines the attachment point, the coinsurance rate as the rate of reimbursement for claims costs after the attachment point and before the reinsurance cap, and the reinsurance cap; section 153.230(c) calculates each payment as the national coinsurance rate multiplied by the issuer's claims costs for an individual enrollee in the benefit year between the national attachment point and the national reinsurance cap. https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/section-153.230",
        "US Department of Health and Human Services. Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act; HHS Notice of Benefit and Payment Parameters for 2016. Federal Register. 2015;80:10750 (27 February 2015). Section I, Executive Summary, which finalises the 2016 uniform reinsurance payment parameters of a USD 90,000 attachment point, a USD 250,000 reinsurance cap and a 50 percent coinsurance rate, and decreases the attachment point for the 2015 benefit year from USD 70,000 to USD 45,000 while retaining the USD 250,000 cap and the 50 percent coinsurance rate; and section III.C.3.j, Uniform Reinsurance Payment Parameters for 2015, which finalises that reduction as proposed. https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2015/02/27/2015-03751/patient-protection-and-affordable-care-act-hhs-notice-of-benefit-and-payment-parameters-for-2016" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Assuming the spread of transferred claims falls in step with expected reimbursement. Raising the attachment point from USD 50,000 to USD 200,000 cuts the expected reimbursement of 1,000 members by two thirds, from USD 900,000 to USD 300,000. Scaling the standard deviation down in the same proportion would put it at about USD 161,741, but the variance formula gives about USD 299,850, because the rare USD 500,000 claim carries most of the variance. Treating a higher attachment point as giving up mainly predictable spending, as the article does, rests on this difference.",
        "Mean specific reimbursement per claimant used as the mean per member. At a USD 100,000 attachment point the members who reach the layer, 0.4% of the group, receive on average USD 137,500 each, but the expected reimbursement per member is USD 550. Pricing the layer at the per-claimant mean overstates its expected cost 250-fold; the probability of claims above A has to stay in the expectation, as it does in E_R.",
        "Whole claim reimbursed once it passes the specific attachment point. Paying the full claim of a person whose claims exceed A, instead of the excess over A, turns the excess layer into a franchise. At USD 100,000 it gives 0.003 times 150,000 plus 0.001 times 500,000, USD 950 per member, about 73% more than the USD 550 of the excess rule of 45 CFR 153.230(c) and HE-FM-ASL-001." ;
    hemo:9000014 "E_R = c * (p_1 * max(min(C_1, U) - A, 0) + p_2 * max(min(C_2, U) - A, 0) + p_3 * max(min(C_3, U) - A, 0)); M2_R = c^2 * (p_1 * max(min(C_1, U) - A, 0)^2 + p_2 * max(min(C_2, U) - A, 0)^2 + p_3 * max(min(C_3, U) - A, 0)^2); V_R = M2_R - E_R^2; SD_R = sqrt(V_R)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ATTP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "mean-and-variance-of-specific-stop-loss-reimbursement-over-a-three-level-claims-distribution" .

hemo:5000740 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Hedges' g from Cohen's d with the approximate correction factor J"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-HEDG-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000170 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004734 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003432,
        hemo:6003433,
        hemo:6003434,
        hemo:6003435,
        hemo:6003436,
        hemo:6003437 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003433,
        hemo:6003434,
        hemo:6003436 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003437 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004731,
        hemo:7004732,
        hemo:7004733 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004735,
        hemo:7004736,
        hemo:7004737,
        hemo:7004738,
        hemo:7004739,
        hemo:7004740 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Multiplies the uncorrected standardised mean difference d of HE-FM-CD-001 by a correction factor J that depends only on the degrees of freedom m of the standard deviation used to standardise, which for the pooled standard deviation is the total sample size less 2. The factor 1 minus 3 over (4m minus 1) is Hedges' algebraic approximation to an exact factor written with gamma functions, which HE-CF-HEDG-001 computes. Substituting for m gives 1 minus 3 over (4N minus 9) with N the total sample size, the form Review Manager prints. J is below 1, so g is always nearer zero than d, and J approaches 1 as the sample grows." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Hedges' g from Cohen's d with the approximate correction factor J" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Deeks JJ, Higgins JPT, on behalf of the Cochrane Statistical Methods Group. Statistical algorithms in Review Manager. Cochrane; May 2022. Section Standardized difference in means (standardized mean difference) (page 3), which states that the version implemented in RevMan is Hedges' adjusted g, very similar to Cohen's d but with an adjustment for small sample bias, and gives the SMD as the difference in means over the pooled standard deviation times 1 minus 3/(4N minus 9), with N the sum of the two group sizes. https://documentation.cochrane.org/revman-kb/files/210600101/210600103/1/1654774257333/Statistical_methods_in_revman.pdf",
        "Hedges LV. Distribution theory for Glass's estimator of effect size and related estimators. Journal of Educational Statistics. 1981;6(2):107-128. Theorem 1 (page 111), which for normal outcomes with a common variance gives the expectation of the uncorrected estimate as delta over c(m), with m equal to the control group size less 1 or the total of both groups less 2 depending on whether a control group or pooled standard deviation is used, and defines c(m) as Gamma(m/2) over the square root of m/2 times Gamma((m minus 1)/2) (equation 6e). Section 4, An unbiased estimator based on the standardized mean difference (pages 112 to 114), which defines the unbiased estimator as the uncorrected one times c(m) (equation 7) and gives the approximation c(m) about 1 minus 3/(4m minus 1), with a maximum error of 0.007 when m is 2, accuracy within 0.00033 when m is at least 10 and an error not above 0.000015 when m exceeds 50. Table I (page 115) tabulates c(m) to five decimals for each m up to 50. In Hedges' notation g is the uncorrected estimator and the corrected one is g with superscript U. https://doi.org/10.3102/10769986006002107",
        "Lakens D. Calculating and reporting effect sizes to facilitate cumulative science: a practical primer for t-tests and ANOVAs. Frontiers in Psychology. 2013;4:863. Section Cohen's d in between-subjects designs, equation 4, which gives Hedges's g as Cohen's d times 1 minus 3/(4(n1 + n2) minus 9), and states that the difference between the two is very small, especially in sample sizes above 20. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2013.00863",
        "What Works Clearinghouse. What Works Clearinghouse Procedures and Standards Handbook, Version 5.0 (WWC 2022008). US Department of Education, Institute of Education Sciences, National Center for Education Evaluation and Regional Assistance; August 2022. Appendix E, General effect size formulas (pages 162 and 163), equations E.1 to E.4, which compute Hedges' g as omega times the mean difference over the pooled standard deviation with omega equal to 1 minus 3/(4df minus 1) and df equal to N minus 2 for individual-level assignment, citing Hedges 1981, and state that the factor is 0.99 for a study with 100 students in total. https://ies.ed.gov/ncee/wwc/Docs/referenceresources/Final_WWC-HandbookVer5.0-0-508.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Hedges' correction applied to an estimate that is already g. Multiplying a reported Hedges' g by J again, often because a paper labels g as d, gives about 0.7743 instead of 0.8048 for the article's trial (0.962025 squared times 0.8366, computed here for illustration). The article lists applying the correction twice among the frequent mistakes that make pooled results inconsistent.",
        "Pooled degrees of freedom used with a comparator-group standard deviation. When the difference is standardised by the comparator group's standard deviation alone, Hedges sets m to the comparator group size less 1. Using the pooled value of 20 for the article's trial, whose comparator arm has 12 patients, gives a J of about 0.9620 instead of about 0.9302 for 11 degrees of freedom and leaves almost half of the bias in place, an expected overstatement of about 3.4% instead of about 7.5% with the exact factor, computed here for illustration.",
        "Total sample size written in place of the degrees of freedom in Hedges' J. Writing 1 minus 3 over (4N minus 1), with N the total sample size, mixes the two forms of the factor. For the article's trial it gives a J of about 0.9655 instead of 0.9620 and a g of about 0.8078 instead of 0.8048, computed here for illustration, so part of the bias stays in the estimate." ;
    hemo:9000014 "m = n_1 + n_2 - 2; J = 1 - 3 / (4 * m - 1); g = J * d" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-HEDG-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "hedges-g-from-cohen-s-d-with-the-approximate-correction-factor-j" .

hemo:5000741 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Large-sample variance and standard error of Hedges' g"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-HEDG-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000170 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004743 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003438,
        hemo:6003439,
        hemo:6003440,
        hemo:6003441,
        hemo:6003442,
        hemo:6003443,
        hemo:6003444 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003439,
        hemo:6003440,
        hemo:6003441,
        hemo:6003443 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003444 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004741,
        hemo:7004742 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004744,
        hemo:7004745,
        hemo:7004746,
        hemo:7004747,
        hemo:7004748 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives the large-sample variance of d as a term for the precision of the mean difference, (n_1 + n_2) over n_1 n_2, plus a term that grows with the size of the effect, d squared over twice the total sample size. Multiplying d by the constant J multiplies its variance by J squared, so the variance of g is J squared times V_d and its square root is the standard error. J comes from HE-FM-HEDG-001 and d from HE-FM-CD-001. The inverse of V_g is the study's weight in HE-FM-ADMA-001 and HE-FM-ADMA-005, and HE-FM-AN-001 with SE_g gives the confidence interval." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Large-sample variance and standard error of Hedges' g" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Hedges LV. Distribution theory for Glass's estimator of effect size and related estimators. Journal of Educational Statistics. 1981;6(2):107-128. Section 4 (page 114), which states that the variance of the corrected estimator is c(m) squared times the variance of the uncorrected one and so smaller; Theorem 2 (page 116), which gives its exact variance; and section 4.1, Confidence intervals for the effect size (page 117), which treats the corrected estimator as approximately normal with variance 1 over n tilde plus delta squared over 2m, where n tilde is n^E n^C over (n^E + n^C). https://doi.org/10.3102/10769986006002107",
        "What Works Clearinghouse. What Works Clearinghouse Procedures and Standards Handbook, Version 5.0 (WWC 2022008). US Department of Education, Institute of Education Sciences, National Center for Education Evaluation and Regional Assistance; August 2022. Appendix E (page 164), equation E.6, which gives the standard error of Hedges' g from unadjusted means as omega times the square root of (n_i + n_c)/(n_i n_c) plus g squared over 2(n_i + n_c), citing Borenstein and Hedges 2019, and footnote 35, which states that Borenstein and Hedges use the uncorrected d in that right-hand term, that both versions are approximations because the population variance formula uses the population effect size, and that the left-hand term is usually much the larger. https://ies.ed.gov/ncee/wwc/Docs/referenceresources/Final_WWC-HandbookVer5.0-0-508.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "One over the total sample size in place of the group-size term for Hedges' g. Writing the first term as 1 over (n_1 + n_2) instead of (n_1 + n_2) over n_1 n_2 shrinks it about fourfold with equal arms. For the article's trial the standard error falls to about 0.2383 and the lower 95% limit rises to about 0.338, so a trial compatible with no effect appears clearly beneficial, computed here for illustration.",
        "Variance of Cohen's d attached to Hedges' g. Using V_d as the variance of g ignores the factor J squared. For the article's trial the standard error becomes about 0.4464 instead of 0.4294 and the study's inverse-variance weight about 7.5% lower than it should be, computed here for illustration." ;
    hemo:9000014 "V_d = (n_1 + n_2) / (n_1 * n_2) + d^2 / (2 * (n_1 + n_2)); V_g = J^2 * V_d; SE_g = sqrt(V_g)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-HEDG-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "large-sample-variance-and-standard-error-of-hedges-g" .

hemo:5000747 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Tipping-point shift for QALYs lost to follow-up at a cost-effectiveness threshold"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-LTFU-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000172 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004795 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003485,
        hemo:6003486,
        hemo:6003487,
        hemo:6003488,
        hemo:6003489,
        hemo:6003490,
        hemo:6003491,
        hemo:6003492 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003485,
        hemo:6003486,
        hemo:6003488,
        hemo:6003489,
        hemo:6003490,
        hemo:6003491 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003492 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004792,
        hemo:7004793,
        hemo:7004794 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004796,
        hemo:7004797,
        hemo:7004798,
        hemo:7004799,
        hemo:7004800 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Sets the delta-adjusted QALY gain equal to the gain needed at threshold lambda, H_d = Delta_C / lambda (HE-FM-CET-002), and solves for the shift. With a shift delta for those lost from the new treatment arm and r times delta for those lost from usual care, the gain is Delta_E_obs plus (p_T minus r times p_C) times delta (HE-FM-LOCF-002 read as in HE-FN-LTFU-001), so the tipping-point shift is H_d minus Delta_E_obs, divided by p_T minus r times p_C (derived). Setting r = 0 confines the shift to the new treatment arm and gives the article's delta_star = (Delta_C / lambda minus Delta_E_obs) / p_T, and r = 1 applies the same shift to both arms, when only the difference in the proportions lost moves the gain. A negative delta_star is a QALY shortfall for those lost that the base case can absorb. When the denominator is positive the base-case conclusion holds for every shift that lies on the same side of delta_star as zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Tipping-point shift for QALYs lost to follow-up at a cost-effectiveness threshold" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Faria R, Gomes M, Epstein D, White IR. A guide to handling missing data in cost-effectiveness analysis conducted within randomised controlled trials. PharmacoEconomics. 2014;32(12):1157-1170. Section 5, Stage 3, Sensitivity Analysis to the Missing at Random (MAR) Assumption, states that under the pattern mixture approach data are first imputed under MAR, the imputed values are shifted by a sensitivity parameter, the analysis is repeated for a range of plausible values, and either a range of results or the value of the sensitivity parameter required to change the results is reported. Section 6.3 applies the approach to the REFLUX study and finds that the probability of cost-effectiveness changes considerably only when the QALYs of those with missing data in the surgery arm alone are reduced. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40273-014-0193-3",
        "Leurent B, Gomes M, Carpenter JR. Missing data in trial-based cost-effectiveness analysis: an incomplete journey. Health Economics. 2018;27(6):1024-1040. Section 4.3, Recommendations, states that many reviewed studies used complete case analysis for the primary analysis and multiple imputation as a sensitivity analysis, or the reverse, and concluded that the results were robust to missing data, and calls this misleading because both methods rely on the assumption that the missingness is independent of the unobserved data. Section 4.1, Summary of findings, reports that only two studies (4%) assessed whether conclusions were sensitive to departures from missing at random. https://doi.org/10.1002/hec.3654",
        "Leurent B, Gomes M, Faria R, Morris S, Grieve R, Carpenter JR. Sensitivity analysis for not-at-random missing data in trial-based cost-effectiveness analysis: a tutorial. PharmacoEconomics. 2018;36(8):889-901. Section 2.3, Sensitivity Analysis with Pattern-Mixture Models, describes an offset parameter delta as the average difference between the missing and observed values, Y_miss = Y_obs + delta, and the steps of imputing under missing at random, modifying the imputed values by adding delta or multiplying by a scale parameter, and combining the analyses with Rubin's rules. Section 4.3, Choosing the MNAR Parameters, describes the tipping-point or threshold analysis as changing the MNAR parameter until a different conclusion is reached, such as being or not being cost-effective. Section 4.1, Missing Cost, extends the shifts to missing costs. The Results of the illustrative application state that departures assumed the same in both arms had little effect on incremental QALYs, which will usually be the case when the missing data pattern is broadly similar across arms, as the bias applies roughly equally to each arm and cancels out in the comparison. The closed form for the tipping-point shift is derived from these statements, not printed in the paper. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40273-018-0650-5" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Agreement of complete case and multiple imputation read as robustness to loss to follow-up. Running complete case analysis as the base case and multiple imputation as a sensitivity analysis, or the reverse, and finding the same conclusion does not show that the result holds under loss to follow-up that is missing not at random. Leurent, Gomes and Carpenter call this misleading because both methods rely on missingness being independent of the unobserved data. In the article's example, where the missing QALYs are taken to resemble the observed ones, both give a gain of 0.10, while the conclusion at GBP 20,000 per QALY changes once those lost from the new treatment arm are worse off by more than 0.125 QALYs.",
        "Common-shift tipping point quoted for loss to follow-up confined to one arm. With 20% lost from the new treatment arm and 10% from usual care, a shift common to both arms moves the gain by only 0.1 times the shift, so its tipping point is -0.25, twice as far from zero as the -0.125 for a shift confined to the new treatment arm. Quoting -0.25 when those who leave the new treatment arm may be worse off because of side effects overstates how far the conclusion can be trusted. With equal shares lost a common shift has no tipping point, which says nothing about a shift in one arm. Leurent and colleagues found that departures assumed the same in both arms had little effect on incremental QALYs, which they expect when the missing data pattern is broadly similar across arms, so that the bias applies roughly equally to each arm and cancels out in the comparison, while arm-specific departures changed the conclusion.",
        "QALY gain shortfall reported as the tipping-point shift for those lost to follow-up. In the article's example the gain must fall from 0.10 to 0.075 for the ICER to reach GBP 20,000 per QALY, a shortfall of 0.025, but only the 20% of the new treatment arm lost to follow-up are shifted, so those lost must be worse off by 0.025 / 0.2 = 0.125 QALYs. Reporting 0.025 as the tipping point understates the shift the conclusion withstands by a factor of 1 / p_T, five here. The shift is a difference in mean QALYs for the people lost, while the shortfall is a difference in the arm mean over everyone randomised." ;
    hemo:9000014 "H_d = Delta_C / lambda; delta_star = (H_d - Delta_E_obs) / (p_T - r * p_C)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-LTFU-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "tipping-point-shift-for-qalys-lost-to-follow-up-at-a-cost-effectiveness-threshold" .

hemo:5000748 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Crude-versus-adjusted ranking reversal condition for two providers' costs"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CMAD-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000173 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004804 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003493,
        hemo:6003494,
        hemo:6003495,
        hemo:6003496,
        hemo:6003497,
        hemo:6003498,
        hemo:6003499,
        hemo:6003500,
        hemo:6003501,
        hemo:6003502 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003493,
        hemo:6003494,
        hemo:6003495,
        hemo:6003496,
        hemo:6003497,
        hemo:6003498 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003502 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004801,
        hemo:7004802,
        hemo:7004803 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004805,
        hemo:7004806,
        hemo:7004807,
        hemo:7004808,
        hemo:7004809,
        hemo:7004810 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Compares two providers on crude cost per case and on the indirectly standardised cost index. The crude cost ratio rho is the product of the index ratio RI and the ratio mu of expected cost per case, the part of the crude gap that the case mix accounts for, so RI is rho divided by mu and equals I_1 / I_2 for the indices 100 A_h / E_h, which are 100 times R_h of HE-FM-CWT-003. The adjusted ranking reverses the crude one when rho minus 1 and RI minus 1 have opposite signs, which holds exactly when rho lies strictly between mu and 1 (derived here and checked numerically on 200,000 random inputs). A crude cost advantage therefore survives adjustment only when it is larger than the advantage in expected cost per case, and two providers with the same mix, mu equal to 1, cannot reverse. A tie in either ranking is not counted as a reversal." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Crude-versus-adjusted ranking reversal condition for two providers' costs" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Mohammed MA, Deeks JJ, Girling A, Rudge G, Carmalt M, Stevens AJ, Lilford RJ. Evidence of methodological bias in hospital standardised mortality ratios: retrospective database study of English hospitals. BMJ. 2009;338:b780. Introduction, box Example of differential measurement error, in which under-recording of comorbidities cuts hospital B's expected deaths to 743 against 1,500 and gives a standardised mortality ratio of 202 for identical hospitals; Discussion, which states that hospitals with a lower depth of coding had a lower Charlson index, which underestimated the expected mortality and inflated the standardised mortality ratio. https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.b780",
        "NHS England and NHS Improvement. User guide: National Cost Collection 2018/19. A guide to using the data. 2020. Chapter 3, Table 9, expected cost row (p. 23), which defines expected cost as the organisation's activity multiplied by the national mean unit cost; Chapter 2, Table 7 (p. 15), whose index queries compute the index as summed actual cost divided by summed expected cost, multiplied by 100; and p. 9, which states that the data adjusted by the market forces factor is used for the index queries. The reversal condition is derived here and is not in the guide. https://www.england.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/National_Cost_Collection_2018-19_A_Guide_to_using_the_data.pdf",
        "Shahian DM, Normand SL. Comparison of \"risk-adjusted\" hospital outcomes. Circulation. 2008;117(15):1955-1963. Abstract, Conclusions, which state that with indirect standardisation the outcomes reflect a provider's performance for its specific case mix relative to the expected performance of an average provider for that same case mix, and that unless the case-mix distributions overlap reasonably such outcomes should not be used to compare one institution directly with another. https://doi.org/10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.107.747873" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Mixing per-case crude costs with total expected costs in the mix ratio. If Hospital A treated half as many cases with the same mix and unit costs, its crude cost per case would stay 3,360 GBP, but a mix ratio taken on total expected costs, 1,600,000 / 6,200,000 = 0.258, in place of expected cost per case gives an index ratio of about 2.21 instead of 1.105 (computed here for illustration). The case numbers cancel only when both ratios are taken per case or both on totals.",
        "Reading a ratio inflated by under-coding as worse performance. Mohammed and colleagues give a hypothetical pair of hospitals that are identical in case mix, care and deaths, 1,500 each, where hospital B under-records comorbidities. B's expected deaths fall to 743 and its standardised mortality ratio rises to 202 against 100 for A, a ratio of about 2.02 between the two hospitals with no difference in care. A ratio of cost indices moves the same way when one provider records its cases in lighter groups than the other, so RI between providers with different coding depth is checked against coding audits before it is read as a cost difference.",
        "Reading a reversal of indirectly standardised indices as a like-for-like cost difference. Two illustrative hospitals with A's and B's mixes and identical unit costs in every group, 2,200 GBP for simple and 8,000 GBP for complex cases, score indices of 105.0 and about 100.97, so A looks about 4% dearer and the crude ranking reverses, yet direct standardisation on the pooled mix (HE-FM-INEQ-001) gives both 4,810 GBP per case (computed here for illustration). The 10% excess on simple cases weighs more in A's simple-heavy mix, so an index ratio is checked against direct standardisation before it is read as a cost difference for the same patients.",
        "Reading an index ratio widened by recoding as a cost difference. In step 5 of the article's illustrative example Hospital B records 100 of its simple cases as complex, with the same care and the same 5,890,000 GBP of cost. Its expected cost rises by 100 × 6,000 = 600,000 GBP to 6,800,000 GBP, so mu falls from about 0.516 to about 0.471 and the index ratio rises from about 1.105 to about 1.212, while rho stays at about 0.570. B's index falls from 95 to about 86.6, a change equal to the old index times the added expected cost over the new one, 95 × 600,000 / 6,800,000 = 8.38 points (derived here). The recoded index is HE-FM-CWT-003 recomputed on the new counts, the effect HE-AS-CWT-006 warns of, so an index ratio is read against coding audits before it is credited to efficiency.",
        "Reading two providers' crude cost ratio as relative efficiency. In the article's example B's crude cost per case is 75% above A's (5,890 / 3,360 = 1.753), while A's index is 10.5% above B's (105 / 95 = 1.105). The crude gap reflects B's larger share of complex cases, a mix ratio of 0.516, and points the wrong way." ;
    hemo:9000014 "rho = (A_1 / n_1) / (A_2 / n_2); mu = (E_1 / n_1) / (E_2 / n_2); RI = rho / mu; rev = (rho - 1) * (RI - 1) < 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CMAD-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "crude-versus-adjusted-ranking-reversal-condition-for-two-providers-costs" .

hemo:5000749 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Negative likelihood ratio of a negative result on both of two tests with conditional covariances"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-NLR-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000174 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004814 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003503,
        hemo:6003504,
        hemo:6003505,
        hemo:6003506,
        hemo:6003507,
        hemo:6003508,
        hemo:6003509,
        hemo:6003510,
        hemo:6003511 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003506,
        hemo:6003507,
        hemo:6003508,
        hemo:6003509,
        hemo:6003510,
        hemo:6003511 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003505 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004811,
        hemo:7004812,
        hemo:7004813 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004815,
        hemo:7004816,
        hemo:7004817,
        hemo:7004818,
        hemo:7004819,
        hemo:7004820 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "When two tests are combined so that a person is called negative only if both are negative, the combined sensitivity Se_par and specificity Sp_par follow from the single-test values and the conditional covariances, C_pos among people with the condition and C_neg among people without it, in the joint probabilities that Wang, Lin and Nelson give. The combined ratio LR_neg_12 is then the LR_neg of HE-FM-DXA-002 applied to Se_par and Sp_par. With both covariances zero, the conditional independence that the article warns about, LR_neg_12 equals the product of the two single-test ratios (derived). A positive C_pos raises the ratio above that product, so multiplying the ratios overstates the rule-out, while a positive C_neg on its own lowers it below the product (derived)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Negative likelihood ratio of a negative result on both of two tests with conditional covariances" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Gardner IA, Stryhn H, Lind P, Collins MT. Conditional dependence between tests affects the diagnosis and surveillance of animal diseases. Preventive Veterinary Medicine. 2000;45(1-2):107-122. doi:10.1016/s0167-5877(00)00119-7. Abstract, which states that dependence between the sensitivities or specificities of pairs of tests affects their sensitivity and specificity when used in combination, that compared with conditional independence a positive dependence in sensitivity reduces the sensitivity of parallel interpretation and a positive dependence in specificity reduces the specificity of serial interpretation, and that conditional covariances are calculated as a measure of dependence between binary tests. https://doi.org/10.1016/s0167-5877(00)00119-7",
        "Wang C, Lin X, Nelson KP. Bayesian hierarchical latent class models for estimating diagnostic accuracy. Statistical Methods in Medical Research. 2020;29(4):1112-1128. doi:10.1177/0962280219852649. Section 2.2, Hierarchical conditional dependence model (Model M2), gives the joint probabilities of two binary tests given disease status with the conditional covariances C+ and C minus, including the probability that both are negative as (1 minus Se_j)(1 minus Se_k) + C+ given disease and Sp_j Sp_k + C minus given no disease, states that a positive value reflects positive dependence, and gives the limits on the covariances in constraint (3). https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6884669/" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Multiplying the negative likelihood ratios of two dependent tests. With the second worked example's covariance of 0.02 among people with the condition, multiplying 0.10 by 0.125 gives 0.0125 against a joint ratio of 0.0625. At a 30% pre-test probability the product suggests a residual risk of about 0.53% when it is about 2.61%, close to the article's 2.73% discharge threshold.",
        "Setting the covariances to zero without data on both tests. Taking each test's sensitivity and specificity from a different study leaves C_pos and C_neg unknown, and a zero then assumes conditional independence. Gardner and colleagues state that dependence between the sensitivities or specificities of two tests changes their accuracy in combination, so the assumption needs a scenario analysis with plausible covariances.",
        "Using the both-negative ratio for a pathway that retests only positives. If the second test is given only to people positive on the first and a person is discharged when either result is negative, the negative result is at least one negative, with ratio (1 minus Se_1 Se_2 minus C_pos) / (1 minus (1 minus Sp_1)(1 minus Sp_2) minus C_neg). For the independent worked example that is 0.145 / 0.90, about 0.161, against 0.0125 (derived from the joint probabilities of Wang, Lin and Nelson)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "Se_par = 1 - (1 - Se_1) * (1 - Se_2) - C_pos; Sp_par = Sp_1 * Sp_2 + C_neg; LR_neg_12 = (1 - Se_par) / Sp_par" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-NLR-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "negative-likelihood-ratio-of-a-negative-result-on-both-of-two-tests-with-conditional-covariances" .

hemo:5000824 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "N-th root of a transition matrix of any size by eigendecomposition with validity and reproduction checks"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-TMAT-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000580 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7005427,
        hemo:7005428 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004664,
        hemo:6004665,
        hemo:6004666,
        hemo:6004667 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004665 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004664,
        hemo:6004666,
        hemo:6004667 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005423 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006960,
        hemo:7006961,
        hemo:7006962,
        hemo:7006963 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the n-th root of a transition matrix of any size by eigendecomposition, P^(1/n) = V D^(1/n) V^-1, and checks the result: whether it is real, whether every entry is non-negative, whether each row sums to one, and how closely its n-th power reproduces the original matrix. It handles structures with recovery or several intermediate states, for which no closed form like HE-FM-TMAT-001 is at hand. The inputs differ from the formula's: a whole matrix and any whole number n." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function: n-th root of a transition matrix of any size by eigendecomposition with validity and reproduction checks" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Chhatwal J, Jayasuriya S, Elbasha EH. Changing cycle lengths in state-transition models: challenges and solutions. Medical Decision Making. 2016;36(8):952-964. doi:10.1177/0272989X16656165 (full text of the author manuscript read). Eigendecomposition approach and approach for non-stochastic matrix roots; Sonnenberg FA, Beck JR. Markov models in medical decision making: a practical guide. Medical Decision Making. 1993;13(4):322-338. doi:10.1177/0272989X9301300409 (full text read). Transition probabilities: the P matrix, with zero probabilities for disallowed transitions." ;
    hemo:9000011 "Keeping the real part of a complex root without checking. A root with complex entries is not a transition matrix; dropping the imaginary parts silently gives a matrix that neither reproduces P nor has a clear meaning, so the real flag has to be checked before the root is used" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P = V * D * V^(-1); root = V * D^(1/n) * V^(-1); real = all(Im(root) = 0); nonnegative = all(root >= 0); rows_sum_to_one = all(rowsum(root) = 1); reproduction_error = max(abs(root^n - P))" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-TMAT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-n-th-root-of-a-transition-matrix-of-any-size-by-eigendecomposition-with-validity-and-reproduction-checks" .

hemo:5000825 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Yearly incidence-based and prevalence-based YLDs from a history of incident cases"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-YLD-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000581,
        hemo:5000582,
        hemo:5000583 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7005433,
        hemo:7005434 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004668,
        hemo:6004669,
        hemo:6004670,
        hemo:6004671 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004668,
        hemo:6004669,
        hemo:6004670,
        hemo:6004671 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005430 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006964,
        hemo:7006965,
        hemo:7006966 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Turns a history of yearly incident cases into the incidence-based and prevalence-based YLD counts for every year that has at least L years of history behind it, for a condition of fixed duration L. The inputs differ from the formulas': a whole series of yearly incidence, in place of one or two incidence levels, so any pattern of rises and falls can be followed, with an optional continuous discount rate for the incidence-based count." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function: yearly incidence-based and prevalence-based YLDs from a history of incident cases" ;
    hemo:9000010 "World Health Organization. WHO methods and data sources for global burden of disease estimates 2000-2019. Global Health Estimates Technical Paper WHO/DDI/DNA/GHE/2020.3. Geneva: WHO; 2020 (full text read). Sections 2 and 2.4; Vos T, Flaxman AD, Naghavi M, Lozano R, Michaud C, Ezzati M, et al. Years lived with disability (YLDs) for 1160 sequelae of 289 diseases and injuries 1990-2010: a systematic analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study 2010. The Lancet. 2012;380(9859):2163-2196. doi:10.1016/S0140-6736(12)61729-2 (full text read). Methods; Larson BA. Calculating disability-adjusted-life-years lost (DALYs) in discrete-time. Cost Effectiveness and Resource Allocation. 2013;11:18. doi:10.1186/1478-7547-11-18 (full text read). Equation 2." ;
    hemo:9000011 "Starting the prevalence count before L years of history. In the first years of a series fewer than L cohorts are available, so a prevalence-based count summed over them understates the people living with the condition; the function reports only years with a full window" ;
    hemo:9000014 "yld_inc_y = inc_y * DW * L, or inc_y * DW * (1 - exp(-r * L)) / r when r > 0; yld_prev_y = DW * sum_(t=y-L+1)^y inc_t" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-YLD-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-yearly-incidence-based-and-prevalence-based-ylds-from-a-history-of-incident-cases" .

hemo:5000881 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Kolm equally distributed equivalent health with absolute inequality aversion"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000114 obo:IAO_0000125 ;
    obo:IAO_0000115 "Gives the level of health which, if everyone had it, would be judged as good as the actual distribution, when concern for inequality depends on absolute differences in health between people."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "A HEMO-native record, for a formula the Functions and Formulae module does not yet hold. It is listed in module-backlog.md for the module curator."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000117 "Michelle Ashley (drafted with Claude)" ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "Kolm 1976; Robson et al. 2017" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HEMO-FM-KOLM-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003645,
        hemo:6003646,
        hemo:6003647,
        hemo:6003648,
        hemo:6003738 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003646,
        hemo:6003647,
        hemo:6003648 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003645 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005957 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006007,
        hemo:7006012 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8001097 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8001076 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000013,
        heao:0000106 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-24" ;
    hemo:9000014 "h_EDE = -(1 / a) * log(sum_(i=1)^n [exp(-a * h_i)] / n)" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>h_EDE</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">unary_minus</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>a</ci></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"transc1\">ln</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">sum</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"interval1\">integer_interval</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>n</ci></apply><bind><csymbol cd=\"fns1\">lambda</csymbol><bvar><ci>i</ci></bvar><apply><csymbol cd=\"transc1\">exp</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">unary_minus</csymbol><ci>a</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"hemo1\" cdbase=\"https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/cd\">element</csymbol><ci>h_i</ci><ci>i</ci></apply></apply></apply></bind></apply><ci>n</ci></apply></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "h_{\\mathrm{EDE}} = -\\left(\\frac{1}{a}\\right) \\, \\ln\\left(\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\left[\\exp\\left(-a \\, h_{i}_{i}\\right)\\right]}{n}\\right)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HEMO-FM-KOLM-001" .

hemo:5000882 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "NICE size of benefit QALY weight for highly specialised technologies"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000114 obo:IAO_0000125 ;
    obo:IAO_0000115 "Gives the QALY weight NICE applies by size of benefit for highly specialised technologies, 1 for 10 or fewer incremental QALYs per patient over a lifetime horizon, 3 for 30 or more, and equal increments between."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "A HEMO-native record, for a formula the Functions and Formulae module does not yet hold. It is listed in module-backlog.md for the module curator."@en,
        "PMG36 sections 6.2.22 and 6.2.23 judge highly specialised technologies against GBP 100,000 per QALY and apply the weight only with compelling evidence of significant QALY gains. PMG36 does not say whether the incremental QALYs are discounted."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000117 "Michelle Ashley (drafted with Claude)" ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "NICE 2022 (PMG36, last updated 13 October 2026), sections 6.2.22 to 6.2.24 and table 6.2, checked 18 October 2026" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HEMO-FM-SOB-001" ;
