hemo:5000895 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Copas selection model publication probability and implied number of unpublished studies"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-COPAS-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003739,
        hemo:6003740,
        hemo:6003741,
        hemo:6003742,
        hemo:6003743,
        hemo:6003744 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003739,
        hemo:6003740,
        hemo:6003741,
        hemo:6003742 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003743,
        hemo:6003744 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the two selection parameters of the Copas selection model, gamma_0 and gamma_1, and the standard errors of the published studies, and returns each study's probability of publication, Phi(gamma_0 + gamma_1 / s), and the number of unpublished studies those parameters imply, M. In a Copas sensitivity analysis gamma_0 and gamma_1 are fixed over a grid, and at each point the mean effect theta, the heterogeneity tau and the correlation rho are estimated by maximising the likelihood of the published estimates conditional on publication. That adjusted estimate has no closed form and comes from software such as the copas function of the R package metasens, so it is not written as a formula here; the publication probability and M are the quantities that label each grid point. The unadjusted estimate the analysis starts from is the common-effect inverse-variance mean of HE-FM-ADMA-001 (random-effects form HE-FM-ADMA-005), and the article's unadjusted 0.3671 is HE-FM-ADMA-001 applied to its twelve illustrative trials. A rank test for small-study effects is HE-FN-BEGG-001, with HE-FM-BEGG-002 and HE-CF-BEGG-001. Carrying an adjusted odds ratio into a decision model uses HE-FM-NMA-004 for the response probability on the new therapy and HE-FM-ICER-001 for the cost per extra responder. Notation follows the Copas Model article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Copas selection model publication probability and implied number of unpublished studies" ;
    hemo:9000014 "copas(gamma_0, gamma_1, s_i, n_g) = (P_i, M)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-COPAS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "copas-selection-model-publication-probability-and-implied-number-of-unpublished-studies" .

hemo:5000896 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Derived demand for medical care from the Grossman demand for the health stock"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-DDHC-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003745,
        hemo:6003746,
        hemo:6003747,
        hemo:6003748,
        hemo:6003749,
        hemo:6003750,
        hemo:6003751,
        hemo:6003752,
        hemo:6003753,
        hemo:6003754,
        hemo:6003755 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003745,
        hemo:6003746,
        hemo:6003747,
        hemo:6003748,
        hemo:6003749,
        hemo:6003750,
        hemo:6003751,
        hemo:6003752 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003753,
        hemo:6003754,
        hemo:6003755 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the elasticity of the marginal efficiency of health capital, the time share of the cost of gross investment, the schooling efficiency parameter and a change in the wage, the marginal cost of gross investment or the price of care, schooling and the depreciation rate to the proportional change in the health stock demanded, in gross investment and in medical care demanded, in the pure investment version of the Grossman model with a zero real-own rate of interest. HE-FM-DDHC-001 gives Grossman's demand function for the health stock and HE-FM-DDHC-002 his reduced-form demand function for medical care with its wage, price, schooling and depreciation coefficients. Both build on live records that this package does not restate, the pure investment condition HE-FM-DFH-002, the stock update HE-FM-DFH-001 (gross investment of about delta H when net investment is small), the Cobb-Douglas gross investment of HE-FM-HPF-001, the least-cost care of HE-FM-HPF-002 and the unit cost pi of HE-FM-HPF-003. Because Grossman suppresses the intercepts, both formulas compare two states, such as two ages of one person or two people, as ratios, and for a change in one price alone each ratio has the constant-elasticity form of HE-FM-XPE-003. Notation follows the Derived Demand for Healthcare article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Derived demand for medical care from the Grossman demand for the health stock" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d(eps, K, rho_H, W, pi, P, E, delta) = (H_ratio, I_ratio, M_ratio)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-DDHC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "derived-demand-for-medical-care-from-the-grossman-demand-for-the-health-stock" .

hemo:5000901 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Copas probit probability that a study with standard error s is published"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-COPAS-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000895 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006162 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003772,
        hemo:6003773,
        hemo:6003774,
        hemo:6003775,
        hemo:6003776,
        hemo:6003777 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003772,
        hemo:6003773,
        hemo:6003774,
        hemo:6003776 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003775,
        hemo:6003777 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006159,
        hemo:7006160,
        hemo:7006161 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006163,
        hemo:7006164,
        hemo:7006165,
        hemo:7006166,
        hemo:7006167 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Under the selection equation of the Copas model a study is published when a latent propensity Z = gamma_0 + gamma_1 / s + delta is above zero, with delta standard normal, so its marginal probability of publication is the standard normal distribution function at the index u = gamma_0 + gamma_1 / s. Phi(gamma_0) is the probability of publishing a study with an infinitely large standard error, and gamma_1 sets how fast the probability rises as the standard error shrinks. The site calculator has no normal distribution function, so Phi_u is entered as a named input from NORM.S.DIST(u, TRUE) or pnorm(u), the convention of HE-FM-MCID-004. That record's normal share above a threshold returns the same number with a threshold of zero, a standard deviation of 1 and a mean of u; the Copas form adds the index in the study's precision with its two selection parameters." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Copas probit probability that a study with standard error s is published" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Carpenter J, Rücker G, Schwarzer G. copas: an R package for fitting the Copas selection model. The R Journal. 2009;1(2):31-36 (full text read). Section Copas selection model, equations (2) to (4), states that study i is observed or published if Z_i = gamma_0 + gamma_1 / s_i + delta_i is above zero, with fixed gamma_0 and gamma_1, so that the marginal probability that study i is observed is Pr(Z_i > 0) = Phi(gamma_0 + gamma_1 / s_i), with Phi the standard normal cumulative distribution function. The text interprets Phi(gamma_0) as the marginal probability of publishing a study with infinite standard error and says gamma_1 is associated with the change in publication probability with increasing precision. https://doi.org/10.32614/RJ-2009-012",
        "Duan R, Piao J, Marks-Anglin A, Tong J, Lin L, Chu H, Ning J, Chen Y. Testing for publication bias in meta-analysis under Copas selection model. arXiv preprint arXiv:2007.00836. 2020 (full text read). Section 2.1, Copas selection model, states that gamma_0 controls the overall proportion of selection and gamma_1 how selection depends on the size of the study, that the sign of gamma_1 is expected to be positive since larger studies are more likely to be accepted for publication, and gives Pr(Z_i > 0 | s_i) = Phi(gamma_0 + gamma_1 / s_i). https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.00836",
        "Schwarzer G, Carpenter JR, Rücker G. metasens, Statistical Methods for Sensitivity Analysis in Meta-Analysis. R package version 1.5-3, 2025 (reference manual read). Function copas, Details, writes the selection model as Z = gamma0 + gamma1 / (SE(i)) + delta(i) and states that the software chooses a grid of gamma0 and gamma1 values corresponding to a range of selection or publication probabilities for the study with the largest treatment effect standard error. The arguments describe gamma0 as the constant in the probit selection model for study publication and gamma1 as the coefficient of study precision (1/standard error). https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/metasens/metasens.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Phi of the Copas intercept read as the probability for the least precise study. Phi(gamma_0) is the limit for an infinitely large standard error, not the probability for the least precise study in the meta-analysis. In the article's moderate scenario Phi(-1.062) is 0.144, while the small trials with standard error 0.40 are published with probability 0.35. The metasens manual calls the cumulative normal of gamma0 approximately the probability that a small study is published, which holds only when gamma_1 / s is close to zero for that study; here it is 0.68 (derived). The copas software reports selection as the probability of publishing the study with the largest standard error, which is Phi(gamma_0 + gamma_1 / s) at that standard error, 0.35 here.",
        "Variance used in place of the standard error in the Copas selection index. Writing gamma_1 / s^2 instead of gamma_1 / s inflates the index of every study with a standard error below 1. In the article's moderate scenario the large trial's probability becomes 1.000 instead of 0.95 and the small trial's 0.7356 instead of 0.35, so a scenario meant to leave 65% of small trials unpublished leaves about 26% unpublished (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "u = gamma_0 + gamma_1 / s; P = Phi_u" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-COPAS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "copas-probit-probability-that-a-study-with-standard-error-s-is-published" .

hemo:5000902 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected number of unpublished studies implied by Copas selection parameters for two standard error groups"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-COPAS-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000895 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006171 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003778,
        hemo:6003779,
        hemo:6003780,
        hemo:6003781,
        hemo:6003782,
        hemo:6003783,
        hemo:6003784,
        hemo:6003785,
        hemo:6003786,
        hemo:6003787,
        hemo:6003788,
        hemo:6003789,
        hemo:6003790 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003778,
        hemo:6003779,
        hemo:6003780,
        hemo:6003781,
        hemo:6003782,
        hemo:6003783,
        hemo:6003786,
        hemo:6003787 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003784,
        hemo:6003785,
        hemo:6003790 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006168,
        hemo:7006169,
        hemo:7006170 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006172,
        hemo:7006173,
        hemo:7006174,
        hemo:7006175,
        hemo:7006176 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Copas and Shi translate a pair of selection parameters into the expected number of unpublished studies, M, the sum over the published studies of (1 minus P_i) / P_i, with P_i the publication probability of HE-FM-COPAS-001 at study i's standard error. The formula is written for two groups of published studies that share a standard error within each group, n_1 at s_1 and n_2 at s_2, as in the article's example of two large and ten small trials; each further standard error adds a term of the same form, which the R and Python code of HE-CF-COPAS-001 handles. Each published study with probability P stands in expectation for (1 minus P) / P unpublished studies of the same precision, so groups with a low publication probability dominate M; in the article's moderate scenario 18.57 of the 18.68 come from the ten small trials. The step that turns P into M is the same algebra as the bed turnover interval of HE-FM-BTO-004 in another setting; the Copas content is the chain from gamma_0, gamma_1 and the standard errors." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected number of unpublished studies implied by Copas selection parameters for two standard error groups" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Carpenter J, Rücker G, Schwarzer G. copas: an R package for fitting the Copas selection model. The R Journal. 2009;1(2):31-36 (full text read). Figure 1, the summary.copas output for nine trials of phenobarbital and periventricular haemorrhage, lists for each probability of publishing the study with the largest standard error the approximate number of studies the model suggests remain unpublished (N.unpubl), 0 at a probability of 1.00 and 9 at 0.37. The paper does not print the formula behind N.unpubl. https://doi.org/10.32614/RJ-2009-012",
        "Huang A, Komukai S, Friede T, Hattori S. Using clinical trial registries to inform Copas selection model for publication bias in meta-analysis. Research Synthesis Methods. 2021;12(5):658-673 (preprint arXiv:2005.14396 full text read; journal abstract read). Preprint section 3.1, Brief review of the sensitivity analysis method by Copas and Shi, states that (alpha_0, alpha_1) were fixed as sensitivity parameters and (theta, tau, rho) estimated by maximising the conditional log-likelihood, and that according to the formula by Copas and Shi one can translate (alpha_0, alpha_1) into the expected number of unpublished studies by M = sum over the N published studies of {1 minus P(Y_i > 0 | s_i)} / P(Y_i > 0 | s_i), which is more interpretable. Their alpha_0, alpha_1 and Y_i are the gamma_0, gamma_1 and Z_i of the article. The abstract proposes using information in clinical trial registries to estimate all unknown parameters. https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.14396" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Average publication probability used for the Copas unpublished count. Because (1 minus P) / P is convex in P, giving all twelve trials of the moderate scenario their average probability of 0.45 returns 14.67 unpublished trials instead of 18.68, about a fifth too few (computed here for illustration). The count has to be built group by group or study by study.",
        "One grid point's implied count taken as the number of missing studies. M follows from the assumed selection, not from the data. In the article's illustration the moderate and severe scenarios imply about 19 and about 57 unpublished trials, yet with the correlation re-estimated both reproduce the group means, so the published trials cannot choose between them. The count is a figure to set against trial registries, which Huang and colleagues use to inform the model.",
        "Total studies run reported as the Copas unpublished count. n / P counts the published studies as well as the unpublished ones. In the moderate scenario about 30.68 trials would have been run, of which 18.68 are unpublished; reporting 30.68 adds the twelve published trials to the count." ;
    hemo:9000014 "u_1 = gamma_0 + gamma_1 / s_1; u_2 = gamma_0 + gamma_1 / s_2; P_1 = Phi_1; P_2 = Phi_2; M = n_1 * (1 - P_1) / P_1 + n_2 * (1 - P_2) / P_2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-COPAS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-number-of-unpublished-studies-implied-by-copas-selection-parameters-for-two-standard-error-groups" .

hemo:5000905 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Net days in accounts receivable from net receivables and average daily net patient revenue"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-DAR-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000897 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006207 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003818,
        hemo:6003819,
        hemo:6003820,
        hemo:6003821,
        hemo:6003822 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003819,
        hemo:6003820,
        hemo:6003821 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003818 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006203,
        hemo:7006204,
        hemo:7006205,
        hemo:7006206 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006208,
        hemo:7006209,
        hemo:7006210,
        hemo:7006211,
        hemo:7006212,
        hemo:7006213 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Divides the net patient receivables on the balance sheet at the end of a period by the average daily net patient service revenue of the same period, the HFMA MAP Key FM-1 net days in accounts receivable, so that receivables of providers of any size read on one scale of days. The measure applies to providers paid claim by claim, where each episode of care has to be coded, billed, adjudicated and collected before it turns into cash. Applied to one payer group the same formula gives that group's own days figure, and the overall figure is the revenue-weighted mean of the group figures by the weighted-mean identity of HE-FM-CRAT-001. Multiplying DAR by r_bar returns the receivables balance, the input to the carrying cost and the cash released that HE-FN-DAR-001 describes." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Net days in accounts receivable from net receivables and average daily net patient revenue" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Healthcare Financial Management Association. MAP Keys, industry-standard revenue cycle KPIs. HFMA MAP Initiative web page, accessed 18 October 2026. FM-1, Average Daily Net Patient Service Revenue, defined as the most recent three-month daily average of total net patient service revenue, with net patient service revenue equal to gross patient service revenue minus contractual allowances, minus charity care provision, then minus the provision for doubtful accounts. The definition counts the days in the three months including the last month reported, so the three months ending June 30 use April (30 days), May (31 days) and June (30 days), 91 days in all, and the FM-1 exclusions for both net A/R and revenue include capitation and premium revenue related to value-based or risk-based payer contracts. https://www.hfma.org/data-and-insights/map-initiative/map-keys/",
        "Healthcare Financial Management Association. MAP Keys, industry-standard revenue cycle KPIs. HFMA MAP Initiative web page, accessed 18 October 2026. Financial Management group, Net Days in Accounts Receivable (A/R) (FM-1), whose equation divides net A/R from the balance sheet by average daily net patient service revenue from the income statement, describes the measure as a trending indicator of overall A/R performance, and defines net A/R as the net patient receivable on the balance sheet, net of credit balances, allowances for uncollectible accounts, discounts for charity care and contractual allowances for third-party payers. https://www.hfma.org/data-and-insights/map-initiative/map-keys/",
        "NHS England. 2026/27 NHS Standard Contract, Service Conditions (Full Length) (PRN02123). Version 1. London, NHS England; November 2025. SC36.12, under which each commissioner that has agreed an Expected Annual Contract Value must make payments on account to the provider, and SC36.13, under which, for an NHS Trust or NHS Foundation Trust, each commissioner pays on the fifteenth day of each month (or another day agreed in writing) one twelfth, or another proportion specified in Schedule 3D, of its Expected Annual Contract Value. https://www.england.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/03-nhssc-26-27-full-length-service-conditions.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "A fixed 90-day denominator in place of calendar days in days in accounts receivable. For the quarter ending 30 June, dividing the USD 91.0 million of revenue by 90 days in place of the 91 calendar days gives r_bar of 1.0111 and 54.40 days in place of 55.0. HFMA counts the actual days of the three months, which differ between quarters and in leap years.",
        "Comparing days in accounts receivable of an NHS trust with hospitals paid per claim. Under the NHS Standard Contract each commissioner pays an NHS trust one twelfth of the expected annual contract value on the fifteenth day of each month, unless another day or proportion is agreed, payments on account that do not wait for claims. A trust's receivables from its main commissioners therefore reflect contract timing and in-year adjustments, and comparing its figure with that of a US hospital paid claim by claim says nothing about collection performance.",
        "Net receivables divided by gross daily charges in days in accounts receivable. Dividing the article's USD 55.0 million of net receivables by illustrative gross daily charges of USD 2.5 million gives 22.0 days, 40% of the 55.0 net days, because list charges are far above what payers pay. Gross receivables go over gross revenue and net over net.",
        "Reading a payer-mix shift as slower collection in days in accounts receivable. In the article's example the overall figure rises to 61.25 days against 55.0, 6.25 days or about 11.4% higher, when the public payer's share of revenue falls by ten points to 40%, although no payer group collects any more slowly. Comparing the group days, or their revenue-weighted mean at fixed revenue shares (the weighted-mean algebra of HE-FM-CRAT-001, there with member numbers as weights), separates the mix effect from the performance of the billing office.",
        "Taking a fall in net days from write-offs or allowances as faster collection. Raising the allowance for uncollectible accounts, or netting growing credit balances against debit balances, shrinks net receivables without any cash coming in. In the article's quarter an extra USD 5.0 million of allowance would take net days from 55.0 to 50.0 with nothing collected (computed here for illustration). HFMA's AR-1 ageing of billed receivables shows whether the fall came from collecting old accounts or from removing them, and its AR-9 net days in credit balance shows whether the credit balances netted off have grown." ;
    hemo:9000014 "r_bar = R / D; DAR = AR / r_bar" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-DAR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "net-days-in-accounts-receivable-from-net-receivables-and-average-daily-net-patient-revenue" .

hemo:5000906 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Heterotrait-monotrait ratio for two constructs with two indicators each"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-DISV-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000898 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006218 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003823,
        hemo:6003824,
        hemo:6003825,
        hemo:6003826,
        hemo:6003827,
        hemo:6003828,
        hemo:6003829,
        hemo:6003830 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003823,
        hemo:6003824,
        hemo:6003825,
        hemo:6003826,
        hemo:6003827,
        hemo:6003828 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003830 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006214,
        hemo:7006215,
        hemo:7006216,
        hemo:7006217 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006219,
        hemo:7006220,
        hemo:7006221,
        hemo:7006222,
        hemo:7006223 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "With two indicators per construct each within-construct mean is a single correlation, r_i1i2 or r_j1j2, and the between-construct mean rbar_ij has four terms, so the heterotrait-monotrait ratio is rbar_ij divided by the square root of r_i1i2 times r_j1j2. This is Eq. 6 of Henseler, Ringle and Sarstedt with K_i and K_j both equal to 2, and HE-CF-DISV-001 takes any numbers of indicators. The authors describe the ratio as an estimate of the correlation between the two constructs, so a value clearly below 1 indicates constructs that differ. Used as a criterion, a value above a cut-off of 0.85 or 0.90 points to a lack of discriminant validity, a level the authors call debatable. In the article's example the questionnaire and the clinician rating serve as the two indicators of physical functioning and of mood." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Heterotrait-monotrait ratio for two constructs with two indicators each" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Henseler J, Ringle CM, Sarstedt M. A new criterion for assessing discriminant validity in variance-based structural equation modeling. Journal of the Academy of Marketing Science. 2015;43(1):115-135. doi:10.1007/s11747-014-0403-8 (open access, full text read on the publisher page). The section on the heterotrait-monotrait ratio of the correlations approach, Eq. 6, which defines the HTMT of constructs i and j with K_i and K_j indicators as the average of the heterotrait-heteromethod correlations, those between the indicators of the two constructs, divided by the geometric mean of the average monotrait-heteromethod correlations, those among the indicators within each construct. The same section states that the HTMT is an estimate of the correlation between the constructs and that the multitrait-multimethod approach requires at least two constructs from the same respondents. The two-indicator form is Eq. 6 with K_i and K_j equal to 2 (derived here). https://doi.org/10.1007/s11747-014-0403-8",
        "Henseler J, Ringle CM, Sarstedt M. A new criterion for assessing discriminant validity in variance-based structural equation modeling. Journal of the Academy of Marketing Science. 2015;43(1):115-135. doi:10.1007/s11747-014-0403-8 (open access, full text read on the publisher page). The section on the heterotrait-monotrait ratio, which states that a value higher than a predefined threshold indicates a lack of discriminant validity, that the exact threshold is debatable, and that some authors suggest 0.85 and others 0.90. The section Key findings and recommendations calls the 0.85 criterion the most conservative and regards a more liberal criterion as warranted for constructs that are conceptually different but hard to distinguish empirically, and the section Further research and concluding remarks states that the HTMT-based criteria assume reflectively measured constructs. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11747-014-0403-8" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Arithmetic mean in the HTMT denominator. Eq. 6 uses the geometric mean of the two within-construct means, which for positive correlations is never larger than their arithmetic mean, so the arithmetic version understates the ratio (derived here). With the article's 0.64 and 0.52 the gap is small, 0.4914 against 0.494, but with illustrative within-construct correlations of 0.90 and 0.30 and a between-construct mean of 0.45 the ratio is 0.866, above 0.85, against 0.75 with the arithmetic mean, which misses the flag (computed here).",
        "Passing HTMT ratio read as clean discriminant evidence. In the article's example the ratio of 0.494 passes easily, yet the questionnaire's two subscales correlate 0.45, only 0.07 below the mood validity value of 0.52, a method problem confined to the self-reports that the pooled ratio averages away. The article notes that if both subscales then enter a mapping function to EQ-5D as separate predictors, part of what each coefficient picks up is a shared reporting style, so the multitrait-multimethod table is read alongside the ratio.",
        "Reverse-coded indicator left in the HTMT ratio. If the clinician mood rating in the article's example were scored so that higher means worse, its three correlations would change sign, the between-construct mean would fall to 0.045 and the product under the square root would be 0.64 times minus 0.52, so the ratio is undefined and Excel returns #NUM!. With both mood indicators reversed the ratio is minus 0.494, below any cut-off however much the constructs overlap (computed here).",
        "Same-method correlations dropped from the HTMT numerator. Averaging only the different-method cells 0.18 and 0.21, the discriminant cells of the multitrait-multimethod matrix, gives 0.195 in place of 0.285 and a ratio of 0.338 in place of 0.494, 32% lower (computed here). Henseler and colleagues define the heterotrait-heteromethod part as all the correlations between the indicators of the two constructs, and in the article's example the dropped same-method value of 0.45 is where the shared method variance shows." ;
    hemo:9000014 "rbar_ij = (r_i1j1 + r_i1j2 + r_i2j1 + r_i2j2) / 4; HTMT_ij = rbar_ij / sqrt(r_i1i2 * r_j1j2)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-DISV-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "heterotrait-monotrait-ratio-for-two-constructs-with-two-indicators-each" .

hemo:5000912 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Phelps welfare loss and optimal coinsurance function for medical insurance"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-MHHI-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004118,
        hemo:6004119,
        hemo:6004120,
        hemo:6004121,
        hemo:6004122,
        hemo:6004123,
        hemo:6004124 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004118,
        hemo:6004119,
        hemo:6004120,
        hemo:6004121,
        hemo:6004122 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004123,
        hemo:6004124 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a coinsurance rate C, the coefficient of absolute risk aversion r, the standard deviation sigma and mean mu of annual medical spending without insurance, and the absolute price elasticity of demand eps to the expected welfare loss per person of a policy with that rate, and maps the same parameters to the coinsurance rate that minimises the loss. The loss is the second-order approximation of Phelps, the risk premium of the share of spending the patient still bears plus the welfare-loss triangle of insurance-induced care. The triangle is the live HE-FM-MH-002 written in spending units (HE-FM-MH-001 gives it from prices and quantities, and HE-FN-MH-001 is the general ex post moral hazard function), and HE-FM-DWL-003 gives the fall in that loss when the rate rises, from spending at the two rates (with straight-line demand it equals the triangle at the lower rate minus the triangle at the higher rate, derived). The risk premium has no live record, since HE-FM-EV-002 gives the second-order expansion only in the output's own units. The cost-sharing split of a plan with a deductible and an out-of-pocket maximum is HE-FM-AV-002, the RAND arc elasticity behind the usual elasticity estimates is HE-FM-MH-003, and the fair premium in the article's Nyman illustration is HE-FM-ACTA-001 with HE-FM-ACTA-002 at zero loadings. Notation follows the Moral Hazard in Health Insurance article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Phelps welfare loss and optimal coinsurance function for medical insurance" ;
    hemo:9000014 "z(C, r, sigma, eps, mu) = (WL, C_star)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-MHHI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "phelps-welfare-loss-and-optimal-coinsurance-function-for-medical-insurance" .

hemo:5000918 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Value measurement function for MCDA in HTA from criterion performance, ranges and swing points"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-MCDH-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004152,
        hemo:6004153,
        hemo:6004154,
        hemo:6004155,
        hemo:6004156,
        hemo:6004157,
        hemo:6004158 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004152,
        hemo:6004153,
        hemo:6004154,
        hemo:6004155 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004156,
        hemo:6004157,
        hemo:6004158 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps each technology's performance on each criterion, the fixed range of each criterion and the swing points given to each criterion to a partial value score per criterion on a 0 to 100 interval scale, a normalised weight per criterion and an overall value per technology. This is the value measurement approach, which the first ISPOR MCDA task force report describes as by far the most widely used in health care. HE-FM-MCDH-001 gives the linear partial value score, HE-FM-MCDH-002 the swing weights and the additive overall value, and HE-CF-MCDH-001 runs both for three technologies with a ranking and a scan of one weight. The second report notes that additive functions are also used in health-related quality of life instruments, and HE-FM-15D-001, the 15D score as an additive weighted sum of level values with importance weights elicited from population samples, is a live instance of that form; here the weights come from swing points elicited over stated ranges. When one weight varies and the others keep their ratio, each total is linear in that weight, and the weight at which the totals of two technologies are equal, a change of ranking only when it is at least 0 and at most 1, takes the form of the switching weight w_s of HE-FM-WE-004, which that record computes only for the QALYs of two groups, here with the other-criteria blend in place of group A QALYs and the first score less that blend in place of group B QALYs (derived). Dividing a cost by an overall value takes the form of HE-FM-ACER-001, and the second report states that such a cost-benefit ratio needs benefits on a ratio scale, which these totals lack. The partial value score has the algebra of 100 times the blend share p of HE-FM-EXD-002, which that record computes only for the expected effects of three options ordered along a frontier; here the ends are the anchors of a criterion's fixed range and run in decreasing order when lower performance is better. The weights have the algebra of the share in HE-FM-CJA-003, which that record computes for conjoint part-worth ranges, and the overall value equals the points-weighted mean (p_1 v_1 + p_2 v_2 + p_3 v_3) / (p_1 + p_2 + p_3), the form of HE-FM-CRAT-001, which computes it for member counts and expected costs (derived). The swing of HE-FM-OWSA-001 is the width of a tornado bar, not a swing weight. Notation follows the MCDA in HTA article, with v_1, v_2 and v_3 for v_j(x_aj) and V for V(a)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Value measurement function for MCDA in HTA from criterion performance, ranges and swing points" ;
    hemo:9000014 "m(x_aj, x_worst_j, x_best_j, p_j) = (v_aj, w_j, V_a)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-MCDH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "value-measurement-function-for-mcda-in-hta-from-criterion-performance-ranges-and-swing-points" .

hemo:5000919 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Designer's problem of a direct mechanism with incentive and participation constraints in its two-type screening form"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-MECH-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004159,
        hemo:6004160,
        hemo:6004161,
        hemo:6004162,
        hemo:6004163,
        hemo:6004164,
        hemo:6004165,
        hemo:6004166,
        hemo:6004167 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004159,
        hemo:6004160,
        hemo:6004161,
        hemo:6004162 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004163,
        hemo:6004164,
        hemo:6004165,
        hemo:6004166,
        hemo:6004167 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the designer's objective, the distribution of participants' private types and a candidate allocation rule x(theta) and payment rule t(theta) to the designer's expected objective, subject to incentive compatibility, under which each type does at least as well reporting truthfully as mimicking any other type, and participation, under which each type does at least as well as by staying out, as in the article's display. The general problem is an optimisation over functions and has no calculator form. Its simplest instance, one supplier whose unit cost is low (theta_L) or high (theta_H) and a purchaser who offers a menu of output and transfer pairs, is the screening model of Laffont and Martimort, in which the allocation is a quantity q and the payment a transfer t. HE-FM-MECH-001 checks whether a given menu satisfies the four constraints and reports the information rents, HE-FM-MECH-002 gives the purchaser's second-best menu with a linear marginal value of output, and HE-CF-MECH-001 compares that menu with shutting out the high-cost type and with the complete-information benchmark when the supplier has a positive outside option. A type's payoff on its own contract, transfer less cost times quantity, takes the form of the margin in HE-FM-CTH-001 with no reimbursed share, which computes it per case of hospital care. The Ellis and McGuire records HE-FN-CTH-001 to HE-CF-CTH-001 set a payment rule for a provider whose parameters are known, with no hidden type. The article's tender profit of GBP 60,000, a margin of GBP 0.60 per pack times 100,000 packs, is a price margin times a volume, the form of HE-FM-CSKM-002 with one risk group, and the second-price rule that sets the price belongs to the auction-theory-health concept, so neither is restated here. Matching and kidney exchange rules are algorithms on a compatibility graph and have no formula record. Notation follows the Mechanism Design article for the general problem and Laffont and Martimort for the two-type case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Designer's problem of a direct mechanism with incentive and participation constraints in its two-type screening form" ;
    hemo:9000014 "m(S, theta_L, theta_H, nu) = (q_L, q_H, t_L, t_H, U_L)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-MECH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "designer-s-problem-of-a-direct-mechanism-with-incentive-and-participation-constraints-in-its-two-type-screening-form" .

hemo:5000922 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Integer optimum of the 0-1 funding problem for four indivisible programmes with its gaps to a funding rule and to the divisible fill"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-IPH-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000913 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006314 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004181,
        hemo:6004182,
        hemo:6004183,
        hemo:6004184,
        hemo:6004185,
        hemo:6004186,
        hemo:6004187,
        hemo:6004188,
        hemo:6004189,
        hemo:6004190,
        hemo:6004191,
        hemo:6004192,
        hemo:6004193,
        hemo:6004194 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004182,
        hemo:6004183,
        hemo:6004184,
        hemo:6004185,
        hemo:6004186,
        hemo:6004187,
        hemo:6004188,
        hemo:6004189,
        hemo:6004190,
        hemo:6004191,
        hemo:6004193 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004192,
        hemo:6004194 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006311,
        hemo:7006312,
        hemo:7006313 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006315,
        hemo:7006316,
        hemo:7006317,
        hemo:7006318,
        hemo:7006319 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each of the 15 packages that fund at least one of four programmes contributes its total QALYs when its total cost is at most the budget and zero when it is not, and H_star is the largest contribution, the optimum of the article's binary problem of maximising the sum of q_i x_i subject to the sum of c_i x_i at most B with each x_i equal to 0 or 1. Funding nothing gives zero, so H_star is zero when no programme fits. G_rule is the health that a funding rule's package, with health H_rule, leaves unbought at the same budget, the loss of benefit that Crown and colleagues say optimisation shows for the choice finally made, and with H_rule taken from HE-FM-CELT-003 it is the gain of the optimum over the league table. L_ind is the health the divisible fill of HE-FM-CELT-001, H_div, buys above the optimum, the health lost purely because programmes cannot be split (derived). The formula is written out for four programmes so that it runs as calculator text, and HE-CF-IPH-001 does the same search for any number." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Integer optimum of the 0-1 funding problem for four indivisible programmes with its gaps to a funding rule and to the divisible fill" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Crown W, Buyukkaramikli N, Sir MY, Thokala P, Morton A, Marshall DA, Tosh JC, Ijzerman MJ, Padula WV, Pasupathy KS. Application of constrained optimization methods in health services research: report 2 of the ISPOR Optimization Methods Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2018;21(9):1019-1028. Section 2, Overview of applications of constrained optimization in health care (accepted manuscript), which describes a planner with a number of investment opportunities and a fixed budget inadequate to fund them all, states that choosing the best set to fund under a fixed budget to meet an objective such as maximising total QALYs can be addressed as an optimisation problem, citing Martello and Toth on knapsack problems, and gives vaccination leaving fewer cases to treat as an example of interactions between investments. The abstract's Conclusions state that the methods give decision makers insight into optimal target solutions and the magnitude of the loss of benefit or increased costs associated with the ultimate policy choice. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2018.05.003",
        "Crown W, Buyukkaramikli N, Thokala P, Morton A, Sir MY, Marshall DA, Tosh J, Padula WV, Ijzerman MJ, Wong PK, Pasupathy KS. Constrained optimization methods in health services research: an introduction. Report 1 of the ISPOR Optimization Methods Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2017;20(3):310-319. Section A Simple Illustration of a Constrained Optimization Problem, which writes the problem of a health care centre as maximising health benefit f_R x_R + f_L x_L subject to a budget constraint c_R x_R + c_L x_L at most B and a time constraint, with the decision variables zero or above and integer, places it in linear optimisation because the objective and constraints are linear while the decision variables must be integers, and warns that rounding a linear solution can give an infeasible solution or, if feasible, one that is not optimal. Section Definition of Constrained Optimization notes that in some problems two or more different decisions may yield the same optimum, and the table of model components contrasts deterministic and stochastic problems. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2017.01.013",
        "Dakin H, Tsiachristas A. Rationing in an era of multiple tight constraints: is cost-utility analysis still fit for purpose? Applied Health Economics and Health Policy. 2024;22(3):315-329 (author manuscript read in Europe PMC, PMC7615833). Section 2.1, Constrained optimisation, which states that constrained optimisation can allocate resources across a healthcare system to maximise population health conditional on the budget and any other constraints, calls this an integer programming approach that has been adapted for healthcare, and frames the allocation as a 0-1 knapsack problem of choosing which items to pack to maximise value without exceeding a weight constraint or packing more than one of each item. Section 2.2 states that the league table, adopting interventions from the lowest ICER upwards until the budget is exhausted, is equivalent to the greedy algorithm for the knapsack problem. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40258-023-00858-w",
        "Stinnett AA, Paltiel AD. Mathematical programming for the efficient allocation of health care resources. Journal of Health Economics. 1996;15(5):641-653. Abstract, which states that previous discussions of allocating health care resources subject to a budget constraint relied on unnecessarily restrictive assumptions, that a general mathematical programming framework can accommodate returns to scale, partial and complete indivisibility and programme interdependence, and that methods are presented for incorporating ethical constraints, including explicit identification of the cost of equity. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0167-6296(96)00493-6" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Adding the gains of programmes that serve overlapping patients. If B and C in the article's table served overlapping patients, so that funding both gained an illustrative 280 QALYs in place of 375, the additive model would still choose B plus C and report 375, while A plus D, at 290, would then be the better package by 10 QALYs (computed here for illustration). Interacting programmes need a joint cost and gain for the combination, or extra variables and constraints, before the search.",
        "Reporting the divisible fill as the health a budget buys from whole programmes. The divisible fill of the article's table reports 392 QALYs for GBP 10 million, but no package of whole programmes buys more than 375, so 17 of the reported QALYs cannot be delivered. H_div is a ceiling on H_star for independent programmes, not an achievable total, as HE-ER-CELT-006 shows for the league table.",
        "Rounding the divisible answer in place of solving the 0-1 funding problem. For the article's programmes the divisible answer funds A and 0.8 of B. Rounding the share of B up gives A plus B at GBP 11 million, over the GBP 10 million budget, and rounding it down gives A alone at 240 QALYs, 135 below the optimum of 375 (computed here for illustration). Crown and colleagues warn that a rounded solution may be infeasible, or feasible but not optimal." ;
    hemo:9000014 "H_star = max(max(max(max(max(max(max(max(max(max(max(max(max(max((c_1 <= B) * q_1, (c_2 <= B) * q_2), (c_3 <= B) * q_3), (c_4 <= B) * q_4), (c_1 + c_2 <= B) * (q_1 + q_2)), (c_1 + c_3 <= B) * (q_1 + q_3)), (c_1 + c_4 <= B) * (q_1 + q_4)), (c_2 + c_3 <= B) * (q_2 + q_3)), (c_2 + c_4 <= B) * (q_2 + q_4)), (c_3 + c_4 <= B) * (q_3 + q_4)), (c_1 + c_2 + c_3 <= B) * (q_1 + q_2 + q_3)), (c_1 + c_2 + c_4 <= B) * (q_1 + q_2 + q_4)), (c_1 + c_3 + c_4 <= B) * (q_1 + q_3 + q_4)), (c_2 + c_3 + c_4 <= B) * (q_2 + q_3 + q_4)), (c_1 + c_2 + c_3 + c_4 <= B) * (q_1 + q_2 + q_3 + q_4)); G_rule = H_star - H_rule; L_ind = H_div - H_star" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-IPH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "integer-optimum-of-the-0-1-funding-problem-for-four-indivisible-programmes-with-its-gaps-to-a-funding-rule-and-to-the-divisible-fill" .

hemo:5000928 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Erlang loss refusal probability, annual refusals and achieved occupancy of a six-bed unit"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CAPP-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000917 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006369 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004216,
        hemo:6004217,
        hemo:6004218,
        hemo:6004219,
        hemo:6004220,
        hemo:6004221 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004216,
        hemo:6004217 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004220,
        hemo:6004221 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006365,
        hemo:7006366,
        hemo:7006367,
        hemo:7006368 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006370,
        hemo:7006371,
        hemo:7006372,
        hemo:7006373,
        hemo:7006374,
        hemo:7006375 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives, for a unit of six staffed beds with Poisson arrivals, the share of arriving patients who find all six beds occupied and are refused, the Erlang loss formula of de Bruin and colleagues written out term by term, with the offered load a equal to arrivals per day times the mean stay. Refusals a year are the annual arrivals times that share, and the achieved occupancy is the load actually carried, a times one minus the refusal probability, over the six beds, de Bruin and colleagues' occupancy rate for the loss model, which takes the form of HE-FM-ALOS-002 solved for o with the patients admitted, not the patients arriving, as the discharges. Achieved occupancy is always below a / 6 because refused patients use no bed-days. The model needs only the mean stay, not the shape of the length of stay distribution. Other numbers of beds follow the same pattern with terms up to a^N / N!, which HE-CF-CAPP-001 computes by recursion." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Erlang loss refusal probability, annual refusals and achieved occupancy of a six-bed unit" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Green L. Queueing analysis in healthcare. In: Hall RW, editor. Patient Flow: Reducing Delay in Healthcare Delivery. New York: Springer; 2006. p. 281-307. Section 2.2.3, Some useful extensions of the M/M/s model, which calls the finite capacity case with no waiting room for arrivals who find all servers busy a pure loss model; section 2.2.2, The M/M/s model, which gives the probability of delay in equation 11-1 for systems in which patients wait; and section 2.2.1, The Poisson process, which reports the Poisson process as a good representation of unscheduled arrivals to ICUs, obstetrics units and EDs. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-33636-7_10",
        "Tuenter HJH. Erlang's loss function and a property of the exponential function. arXiv preprint math/0612052, version 1, 2 December 2006. Preprint section 2, equation (4), which defines Erlang's loss function B(n, lambda) as lambda^n / n! divided by the sum over j from 0 to n of lambda^j / j!, the probability that calls arriving in a Poisson stream of rate lambda to a link of n circuits, each call requesting unit service time, find all circuits occupied and are lost. https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0612052",
        "de Bruin AM, Bekker R, van Zanten L, Koole GM. Dimensioning hospital wards using the Erlang loss model. Annals of Operations Research. 2010;178(1):23-43. Section 4, which assumes that an arriving patient who finds all beds occupied is blocked and leaves the system, and section 4.1, The M/G/c/c queuing model, with expression (i) for the fraction of patients blocked, P_c = ((lambda mu)^c / c!) / sum over k from 0 to c of (lambda mu)^k / k!, where mu is the expected length of stay and c the operational beds, the note that the model is insensitive to the length of stay distribution, expression (ii) for the occupancy rate, one minus P_c times lambda mu, divided by c, and the name offered load for lambda mu. Section 4.2 estimates lambda from the average number of occupied beds with expression (iii). Table 6 gives the coronary care unit's 6 operational beds and 3.52 daily arrivals, and Table 7 its mean stay of 1.694 days, 73.3% occupancy and 26.2% refused admissions. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10479-009-0647-8" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Offered load over beds read as the achieved occupancy of a loss unit. Dividing the offered load by the beds ignores the bed-days refused patients never use. For the coronary care unit a / 6 is 0.994, while the achieved occupancy is 0.733 (computed here), so a plan that reads the first figure as occupancy hides a quarter of its patients being turned away.",
        "Poisson overflow used in place of the Erlang loss probability. The probability that a Poisson count with mean a exceeds six, the complement of the Poisson sum up to the capacity that HE-CF-ARRP-001 uses for hourly arrivals, counts refused patients as if they still occupied beds. For the coronary care unit it gives 0.388 against the loss probability of 0.262, about 48% too high (computed here).",
        "Recorded admissions entered as the arrival rate of the six-bed loss model. Hospital systems record admissions, not refused patients, so admissions understate demand wherever beds run short. For de Bruin and colleagues' coronary care unit, entering the roughly 2.60 patients a day actually admitted (computed here from 3.52 arrivals with 26.2% refused) in place of 3.52 gives a refusal probability of 0.147 in place of 0.262 and about 139 refusals a year in place of 337. De Bruin and colleagues estimate the arrival rate from the average number of occupied beds instead.",
        "Refusal probability read as the chance of waiting for a bed. P_block counts patients turned away. If patients who find the unit full wait instead, the M/M/s probability of delay in Green's equation 11-1, which assumes exponential stays, applies and is higher; for the illustrative six-bed unit with a load of 2.96 it is 0.094 against a refusal probability of 0.050 (computed here). The article's 95-bed unit shows the same gap, a 6.8% chance of waiting against 1.14% refused." ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = lambda * L_bar; P_block = (a^6 / 720) / (1 + a + a^2 / 2 + a^3 / 6 + a^4 / 24 + a^5 / 120 + a^6 / 720); R = 365 * lambda * P_block; o_ach = a * (1 - P_block) / 6" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CAPP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "erlang-loss-refusal-probability-annual-refusals-and-achieved-occupancy-of-a-six-bed-unit" .

hemo:5000929 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Linear partial value score of a technology on one MCDA criterion"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-MCDH-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000918 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006379 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004222,
        hemo:6004223,
        hemo:6004224,
        hemo:6004225 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004223,
        hemo:6004224,
        hemo:6004225 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004222 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006376,
        hemo:7006377,
        hemo:7006378 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006380,
        hemo:7006381,
        hemo:7006382,
        hemo:7006383 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Places a technology's performance on one criterion on a 0 to 100 scale whose ends are the worst and best performance of the criterion's fixed range, so equal changes in performance earn equal changes in score. The same line serves a criterion on which lower performance is better, such as a rate of adverse events, by entering the worse, higher level as x_worst, as the first ISPOR MCDA task force appendix does for its criterion B. Zero marks the bottom of the chosen range, not an absence of value, so differences in score can be compared but ratios cannot. Where equal changes in performance are not equally valued, the score is read from an elicited nonlinear partial value function instead." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Linear partial value score of a technology on one MCDA criterion" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Marsh K, IJzerman M, Thokala P, Baltussen R, Boysen M, Kaló Z, et al. Multiple criteria decision analysis for health care decision making: emerging good practices. Report 2 of the ISPOR MCDA Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2016;19(2):125-137. Step 2b, Report and justify the criteria definitions, states that the use of criteria over multiple decisions, as in HTA, requires a range covering the best and worst performance that could realistically occur (global or fixed scales), while a one-off decision can use the best and worst performance of the alternatives evaluated (local or relative scales). Step 4a, Report and justify the methods used for scoring, notes that a 0 to 100 scale is often used and that, for a range of 6 (scored 0) to 10 (scored 100) with a linear partial value function, going from 6 to 8 is as good as going from 8 to 10, but 10 is not twice as good as 8. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2015.12.016",
        "Thokala P, Devlin N, Marsh K, Baltussen R, Boysen M, Kalo Z, et al. Multiple criteria decision analysis for health care decision making: an introduction. Report 1 of the ISPOR MCDA Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2016;19(1):1-13. Appendix, MCDA Modeling Example, Scoring, develops partial value functions over the feasible range of each criterion, with the score often defined on a scale of 0 to 100 points, and assumes linear functions for criterion A (65 to 90 aa, higher better) and criterion B (5 to 10 bb, lower better) in Figures A1 and A2; Table A2 gives the scores 80 and 32 on criterion A and 40 and 70 on criterion B, and the Uncertainty Analysis paragraph scores 6 bb on criterion B at 80. Criterion C has a nonlinear function (Figure A3), which the appendix says could be developed by the bisection method. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2015.12.003" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Local range of the appraised technologies in place of a fixed MCDA scale. Scoring health gain on the range of the three technologies in hand, 0 to 0.6 QALYs, in place of the fixed 0 to 0.8 range gives A 100 and B 66.67 in place of 75 and 50, and with the article's weights A's total becomes 85 and B's 73.33 in place of 72.5 and 65 (computed here for illustration). The swing weight of 0.50 was judged for a swing of 0.8 QALYs, so it no longer matches the scores, and the scale moves whenever a new technology widens the range. The second ISPOR report keeps local scales for one-off decisions and asks for global scales where criteria are reused, as in HTA.",
        "Reading MCDA partial value scores as ratios. In the article's example severity scores 50 for a moderate and 100 for a severe condition, which does not make treating a severe condition twice as valuable, because zero marks a mild condition, the bottom of the chosen range. The second ISPOR report makes the same point with a range of 6 to 10, on which 10 scores 100 and 8 scores 50 but 10 is not twice as good as 8.",
        "Worst and best ends swapped on a lower-is-better MCDA criterion. Entering 5 bb as x_worst and 10 bb as x_best for the ISPOR appendix's criterion B, on which lower is better, returns 100 less the correct score, 60 in place of 40 for alternative 1 and 30 in place of 70 for alternative 2, so the alternative with the lower, better level is scored as the worse one." ;
    hemo:9000014 "v = 100 * (x - x_worst) / (x_best - x_worst)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-MCDH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "linear-partial-value-score-of-a-technology-on-one-mcda-criterion" .

hemo:5000930 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Swing-weighted additive value of a technology on three MCDA criteria"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-MCDH-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000918 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006388 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004226,
        hemo:6004227,
        hemo:6004228,
        hemo:6004229,
        hemo:6004230,
        hemo:6004231,
        hemo:6004232,
        hemo:6004233,
        hemo:6004234,
        hemo:6004235 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004230,
        hemo:6004231,
        hemo:6004232,
        hemo:6004233,
        hemo:6004234,
        hemo:6004235 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004226 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006384,
        hemo:7006385,
        hemo:7006386,
        hemo:7006387 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006389,
        hemo:7006390,
        hemo:7006391,
        hemo:7006392,
        hemo:7006393,
        hemo:7006394 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Divides each criterion's swing points by the total points to give weights that sum to one, then multiplies each partial value score by its criterion's weight and adds the three products, the additive (weighted-sum) model that both ISPOR MCDA task force reports describe as the usual aggregation in health care. With partial values on a 0 to 100 scale the overall value is on the same scale. The weights act as scaling constants for the ranges over which the swings were judged, so a change of range calls for new points. The formula is written for three criteria, the article's health gain, severity and strength of evidence; more criteria add terms in the same way, and HE-CF-MCDH-001 applies it to several technologies." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Swing-weighted additive value of a technology on three MCDA criteria" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Belton V, Stewart TJ. Multiple criteria decision analysis: an integrated approach. Kluwer Academic Publishers; 2002. Chapter 5, Value Function Methods: Practical Basics (pages 119 to 161), the standard textbook account of multiattribute value theory (MAVT) used in practice for multi-criteria decision support. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4615-1495-4",
        "Marsh K, IJzerman M, Thokala P, Baltussen R, Boysen M, Kaló Z, et al. Multiple criteria decision analysis for health care decision making: emerging good practices. Report 2 of the ISPOR MCDA Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2016;19(2):125-137. Step 6a, Report and justify the aggregation function used, names the additive model as the most commonly applied aggregation formula in health care MCDAs and gives the unnumbered additive function V_j = sum_(i=1)^n s_ij w_i, with V_j the overall value of intervention j, s_ij its score on criterion i and w_i the weight of criterion i, noting that additive functions are also applied in health-related quality of life instruments such as the EQ-5D and SF-6D. Step 2a lists preference independence among the properties of criteria, with health gain and severity as an example of its failure, and nonoverlap, with cost-effectiveness alongside cost or effectiveness as double counting. Step 5a ties scaling constants to elicitation that takes account of the range of performance, best met by swing weighting and decompositional approaches, with direct rating giving flatter weights. Dealing with Budget Constraints states that a ratio scale is needed to measure benefits in a cost-benefit ratio. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2015.12.016",
        "Thokala P, Devlin N, Marsh K, Baltussen R, Boysen M, Kalo Z, et al. Multiple criteria decision analysis for health care decision making: an introduction. Report 1 of the ISPOR MCDA Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2016;19(1):1-13. MCDA Modeling Approaches states that value measurement approaches are by far the most widely used in health care, and Calculating Aggregate Scores that they often use the additive model. Appendix, MCDA Modeling Example, Weighting, assigns 100 points to the criterion whose swing (range of performance) matters most, rates each other criterion against it, and normalises the points by dividing each criterion's points by the sum of points so that the weights add up to 1 (60, 100 and 80 points give 0.25, 0.42 and 0.33); Aggregation multiplies each score by its criterion's weight and sums across criteria, with totals of 58.25 and 55.55 in Table A2. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2015.12.003" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Additive MCDA value for a technology with no health gain. Technology C in the article has no health gain and still scores 50, because severity and strength of evidence earn value on their own, and it ranks first whenever the health gain weight is below 2/7, scoring 75 against A's 71.25 at a weight of 0.25. The second ISPOR report describes this counterintuitive result as the effect of an additive model when health gain and severity interact.",
        "Changing an MCDA criterion range without new swing points. Widening the health gain range to 0 to 1.6 QALYs halves the health gain scores to 37.5 for A and 25 for B. Kept at 0.50, the old weight then gives A 53.75, B 52.5 and C 50 in place of 72.5, 65 and 50 (computed here for illustration), although a swing of 1.6 QALYs would be judged worth more points than a swing of 0.8. The second ISPOR report ties weights that act as scaling constants to elicitation over the range of performance, so a new range needs new points.",
        "Cost per MCDA value point read as relative efficiency. Dividing cost by overall value, GBP 18,000 by 72.5 for A and GBP 8,000 by 65 for B, gives about GBP 248 and GBP 123 per point, the ratio form of HE-FM-ACER-001. The totals are on an interval scale. Adding 20 to every partial value, as if the bottom of each range scored 20, adds 20 to every total because the weights sum to one (derived), and gives GBP 194.59 and GBP 94.12 per point, so the ratio of the two figures becomes 2.07 in place of 2.02 with no change in either technology (computed here for illustration). The second ISPOR report states that a cost-benefit ratio needs benefits measured on a ratio scale." ;
    hemo:9000014 "w_1 = p_1 / (p_1 + p_2 + p_3); w_2 = p_2 / (p_1 + p_2 + p_3); w_3 = p_3 / (p_1 + p_2 + p_3); V = w_1 * v_1 + w_2 * v_2 + w_3 * v_3" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-MCDH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "swing-weighted-additive-value-of-a-technology-on-three-mcda-criteria" .

hemo:5000932 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Second-best two-type procurement menu with a linear marginal value of output"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-MECH-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000919 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006408 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004247,
        hemo:6004248,
        hemo:6004249,
        hemo:6004250,
        hemo:6004251,
        hemo:6004252,
        hemo:6004253,
        hemo:6004254,
        hemo:6004255,
        hemo:6004256,
        hemo:6004257 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004247,
        hemo:6004248,
        hemo:6004249,
        hemo:6004250,
        hemo:6004251 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004256,
        hemo:6004257 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006405,
        hemo:7006406,
        hemo:7006407 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006409,
        hemo:7006410,
        hemo:7006411,
        hemo:7006412,
        hemo:7006413,
        hemo:7006414 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "The purchaser values output at S(q) and, knowing only that the supplier is low-cost with probability nu, chooses the menu that maximises expected S(q) less the transfer subject to the constraints of HE-FM-MECH-001. In Laffont and Martimort's solution the low-cost type produces its first-best output, the high-cost type's output is set where the marginal value equals the virtual cost theta_H + nu / (1 minus nu) (theta_H minus theta_L), and the low-cost type receives the information rent (theta_H minus theta_L) q_H while the high-cost type receives none. With a linear marginal value S'(q) = a minus b q, an illustrative form chosen here, both outputs solve in closed form (derived). The output step takes the form of HE-FM-CTH-002 and the first-best output that of HE-FM-CTH-003, which compute stays of hospital care; here the cost fed in is the virtual cost of (2.28). The max function sets q_H to zero when (2.28) has no positive solution, the authors' shut-down case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Second-best two-type procurement menu with a linear marginal value of output" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Laffont JJ, Martimort D. The Theory of Incentives I. The Principal-Agent Model. Authors' manuscript dated 6 February 2001, deposited in Toulouse Capitole Publications, record 14941 (full text read). Published as The Theory of Incentives. The Principal-Agent Model. Princeton University Press; 2002. doi:10.1515/9781400829453 (not seen). The printed book numbers the chapter 2 sections one lower than the manuscript (its section 2.3 is Incentive Feasible Menu of Contracts) and its equation numbers were not checked, so the section and equation numbers below are the manuscript's. Chapter 2, section 2.3.1 (First-Best Production Levels), (2.4) and (2.5), first-best outputs where marginal value equals each type's marginal cost. Section 2.7.3 (Shut-Down Policy), the high-cost output is set to zero when (2.28) has no positive solution; (2.31), shut-down is optimal when nu Delta theta q-bar SB is at least (1 minus nu) times S(q-bar SB) minus theta-bar q-bar SB; and (2.32), the same condition with a status quo utility U_0 above zero for both types. https://publications.ut-capitole.fr/id/eprint/14941/1/Laffont_14941.pdf",
        "Laffont JJ, Martimort D. The Theory of Incentives I. The Principal-Agent Model. Authors' manuscript dated 6 February 2001, deposited in Toulouse Capitole Publications, record 14941 (full text read). Published as The Theory of Incentives. The Principal-Agent Model. Princeton University Press; 2002. doi:10.1515/9781400829453 (not seen). The printed book numbers the chapter 2 sections one lower than the manuscript (its section 2.3 is Incentive Feasible Menu of Contracts) and its equation numbers were not checked, so the section and equation numbers below are the manuscript's. Chapter 2, section 2.6 (The Optimization Program of the Principal), the principal's problem of maximising nu (S(q) minus t) + (1 minus nu)(S(q-bar) minus t-bar) subject to (2.9) to (2.12), rewritten as expected allocative efficiency less expected information rent in (2.19). Section 2.7.1 (The Asymmetric Information Optimal Contract), (2.24) and (2.25), the efficient type's incentive constraint and the inefficient type's participation constraint bind; (2.26), no distortion for the efficient type; (2.27); and Proposition 2.1, with (2.28) S'(q-bar SB) = theta-bar + nu / (1 minus nu) Delta theta, (2.29) the rent Delta theta q-bar SB, and the transfers theta q* + Delta theta q-bar SB and theta-bar q-bar SB. The closed form for a linear marginal value is derived here. https://publications.ut-capitole.fr/id/eprint/14941/1/Laffont_14941.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Distorting the low-cost type's output as well. Laffont and Martimort's solution leaves the low-cost type's output at first best, since its rent depends only on q_H (2.26). Setting q_L where marginal value equals theta_L plus the same distortion term, 4 + 1 = 5, gives 100 procedures in place of 120 and lowers the commissioner's expected payoff from 260 to 255 (GBP thousand) with no saving in rent (computed here).",
        "Low-cost transfer that leaves out the information rent. Paying the low-cost type only its cost, t_L = 4 x 120 = 480, with the high-cost contract of 80 procedures for GBP 400,000 makes the low-cost type better off on the high-cost contract by GBP 80,000, so it mimics and the menu fails HE-FM-MECH-001 (IC_L of minus 80, computed here).",
        "Prior probability used in place of the odds in the virtual cost. The distortion term is nu / (1 minus nu) times the cost gap, the odds of the low-cost type. Writing nu alone gives a virtual cost of 5.5 in place of 6 with equal priors, a high-cost output of 90 in place of 80 and a rent of GBP 90,000 in place of GBP 80,000 (computed here); with nu = 0.9 it gives 5.9 and an output of 82 where the correct menu shuts the high-cost type out." ;
    hemo:9000014 "q_L = (a - theta_L) / b; theta_v = theta_H + nu / (1 - nu) * (theta_H - theta_L); q_H = max(0, (a - theta_v) / b); U_L = (theta_H - theta_L) * q_H; t_L = theta_L * q_L + U_L; t_H = theta_H * q_H" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-MECH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "second-best-two-type-procurement-menu-with-a-linear-marginal-value-of-output" .

hemo:5000938 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Nash equilibria of a two-player game with two strategies each"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-NASH-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005235,
        hemo:6005236,
        hemo:6005237,
        hemo:6005238,
        hemo:6005239,
        hemo:6005240,
        hemo:6005241,
        hemo:6005242,
        hemo:6005243,
        hemo:6005244,
