    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the value of one unit of a shared resource at full effectiveness, the private cost of a unit, the loss of value that each unit used imposes on every unit and the number of identical users to the use each user chooses when all act alone, the net value left to the group, the share of the attainable value lost and the per-unit fee that brings use back to the joint optimum. Each user keeps the whole value of a unit but bears only their own share of the loss, so the symmetric Nash equilibrium (HE-FM-TOTC-001) exceeds the joint optimum whenever there is more than one user, and the fee of HE-FM-TOTC-002 charges the part of the loss that falls on the others. The joint optimum is the efficient quantity Q_star of HE-FM-PIGT-001 with the slope of marginal private benefit b and of marginal external cost g both set to k and m set to zero, and the open-access limit (a less c) / k as the number of users grows is its Q_0 with b set to k, where the deadweight loss of HE-FM-PIGT-002 equals the whole attainable value. For a finite number of users the equilibrium total, the fee and the lost rent take the form of Q_0 and t_star of HE-FM-PIGT-001 and the deadweight loss of HE-FM-PIGT-002 with b set to k (N + 1) / N, g to k (N less 1) / N and m to zero (derived, and checked here on 448 parameter sets). Those records compute them only for b the slope of a demand curve in an otherwise undistorted market (HE-VR-PIGT-005, HE-AS-PIGT-001); these slopes exist only along the symmetric path of the users' game and depend on N, which no live record holds. The marginal external cost of one more unit is the MEC of HE-FM-EXT-001. Notation follows the Tragedy of the Commons article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equilibrium use, rent dissipation and corrective fee function for a shared depletable resource" ;
    hemo:9000014 "f(a, c, k, N) = (q_E, Q_E, V_E, d_E, t_s)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-TOTC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equilibrium-use-rent-dissipation-and-corrective-fee-function-for-a-shared-depletable-resource" .

hemo:5000940 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected price and equilibrium bids of a single-winner reverse auction with independent uniform costs"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-AUCT-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005258,
        hemo:6005259,
        hemo:6005260,
        hemo:6005261,
        hemo:6005262,
        hemo:6005263,
        hemo:6005264,
        hemo:6005265,
        hemo:6005266 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005258,
        hemo:6005259,
        hemo:6005260,
        hemo:6005261 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005262,
        hemo:6005263,
        hemo:6005264,
        hemo:6005265,
        hemo:6005266 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the lowest and highest possible unit costs c_low and c_high, the number of bidding suppliers N and, for a bid, one supplier's own cost c to the expected lowest cost E_c1, the expected price E_p the purchaser pays, the winner's expected margin M_w, the symmetric equilibrium bid b of a first-price (pay-as-bid) tender and the expected winning bid E_b, for a single-winner procurement auction in which each supplier knows only its own cost. HE-FM-AUCT-001 gives the expected costs, price and margin under the second-price rule, which by revenue equivalence is also the expected price under the first-price rule, and HE-FM-AUCT-002 gives the first-price bid and checks that its expected winning bid equals HE-FM-AUCT-001's price. HE-CF-AUCT-001 runs one tender under both rules and checks the closed forms by a seeded simulation. Both closed forms take the affine form of the Nash bargaining price HE-FM-BARG-001, a lower limit plus a weight times a range, but the weights 2 / (N + 1) and 1 / N come from order statistics of the cost draws, not from bargaining power (derived). The article's supply-risk comparison of one winner against two is an expected cost over delivery branches, HE-FM-CHN-001, with branch probabilities such as 0.81 that both suppliers deliver given by path products, HE-FM-CHN-002, so it is not restated here. The two-type screening of one supplier with a hidden cost is HE-FN-MECH-001, and the Medicare DMEPOS single payment amount, a median, maximum or 75th percentile of winning bids, is a programme rule and has no record here. Notation follows the Auction Theory article, with c_low and c_high for its lowest and highest possible unit costs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected price and equilibrium bids of a single-winner reverse auction with independent uniform costs" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a(c_low, c_high, N, c) = (E_c1, E_p, M_w, b, E_b)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-AUCT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-price-and-equilibrium-bids-of-a-single-winner-reverse-auction-with-independent-uniform-costs" .

hemo:5000942 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Spatial accessibility index function from provider capacity, population and travel times"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-ACCS-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005283,
        hemo:6005284,
        hemo:6005285,
        hemo:6005286,
        hemo:6005287 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005283,
        hemo:6005284,
        hemo:6005285,
        hemo:6005286 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005287 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the capacity of each provider location, the population of each residential location and the travel time or distance between every pair to an accessibility score for each residential location, in providers per head of population. Wang 2012 writes the family in one form, A_i equal to the sum over providers of S_j f(d_ij) divided by the sum over population locations of P_k f(d_kj), with f a distance decay function, so each provider's capacity is shared among the people who can reach it and each resident collects the shares within reach. HE-FM-ACCS-001 is the two-step floating catchment area (2SFCA) method of Luo and Wang, in which f is 1 within a threshold travel time and 0 beyond it, and HE-FM-ACCS-002 is the gravity form with a population demand adjustment, in which f is a negative power of travel time; Luo and Wang show that the first is a special case of the second. Both are written out for the article's two practices and three neighbourhoods, and HE-CF-ACCS-001 and HE-CF-ACCS-002 run them for any number of sites. A count of providers over the residents of one bordered area is a plain ratio of the form of HE-FM-ACR-001 and is not drafted; the time cost borne by residents who travel further is HE-FM-PTC-001, and equity of use relative to need is HE-FN-HEQ-001. Notation follows the Accessibility article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Spatial accessibility index function from provider capacity, population and travel times" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a(S_j, P_k, d_ij, f) = A_i" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-ACCS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "spatial-accessibility-index-function-from-provider-capacity-population-and-travel-times" .

hemo:5000946 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Pure-strategy best-reply check of each cell of a 2 x 2 game with strict and weak equilibria"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-NASH-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000938 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006467 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005313,
        hemo:6005314,
        hemo:6005315,
        hemo:6005316,
        hemo:6005317,
        hemo:6005318,
        hemo:6005319,
        hemo:6005320,
        hemo:6005321,
        hemo:6005322,
        hemo:6005323,
        hemo:6005324,
        hemo:6005325,
        hemo:6005326,
        hemo:6005327,
        hemo:6005328 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005313,
        hemo:6005314,
        hemo:6005315,
        hemo:6005316,
        hemo:6005317,
        hemo:6005318,
        hemo:6005319,
        hemo:6005320 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005321,
        hemo:6005322,
        hemo:6005323,
        hemo:6005324,
        hemo:6005325,
        hemo:6005326,
        hemo:6005327,
        hemo:6005328 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006464,
        hemo:7006465,
        hemo:7006466 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006468,
        hemo:7006469,
        hemo:7006470,
        hemo:7006471,
        hemo:7006472,
        hemo:7006473,
        hemo:7006474 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Tests each of the four strategy pairs of a two-player game in which each player has two strategies. A cell is a pure-strategy Nash equilibrium when the row player's payoff in it is at least its payoff from switching rows against the same column, and the column player's payoff in it is at least its payoff from switching columns against the same row, which is Definition 5.1 of Tadelis applied cell by cell as in his section 5.1.1. The cell is a strict equilibrium when both comparisons hold with a strict inequality, Definition 7 of Slantchev, and a cell that passes the weak test but fails the strict one is a weak equilibrium, because some player has an alternative that does exactly as well. Each output is 1 for a pass and 0 for a fail. A game in which no cell passes, such as matching pennies, still has an equilibrium in mixed strategies (HE-FM-NASH-002)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pure-strategy best-reply check of each cell of a 2 x 2 game with strict and weak equilibria" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Nash JF. Equilibrium points in n-person games. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA. 1950;36(1):48-49 (full text read from the original page scan). Defines a game with a finite set of pure strategies for each player and a payment to each player for each combination, one combination countering another when each player's strategy in it yields the highest obtainable expectation against the other players' strategies in the second, and an equilibrium point as a self-countering combination. https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.36.1.48",
        "Slantchev BL. Game theory: dominance, Nash equilibrium, symmetry. Lecture notes, Department of Political Science, University of California, San Diego; 23 May 2008 (full text read). Section 2.1 (Pure-Strategy Nash Equilibrium), Definition 4, a best response as a strategy with a payoff at least that of every alternative given the others' strategies. Section 2.2 (Strict Nash Equilibrium), Definition 7, a strict Nash equilibrium requires a strictly higher payoff than every other strategy, with the Battle of the Bismarck Sea as an equilibrium that is not strict because one player is indifferent. Section 2.3 (Mixed Strategy Nash Equilibrium), Figure 18, matching pennies has no Nash equilibrium in pure strategies. https://slantchev.ucsd.edu/courses/gt/04-strategic-form.pdf",
        "Tadelis S. Pinning down beliefs: Nash equilibrium. Chapter 5 in Game Theory: An Introduction. Princeton: Princeton University Press; 2013 (publisher's sample chapter, full text read). Section 5.1 (Nash Equilibrium in Pure Strategies), Definition 5.1, a pure-strategy profile is a Nash equilibrium when each player's strategy is a best response to the others', v_i(s_i*, s_-i*) at least v_i(s_i', s_-i*) for every alternative s_i' and every player i. Section 5.1.1 (Pure-Strategy Nash Equilibrium in a Matrix), the three-step method that marks the row player's best reply in each column and the column player's best reply in each row, a cell marked twice being a pure-strategy Nash equilibrium. Section 5.1.2 (Evaluating the Nash Equilibria Solution) states that Nash equilibrium does not guarantee Pareto optimality. https://assets.press.princeton.edu/chapters/s5-10001.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Checking only one player's best reply in a 2 x 2 game. A cell is an equilibrium only when both players are playing a best reply. Testing the row player alone in matching pennies marks the two matched cells as equilibria, although in each of them the column player moves from minus 1 to 1 by switching (computed here), and the game has no pure equilibrium.",
        "Reading an equilibrium cell as the best outcome. The check finds stable cells, not efficient ones, and Tadelis states that Nash equilibrium does not guarantee Pareto optimality. In the Hi-lo game of HE-EX-NASH-004 the second matched cell passes both the weak and the strict test although each player earns 1 there against 10 in the other equilibrium.",
        "Strict inequality used in the weak best-reply test. Writing the weak test with > in place of >= drops every equilibrium that rests on a tie. In the break-even angioplasty game of HE-EX-NASH-002 it finds no pure equilibrium where there are three (computed here), and the article's selection of (Invest, Invest) by removing the weakly dominated choice then has nothing to select from." ;
    hemo:9000014 "NE_11 = (u_11 >= u_21) and (v_11 >= v_12); NE_12 = (u_12 >= u_22) and (v_12 >= v_11); NE_21 = (u_21 >= u_11) and (v_21 >= v_22); NE_22 = (u_22 >= u_12) and (v_22 >= v_21); S_11 = (u_11 > u_21) and (v_11 > v_12); S_12 = (u_12 > u_22) and (v_12 > v_11); S_21 = (u_21 > u_11) and (v_21 > v_22); S_22 = (u_22 > u_12) and (v_22 > v_21)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-NASH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pure-strategy-best-reply-check-of-each-cell-of-a-2-x-2-game-with-strict-and-weak-equilibria" .

hemo:5000947 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Mixed-strategy equilibrium probabilities of a 2 x 2 game from the indifference conditions"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-NASH-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000938 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006478 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005329,
        hemo:6005330,
        hemo:6005331,
        hemo:6005332,
        hemo:6005333,
        hemo:6005334,
        hemo:6005335,
        hemo:6005336,
        hemo:6005337,
        hemo:6005338,
        hemo:6005339,
        hemo:6005340,
        hemo:6005341,
        hemo:6005342,
        hemo:6005343 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005329,
        hemo:6005330,
        hemo:6005331,
        hemo:6005332,
        hemo:6005333,
        hemo:6005334,
        hemo:6005335,
        hemo:6005336 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005343 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006475,
        hemo:7006476,
        hemo:7006477 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006479,
        hemo:7006480,
        hemo:7006481,
        hemo:7006482,
        hemo:7006483,
        hemo:7006484,
        hemo:7006485 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "In a mixed equilibrium of a game in which each player has two strategies, each player randomises over both, which it is willing to do only if both give it the same expected payoff, so each player's mixing probability is set by the other player's payoffs. Equating the row player's expected payoffs from its two strategies, q u_11 + (1 minus q) u_12 = q u_21 + (1 minus q) u_22, gives the probability q_star that the column player chooses its first strategy, and the column player's indifference gives the probability p_star that the row player chooses its first strategy. Slantchev states this condition for 2 x 2 games and solves it for the equilibrium probabilities; the closed forms are that equation solved (derived). The pair is a mixed equilibrium when both values are above 0 and below 1, which is what Proposition 1 of Slantchev requires when each player mixes over two strategies (derived); when q_star is not, the row player has a strictly dominant strategy (derived, for games without the ties of HE-AS-NASH-005), and likewise p_star for the column player. The algebra is that of the decision curve threshold HE-FM-DCA-004, p_t = L / (G + L), and of the blend weight in HE-FM-EXD-002, with a_1 and a_2 in place of G and L, but HE-VR-DCA-013 to HE-VR-DCA-015 define p_t as a patient's risk and G and L as a patient's benefit and harm, while here the probability is the other player's equilibrium choice, set by the payoffs of the player it leaves indifferent and an equilibrium only as a pair with p_star. When a_1 and a_2 are both negative, as in the angioplasty game, listing the row player's strategies in the other order makes both positive and leaves q_star unchanged (derived). Each player's expected payoff at the equilibrium is HE-FM-CHN-001 applied to its payoffs against the other's mix." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Mixed-strategy equilibrium probabilities of a 2 x 2 game from the indifference conditions" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Nash JF. Equilibrium points in n-person games. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA. 1950;36(1):48-49 (full text read from the original page scan). Mixed strategies are probability distributions over the pure strategies and the payoff functions are the expectations of the players; Nash proves, with Kakutani's fixed point theorem, that every such game has an equilibrium point. https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.36.1.48",
        "Slantchev BL. Game theory: dominance, Nash equilibrium, symmetry. Lecture notes, Department of Political Science, University of California, San Diego; 23 May 2008 (full text read). Section 2.3 (Mixed Strategy Nash Equilibrium), matching pennies, each player's expected payoffs from its pure strategies against the other's mix and the unique equilibrium with both probabilities one half; section 2.3.1 (Battle of the Sexes), the row player's payoffs 2q and 1 minus q equal at q = 1/3 and the column player's equal at p = 2/3, the mixed equilibrium (2/3, 1/3) for the row player and (1/3, 2/3) for the column player, and outcome probabilities found by multiplying the two players' equilibrium probabilities. Section 2.4 (Computing Nash Equilibria), Proposition 1, a mixed profile is an equilibrium only if each player's pure strategies in its support give the same payoff and no other strategy gives more, and the statement that a randomising player must be indifferent among the pure strategies in its support. Section 2.4.1 (Myerson's Card Game), the simplified 2 x 2 game, in which any mixture uses both strategies and the equilibrium probabilities are found by equating the payoffs, and the statement that each player's mixed strategy makes the other indifferent. https://slantchev.ucsd.edu/courses/gt/04-strategic-form.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Missing a continuum of equilibria when payoffs tie. In the break-even angioplasty game of HE-EX-NASH-002, a_1 is 0, the formula gives q_star = 1 and interior = 0, and it would be easy to conclude that only pure equilibria exist. With hospital B investing for sure, hospital A earns 0 whatever it does and investing stays a best reply for B against any mix by A, so every probability with which A invests is part of an equilibrium (computed here).",
        "Reporting a value outside the unit interval as a mixing probability. The formula returns a number for any game whose gap sums are not zero, and when investing is dominant it gives 1.3333 (HE-EX-NASH-008), which is not a probability. The game then has no completely mixed equilibrium and its single equilibrium is the pure cell (Invest, Invest), so q_star and p_star are read only when interior is 1.",
        "Setting a player's mixing probability from its own payoffs. The row player's probability keeps the column player indifferent, so it comes from v_jk. In the battle of the sexes of HE-EX-NASH-006, solving each player's own indifference for its own probability gives the row player 1/3 and the column player 2/3 in place of 2/3 and 1/3. At a column probability of two thirds the row player earns 1.3333 from its first strategy and 0.3333 from its second (computed here), so it would switch and the pair is not an equilibrium. In a symmetric game such as the angioplasty game the two values coincide and the error goes unseen." ;
    hemo:9000014 "a_1 = u_11 - u_21; a_2 = u_22 - u_12; q_star = a_2 / (a_1 + a_2); b_1 = v_11 - v_12; b_2 = v_22 - v_21; p_star = b_2 / (b_1 + b_2); interior = (q_star > 0) and (q_star < 1) and (p_star > 0) and (p_star < 1)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-NASH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "mixed-strategy-equilibrium-probabilities-of-a-2-x-2-game-from-the-indifference-conditions" .

hemo:5000950 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected lowest cost, expected price and winner's margin in a second-price reverse auction with uniform costs"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-AUCT-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000940 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006512 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005363,
        hemo:6005364,
        hemo:6005365,
        hemo:6005366,
        hemo:6005367,
        hemo:6005368 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005363,
        hemo:6005364,
        hemo:6005365 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005368 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006509,
        hemo:7006510,
        hemo:7006511 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006513,
        hemo:7006514,
        hemo:7006515,
        hemo:7006516,
        hemo:7006517,
        hemo:7006518,
        hemo:7006519 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "A purchaser buys from the lowest of N bidding suppliers whose unit costs are independent draws from a uniform distribution on the interval [c_low, c_high]. Under the second-price rule each supplier bids its cost and the winner is paid the second-lowest bid, so the expected price is the expected second-lowest cost. Klemperer prints the expected k-th highest of n uniform draws as v_low + ((n + 1 minus k) / (n + 1)) (v_high minus v_low); the lowest cost is the N-th highest and the second-lowest the (N minus 1)-th highest, which give E_c1 and E_p. By revenue equivalence the first-price rule has the same expected price (HE-FM-AUCT-002). The winner's expected margin is the gap between the two, (c_high minus c_low) / (N + 1) (derived), and both the price and the margin fall towards c_low and zero as N rises." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected lowest cost, expected price and winner's margin in a second-price reverse auction with uniform costs" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Klemperer P. Auction theory: a guide to the literature. Author's copy dated May 1999, posted on the author's Nuffield College page (full text read), which states that it is published in Journal of Economic Surveys 1999;13(3):227-286, doi:10.1111/1467-6419.00083 (journal version not seen; page numbers below are the author's copy's). Appendix D (Examples Using the Uniform Distribution), page 57, states that the expected k-th highest value among n values independently drawn from the uniform distribution on [v_low, v_high] is v_low + ((n + 1 minus k) / (n + 1)) (v_high minus v_low), and, under the heading Bidding with Independent Private Values, Revenue Equivalence, and Marginal Revenues, for risk-neutral bidders with independent private values, that everyone bids her true value in a second-price auction, so the seller's expected revenue is the expected second-highest value, v_low + ((n minus 1) / (n + 1)) (v_high minus v_low). Section 5 (Risk Aversion) states that risk aversion has no effect on a bidder's optimal second-price strategy. The procurement forms E_c1 and E_p are the printed fact with k = N and k = N minus 1; the reverse-auction reading is derived here. https://www.nuff.ox.ac.uk/users/klemperer/survey.pdf",
        "Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences. Improvements to auction theory and inventions of new auction formats. Scientific background on the Sveriges Riksbank Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel 2020. 12 October 2020 (full text read). Section 2.2 (Private Values) describes Vickrey's model of statistically independent private valuations with risk-neutral, ex-ante symmetric bidders, states that in the second-price auction each bidder's dominant strategy is to bid exactly what the object is worth to her, and that all four standard formats yield the same expected revenue, the revenue-equivalence theorem. Under the heading Extensions and Further Research in the same section it states that Vickrey proved the theorem for a uniform distribution of values in 1961, and section 2.1 (A General Framework) states that first-price auctions are common vehicles for procuring goods and services and for awarding public contracts. https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2020/09/advanced-economicsciencesprize2020.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Expected lowest cost taken as the tender price. The purchaser pays the second-lowest cost under the second-price rule, or a bid above cost under the first-price rule, never the winner's cost. Budgeting at the expected lowest cost of 2.75 in place of the price of 3.50 understates spend by 0.75 per pack, GBP 750,000 on the article's 1,000,000 packs (computed here).",
        "Number of rivals used in place of the number of bidders. N counts every bidding supplier, the winner included. Entering the two rivals of a three-supplier tender gives an expected price of 4.00 in place of 3.50 and a margin of 1.00 in place of 0.75 (computed here).",
        "Selling-auction weight applied to procurement costs. Klemperer's expected revenue of a selling auction, v_low + ((n minus 1) / (n + 1)) (v_high minus v_low), is the expected second-highest value. Copying it to costs gives the expected second-highest cost, not the second-lowest. With three suppliers the two coincide at 3.50, so the article's example does not reveal the slip, but with five suppliers it gives GBP 4.00 in place of GBP 3.00 and it rises, rather than falls, as bidders are added (computed here)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "E_c1 = c_low + (c_high - c_low) / (N + 1); E_p = c_low + 2 * (c_high - c_low) / (N + 1); M_w = E_p - E_c1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-AUCT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-lowest-cost-expected-price-and-winner-s-margin-in-a-second-price-reverse-auction-with-uniform-costs" .

hemo:5000951 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Symmetric first-price equilibrium bid and expected winning bid in a reverse auction with uniform costs"@en ;
