    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006524,
        hemo:7006525,
        hemo:7006526,
        hemo:7006527,
        hemo:7006528,
        hemo:7006529 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Under the first-price rule the lowest bidder wins and is paid its own bid, so a supplier bids above its cost, trading a higher price on winning against a lower chance of winning. With N suppliers whose costs are independent and uniform on the interval [c_low, c_high], the symmetric equilibrium bid adds to the supplier's cost c the expected gap to the lowest of the other N minus 1 costs, given that they all exceed c, which is (c_high minus c) / N. Klemperer prints the selling form, a bid of v_low + ((n minus 1) / n) (v minus v_low); the procurement form is derived by mirroring each cost to the value c_low + c_high minus c on the same range and checked here by a best-response search and by simulation. Because the bid is linear in cost, the expected winning bid is the bid at the expected lowest cost E_c1 of HE-FM-AUCT-001 (derived), and it equals that formula's expected price E_p, revenue equivalence for this case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Symmetric first-price equilibrium bid and expected winning bid in a reverse auction with uniform costs" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Klemperer P. Auction theory: a guide to the literature. Author's copy dated May 1999, posted on the author's Nuffield College page (full text read), which states that it is published in Journal of Economic Surveys 1999;13(3):227-286, doi:10.1111/1467-6419.00083 (journal version not seen; page numbers below are the author's copy's). Appendix D (Examples Using the Uniform Distribution), page 57, states that in a first-price auction bidder i bids her expected payment conditional on winning an ascending auction, the expected highest of the other n minus 1 values given that they lie below v_i, which is v_low + ((n minus 1) / n) (v_i minus v_low). Footnote 139, pages 57 and 58, derives the same bid from the differential equation for the first-price strategy and takes its expectation at the highest value to confirm revenue equivalence with the second-price auction. Section 5 (Risk Aversion) states that risk aversion makes bidders bid more aggressively in first-price auctions. The procurement bid is derived here from the selling form. https://www.nuff.ox.ac.uk/users/klemperer/survey.pdf",
        "Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences. Improvements to auction theory and inventions of new auction formats. Scientific background on the Sveriges Riksbank Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel 2020. 12 October 2020 (full text read). Section 2.2 (Private Values), under the heading Main Findings and Insights, states that the first-price and Dutch auctions are strategically equivalent and have no dominant strategies, since lowering one's bid lowers the price if one wins but also lowers the probability of winning, and that optimal bids balance the two; it also reports that all four formats yield the same expected revenue in Vickrey's model. https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2020/09/advanced-economicsciencesprize2020.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Bid shading spread over the whole cost range. The margin a supplier adds depends on how far its own cost lies below c_high, (c_high minus c) / N, not on the full range. Adding (c_high minus c_low) / N gives the 2.60 supplier a bid of 3.60 in place of 3.40 and an expected winning bid of 3.75 in place of 3.50, breaking revenue equivalence (computed here).",
        "First-price bids assumed equal to suppliers' costs. Bidding cost is dominant only under the second-price rule. Assuming that a pay-as-bid tender attracts bids at cost puts the expected price at the expected lowest cost, 2.75 per pack in the article's example, in place of 3.50, the same understatement of GBP 750,000 on 1,000,000 packs as HE-ER-AUCT-002 (computed here).",
        "One tender's winning bid read as a test of revenue equivalence. Revenue equivalence concerns expected prices. In the article's draw the first-price rule pays 3.40 and the second-price rule 3.20, a gap of 0.20 in one tender, while both expected prices are 3.50, so a single tender, or a few, cannot show which rule is cheaper (computed here)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "b = c + (c_high - c) / N; E_c1 = c_low + (c_high - c_low) / (N + 1); E_b = E_c1 + (c_high - E_c1) / N" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-AUCT-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "symmetric-first-price-equilibrium-bid-and-expected-winning-bid-in-a-reverse-auction-with-uniform-costs" .

hemo:5000955 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-step floating catchment area accessibility for two practices and three neighbourhoods"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ACCS-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000942 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006563 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005407,
        hemo:6005408,
        hemo:6005409,
        hemo:6005410,
        hemo:6005411,
        hemo:6005412,
        hemo:6005413,
        hemo:6005414,
        hemo:6005415,
        hemo:6005416,
        hemo:6005417,
        hemo:6005418,
        hemo:6005419,
        hemo:6005420,
        hemo:6005421,
        hemo:6005422,
        hemo:6005423 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005407,
        hemo:6005408,
        hemo:6005409,
        hemo:6005410,
        hemo:6005411,
        hemo:6005412,
        hemo:6005413,
        hemo:6005414,
        hemo:6005415,
        hemo:6005416,
        hemo:6005417,
        hemo:6005418 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005421,
        hemo:6005422,
        hemo:6005423 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006560,
