        hemo:7006562 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006564,
        hemo:7006565,
        hemo:7006566,
        hemo:7006567,
        hemo:7006568 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Step one divides each practice's full-time-equivalent GPs by the population of the neighbourhoods within the threshold travel time d_0 of it, giving the practice's supply ratio. Step two gives each neighbourhood the sum of the supply ratios of the practices within d_0 of it. Each comparison such as (d_1X <= d_0) is 1 when the travel time is within the threshold and 0 otherwise, so a neighbourhood outside a practice's catchment adds nothing to its denominator and takes nothing from its ratio. The result is in GPs per head of population, and the article reports it per 10,000 people. Neighbourhoods A, B and C of the article are 1, 2 and 3 here; more practices or neighbourhoods add terms of the same pattern, which HE-CF-ACCS-001 computes for any number of sites." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-step floating catchment area accessibility for two practices and three neighbourhoods" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Guagliardo MF. Spatial accessibility of primary care: concepts, methods and challenges. International Journal of Health Geographics. 2004;3:3. Full text read (PMC394340), formula images included. Subsection Two-step floating catchment area of the section Recent developments, which describes the provider-to-population ratio of each provider's 30-minute drive-time catchment, the summing of the ratios of every catchment over each population point, results in provider-to-population units, the artificially sharp catchment borders, Luo and Wang's sensitivity analysis of 20 to 50 minute thresholds, their plan to adjust for transport options and their demonstration that the method is a special case of the Joseph and Bantock improved gravity model. Section Measuring spatial accessibility describes the border-crossing weakness of supply ratios, and section Defining access states that a study of spatial potential could not demonstrate or disprove a relationship of spatial accessibility with utilisation or population health. https://doi.org/10.1186/1476-072X-3-3",
        "Luo W, Wang F. Measures of spatial accessibility to healthcare in a GIS environment: synthesis and a case study in Chicago region. Environment and Planning B: Planning and Design. 2003;30(6):865-884. Author manuscript read (HHS Author Manuscripts, PMC8238135); the published version, doi 10.1068/b29120, not seen. Section The Evolvement of Floating Catchment Area Methods, equation (3) for the physician to population ratio R_j within the catchment of physician location j, the population locations within the threshold travel time d_0, and equation (4) for the accessibility of location i as the sum of R_j over physician locations within d_0 of i, with the note that the travel-time matrix is computed once and accessed twice and that centroids represent whole tracts. Section The Gravity-Based Method and a Synthesis states that the population-weighted average of accessibility equals the physician to population ratio of the whole study area and that this applies to equation (4). Section The Case Study and Sensitivity Analysis tests seven thresholds of 20 to 50 minutes and reports a smaller variance of the scores at a larger threshold. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8238135/",
        "Wang F. Measurement, optimization, and impact of health care accessibility: a methodological review. Annals of the Association of American Geographers. 2012;102(5):1104-1112. Author manuscript read (NIHPA Author Manuscripts, PMC3547595); the published version, doi 10.1080/00045608.2012.657146, not seen. Section Health Care Accessibility Measures Synthesized, equation (2) for the 2SFCA with R_j = S_j over the population within d_0, the statement that the method's major limitation is its dichotomous approach, equation (3) with a general distance decay f(d), the statement that the best decay function or catchment size cannot be settled without analysing real utilisation, and the separation of place accessibility from individual accessibility. Section Optimization Models in Health Care Accessibility states that the weighted mean of accessibility under equations 1 to 3 equals the ratio of total supply to total demand. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3547595/" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Bordered GP-to-population ratios read as two-step floating catchment accessibility. Counting GPs inside each neighbourhood gives A 8.0, B 0 and C 6.667 per 10,000 in the article's example, which ranks B, with no practice, as worst served. The 2SFCA ranks B best at 4.205 per 10,000, because its residents reach both practices within 30 minutes, and lowers A and C because their practices also serve B. The bordered figure, a plain quotient of the form of HE-FM-ACR-001, ignores patients who cross area borders, the weakness Guagliardo describes for supply ratios.",
        "Each practice's GPs divided by its own neighbourhood only in step one. Dividing X's 8 GPs by A's 10,000 people and Y's 4 GPs by C's 6,000 gives ratios of 8.0 and 6.667 per 10,000, and B, which reaches both, then scores 14.667. The scores weighted by population add up to 41.33 GPs where there are 12, a mean of 11.481 per 10,000 instead of 3.333 (computed here), because GPs who also serve B are counted again. The step one denominator is every population within d_0 of the practice.",
        "Single threshold travel time treated as settled in the two-step floating catchment area. Moving the threshold to an illustrative 20 minutes in the article's example cuts B off from practice Y, so B scores 2.667 and C 6.667 per 10,000 instead of 4.205 and 1.538, and the order of B and C reverses (computed here). Luo and Wang ran seven thresholds of 20 to 50 minutes and found a smaller variance of the scores at larger thresholds, and Wang 2012 notes that the right catchment size cannot be settled without real utilisation data.",
        "Zero two-step floating catchment area score read as residents getting no care. After the article's consolidation neighbourhood C scores 0 because practice X is 45 minutes away, yet its residents may still travel there. The article puts the extra travel at 60 minutes per visit, GBP 15 at an illustrative GBP 15 an hour and GBP 360,000 a year over 24,000 visits, the patient time cost of HE-FM-PTC-001. A zero reflects the hard threshold, and Guagliardo notes that a study of spatial potential could not demonstrate or disprove a relationship with use." ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_X = S_X / ((d_1X <= d_0) * P_1 + (d_2X <= d_0) * P_2 + (d_3X <= d_0) * P_3); R_Y = S_Y / ((d_1Y <= d_0) * P_1 + (d_2Y <= d_0) * P_2 + (d_3Y <= d_0) * P_3); A_1 = (d_1X <= d_0) * R_X + (d_1Y <= d_0) * R_Y; A_2 = (d_2X <= d_0) * R_X + (d_2Y <= d_0) * R_Y; A_3 = (d_3X <= d_0) * R_X + (d_3Y <= d_0) * R_Y" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ACCS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-step-floating-catchment-area-accessibility-for-two-practices-and-three-neighbourhoods" .

hemo:5000956 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Gravity accessibility with a population demand adjustment for two practices and three neighbourhoods"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ACCS-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000942 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7006572 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005424,
        hemo:6005425,
        hemo:6005426,
        hemo:6005427,
