        hemo:7006575,
        hemo:7006576,
        hemo:7006577 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Each practice's demand potential adds the neighbourhood populations weighted by travel time raised to the power minus beta, so nearer residents compete for its GPs more than distant ones. Each neighbourhood's score adds, over both practices, the practice's GPs weighted the same way and divided by its demand potential. Every practice counts, with no threshold, and a larger beta makes distance matter more. It is Wang's equation 1 and Luo and Wang's equation 6, the improved gravity model that Guagliardo attributes to Joseph and Bantock, written for the article's example, and HE-CF-ACCS-002 computes it for any number of sites. The scores are in GPs per head, and their population-weighted mean equals total GPs over total population, as for HE-FM-ACCS-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Gravity accessibility with a population demand adjustment for two practices and three neighbourhoods" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Guagliardo MF. Spatial accessibility of primary care: concepts, methods and challenges. International Journal of Health Geographics. 2004;3:3. Full text read (PMC394340), formula images included. Section Measuring spatial accessibility, the simplest gravity formula, A_i as the sum over j of S_j divided by d_ij to the power beta, the note that it models supply only with no adjustment for demand and the example of two providers serving 1,000 and 5,000 people, the population demand adjustment factor V_j of Joseph and Bantock as the sum over k of P_k divided by d_kj to the power beta, and the improved gravity model, A_i as the sum over j of S_j divided by d_ij to the power beta times V_j, all printed as unnumbered expressions, with the note that beta is usually unknown and must be estimated empirically. https://doi.org/10.1186/1476-072X-3-3",
        "Luo W, Wang F. Measures of spatial accessibility to healthcare in a GIS environment: synthesis and a case study in Chicago region. Environment and Planning B: Planning and Design. 2003;30(6):865-884. Author manuscript read (HHS Author Manuscripts, PMC8238135); the published version, doi 10.1068/b29120, not seen. Section The Gravity-Based Method and a Synthesis, equation (6), the gravity-based index as the sum over physician locations of S_j d_ij to the power minus beta divided by V_j, with V_j the sum over population locations of P_k d_kj to the power minus beta, after equation (5), Hansen's form without V_j; it states that the population-weighted average of the index equals the physician to population ratio of the whole area and shows the 2SFCA to be a special case by recoding travel times to 1 within d_0 and infinity beyond. Section The Case Study and Sensitivity Analysis tests seven values of beta of 1.0 to 2.2, cites 1.85 from a commuting study, reports larger variances at larger beta and higher gravity scores than 2SFCA scores in areas with low accessibility, and notes the trade-off between the number of physicians and travel time at a beta of 1. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8238135/",
        "Wang F. Measurement, optimization, and impact of health care accessibility: a methodological review. Annals of the Association of American Geographers. 2012;102(5):1104-1112. Author manuscript read (NIHPA Author Manuscripts, PMC3547595); the published version, doi 10.1080/00045608.2012.657146, not seen. Section Health Care Accessibility Measures Synthesized, equation (1), with P_k, S_j, d, beta and the numbers of physician and population locations n and m defined, the description of the model as the ratio of supply to demand each discounted by a distance factor, the note that beta requires additional data and work to define and might be region specific, and the statement that the best decay function cannot be settled without analysing real utilisation. Section Optimization Models in Health Care Accessibility states the weighted mean property for equations 1 to 3. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3547595/" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Friction coefficient borrowed without checking how the gravity scores rank. At a beta of 1 the article's neighbourhoods rank A, B, C (4.440, 2.978 and 2.672 per 10,000) and at a beta of 2 they rank A, C, B (5.615, 2.997 and 2.293), while the 2SFCA at 30 minutes ranks B first (computed here). The order that guides a siting or funding decision therefore depends on beta. Wang 2012 notes that beta might be region specific and that the best decay function cannot be settled without real utilisation data.",
        "Gravity sum computed without the population demand potential. Leaving out V_X and V_Y gives the simple gravity form, which Guagliardo notes models supply only. At a beta of 1 it returns 0.9, 0.56 and 0.444 for A, B and C (computed here), GPs per minute rather than GPs per head, and the scores do not change when more people compete for a practice; Guagliardo's example is two providers at the same distance, one serving 1,000 people and one 5,000, which the simple form scores alike.",
        "Zero or token travel time for a neighbourhood that contains a practice. Entering 0 minutes between A and X, where A contains practice X, makes 0 to the power minus beta undefined for any beta above zero, and Excel returns #DIV/0! in DemandPotentialX and in all three scores, which divide by it; a blank time cell counts as 0 and gives the same, and at a beta of 0 Excel returns #NUM! instead (checked in Excel). Entering 1 minute in place of the article's 10 raises A's score at a beta of 1 to 7.875 per 10,000 from 4.440 and lowers B's to 1.463 from 2.978 (computed here), so the time within a neighbourhood is an input that drives the result." ;
    hemo:9000014 "V_X = P_1 * d_1X^(-beta) + P_2 * d_2X^(-beta) + P_3 * d_3X^(-beta); V_Y = P_1 * d_1Y^(-beta) + P_2 * d_2Y^(-beta) + P_3 * d_3Y^(-beta); A_1 = S_X * d_1X^(-beta) / V_X + S_Y * d_1Y^(-beta) / V_Y; A_2 = S_X * d_2X^(-beta) / V_X + S_Y * d_2Y^(-beta) / V_Y; A_3 = S_X * d_3X^(-beta) / V_X + S_Y * d_3Y^(-beta) / V_Y" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ACCS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "gravity-accessibility-with-a-population-demand-adjustment-for-two-practices-and-three-neighbourhoods" .

hemo:5000966 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Four-way dominance classification of an option against its comparator"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000114 obo:IAO_0000125 ;
    obo:IAO_0000115 "Classifies an option against its comparator as dominant, dominated, a duplicate when its cost and effect equal the comparator's within the tie tolerances, or neither, a trade-off in which one costs more and is more effective."@en ;
