        hemo:6000310,
        hemo:6000311,
        hemo:6000312,
        hemo:6000313,
        hemo:6000314,
        hemo:6000315 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000302,
        hemo:6000303,
        hemo:6000304,
        hemo:6000305,
        hemo:6000306,
        hemo:6000307,
        hemo:6000308,
        hemo:6000309,
        hemo:6000310,
        hemo:6000311 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000312,
        hemo:6000313,
        hemo:6000314,
        hemo:6000315 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the components of social time preference to the HM Treasury social time preference rate and its health discount rate, maps a stepped long-term schedule of rates to the discount factor for any year of an appraisal, and maps one answer to a stated choice between ill health at two dates to the constant annual rate it implies. The rates it returns feed the single-amount factor of the Discount Rate page (HE-FM-DR-001) and the stream totals of the Discounting page (HE-FN-DISC-001, HE-FM-DISC-001 to HE-FM-DISC-004 and HE-CF-DISC-001). A health rate derived from growth in the consumption value of health is HE-FM-DDIS-002 under HE-FN-DDIS-001, and the quasi-hyperbolic weight D(k) = beta lambda^k of the article is HE-FM-HYPD-004 under HE-FN-HYPD-001, so neither is repeated here. Notation follows the Time Preference article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Social time preference rate, stepped long-term discount factors and implied individual rates for health" ;
    hemo:9000014 "tp(delta, L, mu, g, t, r_1, r_2, r_3, y, s) = (STPR, r_HD, DF_t, r)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-TPR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "social-time-preference-rate-stepped-long-term-discount-factors-and-implied-individual-rates-for-health" .

hemo:5000154 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Background mortality function for the cycle death probability of a modelled cohort"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-BGM-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000338,
        hemo:6000339,
        hemo:6000340,
        hemo:6000341,
        hemo:6000342 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000338,
        hemo:6000339,
        hemo:6000340 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000341,
        hemo:6000342 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the attained age of a cohort, the cycle length and a disease-specific death probability to the probability of dying of other causes in each model cycle and to the all-cause probability of death, and relates all-cause survival to the survival expected in the general population. The split of the all-cause hazard into a background and an excess hazard is live as HE-FM-BTH-005 and is not restated; the formulas here give its survival-scale form (relative survival), its cycle-probability form for two independent risks, and the expected hazard of a cohort that mixes sexes or ages. A background probability for one cycle comes from a life-table annual probability q_x through the rescaling formula HE-FM-TP-003 with an observed interval of one year (HE-CF-TP-002 performs the same step), and HE-FM-TP-004 splits a cycle's deaths between background and disease death states. A multiplier on background mortality, such as a standardised mortality ratio, enters a cycle probability in the same way as the hazard ratio of HE-FM-HR-004. Related live records cover cure models in the relative survival form (HE-FN-CRM-001 and HE-FM-CRM-001 to HE-FM-CRM-004), cycle probabilities from a cumulative hazard (HE-FN-CUMH-001, HE-FM-CUMH-001 to HE-FM-CUMH-003), general-population QALYs from a life table (HE-FM-ASF-002), the cohort trace that carries the probabilities (HE-FN-CSIM-001) and tunnel states, whose clock restarts on entry while attained age keeps running (HE-FN-TUN-001). Years of life lost (HE-FN-YLL-001) use a normative standard life table, a different purpose from the national table used here. Notation follows the Background Mortality article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Background mortality function for the cycle death probability of a modelled cohort" ;
    hemo:9000014 "b(x_t, Delta, p_dis) = (p_bg, p_all)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-BGM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "background-mortality-function-for-the-cycle-death-probability-of-a-modelled-cohort" .

hemo:5000161 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Country-specific value set comparison function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-CSVS-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000379,
        hemo:6000380,
        hemo:6000381,
        hemo:6000382,
        hemo:6000383,
        hemo:6000384,
        hemo:6000385 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000379,
        hemo:6000380,
        hemo:6000381,
        hemo:6000382 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000383,
        hemo:6000384,
        hemo:6000385 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes one EQ-5D-3L health profile, or the pair of profiles a treatment moves patients between, and the coefficients of two national value sets, and returns the utility gain under each set, the gap between the two gains and the resulting change in the QALY gain and in the incremental cost-effectiveness ratio. The scoring rule itself is live and is not restated here. The main-effects form with a constant and level decrements, which the article writes as U(s) equal to 1 minus c I(s) minus the sum of the level decrements beta_d(l_d), is HE-FM-EQ3L-001 with the N3 decrement set to zero; HE-FM-EQ3L-002 fixes it at the UK MVH tariff, HE-CF-EQ3L-001 scores any constant and N3 set such as the Dutch one, and HE-FM-EQ3L-003 gives the QALY difference between two states under one set. The records here compare sets. HE-FM-CSVS-001 splits the cross-set gap in a utility gain by term, HE-FM-CSVS-002 turns two QALY gains into relative changes in the gain and the ICER (HE-FM-CUA-001), and HE-FM-CSVS-003 gives the count-based D1 form that the US set uses in place of a constant. Five-level value sets belong to the EQ-5D-5L value set concept, and QALYs from different sets are not added (HE-ER-QALY-003). Notation follows the Country-Specific Value Set article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Country-specific value set comparison function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "V(s_0, s_1; theta_A, theta_B) = (g_A, g_B, dg)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-CSVS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "country-specific-value-set-comparison-function" .

hemo:5000164 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Patient time cost function from service use, hours per use and an hourly value of time"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-PTC-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000394,
        hemo:6000395,
        hemo:6000396,
        hemo:6000397,
        hemo:6000398,
        hemo:6000399 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000394,
        hemo:6000395,
        hemo:6000396,
        hemo:6000397,
        hemo:6000398 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000399 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the number of times a patient uses each type of health service, the hours each use takes in travel, waiting and care, and an hourly value of time to the money value of the patient's time in care over a period, per visit and in total. It is the resource costing sum HE-FM-COST-001 with patient hours as the resource and the hourly value as the unit cost, so the package keeps only the steps particular to patient time, which are hours built up from service counts (HE-FM-PTC-001 and HE-CF-PTC-001), the change in an incremental cost-effectiveness ratio when the time cost is added (HE-FM-PTC-002) and a value of time that depends on employment status (HE-FM-PTC-003). Productivity cost is a different cost category. The absence records HE-FN-ABSN-001, HE-FM-ABSN-002 and HE-FM-ABSN-003 value time lost from paid work as lost output, under the human capital approach or within the friction period, whereas patient time cost values the hours spent in care whether they are taken from paid work, unpaid work or leisure, and an hour taken from paid work is counted under one heading only. A value of time from another price year is moved with HE-FM-COST-002, time costs in later years are discounted with HE-FN-DISC-001, and a national total from a mean time cost per patient follows HE-FM-COI-001. Notation follows the Patient Time Cost article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Patient time cost function from service use, hours per use and an hourly value of time" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p(n, t_trav, t_wait, t_care, w) = C_T" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-PTC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "patient-time-cost-function-from-service-use-hours-per-use-and-an-hourly-value-of-time" .

hemo:5000165 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "EQ-5D-5L value set scoring function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-EQ5L-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000400,
        hemo:6000401,
        hemo:6000402,
        hemo:6000403,
        hemo:6000404,
        hemo:6000405,
        hemo:6000406 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000400,
        hemo:6000401,
        hemo:6000402,
        hemo:6000403,
        hemo:6000404,
        hemo:6000405 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000406 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps an EQ-5D-5L profile, the level 1 to 5 reported on each of mobility, self-care, usual activities, pain or discomfort and anxiety or depression, to a single utility under the coefficients of a chosen five-level value set. The five-level instrument describes 3,125 states, and a main-effects value set gives each dimension its own decrement at each of levels 2 to 5, 20 decrements in all, plus a constant where the set reports one. Once the decrement for the reported level of each dimension has been looked up (HE-FM-EQ5L-001), the profile score is the main-effects form of HE-FM-EQ3L-001 with the N3 decrement set to zero, and the three-level tariff itself is HE-FM-EQ3L-002; the 15D counterpart is HE-FM-15D-001. The utilities it returns weight time in QALYs (HE-FM-QALY-001), and a QALY difference between two scored states is HE-FM-EQ3L-003, which applies only when both states are scored with the same set. Scoring one profile under the value sets of two countries belongs to the Country-Specific Value Set page. Notation follows the EQ-5D-5L Value Set article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "EQ-5D-5L value set scoring function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "u5(l_MO, l_SC, l_UA, l_PD, l_AD; theta) = U" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-EQ5L-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "eq-5d-5l-value-set-scoring-function" .

hemo:5000167 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Health production function mapping medical care, own time and schooling to gross investment in health and its least cost"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-HPF-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000415,
        hemo:6000416,
        hemo:6000417,
        hemo:6000418,
        hemo:6000419,
        hemo:6000420,
        hemo:6000421,
        hemo:6000422,
        hemo:6000423,
        hemo:6000424 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000415,
        hemo:6000416,
        hemo:6000417,
        hemo:6000418,
        hemo:6000419,
        hemo:6000420,
        hemo:6000421,
        hemo:6000422 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000423,
        hemo:6000424 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a person's medical care, own time and stock of schooling to gross investment in health, Grossman's I = I(M, TH; E), and, given the price of care, the wage and an outlay or a target, to the least-cost input mix and the unit cost of gross investment. It uses the Cobb-Douglas member of the class that Grossman fitted, with the intercept suppressed, and treats schooling as an efficiency shift. The output I is the gross investment term of the live stock update HE-FM-DFH-001, which carries the stock into the next period, and the unit cost of HE-FM-HPF-003 is the marginal cost of gross investment pi_(t-1) that the pure investment condition HE-FM-DFH-002 takes as an input, since constant returns and fixed input prices make marginal and average cost equal. The package keeps the production side only, with the Cobb-Douglas gross investment in HE-FM-HPF-001, the least-cost split of an outlay in HE-FM-HPF-002, the unit cost and outlay for a target in HE-FM-HPF-003, and the outlay that holds the stock on a target path in HE-CF-HPF-001. A mix that costs more than the least-cost mix for its output is measured by the cost efficiency ratio of HE-FM-EFF-002. The production of care by providers belongs to the healthcare production function concept and is not drafted here. Notation follows the Health Production Function article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health production function mapping medical care, own time and schooling to gross investment in health and its least cost" ;
    hemo:9000014 "g(M, TH, E, P, W, C, K, rho_H) = (I, pi)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-HPF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-production-function-mapping-medical-care-own-time-and-schooling-to-gross-investment-in-health-and-its-least-cost" .

hemo:5000171 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Pigouvian subsidy bill, inframarginal transfer and net welfare with costly public funds"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-PIGS-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000451,
        hemo:6000452,
        hemo:6000453,
        hemo:6000454,
        hemo:6000455,
        hemo:6000456,
        hemo:6000457,
        hemo:6000458,
        hemo:6000459,
        hemo:6000460,
        hemo:6000461 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000451,
        hemo:6000452,
        hemo:6000453,
        hemo:6000454,
        hemo:6000455,
        hemo:6000456,
        hemo:6000457 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000458,
        hemo:6000459,
        hemo:6000460,
        hemo:6000461 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps linear marginal private benefit, constant marginal cost and falling marginal external benefit curves, a per-unit subsidy and the marginal excess burden of taxation to the subsidy bill, the part of it paid to people who would have taken up the activity anyway, the net welfare change of the subsidy and the uniform rate that maximises it. The efficient quantity and the Pigouvian subsidy s = MEB(Q_s) with these curve shapes are HE-FM-EXT-002, within the social marginal benefit framework HE-FM-EXT-001, so the article's efficient 72 thousand courses, its subsidy of GBP 12 and its welfare loss of GBP 90,000 a year without a subsidy are re-applications of HE-FM-EXT-002 and are not drafted again. The subsidy set at the external benefit observed before the subsidy has the same algebra as the tax record HE-FM-PIGT-003 with the sign of the external term reversed (an external cost of minus 30 plus 0.25Q for the article's curves), which returns the article's 75 thousand courses and loss of 5.625. HE-FM-PIGT-003 is written for an external cost of zero or above (HE-VR-PIGT-025, HE-AS-PIGT-006, HE-VT-PIGT-006), and for a subsidy the overshoot lies above the efficient quantity, so the package reads that case through HE-FM-PIGS-002 at a rate equal to the external benefit at Q_p (HE-EX-PIGS-006) and does not draft it again. The excess burden of financing is the marginal excess burden of HE-FM-DWL-004 applied to the subsidy bill. The new steps are the bill and its split between inframarginal and induced users (HE-FM-PIGS-001), the net welfare of any uniform or targeted rate (HE-FM-PIGS-002) and the welfare-maximising uniform rate (HE-FM-PIGS-003). Notation follows the Pigouvian Subsidy article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pigouvian subsidy bill, inframarginal transfer and net welfare with costly public funds" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p(a, b, c, m, g, s, lambda) = (B, T, W, s_opt)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-PIGS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pigouvian-subsidy-bill-inframarginal-transfer-and-net-welfare-with-costly-public-funds" .

hemo:5000182 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Periodic budget impact"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-BIA-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000007 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000069 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000518,
        hemo:6000519,
        hemo:6000520,
        hemo:6000521 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000518,
        hemo:6000519,
        hemo:6000521 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000520 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000065,
        hemo:7000066,
        hemo:7000067,
        hemo:7000068 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000070,
        hemo:7000071,
        hemo:7000072,
        hemo:7000073 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Calculates the change in expenditure during period t by subtracting reference-scenario expenditure from adoption-scenario expenditure." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Periodic budget impact" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Sullivan SD, Mauskopf JA, Augustovski F, Jaime Caro J, Lee KM, Minchin M, et al. Budget impact analysis: principles of good practice. Report of the ISPOR 2012 Budget Impact Analysis Good Practice II Task Force. Value in Health. 2014;17(1):5-14. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2013.08.2291" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Using unchanged current practice as the reference automatically. The reference scenario should represent the expected future without adoption and may include changes in population, treatment patterns, prices and clinical practice." ;
    hemo:9000014 "BI_t = E_A_t - E_R_t" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-BIA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "periodic-budget-impact" .

hemo:5000183 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Cumulative budget impact"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-BIA-002" ;
