        hemo:6003131,
        hemo:6003132 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003129 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004403,
        hemo:7004404,
        hemo:7007364 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004406,
        hemo:7004407,
        hemo:7004408,
        hemo:7004413,
        hemo:7004418 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8001004 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000996 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000009 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-10" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Divides a cost in the source currency by the source country's PPP conversion factor, which gives international dollars, and multiplies by the target country's factor, which gives the target currency. Both factors are local currency units per international dollar, so the route through international dollars follows from their units (derived here), and a target factor of 1 returns international dollars. The World Bank describes PPPs as eliminating differences in price levels between countries in the process of conversion, and the CCEMG-EPPI-Centre Cost Converter page notes that pure exchange-rate conversion, the market rate of HE-FM-BOC-001, does not. Where the cost also comes from another price year, the year is moved first with HE-FM-COST-002 and HE-CF-BOC-001 sets out the two orders." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cost converted between currencies at purchasing power parity" ;
    hemo:9000010 "EPPI Centre, University College London, with the Campbell and Cochrane Economics Methods Group. CCEMG-EPPI-Centre Cost Converter, version 1.7 (January 2024), web page accessed 18 October 2026. Information for users, section How the tool works, which converts the price-year adjusted cost from the original currency to a target currency with conversion rates based on purchasing power parities for GDP and states that IMF and OECD PPP values differ for OECD countries in all price years; and section Applications of the tool, which names health care specific, technology specific and episode specific PPPs as alternatives. https://eppi.ioe.ac.uk/costconversion/",
        "Turner HC, Lauer JA, Tran BX, Teerawattananon Y, Jit M. Adjusting for inflation and currency changes within health economic studies. Value in Health. 2019;22(9):1026-1032. Box 1 (page 1027), which defines the international dollar as buying in the country of interest a comparable amount of goods and services as US$1 buys in the United States and states that a PPP exchange rate, the number of units of a country's currency required to buy the same quantity of goods and services as US$1 buys in the United States, is used to convert a currency unit to international dollars; and the section on the choice of the output currency (page 1029) on the advantages and limits of international dollars. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2019.03.021",
        "World Bank. World Development Indicators. PPP conversion factor, GDP (LCU per international $), indicator PA.NUS.PPP. Definition, which states that the PPP conversion factor is a currency conversion factor and a spatial price deflator, that PPPs convert different currencies to a common currency and in the process equalise their purchasing power by eliminating differences in price levels between countries, and that the factor is for the level of GDP with the US dollar as base currency (sources International Comparison Program, Eurostat and OECD). https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/PA.NUS.PPP",
        "World Health Organization. Global Health Observatory indicator metadata registry, indicator 4590, Tobacco MPOWER Raise taxes, tobacco and nicotine products retail price. Method of measurement, which states that prices in local currency were divided by the IMF World Economic Outlook implied PPP conversion rates for 2022 for conversion into international dollars, and divided by the IMF rate in national currency per US dollar for conversion into US dollars. https://www.who.int/data/gho/indicator-metadata-registry/imr-details/4590" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Market-rate and PPP conversions mixed in one comparison. Turner and colleagues treat US dollars at market rates and international dollars as different output currencies, either or both of which a study may report. Adding or ranking costs from several countries when some were converted at market rates and others at PPPs gives figures in no single unit, so one method is used for each comparison and named.",
        "PPP ratio inverted. Multiplying by the source factor and dividing by the target factor gives about 1,142.86 in place of 875 in the illustrative example, 31% too high (computed here for illustration). Since both factors are local currency per international dollar, the source factor always divides." ;
    hemo:9000014 "C_t = C_s / PPP_s * PPP_t" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>C_t</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><ci>C_s</ci><ci>PPP_s</ci></apply><ci>PPP_t</ci></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "C_{t} = \\frac{C_{s}}{\\mathrm{PPP}_{s}} \\, \\mathrm{PPP}_{t}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-BOC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cost-converted-between-currencies-at-purchasing-power-parity" .

hemo:5000704 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Basket mean, lowest and highest price from five converted reference prices"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-BOC-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000156 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004425 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003133,
        hemo:6003134,
        hemo:6003135,
        hemo:6003136,
        hemo:6003137,
        hemo:6003138,
        hemo:6003139,
        hemo:6003140 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003136,
        hemo:6003137,
        hemo:6003138,
        hemo:6003139,
        hemo:6003140 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003133,
        hemo:6003134,
        hemo:6003135 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004423,
        hemo:7004424 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004426,
        hemo:7004427,
        hemo:7004428,
        hemo:7004429,
        hemo:7004430 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Combines the converted prices c_1 to c_5 of a five-country basket, each from HE-FM-BOC-001, into the mean, the lowest and the highest price, three of the aggregation rules f in the article's P_ref. Kanavos and colleagues report that the lowest price in the basket is often used and that the average or the median is not uncommon, and Rémuzat and colleagues found the average price in use in 15 European countries and the lowest price in 7. The highest price is the PMPRB's highest international price. The median, baskets of other sizes and countries without a price are handled by HE-CF-BOC-002, and HE-FM-BOC-004 turns a reference price into a ceiling." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Basket mean, lowest and highest price from five converted reference prices" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Kanavos P, Fontrier AM, Gill J, Efthymiadou O. Does external reference pricing deliver what it promises? Evidence on its impact at national level. European Journal of Health Economics. 2020;21(1):129-151. Introduction, which states that ex-factory prices are used in the majority of cases and that pricing authorities rely mostly on list prices rather than transaction prices, and that the lowest price in the basket is often used but the average or the median is not uncommon. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10198-019-01116-4",
        "Patented Medicine Prices Review Board. Guidelines for PMPRB Staff, in effect 1 January 2026. Glossary, which defines the highest international price (HIP) as the highest price of the same strength and dosage form of the same patented medicine among the Schedule Countries. https://www.canada.ca/en/patented-medicine-prices-review/services/legislation/guidelines.html",
        "Rémuzat C, Urbinati D, Mzoughi O, El Hammi E, Belgaied W, Toumi M. Overview of external reference pricing systems in Europe. Journal of Market Access and Health Policy. 2015;3:27675. Abstract results, 15 countries using the average price, 7 the lowest price and 7 other methods, with baskets of 1 to 31 countries; and the Results subsection Main calculation methods, which names the average price, the average of the three or four lowest prices and the lowest price among the reference countries. https://doi.org/10.3402/jmahp.v3.27675" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Larger basket assumed to give a lower reference price. Adding a sixth country priced at 130 to Basket A raises the mean from 105 to about 109.17 while the lowest price stays at 80 (computed here for illustration). Under a lowest-price rule an added country can never raise the benchmark, as the article notes, while under the mean or the median it can.",
        "Missing basket price entered as zero. Recording the unlaunched country of Basket B as 0 gives a mean of 77 and a lowest price of 0, against 96.25 and 80 when it is left out, and a ceiling at 1.25 times the mean of 96.25 in place of 120.31 (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_mean = (c_1 + c_2 + c_3 + c_4 + c_5) / 5; P_low = min(min(min(min(c_1, c_2), c_3), c_4), c_5); P_high = max(max(max(max(c_1, c_2), c_3), c_4), c_5)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-BOC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "basket-mean-lowest-and-highest-price-from-five-converted-reference-prices" .

hemo:5000708 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Euler step of one compartment from its inflows, outflows, births and deaths"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CMPM-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000158 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004460 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003156,
        hemo:6003157,
        hemo:6003158,
        hemo:6003159,
        hemo:6003160,
        hemo:6003161,
        hemo:6003162 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003157,
        hemo:6003158,
        hemo:6003159,
        hemo:6003160,
        hemo:6003161,
        hemo:6003162 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003156 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004459 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004461,
        hemo:7004462,
        hemo:7004463,
        hemo:7004464 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Over a short step of length dt the number in a compartment changes by the flows into it from other compartments, less the flows out of it to other compartments, plus births or immigration into it, less deaths or emigration out of it, each held at its value at the start of the step. This is the article's balance equation for dx_j/dt advanced by one Euler step. Each internal flow leaves one compartment and enters another, so summed over all compartments the internal flows cancel and the population total changes only by births less deaths times dt, and stays fixed in a closed population (derived from the balance equation). Hethcote's endemic SIR model is a case with external flows, with births at rate mu N entering the susceptible class and deaths at rates mu S, mu I and mu R leaving the three classes, so that the population size stays constant. The SIR form of the step is HE-FM-CMPM-004." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Euler step of one compartment from its inflows, outflows, births and deaths" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Hethcote HW. The mathematics of infectious diseases. SIAM Review. 2000;42(4):599-653. Section 2.4, The Classic Endemic Model (pages 607 and 608), which gives the SIR model with births and deaths with S(t) + I(t) + R(t) = N, an inflow of newborns into the susceptible class at rate mu N and deaths in the classes at rates mu S, mu I and mu R, and states that the deaths balance the births so that the population size N is constant. https://doi.org/10.1137/S0036144500371907" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Internal flow taken from its source compartment but not added to its destination. Each internal flow appears twice, as an outflow of one compartment and an inflow of another. Taking the article's 29.7 day-1 infections from the susceptibles but leaving them out of the infectious compartment gives 90 infectious people instead of 119.7 and a population of 9,970.3 instead of 10,000 after one day, a loss that a mass-balance check on the compartment totals catches at once." ;
    hemo:9000014 "x_next = x_now + (F_in - F_out + b - d) * dt" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CMPM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "euler-step-of-one-compartment-from-its-inflows-outflows-births-and-deaths" .

hemo:5000732 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Number of EQ-5D-5L health states and main-effects decrements, and the share valued directly"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EQ5L-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000165 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004655,
        hemo:7004656,
        hemo:7004657 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003359,
        hemo:6003360,
        hemo:6003361,
        hemo:6003362,
        hemo:6003363,
        hemo:6003364 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003360,
        hemo:6003361,
        hemo:6003363 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003362,
        hemo:6003364 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004653,
        hemo:7004654 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004658,
        hemo:7004659,
        hemo:7004660,
        hemo:7004661,
        hemo:7004662 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Counts the states that a descriptive system with K dimensions of L levels describes, the number of decrements a main-effects value set needs when level 1 carries none, and the share of states that respondents valued directly. For the EQ-5D-5L the count is 5 to the power 5, 3,125 states, and 5 dimensions times 4 levels above level 1 give 20 decrements, the size of the England model in HE-FM-EQ5L-002. The same counts give 243 states and 10 level decrements for the EQ-5D-3L, whose UK tariff in HE-FM-EQ3L-002 adds a constant and N3 to them. The share valued shows how much of a value set rests on model prediction." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Number of EQ-5D-5L health states and main-effects decrements, and the share valued directly" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Devlin NJ, Shah KK, Feng Y, Mulhern B, van Hout B. Valuing health-related quality of life: an EQ-5D-5L value set for England. Health Economics. 2018;27(1):7-22. Section 1, introduction, which states that the five dimensions and five levels of the EQ-5D-5L describe 3,125 (5 to the power 5) unique health states, against 243 (3 to the power 5) for the three-level EQ-5D; section 2.2, study design, which states that the time trade-off design included 86 states, the worst state 55555 and the five mildest states selected in advance plus 80 states from the design, spread over 10 blocks; section 2.5, which counts 20 parameters (4 levels times 5 dimensions) in the least restrictive model; section 3.4, which refers to the 3,125 health states described by the EQ-5D-5L. https://doi.org/10.1002/hec.3564",
        "Hernández Alava M, Pudney S, Wailoo A. The EQ-5D-5L value set for England: findings of a quality assurance program. Value in Health. 2020;23(5):642-648. Abstract, which states that the time trade-off experiments covered less than 3% of the possible 3125 five-level health states and the discrete choice experiments 12.5% of possible states, and that significant limitations were identified in the valuation data and the statistical analysis behind the English value set. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2019.10.017",
        "Rowen D, Mukuria C, Bray N, Carlton J, Longworth L, Meads D, Oluboyede Y, O'Neill C, Yang Y. A United Kingdom value set for the EQ-5D-5L. Value in Health. 2026;29(5):858-869. Abstract, which reports composite time trade-off interviews for 102 health states with a representative sample of UK adults, 1200 interviews in all, and a value set generated with a random effects Tobit model. Cited at abstract level only, and its coefficients are not used here. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2026.03.008" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Counting EQ-5D-5L states as levels times dimensions. Multiplying 5 levels by 5 dimensions gives 25, the number of answer options on the questionnaire, not the number of states. The states are every combination of the five answers, 5 to the power 5, 3,125.",
        "Reading every EQ-5D-5L value as directly observed. In the England study 86 states were valued by time trade-off, so the values of the other 3,039 states are model predictions. The choice of model therefore shapes most of a five-level value set, and a dispute about the model is a dispute about most of the values." ;
    hemo:9000014 "S = L^K; P = K * (L - 1); f_v = n_v / S" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EQ5L-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "number-of-eq-5d-5l-health-states-and-main-effects-decrements-and-the-share-valued-directly" .

hemo:5000750 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Trial adverse event counts to a per-cycle probability"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-AER-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000217,
        hemo:5000270,
        hemo:5000272 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004825,
        hemo:7004826,
        hemo:7004827 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004258,
        hemo:6004259,
        hemo:6004260,
        hemo:6004261,
        hemo:6004262,
        hemo:6004263,
        hemo:6004264 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004258,
        hemo:6004259,
        hemo:6004260,
        hemo:6004261 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004262,
        hemo:6004263,
        hemo:6004264 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004821 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006654,
        hemo:7006655,
        hemo:7006656,
        hemo:7006657,
        hemo:7006658 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the counts in a trial safety table, the number of patients with an adverse event and the number at risk, with the follow-up period and the model cycle length, and returns the probability of a first episode in one model cycle. It computes the trial proportion with HE-FM-AER-001 and then rescales it with HE-FM-AER-004 through the implied constant rate, so the inputs are counts rather than the proportion the formula takes. The general conversion from an annual probability is HE-CF-TP-002 on the Transition Probability page." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function: trial adverse event counts to a per-cycle probability" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Gidwani R, Russell LB. Estimating transition probabilities from published evidence: a tutorial for decision modelers. PharmacoEconomics. 2020;38(11):1153-1164. Equations 8 to 10 on converting a probability observed over one period to a rate and back to a probability for the model cycle. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40273-020-00937-z" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Dividing the trial proportion by the number of cycles. Dividing 0.18 by six gives 0.03 per cycle, which over six cycles implies a cumulative risk of about 0.167 rather than the 0.18 observed. The proportion has to pass through the rate before it is rescaled" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_trial = n_event / n_risk; r_AE = -log(1 - P_trial) / T_follow; p_cycle = 1 - exp(-r_AE * L_cycle)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-AER-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-trial-adverse-event-counts-to-a-per-cycle-probability" .

hemo:5000751 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "AIC, AIC differences and Akaike weights for candidate survival curves"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-AIC-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000275,
        hemo:5000276,
        hemo:5000277 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004833,
        hemo:7004834,
        hemo:7004835 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004830 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006659,
        hemo:7006660,
        hemo:7006661,
        hemo:7006662 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the maximised log-likelihoods and parameter counts of a set of candidate survival models fitted to the same data, and returns three vectors: AIC, the AIC difference from the best-scoring model and the Akaike weight of each model. It chains HE-FM-AIC-001, HE-FM-AIC-002 and HE-FM-AIC-003, and the second and third steps use the whole vector produced by the step before, so the inputs are lists of models rather than the single values the formulae take." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function: AIC, AIC differences and Akaike weights for candidate survival curves" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Burnham KP, Anderson DR. Multimodel inference: understanding AIC and BIC in model selection. Sociological Methods & Research. 2004;33(2):261-304. Pages 271 to 272, which define the AIC differences, the likelihood of each model given the data and the Akaike weights, normalised to sum to 1 over the model set. https://doi.org/10.1177/0049124104268644" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Comparing weights from candidate sets of different size. Removing the exponential from the six-curve example leaves the log-normal weight at about 0.44 only because the exponential contributed almost nothing; removing the generalised gamma instead would raise it to about 0.59. Weights from different candidate sets cannot be compared, and the set should be reported with them" ;
    hemo:9000014 "AIC = 2 * k - 2 * lnL; delta = AIC - min(AIC); w = exp(-delta / 2) / sum(exp(-delta / 2))" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-AIC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-aic-aic-differences-and-akaike-weights-for-candidate-survival-curves" .

hemo:5000752 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Alpha spent and stopping hazard ratio at a group sequential look"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-ASP-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000280,
        hemo:5000281,
        hemo:5000282,
        hemo:5000283,
        hemo:5000284 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004842,
        hemo:7004843,
        hemo:7004844 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004265,
        hemo:6004266,
        hemo:6004267,
        hemo:6004268,
        hemo:6004269,
        hemo:6004270,
        hemo:6004271,
        hemo:6004272,
        hemo:6004273,
        hemo:6004274,
        hemo:6004275,
        hemo:6004276 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004265,
        hemo:6004266,
        hemo:6004267,
        hemo:6004268,
        hemo:6004269 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004270,
        hemo:6004271,
        hemo:6004272,
        hemo:6004273,
        hemo:6004274,
        hemo:6004275,
        hemo:6004276 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004838 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7006663,
        hemo:7006664,
        hemo:7006665,
        hemo:7006666,
        hemo:7006667 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the counts a trial report gives, deaths at the current and previous looks and the planned final deaths, with the overall one-sided level and the critical value at the look, and returns the cumulative alpha spent under both Lan-DeMets functions, the increment at the look and the hazard ratio needed to stop. It chains HE-FM-ASP-001, HE-FM-ASP-002, HE-FM-ASP-003, HE-FM-ASP-004 and HE-FM-ASP-005. The critical value is an input because, from the second look onwards, it comes from recursive numerical integration over the joint normal distribution of the test statistics, which a spreadsheet formula cannot do; the function computes only the closed-form parts." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function: alpha spent and stopping hazard ratio at a group sequential look" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Anderson KM. gsDesign Technical Manual, chapter 8.1 (spending function definitions). Gives the Lan-DeMets O'Brien-Fleming type and Pocock type functions and prints the Pocock type spend and bounds for four equally spaced looks at alpha of 0.025. https://keaven.github.io/gsd-tech-manual/spendfn.html" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Taking later boundaries as the normal quantile of the increment. At the second Pocock type look the increment is 0.006569, and its normal quantile is about 2.480, but the correct boundary is 2.368. The shortcut ignores the first look and the correlation between looks, so the boundary is too strict and part of the budget is never used. Boundaries after the first look have to come from software that integrates the joint distribution" ;
    hemo:9000014 "t_k = d_k / D; t_prev = d_prev / D; alpha_OF_k = 2 - 2 * Phi(Phi^-1(1 - alpha / 2) / sqrt(t_k)); alpha_P_k = alpha * log(1 + (exp(1) - 1) * t_k); inc_OF_k = alpha_OF_k - (2 - 2 * Phi(Phi^-1(1 - alpha / 2) / sqrt(t_prev))) for t_prev > 0; inc_OF_k = alpha_OF_k for t_prev = 0; inc_P_k = alpha_P_k - alpha * log(1 + (exp(1) - 1) * t_prev); HR_k = exp(-2 * c_k / sqrt(d_k))" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-ASP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-alpha-spent-and-stopping-hazard-ratio-at-a-group-sequential-look" .

hemo:5000753 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Therapy price to annuity instalment, nominal total and present value"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-AP-001" ;
