        hemo:6004734,
        hemo:6004735,
        hemo:6004736,
        hemo:6004737 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005505 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007004,
        hemo:7007005,
        hemo:7007006,
        hemo:7007007,
        hemo:7007008 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the four counts of a cross-sectional survey and three facts about a local population, its size, its exposed share and an annual cost per prevalent case, and returns the attributable annual cost of prevalent cases. It chains three steps: the prevalence ratio HE-FM-PR-001 from the survey, the attributable fraction HE-FM-PR-003 with the local exposed share, and the top-down burden HE-FM-COI-002 applied to the local prevalent cases. The inputs therefore differ from the formula's variables: the survey supplies the ratio and the prevalences, and the local population supplies the cases and costs." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function: attributable annual cost of prevalent cases from survey counts and a prevalence ratio" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Jo C. Cost-of-illness studies: concepts, scopes, and methods. Clinical and Molecular Hepatology. 2014;20(4):327-337. Section on top-down, bottom-up and econometric approaches (PAF from a prevalence and an unadjusted relative risk, applied to aggregate costs); Tamhane AR, Westfall AO, Burkholder GA, Cutter GR. Prevalence odds ratio versus prevalence ratio: choice comes with consequences. Statistics in Medicine. 2016;35(30):5730-5735. Table 3 (prevalence ratio from a two-by-two table)." ;
    hemo:9000011 "Using the prevalence odds ratio from a logistic model. Feeding the survey POR of about 2.4286 into the attributable fraction raises the attributable cases to about 38,182 and the cost to GBP 45.8 million, 27% above the figure from the PR, because the condition is common" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P_1 = a / (a + b); P_0 = c / (c + d); PR = P_1 / P_0; PAF = e * (PR - 1) / (1 + e * (PR - 1)); M = N * (e * P_1 + (1 - e) * P_0); A = PAF * M; C_A = A * k" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-PR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-attributable-annual-cost-of-prevalent-cases-from-survey-counts-and-a-prevalence-ratio" .

hemo:5000835 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Probabilistic economically justifiable price at a stated probability of cost-effectiveness"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-EJP-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000202,
        hemo:5000613 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7005517,
        hemo:7005518,
        hemo:7005519 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004738,
        hemo:6004739,
        hemo:6004740,
        hemo:6004741,
        hemo:6004742,
        hemo:6004743 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004738,
        hemo:6004739,
        hemo:6004740,
        hemo:6004741 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004742,
        hemo:6004743 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005514 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007009,
        hemo:7007010,
        hemo:7007011,
        hemo:7007012 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes matched draws from a probabilistic sensitivity analysis, a threshold and a required probability of cost-effectiveness, and returns two prices per unit. EJP_mean is the price at which expected incremental net benefit is zero, the probabilistic EJP of the article. EJP_alpha is the highest price at which at least a share alpha of the draws is cost-effective at lambda, the stricter reading the article describes. Because each draw's net benefit is linear in price, draw i is cost-effective at a price P exactly when P is at or below its own EJP_i, so the probability of cost-effectiveness at P is the share of draw-level EJPs at or above P and the search over prices needs no further model runs. The inputs differ from the formula's variables: the function takes vectors of draws and a probability level and returns an order statistic as well as a closed-form value." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function: probabilistic economically justifiable price at a stated probability of cost-effectiveness" ;
    hemo:9000010 "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). London: NICE; published 31 January 2022, last updated 31 March 2026. Section 4.7.12 (preferred estimate from probabilistic analysis unless the model is linear) and section 4.7.15 (present the probability that the treatment is cost effective at maximum acceptable ICERs of GBP 25,000 to GBP 35,000 per QALY gained); Zeevat F, Luttjeboer J, Paulissen JHJ, van der Schans J, Beutels P, Boersma C, Postma MJ. Exploratory analysis of the economically justifiable price of a hypothetical RSV vaccine for older adults in the Netherlands and the United Kingdom. Journal of Infectious Diseases. 2022;226(Suppl 1):S102-S109. doi:10.1093/infdis/jiab118. Results of the probabilistic sensitivity analysis. https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Assuming the deterministic EJP is cost-effective with high probability. Zeevat and colleagues found that at their base-case EJP of EUR 16.38 for a hypothetical RSV vaccine at EUR 20,000 per QALY, the probability of cost-effectiveness in the Netherlands was 0.44, and at GBP 72.29 at GBP 20,000 per QALY in the United Kingdom it was 0.59. A price chosen to reach a higher probability lies below the EJP",
        "Averaging draw-level EJPs when units vary. Two illustrative draws with 0.80 QALYs and GBP 1,500 of other costs but 20 and 30 packs have draw-level EJPs of GBP 925 and about GBP 616.67, whose mean is about GBP 770.83. Expected net benefit is zero at GBP 740, the GBP 18,500 per patient divided by the mean of 25 packs (computed here for illustration)" ;
    hemo:9000014 "EJP_i = (lambda * Delta_E_i - Delta_C_other_i) / U_i; EJP_mean = (lambda * sum_(i=1)^N [Delta_E_i] - sum_(i=1)^N [Delta_C_other_i]) / sum_(i=1)^N [U_i]; P_CE(P) = (1/N) * sum_(i=1)^N [1(EJP_i >= P)]; EJP_alpha = max {P : P_CE(P) >= alpha}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-EJP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-probabilistic-economically-justifiable-price-at-a-stated-probability-of-cost-effectiveness" .

hemo:5000836 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Incremental QALYs and ICER after LOCF from arm utility means and dropout fractions"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-LOCF-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000192,
        hemo:5000194,
        hemo:5000616,
        hemo:5000617 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7005526,
        hemo:7005527,
        hemo:7005528 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004744,
        hemo:6004745,
        hemo:6004746,
        hemo:6004747,
        hemo:6004748,
        hemo:6004749,
        hemo:6004750,
        hemo:6004751,
        hemo:6004752,
        hemo:6004753,
        hemo:6004754,
        hemo:6004755,
        hemo:6004756,
        hemo:6004757,
        hemo:6004758 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004744,
        hemo:6004745,
        hemo:6004746,
        hemo:6004747,
        hemo:6004748,
        hemo:6004749,
        hemo:6004750,
        hemo:6004751,
        hemo:6004752 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004753,
        hemo:6004754,
        hemo:6004755,
        hemo:6004756,
        hemo:6004757,
        hemo:6004758 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005522 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007013,
        hemo:7007014,
        hemo:7007015,
        hemo:7007016,
        hemo:7007017 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the inputs a trial-based cost-effectiveness analysis holds, each arm's mean utility at baseline, 6 months and 12 months with complete follow-up, each arm's fraction of dropouts after the 6-month visit and the incremental cost, and returns the incremental QALYs and ICER with complete data and after LOCF. It applies HE-FM-LOCF-001 to each arm's 12-month mean, then the trapezoidal QALY formula HE-FM-QALY-002 with visits at 0, 0.5 and 1 year, where QALYs are 0.25 times the sum of the baseline mean, twice the 6-month mean and the 12-month mean, and then the ICER of HE-FM-ICER-001. Comparing the two ICERs shows how far the imputation alone moves the result." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function: incremental QALYs and ICER after LOCF from arm utility means and dropout fractions" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Lachin JM. Fallacies of last observation carried forward analyses. Clinical Trials. 2016;13(2):161-168. Equations 1 and 4. Faria R, Gomes M, Epstein D, White IR. A guide to handling missing data in cost-effectiveness analysis conducted within randomised controlled trials. PharmacoEconomics. 2014;32(12):1157-1170. Introduction on QALYs as cumulative measures built from longitudinal data and the checklist item advising that last-value carried forward be avoided. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40273-014-0193-3" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Carrying a utility forward past death. Values after death are not missing data. Carrying the last utility forward for a participant who died credits QALYs that were never lived; the function applies only to living participants who stopped providing data",
        "Reading the LOCF ICER as precise. The imputed values carry no uncertainty, so a standard error, confidence interval or acceptability curve computed from LOCF-completed QALYs can be too narrow. The National Research Council panel notes that LOCF may understate uncertainty when imputed values feed an area under the curve, and Faria and colleagues advise against last-value carried forward for missing outcomes in cost-effectiveness analysis" ;
    hemo:9000014 "u_T_12_LOCF = tau_T * u_T_6 + (1 - tau_T) * u_T_12; u_C_12_LOCF = tau_C * u_C_6 + (1 - tau_C) * u_C_12; dQ_full = 0.25 * (u_T_0 + 2 * u_T_6 + u_T_12) - 0.25 * (u_C_0 + 2 * u_C_6 + u_C_12); dQ_LOCF = 0.25 * (u_T_0 + 2 * u_T_6 + u_T_12_LOCF) - 0.25 * (u_C_0 + 2 * u_C_6 + u_C_12_LOCF); ICER_full = dC / dQ_full; ICER_LOCF = dC / dQ_LOCF" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-LOCF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-incremental-qalys-and-icer-after-locf-from-arm-utility-means-and-dropout-fractions" .

hemo:5000838 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Computational function for annualised capital cost per unit of service from several capital inputs"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-ANLS-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000622 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7005580,
        hemo:7005581,
        hemo:7005582 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003845,
        hemo:6003846,
        hemo:6003847,
        hemo:6003848,
        hemo:6003849,
        hemo:6003850,
        hemo:6003851,
        hemo:6003852,
        hemo:6003853,
        hemo:6003854,
        hemo:6003855,
        hemo:6003856,
        hemo:6003857,
        hemo:6004759,
        hemo:6004760 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003845,
        hemo:6003846,
        hemo:6003847,
        hemo:6003848,
        hemo:6003849,
        hemo:6003850,
        hemo:6003851,
        hemo:6003852 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003853,
        hemo:6003854,
        hemo:6003855,
        hemo:6003856,
        hemo:6003857,
        hemo:6004759,
        hemo:6004760 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005577 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007064,
        hemo:7007065,
        hemo:7007066,
        hemo:7007067 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the inputs a costing study usually holds, the price, resale value and useful life of each capital input, one discount rate and the annual volume of service, and returns the annualised capital cost per unit of service alongside the straight-line figure. It applies HE-FM-ANLS-001 to each input over its own life, sums the annual charges and divides by the volume. The Math / Code line shows two inputs, and the R and Python versions take any number." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for annualised capital cost per unit of service from several capital inputs" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Tan-Torres Edejer T, Baltussen R, Adam T, Hutubessy R, Acharya A, Evans DB, Murray CJL, editors. Making choices in health. WHO guide to cost-effectiveness analysis. Geneva, World Health Organization, 2003. Section 3.3.2, which annualises each capital item over its own useful life, net of its discounted resale value, by dividing by the annuity factor A(n, r). https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/42699" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Annualising training over the equipment's life. Spreading the GBP 12,000 training cost over the camera's 5 years instead of its 4-year repeat interval gives about GBP 2,658 a year instead of GBP 3,267, and about GBP 5.12 per screen instead of GBP 5.36. Each input is annualised over its own life" ;
    hemo:9000014 "A_1 = (1 - (1+r)^(-n_1)) / r; A_2 = (1 - (1+r)^(-n_2)) / r; E_1 = (K_1 - S_1 * (1+r)^(-n_1)) / A_1; E_2 = (K_2 - S_2 * (1+r)^(-n_2)) / A_2; E_total = E_1 + E_2; c_unit = E_total / Q; c_SL = ((K_1 - S_1) / n_1 + (K_2 - S_2) / n_2) / Q" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-ANLS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-annualised-capital-cost-per-unit-of-service-from-several-capital-inputs" .

hemo:5000839 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Computational function for the yearly return on capital and price recovered in an annualised charge"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-ANLS-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000623 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7005588,
        hemo:7005589,
        hemo:7005590 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003522,
        hemo:6003858,
        hemo:6003859,
        hemo:6003860,
        hemo:6003861,
        hemo:6003862,
        hemo:6003863,
        hemo:6003864,
        hemo:6003865 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003858,
        hemo:6003859,
        hemo:6003860,
        hemo:6003861 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003522,
        hemo:6003862,
        hemo:6003863,
        hemo:6003864,
        hemo:6003865 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005585 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007068,
        hemo:7007069,
        hemo:7007070,
        hemo:7007071 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the price, rate and useful life of an input with no resale value and a year number t, and returns the balance of the price still tied up at the start of that year, the return on it at rate r and the part of the price recovered by that year's charge. It applies HE-FM-ANLS-002 to find E and then splits E as a loan repayment, which shows where the gap between the annualised and straight-line charges comes from." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for the yearly return on capital and price recovered in an annualised charge" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Turner HC, Sandmann FG, Downey LE, Orangi S, Teerawattananon Y, Vassall A, Jit M. What are economic costs and when should they be used in health economic studies? Cost Effectiveness and Resource Allocation. 2023, volume 21, article 31. Figure 1, which describes the annualised economic cost as depreciation plus the opportunity cost of the funds tied up in the capital item. The year-by-year split is the standard repayment schedule of an annuity at rate r and follows algebraically from HE-FM-ANLS-002. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12962-023-00436-w" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Charging a return on the full price in every year. Adding r times K, GBP 1,750, to the straight-line charge of GBP 10,000 gives GBP 11,750 a year for the camera, above the correct GBP 11,074, because the balance tied up falls as the price is recovered" ;
    hemo:9000014 "A = (1 - (1+r)^(-n)) / r; E = K / A; B_t = K * (1+r)^(t-1) - E * ((1+r)^(t-1) - 1) / r; R_t = r * B_t; P_t = E - R_t" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-ANLS-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-the-yearly-return-on-capital-and-price-recovered-in-an-annualised-charge" .

hemo:5000840 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Computational function for a baseline-risk adjusted odds ratio, events avoided and ICER from a centred meta-regression"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-MREG-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000629 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7005596,
        hemo:7005597,
        hemo:7005598 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003866,
        hemo:6003867,
        hemo:6003868,
        hemo:6003869,
        hemo:6003870,
        hemo:6003871,
        hemo:6003872,
        hemo:6004761,
        hemo:6004762,
        hemo:6004763,
        hemo:6004764,
        hemo:6004765,
        hemo:6004766,
        hemo:6004767,
        hemo:6004768 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003866,
        hemo:6003867,
        hemo:6003868,
        hemo:6003869,
        hemo:6003870,
        hemo:6003871,
        hemo:6003872 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004761,
        hemo:6004762,
        hemo:6004763,
        hemo:6004764,
        hemo:6004765,
        hemo:6004766,
        hemo:6004767,
        hemo:6004768 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005593 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007072,
        hemo:7007073,
        hemo:7007074,
        hemo:7007075 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the inputs a decision modeller holds, a baseline risk for the model population, the fitted coefficients of a centred meta-regression on baseline log-odds and the cost and QALY consequences of an event, and returns the adjusted odds ratio, the absolute effect and the incremental cost-effectiveness ratio. It converts the risk to log-odds, applies HE-FM-MREG-005, exponentiates the result and applies the odds ratio to the baseline odds, so each step uses the result of the one before. The inputs therefore differ from the formula's variables, which need the covariate already on the log-odds scale." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for a baseline-risk adjusted odds ratio, events avoided and ICER from a centred meta-regression" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Dias S, Sutton AJ, Welton NJ, Ades AE. NICE DSU Technical Support Document 3, Heterogeneity, subgroups, meta-regression, bias and bias-adjustment. Decision Support Unit; 2011, last updated April 2012. Section 4.3 on un-centring the effect to any covariate value z and section 4.4 on meta-regression on baseline risk with the covariate on the logit scale. https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD3-Heterogeneity.final-report.08.05.12.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Multiplying the baseline risk by the odds ratio. Treating the odds ratio as a risk ratio gives a treated risk of p_0 times OR_z. At a 5% risk the error is small, 26.9 instead of 26.3 events avoided per 1,000, but at a 35% risk it gives 25.5 instead of 17.0, half as many again. The odds ratio has to be applied to the baseline odds" ;
    hemo:9000014 "z = log(p_0 / (1 - p_0)); theta_z = d + b * (z - x_bar); OR_z = exp(theta_z); p_1 = p_0 / (1 - p_0) * OR_z / (1 + p_0 / (1 - p_0) * OR_z); N_av = 1000 * (p_0 - p_1); C_net = 1000 * c_tx - N_av * s_ev; Q_gain = N_av * q_ev; ICER = C_net / Q_gain" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-MREG-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-a-baseline-risk-adjusted-odds-ratio-events-avoided-and-icer-from-a-centred-meta-regression" .

