        hemo:6003890,
        hemo:6003891,
        hemo:6004772,
        hemo:6004773,
        hemo:6004774,
        hemo:6004775,
        hemo:6004776,
        hemo:6004777,
        hemo:6004778,
        hemo:6004779,
        hemo:6004780 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005620 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007087,
        hemo:7007088,
        hemo:7007089,
        hemo:7007090,
        hemo:7007091 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the inputs a trial planner usually holds, the prior mean and standard deviation of incremental net benefit from the probabilistic analysis, the per-patient standard deviation of net benefit in each arm, the number of patients per arm and the size and lifetime of the beneficiary population, and returns per-person EVSI, per-person EVPI under normality, the share of EVPI the trial captures and population EVSI. It chains HE-FM-EVSI-002 (preposterior variance), HE-FM-EVSI-003 (closed-form EVSI, applied also at the prior variance for EVPI) and HE-FM-EVSI-004 (population EVSI), so its inputs differ from the symbols of any one formula. The calculator version takes the two upper-tail probabilities Q_s and Q_0 as inputs because it has no normal distribution function, and the Excel, R and Python versions compute them. The same EVSI can be checked by the single-loop Monte Carlo scheme of the second ISPOR report, drawing a true incremental net benefit from the prior and a trial estimate around it, and averaging the amount by which the posterior mean falls on the other side of zero from the prior mean, the value of switching in those results." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for per-person and population EVSI of a two-arm trial under normal incremental net benefit" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Jalal H, Goldhaber-Fiebert JD, Kuntz KM. Computing expected value of partial sample information from probabilistic sensitivity analysis using linear regression metamodeling. Medical Decision Making. 2015;35(5):584-595. The EVSI section and Box 2, which compute EVSI from the unit normal loss integral at the prior mean of incremental net benefit and the preposterior variance, the prior variance times n over n plus n0. Population scaling follows equation 15 of Rothery C, Strong M, Koffijberg HE, Basu A, Ghabri S, Knies S, Murray JF, Sanders Schmidler GD, Steuten L, Fenwick E. Value of information analytical methods. Report 2 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(3):277-286. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X15578125" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Total trial size entered as the number per arm. Entering a trial of 200 patients in total as n of 200 per arm doubles the information and gives about GBP 99.82 per patient instead of GBP 61.68 for 100 per arm" ;
    hemo:9000014 "v0 = sd0^2; sigma2 = sd_A^2 + sd_B^2; n0 = sigma2 / v0; nu = n / (n + n0); s2 = nu * v0; s = sqrt(s2); z_s = abs(m0) / s; phi_s = exp(-z_s * z_s / 2) / sqrt(2 * 3.14159265); EVSI = s * (phi_s - z_s * Q_s); z_0 = abs(m0) / sd0; phi_0 = exp(-z_0 * z_0 / 2) / sqrt(2 * 3.14159265); EVPI = sd0 * (phi_0 - z_0 * Q_0); share = EVSI / EVPI; D = (1 + r) * (1 - (1 + r)^(-Y)) / r; EVSI_pop = EVSI * N * D" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-EVSI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-per-person-and-population-evsi-of-a-two-arm-trial-under-normal-incremental-net-benefit" .

hemo:5000844 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Computational function for weighted and unweighted population net health benefit across several groups"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-EQWA-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000240,
        hemo:5000639,
