    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005663 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007110,
        hemo:7007111,
        hemo:7007112,
        hemo:7007113,
        hemo:7007114 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes a probabilistic sample as an analyst holds it, a table of N runs with the sampled values of the parameters of interest and the net benefit of each of D options, fits a least squares regression of each option's net benefit less the baseline's on the chosen parameters, and returns the EVPPI from the fitted values, the EVPI from the raw net benefits and a flag that the estimate lies within its bounds. It chains HE-FM-EVPP-003 with HE-FM-VOI-001 for any number of options, so the inputs differ from the formula's variables, which start from fitted values for three options. A linear model in indicator, polynomial or product terms stands in here for the generalised additive model of Strong, Oakley and Brennan. The Math / Code line takes two options and four runs, with the fitted values from the regression entered as inputs, and the R and Python versions fit the regression for any number of runs and options." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for regression-based EVPPI and its EVPI bound from a probabilistic sample table" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Strong M, Oakley JE, Brennan A. Estimating multiparameter partial expected value of perfect information from a probabilistic sensitivity analysis sample, a nonparametric regression approach. Medical Decision Making. 2014;34(3) 311-326. Equations 8 and 9, the invariance to incremental net benefits and Box 1. Rothery C, Strong M, Koffijberg HE, et al. Value of information analytical methods. Report 2 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(3) 277-286. Algorithm 4. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X13505910" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Interaction left out of the regression. Columns for A and B without their product fit GBP 2,400, 600, -200 and -2,000 and return GBP 550 for the pair in place of GBP 600, the EVPI. Strong, Oakley and Brennan use a tensor product term te(x5,x14) when inputs interact" ;
    hemo:9000014 "EVPPI = (max(g_1, 0) + max(g_2, 0) + max(g_3, 0) + max(g_4, 0)) / 4 - max((g_1 + g_2 + g_3 + g_4) / 4, 0); EVPI = (max(b_1, 0) + max(b_2, 0) + max(b_3, 0) + max(b_4, 0)) / 4 - max((b_1 + b_2 + b_3 + b_4) / 4, 0); ok = (EVPPI >= 0) and (EVPPI <= EVPI)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-EVPP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-regression-based-evppi-and-its-evpi-bound-from-a-probabilistic-sample-table" .

