        hemo:7007118,
        hemo:7007119,
        hemo:7007120 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the inputs for a grid of candidate designs, the sample size and per-person EVSI of each, the people facing the decision each year, the number of years, a discount rate, the study cost inputs and, for only in research, the number of people treated outside each design while it runs. For one design it returns the discount factor, the population EVSI, the expected study cost and the ENBS, and the code below repeats this over the grid to return the largest ENBS and the efficient sample size, or no design when none has a positive ENBS. It chains HE-FM-EVSI-004, HE-FM-ENBS-002 and HE-FM-ENBS-003, so its inputs differ from the formulas' variables. Per-person EVSI for each sample size comes from the methods on the EVSI page." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for the efficient sample size over a grid of study designs by ENBS" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Rothery C, Strong M, Koffijberg HE, et al. Value of information analytical methods. Report 2 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(3) 277-286. Section on the expected net benefit of sampling, where the optimal design is the one that maximises the ENBS, for choices such as sample size and length of follow-up. Fenwick E, Steuten L, Knies S, et al. Value of information analysis for research decisions, an introduction. Report 1 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(2) 139-150. Good practice recommendation 9 and the text before it, where EVSI and ENBS are calculated for a range of designs and, when no design has a positive ENBS, the current evidence is considered sufficient for decision making. Willan AR, Pinto EM. The value of information and optimal clinical trial design. Statistics in Medicine. 2005;24(12) 1791-1806. Abstract, checked on PubMed (15806619), which shows how to find the sample size that maximises the difference between the cost of the trial and the value of the information. Conti S, Claxton K. Dimensions of design space, a decision-theoretic approach to optimal research design. Medical Decision Making. 2009;29(6) 643-660. Abstract, checked on PubMed (19605884), where sample sizes and allocation between treatments are chosen to maximise ENBS. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2020.01.004" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Choosing the design with the largest population EVSI. The article's 1,000-patient trial has the largest population EVSI, GBP 2,800,000, but an ENBS of GBP 500,000, which is GBP 440,000 below the 600-patient design. The efficient design is the one with the largest ENBS, which nets the cost against the value" ;
    hemo:9000014 "D = (1 + r) * (1 - (1 + r)^(-Y)) / r; EVSI_pop = EVSI * N * D; C_n = C_F + c_V * n + s_inf * n * abs(INB); ENBS = EVSI_pop - C_n - N_w * max(INB, 0)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-ENBS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-the-efficient-sample-size-over-a-grid-of-study-designs-by-enbs" .

