        hemo:6004846,
        hemo:6004847 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005690 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007125,
        hemo:7007126,
        hemo:7007127,
        hemo:7007128,
        hemo:7007129 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the names, expected costs and expected QALYs of any number of mutually exclusive strategies and returns the strategies on the cost-effectiveness frontier in cost order with their sequential ICERs, by the conventional elimination that Suen and Goldhaber-Fiebert summarise. It sorts by cost, removes dominated strategies, ties included, with the rule of HE-FM-DOM-002, computes the ratio of each strategy against the one before it, removes the first strategy whose ratio is higher than the next one, as in HE-FM-EXD-001, and recomputes until the ratios rise. The finished ratios are the slopes that HE-FM-CEF-001 reads. The inputs are lists of strategies, not the three named strategies of the formula. The Math / Code line shows one pass for four strategies already in cost order with no strict dominance, and the R and Python versions take any number and repeat the passes." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for the cost-effectiveness frontier by removing dominated and extendedly dominated strategies" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Suen S, Goldhaber-Fiebert JD. An efficient, non-iterative method of identifying the cost-effectiveness frontier. Medical Decision Making. 2016;36(1) 132-136. Introduction, which describes the conventional algorithm as ordering strategies by ascending cost, eliminating those that cost more but provide less benefit, computing pairwise ICERs and then iteratively discarding strategies whose ICER is higher than that of the following strategy until no more can be eliminated, with recalculation after each removal. National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance, the manual (PMG36). Chapter 4, economic evaluation. Published 31 January 2022, last updated 13 October 2026. Section 4.10.8 on the fully incremental analysis with dominated and extendedly dominated technologies removed. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X15583496" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Testing extended dominance before strict dominance. Left in the list, R2 gives a ratio of minus GBP 75,000 per QALY against R1, and the removal rule then drops R1, R2 and R3 in turn. The frontier becomes R0, R4 and R5 with ratios of about GBP 26,667 and 60,000, so at GBP 25,000 the choice is R0 and GBP 500 per person of net monetary benefit is lost against R1 (computed here for illustration)" ;
    hemo:9000014 "r_2 = (C_2 - C_1) / (Q_2 - Q_1); r_3 = (C_3 - C_2) / (Q_3 - Q_2); r_4 = (C_4 - C_3) / (Q_4 - Q_3); exd_2 = r_2 > r_3; exd_3 = r_3 > r_4; r_42 = (C_4 - C_2) / (Q_4 - Q_2)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-CEF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-the-cost-effectiveness-frontier-by-removing-dominated-and-extendedly-dominated-strategies" .

hemo:5000852 a owl:NamedIndividual,
