    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003938,
        hemo:6003939,
        hemo:6004854,
        hemo:6004855,
        hemo:6004856,
        hemo:6004857,
        hemo:6004858,
        hemo:6004859,
        hemo:6004860 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005699 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007130,
        hemo:7007131,
        hemo:7007132,
        hemo:7007133,
        hemo:7007134,
        hemo:7007135 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the names, expected costs and expected QALYs of any number of strategies and a largest value per QALY of interest, and returns the frontier strategies by the one-pass method of Suen and Goldhaber-Fiebert. It calculates net monetary benefit (HE-FM-NMB-001) for every strategy at every value in a grid made of zero, every positive pairwise ICER up to the largest value and that value itself, and keeps the strategies that come out on top somewhere in the grid, with no rounds of elimination. At one value it also returns the net monetary benefit each strategy gives up, L_k of HE-FM-CEF-002. The Math / Code line shows four strategies at one value, and the R and Python versions take any number of strategies and the whole grid." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for the one-pass net monetary benefit search for the cost-effectiveness frontier" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Suen S, Goldhaber-Fiebert JD. An efficient, non-iterative method of identifying the cost-effectiveness frontier. Medical Decision Making. 2016;36(1) 132-136. Introduction and the one-line Matlab implementation that takes the maximum of net monetary benefit over a vector of willingness-to-pay values, section II of the proof, which states that a grid of all positive pairwise ICERs with 0 and the maximum willingness to pay relevant to the analysis is guaranteed to identify all desired frontier strategies, section III on the lowest-cost strategy at zero and the highest-QALY strategy as the value grows without limit, and Figure 2, where not testing a value between two switching values omits a strategy. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X15583496" ;
    hemo:9000011 "A coarse grid of round values. For options A to D, B is chosen only for values per QALY at or above GBP 10,000 and at or below GBP 18,000. A grid of 0, 20,000 and 40,000 returns A and D only, so B drops out of the frontier and the choice at GBP 15,000 is misread as A, which gives up GBP 1,500 per person against B (computed here for illustration). Suen and Goldhaber-Fiebert include every positive pairwise ICER in the grid to prevent this" ;
    hemo:9000014 "N_1 = lambda * Q_1 - C_1; N_2 = lambda * Q_2 - C_2; N_3 = lambda * Q_3 - C_3; N_4 = lambda * Q_4 - C_4; N_max = max(N_1, N_2, N_3, N_4); L_1 = N_max - N_1; L_2 = N_max - N_2; L_3 = N_max - N_3; L_4 = N_max - N_4" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-CEF-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-the-one-pass-net-monetary-benefit-search-for-the-cost-effectiveness-frontier" .

hemo:5000853 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Computational function for health forgone by each option against the health-maximising option"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-EET-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000198,
        hemo:5000668 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7005712,
        hemo:7005713,
        hemo:7005714 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003580,
        hemo:6003581,
        hemo:6003942,
        hemo:6003943,
        hemo:6003944,
        hemo:6003945,
        hemo:6003946,
        hemo:6003947,
        hemo:6003948,
        hemo:6003949,
        hemo:6003950,
        hemo:6004861,
        hemo:6004862,
        hemo:6004863,
        hemo:6004864 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003580,
        hemo:6003581,
        hemo:6003942,
        hemo:6003943,
        hemo:6003944,
        hemo:6003945,
        hemo:6004861,
        hemo:6004862 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003946,
        hemo:6003947,
        hemo:6003948,
        hemo:6003949,
        hemo:6003950,
        hemo:6004863,
        hemo:6004864 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005709 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007136,
        hemo:7007137,
        hemo:7007138,
        hemo:7007139 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the population share of each group and the net health gain per person of each group under each of several options of equal cost, and returns each option's mean net gain, the highest mean gain and the health each option forgoes against it. This is the equity constraint analysis of HE-FM-EET-001 run over a set of options, so the health-maximising option is found by the procedure rather than named beforehand. The Math / Code line shows two groups and three options, and the R and Python versions take any number of groups and options." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for health forgone by each option against the health-maximising option" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Cookson R, Mirelman AJ, Griffin S, Asaria M, Dawkins B, Norheim OF, Verguet S, Culyer AJ. Using cost-effectiveness analysis to address health equity concerns. Value in Health. 2017;20(2):206-212. Section on equity constraint analysis, which compares two or more options given a fixed budget and treats the health loss of the more equitable option as the cost of the equity constraint. Asaria M, Griffin S, Cookson R. Distributional cost-effectiveness analysis: a tutorial. Medical Decision Making. 2016;36(1):8-19. Section on comparing interventions in terms of total health and unfair health inequality, on the health opportunity cost as the difference in total health. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2016.11.027" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Measuring health forgone against the comparator. Taking F against doing nothing gives minus 0.35 years, a gain, and hides the 0.05 years F gives up against E. Health forgone for equity is measured against the health-maximising option" ;
    hemo:9000014 "H_1 = s_worse * g_worse_1 + s_better * g_better_1; H_2 = s_worse * g_worse_2 + s_better * g_better_2; H_3 = s_worse * g_worse_3 + s_better * g_better_3; H_top = max(H_1, max(H_2, H_3)); F_1 = H_top - H_1; F_2 = H_top - H_2; F_3 = H_top - H_3" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-EET-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-health-forgone-by-each-option-against-the-health-maximising-option" .

hemo:5000854 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Per-cycle exit probabilities by time since entry from a survival curve checked against one constant exit probability"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-MKA-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000216,
        hemo:5000474,
        hemo:5000593,
        hemo:5000671 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7005721,
        hemo:7005722,
        hemo:7005723 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003582,
        hemo:6003583,
        hemo:6003584,
        hemo:6003951,
        hemo:6003952,
        hemo:6003953,
        hemo:6003954,
        hemo:6004865,
        hemo:6004866,
        hemo:6004867,
        hemo:6004868,
        hemo:6004869,
        hemo:6004870,
        hemo:6004871,
        hemo:6004872 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003582,
        hemo:6003953,
        hemo:6003954,
        hemo:6004865,
        hemo:6004866,
        hemo:6004867,
        hemo:6004868 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003583,
        hemo:6003584,
        hemo:6003951,
        hemo:6003952,
        hemo:6004869,
        hemo:6004870,
        hemo:6004871,
        hemo:6004872 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005717 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007140,
        hemo:7007141,
        hemo:7007142,
        hemo:7007143,
        hemo:7007144 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes survival at the end of each of the first K cycles since entry to a state, from follow-up of people who entered it or from a model with tunnel states, and returns the exit probability in each cycle among those still in the state, the constant exit probability that a memoryless state fitted to survival at K cycles would use, the largest gap between the two and the mean stay of the fitted memoryless state. Each per-cycle value is h_d of HE-FM-MKA-002 taken from the data, the ratio of adjacent survival values as in HE-FM-CUMH-002, and the constant comes from the rescaling formula HE-FM-TP-003 with the K-cycle survival as the observed value. A memoryless state needs every per-cycle value equal to the constant; a large gap shows that time since entry carries information that one state discards, and the per-cycle values then become the duration-specific inputs of HE-CF-TUN-001. The check is descriptive. With patient-level data Williams and colleagues used a state-arrival extended Cox model with time in the previous state as a covariate to decide whether to accept the assumption. The inputs differ from the formula's symbols, which describe a stay already known to be geometric." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function: per-cycle exit probabilities by time since entry from a survival curve checked against one constant exit probability" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Grinstead CM, Snell JL. Grinstead and Snell's Introduction to Probability. The CHANCE Project version of 4 July 2006, based on the 2nd edition published by the American Mathematical Society. Chapter 5, section 5.1, Example 5.1, page 186, for the memoryless property P(T > r + s | T > r) = q^s of the geometric distribution. Williams C, Lewsey JD, Mackay DF, Briggs AH. Estimation of survival probabilities for use in cost-effectiveness analyses: a comparison of a multi-state modeling survival analysis approach with partitioned survival and Markov decision-analytic modeling. Medical Decision Making. 2017;37(4) 427-439. Section Multi-state Modeling, for a state-arrival extended model with time in the previous state used to help decide whether to accept the Markov assumption. https://math.dartmouth.edu/~prob/prob/prob.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Constant fitted at one horizon read as a fit at every horizon. The constant 0.093869 matches survival at five years by construction but puts one-year survival at about 0.906 against 0.75, so the first-cycle gap of about 0.156 and the article's third fewer post-event years are hidden when only the five-year value is compared" ;
    hemo:9000014 "h_1 = 1 - S_1; h_2 = 1 - S_2 / S_1; h_3 = 1 - S_3 / S_2; h_4 = 1 - S_4 / S_3; h_5 = 1 - S_5 / S_4; q_c = 1 - S_5^(1 / 5); dev = max(abs(h_1 - q_c), abs(h_2 - q_c), abs(h_3 - q_c), abs(h_4 - q_c), abs(h_5 - q_c)); D_c = 1 / q_c" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-MKA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-per-cycle-exit-probabilities-by-time-since-entry-from-a-survival-curve-checked-against-one-constant-exit-probability" .

hemo:5000855 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Computational function for the critical inequality aversion at which two options give equal EDE health"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-DCEA-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000294,
        hemo:5000674,
        hemo:5000675 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7005730,
        hemo:7005731,
        hemo:7005732 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003585,
        hemo:6003955,
        hemo:6003956,
        hemo:6003957,
        hemo:6003958,
        hemo:6003959,
        hemo:6003960,
        hemo:6003961,
        hemo:6003962,
        hemo:6003963,
        hemo:6003964,
        hemo:6003965,
        hemo:6003966,
        hemo:6004873 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003585,
        hemo:6003955,
        hemo:6003956,
        hemo:6003957,
        hemo:6003958,
        hemo:6003959,
        hemo:6003960,
        hemo:6003961,
        hemo:6003962,
        hemo:6003966 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003963,
        hemo:6003964,
        hemo:6003965,
        hemo:6004873 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005726 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007145,
        hemo:7007146,
        hemo:7007147,
        hemo:7007148,
        hemo:7007149 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the population share and the health per person of each social group under two options, such as a programme and its comparator from HE-FM-DCEA-001, and a bracket of inequality aversion values, and returns by bisection the Atkinson epsilon at which the two options give equal EDE health, the point where the change in EDE health of HE-FM-DCEA-002 changes sign. At each step it evaluates the share-weighted EDE of HE-CF-ATK-001 for both options at the midpoint of the bracket, keeps the half in which the difference changes sign and stops when the bracket is narrower than the tolerance, for any number of groups. The two-group closed approximation in the equity-efficiency trade-off package gives about 11.77 for the article's example, while solving the EDE comparison gives about 11.76, the value the article reports. The Math / Code line evaluates the difference for two groups at a given epsilon, the quantity bisection drives to zero, and the Excel, R and Python versions run the search." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for the critical inequality aversion at which two options give equal EDE health" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Asaria M, Griffin S, Cookson R. Distributional cost-effectiveness analysis: a tutorial. Medical Decision Making. 2016;36(1):8-19. Section Analyzing tradeoffs between total health and health inequality using social welfare indices, with the Atkinson EDE formula and Figure 4, which plots the difference in EDE health between two strategies across levels of inequality aversion and reads off the level above which the other strategy is preferred. Press WH, Teukolsky SA, Vetterling WT, Flannery BP. Numerical Recipes, the art of scientific computing. 3rd ed. Cambridge, Cambridge University Press, 2007. Section 9.1 Bracketing and Bisection. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X15583266" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Searching a bracket in which the difference does not change sign. With the upper end set to 10 for the article's equal-sharing case, the difference is positive at both ends, about 600 and 71.4 EDE QALYs, and a search that skips the end check returns 10 as if it were the switching value. The function returns NA in that case, and the bracket is widened" ;
    hemo:9000014 "E_A = (s_1 * hA_1^(1 - epsilon) + s_2 * hA_2^(1 - epsilon))^(1 / (1 - epsilon)); E_B = (s_1 * hB_1^(1 - epsilon) + s_2 * hB_2^(1 - epsilon))^(1 / (1 - epsilon)); D = E_A - E_B; D_pop = N_pop * D" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-DCEA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-the-critical-inequality-aversion-at-which-two-options-give-equal-ede-health" .

hemo:5000856 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Computational function for a level yearly stream under the stepped Green Book schedule and the reduced schedule without pure time preference"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-TPR-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000618,
        hemo:5000679 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7005739,
        hemo:7005740,
        hemo:7005741 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003586,
        hemo:6003587,
        hemo:6003588,
        hemo:6003589,
        hemo:6003590,
        hemo:6003591,
        hemo:6003592,
        hemo:6003967,
        hemo:6003968,
        hemo:6003969,
        hemo:6003970,
        hemo:6004874,
        hemo:6004875,
        hemo:6004876,
        hemo:6004877,
        hemo:6004878 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003586,
        hemo:6003587,
        hemo:6003588,
        hemo:6003589,
        hemo:6003590,
        hemo:6003967,
        hemo:6004874,
        hemo:6004875 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003591,
        hemo:6003592,
        hemo:6003968,
        hemo:6003969,
        hemo:6003970,
        hemo:6004876,
        hemo:6004877,
        hemo:6004878 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005735 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007150,
        hemo:7007151,
        hemo:7007152,
        hemo:7007153,
        hemo:7007154 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes a level yearly amount, a horizon of up to 125 years and two sets of three band rates, the standard schedule and the reduced schedule with pure time preference excluded, and returns the sum of the stepped factors of HE-FM-TPR-003 under each, the two present values and their difference. Paragraph 3.5 of the supplementary guidance reads the difference between the two results as an estimate of the wealth transfer attributable to pure social time preference. Each band's sum is geometric, so the calculator line adds three closed-form sums, and for a horizon of 30 years or less the standard sum is the annuity of HE-FM-DISC-001. The inputs therefore differ from the formula's variables, which describe one year." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for a level yearly stream under the stepped Green Book schedule and the reduced schedule without pure time preference" ;
    hemo:9000010 "HM Treasury. Discounting, Green Book supplementary guidance. London, HM Treasury, February 2026 (ISBN 978-1-918417-18-0), page last updated 5 February 2026. Chapter 3, paragraphs 3.1 to 3.5 with Tables 3.A and 3.B, and Annex A Tables A.1 to A.3, for the standard, health and reduced schedules and the reading of the difference between the standard and reduced results as the wealth transfer attributable to pure social time preference." ;
    hemo:9000011 "Health stream on the standard schedule. One QALY a year for 60 years sums to about 25.38 on the STPR schedule against 39.86 on the health schedule (computed here for illustration), so the discounted health gain is understated by more than a third",
        "Wealth transfer read as the whole effect of discounting. The reduced schedule still discounts at 3.0% and less, since catastrophic risk and the wealth effect remain. For GBP 1,000 a year over 100 years the difference is about GBP 4,176, while the reduced present value of GBP 33,040 is still about a third of the undiscounted GBP 100,000 (computed here for illustration)" ;
    hemo:9000014 "n_1 = min(T, 30); n_2 = max(min(T, 75) - 30, 0); n_3 = max(T - 75, 0); A_S = (1 - (1 + r_1)^(-n_1)) / r_1 + (1 + r_1)^(-30) * (1 - (1 + r_2)^(-n_2)) / r_2 + (1 + r_1)^(-30) * (1 + r_2)^(-45) * (1 - (1 + r_3)^(-n_3)) / r_3; A_Z = (1 - (1 + z_1)^(-n_1)) / z_1 + (1 + z_1)^(-30) * (1 - (1 + z_2)^(-n_2)) / z_2 + (1 + z_1)^(-30) * (1 + z_2)^(-45) * (1 - (1 + z_3)^(-n_3)) / z_3; PV_S = q * A_S; PV_Z = q * A_Z; W = PV_Z - PV_S" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-TPR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-a-level-yearly-stream-under-the-stepped-green-book-schedule-and-the-reduced-schedule-without-pure-time-preference" .

hemo:5000857 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Computational function for ranking allocations by utilitarian, isoelastic and maximin health social welfare"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-SWF-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000680,
        hemo:5000681,
        hemo:5000682 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7005748,
        hemo:7005749,
        hemo:7005750 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003593,
        hemo:6003594,
        hemo:6003595,
        hemo:6003596,
        hemo:6003597,
