        hemo:6004885 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003594,
        hemo:6003595,
        hemo:6003596,
        hemo:6003597,
        hemo:6003598,
        hemo:6003599,
        hemo:6004886,
        hemo:6004887,
        hemo:6004888 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005744 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007155,
        hemo:7007156,
        hemo:7007157,
        hemo:7007158,
        hemo:7007159 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the health of each equal-sized group under each option and an inequality aversion parameter, and returns for every option the utilitarian total, the isoelastic welfare of HE-FM-SWF-001 (HE-FM-SWF-002 at epsilon of 1) and the maximin welfare of HE-FM-SWF-003, with the option ranked first under each. It repeats the article's three-step ranking for any number of options and groups, and it ranks the isoelastic values on a log scale so that very high epsilon values do not underflow. The Math / Code line shows three options and three groups at one epsilon other than 1, and the R and Python versions take any number. The values of epsilon at which two options swap rank belong to the Distributional Cost-Effectiveness package and are not computed here." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for ranking allocations by utilitarian, isoelastic and maximin health social welfare" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Atkinson AB. On the measurement of inequality. Journal of Economic Theory. 1970;2(3):244-263. Page 251, equation (5), for the isoelastic and logarithmic welfare functions, and page 257 for epsilon of 0 ranking by the total and the minimum as the limit when epsilon tends to infinity. https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-0531(70)90039-6" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Isoelastic values that underflow at very high epsilon. At epsilon of 200 each term h_i^(1-epsilon) is far below the smallest double-precision number, so W is zero for all three options and they appear tied. The log-scale key used here still ranks R first, as the maximin form does" ;
    hemo:9000014 "U_P = h_P1 + h_P2 + h_P3; U_T = h_T1 + h_T2 + h_T3; U_R = h_R1 + h_R2 + h_R3; W_P = (h_P1^(1 - epsilon) + h_P2^(1 - epsilon) + h_P3^(1 - epsilon)) / (1 - epsilon); W_T = (h_T1^(1 - epsilon) + h_T2^(1 - epsilon) + h_T3^(1 - epsilon)) / (1 - epsilon); W_R = (h_R1^(1 - epsilon) + h_R2^(1 - epsilon) + h_R3^(1 - epsilon)) / (1 - epsilon); M_P = min(h_P1, min(h_P2, h_P3)); M_T = min(h_T1, min(h_T2, h_T3)); M_R = min(h_R1, min(h_R2, h_R3))" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-SWF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-ranking-allocations-by-utilitarian-isoelastic-and-maximin-health-social-welfare" .

hemo:5000858 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Computational function for the ages at which the GBD age weight crosses a chosen level"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-AGW-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000685 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7005756,
        hemo:7005757,
        hemo:7005758 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003600,
        hemo:6003601,
        hemo:6003602,
        hemo:6003603,
        hemo:6003604,
        hemo:6003605,
        hemo:6003606,
        hemo:6003607 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003600,
        hemo:6003601,
        hemo:6003602,
        hemo:6003603,
        hemo:6003604 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003605,
        hemo:6003606,
        hemo:6003607 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005753 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007160,
        hemo:7007161,
        hemo:7007162,
        hemo:7007163 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the constants C and beta and a level, by default 1, and returns the two ages at which the weight equals that level, one on each side of the peak 1/beta, so the span of ages weighted above uniform can be read off. The crossing ages have no closed form in elementary functions, so the code finds them numerically by root search on each side of the peak. The calculator line checks a pair of ages by returning their weights. The inputs therefore differ from the formula's variables, which take one age." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for the ages at which the GBD age weight crosses a chosen level" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Barendregt JJ, Bonneux L, Van der Maas PJ. DALYs, the age-weights on balance. Bulletin of the World Health Organization. 1996;74(4) 439-443. Page 440 and Figure 1, the function C x e to the minus beta x with C = 0.16243 and beta = 0.04, and the statement that the years lived between ages 9 and 54 have a weight greater than unity, and the summary on page 439, which states that the age range made more important by weighting is 0 to 27 years because the expected years of life lost already emphasise the young. Prüss-Üstün A, Mathers C, Corvalán C, Woodward A. Introduction and methods, assessing the environmental burden of disease at national and local levels. Environmental Burden of Disease Series No. 1. Geneva, World Health Organization, 2003. Section 3.5, DALY parameters, for the standard constants 0.1658 and 0.04." ;
    hemo:9000011 "Span read as the ages whose deaths gain from weighting. The ages 8.5 to 55.5 are where a year lived is weighted above 1, not the ages at death whose loss rises. Barendregt and colleagues show that, because years of life lost already favour the young, the ages that gain from weighting are 0 to 27, and in the article's illustration a death at 40, inside the span, loses 8% under age weighting" ;
    hemo:9000014 "x_peak = 1 / beta; W_lo = C * x_lo * exp(-beta * x_lo); W_hi = C * x_hi * exp(-beta * x_hi)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-AGW-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-the-ages-at-which-the-gbd-age-weight-crosses-a-chosen-level" .

hemo:5000859 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Computational function for age-weighted discounted years lost per death or case with a numerical integral check"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-AGW-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000604,
        hemo:5000686,
        hemo:5000687,
        hemo:5000688 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7005766,
        hemo:7005767,
