        hemo:6004907 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005797 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007184,
        hemo:7007185,
        hemo:7007186,
        hemo:7007187,
        hemo:7007188,
        hemo:7007189 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes a next-generation matrix K with n groups, or the matrices F and V of a compartmental model from which K is F times the inverse of V, and returns R_0 as the spectral radius of K together with the dominant eigenvector scaled to sum to 1, the share of cases in each group once the generations settle. R and Python call an eigenvalue routine. Excel has no eigenvalue function, so it uses power iteration with helper columns, applying K to a vector of cases generation by generation, the growth factor per generation that Diekmann, Heesterbeek and Roberts take as the characterisation of R_0. The calculator line shows the two-group case over three generations started from one case in each group, beside the closed form of HE-FM-BRN-005, and the inputs therefore differ from the formula's variables, which cover two groups only." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for R_0 as the spectral radius of a next-generation matrix of any size" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Diekmann O, Heesterbeek JA, Roberts MG. The construction of next-generation matrices for compartmental epidemic models. Journal of the Royal Society Interface. 2010;7(47):873-885. Section 1 on consecutive generations of infected individuals and the growth factor per generation as the characterisation of R_0, and section 3.2, R_0 = rho(K). van den Driessche P. Reproduction numbers of infectious disease models. Infectious Disease Modelling. 2017;2(3):288-303. Section 3, R_0 = rho(F V^-1), Theorem 1 and the SEIR example with F, V and R_0 = kappa beta S_0/((d + kappa)(d + gamma))." ;
    hemo:9000011 "One-generation ratio on a host and vector matrix. Reading the ratio of successive generation totals as R_0 for the illustrative host and vector matrix gives 2.25 and then about 0.889, never settling, while R_0 is about 1.414; the two-generation ratio or iteration on K plus the identity removes the swing",
        "Spectral bound of F minus V taken as R_0. The largest eigenvalue of F minus V, the Jacobian of the infected compartments, has the same sign as R_0 minus 1 by Theorem 1 of van den Driessche, but it is a growth rate. For the article's SIR infection it is 0.30 minus 0.10, or 0.2 per day, the early growth rate r, not R_0 of 3" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a_1 = k_11 + k_12; a_2 = k_21 + k_22; b_1 = k_11 * a_1 + k_12 * a_2; b_2 = k_21 * a_1 + k_22 * a_2; c_1 = k_11 * b_1 + k_12 * b_2; c_2 = k_21 * b_1 + k_22 * b_2; R_pow = (c_1 + c_2) / (b_1 + b_2); R_two = sqrt((c_1 + c_2) / (a_1 + a_2)); T_K = k_11 + k_22; D_K = k_11 * k_22 - k_12 * k_21; R_0 = (T_K + sqrt(T_K^2 - 4 * D_K)) / 2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-BRN-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-r-0-as-the-spectral-radius-of-a-next-generation-matrix-of-any-size" .

hemo:5000864 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Computational function for a foreign cost moved to a target price year and currency in either order"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-BOC-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000243,
        hemo:5000702,
        hemo:5000703 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7005811,
        hemo:7005812,
        hemo:7005813 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003625,
        hemo:6003626,
        hemo:6003627,
        hemo:6003628,
        hemo:6003629,
        hemo:6004013,
        hemo:6004014,
