        hemo:6004016,
        hemo:6004017 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003625,
        hemo:6003626,
        hemo:6003627,
        hemo:6003628,
        hemo:6003629,
        hemo:6004013,
        hemo:6004014 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004015,
        hemo:6004016,
        hemo:6004017 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005807 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007190,
        hemo:7007191,
        hemo:7007192,
        hemo:7007193,
        hemo:7007194 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes a cost in the source currency and its price year, price indices of the source and target countries, and the exchange rates of the costing year and the base year, and returns the cost in the target currency at base-year prices by the two orders that Turner and colleagues compare. Method 1 converts at the costing-year rate and then inflates with the target country's index. Method 2 inflates with the source country's index and then converts at the base-year rate, the order of the two stages of the CCEMG-EPPI-Centre Cost Converter. Each inflation step is the live price-year adjustment HE-FM-COST-002 and each conversion is HE-FM-BOC-001, or HE-FM-BOC-002 at purchasing power parity. The inputs therefore differ from the formula's variables, which describe one conversion." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for a foreign cost moved to a target price year and currency in either order" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Turner HC, Lauer JA, Tran BX, Teerawattananon Y, Jit M. Adjusting for inflation and currency changes within health economic studies. Value in Health. 2019;22(9):1026-1032. Adjustment methods 1 and 2 (pages 1030 and 1031), Figures 1 and 4, and the Conclusions on the choice of method. EPPI Centre, University College London, with the Campbell and Cochrane Economics Methods Group. CCEMG-EPPI-Centre Cost Converter, version 1.7 (January 2024), web page accessed 18 October 2026. Section How the tool works, on the two-stage computation that first adjusts the price year with a GDP deflator index in the original currency and then converts the currency with purchasing power parities for GDP." ;
    hemo:9000011 "Local inflation applied to a cost already in US dollars. Inflating Turner and colleagues' US$62.52 by the Vietnamese factor 2.36 gives US$147.55 against the correct US$107.58 of their Figure 4, about 37% too high, because the change in the exchange rate between 2006 and 2016 is ignored. The cost is first converted back to dong at the 2006 rate" ;
    hemo:9000014 "C_m1 = C_a * e_a * J_b / J_a; C_m2 = C_a * I_b / I_a * e_b; gap = C_m2 / C_m1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-BOC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-a-foreign-cost-moved-to-a-target-price-year-and-currency-in-either-order" .

