    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007200,
        hemo:7007201,
        hemo:7007202,
        hemo:7007203,
        hemo:7007204,
        hemo:7007205 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the share of a population in a low-risk stratum, the annual death probabilities of a low-risk and a high-risk stratum, a proportional cut in those probabilities from treatment, a utility per year alive and a one-off treatment cost, and returns the life expectancies of six cohort runs (each stratum and one averaged cohort, with and without treatment), the population QALY gain and ICER from the stratified runs, the same results from the averaged cohort, and the relative errors of the averaged cohort. It carries out the article's worked example. Each stratum gain is the difference of two values of the live L(q), HE-FM-ABS-001 less half a cycle, and equals e divided by (1 minus e) q because the half cycle cancels (derived here); the stratified QALY gain weights those gains with the shares of HE-FM-ATE-001, the ICERs follow HE-FM-ICER-001, and the life-expectancy shortfall is gap_L of HE-FM-COHM-001. The calculator line uses the closed form 1/q minus one half; the Excel, R and Python code instead run the Alive and Dead cohort trace numerically for a fixed number of cycles, so they also check the closed form, and the code takes any number of strata. The inputs differ from the formula's symbols, which describe one comparison at a time." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function: two risk strata run as separate cohorts against one averaged cohort, with life expectancy, QALY gains and ICERs" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Davis S, Stevenson M, Tappenden P, Wailoo A. NICE DSU Technical Support Document 15: Cost-effectiveness modelling using patient-level simulation. Sheffield, Decision Support Unit, ScHARR, University of Sheffield; April 2014. Section 3.1 (pages 14 and 15), on the biased estimate from average characteristics and on separate subgroup recommendations or a weighted average across subgroups. Siebert U, Alagoz O, Bayoumi AM, Jahn B, Owens DK, Cohen DJ, Kuntz KM. State-transition modeling: a report of the ISPOR-SMDM Modeling Good Research Practices Task Force-3. Value in Health. 2012;15(6):812-820. Section Starting Cohort, on running the model once for each stratum and aggregating across strata. https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD15_Patient-level_simulation.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Running the average patient. Feeding the mean probability of 0.15 into one cohort gives an ICER of GBP 15,000 per QALY against GBP 8,333 from the stratified runs, 80% too high, and hides the fivefold difference between the stratum ICERs of GBP 5,000 and GBP 25,000 that the article reports" ;
    hemo:9000014 "q_bar = w * q_low + (1 - w) * q_high; L_low_0 = 1 / q_low - 0.5; L_low_1 = 1 / ((1 - e) * q_low) - 0.5; L_high_0 = 1 / q_high - 0.5; L_high_1 = 1 / ((1 - e) * q_high) - 0.5; L_avg_0 = 1 / q_bar - 0.5; L_avg_1 = 1 / ((1 - e) * q_bar) - 0.5; dQ_strat = u * (w * (L_low_1 - L_low_0) + (1 - w) * (L_high_1 - L_high_0)); dQ_avg = u * (L_avg_1 - L_avg_0); ICER_strat = c / dQ_strat; ICER_avg = c / dQ_avg; LE_strat_0 = w * L_low_0 + (1 - w) * L_high_0; under_LE = 1 - L_avg_0 / LE_strat_0; under_dQ = 1 - dQ_avg / dQ_strat; over_ICER = ICER_avg / ICER_strat - 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-COHM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-two-risk-strata-run-as-separate-cohorts-against-one-averaged-cohort-with-life-expectancy-qaly-gains-and-icers" .

