    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Runs a closed SIR model forward in Euler steps of HE-FM-CMPM-004 three times, without a programme, with a programme under a dynamic force of infection and with the same programme under the static force of infection taken from the no-programme run, and sums infections averted by each count over the horizon, as HE-FM-CMPM-005 does for one step. It also returns averted infection costs, optionally discounted with a continuous rate as in HE-FM-CONT-003. The calculator line writes out the article's two one-day steps term by term, and Excel, R and Python run any number of steps." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for an Euler run of a closed SIR model with and without vaccination counting infections averted statically and dynamically" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Pitman R, Fisman D, Zaric GS, Postma M, Kretzschmar M, Edmunds J, Brisson M. Dynamic transmission modeling. A report of the ISPOR-SMDM Modeling Good Research Practices Task Force-5. Value in Health. 2012;15(6):828-834. Section Software Options on the Euler method in spreadsheets; subsection Uncertainty in choice of economic parameters, which states that preventive control programmes are often very sensitive to the discount rate and the time horizon of the analysis; and best practice V-3, which recommends sensitivity analysis on the time horizon and the discount rate. World Health Organization. WHO guide for standardization of economic evaluations of immunization programmes, 2nd edition (WHO/IVB/19.10). Geneva, WHO, 2019. Section 6.3.1 on recalculating the force of infection in dynamic models and holding it constant in static models. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2012.06.011" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Horizon cut before the programme run's epidemic ends. A programme can delay infections as well as prevent them. With the article's inputs the dynamic count of infections averted is about 3,493.3 after 30 days but about 2,376.4 once the epidemic has ended in both runs, because infections avoided early in the programme run partly occur later (computed here for illustration). Pitman and colleagues report that preventive control programmes are often very sensitive to the time horizon of the analysis and recommend sensitivity analysis on it" ;
    hemo:9000014 "F1b = beta * S_0 * I_0 / N * dt; S1b = S_0 - F1b; I1b = I_0 + F1b - gamma * I_0 * dt; S0p = S_0 - V; F1p = beta * S0p * I_0 / N * dt; S1p = S0p - F1p; I1p = I_0 + F1p - gamma * I_0 * dt; F2b = beta * S1b * I1b / N * dt; F2s = beta * I1b / N * S1p * dt; F2d = beta * S1p * I1p / N * dt; A_s = F1b - F1p + F2b - F2s; A_d = F1b - F1p + F2b - F2d; A_i = A_d - A_s; C_s = c_inf * A_s; C_d = c_inf * A_d" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-CMPM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-an-euler-run-of-a-closed-sir-model-with-and-without-vaccination-counting-infections-averted-statically-and-dynamically" .

