    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005879 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007230,
        hemo:7007231,
        hemo:7007232,
        hemo:7007233 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Runs a complete probabilistic sensitivity analysis on a small fixed set of draws. For each draw it takes a pair of independent standard normal values, turns them into a correlated log baseline mortality rate and log hazard ratio with the Cholesky step HE-FM-PSA-001, runs a constant-rate survival model for both options with those same parameters, and stores the new option's incremental net health benefit. It then returns the expected incremental net health benefit (HE-FM-ENB-002), the probability that the new option is cost-effective (HE-FM-CEAC-001, with ties counted as not cost-effective), the two expected losses of HE-FM-PSA-002, the Monte Carlo standard error of the mean (the form of HE-FM-ANTV-002 with one observation per draw) and the number of draws for a target standard error (HE-FM-ABM-003). As in the article, mean survival under a constant mortality rate m is 1 / m years and each life-year is worth q QALYs without discounting, so a draw with log rate b0 and log hazard ratio b1 gives an incremental QALY gain of q times exp(minus b0 minus b1) less exp(minus b0). The Math / Code line writes out five draws; the R and Python versions draw any number." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for a PSA run with correlated log baseline rate and log hazard ratio through a constant-rate survival model" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Steuten L, Thavorn K, Cipriano LE, Guertin JR, McQueen B, Schmidler G, Sullivan S, Wailoo A, Sculpher M. Methods to analyze and present uncertainty in economic evaluation to support HTA decisions. HEMA Report 2. Health Economics Methods Advisory, 2026. Section 4.3 (page 28), which states that decision models are non-linear in their inputs, that evaluating the model at the mean value of each input does not necessarily give the expected costs and health effects, and that probabilistic analysis averages model outcomes over the joint distribution of uncertain inputs, and the reporting list in section 6.6 (page 55), which asks for the number of simulations and evidence that the probabilistic results are stable. National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 13 October 2026. Section 4.7.11, which asks for enough simulations to minimise the effect of Monte Carlo error and suggests reviewing the variance around net benefits or ICERs as the number of simulations increases. Owen AB. Monte Carlo theory, methods and examples. Stanford University, 2013. Chapter 5, Random vectors and objects. Section 5.2, equation 5.3 and the Cholesky decomposition on page 9. https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Fresh baseline draw for the new option. Drawing a separate log baseline rate for the new option while its log hazard ratio is still drawn from the comparator's first standard normal value breaks the correlation of minus 0.5 between the new option's baseline and its hazard ratio, so the log of its treated rate has a variance of 0.05 instead of 0.03 (derived). From the log-normal moments (derived here, and matched by a 4 million draw simulation) the standard deviation of the incremental net health benefit rises from about 0.487 to about 0.693 QALYs, about 2.03 times the variance, so about twice as many draws are needed for the same Monte Carlo standard error (HE-FM-ABM-003), and the expected value moves from about minus 0.0722 to about minus 0.0467 QALYs. The general rule that both options use the same draw is HE-ER-ENB-003" ;
    hemo:9000014 "b0_1 = mu_b0 + s_b0 * z1_1; b1_1 = mu_b1 + s_b1 * (rho * z1_1 + sqrt(1 - rho^2) * z2_1); INHB_1 = q * (exp(-b0_1 - b1_1) - exp(-b0_1)) - dC / lambda; b0_2 = mu_b0 + s_b0 * z1_2; b1_2 = mu_b1 + s_b1 * (rho * z1_2 + sqrt(1 - rho^2) * z2_2); INHB_2 = q * (exp(-b0_2 - b1_2) - exp(-b0_2)) - dC / lambda; b0_3 = mu_b0 + s_b0 * z1_3; b1_3 = mu_b1 + s_b1 * (rho * z1_3 + sqrt(1 - rho^2) * z2_3); INHB_3 = q * (exp(-b0_3 - b1_3) - exp(-b0_3)) - dC / lambda; b0_4 = mu_b0 + s_b0 * z1_4; b1_4 = mu_b1 + s_b1 * (rho * z1_4 + sqrt(1 - rho^2) * z2_4); INHB_4 = q * (exp(-b0_4 - b1_4) - exp(-b0_4)) - dC / lambda; b0_5 = mu_b0 + s_b0 * z1_5; b1_5 = mu_b1 + s_b1 * (rho * z1_5 + sqrt(1 - rho^2) * z2_5); INHB_5 = q * (exp(-b0_5 - b1_5) - exp(-b0_5)) - dC / lambda; ENHB = (INHB_1 + INHB_2 + INHB_3 + INHB_4 + INHB_5) / 5; P_CE = ((INHB_1 > 0) + (INHB_2 > 0) + (INHB_3 > 0) + (INHB_4 > 0) + (INHB_5 > 0)) / 5; EL_new = (max(-INHB_1, 0) + max(-INHB_2, 0) + max(-INHB_3, 0) + max(-INHB_4, 0) + max(-INHB_5, 0)) / 5; EL_comp = (max(INHB_1, 0) + max(INHB_2, 0) + max(INHB_3, 0) + max(INHB_4, 0) + max(INHB_5, 0)) / 5; SD_INHB = sqrt(((INHB_1 - ENHB)^2 + (INHB_2 - ENHB)^2 + (INHB_3 - ENHB)^2 + (INHB_4 - ENHB)^2 + (INHB_5 - ENHB)^2) / 4); MCSE = SD_INHB / sqrt(5); N_req = (SD_INHB / e_target)^2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-PSA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-a-psa-run-with-correlated-log-baseline-rate-and-log-hazard-ratio-through-a-constant-rate-survival-model" .

hemo:5000873 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Computational function for annual patient time cost from service counts with the Yabroff time estimates"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-PTC-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000727 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7005891,
        hemo:7005892,
        hemo:7005893 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004054,
        hemo:6004055,
        hemo:6004056,
        hemo:6004057,
        hemo:6005041,
        hemo:6005042,
        hemo:6005043,
        hemo:6005044,
        hemo:6005045,
