    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004064,
        hemo:6005079,
        hemo:6005080,
        hemo:6005081,
        hemo:6005082 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005905 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007244,
        hemo:7007245,
        hemo:7007246,
        hemo:7007247,
        hemo:7007248 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the stock at the start of a period, the depreciation rate, the stock wanted at the start of the next period, the price of care, the wage, the time share and schooling, and returns the gross investment required, its least-cost outlay and the care and own time it buys. The required gross investment is the stock update HE-FM-DFH-001 solved for I and set to zero when the target is below the depreciated stock, since Grossman notes that health capital cannot be sold and gross investment cannot be negative; the outlay uses the unit cost of HE-FM-HPF-003 and the inputs the split of HE-FM-HPF-002. The R and Python code run the same steps over a path of periods from a starting stock, carrying each period's end stock into the next through HE-FM-DFH-001, the target when gross investment is needed and the depreciated stock otherwise, with depreciation that may rise with age, the pattern behind the article's comparison of a younger and an older person." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for the outlay and least-cost inputs that hold the health stock on a target path" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Grossman M. The human capital model of the demand for health. NBER Working Paper 7078. National Bureau of Economic Research, Cambridge, MA; 1999. Published as The human capital model, in Culyer AJ, Newhouse JP, editors. Handbook of Health Economics. Vol 1A. Elsevier, Amsterdam; 2000. p. 347-408. Section 2.1, equation (2-2), net investment equal to gross investment minus depreciation, with exogenous age-dependent depreciation rates; section 2.2, equation (2-10) and the text after it on the least cost of a given gross investment; section 3.1, which states that since health capital cannot be sold, gross investment cannot be negative. https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w7078/w7078.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Older person kept at the younger person's outlay. Keeping the 800 GBP outlay when depreciation rises to 0.25 buys only 20 units and the stock falls to 95, as in the article; holding it at 100 needs 1,000 GBP, 25% more (computed here for illustration)" ;
    hemo:9000014 "I_req = max(H_target - (1 - delta_t) * H_t, 0); pi = exp(-rho_H * E) * (P / (1 - K))^(1 - K) * (W / K)^K; C = pi * I_req; M = (1 - K) * C / P; TH = K * C / W" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-HPF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-the-outlay-and-least-cost-inputs-that-hold-the-health-stock-on-a-target-path" .

hemo:5000876 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Computational function for specific stop-loss expected cost, spread and premium by attachment point from a claims distribution and group size"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-ATTP-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000335,
