    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005914 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007249,
        hemo:7007250,
        hemo:7007251,
        hemo:7007252,
        hemo:7007253 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the figures an employer or analyst usually holds when choosing a specific attachment point, a claims distribution for one member, the number of members, the coinsurance rate, any cap and a premium loading, and returns for each attachment point the expected number of members above it, the expected and standard deviation of total reimbursement, an illustrative premium and the ratio of spread to mean, the table of the article's worked example. It applies HE-FM-ATTP-001 for one member, then HE-FM-ASL-003 for the total over n independent members, and prices the layer at expected reimbursement times one plus the loading theta, the article's illustrative convention; HE-FM-ACTA-002 sets a proportional loading as a share of the gross premium instead. Its inputs differ from the symbols of HE-FM-ATTP-001 because it needs the group size and loading, and the Excel, R and Python versions loop over a grid of attachment points, which the calculator line takes one at a time." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for specific stop-loss expected cost, spread and premium by attachment point from a claims distribution and group size" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Miccolis RS. On the theory of increased limits and excess of loss pricing. Proceedings of the Casualty Actuarial Society. 1977;64:27-59. Equations 9 and 10 (page 34) for the excess of loss cost function and its expected value, equation 11 (page 34) for the excess layer as the difference of two limited expected values, and section Variance as a Measure of Risk with equation 31 (pages 42 to 44) for the variance and second moment. National Association of Insurance Commissioners. Stop Loss Insurance, Self-Funding and the ACA. White paper. 2015. Section V on premiums developed from an actuary's determination of expected losses. https://www.casact.org/sites/default/files/database/proceed_proceed77_77027.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Whole premium counted as a resource cost. The article notes that from a health system or societal perspective the reimbursement is a transfer between payers and only the administration and capital costs within the loading, with any change in care, are real resource effects. At USD 100,000 the illustrative premium of USD 715,000 holds USD 550,000 of expected reimbursement, so at most USD 165,000 of it can be a resource cost; counting the whole premium overstates that cost more than fourfold" ;
    hemo:9000014 "E_R = c * (p_1 * max(min(C_1, U) - A, 0) + p_2 * max(min(C_2, U) - A, 0) + p_3 * max(min(C_3, U) - A, 0)); M2_R = c^2 * (p_1 * max(min(C_1, U) - A, 0)^2 + p_2 * max(min(C_2, U) - A, 0)^2 + p_3 * max(min(C_3, U) - A, 0)^2); V_R = M2_R - E_R^2; SD_R = sqrt(V_R); N_A = n * (p_1 * (C_1 > A) + p_2 * (C_2 > A) + p_3 * (C_3 > A)); E_T = n * E_R; SD_T = SD_R * sqrt(n); P_T = (1 + theta) * E_T; CV_T = SD_T / E_T" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-ATTP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-specific-stop-loss-expected-cost-spread-and-premium-by-attachment-point-from-a-claims-distribution-and-group-size" .

hemo:5000877 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Exact Hedges' correction factor by the log-gamma function with g and its variance"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-CF-HEDG-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000002 hemo:5000444,
        hemo:5000740,
        hemo:5000741 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7005926,
        hemo:7005927,
        hemo:7005928 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6004071,
        hemo:6004072,
        hemo:6004073,
        hemo:6004074,
        hemo:6004075,
        hemo:6005098,
        hemo:6005099,
        hemo:6005100,
        hemo:6005101 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6004071,
        hemo:6004072,
        hemo:6004073 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6004074,
        hemo:6004075,
        hemo:6005098,
        hemo:6005099,
        hemo:6005100,
        hemo:6005101 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005923 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007254,
        hemo:7007255,
        hemo:7007256,
        hemo:7007257 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Computes the exact correction factor of Hedges, the ratio of gamma functions in his equation 6e, through the logarithm of the gamma function so that it does not overflow in large samples, and returns with it Hedges' g, the approximate factor of HE-FM-HEDG-001 and its error, the variance and standard error of g of HE-FM-HEDG-002 with the exact factor, and the exact variance of the corrected estimate from Hedges' Theorem 2 evaluated with g in place of delta. The site calculator has no gamma function, so the Math / Code line runs the approximate chain of HE-FM-HEDG-001 and HE-FM-HEDG-002, and the exact steps are in the Excel, R and Python code. The inputs are d from HE-FM-CD-001 and the two group sizes." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function: exact Hedges' correction factor by the log-gamma function with g and its variance" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Hedges LV. Distribution theory for Glass's estimator of effect size and related estimators. Journal of Educational Statistics. 1981;6(2):107-128. Equation 6e (page 111) defining c(m), Theorem 2 (page 116) giving the exact variance of the corrected estimator, and Table I (page 115) tabulating c(m) for each m up to 50. https://doi.org/10.3102/10769986006002107" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Gamma function overflow. Computing the ratio with the gamma function itself fails in large trials, because the gamma function exceeds the largest double-precision number above an argument of about 171.6. In Python `math.gamma` raises an overflow error and in R `gamma` returns Inf, so the factor is Inf at 344 degrees of freedom, a total of 346 patients, and NaN from 345 (checked here), while the log-gamma form returns about 0.997818. Excel's GAMMALN avoids the problem in the same way" ;
    hemo:9000014 "m = n_1 + n_2 - 2; J = 1 - 3 / (4 * m - 1); g = J * d; V_d = (n_1 + n_2) / (n_1 * n_2) + d^2 / (2 * (n_1 + n_2)); V_g = J^2 * V_d; SE_g = sqrt(V_g)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-HEDG-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-exact-hedges-correction-factor-by-the-log-gamma-function-with-g-and-its-variance" .

hemo:5000878 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000013 ;
    rdfs:label "Computational function for delta-adjusted QALY gain, ICER and tipping-point shift over a grid for loss to follow-up"@en ;
