        hemo:6005111 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7005931 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007258,
        hemo:7007259,
        hemo:7007260,
        hemo:7007261,
        hemo:7007262,
        hemo:7007263 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes each arm's QALYs after imputation under missing at random, with a flag for the participants lost to follow-up, the incremental cost, the threshold, the shift ratio r and a grid of shifts, adds each shift to the imputed QALYs of those lost (r times the shift in usual care), and returns the delta-adjusted QALY gain, ICER and incremental net health benefit at every shift, with the tipping point found where the net health benefit changes sign by the interpolation of HE-FM-OWSA-002. This is the scan that Leurent and colleagues and Faria and colleagues describe, run on one completed dataset; with several imputed datasets the scan is run on each and the gains are averaged by Rubin's rules. The arm means follow HE-FM-LOCF-001 and HE-FM-LOCF-002 as read in HE-FN-LTFU-001, the ratio is HE-FM-ICER-001 and the net health benefit is HE-FM-CET-002. For an additive shift on QALYs alone the net health benefit is linear in the shift, so the grid returns the closed form of HE-FM-LTFU-001, which the Math / Code line computes from arm summaries. The grid is needed when the conclusion is judged on a quantity that is not linear in the shift, such as the probability of cost-effectiveness from bootstrap samples, or when several parameters are varied together." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for delta-adjusted QALY gain, ICER and tipping-point shift over a grid for loss to follow-up" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Leurent B, Gomes M, Faria R, Morris S, Grieve R, Carpenter JR. Sensitivity analysis for not-at-random missing data in trial-based cost-effectiveness analysis: a tutorial. PharmacoEconomics. 2018;36(8):889-901. Section 2.3, on adding delta to or multiplying the values imputed under missing at random and combining with Rubin's rules, and section 4.3, on the tipping-point approach. Faria R, Gomes M, Epstein D, White IR. PharmacoEconomics. 2014;32(12):1157-1170. Section 5, on repeating the shifted analysis over a range of values and reporting the value of the sensitivity parameter required to change the results. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40273-018-0650-5" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Observed means used when the imputation moved the base case. If the participant lost from the new treatment arm in the table above is imputed at 0.70 under missing at random, for example because of a worse baseline, the base-case gain is 0.084 and the tipping point is -0.045. Feeding the observed-mean gain of 0.10 into HE-FM-LTFU-001 returns -0.125 and overstates the shift the conclusion can absorb by a factor of almost three (computed here for illustration)" ;
    hemo:9000014 "E_T = E_obs_T + p_T * delta; E_C = E_obs_C + r * p_C * delta; Delta_E = E_T - E_C; ICER = Delta_C / Delta_E; INHB = Delta_E - Delta_C / lambda; delta_star = (Delta_C / lambda - (E_obs_T - E_obs_C)) / (p_T - r * p_C)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-LTFU-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-delta-adjusted-qaly-gain-icer-and-tipping-point-shift-over-a-grid-for-loss-to-follow-up" .

hemo:5000879 a owl:NamedIndividual,
