    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007285,
        hemo:7007286,
        hemo:7007287,
        hemo:7007288,
        hemo:7007289 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the names, budget costs and QALY gains of any number of independent programmes, a budget and the names of any programmes a rule requires to be funded, checks every one of the 2^n packages of whole programmes, and returns the best package that fits the budget and includes the required programmes, with its cost, its QALYs and the number of packages that qualify. Run once without and once with the required programmes, it gives the health forgone to the rule, the explicit cost of equity that Stinnett and Paltiel describe and the opportunity cost of an equity constraint that Epstein and colleagues estimate. The inputs are lists of programmes, not the scalar inputs of HE-FM-IPH-001. The Math / Code line shows four programmes with programme 4 required, and the R and Python versions take any number of programmes and any required set." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for the best package of whole programmes by enumerating every package within the budget" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Epstein DM, Chalabi Z, Claxton K, Sculpher M. Efficiency, equity, and budgetary policies: informing decisions using mathematical programming. Medical Decision Making. 2007;27(2):128-137. Abstract, on representing indivisibility in a patient population as a concern for horizontal equity, representing equity concerns as constraints in the programme and estimating the opportunity costs of a range of equity concerns. Stinnett AA, Paltiel AD. Mathematical programming for the efficient allocation of health care resources. Journal of Health Economics. 1996;15(5):641-653. Abstract, on ethical constraints and the explicit identification of the cost of equity. Crown W et al. Application of constrained optimization methods in health services research: report 2 of the ISPOR Optimization Methods Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2018;21(9):1019-1028. Appendix 1, Educational Case Study (accepted manuscript), on using a heuristic in place of an exact solution algorithm such as branch-and-bound for a mixed-integer programming model. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X06297396" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Enumerating a large candidate list. The number of packages doubles with each programme, 16 for 4, 1,048,576 for 20 and 1,073,741,824 for 30, so complete enumeration suits short candidate lists only. Crown and colleagues name branch-and-bound as an exact solution algorithm for mixed-integer models and describe heuristics that give a good but not necessarily optimal solution in reasonable time" ;
    hemo:9000014 "H_star = max(max(max(max(max(max(max(max(max(max(max(max(max(max((c_1 <= B) * q_1, (c_2 <= B) * q_2), (c_3 <= B) * q_3), (c_4 <= B) * q_4), (c_1 + c_2 <= B) * (q_1 + q_2)), (c_1 + c_3 <= B) * (q_1 + q_3)), (c_1 + c_4 <= B) * (q_1 + q_4)), (c_2 + c_3 <= B) * (q_2 + q_3)), (c_2 + c_4 <= B) * (q_2 + q_4)), (c_3 + c_4 <= B) * (q_3 + q_4)), (c_1 + c_2 + c_3 <= B) * (q_1 + q_2 + q_3)), (c_1 + c_2 + c_4 <= B) * (q_1 + q_2 + q_4)), (c_1 + c_3 + c_4 <= B) * (q_1 + q_3 + q_4)), (c_2 + c_3 + c_4 <= B) * (q_2 + q_3 + q_4)), (c_1 + c_2 + c_3 + c_4 <= B) * (q_1 + q_2 + q_3 + q_4)); H_must = q_4 + max(max(max(max(max(max((c_4 + c_1 <= B) * q_1, (c_4 + c_2 <= B) * q_2), (c_4 + c_3 <= B) * q_3), (c_4 + c_1 + c_2 <= B) * (q_1 + q_2)), (c_4 + c_1 + c_3 <= B) * (q_1 + q_3)), (c_4 + c_2 + c_3 <= B) * (q_2 + q_3)), (c_4 + c_1 + c_2 + c_3 <= B) * (q_1 + q_2 + q_3)); E_must = H_star - H_must" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-IPH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-the-best-package-of-whole-programmes-by-enumerating-every-package-within-the-budget" .

