        hemo:7007293,
        hemo:7007294,
        hemo:7007295 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes a ward's arrival rate per day, its mean stay, a refusal target and the yearly cost of a staffed bed and of a refused admission, and returns the Erlang loss probability, refusals a year and achieved occupancy for every number of beds up to a ceiling, the smallest number of beds that meets the refusal target and the number of beds that minimises the total cost of beds and refusals. It computes the loss probability of HE-FM-CAPP-001 for any number of beds by the Erlang B recursion B_0 = 1, B_k = a B_(k-1) / (k + a B_(k-1)), which avoids the large powers and factorials of the direct formula, and its target and cost inputs differ from the formula's symbols. The total cost of N beds is c_b N plus c_r times the refusals at N beds, and one more bed is worth adding while the refusals it avoids, times c_r, exceed c_b. Because each extra bed avoids fewer refusals than the one before, the first bed that fails this test marks the cost minimum (derived; it follows from the strict convexity of the loss function in the number of beds that Tuenter reports from Jagers and van Doorn, and a grid check of 75 cases found no exception). The calculator line writes the recursion out for six beds and returns the net saving of the sixth bed; the searches over N are in the Excel, R and Python code." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for Erlang B refusals, beds for a refusal target and cost-minimising beds" ;
    hemo:9000010 "de Bruin AM, Bekker R, van Zanten L, Koole GM. Dimensioning hospital wards using the Erlang loss model. Annals of Operations Research. 2010;178(1):23-43. Section 6.1 and Table 6, the number of required beds per ward for 2%, 5% and 10% blocking (10 beds at 5% for the coronary care unit), and section 6.3 and Table 7, 29 beds for 5% refused admissions at the three units apart and 22 after merging. Tuenter HJH. Erlang's loss function and a property of the exponential function. arXiv preprint math/0612052, 2006. Preprint section 2, equation (6), the recursion in reciprocal form with initial value B(0, lambda) = 1, and the statement that Jagers and van Doorn showed the continued loss function to be strictly convex in the number of circuits. Gorunescu F, McClean SI, Millard PH. A queueing model for bed-occupancy management and planning of hospitals. Journal of the Operational Research Society. 2002;53(1):19-24. Abstract, on optimising the number of beds to keep the delay probability low and optimising the average cost per day by balancing costs of empty beds against costs of delayed patients. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10479-009-0647-8" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Factorials in one cell for a large ward. Writing the loss formula with FACT and powers in one cell fails above 170 beds, because FACT returns #NUM! for arguments above 170, and powers of the load overflow for large wards in the same way. The recursion keeps every term at or below 1",
        "One occupancy target for every unit size. Sizing the article's small unit at 85% occupancy gives 10 beds and 12.2% refused, about one patient in eight, while the same rule gives 1.14% for the 95-bed unit; the beds for a stated refusal target come from BedsForTarget, not from the occupancy rule" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a = lambda * L_bar; B_1 = a / (1 + a); B_2 = a * B_1 / (2 + a * B_1); B_3 = a * B_2 / (3 + a * B_2); B_4 = a * B_3 / (4 + a * B_3); B_5 = a * B_4 / (5 + a * B_4); B_6 = a * B_5 / (6 + a * B_5); R_5 = 365 * lambda * B_5; R_6 = 365 * lambda * B_6; S_6 = c_r * (R_5 - R_6) - c_b" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-CAPP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-erlang-b-refusals-beds-for-a-refusal-target-and-cost-minimising-beds" .

