        hemo:7007299 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the health gain of three technologies with the fixed range of that criterion, their scores on two further criteria and the swing points of the three criteria, and returns each technology's health gain score by HE-FM-MCDH-001, the weights and overall values by HE-FM-MCDH-002, and the highest overall value. Its inputs are raw performance on the first criterion rather than the partial values of HE-FM-MCDH-002. The R and Python code also rank the technologies, scan the health gain weight on a grid of 101 values, 0, 0.01 and so on up to 1, with the other two weights kept in the ratio of their points, and return, for each pair of technologies, the weight at which their overall values are equal. Each total is linear in that weight, so this weight takes the form of the switching weight w_s of HE-FM-WE-004, which that record computes only for the QALYs of two groups, here with the other-criteria blend in place of group A QALYs and the health gain score less that blend in place of group B QALYs (derived). It marks a change of ranking only when it is at least 0 and at most 1; a value below 0 or above 1 means the pair keeps the same order at every feasible weight, and a pair with equal slopes either never ties or, with equal totals, ties at every weight (derived). Equal overall values are reported as ties, with no rule to break them." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for MCDA totals, ranking and a health gain weight scan for three technologies" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Thokala P, Devlin N, Marsh K, Baltussen R, Boysen M, Kalo Z, et al. Multiple criteria decision analysis for health care decision making: an introduction. Report 1 of the ISPOR MCDA Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2016;19(1):1-13. Appendix, MCDA Modeling Example, Scoring, Weighting and Aggregation (Table A2). Marsh K, IJzerman M, Thokala P, Baltussen R, Boysen M, Kaló Z, et al. Multiple criteria decision analysis for health care decision making: emerging good practices. Report 2 of the ISPOR MCDA Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2016;19(2):125-137. Step 7b, Report and justify the uncertainty analysis, which states that at a minimum deterministic analysis should be undertaken. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2015.12.003" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Ranking at the elicited weights only. Reporting only the ranking at the elicited health gain weight of 0.50 hides that C, with no health gain, ranks first at every health gain weight below 2/7, about 0.29 (derived from the article's example). The second ISPOR report states that, at a minimum, deterministic analysis should be undertaken" ;
    hemo:9000014 "v_A = 100 * (x_A - x_worst) / (x_best - x_worst); v_B = 100 * (x_B - x_worst) / (x_best - x_worst); v_C = 100 * (x_C - x_worst) / (x_best - x_worst); w_1 = p_1 / (p_1 + p_2 + p_3); w_2 = p_2 / (p_1 + p_2 + p_3); w_3 = p_3 / (p_1 + p_2 + p_3); V_A = w_1 * v_A + w_2 * s_A + w_3 * e_A; V_B = w_1 * v_B + w_2 * s_B + w_3 * e_B; V_C = w_1 * v_C + w_2 * s_C + w_3 * e_C; V_top = max(max(V_A, V_B), V_C)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-MCDH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-mcda-totals-ranking-and-a-health-gain-weight-scan-for-three-technologies" .

