    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Weights the person-years that a period life table assigns to each age band after the starting age x by the share of the band free of dependency, adds them and divides by the survivors at x, as Sullivan's method does. The article's three bands (65 to 74, 75 to 84, and 85 and over) are written term by term; more bands add terms in the same way, and the last band is open-ended so that all remaining life is counted. Setting every pi_i to 0 returns life expectancy e_x, person-years over survivors (Imai and Soneji equation 7). That first part is HE-FM-ASF-002 with U_t of 1 and r of 0, its single-year L_t summed within each band to give L_i over l_x (derived), and is cited, not restated. DLE_x, the expected years lived dependent, is the difference and s_A is the share of remaining life lived active. The sum has the form of HE-FM-ASF-002 with r of 0 and U_t replaced by 1 minus pi_i, a use that record does not cover because its U_t is a utility norm (HE-VR-ASF-007); here the weight is a prevalence share with dependent time counting zero." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Sullivan active life expectancy over three age bands with life expectancy and dependent years" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Imai K, Soneji S. On the estimation of disability-free life expectancy: Sullivan's method and its extension. Journal of the American Statistical Association. 2007;102(480):1199-1211. doi:10.1198/016214507000000040 (published version read in full). Section 2.2, equation 6 for the person-years in an interval and equation 7, life expectancy at age x as the person-years of the following intervals divided by l_x. Section 3.1, Sullivan's Method, equation 11, Sullivan's estimator of disability-free life expectancy as the sum of 1 minus the band prevalence times the person-years, divided by l_x, with equation 12 for the prevalence as the sample fraction of disabled respondents in the band. Section 3.2, Proposition 1, unbiasedness and consistency under the three stationarity assumptions of the period life table and constant age-specific disability prevalence, not depending on the interval widths. Section 3.3, Sullivan's own estimator of prevalence from days spent disabled in the previous year, equation 15, called incorrect. https://doi.org/10.1198/016214507000000040",
        "Katz S, Branch LG, Branson MH, Papsidero JA, Beck JC, Greer DS. Active life expectancy. New England Journal of Medicine. 1983;309(20):1218-1224. doi:10.1056/NEJM198311173092005. The abstract describes using life-table techniques to analyse the expected remaining years of functional well-being, in terms of the activities of daily living, for noninstitutionalized elderly people in Massachusetts in 1974, and names these expected years active life expectancy. https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJM198311173092005",
        "Kingston A, Comas-Herrera A, Jagger C. Forecasting the care needs of the older population in England over the next 20 years: estimates from the Population Ageing and Care Simulation (PACSim) modelling study. Lancet Public Health. 2018;3(9):e447-e455. doi:10.1016/S2468-2667(18)30118-X (full text read). Methods, subsection Model outputs, where years with and without dependency at age 65 were calculated for each year by Sullivan's method, applying the age-sex-specific prevalence of dependency to the age-sex-specific life-table population generated from the survival probabilities. The Introduction states that care needs are most often assessed by needing help to undertake one or more basic activities of daily living. https://doi.org/10.1016/S2468-2667(18)30118-X",
        "Mathers CD, Robine JM. How good is Sullivan's method for monitoring changes in population health expectancies? Journal of Epidemiology and Community Health. 1997;51(1):80-86. doi:10.1136/jech.51.1.80. Abstract, which describes a simulation on abridged cohort life tables and states in its main results that the Sullivan method could not detect a sudden change in disability transition rates but provides a good estimate of the true multistate value if there are smooth and relatively regular changes in disability prevalence over the longer term. https://doi.org/10.1136/jech.51.1.80" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Applying an unweighted mean prevalence to life expectancy. Averaging the band prevalences without weights and applying the mean to e_x ignores that the oldest band, where dependency is highest, holds the fewest person-years. On the baseline the mean of 0.2833 gives 13.2583 years, 1.2167 years below the Sullivan value of 14.475 (derived). The person-years of each band are the weights.",
        "Leaving a prevalence cell blank. In Excel a blank cell in DependentShareByBand counts as 0, so that band is read as wholly active and no error appears. A blank prevalence for 85 and over on the baseline raises ActiveLifeExpectancy to 15.975 years against 14.475 (derived). A band with no survey estimate needs a value or an explicit flag before the result is used.",
        "Prevalence from a household survey that leaves out care homes. A survey of people living at home, like the non-institutionalised sample of Katz and colleagues, misses many of the most dependent people. If the measured prevalence at 85 and over were 0.40 in place of a whole-population 0.50, active life expectancy on the baseline would be 14.775 years, 0.3 years too high (illustrative, derived).",
        "Reading the active share without the years of dependence. A rising active life expectancy can come with more years of dependence. In the article's Scenario B active life expectancy rises by 0.5 years, yet the active share falls to 76.8% against 78.2% at baseline and dependent years rise by 0.5, which adds GBP 15,000 of expected care cost per person at GBP 30,000 a year (HE-FM-COST-001). Both levels, ALE_x and DLE_x, are reported with s_A.",
        "Weighting person-years by the dependency prevalence in place of its complement. Multiplying each L_i by pi_i in place of 1 minus pi_i gives the expected years lived dependent, not the active years. On the article's baseline it returns 4.025 years in place of 14.475, and the error is hard to spot when prevalence is near one half in most bands." ;
    hemo:9000014 "e_x = (L_1 + L_2 + L_3) / l_x; ALE_x = ((1 - pi_1) * L_1 + (1 - pi_2) * L_2 + (1 - pi_3) * L_3) / l_x; DLE_x = e_x - ALE_x; s_A = ALE_x / e_x" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ALE-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "sullivan-active-life-expectancy-over-three-age-bands-with-life-expectancy-and-dependent-years" .

