        hemo:6005558 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005539,
        hemo:6005540,
        hemo:6005541,
        hemo:6005542,
        hemo:6005543,
        hemo:6005544,
        hemo:6005545,
        hemo:6005552,
        hemo:6005553,
        hemo:6005554,
        hemo:6005555,
        hemo:6005556 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005546,
        hemo:6005547,
        hemo:6005548,
        hemo:6005549,
        hemo:6005550,
        hemo:6005551,
        hemo:6005557,
        hemo:6005558 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7006636 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007320,
        hemo:7007321,
        hemo:7007322,
        hemo:7007323,
        hemo:7007324 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes a period life table's survivors at the starting age and person-years by age band, the dependency prevalence measured in each band and the number of survey respondents behind each prevalence, and returns active life expectancy, life expectancy, expected dependent years, the active share, the standard error of active life expectancy and a normal 95% interval, for any number of bands. The point estimate is HE-FM-ALE-001. The variance is the standard estimator Imai and Soneji print as their equation 13, the sum over bands of pi_i (1 minus pi_i) L_i squared over N_i, divided by l_x squared; each band's term takes the form of the squared standard error of HE-FM-BINOM-004, with survey respondents in place of that record's patients, times the squared weight L_i over l_x (derived), so the bands are treated as independent binomial samples and the survey respondents N_i are inputs the formula does not hold. For the 1999 United States population with an activities of daily living definition, Imai and Soneji report 14.31 years at 65 with a 95% interval of 14.24 to 14.37 when the death rates are treated as known. The calculator line writes three bands out term by term; the loops over bands are in the Excel, R and Python code." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Computational function for Sullivan active life expectancy with its standard error over any number of age bands" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Imai K, Soneji S. On the estimation of disability-free life expectancy: Sullivan's method and its extension. Journal of the American Statistical Association. 2007;102(480):1199-1211. Section 3.1, equation 13, the standard variance estimator of Sullivan's estimator under an independent binomial process in each age interval, and Table 2, estimated disability-free life expectancy with 95% intervals with the death rates known and estimated; section 3.2, Proposition 2, that the standard variance estimator is consistent and approximately unbiased under the four stationarity assumptions. https://doi.org/10.1198/016214507000000040" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Reading the interval as covering all uncertainty. The interval reflects survey sampling only. Imai and Soneji's Table 2 shows that allowing for sampling variability in the death rates widens the interval at age 20 for the 1999 United States population, 54.40 to 54.53 years with the death rates known and 54.37 to 54.56 with them estimated; misclassification, a survey that leaves out care homes (HE-ER-ALE-004) and departures from stationarity are not in the standard error at all",
        "Weights left unsquared in the variance. Writing the variance as the sum of w_i pi_i (1 minus pi_i) over N_i gives 0.0022 in place of 0.0117 on the baseline, a standard error of 0.0464 in place of 0.1083 and an interval less than half as wide (derived). The weight enters the variance squared because each band's prevalence is multiplied by L_i over l_x" ;
    hemo:9000014 "w_1 = L_1 / l_x; w_2 = L_2 / l_x; w_3 = L_3 / l_x; ALE_x = (1 - pi_1) * w_1 + (1 - pi_2) * w_2 + (1 - pi_3) * w_3; V_ALE = w_1^2 * pi_1 * (1 - pi_1) / N_1 + w_2^2 * pi_2 * (1 - pi_2) / N_2 + w_3^2 * pi_3 * (1 - pi_3) / N_3; SE_ALE = sqrt(V_ALE); lo_ALE = ALE_x - 1.96 * SE_ALE; hi_ALE = ALE_x + 1.96 * SE_ALE" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-CF-ALE-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "computational-function-for-sullivan-active-life-expectancy-with-its-standard-error-over-any-number-of-age-bands" .

hemo:5000965 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Dominated option, the mirror of the dominance rule"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000114 obo:IAO_0000125 ;
    obo:IAO_0000115 "Gives 1 when an option costs no less and is no more effective than its comparator and is strictly worse on one of the two, with equality read within the tie tolerances, and 0 otherwise."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "A HEMO-native record, for a formula the Functions and Formulae module does not yet hold. It is listed in module-backlog.md for the module curator."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000117 "Michelle Ashley (drafted with Claude)" ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "Drummond et al. 2015; Briggs et al. 2006" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HEMO-FM-DOMD-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005564,
        hemo:6005565,
        hemo:6005566,
        hemo:6005567,
        hemo:6005568 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005565,
        hemo:6005566,
        hemo:6005567,
        hemo:6005568 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005564 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7007326 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007377,
        hemo:7007383 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8001647 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8001627 ;
    hemo:1000030 hemo:5000198 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000014 "DOMD = (Delta_C >= -tau_C) and (Delta_E <= tau_E) and (Delta_C > tau_C or Delta_E < -tau_E)" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>DOMD</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"logic1\">and</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"logic1\">and</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">geq</csymbol><ci>Delta_C</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">unary_minus</csymbol><ci>tau_C</ci></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">leq</csymbol><ci>Delta_E</ci><ci>tau_E</ci></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"logic1\">or</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">gt</csymbol><ci>Delta_C</ci><ci>tau_C</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">lt</csymbol><ci>Delta_E</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">unary_minus</csymbol><ci>tau_E</ci></apply></apply></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{DOMD} = \\left(\\Delta_{C} \\ge -\\tau_{C} \\land \\Delta_{E} \\le \\tau_{E}\\right) \\land \\left(\\Delta_{C} > \\tau_{C} \\lor \\Delta_{E} < -\\tau_{E}\\right)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HEMO-FM-DOMD-001" .

