hemo:5000021 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Rate and transition probability conversion function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-TP-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000055,
        hemo:6000056,
        hemo:6000057 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000055,
        hemo:6000056 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000057 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps an event rate and a time interval to the probability that the event occurs within that interval, and back again. Rates are converted rather than probabilities divided, because a probability is tied to the length of the interval over which it was observed. The converted probability then enters a transition matrix used in the cohort update s_(t+1) = s_t P described on the Markov Model page." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Rate and transition probability conversion function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "c(h,Delta) = p" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-TP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "rate-and-transition-probability-conversion-function" .

hemo:5000028 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Network consistency function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-NMA-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Expresses every pairwise relative effect in a connected network through effects relative to a common reference treatment A, on a scale where effects add, such as the log odds ratio. Here d_XY is the effect of Y relative to X, the NICE Decision Support Unit convention. Consistency is the assumption that direct and indirect evidence estimate the same d_XY." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Network consistency function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d_XY = d_AY - d_AX" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-NMA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "network-consistency-function" .

hemo:5000038 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Welfare change measurement and aggregation function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-WE-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Converts each person's gain or loss from a change into money, with the compensating or equivalent variation or the consumer surplus approximation, and then ranks the change for society: by adding the money measures, as the Kaldor-Hicks potential compensation test does, or by combining individual outcomes with an explicit social welfare function that states how gains to one person are set against losses to another." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Welfare change measurement and aggregation function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "W(u_1,...,u_n) = W" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-WE-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "welfare-change-measurement-and-aggregation-function" .

hemo:5000045 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Large-sample normal confidence interval function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-AN-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps an estimate and its estimated sampling variance to an approximate confidence interval by treating the estimator as normally distributed, which asymptotic normality justifies in large samples even when patient-level data are skewed. In trial-based cost-effectiveness analysis the same operation is applied to a difference in mean cost or effect, to the ICER through the delta method, to the pair of differences through Fieller's method, and to incremental net benefit, which is linear in the two differences." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Large-sample normal confidence interval function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "CI(theta_hat, SE, z) = [L, U]" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-AN-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "large-sample-normal-confidence-interval-function" .

hemo:5000046 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Atkinson inequality index and equally distributed equivalent health function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-ATK-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000110,
        hemo:6000111,
        hemo:6000112,
        hemo:6000113 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000110,
        hemo:6000111 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000112,
        hemo:6000113 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a distribution of health across people or equal-sized groups, and a chosen inequality aversion parameter epsilon, to the equally distributed equivalent (EDE) level of health and the Atkinson index. The EDE is the level of health which, if everyone had it, would be judged as good as the actual unequal distribution. The index is the share of mean health that could be given up for full equality without lowering social welfare. In distributional cost-effectiveness analysis the EDE ranks programmes and the index describes how far each one leaves health unevenly spread." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Atkinson inequality index and equally distributed equivalent health function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "f(h_i, epsilon) = (h_EDE, A_epsilon)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-ATK-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "atkinson-inequality-index-and-equally-distributed-equivalent-health-function" .

hemo:5000049 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Autoregressive correlation function for repeated health measurements"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-AR1-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the correlation between adjacent measurements on the same patient, and the distance between two measurements, to the correlation between them under a first-order autoregressive (AR(1)) structure. The correlation falls by the same factor with each step, so one parameter describes the whole within-patient correlation matrix. Combined with a common variance at every visit, the structure gives the variance of any weighted total or difference of the repeated measurements, such as a trial QALY total or a change in utility from baseline. In time-series notation the same pattern comes from the error process e_t = rho * e_(t-1) + u_t, which for a stationary series gives a correlation of rho^s between errors s periods apart." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Autoregressive correlation function for repeated health measurements" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Corr(Y_ij, Y_ik) = rho^abs(j - k); Var(sum_j w_j * Y_ij) = sigma2 * sum_j sum_k w_j * w_k * rho^abs(j - k)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-AR1-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "autoregressive-correlation-function-for-repeated-health-measurements" .

hemo:5000057 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Administrative cost ratio function for payers and health systems"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-ACR-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the administrative cost of a payer or financing system over a period, and a measure of the money it handles over the same period, to the share of that money used for administration rather than claims or care. The general form allows any denominator. The applied formulas fix it as premium revenue, total payer spending or current health expenditure, and set the ratio beside the United States medical loss ratio, its complement and the rebate that follows from it. Notation follows the Administrative Cost Ratio article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Administrative cost ratio function for payers and health systems" ;
    hemo:9000014 "ACR = f(A, D)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-ACR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "administrative-cost-ratio-function-for-payers-and-health-systems" .

hemo:5000058 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Actuarial claims costing, premium loading and claims reserving function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-ACTA-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000117,
        hemo:6000118,
        hemo:6000119,
        hemo:6000120,
        hemo:6000121,
        hemo:6000122,
        hemo:6000123,
        hemo:6000124,
        hemo:6000125,
        hemo:6000126,
        hemo:6000127,
        hemo:6000128,
        hemo:6000129,
        hemo:6000130 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000117,
        hemo:6000118,
        hemo:6000119,
        hemo:6000120,
        hemo:6000121,
        hemo:6000122,
        hemo:6000123,
        hemo:6000124 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000125,
        hemo:6000126,
        hemo:6000127,
        hemo:6000128,
        hemo:6000129,
        hemo:6000130 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps utilisation rates, unit costs and the size of a covered group to the expected claims cost of a health insurance scheme, turns that expected cost into a gross premium or contribution with loadings, measures how predictable the group's average cost is, and estimates the claims incurred but not yet paid at a reporting date. These are the calculations of the Actuarial Analysis article. The plan generosity measure is covered separately on the Actuarial Value page." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Actuarial claims costing, premium loading and claims reserving function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "a(f_i, u_i, N, beta, gamma, sigma, P_m, c_d) = (C_bar, T, G, SD_N, I_m, U_m)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-ACTA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "actuarial-claims-costing-premium-loading-and-claims-reserving-function" .

hemo:5000059 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Average treatment effect function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-ATE-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the two potential outcomes of each patient in a stated population, Y(1) under the treatment and Y(0) under the comparator, to the mean of their difference. Only one potential outcome is observed for any patient, so the mean is computed from subgroup averages or by reweighting observed outcomes under stated conditions. Conditioning on the treated gives the average effect on the treated (ATT), and conditioning on baseline characteristics gives the conditional average effect (CATE). Applied to costs and to QALYs, the two averages are the incremental cost and incremental QALYs used by the pairwise ICER (HE-FM-ICER-001) and the incremental net monetary benefit (HE-FM-NMB-002)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Average treatment effect function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "ATE = E[Y(1) - Y(0)]" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-ATE-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "average-treatment-effect-function" .

hemo:5000061 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Nash bargaining surplus-division function for negotiated health care prices"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-BARG-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the payoffs two negotiating parties obtain from each feasible agreement, their disagreement payoffs and their relative bargaining power to the agreement that maximises the weighted product of their gains over disagreement. In a price negotiation between a payer and a manufacturer the feasible agreements are prices from the manufacturer's reservation price up to the payer's maximum price, and the function returns the price that splits the surplus between them in proportion to their bargaining weights. A weight of one half gives Nash's original symmetric solution. The payer's maximum price can come from a cost-effectiveness threshold (HE-FM-BARG-002), the manufacturer's weight from the parties' patience in an alternating-offers model (HE-FM-BARG-003), and an outside option changes the result only when it binds (HE-FM-BARG-004)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Nash bargaining surplus-division function for negotiated health care prices" ;
    hemo:9000014 "x* = argmax_(x in F) (U_M(x) - d_M)^beta * (U_P(x) - d_P)^(1 - beta)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-BARG-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "nash-bargaining-surplus-division-function-for-negotiated-health-care-prices" .

hemo:5000063 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Baumol unbalanced growth relative cost and labour allocation function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-BAUM-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000131,
        hemo:6000132,
        hemo:6000133,
        hemo:6000134,
        hemo:6000135,
        hemo:6000136,
        hemo:6000137,
        hemo:6000138,
        hemo:6000139,
        hemo:6000140 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000131,
        hemo:6000132,
        hemo:6000133,
        hemo:6000134,
        hemo:6000135,
        hemo:6000136 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000137,
        hemo:6000138,
        hemo:6000139,
        hemo:6000140 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps output per hour, or its annual growth, in health care and in the rest of the economy to the labour cost of a unit of health care relative to a unit of other output, and to the share of working hours that health care needs when the mix of outputs is held fixed. With a common wage in both sectors the wage cancels, so the relative cost depends only on relative productivity. A related top-down form, used in UK fiscal projections, turns economy-wide productivity growth into an allowance for real health spending growth." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Baumol unbalanced growth relative cost and labour allocation function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "f(g_o, g_h, T, x_0, beta, g_econ) = (R_T, x_T, s_hT, B)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-BAUM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "baumol-unbalanced-growth-relative-cost-and-labour-allocation-function" .

hemo:5000065 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Vertical equity in health financing, progressivity and redistribution function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-VEQ-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000148,
        hemo:6000149,
        hemo:6000150,
        hemo:6000151,
        hemo:6000152,
        hemo:6000153,
        hemo:6000154 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000148,
        hemo:6000149,
        hemo:6000150,
        hemo:6000151 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000152,
        hemo:6000153,
        hemo:6000154 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the distribution of health care payments and of ability to pay, across households ranked from poorest to richest, to measures of vertical equity in financing: the Kakwani index of progressivity for each source of finance, the revenue-weighted index for the whole financing mix, and the vertical redistributive effect of compulsory payments on the Gini coefficient of income. Progressivity is measured as a departure from proportionality between payments and ability to pay. Vertical equity in delivery is judged against a need norm that the analyst chooses and defends, so it has no single formula and is not covered here." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Vertical equity in health financing: progressivity and redistribution function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "f(L_X, L_P, w_j, g) = (pi_K, pi_total, V)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-VEQ-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "vertical-equity-in-health-financing-progressivity-and-redistribution-function" .

hemo:5000067 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Bayesian random-effects posterior for a pairwise meta-analysis"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-BMA-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps each trial's effect estimate y_i and standard error s_i, together with priors for the mean effect mu and the between-study standard deviation tau, to a joint posterior distribution for mu and tau. The trial-level true effects are integrated out, so each estimate contributes a normal likelihood with variance s_i^2 + tau^2. The posterior of the mean effect, shrunken trial estimates and the predictive distribution of the effect in a new setting all follow from this joint posterior, and posterior draws can pass directly into a probabilistic cost-effectiveness model. Frequentist pooling of the same data, including the DerSimonian and Laird estimate, Cochran's Q, I-squared and the prediction interval, is covered under aggregate data meta-analysis (HE-FN-ADMA-001)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Bayesian random-effects posterior for a pairwise meta-analysis" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p(mu, tau | y) ∝ p(mu) * p(tau) * prod_(i=1)^k [N(y_i | mu, s_i^2 + tau^2)]" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-BMA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "bayesian-random-effects-posterior-for-a-pairwise-meta-analysis" .

hemo:5000072 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Bootstrap standard error of a trial statistic from resampled data"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-BSE-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a statistic and the data that produced it to the standard deviation of the statistic across samples drawn with replacement from the data, each of the same size as the original sample. The empirical distribution of the observations, with mass 1/n on each, stands in for the unknown population distribution, so no normal shape is assumed. In trial-based economic evaluation the statistic is usually a mean cost, an incremental cost or incremental net benefit, and the resampling copies the trial design by drawing patients within each arm with their costs and effects kept together." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Bootstrap standard error of a trial statistic from resampled data" ;
    hemo:9000014 "SE_B = sd(theta_1, ..., theta_B)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-BSE-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "bootstrap-standard-error-of-a-trial-statistic-from-resampled-data" .

hemo:5000076 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Observed follow-up, Kaplan-Meier survival and restricted mean under administrative censoring"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-ADMC-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps each patient's entry date, the shared data cut-off and the event times seen by the cut-off to the observed follow-up and event indicator, then to the Kaplan-Meier survival curve, the restricted mean survival time up to the last observed follow-up and the share of mean survival that has to come from extrapolation beyond it. Staggered entry makes the censoring time differ between patients, so early recruits are observed for longer than late ones. The constant hazard estimated as events divided by person-time is the rate formula of the Adverse Event Rate page (HE-FM-AER-002), and mean survival with an exponential tail has the form of HE-FM-BTH-002 on the Bathtub Hazard page; neither is repeated here." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Observed follow-up, Kaplan-Meier survival and restricted mean under administrative censoring" ;
    hemo:9000014 "f(e_i, d, T_i) = (Y_i, delta_i, S(t), RMST(tau), P_tail)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-ADMC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "observed-follow-up-kaplan-meier-survival-and-restricted-mean-under-administrative-censoring" .

hemo:5000078 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Health inequity measurement function under stated fairness judgements"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-INEQ-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000175,
        hemo:6000176,
        hemo:6000177,
        hemo:6000178,
        hemo:6000179,
        hemo:6000180,
        hemo:6000181,
        hemo:6000182,
        hemo:6000183,
        hemo:6000184 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000175,
        hemo:6000176,
        hemo:6000177,
        hemo:6000178,
        hemo:6000179,
        hemo:6000180 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000181,
        hemo:6000182,
        hemo:6000183,
        hemo:6000184 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the age-specific rates of ill health in social groups, a standard population, and a decomposition of the standardised gap between groups into parts attributed to named causes, to estimates of health inequity: the part of a measured difference judged avoidable and unfair. Differences due to factors classed as legitimate, such as age, are first removed by direct standardisation. The inequity is then the part of the remaining gap whose causes are classed as illegitimate, with the unexplained remainder treated as acceptable under direct unfairness or as unfair under the fairness gap. The size of a gap is measured with the gaps, slope and relative indices and concentration index on the Health Inequality page (HE-FN-HINQ-001), and indirect standardisation of health care use for need is on the Horizontal Equity page (HE-FM-HEQ-001); neither is repeated here." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health inequity measurement function under stated fairness judgements" ;
    hemo:9000014 "F(r_gk, w_k, Delta_j, u_j, Delta_res, u_res) = (R_g, I_DU, I_FG, s)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-INEQ-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-inequity-measurement-function-under-stated-fairness-judgements" .

hemo:5000079 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Community-rated premium, cross-subsidy and risk equalisation function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-CRAT-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000185,
        hemo:6000186,
        hemo:6000187,
        hemo:6000188,
        hemo:6000189,
        hemo:6000190,
        hemo:6000191,
        hemo:6000192,
        hemo:6000193 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000185,
        hemo:6000186,
        hemo:6000187,
        hemo:6000188,
        hemo:6000189 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000190,
        hemo:6000191,
        hemo:6000192,
        hemo:6000193 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the number of members and the expected annual cost of each risk group in a community-rated pool to the single premium every member pays, the implicit cross-subsidy each group gives or receives, the risk equalisation transfers that offset those cross-subsidies for insurers, and the late-entry loadings that Ireland and Australia add to the premium of people who first buy cover when older. The same difference between payment and expected cost, read from the insurer's side, is the expected profit per member on the Cream Skimming page (HE-FM-CSKM-001)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Community-rated premium, cross-subsidy and risk equalisation function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "F(n_i, c_i, a, y, L_max) = (P, s_i, T_i, L)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-CRAT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "community-rated-premium-cross-subsidy-and-risk-equalisation-function" .

hemo:5000084 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Case weight derivation, case-weighted payment and casemix cost comparison function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-CWT-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the mean costs of patient classification groups, such as diagnosis-related groups (DRGs), to relative case weights, and uses the weights to price hospital cases and to compare hospital costs for a given casemix. The records follow the notation of the Case Weight article, where w_g is the weight of group g and c_bar the mean cost per case across all groups, so a weight of 1 marks a group whose cases cost the same as the average case." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Case weight derivation, case-weighted payment and casemix cost comparison function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "w_g = c_bar_g / c_bar; P_i = w_g * B * a_h; P_i = NWAU_i * NEP; R_h = C_h / (CM_h * c_bar)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-CWT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "case-weight-derivation-case-weighted-payment-and-casemix-cost-comparison-function" .

hemo:5000085 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Case fatality rate estimation, outbreak bias and population mortality function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-CFR-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps counts of deaths, recoveries and cases to the case fatality rate (CFR), the proportion of cases meeting a case definition who die of the disease over a stated period, and relates it to the delay bias of crude estimates during exponential growth and to population mortality through the attack rate. The records follow the notation of the Case Fatality Rate article. Matching the period of a CFR to a model cycle uses HE-FM-TP-003, and the attack rate itself is HE-FM-ATR-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Case fatality rate estimation, outbreak bias and population mortality function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "CFR = f(D, N_c); CFR_r = g(D, R); B = h(d, T_d); M = AR * CFR" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-CFR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "case-fatality-rate-estimation-outbreak-bias-and-population-mortality-function" .

hemo:5000086 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Proportion of days covered from pharmacy fill dates and days' supply function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-PDC-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a patient's dispensing records, each a fill date and a days' supply, and an observation period to the proportion of days covered (PDC), the share of days in the period on which at least one fill provides medicine, each day counted once. Same-drug refills collected before the previous supply runs out are shifted to start the day after it ends, overlaps between different drugs in a class are not shifted, supply running past the end of the period is ignored, and the CMS method removes inpatient and skilled nursing facility stay days. The records follow the notation of the Proportion of Days Covered article. The medication possession ratio, which sums days' supply instead, is HE-FM-MPR-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Proportion of days covered from pharmacy fill dates and days' supply function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "PDC = C / T; a_i = max(f_i, a_prev + S_prev); C = sum_(i=1)^n [(E - a_i + 1) * (E - a_i + 1 > 0) * (E - a_i + 1 < S_i) + S_i * (E - a_i + 1 >= S_i)]; PDC_stay = C_adj / (T - H)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-PDC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "proportion-of-days-covered-from-pharmacy-fill-dates-and-days-supply-function" .

hemo:5000087 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Medication possession ratio from summed days' supply function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-MPR-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a patient's dispensing records for one medicine, each a fill date and a days' supply, to the medication possession ratio (MPR): the days' supply dispensed divided by a count of days. The variants differ in which fills enter the numerator and which days form the denominator: a fixed interval from the first dispensing to the end of the observation period, or a refill interval between the first and last dispensing, with or without the last fill. Because supply is summed without regard to overlap, an uncapped MPR can exceed 1, and it is often capped at 1. The records follow the notation of the Medication Possession Ratio article. The proportion of days covered, which counts unique covered days instead, is HE-FM-PDC-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Medication possession ratio from summed days' supply function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "MPR_fixed = sum_(i=1)^n [S_i] / D; MPR_refill = sum_(i=1)^n [S_i] / (f_n - f_1 + S_n); MPR_m = (sum_(i=1)^n [S_i] - S_n) / (f_n - f_1); MPR_capped = min(MPR, 1)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-MPR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "medication-possession-ratio-from-summed-days-supply-function" .

hemo:5000093 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Deadweight loss of a wedge between marginal value and marginal cost"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-DWL-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a price distortion, such as a tax, a subsidy, a price above cost or insurance that lowers the price at the point of use, to the net surplus it destroys: the value of trades it prevents in excess of their cost, or the cost of trades it adds in excess of their value. Money that only changes hands, such as tax revenue or an insurer's payments, is netted out. With straight-line curves the area is a triangle whose height is the wedge and whose base is the change in quantity. The notation follows the Deadweight Loss article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Deadweight loss of a wedge between marginal value and marginal cost" ;
    hemo:9000014 "DWL = abs(integral_(q_star)^(q_d) (P(q) - MC(q)) dq)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-DWL-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "deadweight-loss-of-a-wedge-between-marginal-value-and-marginal-cost" .

hemo:5000094 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Best-worst scaling design, scoring and choice probability function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-BWS-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a planned series of choice sets, each answered with a best and a worst choice, to a scale of preference or priority. The design is usually a balanced incomplete block design, the simplest analysis counts best and worst choices and standardises their difference, and choice models place items on a latent utility scale through the probability of each best and worst pair. The notation follows the Best-Worst Scaling article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Best-worst scaling design, scoring and choice probability function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "(B_i, W_i, V_j) -> (S_i, P(b, w | C))" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-BWS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "best-worst-scaling-design-scoring-and-choice-probability-function" .

hemo:5000095 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Bayesian interim decision function for an adaptive trial"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-BAD-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a prior distribution for the treatment effect, the data observed so far and pre-specified thresholds to the quantities that interim rules consult: the posterior probability that the effect exceeds a minimum value, which summarises the evidence now, and the predictive probability that the final analysis will succeed, which averages over the results still to come. Response-adaptive rules use the same posterior to set the chance of allocation to each arm. The notation follows the Bayesian Adaptive Design article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Bayesian interim decision function for an adaptive trial" ;
    hemo:9000014 "p(theta | y) = p(y | theta) * p(theta) / p(y); stop if Pr(d > a | y) > c" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-BAD-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "bayesian-interim-decision-function-for-an-adaptive-trial" .

hemo:5000101 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Decomposition of whole-profile ratings into attribute part-worths in conjoint analysis"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-CJA-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a respondent's ratings of profiles, each described by levels of several attributes, to a part-worth for each level under an additive model, so that a profile's value is the intercept plus the part-worths of its levels. One level of each attribute is the reference, with a part-worth of zero. Relative importance and trade-offs between attributes follow from the part-worths. The notation follows the Conjoint Analysis article, whose outpatient clinic example is used throughout." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Decomposition of whole-profile ratings into attribute part-worths in conjoint analysis" ;
    hemo:9000014 "R_j = beta_0 + sum_(k=1)^K sum_(l=1)^(L_k) beta_kl * x_jkl + e_j" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-CJA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "decomposition-of-whole-profile-ratings-into-attribute-part-worths-in-conjoint-analysis" .

hemo:5000102 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Continuous-time transition probabilities and expected state occupancy from transition intensities"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-CONT-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a matrix of constant transition rates (intensities) between health states to the probability of being in each state after any interval, by the matrix exponential, and to the expected, optionally discounted, time spent in each state. A fixed-cycle Markov model approximates this continuous process; the exact interval probabilities show what the approximation should reproduce. The notation follows the Continuous Model article and its illness-death example." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Continuous-time transition probabilities and expected state occupancy from transition intensities" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P(t) = exp(t * Q); d occ(t) / dt = occ(t) * Q" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-CONT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "continuous-time-transition-probabilities-and-expected-state-occupancy-from-transition-intensities" .

hemo:5000104 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Bayesian optimisation of a model calibration loss with a Gaussian process surrogate"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-BOPT-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Searches a bounded parameter region for the values that minimise a goodness-of-fit loss between model outputs and calibration targets when each model run is expensive. A Gaussian process fitted to the losses already observed gives a normal posterior for the loss at any untried value, and an acquisition function, here expected improvement, chooses the next run. The notation follows the Bayesian Optimisation article and its one-parameter incidence calibration." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Bayesian optimisation of a model calibration loss with a Gaussian process surrogate" ;
    hemo:9000014 "theta_star = argmin L(theta); theta_next = argmax EI_n(theta)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-BOPT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "bayesian-optimisation-of-a-model-calibration-loss-with-a-gaussian-process-surrogate" .

hemo:5000107 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Survival estimation from left-truncated records with entry-restricted risk sets"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-LTR-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps records with an entry time, an exit time and an event indicator to survival from the time origin when people come under observation only after the origin. Each person informs survival only beyond entry, conditional on surviving to entry, so risk sets and likelihood contributions start at entry. The notation follows the Left Truncation article and its six registry patients." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Survival estimation from left-truncated records with entry-restricted risk sets" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P(T > t | T > l) = S(t) / S(l); n_j = sum_i I(L_i < t_j <= X_i); S(t) = prod_(t_j <= t) (1 - d_j / n_j)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-LTR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "survival-estimation-from-left-truncated-records-with-entry-restricted-risk-sets" .

hemo:5000109 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Medicare physician fee schedule payment from relative value units and the conversion factor"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-CVF-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Turns the relative value units of a service, adjusted for local practice costs, into a dollar payment by multiplying by the conversion factor, and updates the factor each year by a chain of statutory and budget neutrality adjustments. Relative values set the ratios between services; the conversion factor sets the money level of all of them. Notation follows the Conversion Factor article, with its 2026 factors from the CMS final rule." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Medicare physician fee schedule payment from relative value units and the conversion factor" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Fee = (W * GPCI_W + PE * GPCI_PE + MP * GPCI_MP) * CF; CF_new = CF_prev * BN * (1 + u) * (1 + k)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-CVF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "medicare-physician-fee-schedule-payment-from-relative-value-units-and-the-conversion-factor" .

hemo:5000114 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Utility decrements, their QALY losses and the rules for combining them on a stated baseline"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-DISU-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Expresses a harm such as an adverse event, a second chronic condition or an unpleasant treatment as a fall in utility from a stated baseline, turns it into lost QALYs over the harm's duration, and estimates the utility of people with two conditions from single-condition data by the additive, multiplicative or minimum rule of NICE DSU TSD 12. QALYs from periods of constant utility are HE-FM-QALY-001 and the expected QALY loss from adverse events HE-FM-AER-006. Notation follows the Disutility article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Utility decrements, their QALY losses and the rules for combining them on a stated baseline" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d = U_0 - U_1; Q_loss = d * dur" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-DISU-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "utility-decrements-their-qaly-losses-and-the-rules-for-combining-them-on-a-stated-baseline" .

hemo:5000115 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Dose-response curves and the dose-dependent drug cost behind incremental cost per extra responder"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-DOSE-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Links the expected response to the dose through the sigmoid Emax curve and the dose to the drug cost actually paid, through whole single-use vials and relative dose intensity, so that doses can be compared by the incremental cost per extra unit of effect. The incremental ratio between adjacent doses is HE-FM-ICER-001 with the sequential rules of HE-FM-CEA-001 and HE-FM-DOM-002, and the average ratio against placebo HE-FM-ACER-002. Notation follows the Dose Response article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Dose-response curves and the dose-dependent drug cost behind incremental cost per extra responder" ;
    hemo:9000014 "E_D = E_0 + E_max * D^h / (ED_50^h + D^h)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-DOSE-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "dose-response-curves-and-the-dose-dependent-drug-cost-behind-incremental-cost-per-extra-responder" .

hemo:5000116 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Differential discounting of incremental costs and health effects in a cost-effectiveness ratio"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-DDIS-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Converts the yearly streams of incremental costs and incremental QALYs to present values at two different annual rates, a cost rate and a usually lower health rate, before dividing one by the other. The package covers the ratio for a programme with costs and QALYs in different years, the exact health rate implied by growth in the money value of health, the effective cost rate implied by a growing cost-effectiveness threshold, and the factor by which delay changes the ratio. Separate discounting of two streams in general is HE-FM-DR-003 on the discount rate page and the two-option ratio HE-FM-ICER-001. Notation follows the Differential Discounting article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Differential discounting of incremental costs and health effects in a cost-effectiveness ratio" ;
    hemo:9000014 "ICER = sum_(t=0)^T [dC_t * (1 + r_C)^(-t)] / sum_(t=0)^T [dQ_t * (1 + r_H)^(-t)]; 1 + r_H = (1 + r_C) / (1 + g_v)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-DDIS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "differential-discounting-of-incremental-costs-and-health-effects-in-a-cost-effectiveness-ratio" .

hemo:5000124 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Cure rate model survival and lifetime mean survival with a cure fraction"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-CRM-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a cure fraction, the expected survival of the matched general population and the survival of uncured patients to all-cause survival over time and to the lifetime mean survival and QALYs that drive an appraisal. Cured patients face background mortality only; uncured patients also carry an excess hazard from the disease. The mixture form weights the two groups directly, the non-mixture form bounds the excess cumulative hazard so that relative survival falls to the cure fraction, and mean survival is the cure-fraction weighted average of the two group means. Reused, not repeated here: the all-cause hazard as background plus excess hazard HE-FM-BTH-005, the continuous discount rate log(1 plus d) HE-FM-CONT-003, the per-cycle probability from a hazard HE-FM-TP-001 and the restricted mean from a Kaplan-Meier curve HE-FM-ADMC-003. Notation follows the Cure Rate Model article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cure rate model survival and lifetime mean survival with a cure fraction" ;
    hemo:9000014 "S(t) = S_star(t) * [pi + (1 - pi) * S_u(t)]; T_bar = pi * E_B + (1 - pi) * E_U" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-CRM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cure-rate-model-survival-and-lifetime-mean-survival-with-a-cure-fraction" .

hemo:5000125 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected value of an uncertain cost, health outcome or model output"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-EV-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the probability distribution of an uncertain quantity, such as a cost per patient, a QALY total or a model output that depends on uncertain parameters, to its probability-weighted mean, which carries the units of the quantity. The discrete form applied at chance nodes is HE-FM-CHN-001 on the chance node page, the Monte Carlo mean over probabilistic simulations is HE-FM-ENB-001 and the constant-hazard event probability used below is HE-FM-TP-001. The records here cover the cases in which the mean of a function differs from the function of the means: a product of correlated quantities, a curved output with an uncertain parameter, an event probability under a gamma-distributed hazard and the mean of a log-normal cost. Notation follows the Expected Value article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected value of an uncertain cost, health outcome or model output" ;
    hemo:9000014 "E[X] = sum_(k=1)^K p_k * x_k" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-EV-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-value-of-an-uncertain-cost-health-outcome-or-model-output" .

hemo:5000130 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Prevalence ratio function for cross-sectional comparisons and attributable cost inputs"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-PR-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000223,
        hemo:6000224,
        hemo:6000225 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000223,
        hemo:6000224 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000225 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the prevalence of a condition among exposed people and among unexposed people, measured at the same time, to their ratio, and carries that ratio into the prevalence odds ratio, a population attributable fraction of prevalent cases and subgroup prevalences for cost-of-illness and budget impact work. Applying the attributable fraction to aggregate annual costs is the top-down attributable burden HE-FM-COI-002, and the attack rate ratio HE-FM-ATR-003 has the same ratio form for new cases in an outbreak. Survey-weighted prevalence and the link between prevalence, incidence and duration are HE-FM-XSD-001 and HE-FM-XSD-002. Notation follows the Prevalence Ratio article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Prevalence ratio function for cross-sectional comparisons and attributable cost inputs" ;
    hemo:9000014 "f(P_1,P_0) = PR" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-PR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "prevalence-ratio-function-for-cross-sectional-comparisons-and-attributable-cost-inputs" .

hemo:5000133 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Discounting of yearly cost and QALY streams to present-value totals"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-DISC-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000235,
        hemo:6000236,
        hemo:6000237,
        hemo:6000238 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000235,
        hemo:6000236,
        hemo:6000237 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000238 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a stream of yearly costs or QALYs, an annual discount rate, a time horizon and a timing convention to one discounted total, by multiplying each year's amount by its discount factor and adding the results. Totals for costs and for QALYs are formed separately and then compared across options, for example as an incremental cost per QALY. The present value of a single amount, the general sum over a stream and separate rates for costs and health are on the Discount Rate page (HE-FM-DR-001 to HE-FM-DR-003), and unequal rates on the Differential Discounting page (HE-FN-DDIS-001). This package adds the totals of a level yearly stream counted at the end of each year, at the start of each year and continuously, and the discounted cost per QALY of a one-off treatment with a lasting gain, as in the article's worked example." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Discounting of yearly cost and QALY streams to present-value totals" ;
    hemo:9000014 "D(X_t, r, T) = PV" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-DISC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "discounting-of-yearly-cost-and-qaly-streams-to-present-value-totals" .

hemo:5000137 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected value of sample information function for a proposed study design"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-EVSI-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the current uncertainty about a decision model's parameters, the sampling distribution of the data a proposed study would collect and the net benefit of each option to the expected gain from choosing after the study reports rather than now. EVSI equals the expectation, over the datasets X the study could produce, of the largest posterior expected net benefit among the options, less the largest expected net benefit on current evidence, the form given as equation 10 of the second ISPOR value of information report and equation 2 of Kunst and colleagues. It replaces the perfect knowledge of a parameter subset in EVPPI (HE-FM-VOI-003) with the data of a study of stated design and size, so it is never negative and does not exceed EVPPI for the parameters the study informs or per-person EVPI (HE-FM-VOI-001). This package gives the exact form for a study with two possible results, the normal preposterior variance, closed-form EVSI for two options and population EVSI over the decision's lifetime on the basis used for population EVPI (HE-FM-VOI-002). The expected net benefit of sampling, population EVSI less the cost of the study, has its own package. Notation follows the Expected Value of Sample Information article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected value of sample information function for a proposed study design" ;
    hemo:9000014 "e(NB(d, theta), p(theta), p(X | theta)) = EVSI" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-EVSI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-value-of-sample-information-function-for-a-proposed-study-design" .

hemo:5000138 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Equity-weighted QALY gain, ICER, net health benefit and switching weight function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-EQWA-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000253,
        hemo:6000254,
        hemo:6000255,
        hemo:6000256,
        hemo:6000257,
        hemo:6000258,
        hemo:6000259,
        hemo:6000260 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000253,
        hemo:6000254,
        hemo:6000255,
        hemo:6000256 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000257,
        hemo:6000258,
        hemo:6000259,
        hemo:6000260 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the incremental QALY gain of a programme in each population group, an equity weight for each group, the incremental cost and the value placed on a QALY to the equity-weighted QALY gain, the equity-weighted ICER, the equity-weighted net health benefit and the weight at which the programme would break even. The weights act on the incremental QALYs only, while costs and the health displaced elsewhere stay unweighted, and with every weight equal to 1 the outputs reduce to the unweighted ICER (HE-FM-ICER-001) and incremental net health benefit (HE-FM-CET-002). The weights come from outside the function, for example the NICE severity weight from absolute and proportional shortfall (HE-FM-ASF-001 and HE-FM-ASF-003) or the weight implied by an Atkinson inequality aversion parameter (HE-FM-ATK-004), and neither is redrafted here. The break-even weight differs from the switching weight between two options in the weighted two-group social welfare formula of the Welfare Economics package, because it compares one programme with the health displaced by its cost. Notation follows the Equity-Weighted Analysis article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equity-weighted QALY gain, ICER, net health benefit and switching weight function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "e(w_g, Delta_E_g, Delta_C, lambda) = (Delta_E_w, ICER_w, NHB_w, w_star)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-EQWA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equity-weighted-qaly-gain-icer-net-health-benefit-and-switching-weight-function" .

hemo:5000141 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected value of perfect information function for a single adoption decision"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-EVPI-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000273,
        hemo:6000274,
        hemo:6000275 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000273,
        hemo:6000274 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000275 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the net benefit of every option in every probabilistic draw, at a stated cost-effectiveness threshold, to the expected net benefit forgone per person by choosing on current evidence rather than with all uncertainty removed. Its inputs are the draw by option table of net benefit NB_ds and the threshold lambda, and its output is EVPI, in QALYs when net health benefit is used and in currency when net monetary benefit is used. The mean of the maxima less the maximum of the means is live in the Value of Information package as HE-FM-VOI-001, with population scaling in HE-FM-VOI-002, the partial form in HE-FM-VOI-003 and the error probability in HE-FM-CEAC-003. This package adds the opportunity-loss form, the two-option form in health units, the split of EVPI into error probability and loss when wrong, and the closed form for a normally distributed incremental net benefit. Notation follows the Expected Value of Perfect Information article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected value of perfect information function for a single adoption decision" ;
    hemo:9000014 "evpi(NB_ds, lambda) = EVPI" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-EVPI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-value-of-perfect-information-function-for-a-single-adoption-decision" .

hemo:5000143 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Expected net benefit of sampling as population sample information less the cost of a study"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-ENBS-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps one proposed study design, through its population expected value of sample information and its expected total cost, to the expected net benefit of sampling, the net value of that study. Per-person EVPI and its population form are on the Value of Information page (HE-FM-VOI-001 and HE-FM-VOI-002), and the expected net benefit that fixes the current decision is on the Expected Net Benefit page (HE-FN-ENB-001). EVSI is defined on the EVSI page (HE-FN-EVSI-001), and population EVSI, per-person EVSI scaled by the discounted beneficiary population, is HE-FM-EVSI-004 there. This package adds ENBS for one design, the expected cost of a study with the opportunity loss to participants, the cost of waiting under only in research, the ceiling set by population EVPI and a search for the efficient sample size. Notation follows the Expected Net Benefit of Sampling article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Expected net benefit of sampling as population sample information less the cost of a study" ;
    hemo:9000014 "g(EVSI_pop(n), C(n)) = ENBS(n)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-ENBS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "expected-net-benefit-of-sampling-as-population-sample-information-less-the-cost-of-a-study" .

hemo:5000147 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Memoryless transition property of a Markov state and the sojourn-time distribution it implies"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-MKA-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the state a person occupies at cycle t, the cycle itself and the path H_t before it to the probability of each next state, under the restriction that the path adds nothing once the current state and cycle are known. With a constant probability p_ii of staying in state i the restriction makes the number of cycles spent in i on one visit geometric, and in continuous time a constant exit rate makes the time spent in i exponential, so in both cases the chance of leaving does not depend on the time already spent in the state. The package gives the geometric distribution of a stay with its median, the residual-stay identity behind memorylessness with a check of a survival curve for time since entry, the exponential residual stay and median, and the gap between the discrete and continuous mean stays at the same rate. Existing records are cited and not restated. The mean episode length 1 divided by 1 minus p_jj is HE-FM-MKCH-002, the cohort update is HE-FM-MM-001 with the trace totals of HE-FN-CSIM-001 and HE-FM-CSIM-001, rate and probability conversion is HE-FN-TP-001 with HE-FM-TP-001 to HE-FM-TP-004 and HE-CF-TP-001 and HE-CF-TP-002, exponential survival and the mean 1 divided by lambda are HE-FM-BTH-003 and HE-FM-CONT-002, the fundamental matrix is HE-FM-ABS-001 with its discounted form HE-FM-MKCH-001 and HE-CF-MKCH-001, the six-month root of an annual matrix is HE-FM-TMAT-001, and relaxing the assumption with tunnel states is HE-FN-TUN-001, HE-FM-TUN-001 to HE-FM-TUN-003 and HE-CF-TUN-001. Notation follows the Markov Assumption article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Memoryless transition property of a Markov state and the sojourn-time distribution it implies" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Pr(X_(t+1) = j | X_t = i, H_t) = p_ij(t)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-MKA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "memoryless-transition-property-of-a-markov-state-and-the-sojourn-time-distribution-it-implies" .

hemo:5000151 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Age-weighted healthy years lost under the GBD 1990 exponential age-weight function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-AGW-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000319,
        hemo:6000320,
        hemo:6000321,
        hemo:6000322,
        hemo:6000323,
        hemo:6000324,
        hemo:6000325,
        hemo:6000326,
        hemo:6000327,
        hemo:6000328 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000319,
        hemo:6000320,
        hemo:6000321,
        hemo:6000322,
        hemo:6000323,
        hemo:6000324,
        hemo:6000325,
        hemo:6000326 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000327,
        hemo:6000328 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps an age, or a span of ages from the age at death or onset a over L years, together with the age-weight constants C and beta, the modulation constant K, a discount rate r and, for a case of illness, a disability weight DW, to the weight on a year lived at that age and to the age-weighted, discounted healthy years lost over the span. With K = 0 the age weights drop out and the result is the continuously discounted years of HE-FM-YLL-003 for a death and HE-FM-YLD-002 for an incident case, the per-death and per-case inputs of HE-FN-YLL-001, HE-FN-YLD-001 and the DALY records HE-FN-DALY-001 and HE-FM-DALY-004. The package adds the weight function and its peak, the undiscounted age-weighted years lost, the age-weighted discounted years lost per death of the original DALY, and the same expression for years lived with disability. Notation follows the Age Weighting article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Age-weighted healthy years lost under the GBD 1990 exponential age-weight function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "g(x, a, L, r, K, C, beta, DW) = W, Y" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-AGW-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "age-weighted-healthy-years-lost-under-the-gbd-1990-exponential-age-weight-function" .

hemo:5000153 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Agent state update function of an agent-based model"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-ABM-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the state of agent i at step t, the set of agents in contact with it, the state of the environment, the intervention in force and a random input to the state of that agent at step t+1, the rule X_i(t+1) = F(X_i(t), N_i(t), E(t), A(t), U_i(t)) of the article. In an infection model the rule for a susceptible agent reduces to a per-step infection probability from its k infectious contacts, the complement of the binomial probability of no events (HE-FM-BINOM-002), followed by a comparison of the agent's own uniform random number with that probability. A per-contact probability built from a transmission rate uses the constant-rate conversion HE-FM-TP-001, and a probability observed over another interval is rescaled with HE-FM-TP-003; neither is restated here. Population outputs are averaged over independent runs, and the Monte Carlo standard error of such a mean is the standard deviation across runs divided by the square root of the number of runs, the form of HE-FM-ANTV-002 with one observation per run and the divisor of HE-FM-BSE-001. This package adds the number of runs needed for a target standard error of a mean and of a share of runs. Attack rates and the final size relation are in HE-FN-ATR-001 and HE-CF-ATR-001, the cohort simulation without interaction is HE-FN-CSIM-001, and expected onward infections and the reproduction number belong to the Basic Reproduction Number page. Notation follows the Agent-Based Model article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Agent state update function of an agent-based model" ;
    hemo:9000014 "F(X_i(t), N_i(t), E(t), A(t), U_i(t)) = X_i(t+1)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-ABM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "agent-state-update-function-of-an-agent-based-model" .

hemo:5000159 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Consumer price index from a fixed basket with Jevons elementary aggregates, chain-linking and average inflation rates"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-CPI-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000357,
        hemo:6000358,
        hemo:6000359,
        hemo:6000360,
        hemo:6000361,
        hemo:6000362,
        hemo:6000363,
        hemo:6000364,
        hemo:6000365,
        hemo:6000366,
        hemo:6000367,
        hemo:6000368,
        hemo:6000369,
        hemo:6000370 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000357,
        hemo:6000358,
        hemo:6000359,
        hemo:6000360,
        hemo:6000361,
        hemo:6000362,
        hemo:6000363,
        hemo:6000364,
        hemo:6000365,
        hemo:6000366 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000367,
        hemo:6000368,
        hemo:6000369,
        hemo:6000370 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the base-period and current prices of a fixed basket, with the quantities or expenditure of an earlier weight reference period, to a Lowe-type index level, and combines the matched prices of one elementary aggregate by the Jevons geometric mean. It also joins yearly links into a long series on the 2015 = 100 reference and maps two index values and the months between them to an average annual inflation rate. The ratio of two index values is the I_b / I_a input of the live price-year adjustment HE-FM-COST-002 under HE-FN-COST-001, which is not repeated here, and a ratio of the US CPI for all urban consumers enters the Medicaid additional rebate of HE-FM-340B-002. Currency conversion and purchasing power parities are left to the Basket of Countries page. Notation follows the Consumer Price Index article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Consumer price index from a fixed basket with Jevons elementary aggregates, chain-linking and average inflation rates" ;
    hemo:9000014 "cpi(p_0, p_t, q_b, V_b, I_Dec, L_Jan, L_t, I_1, I_2, n) = (I_0t, J, I_t, g)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-CPI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "consumer-price-index-from-a-fixed-basket-with-jevons-elementary-aggregates-chain-linking-and-average-inflation-rates" .

hemo:5000160 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "League-table allocation of a fixed budget across independent programmes ranked by ICER"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-CELT-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000371,
