        hemo:6000377,
        hemo:6000378 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Takes the incremental cost Delta_C_i and incremental health gain Delta_E_i of each of a set of independent programmes, each measured against its own comparator, and a fixed budget B. It returns the share of each programme funded when programmes are funded in ascending order of ICER until the budget is exhausted, the health the budget buys, the critical ratio of the last programme funded and, under the alternative fixed ratio rule, the budget that a stated threshold implies. Each programme's ratio is the two-option ICER of HE-FM-ICER-001, or the ratio against doing nothing of HE-FM-ACER-002 when doing nothing is the comparator, and is not restated here. A league table ranks independent programmes competing for one budget, while mutually exclusive options for the same patients are ranked on the cost-effectiveness frontier (HE-FN-CEF-001, HE-CF-CEF-001), and the table takes such a set at its sequential ratios along the frontier (HE-FM-CEA-001). Ties and cost-saving programmes follow the dominance rule HE-FM-DOM-001, the threshold as marginal spending over marginal health is HE-FM-CET-001, and the health a new programme displaces at a threshold, Delta_C divided by k, and its incremental net health benefit are HE-FM-CET-002. This package adds the funded share of each programme, the critical ratio, the fill for programmes that must be funded whole and the budget implied by a fixed ratio, with a computational function that fills the table for any number of programmes. Notation follows the Cost-Effectiveness League Table article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "League-table allocation of a fixed budget across independent programmes ranked by ICER" ;
    hemo:9000014 "L(Delta_C_i, Delta_E_i, B, lambda) = (x_i, H, lambda_c, B_lambda)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-CELT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "league-table-allocation-of-a-fixed-budget-across-independent-programmes-ranked-by-icer" .

hemo:5000170 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Hedges' g small-sample correction, sampling variance and bias of the standardised mean difference"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-HEDG-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000445,
        hemo:6000446,
        hemo:6000447,
        hemo:6000448,
        hemo:6000449,
        hemo:6000450 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000445,
        hemo:6000446,
        hemo:6000447 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000448,
        hemo:6000449,
        hemo:6000450 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps Cohen's d, the uncorrected standardised mean difference of HE-FM-CD-001, and the two group sizes to Hedges' g, the same difference multiplied by a correction factor J that removes the upward small-sample bias of d, and to the variance and standard error of g that an inverse-variance meta-analysis needs. It also gives the standard error that Review Manager attaches to the same statistic, and the expected overstatement of a population standardised difference by d at a given number of degrees of freedom. The pooled standard deviation and d itself are HE-FM-CD-001 and are not restated. A confidence interval for g is the large-sample interval HE-FM-AN-001 applied with the standard error of g, the logistic conversion to a log odds ratio is HE-FM-CD-002 with g in place of d, and pooling g across studies is HE-FM-ADMA-001 for a common effect or HE-FM-ADMA-005 for random effects. Notation follows the Hedges' g article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Hedges' g small-sample correction, sampling variance and bias of the standardised mean difference" ;
    hemo:9000014 "hedges(d, n_1, n_2) = (g, V_g, SE_g)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-HEDG-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "hedges-g-small-sample-correction-sampling-variance-and-bias-of-the-standardised-mean-difference" .

hemo:5000194 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "QALYs by linear interpolation between observations"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "This sum and HE-FM-QALY-001 both approximate the integral of utility over time, by linear and by constant interpolation, so neither approximates the other."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-QALY-002" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-QALY-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000012 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000183 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000568,
        hemo:6000569,
        hemo:6000570,
        hemo:6000571,
        hemo:6000572,
        hemo:6003730 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000568,
        hemo:6000569,
        hemo:6000570,
        hemo:6000571 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000572 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000180,
        hemo:7000181,
        hemo:7000182 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000184,
        hemo:7000185,
        hemo:7000186 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000113 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000094 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 rheo:0000091,
        rheo:0000092 ;
    hemo:9000003 "rheo 0.3.0 cq25-qalys-from-records.rq" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Estimates the area under the utility curve when utility is observed at time points and assumed to change in a straight line between them, the trapezoidal method used with repeated utility measurements in trials." ;
    hemo:9000009 "QALYs by linear interpolation between observations" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Drummond MF, Sculpher MJ, Claxton K, Stoddart GL, Torrance GW. Methods for the Economic Evaluation of Health Care Programmes. 4th ed. Oxford: Oxford University Press; 2015. https://global.oup.com/academic/product/methods-for-the-economic-evaluation-of-health-care-programmes-9780199665884",
        "Manca A, Hawkins N, Sculpher MJ. Estimating mean QALYs in trial-based cost-effectiveness analysis: the importance of controlling for baseline utility. Health Economics. 2005;14(5):487-496. https://doi.org/10.1002/hec.944",
        "Richardson G, Manca A. Calculation of quality adjusted life years in the published literature: a review of methodology and transparency. Health Economics. 2004;13(12):1203-1210. https://doi.org/10.1002/hec.901" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Ignoring baseline imbalance. Comparing unadjusted mean areas between trial arms lets chance differences in baseline utility bias incremental QALYs, which can give a misleading ICER.",
        "Interpolating silently across a missed visit. Joining the observations on either side of a missed visit applies the linear-change assumption over a longer interval. The rule is reported and its effect tested rather than left implicit." ;
    hemo:9000014 "QALY_AUC = sum_(t=1)^T [(u_start_t + u_end_t) / 2 * d_t]" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>QALY_AUC</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">sum</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"interval1\">integer_interval</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>T</ci></apply><bind><csymbol cd=\"fns1\">lambda</csymbol><bvar><ci>t</ci></bvar><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">plus</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"hemo1\" cdbase=\"https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/cd\">element</csymbol><ci>u_start_t</ci><ci>t</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"hemo1\" cdbase=\"https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/cd\">element</csymbol><ci>u_end_t</ci><ci>t</ci></apply></apply><cn type=\"integer\">2</cn></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"hemo1\" cdbase=\"https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/cd\">element</csymbol><ci>d_t</ci><ci>t</ci></apply></apply></bind></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{QALY}_{\\mathrm{AUC}} = \\sum_{t=1}^{T} \\left[\\frac{u_{\\mathrm{start},t}_{t} + u_{\\mathrm{end},t}_{t}}{2} \\, d_{t}_{t}\\right]" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-QALY-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "qalys-by-linear-interpolation-between-observations" .

hemo:5000202 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-option CEAC at one threshold"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CEAC-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000016 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000260,
        hemo:7000261 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000611,
        hemo:6000612,
        hemo:6000613,
        hemo:6000614,
        hemo:6000615 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000612,
        hemo:6000613,
        hemo:6000614,
        hemo:6000615 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000611 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000257,
        hemo:7000258,
        hemo:7000259 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000262,
        hemo:7000263,
        hemo:7000264 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Counts the probabilistic simulations in which the intervention has positive incremental net monetary benefit at threshold lambda and divides the count by the number of simulations. The indicator 1(condition) equals 1 when the condition holds and 0 otherwise. Apart from exact ties, the comparator's value is the complement." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-option CEAC at one threshold" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Fenwick E, O'Brien BJ, Briggs A. Cost-effectiveness acceptability curves: facts, fallacies and frequently asked questions. Health Economics. 2004;13(5):405-415. https://doi.org/10.1002/hec.903",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 31 March 2026. Chapter 4 Economic evaluation, section 4.7.15 (cost-effectiveness acceptability curves plot a range of maximum acceptable ICERs against the probability that the intervention is cost effective; the probability at £25,000 to £35,000 per QALY gained and the error probability are presented). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2",
        "van Hout BA, Al MJ, Gordon GS, Rutten FFH. Costs, effects and C/E-ratios alongside a clinical trial. Health Economics. 1994;3(5):309-319. https://doi.org/10.1002/hec.4730030505" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Classifying draws by ICER below the threshold. Counting a draw as cost-effective when its ratio lies below lambda misclassifies north-west draws, where a negative ratio means the intervention is dominated, and south-west draws, where the intervention is favoured only when the ratio exceeds lambda. In the first worked example the fifth draw, at £60,000 per QALY forgone, would be counted against the intervention and the CEAC would fall from 0.6 to 0.4.",
        "Reading the CEAC as a probability of clinical benefit. A CEAC of 0.6 is the share of simulations with positive incremental net monetary benefit at one threshold. It is not the probability that the intervention improves health or saves money, and it says nothing about the size of the gain or loss in either group of simulations.",
        "Treating the CEAC as a significance test. Requiring the curve to exceed 0.95 before adoption imposes a statistical convention on a decision problem. The adoption decision rests on expected net benefit, and the spread of net benefit bears on whether further evidence is worth acquiring." ;
    hemo:9000014 "CEAC = (1/N) * sum_(i=1)^N [1(lambda * Delta_E_i - Delta_C_i > 0)]" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CEAC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-option-ceac-at-one-threshold" .

hemo:5000208 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Population EVPI over the decision's lifetime"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "In the module T is the last year index, so the sum has T plus 1 terms. HEAO's hook writes the same sum from 0 to T minus 1, with T the number of years."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-VOI-002" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-VOI-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000018 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000305 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000639,
        hemo:6000640,
        hemo:6000641,
        hemo:6000642,
        hemo:6000643,
        hemo:6000644 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000640,
        hemo:6000641,
        hemo:6000642,
        hemo:6000644 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000639 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000303,
        hemo:7000304 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000306,
        hemo:7000307,
        hemo:7000314 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000197 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000180 ;
    hemo:1000034 hemo:4000013 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000078 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-20" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Multiplies per-person EVPI by the discounted number of people expected to face the decision in each period while it remains relevant. The result is compared with the cost of research: when it is below the cost of any feasible study, further research is not worthwhile on these grounds." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Population EVPI over the decision's lifetime" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Fenwick E, Steuten L, Knies S, Ghabri S, Basu A, Murray JF, Koffijberg HE, Strong M, Sanders Schmidler GD, Rothery C. Value of information analysis for research decisions: an introduction. Report 1 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(2):139-150. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2020.01.001",
        "Philips Z, Claxton K, Palmer S. The half-life of truth: what are appropriate time horizons for research decisions? Medical Decision Making. 2008;28(3):287-299. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X07312724" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Scaling by everyone with the condition indefinitely. Multiplying per-person EVPI by all current and future patients, with no time horizon or exclusion of those treated before evidence arrives, overstates the value of research." ;
    hemo:9000014 "EVPI_pop = EVPI * sum_(t=0)^T [N_t / (1+r)^t]" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>EVPI_pop</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><ci>EVPI</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">sum</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"interval1\">integer_interval</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">0</cn><ci>T</ci></apply><bind><csymbol cd=\"fns1\">lambda</csymbol><bvar><ci>t</ci></bvar><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"hemo1\" cdbase=\"https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/cd\">element</csymbol><ci>N_t</ci><ci>t</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">power</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">plus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>r</ci></apply><ci>t</ci></apply></apply></bind></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{EVPI}_{\\mathrm{pop}} = \\mathrm{EVPI} \\, \\sum_{t=0}^{T} \\left[\\frac{N_{t}_{t}}{\\left(1 + r\\right)^{t}}\\right]" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-VOI-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "population-evpi-over-the-decision-s-lifetime" .

hemo:5000238 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Absolute and proportional QALY shortfall"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-ASF-001" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ASF-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000029 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000529 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000755,
        hemo:6000756,
        hemo:6000757,
        hemo:6000758 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000757,
        hemo:6000758 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000755,
        hemo:6000756 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000526,
        hemo:7000527,
        hemo:7000528 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000530,
        hemo:7000531,
        hemo:7000536,
        hemo:7000541 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000338 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000324,
        hemo:8000330 ;
    hemo:1000031 hemo:5000240 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 oheo:0000270,
        oheo:0000271 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Absolute shortfall subtracts the expected remaining QALYs of people with the condition under current care from those of the matched general population. Proportional shortfall divides the same difference by the general-population QALYs. Both look forward from the point at which the technology would be used, so health already lost does not count, and both are discounted at the reference-case rate." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Absolute and proportional QALY shortfall" ;
    hemo:9000010 "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. Interim methods statement: implementing the EQ-5D-5L value set (PMG51). Published 27 August 2026. Statement section 1.12 (the EQ-5D-5L value set is used for general-population utilities, including absolute and proportional QALY shortfall), process considerations sections 1.17 and 1.18 (new topics only; ongoing topics continue with 3L values) and appendix C, questions 21 and 22 (5L utilities and 5L population norms for severity, with the table 6.1 cut-offs unchanged). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg51/chapter/statement-on-using-the-eq-5d-5l-value-set",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 13 October 2026. Chapter 6 Committee recommendations, sections 6.2.12 to 6.2.17 (severity as future health lost with standard care; absolute and proportional QALY shortfall definitions; general population with the same age and sex distribution; discounting at the reference-case rate). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2",
        "Wailoo A. NICE DSU Technical Support Document 23: A guide to calculating severity shortfall for NICE evaluations. Sheffield: Decision Support Unit, ScHARR, University of Sheffield; January 2024, updated September 2026. Section 2.1 (AS = Qh minus Qc and PS = (Qh minus Qc) / Qh, forward-looking definition and ranges), section 4.4 (carer quality of life excluded), sections 3.6 and 4.1 (3.5% discount rate, even where another rate is accepted for the cost-effectiveness analysis), section 5 worked examples with EQ-5D-3L and EQ-5D-5L, and appendix 1 step iv (weighting by sex). https://doi.org/10.15131/shef.data.33850720" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Matching on age but not on sex. Life expectancy and utility norms differ by sex, so the DSU guide computes Q_h separately for males and females and weights them by the proportion female in the patient population. A single-sex or whole-population norm misstates Q_h and both shortfall measures.",
        "Undiscounted or non-reference-case discounting. Shortfall calculated from undiscounted QALYs, or at a discount rate other than 3.5%, is on a different scale from the cut-offs of 12 and 18 QALYs and can move a condition into a higher band." ;
    hemo:9000014 "AS = Q_h - Q_c; PS = (Q_h - Q_c) / Q_h" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"logic1\">and</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>AS</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><ci>Q_h</ci><ci>Q_c</ci></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>PS</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><ci>Q_h</ci><ci>Q_c</ci></apply><ci>Q_h</ci></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{AS} = Q_{h} - Q_{c} ;\\quad \\mathrm{PS} = \\frac{Q_{h} - Q_{c}}{Q_{h}}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ASF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "absolute-and-proportional-qaly-shortfall" .

hemo:5000246 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Absence cost by the human capital approach"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "Days are converted to weeks by the constant 7, the number of days in a week, the kind of number HEMO allows."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-ABSN-002" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ABSN-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000032 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000618 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000787,
        hemo:6000788,
        hemo:6000789,
        hemo:6000790 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000788,
        hemo:6000789,
        hemo:6000790 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000787 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000616,
        hemo:7000617 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000619,
        hemo:7000620 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000423 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000413 ;
    hemo:1000026 hemo:5000247 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000005,
        heao:0000023 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-16" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Values every hour of absence at the value of an hour of work. D_a counts calendar days absent, so D_a divided by 7 gives weeks absent, and multiplying by the usual weekly hours and the hourly value gives the cost. The human capital approach usually values time at gross earnings including employer-paid costs such as payroll taxes and benefits, the full cost of employee compensation." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Absence cost by the human capital approach" ;
    hemo:9000010 "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 31 March 2026. Chapter 4 Economic evaluation, sections 4.2.7 and 4.2.9 (NHS and PSS perspective on costs; productivity costs should not be included) and 4.4.23 (productivity costs excluded from the reference case, may be presented separately when a critical component of the value of the technology). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2",
        "Pike J, Grosse SD. Friction cost estimates of productivity costs in cost-of-illness studies in comparison with human capital estimates: a review. Applied Health Economics and Health Policy. 2018;16(6):765-778. Section II, human capital approach (value of lost productive time; standard practice to use gross earnings including payroll taxes and employer-paid benefits, the full cost of employee compensation). https://doi.org/10.1007/s40258-018-0416-4",
        "Tan-Torres Edejer T, Baltussen R, Adam T, Hutubessy R, Acharya A, Evans DB, Murray CJL, editors. Making choices in health: WHO guide to cost-effectiveness analysis. Geneva: World Health Organization; 2003. Section 3.2.2 Transfer payments (sickness benefits signify a change in command over resources, not a change in the aggregate value of resources, and are generally excluded). https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/42699" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Including productivity costs in the NICE reference case. The NICE reference case takes an NHS and personal social services perspective on costs and excludes productivity costs. They can be shown separately as additional information when they may be a critical part of the value of the technology.",
        "Valuing absence by sick pay or benefits. Sick pay and sickness benefits are transfer payments: they change who bears the loss of production, not its size, and are left out of the productivity cost to society." ;
    hemo:9000014 "C_HC = D_a / 7 * H * w" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>C_HC</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><ci>D_a</ci><cn type=\"integer\">7</cn></apply><ci>H</ci><ci>w</ci></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "C_{\\mathrm{HC}} = \\frac{D_{a}}{7} \\, H \\, w" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ABSN-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "absence-cost-by-the-human-capital-approach" .

hemo:5000247 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Absence cost by the friction cost method"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "Koopmanschap et al. 1995; the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-ABSN-003" ;
