    hemo:9000001 heao:0000024 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-16" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Counts absence only up to the friction period, the time an employer needs to restore production, usually by replacing the worker, and multiplies the counted hours by the value of an hour and by the elasticity of production with respect to working time. Absences shorter than the friction period count in full. When the hourly value already allows for the elasticity, as the Dutch reference price does, e is set to 1." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Absence cost by the friction cost method" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Hakkaart-van Roijen L, Peeters S, Kanters T. Costing manual: methods and reference prices for economic evaluations in healthcare, 2024 version. Diemen: Zorginstituut Nederland; 2024. Section 6.1.2.1 (friction cost method recommended; friction period as average vacancy term plus 4 weeks; 115 calendar days, 16.4 weeks, for 2018 to 2022; table 6-2 productivity costs of 39.88 euros per hour in 2022, labour costs including an adjustment for the elasticity of labour; example 9). https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/module-costing-manual.pdf",
        "Koopmanschap MA, Rutten FFH, van Ineveld BM, van Roijen L. The friction cost method for measuring indirect costs of disease. Journal of Health Economics. 1995;14(2):171-189. Original statement of the friction cost method, limiting production losses to the period needed to restore production; its estimates for the Netherlands were considerably lower than human capital estimates. https://doi.org/10.1016/0167-6296(94)00044-5",
        "Pike J, Grosse SD. Friction cost estimates of productivity costs in cost-of-illness studies in comparison with human capital estimates: a review. Applied Health Economics and Health Policy. 2018;16(6):765-778. Section II, friction cost approach (production lost during the friction period often assumed to be 80% of gross production because of the elasticity of production with respect to working time, citing Koopmanschap and colleagues; information needed on the start and length of the friction period; chains of vacancies). https://doi.org/10.1007/s40258-018-0416-4" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Applying the elasticity twice. The Dutch reference price of 39.88 euros an hour is based on labour costs that already include an adjustment for the elasticity of labour. Multiplying it by a further 0.8 understates the friction cost by a fifth.",
        "Comparing costs across valuation methods. Human capital and friction cost estimates of the same absence can differ several-fold, so productivity costs from studies using different methods, friction periods or hourly values are not directly comparable.",
        "Counting the friction period from the survey date. Capping the absence at the friction period from the start of the recall period, not the start of the absence, counts days already beyond the cap for workers whose absence began earlier." ;
    hemo:9000014 "C_FC = min(D_a, D_F) / 7 * H * w * e" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>C_FC</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"minmax1\">min</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"set1\">set</csymbol><ci>D_a</ci><ci>D_F</ci></apply></apply><cn type=\"integer\">7</cn></apply><ci>H</ci><ci>w</ci><ci>e</ci></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "C_{\\mathrm{FC}} = \\frac{\\min\\left(D_{a}, D_{F}\\right)}{7} \\, H \\, w \\, e" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ABSN-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "absence-cost-by-the-friction-cost-method" .

hemo:5000370 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Concentration index of health for grouped data"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-HINQ-005" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000064 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7001495 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6001345,
        hemo:6001346,
        hemo:6001347 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6001346,
        hemo:6001347 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6001345 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7001492,
        hemo:7001493,
        hemo:7001494 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7001496,
        hemo:7001497,
        hemo:7001498,
        hemo:7001499,
        hemo:7001500 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives the concentration index from the covariance between health and fractional rank in the socioeconomic distribution. For grouped data the fractional rank of each group is the cumulative population share up to the midpoint of the group, and the mean and covariance are weighted by population share, so cov_hr is the same covariance as in the slope index. The index is positive when health is concentrated among the better-off and negative when it is concentrated among the worse-off." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Concentration index of health for grouped data" ;
    hemo:9000010 "O'Donnell O, van Doorslaer E, Wagstaff A, Lindelow M. Analyzing health equity using household survey data: a guide to techniques and their implementation. Washington, DC: World Bank; 2008. Chapter 8, The concentration index. Equation 8.3 (covariance form), equation 8.5 (fractional rank of a group at the midpoint of its interval), equation 8.4 (Fuller and Lury formula for grouped data), equations 8.10 to 8.12 (index from the slope of health on rank), and pages 96 to 97 on bounds, scale properties and the crossing curve. https://www.worldbank.org/content/dam/Worldbank/document/HDN/Health/HealthEquityCh8.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Reading a smaller concentration index as narrower absolute health inequality. The concentration index is a relative measure. Under Programme A it falls from 0.0294 to 0.0290 while the absolute gap stays at 10 years and the slope index at 12.5 years, because adding the same amount to everyone changes the index without changing any absolute difference.",
        "Treating a zero concentration index as no socioeconomic health inequality. An index of zero can arise because the concentration curve lies on the line of equality or because it crosses the line and the areas on each side cancel out. The curve is examined alongside the index before concluding that there is no socioeconomic-related inequality." ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = 2 * cov_hr / mu" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-HINQ-005" ;
    hemo:9000023 "concentration-index-of-health-for-grouped-data" .

hemo:5000474 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Cycle transition probability from the rise in cumulative hazard across a cycle"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CUMH-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000092 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7002277 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6001831,
        hemo:6001832,
        hemo:6001833 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6001832,
        hemo:6001833 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6001831 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7002274,
        hemo:7002275,
        hemo:7002276 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7002278,
        hemo:7002279,
        hemo:7002280,
        hemo:7002281,
        hemo:7002282 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives the probability that a person event free at the start of a model cycle has the event by its end, from the cumulative hazard at the end of the cycle and at its start. Because survival is exp(minus H), the ratio S(t) / S(t minus Delta) is exp of minus the rise in H, so the conversion is exact for any hazard shape and the cohort trace reproduces the fitted curve. Time t runs from the origin of the fitted curve, so in a cohort model it is the time since model start at the cycle's end. With a constant hazard it reduces to HE-FM-TP-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cycle transition probability from the rise in cumulative hazard across a cycle" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Alarid-Escudero F, Krijkamp E, Enns EA, Yang A, Hunink MGM, Pechlivanoglou P, Jalal H. A tutorial on time-dependent cohort state-transition models in R using a cost-effectiveness analysis example. Medical Decision Making. 2023;43(1):21-41. Section 4.2, equations 2 to 6: the rate in cycle tau is the difference in cumulative hazards between consecutive cycles, H(tau) minus H(tau minus 1), and the cycle-specific transition probability is 1 minus exp of minus that rate; substituting a Weibull cumulative hazard gives the tunnel-state probabilities. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X221121747",
        "Latimer NR. NICE DSU Technical Support Document 14: Survival analysis for economic evaluations alongside clinical trials, extrapolation with patient-level data. Sheffield: Decision Support Unit, ScHARR, University of Sheffield; June 2011, last updated March 2013. Section 2.9: only the hazard ratio from the chosen parametric model, fitted with treatment as a covariate, should be applied to the control curve from that model, not one from a different parametric model or a Cox model. https://doi.org/10.15131/shef.data.33745843" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Using cumulative event probability as a per-cycle probability. Taking 1 minus S(t) as the probability for the cycle ending at t applies the risk accumulated since time zero to one cycle: 0.2464 in year 2 instead of 0.1671 in the article's example.",
        "Using cumulative hazard increments as cycle probabilities. Treating each rise in H as the cycle probability overstates deaths in every cycle: in the article's example, survival at year 6 falls to 0.1786 instead of the fitted 0.2300. The gap grows with cycle length and risk." ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_t = 1 - exp(-(H_t - H_prev))" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CUMH-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cycle-transition-probability-from-the-rise-in-cumulative-hazard-across-a-cycle" .

hemo:5000503 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Years lived with disability from prevalent cases and a disability weight"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "The prevalence-based years lived with disability of the Global Burden of Disease study from 2010 onwards. P is the person-time lived with the sequela, equal to the prevalent cases over a one-year period."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-DWT-001" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-DWT-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000099 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7002502 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6001977,
        hemo:6001978,
        hemo:6001979 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6001978,
        hemo:6001979 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6001977 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7002500,
        hemo:7002501 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7002503,
        hemo:7002504,
        hemo:7002505,
        hemo:7002506 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8001576 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8001570 ;
    hemo:1000031 hemo:5000643 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000002,
        heao:0000038 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-05" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Multiplies the number of people living with a sequela over one year by its disability weight, the GBD 2010 prevalence method, without age weighting or discounting. A weight of 0.05 means that 20 years lived in the state count as one year of healthy life lost. YLDs for a disease are the sum over its sequelae." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Years lived with disability from prevalent cases and a disability weight" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Salomon JA, Haagsma JA, Davis A, et al. Disability weights for the Global Burden of Disease 2013 study. The Lancet Global Health. 2015;3(11):e712-e723. doi:10.1016/S2214-109X(15)00069-8 (abstract read). Results: weights common to GBD 2010 and GBD 2013 were highly correlated (r 0.992), but revised health state descriptions changed some weights substantially, including complete hearing loss, 0.033 in GBD 2010 and 0.215 in GBD 2013. https://doi.org/10.1016/S2214-109X(15)00069-8",
        "Vos T, Flaxman AD, Naghavi M, et al. Years lived with disability (YLDs) for 1160 sequelae of 289 diseases and injuries 1990-2010: a systematic analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study 2010. The Lancet. 2012;380(9859):2163-2196. doi:10.1016/S0140-6736(12)61729-2. Methods: YLDs are computed as the prevalence of a sequela multiplied by the disability weight for that sequela without age weighting or discounting; YLDs for a disease or injury are the sum of the YLDs for its sequelae. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6350784/",
        "World Health Organization. WHO methods and data sources for global burden of disease estimates 2000-2019. Global Health Estimates Technical Paper WHO/DDI/DNA/GHE/2020.3. Geneva: WHO; 2020. Section 2: age weighting and time discounting dropped and YLDs calculated from prevalence rather than incidence, adjusted for independent comorbidity; equation 2, YLD_i = DW_i x p_i. https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/gho-documents/global-health-estimates/ghe2019_daly-methods.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Entering 1 minus a utility value as the disability weight. Disability weights and QALY utility weights come from different tasks and constructs, so 1 minus a utility value is not a disability weight, and the reverse conversion is not a QALY weight.",
        "Mixing weight sets. GBD 2010, GBD 2013 and WHO revisions differ for some states; complete hearing loss moves from 0.033 to 0.215, so a study must name its set." ;
    hemo:9000014 "YLD = P * DW" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>YLD</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><ci>P</ci><ci>DW</ci></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{YLD} = P \\, \\mathrm{DW}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-DWT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "years-lived-with-disability-from-prevalent-cases-and-a-disability-weight" .

hemo:5000581 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Incidence-based years lived with disability for one sequela without discounting"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "The incidence-based years lived with disability without discounting, the convention of the Global Burden of Disease study of 1990. The DALY of HEMO-HOOK-05 uses the prevalence-based form HE-FM-DWT-001."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-YLD-001" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-YLD-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000120 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003098 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002413,
        hemo:6002414,
        hemo:6002415,
        hemo:6002416 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002414,
        hemo:6002415,
        hemo:6002416 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002413 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003096,
        hemo:7003097 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003099,
        hemo:7003100,
        hemo:7003101,
        hemo:7003106 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000528 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000520 ;
    hemo:1000031 hemo:5000643 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000038 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Counts the new cases of a sequela in the reference year and multiplies them by the disability weight and by the average duration of a case until remission or death, so all of each case's future health loss is assigned to the year, and the age, in which it begins. This is the form used by the GBD 1990 study and WHO's updates before 2010, here without their discounting and age weighting." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Incidence-based years lived with disability for one sequela without discounting" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Murray CJL. Quantifying the burden of disease: the technical basis for disability-adjusted life years. Bulletin of the World Health Organization. 1994;72(3):429-445 (abstract read). Abstract: sets out the technical basis of the disability-adjusted life year, with a three per cent discount rate and an exponential age-weighting function. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2486718/",
        "World Health Organization. WHO methods and data sources for global burden of disease estimates 2000-2019. Global Health Estimates Technical Paper WHO/DDI/DNA/GHE/2020.3. Geneva: WHO; 2020 (full text read). Section 2.4: the incidence-based approach will not reflect the current prevalent burden of a condition for which incidence has been substantially reduced, needs estimates of both incidence and duration, and assigns all YLDs to the ages at onset; both GBD 2010 and WHO switched to a prevalence-based approach; YLDs for congenital hearing loss fall at age 0 in an incidence perspective. https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/gho-documents/global-health-estimates/ghe2019_daly-methods.pdf",
        "World Health Organization. WHO methods and data sources for global burden of disease estimates 2000-2019. Global Health Estimates Technical Paper WHO/DDI/DNA/GHE/2020.3. Geneva: WHO; 2020 (full text read). Section 2: the GBD 1990 and subsequent WHO updates used an incidence perspective; the number of incident cases is multiplied by the average duration of the disease and a weight factor from 0 (perfect health) to 1 (dead), YLD = I x DW x L, with L the average duration of the case until remission or death. https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/gho-documents/global-health-estimates/ghe2019_daly-methods.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Comparing an incidence-based YLD with a figure from GBD 2010 onwards. GBD 2010 and WHO switched to prevalence-based YLDs, so an incidence-based figure counts a different quantity; the two agree only when incidence and duration have been stable for a long time.",
        "Placing the health loss at the age of onset when ages of experience matter. In an incidence perspective all YLDs for congenital hearing loss fall at age 0, whereas in a prevalence perspective they are spread fairly evenly across age groups, which suits what WHO notes policy-makers are often more interested in, the ages at which health loss is experienced.",
        "Reading the fall in an incidence-based count as a fall in current burden. WHO lists as the first drawback of the incidence approach that it does not reflect the current prevalent burden of a condition whose incidence has fallen substantially; in the article's example the count halves at once while people with earlier onset still live with the condition." ;
    hemo:9000014 "YLD_inc = I * DW * L" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>YLD_inc</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><ci>I</ci><ci>DW</ci><ci>L</ci></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{YLD}_{\\mathrm{inc}} = I \\, \\mathrm{DW} \\, L" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-YLD-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "incidence-based-years-lived-with-disability-for-one-sequela-without-discounting" .

hemo:5000630 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Simple hyperbolic discount factor at a delay of t periods"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "Frederick et al. 2002 is the source HEAO gives for this form. Its footnote 13, on page 360, names Herrnstein (1981) and Mazur (1987) for D(t) = 1/(1 + alpha t), as reviewer C read in the published article on 19 October 2026."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-HYPD-001" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-HYPD-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000136 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003551 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002676,
        hemo:6002677,
        hemo:6002678 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002677,
        hemo:6002678 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002676 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003548,
        hemo:7003549,
        hemo:7003550 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003552,
        hemo:7003553,
        hemo:7003554,
        hemo:7003558,
        hemo:7003562,
        hemo:7003566 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000674 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000664 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000028,
        heao:0000044 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-02" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives the weight on an outcome t periods ahead under the simple hyperbola that Frederick, Loewenstein and O'Donoghue attribute to Herrnstein and to Mazur. The weight is 1 for an outcome now and falls as the delay grows, but more slowly at long delays than the constant-rate factor 1/(1 + r)^t of HE-FM-DR-001, so distant outcomes keep more weight. The parameter alpha sets how quickly the rate of impatience declines, and the per-period rate it implies is in HE-FM-HYPD-002." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Simple hyperbolic discount factor at a delay of t periods" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Attema AE, Brouwer WBF, Claxton K. Discounting in economic evaluations. PharmacoEconomics. 2018;36(7) 745-758. Section on discount models, which gives the constant model with the factor 1/(1 + D)^t, describes hyperbolic discounting as explaining individual behaviour better but leading to inconsistencies over time, and states that almost all guidelines prescribe constant discount rates. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40273-018-0672-z",
        "Frederick S, Loewenstein G, O'Donoghue T. Time discounting and time preference: a critical review. Journal of Economic Literature. 2002;40(2) 351-401. Section 4.1 on hyperbolic discounting, page 360 and footnote 13, which lists the hyperbolic forms proposed, including D(t) = 1/(1 + alpha t) suggested by Herrnstein (1981) and Mazur (1987), and states that a hyperbolic form fitted to such data fits better than the exponential form. https://doi.org/10.1257/002205102320161311",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance, the manual (PMG36). Chapter 4, economic evaluation. Published 31 January 2022, last updated 13 October 2026. Section 4.5.1, for the reference case costs and health effects should be discounted at the same rate of 3.5% per year. https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Hyperbolic parameter applied as a compounding rate. Writing 1/(1 + alpha)^t in place of 1/(1 + alpha t) turns the hyperbola into the constant-rate factor. With alpha = 0.4 the weight two periods ahead becomes 0.5102 instead of 0.5556 (computed here for illustration), and the declining rate that produces preference reversals disappears.",
        "Monthly hyperbolic parameter used with a delay in years. With alpha = 0.4 a month an outcome one year ahead has a weight of 1 divided by 5.8, about 0.172. Entering t = 1 with the monthly alpha gives 0.714, more than four times too high (computed here for illustration)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "D_H = 1 / (1 + alpha * t)" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>D_H</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">plus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><ci>alpha</ci><ci>t</ci></apply></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "D_{H} = \\frac{1}{1 + \\alpha \\, t}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-HYPD-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "simple-hyperbolic-discount-factor-at-a-delay-of-t-periods" .

hemo:5000637 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Closed-form EVSI for two options with normal incremental net benefit"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "HEMO computes the upper-tail probability Q_z from z with the standard normal distribution function and writes the constant pi, where the module takes Q_z as an input and writes pi as 3.14159265, so that a test cannot give a Q_z that does not match z."@en,
        "The general EVSI is an expectation over the study data of a maximum of posterior expectations, which the 0.1 grammar does not evaluate. This closed form for two options with normal incremental net benefit is the formalised member of the family, so HEMO-HOOK-19 is stated in part."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-EVSI-003" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EVSI-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000137 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003621 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002718,
        hemo:6002719,
        hemo:6002720,
        hemo:6002721,
        hemo:6002722,
        hemo:6002723,
        hemo:6002724 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002719,
        hemo:6002720 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002718 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003619,
        hemo:7003620 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003622,
        hemo:7003623,
        hemo:7003624,
        hemo:7003632,
        hemo:7003640 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000722 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000683,
        hemo:8000689,
        hemo:8000696,
        hemo:8000712,
        hemo:8001581 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000077 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-19" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Applies the unit normal loss integral to the preposterior distribution of incremental net benefit, which is normal with the prior mean m0 and the preposterior variance s2 of HE-FM-EVSI-002. EVSI is the expected gain from switching away from the option preferred now in the results that reverse the sign of the posterior mean, s times phi_z less the absolute value of m0 times Q_z, where z is the absolute prior mean in preposterior standard deviations. phi_z is the standard normal density written out with exp and sqrt, and Q_z is the standard normal upper-tail probability at z, entered from Excel's 1-NORM.S.DIST(z,TRUE) because the calculator has no normal distribution function. With s2 equal to the prior variance the same expression gives per-person EVPI under normality, the bound in HE-FM-VOI-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Closed-form EVSI for two options with normal incremental net benefit" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Heath A, Kunst NR, Jackson C, Strong M, Alarid-Escudero F, Goldhaber-Fiebert JD, Baio G, Menzies NA, Jalal H. Calculating the expected value of sample information in practice. Considerations from 3 case studies. Medical Decision Making. 2020;40(3):314-326. The results for the chronic pain case study and Figure 3 report EVSI estimates from all four methods increasing with the sample size of the future trial and remaining below EVPPI, which marks the upper limit. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X20912402",
        "Jalal H, Goldhaber-Fiebert JD, Kuntz KM. Computing expected value of partial sample information from probabilistic sensitivity analysis using linear regression metamodeling. Medical Decision Making. 2015;35(5):584-595. Equation 2 gives the unit normal loss integral for normal incremental net benefit with mean mu and variance sigma squared as mu times Phi(minus mu over sigma) less sigma over the square root of 2 pi times exp(minus mu squared over 2 sigma squared), with mu the incremental net benefit of the option chosen now, and Box 2 computes EVSI as minus that integral at the prior mean and the preposterior variance. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X15578125",
        "Lee TY, Gustafson P, Sadatsafavi M. Closed-form solution of the unit normal loss integral in 2 dimensions, with application in value-of-information analysis. Medical Decision Making. 2023;43(5):621-626. The introduction defines the unit normal loss integral as the expectation of max(Y, 0) for normal Y with mean mu and standard deviation sigma, equal to mu times 1 minus Phi(minus mu over sigma) plus sigma times phi(minus mu over sigma). It states that the closed form applies to comparisons of 2 strategies on incremental net benefit and has been extended to EVSI (Willan and Pinto 2005), and the note derives a 2-dimensional form for 3 strategies. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X231171166" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Cumulative normal probability used in place of the upper-tail probability. Entering Phi(z), about 0.807 for 100 per arm, as Q_z gives about minus GBP 245 per patient, a negative EVSI that cannot occur.",
        "Prior variance used in place of the preposterior variance in the closed form. Putting the prior variance of 1,000,000 into the formula returns per-person EVPI, about GBP 197.80, whatever the trial size, so a trial of 100 per arm is credited with more than three times its EVSI of about GBP 61.68." ;
    hemo:9000014 "s = sqrt(s2); z = abs(m0) / s; phi_z = exp(-z * z / 2) / sqrt(2 * pi); Q_z = 1 - Phi(z); EVSI = s * (phi_z - z * Q_z)" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"logic1\">and</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>s</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">root</csymbol><ci>s2</ci><cn type=\"integer\">2</cn></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>z</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">abs</csymbol><ci>m0</ci></apply><ci>s</ci></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>phi_z</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"transc1\">exp</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">unary_minus</csymbol><ci>z</ci></apply><ci>z</ci></apply><cn type=\"integer\">2</cn></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">root</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">2</cn><csymbol cd=\"nums1\">pi</csymbol></apply><cn type=\"integer\">2</cn></apply></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>Q_z</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><apply><csymbol cd=\"hemo1\" cdbase=\"https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/cd\">normal_cdf</csymbol><ci>z</ci></apply></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>EVSI</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><ci>s</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><ci>phi_z</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><ci>z</ci><ci>Q_z</ci></apply></apply></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "s = \\sqrt{\\mathrm{s2}} ;\\quad z = \\frac{\\left|\\mathrm{m0}\\right|}{s} ;\\quad \\phi_{z} = \\frac{\\exp\\left(\\frac{-z \\, z}{2}\\right)}{\\sqrt{2 \\, \\pi}} ;\\quad Q_{z} = 1 - \\Phi\\left(z\\right) ;\\quad \\mathrm{EVSI} = s \\, \\left(\\phi_{z} - z \\, Q_{z}\\right)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EVSI-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "closed-form-evsi-for-two-options-with-normal-incremental-net-benefit" ;
    hemo:9000030 "s = sqrt(s2); z = abs(m0) / s; phi_z = exp(-z * z / 2) / sqrt(2 * 3.14159265); EVSI = s * (phi_z - z * Q_z)" .

