    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000013,
        heao:0000073 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-13" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Multiplies the incremental QALY gain in each group by that group's equity weight to give the group's equity-weighted value, and adds these values over the groups that the programme affects. The calculator form is written for two groups, V_1 and V_2, and each further group adds one more term w_g times Delta_E_g to the sum. A weight above 1 counts each QALY gained in a priority group as more than one QALY, and a weight of 1 leaves a group's gain unchanged. The result replaces the unweighted QALY gain in the ICER and in net health benefit, so the comparison with costs follows the usual rules once the weights are fixed." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Equity-weighted QALY gain summed over population groups" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Cookson R, Mirelman AJ, Griffin S, Asaria M, Dawkins B, Norheim OF, Verguet S, Culyer AJ. Using cost-effectiveness analysis to address health equity concerns. Value in Health. 2017;20(2):206-212. Section on equity-weighting analysis, which applies equity weights to the health benefits of people with different characteristics, or an equity parameter that implies such weights given the existing distribution of health, and notes that many proposed weighting systems rest on current severity of illness or lifetime health. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2016.11.027",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 13 October 2026. Chapter 6 Committee recommendations, section 6.2.11 (modifiers are factors that go beyond QALYs, taken into account qualitatively or through QALY weighting), section 6.2.12 (unmet need reflected within the severity definition), sections 6.2.16 and 6.2.18 with table 6.1 (greater weight to QALYs for severe conditions, weights of 1, 1.2 and 1.7). Chapter 4, section 4.12.7 (results of a distributional analysis not weighted by social characteristics). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations",
        "Wailoo A, Bell Gorrod H, Dunning L, Kenny J, Leckenby E, Shah K. The NICE experience of designing and utilising severity weights. Health Policy OPEN. 2026;10:100156. Section 1 Background, which states that the severity weights are applied to the health benefits, the incremental QALYs, estimated in the cost-effectiveness assessment, and that the definition giving the highest weight is used. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.hpopen.2025.100156",
        "Wailoo A, Tsuchiya A, McCabe C. Weighting must wait: incorporating equity concerns into cost-effectiveness analysis may take longer than expected. PharmacoEconomics. 2009;27(12):983-989. Abstract, which states that equity weights have been assumed to enter as a separate simple calculation after estimating cost per unweighted QALY, and that in many situations such simple approaches will not reflect the preferences on which the weights are based. https://doi.org/10.2165/11314100-000000000-00000" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Averaging per-patient weighted gains across groups of unequal size. Averaging the per-patient weighted gains of 1.02 and 0.8 QALYs and multiplying by 1,000 patients gives 910 weighted QALYs instead of 866. Subgroup A is only 30% of the patients, so each group's weighted gain has to be multiplied by its own number of patients before the groups are added.",
        "Weighting severity and unmet need as two separate concerns. PMG36 states that the extent of unmet health need is reflected within the severity definition (section 6.2.12). A weight of 1.7 for severity combined with a further 1.2 for unmet need multiplies the gain by 2.04 and counts the same shortfall twice." ;
    hemo:9000014 "V_1 = w_1 * Delta_E_1; V_2 = w_2 * Delta_E_2; Delta_E_w = V_1 + V_2" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"logic1\">and</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>V_1</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><ci>w_1</ci><ci>Delta_E_1</ci></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>V_2</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><ci>w_2</ci><ci>Delta_E_2</ci></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>Delta_E_w</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">plus</csymbol><ci>V_1</ci><ci>V_2</ci></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "V_{1} = w_{1} \\, \\Delta_{E,1} ;\\quad V_{2} = w_{2} \\, \\Delta_{E,2} ;\\quad \\Delta_{E,w} = V_{1} + V_{2}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EQWA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "equity-weighted-qaly-gain-summed-over-population-groups" .

hemo:5000649 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "UK MVH time trade-off EQ-5D-3L tariff from five reported levels"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EQ3L-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000140 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003843 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002792,
        hemo:6002793,
        hemo:6002794,
        hemo:6002795,
        hemo:6002796,
        hemo:6002797,
        hemo:6002798,
        hemo:6002799 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002793,
        hemo:6002794,
        hemo:6002795,
        hemo:6002796,
        hemo:6002797 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002792 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003840,
        hemo:7003841,
        hemo:7003842 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003844,
        hemo:7003845,
        hemo:7003846,
        hemo:7003847,
        hemo:7003848,
        hemo:7003849,
        hemo:7003850 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Scores a profile with the coefficients of Dolan's UK time trade-off tariff, the constant 0.081, the level 2 and level 3 decrements of each dimension and the N3 decrement 0.269. The level 2 indicator of a dimension is written min(l minus 1, 1) minus max(l minus 2, 0) and the level 3 indicator max(l minus 2, 0), which the site calculator can evaluate. The scale runs down to minus 0.594 for state 33333, below the value of dead." ;
    hemo:9000009 "UK MVH time trade-off EQ-5D-3L tariff from five reported levels" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Dolan P, Gudex C, Kind P, Williams A. A social tariff for EuroQol. Results from a UK general population survey. Centre for Health Economics Discussion Paper 138. York, University of York, 1995. Table 1, coefficients for TTO tariffs (constant 0.081, mobility 0.069 and 0.314, self-care 0.104 and 0.214, usual activity 0.036 and 0.094, pain or discomfort 0.123 and 0.386, anxiety or depression 0.071 and 0.236, N3 0.269), the worked example for state 11223 giving 0.255 and the appendix TTO tariff for all 243 states, with 33333 at minus 0.594. Scanned text, read from the page images. https://www.york.ac.uk/media/che/documents/papers/discussionpapers/CHE%20Discussion%20Paper%20138.pdf",
        "Janssen B, Szende A, Ramos-Goñi JM. Data and methods. In Szende A, Janssen B, Cabases J, editors. Self-reported population health. An international perspective based on EQ-5D. Dordrecht, Springer, 2014, chapter 2. Table 2.2, UK row (MVH Group 1995, random effects model, mean absolute difference 0.039), which prints the same twelve coefficients as negative values, constant minus 0.081 to N3 minus 0.269. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-007-7596-1_2" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Leaving out the UK EQ-5D-3L constant for a mild state. Subtracting only the mobility decrement for 21111 gives 0.931 instead of 0.850. The constant 0.081 is charged for any departure from full health, so omitting it overstates every state other than 11111 by the same amount and inflates utilities most visibly near the top of the scale.",
        "Reading an EQ-5D-3L profile in reverse dimension order. The five digits run mobility, self-care, usual activities, pain or discomfort and anxiety or depression. Reading 11223 backwards scores 32211, 0.196 in the UK tariff instead of 0.255, because the dimensions carry different decrements." ;
    hemo:9000014 "D = min(l_MO + l_SC + l_UA + l_PD + l_AD - 5, 1); N_3 = min(max(l_MO - 2, 0) + max(l_SC - 2, 0) + max(l_UA - 2, 0) + max(l_PD - 2, 0) + max(l_AD - 2, 0), 1); U = 1 - 0.081 * D - 0.069 * (min(l_MO - 1, 1) - max(l_MO - 2, 0)) - 0.314 * max(l_MO - 2, 0) - 0.104 * (min(l_SC - 1, 1) - max(l_SC - 2, 0)) - 0.214 * max(l_SC - 2, 0) - 0.036 * (min(l_UA - 1, 1) - max(l_UA - 2, 0)) - 0.094 * max(l_UA - 2, 0) - 0.123 * (min(l_PD - 1, 1) - max(l_PD - 2, 0)) - 0.386 * max(l_PD - 2, 0) - 0.071 * (min(l_AD - 1, 1) - max(l_AD - 2, 0)) - 0.236 * max(l_AD - 2, 0) - 0.269 * N_3" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EQ3L-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "uk-mvh-time-trade-off-eq-5d-3l-tariff-from-five-reported-levels" .

hemo:5000651 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Per-person EVPI as the mean opportunity loss of the current decision"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "The module states the outcome as QALYs or money. HEMO formalises the money version, and the net health benefit version has the same form in QALYs."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-EVPI-001" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EVPI-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000141 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003863 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002808,
        hemo:6002809,
        hemo:6002810,
        hemo:6002811,
        hemo:6002812,
        hemo:6003735 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002809,
        hemo:6002810,
        hemo:6002812 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002811 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003860,
        hemo:7003861,
        hemo:7003862 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003864,
        hemo:7003865,
        hemo:7003866,
        hemo:7003867,
        hemo:7003873,
        hemo:7003879 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000869 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000852,
        hemo:8000858 ;
    hemo:1000027 hemo:5000207 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000075 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-17" ;
    hemo:9000008 "In each draw s the opportunity loss L_s is the highest net benefit available in that draw less the net benefit, in the same draw, of the option d* chosen on current evidence. EVPI is the mean of L_s over the S equally weighted draws. Because d* is the option with the highest mean net benefit, the result equals the mean of the maxima less the maximum of the means in HE-FM-VOI-001, and it also shows which draws carry the loss. L_s is zero in every draw where d* is also the best option." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Per-person EVPI as the mean opportunity loss of the current decision" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Fenwick E, Steuten L, Knies S, Ghabri S, Basu A, Murray JF, Koffijberg HE, Strong M, Sanders Schmidler GD, Rothery C. Value of information analysis for research decisions, an introduction. Report 1 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(2):139-150. Box 4, which computes EVPI in net health benefit as the mean of the maximum NHB over iterations less the mean NHB of the option chosen on current evidence, and alternatively as the opportunity loss of the current decision in each iteration (column 7) averaged over all iterations, with no loss in the iterations where the current choice is correct. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2020.01.001",
        "Rothery C, Strong M, Koffijberg HE, Basu A, Ghabri S, Knies S, Murray JF, Sanders Schmidler GD, Steuten L, Fenwick E. Value of information analytical methods. Report 2 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(3):277-286. Section on the expected value of perfect information, which defines EVPI as the expected value of a decision with perfect information less the expected value of the decision on current knowledge, approximates the first term by the mean over samples of the maximum utility, and describes the calculation as a single-loop Monte Carlo scheme that needs no sampling beyond the probabilistic analysis. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2020.01.004" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Computing EVPI from a single deterministic run. With one set of inputs there is one best option and no loss, so an EVPI computed from a base case is zero by construction, whatever the true uncertainty. EVPI needs the full set of probabilistic draws.",
        "Holding an option other than the highest-mean option fixed in the loss. If standard care is held fixed in the article's example although the drug has the higher mean, the mean loss is 0.65 / 5 = 0.13 QALYs, which is the EVPI of 0.06 plus the 0.07 QALYs of expected net benefit given up by the wrong current choice. The fixed option must be the one with the highest mean over the same draws." ;
    hemo:9000014 "L_s = NBmax_s - NBcur_s; EVPI = sum_(s=1)^S [L_s] / S" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"logic1\">and</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>L_s</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><ci>NBmax_s</ci><ci>NBcur_s</ci></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>EVPI</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">sum</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"interval1\">integer_interval</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>S</ci></apply><bind><csymbol cd=\"fns1\">lambda</csymbol><bvar><ci>s</ci></bvar><apply><csymbol cd=\"hemo1\" cdbase=\"https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/cd\">element</csymbol><ci>L_s</ci><ci>s</ci></apply></bind></apply><ci>S</ci></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "L_{s} = \\mathrm{NBmax}_{s} - \\mathrm{NBcur}_{s} ;\\quad \\mathrm{EVPI} = \\frac{\\sum_{s=1}^{S} \\left[L_{s}_{s}\\right]}{S}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EVPI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "per-person-evpi-as-the-mean-opportunity-loss-of-the-current-decision" .

hemo:5000655 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-level nested Monte Carlo EVPPI estimator from incremental net benefit"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-EVPP-001" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EVPP-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000142 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003916 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002829,
        hemo:6002830,
        hemo:6002831,
        hemo:6002832,
        hemo:6002833,
        hemo:6002834,
        hemo:6002835,
        hemo:6002836,
        hemo:6002837 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002830,
        hemo:6002831,
        hemo:6002832,
        hemo:6002833,
        hemo:6002836,
        hemo:6002837 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002829 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003913,
        hemo:7003914,
        hemo:7003915 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003917,
        hemo:7003918,
        hemo:7003919,
        hemo:7003920,
        hemo:7003930,
        hemo:7003940 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000910 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000874,
        hemo:8000882,
        hemo:8000890 ;
    hemo:1000026 hemo:5000967 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000033,
        heao:0000076 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-18" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Draws K values of the parameters of interest theta_i in an outer loop and, for each, J values of the remaining parameters theta_c in an inner loop, averages the incremental net benefit of the new option over the inner draws and keeps the larger of that mean and zero, the value of standard care. The average of the K kept values, less the larger of zero and the mean of the inner means, estimates EVPPI for theta_i, the double-loop scheme of Brennan, Kharroubi, O'Hagan and Chilcott and of Algorithm 2 in the second ISPOR report. The calculator form is written for K = 2 outer and J = 2 inner draws, with INB_kj the run at outer draw k and inner draw j; larger loops add terms in the same pattern, as the Excel and the Computational Function do. With more than two options the maximum is taken over the inner means of every option, and Strong, Oakley and Brennan note that the value is unchanged when net benefits are expressed relative to a baseline option. When theta_i holds every parameter the inner runs at each outer draw are identical and the estimator becomes the per-person EVPI of HE-FM-VOI-001." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-level nested Monte Carlo EVPPI estimator from incremental net benefit" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Brennan A, Kharroubi S, O'Hagan A, Chilcott J. Calculating partial expected value of perfect information via Monte Carlo sampling algorithms. Medical Decision Making. 2007;27(4) 448-470. Abstract, which describes the generalised Monte Carlo algorithm with an outer loop sampling the parameters of interest and, conditional on these, an inner loop sampling the remaining uncertain parameters, states that maxima of Monte Carlo estimates of expectations are biased upwards so that small samples bias the estimate, and reports that fewer samples on the outer level and more on the inner level could be efficient. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X07302555",
        "Rothery C, Strong M, Koffijberg HE, Basu A, Ghabri S, Knies S, Murray JF, Sanders Schmidler GD, Steuten L, Fenwick E. Value of information analytical methods. Report 2 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(3) 277-286. Section on EVPPI computation, Equation 13 with K outer samples of theta_i and J inner samples of theta_c from the conditional distribution, Algorithm 2 steps 1 to 11, and good practice recommendation 5 on choosing inner and outer loop sizes for acceptable bias and precision. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2020.01.004",
        "Strong M, Oakley JE, Brennan A. Estimating multiparameter partial expected value of perfect information from a probabilistic sensitivity analysis sample, a nonparametric regression approach. Medical Decision Making. 2014;34(3) 311-326. Methods section, the nested 2-level estimator with K outer and J inner simulations and the upward bias induced by the maximisation step, and Table 2 of case study 1, where 1,000 outer by 10 inner runs gave 351.92 (SE 25.20, upward bias 130.69) against the reference 247.95 for parameter set 1, in pounds. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X13505910" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Inner draws from the marginal distribution when parameters are correlated. When theta_c is correlated with theta_i, drawing the inner values from the marginal distribution of theta_c ignores what each value of theta_i implies for them, so the inner means and the estimate are wrong. Strong, Oakley and Brennan describe this conditional sampling as a limitation of the two-level method, and the regression estimator HE-FM-EVPP-003 needs only the joint sample.",
        "Inner loop too small for the nested EVPPI estimator. In the first case study of Strong, Oakley and Brennan, 1,000 outer by 10 inner runs gave GBP 351.92 for their parameter set 1 against a reference value of GBP 247.95, with an estimated upward bias of GBP 130.69. In the article's example a single inner run for each draw of A returns the EVPI of GBP 600 in place of the EVPPI for A of GBP 550. An estimate above the EVPI is a sign of Monte Carlo error, not a finding." ;
    hemo:9000014 "ibar_1 = (INB_11 + INB_12) / J; ibar_2 = (INB_21 + INB_22) / J; EVPPI_2L = (max(ibar_1, 0) + max(ibar_2, 0)) / K - max((ibar_1 + ibar_2) / K, 0)" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"logic1\">and</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>ibar_1</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">plus</csymbol><ci>INB_11</ci><ci>INB_12</ci></apply><ci>J</ci></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>ibar_2</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">plus</csymbol><ci>INB_21</ci><ci>INB_22</ci></apply><ci>J</ci></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>EVPPI_2L</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">plus</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"minmax1\">max</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"set1\">set</csymbol><ci>ibar_1</ci><cn type=\"integer\">0</cn></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"minmax1\">max</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"set1\">set</csymbol><ci>ibar_2</ci><cn type=\"integer\">0</cn></apply></apply></apply><ci>K</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"minmax1\">max</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"set1\">set</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">plus</csymbol><ci>ibar_1</ci><ci>ibar_2</ci></apply><ci>K</ci></apply><cn type=\"integer\">0</cn></apply></apply></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{ibar}_{1} = \\frac{\\mathrm{INB}_{\\mathrm{11}} + \\mathrm{INB}_{\\mathrm{12}}}{J} ;\\quad \\mathrm{ibar}_{2} = \\frac{\\mathrm{INB}_{\\mathrm{21}} + \\mathrm{INB}_{\\mathrm{22}}}{J} ;\\quad \\mathrm{EVPPI}_{\\mathrm{2L}} = \\frac{\\max\\left(\\mathrm{ibar}_{1}, 0\\right) + \\max\\left(\\mathrm{ibar}_{2}, 0\\right)}{K} - \\max\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{ibar}_{1} + \\mathrm{ibar}_{2}}{K}, 0\\right)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EVPP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-level-nested-monte-carlo-evppi-estimator-from-incremental-net-benefit" .

hemo:5000658 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "ENBS of one study design from its population EVSI and the study cost"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-ENBS-001" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ENBS-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000143 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003970 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002848,
        hemo:6002849,
        hemo:6002850 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002849,
        hemo:6002850 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002848 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003967,
        hemo:7003968,
        hemo:7003969 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003971,
        hemo:7003972,
        hemo:7003973,
        hemo:7003977,
        hemo:7003981 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000925 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000919 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000101 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-22" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives the net value of one proposed design of sample size n as its population EVSI less the expected total cost of the study, the definition used by the ISPOR task force. Population EVSI comes from HE-FM-EVSI-004, which multiplies the design's per-person EVSI by the discounted number of people whose treatment the result will inform, scaled in the same way as population EVPI in HE-FM-VOI-002. A positive value means the design is expected to repay its cost, and the design with the greatest value among those feasible is the efficient one (HE-CF-ENBS-001)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "ENBS of one study design from its population EVSI and the study cost" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Claxton K, Posnett J. An economic approach to clinical trial design and research priority-setting. Health Economics. 1996;5(6) 513-524. Abstract, checked on PubMed (9003938), which presents a decision-analytic approach to trial design that takes explicit account of the costs of sampling and the benefits of sample information, and a set of screens or hurdles for research proposals. https://doi.org/10.1002/(SICI)1099-1050(199611)5:6%3C513::AID-HEC237%3E3.0.CO;2-9",
        "Fenwick E, Steuten L, Knies S, et al. Value of information analysis for research decisions, an introduction. Report 1 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(2) 139-150. Sections on estimating the value of specific research and identifying the appropriate research design, with good practice recommendations 8 and 9. The difference between population EVSI for a specific study and its expected total cost is the ENBS, a positive ENBS is a sufficient condition for the worth of a specific study, and the most efficient design is the one with the greatest ENBS. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2020.01.001",
        "Rothery C, Strong M, Koffijberg HE, et al. Value of information analytical methods. Report 2 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(3) 277-286. Section on the expected net benefit of sampling, which defines ENBS as the difference between population EVSI and the cost of the data collection, a measure of the net value of any particular study. Section on reporting of results, equation 15, where the per-person value is multiplied by the sum over periods 0 to T of I_t divided by (1 + d)^t, with I_t the incidence in period t, T the time horizon and d the discount rate for a single period. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2020.01.004" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Future cohorts left undiscounted in ENBS. Leaving the 2,000 people a year in years 1 to 4 undiscounted when forming EVSI_pop counts 10,000 people instead of about 9,346 and raises the illustrative ENBS of the 600-patient trial to GBP 1,540,000 instead of about GBP 1,343,848 (computed here for illustration). Rothery and colleagues discount the value of research over the time horizon, as HE-FM-EVSI-004 does.",
        "People treated before the study reports counted as beneficiaries. If 2,000 of the article's 8,000 people would be treated before the 600-patient trial reports, only 6,000 can benefit and ENBS falls to GBP 340,000 instead of GBP 940,000 (computed here for illustration). Eckermann and Willan reduce the number who benefit by those treated inside and outside the trial until its evidence is available.",
        "Study cost set against per-person EVSI instead of population EVSI. Comparing the per-person EVSI of GBP 300 for the article's 600-patient trial with its cost of GBP 2,000 per participant suggests the study cannot pay its way, yet its ENBS is GBP 940,000. The cost belongs against the population value, after scaling and discounting." ;
    hemo:9000014 "ENBS = EVSI_pop - C_n" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>ENBS</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><ci>EVSI_pop</ci><ci>C_n</ci></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{ENBS} = \\mathrm{EVSI}_{\\mathrm{pop}} - C_{n}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ENBS-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "enbs-of-one-study-design-from-its-population-evsi-and-the-study-cost" .

hemo:5000967 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Nested Monte Carlo EVPPI estimator for K outer and J inner draws"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000114 obo:IAO_0000125 ;
    obo:IAO_0000115 "Gives the expected value of partial perfect information per person about a parameter set from a nested Monte Carlo sample of the incremental net benefit of one option over another, K outer draws of the parameters of interest and J inner draws of the others for each."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "A HEMO-native record, for a formula the Functions and Formulae module does not yet hold. It is listed in module-backlog.md for the module curator."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000117 "Michelle Ashley (drafted with Claude)" ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "Strong et al. 2014; Briggs et al. 2006" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HEMO-FM-EVPPI-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6005577,
        hemo:6005578,
        hemo:6005579,
        hemo:6005580,
        hemo:6005581,
        hemo:6005582 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6005578,
        hemo:6005579,
        hemo:6005580 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6005577 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7007329,
        hemo:7007330 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7007475 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8001763 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8001727 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000033,
        heao:0000076 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-18" ;
    hemo:9000014 "EVPPI = sum_(k=1)^K [max(0, sum_(j=1)^J [INB_(k, j)] / J)] / K - max(0, sum_(k=1)^K [sum_(j=1)^J [INB_(k, j)]] / (K * J))" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>EVPPI</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">sum</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"interval1\">integer_interval</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>K</ci></apply><bind><csymbol cd=\"fns1\">lambda</csymbol><bvar><ci>k</ci></bvar><apply><csymbol cd=\"minmax1\">max</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"set1\">set</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">0</cn><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">sum</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"interval1\">integer_interval</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>J</ci></apply><bind><csymbol cd=\"fns1\">lambda</csymbol><bvar><ci>j</ci></bvar><apply><csymbol cd=\"hemo1\" cdbase=\"https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/cd\">element</csymbol><ci>INB</ci><ci>k</ci><ci>j</ci></apply></bind></apply><ci>J</ci></apply></apply></apply></bind></apply><ci>K</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"minmax1\">max</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"set1\">set</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">0</cn><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">sum</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"interval1\">integer_interval</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>K</ci></apply><bind><csymbol cd=\"fns1\">lambda</csymbol><bvar><ci>k</ci></bvar><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">sum</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"interval1\">integer_interval</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>J</ci></apply><bind><csymbol cd=\"fns1\">lambda</csymbol><bvar><ci>j</ci></bvar><apply><csymbol cd=\"hemo1\" cdbase=\"https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/cd\">element</csymbol><ci>INB</ci><ci>k</ci><ci>j</ci></apply></bind></apply></bind></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><ci>K</ci><ci>J</ci></apply></apply></apply></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{EVPPI} = \\frac{\\sum_{k=1}^{K} \\left[\\max\\left(0, \\frac{\\sum_{j=1}^{J} \\left[\\mathrm{INB}_{k}\\right]}{J}\\right)\\right]}{K} - \\max\\left(0, \\frac{\\sum_{k=1}^{K} \\left[\\sum_{j=1}^{J} \\left[\\mathrm{INB}_{k}\\right]\\right]}{K \\, J}\\right)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HEMO-FM-EVPPI-001" .

