hemo:5000041 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Adverse event rate to model input function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-AER-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the adverse event counts in a trial safety table, with their denominators of patients or person-time, to the probability of an event in one model cycle, and maps those probabilities to the expected adverse event cost and QALY loss per patient. The general rate and probability conversions are on the Transition Probability page (HE-FN-TP-001); the records here apply them to adverse event data and add the cost and QALY weighting." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Adverse event rate to model input function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "f(n, N, T, t) = p_t; g(p_k, c_k, u_k, tau_k) = (C_AE, Q_AE)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-AER-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "adverse-event-rate-to-model-input-function" .

hemo:5000042 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Penalised-likelihood ranking of candidate survival models"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-AIC-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the maximised log-likelihood and the number of estimated parameters of each model fitted to the same data to an information criterion, a fit statistic with a penalty for each extra parameter. Lower values rank higher, and only differences between models fitted to the same observations carry meaning. In health technology assessment the candidates are usually parametric survival curves fitted to trial time-to-event data before extrapolation. The criteria measure fit within follow-up only, so they inform but do not settle the choice of curve." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Penalised-likelihood ranking of candidate survival models" ;
    hemo:9000014 "IC(lnL, k, n) = -2 * lnL + penalty(k, n)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-AIC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "penalised-likelihood-ranking-of-candidate-survival-models" .

hemo:5000043 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Alpha spending function for group sequential trials"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-ASP-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the information fraction reached at an analysis of a group sequential trial to the cumulative one-sided type I error that may have been used by that point. The function rises from zero at the start of the trial to the overall significance level at the planned end. Each efficacy boundary is then set so that the probability, under the null hypothesis, of crossing any boundary up to and including analysis k equals the cumulative spend, with correlation sqrt(t_l / t_k) between the test statistics at analyses l and k. That step needs recursive numerical integration and is done in group sequential software, so the records here cover the closed-form parts: the information fraction, the two Lan-DeMets spending functions, the increment available at each look and the hazard ratio that crosses a given boundary." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Alpha spending function for group sequential trials" ;
    hemo:9000014 "alpha(0) = 0; alpha(1) = alpha; t_k = I_k / I_max; P_0(Z_1 >= c_1 or ... or Z_k >= c_k) = alpha(t_k)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-ASP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "alpha-spending-function-for-group-sequential-trials" .

hemo:5000051 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Arrival process count, gap and arrival-time function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-ARRP-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps an arrival rate, constant or varying with time, to the number of patients or other entities that arrive in a window, the gaps between successive arrivals and the clock time of each arrival. The three descriptions carry the same information: S_n is the time of the nth arrival, X_i the gap before arrival i and N(t) the number of arrivals up to time t, in the notation of the Arrival Process article. The records cover Poisson counts, time-varying rates, sampling of gaps and arrival times in a discrete event simulation, and the single-server queueing result that links the arrival rate to waiting." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Arrival process count, gap and arrival-time function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "S_n = X_1 + X_2 + ... + X_n; N(t) = max{n : S_n <= t}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-ARRP-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "arrival-process-count-gap-and-arrival-time-function" .

hemo:5000054 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Attack rate, secondary attack rate and final epidemic size function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-ATR-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps counts of new cases and of people at risk over a stated period to an attack rate, a risk expressed as a proportion rather than a rate per unit of time. The same structure gives the secondary attack rate among contacts of primary cases, and comparing attack rates between groups gives the attack rate ratio and vaccine efficacy or effectiveness. Under the assumptions of the simple susceptible, infectious and recovered model, the final attack rate of a completed epidemic is linked to the basic reproduction number R_0 through the final size relation. In an economic evaluation the attack rate is either a fixed input probability of infection, as in a static model, or an output that changes with the intervention, as in a dynamic transmission model." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Attack rate, secondary attack rate and final epidemic size function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "AR = A / N; SAR = S / (H - P); RR = AR_e / AR_ne; VE = 1 - AR_v / AR_u; z = s_0 * (1 - exp(-R_0 * z))" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-ATR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "attack-rate-secondary-attack-rate-and-final-epidemic-size-function" .

hemo:5000056 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Aggregate stop-loss payment and expected payment function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-ASL-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a self-funded employer's eligible claims for the contract year, net of specific stop-loss recoveries, to the payment due from the aggregate stop-loss carrier and the claims the employer retains, given the aggregate attachment point and any maximum aggregate payment. Applied to the distribution of the year's claims instead of one realised year, the same rule gives the carrier's expected payment, the net stop-loss premium before any loading. The records follow the notation of the Aggregate Stop-Loss article, where S is net claims for the year, A is the aggregate attachment point and L is the carrier's payment." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Aggregate stop-loss payment and expected payment function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "L = f(S, A, M); R = S - L; EL = g(A, E_S, SD_S)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-ASL-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "aggregate-stop-loss-payment-and-expected-payment-function" .

hemo:5000060 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Survival and mean survival from a bathtub-shaped hazard"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-BTH-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a hazard that is high soon after a procedure, falls to a stable level and later rises with age to the survival curve and the mean survival that a health economic model needs. Survival at time t is the exponential of minus the cumulative hazard up to t, and mean survival is the area under the survival curve. The formulae below apply this to a piecewise-constant hazard schedule, to a constant hazard fitted to early follow-up, to a sum of two Weibull hazards and to an all-cause hazard built from background and excess components. Turning an interval hazard into a cycle transition probability uses the conversion already given on the Transition Probability page (HE-FM-TP-001), which is not repeated here." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Survival and mean survival from a bathtub-shaped hazard" ;
    hemo:9000014 "S(t) = exp(-H(t)), H(t) = integral_0^t h(u) du; E_T = integral_0^inf S(t) dt" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-BTH-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "survival-and-mean-survival-from-a-bathtub-shaped-hazard" .

hemo:5000064 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Health inequality measures across ordered population groups"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-HINQ-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000141,
        hemo:6000142,
        hemo:6000143,
        hemo:6000144,
        hemo:6000145,
        hemo:6000146,
        hemo:6000147 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000141,
        hemo:6000142 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000143,
        hemo:6000144,
        hemo:6000145,
        hemo:6000146,
        hemo:6000147 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the mean health of population groups, ordered from the most to the least disadvantaged, and the share of the population in each group to summary measures of socioeconomic-related health inequality: the absolute and relative gaps between the extreme groups, the slope index of inequality, the relative index of inequality in two forms and the concentration index. The measures describe how health varies with socioeconomic position and do not say whether that variation is unfair, which is the separate judgement of health inequity. The Atkinson index, which ignores the ranking of groups, has its own records on the Atkinson index page (HE-FN-ATK-001) and is not repeated here." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health inequality measures across ordered population groups" ;
    hemo:9000014 "M(h_j, f_j) = (AG, RG, SII, RII, C)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-HINQ-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-inequality-measures-across-ordered-population-groups" .

hemo:5000066 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Horizontal equity measurement function for health care delivery and finance"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-HEQ-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000155,
        hemo:6000156,
        hemo:6000157,
        hemo:6000158,
        hemo:6000159,
        hemo:6000160,
        hemo:6000161,
        hemo:6000162,
        hemo:6000163,
        hemo:6000164,
        hemo:6000165,
        hemo:6000166 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000155,
        hemo:6000156,
        hemo:6000157,
        hemo:6000158,
        hemo:6000159,
        hemo:6000160,
        hemo:6000161 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000162,
        hemo:6000163,
        hemo:6000164,
        hemo:6000165,
        hemo:6000166 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps data on the use of health care, proxies of need and compulsory health payments, for people ranked by income or another measure of living standards, to measures of horizontal equity: need-expected use from a need regression, need-standardised use, the horizontal inequity index for the delivery of care, its rule-of-75 reading, and the horizontal inequity term in the decomposition of the redistributive effect of health finance. Equal treatment for equal need is judged against the average relationship between need and use in the sample, so the vertical norm is assumed rather than tested. The grouped-data concentration index (HE-FM-HINQ-005 on the Health Inequality page and HE-FM-VEQ-002 on the Vertical Equity page) and the Kakwani index (HE-FM-VEQ-001) have their own records and are not repeated here." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Horizontal equity measurement function for health care delivery and finance" ;
    hemo:9000014 "f(y_i, x_ji, z_ki, f_t, V, H, R) = (yX_i, yIS_i, HI, S, RE)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-HEQ-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "horizontal-equity-measurement-function-for-health-care-delivery-and-finance" .

hemo:5000068 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Bed turnover rate and turnover interval function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-BTO-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the discharges, available beds and occupied bed-days of a period to the two measures reported as bed turnover: the turnover rate, the number of patients each bed served, and the turnover interval, the average time a bed stands empty between one patient and the next. It also expresses both through bed occupancy and average length of stay, and spreads the annual cost of a staffed bed over the patients it served. The records follow the notation of the Bed Turnover article. The throughput identity linking annual discharges to beds, occupancy and average length of stay is held on the Average Length of Stay page (HE-FM-ALOS-002) and is referenced here, not repeated." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Bed turnover rate and turnover interval function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "T = f(D, B_bar); TI = g(n, B_bar, U, D); T = h(o, L_bar); TI = k(o, L_bar); c_D = m(C_bed, T)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-BTO-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "bed-turnover-rate-and-turnover-interval-function" .

hemo:5000069 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Beta distribution density, moments and fitting for an uncertain probability"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-BETA-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps two positive shape parameters, alpha and beta, to a probability density on the unit interval that describes uncertainty about a probability, such as a transition probability in a decision tree or Markov model. The parameters are fitted from event counts, with alpha counting events and beta non-events, or from a reported mean and standard error by the method of moments, and the fitted distribution is sampled in probabilistic sensitivity analysis. The formulae below give the mean and variance, the two fitting routes, the gamma alternative for a utility decrement and the Dirichlet extension for states with three or more exits. Gamma(.) in the density is the gamma function." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Beta distribution density, moments and fitting for an uncertain probability" ;
    hemo:9000014 "f(p | alpha, beta) = Gamma(alpha + beta) / (Gamma(alpha) * Gamma(beta)) * p^(alpha - 1) * (1 - p)^(beta - 1), 0 < p < 1" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-BETA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "beta-distribution-density-moments-and-fitting-for-an-uncertain-probability" .

hemo:5000070 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Benefit-cost ratio comparison and classification function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-BCRX-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000167,
        hemo:6000168,
        hemo:6000169,
        hemo:6000170,
        hemo:6000171,
        hemo:6000172,
        hemo:6000173,
        hemo:6000174 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000167,
        hemo:6000168 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000169,
        hemo:6000170,
        hemo:6000171,
        hemo:6000172,
        hemo:6000173,
        hemo:6000174 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the present values of monetised benefits and costs of one or more options, split where needed by how consequences are classified and by who bears the costs, to ratio summaries and to their link with net present value. The basic ratio and net present value are given under cost-benefit analysis (HE-FM-CBA-002 and HE-FM-CBA-001); PVB and PVC on this page are the same quantities as PV_B and PV_C there. The formulas below relate the ratio to net present value, apply it between mutually exclusive options, and show how the classification of savings, transfers and costs outside the public sector changes it." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Benefit-cost ratio comparison and classification function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "r(PVB, PVC) = (NPV, BCR_YX, BCR_gross, BCR_net, BCR_T, RPSC)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-BCRX-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "benefit-cost-ratio-comparison-and-classification-function" .

hemo:5000096 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Cross-price elasticity of demand for one health care good with respect to another good's price"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-XPE-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a change in the price of good j to the proportional change in the quantity of good i demanded, holding income, preferences and other prices constant. A positive value marks substitutes, as with a branded medicine and its generic; a negative value marks complements; the order of the subscripts matters. Own-price elasticity is the case i = j. The notation follows the Cross-Price Elasticity article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Cross-price elasticity of demand for one health care good with respect to another good's price" ;
    hemo:9000014 "eps_ij = (dQ_i / dP_j) * (P_j / Q_i)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-XPE-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "cross-price-elasticity-of-demand-for-one-health-care-good-with-respect-to-another-good-s-price" .

hemo:5000097 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Difference-in-differences estimate of a policy effect from treated and comparison groups"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-DID-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps outcomes observed before and after a policy in a group exposed to it and a group that is not to an estimate of the policy's average effect on the exposed: the change in the exposed group minus the change in the comparison group. Fixed differences between the groups and shocks common to both cancel. The estimate is causal only under parallel trends and no anticipation. The notation follows the Difference-in-Differences article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Difference-in-differences estimate of a policy effect from treated and comparison groups" ;
    hemo:9000014 "tau = (E[Y_1 | D = 1] - E[Y_0 | D = 1]) - (E[Y_1 | D = 0] - E[Y_0 | D = 0])" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-DID-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "difference-in-differences-estimate-of-a-policy-effect-from-treated-and-comparison-groups" .

hemo:5000098 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Decision curve net benefit of a risk model or test across threshold probabilities"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-DCA-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a risk model's or test's classifications in a validation sample, at a chosen threshold probability, to net benefit: true positives per patient minus false positives per patient weighted by the odds at the threshold, so that both are counted in units of true positives. Repeating the calculation over a preset range of thresholds, for the model and for intervening in everyone or no one, gives the decision curve. The notation follows the Decision Curve Analysis article; the economic net benefit of an option at a cost-effectiveness threshold is a different quantity (HE-FM-NMB-001)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Decision curve net benefit of a risk model or test across threshold probabilities" ;
    hemo:9000014 "NB(p_t) = TP(p_t) / n - FP(p_t) / n * p_t / (1 - p_t)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-DCA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "decision-curve-net-benefit-of-a-risk-model-or-test-across-threshold-probabilities" .

hemo:5000100 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Grouping patients or other units by dissimilarity in cluster analysis"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-CLU-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the clustering variables measured on each unit, such as a patient's counts of emergency and outpatient care, to a set of groups found in the data without an outcome variable. Combinatorial methods such as k-means assign each unit to one group by minimising the dissimilarity within groups; mixture models such as latent class analysis give each unit a probability of belonging to each class. The notation follows the Cluster Analysis article, whose six-patient example is used throughout." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Grouping patients or other units by dissimilarity in cluster analysis" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d(x_i, x_j) = sum_(v=1)^p (x_iv - x_jv)^2; W(K) = sum_(k=1)^K sum_(i in C_k) ||x_i - xbar_k||^2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-CLU-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "grouping-patients-or-other-units-by-dissimilarity-in-cluster-analysis" .

hemo:5000103 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Inverse probability of censoring weighting for dependent censoring"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-DCEN-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps patients who remain under observation to weights equal to the inverse of their probability of having remained uncensored, given their covariate history, so that they also represent similar patients who were censored. Weighted versions of the Kaplan-Meier estimator, the Cox model and mean cost estimators then remove the bias that dependent censoring causes, provided every factor predicting both censoring and outcome is measured. The notation follows the Dependent Censoring article and its switchers-censored control arm." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Inverse probability of censoring weighting for dependent censoring" ;
    hemo:9000014 "w_i(t) = prod_(k=0)^t 1 / P[C_i(k) = 0 | Cbar_i(k-1) = 0, Lbar_i(k), T_i > k]" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-DCEN-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "inverse-probability-of-censoring-weighting-for-dependent-censoring" .

hemo:5000106 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Diagnostic test accuracy measures and the expected value of test results"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-DXA-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the cross-classification of index test results against a reference standard to the measures of accuracy, conditional on disease status (sensitivity, specificity) or on the test result (predictive values), and combines accuracy with prevalence and the consequences of true and false results into the expected net monetary benefit of testing. The notation follows the Diagnostic Accuracy article and its two triage tests." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Diagnostic test accuracy measures and the expected value of test results" ;
    hemo:9000014 "Se = TP / (TP + FN); Sp = TN / (TN + FP); NMB = p * Se * G - (1 - p) * (1 - Sp) * L - c" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-DXA-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "diagnostic-test-accuracy-measures-and-the-expected-value-of-test-results" .

hemo:5000118 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Anchor-based thresholds for a minimal clinically important difference and their use for responder shares and trial size"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-MCID-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a meaningful step on an external anchor, usually a patient's global rating of change, onto the score scale of an outcome measure. The between-group estimate subtracts the mean change of patients reporting no change from that of patients reporting a small change; the ROC cut-off chooses the change score that best separates anchor-improved patients from the rest; the 95 per cent limit cut-off sits above almost all unchanged patients. A threshold then gives the share of patients reaching it in each arm, and a between-group difference sets the target difference of a sample size calculation. Distribution-based yardsticks are HE-FM-DMID-001 to HE-FM-DMID-003 on the Distribution-Based MID page. Notation follows the Minimal Clinically Important Difference article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Anchor-based thresholds for a minimal clinically important difference and their use for responder shares and trial size" ;
    hemo:9000014 "MID_between = d_M - d_S; c_star = argmin_c [(1 - Se_c) + (1 - Sp_c)]; c_95 = d_S + 1.645 * s_S; n = 2 * (z_a + z_b)^2 * sigma^2 / delta^2" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-MCID-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "anchor-based-thresholds-for-a-minimal-clinically-important-difference-and-their-use-for-responder-shares-and-trial-size" .

hemo:5000122 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Partitioned survival state occupancy and area-under-the-curve QALY function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-PSM-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a set of survival curves that are not mutually exclusive, usually progression-free survival (PFS) and overall survival (OS) for each treatment arm, to the share of the cohort in each health state over time, and then to mean time in each state and QALYs as utility-weighted areas under the curves. State membership is read from the curves rather than built from transition probabilities, which is what separates a partitioned survival model from a state transition model. Linked records cover the parts this package does not repeat: the restricted mean from a Kaplan-Meier curve (HE-FM-ADMC-003), QALYs summed over periods (HE-FM-QALY-001), discounted totals (HE-FM-DR-002), a treatment curve from a baseline curve and a hazard ratio (HE-FM-HR-003) and the continuous discount rate (HE-FM-CONT-003)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Partitioned survival state occupancy and area-under-the-curve QALY function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "g(S_PFS(t), S_OS(t), u_PF, u_PD) = (P_PF(t), P_PD(t), P_D(t), QALY)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-PSM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "partitioned-survival-state-occupancy-and-area-under-the-curve-qaly-function" .

hemo:5000135 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Precision-weighted regression of study effect estimates on study characteristics"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-MREG-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000244,
        hemo:6000245,
        hemo:6000246,
        hemo:6000247,
        hemo:6000248,
        hemo:6000249 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000244,
        hemo:6000245,
        hemo:6000246 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000247,
        hemo:6000248,
        hemo:6000249 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the effect estimate y_i of each study in a meta-analysis, its within-study variance v_i and the study's value x_i of a characteristic such as dose, mean age or baseline risk to a regression line for the expected effect, together with the between-study variance tau2 that the characteristic leaves unexplained. Studies are weighted by the precision of their estimates, and the random-effects form adds the residual between-study variance to each study's own variance. Ratio measures enter as natural logarithms. In health technology assessment the fitted line gives the relative effect expected for the decision population instead of the average over the trials, and the slope is an association between studies, not a randomised comparison." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Precision-weighted regression of study effect estimates on study characteristics" ;
    hemo:9000014 "MR(y_i, v_i, x_i) = (beta_0, beta_1, tau2)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-MREG-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "precision-weighted-regression-of-study-effect-estimates-on-study-characteristics" .

hemo:5000136 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Hyperbolic discount weight function with impatience that declines with delay"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-HYPD-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000250,
        hemo:6000251,
        hemo:6000252 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000250,
        hemo:6000251 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000252 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps a delay t, counted from the moment of choice, and the parameters of a chosen non-constant discount function to the weight placed on an outcome t periods ahead and to the per-period discount rate that the weights imply. Under the constant-rate factor of the Discount Rate page (HE-FM-DR-001), and in the stream totals of the Discounting page (HE-FN-DISC-001 and its formulas), the rate between two adjacent periods is the same at every delay, whereas the forms here let it fall as the delay grows. The package gives the simple hyperbola and its per-period rate, the generalised hyperbola of Loewenstein and Prelec, the quasi-hyperbolic beta-delta form, and a check for a preference reversal between a smaller-sooner and a larger-later outcome. Notation follows the Hyperbolic Discounting article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Hyperbolic discount weight function with impatience that declines with delay" ;
    hemo:9000014 "D(t, theta) = w_t" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-HYPD-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "hyperbolic-discount-weight-function-with-impatience-that-declines-with-delay" .

hemo:5000139 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Disability-adjusted life years for a cause or a scenario from years of life lost and years lived with disability"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-DALY-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000261,
        hemo:6000262,
        hemo:6000263,
        hemo:6000264,
        hemo:6000265 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000261,
        hemo:6000262,
        hemo:6000263,
        hemo:6000264 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000265 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the deaths and the non-fatal health loss from a cause, or in one scenario of an economic evaluation, to disability-adjusted life years (DALYs) by adding the years of life lost (YLL) to the years lived with disability (YLD), each calculated by its own formula. YLLs are deaths times a standard loss function of the age at death (HE-FN-YLL-001, HE-FM-YLL-001, with the discounted form HE-FM-YLL-003 and the age-group total HE-CF-YLL-001), and YLDs are prevalent cases times a disability weight (HE-FM-DWT-001) or incident cases times the weight and the duration (HE-FN-YLD-001, HE-FM-YLD-001, discounted in HE-FM-YLD-002). This package adds the sum itself, the DALYs of a cohort in one scenario, DALYs averted and cost per DALY averted between two scenarios, the continuously discounted cohort form and the DALY rate per 100,000 population. DALYs averted by a change in disability weights alone, with mortality unchanged, are HE-FM-DWT-005, and the general incremental ratio is HE-FM-ICER-001. Notation follows the Disability-Adjusted Life Year article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Disability-adjusted life years for a cause or a scenario from years of life lost and years lived with disability" ;
    hemo:9000014 "d(N_a, L_a, P_s, DW_s) = DALY" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-DALY-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "disability-adjusted-life-years-for-a-cause-or-a-scenario-from-years-of-life-lost-and-years-lived-with-disability" .

hemo:5000150 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Health social welfare function ranking distributions of health"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-SWF-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000316,
        hemo:6000317,
        hemo:6000318 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000316,
        hemo:6000317 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000318 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the health of n people, or of n groups of equal size, and the parameters theta that state a distributional value judgement to a single social welfare value W, so that allocations can be ranked when they differ in who gains as well as in how much is gained. When W rises whenever one person's health rises and nobody's falls, every Pareto improvement raises welfare. The package gives Atkinson's isoelastic additive form for epsilon other than 1, its logarithmic form at epsilon of 1, the maximin limit, the two-group CES form of Wagstaff and the slope of its social indifference curve. The equally distributed equivalent (EDE) health that gives the same W is live as HE-FM-ATK-002 and HE-FM-ATK-003, the Atkinson index as HE-FM-ATK-001, the relative weight on a small gain as HE-FM-ATK-004 and equity-weighted QALY totals as HE-FM-EQWA-001, and HE-FM-WE-004 holds the linear two-group welfare with a switching weight; none is redrafted here. The health forgone for equity and the inequality aversion at which two options swap rank are left to the Equity-Efficiency Trade-off and Distributional Cost-Effectiveness packages. Notation follows the Social Welfare Function article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Health social welfare function ranking distributions of health" ;
    hemo:9000014 "W(h_i, theta) = W" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-SWF-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "health-social-welfare-function-ranking-distributions-of-health" .

hemo:5000155 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Basic reproduction number function from transmission parameters or a next-generation matrix"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-BRN-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000343,
        hemo:6000344,
        hemo:6000345,
        hemo:6000346 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000343,
        hemo:6000344,
        hemo:6000345 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000346 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the transmission parameters of an infection in a fully susceptible population, either a transmission rate and a recovery rate or a next-generation matrix of expected new cases by group, to the basic reproduction number R_0, a count of cases per case with no time unit. The same package derives the effective reproduction number once part of the population is immune, the herd immunity threshold with the critical vaccination coverage, and the reproduction number implied by an early exponential growth rate. R_0 estimated after a completed epidemic from its final attack rate is HE-FM-ATR-005, and the final attack rate and infections averted for a given R_0 and pre-epidemic coverage are computed in HE-CF-ATR-001, so neither is restated here. Vaccine effectiveness from attack rates (HE-FM-ATR-004) supplies E, the epidemic doubling time used in HE-FM-CFR-003 converts to the growth rate r, a per-cycle recovery probability for a cohort model follows the rate conversion of HE-FN-TP-001, and the economic treatment of herd protection as an external benefit is HE-FM-EXT-002. Notation follows the Basic Reproduction Number article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Basic reproduction number function from transmission parameters or a next-generation matrix" ;
    hemo:9000014 "R(beta, gamma, K) = R_0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-BRN-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "basic-reproduction-number-function-from-transmission-parameters-or-a-next-generation-matrix" .

hemo:5000158 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Compartment flow balance function of a compartmental model"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-CMPM-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the numbers of people in the compartments of a population model, the flows between compartments per unit time and any inflow from or outflow to outside the model to the rate of change of each compartment, or, after a time step of length dt, to the next set of compartment counts. The flows can be fixed rates times compartment sizes, as in a static model, or can depend on the number infectious through the force of infection, which makes the model dynamic and nonlinear. A Markov cohort update (HE-FN-MM-001, HE-FM-MM-001) applies one fixed transition matrix to a closed cohort, the agent-based update HE-FN-ABM-001 with the per-contact infection probability HE-FM-ABM-001 follows individuals instead of pools, and a per-cycle probability from a constant rate follows HE-FM-TP-001. Reproduction numbers of the SIR model and of stratified models are in HE-FN-BRN-001 (HE-FM-BRN-001 to HE-FM-BRN-005 and HE-CF-BRN-001), and the final size of an epidemic and R_0 estimated from it are in HE-FN-ATR-001, HE-CF-ATR-001 and HE-FM-ATR-005, so none of them is restated here. Notation follows the Compartmental Model article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Compartment flow balance function of a compartmental model" ;
    hemo:9000014 "g(x_j, f_jk, b_j, d_j) = dx_j/dt" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-CMPM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "compartment-flow-balance-function-of-a-compartmental-model" .

hemo:5000199 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Multi-option dominance screen"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-DOM-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000014 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000241 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000589,
        hemo:6000590,
        hemo:6000591,
        hemo:6000592,
        hemo:6000593,
        hemo:6000594 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000589,
        hemo:6000590,
        hemo:6000591,
        hemo:6000592,
        hemo:6000593 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000594 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000239,
        hemo:7000240 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000242,
        hemo:7000243,
        hemo:7000244 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Flags option k as dominated when any other option in the set costs no more, produces no less health and is strictly better on at least one of the two. Flagged options are removed before sequential ICERs are calculated." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Multi-option dominance screen" ;
    hemo:9000010 "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 31 March 2026. Chapter 4 Economic evaluation, section 4.10.8 (fully incremental analysis with dominated and extendedly dominated technologies removed). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Removing an option on cost alone. Excluding the more expensive of two options without checking effects removes options that buy additional health. An option is dominated only when another costs no more and produces no less, with at least one strict improvement.",
        "Screening against the baseline only. Checking each option against current care alone misses options dominated by another new option, such as the third option by the fourth in the five-option example." ;
    hemo:9000014 "DOM_k = 1 if some j (j = 1 to J, j not equal to k) has C_j <= C_k and E_j >= E_k and (C_j < C_k or E_j > E_k); otherwise DOM_k = 0" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-DOM-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "multi-option-dominance-screen" .

hemo:5000294 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Atkinson equally distributed equivalent health at epsilon of 1"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "The geometric mean takes logarithms of health in years and exponentiates their mean, which keeps the unit of health but which the dimension rule of the 0.1 runner cannot follow."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-ATK-003" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ATK-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000046 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000972 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6001022,
        hemo:6001023,
        hemo:6001024,
        hemo:6003734 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6001023,
        hemo:6001024 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6001022 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000970,
        hemo:7000971 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000973,
        hemo:7000974,
        hemo:7000978 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000498 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000485 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:1000057 hemo:5000293 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:2000024 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000013 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-15" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives the equally distributed equivalent health when epsilon equals 1, where the general formula HE-FM-ATK-002 is undefined. The EDE is then the geometric mean of the n health values, the nth root of their product, written here as the exponential of the mean natural logarithm. The function log is the natural logarithm. It corresponds to Atkinson's logarithmic welfare function and is the limit of the general formula as epsilon tends to 1." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Atkinson equally distributed equivalent health at epsilon of 1" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Asaria M, Griffin S, Cookson R. Distributional cost-effectiveness analysis: a tutorial. Medical Decision Making. 2016;36(1):8-19. Table 8, which reports the Atkinson index at epsilon of 1 for the QALE distributions of the bowel cancer screening strategies, 0.00171 for no screening. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X15583266",
        "Atkinson AB. On the measurement of inequality. Journal of Economic Theory. 1970;2(3):244-263. Page 251, equation (5), which gives the welfare function as the natural logarithm of income when epsilon equals 1. https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-0531(70)90039-6" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Zero or negative values in the geometric-mean EDE. Applying the geometric mean to a health measure that can be zero or negative, such as QALYs gained or net health benefit, fails: Excel GEOMEAN returns #NUM!, and a single zero computed by hand gives an EDE of zero and an index of 1. The formula needs levels of health, such as QALE, not changes." ;
    hemo:9000014 "h_EDE = exp(sum_(i=1)^n [log(h_i)] / n)" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>h_EDE</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"transc1\">exp</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">sum</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"interval1\">integer_interval</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>n</ci></apply><bind><csymbol cd=\"fns1\">lambda</csymbol><bvar><ci>i</ci></bvar><apply><csymbol cd=\"transc1\">ln</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"hemo1\" cdbase=\"https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/cd\">element</csymbol><ci>h_i</ci><ci>i</ci></apply></apply></bind></apply><ci>n</ci></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "h_{\\mathrm{EDE}} = \\exp\\left(\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\left[\\ln\\left(h_{i}_{i}\\right)\\right]}{n}\\right)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ATK-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "atkinson-equally-distributed-equivalent-health-at-epsilon-of-1" .

hemo:5000620 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Continuously discounted total of an even yearly flow over a time horizon"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-DISC-003" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-DISC-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000133 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003459 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002629,
        hemo:6002630,
        hemo:6002631,
        hemo:6002632,
        hemo:6002633 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002630,
        hemo:6002631,
        hemo:6002633 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002629 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003457,
        hemo:7003458 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003460,
        hemo:7003461,
        hemo:7003462,
        hemo:7003468,
        hemo:7006003 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000625 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000604,
        hemo:8000611 ;
    hemo:1000034 hemo:4000016 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Treats the amount q as arising evenly through each year instead of at one point, and integrates it against the continuous discount factor exp(minus rho t) from 0 to T. Larson gives the same integral, with an amount of 1, for years of life lost. The continuous rate rho, the natural log of (1 + r), gives the same factor at every whole year as the annual rate r, so the result lies between the end-of-year and start-of-year totals of HE-FM-DISC-001 and HE-FM-DISC-002. The function log is the natural logarithm." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Continuously discounted total of an even yearly flow over a time horizon" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Larson BA. Calculating disability-adjusted-life-years lost (DALYs) in discrete-time. Cost Effectiveness and Resource Allocation. 2013;11 article 18. Equation 1, the integral of exp(minus rt) from 0 to L, equal to (1/r)(1 minus exp(minus rL)), with 8.6394 for 10 years at 3%, the statement that an annual rate of exp(0.03) minus 1 gives the identical factor, and Table 3 with the continuous-time adjustment factor. https://doi.org/10.1186/1478-7547-11-18" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Annual discount rate used as the continuous rate for an even flow. Putting 0.035 in place of rho for the article's gain gives about 14.86 discounted QALYs instead of 14.97 (computed here for illustration). The annual and continuous rates differ, and the continuous rate is the natural log of (1 + r).",
        "Continuous QALY totals set against end-of-year cost totals. Discounting QALYs continuously while costs or the comparator use end-of-year counting mixes conventions. In the article's example the cost per QALY at 3.5% falls from about GBP 20,389 to GBP 20,041, about 1.7% lower (computed here for illustration), only because of the convention." ;
    hemo:9000014 "rho = log(1 + r); Q_c = q * (1 - exp(-rho * T)) / rho" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"logic1\">and</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>rho</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"transc1\">ln</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">plus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>r</ci></apply></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>Q_c</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><ci>q</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><apply><csymbol cd=\"transc1\">exp</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">unary_minus</csymbol><ci>rho</ci></apply><ci>T</ci></apply></apply></apply></apply><ci>rho</ci></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\rho = \\ln\\left(1 + r\\right) ;\\quad Q_{c} = \\frac{q \\, \\left(1 - \\exp\\left(-\\rho \\, T\\right)\\right)}{\\rho}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-DISC-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "continuously-discounted-total-of-an-even-yearly-flow-over-a-time-horizon" .

hemo:5000621 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Discounted cost per QALY of a one-off treatment cost with a level yearly QALY gain"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-DISC-004" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-DISC-004" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000133 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003476 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002634,
        hemo:6002635,
        hemo:6002636,
        hemo:6002637,
        hemo:6002638 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002635,
        hemo:6002636,
        hemo:6002637,
        hemo:6002638 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002634 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003474,
        hemo:7003475 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003477,
        hemo:7003478,
        hemo:7003484 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000651 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000634 ;
    hemo:1000030 hemo:5000618 ;
    hemo:1000034 hemo:4000012 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 rheo:0000032 ;
    hemo:9000002 heto:4201203 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Divides an incremental cost paid entirely at the start (year 0), which discounting leaves unchanged, by the discounted total of a level yearly QALY gain counted at the end of each year, as in HE-FM-DISC-001. Only the QALYs shrink, so the ratio rises with the rate. This is the calculation behind the article's worked example." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Discounted cost per QALY of a one-off treatment cost with a level yearly QALY gain" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Attema AE, Brouwer WBF, Claxton K. Discounting in economic evaluations. PharmacoEconomics. 2018;36(7) 745-758. Section on discounting health, where healthcare resources are ultimately transformed into health, so that if they are discounted health effects should be discounted at the same rate. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40273-018-0672-z",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance, the manual (PMG36). Chapter 4, economic evaluation. Published 31 January 2022, last updated 13 October 2026. Sections 4.5.1 (costs and health effects discounted at the same rate of 3.5% a year), 4.5.3 (1.5% a year for both when all the criteria are met) and 4.5.4 (a highly plausible case for the maintenance of benefits). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Expecting discounting to leave a one-off treatment ratio unchanged. A common rate leaves the ratio unchanged only when costs and QALYs fall in the same years in the same proportions, as for a lifelong drug. With the whole cost in year 0 the ratio rises by about 63% at 3.5% over 30 years.",
        "Reading the 1.5 per cent ratio as the reference-case result. Lowering the rate from 3.5% to 1.5% cuts the article's ratio from about GBP 20,389 to GBP 15,615. The 1.5% result is a non-reference-case analysis, and NICE also asks for a highly plausible case that the benefit is maintained." ;
    hemo:9000014 "ICER = C_0 * r / (q * (1 - (1 + r)^(-T)))" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>ICER</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><ci>C_0</ci><ci>r</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><ci>q</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">power</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">plus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>r</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">unary_minus</csymbol><ci>T</ci></apply></apply></apply></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{ICER} = \\frac{C_{0} \\, r}{q \\, \\left(1 - \\left(1 + r\\right)^{-T}\\right)}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-DISC-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "discounted-cost-per-qaly-of-a-one-off-treatment-cost-with-a-level-yearly-qaly-gain" .

hemo:5000638 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Population EVSI for a constant annual beneficiary population over the decision's lifetime"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-EVSI-004" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EVSI-004" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000137 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003650 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002725,
        hemo:6002726,
        hemo:6002727,
