        hemo:6002730 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002726,
        hemo:6002727,
        hemo:6002728,
        hemo:6002729 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002725 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003648,
        hemo:7003649 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003651,
        hemo:7003652,
        hemo:7003653,
        hemo:7003660,
        hemo:7003667 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000752 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000727,
        hemo:8000744 ;
    hemo:1000030 hemo:5000619 ;
    hemo:1000034 hemo:4000013 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000100 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-21" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Scales per-person EVSI to everyone whose treatment the study result would inform, N people a year for Y years, with later cohorts discounted at rate r, the population value of research in equation 15 of the second ISPOR value of information report. The factor D is the present value of one person a year in years 0 to Y minus 1, so the first cohort is not discounted, as in population EVPI (HE-FM-VOI-002), which allows a different N_t in each period. Population EVSI is the figure set against the cost of the study, and their difference, the expected net benefit of sampling, has its own package." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Population EVSI for a constant annual beneficiary population over the decision's lifetime" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Fenwick E, Steuten L, Knies S, Ghabri S, Basu A, Murray JF, Koffijberg HE, Strong M, Sanders Schmidler GD, Rothery C. Value of information analysis for research decisions. An introduction. Report 1 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(2):139-150. The section on the beneficiary population and Good Practice Recommendation 3 ask analysts to justify the effective time horizon and explore alternatives in scenario analyses, because VOI estimates can be highly sensitive to it, and note that the beneficiary population is usually adjusted for the time a study takes to finish. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2020.01.001",
        "Rothery C, Strong M, Koffijberg HE, Basu A, Ghabri S, Knies S, Murray JF, Sanders Schmidler GD, Steuten L, Fenwick E. Value of information analytical methods. Report 2 of the ISPOR Value of Information Analysis Emerging Good Practices Task Force. Value in Health. 2020;23(3):277-286. Equation 15 gives population value of information as the per-person value times the sum over periods t from 0 to T of the incidence I_t divided by (1+d)^t, and the text states that the value of research is discounted over the time horizon and that its benefits are realised only when the study findings are reported. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2020.01.004" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Counting patients treated before the study reports in population EVSI. If S1 takes two years to report, only the last three of the five annual cohorts can benefit, and population EVSI at 3.5% is about GBP 395,207 instead of GBP 682,270. Counting every cohort from year 0 overstates the value of a slow study.",
        "Per-patient EVSI compared with the total cost of a study. Setting S1's GBP 73 per patient against an illustrative study cost of GBP 0.4 million makes the study look far too expensive, while its population EVSI over 10,000 future patients is about GBP 0.73 million undiscounted. Per-person values are scaled to the beneficiary population before any comparison with study cost." ;
    hemo:9000014 "D = (1 + r) * (1 - (1 + r)^(-Y)) / r; EVSI_pop = EVSI * N * D" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"logic1\">and</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>D</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">plus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>r</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">power</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">plus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>r</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">unary_minus</csymbol><ci>Y</ci></apply></apply></apply></apply><ci>r</ci></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>EVSI_pop</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><ci>EVSI</ci><ci>N</ci><ci>D</ci></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "D = \\frac{\\left(1 + r\\right) \\, \\left(1 - \\left(1 + r\\right)^{-Y}\\right)}{r} ;\\quad \\mathrm{EVSI}_{\\mathrm{pop}} = \\mathrm{EVSI} \\, N \\, D" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-EVSI-004" ;
    hemo:9000023 "population-evsi-for-a-constant-annual-beneficiary-population-over-the-decision-s-lifetime" .

hemo:5000645 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "DALYs averted and cost per DALY averted by a programme against its comparator"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-DALY-003" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-DALY-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000139 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003749 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002764,
        hemo:6002765,
        hemo:6002766,
        hemo:6002767,
        hemo:6002768,
        hemo:6002769 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002765,
        hemo:6002766,
        hemo:6002768,
        hemo:6002769 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002767 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003747,
        hemo:7003748 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003750,
        hemo:7003751,
        hemo:7003758 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000808 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000794,
        hemo:8000800 ;
    hemo:1000031 hemo:5000890 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Subtracts the DALYs a cohort incurs with the programme from those it incurs without it, and divides the extra cost by the difference. Because DALYs count health lost, the comparator's DALYs come first in the denominator, which makes the ratio HE-FM-ICER-001 with the effect measured as DALYs averted. Both scenarios cover the whole cohort over the same time horizon, so deaths and disability both count, unlike HE-FM-DWT-005, where only disability weights change." ;
    hemo:9000009 "DALYs averted and cost per DALY averted by a programme against its comparator" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Bertram MY, Lauer JA, De Joncheere K, Edejer T, Hutubessy R, Kieny MP, Hill SR. Cost-effectiveness thresholds, pros and cons. Bulletin of the World Health Organization. 2016;94(12) 925-930 (full text read). The 2001 Commission on Macroeconomics and Health thresholds based on multiples of GDP per capita, the 2005 WHO-CHOICE wording of one and three times per capita income per DALY averted, and the statement that none of these thresholds should be used alone as a decision rule for funding. https://doi.org/10.2471/BLT.15.164418",
        "Bertram MY, Lauer JA, Stenberg K, Tan Torres Edejer T. Methods for the economic evaluation of health care interventions for priority setting in the health system, an update from WHO CHOICE. International Journal of Health Policy and Management. 2021;10(11) 673-677 (full text read). The update keeps cost per DALY because it is the preferred outcome metric in low- and middle-income settings, presents health benefits undiscounted and discounted at 3% with costs at 3%, and recommends against using a cost-effectiveness threshold as a decision rule. https://doi.org/10.34172/ijhpm.2020.244",
        "Fox-Rushby JA, Hanson K. Calculating and presenting disability adjusted life years (DALYs) in cost-effectiveness analysis. Health Policy and Planning. 2001;16(3) 326-331 (abstract read). DALYs used as an outcome indicator in economic evaluations and league tables, the finding that many analyses are not comparable or transferable because their assumptions differ or are unclear, and a set of minimum reporting criteria. https://doi.org/10.1093/heapol/16.3.326",
        "Neumann PJ, Anderson JE, Panzer AD, Pope EF, D'Cruz BN, Kim DD, Cohen JT. Comparing the cost-per-QALYs gained and cost-per-DALYs averted literatures. Gates Open Research. 2018;2 article 5 (full text read). Background, cost per DALY averted as one of the two common metrics of cost-effectiveness analysis; Introduction, QALY weights run from 0 for dead to 1 for perfect health and reflect health state attributes, while for DALYs 1 corresponds to dead and each weight corresponds to a specific disease. https://doi.org/10.12688/gatesopenres.12786.2" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Denominator taken as the programme's DALYs less the comparator's. Copying the QALY form E_1 minus E_0 with DALYs gives 552 less 1,440, or minus 888, and a ratio of minus USD 500, which reads as a programme that saves money and loses health. With a loss measure the comparator's DALYs come first.",
        "Multiples of GDP per capita applied as a decision rule for cost per DALY averted. Calling the article's USD 500 per DALY averted cost-effective because it lies below one or three times GDP per capita applies a rule that Bertram and colleagues, writing for WHO in 2016, said should not be used alone as a decision rule for funding. The 2021 WHO-CHOICE update recommends against using any threshold as a decision rule.",
        "Reading a cost per DALY averted against a cost per QALY threshold. A cost per DALY averted is not read directly against a threshold set per QALY gained. DALY weights run the other way, with 1 for death, and each corresponds to a specific disease, while QALY weights value health state attributes, and burden DALYs use a standard life table where QALYs follow modelled survival." ;
    hemo:9000014 "DALY_av = DALY_0 - DALY_1; ICER = (C_1 - C_0) / DALY_av" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"logic1\">and</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>DALY_av</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><ci>DALY_0</ci><ci>DALY_1</ci></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>ICER</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><ci>C_1</ci><ci>C_0</ci></apply><ci>DALY_av</ci></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{DALY}_{\\mathrm{av}} = \\mathrm{DALY}_{0} - \\mathrm{DALY}_{1} ;\\quad \\mathrm{ICER} = \\frac{C_{1} - C_{0}}{\\mathrm{DALY}_{\\mathrm{av}}}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-DALY-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "dalys-averted-and-cost-per-daly-averted-by-a-programme-against-its-comparator" .

hemo:5000702 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Foreign price or cost converted to the home currency at a market exchange rate"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-BOC-001" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-BOC-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000156 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7004388 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6003126,
        hemo:6003127,
        hemo:6003128 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6003127,
        hemo:6003128 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6003126 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7004386,
        hemo:7004387 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7004389,
        hemo:7004390,
        hemo:7004391,
        hemo:7004395,
        hemo:7004399 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000991 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000985 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000008 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-10" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Multiplies a price or cost observed in another country's currency by the exchange rate expressed as home-currency units per unit of that currency, the p_i e_i term of the article's reference-price rule. The same step converts any cost record between currencies. A rate published the other way round, as local currency units per US dollar in the GLOBE Model final rule, is divided into the price instead. The converted prices feed the basket aggregates of HE-FM-BOC-003, and conversion at purchasing power parity is HE-FM-BOC-002." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Foreign price or cost converted to the home currency at a market exchange rate" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services. Global Benchmark for Efficient Drug Pricing (GLOBE) Model. Final rule. Federal Register. 2026;91:62936 (published 2 October 2026). Preamble section II.G.6.b (page 63037), which converts net sales from local currency to US dollars by dividing by an exchange rate with 3 decimal places expressed as local currency units per US dollar, 0.800 for reference country A; section II.G.6.c on exchange rate considerations; and 42 CFR 513.610(c)(2)(i)(A)(3) (page 63113), one rate per reference country for the quarter from the World Bank Atlas, IMF, Federal Reserve or country-specific sources. https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/10/02/2026-20281/global-benchmark-for-efficient-drug-pricing-globe-model",
        "Patented Medicine Prices Review Board. Guidelines for PMPRB Staff, in effect 1 January 2026. Paragraph 47, under Review Process, which states that staff convert local currency prices filed for the Schedule Countries into Canadian dollars using exchange rates calculated as the simple average of the thirty-six monthly average noon spot exchange rates published by the Bank of Canada. https://www.canada.ca/en/patented-medicine-prices-review/services/legislation/guidelines.html",
        "Turner HC, Lauer JA, Tran BX, Teerawattananon Y, Jit M. Adjusting for inflation and currency changes within health economic studies. Value in Health. 2019;22(9):1026-1032. Box 1 (page 1027), which defines the market exchange rate as the rate that determines a currency's value in relation to other currencies; the section on the choice of the output currency (page 1029), which notes that market exchange rates can vary significantly even over short periods and gives the Myanmar rates of MMK5.44 per US dollar in 2011 and MMK640.65 in 2012; and Figure 1 (page 1030), which converts VND2,360,037 in 2016 prices to US$107.59 at 21,935 dong per US dollar. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2019.03.021" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Exchange rate applied in the wrong direction. Multiplying by a rate published as local currency units per US dollar, in place of dividing by it, turns 120 at 0.800 into 96 US dollars instead of 150, a 36% understatement (computed here for illustration). For a currency quoted at thousands of units per US dollar the result is wrong by the square of the rate.",
        "Exchange rate taken from a different period than the price. Turner and colleagues report that the market rate for Myanmar moved from 5.44 kyat per US dollar in 2011 to 640.65 in 2012. Converting 1,000 kyat at the 2011 rate gives about US$183.82 and at the 2012 rate about US$1.56, a factor of about 118 (computed here for illustration), so the rate period is matched to the price and reported." ;
    hemo:9000014 "c_i = p_i * e_i" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>c_i</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><ci>p_i</ci><ci>e_i</ci></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "c_{i} = p_{i} \\, e_{i}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-BOC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "foreign-price-or-cost-converted-to-the-home-currency-at-a-market-exchange-rate" ;
    hemo:9000026 rheo:0000082 .

