    hemo:9000023 "time-homogeneous-cohort-state-update" ;
    hemo:9000026 rheo:0000134 .

hemo:5000196 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Incremental net monetary benefit"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "NICE PMG36 section 6.3.2 asks for net benefit at GBP 25,000 and at GBP 35,000 per QALY. The flagship's ICER of GBP 29,360 lies between them, so its INMB changes sign between the two values. An INMB of exactly 0 counts as cost-effective, the tie convention RHEO cq38 uses."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "Drummond et al. 2015; NICE 2022 (PMG36, last updated 13 October 2026), section 6.3.2, checked 19 October 2026; the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-NMB-002" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-NMB-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000013 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000203 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000577,
        hemo:6000578,
        hemo:6000579,
        hemo:6000580 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000577,
        hemo:6000578,
        hemo:6000579 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000580 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000201,
        hemo:7000202 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000204,
        hemo:7000205,
        hemo:7000206,
        hemo:7000211,
        hemo:7007332,
        hemo:7007337 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000139 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000131 ;
    hemo:1000030 hemo:5000195 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000011 ;
    hemo:9000002 heto:4201203 ;
    hemo:9000003 "rheo 0.3.0 cq38-results-against-threshold.rq" ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-11" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Calculates the difference in net monetary benefit between an intervention and its comparator at threshold lambda. A positive value favours the intervention in every quadrant of the cost-effectiveness plane." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Incremental net monetary benefit" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Stinnett AA, Mullahy J. Net health benefits: a new framework for the analysis of uncertainty in cost-effectiveness analysis. Medical Decision Making. 1998;18(2 Suppl):S68-S80. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X98018002S09",
        "Tambour M, Zethraeus N, Johannesson M. A note on confidence intervals in cost-effectiveness analysis. International Journal of Technology Assessment in Health Care. 1998;14(3):467-471. https://doi.org/10.1017/S0266462300011442" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Applying the ICER rule when Delta_E is negative. In the south-west quadrant an ICER below lambda means that the savings per QALY forgone are too small, so reading it as favourable reverses the conclusion that INMB gives directly.",
        "Reading a positive INMB as affordability. INMB compares value at a threshold and says nothing about budget impact or the final adoption decision." ;
    hemo:9000014 "INMB = lambda * Delta_E - Delta_C" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>INMB</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><ci>lambda</ci><ci>Delta_E</ci></apply><ci>Delta_C</ci></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{INMB} = \\lambda \\, \\Delta_{E} - \\Delta_{C}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-NMB-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "incremental-net-monetary-benefit" ;
    hemo:9000026 rheo:0000117 .

hemo:5000207 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Per-person EVPI from probabilistic draws"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-VOI-001" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-VOI-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000018 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000291 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000635,
        hemo:6000636,
        hemo:6000637,
        hemo:6000638,
        hemo:6003731 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000636,
        hemo:6000637,
        hemo:6000638 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000635 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000288,
        hemo:7000289,
        hemo:7000290 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000292,
        hemo:7000293,
        hemo:7000298 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000175 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000162 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000015,
        heao:0000075 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-17" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Averages, over S equally weighted draws, the highest net benefit available in each draw, the value of choosing with perfect information, and subtracts the highest expected net benefit among the options, the value of the decision made on current information. The result is the expected opportunity loss of the current decision per person and an upper bound on the value of research for that person." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Per-person EVPI from probabilistic draws" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Briggs A, Claxton K, Sculpher M. Decision Modelling for Health Economic Evaluation. Oxford: Oxford University Press; 2006. https://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780198526629.001.0001",
        "Claxton K. The irrelevance of inference: a decision-making approach to the stochastic evaluation of health care technologies. Journal of Health Economics. 1999;18(3):341-364. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0167-6296(98)00039-3" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Maximising within each draw for the current decision. Taking the best option in each draw for the second term gives current information the benefit of perfect information and reduces EVPI to zero.",
        "Reading a high error probability as high EVPI. EVPI combines the probability of a wrong decision with the size of the loss. In the worked example on the CEAC page, the chosen option is wrong in 80% of draws but EVPI is only £200 per person, because the losses are small." ;
    hemo:9000014 "EVPI = sum_(s=1)^S [NBmax_s] / S - ENB_cur" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>EVPI</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">sum</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"interval1\">integer_interval</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>S</ci></apply><bind><csymbol cd=\"fns1\">lambda</csymbol><bvar><ci>s</ci></bvar><apply><csymbol cd=\"hemo1\" cdbase=\"https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/cd\">element</csymbol><ci>NBmax_s</ci><ci>s</ci></apply></bind></apply><ci>S</ci></apply><ci>ENB_cur</ci></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{EVPI} = \\frac{\\sum_{s=1}^{S} \\left[\\mathrm{NBmax}_{s}_{s}\\right]}{S} - \\mathrm{ENB}_{\\mathrm{cur}}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-VOI-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "per-person-evpi-from-probabilistic-draws" .

hemo:5000213 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Incremental net health benefit at threshold k"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-CET-002" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-CET-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000020 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000350 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000660,
        hemo:6000661,
        hemo:6000662,
        hemo:6000663,
        hemo:6000664 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000662,
        hemo:6000663,
        hemo:6000664 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000660,
        hemo:6000661 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000348,
        hemo:7000349 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000351,
        hemo:7000352,
        hemo:7000353,
        hemo:7000359,
        hemo:7007352,
        hemo:7007358 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000221 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000207,
        hemo:8000213 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000012,
        rheo:0000155 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-12" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Converts the incremental cost of an intervention into the health expected to be displaced elsewhere, Delta_C divided by k, and subtracts it from the incremental health gain. A positive value means population health increases overall; a negative value means the health gained does not offset the health lost from care not funded elsewhere. It gives the same decision as incremental net monetary benefit at the same threshold." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Incremental net health benefit at threshold k" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Claxton K, Martin S, Soares M, Rice N, Spackman E, Hinde S, Devlin N, Smith PC, Sculpher M. Methods for the estimation of the National Institute for Health and Care Excellence cost-effectiveness threshold. Health Technology Assessment. 2015;19(14):1-503, v-vi. https://doi.org/10.3310/hta19140",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 31 March 2026. Chapter 6 Committee recommendations, sections 6.2.11 (modifiers applied qualitatively or through QALY weighting), 6.3.1 (the maximum acceptable ICER reflects the opportunity cost of programmes displaced) and 6.3.2 (net health benefits at £25,000 and £35,000 per QALY, with and without QALY weighting; positive values increase population health). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations",
        "Stinnett AA, Mullahy J. Net health benefits: a new framework for the analysis of uncertainty in cost-effectiveness analysis. Medical Decision Making. 1998;18(2 Suppl):S68-S80. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X98018002S09" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Reading an ICER below NICE's range as a health gain. An ICER below £25,000 per QALY can still reduce population health if the opportunity cost of spending is lower. In the worked example the ICER is £15,000 per QALY, yet INHB is negative at k of £12,936 per QALY.",
        "Treating negative unweighted INHB as automatic rejection. NICE states that a technology with negative unweighted net health benefit may still be recommended when decision-making modifiers apply, because the health it produces is given greater value." ;
    hemo:9000014 "H_d = Delta_C / k; INHB = Delta_E - Delta_C / k" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"logic1\">and</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>H_d</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><ci>Delta_C</ci><ci>k</ci></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>INHB</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><ci>Delta_E</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><ci>Delta_C</ci><ci>k</ci></apply></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "H_{d} = \\frac{\\Delta_{C}}{k} ;\\quad \\mathrm{INHB} = \\Delta_{E} - \\frac{\\Delta_{C}}{k}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-CET-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "incremental-net-health-benefit-at-threshold-k" .

hemo:5000215 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Constant rate from a transition probability"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "Fleurence and Hollenbeak 2007; the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-TP-002" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-TP-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000021 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000378 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000668,
        hemo:6000669,
        hemo:6000670 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000669,
        hemo:6000670 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000668 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000377 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000379,
        hemo:7000380,
        hemo:7000384 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000255 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000245 ;
    hemo:1000028 hemo:5000214 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000002 heto:4201802 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Recovers the constant rate implied by a probability observed over an interval of length Delta. The function log is the natural logarithm. The rate can then be rescaled to any cycle length or combined with a hazard ratio." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Constant rate from a transition probability" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Gidwani R, Russell LB. Estimating transition probabilities from published evidence: a tutorial for decision modelers. PharmacoEconomics. 2020;38(11):1153-1164. Equation 8 and the worked 12-month to 3-month example. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40273-020-00937-z" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Using Excel LOG instead of LN. Excel LOG uses base 10 by default. The formula with LOG gives about 0.0969 per year for a one-year probability of 0.20 instead of 0.2231, understating the rate by a factor of about 2.3." ;
    hemo:9000014 "h = -log(1 - p) / Delta" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>h</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">unary_minus</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"transc1\">ln</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>p</ci></apply></apply></apply><ci>Delta</ci></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "h = \\frac{-\\ln\\left(1 - p\\right)}{\\Delta}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-TP-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "constant-rate-from-a-transition-probability" .

hemo:5000240 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Severity QALY weight from the shortfall bands"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "HEMO takes the severity cut-offs and weights as inputs, because they are NICE policy figures that can change. The module writes 12, 18, 0.85, 0.95, 0.2 and 0.5 into the formula."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "NICE 2022 (PMG36, last updated 13 October 2026), section 6.2.18 and table 6.1, checked 18 October 2026; the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-ASF-003" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ASF-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000029 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000553 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000765,
        hemo:6000766,
        hemo:6000767,
        hemo:6005590,
        hemo:6005591,
        hemo:6005592,
        hemo:6005593,
        hemo:6005594,
        hemo:6005595 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000766,
        hemo:6000767,
        hemo:6005590,
        hemo:6005591,
        hemo:6005592,
        hemo:6005593,
        hemo:6005594,
        hemo:6005595 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000765 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000551,
        hemo:7000552 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000554,
        hemo:7000555,
        hemo:7000559,
        hemo:7000563 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000369 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000343 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000018,
        heao:0000050 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-14" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Returns the NICE severity weight from absolute and proportional shortfall. The weight is 1.7 if absolute shortfall is at least 18 or proportional shortfall at least 0.95, 1.2 if absolute shortfall is at least 12 or proportional shortfall at least 0.85, and 1 otherwise. The measure implying greater severity decides, and a value on a cut-off takes the higher band. Each comparison returns 1 when true and 0 when false." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Severity QALY weight from the shortfall bands" ;
    hemo:9000010 "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 13 October 2026. Chapter 6 Committee recommendations, section 6.2.18 and table 6.1 (weights of 1, 1.2 and 1.7; proportional shortfall cut-offs 0.85 and 0.95; absolute shortfall cut-offs 12 and 18; whichever implies greater severity; values on a cut-off take the higher level), sections 6.2.12, 6.2.19 and 6.2.20 (scope) and section 6.3.2 (net health benefit with and without the QALY weighting). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2",
        "Wailoo A. NICE DSU Technical Support Document 23: A guide to calculating severity shortfall for NICE evaluations. Sheffield: Decision Support Unit, ScHARR, University of Sheffield; January 2024, updated September 2026. Section 1 (weights are applied to incremental QALYs and the definition giving the highest weight is used) and section 6 (judgement when estimates are close to a threshold). https://doi.org/10.15131/shef.data.33850720" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Applying the weight to total QALYs. The weight multiplies the incremental QALYs of the new technology over current care, not the total QALYs that patients would have anyway.",
        "Taking the weight from one measure only. Checking only proportional shortfall gives a weight of 1.2 in the young-population example, where absolute shortfall of 18.5 gives 1.7. Both measures are assessed and the higher weight is used." ;
    hemo:9000014 "w = 1 + (w_a - 1) * ((AS >= AS_a) or (PS >= PS_a)) + (w_b - w_a) * ((AS >= AS_b) or (PS >= PS_b))" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>w</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">plus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><ci>w_a</ci><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"hemo1\" cdbase=\"https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/cd\">indicator</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"logic1\">or</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">geq</csymbol><ci>AS</ci><ci>AS_a</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">geq</csymbol><ci>PS</ci><ci>PS_a</ci></apply></apply></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><ci>w_b</ci><ci>w_a</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"hemo1\" cdbase=\"https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/cd\">indicator</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"logic1\">or</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">geq</csymbol><ci>AS</ci><ci>AS_b</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">geq</csymbol><ci>PS</ci><ci>PS_b</ci></apply></apply></apply></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "w = 1 + \\left(w_{a} - 1\\right) \\, \\mathbb{1}\\left[\\mathrm{AS} \\ge \\mathrm{AS}_{a} \\lor \\mathrm{PS} \\ge \\mathrm{PS}_{a}\\right] + \\left(w_{b} - w_{a}\\right) \\, \\mathbb{1}\\left[\\mathrm{AS} \\ge \\mathrm{AS}_{b} \\lor \\mathrm{PS} \\ge \\mathrm{PS}_{b}\\right]" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ASF-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "severity-qaly-weight-from-the-shortfall-bands" ;
    hemo:9000030 "w = 1 + 0.2 * ((AS >= 12) or (PS >= 0.85)) + 0.5 * ((AS >= 18) or (PS >= 0.95))" .

hemo:5000242 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Total cost from resource quantities and unit costs"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-COST-001" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-COST-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000031 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000578 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000771,
        hemo:6000772,
        hemo:6000773,
        hemo:6000774,
        hemo:6003732 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000772,
        hemo:6000773,
        hemo:6000774 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000771 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000575,
        hemo:7000576,
        hemo:7000577 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000579,
        hemo:7000580,
        hemo:7000585 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000391 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000378 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000004,
        rheo:0000150,
        rheo:0000151 ;
    hemo:9000002 heto:4202001,
        heto:4202002 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-08" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Multiplies the quantity of each resource used by its unit cost and adds the products over all resource types. Keeping quantities and unit costs separate shows whether a cost difference comes from different resource use or from different prices, and lets an analyst in another setting substitute local unit costs. The same sum over options gives the cost terms C_j used in the ICER and net monetary benefit." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Total cost from resource quantities and unit costs" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Drummond MF, Sculpher MJ, Claxton K, Stoddart GL, Torrance GW. Methods for the Economic Evaluation of Health Care Programmes. 4th ed. Oxford: Oxford University Press; 2015. Costing chapter: identification, measurement and valuation of resource use, with total cost as quantities multiplied by unit costs. https://global.oup.com/academic/product/methods-for-the-economic-evaluation-of-health-care-programmes-9780199665884",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 31 March 2026. Chapter 4 Economic evaluation, sections 4.2.7 and 4.2.9 (NHS and PSS perspective on costs), 4.4.1 (resources valued at prices relevant to the NHS and PSS, identified systematically) and 4.4.4 (prices that reflect those paid in the NHS, including known price reductions). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2",
        "Tan-Torres Edejer T, Baltussen R, Adam T, Hutubessy R, Acharya A, Evans DB, Murray CJL, editors. Making choices in health: WHO guide to cost-effectiveness analysis. Geneva: World Health Organization; 2003. Section 3.2.1 (distinguishing quantities from the prices used to value them, the ingredients approach, and economic prices reflecting opportunity cost). https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/42699" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Charges or list prices used as unit costs. A charge, tariff or list price reflects negotiation and cross-subsidy and can sit well above or below the resources used. The NICE manual asks for prices that reflect as closely as possible those paid in the NHS.",
        "Overheads counted twice. Adding a separate overhead line to a unit cost that already includes overheads, such as a national average cost per healthcare resource group, counts the same resources twice.",
        "Staff time and unit cost in different units. Multiplying 90 minutes of nurse time by an hourly cost of 60 pounds gives 5,400 pounds instead of 90. Quantities and unit costs are converted to the same unit before they are multiplied." ;
    hemo:9000014 "C = sum_(i=1)^n [q_i * p_i]" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>C</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">sum</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"interval1\">integer_interval</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>n</ci></apply><bind><csymbol cd=\"fns1\">lambda</csymbol><bvar><ci>i</ci></bvar><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"hemo1\" cdbase=\"https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/cd\">element</csymbol><ci>q_i</ci><ci>i</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"hemo1\" cdbase=\"https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/cd\">element</csymbol><ci>p_i</ci><ci>i</ci></apply></apply></bind></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "C = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\left[q_{i}_{i} \\, p_{i}_{i}\\right]" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-COST-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "total-cost-from-resource-quantities-and-unit-costs" .

hemo:5000243 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Price-year adjustment of a cost with an inflation index"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-COST-002" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-COST-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000031 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000592 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000775,
        hemo:6000776,
        hemo:6000777,
        hemo:6000778 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000776,
        hemo:6000777,
        hemo:6000778 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000775 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000590,
        hemo:7000591 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000593,
        hemo:7000594,
        hemo:7000599 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000404 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000396 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000007,
        rheo:0000152 ;
    hemo:9000002 heto:4200802 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-09" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Moves a cost observed in price year a to price year b by multiplying it by the ratio of an appropriate price index in the two years. When the index is published as annual percentage increases, as the NHS Cost Inflation Index is, the index is built by chaining: each year's value is the previous value multiplied by one plus that year's increase. The NICE manual names the NHS Cost Inflation Index and the PSS Pay and Prices Index, both reported in the Unit Costs of Health and Social Care manual, or the ONS consumer price index." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Price-year adjustment of a cost with an inflation index" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Jones K, Weatherly H, Barker A, Birch S, Castelli A, Chalkley M, Dargan A, Findlay D, Hinde S, Markham S, Smith D, Teo H. Unit Costs of Health and Social Care 2025 Manual. Canterbury and York: Care and Outcomes Research Centre, University of Kent, and Centre for Health Economics, University of York; 2026. Section 12.1, table 12.1.1 (NHS Cost Inflation Index, annual percentage increases for NHS providers, including overall 2.47% for 2023/24 and a provisional 4.02% for 2024/25) and tables 12.1.2 to 12.1.4 (PSS Pay and Prices Index), and the scoping review article on the use of these indices to derive 2024/25 unit costs. https://doi.org/10.22024/UniKent/01.02.115569",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 31 March 2026. Chapter 4 Economic evaluation, section 4.4.12 (costs from previous years adjusted using inflation indices appropriate to the cost perspective, such as the NHS cost inflation index and the PSS pay and prices index from the unit costs report, or the ONS consumer price index). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2",
        "Tan-Torres Edejer T, Baltussen R, Adam T, Hutubessy R, Acharya A, Evans DB, Murray CJL, editors. Making choices in health: WHO guide to cost-effectiveness analysis. Geneva: World Health Organization; 2003. Section 3.2.6 (costs from other years adjusted to the base year in constant prices; measures of inflation including the consumer price index and the GDP deflator). https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/42699" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Applying only the latest year's increase. Uprating a 2022/23 cost to 2024/25 prices with the 2024/25 increase alone gives 520.10 pounds instead of about 532.95 pounds. Every year between the two price years is included.",
        "Leaving provisional index values unflagged. The 2024/25 NHS Cost Inflation Index figures are provisional and will be revised in the next manual, so costs uprated with them are reported with the index edition used.",
        "Treating inflation adjustment as discounting. Indexing a cost to a later price year and discounting a future cost to present value answer different questions. A cost is first expressed in the common price year and then, if it falls in a future year of the model, discounted." ;
    hemo:9000014 "C_b = C_a * I_b / I_a" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>C_b</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><ci>C_a</ci><ci>I_b</ci></apply><ci>I_a</ci></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "C_{b} = \\frac{C_{a} \\, I_{b}}{I_{a}}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-COST-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "price-year-adjustment-of-a-cost-with-an-inflation-index" .

hemo:5000293 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Atkinson equally distributed equivalent health for epsilon other than 1"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "The equally distributed equivalent raises health in years to the power 1 minus epsilon and back, so it is homogeneous of degree 1 in health and keeps its unit, which the dimension rule of the 0.1 runner, built for whole-number powers, cannot follow."@en,
        "h_i and h_EDE are quality-adjusted life expectancy, so QALYs. The equally distributed equivalent holds for any health measure on a ratio scale."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-ATK-002" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ATK-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000046 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000952 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6001018,
        hemo:6001019,
        hemo:6001020,
        hemo:6001021,
        hemo:6003733 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6001019,
        hemo:6001020,
        hemo:6001021 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6001018 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000949,
        hemo:7000950,
        hemo:7000951 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000953,
        hemo:7000954,
        hemo:7000955,
        hemo:7000960,
        hemo:7000965 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000472 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000451 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:2000024 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000013,
        heao:0000105 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-15" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives the equally distributed equivalent health as the power mean of the n health values with exponent one minus epsilon. At epsilon of 0 it equals the arithmetic mean, so only total health counts; at epsilon of 2 it is the harmonic mean; and as epsilon rises it moves towards the health of the worst-off person, the maximin rule. Each h_i is the health of one person, or of one of n groups of equal size." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Atkinson equally distributed equivalent health for epsilon other than 1" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Asaria M, Griffin S, Cookson R. Distributional cost-effectiveness analysis: a tutorial. Medical Decision Making. 2016;36(1):8-19. Section on evaluating social distributions of health, which gives the Atkinson social welfare index for EDE health with inequality aversion parameter epsilon. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X15583266",
        "Atkinson AB. On the measurement of inequality. Journal of Economic Theory. 1970;2(3):244-263. Page 251, equation (5), which derives the welfare function under constant relative inequality aversion with epsilon of zero or above, and page 257, which gives the measure for discrete distributions and the limiting cases of epsilon of 0 and epsilon tending to infinity. https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-0531(70)90039-6" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Setting epsilon to exactly 1 in the general Atkinson EDE formula. At epsilon of 1 the outer exponent one over one minus epsilon divides by zero, and Excel returns #DIV/0!. The EDE at epsilon of 1 is the geometric mean, HE-FM-ATK-003, which is the limit of this formula as epsilon tends to 1.",
        "Treating groups of unequal size as equal in the Atkinson EDE. Entering one value per group when the groups differ in size gives each group the same weight. With baseline QALE of 60 and 72 years and the less healthy group making up 20% of the population, the share-weighted EDE at epsilon of 2 is about 69.23 years, whereas entering the two values once each gives about 65.45 years." ;
    hemo:9000014 "h_EDE = (sum_(i=1)^n [h_i^(1-epsilon)] / n)^(1/(1-epsilon))" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>h_EDE</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">power</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">sum</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"interval1\">integer_interval</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>n</ci></apply><bind><csymbol cd=\"fns1\">lambda</csymbol><bvar><ci>i</ci></bvar><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">power</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"hemo1\" cdbase=\"https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/cd\">element</csymbol><ci>h_i</ci><ci>i</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>epsilon</ci></apply></apply></bind></apply><ci>n</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>epsilon</ci></apply></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "h_{\\mathrm{EDE}} = \\left(\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\left[h_{i}_{i}^{1 - \\epsilon}\\right]}{n}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{1 - \\epsilon}}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ATK-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "atkinson-equally-distributed-equivalent-health-for-epsilon-other-than-1" .

hemo:5000648 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "EQ-5D-3L utility as one minus a constant, level decrements and an N3 term"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "HEMO states the form. The coefficients of each named value set are data held in OHEO, not in HEMO. This member of HEMO-HOOK-03 covers the additive form of EQ-5D-3L value sets only. NICE PMG51 of 27 August 2026 moves the reference case to the EQ-5D-5L value set, whose functional form HEMO has not yet checked at source, so no 5L member is stated. N_3 is 1 only when some dimension is at level 3, so N_3 is at most D."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-EQ3L-001" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-EQ3L-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000140 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003782 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002782,
        hemo:6002783,
        hemo:6002784,
        hemo:6002785,
        hemo:6002786,
        hemo:6002787,
        hemo:6002788,
        hemo:6002789,
        hemo:6002790,
        hemo:6002791 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002783,
