hemo:5000022 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Relative hazard function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-HR-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the hazards of an event in a treatment group and a comparator group at the same time t to their ratio. Under proportional hazards the ratio is constant over time, and it can then be applied to a baseline survival curve or a baseline transition probability to obtain absolute outcomes for the treatment group." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Relative hazard function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "g(h_T(t),h_C(t)) = HR(t)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-HR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "relative-hazard-function" .

hemo:5000074 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Binomial distribution of an event count among patients at risk"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-BINOM-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Gives the probability that exactly r of n patients have an event within a fixed period when each patient has the same probability p of the event and outcomes are independent. Read as a function of p for an observed count, the same expression is the binomial likelihood, proportional to p^r (1 minus p)^(n minus r), which peaks at the observed proportion r/n. The formulae below give the probability function, the chance of a zero count, the mean and variance of the count, the estimated probability with its standard error, the Wald and Wilson intervals and the logit model for binomial data in NICE DSU evidence synthesis. The conjugate beta update of a binomial probability is set out on the Beta Distribution page (HE-FM-BETA-002), and the general Wald interval for any estimate on the Asymptotic Normality page (HE-FM-AN-001)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Binomial distribution of an event count among patients at risk" ;
    hemo:9000014 "P(R = r) = C(n, r) * p^r * (1 - p)^(n - r), r = 0, 1, ..., n" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-BINOM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "binomial-distribution-of-an-event-count-among-patients-at-risk" .

hemo:5000075 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Chi-square distribution of a sum of squared standard normal deviates"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-CHISQ-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the degrees of freedom k to the distribution of the sum of k independent squared standard normal variables. Health economic models meet it as the reference for test statistics, each read from the right-hand tail: a likelihood ratio between nested survival models, Cochran's Q for heterogeneity in a meta-analysis and Pearson's statistic for calibration targets. It is the gamma distribution with shape k/2 and scale 2. The formulae for Cochran's Q, I-squared and the between-study variance are on the Aggregate Data Meta-Analysis page (HE-FM-ADMA-002 to HE-FM-ADMA-004). AIC and BIC, which TSD 14 prefers for choosing between survival curves, are on the Akaike Information Criterion page (HE-FM-AIC-001 and HE-FM-AIC-004) and the Bayesian Information Criterion page (HE-FM-BIC-001)." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Chi-square distribution of a sum of squared standard normal deviates" ;
    hemo:9000014 "X = sum_(i=1)^k [Z_i^2], X ~ chi^2_k" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-CHISQ-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "chi-square-distribution-of-a-sum-of-squared-standard-normal-deviates" .

hemo:5000099 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Disability weight scaling of time lived with a non-fatal health state"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-DWT-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 false ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps time lived in a non-fatal health state to healthy time lost by multiplying it by the state's disability weight, on a scale where 0 is equivalent to full health and 1 to death. The products give years lived with disability (YLD), which add to years of life lost to give DALYs. When people have several conditions, weights are combined multiplicatively and the combined loss is shared among the conditions. The notation follows the Disability Weight article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Disability weight scaling of time lived with a non-fatal health state" ;
    hemo:9000014 "YLD = sum_(s) [P_s * DW_s]" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-DWT-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "disability-weight-scaling-of-time-lived-with-a-non-fatal-health-state" .

hemo:5000156 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000012 ;
    rdfs:label "Basket-of-countries reference price and currency conversion function"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FN-BOC-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000347,
        hemo:6000348,
        hemo:6000349,
        hemo:6000350 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000347,
        hemo:6000348,
        hemo:6000349 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000350 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000018 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Maps the prices of one medicine observed in a basket of reference countries, each in its own currency, and the exchange rates or purchasing power parities that express them in one currency, to a domestic reference price under an aggregation rule and to a ceiling for a proposed domestic price. The same conversions move any cost record between currencies. A cost from another year is first moved to the common price year with the live price-year adjustment HE-FM-COST-002 of the resource costing function HE-FN-COST-001, and the index itself belongs to the Consumer Price Index concept. A basket price reflects what other countries list, while the economically justifiable price HE-FM-EJP-001 and the break-even price HE-FM-BEA-001 give the highest price at which a technology is cost-effective at home. Notation follows the Basket of Countries article." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Basket-of-countries reference price and currency conversion function" ;
    hemo:9000014 "r(p_i, e_i, f) = P_ref" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FN-BOC-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "basket-of-countries-reference-price-and-currency-conversion-function" .

hemo:5000189 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Present value of a single future value"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "A single amount at a stated time t needs no discount timing convention, so none is stated."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-DR-001" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-DR-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000010 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000115 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000542,
        hemo:6000543,
        hemo:6000544,
        hemo:6000545 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000543,
        hemo:6000544,
        hemo:6000545 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000542 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000113,
        hemo:7000114 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000116,
        hemo:7000117,
        hemo:7000118,
        hemo:7005977 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000026 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000016 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000027,
        heao:0000044 ;
    hemo:9000002 heto:4201002 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-01" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Converts one cost or health outcome occurring at time t to its value at the reference time." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Present value of a single future value" ;
    hemo:9000010 "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 31 March 2026. Section 4.5, Discounting (4.5.1 to 4.5.5). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Entering a percentage as a whole number. Entering 3.5 instead of 3.5% or 0.035 applies a 350% annual rate and severely understates present value." ;
    hemo:9000014 "PV_t = V_t/(1+r)^t" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>PV_t</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><ci>V_t</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">power</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">plus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>r</ci></apply><ci>t</ci></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{PV}_{t} = \\frac{V_{t}}{\\left(1 + r\\right)^{t}}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-DR-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "present-value-of-a-single-future-value" ;
    hemo:9000026 rheo:0000093 .

hemo:5000193 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "QALYs from periods of constant utility"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-QALY-001" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-QALY-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000012 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000167 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000564,
        hemo:6000565,
        hemo:6000566,
        hemo:6000567,
        hemo:6003729 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000564,
        hemo:6000565,
        hemo:6000566 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000567 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000162,
        hemo:7000163,
        hemo:7000164,
        hemo:7000165,
        hemo:7000166 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000168,
        hemo:7000169,
        hemo:7000170,
        hemo:7000175 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000089 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000076 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000002,
        oheo:0000261,
        rheo:0000091,
        rheo:0000092 ;
    hemo:9000002 heto:4200503 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-04" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Multiplies the utility of each period by the time spent in it, in years, and adds the products across periods. This is the calculation NICE describes for the health states experienced within the time horizon." ;
    hemo:9000009 "QALYs from periods of constant utility" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Drummond MF, Sculpher MJ, Claxton K, Stoddart GL, Torrance GW. Methods for the Economic Evaluation of Health Care Programmes. 4th ed. Oxford: Oxford University Press; 2015. https://global.oup.com/academic/product/methods-for-the-economic-evaluation-of-health-care-programmes-9780199665884",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 31 March 2026. Chapter 4 Economic evaluation, sections 4.2.15 (assumptions underlying the QALY), 4.3.2 (time in each health state multiplied by its utility, valued relative to perfect health and death), 4.3.5 (different methods produce utility values that cannot always be compared) and 4.5.1 (3.5% a year for costs and health effects). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Adding QALYs from different value sets. QALYs calculated with different instruments or tariffs are not on a common scale, so adding them across periods or comparing them between options mixes incompatible measures.",
        "Durations left in months. Entering a six-month period as 6 rather than 0.5 multiplies that period's QALYs by 12.",
        "Leaving the timing convention unstated when discounting. Three years at utility 0.80 discounted at 3.5% give 2.320 QALYs when each year is counted at its start but 2.241 when counted at its end, so the convention is reported and applied to every option.",
        "Replacing a missing utility with zero. A missing observation is unknown. Setting it to zero equates it with death and understates QALYs; an explicit interpolation, imputation or modelling rule is needed." ;
    hemo:9000014 "QALY = sum_(t=1)^T [u_t * d_t]" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>QALY</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">sum</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"interval1\">integer_interval</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>T</ci></apply><bind><csymbol cd=\"fns1\">lambda</csymbol><bvar><ci>t</ci></bvar><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"hemo1\" cdbase=\"https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/cd\">element</csymbol><ci>u_t</ci><ci>t</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"hemo1\" cdbase=\"https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/cd\">element</csymbol><ci>d_t</ci><ci>t</ci></apply></apply></bind></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{QALY} = \\sum_{t=1}^{T} \\left[u_{t}_{t} \\, d_{t}_{t}\\right]" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-QALY-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "qalys-from-periods-of-constant-utility" .

hemo:5000195 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Option-level net monetary benefit"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "Drummond et al. 2015; the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-NMB-001" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-NMB-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000013 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000194 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000573,
        hemo:6000574,
        hemo:6000575,
        hemo:6000576 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000573,
        hemo:6000574,
        hemo:6000575 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000576 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000192,
        hemo:7000193 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000195,
        hemo:7000196 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000126 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000118 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 heao:0000011,
        heao:0000030 ;
    hemo:9000002 heto:4201203 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-11" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Calculates the net monetary benefit of a single option at threshold lambda from that option's own expected effect and expected cost." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Option-level net monetary benefit" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Briggs A, Claxton K, Sculpher M. Decision Modelling for Health Economic Evaluation. Oxford: Oxford University Press; 2006. https://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780198526629.001.0001",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 31 March 2026. Chapter 4 Economic evaluation, section 4.2.16 (net health benefits at £25,000 and £35,000 per QALY; net monetary benefits may be shown alongside ICERs and net health benefits). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Reporting NMB without the threshold. An NMB figure has no meaning without the stated lambda, and values calculated at different thresholds, populations or price bases cannot be compared.",
        "Using incremental values in option-level NMB. Calculating NMB_j from incremental cost and effect against another option gives incremental net benefit, not the option's own NMB, and mixes the two quantities when options are ranked." ;
    hemo:9000014 "NMB_j = lambda * E_j - C_j" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>NMB_j</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><ci>lambda</ci><ci>E_j</ci></apply><ci>C_j</ci></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{NMB}_{j} = \\lambda \\, E_{j} - C_{j}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-NMB-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "option-level-net-monetary-benefit" .

hemo:5000216 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Transition probability rescaled to a new cycle length"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "Fleurence and Hollenbeak 2007; the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-TP-003" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-TP-003" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000021 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000390 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000671,
        hemo:6000672,
        hemo:6000673,
        hemo:6000674 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000672,
        hemo:6000673,
        hemo:6000674 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000671 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000388,
        hemo:7000389 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000391,
        hemo:7000392,
        hemo:7000397 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000280 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000268 ;
    hemo:1000030 hemo:5000214 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 rheo:0000016 ;
    hemo:9000002 heto:4201802 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Converts a probability observed over one interval into the probability for a model cycle of a different length, under a constant hazard. It combines the two conversions above without computing the rate explicitly." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Transition probability rescaled to a new cycle length" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Chhatwal J, Jayasuriya S, Elbasha EH. Changing cycle lengths in state-transition models: challenges and solutions. Medical Decision Making. 2016;36(8):952-964. Introduction (dividing an annual transition probability by 12 is not appropriate) and the section on the traditional approach, which gives the rescaling formula and shows that it applies only to two-state models. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X16656165",
        "Gidwani R, Russell LB. Estimating transition probabilities from published evidence: a tutorial for decision modelers. PharmacoEconomics. 2020;38(11):1153-1164. Equation 10 and the section on changing cycle length when there are three or more state transitions. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40273-020-00937-z" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Dividing an annual probability by 12. Dividing 0.20 by 12 gives a monthly probability of about 0.01667. Applied for 12 months it implies an annual probability of about 0.1826 rather than 0.20, so the model understates the event. The error grows with the size of the probability.",
        "Rescaling each exit of a multi-exit state separately. In a state with more than one exit, rescaling each destination probability separately with this formula gives a row that no longer reproduces the source data. In the article's example with annual exit probabilities of about 0.10876 and 0.07251, separate rescaling to three months keeps about 0.8248 of the cohort in the starting state after four cycles rather than 0.8187." ;
    hemo:9000014 "p_new = 1 - (1 - p_old)^(Delta_new / Delta_old)" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>p_new</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">power</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>p_old</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><ci>Delta_new</ci><ci>Delta_old</ci></apply></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "p_{\\mathrm{new}} = 1 - \\left(1 - p_{\\mathrm{old}}\\right)^{\\frac{\\Delta_{\\mathrm{new}}}{\\Delta_{\\mathrm{old}}}}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-TP-003" ;
    hemo:9000023 "transition-probability-rescaled-to-a-new-cycle-length" .

hemo:5000619 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Discounted total of a level yearly amount counted at the start of each year"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-DISC-002" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-DISC-002" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000133 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003439 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002625,
        hemo:6002626,
        hemo:6002627,
        hemo:6002628 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002626,
        hemo:6002627,
        hemo:6002628 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002625 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003437,
        hemo:7003438 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003440,
        hemo:7003441,
        hemo:7003442,
        hemo:7003447,
        hemo:7003452,
        hemo:7005999 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000595 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000576 ;
    hemo:1000029 hemo:5000620 ;
    hemo:1000030 hemo:5000190 ;
    hemo:1000031 hemo:5000837 ;
    hemo:1000034 hemo:4000013 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000008 "Credits each year's amount at the start of the year, so the T amounts fall in years 0 to T minus 1 and the first is not discounted. Every factor is one period earlier than under end-of-year counting, so the total is (1 + r) times the total in HE-FM-DISC-001. Larson describes this convention as the same as a stream of payments each made at the beginning of the year." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Discounted total of a level yearly amount counted at the start of each year" ;
    hemo:9000010 "HM Treasury. The Green Book (2026). London, HM Treasury, updated 5 February 2026. Table 8, Discounting and present values, which applies the factors 1.000 for year 0 to 0.8420 for year 5 to social benefits constant in real terms. https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-green-book-appraisal-and-evaluation-in-central-government/the-green-book-2026",
        "Larson BA. Calculating disability-adjusted-life-years lost (DALYs) in discrete-time. Cost Effectiveness and Resource Allocation. 2013;11 article 18. Table 1 and the text before it, where time runs from 0 to 9 so that each of 10 years is counted at the beginning of the year, the same as a stream of payments each made at the beginning of the year, giving 8.786 at 3%. https://doi.org/10.1186/1478-7547-11-18" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Timing convention changed between costs and QALYs. Counting QALYs at the start of each year while the analysis states end-of-year counting raises the article's discounted gain from 14.71 to 15.23 QALYs and lowers the cost per QALY at 3.5% from about GBP 20,389 to GBP 19,700 (computed here for illustration). The convention is stated and applied the same way to every option." ;
    hemo:9000014 "Q_s = q * (1 + r) * (1 - (1 + r)^(-T)) / r" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>Q_s</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">times</csymbol><ci>q</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">plus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>r</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">power</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">plus</csymbol><cn type=\"integer\">1</cn><ci>r</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">unary_minus</csymbol><ci>T</ci></apply></apply></apply></apply><ci>r</ci></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "Q_{s} = \\frac{q \\, \\left(1 + r\\right) \\, \\left(1 - \\left(1 + r\\right)^{-T}\\right)}{r}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-DISC-002" ;
    hemo:9000023 "discounted-total-of-a-level-yearly-amount-counted-at-the-start-of-each-year" .

