hemo:5000192 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Two-option incremental cost-effectiveness ratio"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-ICER-001" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-ICER-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000011 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000145,
        hemo:7000146 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000559,
        hemo:6000560,
        hemo:6000561,
        hemo:6000562,
        hemo:6000563 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000559,
        hemo:6000560,
        hemo:6000561,
        hemo:6000562 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000563 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000140,
        hemo:7000141,
        hemo:7000142,
        hemo:7000143,
        hemo:7000144 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000147,
        hemo:7000148,
        hemo:7000149,
        hemo:7000150,
        hemo:7000156 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8000071 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000061 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 rheo:0000032 ;
    hemo:9000002 heto:4000012,
        heto:4201203 ;
    hemo:9000008 "Divides the difference in expected cost by the difference in expected health effect, both taken as the intervention minus the comparator. The result reads as cost per unit of effect gained only when the effect difference is positive." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Two-option incremental cost-effectiveness ratio" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Black WC. The CE plane: a graphic representation of cost-effectiveness. Medical Decision Making. 1990;10(3):212-214. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X9001000308",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 31 March 2026. Chapter 4 Economic evaluation, section 4.2.16 (ICERs as the ratio of expected additional total cost to expected additional QALYs, with standard decision rules reflecting dominance and extended dominance). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 31 March 2026. Chapter 6 Committee recommendations, sections 6.3.4, 6.3.7 and 6.3.8 (ICERs of £25,000 and £35,000 per QALY gained) and 6.3.9 (the usual levels of £25,000 to £35,000 per QALY apply in the south-west quadrant). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations",
        "Weinstein MC, Stason WB. Foundations of cost-effectiveness analysis for health and medical practices. New England Journal of Medicine. 1977;296(13):716-721. https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJM197703312961304" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Averaging ICERs across simulations. The mean of ICERs from probabilistic simulations is not the ratio of mean incremental cost to mean incremental effect, and individual ratios become unstable when incremental effects are close to zero. The ICER is formed from expected values, and expected net benefit is used to compare options under uncertainty.",
        "Comparing ICERs of non-adjacent options. With several mutually exclusive options, ICERs calculated against one common baseline, or between options that are not adjacent on the frontier, can make a dominated or extendedly dominated option look acceptable. The pairwise formula applies to adjacent options only after dominated options have been removed, which is the sequential ICER on the cost-effectiveness analysis page.",
        "Ranking negative ICERs by size. A negative ICER arises both when the intervention dominates and when it is dominated, and its magnitude carries no ranking information: larger savings push the ratio towards minus infinity while larger health gains pull it towards zero.",
        "Reading a south-west ratio as cost per unit gained. When incremental cost and incremental effect are both negative the ICER is positive, but it measures the saving per unit of effect forgone. A value below the threshold then favours the comparator, so applying the usual rule reverses the correct conclusion." ;
    hemo:9000014 "ICER = (C_1 - C_0) / (E_1 - E_0)" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>ICER</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">divide</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><ci>C_1</ci><ci>C_0</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">minus</csymbol><ci>E_1</ci><ci>E_0</ci></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{ICER} = \\frac{C_{1} - C_{0}}{E_{1} - E_{0}}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-ICER-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "two-option-incremental-cost-effectiveness-ratio" .

hemo:5000643 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "DALYs for a cause as the sum of its years of life lost and years lived with disability"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "HEMO-HOOK-05 means the DALY of the Global Burden of Disease study from 2010 onwards, with prevalence-based years lived with disability (HE-FM-DWT-001) and no discounting or age weighting, the conventions module assumption HE-AS-DALY-002 states. Years of life lost and years lived with disability are both healthy years lost, so both are DALYs."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-DALY-001" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-DALY-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000139 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7003726 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6002755,
        hemo:6002756,
        hemo:6002757 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6002756,
        hemo:6002757 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6002755 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7003724,
        hemo:7003725 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7003727,
        hemo:7003728,
        hemo:7003729,
        hemo:7003733,
        hemo:7003737 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8001531 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000788 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000001 oheo:0000263,
        rheo:0000094 ;
    hemo:9000004 "HEMO-HOOK-05" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Adds the fatal and non-fatal parts of the health loss from one cause, both calculated for the same cause, sex, age group and year, as in WHO's Global Health Estimates and the Global Burden of Disease (GBD) study. One DALY is one lost year of healthy life, so a death and a period of illness are counted on one scale. Each part comes from its own formula, and the DALY is their sum." ;
    hemo:9000009 "DALYs for a cause as the sum of its years of life lost and years lived with disability" ;
    hemo:9000010 "GBD 2021 Diseases and Injuries Collaborators. Global incidence, prevalence, years lived with disability (YLDs), disability-adjusted life-years (DALYs), and healthy life expectancy (HALE) for 371 diseases and injuries in 204 countries and territories and 811 subnational locations, 1990-2021, a systematic analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study 2021. The Lancet. 2024;403(10440) 2133-2161 (full text read). Methods, where YLLs are deaths multiplied by the standard life expectancy at the age of death and DALYs are calculated by summing YLDs and YLLs. Results, with 907 million YLDs (31.3%) and 1.98 billion YLLs (68.7%) of the 2.88 billion DALYs in 2021. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(24)00757-8",
        "World Health Organization. WHO methods and data sources for global burden of disease estimates 2000-2019. Global Health Estimates Technical Paper WHO/DDI/DNA/GHE/2020.3. Geneva, WHO, 2020 (full text read). Section 2, which calculates DALYs for a cause as the sum of the YLLs from that cause and the YLDs for people living in states of less than good health resulting from it, DALY(c,s,a,t) = YLL(c,s,a,t) + YLD(c,s,a,t) for cause c, age a, sex s and year t. Section 2.1 lists the GBD 2010 changes, including adjustment for comorbidity in YLDs and no discounting or unequal age weights, and section 2.3 the original 3% rate and age weights. https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/gho-documents/global-health-estimates/ghe2019_daly-methods.pdf" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Adding discounted years of life lost to undiscounted years lived with disability. Discounting one part and not the other mixes conventions. In the article's untreated cohort, YLLs discounted continuously at 3% (493.84) added to undiscounted YLDs (540) give 1,033.84 DALYs, which matches neither the undiscounted 1,440 nor the discounted 960.37 (computed here for illustration).",
        "Summing cause-specific DALYs built from YLDs not adjusted for comorbidity. YLDs computed separately for each cause count the health loss of people with several conditions more than once, so a sum over causes overstates total DALYs. GBD 2010 and later rounds adjust YLDs for comorbidity before adding them to YLLs (HE-FM-DWT-004 on the disability weight page)." ;
    hemo:9000014 "DALY = YLL + YLD" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>DALY</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">plus</csymbol><ci>YLL</ci><ci>YLD</ci></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{DALY} = \\mathrm{YLL} + \\mathrm{YLD}" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-DALY-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "dalys-for-a-cause-as-the-sum-of-its-years-of-life-lost-and-years-lived-with-disability" .

