hemo:5000198 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        hemo:0000010 ;
    rdfs:label "Pairwise dominance rule including ties"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "HEMO states the tie tolerances tau_C and tau_E as inputs, because modelled costs and QALYs are rarely exactly equal in floating point, so a difference no larger than its tolerance counts as equal. With both tolerances 0 this is the module's formula."@en,
        "The one HEMO dominance rule, weak or Pareto dominance (decision 14, confirmed by the curators on 19 October 2026). An option dominates when it costs no more and is no less effective and is strictly better on one of the two, with equality read within the stated tolerances. Its mirror is HEMO-FM-DOMD-001, strict dominance is HEMO-FM-DOMS-001 and the four-way classification is HEMO-FM-DOMC-001. Extended dominance stays a separate rule, HE-FM-EXD-001, applied after this screen. RHEO competency query cq38 differs in two cases, equal QALYs at a lower cost, which HEMO calls dominant and cq38 calls less effective and less costly, and identical cost and QALYs, which HEMO calls a duplicate and cq38 calls dominated."@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000119 "Drummond et al. 2015; Briggs et al. 2006; the Source rows of the Functions and Formulae module record HE-FM-DOM-001" ;
    dcterms:identifier "HE-FM-DOM-001" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/hemo/hemo-formulae.owl> ;
    hemo:1000001 hemo:5000014 ;
    hemo:1000005 hemo:7000224 ;
    hemo:1000006 hemo:6000586,
        hemo:6000587,
        hemo:6000588,
        hemo:6005588,
        hemo:6005589 ;
    hemo:1000007 hemo:6000586,
        hemo:6000587,
        hemo:6005588,
        hemo:6005589 ;
    hemo:1000008 hemo:6000588 ;
    hemo:1000009 hemo:7000221,
        hemo:7000222,
        hemo:7000223 ;
    hemo:1000010 hemo:7000225,
        hemo:7000226,
        hemo:7000227,
        hemo:7000231,
        hemo:7000235,
        hemo:7005982,
        hemo:7005986,
        hemo:7007346 ;
    hemo:1000011 hemo:8001530 ;
    hemo:1000018 hemo:8000144 ;
    hemo:1000026 hemo:5000199 ;
    hemo:1000035 hemo:4000017 ;
    hemo:2000019 true ;
    hemo:9000002 heto:4201202 ;
    hemo:9000003 "rheo 0.3.0 cq38-results-against-threshold.rq" ;
    hemo:9000008 "Classifies whether an intervention dominates its comparator: it costs no more, produces no less health and is strictly better on at least one of the two. The intervention is dominated when the same rule holds with the comparison reversed." ;
    hemo:9000009 "Pairwise dominance rule including ties" ;
    hemo:9000010 "Black WC. The CE plane: a graphic representation of cost-effectiveness. Medical Decision Making. 1990;10(3):212-214. https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X9001000308",
        "Drummond MF, Sculpher MJ, Claxton K, Stoddart GL, Torrance GW. Methods for the Economic Evaluation of Health Care Programmes. 4th ed. Oxford: Oxford University Press; 2015. https://global.oup.com/academic/product/methods-for-the-economic-evaluation-of-health-care-programmes-9780199665884",
        "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36). Published 31 January 2022, last updated 31 March 2026. Chapter 4 Economic evaluation, sections 4.2.16 (standard decision rules reflecting dominance and extended dominance) and 4.10.8 (dominated technologies, described as more costly and less effective than another technology, removed from the fully incremental analysis). https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2" ;
    hemo:9000011 "Forgetting ties. Requiring both differences to be non-zero leaves equal-cost or equal-effect results unclassified, so an inferior tied option can remain in the analysis.",
        "Reading a negative ICER as dominance. A negative ratio arises both when the intervention dominates and when it is dominated. Only the signs of Delta_C and Delta_E show which." ;
    hemo:9000014 "DOM = (Delta_C <= tau_C) and (Delta_E >= -tau_E) and (Delta_C < -tau_C or Delta_E > tau_E)" ;
    hemo:9000015 "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">eq</csymbol><ci>DOM</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"logic1\">and</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"logic1\">and</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">leq</csymbol><ci>Delta_C</ci><ci>tau_C</ci></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">geq</csymbol><ci>Delta_E</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">unary_minus</csymbol><ci>tau_E</ci></apply></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"logic1\">or</csymbol><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">lt</csymbol><ci>Delta_C</ci><apply><csymbol cd=\"arith1\">unary_minus</csymbol><ci>tau_C</ci></apply></apply><apply><csymbol cd=\"relation1\">gt</csymbol><ci>Delta_E</ci><ci>tau_E</ci></apply></apply></apply></apply></math>" ;
    hemo:9000016 "\\mathrm{DOM} = \\left(\\Delta_{C} \\le \\tau_{C} \\land \\Delta_{E} \\ge -\\tau_{E}\\right) \\land \\left(\\Delta_{C} < -\\tau_{C} \\lor \\Delta_{E} > \\tau_{E}\\right)" ;
    hemo:9000018 "HE-FM-DOM-001" ;
    hemo:9000023 "pairwise-dominance-rule-including-ties" ;
    hemo:9000030 "DOM = (Delta_C <= 0) and (Delta_E >= 0) and (Delta_C < 0 or Delta_E > 0)" .

